window.ATLAS_PROOFS={"ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md":"# AC — coupled boundary channels and a global endpoint-energy identity\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\nno mathematical programs, CAS, enumeration or Lean. This is a bounded\nidentity for arbitrary exact invariant colorings, not a proof of Collatz\nor a construction of a nonconstant invariant. Independent review is recorded\nin [the AC review](REVIEW_0485_ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BRIDGE.md).\n\n## AC1. Definitions and scope\n\nLet T(n)=n/2 for even n and T(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n, and suppose\nbeta:N_positive -> {0,1} satisfies beta(T(n))=beta(n). Define\n\n    d(n)=|beta(n+1)-beta(n)|,\n    e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1),\n    L(n)=d(n)d(2n),   Rb(n)=d(n)d(2n+1).\n\nA birth is an index with e(n)=1. The definitions and retained birth\ngraph are those of [SR](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md);\nthe global birth rows are in\n[PC](PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md). All sums below use\nactual color indicators, not freely assigned symbolic masks.\n\nSince beta(2n)=beta(n) and beta(2n+2)=beta(n+1), the binary child\nidentity is d(2n) XOR d(2n+1)=d(n). The four possible states, listed as\n(d(n),d(2n),d(2n+1)), are\n\n    0=(0,0,0), E=(0,1,1), L=(1,1,0), Rb=(1,0,1).\n\nConsequently, pointwise,\n\n    d=L+Rb,   d(2n)=e(n)+L(n),   d(2n+1)=e(n)+Rb(n).       (AC1)\n\nWe have d(1)=0. For odd q, beta(4q+1)=beta(6q+2)=beta(3q+1)\n=beta(q)=beta(4q), so d(4q)=0. Hence e(n)=0 when n=2 mod4.\nAlso L(n)=0 if n=2 mod4 (its child d(2n) vanishes), and if n=4 mod8\n(d(n) vanishes). In particular, every even L-state is divisible by eight.\n\nDefine S(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n and R(n)=3n/4 for 4|n. Their respective\nimages are the positive integers in residues 2 and 0 modulo3. Every\nbirth has exactly one arithmetic outgoing branch. Retain a branch only\nwhen its target is also a birth. The images are disjoint and each branch\nis injective. Define the following birth subsets:\n\n- Z_S: odd birth sources whose S-target is not a birth;\n- Z_R: even birth sources whose R-target is not a birth;\n- D_S: births in residue2 whose unique arithmetic S-predecessor is not a birth;\n- D_R: births in residue0 whose unique arithmetic R-predecessor is not a birth;\n- P: births in residue1, which have no arithmetic predecessor.\n\nZ_S and Z_R are source indices; D_S and D_R are target indices. Keeping\nthat distinction is essential for the coefficients below.\n\n## AC2. The conserved channels\n\nFor odd n and y=S(n), invariance gives beta(2y)=beta(3n+1)=beta(n)\nand beta(2y+1)=beta(3n+2)=beta(2n+1). Therefore d(2y)=d(2n) and\n\n    e(S(n))+L(S(n))=e(n)+L(n).                         (AC2)\n\nFor n=4m and y=R(n)=3m, invariance gives\nbeta(8m+1)=beta(12m+2)=beta(6m+1) and\nbeta(8m+2)=beta(4m+1)=beta(6m+2). Thus d(2y+1)=d(2n+1) and\n\n    e(R(n))+Rb(R(n))=e(n)+Rb(n).                       (AC3)\n\nOnly the total channels e+L and e+Rb are conserved. Individual L and\nRb need not be conserved. In the four-state table D_S is exactly a\ntransition L -> E on S, and Z_S is E -> L. Likewise D_R is Rb -> E\non R, and Z_R is E -> Rb. These are constraints on actual colors, not\na claim that arbitrary paths in the table are realizable.\n\n## AC3. Uniform finite-cutoff convention\n\nFor a bounded indicator f write H_f(X)=sum_(1\u003c=n\u003c=X) f(n)/n, X>=1.\nAll masses in AC4–AC7 have the SAME cutoff X unless stated otherwise.\nEvery O(1) below has an absolute bound independent of X and beta.\n\nHere is the elementary transport justification. For fixed positive a and\nfixed b, on any integer subset where an+b is positive,\n\n    1/(an+b)=a^(-1)/n+O(n^(-2)).\n\nThe constant depends only on the fixed map; finitely many small n are\nabsorbed into it. The sum of the error over any indicator subset is\nbounded by a constant times sum n^(-2). Changing a cutoff from X to\n(X-b)/a adds or removes a fixed multiplicative annulus, whose harmonic\nsum is uniformly bounded; the finitely many small-X cases are harmless.\nThis proves every affine reindexing used below with a uniform O(1).\nFor linear maps b=0 there is no reciprocal correction, but there is\nstill a cutoff correction. All subtractions below are of finite sums.\n\nWrite A,B for odd/even birth masses; E=A+B; E_i for birth mass in\nresidue i modulo3, so E=E_0+P+E_2 and P=E_1. Write L_i,Rb_i for\nchannel masses by residue, and L,Rb,D for total channel and boundary\nmasses. Set\n\n    U=L_odd, W=L_even, V=Rb_(4|n), so L=U+W.\n\nUse the same symbols Z_S,Z_R,D_S,D_R for their harmonic masses.\n\n## AC4. Binary parity and residue balances\n\nSumming the two child identities in AC1, with d(1)=0, gives\n\n    D=(E+L)/2+(E+Rb)/2+O(1),\n    D=L+Rb=2E+O(1).                                  (AC4)\n\nActive boundaries divisible by four have total mass W+V, because every\neven L-state is divisible by four. Reindex d(2n)=e(n)+L(n) over even n:\n\n    W+V=(B+W)/2+O(1), hence W+2V=B+O(1).              (AC5)\n\nFor each residue i, let X_i and Y_i denote even and odd active-boundary\nmasses in that residue. AC1 and finite transport give\n\n    X_(2i mod3)=(E_i+L_i)/2+O(1),\n    Y_(2i+1 mod3)=(E_i+Rb_i)/2+O(1),\n    X_i+Y_i=D_i=L_i+Rb_i.                            (AC6)\n\nThe last line is exact. Thus parity is retained before summing, not\nassumed independent of residue. For residue0 and residue2 respectively:\n\n    L_0+2Rb_0-Rb_1=E_0+E_1+O(1),\n    2L_2+Rb_2-L_1=E_1+E_2+O(1).                     (AC7)\n\nSubtract the second equation from the first. The E_1 terms cancel, and\nthe left side is L-3L_2+3Rb_0-Rb. Therefore\n\n    Rb-L=3(Rb_0-L_2)-E_0+E_2+O(1).                  (AC8)\n\nThe E_1 terms must not be omitted separately; their cancellation here\nis an explicit algebraic step. No additional parity/residue lemma is\nrequired beyond AC6.\n\n## AC5. Coupling the binary and arithmetic transports\n\nSince S and R cover exactly residues2 and0 respectively, AC2–AC3 give\n\n    E_2+L_2=(2/3)(A+U)+O(1),\n    E_0+Rb_0=(4/3)(B+V)+O(1).                       (AC9)\n\nHere the even-birth source mass of the R channel is exactly B, since\neven births are divisible by four. From AC4–AC5,\n\n    Rb-L=D-2L=2A-2U+4V+O(1).                        (AC10)\n\nSubstitution of AC9 into AC8 also gives\n\n    Rb-L=4B-2A+4V-2U-4E_0+4E_2+O(1).              (AC11)\n\nEquate AC10 and AC11. Then use E=A+B=E_0+P+E_2 to obtain\n\n    B-A=E_0-E_2+O(1),\n    A=E_2+P/2+O(1),  B=E_0+P/2+O(1).               (AC12)\n\nThese are global finite-cutoff identities for every exact beta, including\nall internal vertices. They do not assume endpoint negligibility or\nfiniteness of any infinite harmonic series.\n\n## AC6. The selected endpoint-energy bridge\n\nPartition the birth targets according to their unique predecessors.\nThe exact image decomposition of PC7, with finite harmonic cutoffs, is\n\n    E_2=(2/3)(A-Z_S)+D_S+O(1),\n    E_0=(4/3)(B-Z_R)+D_R+O(1).                       (AC13)\n\nCombine with AC12 and solve the two linear equations:\n\n    A=(3/2)P-2Z_S+3D_S+O(1),\n    B=4Z_R-3D_R-(3/2)P+O(1),\n    E=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1).                     (AC14)\n\nIn particular, an absolute C exists such that, for all X and beta,\n\n    E(X) \u003c= 4Z_R(X)+3D_S(X)+C,\n    P(X) \u003c= (8/3)Z_R(X)-2D_R(X)+C.                 (AC15)\n\nConsequently the following are equivalent, for each beta:\n\n    sum e(n)/n is finite;\n    both sum_(n in Z_R)1/n and sum_(n in D_S)1/n are finite.       (AC16)\n\nThe forward implication uses that both sets are birth subsets. For the\nreverse implication, bound the two positive terms in AC15 and then use\nmonotone convergence of the finite birth sums. No difference of divergent\nseries is taken. AC16 is a reduction, not a proof of either finiteness.\nIf E(X) diverges, AC15 implies\n\n    liminf_(X->infinity) [4Z_R(X)+3D_S(X)]/E(X) >= 1.             (AC17)\n\nThis is a GLOBAL weighted transport identity; it does not assert a\nuniform bound for the mass of each component by its individual origin.\nIt therefore does not remove the earlier unmasked component barrier by\nassuming away its examples.\n\n## AC7. Dirichlet transfer and the exclusion of PC12\n\nThe same bounded identity transfers directly to s>1 without guessing\nlimits of parity fractions. Put Q(X)=E(X)-4Z_R(X)-3D_S(X)\n+2Z_S(X)+3D_R(X). AC14 says |Q(X)|\u003c=C. For epsilon=s-1>0,\nfinite partial summation, followed by the absolutely convergent limit,\ngives the corresponding signed Dirichlet sum\n\n    Q_s=epsilon integral_(1 to infinity) Q(t)t^(-epsilon-1) dt.\n\nExplicitly, if q(n)=e(n)-4*1_Z_R(n)-3*1_D_S(n)\n+2*1_Z_S(n)+3*1_D_R(n), then Q(X)=sum_(n\u003c=X) q(n)/n,\nwhereas Q_s=sum q(n)n^(-s). Thus the summation is against n^(-epsilon)\nwith coefficients q(n)/n. The factor is epsilon=s-1, not s, because\nQ is already harmonically weighted; it is not the counting sum of q(n).\n\nThus |Q_s|\u003c=C for EVERY s>1, and\n\n    E_s=4Z_R,s+3D_S,s-2Z_S,s-3D_R,s+O(1),            (AC18)\n\nwith a uniform bound as s decreases to1. If E_s tends to infinity along\na sequence, (4Z_R,s+3D_S,s)/E_s has liminf at least1. Therefore a\ndivergent energy with all origin and terminal ratios tending to zero\nis impossible. This excludes precisely the endpoint/origin-negligible\nregime left open for actual masks in PC12.\n\nThe PC scalar assignment remains feasible for the OLD selected projection.\nIt was never an actual beta construction. AC6–AC9 add constraints missing\nfrom that projection; the new exclusion does not invalidate the correctly\nscoped old scalar feasibility statement. No claim that all other divergent\nregimes have now been excluded is made.\n\nFor comparison with exact channel Dirichlet bookkeeping: the affine\ncorrection C_all,s in E_2,s+L_2,s is over all odd e+L sources; C_ret,s\nin PC7 is over retained odd births ONLY. Their difference is the\nnonnegative, uniformly bounded correction over odd L sources plus Z_S\nsources. Thus the subtracted L-channel row uses C_all,s-C_ret,s, not\nthe correction over L alone. The direct finite-cutoff proof above avoids\nneeding either correction's numerical value.\n\n## AC8. Remaining work and dependencies\n\nThe missing input is now a finite harmonic-energy bound on Z_R and D_S,\nor a new joint estimate whose positive and negative charges in AC14\nforce bounded E(X). Existing separate upper estimates with large enough\ncoefficients do not provide this. P is controlled by Z_R in AC15, but\nneither quantity has been proved finite. A promising next bounded question\nis whether the positive charge 4Z_R+3D_S can be matched with the negative\ncharges 2Z_S+3D_R along actual active-boundary paths, retaining shared-color\nconstraints and all initial path depths.\n\nAC does not use the named PD170 conditional input. Finite birth energy,\nif later proved, still requires the separately recorded pointwise bridge\nfor a full Collatz conclusion. Its full analytic dependencies and Lean\nbuild have not been independently recertified. No nonconstant beta,\ncounterexample, all-cycle exclusion, complete proof, completion percentage,\ntime estimate or literature-priority claim is supplied here.\n\nThe source role's targeted primary-source reading is recorded in\n[0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_ACTIVE_COUPLING_SOURCE_AUDIT.md). No theorem from an\nunread or withdrawn paper is used in AC1–AC18. Four roles consist of root\nand reused helpers0431,0459,0483; no additional distinct agents are counted.\nThe results and updated map are local; the completed public snapshot425\nand distribution/cleanup are separate and unchanged.\n","ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md":"# CE — the critical birth energy and the actual contradiction target\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual analysis only.\nNo numerical experiments, CAS, enumeration or Lean. This corrects the\nchoice of a sufficient upper-bound target after PBK and TS. It does not\nestablish that upper bound or prove Collatz.\n\n## 1. Exact finite energy identity\n\nLet β:N₊→{0,1} satisfy β(Tn)=β(n) for shortcut T. Put\n\n    d(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)|,       e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1),\n    R(X)=Σ_(1≤n\u003cX)e(n),\n    D₁(N)=Σ_(1≤n≤N)d(n)/n,   E₁(N)=Σ_(1≤n≤N)e(n)/n.          (CE1)\n\nThe sums D₁,E₁ include the endpoint N; the counting function R does not.\nExact doubling gives d(1)=0 and the archived birth law\n\n    d(2n)+d(2n+1)=d(n)+2e(n).                                (CE2)\n\nFor an integer N≥1 multiply CE2 by 1/(2n) and sum. Define\n\n    Δ(N)=Σ_(n=1)^N d(2n+1)[1/(2n)−1/(2n+1)].\n\nThe left side of the weighted CE2 sum is D₁(2N+1)+Δ(N), while\nthe right side is D₁(N)/2+E₁(N). Hence, exactly,\n\n    E₁(N)−D₁(N)/2 = Σ_(m=N+1)^(2N+1)d(m)/m + Δ(N).          (CE3)\n\nBoth terms are nonnegative. The shell contains N+1 terms, each at most\n1/(N+1), so it is at most 1. Moreover,\n\n    0≤Δ(N)≤Σ_(n≥1)∫_(2n)^(2n+1)t^(−2)dt\n          ≤∫_2^∞t^(−2)dt=1/2.\n\nConsequently,\n\n    0≤E₁(N)−D₁(N)/2≤3/2,            N≥1.                    (CE4)\n\nThus D₁(N)=2E₁(N)+O(1), with an absolute bounded error, and\nD₁(N)/E₁(N)→2 whenever either sum diverges. This is more precise at\nfinite cutoffs than merely asserting equivalent convergence. It is not\nan upper bound for E₁ or D₁ separately. The convergence equivalence and\nthe critical criterion themselves were already in H175, BT14–BT20.\n\n## 2. What two positive color densities already force\n\nAssume additionally that for some δ>0,\n\n    lower_density{β=0}≥δ,    lower_density{β=1}≥δ.              (CE5)\n\nThese are exactly the explicit density assumptions of PBK. If E₁(∞)\nwere finite, CE4 would make D₁(∞) finite. The unconditional part of\n[SB174.1–7](../../../work/round174-n-summable-boundary-rigidity.md)\nthen gives β=c outside a set of natural density zero, for some c∈{0,1}.\nThis contradicts CE5. Therefore\n\n    Σ_(n≥1)e(n)/n=∞.                                         (CE6)\n\nNo additional PD input is needed after CE5 has been assumed. PD170 is\nthe named external route used elsewhere to supply CE5 for an arbitrary\nnonconstant exact invariant. It is not an extra hypothesis separating\nthe critical conclusion CE6 from the explicitly conditional PBK theorem.\n\nFor clarity, the unconditional SB174 step preserves the actual β:\nfinite harmonic boundary energy makes the dyadic profiles\nF_k(x)=β(floor(2^k x)) converge in L¹ and almost everywhere on [1,2).\nThe limit is binary and extends by F(2x)=F(x). The exact odd branch,\ntogether with the vanishing average boundary, gives F(3x)=F(x) almost\neverywhere. On the logarithmic circle this is invariance under the\nirrational rotation log₂3, forcing a constant binary limit. Summing\nthe dyadic windows gives a color of natural density zero. This last\ncontradiction uses CE5; no assertion about isolated points is inferred\nfrom an almost-everywhere statement alone.\n\n## 3. The counting growth already required along a subsequence\n\nCE6 implies the following quantified statement:\n\n    for every 0\u003cε\u003c1, C>0 and X₀, there is X≥X₀ such that\n    R(X)>C X^(1−ε).                                          (CE7)\n\nIndeed, if R(X)≤C X^(1−ε) for all sufficiently large X, then on\neach dyadic shell [2^j,2^(j+1)) the harmonic contribution is at most\n\n    2^(−j) R(2^(j+1))≤C 2^(1−ε) 2^(−εj).\n\nThe geometric series converges, contradicting CE6. Finite initial\nshells do not affect convergence. Since R(X)≤X, CE7 yields\n\n    limsup_(X→∞) log(1+R(X))/log X = 1.                       (CE8)\n\nThis is a subsequential growth exponent. It does not give R(X)≥cX\non any sequence, a positive density, or R(X)≥X^(1−ε) at every large X.\nPBK separately gives the uniform eventual lower bound D X^(1/10),\nso under its density assumptions the two conclusions are compatible:\n\n    liminf log(1+R(X))/log X ≥ 1/10,\n    limsup log(1+R(X))/log X = 1.\n\nThe second statement is an elementary corollary of the archived\ncritical criterion, not a newly proved general Collatz theorem.\n\n## 4. The sufficient upper target is weaker than a PBK contradiction\n\nEither of the following estimates for the actual R, with ε>0, would\nmake E₁(∞) finite:\n\n    R(X)=O(X^(1−ε)),\n    R(X)=O(X/(log X)^(1+ε)).                                 (CE9)\n\nThe first was checked by the shell estimate above. For the second,\nthe same shell contribution is O(j^(−1−ε)), which is summable.\nThese sufficient criteria are already present in SB174/H175; we are\nrestoring them as the correct target rather than imposing the stronger\nrequirement R(X)=o(X^(1/10)). Even a uniform X^0.99 bound would suffice\nunder CE5, although it would not directly contradict the displayed\nPBK exponent1/10.\n\nNone of CE9 has been proved for an actual nonconstant exact invariant.\nFinding an independent incompatible upper bound would be a valid proof\nby contradiction. Existing conditional lower bounds do not show that\nsuch a proof is impossible.\n\nTS still obstructs obtaining CE9 from its cover masses alone: those\nupper majorants remain at least of order X/√(log X) in the useful\ncommon-horizon regime. Lower bounds for that cover mass are never\nlower bounds for the actual R.\n\n## 5. A residue transfer with the missing color explicitly retained\n\nThe residue audit reproduces the structural exclusions\ne(8m+1)=e(8m+2)=e(8m+6)=0. The first is already a consequence of\n[PA179.4, PA8](../../../work/round179-k-pair-coalescence-algebra.md):\nd(16m+2)=0 follows from four-step structural meeting on that class.\nThe other two are the old H175 exclusion e(4m+2)=0. They are not new\nproof progress and the bound R(X)≤5X/8+O(1) is too weak for CE9.\n\nFor every integer M≥0, the exact residue formulas instead give the\nfollowing useful way to display the unresolved comparison:\n\n    e(4M+1) XOR e(3M+1)\n       =(1−d(3M+1))d(9M+4).                                 (CE10)\n\nProof: set a=β(3M+1), b=β(3M+2), c=β(9M+4), h=β(9M+5).\nThen e(4M+1)=1{a=b≠c} and e(3M+1)=1{a=b≠h}. If a≠b both\nvanish; if a=b their XOR is |c−h|. This proves CE10.\n\nFor M≥1 the second birth index is smaller, but equality of the births\nrequires control of the right side. Simply dropping it would be an\nincorrect descent. When e(4M+1)=1, its mask is1; a successful transfer\ntherefore specifically requires d(9M+4)=0. If that boundary is1,\nthe smaller birth vanishes. The individual condition at a birth does\nnot establish that the missing boundary vanishes for every such M.\nNo global invariant realizing arbitrary locally assigned colors is\nclaimed, and no impossibility theorem for a different descent is proved.\n\n## 6. Remaining step and source scope\n\nThe concrete next target is the critical energy of actual births,\nusing exact relations such as CE10 with their defect terms retained.\nAn upper estimate for those defects must retain their correlation with\nthe birth condition. Replacing them by independent densities, discarding\nthem, or using only structural survival would repeat the known gaps.\n\nClassical results on ×2,×3-invariant closed sets or invariant measures\ndo not supply the missing profile compactness. The source audit records\ntheir distinct hypotheses and corrects an initially misattributed survey.\nThose external theorems are not inputs to CE1–CE10.\n\nFull Collatz, all positive cycles and isolated bad starts remain open.\nThis file records a finite energy identity, a corrected comparison of\nexisting criteria, and a defect-preserving local transfer. No external\nreview, formal verification or worldwide novelty is asserted.\n","BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md":"# Оценка Бейкера и разрыв двух высот\n\n16 сентября 2026. Аналитическая проверка 0217 и координатора.\nМатематических исполнений — 0. Известный внешний вход:\n[Evertse, Linear Forms in Logarithms, April 2011, Corollary 1.6,\nстр. 2–3](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~evertsejh/dio2011-linforms.pdf).\nПроверены эта формулировка и её доказательство, не все 13 страниц.\n\nДля q≥1,0≤R≤q положим k=2^q−3^R и M=max(2^q,3^R).\nРавенство k=0 невозможно по единственности разложения на простые.\nПрименяя известное следствие Бейкера к отношению меньшей степени\nк большей, с базами 2,3 и целыми показателями ±q,∓R, получаем\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{M/|k|\\le(eq)^C.}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь C зависит только от фиксированных баз 2 и 3. Численно C не\nвычислялась. Нулевой показатель R=0 и q=1 допустимы.\n\nПусть теперь U имеет длину i и S≥1 единиц, W — длину q и R единиц,\nc=C_W, A=2^i c−kC_U≠0, v=v₃(A), A#=|A|/3^v.\nПримитивная высота вектора (A,2^q3^S,3^(S+R)) равна h. Точно\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}=3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right).\n\\]\n\nПри k\u003c0 имеем A>0 и A/3^S≥|k|/3. Поэтому\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\\le h/A^\\#\n \\le3(eq)^C3^{\\max(v-S,0)}.}\n\\]\n\nЭто единая оценка по всем словам U,W данной области. Если отдельно\nустановлено v\u003cS, то log(h/A#)=O(log(q+1)). При q\u003cN,N→∞ разрыв\nравен o(N), без фиксированного зазора между степенями и без q=o(N).\nПодходящие условия исключения v≥S даны в\n[лемме о целой ветви](INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md).\n\nБолее общо, если для некоторого фиксированного K₀>0 отдельно получено\n|A|/3^S≥|k|/K₀ и v\u003cS, то h/A#≤max(1,K₀)(eq)^C при любом знаке k.\nВ частности, точные условия F и G в\n[0210/0211](GROWING_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md) дают K₀=6 на хвосте;\nпосле этого Бейкер автоматически контролирует прежние положительные\nчасти qlog2−log|k| и Rlog3−log|k| при q\u003cN.\n\nПри k>0 нижняя оценка |A| сама по себе из Бейкера не следует:\nв A возможна обычная отмена. При S=0 оценка C_U/3^S≥1/3 неприменима.\nТочный нуль A бывает лишь при полностью нулевых U,W; тогда A#\nне определено. В ветви k\u003c0 нуль невозможен.\n\nЭто применение известной теоремы, а не её открытие. Оценка не\nобеспечивает ни нужных повторов, ни положительной маржи для всех\nцелых стартов, ни полного доказательства Коллатца.\n\n[Приёмка](REVIEW_0217.md) · [полный отчёт](task-0217.md).\n","BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md":"# SR — a partial birth graph, two sparse cores and an origin estimate\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\nno mathematical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean. The statements concern\nan arbitrary exact binary invariant. No nonconstant invariant, full Collatz\nproof, exclusion of all cycles or novelty claim is supplied.\n\n## SR1. Definitions and inherited facts\n\nLet T(n)=n/2 for even n and T(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n. Let\nβ:N>0→{0,1} satisfy β(T(n))=β(n). Write\n\n    d(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)|, e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1),\n    Eβ(X)=Σ_(n≤X)e(n)/n.\n\nA birth is an n with e(n)=1. Its outer color is\nβ(n)=β(n+1)≠β(3n+2). The XOR identity\nd(2n) XOR d(2n+1)=d(n) implies d(n)=0 at a birth.\nEvery active boundary k has a unique nearest birth ancestor b, found by\nrepeatedly taking floor(k/2) until the first zero boundary. This works\nbecause d(1)=0. Each birth generates two active paths, with at most two\nboundaries at depth ℓ, each at least 2^ℓ b; the first left boundary is\neven. Also e(n)=1 implies e(2n)=e(2n+1)=0.\n\nWe use e(1)=e(2)=0 and e(4m+2)=0 from SG1. Also e(8m+1)=0:\nthe paths from n=8m+1 and 3n+2=24m+5 meet at 9m+2, via\n12m+2→6m+1→9m+2 and 36m+8→18m+4→9m+2, respectively.\nThus β(n)=β(3n+2), which excludes a birth. We also use the odd rule\nS(n)=(3n+1)/2 and the inverse-S origin estimate from\n[SG1–SG5](SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md):\n\n    e(S(n))=1−d(S(n)) for an odd birth n,\n    E_(D_S)(X)≤(4/9)Eβ(X/6).\n\nHere D_S consists of births in residue 2 modulo 3 whose unique arithmetic\nS-predecessor is not a birth. These are exact color-mask statements.\n\n## SR2. An even rule with a smaller index\n\nFor an even birth n=4m of outer color c, invariance gives\n\n    β(m)=β(3m+1)=c, β(6m+1)=1−c.\n\nSet R(n)=3n/4=3m and x=β(3m). Then\nd(6m)=x XOR (1−c), d(6m+1)=1, and d(3m)=x XOR c. Therefore\n\n    e(R(n))=1−d(R(n)).                                (SR2)\n\nIf the output is a birth, it is smaller and has color c. Otherwise R(n)\nis an active boundary and its nearest birth b satisfies\nb≤floor(3n/8)\u003cn. This holds for every even birth, not just S-origins.\nA pure R-chain is finite in both directions: the forward rule consumes\ntwo factors of 2 and its inverse consumes one factor of 3. If a is the\nsmallest, terminal member, its component weight is at most 4/a. This\ndoes not give 4 divided by the directed origin, which can be much larger.\n\n## SR3. The injective union graph and its actual mask\n\nDefine a partial arithmetic map on positive integers by\n\n    F(n)=S(n) if n is odd;\n    F(n)=R(n) if 4 divides n;\n    F(n) undefined if n≡2 mod4.\n\nEvery birth is in this domain. The S-image is precisely {2 mod3},\nand the R-image is precisely {0 mod3}. Each branch is injective and\nthe two images are disjoint, so F is injective. Its partial inverse is\n\n    B(y)=4y/3 if y≡0 mod3;\n    B(y)=(2y−1)/3 if y≡2 mod3;\n    B(y) undefined if y≡1 mod3.\n\nAll defined images and inverses are positive. The retained graph Fβ\nhas all births as vertices and retains n→F(n) only when F(n) is also\na birth. It is an injective partial functional graph. Mixed cycles and\ninfinite components have not been excluded.\n\nThis arithmetic map is a familiar bounded-exponent Collatz restriction\nin another coordinate: μ=2n+1 is odd. On the S branch,\nT(μ)=2S(n)+1 with exact odd acceleration exponent 1. On the R branch,\nμ=8m+1 and T²(μ)=6m+1=2R(n)+1, with exact exponent 2. No new\narithmetic map or priority is claimed on the basis of this coordinate.\n\n## SR4. Exact forward word counts\n\nA fixed word of length k, with s S-letters and r R-letters, has its\nexact arithmetic domain in one residue class modulo 2^A, A=s+2r.\nTo check the induction, after j letters write the output as\n(3^j n+C)/2^(s_j+2r_j). On the current residue class, varying n by\nits modulus changes this output by the odd multiplier 3^j. Requiring\nan odd output for the next S guard therefore selects one of two lifts;\nrequiring divisibility by four for the next R guard selects one of\nfour lifts. Every word has infinitely many positive realizations.\nDistinct words have disjoint domains because their first different\nguards are disjoint. Birth masks can only remove realizations.\n\nLet N_k^+(X) count positive starts at most X admitting k F-steps.\nCounting a residue class by at most X/2^A+1 gives\n\n    N_k^+(X)≤X(1/2+1/4)^k+2^k=X(3/4)^k+2^k.          (SR4)\n\nLet Γ+ consist of starts admitting all forward iterates, including\ncyclic starts. Put α=ln2/ln(8/3)\u003c1 and k=floor(log_(8/3)X) for X≥1.\nThen 2^k≤X^α and X(3/4)^k≤(4/3)X^α, so\n\n    N_(Γ+)(X)≤(7/3)X^α,\n    Σ_(n∈Γ+)1/n≤C+=7/[3(1−α)].                       (SR5)\n\nThe second bound follows directly from\nΣ1/n=∫_1^∞N(t)t^(−2)dt by nonnegative interchange of sum and integral.\nIt does not assume the series finite in advance or an orbit growth rate.\n\n## SR5. Backward words and a second summable core\n\nA fixed word of k B-branches selects exactly one residue class modulo\n3^k. After j inverse steps the affine multiplier in the numerator is\na product of 2 and 4, hence a unit modulo 3. One more guard selects one\nof three lifts of the previous residue. As above, positivity follows\nfrom each guard and different words are disjoint. Hence\n\n    N_k^−(X)≤X(2/3)^k+2^k.\n\nFor Γ−, the set admitting all backward iterates, let γ=ln2/ln3\u003c1\nand k=floor(log_3X). Then 2^k≤X^γ and\nX(2/3)^k≤(3/2)X^γ. The deliberately loose constants give\n\n    N_(Γ−)(X)≤(5/2)X^γ,\n    Σ_(n∈Γ−)1/n≤C−=5/[2(1−γ)].                       (SR6)\n\nThese are deterministic residue counts; no parity independence or\nalmost-everywhere stopping theorem is used.\n\n## SR6. What the two core estimates remove from the energy problem\n\nEach connected component of Fβ is a finite acyclic chain, a directed\ncycle, a one-sided infinite chain, or a two-sided infinite chain.\nInjectivity rules out branching and a tail feeding a cycle. Every vertex\nof a component other than a finite acyclic chain admits infinitely many\nforward or backward iterates. A finite cycle admits both. Therefore all\nthese vertices lie in Γ+∪Γ−, even though masks may remove other\narithmetic survivors.\n\nLet E_fin(X) be the harmonic sum of births at most X whose retained\ncomponent is a finite acyclic chain. Then\n\n    E_fin(X)≤Eβ(X)≤E_fin(X)+C++C−.                    (SR7)\n\nIn particular finite total birth energy is equivalent to finite total\nmass of the finite acyclic components. Neither finiteness has been\nproved. Summability of the other components does not mean that they are\nempty or that their cycles cannot exist.\n\nFor each fixed k, the arithmetic forward-prefix set contains whole\nnonempty residue classes and has divergent harmonic sum. The backward\nprefix set has the same property. Thus the bounds for the infinite\nintersections cannot be used as uniform bounds for finite-prefix mass.\n\n## SR7. Exact inverse-R origins\n\nFor a birth n=3m of outer color c, the arithmetic predecessor is q=4m.\nExact paths and the birth colors give\n\n    β(q)=β(m), β(q+1)=β(3m+1)=c,\n    β(3q+2)=β(6m+1)=1−c.\n\nFor example 4m+1→6m+2→3m+1, and\n6m+1→9m+2 shows the last color from the birth at 3m. Consequently\n\n    e(q)=1 iff β(m)=c.                               (SR8)\n\nIf m is odd, β(m)=β(3m+1)=c, so q is automatically a birth. An\ninverse-R failure therefore has even m. Since n=3m is then an even\nbirth, the exclusion e(4a+2)=0 forces 4|m. Write\n\n    m=2^h u, h≥2, u odd, k=3u; n=2^h k.\n\nFailure means β(k)=c≠β(u)=β(k+1), so d(k)=1. Its nearest birth obeys\n\n    b≤floor(k/2)\u003cn/2^(h+1)≤n/8.                     (SR9)\n\nNo assertion about d(k+1) is needed or established here.\n\n## SR8. Charging all inverse-R origins\n\nLet D_R be births in residue 0 modulo 3 whose R-predecessor is not a\nbirth. For a fixed odd k in SR7, possible targets 2^h k, h≥2, cannot\nhave adjacent exponents, because a birth n excludes birth 2n. Grouping\nthe exponent pairs (2,3),(4,5),… therefore gives\n\n    Σ_(admissible h)1/(2^h k)≤1/(3k).\n\nCharge k to its unique nearest birth b. Along the right active path,\nodd k can first occur at depth 1; their reciprocal sum is at most 1/b.\nOn the left path the first boundary is even, so odd k can first occur\nat depth 2; their reciprocal sum is at most 1/(2b). The two paths\nare disjoint, and all targets have a unique k. Multiplying these bounds\nby 1/3 yields the complete origin estimate\n\n    E_(D_R)(X)≤(1/2)Eβ(X/8).                          (SR10)\n\nThe cutoff follows from SR9 for each counted target. Larger targets\ncan be included on the right as a nonnegative overestimate.\n\n## SR9. The coefficient 17/18 applies only to origins\n\nLet O_F be the vertices with no incoming retained edge, and let\nP_1={n:e(n)=1, n≡1 mod3}. The disjoint arithmetic images give exactly\n\n    O_F=P_1 ⊔ D_S ⊔ D_R,\n    E_(O_F)(X)≤E_(P_1)(X)\n                 +(4/9)Eβ(X/6)+(1/2)Eβ(X/8).         (SR11)\n\nThe image-origin coefficients sum to 17/18. This is not a contraction\nfor total birth energy: the left side counts only origins, and P_1 is\nunbounded by this argument. Finite Fβ-components may move above and\nbelow their origins. Their total mass needs its own bound. At cutoff X\nan origin can even exceed X while a later vertex lies below X.\nThe earlier SG bound with coefficient 7/9 remains a separate valid\nfull-component estimate for the S-only graph; it is not transferred\nunchanged to mixed Fβ-components.\n\n## SR10. A barrier to a purely arithmetic origin factor\n\nFor L≥1 choose positive odd v with v≡1 mod3 and 3^L v≡1 mod8.\nOne can take v≡1 mod24 when L is even and v≡19 mod24 when L is odd.\nLet a=4^L v. It has no arithmetic predecessor because a≡1 mod3.\nIts first L steps are R, ending at y=3^L v, which is odd and 1 mod8.\nIts next output S(y) is 2 mod4, where F is undefined. This is its full\nfinite arithmetic component. If W is its harmonic mass, then\n\n    aW≥a/y=(4/3)^L →∞.                               (SR12)\n\nThus no universal W≤C/a holds on the unmasked arithmetic graph.\nThis is not a counterexample for actual birth components: e(y)=0 by\nthe 1 mod8 exclusion, so the displayed full component is never retained.\nA useful bound must use the actual masks or a different choice of\nweighting point, with all resulting multiplicities accounted for.\n\n## SR11. Rejected shortcuts and the remaining obligation\n\nThe initial primitive S-split gives the valid but noncontractive bound\n\n    Eβ(X)≤3E_(Q_odd)(X)+3Eβ(3X/4)+(7/9)Eβ(X/6)+1/4,\n\nwhere Q_odd consists of odd births outside 2 mod3. Its feedback\ncoefficients sum to 34/9; rescaling the argument does not justify\nEβ(3X/4)≤(3/4)Eβ(X). Neither primitive odd energy nor total energy\nhas been bounded by this route.\n\nFor an even birth 4m, K=6m+1 is active. The exact coupling is\ne(S(K))=d(2K)(1−d(S(K))); omitting the neighbor mask is false.\nFailure does not force the right active child. A full retained S-run\nalready contains its terminal inverse; that inverse is not a new descent.\nMinimality forbids a smaller birth, not a larger return.\n\nThe next concrete obligation is a mask-sensitive bound for the total\nmass of finite acyclic Fβ-components, accounting for both endpoints\nor suitable internal minima. P_1 and multiplicities must be retained.\nEven finite energy would need a separate justified implication to\nconstancy of β, unless a direct universal descent is established.\nNo percentage or completion time for Collatz is justified.\n\n## SR12. Primary-source scope and audit\n\nRoot read the introduction and beginning of Section 2 of Applegate and\nLagarias, [Density Bounds for the 3x+1 Problem II: Krasikov Inequalities](https://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/applegateII.pdf),\nmanuscript dated 15 December 1993 on its first page. The inverse constraints\nuse residue information modulo powers of 3. Their lower bounds for inverse\ntrees address a different quantity from SR4–SR6. No computed exponent,\nlinear-program certificate or general termination claim is imported.\nThe PDF text was read; an attempted page image was unavailable. A recent\nsearch crawl date must not be treated as the paper's publication date.\n\nThe earlier [SG source check](SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md)\nrecords the precise scope of the finite affine-word comparison. All SR\ninequalities above are derived directly. This limited reading is not an\nexhaustive novelty review. Independent manual review and corrections are\nrecorded in [the SR acceptance](REVIEW_0485_BIDIRECTIONAL_CORES.md).\n","BLOCK_DESCENT_HEIGHT_DICHOTOMY.md":"# Обычная отмена и настоящий спуск блока\n\n16 сентября2026. Кандидат координатора проверен0227 и независимо0229;\nоба полных отчёта прочитаны, формулировки исправлены. Математических\nисполнений —0. Это локальная алгебраическая лемма с известным входом Бейкера.\n\nПусть ξ=a/b>0, b>0 нечётно, m_t=T^tξ, а U,W — настоящие\nпоследовательные блоки кода: |U|=i, |W|=q≥1, j=i+q, S и R —\nчисла единиц. Обозначим c=C_W,k=2^q−3^R,A=2^ic−kC_U.\nТогда точно\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{A=k3^S\\xi+2^j(m_j-m_i).}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nДействительно, 2^im_i=3^Sξ+C_U и\n2^q(m_j−m_i)=c−km_i; подстановка даёт(1).\n\nДля любого блока **без уменьшения**, то есть m_j≥m_i, A>0 и\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{\\frac h{A^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C3^{\\max(v_3(A)-S,0)}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь h — примитивная высота тройки(A,2^q3^S,3^(S+R)),\nA#=|A|/3^v₃(A), C — константа известного Бейкера для баз2,3.\n\n## Проверка обеих знаковых ветвей\n\nПри k>0 и m_j≥m_i тождество(1) даёт A/3^S≥kξ. Поэтому\nточная формула h/A# и max(2^q,3^R)/|k|≤(eq)^C дают(2)\nс даже меньшим коэффициентом max(1,1/ξ).\n\nПри k\u003c0 автоматически m_j−m_i=(c+|k|m_i)/2^q>0.\nЕсли S≥1, то A/3^S≥|k|/3, как в\n[предыдущем сравнении](BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md), что даёт коэффициент3.\nЕсли S=0, имеем A=2^ic>0, v₃(A)=0 и c≥3^(R−1), поскольку R≥1.\nТак как2^q\u003c3^R, непосредственно h/A#≤3. Нулевой префикс таким\nобразом не требует ложной оценки C_U/3^S≥1/3.\n\n## Что означает большой разрыв высот\n\nДля семьи настоящих блоков одного ξ, с N→∞, q\u003cN, A≠0,\nпредположим, что независимо доказано\n\n\\[\n \\max(v_3(A)-S,0)=o(N).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nТогда на всех блоках без уменьшения(2) даёт log(h/A#)=o(N).\nСледовательно, если на подпоследовательности log(h/A#)≥εN,\nε>0 фиксировано, её достаточно большие члены удовлетворяют\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{m_j\u003cm_i.}\n\\]\n\nУсловие v₃(A)≤S уже достаточно для полиномиального контроля;\nv₃(A)\u003cS — один более сильный достаточный случай. Для применения\n[леммы полной точности](FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md), которая\nможет исключить v≥S, дополнительно нужны настоящее совпадение с\nUW∞ до N=i+q+L, ξ≠Φ₂(UW∞) и её условия роста. Одни фактические\nU,W дают лишь первые i+q символов; лишние L не предполагаются молча.\n\n## Края и предел результата\n\nA=0 ровно для полностью нулевых U,W. Такие положительные блоки\nстрого уменьшают значение; h/A# к нулю не применяется. При R=0\nвсегда m_j=m_i/2^q\u003cm_i. При m_j=m_i возможен только k>0,\nи A=k3^Sξ>0; оценка(2) верна. Однако повтор состояния даёт\nξ=Φ₂(UW∞), поэтому лемма полной точности с условием ξ≠r\nк этому циклическому случаю не применяется.\n\nСпуск m_j\u003cm_i не означает m_j\u003cξ. Бесконечно много отдельных\nспусков не образуют автоматически цепь с совпадающими концами.\nЛемма классифицирует возможный большой разрыв уже выбранных\nприближений; она не создаёт нужные повторы или спуск ниже старта,\nне исключает все дополнительные циклы и не решает Коллатца.\nМировой приоритет этой алгебраической записи не установлен.\n\n[Приёмка0227/0229](REVIEW_0227_0229.md) · [0227](task-0227.md) · [0229](task-0229.md).\n","COALESCENCE_AND_FINITE_PLATEAU_DEADLINES.md":"# 0473 — dominant coalescence and a finite plateau deadline\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Internally checked after independent audit and full root reading. No numerical\nexecution, enumeration, CAS, factorization or children. No novelty claim.\n\nUse the shortcut plus map T(x)=(3x+1)/2 for odd x, x/2 for even x.\nC_s(x)=3^{q_s(x)}/2^s. E_L consists of positive x with C_s(x)>1 for\nall 1\u003c=s\u003c=L, and mu_L=min E_L. E_L is infinite and periodic, not finite.\nSet C_0=1, E_0=positive integers. tau(x)=infinity means all positive\nprefix coefficients exceed 1. It is not itself a complete Collatz proof.\n\n## J1. Exact transfer at an actual common endpoint\n\nSuppose genuine positive-integer orbit segments satisfy\n\n    T^k(n)=T^r(m)=y, k>=1, r>=1, 0\u003cm\u003cn,\n    C_i(m)>1 for 1\u003c=i\u003c=r,\n    C_r(m)>=C_k(n).\n\nFor any L>=k such that n in E_L, the concatenated m trajectory has\n\n    C_{r+s}(m)=C_r(m) C_{k+s}(n)/C_k(n)>1,\n    1\u003c=s\u003c=L-k.\n\nThus m in E_{r+L-k}. If tau(n)=infinity, tau(m)=infinity.\nIf r>=k, nesting E_(j+1) subset E_j shows that a witness excludes\nn=mu_L for every L>=k.\nIf r\u003ck, the finite conclusion loses k-r levels: m in E_{L-(k-r)}.\nIt is WRONG to conclude m in E_L just from infinite-tail transfer.\n\nThis is an actual coalescence of two integer orbits, not a formal real\ninverse. The coefficient condition is sufficient, not necessary.\n\n## J2. A deadline for a plateau, despite the shorter replacement\n\nAssume J1 and n=mu_s for some s>=1. Since m belongs to E_r and m\u003cn,\nnecessarily s>r. If r>=k, then s>r>=k and J1 with L=s contradicts\nn=mu_s. Thus any such surviving minimum must have r\u003ck.\n\nPut d=k-r>0. If n belonged to E_{s+d}, then s+d>k because s>r.\nJ1 would place m in E_s, contradicting minimality. Therefore\n\n    n not in E_{s+d}, and mu_{s+d}>n=mu_s.\n\nSo this particular witness bounds the remaining plateau by d steps.\nIt does not require a uniform d over all n. Existence of a witness for\nall hypothetical permanently surviving positive integers is NOT proved.\n\n## J3. A fully explicit family crossing the multiple-of-three obstruction\n\nFor any integer t>=0, put\n\n    n=128t+79, m=108t+67, y=162t+101.\n\nThe trajectory from n has the exact word 111100:\n\n    n+1=16(8t+5),\n    T^j(n)=3^j 2^{4-j}(8t+5)-1, 0\u003c=j\u003c=4,\n    T^4(n)=648t+404=4(162t+101),\n    T^5(n)=324t+202, T^6(n)=y.\n\nThe first four inputs are odd and the next two inputs even; y is odd.\nThe trajectory from m reaches y in one odd step. Also\n\n    m=(27n+11)/32\u003cn, n-m=20t+12,\n    C_6(n)=81/64, C_1(m)=3/2>81/64.\n\nAll coefficient prefixes of the six-step word exceed 1. Therefore\nJ1 applies with k=6,r=1 and J2 gives\n\n    if mu_s=128t+79, then mu_{s+5}>mu_s.\n\nIn particular no member of this family is the smallest tau=infinity\nseed. No claim is made that any member actually is a finite mu_s.\n\nFor t=3u+1 this is the infinite family n=384u+207, all divisible by3,\nwith smaller m=324u+175. These starts cannot be endpoints of ANY\nadmissible predecessor from I5 of472, but the present argument uses\ntheir FUTURE common endpoint and is not subject to that obstruction.\nHand example:207->311->467->701->1052->526->263, and175->263.\nThis is finite arithmetic, not evidence for a divergent orbit.\n\n## J4. What coalescence would need to prove full Collatz\n\nFor convergence to1, a coefficient comparison is not required. If every\nn>1 has some smaller positive m and finite nonnegative k,r such that\nT^k(n)=T^r(m), then strong induction proves that every n reaches1.\nThe base1 is already done; convergence of m implies that of its forward\ntail y, hence that of n. Even n already has m=n/2,k=1,r=0.\n\nConversely if Collatz is true, choose m=1 and a forward hit of1 for n,\nwith r=0. Thus the unrestricted universal existence statement is just\nan equivalent criterion, not progress by itself. J3 supplies real\nwitnesses for a limited family; it does not prove universal existence.\n\nFor a least counterexample to convergence, ANY smaller coalescing start\ncontradicts minimality. For a least tau=infinity start, use J1's stronger\nprefix/coefficient conditions. Do not confuse these two minimal objects.\nThe reduction from coefficient survival to boundedness in470 used a\nseparate G100 input; it does not independently exclude nontrivial cycles.\n\n## J5. Scope and prior archive\n\nThe formulas for runs, inverse odd steps and affine coefficients are\nknown. The local archive GV166 and CB167 already used forward paths\nfollowed by pure odd predecessors and has entire families with no\nsuch size compensation through a specified finite prefix. J3 is not\na claim to have invented forward/backward descent. The new item to\naudit here is the finite-horizon loss and its exact plateau deadline.\n\n## J6. A bound for the first coefficient failure\n\nDefine tau(x)=min{s>=1:C_s(x)\u003c1}, with infinity if absent.\nC_s=1 is impossible for s>0: 3^q=2^s fails both for q=0 and q>0.\nUnder J1, if tau(m)=a is finite then a>r. Put L=a+k-r>k.\nThe common-tail identity directly gives\n\n    C_L(n)=C_k(n) C_a(m)/C_r(m)\u003c1.\n\nConsequently tau(n)\u003c=tau(m)+k-r. In J3 this is\n\n    tau(128t+79)\u003c=tau(108t+67)+5\n\nwhenever the right-hand tau is finite. No uniform bound on tau(m)\nhas been supplied. Infinite-seed exclusion already follows directly\nfrom J1; stabilization of mu_L is not a required additional input.\n\n\n[Full corrected audit](task-0473.md) · [Acceptance and limits](REVIEW_0473.md) · [Primary-source and archive comparison](../../../work/collatz-source-scope-0473.md).\n","COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md":"# CZ — exact coalescence, forced defect zeros and a conditional density lemma\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual analysis.\nNo numerical program, enumeration, CAS or Lean. Full Collatz remains open.\n\n## CZ1. Setup and the precise target\n\nUse the shortcut map T(n)=n/2 for even n and (3n+1)/2 for odd n.\nLet beta be any exact binary T-invariant. Define on all positive integers\n\n    g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h(n)=|g(n)|,\n    d(j)=|beta(j+1)-beta(j)|.\n\nOn odd n put S(n)=(3n+1)/2 and U(n)=oddpart(S(n)). The previously\nproved DF relations are\n\n    g(2n)=g(n), h(2n)=h(n),\n    g(S(n))-g(n)=beta((9n+3)/2)-beta((9n+1)/2).       (CZ1)\n\nThe DF example f_F was binary, dyadically invariant and had finite total\nU-change cost but infinite odd harmonic mass. Its realization as h for\nan exact beta was not established or excluded at that stage. The following\nactual orbit identity now EXCLUDES that particular realization. This is\na new bounded conclusion, not a retroactive claim about the earlier audit.\n\n## CZ2. Two five-step paths meet identically\n\nFor every integer t>=0 set n=32t+23. All arrows below are actual T steps:\n\n    n  -> 48t+35 -> 72t+53 -> 108t+80 -> 54t+40 -> 27t+20,\n    3n ->144t+104-> 72t+52 -> 36t+26 -> 18t+13 -> 27t+20.\n\nThe input parity words are 11100 and 10001 respectively. The first\npath has three odd inputs, the second two. Every claimed odd input has\nan even coefficient of t and an odd constant, and every even input has\neven coefficient and constant. Thus this is valid for ALL t>=0, with\nno assumption about the parity of the common endpoint.\n\nEquivalently the two affine formulas are\n\n    T^5(n)=(27n+19)/32,\n    T^5(3n)=(9(3n)+19)/32.\n\nThey give the exact invariant equality\n\n    beta(n)=beta(3n), g(n)=h(n)=0 for n=23 mod32.     (CZ2)\n\nIn the fully accelerated odd notation one can also write\n\n    U^3(n)=U^2(3n)=oddpart(27t+20)=oddpart(27n+19).\n\nThis is finite coalescence, not a claim that the endpoint reaches 1.\nThe common numerator constant 19 is essential: equality of slopes or\nparity counts alone would not prove the common endpoint.\n\n## CZ3. An exact preservation rule\n\nFor any positive odd q, beta(4q)=beta(q). Also\n\n    4q+1 -> 6q+2 -> 3q+1 -> (3q+1)/2 = T(q),\n\nso beta(4q+1)=beta(q) and d(4q)=0. For x=16k+15,\n\n    (9x+1)/2=72k+68=4(18k+17),\n\nwith 18k+17 odd. Applying CZ1 gives the SIGNED preservation law\n\n    g(S(x))=g(x), h(S(x))=h(x) for x=15 mod16.       (CZ3)\n\nThis law previously provided no descent. Coupled with CZ2 it now\npropagates proven zero values backwards through an explicit family.\n\n## CZ4. A disjoint family with unbounded parameter L\n\nLet L>=3 and define a_L=3 for odd L, a_L=1 for even L. Set\n\n    Z_L={2^L(4t+a_L)-1 : t>=0}, Z=union_(L>=3) Z_L.\n\nFor n=2^L m-1 with m=4t+a_L, direct induction gives\n\n    S^j(n)=2^(L-j)3^j m-1, 0\u003c=j\u003c=L-3.              (CZ4)\n\nAt every step j\u003cL-3, L-j>=4, so its source is 15 mod16 and CZ3\npreserves g. All these iterates are positive odd integers. At the last\nstep the value is 8*3^(L-3)m-1. Since\n\n    3^(L-3) m=3 mod4,\n\nthat endpoint is 23 mod32 and has zero defect by CZ2. Therefore\n\n    h(n)=g(n)=0 for every n in Z.                   (CZ5)\n\nWhen L=3 there are zero preservation steps and CZ2 applies directly.\nNo negative-power iteration is used. When L=4, Z_4 is 15 mod64.\nDifferent Z_L are disjoint because v_2(n+1)=L on Z_L. Each is one\nresidue class modulo 2^(L+2), with positive first representative\n2^L a_L-1 less than that modulus.\n\nThe induction proves the entire infinite family analytically; no finite\nlist or numerical sample is being substituted for the parameter L.\n\n## CZ5. Natural and harmonic size; exclusion of the DF realization\n\nWrite A_Z(X)=#{n\u003c=X:n in Z}. Only L\u003c=floor(log_2(X+1)) can contribute.\nFor each such L its count is X/2^(L+2)+O(1), uniformly in L. Summing\nthese O(1) terms costs O(log(X+1)); the omitted density tail times X\nis O(1). Consequently\n\n    A_Z(X)=X/16+O(log(X+1)),\n    sum_(n\u003c=X,n in Z)1/n=(1/16)log X+O(1).           (CZ6)\n\nFor the second formula use partial summation. The count-error integral\nis bounded by a constant times integral_1^infinity log(t+1)/t^2 dt,\nwhich converges. Thus this O(1) is uniform in X, not an unproved\nexchange of infinitely many residue-class asymptotics.\n\nAll elements of Z are odd. Its density among all positive integers is\n1/16, or 1/8 relative to the positive odd integers. These are the sizes\nof the EXHIBITED zero set; the full zero set of h may be larger.\nSince the odd harmonic sum is (1/2)log X+O(1),\n\n    G(X)=sum_(n\u003c=X,n odd)h(n)/n \u003c=(7/16)log X+C.     (CZ7)\n\nC is absolute and independent of beta. In particular, no exact beta\ncan have h=1 outside a finite set of odd integers: infinitely many\nmembers of Z would violate that condition. For every finite odd F,\nthe DF function f_F is 1 at all but finitely many members of Z.\nIt therefore cannot be the defect h of any exact beta. The original\nDF counterexample remains correct for its relaxed class of functions.\n\nCZ7 is a logarithmic upper bound, NOT finite G. A positive fraction of\nforced zeros does not imply summability on the complement. We do not\nconstruct an actual nonconstant beta with divergent complementary mass.\n\n## CZ6. Conditional density from finite event variation\n\nThere is an additional valid implication, stronger than what the relaxed\nDF example permitted. Suppose separately that\n\n    V_h=sum_(n odd) |h(U(n))-h(n)|/n \u003c infinity.      (HYP)\n\nThis hypothesis has NOT been proved for an actual beta. Define\nDelta(n)=|h(T(n))-h(n)|. Dyadic invariance makes Delta=0 at even n\nand Delta(n)=|h(U(n))-h(n)| at odd n. Thus its harmonic sum is V_h.\nIts support D has natural density zero: for any fixed M,\n\n    #{n\u003c=X:n in D}/X\n       \u003c=M/X+sum_(n>=M,n in D)1/n,\n\nthen take X to infinity and M to infinity in that order.\n\nFor EACH FIXED j, (T^j)^(-1)(D) also has natural density zero. Indeed\nT^j has finitely many parity branches. On each it is an injective\naffine function with positive slope and bounded constant, mapping [1,X]\ninto [1,C_j X+C'_j]. The number of preimages is bounded by a finite\nmultiple of #{m\u003c=C_j X+C'_j:m in D}=o(X). No uniform-in-j claim is made.\n\nThe cylinder 23 mod32 corresponds to the parity word 11100. For fixed\nr>=1, let A_r contain n for which none of the r disjoint five-bit\nblocks at positions 0,5,...,5(r-1) is 11100. The exact parity-word\nbijection modulo 2^(5r) gives\n\n    natural density(A_r)=(31/32)^r.                (CZ8)\n\nIf h(n)=1 and n is outside A_r, then some T^(5i)(n) is in the zero\ncylinder. At least one Delta event must occur before that time. Hence\n\n    {h=1} subset A_r union union_(0\u003c=j\u003c5r) (T^j)^(-1)(D).\n\nThe finite union of event pullbacks on the right has density zero.\nFor each FIXED r,\ntake the upper density first; only afterwards send r to infinity:\n\n    V_h\u003cinfinity implies natural density({h=1})=0.  (CZ9)\n\nPartial summation also gives G(X)=o(log X) under HYP. Neither statement\ngives finite G or pointwise h=0. In particular, h is not assumed to be\nT-invariant, and PD cannot be applied to its support as if it were.\n\nThis argument works for any binary dyadically invariant function zero on\n23 mod32 with finite V. The additional infinite zero constraint is why\nit does not contradict DF's finite-mask counterexample.\n\n## CZ7. A genuine selected-class descent with a sign swap\n\nThere is a second useful exact identity. Put\n\n    n=128s+7, u=18s+1=(9n+1)/64, s>=0.\n\nThe following are actual shortcut paths with the displayed parities\nvalid independently of s:\n\n    n ->192s+11->288s+17->432s+26->216s+13\n      ->324s+20->162s+10->81s+5,\n    3n->576s+32->288s+16->144s+8->72s+4->36s+2->18s+1=u.\n\nSince 3u=54s+3 is odd and T(3u)=81s+5, these identities imply\n\n    beta(n)=beta(3u), beta(3n)=beta(u),\n    g(n)=-g(u), h(n)=h(u).                          (CZ10)\n\nThe sign swap must not be lost when working with g; it disappears only\nafter taking its absolute value. Here n=(64u-1)/9>=7u, because u>=1.\nIn particular u\u003cn for every source, including n=7,u=1.\n\nLet D={n positive:n=7 mod128}. The above map is a bijection from D\nto the positive targets u=1 mod18. For any finite cutoff X,\n\n    G_D(X)=sum_(n\u003c=X,n in D)h(n)/n\n       \u003c=(1/7) sum_(u\u003c=X/7,u=1 mod18)h(u)/u\n       \u003c=G(X/7)/7.                                 (CZ11)\n\nThis inequality has no O(1) term: the reciprocal bound and target cutoff\nboth follow from n>=7u. Since D has v_2(n+1)=3 and oddpart(n+1)=1\nmod4, it is disjoint from Z. Define the remaining odd core\n\n    C={positive odd n} minus (Z union D).\n\nThe exact decomposition G(X)=G_C(X)+G_D(X), CZ11 and monotonicity give\n\n    G_C(X)\u003c=G(X)\u003c=(7/6)G_C(X).                      (CZ12)\n\nThus finite G is equivalent to finite harmonic h-mass on C. This is\na bounded strengthening of the remaining criterion, not a proof that\neither mass is finite. The exhibited core has natural density 55/128\namong all positive integers, or 55/64 relative to odd integers; that\nis its geometric size, not a fraction of the conjecture left unsolved.\nCZ7's logarithmic upper bound remains valid and is not improved by\nsubstituting that density into CZ12. A least positive odd index with\nh=1, if one exists, cannot belong to Z or D. No descent is asserted\nfor every point of C.\n\n## CZ8. Source and full-goal boundaries\n\nRoot freshly checked the exact affine-iterate and parity-cylinder\nstatements in Rozier–Terracol, Introduction equation (2) and the\ndiscussion after Lemma 2.3:\n[primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948).\nThey supply the standard finite coding used in CZ6. CZ2's particular\npair of words and CZ4's family are derived directly above; neither is\nattributed to that paper and no novelty is claimed.\n\nRoot also checked Theorem 1.1 and the preceding discussion in\nApplegate–Lagarias,\n[The 3x+1 Semigroup](https://arxiv.org/html/math/0411140).\nMembership in their weak semigroup allows products not corresponding to\none genuine orbit. It therefore cannot replace the explicit common\nendpoint in CZ2. No semigroup theorem is imported into the proof.\n\nCZ uses no PD170 input. The historical internal R170 acceptance and the\nseparately unrecertified full analytic/Lean dependency closure are unchanged.\nThe inherited SB equivalence of finite E, ZR and G is also unchanged.\nThe remaining core C is uncontrolled at the strength needed for\nsummability. Even if HYP were proved, CZ9 would leave this gap.\n\nNext: extend the genuine affine merge/swap rules to a controlled\nfamily of core states, keeping source guards, integer targets and\nthe sign of g; alternatively obtain a core mass estimate whose error\nis summable over scales.\nThere is no justification for replacing that requirement by merely\nenlarging a finite zero cover or proving density zero without a rate.\nFull proof, counterexample, novelty, percent solved and completion date\nare not claimed. All results are local; public snapshot425 is unchanged.\n\n[DF predecessor](DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md) ·\n[0431 audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_COFINITE_DEFECT_RIGIDITY.md) ·\n[0459 audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_MULTIPLICATIVE_COLOR_COCYCLE.md) ·\n[0483 source/scope audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_MULTIPLICATIVE_COLOR_SOURCE_SCOPE.md).\n","COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md":"# SC — exact color strips reduce four growth alternatives to two\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\nindependent audits accepted in [the review](REVIEW_0485_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md).\nNo mathematical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean.\nUse the shortcut map T(n)=n/2 on even inputs and (3n+1)/2 on odd inputs.\nThroughout β is an arbitrary exact binary T-invariant on positive integers;\nthe existence of a nonconstant β is not assumed. Definitions and ancestry\nare from [BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md).\n\n## SC1. A two-sequence description of a finite color strip\n\nFix a positive odd u. For integers 0≤s≤h put\n\n    X_{h,s}=3^s 2^(h−s)u−1,\n    C_h=β(3^h u−1), B_s=β(3^s u),\n\nwhenever X_{h,s}>0. The excluded corner u=1,h=s=0 would be zero;\nit is never used. Each of the first h−s inputs of X_{h,s} is odd,\nand the exact successive states are\n\n    T^i(X_{h,s})=3^(s+i)2^(h−s−i)u−1, 0≤i≤h−s.\n\nAll states are positive, including the even endpoint 3^h u−1.\nRemoving the powers of two from X+1, and applying the same odd word\nto 3X+2, gives\n\n    β(X_{h,s})=C_h,\n    β(X_{h,s}+1)=B_s,\n    β(3X_{h,s}+2)=C_{h+1}.                            (SC1)\n\nConsequently\n\n    d(X_{h,s})=|C_h−B_s|,\n    e(X_{h,s})=1 iff C_h=B_s≠C_{h+1}.                 (SC2)\n\nOn a row with C_h≠C_{h+1}, e(X)+d(X)=1. This last identity must\nnot be used on a row without a color change. This is a re-expression\nof genuine finite odd words, not a claim that B and C are free sequences.\n\n## SC2. The elementary merging condition\n\nLet q=3^h u. If q≡3 mod4, the genuine paths\n\n    q−1 →(q−1)/2 →(3q−1)/4,\n    3q−1 →(3q−1)/2 →(3q−1)/4\n\nmeet. Their parity conditions are exact: (q−1)/2 is odd and\n3q−1 is divisible by4. Hence C_h=C_{h+1}. By SC2, the entire\nfinite row has e(X_{h,s})=0. The color equality by itself needs SC1\nto give this row conclusion; no extra color independence is used.\n\nTherefore if e(2^a u−1)=1 with a≥1, then\n\n    3^a u≡1 mod4.                                      (SC3)\n\nFor a≥2, 3^(a−1)u≡3 mod4, so the same merging identity yields\n\n    C_{a−1}=C_a.                                        (SC4)\n\nThe meeting mechanism is archival: see the explicit two paths in\n[R143, §2](../../../work/round143-l-general-merging.md). No priority\nclaim is made for it or for the finite-odd-word formula.\n\n## SC3. Eliminate G₁ and close the G₅ branch below its input\n\nLet n≡3 mod4 be an actual birth, write n=2^a u−1 with a≥2 and\nu odd, and retain the BR5 definitions\n\n    k=H(n)=(9n+5)/4, p=floor(k/2).\n\nIf a≥3 (equivalently n≡7 mod8), then\n\n    k=X_{a,2}, p=X_{a−1,2},\n    β(k)=C_a=C_{a−1}=β(p),\n    β(k+1)=B_2=β(p+1).\n\nThus d(k)=d(p). The BR5 combination d(k)=1,d(p)=0 is impossible.\nThe purported G₁ birth-growth branch never occurs for an actual birth.\nIf d(k)=1, both boundaries are active and the BR5 ancestor obeys\n\n    a'≤floor((9n+5)/16)\u003cn.                              (SC5)\n\nIf a=2 (n≡3 mod8), (SC3) gives u≡1 mod4, hence n≡3 mod16.\nHere k=9u−1 is divisible by4 and p=k/2=(9n+5)/8 is even. When\nd(k)=1,d(p)=0, BR5 gives e(p)=1, and the even transfer BR2 gives\n\n    a'≤floor((3p+2)/4)=floor((27n+31)/32)\u003cn              (SC6)\n\nfor n>31/5. The only smaller positive input in 3 mod16 is n=3;\ne(3)=0 since 3,4,11 all reach the same finite cycle. For example,\n3→5→8→4→2→1, and 11→17→26→13→20→10→5.\nIf instead d(k)=d(p)=1, (SC5) already gives descent.\n\nIt follows for every actual birth n≡3 mod4 that\n\n    either there is an actual birth a'\u003cn,\n    or e(H(n))=1 with d(H(n))=0.                        (SC7)\n\nThis is a dichotomy of existence, not a claim that its two conclusions\ncannot both hold through different routes. G₅ remains a valid raw BR\nalternative; its even output now composes with a strict descent below\nthe original input. G₁ is excluded using the additional color identity.\n\n## SC4. Only two essential growing transitions remain\n\nCombine SC7 with BR2–BR4. Every actual birth admits a smaller birth,\nunless one retains one of the following growing alternatives:\n\n| Input | Possible retained growth | Necessary guards |\n|---|---|---|\n| n≡13 mod16 | G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8 | e(G₃(n))=1 and d(G₃(n))=d(G₃(n)+1)=0 |\n| n≡3 mod4 | H(n)=(9n+5)/4 | e(H(n))=1 and d(H(n))=0 |\n\nEven births and births5mod16 have a smaller birth. Births1mod8 are\nexcluded. This covers all positive classes without calling every\narithmetic output a birth. It improves the four-mode BR table but does\nnot show that all remaining mixed chains descend below their start.\n\nLater [OR11–OR13](INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md) proves that the\nlisted G₃ guard d(G₃(n)+1)=0 is automatic at an actual input birth in\n13mod16. The table remains valid; this guard is redundant on that domain.\n\nThere is also a useful exact relation to the ordinary orbit of the\nmiddle integer μ=2n+1. For the retained growing transitions,\n\n    2H(n)+1 = T²(μ),   n≡3 mod4,                        (SC8)\n    2G₃(n)+1 = T³(μ),  n≡5 mod8.                       (SC9)\n\nThe words are respectively 11 and110. For SC9, μ≡11 mod16 makes\nthe third input even. The right hand sides are (9μ+5)/4 and\n(9μ+5)/8. At a birth β(n)=β(n+1)≠β(μ); hence along every retained\ngrowing transition both the outer color β(n) and the opposite middle\ncolor are preserved. In particular, G₃ uses the actual output-birth\nguard: β(G₃(n))=1−β(2G₃(n)+1)=1−β(μ)=β(n). SC9 alone would\nnot give this outer-color equality. The structural G₃ step on n is not reclassified\nas a universal ordinary-orbit step on n; the ordinary orbit is that of μ.\n\nAn uninterrupted growing chain strictly increases its birth indices.\nThis only excludes cycles made exclusively of these growing edges.\nIt does not exclude positive Collatz cycles, whose routing may include\nsmaller transfers, or an infinite growing chain from one fixed start.\n\n## SC5. Exact masks in a full H ladder\n\nFor a≥2 let n_j=9^j2^(a−2j)u−1, 0≤j≤floor(a/2), and assume\ne(n_0)=1. Put c=C_a. The paired genuine odd series imply\n\n    β(n_j)=c, β(3n_j+2)=1−c,\n    e(n_j)=1 iff B_{2j}=c,\n    d(n_j)=1−e(n_j).                                   (SC10)\n\nAn actual H-routing prefix through n_r therefore requires and is\nimplied by B_0=B_2=...=B_{2r}=c. If the first failed H mask occurs\nat j≥1 with a−2j≥1, (SC4) also gives d(floor(n_j/2))=1. Its\nnearest birth ancestor satisfies\n\n    a'≤floor(n_j/4)\n      =floor((9^j2^(a−2j)u−1)/4).                       (SC11)\n\nThis is certainly below n_0 when j=1, but the displayed bound alone\ndoes not imply that for j≥2: the leading ratio is (9/4)^j/4.\nThe end with a−2j=0 is even and needs the separate even case from\nSC3; a depth-two formula for an odd n_j must not be used there.\n\nUnder the extra hypothesis that n_0 is the smallest birth, all integers\n1,...,2n_0 have color β(1); hence C_h=β(1) for all1≤h≤a and also\nh=0 if u>1. The probe color B_{2j} is controlled by this prefix only\nwhile 9^j u≤2n_0. No all-j assertion about powers of3 follows.\n\n## SC6. The BR reset family remains a conditional test\n\nTake L=6t+3, A=L+1=6t+4, u_*=(2^L+1)/9 and\nm=16u_*−3. In this paragraph the strip core is1, so C_h=β(3^h−1);\nit is different from the auxiliary u_*. The BR candidate is\nG₃(m)=2^A−1. If m is the smallest\nbirth, the small BR branch is impossible, so this is an actual G₃\nbirth, with its masks. Since 2^A−1\u003c2m, C_A=β(1), whereas\nC_{A+1}≠β(1). The H probes thereafter are β(9^j).\n\nThe smallest-birth prefix supplies β(9^j)=β(1) only when9^j≤2m.\nAll opposite-color points Q_j=3*9^j2^(A−2j)−1 already exceed2m.\nThe end of the arithmetic ladder is3^A−1, far above m as A grows.\nNeither SC10 nor the finite prefix excludes all these hypothetical\nminimal births. At a failed odd H mask the alternative large G₁\nbranch is now excluded by SC3, but SC11 still need not fall below m.\n\nThe exact remaining target is to control the first smaller transfer\nafter an arbitrary mixture of the two growth modes, relative to the\ninitial birth index, or to prove a joint critical-weight estimate with\nall actual masks and multiplicities. This has not been done here.\n\n## Scope and status\n\nThe strip, merging, reduction to two essential growth modes and middle\norbit identities are bounded statements for exact binary invariants.\nThe minimum-birth reformulation is archival and equivalent to a first\ncolor change; for the reach-one indicator it is a least bad integer.\nNo global descent, full Collatz proof, external novelty, or universal\nstatement about the convergence of powers of3 is claimed.\n","COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md":"# BR — complete local birth routing; global descent remains open\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual analysis only.\nThis extends the accepted MA routing to the remaining residue classes.\nNo numerical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean was run. All assertions\nrefer to an arbitrary exact binary shortcut-Collatz invariant β; no\nnonconstant invariant is asserted to exist.\n\n## BR1. Definitions and inherited ancestry\n\nWrite\n\n    d(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)|, e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1), E(N)=Σ_(n≤N)e(n)/n.\n\nThe archived identities are\n\n    d(1)=0,\n    d(2n) XOR d(2n+1)=d(n),\n    e(n)=1 iff β(n)=β(n+1)≠β(3n+2).\n\nEvery active boundary d(k)=1 has a unique nearest birth ancestor a:\nascend through floor(k/2) until the first zero boundary; that parent\nhas two active children and e(a)=1. On each depth ℓ≥1, at most two\nboundaries have this ancestor, each at least 2^ℓ a. We use this BP176/MA\nfact, not a new branching theorem. Also e(1)=0, e(4m+2)=0 and\ne(8m+1)=0 are inherited exclusions.\n\n## BR2. Every even birth has a smaller birth\n\nLet n=2s and e(n)=1. Exact invariance gives\n\n    β(s)=β(3s+2)≠β(3s+1), so d(3s+1)=1.\n\nIts nearest birth ancestor satisfies\n\n    a≤floor((3s+1)/2)=floor((3n+2)/4)\u003cn,                    (BR2)\n\nfor n≥4. The edge n=2 is excluded by e(2)=0. Thus every actual even\nbirth admits a strict smaller-birth transfer. This does not assert\nthat even births vanish. It recovers, in particular, the old implication\nthat a smallest birth must be odd.\n\nFor n=4m, the charged boundary is k=6m+1 and\n\n    1/n=3/(2k)+3/[2k(k−1)].\n\nDistinct n give distinct k≥7. The depth sum is at most 2/a, while\nthe global error is bounded by Σ_(k≥7)3/[2k(k−1)]=1/4. Hence\n\n    U_0(N):=Σ_(n≤N,n≡0 mod4)e(n)/n ≤3E(N)+1/4.             (BR3)\n\nThis harmonic coefficient is greater than one; qualitative descent\nalone has not supplied a contraction in the total energy.\n\n## BR3. A stronger combined small branch for n≡1 mod4\n\nAny birth in this class is n≡5 mod8. Put\n\n    n=4M+1, p=(9n+3)/8, r=(3n+1)/4.\n\nCE10 says e(n) XOR e(r)=z, where\nz=(1−d(3M+1))d(9M+4). If z=0, e(r)=1 and r is even;\nBR2 then provides the active boundary\n\n    d(3r/2+1)=d(p+1)=1.\n\nIf z=1, the MA ancestry split gives d(p)=1 or e(p)=1. Thus\n\n    e(n)=1 ⇒ d(p)=1 or d(p+1)=1 or e(p)=1.                 (BR4)\n\nChoose the routing deterministically: use boundary p if active;\notherwise use p+1 if active; otherwise choose the larger birth p.\nThe small branch has nearest birth ancestor\n\n    a≤floor((p+1)/2)=floor((9n+11)/16)\u003cn.                   (BR5)\n\nIn the remaining growth branch, e(p)=1 and d(p)=d(p+1)=0.\nThe additional zero-boundary condition is retained; arithmetic\nintegrality alone never licenses a transition of actual births.\n\nThe combined small-branch energy is at most (9/4)E(N)+O(1).\nIndeed n=8t+5 gives p=9t+6. Its charged boundary is either 9t+6\nor 9t+7. These pairs are disjoint for different t, so every chosen\nboundary k is counted once. The two inverse formulas are\n\n    n=(8k−3)/9 or n=(8k−11)/9,\n    1/n=(9/8)/k+O(k^−2).\n\nThe error sums globally over distinct k, and Σ_(k charged to a)1/k≤2/a.\nThis avoids double-counting the old A and B alternatives separately.\nIt is a joint upper bound, not an estimate of a separate hypothetical\nindependent distribution of their masks.\n\n## BR4. A completed two-stage descent on 5 modulo 16\n\nFor n≡5 mod16, p=(9n+3)/8 is even. If the BR3 small branch applies,\nthere is already a smaller birth. Otherwise e(p)=1, and BR2 gives\n\n    a≤floor((3p+2)/4)=floor((27n+25)/32)\u003cn   (n>5).         (BR6)\n\nFor n=5, the growing candidate p=6 is excluded by e(6)=0, so BR3\nmust use its strict small branch if e(5)=1 were assumed. In fact\ne(5)=0 directly from the ordinary finite orbits of 5,6,17.\nConsequently every birth in 5 modulo 16 has some smaller birth.\nThis excludes that class for the smallest birth, not for all births.\n\nThere is an elementary orbit interpretation, which prevents overstating\nthe novelty of this finite class. If n=16t+5, t≥1, its middle point\nμ=2n+1=32t+11 follows\n\n    32t+11 →48t+17 →72t+26 →36t+13 →54t+20 →27t+10\u003c2n.\n\nThe colors of μ and n are different at a birth. Both n and the displayed\nendpoint are below 2n, so a boundary between them has a birth ancestor\nbelow n. Thus this class is also covered by ordinary finite descent.\nNo new universal stopping-time theorem is asserted.\n\n## BR5. Complete alternatives for n≡3 mod4\n\nWrite n=4r−1, r≥1, and put\n\n    k=9r−1=(9n+5)/4, p=floor(k/2).\n\nExact steps give β(n)=β(k), β(n+1)=β(r), and\nβ(2n+1)=β(3k+2). Hence a birth implies\n\n    β(k)=β(r)≠β(3k+2).\n\nIf d(k)=0, this is e(k)=1. If d(k)=1 and d(p)=0,\nthe binary law gives e(p)=1. If both boundaries are active, the\nnearest birth ancestor of k lies at depth at least two. Thus\n\n    d(k)=0:                larger birth k=(9n+5)/4;\n    d(k)=1, d(p)=0:        larger birth p;\n    d(k)=1, d(p)=1:        birth a≤floor((9n+5)/16)\u003cn.       (BR7)\n\nThe larger p is explicit:\n\n    p=(9n+5)/8 if n≡3 mod8,\n    p=(9n+1)/8 if n≡7 mod8.\n\nFor r=2t+1, p−n=t+1; for r=2t, p−n=t≥1. Thus both\npurported large branches really increase the index, including the\nsmall arithmetic edge r=1. The small branch is strict since\n(9r−1)/4\u003c4r−1 for r≥1.\n\nEach large branch is injective in its input: k increases with r,\nand floor((9r−1)/2) also strictly increases with r. Their respective\nharmonic coefficients are 9/4 and 9/8, with globally summable errors:\n\n    1/n=9/(4k)+45/[4k(4k−5)],\n    1/n=9/(8p)+O(p^−2).\n\nThe small branch has coefficient at most9/4 by the depth≥2 count.\nPossible overlap of different branch images has not been discarded.\nNone of these bounds yields a coefficient less than one for U_3.\n\nThe separate neighboring-boundary route in the 0459 report remains\nvalid: d(6m+4)=1 or d(6m+5)=1 gives a smaller ancestor for n=4m+3.\nIts complement is a color mask, not an unlicensed choice of colors.\nBR7 applies to that complement as well as to the whole class.\n\n## BR6. Four arithmetic growth modes and their limitation\n\nThe routing above covers all positive birth indices. Its growing\ntransitions belong to this table; every row still requires its birth\nand boundary conditions from BR3 or BR5.\n\n| Mode | Required input class | Output | Identity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| G₃ | n≡5 mod8 | (9n+3)/8 | G₃(n)+3=(9/8)(n+3) |\n| G₅ | n≡3 mod8 | (9n+5)/8 | G₅(n)+5=(9/8)(n+5) |\n| G₁ | n≡7 mod8 | (9n+1)/8 | G₁(n)+1=(9/8)(n+1) |\n| H | n≡3 mod4 | (9n+5)/4 | H(n)+1=(9/4)(n+1) |\n\nA consecutive run of any fixed G_c has length at most\nfloor(v₂(n+c)/3); a consecutive H run has length at most\nfloor(v₂(n+1)/2). These statements follow by subtracting3 or2\nfrom the respective valuation at each integral step. They do not\nbound the length of a sequence that switches modes.\n\nHere is a purely arithmetic demonstration of that distinction. Let\nL=6t+3, t≥0, and define u=(2^L+1)/9, n_0=16u−3. Then u is a positive\nodd integer, n_0≡13 mod16, and v₂(n_0+3)=4 independently of t. Yet\n\n    n_1=G₃(n_0)=2^(L+1)−1,\n    v₂(n_1+1)=L+1.\n\nThe arithmetic H rule can consequently be applied (L+1)/2 consecutive\ntimes, ending at 9^((L+1)/2)−1. This is an unbounded run after a single\nmode switch from a fixed initial valuation4. Integrality uses\n2^L≡−1 mod9; no trajectory or numerical search is used.\n\nThese integers are NOT asserted to be actual births, and their\ncolors need not satisfy the routing masks. The example refutes only\nthe inference from finite individual-mode runs to a uniform bound on\nthe switched arithmetic process. It is not a counterexample to\nCollatz, nor a proof that no ranking using the actual colors can work.\n\n## BR7. The remaining mathematical obligation\n\nEven births and the class5mod16 now have qualitative smaller-birth\ntransfers; all other cases have explicit alternatives with their masks.\nWhat is missing is a joint argument that forces descent below the\ninitial index after arbitrarily many mode changes, or bounds the\ncritical energy with all multiplicities and masks retained. A complete\nlocal case split is not a proof that its trajectories terminate.\n\nThe smaller-birth obligation would suffice without a density theorem:\nif every birth had a smaller birth, a nonempty birth set would contradict\nits least element. An active boundary would have a birth ancestor, so\nall boundaries would also vanish and β would be constant. Applying this\nto the indicator of reaching1 would prove Collatz. This is a sufficient\ncriterion, not a completed proof of its universal premise.\n\nThe finite-color/minimum-birth argument and the old birth law already\nexplain much of this framework. Only the exact combinations checked\nhere are claimed. No external novelty claim, general cycle exclusion,\nglobal upper bound Σe(n)/n\u003c∞, or full Collatz proof is made.\n","CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER.md":"# CX: critical weights, density direction, and finite-centre potential obstructions\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nNo mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS or Lean. This is a restricted\ninvestigation of the full Collatz goal, not a proof of the conjecture.\nWorld priority is not claimed. Internal audit links are below.\n\n## CX1. The critical weight retains an exact arithmetic correction\n\nUse the shortcut T and the structural return map R from\n[PR](POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md). For a genuine positive path\nx_0=m,...,x_K=n with P odd steps, put A=2^K/3^P and\n\n    S=sum_(j\u003cK, x_j odd) log(1+1/(3x_j)).\n\nMultiplication of the individual step ratios gives\n\n    n/m=A^(-1)*exp(S),\n    A^(-1)=(n/m)*exp(-S)=n/(m+B),\n    exp(S)=1+B/m,                          (CX1)\n\nwhere m=A*n-B. For P=0, S=B=0 even on a nonempty all-even path.\nFor P>=1, B=C/3^P>=1/3. Every nonempty structural block has P>=1.\n\nFor two synchronous paths (m,m+1) to (n,n+1) with common K,P,\n\n    S_R-S_L=log(m(n+1)/(n(m+1))),\n    B_R-B_L=A-1.                          (CX2)\n\nTheir joint elementary upper bound is still only shifted harmonic:\n\n    A^(-1)\u003c=min(n/(m+1/3),(n+1)/(m+4/3)).  (CX3)\n\nThe correction is the old affine intercept in multiplicative coordinates;\nintroducing it does not create a probabilistic saving. Even genuine\nfirst-hit paths to1 can have arbitrarily large sources and B=1/3:\nfor s>=2, m_s=(4^s-1)/3 is odd and its path is one odd step followed\nby 2s-1 halvings. These are ordinary paths, not asserted structural pairs.\nThus a lower bound on S growing with every source fails for ordinary paths.\n\nThere is also an exact elementary restriction on the structural target.\nAfter any odd shortcut step, its output is nonzero modulo3, and every\nlater step preserves that property. A structural block has an odd step\non each side. Hence both n and n+1 are units modulo3, forcing n=1 mod3.\nConsequently W_q(n)=1 for every n not congruent to1 modulo3: only the\nempty schedule exists. This is a target restriction, not a fraction of\nthe Collatz conjecture proved. The critical remaining targets have n=1 mod3.\n\n## CX2. Which weighted growth would actually imply source density\n\nFor fixed n, let M_R(n) be the structural predecessor set and let A_m\nrefer to the first R-hit of n from m. The empty path m=n has B_m=0.\nFrom PR's exact cycle factor c_1(n),\n\n    W_1(n;X)=c_1(n)*sum_(m in M_R(n),m\u003c=X) n/(m+B_m).\n\nLet N(X)=#(M_R(n) intersect [1,X]), H(X)=sum_(m in M_R(n),m\u003c=X)1/m,\nand D=limsup_(X->infinity)N(X)/X. Abel summation gives\n\n    H(X)=N(X)/X+integral_1^X N(t)/t^2 dt,\n    limsup H(X)/log X \u003c= D.              (CX4)\n\nIndeed N(t)/t\u003c=D+epsilon for every sufficiently large t; the finite\ninitial range contributes O(1). Since W_1(n;X)\u003c=c_1(n)*n*H(X),\n\n    eta:=limsup W_1(n;X)/log X \u003c= c_1(n)*n*D.\n\nThus the additional LOWER-growth premise eta>0 would imply\n\n    upper_density(M_R(n)) >= eta/(c_1(n)*n)>0. (CX5)\n\nNo such lower growth has been proved here. Mere divergence of W_1 is\ninsufficient; finite W_1 gives eta=0 and does not supply this route.\nA proposed linear lower bound in X would contradict PR's O(log X)\nupper envelope, and was removed from the helper's preliminary report.\n\nIn the other direction, if a separate arithmetic bound B_m\u003c=Lambda(m+1)\nheld for all first-hit sources, then finite W_1 would imply a convergent\nharmonic sum on M_R(n), hence upper density zero. Without that intercept\nbound, no such implication is established for the actual Collatz set.\n\nGeneric countermodels demonstrate the missing interface: on a one-edge\nstar R(m)=1 with positive affine inverse intercepts, take all m>=2 and\nA_m=(m+1)^2. Source density is1 but W_1 is finite. Alternatively take\nm_j=ceil(j log(j+1)), j>=2, and A_m=m+1. Sources have density0 but\nW_1 diverges. The m_j are distinct and comparable to j log j.\nThese examples are not Collatz paths and do not disprove an implication\nthat might follow from additional Collatz-specific arithmetic.\n\n## CX3. The full paired-predecessor gap remains separate\n\nWith P(a)={m>0: some iterate reaches a}, define\nQ(a,c)={m>0:m in P(a),m+1 in P(c)}. Then M_R(n) is contained in\nQ(n,n+1), but this can be strict. The pair (6,7) first reaches (1,2)\nat shortcut time10, with odd counts4 and5. There is no earlier positive\nordered adjacency; afterwards the endpoints alternate (1,2),(2,1),\nwith equal extra odd counts at each subsequent ordered return. The odd\ncount difference therefore never disappears, so 6 is in Q(1,2) but\nnot in M_R(1).\n\nThe older PL8 paired-density obligation uses fixed targets a,c with\n3 not dividing a*c, and permits different hitting times. Normalizing two\nneighboring starts to such targets separately need not preserve adjacency\nof the targets. This older restriction is recorded in\n[PL176](../../../work/round176-g-paired-orbit-literature.md).\nIf either target is divisible by3, its predecessors are only its doublings,\nso no positive-density statement for that pair is possible.\n\nThus even CX5 for some structural target pairs would still need the right\ntarget coverage to imply the PL8 obligation. Neither critical upper control\nnor a result about one structural seed is being substituted for full Collatz.\n\n## CX4. A precise supersolution question\n\nFor q>0 define on positive integers\n\n    (L_q V)(n)=sum_(i admissible at n) alpha_i^(-q)*V(F_i(n)),\n\nusing all minimal ordered structural blocks. The terms are nonnegative.\nA finite V>=1 satisfying V>=1+L_q V would bound W_q by iteration.\nIt must in particular satisfy the weaker superharmonic inequality\n\n    L_q V\u003c=V.                             (CX6)\n\nIf (CX6) holds for a positive finite V, then positivity gives\nL_q^j V\u003c=V for every finite j. Countable sums cause no interchange issue:\nnonnegative iterates are defined monotonically and are bounded by V.\n\nThe next theorem rules out a specific large family of proposed V. It does\nnot rule out every superharmonic function or critical W_1 finiteness.\n\n## CX5. No power with a common slowly varying scale and periodic factors\n\nFix q>0, an integer modulus d>=1, a real exponent s, and constants\nc_0,...,c_(d-1)>0. Suppose V has the following asymptotic on each residue:\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod d)+o(1))*n^s*ell(n),    n->infinity. (CX7)\n\nHere ell>0 satisfies ell(a*n+b)/ell(n)->1 for fixed a>0 and b,\nalong positive integral arguments used below. This affine scaling\ncondition is stated explicitly; standard slowly varying functions with\nthe uniform convergence property satisfy it. Examples include constants,\npowers of log n, and powers of log log n on their eventual domains.\nFinitely many residue classes make the o(1) errors uniform over residues.\n\nThen no finite positive V with (CX7) can satisfy (CX6) for all positive n.\n\nFirst use the genuine ordered contraction from GB/IR:\n\n    H(n)=(4194304*n-18252850)/4782969,\n    alpha=4194304/4782969\u003c1.\n\nFactor H into r>=1 minimal ordered blocks. For every integer t>=1,\nH^t is a schedule of rt blocks. Its guard is the nonempty progression\n3^(14t) dividing 139n+4310. Positive representatives can tend to infinity.\nThe corresponding term of the iterated transfer gives\n\n    V(n) >= alpha^(-qt)*V(H^t(n)).         (CX8)\n\nWrite c_min and c_max for the extrema of the positive c_j. For fixed t,\nH^t(n)=alpha^t*n+O_t(1). Taking n->infinity in its progression, (CX7)\nand the scaling condition imply\n\n    1 >= alpha^((s-q)t)*c_min/c_max.       (CX9)\n\nThis holds for every t. If s\u003cq, the right side tends to infinity as\nt grows. Hence s>=q is necessary. The order of limits is first n for\neach fixed t, then t; no uniform slow-variation assertion in t is used.\n\nNext use two genuine irreducible expanding blocks:\n\n    L(n)=(4n-1)/3,       n=1 mod3,\n    M(n)=(32n-5)/9,      n=1 mod9.\n\nL has forward words OE/EO. M has words OOEEE/EEOEO; its prefix odd-count\npairs before time5 are (1,0),(2,0),(2,1),(2,1), so no proper structural\ncut exists. These really are individual terms of L_q, not arbitrary\ncomposite rules counted as extra branches.\n\nAlong n=1+9*d*j with j->infinity, both guards hold, and\n\n    L(n)=1+12*d*j=1 mod d,\n    M(n)=3+32*d*j=3 mod d.\n\nThe two-term lower bound and (CX7) now give\n\n    liminf (L_q V)(n)/V(n)\n       >=(4/3)^(s-q)+(32/9)^(s-q)*c_(3 mod d)/c_(1 mod d)\n       >1,                                (CX10)\n\nsince s>=q. This contradicts (CX6). The formula also covers d=1,2,3:\ncoincident residue labels simply give the corresponding same coefficient.\n\nThe proof uses actual Collatz blocks and their full congruences. It rules\nout, for every q>0, a common power-and-slow-scale potential even after\nmultiplying it by arbitrary positive periodic residue weights. In\nparticular, C*n^s and positive periodic multiples of n^s*(log n)^u fail.\n\n## CX6. Limits and next mathematical decision\n\nThe theorem in CX5 does not cover different powers on different residues,\nzero leading coefficients with lower-order positive terms, dependence on\nunbounded residue precision, or other irregular potentials. For q>1 PR\nalready proves W_q finite, and W_q itself solves W_q=1+L_q W_q. Thus\nsuch finite supersolutions exist in that range, but cannot have (CX7).\nFor q=1 and targets n=1 mod3, their existence remains undecided here.\n\nThe critical research direction must therefore be chosen by its role:\nan upper Green bound requires more irregular arithmetic information;\nthe paired-density route requires a suitable LOWER count, with equation (CX5) giving\none sufficient weighted version if its additional premise can be proved.\nNeither route currently yields all-start convergence or a counterexample.\n\nIndependent internal checks:\n\n- [0459: exact correction and potential arithmetic](FOLLOWUP_0459_CRITICAL_AFFINE_CORRECTION.md).\n- [0485: density interface and no-go proof](FOLLOWUP_0485_CRITICAL_COVERAGE_INTERFACE.md).\n- [0483: nearby primary sources and their limits](FOLLOWUP_0483_CRITICAL_WEIGHT_SOURCES.md).\n\nThe source role separates discrete orbit products from the continuous\nLaplace products in Berg--Krueppel. Root independently read the introduction\nand Theorem1.1 with its proof in the [primary publisher PDF](https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/34973?nt=1),\nand the [official Berg--Meinardus metadata](https://www.math.uni-rostock.de/math/pub/romako/heft48/berg2.html).\nNo continuous-density or Haar-average theorem is used as a pointwise\ninteger theorem in CX1--CX6. The older PL176 proof is used only to identify\nits conditional target obligation; its earlier single-target PD input has\nnot been re-proved or newly imported in this turn.\n\n## CX7. Finitely many 3-adic valuation factors also fail\n\nThis extension is a manual argument about the same exact operator, not a\ntheorem imported from the literature. Let E be a finite set of distinct\ncentres in Z_3, with real exponents gamma_a. Suppose, away from the finitely\nmany centres which are themselves positive integers,\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod d)+o(1))*n^s*ell(n)\n         * product_(a in E) 3^(gamma_a*v_3(n-a)).       (FC1)\n\nAll c_j are strictly positive and ell has the affine scaling property in\nCX5. The exceptional positive integer centres receive arbitrary finite\npositive values. The error in (FC1) is relative to the displayed positive\nscale. Set gamma_b=0 for any b outside E. We show that no finite positive V\nof this form satisfies L_q V\u003c=V for any q>0.\n\n### FC1. A positive valuation exponent propagates to infinitely many centres\n\nFor F=L or M, write P_F=1 or2, respectively, and alpha_F=4/3 or32/9.\nThe inverse affine maps on Z_3 are\n\n    G_L(a)=(3a+1)/4,   G_M(a)=(9a+5)/32.\n\nFix a centre a with gamma_a>0 and put b=G_F(a). Then F(b)=a and b\nsatisfies F's terminal guard. For any sufficiently large integer R, the\nannulus v_3(n-b)=R contains an unbounded arithmetic progression of positive\nintegers n. This is true for every b in Z_3: specify its first R digits\nand choose a different next digit. Take R>P_F and beyond all the finite\nseparations between b and the other input centres, and between a and the\nother output centres (with the latter bound increased by P_F).\n\nAlong that progression F(n) is an integer, tends to positive infinity,\nand\n\n    v_3(F(n)-a)=R-P_F.\n\nThe valuations at every centre other than b at the input and a at the\noutput are constant, independent of R once these bounds hold. Thus, first\ntaking n to infinity for fixed R, the F-term has lower limiting ratio\n\n    alpha_F^(-q)*V(F(n))/V(n)\n       >=_(liminf) C*(c_min/c_max)*alpha_F^(s-q)\n                    *3^((gamma_a-gamma_b)*R),        (FC2)\n\nwhere C>0 is independent of R; it includes 3^(-P_F*gamma_a).\nThe superharmonic inequality bounds this ratio by1. If gamma_a>gamma_b,\nletting R grow contradicts (FC2). Therefore gamma_b>=gamma_a>0.\n\nThe finite set E_+ of positive-exponent centres would consequently be\nclosed under both G_L and G_M. But\n\n    G_L^j(a)=1+(3/4)^j*(a-1).\n\nTwo equal iterates in a finite orbit force a=1, since no nonzero power\nof3/4 equals1 in Q_3. Thus E_+ can only be a subset of {1}. Closure under\nG_M is then impossible because G_M(1)=7/16 differs from1. We have proved\n\n    gamma_a\u003c=0 for every a in E.                     (FC3)\n\n### FC2. The contraction still forces s>=q\n\nLet z=-4310/139 be H's fixed point. For every fixed k>=1 choose an exact\nannulus v_3(n-z)=R with\n\n    R>14k+max(0, v_3(z-a): a in E, a!=z).\n\nIt lies inside the H^k guard. Its positive integer representatives tend\nto infinity, and\n\n    H^k(n)-z=alpha^k*(n-z),\n    v_3(H^k(n)-z)=R-14k.\n\nAt all other centres the input and output valuations agree. The ratio of\nthe valuation products in (FC1) is exactly 3^(-14k*gamma_z), where an\nabsent centre has gamma_z=0. This ratio is at least1 by (FC3). The fixed-k\npath inequality, followed by n tending to infinity in the annulus, gives\n\n    1 >= (c_min/c_max)*alpha^((s-q)k)*3^(-14k*gamma_z)\n      >= (c_min/c_max)*alpha^((s-q)k).\n\nTaking k arbitrarily large forces s>=q. We did not fix one integer n\nsupporting all k, or interchange the limits.\n\n### FC3. Two expanding branches contradict the remaining possibility\n\nChoose one fixed R large enough that R>=v_3(9d), that R-1 exceeds every\nv_3(1-a) for a!=1, and that R-2 exceeds every v_3(3-a) for a!=3.\nThere is an unbounded progression\n\n    n=1+9d*j,   v_3(n-1)=R.\n\nBoth L and M are admissible. The input and L(n) have residue1 modulo d.\nAll their valuation factors cancel except at centre1, where the valuation\ndrops by1. Hence, taking n to infinity in this fixed annulus,\n\n    alpha_L^(-q)*V(L(n))/V(n)\n        -> (4/3)^(s-q)*3^(-gamma_1) >=1.             (FC4)\n\nMeanwhile M(n)=3+32d*j has residue3 modulo d and\nv_3(M(n)-3)=R-2. The restrictions on R make every input and output centre\nvaluation fixed and finite. Its branch ratio therefore tends to a fixed\nstrictly positive number C_R. Even if this number is very small, R is\nfixed while n tends to infinity. The two-term sum has lower limit at\nleast1+C_R>1, contradicting L_q V\u003c=V.\n\nThis proves the claimed finite-centre obstruction. The single factor\n3^(gamma*v_3(n-1)) is a special case, independently audited by both\n0459 and0485. The finite-centre argument also permits nonrational centres\nin Z_3; only their finite residue digits and ultrametric separations enter.\n\nThe hypotheses matter: this does not exclude different real exponents by\nresidue, infinitely many centres, zero leading coefficients with positive\nlower-order terms, or arbitrary arithmetic functions. In particular PR's\nfinite W_q for q>1 is not contradicted. Neither W_1 finiteness nor a\npositive lower count for the full pair set Q(a,c) follows from this no-go.\n\n## CX8. Different powers by residue: exact constraints and three small moduli\n\nThis is a separate extension, with no valuation factors. Let d=3^h, h>=1,\nand suppose\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod d)+o(1))*n^(s_(n mod d))*ell(n),    (RE1)\n\nwhere all c_r>0, all s_r are finite real numbers, and ell is the same\naffine-slow scale. Assume again L_q V\u003c=V. We prove necessary restrictions\nfor every h, and a contradiction for h=1,2,3 only.\n\nFor either genuine branch F=L,M, the corresponding map G_F of residue\nclasses is well-defined since its denominator is a power of2. Comparing\none branch on an unbounded positive progression with source residue r\ngives\n\n    s_r \u003c= s_(G_F(r)).                              (RE2)\n\nFor L, G_L^h(r)=1 modulo3^h. Thus S=max_r s_r=s_1. For H^k with14k>=h,\nthe terminal residue is z=-4310/139 modulo3^h, while its source residue\ncan be prescribed arbitrarily: increasing the terminal by3^(14k) changes\nthe source by2^(22k), a unit modulo3^h. The H^k path therefore forces\ns_z>=s_r for every r, so s_z=S. With both endpoint exponents S, the\nH^k inequality and arbitrarily large k give S>=q, just as in CX5.\n\nConversely n=1+3^(h+1)t and L(n)=1+4*3^h*t both have residue1 modulo d.\nThe one L-term, after n tends to infinity, gives\n\n    1 >= (4/3)^(s_1-q),\n\nso s_1\u003c=q. Consequently the exact necessary exponent constraint is\n\n    s_r\u003c=q for all r,    s_1=s_z=q.                  (RE3)\n\nThere are also coefficient constraints. For any r with s_r=q, choose the\nL^h path with terminal residue1 and source residue r. Its slope factor\ncancels at exponent q, giving c_r\u003c=c_1. The H^k path with source residue1\ngives c_z>=c_1, whence c_z=c_1. More generally, if s_r=q, (RE2) forces\ns_(G_F(r))=q, and the single-branch coefficient inequality gives\n\n    c_r \u003c= c_(G_F(r)) \u003c= c_1.                      (RE4)\n\nThus every forward residue image under G_L,G_M of a class with exponent q\nand coefficient c_1 again has these same leading data.\n\nThe following explicit guarded progressions now finish three cases.\n\n| Modulus | Positive terminal progression | L source | M source | Leading classes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 3 | n=19+27t | 25+36t | 67+96t | input and both outputs are1 |\n| 9 | n=46+81t | 61+108t | 163+288t | input1; outputs7=z and1 |\n| 27 | n=73+243t | 97+324t | 259+864t | input19; both outputs16=z |\n\nFor modulus27, z=16 and G_L(16)=19, G_L(19)=1, so (RE3)--(RE4)\ngive s_16=s_19=q and c_16=c_19=c_1. In each table row both individual\nbranch contributions divided by V(n) tend to1. Their sum tends to2,\ncontradicting L_q V\u003c=V. Hence (RE1) is impossible for d=3,9,27; d=1\nwas already covered by CX5.\n\nFor h>=4, (RE2)--(RE4) remain proved necessary restrictions, but this\nargument does not establish a contradiction or construct a supersolution.\nTwo residue paths need compatible higher-digit guards to be usable at\nthe same terminal n. Separate existence of each path is insufficient.\nThe preliminary all-h shortcut was withdrawn for this reason. Passing to\na coarser modulus would also be invalid: distinct powers need not combine\ninto one uniform asymptotic on the coarser class.\n\nAn independent working report and correction history are in\n[0485: residue-dependent exponents](FOLLOWUP_0485_RESIDUE_EXPONENTS.md).\nThis result excludes another specified potential class only; it neither\ndecides the genuine critical sum nor supplies a lower count for Q(a,c).\n","DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md":"# DF — finite defect flux and a limitation of variation control\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nNo numerical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean. Full Collatz is unresolved.\n\n## DF1. Exact binary event law\n\nLet beta be any binary invariant of the shortcut Collatz map T. Write\n\n    S(n)=(3n+1)/2, U(n)=oddpart(S(n))                 (n odd),\n    g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h(n)=|g(n)|,\n    d(j)=|beta(j+1)-beta(j)|, v(n)=(9n+1)/2.\n\nDyadic invariance gives g(2n)=g(n), h(2n)=h(n). Moreover\n\n    beta(U(n))=beta(n),\n    g(U(n))-g(n)=beta(v(n)+1)-beta(v(n)).             (DF1)\n\nBoth g-values compare a binary value with the SAME base beta(n).\nConsequently their signs cannot switch from +1 to -1 or conversely, and\n\n    h(U(n)) = h(n) XOR d(v(n)).\n\nFor odd sources put\n\n    c_plus(n)=(1-h(n))d(v(n)),\n    c_minus(n)=h(n)d(v(n)).\n\nThen, exactly,\n\n    h(U(n))-h(n)=c_plus(n)-c_minus(n),\n    c_plus(n)+c_minus(n)=d(v(n)).                    (DF2)\n\nThe inequality h\u003c=1 is only the binary range, not mass contraction.\n\n## DF2. Correct channel table and a source restriction\n\nSet q=3n and v=S(q). The four states at an index j are the triples\n(d(j),d(2j),d(2j+1)):\n\n    0=(0,0,0), E=(0,1,1), L=(1,1,0), Rb=(1,0,1).\n\nThe inherited S channel e+L is conserved. Also h(n)=d(q), whereas\nh(U(n))=d(q) XOR d(v); the latter is NOT just d(v).\n\n| q-state | v-state | h(n) | h(U(n)) | Event |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | none |\n| 0 | Rb | 0 | 1 | creation outside ZS |\n| E | E | 0 | 0 | none |\n| E | L | 0 | 1 | creation, ZS source q |\n| L | E | 1 | 1 | retention, DS target v |\n| L | L | 1 | 0 | death |\n| Rb | 0 | 1 | 1 | retention |\n| Rb | Rb | 1 | 0 | death |\n\nThese are channel-compatible local possibilities, not claims that every\nrow is realized by a globally nonconstant beta. In particular, creation\ncannot all be charged to ZS: the 0-to-Rb row is not a birth at q.\nSince S maps odd integers into residue 2 modulo 3, q=3n has no\nS-predecessor. Such q are S-chain bases, so pairing these creations\nwith preceding events on their S-chains supplies no earlier payment.\nActual h-deaths in the last table are not falls of the boundary state d.\n\n## DF3. An exact finite-cutoff kernel identity\n\nThe following lemma holds for any real f on positive odd integers with\n|f|\u003c=M. Extend it dyadically if desired. For i in {0,1,2}, put\n\n    F_i(X)=sum_(m odd, m\u003c=X, m=i mod3) f(m)/m.\n\nEvery odd inverse of U at m is exactly\n\n    n=(2^k m-1)/3,\n    k>=1, 2^k m=1 mod3.\n\nThe source cutoff n\u003c=X is 2^k m\u003c=3X+1. Define\n\n    W(m)=sum_(k>=1, 2^k m=1 mod3) 3/(2^k m-1),\n    a_m=0,1,2 respectively for m=0,1,2 mod3,\n    epsilon(m)=W(m)-a_m/m.\n\nThere are no inverses for 3|m. For m=1 mod3 admissible k are even,\nstarting at 2; for m=2 mod3 they are odd, starting at 1. Thus the\ngeometric sums are exactly a_m/m. Every admissible A=2^k m is at least\n4, and\n\n    0 \u003c= 3/(A-1)-3/A \u003c= 4/A^2.\n\nIt follows without any orbit statistics that\n\n    0\u003c=epsilon(m)\u003c=4/(3m^2),\n    sum_(m odd) epsilon(m)\u003c=8/3.                    (DF3)\n\nThe last coarse bound uses sum_(m>=1) m^(-2)\u003c=2. Now define the finite\ncutoff remainder explicitly:\n\n    R_f(X) = sum_(n odd,n\u003c=X,U(n)>X) f(U(n))/n\n       - sum_(m odd,m\u003c=X) f(m)\n         sum_(k admissible,2^k m>3X+1) 3/(2^k m-1).\n\nDirect reindexing, involving only finite source sums and convergent\nindividual inverse columns, gives for all X>=1\n\n    sum_(n odd,n\u003c=X) [f(U(n))-f(n)]/n\n      =F_2(X)-F_0(X)\n       +sum_(m odd,m\u003c=X) epsilon(m)f(m)+R_f(X).      (DF4)\n\nHere |R_f(X)|\u003c=4M uniformly. If 0\u003c=f\u003c=M, the sharper\n|R_f(X)|\u003c=2M holds. Details of both cutoff boundaries:\n\n* An extra target m>X requires n>(2X-1)/3. For X>=2 these n lie in\n  (X/2,X], whose full reciprocal sum is at most log(2)+1\u003c2. For\n  1\u003c=X\u003c2 the only odd source is 1, with U(1)=1, so there is no extra.\n* For each m\u003c=X let K be the first k with 2^k m>3X+1. Ignoring the\n  admissibility restriction only enlarges the nonnegative tail. Its\n  weight is at most 4 sum_(k>=K) 1/(2^k m)\u003c8/(3X+1). There are at\n  most (X+1)/2 positive odd m\u003c=X. The sum of the tails is therefore\n  at most 4(X+1)/(3X+1)\u003c=2.\n\nFor signed f add the two error bounds. For nonnegative f, R is the\ndifference of two numbers in [0,2M], proving the sharper bound.\nNo unjustified passage of a Dirichlet identity to s=1 is needed.\n\n## DF4. Application to actual defect events\n\nWrite G_i(X) for F_i(X) with f=h, G=G_0+G_1+G_2, and let C_plus,\nC_minus be the corresponding harmonic sums of c_plus,c_minus on odd\nsources through X. DF2 and DF4 yield the unconditional finite identity\n\n    C_plus(X)-C_minus(X)\n      =G_2(X)-G_0(X)+Epsilon_h(X)+R_h(X),            (DF5)\n    0\u003c=Epsilon_h(X)\u003c=8/3, |R_h(X)|\u003c=2.\n\nThis is a relation between TWO UNKNOWN differences. It does not prove\nthat either difference is bounded, and does not force G_0 and G_2 to\ndiverge together if G diverges. G_1 is absent from this one balance.\n\nIf G(infinity)\u003cinfinity is separately assumed, sum W(m)h(m) converges:\nW(m)\u003c=2/m+4/(3m^2). Both cutoff remainder terms then tend to zero\nby tails/dominated convergence. Also\n\n    sum |h(U(n))-h(n)|/n\n       \u003c=sum h(U(n))/n+sum h(n)/n\u003cinfinity.\n\nOnly under that assumption can DF5 be passed to its exact infinite\nidentity with R=0. Its nonnegative epsilon correction is not a new\nproof of the assumed convergence.\n\n## DF5. A rigorous obstruction to a relaxed coercivity argument\n\nOne might next try to prove that the total event cost\n\n    V_f=sum_(n odd) |f(U(n))-f(n)|/n\n\ncontrols sum_(n odd) f(n)/n for every bounded binary dyadically invariant\nf that vanishes on a verified finite initial set. This implication is\nfalse for the unchanged, actual integer map U.\n\nLet F be ANY finite set of positive odd integers, and set\n\n    f_F(n)=0 if oddpart(n) belongs to F, and 1 otherwise.\n\nThis function is binary and exactly dyadically invariant. Including\nall odd integers \u003c=B in F makes f_F vanish at every positive n\u003c=B.\nOn odd integers its harmonic mass is the odd harmonic series with only\nfinitely many terms removed, and hence is infinite. But\n\n    |f_F(U(n))-f_F(n)| \u003c= 1_F(n)+1_F(U(n)),\n\nso exact nonnegative inverse reindexing gives\n\n    V_f \u003c= sum_(n in F) 1/n + sum_(m in F) W(m)\u003cinfinity.       (DF6)\n\nBoth creation and death sums are therefore finite in this example.\nIt follows that even controlling the TOTAL change, not just its signed\ndifference, is insufficient for that relaxed class of functions.\nThe example also accommodates any FINITE prescribed set of zero values.\n\nCrucial boundary: f_F is NOT asserted to equal |beta(3n)-beta(n)| for\nan exact Collatz invariant. It need not satisfy the shared-color channel\nrelations in DF2. It is neither a counterexample to Collatz nor a proof\nthat controlling V_h can never help for actual defects. It excludes\nprecisely a deduction based only on binary range, dyadic invariance,\nfinite initial zero values, the U-transport kernel, and finite V_f.\nA successful argument must add a property using the actual common beta,\nor a justified condition determining the defect after long iteration.\n\n## DF6. Goal boundary and sources\n\nThe inherited SB equivalence of finite birth energy and finite G remains\nunchanged; neither is proved finite. DF corrects two misleading draft\ninferences before accepting any new map node. Four roles independently\naudit the table, uniform cutoffs, relaxed example and source boundary.\nInternal agreement is not external peer review or formal certification.\n\nThe finite-variation-to-pointwise bridge still has its named PD170 input.\nThe historical R170 internal acceptance is preserved; this pass neither\nreproves nor revokes its full analytic dependency chain. Root freshly\nread the primary Mazur paper's Appendix A and formal-scope paragraph:\nProposition 3.1 is assembled from cited mixing/coverage inputs and is not\nan independently advertised formal declaration. The source explicitly\ndistinguishes finite auxiliary weighted coverage from distinct integer\ncounting. No source theorem is newly imported into DF.\n\n[Primary Mazur paper](https://www.proofatlas.ai/papers/positive-lower-density-collatz-predecessors/paper-v1.pdf)\n· [SB](SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md)\n· [Creation-channel audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_DEFECT_CREATION_CHANNELS.md)\n· [Finite-cutoff audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_FINITE_CUTOFF_DEFECT_BALANCE.md)\n· [Rigidity/source audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_DEFECT_RIGIDITY_BRIDGE.md).\n\nThe next genuine requirement is a positive estimate for the ACTUAL h that\nuses an additional common-color restriction not satisfied by DF6. Merely\nproving summable creation/death variation without that interface would\nleave a missing inference. No novelty, solved percentage or completion\ndate is claimed. All new research is local; public snapshot425 is unchanged.\n","ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md":"# EC — endpoint counts control total birth counts, with an unproved sparsity input\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root analytic derivation;\nno mathematical programs, CAS, enumeration or Lean. Previous BM was progress.\nThe full Collatz objective remains incomplete. These are exact inequalities\nand conditional consequences, not a proof of their sparsity hypotheses.\n\n## EC1. Definitions and the quantity that was missing\n\nUse the arbitrary exact binary invariant beta, births e(n), injective\nretained graph F_beta and arithmetic prefix counts from\n[SR](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md). No nonconstant beta\nis constructed. Write, with inclusive cutoffs,\n\n    R_beta(X) = #{n \u003c= X : e(n)=1},\n    Z_beta(X) = #{terminal births n \u003c= X},\n    O_beta(X) = #{origin births n \u003c= X}.\n\nA terminal has no outgoing retained edge; an origin has no incoming\nretained edge. They belong to any component type that has the appropriate\nend, not only to finite components. For every birth n, arithmetic F(n)\nexists and e(F(n))=1-d(F(n)). Thus terminals are exactly births satisfying\nd(F(n))=1. Origins retain the exact SR partition P_1 disjoint union D_S\ndisjoint union D_R. These masks are not replaced by independent choices.\n\nThe [BM barrier](FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md)\nrules out a uniform unmasked harmonic factor from the minimum or endpoints.\nThe following counting argument does not require such a component factor.\n\n## EC2. Forward transport, retaining each depth\n\nFix X>=1 and an integer k>=1. Every birth n\u003c=X either admits at least k\nforward retained steps, or first reaches a terminal t after exactly j\u003ck\nsteps. The first class is a subset of the arithmetic k-prefix set and\nhas size at most X(3/4)^k+2^k.\n\nEvery arithmetic step satisfies F(u)+1 \u003c= (3/2)(u+1), so in the second\nclass t \u003c= (3/2)^j(X+1)-1. For a fixed terminal and fixed j there is at\nmost one such predecessor, by injectivity. Consequently\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k + 2^k\n                + sum_(j=0..k-1) Z_beta((3/2)^j(X+1)-1).       (EC1)\n\nNo factor k is needed outside the sum. Replacing it by k times its\nlargest term is valid but loses useful information. Classification is\nvertex by vertex: cycles and infinite-forward paths enter the first\nclass, whereas a backward-infinite path ending at a terminal may enter\nthe second. Neither type is discarded or declared nonexistent.\n\n## EC3. Backward transport\n\nThe arithmetic inverse B has branches 4y/3 and (2y-1)/3 on their exact\nguards. In both cases B(y)\u003c=4y/3. A birth n\u003c=X either admits k retained\nbackward steps or reaches its origin after j\u003ck backward steps. In the\nlatter case the origin is at most (4/3)^j X. A fixed origin has at most\none descendant at a fixed depth. SR's backward prefix estimate gives\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= X(2/3)^k + 2^k\n                + sum_(j=0..k-1) O_beta((4/3)^j X).            (EC2)\n\nVertices with an infinite backward history are covered by the prefix\nterm. An origin can exceed X even if its descendant is \u003c=X; the displayed\nlarger cutoff is essential. Equations EC1 and EC2 are unconditional for\nthe stated exact invariant and retained graph.\n\n## EC4. A power saving for either endpoint set would suffice\n\nAssume first Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta), with 0\u003c=theta\u003c1. For theta>0 the\ndepth sum in EC1 is geometric, bounded by a constant times\nX^theta(3/2)^(k theta). For theta=0 it is O(k).\n\nSet alpha=ln2/ln(8/3) and\n\n    c_Z = min{1/ln(8/3),\n              (1-theta)/(ln(4/3)+theta ln(3/2))},\n    rho_Z = max{alpha,\n                 theta ln2/(ln(4/3)+theta ln(3/2))}.\n\nTaking k=floor(c_Z ln X) balances the decreasing exponent\n1-c_Z ln(4/3) against the increasing exponents c_Z ln2 and\ntheta(1+c_Z ln(3/2)). This proves\n\n    Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)  =>  R_beta(X)=O(X^rho_Z), rho_Z\u003c1.    (EC3)\n\nFor theta=0, O(k) is absorbed by X^alpha. There is no extra ln X.\nThe balance switches at theta=1/2. Floors change only constants.\n\nSimilarly, with gamma=ln2/ln3,\n\n    c_O = min{1/ln3,\n              (1-theta)/(ln(3/2)+theta ln(4/3))},\n    rho_O = max{gamma,\n                 theta ln2/(ln(3/2)+theta ln(4/3))},\n\nEC2 gives\n\n    O_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)  =>  R_beta(X)=O(X^rho_O), rho_O\u003c1.    (EC4)\n\nAny of these full-count power savings would make sum e(n)/n finite by\nintegrating R_beta(t)/t^2. No assumed estimate on Z_beta or O_beta is\nproved by this optimization.\n\n## EC5. General monotone sparsity profiles\n\nLet H be either Z_beta or O_beta. Use the corresponding row:\n\n| Endpoint set H | Prefix r | Growth g | eta=-ln(r)/ln2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Z_beta | 3/4 | 3/2 | ln(4/3)/ln2 |\n| O_beta | 2/3 | 4/3 | ln(3/2)/ln2 |\n\nIn both rows g=2r. Suppose L:[X_0,infinity)->[2,infinity) is\nnondecreasing and H(Y)\u003c=C Y/L(Y) for every sufficiently large Y.\nChoose k=floor(log_2 L(X)). All endpoint cutoffs Y_j in EC1/EC2 are\nat least X, and Y_j\u003c=2X g^j. Hence\n\n    sum_(j\u003ck) H(Y_j) \u003c= C' X g^k/L(X),\n    X r^k \u003c= r^(-1) X/L(X)^eta,\n    2^k \u003c= L(X),\n    g^k/L(X) \u003c= L(X)^(-eta).\n\nThe geometric sum was taken before replacing its terms by their maximum.\nThis proves the conditional profile transfer\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= C'' X/L(X)^eta + L(X).                       (EC5)\n\nNo slow-variation assumption is needed. The final L(X) term is the\nresidue-class counting error; it must not be omitted for a general L.\nA sufficient condition for finite birth energy is\n\n    integral^infinity [1/(t L(t)^eta) + L(t)/t^2] dt \u003c infinity. (EC6)\n\nThis follows by the nonnegative counting integral, without assuming\nenergy convergence in advance.\n\n## EC6. Logarithmic and critical profiles\n\nFor L(X)=(ln X)^q, q>0, the residue error in EC5 is negligible compared\nwith X/(ln X)^(q eta). Thus\n\n    H(Y)=O(Y/(ln Y)^q)\n       => R_beta(X)=O(X/(ln X)^(q eta)).                       (EC7)\n\nFinite energy follows when q eta>1. Explicit sufficient source thresholds:\n\n    terminals: q > ln2/ln(4/3),\n    origins:   q > ln2/ln(3/2).                              (EC8)\n\nAt q=1/eta this argument alone gives only O(X/ln X), whose harmonic\ncomparison integral diverges. A precise refinement is available: put\nq_0=1/eta and L(X)=(ln X)^q_0 (ln ln X)^h for h>0. Then\n\n    H(Y)=O(Y/[(ln Y)^q_0 (ln ln Y)^h])\n       => R_beta(X)=O(X/[ln X (ln ln X)^(h eta)]).             (EC9)\n\nThis suffices for finite energy when h>q_0. The profiles are increasing\nand at least 2 beyond a fixed threshold. Small cutoffs contribute only a\nfinite amount. These are sufficient thresholds from this estimate; no\noptimality or impossibility below them is claimed.\n\n## EC7. What has and has not been reduced\n\nThe endpoint count concerns fewer vertices than the full birth count,\nbut the needed endpoint sparsity is still unproved. BM's inequality\nE_terminal(X)\u003c=2E_beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2 is an energy bound in terms of the\nsame unknown energy; it supplies none of EC3, EC4 or EC8. Likewise SR's\n17/18 coefficient is only an origin contribution and leaves P_1 open.\n\nThus the next independent target can be a uniform counting bound for\nactual terminal masks e(n)d(F(n)), or for the actual origin partition,\nstrong enough for EC3/EC4/EC8. Merely enlarging either set to its\narithmetic guards does not establish that sparsity. The proof above\naccounts for all internal vertices and all component types once such a\ncounting input exists; there is no remaining hidden uniform-length or\nendpoint-multiplicity assumption.\n\nUnder the explicitly named PD170 input, CE/BM already show that a\nnonconstant beta cannot have finite birth energy. Combining that\nconditional fact with EC3/EC4 implies that both endpoint sets would have\nupper counting exponent 1:\n\n    limsup ln(1+Z_beta(X))/ln X =\n    limsup ln(1+O_beta(X))/ln X = 1.                           (EC10)\n\nIf either exponent were \u003c1, an eventual power upper bound with exponent\ntheta\u003c1 would contradict EC3/EC4 and PD. This conditional contrapositive\ndoes not construct a nonconstant invariant, prove endpoint density, or\ngive an unconditional lower bound.\n\nWithout PD, finite energy supplies the recorded density-one constancy\nconclusion, not pointwise constancy. Direct universal smaller-birth\ndescent would be a separate way to avoid that dependency.\n\n## EC8. Source and audit limits\n\nThe counts and profile transfers above are derived from the local SR\nword lifting, injectivity and finite sums. Root and the source-audit role\nread [Mazur's eight-page manuscript](https://www.proofatlas.ai/papers/positive-lower-density-collatz-predecessors/paper-v1.pdf),\nv1.0 dated 6 September 2026. Its all-target statement matches the named\nPD input. Its Proposition 3.1 imports the main analytic estimates; the\nvisible counting argument does not independently establish that input.\nThe manuscript reports automated rather than human peer review. Neither\nthe complete inherited analytic proof nor a full Lean rebuild was\nindependently verified here. PD remains an explicit conditional input.\n\nRoot also freshly read the abstract and bibliographic record of\n[Krasikov–Lagarias](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0205002), with a sublinear\npredecessor lower bound. That is a different quantity from the endpoint\nupper bounds needed here; the full paper was not re-read this pass.\nNo exhaustive priority search or novelty claim is made.\n\nIndependent scopes, corrections and service checks are recorded in\n[the EC review](REVIEW_0485_ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT.md). Full Collatz,\nthe endpoint sparsity input and a PD-free pointwise bridge remain open.\n","EXPONENT_ONE_RESIDUAL_CORE.md":"# EA — pay exponent-one columns from the existing complement\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Root manual proof; no computation.\nKeep all definitions and exact beta masks of\n[WC](TERNARY_WEIGHTED_FIBER_CONTRACTION.md). This is a new bounded\nreduction, not a proof of finite energy or of Collatz.\n\n## EA1. A previously excluded first source already belongs to R\n\nFor odd3-free m, an actual e(3m)=1 implies h(m)=0 and h(4m+1)=1\nby PB2's universal event implication. Thus exponent1 is primitive when\nit is an actual birth. Its only source is p1=4m+1. Since v2(p1-1)=2\nand3 does not divide m, p1 lies outside N and outside Pplus; its mass\nalready belongs to R_omega. Let Q_omega(X) be the mass of these p1.\nThe map m to p1 is injective and Q_omega(X)\u003c=R_omega(X).\n\n## EA2. A finite tail coefficient is sufficient here\n\nConsider a column with e(3m)=1. Its actual primitive exponents a>=2\nare still defined by constancy of beta along the full column through a.\nTheir source fibers are precisely those in WC5. Removing actual masks\nonly enlarges the nonnegative tail. The geometric majorants in WC6 give:\n\n    m=1 mod3: sum_(a>=2) W^0_(a,omega)=1/(16m),\n              first actual weight=1/[4(4m+1)];\n    m=2 mod3: sum_(a>=2) W^0_(a,omega)=1/(8m),\n              first actual weight=1/(4m+1).\n\nIndeed in the first case the two geometric sums give\n(1/4m)[1/2-1/4]=1/(16m); in the second only the geometric series\nwith ratio1/3 remains. The resulting tail/first ratios are respectively\nat most5/4 and5/8, since m>=1. If X\u003cp1 there is no retained later\nsource; otherwise the first source is fully retained, so these same\nbounds apply to every finite cutoff. In particular\n\n    Jprim_omega(from columns with e(3m)=1;X) \u003c=(5/4)Q_omega(X). (EA1)\n\nThe old CF exponent-one arithmetic barrier excluded a coefficient below\none in the first case. EA1 does not require one: Q is already part of R.\nThere is no contradiction with CF10 and no cancellation of unknown mass.\n\n## EA3. A smaller actual core\n\nIn columns with e(3m)=0 keep the original first primitive exponent\nA>=2 and call the mass of its whole retained fiber Fstrict_omega(X).\nIt is a subset of the old F_omega. WC's two-cutoff estimate gives\n\n    Jprim_omega(X)\u003c=(5/4)Q_omega(X)+(149/84)Fstrict_omega(X). (EA2)\n\nTogether with WC5 and Q\u003c=R this gives\n\n    Cstrict_omega(X):=R_omega(X)+Fstrict_omega(X) \u003c= G_omega(X)\n      \u003c=2R_omega(X)+(5/2)Q_omega(X)+(149/42)Fstrict_omega(X)\n      \u003c=(9/2)Cstrict_omega(X).                                  (EA3)\n\nAll sums have the same finite cutoff. The lower comparison uses source\ndisjointness, and the last step uses149/42\u003c9/2. This new actual core\nis contained in WC's old R+F core; no strict containment is asserted\nwithout existence of the corresponding actual births. Finite core and\nfinite G are equivalent, but neither is proved finite.\n\n## EA4. The remaining first-fiber mask now covers A=2\n\nFor a retained first column with A>=2 put y=3^(A-1)m and B=beta(m).\nPrimitive constancy gives beta(y)=B. Put u=y/3=3^(A-2)m. Since\nT(u)=(3u+1)/2=T(y+1), the genuine common endpoint gives\nbeta(y+1)=beta(u)=B. If A=2, e(y)=e(3m)=0\nby this column's definition. If A>=3, e(y)=0 by firstness as in CF7.\nThus beta(2y+1)=B for ALL remaining first columns, without a minimum-h\nassumption. Let v=2y+1\u003cp_A=4y+1. If h(v)=1 it is a smaller signed\ndefect. If h(v)=0 the exact residual colors are\n\n    (beta(6y),...,beta(6y+4))=(B,1-B,B,B,B).           (EA4)\n\nThe last entry follows from T(2y+1)=3y+2. This is still a compatible\nlocal mask rather than a contradiction or an assertion of an actual\nnonconstant invariant. EA does not justify setting h(v)=0 without its\ncase assumption. The first-fiber branch and the complementary R remain\nthe next analytical obligations. No external novelty claim is made.\n","FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md":"# BM — finite-component weights and a balanced arithmetic barrier\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual root derivation and\nbounded audit, with no numerical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean.\nPrevious SR was progress; the full Collatz goal remains incomplete.\n\n## BM1. Setup\n\nUse the exact binary T-invariant β, active boundary d(n), birth e(n),\nand the retained partial graph Fβ from\n[SR1–SR12](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md).\nThe arithmetic map is S(n)=(3n+1)/2 on odd n and R(n)=3n/4 on\nmultiples of four. F is undefined at 2 modulo 4, is injective, and\nhas no predecessor for an integer congruent to 1 modulo 3.\n\nFor a finite acyclic retained component C let m=min C and\nW(C)=Σ_(n∈C)1/n. The numerical minimum need not be either endpoint.\nSR gives N_k^+(X)≤X(3/4)^k+2^k for starts admitting k arithmetic steps.\nAll statements below retain the distinction between this arithmetic\nenvelope and components consisting of actual births.\n\n## BM2. An individual bound from the numerical minimum\n\nIn a finite directed chain at most k vertices, its terminal suffix,\nhave fewer than k further retained edges. All other vertices admit k\narithmetic F-steps. Thus, for every X≥m and integer k≥0,\n\n    N_C(X)≤X(3/4)^k+2^k+k.\n\nThis remains valid for k larger than the chain length. Put\nb=8/3, α=ln2/ln b, k=floor(log_b X). Then\n\n    N_C(X)≤(7/3)X^α+log_b X,\n    W(C)≤[7/(3(1−α))]m^(α−1)+(ln m+1)/(m ln b).       (BM1)\n\nThe second line follows by integrating N_C(X)/X² from m to infinity.\nThis is an individual bound. Distinct components have distinct minima,\nbut distinctness alone does not bound the sums of m^(α−1) or\n(ln m)/m. It does not imply finite total birth energy. The SR origin\nestimate and BM1 concern different objects and are not comparable as\nstronger/weaker inequalities.\n\n## BM3. Words whose cumulative slopes stay bounded\n\nFix k≥1. Construct a word of k S/R letters from its cumulative slope:\nstart at λ_0=1, choose S when λ_(j−1)≤1, otherwise choose R. Then\n\n    λ_j=(3/2)λ_(j−1) or (3/4)λ_(j−1),\n    3/4≤λ_j≤3/2,              0≤j≤k.\n\nThe last assertion is immediate by induction, using the choice of branch.\nFor the affine output at stage j write n_j=λ_j n+B_j. If S occurs\nat step i, indexed so that λ_i is the multiplier after that step,\n\n    B_j=λ_j Σ_(1≤i≤j, step i is S) 1/(2λ_i),\n    0≤B_j≤j.                                        (BM2)\n\nEach summand, after multiplication by λ_j, is at most 1. The additive\nterm is retained; replacing the trajectory by its slope alone would\nnot prove the required integer statement.\n\n## BM4. Positive integer realization of a whole finite component\n\nLet s and r be the word's S/R counts, A=s+2r≤2k. SR's direct lifting\nargument gives one exact word-domain class modulo 2^A. Changing n by\n2^A changes the final integer n_k by 3^k, an odd number. Requiring\nn_k≡2 mod4 therefore selects one of four lifts, giving one class\nmodulo 2^(A+2). Add n≡1 mod3 by the Chinese remainder theorem.\n\nChoose the least representative n≥k in the resulting class. Then\n\n    k≤n\u003ck+3·2^(A+2)≤k+12·4^k≤13·4^k.                (BM3)\n\nThere is no incoming arithmetic edge to n, and no outgoing edge from\nn_k. The intervening guarded steps are all defined and positive.\nThey are distinct: a repetition would create a cycle with a tail,\ncontrary to injectivity and absence of a predecessor at n. Hence these\nk+1 vertices form a complete finite arithmetic component C_k.\nEquations BM2–BM3 give, for every j,\n\n    3n/4≤n_j≤3n/2+k≤5n/2.                            (BM4)\n\nNo numerical search for the representative or the word was performed.\nThis is a parametric existence proof by congruence lifting.\n\n## BM5. No uniform arithmetic factor from minima or two endpoints\n\nFor m_k=min C_k, BM4 implies\n\n    m_k W(C_k)≥3(k+1)/10,\n    W(C_k)/(1/n+1/n_k)≥6(k+1)/35.                    (BM5)\n\nFor the second bound use 1/n+1/n_k≤7/(3n). Since\n3k/4≤m_k≤n≤13·4^k, these minima tend to infinity, and also\n\n    m_k W(C_k)≥[3/(10 ln4)]ln m_k−[3 ln13/(10 ln4)].  (BM6)\n\nConsequently no fixed K works in either W(C)≤K/min C or\nW(C)≤K(1/origin+1/terminal) on all finite arithmetic F-components.\nBM6 exhibits a logarithmic lower loss; it does not assert an optimal\nupper bound of logarithmic order.\n\nThis does NOT disprove either estimate for actual birth components.\nThe final n_k is 2 modulo 4 and is not a birth. No nonconstant exact\nβ or realization of all masks on these chains is claimed. For k≥2,\nthe earlier vertices n_0,…,n_(k−2) avoid both 2 modulo 4 and 1 modulo 8:\nthe former would stop F immediately, and the latter would make its next\noutput 2 modulo 4. Thus those two elementary residue exclusions alone\ndo not prevent long arithmetic prefixes of this type. The full color\nconstraints remain essential and have not been replaced by these guards.\n\n## BM6. Terminal masks, spacing and a bounded endpoint estimate\n\nLet x be any birth with no outgoing retained F-edge, and let y=F(x).\nEvery birth is in F's domain, so terminality means e(y)=0. SR's exact\nrules give d(y)=1. Let c be the outer birth color of x.\n\nFor an S-terminal, β(y)=c, β(y+1)=1−c and β(2y+1)=1−c.\nThus its active child is 2y; also y≡2 mod3. For an R-terminal,\nβ(y)=1−c, β(y+1)=c and β(2y+1)=1−c. Its active child is 2y+1,\nand y≡0 mod3. In both cases the forced next active boundary is\n1 modulo 3. It cannot be another endpoint target, because such targets\nbelong to the arithmetic image of F, which omits that residue.\n\nAll endpoint targets on one active ancestry path are therefore at\nnonadjacent depths. Charging them to their unique nearest birth b gives\nΣ1/y≤2/(3b) on each of its two paths, hence at most 4/(3b) in total.\nThe exact costs are\n\n    1/x_S=3/(2y)+3/[2y(2y−1)],\n    1/x_R=3/(4y)≤3/(2y).\n\nInjectivity makes all targets distinct. Summing the correction for S\ntargets y≥2 gives at most Σ_(y≥2)3/[2y(y−1)]=3/2. The cutoffs are\nb≤floor((3x+1)/4) for S and b≤floor(3x/8) for R. Consequently\n\n    E_terminal(X)≤2Eβ((3X+1)/4)+3/2.                 (BM7)\n\nThis includes the terminals of finite components and is only an endpoint\nbound. It is neither a contraction nor a bound on internal vertices.\nThe potential 1/n has opposite signed changes on S and R; that fact\nalone does not rule out other potentials using more information.\n\nFor a finite retained component with minimum m and terminal x, the\nexplicit sufficient pointwise descent condition is\n\n    F(x)\u003c2m.                                         (BM8)\n\nThen its charged birth b≤floor(F(x)/2)\u003cm lies outside that component.\nEquivalently BM8 is 3x+1\u003c4m for an S-terminal, or 3x\u003c8m for an\nR-terminal. These are sufficient conditions, not necessary ones: the\nactual nearest birth can be deeper than the displayed bound. They have\nnot been established for every component, and do not treat infinite\nor cyclic components.\n\n## BM7. Compressing a single R-run recovers existing growth modes\n\nFor an odd birth n>1 write 3n+1=2^a u, u odd. Follow the first S\nstep and then the available R-steps, as long as outputs remain births.\nAny first failure gives a birth below n: at the S-output it is at\nmost floor((3n+1)/4), and at an R-output it is at most floor(3x/8)\nwith x≤S(n), hence below n. If a is even, the complete arithmetic\nrun would end in 2 modulo 4, so a failure is unavoidable.\n\nIf a=2r+1 is odd and the whole run is retained, its odd output is\n\n    y=3^r u=3^r(3n+1)/2^(2r+1).\n\nFor r≥2, 2^(2r+1)−3^(r+1)≥5 and y\u003cn. The remaining growing\npossibilities are r=0 (S) and r=1 (G₃=(9n+3)/8, n≡13 mod16).\nThe old [SC analysis](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md) already pairs\nthe relevant S-steps and obtains H/G₃ alternatives with their masks.\nThis is a useful connection to that earlier work, not a proof that the\nentire mixed F-graph is identical to the old increasing graph, nor a\nnew solution route obtained merely by changing notation.\n\n## BM8. Finite energy still leaves a pointwise obligation\n\nRoot and the source-audit role re-read the complete\n[CE energy interface](ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md) and\n[SB174 dyadic-profile proof](../../../work/round174-n-summable-boundary-rigidity.md).\nTheir unconditional conclusion is\n\n    Σe(n)/n\u003c∞ ⇒ Σd(n)/n\u003c∞\n               ⇒ β equals one color outside a set of natural density zero.\n\nThe first implication follows from 0≤E_1(N)−D_1(N)/2≤3/2.\nFor the second, summable differences make β(floor(2^k x)) converge in\nlocal real L¹ and almost everywhere. Exact odd invariance gives a limit\ninvariant under ×3 as well as ×2. Irrational rotation by log₂3 forces\na constant limit; returning to integers gives a density statement.\nIt does not set the color at every fixed exceptional integer.\n\nThe archive already has a named additional premise PD170, with an\ninternally accepted synthesis in\n[QS170](../../../work/round170-qualitative-density-synthesis.md): every\nfixed positive target not divisible by 3 has a predecessor set of positive\nlower natural density. Its pointwise implication is immediate and should\nnot be described as missing under that premise. If β(n) differs from the\ndensity-one color, write n=2^j u with u odd and set a=3u+1. Then a is\nnot divisible by 3 and has the exceptional color. Every ordinary Collatz\npredecessor of a has that color, contradicting density zero under PD.\nThus finite energy plus PD implies pointwise constancy. This is the\nexisting conditional bridge, not a new result or a withdrawal of QS170.\n\nRoot re-read QS170's statement, synthesis and recorded acceptance, and\nfreshly checked Theorem 1.1, Proposition 3.1 and Sections 4–5 in Mazur's\n[Positive Lower Density of Collatz Predecessors](https://www.proofatlas.ai/papers/positive-lower-density-collatz-predecessors/paper-v1.pdf),\nmanuscript v1.0 dated 6 September 2026. The theorem states PD, while\nProposition 3.1 collects its inherited analytic input. The full analytic\ndependency chain and formal build were not re-audited in this pass.\n\nUnder the named PD input, the missing energy bound remains the main\nobligation of this route. Without that input, a separate pointwise bridge\nis still needed. Direct universal smaller-birth descent would avoid it,\nbut the sufficient test (BM8) is not universal. These dependency choices\nmust remain explicit rather than silently adding or removing hypotheses.\n\n## BM9. Source scope and conclusion\n\nRoot freshly read the introduction, map definitions, definitions 1–2\nand Proposition 1 of Elia–Tucker,\n[Consecutive Integers and the Collatz Conjecture](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141),\nv1 dated 30 November 2015. The paper distinguishes ordinary and shortcut\nmaps and invokes finite parity-class coding, attributed there to Terras.\nIt does not supply BM's full-component mass estimate. Terras's original\nproof was not read afresh; the lifting used here is proved directly.\nAttempts to reload Bernstein–Lagarias failed by redirect/timeout; no\nfresh reading of that PDF is claimed. No exhaustive priority check or\nnovelty claim is made.\n\nIndependent reports and corrections are in\n[the BM review](REVIEW_0485_FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS.md).\nFull Collatz, all-cycle exclusion and aggregate finite-component mass\nremain unproved. A PD-free pointwise energy bridge is not obtained.\nNo completion time or percentage follows from these bounded results.\n","FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION.md":"# CF — first primitive fibers: signed descent and a common-cutoff weight bound\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root analytical derivation.\nNo numerical programs, enumeration, CAS, Lean or GPU. Full Collatz remains\nopen. The PB scope audit is still running; this successor does not certify\nthat unfinished audit or a completed four-role acceptance.\n\n## CF1. First means first among primitive births divisible by nine\n\nLet beta be an arbitrary exact binary invariant of shortcut Collatz T.\nKeep g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h=|g| and the birth indicator e from\n[PB](PLUS_BIRTH_ROUTING_AND_CRITICAL_WEIGHTS.md).\nFor a positive odd m not divisible by3 define\n\n    A_m={a>=2: e(3^a m)=1 and h(3^j m)=0 for 0\u003c=j\u003ca}.\n\nIf this set is nonempty, let A=A(m)=min A_m. An empty column contributes\nzero to all sums. No actual birth or nonconstant invariant is assumed to\nexist. The exponent1 is deliberately NOT included in this definition.\n\nFor every a in A_m, the primitive mask gives B=beta(m),\nbeta(3^j m)=B for0\u003c=j\u003c=a and beta(2*3^a m+1)=1-B. The PB plus selector\nis then forced at u_a=3^(a-1)m. Its complete source fiber is\n\n    n_(a,l)=1+4^(l+1)3^(a-1-l)m, 0\u003c=l\u003c=a-1,\n    g(n_(a,l))=1-2B, h(n_(a,l))=1.                  (CF1)\n\nThe first fiber means a=A, including all its l values, not only p_A=n_(A,0).\n\n## CF2. Disjoint fibers and a strict signed descent\n\nThe source label is recoverable from n:\n\n    l=(v2(n-1)-2)/2,\n    a=1+l+v3(n-1),\n    m=(n-1)/(4^(l+1)3^(a-1-l)).                    (CF2)\n\nThe assumption that m is odd and3-free is essential here. Thus different\n(m,a,l) yield different sources, even when sums are reorganized by column.\n\nIf a>A is another actual primitive exponent, set l'=min(l,A-1) and\nq=n_(A,l'). Both g values in CF1 are the same. For l\u003c=A-1,\n(n_(a,l)-1)/(q-1)=3^(a-A)>=3. For l>=A the ratio is\n4^(l+1-A)3^(a-1-l)>=4. Hence\n\n    g(n_(a,l))=g(q), q\u003c n_(a,l),\n    q \u003c= (n_(a,l)+2)/3.                            (CF3)\n\nThe target lies in the actual first primitive fiber and remains below\nthe same cutoff as its source. Different later sources may share that\ntarget; their weights are controlled below. This argument uses firstness\nonly to select an actual earlier fiber, and does not assert descent from\nthe first fiber itself.\n\n## CF3. Complete weights and their denominator correction\n\nLet W_(a,X)(m) be the sum of 1/n_(a,l) over retained members n_(a,l)\u003c=X,\nand W_a(m) the complete sum. Put alpha=3/4. Its arithmetic majorant is\n\n    W_a(m) \u003c W_a^0(m)=[1-alpha^a]/[3^(a-1)m].       (CF4)\n\nDropping all later actual masks, the total tail after A is bounded by\n\n    T_A(m)=sum_(a>A) W_a^0(m)\n          =[3^(1-A)/2-4^(-A)]/m.                  (CF5)\n\nThese are geometric sums with ratios1/3 and1/4. If q0=alpha^A\u003c=9/16,\n\n    T_A/W_A^0=(1/2-q0/3)/(1-q0)\u003c=5/7.\n\nFor a direct algebraic check, the last inequality is equivalent to\n16q0\u003c=9. For x_l=n_(A,l)-1, all x_l>=4*3^(A-1)m>=12, so\n1/(1+x_l)>=(12/13)/x_l. Summing gives\n\n    W_A >=(12/13)W_A^0,\n    sum_(a>A) W_a \u003c=T_A \u003c=(65/84)W_A.             (CF6)\n\nThe second inequality concerns the full arithmetic tail; retaining only\nactual later primitive exponents can only reduce it. A bound relative to\nthe first birth weight1/(3^A m) is different and need not be subunit.\n\n## CF4. Why the same bound survives truncation\n\nLet N_A=n_(A,A-1)=1+4^A m, the largest member of the first fiber.\nIf X>=N_A, its entire fiber is retained and CF6 immediately applies.\n\nIf X\u003cN_A, any later retained source must have l\u003c=A-2: for l>=A-1,\nthe smallest later source already exceeds N_A. For the remaining l,\nwrite x_l=4^(l+1)3^(A-1-l)m. A later source with the same l is\n1+x_l*3^r, r=a-A>=1, and its first-fiber target is1+x_l\u003cX.\nFor each retained target,\n\n    sum_(r>=1) 1/(1+x_l*3^r)\n      \u003c=1/(2x_l) \u003c=(13/24)/(1+x_l).               (CF7)\n\nThe last inequality follows from x_l>=12. The left side enlarges the\ntruncated source set. All assigned targets are retained, so summing CF7\ngives a bound by(13/24)W_(A,X). Since13/24\u003c65/84, both cutoff cases imply\n\n    sum_(a in A_m,a>A) W_(a,X)(m)\n           \u003c=(65/84)W_(A,X)(m).                   (CF8)\n\nIf no source or target is retained, the corresponding sums are zero.\nNo comparison of a truncated tail with an unavailable full first fiber\nis made in the second case.\n\n## CF5. Global first-fiber reduction\n\nLet F(X)=sum_(m with A_m nonempty) W_(A(m),X)(m). By CF2 these first\nfibers are disjoint. TL's Jprim(X) is the mass of ALL actual primitive\nPB fibers, so exact partition and CF8 give, at every finite X,\n\n    F(X) \u003c= Jprim(X) \u003c=(149/84)F(X).               (CF9)\n\nOnly finitely many columns have retained sources at each X. There is\nno assumption of convergence of F, Jprim, G or birth energy. In\nparticular, uniform boundedness of Jprim(X) is equivalent to uniform\nboundedness of F(X), but neither is proved here.\n\nCombining this result with [RF5](RESIDUE_FILTERED_LAST_CHANGE_WEIGHTS.md)\nmerely yields M(X)\u003c=(149/84)F(X)+sum_(t\u003c=X) b(t)h(t)/t. The unresolved\nfirst-fiber term is positive, and RF's other target classes remain.\nThe global SP core bound has not been replaced or strengthened by this\nformal substitution. CF3 removes later primitive fibers from a possible\nminimum-defect location; the first fibers are left untouched.\n\n## CF6. A boundary: starting at exponent one changes the arithmetic bound\n\nOne might instead include primitive births at exponent1 to eliminate\nmore first fibers. The proof above uses A>=2. For the purely arithmetic\ncase A=1 let x=4m. The first fiber is the single integer1+x, while the\nfull later arithmetic tail is\n\n    S(x)=sum_(s>=1)1/(1+x*3^s)\n          +sum_(r>=1,s>=0)1/(1+x*4^r*3^s).\n\nThe reciprocal multipliers sum to1; their squares sum to\n1/8+(1/15)(9/8)=1/5. Since1/(1+y)>=1/y-1/y^2 for y>0,\n\n    S(x)>=1/x-1/(5x^2)>1/(x+1), x>=4.             (CF10)\n\nThe final difference is at least(4x-1)/(5x^2(x+1))>0. Thus the complete\narithmetic tail cannot have a coefficient below one relative to that\nsingle first source. The positive convergent series also eventually\nviolates such a coefficient at a finite cutoff. This does NOT construct\nactual primitive births at all these exponents: selector and color\nmasks were removed. It rules out only extending the same unmasked\nweight argument to A=1 without new input.\n\n## CF7. What firstness adds to the remaining actual mask\n\nFor A>=3, put y=3^(A-1)m. Its exponent A-1 still belongs to the\na>=2 domain. Primitive constancy through A and the minimal choice of A\nimply e(y)=0, whereas e(3y)=1. Genuine paths give\nbeta(y+1)=beta(y/3)=B; hence e(y)=0 forces beta(2y+1)=B.\nThe candidate v=2y+1 satisfies v\u003cp_A=4y+1. If h(v)=1, it is a\nstrictly smaller defect with g(v)=1-2B, the same signed value as the\nwhole first fiber. If h(v)=0, the remaining local mask is\n\n    beta(6y)=B,\n    beta(6y+1)=1-B,\n    beta(6y+2)=beta(6y+3)=B.                      (CF11)\n\nHere beta(6y)=beta(3y)=B and beta(6y+2)=beta(3y+1)=beta(y)=B;\nbeta(6y+3)=beta(3v)=beta(v)=B uses the assumed h(v)=0. This mask\nis an exact residual condition, not a contradiction. When A=2,\nfirstness as defined here supplies no e(y)=0 statement at exponent1.\nThat boundary must be handled separately.\n\nThe subsequent [minimum-defect lemma MC](MINIMUM_DEFECT_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md)\ndoes handle A=2 under the ADDITIONAL assumption that p_A is the least\nodd h-defect. Then e(y)=1 would create the smaller h-defect4y/3+1.\nThis forces e(y)=0 in that subclass, but does not eliminate the first\nfiber or change the unrestricted CF estimate.\n\nTwo primitive births on the same column have the same opposite middle\ncolor. Merging their middle paths therefore does not alone yield a\ncontradiction. The report\n[two-birth audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_TWO_PRIMITIVE_BIRTHS.md) records the\nfailed proposal and the corrected common endpoint T(z+1)=T(z/3).\n\nThe later [residual-identity attempt](FOLLOWUP_0431_FIRST_FIBER_RESIDUAL_IDENTITY.md)\nalso gives no new descent. Root found its erroneous color assignment:\nT(2y+1)=3y+2 forces beta(6y+4)=beta(2y+1)=B when A>=3.\nThus d(6y+2)=d(6y+3)=h(2y+1), not a complementary pair. The\nproposed signed decomposition was withdrawn, the full file corrected,\nand root reread it. In particular h(2y+1)=0 leaves both those boundaries\nzero and does not cancel the nonzero first-fiber defect. CF1–CF11 above\ndo not rely on the withdrawn formula.\n\n## CF8. Audit and source scope\n\nRoot independently verified all arithmetic above and read the two\nfirst-column reports:\n[0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_FIRST_COLUMN_FIBER_DESCENT.md),\n[0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_FIRST_PRIMITIVE_COLUMN.md).\nBoth roles then completed full-file audits, including CF10–CF11, and root\nread those final appendices on19 September2026. Root also corrected the\nearlier majorant-only wording: a displayed upper majorant above one does\nnot rule out a sharper actual-mask bound. PB's fourth-role scope audit\nremains pending. The map must retain that pending distinction; these\nthree completed readings are not a completed four-role acceptance.\n\nThe finite-path background is the same primary affine/parity framework\nchecked in [RF](RESIDUE_FILTERED_LAST_CHANGE_WEIGHTS.md) against\n[Rozier–Terracol](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948), equation(2).\nThat paper is not a source for CF's inverse-weight bound. The geometric\ninequalities here are proved explicitly; external novelty is not claimed.\nAll work is local. The next obligation is control or descent of the\nACTUAL FIRST primitive fibers, including CF11 and the A=2 boundary.\nIt has not been obtained by renaming the primitive mass F.\n","FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md":"# FL: a fixed pair-lift rule cannot create an infinite set of new sources\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Root derivation; independently checked\nin FOLLOWUP_0459_INCIDENCE_ARITHMETIC_AUDIT.md, section5.\nHand proof only; no mathematical code or enumeration. This is a limitation\nof fixed periodic inverse rules, not a theorem excluding arbitrary bad orbits.\n\n## FL1. Structural, ordered consecutive-pair lifts\n\nA shortcut parity word of length K and odd count P has forward map\n(3^P x+C)/2^K. Its inverse affine expression is\n\n    F(n)=(2^K n-C)/3^P.\n\nSuppose two fixed inverse words structurally send (n,n+1) to\n(F(n),F(n)+1): their affine difference is identically one on the\nadmissible progression, not an accidental equality at a single seed.\nEquality of slopes forces equal lengths K and equal odd counts P, by\nunique prime factorization. If the right word has constant C_R, then\n\n    C_R=C+2^K-3^P.\n\nThe right inverse expression is G(n+1), not F(n+1); the words generally\nhave different constants. A nonidentity structural lift has P>=1.\nIndeed P=0 means both words are entirely even, C=C_R=0; their lifted\ngap is 2^K, which is one only for the empty word K=0.\n\n## FL2. Exact integer repetition budget\n\nFix K>=1, P>=1, C an integer, and an integer seed n. Define\n\n    D=2^K-3^P, E=Dn-C.\n\nThen D is nonzero and coprime to3. For each j>=0,\n\n    F^j(n)=(2^(Kj) E+C 3^(Pj))/(D 3^(Pj)).                 (FL1)\n\nProof: the affine fixed point is C/D and\nF(x)-C/D=(2^K/3^P)(x-C/D). Alternatively direct induction gives FL1.\nThe numerator is divisible by D: modulo D, 2^K=3^P and E=-C, so\nthe numerator vanishes. Since gcd(D,3)=1 and 2^(Kj) is a3-unit,\n\n    F^j(n) is an integer  iff  3^(Pj) divides E.           (FL2)\n\nThus, if E!=0, the largest number of successive integer repetitions is\n\n    floor(v_3(E)/P).                                     (FL3)\n\nHere v_3 of a negative integer means the valuation of its absolute value.\nAll preceding iterates are then integers too. Positivity, exact parity,\nadmissible word cylinders and first-hit killing can only shorten this\nalgebraic budget; FL2 does not silently assert those additional properties.\nIf E=0, F(n)=n, so unlimited algebraic repetitions create no new source.\n\n## FL3. A fixed periodic schedule is subject to the same obstruction\n\nFor a prescribed finite list of structural ordered lifts F_1,...,F_s,\ncompose a full period. The composition is again\n\n    F_*(n)=(2^(K_*) n-C_*)/3^(P_*),\n    K_*=sum K_i, P_*=sum P_i, C_* an integer.\n\nIf at least one rule is nonidentity, P_*>0. Apply FL2 at period boundaries.\nIf E_*!=0, only finitely many complete integer periods are possible.\nIf E_*=0, the period boundary returns to its seed, and the prescribed\npartial-period positions repeat. In both cases the schedule creates only\nfinitely many different source pairs from that seed. A fixed finite family\nof prescribed periodic schedules still has a finite union of such sources.\n\nThis does NOT cover an unbounded family of periods, arbitrary switching\namong rules, unequal/asynchronous source words, or a rule chosen anew from\nthe current source. Those are precisely places where further arguments\nwould be necessary. This result does not prove their failure or success.\n\n## FL4. The elementary two-step example\n\nFor m>=0, direct shortcut steps give\n\n    T^2(4m+1)=3m+1, T^2(4m+2)=3m+2.\n\nTherefore the ordered inverse pair at (n,n+1), n=3m+1, is\n\n    (L(n),L(n)+1)=((4n-1)/3,(4n+2)/3).\n\nThe left lift L has K=2,P=1,C=1, so D=1,E=n-1. A fixed n!=1 admits\nat most v_3(n-1) successive integer lifts. The seed1 gives the unchanged\npair(1,2). The terminal pair is fixed while the permitted sources change.\n\n## FL5. Meaning for the current proof attempt\n\nMerely finding a closed repeated inverse rule for adjacent pairs cannot\nturn a single seed into a positive-density family. One must control genuine\nbranching or a varying family of rules, including their admissibility and\nsizes. This rules out the specific periodic amplification shortcut being\nconsidered; it does not supply the missing lower bound for Q(a,c).\n\nRoot checked the primary definitions of C/T, Theorem2.1 and Definitions1–2\nin [Elia–Tucker](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141). Definition2 describes\nforward block prefixes preserving ordered adjacency; it is prior art for\nthe structural setup. The explicit shortcut lift above and FL1–FL3 were\nchecked algebraically here. No priority claim is made, and the paper is not\ncited as supplying the missing fixed-target pair density theorem. Its\nordinary-C coalescence endpoint6m+4 must not be confused with shortcut\nendpoint3m+2.\n","FIXED_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md":"# Фиксированный период и один рациональный старт\n\n16 сентября 2026. Ручная приёмка 0207/0209. Программ, тестов, CAS\nи перебора не использовано.\n\nПусть ξ=a/b∈Z₂, b>0 нечётно, фиксирован, а его паритетный код не\nпериодичен в конечном счёте. Для префикса U длины i обозначим число\nединиц через S, а m_i=T^i(ξ). Зафиксируем непустое слово W длины q,\nчисло его единиц R, c=C_W и k=2^q−3^R≠0. Продолжение UW∞ имеет старт\n\n\\[\n r=\\frac{A}{3^S k},\\qquad A=2^i c-kC_U.\n\\]\n\nДля A≠0 пусть v=v₃(A), A#=|A|/3^v и h — примитивная высота тройки\n(A,2^q3^S,3^(S+R)). Тогда на всех достаточно длинных префиксах\n\n\\[\n v\u003cS,\\qquad\n 1\\le\\frac h{A^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{6\\,2^q}{|k|},\\frac{6\\,3^R}{|k|}\\right).\n\\]\n\nПорог зависит от ξ и W. Для любого фиксированного конечного набора W\nможно взять общий порог и общую константу.\n\n## Почему исчезает целая ветвь числителя\n\nИз непериодичности следует S_i→∞. При v≥S число z=A/3^S целое.\nАффинная формула и нечётность знаменателя b дают\n\n\\[\n 3^S a+bC_U\\equiv0\\pmod{2^i},\\qquad\n 3^S z\\equiv-kC_U\\pmod{2^i},\\qquad 2^i\\mid ka-bz.\n\\]\n\nИз C_U≤2^(i−S)3^S следует |z|≤(c+|k|)2^(i−S). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n |ka-bz|/2^i\\le |ka|2^{-i}+b(c+|k|)2^{-S}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nНа хвосте это вынуждает ka=bz, то есть ξ=z/k=r. Противоречие\nнепериодичности следует из конкретного равенства Φ₂(UW∞)=r=ξ.\nОбщий переход «рациональность означает периодичность» не используется.\nДелимость на 3 числа k не мешает сравнению по модулю 2^i.\n\n## Нижняя оценка обычного размера A\n\nВсе m_i лежат на сетке (1/b)Z. Если бы |m_i| не уходило в бесконечность,\nнекоторое состояние повторилось бы в ограниченном интервале, и код\nстал бы периодическим. Значит |m_i|→∞. Также m_i≠0, поскольку 0\nфиксировано, а S≥1 на хвосте.\n\nПоложим t_i=C_U/3^S≥1/3. Аффинная формула даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac A{3^S}=-kt_i+c\\frac{\\xi+t_i}{m_i}.\n\\]\n\nПри c>0 и |m_i|≥2c(1+3|ξ|)/|k| второй член по модулю не больше\n|k|t_i/2. Поэтому |A|/3^S≥|k|t_i/2≥|k|/6.\nПри c=0 сразу |A|/3^S=|k|t_i≥|k|/3. В обоих случаях A≠0 на хвосте.\nПодстановка в точную формулу\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}=3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right)\n\\]\n\nзавершает доказательство.\n\n## Область вывода\n\nДля совместимых приближений UW∞ с N→∞ начало i обязательно уходит\nв бесконечность. Иначе ограниченная подпоследовательность фиксировала бы\nU и W, и совпадение всех конечных префиксов дало бы периодический код.\nЭто верно и для конечного набора W. Следовательно, на таких семьях\nlog h−log A#=O(1); положительные относительные маржи эквивалентны.\n\nСлучаи k\u003c0, k>0 и 3|k покрыты. При R=0 имеем W=0^q,c=0.\nПри S=0,C_W≠0 имеем A=2^i C_W и v₃(A)=0, поскольку последняя\nединица W даёт единственный не делящийся на 3 член; этот начальный\nкрай в хвостовое доказательство не включается. При A=0 величина A#\nне определена, а кандидат имеет старт 0; нижняя оценка исключает его\nна хвосте. Ни одна оценка не утверждает существования самой маржи.\n\nПолный Коллатц и вопрос о растущем множестве периодов остаются открытыми.\n[Приёмка](REVIEW_0207_0209.md) · [0207](task-0207.md) · [0209](task-0209.md).\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_ACTIVE_BOUNDARY_COUPLING.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — coupling of odd active boundaries to S and A/B branches\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded manual analysis\nafter PC and WS. No mathematical programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or\nnew agents were used. The report concerns an arbitrary exact binary\nT-invariant; it constructs no nonconstant invariant.\n\n## 1. Exact identities at an odd active boundary\n\nLet \\(q\\) be odd and put\n\\[\n y=S(q)=\\frac{3q+1}{2}.\n\\]\nExact invariance gives\n\\[\n \\beta(2q)=\\beta(q),\\qquad\n \\beta(2q+1)=\\beta(3q+2),\\qquad\n \\beta(3q+1)=\\beta(q).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n d(3q+1)=d(2q).                                  \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe boundary XOR identity at \\(y\\), since \\(2y=3q+1\\), gives\n\\[\n d(2y)\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}d(2y+1)=d(y).\n\\]\nCombining this with (1),\n\\[\n e(y)=d(2y)d(2y+1)\n      =d(2q)\\bigl(1-d(y)\\bigr).                    \\tag{2}\n\\]\nEquation (2) is exact; it does not assume that \\(q\\) is a birth.\n\nIf \\(q\\) is active, \\(d(q)=1\\), its two binary children have exactly one\nactive boundary:\n\\[\n \\begin{array}{c|cc|c}\n \\text{branch}&d(2q)&d(2q+1)&\\text{next odd active boundary}\\\\ \\hline\n A&0&1&2q+1\\\\\n B&1&0&4q+1\n \\end{array}                                      \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe B row uses \\(d(4q)=0\\) for odd \\(q\\), hence\n\\(d(4q+1)=1\\). Thus \\(d(3q+1)=1\\) is exactly the B condition.\n\n## 2. Exact D_S correspondence and its multiplicity\n\nFrom (2), for an odd active \\(q\\),\n\\[\n e(S(q))=1\n \\iff d(2q)=1\\ \\text{and}\\ d(S(q))=0.              \\tag{4}\n\\]\nConsequently the S-image origin class has the exact description\n\\[\n D_S\n =\\left\\{S(q):\n q\\text{ odd},\\ d(q)=1,\\ d(2q)=1,\\ d(S(q))=0\\right\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe map \\(q\\mapsto S(q)\\) is injective, so (5) preserves multiplicity\nexactly. Every such \\(q\\) lies on the B row of (3), and the associated\nbirth \\(S(q)\\) has residue \\(2\\pmod3\\). If \\(d(S(q))=1\\), the same B\ncondition instead gives an active nonbirth boundary \\(S(q)\\), with\n\\[\n d(2S(q))=1,\\qquad d(2S(q)+1)=0.                    \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor a nearest birth ancestor \\(b\\) of a \\(D_S\\)-source \\(q\\), the\nestablished strip mask gives depth at least two. The charged odd sources\non either active path have nonadjacent dyadic depths, so\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\substack{q\\text{ charged to }b\\\\q\\in D_S\\text{-source}}}\n \\frac1q\\le \\frac{2}{3b}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n \\frac1{S(q)}=\\frac{2}{3q+1}\\le\\frac{2}{3q},\n\\]\nthe inherited bound\n\\[\n E_{D_S}(X)\\le \\frac49 E_\\beta(X/6)                 \\tag{7}\n\\]\nis recovered without losing multiplicity. The B condition restricts the\nsummation set and does not by itself improve the \\(2/(3b)\\) spacing\nbound: no further separation of the \\(d(S(q))=0\\) sources has been\nproved.\n\nThere is also an exact arithmetic relation along the B row:\n\\[\n q_B=4q+1,\\qquad S(q_B)=4S(q)=4y.                   \\tag{8}\n\\]\nThis relates the binary B successor to a second S-image candidate, but\nit is not an injective map from all active \\(S(q)\\)-boundaries to births;\nwhen \\(d(y)=1\\), both \\(q_B\\) and \\(y\\) are active boundaries in\ndifferent ancestry coordinates.\n\n## 3. The A branch is complementary, not another S-origin channel\n\nOn the A row, \\(d(2q)=0\\), \\(d(2q+1)=1\\), so (2) gives\n\\[\n e(S(q))=0                                      \\tag{9}\n\\]\nfor both values of \\(d(S(q))\\). If \\(d(S(q))=1\\), then\n\\[\n d(2S(q))=0,\\qquad d(2S(q)+1)=1,                  \\tag{10}\n\\]\nand the odd boundary \\(2S(q)+1=3q+2\\) is active. If \\(d(S(q))=0\\),\nboth \\(2S(q)\\) and \\(2S(q)+1\\) are inactive at this local split.\nIn either case the A branch supplies no \\(D_S\\) birth.\n\nThe always-active A successor \\(q_A=2q+1\\) and the conditional active\nboundary \\(3q+2\\) in (10) cannot be added as two disjoint charges to a\nsingle nearest birth without a new multiplicity lemma. Their nearest\nbirth maps may overlap with each other and with the ancestry of \\(q\\).\nThus the A row gives an exact exclusion of an S-origin channel, but no\nnew global boundary-energy contraction.\n\n## 4. Relation to odd primitive births\n\nAn odd primitive birth \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) has \\(d(n)=0\\), so it is not\nan input \\(q\\) to the active-boundary correspondence above. Its known\nrelation is instead \\(d(3n+1)=1\\), with \\(3n+1=2S(n)\\), and\n\\[\n e(S(n))=1-d(S(n)).\n\\]\nHence it either has a larger retained S-birth or an active S-target with\na smaller ordinary birth ancestor. The active \\(q\\) coupling in (1)--(10)\ndoes not force the second case and does not transfer an odd \\(P_1\\) birth\nto a smaller \\(P_1\\) birth.\n\nThe exact B/A split is therefore useful for organizing the boundary\nenergy and for identifying \\(D_S\\), but it leaves the odd primitive mask\nand, at this preliminary stage, the PC12 critical bulk regime open.\nSections7–8 below supersede that PC12 limitation by combining additional\njoint channel identities. No universal descent, terminal counting bound,\nor total-energy contraction follows from this B/A split alone.\n\n## 5. Scope\n\nProved here are the identities (1)--(2), the exact B-branch\ncharacterization (4)--(5), the A-branch exclusion (9), and the\nmultiplicity-preserving recovery of (7). The arithmetic relation (8) is\nrecorded as a coupling interface only. Actual color realizations of\narbitrary A/B words, a bounded multiplicity estimate for the two\ncoordinate systems, and any global conclusion remain unproved.\n\n## 6. Four-state refinement and the even \\(L\\)-restriction\n\nDefine\n\\[\n L(n)=d(n)d(2n),\\qquad\n R_b(n)=d(n)d(2n+1).\n\\]\nThe XOR identity gives\n\\[\n \\ell(n):=e(n)+L(n)=d(2n),\\qquad\n r(n):=e(n)+R_b(n)=d(2n+1).                         \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThus the four mutually exclusive states are\n\\[\n \\begin{array}{c|cc|cc}\n \\text{state}&e&L&R_b&(\\ell,r)\\\\ \\hline\n 0&0&0&0&(0,0)\\\\\n L&0&1&0&(1,0)\\\\\n R_b&0&0&1&(0,1)\\\\\n e&1&0&0&(1,1).\n \\end{array}                                       \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\), \\(y=S(n)\\) preserves \\(\\ell\\) exactly. Indeed,\n\\[\n d(2y)=d(3n+1)\n =|\\beta(3n+1)-\\beta(3n+2)|\n =|\\beta(n)-\\beta(3n+2)|=d(2n).                    \\tag{13}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n \\{e,L\\}\\longrightarrow\\{e,L\\},\\qquad\n \\{0,R_b\\}\\longrightarrow\\{0,R_b\\}                 \\tag{14}\n\\]\nunder the arithmetic \\(S\\)-map. The exact edge interpretations are:\n\\(L\\to e\\) is a \\(D_S\\) origin, \\(e\\to L\\) is an S-terminal, and\n\\(e\\to e\\) is a retained S-edge. The table does not assert that any\nallowed target state exists.\n\nFor \\(n=4m\\), \\(y=R(n)=3m\\) preserves \\(r\\). The relevant colors are\n\\[\n \\beta(8m+1)=\\beta(6m+1),\\qquad\n \\beta(8m+2)=\\beta(4m+1)=\\beta(3m+1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\beta(6m+2)=\\beta(3m+1).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n d(2y+1)=d(6m+1)=d(8m+1)=d(2n+1).                 \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n \\{e,R_b\\}\\longrightarrow\\{e,R_b\\},\\qquad\n \\{0,L\\}\\longrightarrow\\{0,L\\}                    \\tag{16}\n\\]\nunder \\(R\\). Here \\(R_b\\to e\\) is exactly a \\(D_R\\) origin,\n\\(e\\to R_b\\) is an R-terminal, and \\(e\\to e\\) is a retained R-edge.\nAgain these are permitted state transitions, not existence claims.\n\nThere is a useful parity restriction on the \\(L\\)-state. If\n\\(\\nu_2(n)=1\\), then \\(2n\\equiv4\\pmod8\\), so \\(d(2n)=0\\) by\n\\(d(8t+4)=0\\). If \\(\\nu_2(n)=2\\), then \\(n=4u\\) with \\(u\\) odd and\n\\(d(n)=d(4u)=0\\) by the same identity. Hence\n\\[\n        L(n)=1\\ \\text{and}\\ n\\ \\text{even}\n        \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 8\\mid n.          \\tag{17}\n\\]\nThis removes the \\(v_2=1,2\\) arithmetic cases for even left-active\nboundaries, but does not bound the \\(8\\mid n\\) cases or their\nmultiplicity.\n\nThe four-state table gives an exact cross-coupling language for the\nprevious B/A and S/R analyses. It does not identify the A/B tree with\nthe retained S/R graph, and it does not yield a smaller-birth estimate\nfor odd \\(P_1\\) or a total-energy contraction without an additional\nmask or multiplicity theorem.\n\n## 7. Audit of the proposed critical residue contradiction\n\nUse logarithmic normalized limits along a subsequence on which the birth\nenergy diverges. Let \\(E=1\\), let \\(A,B\\) be the odd/even birth masses,\nlet \\(L,R_b\\) be the total \\(L,R_b\\) mask masses, and put\n\\(U=L_{\\mathrm{odd}}\\), \\(W=L_{\\mathrm{even}}\\), and\n\\(V=R_{b,\\,4\\mid n}\\). The exact pointwise identity\n\\(d(n)=L(n)+R_b(n)\\), together with binary dilation, gives\n\\[\n L+R_b=2.                                           \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe even-child calculation is also valid. Since \\(e(4m+2)=0\\),\n\\(R_b(2u)=d(2u)\\) for odd \\(u\\), and hence\n\\(R_{b,\\,n\\equiv2\\ (4)}=(A+U)/2\\). Splitting the even boundary mass\nand using \\(d(8t+4)=0\\) gives\n\\[\n W+2V=B.                                             \\tag{19}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n R_b-L=1+A-B-2U+4V.                                  \\tag{20}\n\\]\nUnder the PC12 values \\(A=B=1/2\\), this is\n\\[\n R_b-L=1-2U+4V.                                     \\tag{21}\n\\]\nThese identities retain the finite-cutoff \\(O(1)\\) corrections before\nnormalization.\n\nThe proposed identity is in fact a consequence of these equations; the\nearlier objection in this report is withdrawn. Let\n\\(D_i=L_i+R_{b,i}\\) be the total \\(d\\)-mass in residue \\(i\\pmod3\\).\nThe correct residue-resolved child equations are\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n 2D_0&=E_0+L_0+E_1+R_{b,1},\\\\\n 2D_1&=E_2+L_2+E_0+R_{b,0},\\\\\n 2D_2&=E_1+L_1+E_2+R_{b,2},\n \\end{aligned}                                      \\tag{23}\n\\]\nup to bounded affine-cutoff errors. They arise because a target residue\nreceives both an even child from one source residue and an odd child from\nanother. Equivalently, the exact parity-resolved forms are\n\\[\n D^{\\mathrm{even}}_{2i}=\\frac12(E_i+L_i),\\qquad\n D^{\\mathrm{odd}}_{2i+1}=\\frac12(E_i+R_{b,i}),        \\tag{24}\n\\]\nagain modulo bounded cutoff terms. From the first and third lines of\n(23), using \\(D_0=L_0+R_{b,0}\\) and \\(D_2=L_2+R_{b,2}\\), one gets\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n L_0+2R_{b,0}-R_{b,1}&=E_0+E_1+O(1),\\\\\n 2L_2+R_{b,2}-L_1&=E_1+E_2+O(1).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSubtracting gives\n\\[\n R_b-L=3(R_{b,0}-L_2)-E_0+E_2+O(1).             \\tag{22}\n\\]\nNo extra parity identity is needed. This corrects the former 4/3\n``discrepancy'' diagnosis: under the PC12 scalar values, (22) agrees with\n(21) only after the full residue equations are imposed, and those\nequations instead force the relation below.\n\nFor the finite harmonic version, the channel identities are\n\\[\n E_2+L_2=\\frac23(A+U)+O(1),\\qquad\n E_0+R_{b,0}=\\frac43(B+V)+O(1).                  \\tag{23a}\n\\]\nCombining (20), (22), and (23a), and using \\(A+B=E=1\\) after\nnormalization, gives\n\\[\n                    B-A=E_0-E_2+O(1).           \\tag{24}\n\\]\nThus the PC12 assignment \\(A=B=1/2\\), \\(E_0=2/3\\), \\(E_2=1/3\\)\ncannot be the limiting profile if the displayed finite-cutoff channel\nidentities hold: it violates (24). This is a genuine correction to the\nprevious version of this report, but it is only an exclusion of that\nscalar profile, not a beta-realization or a global energy theorem.\n\nThe endpoint/origin decomposition has no internal-channel terms:\n\\[\n E_2=\\frac23(A-Z_S)+D_S+O(1),\\qquad\n E_0=\\frac43(B-Z_R)+D_R+O(1).                      \\tag{25}\n\\]\nThis is the point at which my previous audit made its algebraic error: it\nincorrectly wrote \\(E_2+L_2\\) and \\(E_0+R_{b,0}\\) in place of the two\ntarget-birth masses in (25). Substituting (25) into\n\\(A=E_2+P/2\\) and \\(B=E_0+P/2\\), which follows from (24) and\n\\(E=E_0+P+E_2\\), gives directly\n\\[\n A=\\frac32P-2Z_S+3D_S+O(1),\\qquad\n B=4Z_R-3D_R-\\frac32P+O(1),\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n E=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1).                      \\tag{26}\n\\]\nThe previously claimed extra term \\(3(R_{b,0}-L_2)\\) was therefore\nspurious. The full AC1--AC18 finite-cutoff bridge is consistent: its\ntransport errors are uniformly bounded by the affine reciprocal estimate\nand its Dirichlet version follows by finite partial summation. It still\nreduces finite energy to control of the positive origin/terminal masses;\nit does not itself prove that control or Collatz.\n\n## 8. Full AC1--AC18 read and final scope\n\nI read the complete\n`ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md`, including AC1--AC18.\nAC1--AC3 correctly define the four states, the S/R conserved channels, and\nthe common finite-cutoff convention. AC4--AC8 correctly preserve parity\nbefore residue summation and derive the residue identity (22); the uniform\n\\(O(1)\\) errors are justified by \\(1/(an+b)=a^{-1}/n+O(n^{-2})\\) and a\nbounded cutoff annulus. AC9--AC12 then give\n\\[\n B-A=E_0-E_2+O(1),\\qquad\n A=E_2+P/2+O(1),\\quad B=E_0+P/2+O(1).\n\\]\nAC13 uses the target-birth decomposition (25), with \\(E_2,E_0\\) alone;\nsolving it gives AC14 exactly and no residual \\(R_{b,0}-L_2\\) term.\nAC15--AC16 are valid finite-sum consequences with no subtraction of\ndivergent positive series. AC17 is the corresponding lower-ratio statement.\nFinally, AC18's partial-summation identity is valid for every \\(s>1\\) and\npreserves a uniform bound as \\(s\\downarrow1\\). The bridge excludes only the\nendpoint/origin-negligible divergent regime; it supplies no independent\nfinite-energy bound and no Collatz conclusion.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_BLOCK_INVERSE_WEIGHTS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — inverse branches and weights for the \\(L\\)-block\n\n## Scope and notation\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the shortcut map and let \\(\\beta(Ty)=\\beta(y)\\). Write\n\\[\ng(y)=\\beta(3y)-\\beta(y),\\qquad h(y)=|g(y)|.\n\\]\nThe accepted rules used below are:\n\n1. \\(g(Sr)=g(r)\\) for odd \\(r\\equiv15\\pmod{16}\\), where\n   \\(S(r)=(3r+1)/2\\);\n2. for \\(x\\equiv7\\pmod{128}\\), with \\(P=(9x+1)/64\\),\n   \\(g(x)=-g(P)\\).\n\nThis report audits the combined block\n\\[\nn=2^Lm-1,\\qquad L\\ge3,\\quad m\\ {\\rm odd},\n\\]\nafter \\(L-3\\) \\(S\\)-steps. It gives exact guards, inverse branches, cutoff\nweights, and the remaining growth obstruction. It does not claim universal\ndescent or finite defect mass.\n\n## Exact guard and signed transfer\n\nInduction gives\n\\[\nS^j(2^Lm-1)=2^{L-j}3^jm-1.\n\\]\nAt \\(j\\le L-4\\), the source has \\(L-j\\ge4\\), hence is \\(15\\bmod16\\);\nthe signed preservation rule applies at every such step. At the endpoint\n\\[\nx=S^{L-3}(n)=8\\cdot3^{L-3}m-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe selected class \\(x\\equiv7\\pmod{128}\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n3^{L-3}m\\equiv1\\pmod{16}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nUnder (2),\n\\[\nP=\\frac{9x+1}{64}\n =\\frac{3^{L-1}m-1}{8} \\in\\mathbb Z_{\\rm odd}, \\tag{3}\n\\]\nand \\(P\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\). The first \\(L-3\\) steps preserve \\(g\\), while\nthe selected-class rule swaps its sign, so\n\\[\ng(n)=g(x)=-g(P),\\qquad h(n)=h(P). \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe sign in (4) is essential for \\(g\\); only the absolute defect is\nunchanged.\n\nFor later use, define the block source classes\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_L\n :=\\{\\,2^L(16t+b_L)-1:t\\ge0\\,\\}, \\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(b_L\\in\\{1,3,5,\\ldots,15\\}\\) is the unique residue satisfying\n\\[\n3^{L-3}b_L\\equiv1\\pmod{16}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThen (2) is exactly \\(n\\in\\mathcal B_L\\). The residues \\(b_L\\) cycle as\n\\(1,11,9,3\\) according as \\(L-3\\equiv0,1,2,3\\pmod4\\). Every source in\n\\(\\mathcal B_L\\) has \\(v_2(n+1)=L\\), so these classes are disjoint in \\(L\\).\n\n## Exact inverse branches\n\nPut\n\\[\nA(P)=8P+1,\\qquad e(P)=v_3(A(P)).\n\\]\nThe forward formula (3) is inverted at fixed \\(L\\) by\n\\[\nm=\\frac{A(P)}{3^{L-1}},\\qquad\nn_L(P)=2^L\\frac{A(P)}{3^{L-1}}-1. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThe exact target condition is\n\\[\nP\\ {\\rm odd},\\qquad 3^{L-1}\\mid 8P+1. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nIndeed, (8) makes \\(m\\) odd. Since \\(P\\) is odd,\n\\(8P+1\\equiv9\\pmod{16}\\), so (7) automatically gives\n\\(3^{L-3}m\\equiv1\\pmod{16}\\). Also \\(L\\ge3\\) in (8) implies\n\\(9\\mid8P+1\\), hence \\(P\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\). Conversely, every source\nguarded by (2) produces an odd \\(P\\) satisfying (8). Thus the inverse\nbranches are exact, with no omitted parity condition.\n\nFor a fixed odd \\(P\\), all admissible \\(L\\)'s are therefore\n\\[\n3\\le L\\le e(P)+1. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThey are distinct because \\(v_2(n_L(P)+1)=L\\). The target \\(P\\) can have\nseveral inverse branches, but their multiplicity is exactly\n\\[\n\\#\\{L:\\mathcal B_L\\to P\\}=\\max(e(P)-1,0), \\tag{10}\n\\]\nnot an unproved bounded multiplicity. The finite source cutoff is also\nexact:\n\\[\nn_L(P)\\le X\n\\iff\n\\frac{2^L(8P+1)}{3^{L-1}}\\le X+1. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(P\\), the left side decreases with \\(L\\), so the admissible\ncutoff levels form a terminal interval inside (9).\n\nCombining (4), (7), and (11) gives the exact united-block identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}\\mathcal B_L}}\n \\frac{h(n)}n\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{P\\ {\\rm odd}\\\\e(P)\\ge2}} h(P)\n \\sum_{\\substack{3\\le L\\le e(P)+1\\\\\n 2^L(8P+1)/3^{L-1}\\le X+1}}\n \\frac{1}{\\,2^L(8P+1)/3^{L-1}-1\\,}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\nThis identity keeps both inverse multiplicity and the cutoff; replacing\nthe inner sum by one branch would lose actual sources.\n\n## Denominator-\\(1\\) weight\n\nFor every admissible \\(L\\ge3\\), the integer\n\\(m=A(P)/3^{L-1}\\) is at least \\(1\\), so\n\\[\nn_L(P)=2^Lm-1\\ge\\frac78\\,2^Lm.\n\\]\nTherefore the full, cutoff-free weight above a fixed target obeys\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(P)\n&:=\\sum_{L=3}^{e(P)+1}\\frac1{n_L(P)}\\\\\n&\\le \\frac8{7A(P)}\n \\sum_{L=3}^{e(P)+1}\\frac{3^{L-1}}{2^L}\\\\\n&=\\frac{18}{7A(P)}\n \\left(\\left(\\frac32\\right)^{e(P)-1}-1\\right). \\tag{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWriting \\(A(P)=3^{e(P)}q\\), \\(3\\nmid q\\), gives the useful alternative\n\\[\nW(P)\u003c\\frac{6}{7q\\,2^{e(P)-1}}\n =\\frac{12}{7q\\,2^{e(P)}}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nWith \\(\\alpha=\\log_3 2\u003c1\\), \\(q\\,2^{e(P)}\n\\ge(3^{e(P)}q)^\\alpha=A(P)^\\alpha\\), hence\n\\[\nW(P)\\le \\frac{12}{7(8P+1)^\\alpha}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThe cutoff inner sum in (12) is bounded by \\(W(P)\\). Formula (15) is a\ngenuine aggregate inverse-weight estimate, but its exponent is\n\\(\\alpha\u003c1\\); the trivial series \\(\\sum P^{-\\alpha}\\) diverges. Thus this\nblock estimate alone cannot prove finite \\(G\\).\n\n## Source-side harmonic size and the growth tail\n\nEach \\(\\mathcal B_L\\) is one residue class modulo \\(2^{L+4}\\). Uniform\npartial summation over these progressions gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in\\mathcal B_L}}\\frac1n\n =\\frac{\\log X}{2^{L+4}}+O\\!\\left((L+1)2^{-L}\\right)\n\\]\nfor contributing \\(L\\), with no contribution once \\(2^L>X+1\\). Summing\nover all \\(L\\ge3\\) yields the unconditional source-side bound\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}\\mathcal B_L}}\\frac1n\n =\\frac1{64}\\log X+O(1). \\tag{16}\n\\]\nThis is a bound for the selected source family, not a finite-energy result.\n\nFor \\(L\\ge8\\),\n\\[\nP-n=\\frac{(3^{L-1}-2^{L+3})m+7}{8}>0, \\tag{17}\n\\]\nso these are genuine growing branches. Their source classes still have a\nsummable density tail:\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\ge8}\n \\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in\\mathcal B_L}}\\frac1n\n =\\frac1{2048}\\log X+O(1). \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThus \\(L\\ge8\\) cannot be treated as a smaller-index descent, although its\nunweighted source mass is a controlled logarithmic tail.\n\nFor \\(3\\le L\\le7\\), \\(P\u003cn\\). From the denominator estimate and (12),\nwith\n\\[\nY_L(X)=\\left\\lfloor\n \\frac{3^{L-1}(X+1)-2^L}{8\\cdot2^L}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\none has the exact target-restricted bound\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in\\mathcal B_L}}\\frac{h(n)}n\n\\le\n\\frac{3^{L-1}}{7\\,2^L}\n\\sum_{\\substack{P\\le Y_L(X)\\\\P\\ {\\rm odd}\\\\\n3^{L-1}\\mid8P+1}}\\frac{h(P)}P. \\tag{19}\n\\]\nThe denominator \\(8\\cdot2^L\\) is the corrected cutoff factor: it comes\nfrom \\(2^L(8P+1)\\le3^{L-1}(X+1)\\).\nThe target restriction in (19) should be retained. Collapsing every target\nsum to \\(G(X)\\) gives a coefficient\n\\[\n\\sum_{L=3}^{7}\\frac{3^{L-1}}{7\\,2^L}\n=\\frac9{28}\\left(\\left(\\frac32\\right)^5-1\\right)>2, \\tag{20}\n\\]\nso that coarse collapse is not a contraction. The exact inverse identity\n(12), the restricted sums (19), and the sparse growth tail (18) were the\nearly intermediate conclusions before the \\(W_0+E\\) decomposition and the\nLB5 contraction; those later refinements supersede this interim wording.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe combined block is rigorously described by the guard (2), the signed\ntransfer \\(g(n)=-g(P)\\), the exact inverse multiplicity (9)--(10), and the\nfinite-cutoff identity (12). The denominator-\\(1\\) is controlled uniformly\nby (13)--(15), while the all-\\(L\\) source family has harmonic size\n\\((1/64)\\log X+O(1)\\) and its \\(L\\ge8\\) growth tail has size\n\\((1/2048)\\log X+O(1)\\).\n\nNo universal finite-energy conclusion follows: the \\(L\\ge8\\) branches grow,\nand the available target weight has exponent \\(\\log_3 2\u003c1\\). The remaining\ncore and any summable control of these block transfers are still open.\n\n## Exact denominator decomposition and a sharp weight obstruction\n\nFor an eligible target \\(u\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\), put\n\\[\nA=8u+1,\\qquad v=v_3(A),\n\\qquad\nn_L(u)=2^L\\frac{A}{3^{L-1}}-1\n\\quad(3\\le L\\le v+1).\n\\]\nDefine the full, uncutoff target weight\n\\[\nW(u)=\\sum_{L=3}^{v+1}\\frac1{n_L(u)},\n\\qquad\nW_0(u)=\\sum_{L=3}^{v+1}\\frac1{n_L(u)+1}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac1{n_L+1}=\\frac{3^{L-1}}{2^LA},\n\\]\nthe geometric sum is exact:\n\\[\nW_0(u)=\n\\frac9{4(8u+1)}\n\\left(\\left(\\frac32\\right)^{v-1}-1\\right). \\tag{21}\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\nW(u)-W_0(u)\n=\\sum_{L=3}^{v+1}\\frac1{n_L(u)(n_L(u)+1)}\n=:E(u)\\ge0. \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nEvery pair \\((u,L)\\) in (21)--(22) corresponds to one distinct source\n\\(n_L(u)\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}\\mathcal B_L\\), and every source in that union\narises uniquely this way. All such sources are at least \\(7\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{u\\ {\\rm eligible}}}E(u)\n=\n\\sum_{n\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}\\mathcal B_L}\\frac1{n(n+1)}\n\\le\\sum_{n=7}^{\\infty}\\frac1{n(n+1)}\n=\\frac17. \\tag{23}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(0\\le E_h:=\\sum_u h(u)E(u)\\le1/7\\). As extended\nnonnegative sums, with no finiteness assumption,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}\\mathcal B_L}}\\frac{h(n)}n\n=\n\\frac94\\sum_{\\substack{u\\equiv1\\ (18)}}\\!\n h(u)\\frac{(3/2)^{v_3(8u+1)-1}-1}{8u+1}\n+E_h,\\qquad 0\\le E_h\\le\\frac17. \\tag{24}\n\\]\nThis is a global identity for the complete block union. At a finite source\ncutoff \\(X\\), the inner sum must instead be\n\\[\nW_X(u)=\n\\sum_{\\substack{3\\le L\\le v_3(8u+1)+1\\\\n_L(u)\\le X}}\n\\frac1{n_L(u)}; \\tag{25}\n\\]\ntargets \\(u\\) with \\(n_L(u)\\le X\\) can exceed \\(X\\) on the growing\n\\(L\\ge8\\) branches. Thus (24) cannot be silently used as a same-cutoff\nidentity.\n\nThere is no constant \\(C\\) with \\(W(u)\\le C/u\\) for every eligible \\(u\\).\nTake \\(v=4k+2\\) and\n\\[\nu_k=\\frac{3^v-1}{8}.\n\\]\nThen \\(u_k\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\), \\(v_3(8u_k+1)=v\\), and the last branch\n\\(L=v+1\\) has \\(m=1\\) and\n\\[\nn_{v+1}(u_k)=2^{v+1}-1.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nu_kW(u_k)\\ge\\frac{u_k}{2^{v+1}-1}\n\\sim\\frac1{16}\\left(\\frac32\\right)^v\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\tag{26}\n\\]\nThis obstruction is arithmetic and does not assert that the corresponding\ndefect values \\(h(u_k)\\) are nonzero. It shows only that a uniform\n\\(1/u\\)-type bound for the full inverse weight is unavailable.\n\n## Full audit of root's LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER\n\nThe complete LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER file was read. LB1's\nguarded signed transfer agrees with the preceding block audit:\n\\(P(n)=(3^{L-1}m-1)/8\\), \\(g(n)=-g(P(n))\\), and the classes are\ndisjoint by \\(v_2(n+1)=L\\). The \\(P\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\) conclusion and the\ndisjointness from \\(Z\\) are correct.\n\nLB2's descent threshold is exact:\n\\[\nP(n)\u003cn\\iff (2^{L+3}-3^{L-1})m>7.\n\\]\nThe coefficients \\(55,101,175,269,295\\) for \\(L=3,\\ldots,7\\), followed by\n\\(-139\\) at \\(L=8\\), are correct. The affine multiplier and the necessary\ncycle inequality are correctly stated as necessary conditions only; no\nmixed even-cycle exclusion is inferred.\n\nLB3's inverse branches are complete. The finite-cutoff identity\n\\[\nG_B(X)=\\sum_{u\\equiv1\\ (18)}h(u)W_X(u)\n\\]\nretains every inverse branch. The target upper bound is also valid: from\n\\(n_L(u)\\le X\\) one gets \\(L\\le\\log_2(X+1)\\) and\n\\[\nu\\le \\frac{(X+1)^{\\log_2 3}}{24}-\\frac18.\n\\]\nThis is only a safe growth bound; it does not justify replacing the target\ncutoff by \\(u\\le X\\).\n\nLB4 agrees with the exact \\(W_0+E\\) decomposition and the global\n\\(E\\le1/7\\) bound. The explicit family \\(v=4k+2\\),\n\\(u=(3^v-1)/8\\), correctly rules out a uniform \\(W(u)\\le C/u\\) estimate.\nIt is an arithmetic weight obstruction only and makes no claim about\nnonzero actual defects on those targets.\n\nLB5 is correct, including the finite-cutoff direction. For \\(L=3,4,5\\),\n\\(P(n)\u003cn\\), so every target lies below \\(X\\) when its source lies below\n\\(X\\). Replacing\n\\[\n\\frac1n=\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}\n\\]\ncosts at most \\(1/7\\) in total. Since\n\\[\n\\frac1{n_L+1}\n=\\frac{3^{L-1}}{2^L(8u+1)}\n\\le\\frac{3^{L-1}}{2^{L+3}}\\frac1u,\n\\]\nthe three coefficients are\n\\[\n\\frac9{64}+\\frac{27}{128}+\\frac{81}{256}\n=\\frac{171}{256}\u003c1.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nG_{B_{3:5}}(X)\\le\\frac{171}{256}G(X)+\\frac17.\n\\]\nAfter removing the zero set \\(Z\\) and defining \\(C_5\\) as the remaining\nodd core, the rearrangement is exactly\n\\[\nG_{C_5}(X)\\le G(X)\n\\le\\frac{256}{85}G_{C_5}(X)+\\frac{256}{595}.\n\\]\nNo factor or inequality direction is missing.\n\nLB6's source-density and harmonic estimates are consistent. Its function\n\\(f\\) is explicitly only a model of the unsigned guarded identities, not an\nactual beta defect; the noted \\(f(3)\\) contradiction and divergent\n\\(U\\)-variation correctly prevent treating it as an exact invariant.\n\nLB7 preserves the correct scope: no PD input, no semigroup substitution for\nactual paths, and no claim of finite \\(G\\) or finite \\(C_5\\). No errors were\nfound in LB1--LB7.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_COFINITE_DEFECT_RIGIDITY.md":"# Follow-up 0431: a forced zero progression for the multiplicative defect\n\n## Current result\n\nThe current bounded result is the disjoint forced-zero family \\(Z\\), with\nnatural density \\(1/16\\), together with the selected-class descent\n\\(D=\\{128s+7\\}\\) and the conditional finite-variation lemma\n\\(V_h\u003c\\infty\\).\nThe earlier verdicts below the initial extensions are historical checkpoints;\nthe final CZ audit and the (D)-descent are the current scope.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis note audits the exact binary \\(T\\)-invariant consequences for\n\\[\nh(n):=|\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n)|\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\qquad\nT(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\nx/2,&x\\equiv0\\pmod 2,\\\\\n(3x+1)/2,&x\\equiv1\\pmod 2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe only input is \\(\\beta(Tx)=\\beta(x)\\). No hypothetical coloring is\nconstructed, and no assertion about convergence of all Collatz orbits is\nused.\n\n## Exact affine merge\n\nLet \\(n=32t+23\\), \\(t\\ge0\\). The two \\(T\\)-paths have the following parity\nand value sequence:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rclcl}\nn&\\to&48t+35&\\to&72t+53\\\\\n &&&\\to&108t+80\\to54t+40\\to27t+20,\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwhere the first three displayed transitions use the odd rule and the last\ntwo use division by \\(2\\). On the other side,\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rclcl}\n3n&\\to&144t+104&\\to&72t+52\\to36t+26\\\\\n &&&\\to&18t+13\\to27t+20.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHere the first transition uses the odd rule, the next three are divisions by\n\\(2\\), and the last transition uses the odd rule. The displayed parity\nclaims are uniform in \\(t\\): the first two entries on the left are odd,\n\\(108t+80\\) is divisible by \\(4\\), \\(3n\\) is odd,\n\\(144t+104\\) is even, and \\(18t+13\\) is odd. Thus every arrow is a valid\narrow of the same map, including the endpoint \\(t=0\\).\n\nBoth paths have length five and meet at \\(27t+20\\). Therefore\n\\[\nT^5(n)=T^5(3n),\n\\qquad\n\\beta(n)=\\beta(3n),\n\\qquad\nh(32t+23)=0. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe same calculation can be written without the expanded path. For odd\n\\(n\\), put \\(S(n)=(3n+1)/2\\), and let \\(U\\) apply \\(S\\) followed by removal\nof all powers of \\(2\\). On \\(n=32t+23\\),\n\\[\nS(n)=48t+35,\\quad S^2(n)=72t+53,\\quad\nS^3(n)=108t+80=4(27t+20),\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\nS(3n)=144t+104=8(18t+13),\\qquad\nS(18t+13)=27t+20.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nU^3(n)=U^2(3n)=\\operatorname{oddpart}(27t+20)\n=\\operatorname{oddpart}(27n+19). \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe last equality uses \\(27n+19=32(27t+20)\\). Formula (2) is only a\nrepackaging of the verified \\(T\\)-path merge; it does not add an orbit\nconvergence assumption.\n\n## Harmonic and cofinite consequences\n\nThe progression in (1) is an actual forced zero set for every exact binary\n\\(T\\)-invariant. Its reciprocal mass satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\equiv23\\ (32)}}\\frac1n\n=\\sum_{0\\le t\\le (X-23)/32}\\frac1{32t+23}\n=\\frac1{32}\\log X+O(1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nIn particular, for all sufficiently large \\(X\\), this is at least\n\\(\\frac1{32}\\log X-O(1)\\). Hence \\(h(n)=1\\) cannot hold for every\nsufficiently large odd \\(n\\): the progression \\(32t+23\\) supplies infinitely\nmany odd \\(n\\) with \\(h(n)=0\\), with logarithmically divergent reciprocal\nmass. Also \\(h(2n)=h(n)\\), directly from invariance under \\(T(2n)=n\\) and\n\\(T(6n)=3n\\), so the zero identities propagate to their even dilates as\nwell, although the odd progression already gives (3).\n\nThis is a strict obstruction to a cofinite \\(h\\equiv1\\) regime. It does\nnot prove that \\(h=1\\) occurs infinitely often, does not prove finiteness or\ninfiniteness of the global defect set, and does not imply that \\(\\beta\\) is\nnonconstant.\n\n## Attempted extension and exact remaining gap\n\nThe merge comes from the two affine words\n\\[\n\\text{left: }S^3\\text{ followed by }/2^2,\n\\qquad\n\\text{right: }S\\text{, then }/2^3\\text{, then }S.\n\\]\nBoth words produce the common affine value\n\\[\n\\frac{27n+19}{32}.\n\\]\nThe divisibility condition is exactly\n\\[\n27n+19\\equiv0\\pmod{32}\n\\iff n\\equiv23\\pmod{32},\n\\]\nand this residue also supplies the required intermediate parities. This\ngives the full residue class in (1), rather than only isolated examples.\n\nNo further residue cover is established here: other affine word pairs may\nhave additional congruence classes, but their intermediate parity and\ndivisibility guards have not been proved in this audit. In particular, the\nexistence of the progression (1) cannot be upgraded to a statement that all\nlarge odd inputs have \\(h=0\\), nor to a quantitative upper bound for the\nremaining \\(h=1\\) set.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe exact identity \\(h(32t+23)=0\\) is proved for every \\(t\\ge0\\), by two\nlength-five \\(T\\)-paths that meet. Therefore the proposed cofinite odd\n\\(h=1\\) behavior is impossible, and the forced zero set has reciprocal mass\nat least \\(\\frac1{32}\\log X-O(1)\\) up to \\(X\\). The complementary defect set\nand any stronger residue covering remain open in this bounded analysis.\n\n## Extension: a disjoint family of forced zeros\n\nThere is a larger family obtained by propagating the exact preservation\nidentity on the residue \\(15\\bmod16\\). For odd \\(r\\equiv15\\pmod{16}\\), put\n\\[\nv(r)=\\frac{9r+1}{2}=4q,\\qquad q\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\]\nThe universal strip identity \\(d(4q)=0\\) for odd \\(q\\) gives\n\\[\ng(S(r))-g(r)=\\beta(v(r)+1)-\\beta(v(r))=0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n(The strip identity follows directly from\n\\(\\beta(4q)=\\beta(q)\\), \\(\\beta(4q+1)=\\beta(3q+1)=\\beta(q)\\), using\n\\(T(q)=(3q+1)/2\\).) Thus \\(g\\), and hence \\(h\\), is preserved at every\nsuch step.\n\nLet \\(L\\ge3\\), let \\(m\\) be odd, and write\n\\[\nn=2^L m-1.\n\\]\nRepeated odd steps have the exact affine formula\n\\[\nS^j(n)=2^{L-j}3^j m-1=:n_j \\qquad(0\\le j\\le L-3). \\tag{5}\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le j\\le L-4\\), the exponent \\(L-j\\ge4\\), so\n\\(n_j\\equiv15\\pmod{16}\\). Therefore (4) applies at each of these steps and\n\\[\nh(n)=h(n_{L-3}). \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe endpoint is\n\\[\nn_{L-3}=8\\cdot3^{L-3}m-1.\n\\]\nIt is \\(23\\bmod32\\) exactly when\n\\[\n3^{L-3}m\\equiv3\\pmod4. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(3^{L-3}\\equiv1\\pmod4\\) for \\(L\\) odd and is \\(3\\pmod4\\) for \\(L\\)\neven, define\n\\[\na_L=\\begin{cases}3,&L\\ \\text{odd},\\\\1,&L\\ \\text{even},\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nZ_L:=\\{\\,2^L(4t+a_L)-1:t\\ge0\\,\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nThen (6), (7), and the previously proved \\(23\\bmod32\\) merge imply\n\\[\nh(n)=0\\qquad(n\\in Z_L,\\ L\\ge3). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe endpoint \\(L=3\\) is the original class \\(32t+23\\), with no\npreservation step needed. For \\(L=4\\), there is exactly one preservation\nstep from the \\(15\\bmod16\\) source to the endpoint; for larger \\(L\\), the\nsame argument has \\(L-3\\) steps. Every \\(4t+a_L\\) is odd, so\n\\(v_2(n+1)=L\\); consequently the sets \\(Z_L\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nFor \\(X\\ge1\\), each \\(Z_L\\) contributes\n\\[\n\\#(Z_L\\cap[1,X])=\\frac{X}{2^{L+2}}+O(1)\n\\]\nwhen it is nonempty, and no \\(Z_L\\) with \\(2^L>X+1\\) contributes. Summing\nover \\(L\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}Z_L\\cap[1,X]\\Bigr)\n=\\frac{X}{16}+O(\\log X). \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe corresponding reciprocal sum has the sharper estimate\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\ n\\in\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}Z_L}}\\frac1n\n=\\frac1{16}\\log X+O(1). \\tag{11}\n\\]\nIndeed, uniformly for the contributing \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in Z_L}}\\frac1n\n=\\frac{\\log X}{2^{L+2}}+O\\!\\left((L+1)2^{-L}\\right),\n\\]\nand \\(\\sum_{L\\ge3}(L+1)2^{-L}\u003c\\infty\\). This also handles the final\nlevels whose first term is close to \\(X\\).\n\nUnder the DF convention that \\(G(X)\\) sums only odd sources,\n\\[\nG(X):=\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\ n\\ {\\rm odd}}}\\frac{h(n)}n,\n\\]\nthe odd harmonic series and (11) give\n\\[\nG(X)\\le\\frac12\\log X-\\frac1{16}\\log X+O(1)\n=\\frac7{16}\\log X+O(1). \\tag{12}\n\\]\nIf one instead defines the defect mass over all positive integers, the\ndyadic identity \\(h(2n)=h(n)\\) gives the corresponding bound\n\\(7\\log X/8+O(1)\\); (12) is the odd-source normalization used in DF.\n\nThis is a genuine forced-zero family for the exact color invariant. It\nrules out cofinite \\(h=1\\) behavior even more strongly, but it does not\nshow that the remaining \\(h=1\\) mass is finite, nor that \\(h=1\\) occurs\ninfinitely often. It also does not provide a full residue cover beyond the\ndisjoint \\(Z_L\\) family.\n\n## Final audit of root's CZ1--CZ7 file\n\nThe complete COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md file was read.\nThe finite-path and propagation claims are consistent.\n\n* CZ1 uses only the exact identities \\(g(2n)=g(n)\\),\n  \\(h(2n)=h(n)\\), and\n  \\(g(S(n))-g(n)=\\beta((9n+3)/2)-\\beta((9n+1)/2)\\). It correctly\n  distinguishes the actual \\(h\\) from the relaxed DF function \\(f_F\\).\n* CZ2 has valid parity words \\(11100\\) and \\(10001\\), with the affine\n  endpoints\n  \\[\n  T^5(n)=\\frac{27n+19}{32},\\qquad\n  T^5(3n)=\\frac{9(3n)+19}{32}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(n=32t+23\\) these coincide at \\(27t+20\\). The common endpoint\n  does not need to reach \\(1\\), and the conclusion \\(g(n)=h(n)=0\\) is\n  forced by invariance alone.\n* CZ3 is exact: for odd \\(q\\),\n  \\(\\beta(4q)=\\beta(q)\\) and\n  \\(4q+1\\to6q+2\\to3q+1\\to T(q)\\), hence \\(d(4q)=0\\).\n  Therefore \\(g(S(x))=g(x)\\) for \\(x\\equiv15\\pmod{16}\\).\n* CZ4 has the correct induction\n  \\(S^j(2^Lm-1)=2^{L-j}3^jm-1\\). The preservation steps are exactly\n  \\(0\\le j\u003cL-3\\); the endpoint has the required \\(23\\bmod32\\) residue\n  when \\(3^{L-3}m\\equiv3\\pmod4\\). The \\(L=3\\) and \\(L=4\\) edges are\n  valid, all iterates are positive odd integers, and\n  \\(v_2(n+1)=L\\) makes the \\(Z_L\\) disjoint.\n* CZ5's cutoff count is correct:\n  \\(A_Z(X)=X/16+O(\\log(X+1))\\). Its partial-summation argument also\n  justifies the uniform reciprocal estimate\n  \\(\\sum_{n\\le X,n\\in Z}1/n=(1/16)\\log X+O(1)\\), rather than merely\n  summing nonuniform progression asymptotics. Consequently the DF\n  odd-source convention gives \\(G(X)\\le(7/16)\\log X+O(1)\\). This is\n  logarithmic only; it is not finite \\(G\\).\n* CZ6 is conditional on the explicitly unproved hypothesis\n  \\[\n  V_h=\\sum_{n\\ {\\rm odd}}\\frac{|h(U(n))-h(n)|}{n}\u003c\\infty.\n  \\]\n  Under that hypothesis, the event support has density zero, and each\n  fixed-depth inverse image also has density zero because there are only\n  finitely many injective affine parity branches. The \\(5\\)-bit cylinder\n  \\(23\\bmod32\\) has word \\(11100\\); avoiding \\(r\\) disjoint blocks has\n  density \\((31/32)^r\\). The finite-depth inclusion then yields density\n  zero for \\(\\{h=1\\}\\), after taking the upper-density limit for fixed\n  \\(r\\) and only then sending \\(r\\to\\infty\\). Partial summation gives\n  \\(G(X)=o(\\log X)\\) under this hypothesis, not finite \\(G\\).\n* CZ7 correctly keeps the explicit coalescence separate from external\n  semigroup statements and uses no PD input. The complement of the\n  forced-zero family remains uncontrolled at the summability strength\n  needed for the full goal.\n\nThus CZ2--CZ5 strengthen the unconditional forced-zero conclusion and\nexclude the finite-mask \\(f_F\\) from being an actual defect \\(h\\). CZ6 is\na valid conditional density statement only; it makes no unconditional\nfinite-\\(G\\) claim and does not construct a nonconstant exact invariant.\n\n## Bounded descent on a complementary \\(v_2(n+1)=3\\) class\n\nThere is also an exact comparison for\n\\[\nn=128s+7,\\qquad u=18s+1=\\frac{9n+1}{64}.\n\\]\nThe two actual \\(T\\)-paths are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn&\\to192s+11\\to288s+17\\to432s+26\\to216s+13\\\\\n &\\to324s+20\\to162s+10\\to81s+5=T(3u),\\\\\n3n&\\to576s+32\\to288s+16\\to144s+8\\to72s+4\\\\\n &\\to36s+2\\to u.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe first path uses odd steps at \\(n,192s+11,288s+17,216s+13\\), followed\nby divisions at the even entries; the second uses one odd step followed by\nfive divisions. Every displayed parity is uniform in \\(s\\ge0\\), and the\nendpoint identities \\(3u=54s+3\\) and \\(T(3u)=81s+5\\) are exact. Hence\n\\[\n\\beta(n)=\\beta(3u),\\qquad\n\\beta(3n)=\\beta(u),\\qquad\ng(n)=-g(u),\\qquad h(n)=h(u). \\tag{13}\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\nn-u=110s+6>0,\\qquad n\\ge7u\n\\]\n(the latter has equality only at \\(s=0\\)). Thus this is a genuine\nsmaller-index transfer for this residue class, not a universal descent.\n\nLet \\(D=\\{128s+7:s\\ge0\\}\\). Since \\(n+1=8(16s+1)\\), every member of\n\\(D\\) has \\(v_2(n+1)=3\\) and odd quotient \\(1\\bmod4\\). The \\(L=3\\) member\nof the \\(Z_L\\) family has odd quotient \\(3\\bmod4\\), while \\(L\\ge4\\) has a\ndifferent exact valuation. Therefore \\(D\\) is disjoint from\n\\(Z=\\bigcup_{L\\ge3}Z_L\\).\n\nWith the odd-source defect mass convention,\n\\[\nG_D(X):=\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in D}}\\frac{h(n)}n,\n\\]\n(13), \\(n\\ge7u\\), and \\(u\\le X/7\\) whenever \\(n\\le X\\), give\n\\[\nG_D(X)\\le\\frac17\\,G(X/7). \\tag{14}\n\\]\nDefine \\(G_{\\rm core}\\) by removing \\(Z\\cup D\\) from the odd indices. Since\n\\(h=0\\) on \\(Z\\),\n\\[\nG(X)=G_{\\rm core}(X)+G_D(X)\n\\le G_{\\rm core}(X)+\\frac17G(X/7)\n\\le G_{\\rm core}(X)+\\frac17G(X),\n\\]\nand consequently\n\\[\nG(X)\\le\\frac76\\,G_{\\rm core}(X). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThus finite odd defect mass is equivalent to finite core mass for this\nparticular decomposition, with the inequality understood at every finite\ncutoff. This does not establish finiteness of either quantity and does not\nsupply a descent outside \\(D\\).\n\n## Final confirmation of the updated CZ7--CZ8 audit\n\nThe updated root file was reread through CZ8. The selected descent is\nconsistent with the preceding report: the two paths have the stated\nparities, \\(u=(9n+1)/64=18s+1\\), \\(n=(64u-1)/9\\ge7u\\), and the signed\nidentity is \\(g(n)=-g(u)\\), with \\(h(n)=h(u)\\). The map from\n\\(D=\\{n\\equiv7\\pmod{128}\\}\\) to \\(u\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\) is bijective, so the\nfinite-cutoff inequality \\(G_D(X)\\le G(X/7)/7\\) has no hidden boundary\nterm. The class \\(D\\) is disjoint from \\(Z\\), and the core decomposition\ntherefore gives \\(G_C(X)\\le G(X)\\le(7/6)G_C(X)\\).\n\nThe updated CZ8 source and scope statements introduce no mathematical\nchange or conflict: they preserve the explicit-orbit requirement, import\nno PD input, and leave the core summability problem open. The conditional\n\\(V_h\u003c\\infty\\) conclusion remains density zero and \\(G=o(\\log X)\\), not\nfinite \\(G\\). The initial verdict sections above the extensions are\nhistorical checkpoints; the current scope is \\(Z\\)-density \\(1/16\\), the\n\\(D\\)-descent, and that conditional lemma.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_COMPONENT_PACKING.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — packing finite retained components by minima and lengths\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no program, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean or rendering was used. The current PROJECT_STATE, SR1–SR12\nand BM1–BM9 were read before this audit. The aim is a checkable global\npacking inequality, with the remaining summability requirement stated\nexplicitly.\n\n## 1. Length-sensitive version of the BM individual bound\n\nLet C be a finite acyclic retained F-component, written as a directed chain\nof L_C edges and L_C+1 vertices. Let m_C=min C and\n\n    W(C)=sum_(n in C) 1/n.\n\nFor any k>=0, only the last min(k,L_C+1) vertices can fail to have k\nfurther retained steps. Every other vertex admits k arithmetic F-steps, so\nSR4 gives\n\n    N_C(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k + 2^k + min(k,L_C+1).        (1)\n\nThe harmless distinction between L_C and L_C+1 can be absorbed by replacing\nL_C with the vertex count; below L means the suffix-size parameter L_C+1.\nChoose b=8/3, alpha=ln(2)/ln b, and\nk=floor(log_b X). As in BM1,\n\n    X(3/4)^k + 2^k \u003c= (7/3)X^alpha.                  (2)\n\nThus for X>=m_C,\n\n    N_C(X) \u003c= (7/3)X^alpha + min(L,log_b X).          (3)\n\nIntegrating from m=m_C to infinity gives\n\n    W(C) \u003c= A m^(alpha-1) + I_L(m),\n    A=7/[3(1-alpha)],                                (4)\n\nwhere the suffix integral is exact:\n\n    I_L(m)=L/m,                                      if L\u003c=log_b m,\n\n    I_L(m)=(ln m+1)/(m ln b) - 1/(b^L ln b),         if L>log_b m.\n\nThe second line follows by splitting the integral at X=b^L. In particular,\n\n    I_L(m) \u003c= (1/m) min(L, log_b m + 1/ln b).         (5)\n\nThis improves the uniform BM1 display for short components: the terminal\nallowance is their actual length rather than an unconditional logarithm.\n\n## 2. A global packing inequality through minima\n\nLet M be the set of numerical minima m_C of finite retained components, and\nlet L_m be the corresponding suffix-size parameter. Components are disjoint,\nso their minima are distinct. Summing (4) over components whose minima are\nat most X gives the checkable finite-cutoff estimate\n\n    E_fin(X) \u003c= A sum_(m in M, m\u003c=X) m^(alpha-1)\n                + sum_(m in M, m\u003c=X) I_(L_m)(m).      (6)\n\nUsing (5), a simpler sufficient form is\n\n    E_fin(X) \u003c= A sum_(m\u003c=X, m in M) m^(alpha-1)\n       + sum_(m\u003c=X, m in M) min(L_m,log_b m+1/ln b)/m. (7)\n\nNo convergence claim is hidden in (6) or (7). They are global estimates\nconditional only on the actual minimum set and component lengths, and they\nretain the multiplicity of the short suffix through L_m.\n\nA useful summability criterion follows immediately: finite total mass would\nfollow if both series on the right of (7) were uniformly bounded as X grows.\nSR/BM establish neither condition. Distinctness of M alone is insufficient;\nthe first weight has exponent alpha-1 in (-1,0), and the second can diverge\nwhenever short-chain lengths or logarithmic caps occur too often.\n\n## 3. Exact minimum classification and the missing sparse input\n\nThe arithmetic map has one extra structural fact. If m_C is not the terminal\nvertex and is not the origin, its incoming retained edge cannot be S, since\nS(q)>q. It must be an R-edge p->m_C. Since an outgoing R-edge from an even\nm_C would be smaller than m_C, such an internal minimum is odd and satisfies\n\n    p=4m_C/3,       m_C=0 mod 3.                     (8)\n\nThus every minimum is one of:\n\n1. an origin of its retained component;\n2. a terminal vertex; or\n3. an odd R-output with an even birth predecessor p=4m_C/3.\n\nThis classification is exact but does not make the minimum set sparse. The\norigin partition P_1 disjoint union D_S disjoint union D_R controls origins\nonly. BM6/SR endpoint charging controls terminal targets only. Case 3 can\nhave an internal minimum with a retained predecessor and remains outside\nboth of those estimates as a minimum object.\n\nEquation (8) gives a bookkeeping relation for reciprocal minima,\n1/m_C=4/(3p), but it is not a contraction: the minimum has larger weight\nthan its predecessor. No current mask lemma supplies a summable bound for\nthese case-3 minima or for the lengths L_m attached to them.\n\n## 4. Why coding alone cannot close (7)\n\nForward coding controls the set of vertices with a long continuation. It\ndoes not control the number of terminal suffix vertices across all distinct\ncomponents. The suffix term in (7) is exactly where this information is\nlost. Replacing it by log_b m for every component reproduces BM1 and can\nturn into a divergent series; replacing it by L_m is sharper for short\ncomponents but still requires a weighted length sum.\n\nThe balanced CRT construction in BM3–BM5 supplies a precise arithmetic\nbarrier. For every k it gives a finite arithmetic component with k+1 vertices,\nall values comparable to its origin n, and\n\n    m W(C) >= 3(k+1)/10.                              (9)\n\nIts terminal is 2 mod 4, so it is not an actual birth component. Therefore\n(9) does not refute an actual mask-sensitive estimate, but it proves that\nan arithmetic packing argument cannot replace the missing masks by a\nconstant endpoint/origin factor. The same family has\nm\u003c=13*4^k, hence mW(C) has a logarithmic lower loss in m.\n\n## 5. What a genuine global strengthening would require\n\nA sufficient new input would be any one of the following:\n\n- a sparse bound on M strong enough to make the first series in (7)\n  summable, together with a bound on the short-chain weighted sum;\n- a terminal-suffix charging rule that maps each suffix vertex to distinct\n  smaller minima with summable weights;\n- a mask-sensitive depth loss that reduces the forward-prefix count for\n  actual retained paths by a summable factor;\n- a direct bound on the primitive P_1 minima plus a separate treatment of\n  internal R-output minima (8).\n\nNone is present in SR1–SR12 or BM1–BM9. The exact current conclusion is\ntherefore (6)/(7), not finite total birth energy. Under PD170, the separate\npointwise bridge remains conditionally available once such an energy upper\nbound is proved; without PD170 only the density-level conclusion remains.\nNo global Collatz, cycle-exclusion, or novelty claim follows.\n\n## 6. Conditional global count from terminal births\n\nDefine\n\n    R_beta(X)=# {births n\u003c=X},\n    Z_beta(Y)=# {terminal births t\u003c=Y},\n\nwhere terminal means that the arithmetic F(t) is defined but is not a\nbirth, so the retained component has no outgoing edge at t. For any k>=1,\npartition births n\u003c=X into those with at least k retained successors and\nthose reaching a terminal in fewer than k retained steps.\n\nThe first class is contained in the arithmetic k-prefix set, hence has size\nat most (X(3/4)^k+2^k). For the second class, if t is its terminal and\nthere are j\u003ck retained steps from n to t, then each F-step satisfies\n\n    F(u)+1 \u003c= (3/2)(u+1).\n\nTherefore\n\n    t \u003c= (3/2)^k(X+1)-1.\n\nInjectivity gives at most one predecessor at each depth, so a fixed terminal\nhas at most k such sources with j=0,...,k-1. This proves the exact counting\ninequality\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k+2^k\n                  + k Z_beta((3/2)^k(X+1)-1).          (10)\n\nVertices admitting at least k forward retained steps are in the first class;\nthis includes cycles and infinite-forward paths. A one-sided component with\nan infinite backward tail but a terminal forward end can have vertices in\nthe second class, according to their remaining forward depth. Finite\ncomponents are partitioned by their unique terminal as stated.\n\n## 7. Optimization under a terminal counting hypothesis\n\nAssume conditionally that\n\n    Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta),       0\u003c=theta\u003c1.            (11)\n\nTake k=floor(c ln X), with c>0 fixed. Up to constants depending on c,\n(10) has three exponents:\n\n    p_1=1-c ln(4/3),\n    p_2=c ln 2,\n    p_3=theta(1+c ln(3/2)),\n\nand an additional factor k=O(ln X) on the terminal term. Put\n\n    a=ln(4/3),  b=ln(3/2),  l=ln 2,\n    alpha=l/(a+l)=ln2/ln(8/3).\n\nBalancing the first two terms gives (c_0=1/(a+l)) and exponent alpha.\nBalancing the first and third terms gives\n\n    c_1=(1-theta)/(a+theta b),\n    p_1=p_3=theta l/(a+theta b).                    (12)\n\nThe switch occurs at theta=1/2, because\n(c_1\\le c_0) exactly when theta>=1/2. Therefore the safe choice\n\n    c=min{1/ln(8/3), (1-theta)/(ln(4/3)+theta ln(3/2))}\n\nmakes every term in (10) at most (O(X^rho\\ln X)), where\n\n    rho=max{ alpha,\n             theta ln2/[ln(4/3)+theta ln(3/2)] }\u003c1.    (13)\n\nFor theta\u003c=1/2, c=c_0 and the terminal exponent is at most alpha. For\n1/2\u003ctheta\u003c1, c=c_1, the first and terminal exponents equal the second\nquantity in (13), and (p_2\u003crho). The floor in k changes only constants;\n(X+1\u003c=2X) for X>=1 handles the terminal argument. At theta=0 the same\nformula gives c=c_0 and rho=alpha.\n\nThus (11) conditionally implies\n\n    R_beta(X)=O(X^rho ln X),       rho\u003c1.              (14)\n\nSince rho\u003c1, another layer-cake integration would then give finite total\nbirth energy. The PD170/QS170 pointwise bridge could be applied only after\nthis conditional energy conclusion; without that named premise it would\nstill give only the density-level invariant conclusion.\n\nNo terminal power saving (Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)) is proved here. The\nargument also makes no claim that beta is nonconstant or that cycles are\nabsent; cycles are simply counted by the arithmetic-prefix term.\n\nThis supplies a genuine conditional packing route for the global finite\ncomponent problem, but it does not establish its hypothesis. In particular,\nterminal endpoint estimates such as BM6 bound a weighted terminal energy,\nnot the counting function Z_beta(Y) with a power saving. The missing step is\ntherefore precisely a pointwise/counting terminal sparsity theorem, not an\nunrecorded optimization detail.\n\n## 8. Exact-depth terminal refinement\n\nThe factor k in (10) is only a coarse grouping. If a short component source\nn reaches its terminal t after exactly j retained steps, then injectivity\nallows at most one such source for a fixed terminal t and fixed depth j.\nThe growth inequality along j steps gives\n\n    t \u003c= (3/2)^j(X+1)-1.\n\nTherefore the sharper exact-depth count is\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k+2^k\n       + sum_(j=0)^(k-1) Z_beta((3/2)^j(X+1)-1).       (15)\n\nThe previous (kZ_beta((3/2)^k(X+1)-1)) bound follows immediately by\nmonotonicity of Z_beta, so no earlier conclusion is invalidated.\n\nUnder the conditional hypothesis (Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)), theta>0, the\nterminal sum in (15) is geometric:\n\n    sum_(j\u003ck) Z_beta((3/2)^j(X+1)-1)\n      = O_theta((X+1)^theta (3/2)^(k theta)).           (16)\n\nWith k=floor(c ln X), the three exponents remain\n(1-c\\ln(4/3)), (c\\ln2), and\n(theta(1+c\\ln(3/2))). The same choice of c and rho in (12)–(13)\ntherefore gives the stronger conditional conclusion\n\n    R_beta(X)=O(X^rho),       0\u003ctheta\u003c1, rho\u003c1.        (17)\n\nWhen theta=0, the terminal sum in (15) is O(k)=O(ln X); with\nc=1/ln(8/3), this is absorbed by the prefix term (X^alpha), so\n(R_beta(X)=O(X^alpha)) still holds without a logarithmic factor.\nConsequently the conditional finite-energy conclusion also holds with no\nextra logarithm in either case. The terminal power-saving hypothesis itself\nremains unproved, and no nonconstant-beta or cycle-exclusion claim is made.\n\n## 9. Unified monotone-profile optimization\n\nThe exact-depth argument has a common form. Suppose a source counting\nfunction H satisfies, for Y>=X_0,\n\n    H(Y) \u003c= C Y/L(Y),\n\nwhere L is nondecreasing and L(Y)>=2. Suppose the prefix envelope is\nX r^k+2^k and a source at depth j has size at most g^j(X+1), with\n\n    0\u003cr\u003c1,       g=2r.                                (18)\n\nThe terminal profile is r=3/4, g=3/2. The corresponding origin profile is\nr=2/3, g=4/3. No slow-variation property of L is needed: since\n(g^jX>=X), monotonicity gives (L(g^jX)>=L(X)).\n\nChoose\n\n    k=floor(log_2 L(X)),\n    eta=-ln(r)/ln 2.\n\nFor X sufficiently large, the prefix term obeys\n\n    X r^k \u003c= r^(-1) X/L(X)^eta,\n    2^k \u003c= L(X).                                     (19)\n\nThe exact-depth source sum obeys\n\n    sum_(j\u003ck) H(g^j(X+1)-1)\n      \u003c= C' X g^k/L(X)\n      \u003c= C'' X/L(X)^eta,                              (20)\n\nbecause (g^k\u003c=L(X)^{\\ln g/\\ln2}) and\n(ln g/\\ln2-1=\\ln r/\\ln2=-\\eta). Thus the unified conditional count is\n\n    R(X) \u003c= C'' X/L(X)^eta + L(X).                    (21)\n\nThe finite initial range below X_0 is absorbed into the constant. The\nterminal profile specializes to the exact-depth refinement above; the\norigin profile applies whenever the corresponding origin decomposition\nhas (r=2/3,g=4/3) and source count H.\n\nLayer-cake then gives finite harmonic energy whenever\n\n    integral_(X_0)^infty [1/(t L(t)^eta) + L(t)/t^2] dt \u003c infinity.  (22)\n\nThis is a conditional profile theorem, not a terminal or origin sparsity\nresult. The only regularity used is monotonicity of L, and no assertion is\nmade that either profile's hypothesis holds for the actual birth graph.\n\n## 10. Final audit of endpoint transport profiles\n\nThe complete `ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md` was read,\nincluding EC1–EC8. The requested EC1–EC6 checks pass.\n\n- EC1–EC2 correctly partition each birth vertex by exact remaining forward\n  or backward retained depth. At a fixed terminal/origin and fixed depth,\n  injectivity gives at most one source. The cutoffs\n  ((3/2)^j(X+1)-1) and ((4/3)^jX) are the required ones; replacing the\n  exact terminal sum by k times its largest term is only a coarser bound.\n- The component classification is vertexwise. Cycles and infinite-forward\n  paths are in every forward-prefix class; a backward-infinite component\n  with a terminal forward end contributes by its remaining forward depth.\n  Infinite-backward histories are covered by the backward-prefix class.\n  No component type is silently discarded.\n- EC3–EC4 have the correct power exponents. The terminal row uses\n  ((r,g)=(3/4,3/2)), while the origin row uses ((2/3,4/3)), and the\n  denominators in (c_Z,c_O) follow from balancing the prefix and endpoint\n  powers. In either row ρ\u003c1 for θ\u003c1.\n- EC5 is valid for any nondecreasing (L\\ge2); it uses only\n  (Y_j\\ge X), (L(Y_j)\\ge L(X)), and (g=2r). The residue error\n  (L(X)) is retained. EC6's logarithmic and critical-log thresholds follow\n  from the displayed energy integral.\n\nThese are conditional transfers from an endpoint counting hypothesis. No\nbound (Z_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)), (O_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta)), or corresponding\nmonotone-profile sparsity is proved by SR/BM/EC. Hence EC does not close the\nactual finite-component mass problem. Under the separately named PD170\nbridge, the conditional finite-energy consequence can proceed to pointwise\nconstancy; without PD170 it reaches only density-level constancy. No\nactual-birth counterexample, cycle exclusion, or novelty claim is made.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_DEFECT_BOUNDARY_TRANSFER.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — exact transfer from the \\(4k+3\\) defect class\n\n## Exact paths and signed identity\n\nLet\n\\[\ng(n)=\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n),\\qquad h(n)=|g(n)|,\\qquad\nd(q)=|\\beta(q+1)-\\beta(q)|,\n\\]\nfor an exact binary \\(T\\)-invariant. For every \\(k\\ge0\\), put\n\\(n=4k+3\\). The two shortcut paths are\n\\[\nn\\longrightarrow6k+5\\longrightarrow9k+8\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n3n=12k+9\\longrightarrow18k+14\\longrightarrow9k+7.\n\\]\nThe first path uses two odd inputs. The second uses one odd input followed\nby one even division. These parity claims hold for every \\(k\\ge0\\),\nincluding \\(k=0\\).\n\nInvariance therefore gives\n\\[\n\\beta(n)=\\beta(9k+8),\\qquad\n\\beta(3n)=\\beta(9k+7),\n\\]\nand hence the exact signed transfer\n\\[\ng(4k+3)=\\beta(9k+7)-\\beta(9k+8),\\qquad\nh(4k+3)=d(9k+7). \\tag{1}\n\\]\nEquivalently, with\n\\[\nq=\\frac{9n+1}{4}=9k+7,\\qquad n=\\frac{4q-1}{9},\n\\]\nthe class \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) transfers exactly to the boundary class\n\\(q\\equiv7\\pmod9\\).\n\nNo convergence or finite-mass assumption is used in (1).\n\n## Exact harmonic cutoff and weight\n\nDefine\n\\[\nH_3(X):=\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\ n\\equiv3\\ (4)}}\\frac{h(n)}n,\n\\qquad\nD_7(Y):=\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y\\\\q\\equiv7\\ (9)}}\\frac{d(q)}q.\n\\]\nThe inverse relation \\(n=(4q-1)/9\\) gives the exact cutoff\n\\[\nq\\le Y(X):=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{9X+1}{4}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nUsing (1),\n\\[\nH_3(X)\n=\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y(X)\\\\q\\equiv7\\ (9)}}\n \\frac{9d(q)}{4q-1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus the exact source weight is \\(9/(4q-1)\\), not an asymptotic\nreplacement by \\(9/(4q)\\).\n\nThe denominator correction is positive and summable:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_3(X)\n&=\\frac94D_7(Y(X))+R_3(X),\\\\\nR_3(X)\n&=\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y(X)\\\\q\\equiv7\\ (9)}}\n \\frac{9d(q)}{4q(4q-1)},\\qquad R_3(X)\\ge0. \\tag{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(q\\ge7\\) and \\(4q-1\\ge3q\\),\n\\[\n0\\le R_3(X)\n\\le\\frac34\\sum_{q\\ge1}\\frac1{q^2}\u003c\\frac32. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nConsequently, at every finite cutoff,\n\\[\n\\frac94D_7(Y(X))\n\\le H_3(X)\n\\le\\frac94D_7(Y(X))+\\frac32. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThis is a genuine finite-cutoff identity and does not assume that either\n\\(H_3\\) or the full defect mass is finite.\n\n## Relation to the archive and scope\n\nThe archived file work/round175-h-boundary-transport.md, BT8, already\ncontains the one-sided path identity\n\\[\nb(4k+3)=b(6k+5),\n\\]\nand BT9 records its neighboring-boundary consequences. The companion\n\\(3n\\)-path and the explicit multiplicative-defect formula (1) were not\nfound as a separately stated formula in the scanned SB/DF and archival\nsearch. They follow directly from the displayed second path above, so this\nreport makes no novelty or priority claim.\n\nThe SB archive already records \\(d(3u)=|g(u)|\\) and the neighboring\nboundary transport, and SB18 warns that taking absolute values can return\nto the original defect energy. Thus (1) is a useful exact routing formula\nfor the \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) slice, but it is not an independent mass\ncontraction or a new universal descent.\n\nThe transfer controls only the \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) part of \\(h\\), by a\nboundary sum on \\(q\\equiv7\\pmod9\\). It does not bound \\(D_7\\), does not\ncontrol the \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod4\\) class, and does not imply finite \\(G\\).\nIt is also not a circular use of finite \\(G\\): all statements (1)--(5)\nhold with finite cutoffs and with possibly divergent limiting sums.\n\n## Companion transfer from \\(n=16s+1\\)\n\nA second direct path gives a disjoint target slice. Put \\(n=16s+1\\) and\n\\(q=18s+1\\). Then\n\\[\nn\\to24s+2\\to12s+1\\to18s+2,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\n3n\\to72s+5\\to108s+8\\to54s+4\\to27s+2,\n\\qquad\nq=18s+1\\to27s+2.\n\\]\nThe first path has odd, even, odd inputs; the second has two odd inputs\nfollowed by two even divisions, and the last displayed path has one odd\ninput. All\narrows are valid for \\(s\\ge0\\), including \\(s=0\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\beta(n)=\\beta(18s+2),\\qquad\n\\beta(3n)=\\beta(18s+1),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\ng(16s+1)=\\beta(18s+1)-\\beta(18s+2),\\qquad\nh(16s+1)=d(18s+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nq=\\frac{9n-1}{8}=18s+1,\\qquad n=\\frac{8q+1}{9}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine\n\\[\nH_1(X):=\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\equiv1\\ (16)}}\\frac{h(n)}n,\n\\qquad\nD_1(Y):=\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y\\\\q\\equiv1\\ (18)}}\\frac{d(q)}q.\n\\]\nThe exact cutoff from (7) is\n\\[\nq\\le Y_1(X):=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{9X-1}{8}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand (6) gives\n\\[\nH_1(X)=\n\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y_1(X)\\\\q\\equiv1\\ (18)}}\n \\frac{9d(q)}{8q+1}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nReplacing the denominator by \\(8q\\) produces the exact correction\n\\[\nH_1(X)=\\frac98D_1(Y_1(X))-R_1(X),\\qquad\nR_1(X)=\n\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le Y_1(X)\\\\q\\equiv1\\ (18)}}\n \\frac{9d(q)}{8q(8q+1)}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nHere \\(R_1(X)\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\nR_1(X)\\le\\frac9{64}\\sum_{q\\ge1}\\frac1{q^2}\u003c\\frac9{32}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThus (8)--(10) are finite-cutoff identities with no finite-\\(G\\)\nassumption.\n\nThe source class \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod{16}\\) is disjoint from the earlier\n\\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) class. Their target classes are disjoint as well:\n\\(q\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\) implies \\(q\\equiv1\\pmod9\\), whereas the earlier\ntarget has \\(q\\equiv7\\pmod9\\). Hence the joint positive routing is\n\\[\nH_3(X)+H_1(X)\n=\\frac94D_7(Y_3(X))+\\frac98D_1(Y_1(X))\n +R_3(X)-R_1(X), \\tag{11}\n\\]\nwith the previously established \\(0\\le R_3\u003c3/2\\) and (10). A coefficient\nmatched signed comparison is also exact:\n\\[\nH_3(X)-2H_1(X)\n=\\frac94\\bigl(D_7(Y_3(X))-D_1(Y_1(X))\\bigr)\n +R_3(X)+2R_1(X), \\tag{12}\n\\]\nwhere the correction lies in \\([0,33/16)\\). Since the target cutoffs differ\nand the target classes are disjoint, neither (11) nor (12) supplies a\ncancellation or a contraction of the full defect mass.\n\n## Strict-descent family outside the \\(7\\bmod8\\) block\n\nThere is a further exact signed transfer. Let \\(k\\ge0\\), let \\(m\\) be\npositive odd, assume\n\\[\n3^{k+1}m\\equiv7\\pmod8,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nn=1+2^{2k+4}m,\\qquad\np=1+4\\cdot3^{k+1}m. \\tag{13}\n\\]\nSet \\(a=4^km\\) and \\(q_0=1+18a\\). The direct paths for\n\\(n=16a+1\\) and \\(3n\\) give\n\\[\ng(n)=-\\delta(q_0),\\qquad\n\\delta(q):=\\beta(q+1)-\\beta(q). \\tag{14}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(n\\to24a+2\\to12a+1\\to18a+2\\), while\n\\(q_0\\to27a+2\\), and\n\\(3n\\to72a+5\\to108a+8\\to54a+4\\to27a+2\\).\n\nFor \\(q\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), the boundary map\n\\[\nF(q)=\\frac{3q+1}{4}\n\\]\npreserves the signed boundary:\nwriting \\(q=4r+1\\),\n\\[\n\\delta(q)=\\beta(2r+1)-\\beta(3r+1)\n=\\beta(3r+2)-\\beta(3r+1)=\\delta(F(q)). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThe first equality uses the paths from \\(4r+2\\) and \\(4r+1\\), and the\nsecond uses \\(T(2r+1)=3r+2\\). For \\(k\\ge1\\), apply (15) successively to\n\\[\nq_i=1+18\\cdot3^i4^{k-i}m,\\qquad 0\\le i\\le k-1.\n\\]\nThe final value is\n\\[\nq_k=1+18\\cdot3^km.\n\\]\nThe case \\(k=0\\) has no \\(F\\)-step. Since\n\\(3^{k+1}m\\equiv7\\pmod8\\), write\n\\(3^{k+1}m=8t+7\\); then\n\\[\nq_k=48t+43=4r-1,\\qquad r\\equiv3\\pmod4. \\tag{16}\n\\]\nThe parameter relation gives\n\\[\np=\\frac{8r-1}{3}=1+4\\cdot3^{k+1}m. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nFor this \\(r\\), the following genuine paths verify the final sign transfer:\n\\[\nq_k\\to6r-1\\to9r-1,\n\\]\n\\[\n9r-1\\to\\frac{9r-1}{2}\\to\\frac{27r-1}{4},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n3p=8r-1\\to12r-1\\to18r-1\\to27r-1.\n\\]\nThe middle path is valid because \\(r\\equiv3\\pmod4\\); the other path has\nodd inputs throughout. Also \\(q_k+1=4r\\) has color \\(\\beta(r)\\), while\n\\(p\\to4r\\), so \\(\\beta(p)=\\beta(r)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\beta(q_k)=\\beta(3p),\\qquad\n\\beta(q_k+1)=\\beta(p),\n\\]\nand (14)--(17) yield\n\\[\ng(n)=g(p),\\qquad h(n)=h(p). \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThis is a strict descent because\n\\[\np-1=\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{k+1}(n-1)\u003cn-1. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n## Inverse branches, guards, and weights\n\nThe condition in (13) implies\n\\(3^{k+1}m\\equiv15\\pmod{24}\\), so\n\\[\np\\equiv61\\pmod{96}. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nConversely, for any \\(p\\equiv61\\pmod{96}\\), let\n\\(v=v_3(p-1)\\). The exact inverse branches are\n\\[\nk=0,\\ldots,v-1,\\qquad\nm_k=\\frac{p-1}{4\\cdot3^{k+1}},\\qquad\nn_k(p)=1+\\left(\\frac43\\right)^{k+1}(p-1). \\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe quotient \\(m_k\\) is positive odd, and\n\\((p-1)/4\\equiv7\\pmod8\\), so every listed branch satisfies the original\nguard. Distinct \\(k\\)'s have distinct \\(v_2(n_k-1)=2k+4\\). Thus the\ninverse multiplicity is exactly \\(v_3(p-1)\\).\n\nAt finite source cutoff \\(X\\), the exact target condition is\n\\[\nn_k(p)\\le X\n\\iff\np\\le1+(X-1)\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{k+1}. \\tag{22}\n\\]\nThe full aggregate inverse weight obeys\n\\[\nW_{\\rm sd}(p):=\\sum_{k=0}^{v-1}\\frac1{n_k(p)}\n\\le\\frac1{p-1}\\sum_{k=0}^{v-1}\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{k+1}\n\\le\\frac3{p-1}. \\tag{23}\n\\]\nThis is a strict-descent family, but the coefficient \\(3\\) is not a\ncontraction against the target weight \\(1/p\\). No finite-\\(G\\) conclusion\nfollows from (23) alone.\n\nThe source class at fixed \\(k\\) is one residue class modulo\n\\(2^{2k+7}\\), because the guard selects one odd residue of \\(m\\bmod8\\).\nThe classes are disjoint by \\(v_2(n-1)=2k+4\\), and their unweighted\nharmonic union has coefficient\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge0}2^{-(2k+7)}=\\frac1{96}\n\\]\nup to the usual \\(O(1)\\) cutoff error. This is only an arithmetic source\nsize; it does not bound the actual \\(h\\)-weighted mass.\n\nThe target class \\(p\\equiv61\\pmod{96}\\) is a distinct parametrization of\nthis routing rule, but it is not globally disjoint from the earlier\ntarget slices \\(q\\equiv7\\pmod9\\) or \\(q\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\); those congruence\nclasses can intersect. Thus (18) adds a genuine strict-descent routing\nrule, while overlap bookkeeping and the inverse weights still do not\nclose the remaining defect-mass estimate.\n\n## Дополнение: семейство \\(A^-_k\\) с суммарным сжатием\n\nРассмотрим отдельно\n\n\\[\n n=1+2^{2k+4}m=1+16\\,4^k m,\n \\qquad k\\ge0,\\quad m>0\\ {\\rm odd},\\quad 3^k m\\equiv1\\pmod 8.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nПоложим \\(a=4^km\\) и \\(q_0=1+18a\\). Прямые \\(T\\)-пути имеют вид\n\n\\[\n n\\to24a+2\\to12a+1\\to18a+2,\n\\]\n\\[\n 3n\\to72a+5\\to108a+8\\to54a+4\\to27a+2,\n \\qquad q_0\\to27a+2.\n\\]\nСледовательно, если \\(\\delta(q)=\\beta(q+1)-\\beta(q)\\), то\n\n\\[\n g(n)=-\\delta(q_0).\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nДля \\(q=4r+1\\) используется точное тождество\n\n\\[\n \\delta(q)=\\beta(2r+1)-\\beta(3r+1)\n          =\\beta(3r+2)-\\beta(3r+1)\n          =\\delta\\!\\left(\\frac{3q+1}{4}\\right).\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nПосле \\(k\\) применений (26),\n\n\\[\n q_i=1+18\\,3^i4^{k-i}m,\\qquad 0\\le i\\le k,\n\\]\nи все шаги \\(i\u003ck\\) имеют \\(q_i\\equiv1\\pmod4\\). На конце\n\n\\[\n q_k=1+18\\,3^km\\equiv19\\pmod {48}.\n\\]\nПусть \\(r=(q_k+1)/4\\). Тогда \\(r\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), \\(r\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), и\n\n\\[\n p=\\frac{2r-1}{3}=3^{k+1}m.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь \\(q_k\\to6r-1\\to9r-1\\), тогда как \\(3p=2r-1\\to3r-1\\). Применяем принятый SC-инвариант\n\n\\[\n \\beta(9r-1)=\\beta(3r-1)\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nи \\(p\\to r\\), а также \\(\\beta(q_k+1)=\\beta(4r)=\\beta(r)\\). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n \\beta(q_k)=\\beta(3p),\\qquad \\beta(q_k+1)=\\beta(p),\\qquad\n g(n)=g(p),\\quad h(n)=h(p).\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nЭто именно цветовой перенос по настоящим \\(T\\)-путям; одна лишь целочисленность (27) его не заменяет.\n\nЕсть важная арифметическая поправка к иногда приписываемой этому семейству формуле. Для (24), (27) верно\n\n\\[\n p=\\frac{3^{k+1}}{16\\,4^k}(n-1)\n   =\\frac3{16}\\left(\\frac34\\right)^k(n-1),\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nа не \\(p-1=(3/4)^{k+1}(n-1)\\). Например, при \\(k=0,m=1\\) имеем \\(n=17,p=3\\). Тем не менее \\(p\u003cn\\) безусловно, поскольку\n\n\\[\n n-p=1+m\\bigl(16\\,4^k-3^{k+1}\\bigr)>0.\n\\tag{31}\n\\]\n\nОбратная параметризация точна. Из (27) следует \\(p\\equiv3\\pmod {24}\\). Для любого \\(p\\equiv3\\pmod {24}\\), если \\(v=v_3(p)\\), то все и только ветви\n\n\\[\n k=0,\\ldots,v-1,\\qquad m_k=\\frac p{3^{k+1}},\\qquad\n n_k(p)=1+\\frac{16}{3}\\left(\\frac43\\right)^k p\n\\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nудовлетворяют (24). Действительно, \\(p/3\\equiv1\\pmod8\\), а \\(m_k\\) остаётся нечётным; различные \\(k\\) различаются по \\(v_2(n_k-1)=2k+4\\). При конечном cutoff точное условие есть\n\n\\[\n n_k(p)\\le X\n \\iff\n p\\le\\frac3{16}\\left(\\frac34\\right)^k(X-1).\n\\tag{33}\n\\]\n\nПоскольку \\(n_k(p)>\\frac{16}{3}(4/3)^kp\\), получаем без ошибки cutoff\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\substack{0\\le k\u003cv\\\\n_k(p)\\le X}}\\frac1{n_k(p)}\n \u003c\\frac3{16p}\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\left(\\frac34\\right)^k\n =\\frac3{4p}.\n\\tag{34}\n\\]\n\nТаким образом, при \\(G_A(X)=\\sum_{n\\in A,\\,n\\le X}h(n)/n\\),\n\n\\[\n G_{A^-}(X)\\le\\frac34\\,G_{p\\equiv3\\ (24)}(X).\n\\tag{35}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь target cutoff не потерян: из \\(n_k(p)\\le X\\) и \\(n_k(p)>p\\) следует \\(p\u003cX\\), а (33) даёт точное ограничение по каждой ветви.\n\nСочетая (35) с уже проверенным LB-неравенством\n\n\\[\n G_{B_{3:5}}(X)\\le\\frac{171}{256}\\,G_{p\\equiv1\\ (18)}(X)+\\frac17,\n\\tag{36}\n\\]\n\nполучаем, поскольку классы \\(p\\equiv3\\pmod{24}\\) и \\(p\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\) дизъюнктны,\n\n\\[\n G_{A^-}(X)+G_{B_{3:5}}(X)\n \\le \\frac34G(X)+\\frac17.\n\\tag{37}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(Z\\) обозначает ранее установленное нулевое семейство, то \\(G_Z=0\\); оно также дизъюнктно с \\(A^-\\), поскольку \\(A^-\\subset1\\pmod {16}\\), тогда как \\(Z\\subset7\\pmod8\\). Поэтому для\n\n\\[\n C_{\\rm new}=\\{\\text{positive odd }n\\}\\setminus(Z\\cup A^-\\cup B_{3:5})\n\\]\n\nимеем точное разбиение и итоговую оценку\n\n\\[\n G(X)\\le G_{C_{\\rm new}}(X)+\\frac34G(X)+\\frac17,\\qquad\n G(X)\\le4G_{C_{\\rm new}}(X)+\\frac47.\n\\tag{38}\n\\]\n\nЭто не доказывает конечность \\(G\\): оно переносит весь вопрос на новый core. Семейство \\(A^+\\) из предыдущего раздела не следует автоматически вычитать из core: для него имеется лишь коэффициент \\(3/(p-1)\\), без аналогичного сжатия. Поэтому (38) применимо именно к указанному \\(Z\\cup A^-\\cup B_{3:5}\\), если дополнительное удаление \\(A^+\\) отдельно не обосновано.\n\n\n## Исторический локальный аудит интерфейса для всего \\(N\\) (заменён SP3)\n\nСледующий блок был написан до появления selector-леммы SP3; его замечание\nо незакрытых чётных (r) сохранено как журнал проверки, но не является\nитоговым verdict. Исправленный итог находится в разделе «Финальный аудит\nполного SP1--SP15» ниже.\n\nДля \\(N=\\{1+2^{2k+4}m:k\\ge0,\\ m>0\\ {\\rm odd}\\}\\) положим \\(s=3^km\\). После общих \\(k\\) шагов (26) конец равен\n\\(q=1+18s=4r-1\\), \\(r=(q+1)/4\\). Остатки дают точную таблицу:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\ns\\pmod8&q\\pmod{48}&r\\pmod4&\\text{доступный перенос}\\\\ \\hline\n1&19&1&A^-:\\ p_-=3s\\\\\n5&43&3&A^+:\\ p_+=1+12s\\\\\n3&7&2&\\text{не закрыт (r even)}\\\\\n7&31&0&\\text{не закрыт (r even)}\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{39}\n\\]\n\nПервая строка — (24)--(35). Во второй строке прежний \\(A^+\\)-маршрут действительно даёт \\(g(n)=g(p_+)\\), но его guard есть \\(s\\equiv5\\pmod8\\); его точная цель удовлетворяет\n\\[\np_+=1+12s\\equiv61\\pmod {96},\n\\tag{40}\n\\]\nа не только \\(13\\pmod {24}\\). Обратная формула для этой proven target class, с \\(j=1,\\ldots,v_3(p-1)\\), есть\n\\[\nn_j(p)=1+\\left({4\\over3}\\right)^j(p-1),\\qquad\n\\sum_{j:n_j\\le X}{1\\over n_j}\u003c {3\\over p-1}.\n\\tag{41}\n\\]\nТак как \\(p\\ge61\\) в (40), это, в частности, меньше \\((13/4)/p\\). Если заявляется весь класс \\(p\\equiv13\\pmod{24}\\), требуется дополнительный color-transfer lemma for the residues \\(p\\pmod{96}\\) outside (40); одной арифметической параметризацией он не следует.\n\nСтроки \\(s\\equiv3,7\\pmod8\\) нельзя включить в двухвыборный интерфейс без нового тождества: здесь \\(r\\) чётно, и ни SC (28) для \\(r\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), ни \\(A^+\\)-слияние для \\(r\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) применимы. Поэтому доказанное утверждение пока имеет одну из двух форм: либо \\(N\\) определяется с guard \\(s\\equiv1\\) или \\(5\\pmod8\\), либо для двух чётных строк (39) нужен отдельный actual-color proof. Это точный оставшийся пробел, а не контрпример к возможному будущему интерфейсу.\n\nУсловная bookkeeping-часть верна. Для proven \\(A^-\\) и \\(B_{3:5}\\) цели \\(p\\equiv3\\pmod{24}\\) и \\(p\\equiv1\\pmod{18}\\) дизъюнктны, поэтому (37) сохраняется. Если дополнительно доказан полный selector на \\(N=N^-\\sqcup N^+\\), причем \\(N^+\\)-цели лежат в core и (41) даёт\n\\(G_{N^+}(X)\\le(13/4)G_{C_*}(X)\\), то из\n\\[\nG(X)\\le G_{C_*}(X)+G_{N^+}(X)+G_{N^-}(X)+G_{B_{3:5}}(X)\n\\]\nследует\n\\[\nG(X)\\le G_{C_*}(X)+{13\\over4}G_{C_*}(X)+{3\\over4}G(X)+{1\\over7},\\qquad\nG(X)\\le17G_{C_*}(X)+{4\\over7}.\n\\tag{42}\n\\]\nЭто conditional interface only: (42) не устраняет незакрытые \\(s\\equiv3,7\\) строки и не доказывает конечность \\(G\\). Пересечения с нулевыми семействами безопасны при верхней оценке, поскольку их \\(h\\)-масса равна нулю; для exact set decomposition достаточно брать \\(C_*\\) как дополнение их объединения.\n\n\n## Финальный аудит полного SP1--SP15\n\nПолное чтение \\`SHIFTED_BOUNDARY_DESCENT_AND_CORE.md\\` выполнено. SP1--SP4\nсогласуются с точными \\(T\\)-путями и с прежними архивными тождествами.\nКлючевое усиление SP3 действительно устраняет мой прежний локальный\nзапрос о строках \\(r\\) чётного: при \\(r=2^a w\\), \\(w\\) нечётном, тождества\n\\[\n\\beta(4r-1)=C_{a+2},\\quad \\beta(4r)=\\beta(w),\\quad\n\\beta(3p_-)=C_{a+1},\\quad \\beta(3p_+)=C_{a+3}\n\\]\nверны, а ровно одна из \\(3^{a+1}w\\) и \\(3^{a+2}w\\) равна \\(3\\bmod4\\).\nSC2 затем выбирает \\(p_-\\) или \\(p_+\\), независимо от \\(\\beta\\). Поэтому\nSP7 правомерно использует весь необходимый класс \\(p=13\\bmod24\\), но только\nпри условии, что арифметический selector в SP3 выбирает plus; это условие\nне следует из одной конгруэнции и в SP7 явно сохранено.\n\nSP6 проверен: для фиксированного minus-target \\(p\\), \\(q=1+6p\\) и selector\nне зависят от обратной глубины \\(k\\). Точно \\(0\\le k\u003cv_3(p)-1+1\\), источник\n\\(n_k=1+(16/3)(4/3)^kp\\), и \\(\\sum1/n_k\u003c3/(4p)\\). SP7 также верен:\nдля plus-target \\(p=13\\bmod24\\), \\(j=1,\\ldots,v_3(p-1)\\), источник\n\\(n_j=1+(4/3)^j(p-1)\\); selector зависит от фиксированного\n\\(q=(3p-1)/2\\), а не от \\(j\\). Положительность, нечётность, общий cutoff\nи \\(p\u003cn_j\\) сохранены.\n\nSP8--SP15 дают заявленную оценку при конечном cutoff. Minus targets are\nodd multiples of \\(3\\); LB \\(B_{3:5}\\)-targets are \\(1\\bmod18\\), hence\ndisjoint. Plus targets are \\(5\\bmod8\\), while the removed source families\n\\(N,Z^+,Z,B_{3:5}\\) lie in \\(1\\bmod8\\) or \\(7\\bmod8\\), so the plus-target\nmass lies in \\(C_*\\). Thus (SP13)--(SP15) imply\n\\[\nG_{C_*}(X)\\le G(X)\\le17G_{C_*}(X)+\\frac47\n\\]\nand only the equivalence of finite masses; no finiteness of either side is\nproved.\n\nThere was a counting error in this audit, not in root's SP5. The correct\ngeometric ratio is (1/4), since increasing (k) by one increases the\nmodulus exponent by (2). A fixed (N)-class has modulus (2^{2k+5}),\nso its first two densities are (1/32,1/128), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge0}2^{-(2k+5)}=\\frac1{32}\\frac1{1-1/4}=\\frac1{24}.\n\\]\nA fixed (Z_k^+\\)-class has modulus (2^{2k+6}) with (k\\ge1), so its\nfirst two densities are (1/256,1/1024), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge1}2^{-(2k+6)}=\\frac1{256}\\frac1{1-1/4}=\\frac1{192}.\n\\]\nThe earlier (1/30,1/240) figures in this report used the wrong ratio and\nare withdrawn. The (+1) in each source formula only changes the first\nrepresentative and the finite counting error: for \\(N\\), a class can\ncontribute only when \\(2^{2k+4}+1\\le X\\), and for \\(Z^+\\) only when\n\\(2^{2k+3}+1\\le X\\). Thus there are \\(O(\\log(X+1))\\) active classes and\nthe omitted geometric tails contribute \\(O(1)\\) after multiplication by\n\\(X\\). These density slips are not used in SP10--SP15 and do not change\nthe \\(17,4/7\\) interface.\n\nThis supersedes the immediately preceding local table in this report that\ncalled the \\(r\\)-even rows unresolved. The exact remaining scope is only\nthe remaining core \\(C_*\\): SP15 does not establish its summability and\ndoes not prove finite \\(G\\).\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_DEFECT_CREATION_CHANNELS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — defect creation through the two S-channel states\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded manual analysis\nafter the multiplicative-defect report. No programs, CAS, enumeration,\nLean, or new agents were used. All transitions below are necessary local\nconditions for an exact invariant; no listed transition is asserted to be\nglobally realized by a nonconstant beta.\n\n## 1. The common-color reduction\n\nLet \\(n\\) be odd and put\n\\[\n q=3n,\\qquad v=S(q)=\\frac{9n+1}{2},\\qquad w=S(n)=\\frac{3n+1}{2}.\n\\]\nExact invariance gives\n\\[\n \\beta(v)=\\beta(q),\\qquad \\beta(q+1)=\\beta(n),\\qquad\n \\beta(v+1)=\\beta(3w).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n h(n)=|\\beta(q)-\\beta(q+1)|=d(q),\n\\]\nand the old defect update is the comparison of the two common colors at\n\\(v,v+1\\):\n\\[\n g(w)-g(n)=\\beta(v+1)-\\beta(v).                     \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe state of \\(v\\) is one of\n\\[\n 0=(0,0,0),\\quad E=(0,1,1),\\quad\n L=(1,1,0),\\quad R_b=(1,0,1),\n\\]\nwhere the coordinates are \\((d(v),d(2v),d(2v+1))\\).\n\n## 2. Complete channel-compatible transition table\n\nThe S-channel invariant is\n\\[\n e(v)+L(v)=e(q)+L(q).                               \\tag{2}\n\\]\nConsequently the full list of channel-compatible state transitions is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}\n\\text{state of }q&\\text{state of }v&h(n)&h(w)&\\text{event}\\\\ \\hline\n0&0&0&0&\\text{none}\\\\\n0&R_b&0&1&\\text{creation, non-}Z_S\\\\\nE&E&0&0&\\text{none}\\\\\nE&L&0&1&\\text{creation and }Z_S\\\\\nL&E&1&1&\\text{retained; }D_S\\text{ target}\\\\\nL&L&1&0&\\text{death}\\\\\nR_b&0&1&1&\\text{retained}\\\\\nR_b&R_b&1&0&\\text{death}\n\\end{array}                                        \\tag{3}\n\\]\nHere \\(h(w)=1\\) means that a nonzero defect is present at the image;\ncreation additionally requires \\(h(n)=0\\). The table is\nobtained by observing that \\(d(q)=0\\) in states \\(0,E\\), \\(d(q)=1\\) in\nstates \\(L,R_b\\), and \\(d(v)=0\\) in states \\(0,E\\), \\(d(v)=1\\) in states\n\\(L,R_b\\). Thus \\(h(w)=h(n)\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}d(v)\\). The \\(E\\to L\\)\nrow is \\(Z_S\\): \\(q\\) is a birth and \\(v=S(q)\\) is nonbirth. The\n\\(0\\to R_b\\) row is a genuine creation mask that is not \\(Z_S\\), because\n\\(q\\) is not a birth. The \\(L\\to E\\) row retains \\(h=1\\) and has a\n\\(D_S\\) target; \\(L\\to L\\) and \\(R_b\\to R_b\\) are the actual\n\\(h=1\\to0\\) death rows, while \\(R_b\\to0\\) retains \\(h=1\\).\n\nThe table lists channel-compatible possibilities only. The common colors\nand the global T-orbits may remove some rows; no converse realization is\nclaimed.\n\n## 3. Auxiliary channel variation, not defect death\n\nOn an S-chain the channel value \\(e+L\\) is constant. Define a binary\nstate \\(z\\) on that channel by\n\\[\n z=\n \\begin{cases}\n R_b,&e+L=0,\\\\\n L,&e+L=1.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe two creation rows in (3) are \\(z:0\\to1\\), but the \\(z:1\\to0\\)\nrows are \\(L\\to E\\) and \\(R_b\\to0\\); these are \\(h=1\\) retention rows,\nnot defect deaths. Let \\(q_0\\to q_1\\to\\cdots\\) be a finite odd S-chain\nprefix, with \\(q_{j+1}=S(q_j)\\). Since \\(q_{j+1}>q_j\\), the weights\n\\(a_j=1/q_j\\) decrease. Abel summation gives\n\\[\n \\sum_j\\frac{z(q_{j+1})-z(q_j)}{q_j}\n \\le \\frac1{q_0}.                                  \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThus, writing \\(U_S^{z}\\) for all \\(0\\to1\\) auxiliary transitions and\n\\(D_S^{z}\\) for all \\(1\\to0\\) auxiliary transitions,\n\\[\n \\sum_{q\\in U_S^{z}}\\frac1q\n \\le\n \\sum_{q\\in D_S^{z}}\\frac1q\n +\\sum_{\\text{S-chain bases }q_0}\\frac1{q_0}.        \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThis is the exact finite-prefix inequality after retaining the terminal\npotential; no cutoff \\(O(1)\\) is needed in this auxiliary statement. It is\nonly an auxiliary boundary-state variation. It is not\nan inequality for actual defect deaths. Moreover, the indices \\(q=3n\\)\nused for defect creation satisfy \\(q\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), whereas the image of\n\\(S\\) is \\(2\\pmod3\\). Thus each such q is an S-chain base; restricting\n\\(U_S^z\\) to \\(q=3n\\) leaves the uncontrolled base term itself. No useful\ncreation bound follows from (5).\n\n## 4. Exact remaining mask and scope\n\nThe only creation row outside \\(Z_S\\) is\n\\[\n q=3n\\text{ in state }0,\\qquad S(q)\\text{ in state }R_b.       \\tag{7}\n\\]\nIt has \\(d(q)=0\\), \\(d(S(q))=1\\), and no birth at the source. Any attempt\nto charge all positive defect creation to \\(Z_S\\) therefore misses (7).\nThe exact identities provide no smaller positive defect for this row.\n\nEquations (3)--(7) preserve all shared colors and distinguish local\nnecessity from global realization. Equation (5) is only an auxiliary\nvariation estimate and cannot be restricted to the \\(q=3n\\) creations\nwithout retaining their base mass. No strict global descent, summability\nof all creation masks, finite-energy conclusion, or Collatz conclusion\nfollows.\n\n## 5. Full DF1--DF6 audit\n\nI read the complete\nDEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md, including DF1--DF6. DF1 is the\nexact signed update for \\(g\\) and \\(h\\). DF2 now agrees with the corrected\ntable (3): \\(L\\to E\\) and \\(R_b\\to0\\) retain \\(h=1\\), while \\(L\\to L\\)\nand \\(R_b\\to R_b\\) are the \\(h\\)-death rows. The restriction\n\\(q=3n\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), hence no S-predecessor and chain-base status, is\nalso correct.\n\nDF3's inverse kernel uses\n\\(n=(2^k m-1)/3\\) with \\(2^k m\\equiv1\\pmod3\\); its geometric main terms,\nsummable reciprocal defect, and finite cutoff remainder are consistent.\nDF4 is consequently a signed relation between two unknown residue\ndifferences and does not control total defect mass.\n\nDF6 is a valid obstruction only for the stated relaxed class: \\(f_F\\) is\ndyadically invariant and has finite total variation under \\(U\\), while\nits odd harmonic mass diverges. The common-color restriction alone does\ninvalidate using \\(f_F\\) as an actual counterexample, because \\(f_F\\) is\nnot asserted to equal \\(|\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n)|\\) for one global beta and\nneed not satisfy the XOR/channel table. This exclusion is explicit in\nDF6 and is not a defect in that relaxed obstruction.\n\nOne concrete condition a next proof must add is a common-color\nrealizability estimate for the non-\\(Z_S\\) creation row\n\\[\n q=3n:\\quad 0\\longrightarrow R_b\\quad\\text{under }q\\mapsto S(q).\n\\]\nControlling only dyadic invariance, the \\(U\\)-kernel, and total variation\ncannot charge this row to an earlier positive defect. The required input\nmust use the same beta in \\(h(n)=d(3n)\\) and in\n\\(d(j)=|\\beta(j+1)-\\beta(j)|\\), together with the resulting local XOR and\nchannel constraints. No actual beta realization is supplied by DF6, and\nno finite-energy or Collatz conclusion follows.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_EXACT_CHAIN_WEIGHT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — exact weight of a retained growth chain\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only: no numerical\nprogram, CAS, enumeration or Lean was used. The question is whether the\nchain factor \\(9\\) in BC/OR can be reduced by using the centers\n\\(n+1,n+3\\) and the order of a mixed H/G₃ word.\n\n## 1. Exact centered potentials\n\nFor one H step \\(m=H(n)=(9n+5)/4\\),\n\n    m+1=(9/4)(n+1),\n\nwhile for one G₃ step \\(m=G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8\\),\n\n    m+3=(9/8)(n+3).\n\nThus the natural potentials contract exactly:\n\n    1/(n+1)=(9/4)/(m+1)       for H,\n    1/(n+3)=(9/8)/(m+3)       for G₃.\n\nTheir block telescoping forms are\n\n    Σ_(j=0..h−1) 1/(n_j+1)\n      =(9/5)[1/(n_0+1)−1/(n_h+1)]                 (H block),\n\n    Σ_(j=0..g−1) 1/(n_j+3)\n      =9[1/(n_0+3)−1/(n_g+3)]                     (G₃ block).\n\nThe target weight is \\(1/n\\), not either centered potential. The exact\nconversion is\n\n    1/n=1/(n+c)+c/[n(n+c)],  c=1 or3.\n\nThe second term is positive. At a switch of centers, the telescoping\npotential also acquires the boundary mismatch\n\n    1/(n+1)−1/(n+3)=2/[(n+1)(n+3)].\n\nHence centered potentials give useful mode-sensitive estimates and\nexplicit \\(O(n^{-2})\\) switch corrections, but they do not by themselves\ngive a uniform constant below 9 for the uncentered chain weight.\n\n## 2. Exact mixed-word upper bound\n\nFor a prefix of a retained word, let \\(h_j,g_j\\) be the numbers of H and\nG₃ steps among its first \\(j\\) edges, and put\n\n    λ_j=(9/4)^(h_j)(9/8)^(g_j)=9^j/2^B_j.\n\nBC2 applied to every prefix gives a center \\(c_j\\in[1,3]\\) such that\n\n    n_j=λ_j(n_0+c_j)−c_j.\n\nTherefore, for \\(j\\ge1\\),\n\n    n_j>λ_j n_0,\n    1/n_j\u003cλ_j^−1/n_0.\n\nFor a chain of length \\(r\\) this gives the exact word-sensitive bound\n\n    Σ_(j=0..r)1/n_j\n      ≤(1/n_0)Σ_(j=0..r)\n        (4/9)^(h_j)(8/9)^(g_j).                       (1)\n\nThe order matters through the prefixes. For fixed total counts, swapping\nan adjacent H,G into G,H increases the right side by the positive\none-prefix difference \\(8/9−4/9\\), while leaving all later products\nunchanged. Thus the largest bound for \\(g\\) G₃ and \\(h\\) H steps has all\nG₃ steps first:\n\n    S_(g,h) =\n      Σ_(j=0..g)(8/9)^j\n      +(8/9)^g Σ_(k=1..h)(4/9)^k.                      (2)\n\nThis is a valid improvement whenever the actual word has an early H\nstep or a controlled G₃ prefix. It is not a uniform improvement over\nall words, since \\(S_(g,0)\\uparrow9\\) as \\(g\\to\\infty\\).\n\nThe affine centers \\(c_j\\) can sharpen (1) for a fixed finite word; they\ncannot lower its uniform supremum because \\(c_j\\) stays bounded while\nthe leading term is \\(λ_j n_0\\).\n\n## 3. The factor 9 is sharp on arithmetic primitive starts\n\nThere are arbitrarily long arithmetic G₃ prefixes whose start has no\narithmetic predecessor. Fix \\(L\\ge1\\), choose an arbitrarily large\npositive \\(v\\) with\n\n    v≡0 (mod 2),  v≡1 (mod 9),\n\nand set\n\n    a=8^L v−3.\n\nAfter \\(j\\) G₃ steps, \\(0\\le j\\le L\\), the arithmetic value satisfies\n\n    a_j+3=9^j 8^(L−j)v.\n\nFor every input \\(j\u003cL\\),\n\\[\nv_2(a_j+3)=3(L-j)+v_2(v)\\ge4,\n\\]\nso \\(a_j\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\) and the G₃ step is arithmetically\ndefined. The starting residue modulo 9 is\n\n    a≡7 (mod 9)  if L is even,\n    a≡5 (mod 9)  if L is odd.\n\nIt is therefore neither \\(8\\bmod9\\) (an H image) nor\n\\(6\\bmod9\\) (a G₃ image), so it has no arithmetic predecessor in the\ncombined map. This is an arithmetic primitive start, with no assertion\nthat any of its births or guards are actual.\n\nFor fixed \\(L\\), let \\(v\\) tend to infinity. Then\n\n    a/a_j →(8/9)^j,\n\nand hence\n\n    lim_(v→∞) a Σ_(j=0..L)1/a_j\n      =Σ_(j=0..L)(8/9)^j.\n\nLetting \\(L\\to\\infty\\) makes the last quantity tend to \\(9\\). Any\nuniform inequality\n\n    Σ_(j in a retained arithmetic chain)1/n_j ≤ C/a\n\nwith \\(C\u003c9\\) is therefore false on the arithmetic superset, even before\nthe nonnegative tail after the displayed prefix is included. The\ncentered G₃ identity explains the same limit:\n\\(\\sum_j1/(a_j+3)\\) has geometric coefficient exactly \\(9\\), while the\npositive conversion from \\(1/(a_j+3)\\) to \\(1/a_j\\) cannot improve it\nuniformly.\n\nThe guard requirement here is precisely\n\\(v_2(a+3)\\ge3L+1\\); writing \\(a+3=8^Lv\\) reduces it to \\(v_2(v)\\ge1\\).\nThe CRT choice \\(v\\equiv0\\pmod2,\\ v\\equiv1\\pmod9\\) simultaneously\nprovides the guard and removes both arithmetic inverse residues.\n\n## 4. Consequences for OR10 and origin types\n\nThe mixed-word estimate (1) can reduce the chain factor for a specified\nword or under an independent lower bound on early H frequency. No such\nbound is present in BC/OR, and actual birth masks cannot be replaced by\nthe arithmetic construction above.\n\nFor an origin in the image \\(D_\\beta\\), OR9 supplies\n\\[\nE_D(X)\\le(8/9)E_\\beta(2X/9).\n\\]\nIf every such origin had a uniform chain factor \\(C_D\u003c9\\), its\ncontribution to the full energy would improve the coefficient \\(8\\) in\nOR10 to \\((8/9)C_D\\). The arithmetic word analysis supplies no such\n\\(C_D\\): it permits arbitrarily long initial G₃ blocks, and no actual\nmask theorem rules them out for an image origin. Therefore the\ncoefficient \\(8\\) is not reduced by potentials alone.\n\nThe arithmetic primitive family above concerns starts outside both\nimages and shows why the universal BC factor \\(9\\) cannot be improved\non the unmasked arithmetic graph. It does not construct actual births,\ndoes not prove that \\(D_\\beta\\) realizes the same words, and does not\nexclude a future color-sensitive improvement for actual chains.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe exact centered telescoping identities, the mixed-prefix bound (1),\nthe worst ordering (2), and the CRT primitive-start construction are\nproved arithmetic statements. They leave open a mask-dependent bound on\nactual origins, a finite harmonic bound for \\(P_\\beta\\) or \\(A_\\beta\\),\nand global descent. No full Collatz conclusion follows.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_FINITE_COMPONENT_MINIMUM.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — finite retained F-components and their minimum\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no program, CAS,\nenumeration or Lean was used. The arithmetic map and word counts are those\nof SR1–SR12. This note addresses one finite acyclic retained component at\na time and does not claim a global energy bound.\n\n## 1. Coding count for a finite component\n\nLet C be a finite acyclic component of the retained graph F_beta.\nInjectivity and functionality give a unique directed chain\n\n    v_0 -> v_1 -> ... -> v_(L-1).\n\nLet m=min C be its numerical minimum, which need not be the origin or the\nterminal vertex. For any integer k>=0, at most the last k vertices of this\ndirected chain have fewer than k further F-steps. Every other vertex admits\na length-k arithmetic F-word. Therefore the forward-prefix estimate SR4\ngives, for every X>=m,\n\n    N_C(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k + 2^k + k,                    (1)\n\nwhere N_C(X)=#(C cap [1,X]). The +k term is essential: it is the terminal\nsuffix with no length-k continuation, and it cannot be assigned to a single\narithmetic residue class.\n\nChoose\n\n    b=8/3,       alpha=ln(2)/ln b,       k=floor(log_b X).\n\nThen 0\u003calpha\u003c1, 2^k\u003c=X^alpha, and\n\n    X(3/4)^k \u003c (4/3)X^alpha.\n\nConsequently\n\n    N_C(X) \u003c= (7/3)X^alpha + log_b X.                (2)\n\nThis estimate is valid even when k>=L, since then the suffix term may\nsimply dominate the actual component size.\n\n## 2. Exact individual harmonic bound\n\nSince C is contained in [m,infinity), layer-cake gives\n\n    W(C):=sum_(n in C)1/n\n          = integral_m^infty N_C(X) X^(-2) dX.\n\nUsing (2),\n\n    W(C) \u003c= [7/(3(1-alpha))] m^(alpha-1)\n             + [ln m+1]/[m ln b].                    (3)\n\nThe second integral is\n\n    integral_m^infty (log_b X) X^(-2)dX\n      = (ln m+1)/(m ln b).\n\nThus the proposed order O(m^(alpha-1)+log(m)/m) is correct, with an\nexplicit constant. The first term comes from all long-prefix vertices; the\nlogarithmic term is the accumulated terminal-suffix allowance.\n\nThis is an individual all-vertex estimate from the internal minimum. It\nand the origin-only coefficient 17/18 concern different objects and are\nnot comparable as inequalities. The estimate is still based on arithmetic\nprefixes, with actual retention used only to identify which suffix\nvertices are exceptional.\n\n## 3. Why this does not yet sum globally\n\nLet M be the set of minima of finite retained components. The components\nare disjoint, so their minima are distinct, but no sufficiently sparse\nbound on M has been proved. Summing (3) would require controlling\n\n    sum_(m in M) m^(alpha-1)\n    and sum_(m in M) (ln m)/m.\n\nThe exponent alpha-1 lies strictly between -1 and 0, so the first series\ncan diverge even for a set with positive density. The second also has no\nuniform finite bound from distinctness alone. The origin partition leaves\nthe primitive class P_1 uncontrolled, and a component minimum need not be\nits origin. Therefore (3) does not convert the SR origin estimate into a\nglobal finite-energy theorem.\n\nThe arithmetic barrier in SR10, equation (SR12), is consistent with this scope. For\na=4^L v and y=3^L v, the finite arithmetic chain has\n\n    a W_arith >= a/y=(4/3)^L,\n\nwhile its endpoint y=1 mod 8 is not an actual birth. The barrier prevents\ntransferring an unmasked component factor directly to the actual graph; it\ndoes not refute (3), whose right side is measured from the internal minimum\nrather than the arithmetic origin.\n\n## 4. Available mask-sensitive information\n\nEvery actual vertex u in C is a birth, hence\n\n    e(u)=1,       e(2u)=e(2u+1)=0.                   (4)\n\nFor a retained S-edge q->S(q) with q odd, the exact odd rule also gives\nd(S(q))=0. For a retained R-edge, the exact R relation records the\ncorresponding output mask e(R(q))=1. These conditions remove arithmetic\nwords, but no uniform positive fraction of the word residue classes has\nbeen proved to be removed at every depth. In particular, (4) does not\nimprove the X(3/4)^k prefix count by itself: it is a color condition, not\nan independent congruence count for the arbitrary binary invariant.\n\nA stronger result would need one of the following: a depth-uniform mask\nloss along every finite chain, a charging rule that maps terminal suffixes\nto distinct smaller component minima with summable weight, or a direct\nbound on the primitive P_1 minima. None is established by SR1–SR12.\n\nThe exact conclusion is therefore the individual estimate (3), together\nwith the open global summation problem. No cycle exclusion, constancy of\nbeta, or full Collatz conclusion follows.\n\n## 5. A sharper arithmetic barrier against a universal C/m factor\n\nThere is an arithmetic family showing that no universal bound W(C)\u003c=C/m\ncan hold for all finite F-components, even when m is their internal\nminimum. Fix a word length k>=1 and define its branch word adaptively by\nwriting lambda_0=1 and taking\n\n    lambda_j=1.5 lambda_(j-1)  if lambda_(j-1)\u003c=1,\n    lambda_j=.75 lambda_(j-1)  if lambda_(j-1)>1.\n\nThen every lambda_j lies in [.75,1.5]. The corresponding affine outputs\nhave the form\n\n    n_j=lambda_j n+B_j,\n\nwhere the S-affine increments are positive and the R increments vanish.\nWriting the S-state indices in the order in which they occur gives\n\n    B_j=lambda_j sum_(i:S-state at i\u003c=j) 1/(2 lambda_i),\n    0\u003c=B_j\u003c=j.                                      (5)\n\nThe word guards select one residue class modulo 2^A, A=s+2r, where s and\nr are its S and R counts. Imposing the terminal condition\nn_k=2 mod 4 selects one of four lifts, hence one class modulo 2^(A+2).\nAdding n=1 mod 3 by CRT gives one positive class modulo\n3*2^(A+2). Choose a representative n>=k. This is an arithmetic origin\nbecause the two F-images are 2 mod 3 and 0 mod 3, whereas n=1 mod 3.\nThe terminal n_k=2 mod 4 is outside the domain of F.\n\nSince A\u003c=2k, the first representative can be chosen with\n\n    n \u003c= k+12*4^k \u003c= 13*4^k.                          (6)\n\nThe affine bounds and n>=k give, for every 0\u003c=j\u003c=k,\n\n    .75n \u003c= n_j \u003c= 1.5n+k \u003c= 2.5n.                   (7)\n\nThe vertices are distinct: F is injective, and a repetition before the\nterminal point would create a cycle and prevent the displayed terminal\nexit. The complete finite arithmetic component therefore has k+1 displayed\nvertices, and with m=min C,\n\n    m>=.75n,\n    W(C)>= (k+1)/(2.5n),\n    m W(C)>= 3(k+1)/10.                               (8)\n\nThus no constant C can satisfy W(C)\u003c=C/m uniformly on the unmasked\nfinite F-components. The same construction rules out a bound by a fixed\nconstant times the sum of the reciprocal weights of only the origin and\nterminal vertices: both endpoint reciprocals are O(1/n), while W(C) is\nat least (k+1)/(2.5n).\n\nUsing (6), one also gets a logarithmic form. Since m\u003c=n\u003c=13*4^k,\n\n    k >= (ln m-ln 13)/ln 4,\n\nand hence (8) implies\n\n    mW(C) >= (3/(10 ln 4)) ln m - 3 ln(13)/(10 ln 4).\n\nThis is only an arithmetic obstruction. The terminal n_k=2 mod 4 cannot\nbe an actual birth, so the family does not realize an actual retained\nbirth component. A proof of an endpoint/minimum estimate must use the actual\nbirth masks rather than arithmetic weights alone; this does not refute the individual upper\nbound (3), whose decay is much slower than log(m)/m.\n\n## 6. Independent audit of BM1–BM9\n\nThe complete `FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md` was read.\nThe following checks pass.\n\n- BM1–BM2 reproduce the individual minimum bound correctly. The origin\n  coefficient 17/18 and this all-vertex minimum estimate concern different\n  quantities and are not ordered as stronger or weaker inequalities.\n- BM3–BM4 correctly keep the cumulative slopes in [3/4,3/2], retain the\n  affine term B_j, impose the terminal class with four 2-adic lifts, and\n  use CRT with n=1 mod 3. Since A=s+2r\u003c=2k, the representative bound\n  n\u003ck+12*4^k\u003c=13*4^k is valid. The resulting arithmetic component has no\n  predecessor at its origin, no outgoing edge at n_k, and distinct\n  intermediate vertices.\n- BM5 is arithmetically correct in its stated scope. From\n  m>=3n/4 and W>=(k+1)/(5n/2), one gets mW>=3(k+1)/10. Also\n  1/n+1/n_k\u003c=7/(3n), hence\n  W/(1/n+1/n_k)>=6(k+1)/35. The logarithmic lower bound follows from\n  m\u003c=n\u003c=13*4^k, with no claim of optimality.\n- The residue statement is correctly limited to n_0,...,n_(k-2). A\n  2 mod 4 value would stop the word earlier, and a 1 mod 8 value in that\n  prefix would send its next S-output to 2 mod 4. The penultimate value\n  may be 1 mod 8 because its next output is allowed to be the terminal\n  2 mod 4 value.\n- BM6's terminal-mask calculation is consistent: S-terminals have active\n  child 2y, R-terminals active child 2y+1, and these forced children are\n  1 mod 3. Endpoint targets therefore have spacing at least two depths on\n  each active path. The per-path sum 2/(3b), total endpoint coefficient\n  2E_beta((3X+1)/4), and correction 3/2 are valid as an endpoint-only\n  estimate. BM8's condition F(x)\u003c2m is sufficient, not necessary, and is\n  not a universal pointwise descent.\n- BM7's compressed R-run is explicitly scoped as a connection to earlier\n  S/H/G3 analysis, not an identification of the whole mixed graph.\n- BM8 correctly leaves the final pointwise bridge from finite energy to an\n  everywhere constant invariant unresolved.\n\nThe balanced CRT family is arithmetic only: its terminal value is 2 mod 4\nand cannot be an actual birth. Thus BM5–BM6 do not furnish an actual-birth\ncounterexample. BM8's pointwise gap and the aggregate mass of actual finite\ncomponents remain open. No correction to the BM root file is required.\n\n## 7. Final BM8 scope correction\n\nThe BM8 pointwise bridge must be stated conditionally. The named PD170/QS170\nbridge remains retained in the archive: under that additional premise,\nfinite energy implies pointwise constancy of beta. The intended mechanism\nis that an exceptional integer produces a same-color value a=3u+1 whose\nancestors have positive lower density, contradicting the density-zero\nconclusion. This report does not re-prove the full PD analytic input.\n\nWithout PD170 (or another separately justified pointwise bridge), the\nfinite-energy implication established here reaches only density\nconstancy. Thus the PD-free pointwise step remains open, while under PD170\nthe outstanding BM task is the upper bound for total actual finite-\ncomponent energy. BM1–BM7 and the arithmetic barriers above are unchanged.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_FIRST_COLUMN_FIBER_DESCENT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — first-column primitive-fiber descent audit\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual audit only; no programs, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean, or numerical experiments.\n\n## Scope and conditional setup\n\nFix an odd \\(m>0\\) with \\(3\\nmid m\\). Let\n\\[\nz_a=3^a m,\\qquad\nx_{a,\\ell}=n_{a,\\ell}-1\n      =4^{\\ell+1}3^{a-1-\\ell}m,\\qquad\nn_{a,\\ell}=1+x_{a,\\ell},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(0\\le\\ell\\le a-1\\). The established primitive-fiber\ntransfer says that an actual primitive birth at \\(z_a\\) gives actual\nmultiplicative-defect sources \\(n_{a,\\ell}\\) for all these \\(\\ell\\), each with\n\\[\ng(n_{a,\\ell})=\\beta(3n_{a,\\ell})-\\beta(n_{a,\\ell})\n            =1-2\\beta(m),\\qquad |g(n_{a,\\ell})|=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThis audit does not assert that any primitive birth exists. For a column\nwith at least one such birth, let \\(A\\ge2\\) be its least exponent. A column\nwith no actual primitive birth contributes nothing to the quantities below.\n\nThe source labels are injective, including across different \\(m\\)'s. Indeed,\nfrom \\(x_{a,\\ell}\\) one recovers\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac{v_2(x_{a,\\ell})}{2}-1,\\qquad\na-1-\\ell=v_3(x_{a,\\ell}),\\qquad\nm=x_{a,\\ell}/(4^{\\ell+1}3^{a-1-\\ell}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThus no source is counted twice when columns are summed.\n\n## Descent of every later fiber to the first fiber\n\nFor \\(a>A\\), charge the source with index \\(\\ell\\) to the first-fiber\nindex\n\\[\n\\ell'=\\min(\\ell,A-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\ell\\le A-1\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{a,\\ell}}{x_{A,\\ell}}\n=3^{a-A}\\ge3. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\ell\\ge A\\), then the target is \\(x_{A,A-1}=4^A m\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{a,\\ell}}{x_{A,A-1}}\n=4^{\\ell+1-A}3^{a-1-\\ell}\\ge4. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe last inequality follows because \\(\\ell\\ge A\\), \\(a\\ge A+1\\), and\n\\(\\ell\\le a-1\\); increasing \\(\\ell\\) multiplies the displayed ratio by\n\\(4/3\\), so its minimum is at \\((a,\\ell)=(A+1,A)\\).\n\nThe first fiber is largest at \\(\\ell=A-1\\), since\n\\(x_{A,\\ell+1}/x_{A,\\ell}=4/3\\). Its unshifted arithmetic weight is\n\\[\nW_0:=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{A-1}\\frac1{x_{A,\\ell}}\n=\\frac{1-(3/4)^A}{3^{A-1}m}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThe complete later-fiber arithmetic tail is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT\n&:=\\sum_{a=A+1}^{\\infty}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{a-1}\\frac1{x_{a,\\ell}}\\\\\n&=\\frac1m\\sum_{a=A+1}^{\\infty}\n    \\frac{1-(3/4)^a}{3^{a-1}}\n=\\frac1m\\left(\\frac{3^{1-A}}2-4^{-A}\\right). \\tag{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor \\(q=(3/4)^A\\), \\(A\\ge2\\) gives \\(0\u003cq\\le9/16\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{T}{W_0}\n=\\frac{\\frac12-\\frac q3}{1-q}\n\\le\\frac{\\frac12-\\frac{3}{16}}{1-\\frac{9}{16}}\n=\\frac57. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThe inequality is monotone in \\(q\\), since the derivative of the middle\nfraction is \\(1/6(1-q)^{-2}>0\\).\n\nThe actual first-fiber weight uses \\(1/n=1/(1+x)\\), not \\(1/x\\). Every\nfirst-fiber \\(x_{A,\\ell}\\ge12\\) (the smallest possible case is\n\\(A=2,m=1,\\ell=0\\)), hence\n\\[\nW_{\\rm first}:=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{A-1}\\frac1{1+x_{A,\\ell}}\n\\ge\\frac{12}{13}W_0. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nTherefore the complete later tail satisfies\n\\[\nT\\le \\frac57W_0\\le\\frac{65}{84}W_{\\rm first}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThis is an upper bound for actual later defect sources because\n\\(1/(1+x)\\le1/x\\).\n\n## Common-cutoff verification\n\nLet\n\\[\nF_m(X):=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{A-1}\n  \\frac{\\mathbf 1_{\\,1+x_{A,\\ell}\\le X}}{1+x_{A,\\ell}},\n\\quad\nJ_m(X):=\\sum_{a>A}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{a-1}\n  \\frac{\\mathbf 1_{\\,1+x_{a,\\ell}\\le X}}{1+x_{a,\\ell}},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nwhere the second sum is restricted to the actual transferred primitive\ndefect sources. There are two cutoff regimes.\n\nIf \\(X\u003c1+x_{A,A-1}=1+4^A m\\), a later source with\n\\(\\ell\\ge A-1\\) cannot be \\(\\le X\\): for \\(\\ell=A-1\\) its unshifted\ncoordinate is \\(3^r x_{A,A-1}>x_{A,A-1}>X-1\\), and the coordinates for\n\\(\\ell\\ge A\\) are even larger by (6). Thus every retained later source\nhas \\(\\ell\\le A-2\\). For a fixed such \\(\\ell\\), writing\n\\(x_\\ell=x_{A,\\ell}\\), later exponents are \\(a=A+r\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}\\frac1{1+x_\\ell3^r}\n\\le\\sum_{r\\ge1}\\frac1{x_\\ell3^r}\n=\\frac1{2x_\\ell}\n\\le\\frac{13}{24}\\frac1{1+x_\\ell}, \\tag{13}\n\\]\nwhere \\(x_\\ell\\ge12\\) was used in the last step. Moreover, if a later\nsource is \\(\\le X\\), its charged first-fiber target is also \\(\\le X\\),\nbecause the target coordinate is strictly smaller. Hence\n\\[\nJ_m(X)\\le\\frac{13}{24}F_m(X)\n\\quad\\text{when }X\u003c1+4^A m. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(X\\ge1+4^A m\\), the whole first fiber is present, and (8)--(11) give\n\\[\nJ_m(X)\\le\\frac{65}{84}F_m(X). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nCombining (14)--(15), the uniform same-cutoff estimate is\n\\[\nJ_m(X)\\le\\frac{65}{84}F_m(X)\\qquad(X\\ge1). \\tag{16}\n\\]\nThe stronger \\(13/24\\) coefficient in the first regime is recorded only\nto make the cutoff direction explicit.\n\n## Global consequence and exact scope\n\nLet \\(F(X)=\\sum_mF_m(X)\\) over columns having a first primitive exponent,\nand let \\(J_{\\rm prim}(X)=F(X)+\\sum_mJ_m(X)\\) be the mass of all transferred\nprimitive-fiber defect sources at indices at most \\(X\\). Injectivity (3) and\n(16) give\n\\[\nF(X)\\le J_{\\rm prim}(X)\n\\le\\left(1+\\frac{65}{84}\\right)F(X)\n=\\frac{149}{84}F(X). \\tag{17}\n\\]\nThis is a valid finite-cutoff comparison; it does not assume that the set\nof primitive births is nonempty, and it does not claim that \\(F(X)\\) or\n\\(J_{\\rm prim}(X)\\) is bounded as \\(X\\to\\infty\\). Columns without an\nactual primitive birth contribute zero here. The rest of the birth mass\noutside the transferred primitive fibers is not controlled. Consequently (17) is not a\nfinite-energy or full-Collatz conclusion. The only required input beyond\nthe displayed arithmetic is the previously established conditional\nprimitive-fiber transfer from each actual \\(z_a\\).\n\n\n## Appendix: full audit of FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION (CF1--CF11)\n\nThe complete root file \\`FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION.md\\` was read.\nIts CF1--CF9 reduction agrees with the preceding report, with the\nconditional scope made more explicit: \\(A\\) is the least exponent in\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_m=\\{a\\ge2:e(3^am)=1,\\ h(3^jm)=0\\ (0\\le j\u003ca)\\}.\n\\]\nThus no primitive exponent-one event is silently included. CF1's fiber\nformula and common signed value follow from the already established PB\nselector; this is a conditional transfer from each actual event, not an\nexistence assertion.\n\nCF2's recovery formulas are exact:\n\\(v_2(n-1)=2(\\ell+1)\\) recovers \\(\\ell\\), then\n\\(v_3(n-1)=a-1-\\ell\\) recovers \\(a\\), and the remaining factor recovers\n\\(m\\). CF3's ratio and \\(q\\le(n+2)/3\\) are valid for both \\(\\ell\\le A-1\\)\nand \\(\\ell\\ge A\\). The geometric sums in CF4--CF6 are correct; in\nparticular \\(16(3/4)^A\\le9\\) is equivalent to \\(A\\ge2\\), and the\ndenominator correction \\(x\\ge12\\) gives \\(12/13\\). CF7--CF9 correctly\npreserve the same cutoff: a later source being retained forces its charged\nfirst-fiber target to be retained.\n\nThe CF10 boundary is also correct. For \\(A=1\\), \\(x=4m\\), the later\narithmetic multipliers split as \\(3^s\\), \\(s\\ge1\\), and \\(4^r3^s\\),\n\\(r\\ge1,s\\ge0\\). Their reciprocal sums are \\(1/2+1/2=1\\), while the\nreciprocal-square sums are \\(1/8+3/40=1/5\\). Therefore\n\\[\n S(x)\\ge x^{-1}-(5x^2)^{-1}> (x+1)^{-1}\\quad(x\\ge4).\n\\]\nThis is only an unmasked arithmetic obstruction to extending the\nsubunit-tail argument to \\(A=1\\). It is not an actual-birth realization or\na counterexample to the invariant.\n\nCF11's firstness argument is valid for \\(A\\ge3\\): with\n\\(y=3^{A-1}m\\), \\(e(y)=0\\) follows because otherwise \\(A-1\\) would belong\nto \\(\\mathcal A_m\\), while \\(e(3y)=1\\). The genuine transfers\n\\[\n T(y+1)=T(y/3)=(y+1)/2\n\\]\ngive \\(\\beta(y+1)=\\beta(y/3)=B\\). Hence \\(e(y)=0\\) forces\n\\(\\beta(2y+1)=B\\). Put \\(v=2y+1\u003c4y+1=p_A\\). If \\(h(v)=1\\), this is a\nstrictly smaller defect index. If \\(h(v)=0\\), the displayed residual\ncolors are genuine:\n\\[\n \\beta(6y)=B,\\quad \\beta(6y+1)=1-B,\\quad\n \\beta(6y+2)=\\beta(6y+3)=B.\n\\]\nHere the first, second, and third identities use respectively the even\ntransfer from \\(3y\\), the birth mask at \\(3y\\), and the even transfer from\n\\(3y+1\\); the last uses \\(6y+3=3v\\) and \\(h(v)=0\\). The case \\(A=2\\) is\ncorrectly excluded: firstness over \\(a\\ge2\\) says nothing about\n\\(e(3m)\\).\n\nThe final CF scope is sound. CF9 compares only actual transferred\nprimitive-fiber source mass and leaves the first fibers themselves\nuncontrolled. CF10 is not an actual counterexample, CF11 is a conditional\nsmaller-defect-or-residual-mask alternative, and neither yields finite\nenergy or a full-Collatz conclusion. The earlier two-birth report now\ncontains the corrected path identity\n\\(T(z_j+1)=T(z_j/3)=(z_j+1)/2\\) and the zero-defect range\n\\(j=0,\\ldots,b-1\\).\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_FIRST_FIBER_RESIDUAL_IDENTITY.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — signed residual identity in the first primitive fiber\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no programs, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean, or numerical experiments.\n\n## Conditional setup\n\nUse CF1 and CF7 from\n`FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION.md`. Let \\(m\\) be odd and \\(3\\nmid m\\),\nlet \\(A\\ge2\\) be the first primitive exponent in the stated \\(\\mathcal\nA_m\\), and put\n\\[\n y=3^{A-1}m,\\qquad z_A=3y,\\qquad\n p=p_A=4y+1,\\qquad v=2y+1.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\n B=\\beta(y)=\\beta(3y),\\qquad s=1-2B\\in\\{-1,1\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe established primitive-fiber selector gives\n\\[\n \\beta(p)=B,\\qquad \\beta(3p)=1-B,\\qquad g(p)=s. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe first equality also follows from the genuine path\n\\(p\\to6y+2\\to3y+1\\), since the birth at \\(3y\\) gives\n\\(\\beta(3y+1)=B\\). The second is the signed PB transfer and is retained as\na conditional input.\n\nThe path identity\n\\[\n T(y+1)=T(y/3)=(y+1)/2\n\\]\ngives \\(\\beta(y+1)=\\beta(y/3)=B\\). For \\(A\\ge3\\), firstness implies\n\\(e(y)=0\\); hence\n\\[\n \\beta(v)=\\beta(2y+1)=B. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nNo such conclusion is available from firstness when \\(A=2\\).\n\n## Correct residual colors for \\(A\\ge3\\)\n\nLet\n\\[\n C=\\beta(3v)=\\beta(6y+3).\n\\]\nThe genuine even paths and the birth mask at \\(3y\\) give\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\n t&6y&6y+1&6y+2&6y+3\\\\ \\hline\n \\beta(t)&B&1-B&B&C .\n\\end{array} \\tag{4}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(\\beta(6y)=\\beta(3y)=B\\);\n\\(\\beta(6y+1)=1-B\\) is the middle color of the birth at \\(3y\\);\n\\(\\beta(6y+2)=\\beta(3y+1)=B\\); and \\(6y+3=3v\\).\nThe actual path is instead\n\\[\n T(v)=T(2y+1)=3y+2,\\qquad\n \\beta(6y+4)=\\beta(3y+2)=\\beta(v)=B. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe last equality uses (3); the birth at \\(3y\\) supplies the opposite\ncolor at \\(6y+1\\) (and at \\(9y+2\\)), not at \\(3y+2\\).\n\nConsequently the two adjacent residual boundary defects are\n\\[\n d(6y+2)=|C-B|=h(v),\\qquad\n d(6y+3)=|B-C|=h(v)=d(3v). \\tag{6}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\beta(v)=B\\),\n\\[\n g(v)=C-B=s\\,h(v). \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThere is no resulting identity expressing \\(g(p)\\) through \\(g(v)\\):\n(2) fixes \\(g(p)=s\\) by the primitive-fiber selector, while (6)--(7)\nonly describe \\(g(v)\\) and two equal adjacency defects. The previously\nwritten formulas (8)--(9) were false because they assigned the birth's\nopposite color to \\(3y+2\\). They are withdrawn here.\n\nBecause\n\\[\n v=2y+1\u003cp=4y+1\u003c3v=6y+3 \\qquad(y\\ge1), \\tag{8}\n\\]\nthe index \\(v\u003cp\\) is a possible smaller defect only when \\(h(v)=1\\), but\n(6)--(7) do not force that case. When \\(h(v)=0\\), both residual adjacency\ndefects are zero, while \\(g(p)=s\\ne0\\). Thus no signed decomposition or\nunconditional smaller-target transfer follows.\n\nThe residual mask also shows \\(d(6y)=d(6y+1)=1\\), but these fixed defects\ndo not produce a smaller multiplicative defect \\(h\\). The corrected\nconclusion is only the residual identity (6)--(7), not a contraction of\nharmonic mass.\n\n## The boundary case \\(A=2\\)\n\nHere \\(y=3m\\), and the definition of firstness over \\(a\\ge2\\) does not\nimply \\(e(y)=0\\). The genuine paths still give\n\\[\n \\beta(y)=\\beta(y+1)=B,\\qquad\n \\beta(6y+1)=1-B,\\qquad\n \\beta(6y+2)=B. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nWith \\(V=\\beta(v)=\\beta(2y+1)\\), the actual path \\(T(v)=3y+2\\) gives\n\\[\n \\beta(6y+4)=\\beta(3y+2)=V. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nHere \\(e(y)=1\\) is exactly the possibility \\(V=1-B\\), while \\(e(y)=0\\)\ngives \\(V=B\\). If \\(C=\\beta(3v)\\), then only\n\\[\n g(p)=s,\\qquad g(v)=C-V,\\qquad d(3v)=|C-V|=h(v) \\tag{11}\n\\]\nis forced. Without \\(V=B\\), no relation between \\(g(p)\\) and \\(g(v)\\) of\nthe withdrawn form follows. Therefore the \\(A=2\\) case supplies no\nunconditional signed smaller-defect identity from this local mask alone.\nIf an independent argument establishes \\(e(y)=0\\), the \\(A\\ge3\\)\ncalculation applies with \\(V=B\\); that is an additional hypothesis.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe earlier signed formulas (8)--(9) are explicitly retracted. For\n\\(A\\ge3\\), the valid information is only (6)--(7): \\(v\\) is smaller than\n\\(p\\) and may carry a same-sign defect if \\(h(v)=1\\), but that condition is\nnot forced, and when \\(h(v)=0\\) both residual adjacency defects vanish.\nFor \\(A=2\\), even \\(\\beta(v)\\) is unforced. No new smaller-birth transfer,\nfinite-energy estimate, actual primitive-birth existence, nonconstant\ninvariant, or full-Collatz conclusion is obtained.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_GROWTH_ORIGIN_MASKS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — exact masks at the origin of an actual-growth chain\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no numerical\nprogram, CAS, enumeration or Lean was used. The purpose is to identify\nthe incoming edge in the actual masked growth graph from SC/BC. An\narithmetic inverse is never treated as an actual edge without its birth\nand color guards.\n\n## 1. The two inverse candidates\n\nWrite\n\n    d(j)=|β(j+1)−β(j)|,  e(j)=d(2j)d(2j+1).\n\nThe exact birth law is\n\n    e(j)=1  iff  β(j)=β(j+1)≠β(3j+2),\n\nand the XOR identity gives \\(e(j)=1\\Rightarrow d(j)=0\\).\n\nThe retained arithmetic maps are\n\n    H(q)  = (9q+5)/4,   q≡3 (mod 4),\n    G₃(q) = (9q+3)/8,   q≡13 (mod 16).\n\nTheir images are disjoint:\n\n    H(4t+3)=9t+8       ≡8 (mod 9),\n    G₃(16t+13)=18t+15 ≡15 (mod 18).\n\nTherefore a positive index \\(n\\) has at most one arithmetic predecessor\nin the retained maps. Explicitly,\n\n    q_H(n)=(4n−5)/9       when n≡8 (mod 9),\n    q_3(n)=(8n−3)/9       when n≡15 (mod 18).\n\nThe displayed residue conditions also put \\(q_H\\) in \\(3\\bmod4\\) and\n\\(q_3\\) in \\(13\\bmod16\\). Positivity starts at \\(n=8\\) and \\(n=15\\),\nrespectively. The two cases cannot overlap.\n\n## 2. Exact incoming-edge indicator\n\nThe SC guards give an exact distinction between an arithmetic inverse and\nan actual incoming edge. For an already actual birth \\(n\\), an H-edge\n\\(q_H\\to n\\) exists exactly when\n\n    e(q_H)=1.\n\nIndeed the input domain is supplied by the residue class, the input\nbirth supplies the H-side birth condition, and the assumed \\(e(n)=1\\)\nalready implies \\(d(n)=0\\), the only output boundary guard recorded for\nH.\n\nFor a G₃ candidate, an actual edge \\(q_3\\to n\\) exists exactly when\n\n    e(q_3)=1  and  d(n+1)=0.\n\nThe product form is the uniform guard formula. On the actual\n\\(13\\bmod16\\) G₃ input strip, however, Sections 5 and 8 prove that\n\\(e(n)=1\\) implies\n\\[\ne(q_3)=1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d(n+1)=0.\n\\]\nThus the neighboring guard is redundant once the inverse birth is\nknown; it is retained in the product below to display the original\nmask explicitly. Since \\(e(n)=1\\) also supplies \\(d(n)=0\\), the exact\nincoming-edge indicator on the birth set is\n\n    P(n)=\n      1_{n≡8 (mod 9)} e(q_H(n))\n      +1_{n≡15 (mod 18)} e(q_3(n))(1−d(n+1)).\n\nThe two summands are mutually exclusive. Consequently the exact origin\nindicator for the actual masked-growth graph is\n\n    O(n)=e(n)(1−P(n)).                                  (1)\n\nThis is an origin in the retained growth graph: it means that the birth\nhas no incoming H/G₃ edge. It does not mean that \\(n\\) has no smaller\nbirth by some separate ancestry route.\n\nIn boundary form, the masks in (1) are\n\n    e(q_H)=d(2q_H)d(2q_H+1),\n    e(q_3)=d(2q_3)d(2q_3+1).\n\nThus the inverse formula does not replace the actual masks by an\narithmetic residue test.\n\n## 3. What is and is not automatic from \\(e(n)=1\\)\n\nFor an H candidate, \\(e(n)=1\\) alone does not give the two active\nboundaries \\(d(2q_H)=d(2q_H+1)=1\\) required for \\(e(q_H)=1\\). The\nforward SC derivation proves that an actual birth \\(q_H\\), together with\nthe actual output birth, produces the H transition. Its identities do\nnot provide the reverse implication from the output birth to the input\nbirth.\n\nFor a G₃ candidate, \\(e(n)=1\\) alone gives neither \\(e(q_3)=1\\) nor\nthe neighboring guard as a separate input. The image-specific path\ncalculation in Sections 5 and 8 gives the exact equivalence\n\\(e(q_3)=1\\iff d(n+1)=0\\); consequently, once \\(e(q_3)=1\\) is known,\nthe guard is automatic. What \\(e(n)=1\\) alone does not give is either\nside of this equivalent condition. No color independence is being\nassumed here; the point is only that arithmetic integrality alone does\nnot derive the missing mask.\n\nEquivalently, the direct color conditions still have to be checked:\n\n    β(q)=β(q+1)≠β(3q+2)\n\nfor the predecessor birth. In the G₃ image, \\(d(n+1)=0\\) is equivalent\nto that predecessor birth under \\(e(n)=1\\), by Sections 5 and 8.\nArithmetic integrality establishes only the candidate \\(q\\), not these\ncolor equalities.\n\n## 4. When a failed inverse mask does charge a smaller birth\n\nThe failure of an incoming edge can sometimes be converted into a\nstrictly smaller birth, but the conversion uses an active boundary.\nFor either candidate \\(q\\), if\n\n    d(q)=1  or  d(q+1)=1,\n\nthe nearest-birth ancestry of that active boundary gives a birth\n\\(a\\le\\lfloor(q+1)/2\\rfloor\u003cn\\). This is a genuine smaller-birth\ntransfer because \\(q_H\u003cn\\) and \\(q_3\u003cn\\). It is a sufficient local\ncharge, not a necessary characterization of every possible smaller\ntransfer.\n\nIf \\(d(q)=0\\), the XOR identity says\n\\(d(2q)=d(2q+1)\\); hence\n\n    e(q)=1 iff d(2q)=d(2q+1)=1,\n\nand \\(e(q)=0\\) forces both of those child boundaries to be zero. As a\ngeneral warning this is a possible local mask pattern, but it is not a\nresidual inverse-origin case for an actual birth in either retained\nimage: Section 5 forces \\(d(q)=d(\\floor(q/2))=1\\) when the H inverse\nbirth fails, and Section 5 gives the same two active levels for a failed\nG₃ inverse. The exact origin formula (1) still cannot be replaced by a\nresidue-only formula, because the surviving image masks depend on\n\\(\\beta(u)\\) or \\(d(n+1)\\).\n\nFor a G₃ candidate there is one additional direct charge: if\n\\(d(n+1)=1\\), its nearest-birth ancestor is at most\n\\(\\lfloor(n+1)/2\\rfloor\u003cn\\). Therefore every G₃ birth with this failed\nneighbor guard is already accompanied by a smaller birth, although it\nis an origin with respect to the retained growth graph.\n\n## 5. Image-specific reverse dichotomies\n\nThe two image classes permit a stronger conclusion than the general\nmask formula.\n\n### H image\n\nWrite an H-image birth as\n\n    n=9u−1,  q=4u−1=H^{-1}(n),\n    u=2^a v,  v odd.\n\nIn the strip notation of SC, with the base \\(v\\),\n\n    n=X_{a+2,2}=3^2 2^a v−1,\n    q=X_{a+2,0}=2^(a+2)v−1,\n    floor(q/2)=X_{a+1,0}.\n\nSince \\(e(n)=1\\), SC2 gives\n\\(C_{a+2}=B_2\\ne C_{a+3}\\). Its merging condition gives\n\\(3^{a+2}v\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), and then SC4 gives\n\n    C_{a+1}=C_{a+2}=:c.\n\nThe genuine paths in the same strip therefore give\n\n    β(q)=β(floor(q/2))=c,\n    β(q+1)=β(floor(q/2)+1)=β(v)=B_0.\n\nMoreover \\(e(q)=1\\) exactly when \\(B_0=c\\): the third color is\n\\(C_{a+3}\\ne c\\), already supplied by \\(e(n)=1\\). If \\(B_0\\ne c\\),\nthen both \\(d(q)=1\\) and \\(d(\\floor(q/2))=1\\), so their nearest birth\nancestor satisfies\n\n    a_q≤floor(q/4)\u003cn.\n\nThus every actual birth in the H image either has the actual birth\npredecessor \\(q\\), which is already a smaller birth, or has two active\nboundaries \\(q,\\floor(q/2)\\), which give a smaller birth.\n\n### G₃ image\n\nWrite\n\n    n=18t+15,  q=16t+13=G₃^{-1}(n),\n    c=β(n)=β(n+1),  \\bar c=β(2n+1).\n\nDirect genuine T paths are\n\n    q →24t+20→12t+10→6t+5→9t+8,\n    n+1=18t+16 →9t+8,\n    q+1→8t+7→12t+11→18t+17=n+2,\n    2q+1=32t+27→48t+41→72t+62→36t+31=2n+1.\n\nConsequently\n\n    β(q)=β(n+1)=c,\n    β(q+1)=β(n+2),\n    β(3q+2)=β(2q+1)=\\bar c.\n\nSince \\(e(n)=1\\) gives \\(\\bar c\\ne c\\), this proves the exact\nequivalence\n\n    e(q)=1  iff  d(n+1)=0.                              (2)\n\nIf \\(d(n+1)=0\\), \\(q\\) is a smaller actual birth. If\n\\(d(n+1)=1\\), then \\(d(q)=1\\). With \\(p=\\floor(q/2)=8t+6\\), the paths\n\n    p→4t+3→6t+5→9t+8,\n    p+1=8t+7→12t+11→18t+17\n\ngive \\(d(p)=1\\) as well. Hence the nearest birth ancestor of \\(q\\)\nsatisfies \\(a_q\\le\\floor(q/4)\u003cn\\). Every actual birth in the G₃ image\ntherefore also has a smaller birth.\n\n### Consequence for origins and weighted charge\n\nThe exact indicator (1) remains the origin indicator for the masked\ngrowth graph, but its image part has a strict smaller-birth transfer:\nan H-image origin is charged by \\(q,\\floor(q/2)\\), and a G₃-image\norigin by \\(q,\\floor(q/2)\\). The corresponding \\(q\\)'s are distinct\nbecause the two image maps are injective and their images are disjoint.\nGrouping active \\(q\\)'s by their nearest birth ancestor \\(a\\), the\ndepth-\\(\\ell\\ge2\\) ancestry bound gives\n\n    sum_{q charged to a} 1/q\n       ≤ sum_{\\ell≥2} 2/(2^\\ell a) = 1/a.\n\nFor \\(n\\) in the H or G₃ image,\n\n    1/n≤4/(9q)  (H),       1/n≤8/(9q)  (G₃).\n\nThus, with \\(D(X)\\) the harmonic mass of actual births\n\\(n\\le X\\) that lie in one of these images and are graph origins,\n\n    D(X)≤(8/9) E_β(2X/9).                                (3)\n\nIndeed every charged ancestor satisfies \\(a\\le q/4\u003c2X/9\\) when\n\\(n\\le X\\), and the larger G₃ coefficient \\(8/9\\) covers both modes.\nThis is a genuine weighted local estimate with no \\(O(1)\\) term. It is\nnot a contraction for the total birth energy: origins outside the two\nimage classes, and the finite-chain bookkeeping in BC, remain.\n\n## 6. Scope\n\nEquation (1) is an exact mask formula for beginnings of actual H/G₃\nchains. Sections 5 proves that every birth in either arithmetic image\nhas a smaller birth, even when it is an origin of the retained growth\ngraph. This excludes both image classes as locations of a minimum birth\nand gives (3), but it does not handle births outside these images,\nbound the total origin mass, or establish global descent.\n\n## 7. Audit of OR1--OR10 and the progression \\(144t+99\\)\n\nThe root report INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md was read in full.\nOR1 is correct: \\(H(4t+3)=9t+8\\) and \\(G_3(16t+13)=18t+15\\), and\nthe two image classes are disjoint. The inverse formulas and their\npositivity edges \\(n\\ge8\\) and \\(n\\ge15\\) are correct.\n\nOR2--OR4 agree with the H-strip calculation above. For\n\\(n=9u-1,\\ q=4u-1\\), the genuine paths give\n\\(\\beta(q)=\\beta(n)=c\\), \\(\\beta(2q+1)=1-c\\), and\n\\(\\beta(q+1)=\\beta(u)\\). Writing \\(u=2^a v\\) makes\n\\(n=X_{a+2,2}\\), \\(q=X_{a+2,0}\\), and\n\\(\\floor(q/2)=X_{a+1,0}\\); the merger \\(C_{a+1}=C_{a+2}\\) proves\n\\(d(q)=d(\\floor(q/2))=|\\beta(u)-c|\\). The \\(u=1\\) edge is safe:\nthe failure alternative would require a positive birth ancestor\n\\(\\le\\floor(q/4)=0\\), so it cannot occur.\n\nOR5--OR7 are also exact. The listed paths are valid shortcut paths, and\n\\(e(q)=1-d(n+1)\\), \\(d(q)=d(\\floor(q/2))=d(n+1)\\) follow from the\nmiddle path \\(2q\\!+\\!1\\to\\cdots\\to2n\\!+\\!1\\). When the guard fails,\nthe two active levels give a birth at most \\(\\floor(q/4)\u003cn\\).\n\nOR8 correctly describes origins of **all** actual retained-growth\ncomponents, not just finite-component beginnings:\n\n    outside I_H∪I_G:  o(n)=e(n),\n    n=9u−1:           o(n)=e(n)|β(u)−β(n)|,\n    n=18t+15:         o(n)=e(n)d(n+1).\n\nThe set \\(A_\\beta\\) of finite-component origins is a separate subset of\n\\(O_\\beta\\); no finite local formula for \\(A_\\beta\\) is being asserted.\nOR9 correctly charges only \\(D_\\beta=O_\\beta\\cap(I_H\\cup I_G)\\).\nThe inverse \\(q\\)'s are distinct, their ancestry depth is at least two,\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{q charged to }b}\\frac1q\n\\le \\sum_{\\ell\\ge2}\\frac{2}{2^\\ell b}=\\frac1b.\n\\]\nWith \\(1/n\\le4/(9q)\\) for H and \\(1/n\\le8/(9q)\\) for G₃, and\n\\(b\\le q/4\u003c2X/9\\), the combined bound\n\\[\nE_D(X)\\le \\frac89 E_\\beta(2X/9)\n\\]\nhas no error term. The cutoff is \\(2X/9\\), not \\(X\\).\n\nOR10 correctly applies the BC chain weight at most \\(9\\) times the\norigin weight:\n\\[\nE_\\beta(X)\\le9E_{O_\\beta}(X)\n\\le9E_{P_\\beta}(X)+8E_\\beta(2X/9).\n\\]\nThe coefficient \\(8\\) appears after multiplying the origin-in-image\nbound by the chain factor \\(9\\); \\(8/9\\) is not a full-energy\ncontraction. The unknown term is the mass of primitive origins\n\\(P_\\beta\\), and \\(A_\\beta\\) cannot be substituted for all origins.\n\nFinally, the arithmetic family \\(n=144t+99\\) is checked:\n\\(n\\equiv0\\pmod9\\), \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod{16}\\), and\n\\[\nH(n)=324t+224\\equiv0\\pmod4.\n\\]\nIt is therefore outside both image classes and has only this one\narithmetic growth step before the partial map is undefined. Its\n\\(\\sum_t1/(144t+99)\\) diverges as an arithmetic superset. This does not\nassert that any member is an actual birth. Conditional on actual birth,\nthe retained component is finite (one edge if the output birth mask\nholds, otherwise a singleton), so such a member belongs to \\(A_\\beta\\).\n\n## 8. A sharper one-sided mask for a G₃ input\n\nLet \\(q=16t+13\\) and \\(n=G_3(q)=18t+15\\), without assuming that \\(n\\)\nis a birth. The genuine paths already listed in OR5 give\n\n    β(q)=β(n+1),   β(q+1)=β(n+2),\n    d(q)=d(n+1).\n\nIf \\(q\\) is an actual birth of color\n\\(c=\\beta(q)=\\beta(q+1)\\), its middle color is \\(1-c\\). The middle path\n\\(2q+1\\to\\cdots\\to2n+1\\) therefore gives\n\\(\\beta(3n+2)=\\beta(2n+1)=1-c\\), while\n\\(\\beta(n+1)=\\beta(n+2)=c\\). Hence the output neighbor guard\n\\(d(n+1)=0\\) is automatic. Moreover,\n\n    e(n)=1  iff  β(n)=c.\n\nIf the G₃ growth step fails after an actual birth \\(q\\), the only\npossible failure is therefore\n\n    β(n)=1-c,  d(n)=1,  e(n)=0,\n\nnot \\(d(n+1)=1\\). With the signed difference\n\\(\\delta(n)=\\beta(n+1)-\\beta(n)\\), this failure has the exact sign\n\n    \\delta(n)=c-(1-c)=-(1-2c).\n\nThis is a color implication on the \\(13\\bmod16\\) G₃ input strip; it\ndoes not extend to the whole \\(5\\bmod8\\) class without the G₃ input\nhypothesis and the actual-birth assumption on \\(q\\).\n\n## 9. Audit of OR11--OR13\n\nThe added OR11--OR13 block in the root report is consistent with the\npreceding formulas. OR11 is exactly the four path calculation above:\n\\(\\beta(q)=\\beta(n+1)\\), \\(\\beta(q+1)=\\beta(n+2)\\), and\n\\(d(q)=d(n+1)\\) for \\(q=16t+13\\), \\(n=18t+15\\).\n\nOR12 correctly separates the two colors after an actual birth \\(q\\):\nif \\(\\beta(n)=c\\), then \\(e(n)=1\\); if \\(\\beta(n)=1-c\\), then\n\\(d(n)=1\\), \\(e(n)=0\\), and\n\\(\\delta(n)=\\beta(n+1)-\\beta(n)=c-(1-c)=-(1-2c)\\). The \\(n+1\\)\nfailure is excluded on this strip. This is the same conditional\nstatement as Section 8 and does not remove masks from the broader\n\\(5\\bmod8\\) routing.\n\nOR13 is a necessary interval/sign condition under its stated\nsame-component ancestry hypothesis: a failed H boundary lies in the\nfirst half of the dyadic block, while a failed G₃ boundary lies in the\nsecond half. The condition also requires every boundary on the prefix\nto be active. The depth and sign restrictions are therefore useful\nfilters for a possible self-return, but they do not by themselves\nexclude all returns to an earlier component member or provide a global\nrank.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_MINIMUM_FIRST_FIBER_MASK.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — minimum odd defect and the first primitive fiber mask\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no programs, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean, or numerical experiments.\n\n## Setup and minimality\n\nLet \\(h(n)=|\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n)|\\), with \\(h(2t)=h(t)\\). Assume a\npositive odd \\(q\\) with \\(h(q)=1\\) is minimal among all positive odd\nindices with nonzero \\(h\\). Then every positive \\(r\u003cq\\), odd or even,\nhas \\(h(r)=0\\): for even \\(r\\), repeatedly remove powers of two and use\n\\(h(2t)=h(t)\\).\n\nAssume in addition that \\(q\\) is the first primitive-fiber source\n\\[\nq=p_A=4y+1,\\qquad y=3^{A-1}m,\\qquad m\\ {\\rm odd},\\ 3\\nmid m,\n\\qquad A\\ge2.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nB=\\beta(y)=\\beta(3y),\\qquad s=1-2B,\\qquad\nt=\\frac{3y+1}{2},\\qquad v=2y+1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe primitive-fiber transfer gives\n\\[\n\\beta(q)=B,\\qquad \\beta(3q)=1-B,\\qquad g(q)=s. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe genuine paths\n\\[\ny\\longmapsto t,\\qquad\nq=4y+1\\longmapsto6y+2\\longmapsto3y+1=2t\n\\]\ngive \\(\\beta(t)=\\beta(q)=B\\). Since \\(t,v\u003cq\\), minimality gives\n\\[\nh(t)=h(v)=0. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThis is the first extra input beyond CF7; it uses minimality of \\(h\\), not\nminimality of the birth indicator \\(e\\).\n\n## Minimality also forces \\(e(y)=0\\) when \\(A=2\\)\n\nPut \\(u=y/3=3^{A-2}m\\), which is positive and odd for every \\(A\\ge2\\).\nThe exact PB2 event identity applies on the arithmetic plus selector:\n\\[\ne(3u)=(1-h(u))h(4u+1). \\tag{M1}\n\\]\nFor every positive odd \\(u\\), \\(e(3u)=1\\) first forces that plus selector,\nso (M1) yields the implication\n\\[\ne(3u)=1\\Longrightarrow h(u)=0,\\quad h(4u+1)=1. \\tag{M1'}\n\\]\nThis is a genuine selector/path implication, not a free color assignment.\nIf \\(e(y)=e(3u)=1\\), minimality gives \\(h(u)=0\\), so (M1') forces\n\\(h(4u+1)=1\\). But\n\\[\n4u+1=\\frac{4y}{3}+1\u003c4y+1=q,\n\\]\ncontradicting minimality of \\(q\\). Hence\n\\[\ne(y)=0,\\qquad \\beta(v)=B \\quad\\text{for every }A\\ge2. \\tag{M2}\n\\]\nThe earlier CF statement that \\(A=2\\) supplies no \\(e(y)=0\\) was correct\nwithout the additional minimal-\\(h\\) hypothesis, but is strengthened here\nunder that hypothesis.\n\n## The case \\(A\\ge2\\) after the upgrade\n\nBy (M2), \\(e(y)=0\\), hence\n\\[\n\\beta(v)=B.\n\\]\nThe path \\(T(v)=3y+2\\) then gives\n\\[\n\\beta(3y+2)=B,\\qquad\n\\beta(6y+4)=B. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe birth at \\(3y\\) gives the opposite middle color\n\\(\\beta(6y+1)=1-B\\), while\n\\[\n\\beta(6y)=B,\\qquad \\beta(6y+2)=\\beta(3y+1)=B. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThus the forced local mask is\n\\[\n\\beta(6y),\\ldots,\\beta(6y+4)\n  =(B,1-B,B,B,B). \\tag{6}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(e(y)=0\\) and \\(e(3y)=1\\) coexist with the two minimality\nconsequences \\(h(t)=h(v)=0\\).\n\nThere is also a new forced zero at the smaller index \\(2y\\). Indeed,\n\\[\n\\beta(4y)=\\beta(2y)=\\beta(y)=B,\\qquad\n\\beta(4y+1)=\\beta(q)=B,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nd(4y)=0,\\qquad d(4y+1)=d(q)=0,\\qquad e(2y)=0. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\), the path \\(2t+1=3y+2\\) and (4) give\n\\[\n\\beta(2t)=\\beta(t)=B,\\qquad \\beta(2t+1)=B,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nd(2t)=0,\\qquad e(t)=0. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nEquations (7)--(8) are genuine additional \\(e\\)-mask zeros at indices\nstrictly below \\(q\\); they do not require treating beta values as free.\n\nThe value of \\(e(q)\\) itself is constrained but not determined. The\nfinite paths\n\\[\n2q\\longmapsto q,\\qquad\n2q+2\\longmapsto q+1\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\beta(2q)=\\beta(q)=B,\\qquad\n\\beta(2q+2)=\\beta(q+1)=B,\n\\]\nwhere \\(d(q)=0\\) follows from (M2) and\n\\(\\beta(q+1)=\\beta(v)=B\\).\nWriting \\(W=\\beta(2q+1)\\), one obtains\n\\[\nd(2q)=|W-B|=d(2q+1),\\qquad\ne(q)=d(2q)d(2q+1)=d(2q)=d(2q+1). \\tag{9}\n\\]\nMinimality does not determine \\(W\\), because \\(2q+1>q\\). Thus this is an\nexact forced mask relation; no actual realization of either value of\n\\(e(q)\\) is asserted.\n\nThe smaller defect \\(v\\) remains only a conditional possibility:\n\\(h(v)=0\\) is forced by minimality, so it cannot serve as a smaller\nnonzero defect. Likewise \\(h(t)=0\\) is forced. The paths and masks above\ntherefore produce no descent from \\(q\\), despite the new lower-index\nbirth zeros.\n\n## The boundary case \\(A=2\\) after the minimality upgrade\n\nHere \\(y=3m\\), so the new PB2 argument uses \\(u=m\\) and proves\n\\(e(y)=0\\), even though CF firstness alone did not. Thus\n\\(\\beta(v)=B\\), and the entire \\(A\\ge3\\) derivation applies unchanged:\n\\[\n\\beta(6y),\\ldots,\\beta(6y+4)=(B,1-B,B,B,B),\n\\]\n\\[\ne(2y)=e(t)=0,\\qquad d(q)=0,\\qquad\ne(q)=d(2q)=d(2q+1). \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe qualification is essential: without minimality of the odd defect,\nCF's original \\(A=2\\) boundary remains unresolved.\n\n## Scope and verdict\n\nThe minimal odd-defect hypothesis adds the exact lower-index facts\n\\(h(t)=h(v)=0\\) and, via PB2, \\(e(y)=0\\) for every \\(A\\ge2\\). It therefore\nforces the local color word \\((B,1-B,B,B,B)\\), \\(e(2y)=e(t)=0\\),\n\\(d(q)=0\\), and the relation \\(e(q)=d(2q)=d(2q+1)\\). The last bit is not\ndetermined by these identities at indices above \\(q\\), so no new arithmetic class\nis excluded and no smaller nonzero defect is produced. The distinction\nfrom CF is exactly that the \\(A=2\\) upgrade uses minimality plus PB2.\nNo global descent, finite-energy estimate, actual nonconstant invariant,\nor full-Collatz conclusion follows.\n\n\n## Appendix: full audit of MINIMUM_DEFECT_BIRTH_CUTOFF (MC1--MC7)\n\nThe complete root file `MINIMUM_DEFECT_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md` was read.\nMC1's finite paths are genuine: with\n\\(r=(3u+1)/2=2^aw\\), \\(p=4u+1\\) reaches \\(r\\) through\n\\(6u+2\\) and \\(3u+1=2r\\), while \\(u\\) reaches \\(r\\) directly.\nThe odd-word identities give the listed \\(C_{a+1},C_{a+2},C_{a+3}\\)\ncolors with positive arguments.\n\nThe merge criterion is correctly applied. If\n\\(3^{a+1}w\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), the two genuine paths merge and force\n\\(C_{a+1}=C_{a+2}\\), contradicting \\(e(3u)=1\\). Hence the event forces\nthe plus residue, after which the next power \\(3^{a+2}w\\equiv3\\pmod4\\)\nmerges \\(C_{a+2}=C_{a+3}\\). This proves the genuine implication\n\\[\n e(3u)=1\\Longrightarrow h(u)=0,\\quad h(4u+1)=1\n\\]\nfor every positive odd \\(u\\). It is correctly distinguished from the\nunguarded PB2 equality, which holds only after the plus selector.\n\nMC2's cutoff is exact: for the least odd \\(h\\)-defect \\(q\\), an event\n\\(e(3u)=1\\) with \\(4u+1\u003cq\\) would create an odd nonzero defect below \\(q\\).\nThe equivalent bound \\(3u\\ge3(q-1)/4\\) has the correct weak endpoint.\nMC3 therefore closes the \\(A=2\\) case when \\(q=4y+1\\), using\n\\(u=y/3\\); this is a minimum-\\(h\\) consequence and is not available in\nthe unqualified CF setup.\n\nMC4--MC6 are consistent. Minimality gives \\(h(t)=h(v)=0\\), MC3 gives\n\\(e(y)=0\\), and the common endpoint gives \\(\\beta(y+1)=B\\). The resulting\nfive-color mask is exactly\n\\[\n(\\beta(6y),\\ldots,\\beta(6y+4))=(B,1-B,B,B,B).\n\\]\nIt yields \\(e(y)=e(2y)=e(t)=0\\) and \\(d(q)=0\\), with no free color\nassignment.\n\nMC7 is also correct:\n\\[\n2q\\to q,\\qquad 2q+2\\to q+1\n\\]\ngive \\(\\beta(2q)=\\beta(2q+2)=B\\), so for\n\\(W=\\beta(2q+1)\\),\n\\[\ne(q)=d(2q)=d(2q+1)=|W-B|.\n\\]\nThe correction in MC5 is accurate: \\(T(2q+1)=3q+2\\), not \\(3q+1\\).\nThe separate identity \\(\\beta(4q+1)=B\\) remains true through\n\\(4q+1\\to6q+2\\to3q+1\\) and \\(T(q)=(3q+1)/2\\), but it is unnecessary\nfor MC7; the earlier suggestion \\(\\beta(4q+1)=W\\) was false.\n\nThe file preserves the required scopes. MC1--MC7 do not assert actual\nrealization of either value of \\(e(q)\\), do not prove finite energy or a\nnew residue exclusion, and do not establish full Collatz. They remove\nonly the \\(A=2\\) boundary under the additional least-odd-defect\nhypothesis.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_MIXED_BLOCK_COMPENSATION.md":"# FOLLOWUP-0431 — накопление в смешанном блоке \\(G_3/H\\)\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная проверка SC без программ, CAS,\nпереборов и Lean. Все формулы ниже арифметические; на каждом переходе\nотдельно сохраняются residue-условие и настоящая цветовая маска.\n\n## Слово режимов и точная аффинная формула\n\nПусть \\(n_0\\) — действительный birth и задано слово длины \\(r\\) с\n\\(b_i\\in\\{3,2\\}\\), где \\(b_i=3\\) означает \\(G_3\\), а \\(b_i=2\\)\nозначает \\(H\\). Положим\n\\[\n B_0=0,\\qquad B_i=\\sum_{h=1}^i b_h,\n\\qquad \\gamma_3=3,\\quad\\gamma_2=5.\n\\]\nАрифметические кандидаты определяются рекурсией\n\\[\n n_i=\\frac{9n_{i-1}+\\gamma_{b_i}}{2^{b_i}}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nЕё точное раскрытие:\n\\[\n n_r=\n \\frac{9^r n_0+\n \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^r\n 9^{\\,r-i}\\gamma_{b_i}2^{B_{i-1}}}\n {2^{B_r}}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nЭто накопленный аффинный член; порядок \\(G_3/H\\) входит через\n\\(B_{i-1}\\) и \\(\\gamma_{b_i}\\).\nЕсли в слове \\(g\\) букв \\(G_3\\) и \\(h\\) букв \\(H\\), то\n\\(B_r=3g+2h\\), поэтому главный множитель \\(9^r/2^{B_r}\\)\nзависит только от чисел \\(g,h\\), а не от их порядка. Порядок меняет\nименно накопленный аффинный член (2).\n\nДля среднего \\(\\mu_i=2n_i+1\\) обе карты имеют один и тот же numerator:\n\\[\n \\mu_i=\\frac{9\\mu_{i-1}+5}{2^{b_i}}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nПоэтому\n\\[\n \\mu_r=\n \\frac{9^r\\mu_0+\n 5\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^r9^{\\,r-i}2^{B_{i-1}}}\n {2^{B_r}},\n\\qquad \\mu_0=2n_0+1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nи \\(n_r=(\\mu_r-1)/2\\). Формула (4) даёт требуемый накопленный\nmiddle-affine term\n\\[\n S_r(b_1,\\ldots,b_r)=\n \\sum_{i=1}^r9^{\\,r-i}2^{B_{i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nИнтегральность конечной правой части (2) или (4) сама по себе\nнедостаточна: каждый prefix \\(n_i\\) должен быть целым и лежать в\nнужном классе. Для действительного retained \\(G_3\\) вход обязан иметь\n\\(n_{i-1}\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\), output должен удовлетворять\n\\[\n e(n_i)=1,\\qquad d(n_i)=d(n_i+1)=0.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nДля действительного retained \\(H\\) вход обязан иметь\n\\(n_{i-1}\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), output должен удовлетворять\n\\[\n e(n_i)=1,\\qquad d(n_i)=0.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nИменно (5)--(6), а не наличие целых \\(n_i\\), разрешают продолжать слово.\nВ middle-переменной это соответственно \\(\\mu_{i-1}\\equiv27\\pmod{32}\\)\nдля \\(G_3\\) и \\(\\mu_{i-1}\\equiv7\\pmod8\\) для \\(H\\); эти более сильные\nresidue-условия включают арифметическую делимость в (3).\n\n## Средняя орбита\n\nДля одного retained перехода SC даёт\n\\[\n \\mu_i=\n \\begin{cases}\n (9\\mu_{i-1}+5)/8,&G_3,\\\\[2mm]\n (9\\mu_{i-1}+5)/4,&H.\n \\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nЭто ровно \\(T^3(\\mu_{i-1})\\) в первом случае и \\(T^2(\\mu_{i-1})\\)\nво втором, при соответствующих parity words. Для слова \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_r\\)\nформула (4) является одной общей affine-композицией; нельзя заменять её\nформальной целочисленностью утверждение, что все промежуточные middle\nцвета и birth-маски существуют.\n\nКаждая из карт увеличивает положительный birth-index:\n\\[\n G_3(n)-n=\\frac{n+3}{8}>0,\\qquad\n H(n)-n=\\frac{5(n+1)}4>0.\n\\]\nПоэтому \\(n_r>n_0\\) для непустого блока. Это не даёт меньшего birth,\nпока не предъявлена активная boundary ancestry.\n\n## Точный критерий ancestry-компенсации\n\nПусть после арифметического блока найден действительный active boundary\n\\(K\\), и пусть\n\\[\n d(K)=d(\\lfloor K/2\\rfloor)=\\cdots\n =d\\!\\left(\\left\\lfloor\\frac K{2^{\\ell-1}}\\right\\rfloor\\right)=1,\n\\qquad\n d\\!\\left(\\left\\lfloor\\frac K{2^\\ell}\\right\\rfloor\\right)=0.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nТогда каноническая ancestry даёт birth\n\\[\n a_*=\\left\\lfloor\\frac K{2^\\ell}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nОн строго ниже первоначального \\(n_0\\) тогда и только тогда, когда\n\\[\n K\u003c2^\\ell n_0.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nЭто точное условие, включая strict inequality; оно не заменяется\nусловием, что \\(K\\) просто меньше какого-либо позднего block output.\n\nЕсли первый active boundary равен \\(K=n_r+\\varepsilon\\), где\n\\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) определяется настоящей failed output mask,\nто (10) эквивалентно\n\\[\n \\mu_r+2\\varepsilon\u003c2^{\\ell+1}n_0+1,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nили, после подстановки (4),\n\\[\n 9^r(2n_0+1)+5S_r\n \u003c\n 2^{B_r}\\bigl(2^{\\ell+1}n_0+1-2\\varepsilon\\bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nФормула (12) — проверяемый критерий компенсации для конкретного слова,\nконкретной failed mask и конкретной depth \\(\\ell\\). Если первый active\nboundary является родителем или иным boundary, он должен быть подставлен\nв (10) непосредственно; арифметический \\(n_r\\) один его не определяет.\n\nДля SC H-ladder при первом нечётном failed output известно лишь\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\), поскольку output и его parent активны; точная \\(\\ell\\)\nзадаётся первым нулевым \\(d\\) глубже по ancestry. Поэтому (10) даёт\n\\(K\u003c2^\\ell n_0\\), а не фиксированное \\(K\u003c4n_0\\). На первом переходе\nreset-семьи нижняя информация \\(\\ell\\ge2\\) уже достаточна для первого\nкритерия \\(K\u003c4n_0\\), но точная глубина не равна обязательно \\(2\\);\nна поздних\nпереходах это автоматически не так. При конечном чётном output сначала\nнужно решить, является ли он birth: если \\(e(K)=0\\), BR2 к нему\nнеприменим; надо рассмотреть его родителя и его boundary, как в SC3.\n\n## Почему универсальный закон компенсации не получен\n\nДля слова длины \\(r\\)\n\\[\n 2r\\le B_r\\le3r,\\qquad\n \\frac{9^r n_0}{2^{B_r}}\u003cn_r,\n\\]\nа положительный \\(S_r\\) дополнительно увеличивает output. Чистые\n \\(H\\)-блоки имеют основной multiplier \\(9/4\\) на шаг, чистые \\(G_3\\)\n блоки — \\(9/8\\). Для смешанного слова главный multiplier зависит только\nот числа букв каждого типа через \\(B_r\\); порядок меняет накопленный\nаффинный член через \\(S_r\\). Чтобы ancestry гарантированно вернулась ниже \\(n_0\\),\nнужно structural lower bound на \\(\\ell\\), удовлетворяющий (12).\n\nSC даёт только локальные bounds: например, у первого нечётного failed\n\\(H\\)-output известно \\(\\ell\\ge2\\), а у \\(G_3\\)-failure boundary может быть\n\\(n_r\\) или \\(n_r+1\\) и глубина зависит от actual mask. Нет доказанного\nзакона, который связывал бы эту глубину с \\(B_r\\) так, чтобы (12)\nвыполнялось для каждого admissible mixed word. Равенство среднего\n\\(\\mu_r\\) формально описывает накопление, но не создаёт такую глубину.\n\nИтак, арифметический блок имеет точную формулу (2)--(4), а его\nкомпенсация имеет точный критерий (8)--(12). Универсального закона\n\\(G_3/H\\)-компенсации в принятом SC-разборе не получено: остаётся\nнезакрытой именно совместная задача о действительных output masks,\nпервой active boundary и её depth относительно исходного \\(n_0\\).\n\n## Проверка SC-кодировки и оценки arithmetic growth core\n\nПусть \\(\\Gamma\\) обозначает множество стартов, для которых частичная\nкарта с доменами \\(H:n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) и\n\\(G_3:n\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\) допускает бесконечный arithmetic growth\npath. Настоящие birth-маски могут только уменьшить это множество.\nКаждый endpoint бесконечного path снова нечётен, поскольку оба домена\nсодержат только нечётные числа.\n\nДля слова \\(w\\in\\{H,G_3\\}^r\\) пусть \\(B(w)\\) — сумма длин его middle\nparity words: \\(H\\) даёт \\(11\\) и длину \\(2\\), \\(G_3\\) даёт \\(110\\) и\nдлину \\(3\\). К слову из \\(B(w)\\) битов добавляется terminal \\(11\\),\nвыражающий нечётность последнего endpoint. Получается parity word длины\n\\(B(w)+2\\) для \\(\\mu_0=2n_0+1\\). Для любой фиксированной parity word\nобычная обратная affine-рекурсия фиксирует ровно один класс \\(\\mu_0\\)\nпо модулю \\(2^{B(w)+2}\\); следовательно, \\(n_0\\) лежит в одном классе\nпо модулю \\(2^{B(w)+1}\\). Это лишь кандидатный класс: actual residues\nи цветовые guards могут его удалить; для последующей верхней оценки\nдоказывать непересечение классов разных слов не требуется.\n\nПри \\(g\\) букв \\(G_3\\) и \\(h=r-g\\) букв \\(H\\)\n\\(B(w)=3g+2h\\). Поэтому для \\(X\\ge1\\) число стартов, допускающих\nхотя бы один такой \\(r\\)-префикс, оценивается сверху\n\\[\n M_r(X)\\le\n \\sum_{w\\in\\{H,G_3\\}^r}\n \\left(\\frac{X}{2^{B(w)+1}}+1\\right)\n =\n \\frac X2\\left(\\frac14+\\frac18\\right)^r+2^r\n =\n \\frac X2\\left(\\frac38\\right)^r+2^r.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nЗдесь порядок букв влияет на конкретный affine term, но не на\n\\(B(w)=3g+2h\\), если \\(g,h\\) фиксированы.\n\nЕсли \\(2^r\\le\\sqrt X\u003c2^{r+1}\\), то\n\\[\n \\frac X2\\left(\\frac38\\right)^r\n \\le\\frac{X}{2^{r+1}}\u003c\\sqrt X,\n\\]\nгде первое неравенство использует \\(3/8\\le1/2\\), а второе —\n\\(2^r>\\sqrt X/2\\). Вместе с \\(2^r\\le\\sqrt X\\) это даёт\n\\[\n \\#(\\Gamma\\cap[1,X])\\le M_r(X)\\le2\\sqrt X.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nЭто действительно верхняя оценка: любой infinite path проходит каждый\nвыбранный \\(r\\)-префикс, тогда как в \\(M_r\\) учитываются даже классы без\nпроверенных birth-масок.\n\nИз (14) следует интегральная гармоническая оценка через summation by\nparts:\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\Gamma\\\\ n\\le X}}\\frac1n\n =\\frac{N_\\Gamma(X)}X+\n \\int_1^X\\frac{N_\\Gamma(t)}{t^2}\\,dt\n \\le\\frac{2}{\\sqrt X}\n +2\\int_1^X t^{-3/2}\\,dt\n \\le4.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nПереход \\(X\\to\\infty\\) даёт \\(\\sum_{n\\in\\Gamma}1/n\\le4\\). Это\nарифметический/core bound; он не устанавливает существование любой\nконкретной infinite actual-birth path и не превращает конечный block\nв реализованный цветовой маршрут.\n\n## Аудит MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE, BC1--BC10\n\nBC1 совпадает с (1)--(4). Дополнительно композиция каждого шага имеет\nвид\n\\[\n F_i(n)=\\lambda_i n+(\\lambda_i-1)c_i,\\qquad\n c_H=1,\\quad c_{G_3}=3.\n\\]\nПосле композиции\n\\[\n n_r=\\lambda_w(n_0+c_w)-c_w,\\qquad 1\\le c_w\\le3,\n\\]\nпоскольку коэффициенты при \\(c_i\\) положительны и в сумме равны\n\\(\\lambda_w-1\\). Поэтому рост \\(\\lambda_w>1\\) точен, но это не ранг\nдля actual-birth процесса. При фиксированных количествах \\(H,G_3\\)\n\\(\\lambda_w\\) зависит только от этих количеств; порядок меняет\nаффинный центр \\(c_w\\) и \\(S_r\\).\n\nBC2 верен с точной глубиной \\(\\ell\\): два активных уровня дают только\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\), а первый нулевой \\(d\\) определяет фактическое \\(\\ell\\).\nУсловие выхода ниже исходного birth ровно \\(K\u003c2^\\ell n_0\\), с\nразвёрнутой формой (12). Для конечного чётного output при \\(e(K)=0\\)\nBR2 к нему не применяется; сначала нужен родительский случай SC3.\n\nBC3--BC4 проверены как арифметическое утверждение о частичной карте\n\\(F\\). Для \\(\\Gamma\\), состоящей из бесконечных \\(F\\)-цепей, каждый\nendpoint нечётен, поэтому длина-\\(r\\) mode-word \\(W\\), дополненная\nterminal \\(11\\), действительно является необходимым parity guard.\nH даёт \\(11\\), G3 — \\(110\\); соседний первый бит и terminal suffix\nобеспечивают соответственно \\(111\\) и \\(11011\\), то есть точные\nдомены \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) и \\(n\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\). Обратная parity\nрекурсия даёт ровно один класс по модулю \\(2^{B(w)+1}\\) для \\(n\\).\nЭто не требует конечных исключений: возможные невалидные masks только\nудаляют кандидатов.\n\nИз этого BC7 действительно следует\n\\[\n \\#(\\Gamma\\cap[1,X])\n \\le \\frac X2\\left(\\frac38\\right)^r+2^r\n \\le2\\sqrt X\n\\]\nпри \\(2^r\\le\\sqrt X\u003c2^{r+1}\\). BC9 затем даёт полной массой\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\in\\Gamma}\\frac1n\n =\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{N_\\Gamma(t)}{t^2}\\,dt\n \\le4.\n\\]\nЭто верхняя оценка arithmetic core без утверждения \\(\\Gamma=\\varnothing\\)\nили \\(\\Gamma\\ne\\varnothing\\).\n\nBC10 также корректен. Для фиксированного \\(\\beta\\) actual infinite-growth\nbirths \\(I_\\beta\\) лежат в \\(\\Gamma\\), а \\(R_\\beta\\) состоит из birth\nстартов, где процедура останавливается на конечном growing block.\nИмеем без PD\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)-E_{R_\\beta}(X)\n =\\sum_{\\substack{n\\le X\\\\n\\in I_\\beta}}\\frac1n\n \\le4.\n\\]\nСледовательно, divergence \\(E_\\beta\\) эквивалентна divergence\n\\(E_{R_\\beta}\\), но BC10 не даёт верхней оценки для самого\n\\(E_{R_\\beta}\\).\n\nНаконец, фиксированное множество \\(S_r\\) конечного \\(r\\) не имеет\nконечной гармонической массы: оно содержит, при наличии кандидатов,\nарифметические прогрессии, и каждая отдельная прогрессия уже имеет\nрасходящийся ряд \\(\\sum1/n\\). Конечная масса возникает только после\nпересечения всех длин, то есть для \\(\\Gamma\\) через равномерную\n\\(\\sqrt X\\)-оценку; её нельзя приписывать конечному block-prefix \\(S_r\\).\n\n## Аудит BC11--BC13: фактический masked-growth graph\n\nФормулы BC11 точны:\n\\[\n H(4t+3)=9t+8\\equiv8\\pmod9,\\qquad\n G_3(16t+13)=18t+15\\equiv6\\pmod9.\n\\]\nОбе ветви инъективны на своих доменах, а образы не пересекаются.\nПоэтому после ограничения на actual births и retained output masks\nin-degree и out-degree графа не превосходят единицы. Все рёбра строго\nувеличивают индекс, так что обратная цепь конечна; каждый компонент\nимеет единственный origin и является конечной либо односторонней\nбесконечной цепью. Это граф роста, а не обычная orbit-цепь \\(T\\).\n\nОценка BC12 также верна: каждая growth-карта удовлетворяет\n\\(F(n)>(9/8)n\\), поэтому для конечной цепи\n\\(n_j\\ge(9/8)^j a\\) и\n\\[\n \\sum_{j=0}^{L}\\frac1{n_j}\n \\le\\frac1a\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\left(\\frac89\\right)^j\n =\\frac9a.\n\\]\nЕсли \\(A_\\beta\\) — origins конечных компонентов, то origins являются\nbirths и принадлежат \\(R_\\beta\\). Все vertices конечного компонента\nтакже принадлежат \\(R_\\beta\\), а разные компоненты не пересекаются.\nСледовательно, для каждого \\(X\\)\n\\[\n E_A(X)\\le E_R(X)\\le9E_A(X),\\qquad\n E_A(X)\\le E_\\beta(X)\\le9E_A(X)+4.\n\\]\nЭто убирает кратность потенциальных mode-words именно в bookkeeping\nфактического графа. Оно не даёт верхней оценки для \\(E_A\\): terminal\nsmaller-birth transfer может вести в тот же или другой компонент, но\ndecreasing order origins не доказан.\n\nАудит подтверждает BC10: \\(I_\\beta\\subset\\Gamma\\), а\n\\(0\\le E_\\beta-E_R\\le4\\) следует из полной массы \\(\\Gamma\\), без PD.\nПри этом \\(S_r\\) для фиксированного конечного \\(r\\), если непусто,\nсодержит целые арифметические классы и само имеет расходящуюся\nгармоническую массу; конечность относится к \\(\\Gamma\\), то есть к\nпересечению всех префиксных условий, а не к отдельному \\(S_r\\).\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_MOD16_CHARGE_FILTER.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — mod-16 filter for last-change charge weights\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual audit of the proposed TL6\nresidue refinement. No programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean or numerical\nexperiments were used.\n\n## Selector orbit\n\nFor \\(u\\) odd, write \\(r=(3u+1)/2=2^a w\\), \\(w\\) odd. The SP selector\nis plus exactly when \\(3^{a+2}w\\equiv3\\pmod4\\). For\n\\[\nu\\equiv1,3,9,11\\pmod{16},\n\\]\ndirect reduction gives:\n\\[\nu\\equiv1\\pmod{16}\\Rightarrow r\\equiv2\\pmod8,\\ a=1,\\ w\\equiv1\\pmod4,\n\\]\nso the selector is plus, while \\(u\\equiv3,9,11\\pmod{16}\\) gives the\nminus selector. Hence on the multiplication-by-three orbit\n\\(\\{1,3,9,11\\}\\pmod{16}\\), plus occurs exactly at \\(u\\equiv1\\pmod{16}\\).\n\nFor a last-change branch \\(u=3^{k-1}t\\), this gives the first allowed\n\\(k\\) and its step-four progression:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nt\\bmod16&k\\bmod4&K\\\\ \\hline\n1&1&5\\\\\n3&0&4\\\\\n9&3&3\\\\\n11&2&2\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe lower bounds \\(K\\) incorporate \\(k\\ge2\\). This filter is only for\nthe displayed four residue classes; the other odd classes modulo \\(16\\)\nare not covered by this calculation.\n\n## Geometric charge and cutoff\n\nLet \\(v=v_3(t)\\). For \\(k=K+4j\\), the complete PB fibre attached to\n\\(u=3^{k-1}t\\) is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(3/4)^{v+k}}{3^{k-1}t}.\n\\]\nAt source cutoff \\(4u+1\\le X\\), each such branch satisfies\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{X-1}{4\\cdot3^{k-1}}\n \\le\\frac{X-1}{4\\cdot3^{K-1}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nDropping the sharper \\(j\\)-dependent cutoff only enlarges the nonnegative\nsum and introduces no \\(O(1)\\) error.\n\nThe full \\(j\\ge0\\) sum is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_K(v)\n&=\\sum_{j\\ge0}3^{1-K-4j}\n  \\bigl[1-(3/4)^{v+K+4j}\\bigr]\\\\\n&=\\frac{3^{5-K}}{80}\n -\\frac{3\\cdot4^{4-K}}{255}(3/4)^v. \\tag{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe ratio in the first sum is \\(3^{-4}=1/81\\); in the second it is\n\\((3/4)^4/3^4=1/256\\). Explicitly:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nK&C_K(v)\\\\ \\hline\n2&27/80-(16/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n3&9/80-(4/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n4&3/80-(1/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n5&1/80-(1/340)(3/4)^v\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nTherefore the filtered nonprimitive bundle mass is bounded, at common\ncutoff \\(X\\), by\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{t\\le(X-1)/(4\\cdot3^{K-1})\\\\\nt\\ {\\rm odd},\\ t\\bmod16\\ {\\rm in\\ the\\ selected\\ class}}}\n C_K(v_3(t))\\,\\frac{h(t)}t,\n\\]\nwith the corresponding \\(K\\) from the table. Actual last-change and birth\nmasks can only reduce this arithmetic upper bound.\n\n## Joint coefficients for \\(v_3(t)\\ge1\\)\n\nFor \\(t\\equiv1\\pmod{16}\\), the direct selector is plus, so its direct\ncoefficient is \\(1-(3/4)^{v+1}\\). For \\(t\\equiv3,9,11\\pmod{16}\\), it is\nminus, so the direct N-minus coefficient is \\(3/4\\). Adding the filtered\n\\(C_K(v)\\) gives:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nt\\bmod16&\\text{joint coefficient}\\\\ \\hline\n1&81/80-(64/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n3&63/80-(1/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n9&69/80-(4/85)(3/4)^v\\\\\n11&87/80-(16/85)(3/4)^v\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor \\(v=0\\), no direct \\(A_\\pm\\) term is present and only \\(C_K(0)\\)\napplies. The first coefficient is \\(\u003c1\\) only for sufficiently small\n\\(v\\); the \\(t\\equiv11\\) coefficient already exceeds \\(1\\) for large\n\\(v\\). Thus this is a residue-sensitive upper bound, not a contraction.\n\nThe exact source cutoff, the arithmetic multiplicities, and the two\ngeometric ratios are correct. The remaining \\(5,7,13,15\\bmod16\\) orbits,\nthe primitive ternary birth mass, and the actual-color masks are\nuncontrolled. No finite \\(G\\), finite \\(E_9\\), or full Collatz conclusion\nfollows.\n\n\n## Финальный аудит полного RF1--RF6\n\nПолный \\`RESIDUE_FILTERED_LAST_CHANGE_WEIGHTS.md\\` прочитан. RF1--RF2\nкорректно удерживают arithmetic selector: на орбите\n\\(\\{1,3,9,11\\}\\bmod16\\) plus бывает только при \\(u\\equiv1\\bmod16\\).\nRF3 правильно относится только к indirect nonprimitive contribution:\nего cutoff\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{X-1}{4\\cdot3^{K(t)-1}}\n\\]\nполучен из первого PB-source \\(p=1+4\\cdot3^{k-1}t\\). Он не должен\nнакладываться на direct \\(A_\\pm\\)-mass, и RF5 именно это различие\nсохраняет.\n\nThe recovery of actual fibres is sound: \\(v_2(n_\\ell-1)=2\\ell+2\\)\nrecovers \\(\\ell\\), the affine formula recovers \\(u\\), and the actual\nlast-change rule recovers \\(t,k\\). Dropping the remaining birth masks and\nthe sharper \\(\\ell\\)-dependent cutoff enlarges a nonnegative finite-cutoff\nsum; no infinite-energy assumption is hidden.\n\nRF3's geometric formula and the four \\(C_K(v)\\) values are correct.\nRF4's \\(v=0\\) branch correctly has no direct \\(A_\\pm\\) term, while\n\\(v\\ge1\\) combines the selector-exclusive direct coefficient with the\nindirect \\(C_K(v)\\). The displayed joint coefficients in RF4 are exactly\nthe sums, and the class-3 and class-9 subunit observations include the\n\\(v=0\\) case. RF5 correctly extends positive bounds to a common \\(t\\le X\\)\ncutoff only after separating the indirect support condition.\n\nRF5's signed table is also consistent with TL5: direct plus retention has\n\\(g(4t+1)=g(t)\\), while a nonprimitive last-change source has\n\\(g(4\\cdot3^{k-1}t+1)=-g(t)\\). The fibres are distinct, so opposite signs\ndo not cancel the positive harmonic upper bound. No actual simultaneous\nrealization is claimed.\n\nFinal scope is correct: the filter improves selected indirect classes but\ndoes not cover the other mod-16 orbit, primitive \\(E_9\\)-mass, or all\nactual masks. RF5 supplies no global contraction or finite-\\(G\\) proof.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_ORIGIN_MASKS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — exact masks for the primitive class \\(P_1\\)\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). This is a bounded manual\naudit of the residue-one birth class. No mathematical program, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean run, or sub-agent was used. The exact binary invariant\n\\(\\beta\\) is treated abstractly; no nonconstant invariant is constructed.\n\n## 1. Definitions and the residue-one class\n\nWrite\n\n\\[\n d(j)=|\\beta(j+1)-\\beta(j)|,\n \\qquad e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1).\n\\]\n\nA birth satisfies\n\n\\[\n \\beta(n)=\\beta(n+1)=c,\n \\qquad \\beta(3n+2)=1-c.                         \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe arithmetic images of the two retained maps are \\(0\\pmod3\\) and\n\\(2\\pmod3\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n P_1=\\{n:e(n)=1,\\ n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\}\n\\]\n\nis the primitive arithmetic-origin class. This statement concerns the\narithmetic graph; it does not say that a member of \\(P_1\\) has no smaller\nbirth in the binary ancestry tree.\n\nFrom (1), every birth has\n\n\\[\n \\beta(2n)=c,\n \\quad \\beta(2n+1)=1-c,\n \\quad d(2n)=d(2n+1)=1,\n \\quad d(n)=0.                                      \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe known identity \\(e(4m+2)=0\\) is exact: for \\(n=4m+2\\),\n\n\\[\n \\beta(n)=\\beta(2m+1)=\\beta(3m+2)\n =\\beta(12m+8)=\\beta(3n+2),\n\\]\n\nso (1) fails. Consequently every even birth is \\(n=4m\\).\n\n## 2. The proposed lift \\(n\\mapsto4n+1\\) always fails\n\nFor arbitrary \\(n\\), exact invariance gives\n\n\\[\n \\beta(4n+1)=\\beta(3n+1),\\quad\n \\beta(4n+2)=\\beta(2n+1),\\quad\n \\beta(12n+5)=\\beta(9n+4).                          \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence, writing \\(n'=4n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n e(n')=1\n \\iff\n \\beta(3n+1)=\\beta(2n+1)\\ne\\beta(9n+4).            \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(n\\) be a birth of color \\(c\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, then \\(3n+1=2T(n)\\), so\n  \\(\\beta(3n+1)=\\beta(n)=c\\), whereas\n  \\(\\beta(2n+1)=\\beta(3n+2)=1-c\\). Therefore\n  \\(d(4n+1)=1\\), and (4) gives \\(e(4n+1)=0\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, write \\(n=4m\\). Then\n  \\[\n   \\beta(3n+1)=\\beta(12m+1)=\\beta(9m+1),\n   \\qquad\n   \\beta(9n+4)=\\beta(36m+4)=\\beta(9m+1).\n  \\]\n  The first and third colors in (4) coincide, so again \\(e(4n+1)=0\\).\n\nThus the exact conclusion is\n\n\\[\n                    e(n)=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e(4n+1)=0. \n                                                               \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(n\\mapsto4n+1\\) supplies no retained lift for \\(P_1\\),\nor for any other birth class. In the odd case it supplies the stronger\nactive-boundary identity\n\n\\[\n d(4n+1)=d(3n+1)=1.                                  \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor an even birth the value \\(d(3n+1)\\) is not determined by (1); the\nfailure of the lift instead follows from the equality of the two colors\nin (3). This distinction matters: no extra color value is being assumed.\n\n## 3. The exact odd \\(P_1\\) alternative through \\(3n+2\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\in P_1\\) be odd. Write \\(n=6k+1\\), with \\(k\\ge1\\) because\n\\(e(1)=0\\), and put\n\n\\[\n S(n)=\\frac{3n+1}{2}=9k+2.                          \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe birth mask gives \\(\\beta(3n+1)=c\\) and\n\\(\\beta(3n+2)=1-c\\). Since \\(3n+1=2S(n)\\), the XOR identity at \\(S(n)\\)\nand the odd-step identity give\n\n\\[\n e(S(n))=1-d(S(n)).                                  \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore exactly the following local alternative holds:\n\n1. if \\(d(S(n))=0\\), then \\(S(n)=9k+2\\) is a larger birth in the\n   \\(2\\pmod3\\) image and the \\(S\\)-edge is retained;\n2. if \\(d(S(n))=1\\), then \\(S(n)\\) is an active boundary and its nearest\n   birth ancestor \\(b\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n     b\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{S(n)}2\\right\\rfloor\n      =\\left\\lfloor\\frac{9k+2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn.   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\nThis is an exact mask-sensitive alternative, but it is the already-known\nodd-\\(S\\) local rule specialized to \\(P_1\\); it is not a new global\ndescent theorem. The first branch can occur at a larger index, so (8)\nalone cannot bound all primitive starts by smaller births.\n\n## 4. Even \\(P_1\\) births have a smaller-birth/active-boundary exit\n\nLet \\(n=4m\\in P_1\\) have color \\(c\\). The exact even-birth identities are\n\n\\[\n \\beta(m)=\\beta(3m+1)=c,\n \\qquad \\beta(6m+1)=1-c.                            \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(K=3m\\). Then the standard R-mask calculation gives\n\n\\[\n e(K)=1-d(K).                                        \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(d(K)=0\\), \\(K\\) itself is a birth and \\(K\u003cn\\). If \\(d(K)=1\\), its\nnearest birth ancestor \\(b\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac K2\\right\\rfloor\u003cn.          \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus every even member of \\(P_1\\) has a strictly smaller birth available\nthrough this \\(R\\)-exit, either directly or through the active-boundary\nancestor. This is a genuine smaller-birth statement, but it does not by\nitself give a bounded-multiplicity energy estimate for all even \\(P_1\\)\nmembers: several distinct \\(K\\)'s can be charged along the two ancestry\npaths of one birth.\n\nFor completeness, the proposed lift is still excluded here by the\nsecond case of §2. In terms of the unresolved color\n\\(\\xi=\\beta(9m+1)\\), both \\(\\beta(3n+1)\\) and \\(\\beta(9n+4)\\) equal\n\\(\\xi\\); no value of \\(\\xi\\) is needed.\n\n## 5. What remains genuinely primitive\n\nThe exact arguments reduce the unresolved universal-smaller-birth question\nfor \\(P_1\\) to odd starts. They do not by themselves remove even members\nfrom the arithmetic-origin energy \\(E_{P_1}\\). For an odd start, (5) rules out the suggested lift\n\\(4n+1\\), while (8) gives either a larger retained \\(S\\)-birth or a\nsmaller ordinary birth. The larger branch is compatible with all known\nidentities and can therefore not be discarded as a descent.\n\nNo uniform bound for \\(E_{P_1}(X)\\), no injective charging map with a\nproved bounded multiplicity, and no universal smaller-birth theorem for\nodd \\(P_1\\) follows from these masks. In particular, the exact equality\n\\(\\beta(3n+2)=1-\\beta(n)\\) supplies the active boundary \\(3n+1\\) in the\nodd case, but does not determine \\(d(S(n))\\). A claim that it forces the\nsecond branch of (8) would be an unsupported extra color assumption.\n\nThe report therefore proves the universal lift exclusion (5), the exact\nodd \\(P_1\\) alternative (8)--(9), and the even \\(P_1\\) \\(R\\)-exit\n(11)--(12). The remaining odd primitive mask is explicitly identified;\nno actual counterexample, nonconstant invariant, or global impossibility\nis asserted.\n\n## 6. Audit of the proposed even-birth mass estimate\n\nFor a positive even birth \\(n=4m\\), put \\(q=6m+1\\). The even-birth\nidentities give \\(d(q)=1\\), \\(q\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), and\n\n\\[\n \\frac1n=\\frac{3}{2q}+\\frac{3}{2q(q-1)}.              \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(q\\)'s are distinct and \\(q\\ge7\\). Hence the total correction is\n\n\\[\n \\sum_q\\frac{3}{2q(q-1)}\n \\le \\frac32\\sum_{q=7}^{\\infty}\\frac1{q(q-1)}\n =\\frac14.                                           \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe proposed potential vector \\(V_0=1,V_1=2,V_2=1/4\\) is a valid\nsupersolution for the main weights \\(3/(2q)\\) on odd active boundaries:\n\n\\[\n V_i\\ge\\frac32\\,\\mathbf1_{i=1}\n +\\max\\left(\\frac{V_{2i+1}}2,\\frac{V_{i+1}}4\\right),\n\\]\n\nwith equality in all three residue classes. This part of the candidate\nis correct.\n\nThe potential already includes the reward \\(3/2\\) at every charged\nresidue-one boundary. If the first odd boundary on the left has depth\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\), it is \\(q_L=2^\\ell b+1\\). Applying the same potential to\nall later charged boundaries therefore gives the safe bound\n\n\\[\n \\frac{V_{q_L\\bmod3}}{q_L}\n \\le \\frac{V_{q_L\\bmod3}}{2^\\ell b}.                 \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nTaking all allowed \\(\\ell\\), rather than only one favorable depth, gives\nthe left coefficients\n\n\\[\n (C_{L,0},C_{L,1},C_{L,2})\n =\\left(\\frac12,\\frac18,\\frac14\\right).              \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor example, when \\(b\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) and \\(\\ell=2\\), \\(q_L=4b+1\\) has\nresidue \\(1\\), so (15) gives \\(2/(4b)=1/(2b)\\). When\n\\(b\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), the odd depths \\(\\ell=3\\) give residue \\(0\\) and\ncoefficient \\(1/8\\); when \\(b\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), \\(\\ell=2\\) gives residue\n\\(0\\) and coefficient \\(1/4\\). These arithmetic paths are only\npossibilities of the boundary grammar here; no actual color realization\nis asserted.\n\nThe right path starts at \\(q_R=2b+1\\). The same calculation gives\n\n\\[\n (C_{R,0},C_{R,1},C_{R,2})\n =\\left(1,\\frac12,\\frac18\\right),                    \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q_R\\) has residues \\(1,0,2\\), respectively, for\n\\(b\\equiv0,1,2\\pmod3\\). Therefore the grammar alone yields the valid\nuniform classwise main coefficients\n\n\\[\n (C_0,C_1,C_2)\n =\\left(\\frac32,\\frac58,\\frac38\\right),               \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nwith the correction \\(1/4\\) from (14).\n\nFor even births \\(n\\le X\\), \\(q\\le3X/2+1\\), and the nearest birth\nancestor of the active \\(q\\) satisfies\n\\(b\\le\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor(3X+2)/4\\rfloor=:Y\\).\nThus the justified conclusion is\n\n\\[\n E_{\\rm even}(X)\n \\le \\frac32 E_\\beta(Y;b\\equiv0\\!\\!\\pmod3)\n   +\\frac58 E_\\beta(Y;b\\equiv1\\!\\!\\pmod3)\n   +\\frac38 E_\\beta(Y;b\\equiv2\\!\\!\\pmod3)\n   +\\frac14.                                         \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThe proposed coefficients are therefore valid consequences of the WS\nodd-boundary grammar and the displayed potential. The earlier version of\nthis audit multiplied \\(V_i/q\\) by \\(3/2\\) once more; that was an\novercount, because the \\(3/2\\) reward is already present in the\nsupersolution. Formula (19) is an analytic upper bound for the\neven-birth mass, but it is not a contraction and does not resolve the odd\n\\(P_1\\) primitive class.\n\n## 7. Full audit of PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS\n\nThe complete root file PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md was\nread. PC1 is consistent with the already proved masks: even births are\n\\(4m\\), the boundary \\(q=6m+1\\) is odd, active and \\(1\\pmod3\\), and the\nlift \\(n\\mapsto4n+1\\) is excluded.\n\nPC2--PC6 check as follows. The decomposition\n\\[\n 1/(4m)=3/(2q)+3/[2q(q-1)]\n\\]\nand the \\(1/4\\) correction use only distinct \\(q\\ge7\\). The potential\n\\(W=(1,2,1/4)\\) satisfies all three PC4 equalities and already includes\nthe \\(3/2\\) reward. The right-path coefficients \\(1,1/2,1/8\\), the\nleft-path coefficients \\(1/2,1/8,1/4\\), and PC6's\n\\((3/2,5/8,3/8)\\) therefore have the correct factors and cover all even\nbirths, including internal vertices. The cutoff\n\\(Y=\\lfloor(3X+2)/4\\rfloor\\) follows from \\(q\\le3X/2+1\\) and\n\\(b\\le\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor\\).\n\nThe Dirichlet identities PC7--PC10 are valid for \\(s>1\\): the \\(S/R\\)\nimages are disjoint, correction \\(C_s\\) is absolutely bounded, and no\nfinite-component assumption is used. They must not be read as\ndifferences of divergent harmonic sums at \\(s=1\\). PC11 and PC12 are\ncorrect normalized subsequential balances under their stated hypotheses.\nThe PC12 mass allocation is explicitly only a feasible assignment to\nselected scalar equations; it is not a beta-realization, an actual birth\nconfiguration, or a Collatz counterexample.\n\nThe fixed-\\(k\\) prefix estimate PC13 and terminal transport PC14 are\nvalid. Their consequence requires the conditional hypothesis that\nterminal (or origin) Dirichlet mass is negligible relative to \\(E_s\\).\nIt permits a subcritical divergence and does not prove finite energy.\nPC6 therefore improves the full even-subfamily estimate while leaving the\nodd \\(P_1\\) primitive mask and the global contraction problem open.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_TERNARY_DESCENT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — primitive ternary births: residue descent and remaining mask\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual bounded analysis only. This report\nstarts from the corrected TL1--TL9 audit and studies primitive ternary births.\nNo programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean or numerical experiment were used.\n\n## Primitive mask\n\nLet \\(z=3^a m\\) be an odd actual birth with \\(a\\ge2\\), \\(m\\) odd and\n\\(3\\nmid m\\). The primitive condition is\n\\[\n\\beta(m)=\\beta(3m)=\\cdots=\\beta(3^a m)=B,\n\\qquad\n\\beta(2\\cdot3^a m+1)=1-B. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe first equality is the ternary-column mask; the second is the birth\nmiddle-color condition. It does not imply any return identity for\n\\(\\beta(3^{a+1}m)\\).\n\n## A nonempty impossible subclass\n\nThe inherited exact exclusion \\(e(8q+1)=0\\) applies whenever\n\\(z\\equiv1\\pmod8\\). Thus no primitive birth can occur when\n\\[\n3^a m\\equiv1\\pmod8. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nBecause \\(3^{-a}\\equiv3^a\\pmod8\\), this is the explicit parameter family\n\\[\nm\\equiv\n\\begin{cases}\n1\\pmod8,&a\\ \\text{even},\\\\\n3\\pmod8,&a\\ \\text{odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad 3\\nmid m. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nEach fixed \\(a\\) has infinitely many positive odd \\(m\\) satisfying (3)\nand \\(3\\nmid m\\), so this is a genuine nonempty arithmetic subclass.\nThe conclusion is only mask impossibility, not a statement about arbitrary\ncolor assignments.\n\nThe known \\(z\\equiv23\\pmod{32}\\) identity \\(h(z)=0\\) is different: it\ndoes not by itself exclude \\(e(z)=1\\), because a birth can have zero\nmultiplicative defect at its index. I therefore do not count that class\nas a primitive-birth exclusion.\n\n## A nonempty smaller-birth subclass\n\nThe accepted BR two-stage routing proves that every actual birth\n\\(n\\equiv5\\pmod{16}\\) has a strictly smaller actual birth (with the finite\nedge \\(n=5\\) handled separately). A primitive ternary birth \\(z=3^a m\\)\ntherefore descends whenever\n\\[\n3^a m\\equiv5\\pmod{16}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nm\\equiv5\\cdot3^{-a}\\pmod{16},\\qquad 3\\nmid m. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nFor each \\(a\\ge2\\), this gives infinitely many admissible odd \\(m\\);\nthe corresponding \\(z\\) is automatically larger than the exceptional\nedge \\(5\\). The BR route is an actual birth transfer, not merely an\nintegral affine candidate.\n\nThe classes (2) and (4) are disjoint modulo \\(16\\): (2) gives\n\\(z\\equiv1\\) or \\(9\\pmod{16}\\), while (4) gives \\(z\\equiv5\\pmod{16}\\).\nPrimitive births in the remaining odd residue classes, including\n\\(z\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\) and the \\(3,7,11,15\\pmod{16}\\) classes not already\ncovered by (2), are not resolved by these local identities.\n\n## Exact scope\n\nThe primitive mask (1) has no earlier \\(h\\)-change to which TL5--TL6 can\ncharge the birth. The residue exclusions above prove a nonempty impossible\nfamily and a nonempty actual-smaller-birth family, but they do not control\nthe remaining primitive \\(E_9\\)-mass. No universal primitive descent,\nfinite \\(E_9\\), finite \\(G\\), or full Collatz conclusion follows.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_ZERO_CLASS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — primitive S-sources in the zero residue class\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no program, CAS,\nenumeration or Lean was used. This note isolates actual S-primitive births\noutside the image I_S={2 mod 3}, with emphasis on odd n congruent to 0\nmodulo 3. It does not replace actual color masks by arithmetic candidates.\n\n## 1. The exact strip form of an odd zero-class start\n\nLet n be an odd birth and write\n\n    n+1=2^h v,   h>=1, v odd,\n    n=2^h v-1 = X_(h,0) for the SG strip with base v.\n\nThe strip identities give\n\n    beta(n)=C_h,  beta(n+1)=B_0,  beta(3n+2)=C_(h+1).\n\nThus the birth condition is\n\n    C_h=B_0 != C_(h+1).                              (1)\n\nIf additionally n is 0 modulo 3, then\n\n    2^h v = n+1 = 1 (mod 3),\n\nso v is 1 modulo 3 when h is even and 2 modulo 3 when h is odd.\nThis is only the residue condition; it does not determine any beta color.\n\nThe cyclic merger used in SG4 supplies the only immediate strip\nrestriction of this type. If 3^h v were 3 modulo 4, the two genuine\npaths into (3^(h+1)v-1)/4 would force C_h=C_(h+1), contradicting (1).\nTherefore every such actual birth satisfies\n\n    3^h v = 1 (mod 4),\n    v = (-1)^h (mod 4).                               (2)\n\nConditions (2) and the zero-class condition modulo 3 are compatible for\nevery h: choose v congruent to (-1)^h modulo 12. This is only an\narithmetic compatibility family, not a realization of actual births.\nThere is no additional 3-adic cyclic merger in SG: the merger identity\nuses the mod-4 parity of 3^h v.\n\n## 2. The exact S-chain and its terminal transfer\n\nFor the odd start n, define the arithmetic odd-run values\n\n    n_j+1=(3/2)^j(n+1)=3^j 2^(h-j) v,\n    n_j=3^j 2^(h-j) v-1,       0\u003c=j\u003c=h.                (3)\n\nFor j\u003ch, n_j is odd and S(n_j)=n_(j+1); n_h is even. The actual\nretained chain follows (3) only while every output n_(j+1) is an actual\nbirth. If an output birth mask fails, d(n_(j+1))=1 and ancestry gives\n\n    b_j \u003c= floor(n_(j+1)/2).                           (4)\n\nThe first failure j=0 is below the original n whenever n>3, because\nfloor(S(n)/2)\u003cn. For later j, (4) need not be below n: the leading ratio\nis approximately (3/2)^(j+1)/2.\n\nIf all odd outputs survive, n_h is an even birth. BR2 then gives only\n\n    b_h \u003c= floor((3 n_h+2)/4)\n        = floor((3^(h+1) v-1)/4).                      (5)\n\nThe original start is n=2^h v-1. The leading ratio of the right side of\n(5) to n is\n\n    3^(h+1)/2^(h+2) = (3/4)(3/2)^h,\n\nwhich is already 9/8 for h=1 and increases. Thus the terminal even\ntransfer is smaller than n_h but is not forced to be smaller than the\noriginal zero-class start n.\n\nIf the full retained odd run is actual, the terminal inverse\n\n    p=(2 n_h-1)/3 = 2*3^(h-1)v-1 = n_(h-1)\n\nis already the preceding actual birth in the same S-chain (and equals\nthe original n when h=1). It is not a new unknown mask in that case.\nValuations at 3 still do not improve the comparison with the original\nstart. The terminal value n_h=3^h v-1 is 2 modulo 3, so it lies in the\nS image and has the inverse candidate\n\n    p=(2 n_h-1)/3 = 2*3^(h-1)v-1.\n\nIf the full retention hypothesis is dropped and one considers only an\narithmetic terminal candidate, whether p is a birth is a separate\ncolor-mask question. Under full retention, p is the known preceding\nbirth. In either interpretation its size relative to n is approximately\n(2/3)(3/2)^h, which is 1 at h=1 and larger for h>=2. Therefore this\ninverse relation supplies no uniform descent to the original n.\n\n## 3. The remaining exact masked branch\n\nFor an actual odd birth n congruent to 0 modulo 3, the complete verified\nalternative is:\n\n1. At some first odd output failure, a smaller birth exists as in (4),\n   but it is guaranteed below n only for the initial failure range.\n2. If all odd outputs through n_h survive, the even terminal transfer\n   (5) supplies a smaller birth below n_h, with no comparison to n.\n3. Under full retention the terminal inverse p is n_(h-1), already on\n   the chain; without full retention an arithmetic candidate p has an\n   independent birth mask. In neither case does it give descent below\n   the original n.\n\nThe starting birth itself is primitive for the S graph because n is 0\nmodulo 3 and the S image is 2 modulo 3. This says only that it has no\nincoming S edge. It does not make it a finite-energy source with a known\nweight, nor does it imply that any arithmetic candidate in the CRT family\nis an actual birth.\n\n## 4. Scope and verdict\n\nThe zero-class strip yields the exact restrictions (1)--(2) and the\nterminal formulas (3)--(5). They do not produce a universal smaller birth\nrelative to the original n. The unresolved branch is an actual masked\nodd run through all surviving S outputs followed by the even terminal\ntransfer, together with possible returns through the terminal inverse.\nNo 3-adic valuation argument closes that branch, and no global descent or\nCollatz conclusion follows.\n\n## 5. Corrected three-mask split for an even primitive birth\n\nEvery birth n has e(2n)=e(2n+1)=0: the XOR identity makes the two\nchild boundaries differ whenever e(n)=1. For an even birth n=4m of color\nc, the BR2 boundary is\n\n    K=6m+1,   beta(K)=1-c,   beta(K+1)=c,\n    d(K)=1,   delta(K)=2c-1.\n\nPut S(K)=(3K+1)/2. Since beta(S(K))=beta(K), the exact boundary\nidentities for active odd K are\n\n    d(2S(K))=d(2K),\n    e(S(K))=d(2K) * (1-d(S(K))).                      (6)\n\nThe second factor is essential: d(2S(K)+1)=1-d(S(K)) when d(2K)=1,\nbecause beta(2S(K)+1)=beta(3K+2) is then opposite beta(K). Thus\n\n    e(S(K))=1  =>  d(2K)=1 and d(S(K))=0,\n\nbut the converse is not implied by the birth at 4m. The birth condition\nfixes beta(3m+1)=c, while beta(S(K)+1)=beta(9m+3) remains uncontrolled.\n\nThe failure split is exact:\n\n1. d(2K)=0. Then d(2K+1)=1 by d(K)=1, so the active child is the\n   odd boundary 2K+1; S(K) is not a birth.\n2. d(2K)=1 and d(S(K))=1. The active child is the even boundary 2K,\n   while the neighboring boundary S(K) is active; again S(K) is not a\n   birth.\n3. d(2K)=1 and d(S(K))=0. Then e(S(K))=1, and S(K) is a D_S origin\n   because K is not a birth. This is the only branch carrying the\n   image-component weight A_h/K, h=v2(K+1).\n\nIn the first branch the active child sign is\n`delta(2K+1)=2c-1`; in the second and third branches the left-child sign\nis `delta(2K)=2c-1`. The signs do not decide the uncontrolled neighbor\nmask.\n\nThe even primitive birth itself has the exact weight\n\n    1/n = 1/(4m) = 3/[2(K-1)].                        (7)\n\nConsequently, only on branch 3 can one couple it to the complete\nD-component estimate:\n\n    1/n + W(S(K))\n      \u003c= 3/[2(K-1)] + A_(v2(K+1))/K.                  (8)\n\nOn branches 1 and 2 the second term in (8) is absent; there is no valid\nreplacement by A_h/K. Formula (8) is a strict per-event compatibility\nbound, not a contraction or a bound on all even primitive births. The\nremaining masks and multiplicities of K still require a separate\nancestry sum.\n\n## 6. Updated verdict\n\nThe zero-class conclusions in Sections 1--4 remain unchanged. The\ncorrected split (6) shows that the proposed equivalence with d(2K)=1\nmust be withdrawn: only the implication from e(S(K))=1 to the left\nchild and zero neighbor is valid. Even-primitive cost can be coupled to\nan S-image component only on that successful branch, as in (8). No\nuniform joint contraction follows from the available masks.\n\n## 7. An additional R-transition for even primitive births\n\nThere is a separate exact transfer for an even birth n=4m. Write its\nouter color as c=beta(n)=beta(m). The birth mask gives\n\n    beta(3m+1)=c,   beta(6m+1)=1-c.                    (9)\n\nPut R(n)=3n/4=3m, and abbreviate x=beta(3m). Then\n\n    d(6m)=x xor (1-c),\n    d(6m+1)=(1-c) xor c=1.\n\nSince d(3m)=x xor c, this proves the exact identity\n\n    e(R(n))=d(6m)d(6m+1)=1-d(3m)=1-d(R(n)).            (10)\n\nThe color x=beta(3m) is not fixed by the original birth; this is why\n(10) is a relation with d(R(n)), rather than a claim that R(n) is\nalways a birth. If d(R(n))=0, then R(n) is a birth of outer color c,\nand it is a strict smaller birth. If d(R(n))=1, the active boundary at\nR(n) gives a nearest birth\n\n    b \u003c= floor(R(n)/2)=floor(3m/2)=floor(3n/8),       (11)\n\nby the usual ancestor rule. Thus every even primitive birth has this\ntwo-way R alternative; it is a genuine smaller-birth statement in both\nbranches, with the retained branch colored c.\n\nThe pure R arithmetic chain is finite in the forward direction: each\nstep (n\\mapsto3n/4) requires (4\\mid n) and decreases (v_2(n)) by\ntwo. Its inverse is (a\\mapsto4a/3), available only when (3\\mid a),\nand decreases the available (v_3) by one when followed backwards.\nConsequently a pure R component with smallest terminal value (a) has\n\n    sum_j 1/n_j \u003c= (1/a) sum_{j>=0}(3/4)^j = 4/a.     (12)\n\nThis is only a component estimate; it does not include mixed S/R returns.\n\nThe union of the two retained maps is injective on their stated domains:\n\n    S(q)=(3q+1)/2  for odd q,       R(n)=3n/4  for 4|n.\n\nThe S image is (2\\pmod 3), the R image is (0\\pmod 3), and the two\ndomains (odd q versus multiples of four) are disjoint. Each map is\ninjective, so their images cannot collide. The arithmetic image therefore\ncontains no (1\\pmod3) input, but this does not exclude infinite mixed\ncomponents or cycles; those require actual retained color masks.\n\nFor the middle integer μ=2n+1, the transition bookkeeping is exact:\nthe S output has middle (T(μ)) when the input is the odd q, while for\n(n=4m), μ=8m+1 and\n\n    2R(n)+1=6m+1=T^2(μ).                            (13)\n\nThe R identity is simply (T(8m+1)=12m+2) and\n(T(12m+2)=6m+1). These middle-orbit formulas do not by themselves prove\nthat a mixed S/R chain is retained forever or that it has no cycle.\n\nThe R alternative strengthens the local even-birth routing, but it does\nnot close the primitive-source energy problem: the R image covers only\nthe (0\\pmod3) class, the S image only (2\\pmod3), and primitive births\nin the (1\\pmod3) class remain outside this union.\n\n## 8. Coding count for indefinitely defined arithmetic F-words\n\nFor the arithmetic partial map\n\n    S(n)=(3n+1)/2  on odd n,\n    R(n)=3n/4       on n=0 mod 4,\n\nconsider a fixed word of length k, with o S-letters and r R-letters.\nReading the branch conditions backwards shows inductively that its exact\ndomain is one residue class modulo\n\n    2^A,       A=o+2r.                               (14)\n\nIndeed, an S condition consumes one binary parity bit and an R condition\nconsumes two; multiplication by 3 is odd and therefore invertible modulo\nevery power of two. The affine constants only change the residue. Each\nword consequently has infinitely many positive arithmetic realizations,\nand distinct words are disjoint because their first differing branch asks\nfor the disjoint conditions odd versus 0 mod 4.\n\nLet N_k(X) count the positive starts n\u003c=X for which a length-k word is\ndefined. Counting one residue class by X/2^A+1 gives\n\n    N_k(X) \u003c= X sum_{o+r=k} binom(k,r) 2^{-(o+2r)} + 2^k\n            = X(3/4)^k + 2^k.                        (15)\n\nLet Gamma+ be the arithmetic set admitting an indefinitely continued F\ntrajectory. It is contained in every length-k prefix set. Put\n\n    alpha=ln(2)/ln(8/3),       k=floor(log_(8/3) X).\n\nFor X>=1, 2^k\u003c=X^alpha, while\n\n    X(3/4)^k \u003c (4/3)X^alpha,\n\nbecause k>log_(8/3)X-1. Hence\n\n    #(Gamma+ cap [1,X]) \u003c= (7/3)X^alpha.                (16)\n\nSince 0\u003calpha\u003c1, the layer-cake/integration bound yields\n\n    sum_{n in Gamma+} 1/n \u003c= 7/[3(1-alpha)].            (17)\n\nThis is a summable arithmetic infinite-forward core. It uses no actual\nbirth mask, so the actual retained F-growth set is only a subset. The\nbound does not exclude a finite cycle or any other finite collection of\nsurvivors, and it does not assert that every arithmetic word is realized\nby actual births.\n\n## 9. Inverse-R origins and their weighted bound\n\nLet n=3m be an actual birth with outer color c=beta(n), and let q=4m\nbe its arithmetic inverse under R. Exact T-invariance gives\n\n    beta(q)=beta(m),\n    beta(q+1)=beta(3m+1)=c,\n    beta(3q+2)=beta(6m+1)=1-c.\n\nTherefore\n\n    e(q)=1  iff  beta(m)=c.                           (18)\n\nWhen m is odd, beta(m)=beta(3m+1)=c, so an inverse-R failure can\noccur only for even m. If n is even, the previously established\nidentity e(4r+2)=0 forces 4|m. Write\n\n    m=2^h u,   u odd,   h>=2,   k=3u.\n\nThen n=2^h k, and failure of (18) says\n\n    beta(u) != beta(3u)=c,\n    beta(k+1)=beta(3u+1)=beta(u),\n\nso d(k)=1. The nearest-birth rule supplies\n\n    b \u003c= floor(k/2) \u003c n/2^(h+1) \u003c= n/8.               (19)\n\nFor a fixed odd (k), possible failed inverse-R origins (2^h k) have\nnonadjacent exponents (h>=2): adjacent h would give both a birth and\nits doubled birth, contradicting (e(2a)=0) for every birth a. Hence\n\n    sum_{h>=2, h nonadjacent} 1/(2^h k) \u003c= 1/(3k).      (20)\n\nNow charge the odd active boundaries k to their nearest birth b. On the\nright active branch the first odd boundary may occur at depth one, giving\n(sum 1/k\\le1/b). On the left branch the first odd boundary is at depth\nat least two, giving (sum1/k\\le1/(2b)). The two paths are disjoint,\nso altogether\n\n    sum_(k charged to b) 1/k \u003c= 3/(2b).                (21)\n\nCombining (20)--(21), and using (b\\le n/8\\le X/8) for origins\n(n\\le X), yields the origin-only estimate\n\n    E_(D_R)(X) \u003c= (1/2) E_beta(X/8).                   (22)\n\nThis is a bound for inverse-R origins, not for their full mixed S/R\ncomponents. Together with the established S-origin estimate it gives,\nfor the disjoint origin classes\n\n    O_F=P_1-births (n=1 mod 3)  disjoint union  D_S  disjoint union  D_R,\n\n    E_(O_F)(X) \u003c= E_(P_1)(X)\n                 + (4/9)E_beta(X/6) + (1/2)E_beta(X/8).\n\nThe displayed image-origin coefficients sum to (17/18) before any\ncomponent-mass factor. This does not bound E_(P_1), and therefore does\nnot by itself close the full energy argument.\n\n## 10. Arithmetic R-blocks are a barrier to unmasked component bounds\n\nFix L>=1 and an odd v>0 satisfying\n\n    v=1 mod 3,       3^L v=1 mod 8.\n\nEquivalently, one may take v=1 mod 24 for even L and v=19 mod 24 for\nodd L. Set\n\n    a=4^L v.\n\nThen a=1 mod 3, so it has no arithmetic F-predecessor: S-images are\n2 mod 3 and R-images are 0 mod 3. Its R-run is\n\n    n_j=4^(L-j)3^j v,       0\u003c=j\u003c=L,\n\nending at y=n_L=3^L v, which is odd and 1 mod 8. The next defined\narithmetic step is\n\n    z=S(y)=(3y+1)/2=2 mod 4,\n\nso z is terminal for F. The inverse classes show that these displayed\nvertices form the complete finite arithmetic F-component: the initial\nclass has no inverse, each intermediate R-output has its displayed\npredecessor, and z has the displayed S-predecessor.\n\nIf W_arith(a) denotes the reciprocal mass of this component, then\n\n    a W_arith(a)\n      >= sum_{j=0}^L a/n_j\n       = sum_{j=0}^L (4/3)^j\n      >= (4/3)^L.                                     (23)\n\nThus the normalized finite-component factor is unbounded with L. This\ndoes not contradict the origin estimate 17/18: that estimate counts only\nactual origins, while (23) is an unmasked arithmetic component mass. It\ndoes show why the origin coefficient cannot be applied directly to full\nF-component mass without a genuine birth-mask/retention argument.\n\nThe endpoint y=1 mod 8 is structurally not an actual birth, so this\nfamily is not an actual-birth counterexample; it is only the arithmetic\nbarrier. Likewise, the arithmetic coding core Gamma+ controls starts\nwith an indefinitely continued forward F trajectory, not finite chains\nsuch as this one.\n\nFor the retained birth graph, every vertex of a cycle or of an infinite\nforward component lies in the corresponding forward core Gamma+; vertices\nin a component with an infinite backward tail lie in the backward core\nGamma-. Hence cycles and infinite retained F-components are contained in\nGamma+ union Gamma- (with a cycle in both directions). This containment\ndoes not put every finite arithmetic chain, nor every arithmetic word,\ninto the actual birth graph.\n\n## 11. Full audit of SR1--SR12\n\nThe complete file\n`BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md` was read in full. The\nmanual checks confirm the following points.\n\n1. SR1 uses the exact XOR identity correctly: active boundaries have a\nunique nearest zero boundary/birth ancestor, each birth has two active\npaths, and the doubled children of a birth are not births.\n2. SR2 is the exact even-birth R alternative. With\n(x=\\beta(3m)), (d(6m)=x\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}(1-c)),\n(d(6m+1)=1), and (d(3m)=x\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}c), one gets\n(e(3m)=1-d(3m)). The stated smaller-birth bound and pure-R factor\n4/a are valid in their stated directions.\n3. SR3's S/R images, inverse formulas, injectivity, and middle-orbit\nidentities are exact. The retained graph remains only a partial masked\nsubgraph, so mixed cycles and infinite components are correctly left\nopen.\n4. SR4--SR6 correctly count forward words modulo (2^{s+2r}), backward\nwords modulo (3^k), and the summable cores \\(\\Gamma_+\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma_-\\). The (7/3) and (5/2) constants follow from the stated\nfloor choices; no growth or independence assumption is used.\n5. SR7--SR8 correctly derive the inverse-R mask. The use of\n(e(4a+2)=0) is necessary to conclude (4\\mid m) in the even case;\nthat hypothesis is explicitly present.\n6. SR9--SR11 correctly distinguish origin energy from full component\nmass. The (D_R) cutoff and (1/2) coefficient, the disjoint origin\npartition, and the (17/18) pre-component coefficient all agree with\nthe direct audit above. The warning about origins above a cutoff while a\nlater R-vertex lies below it is material.\n7. SR10's arithmetic barrier is valid: (aW\\ge a/y=(4/3)^L) already\nfollows from the endpoint alone. Since (y\\equiv1\\pmod8) is not a\nbirth, it is not an actual-birth counterexample.\n\nThe only scope qualification is that \\(\\Gamma_-\\) is the arithmetic\nbackward core; actual retained backward-infinite components form a\nsubset. Consequently every cycle and every infinite retained component\nis covered by \\(\\Gamma_+\\cup\\Gamma_-\\), while finite arithmetic chains\nand finite-prefix residue classes are not covered by the summable-core\nbounds. No correction to SR1--SR12 is required.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_RESET_COLOR_TRACE.md":"# FOLLOWUP-0431 — цветовой trace арифметической reset-семьи\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная проверка BR6 без программ, CAS,\nпереборов и Lean. Здесь проверяются только точные цветовые следствия\nинвариантности \\(\\beta\\); арифметическая целочисленность сама по себе не\nсчитается существованием birth.\n\n## Параметры и пределы reset-семьи\n\nПоложим\n\\[\n L=6t+3,\\qquad A=L+1=6t+4,\\qquad\n u=\\frac{2^L+1}{9},\\qquad n_0=16u-3,\\qquad q=\\frac A2=3t+2.\n\\]\nТогда \\(u\\) нечётно, \\(2^L=9u-1\\), и для \\(0\\le j\\le q\\)\n\\[\n A_j=9^j2^{A-2j},\\qquad p_j=A_j-1,\\qquad\n Q_j=3A_j-1=3p_j+2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nДля \\(j\u003cq\\) число \\(A_j\\) делится на \\(4\\), поэтому арифметически\n\\[\n H(p_j)=\\frac{9p_j+5}{4}=p_{j+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nНа \\(j=q\\) имеем \\(p_q=9^q-1\\), уже чётный; это последний output\nарифметической \\(H\\)-цепи, а не следующий \\(H\\)-input.\n\nИз \\(2^L=9u-1\\) следует\n\\[\n n_0=16u-3,\\qquad p_0=2^{A}-1=18u-3,\\qquad\n 2n_0=32u-6.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nСледовательно \\(n_0\u003cp_0\u003cp_0+2\u003c2n_0\\) для \\(u\\ge1\\). Это расположение\nважно только при дополнительной гипотезе о едином цвете префикса.\n\n## Две настоящие нечётные серии\n\nЕсли \\(B\\) делится на \\(4\\), две нечётные ветви shortcut map дают\n\\[\n T(B-1)=\\frac{3B}{2}-1,\\qquad\n T^2(B-1)=\\frac{9B}{4}-1.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nТак как \\(\\beta\\) \\(T\\)-инвариантна, (4) и (1) дают для всех\n\\(0\\le j\u003cq\\)\n\\[\n \\beta(p_j)=\\beta(p_{j+1}).\n\\]\nПри \\(j=q-1\\) это также связывает \\(p_{q-1}\\) с \\(p_q\\). Поэтому\nвсе \\(p_j\\), \\(0\\le j\\le q\\), имеют один цвет\n\\[\n C_A:=\\beta(3^A-1),\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nпоскольку последовательное применение нечётной ветви из\n\\(2^A-1\\) проходит через \\(3^{2j}2^{A-2j}-1=p_j\\) и заканчивается\n\\(3^A-1\\).\n\nАналогично \\(Q_j=3^{2j+1}2^{A-2j}-1\\) лежит в параллельной нечётной\nсерии с общим цветом\n\\[\n C_{A+1}:=\\beta(3^{A+1}-1),\n\\qquad\n \\beta(Q_j)=C_{A+1}\\quad(0\\le j\\le q).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nНа конце \\(j=q\\) это \\(Q_q=3\\cdot9^q-1=3^{A+1}-1\\), как и требуется.\n\n## Точная маска для настоящего \\(H\\)-шага\n\nИз \\(p_j+1=A_j\\) и повторного деления пополам следует\n\\[\n \\beta(p_j+1)=\\beta(A_j)=\\beta(9^j).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nПоэтому при действительном условии output-birth\n\\(e(p_j)=1\\) и boundary \\(d(p_j)=0\\) имеем точную цветовую формулу\n\\[\n \\beta(9^j)=\\beta(p_j)=\\beta(p_j+1)\n \\ne \\beta(Q_j)\n =\\beta\\!\\left(3\\cdot9^j2^{A-2j}-1\\right).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nБолее сильная форма получается, если \\(e(p_0)=1\\). Тогда (8) при\n\\(j=0\\) даёт \\(C_A\\ne C_{A+1}\\), и бинарность цветов вместе с (5)--(7)\nдаёт для всех \\(0\\le j\\le q\\)\n\\[\n e(p_j)=1\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\beta(9^j)=C_A,\n\\qquad\n d(p_j)=1-e(p_j).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(d(p_j)=0\\) действительно является boundary-условием H-output,\nа не следствием одной арифметической формулы.\n\nДля перехода \\(p_{j-1}\\to p_j\\) при \\(1\\le j\\le q\\) необходимо, чтобы\nпредыдущий input был действительным birth и \\(d(p_j)=0\\). Тогда BR5\nдаёт \\(e(p_j)=1\\), то есть при уже установленном \\(e(p_0)=1\\) полный\nреальный \\(H\\)-run до \\(p_r\\) эквивалентен условиям\n\\[\n \\beta(9^j)=C_A\\quad(1\\le j\\le r).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nСамо условие \\(e(p_j)=1,d(p_j)=0\\), взятое без действительного\nпредыдущего input-birth, не доказывает существование перехода.\n\n## Что добавляет гипотеза минимального рождения\n\nУсилим гипотезу: \\(n_0\\) — минимальное действительное рождение и все\nчисла \\(1,\\ldots,2n_0\\) имеют один цвет \\(c=\\beta(1)\\). Из (3)\n\\[\n \\beta(p_0)=\\beta(p_0+1)=\\beta(p_0+2)=c,\n\\qquad d(p_0)=d(p_0+1)=0.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nТак как \\(n_0\\equiv5\\pmod8\\) и \\(e(n_0)=1\\), BR3 при обеих нулевых\nграницах вынуждает \\(e(p_0)=1\\). Следовательно\n\\[\n C_A=c,\\qquad C_{A+1}=1-c,\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nи (9) упрощается до\n\\[\n e(p_j)=1\\Longleftrightarrow\\beta(9^j)=c,\n\\qquad\n d(p_j)=1\\Longleftrightarrow\\beta(9^j)=1-c.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nТо есть минимальность не исключает reset-цепь: она фиксирует её две\nпараллельные серии цветов, а каждый настоящий \\(H\\)-output требует\nцвета \\(c\\) у соответствующей степени \\(9^j\\).\n\nПрямого противоречия с префиксом нет. Действительно,\n\\[\n Q_j=3A_j-1\\ge Q_0=54u-7>32u-6=2n_0,\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nтогда как \\(9^j\\) контролируется гипотезой префикса только пока\n\\(9^j\\le2n_0\\). Для таких малых \\(j\\) требование\n\\(\\beta(9^j)=c\\) автоматически согласуется с (11); при больших \\(j\\)\nцвет степеней \\(9\\) гипотезой не задан. Также \\(p_j>2n_0\\) уже при\n\\(j\\ge1\\), так что единственный reset-output внутри префикса — \\(p_0\\).\n\nЕсли для некоторого \\(j\\ge1\\) \\(\\beta(9^j)\\ne c\\), то (13) останавливает\nименно \\(H\\)-ветвь через \\(d(p_j)=1\\). Для ladder-индексов с\n\\(A-2j\\ge1\\) дополнительная strip-идентичность даёт также\n\\(d(\\lfloor p_j/2\\rfloor)=1\\), поэтому ближайший birth удовлетворяет\n\\(a'\\le\\lfloor p_j/4\\rfloor\\). Для reset-семьи при первом возможном\nсбое \\(j=1\\) это действительно меньше \\(n_0\\); при \\(j\\ge2\\) одна эта\nоценка уже не обязана быть меньше \\(n_0\\). На конечном чётном output\n\\(p_q\\) нужно различать случаи: если \\(d(p_q)=0\\), то по (9)\n\\(e(p_q)=1\\) и к самому чётному birth применим BR2; если маска впервые\nсбилась, то \\(d(p_q)=1\\) и \\(e(p_q)=0\\), так что BR2 к \\(p_q\\) применять\nнельзя. В последнем случае для \\(r=\\lfloor p_q/2\\rfloor\\): при\n\\(d(r)=0\\) имеем \\(e(r)=1\\), причём \\(r\\) чётен и к нему применим BR2;\nпри \\(d(r)=1\\) ancestry уходит на более глубокого предка. Если же все условия\n\\(\\beta(9^j)=c\\) выполняются, то маски всей арифметической \\(H\\)-цепи\nсовместимы; это также не даёт противоречия с минимальностью, поскольку\nвсе \\(p_j\\) после \\(p_0\\) выше префикса.\n\n## Аудит COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES\n\nSC1 и SC2 проверены: конечное нечётное слово действительно даёт\n\\[\n \\beta(X_{h,s})=C_h,\\qquad\n \\beta(X_{h,s}+1)=B_s,\\qquad\n \\beta(3X_{h,s}+2)=C_{h+1},\n\\]\nа слияние двух путей при \\(3^hu\\equiv3\\pmod4\\) действительно вынуждает\n\\(C_h=C_{h+1}\\). Поэтому действительное \\(e(2^au-1)=1\\) требует\n\\(3^au\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), а при \\(a\\ge2\\) получается \\(C_{a-1}=C_a\\).\n\nSC3 корректно устраняет \\(G_1\\). При \\(a\\ge3\\) имеем\n\\(k=X_{a,2}\\), \\(p=X_{a-1,2}\\), \\(C_a=C_{a-1}\\) и\n\\(\\beta(k+1)=\\beta(p+1)\\), откуда \\(d(k)=d(p)\\); комбинация\n\\(d(k)=1,d(p)=0\\) невозможна. При \\(a=2\\) условие даёт\n\\(u\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), \\(p\\) чётно, и даже если \\(d(k)=1,d(p)=0\\),\neven-transfer даёт\n\\[\n a'\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{27n+31}{32}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn\n \\qquad(n>31/5).\n\\]\nЕдинственный меньший положительный край \\(n=3\\) не является birth.\nСледовательно, \\(G_5\\) ниже исходного input закрывается, а \\(G_1\\)\nдействительно не остаётся актуальным.\n\nSC4 верно оставляет только \\(G_3\\) на \\(13\\bmod16\\) и \\(H\\) на\n\\(3\\bmod4\\), с их настоящими birth- и boundary-масками. Для настоящего\noutput-birth \\(p=G_3(n)\\) сохранение цвета действительно следует:\n\\[\n \\beta(p)=1-\\beta(2p+1)=1-\\beta(\\mu)=\\beta(n),\n\\]\nпоскольку \\(2p+1=T^3(\\mu)\\), а birth \\(n\\) имеет\n\\(\\beta(n)\\ne\\beta(\\mu)\\). Это условное цветовое равенство, не\nутверждение для арифметического output без маски. Идентичности\nчерез среднюю точку \\(\\mu=2n+1\\) арифметически верны, но сами по себе\nне превращают обычную орбиту \\(\\mu\\) в спуск для birth-индекса.\n\nSC5 подтверждает и уточняет предыдущий trace: при первом сбое\n\\(B_{2j}\\ne C_a\\) и \\(a-2j\\ge1\\) действительно активны обе границы\n\\(d(n_j)=d(\\lfloor n_j/2\\rfloor)=1\\), поэтому\n\\[\n a'\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n_j}{4}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nЭта оценка ниже исходного \\(n_0\\) автоматически лишь для первого\nсбоя; для \\(j\\ge2\\) она может быть выше исходного входа. Для конечного\nчётного output нельзя автоматически применять BR2: при \\(d(p_q)=1\\)\nимеем \\(e(p_q)=0\\); нужно рассмотреть его родителя\n\\(r=\\lfloor p_q/2\\rfloor\\): при \\(d(r)=0\\) это чётный birth, к которому\nприменим BR2, а при \\(d(r)=1\\) требуется более глубокая ancestry.\n\nФраза root-файла о \\(C_h=\\beta(1)\\) под гипотезой минимального birth\nверна, но её основание — не размер endpoint \\(3^hu-1\\). Для\n\\(0\\le h\\le a\\) нужно применить SC1 к\n\\(X_{h,0}=2^hu-1\\le n_0\\le2n_0\\), что даёт\n\\(\\beta(X_{h,0})=C_h=\\beta(1)\\); при \\(h=0\\) требуется \\(u>1\\).\n\nSC6 для reset-семьи согласуется с этим trace: здесь \\(C_A=\\beta(3^A-1)\\)\nотносится к core-1 ladder \\(p_j=9^j2^{A-2j}-1\\), тогда как \\(u_*\\)\nопределяет только reset-start \\(n_0=16u_*-3\\). Условие \\(p_0\u003c2n_0\\)\nфиксирует \\(C_A=\\beta(1)\\), а probes \\(B_{2j}=\\beta(9^j)\\) остаются неизвестными\nвне префикса. Исправленная граница такова: первый сбой маски не ведёт к\nG1, но его меньший предок может оставаться выше исходного \\(n_0\\) при\n\\(j\\ge2\\). Полного uniform descent в root-файле не получено.\n\n## Итог и точный пробел\n\nПроверено безусловно: арифметический reset индексирует две настоящие\nнечётные серии, а при \\(e(p_0)=1\\) точная маска каждого \\(p_j\\) имеет\nвид (9). При минимальном \\(n_0\\) имеем \\(C_A=\\beta(1)\\), \\(C_{A+1}\\)\nпротивоположен, и реальный \\(H\\)-run требует ровно согласования\n\\(\\beta(9^j)\\) с \\(C_A\\) на его длине.\n\nВ этом разборе равномерного исключения reset-семьи из этих данных не\nполучено. Для дальнейшего исключения\nнужен новый контроль цветов степеней \\(9\\) вне префикса или доказательство,\nчто первое отклонение маски обязательно вызывает меньший birth ниже\n\\(n_0\\). Такое утверждение не следует из арифметической интегральности и\nпо существу требует дополнительного контроля маршрутов степеней \\(3\\);\nоно не получено предположением только о цветах до \\(2n_0\\). Поэтому\nreset-семья остаётся точным arithmetic candidate, а не доказанным\nконтрпримером и не доказанно исключённым настоящим birth-family.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_SIGNED_ENDPOINT_MASKS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — signed endpoint masks and exact R-column compensation\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual audit after AC and\nWS. No programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents were used. The\nstatements below concern an arbitrary exact binary T-invariant and actual\ncolor masks; no symbolic relaxation is treated as a realization.\n\n## 1. The two channel transitions\n\nFor an odd source \\(q\\), put \\(y=S(q)=(3q+1)/2\\). The exact channel\nidentity is\n\n\\[\n e(y)+L(y)=e(q)+L(q).\n\\]\nOn the channel-one states this gives the only four possibilities\n\\[\n E\\to E,\\quad E\\to L,\\quad L\\to E,\\quad L\\to L,\n\\]\nwhere \\(E\\) denotes \\(e=1\\) and \\(L\\) denotes \\(L=1\\). Thus\n\\[\n Z_S=\\{q:q\\text{ odd},\\ e(q)=1,\\ e(Sq)=0\\},\n\\]\nand its target is an \\(L\\)-state, while\n\\[\n D_S=\\{Sq:q\\text{ odd},\\ L(q)=1,\\ e(Sq)=1\\},\n\\]\nand its target is an \\(E\\)-state. Equivalently, on every odd S-edge,\nwith \\(u(n)=e(n)\\) on the channel-one states,\n\\[\n \\Delta_Su(q):=u(Sq)-u(q)\n =1_{D_S}(Sq)-1_{Z_S}(q).                         \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThis is an exact signed mask statement; retained \\(E\\to E\\) and \\(L\\to L\\)\nedges have zero increment.\n\nFor a source \\(n=4m\\), put \\(y=R(n)=3m\\). The exact channel identity is\n\\[\n e(y)+R_b(y)=e(n)+R_b(n).\n\\]\nThe channel-one transitions are\n\\[\n E\\to E,\\quad E\\to R_b,\\quad R_b\\to E,\\quad R_b\\to R_b.\n\\]\nHere \\(E\\to R_b\\) is \\(Z_R\\), and \\(R_b\\to E\\) is \\(D_R\\). Since\n\\(y=3n/4\\), their signed harmonic weights match exactly on an edge:\n\\[\n \\frac4n=\\frac3y.\n\\]\nConsequently the R contribution is an oriented channel variation, subject\nonly to the common-cutoff boundary:\n\\[\n 4Z_R-3D_R\n =3\\sum_{R\\text{-edges}}\\frac{1_{E\\to R_b}-1_{R_b\\to E}}{y}+O(1).\n                                                               \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe \\(O(1)\\) is only the fixed affine cutoff annulus.\n\n## 2. Exact characterization of an R-terminal target\n\nWrite \\(y=3m\\) and \\(n=4m=4y/3\\). Since\n\\(\\beta(2y+2)=\\beta(y+1)\\),\n\\[\n R_b(y)=1\n \\iff \\beta(y)=\\beta(2y+1)\\ne\\beta(y+1).         \\tag{3}\n\\]\nIf \\(R_b(y)=1\\), then \\(d(2y)=0\\), hence \\(e(y)=0\\). Also\n\\[\n d(n)=0\n \\iff \\beta(4y/3)=\\beta(4y/3+1).\n\\]\nWhen both (3) and this last equality hold, the colors at \\(2n,2n+1\\)\nare opposite, so \\(e(n)=1\\). Conversely, a \\(Z_R\\)-edge has \\(e(n)=1\\),\n\\(e(y)=0\\), \\(R_b(y)=1\\), and the channel identity forces \\(d(n)=0\\).\nTherefore the exact criterion is\n\\[\n n\\in Z_R\n \\iff R_b(y)=1\\ \\text{and}\\ d(4y/3)=0.             \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(y=3m\\), \\(m\\) is odd and \\(d(4m)=0\\) by the exact odd-input\nidentity. Hence in this case\n\\[\n n\\in Z_R\n \\iff y\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\ y\\text{ odd},\\ R_b(y)=1.  \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThus an odd R-terminal is exactly an odd active \\(A\\)-boundary in residue\nzero: \\(d(y)=d(2y+1)=1\\) and \\(d(2y)=0\\). This is a real color mask,\nnot a residue assignment.\n\n## 3. Even R-targets and their odd part\n\nLet \\(y=2^h q\\), where \\(q\\) is odd and \\(3\\mid q\\), and write \\(q=3u\\).\nThe predecessor is \\(n=2^{h+2}u\\). The channel colors give\n\\(\\beta(y)=\\beta(q)\\), \\(\\beta(n)=\\beta(u)\\), and\n\\(\\beta(q+1)=\\beta(u)\\). Under \\(R_b(y)=1\\), the two colors opposite to\n\\(\\beta(y+1)\\) are equal, so\n\\[\n d(q)=1 \\iff d(n)=0.                                \\tag{6}\n\\]\nUnder \\(e(y)=1\\), \\(\\beta(y)=\\beta(y+1)\\), and the same two-color check\ngives\n\\[\n d(q)=1 \\iff d(n)=1.                                \\tag{7}\n\\]\nIt follows that both endpoint types have the exact common odd-part mask\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n Z_R&:\\quad R_b(2^h q)=1,\\ d(q)=1,\\\\\n D_R&:\\quad e(2^h q)=1,\\ d(q)=1,\n \\end{aligned}\\qquad(q\\text{ odd},\\ 3\\mid q).       \\tag{8}\n\\]\nThe \\(h=0\\) case is (5). For \\(h=1\\), the identity \\(d(4q)=0\\) gives\n\\(R_b(2q)=d(2q)\\), so the target is an R-terminal exactly when the odd\nbase \\(q\\) is the corresponding \\(B\\)-boundary; no extra predecessor\ncondition is needed. Its predecessor is \\(n=8u\\), where \\(q=3u\\), and\nthe filter and colors already force \\(d(n)=0\\). For \\(h=2\\),\n\\(R_b(4q)=0\\). For \\(h\\ge3\\),\nthere is no further universal valuation exclusion; the mask in (8) must\nbe retained. In particular, no conclusion about all even targets follows\nfrom the \\(h=2\\) zero alone.\n\nDefine \\(a_h=d(2^h q)\\). The binary child identity gives, exactly,\n\\[\n e(2^h q)=(1-a_h)a_{h+1},\\qquad\n R_b(2^h q)=a_h(1-a_{h+1}),\\qquad\n R_b-e=a_h-a_{h+1},                         \\tag{9}\n\\]\nand \\(a_2=d(4q)=0\\). Combining (8) and (9), the entire signed R endpoint\nmass has the common-column formula\n\\[\n 4Z_R(X)-3D_R(X)\n =3\\sum_{\\substack{q\\le X\\\\ q\\text{ odd},\\ 3\\mid q\\\\ d(q)=1}}\n \\left(\\frac2q-\n   \\sum_{\\substack{h\\ge0\\\\2^h q\\le X}}\n       \\frac{d(2^h q)}{2^h q}\\right)+O(1).          \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe first \\(2/q\\) is harmlessly rewritten using \\(d(q)=1\\); after that\nrewrite (10) is \\(3(D_{\\rm odd,0}-D_{\\rm even,0})+O(1)\\), where the first\nsum is over active odd bases \\(q\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) and the second is the\neven descendants \\(2^h q\\), \\(h\\ge1\\), with the same base filter. The\ncutoff error includes the fixed R-source/target annulus and the omitted\ncolumn-top terms, whose denominators are distinct indices in \\((X/2,X]\\).\n\nFormula (10) is a genuine signed compensation between \\(Z_R\\) and \\(D_R\\).\nThe column bracket is nonnegative; with \\(a_0=1,a_2=0\\), its infinite\nvalue is at least \\(1/(4q)\\) before the uniformly bounded truncation\nboundary is accounted for. This still is not a finiteness result: the\nactive odd base mass may diverge.\n\n## 4. The S signed variation and its limitation\n\nFrom (1), \\(3/y=6/(3q+1)\\), so on a common cutoff\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n 3D_S(X)-2Z_S(X)\n &=2\\sum_{q\\ \\mathrm{odd}}\\frac{\\Delta_Su(q)}q\n   +\\sum_{\\substack{q\\ \\mathrm{odd}\\\\\\Delta_Su(q)=1}}\n       \\left(\\frac3{S(q)}-\\frac2q\\right)+O(1)\\\\\n &=2\\sum_{q\\ \\mathrm{odd}}\\frac{\\Delta_Su(q)}q\n   -2\\sum_{\\substack{q\\ \\mathrm{odd}\\\\\\Delta_Su(q)=1}}\n       \\frac1{q(3q+1)}+O(1).                        \\tag{11}\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe correction is absolutely bounded. On one finite S-chain\n\\(q_0\\to q_1\\to\\cdots\\), the weights \\(1/q_j\\) decrease, so Abel\nsummation bounds the variation term by \\(2/q_0\\) in absolute value,\napart from the displayed summable correction. This is local forced\ncancellation between an \\(E\\to L\\) terminal and a later \\(L\\to E\\)\norigin. Summing over all chain bases still requires a bound on the bases;\nthere is no global finiteness conclusion.\n\n## 5. What the odd A/B paths do and do not force\n\nFor an odd active boundary \\(q\\), exactly one of the following holds:\n\\[\n A:\\ R_b(q)=1,\\qquad B:\\ L(q)=1.\n\\]\nBy (5), an A-boundary in residue zero is a \\(Z_R\\) target. By (1), a\nB-boundary produces a \\(D_S\\) target \\(S(q)\\) precisely when\n\\(d(S(q))=0\\); if \\(d(S(q))=1\\), it remains an \\(L\\)-state without that\npositive charge. A ZS edge is the reverse \\(E\\to L\\) S transition, and a\nDR edge is the reverse \\(R_b\\to E\\) R transition. These masks are\nmutually exclusive on each individual channel edge, but their charges\nneed not be paired at the same odd boundary.\n\nThe known path spacing remains the WS spacing: after an R-target, the next\ntarget is at least two odd-boundary depths later, and a gap-two target, if\npresent, is again R-type; after an S-target, a gap-two target cannot be\nS-type. The state of the intermediate odd boundary is not fixed by the\nterminal mask alone, so a stronger universal spacing or a forced\npositive/negative cancellation does not follow from AC/WS.\n\n## 6. Final verdict and exact gap\n\nThe exact endpoint masks are now coupled as follows:\n\n* \\(Z_R\\) and \\(D_R\\) share the odd-base filter \\(q\\) with \\(3\\mid q\\)\n  and \\(d(q)=1\\), and their whole signed contribution has the column\n  identity (10).\n* \\(Z_S\\) and \\(D_S\\) are the two orientations of the same S channel;\n  their signed contribution is the weighted variation (11), with bounded\n  affine correction.\n* Odd A/B paths identify the local masks (A gives Rb, B gives L), but do\n  not bound the number or harmonic mass of the chain bases.\n\nThis was the initial endpoint verdict before the stronger S-side estimate\nin §7; it is superseded there. The AC signed bridge has a real\nmask-sensitive cancellation structure, and §7 reduces finite birth energy\nexactly to finiteness of \\(Z_R\\), but does not prove that remaining\nfiniteness or Collatz.\n\n## 7. Audit of the stronger S-side bound\n\nThe AC channel equations give one further exact signed identity. Subtracting\nthe target-birth decomposition from the S-channel transport gives\n\\[\n H:=3D_S-2Z_S=2L_{\\rm odd}-3L_2+O(1).              \\tag{12}\n\\]\nHere \\(L_2\\) is all \\(L\\)-mass in residue \\(2\\pmod3\\), while\n\\(L_{\\rm odd}\\) is all odd \\(L\\)-mass. This follows directly from\n\\[\n E_2+L_2=\\tfrac23(A+L_{\\rm odd})+O(1),\\qquad\n E_2=\\tfrac23(A-Z_S)+D_S+O(1).\n\\]\n\nOn an odd active boundary of residue \\(i\\), an \\(A\\)-state has \\(L=0\\)\nand reward \\(0\\) for \\(H\\); a \\(B\\)-state has \\(L=1\\) and reward\n\\[\n (r_0,r_1,r_2)=(2,2,-1).\n\\]\nThe potential\n\\[\n (Q_0,Q_1,Q_2)=(5/2,2,0)\n\\]\nis a valid supersolution for both exact odd-boundary transitions:\n\\[\n Q_i\\ge Q_{2i+1}/2,\\qquad\n Q_i\\ge r_i+Q_{i+1}/4 \\qquad (i\\bmod3).              \\tag{13}\n\\]\nThe six checks are respectively\n\\((5/2\\ge1,\\ 2\\ge5/4,\\ 0\\ge0)\\) and\n\\((5/2=2+2/4,\\ 2=2+0/4,\\ 0\\ge-1+(5/2)/4)\\).\nThe inequalities use \\(1/(2q+1)\\le1/(2q)\\) and\n\\(1/(4q+1)\\le1/(4q)\\), so they remain valid for actual affine paths.\n\nFor a birth \\(b\\), the right path starts at \\(2b+1\\), giving initial\ncoefficients\n\\[\n (1,\\ 5/4,\\ 0)\\quad\\text{for }b\\bmod3=(0,1,2).      \\tag{14}\n\\]\nThe left path starts at \\(2b\\). If its first odd boundary is at depth\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\), then the preceding even active nodes are \\(2^j b\\) for\n\\(1\\le j\\le\\ell-2\\), and each such node is an \\(L\\)-state. A node with\n\\(2^j b\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) contributes \\(-3/(2^j b)\\); all other prefix nodes\nhave zero \\(H\\)-reward. The first odd boundary is\n\\(2^\\ell b+1\\), so (13) bounds the remaining odd part.\nTaking the supremum over every \\(\\ell\\ge2\\), and also the never-odd case,\ngives the left coefficients\n\\[\n (1/2,\\ 0,\\ 5/8)\\quad\\text{for }b\\bmod3=(0,1,2).    \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThe residue check is explicit: for \\(b=0\\) the prefix has no residue-2\nterm and the maximum is \\(Q_1/4=1/2\\); for \\(b=1\\), depth \\(2\\) gives\n\\(Q_2/4=0\\) and every later depth has a prior negative term; for \\(b=2\\),\ndepth \\(2\\) gives \\(Q_0/4=5/8\\) and later depths are no larger. Infinite\neven prefixes only add nonpositive terms.\n\nThus the actual-mask bound is\n\\[\n H(X)\\le \\tfrac32E_0(X)+\\tfrac54P(X)+\\tfrac58E_2(X)+O(1). \\tag{16}\n\\]\nNo independent branch realization is assumed: each term is assigned to the\nunique birth ancestor of its active boundary, and the negative even prefix\nis retained rather than dropped.\n\nFinally, AC12 and AC14 give\n\\[\n E_0=4Z_R-3D_R-2P+O(1),\\qquad\n E_2=E-4Z_R+3D_R+P+O(1).\n\\]\nSubstituting these into \\(E=4Z_R-3D_R+H+O(1)\\) and (16) yields\n\\[\n E\\le20Z_R-15D_R-3P+O(1)\\le20Z_R+O(1).             \\tag{17}\n\\]\nSince \\(Z_R\\) is a birth subset, this proves the finite-harmonic\nequivalence\n\\[\n \\sum e(n)/n\u003c\\infty\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\sum_{n\\in Z_R}1/n\u003c\\infty,                         \\tag{18}\n\\]\nwithin the already verified AC finite-cutoff scope. The proof does not\nbound \\(Z_R\\) itself and therefore does not by itself prove finite energy\nor Collatz.\n\n## 8. Full SB1--SB18 read and final audit scope\n\nI read the complete\nSIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md, including SB1--SB18.\nSB1--SB4 correctly derive the signed \\(K=2L_{\\rm odd}-3L_2\\) potential,\nthe six \\(Q=(5/2,2,0)\\) inequalities, and the finite-prefix telescope.\nSB8's depth split is valid for every first odd depth, the never-odd case,\nand cutoffs before that depth; its right and left coefficients agree with\n(14)--(15).\n\nSB5's exact R-column masks agree with (8). In particular, at \\(h=1\\) no\nextra \\(d(8q)\\) condition exists: the odd-base filter and colors already\nforce the predecessor birth, whose index is \\(8u\\) for \\(q=3u\\). SB6\ncorrectly accounts for both the affine source/target cutoff annulus and\nthe column-truncation boundary; the latter is the top-index sum in\n\\((X/2,X]\\), also uniformly \\(O(1)\\). Its bracket is nonnegative.\n\nSB7's first-fall lower bound \\(1/2\\), later-fall upper bound \\(1/6\\), and\nthe cutoff ranges in SB15 are correct. The exact defect conversion\n\\(Q(Y)=G(Y/3)/3\\) gives SB16, and SB10 then gives both directions in\nSB17. These are reductions to the nonnegative multiplicative defect\n\\(G\\), not a proof that \\(G\\) is finite. SB18 is an exact commutator\nidentity, but its absolute summability remains an open input. No root\nstate, map, or queue was changed in this audit.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — audit of the single odd-growth graph\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no mathematical\nprogram, CAS, enumeration or Lean was used. This reviews the proposed\nreplacement of the H/G₃ graph by the single odd shortcut\n\\[\nS(q)=\\frac{3q+1}{2}\\qquad(q\\ \\text{odd}),\n\\]\nretained only when the output is an actual birth.\n\n## 1. The exact one-step color dichotomy\n\nLet \\(q\\) be an odd birth of color\n\\[\nc=\\beta(q)=\\beta(q+1),\\qquad\n\\beta(3q+2)=1-c.\n\\]\nSince \\(S(q)=T(q)\\),\n\\[\n\\beta(S(q))=c,\\qquad\n\\beta(2S(q)+1)=\\beta(3q+2)=1-c.\n\\]\nThe birth law at \\(m=S(q)\\) therefore gives the exact identity\n\\[\ne(m)=1-d(m).                                           \\tag{1}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(e(m)=1\\) precisely when \\(\\beta(m+1)=c\\), which is precisely\n\\(d(m)=0\\).\n\nConsequently:\n\n- if \\(e(S(q))=1\\), then \\(S(q)\\) is an actual birth of the same color\n  and the retained edge \\(q\\to S(q)\\) is valid;\n- if \\(e(S(q))=0\\), then \\(d(S(q))=1\\). Its nearest birth ancestor\n  satisfies\n  \\[\n  b\\le\\floor{S(q)/2}\n   =\\floor{(3q+1)/4}\u003cq \\qquad(q\\ge3).                    \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\nThe edge case \\(q=1\\) cannot occur because \\(e(1)=0\\). Thus the\none-step color calculation and the active-boundary failure alternative\nare correct.\n\n## 2. Even births and the actual S-graph\n\nFor an even birth \\(n=2s\\), the exact invariant calculation gives\n\\[\n\\beta(s)=\\beta(3s+2)\\ne\\beta(3s+1),\n\\]\nso \\(d(3s+1)=1\\). BR2 then supplies a birth\n\\[\nb\\le\\floor{(3s+1)/2}\n =\\floor{(3n+2)/4}\u003cn \\qquad(n\\ge4).                     \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe edge \\(n=2\\) is excluded by \\(e(2)=0\\).\n\nDefine the single-odd retained graph to have an edge \\(q\\to S(q)\\) only\nwhen \\(q\\) is an odd actual birth and \\(e(S(q))=1\\). The map is\ninjective: for \\(q=2k+1\\),\n\\[\nS(q)=3k+2\\equiv2\\pmod3,\n\\]\nand the inverse on its image is \\(q=(2m-1)/3\\). Thus every vertex has\nat most one incoming retained edge.\n\n## 3. Finiteness and exact chain weight\n\nFor a retained S-chain \\(n_0=a,n_1,\\ldots\\), while the inputs remain\nodd,\n\\[\nn_j+1=\\left(\\frac32\\right)^j(a+1).                     \\tag{4}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nv_2(n_j+1)=v_2(a+1)-j.\n\\]\nAfter \\(r=v_2(a+1)\\) odd inputs, \\(n_r+1\\) is odd and \\(n_r\\) is even,\nso there is no further S-edge. Every actual S-component is therefore\nfinite, irrespective of the color masks.\n\nThe endpoint formula also gives\n\\[\nn_j=\\left(\\frac32\\right)^j(a+1)-1\n   >\\left(\\frac32\\right)^j a\\quad(j\\ge1).\n\\]\nHence the full component weight obeys\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{r}\\frac1{n_j}\n \\le \\frac1a\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac23\\right)^j\n =\\frac3a.                                             \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThis is a valid replacement for the BC factor \\(9\\) for this particular\nS-graph. The graph includes even terminal births, but they have no\noutgoing S-edge.\n\n## 4. Why this is not yet global descent\n\nThe local alternatives do not compare every terminal transfer with the\noriginal chain start. Suppose an odd birth \\(a\\) follows retained S-edges\nthrough odd births until an even endpoint. Put\n\\[\nr=v_2(a+1),\\qquad a+1=2^r w,\\quad w\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nn_r=3^r w-1\n\\]\nis the even endpoint. Applying (3) there gives only\n\\[\nb\\le\\floor{\\frac{3n_r+2}{4}}\n =\\floor{\\frac{3^{r+1}w-1}{4}}.                       \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe original start is \\(a=2^r w-1\\). The leading ratio in (6) versus\n\\(a\\)\nis\n\\[\n\\frac{3^{r+1}}{2^{r+2}}\n =\\frac34\\left(\\frac32\\right)^r,\n\\]\nwhich is already \\(9/8>1\\) for \\(r=1\\) and grows thereafter. Thus (6)\ndoes not imply \\(b\u003ca\\); it only gives \\(b\u003cn_r\\).\n\nLikewise, if an intermediate output \\(n_{j+1}\\) fails (1), its active\nboundary gives \\(b\\le\\floor{n_{j+1}/2}\\). This is below \\(a\\) for the\nfirst output in the usual range, but after one or more retained S-steps\n\\(n_{j+1}/2\\) can exceed \\(a\\). Therefore finite S-chain length and\none-step descent from the current endpoint do not supply a decreasing\nrank from the original birth.\n\nThe exact conclusion is: every odd birth either receives a smaller birth\nbefore the next retained S-edge, or moves along a finite retained S-chain\nto an even terminal birth, where BR2 gives a smaller birth relative to\nthat terminal index. A further comparison of that birth with the\noriginal start is missing. No global smaller-birth theorem follows\nwithout that comparison.\n\n## 5. Scope\n\nThe one-step identity (1), even-birth transfer (3), injectivity, finite\ncomponent structure, and weight bound (5) are valid with actual masks.\nThe construction does not assert a global descent after an even exit,\ndoes not exclude returns to a prior component or another component, and\ndoes not prove full Collatz. It does show that, if this graph is useful\nfor energy bookkeeping, its internal chain factor is \\(3\\), while the\nterminal-transfer problem remains outside the graph.\n\n## 6. Audit of image origins and the proposed \\(4/9\\) charge\n\nHistorical limited-local audit, superseded in scope by the explicit SG4\ninverse lemma and §§8–9 below. The warning concerns inference from the\nchild identities alone, not the final result for an exact T-invariant.\n\nLet \\(D_S\\) be the origins of actual S-components that lie in the image\nof \\(S\\). If \\(n=S(q)\\) is a birth and is an S-origin, then \\(q\\) is not\na birth. With \\(c=\\beta(n)=\\beta(q)\\), the exact paths give\n\\[\n\\beta(q+1)=1-c,\\qquad\n\\beta(2q+1)=\\beta(3q+2)=1-c.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nd(q)=1,\\qquad d(2q)=1,\\qquad d(2q+1)=0.                \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThe last two identities prove that the unique active child of the active\nboundary \\(q\\) is \\(2q\\), which is even.\n\nHowever, (7) does **not** imply \\(d(\\floor{q/2})=1\\). Writing\n\\(q=2s+1\\), one has\n\\[\nd(q)=|\\beta(s+1)-\\beta(q)|=1,\n\\]\nbut \\(d(s)=|\\beta(s+1)-\\beta(s)|\\) depends on the still-uncontrolled\ncolor \\(\\beta(s)\\). If \\(d(s)=0\\), then \\(s\\) itself is the nearest\nbirth ancestor and the charge depth is \\(\\ell=1\\). No identity in the\nlocal child calculation excludes this case. Therefore \\(\\ell\\ge2\\)\nand the bound \\(2/(3b)\\) are not established by (7) alone. SG4's separate\nstrip merger establishes them for the actual S-image case, as recorded below.\n\nWhat does follow is a weaker multiplicity estimate. All charged\n\\(q\\)'s are odd. On a fixed one of the two active descendant branches\nof a birth ancestor \\(b\\), two charged depths cannot be adjacent: after\na charged odd \\(q\\), (7) forces the next active boundary to be the even\nchild \\(2q\\). The branch containing the odd depth-one child contributes\nat most\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac1{2^{1+2k}b}=\\frac{2}{3b},\n\\]\nand the other branch has an even depth-one child, so its first possible\ncharged depth is two and it contributes at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac1{2^{2+2k}b}=\\frac{1}{3b}.\n\\]\nHence the unconditional bound from the stated facts is\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{q charged to }b}\\frac1q\\le\\frac1b.      \\tag{8}\n\\]\nSince \\(n=S(q)=(3q+1)/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1n\\le\\frac{2}{3q}.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\le X\\), \\(q\u003c(2X)/3\\), and with only \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) one has\n\\(b\\le q/2\u003cX/3\\). Therefore the checked image-origin estimate is\n\\[\nE_{D_S}(X)\\le \\frac23 E_\\beta(X/3).                    \\tag{9}\n\\]\nSG4 now supplies \\(\\ell\\ge2\\) for every such actual image charge, so\n(8) improves to \\(2/(3b)\\), the cutoff becomes \\(X/6\\), and the proposed\n\\[\nE_{D_S}(X)\\le\\frac49E_\\beta(X/6)\n\\]\nfollows. The point is that this depth statement comes from SG4's strip\nmerger, not from the unique-child fact alone.\n\n## 7. Arithmetic sharpness of the factor \\(3\\)\n\nThe unmasked S arithmetic graph has primitive starts with arbitrarily\nlong odd runs. Take\n\\[\na=4^L-1.\n\\]\nBecause \\(4^L\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), \\(a\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), whereas every S-image\ninteger is \\(2\\pmod3\\); hence \\(a\\) has no S arithmetic predecessor.\nFor \\(0\\le j\\le2L\\),\n\\[\nn_j+1=3^j2^{2L-j},\\qquad\nn_j=3^j2^{2L-j}-1.\n\\]\nThe inputs \\(n_0,\\ldots,n_{2L-1}\\) are odd and the endpoint \\(n_{2L}\\)\nis even, so the odd run has length \\(2L\\). For every fixed \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{n_j}\\longrightarrow\\left(\\frac23\\right)^j\n\\quad(L\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nand the geometric upper bound supplies domination. Consequently\n\\[\n\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}a\\sum_{j=0}^{2L}\\frac1{n_j}\n =\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\left(\\frac23\\right)^j=3.                 \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThis proves that \\(3/a\\) cannot be uniformly improved on the arithmetic\nsuperset. The family is not asserted to consist of actual births, so\n(10) is not an actual-mask counterexample.\n\n## 8. Audit of the proposed image-origin improvement\n\nThe forced-child calculation proves only that a charged inverse q is\nactive and that its unique active child is the even boundary 2q. It does\nnot by itself determine d(floor(q/2)). Writing q=2s+1, d(q)=1 fixes\nbeta(s+1) != beta(q), while d(s) still depends on beta(s).\n\nThe later strip argument SG4 supplies the missing fact for actual births\nin the S image: q=2^h v-1 and n=S(q)=X_(h,1) imply\nC_(h-1)=C_h. Therefore d(q)=d(floor(q/2)), and if q is not a birth both\nare active. The earlier forced-child warning is superseded for this\nactual image case; it remains valid only as a statement about the local\nchild calculation alone.\n\nThe recurrence used for later charges is valid once a first charge is\nfixed. If the next charged boundary is r>=2 levels below q, then\nq' >= 2^r q and v2(q'+1) \u003c= r-1. With\nA_(r-1)=sum_{j=1}^{r-1}(2/3)^j and R=2/9,\n\n    (A_(r-1)+R)/2^r \u003c= R.\n\nFor r=2 this is equality; r=3 gives 1/6; and r>=4 is bounded by\n(2+2/9)/16=5/36. Thus the future mass after a charged q is at most R/q.\n\nBecause SG4 proves every first charge has depth at least two, the left\nbranch contributes at most R/b=2/(9b). The right branch first charge\nalso has q>=4b, so its own component costs at most 2/(4b), and its\nfuture costs at most R/(4b). It contributes at most\n(2+R)/(4b)=5/(9b). The total is therefore 7/(9b).\n\nFor a component origin n\u003c=X, SG4 gives its charged ancestor b\u003cX/6.\nConsequently the complete image-origin component mass obeys\n\n    M_DS(X) \u003c= (7/9) E_beta(X/6),\n\nand, with the factor-3 bound for components whose origins lie outside\nthe image,\n\n    E_beta(X) \u003c= 3 E_PS(X) + (7/9) E_beta(X/6).\n\nThe cutoff, branch multiplicities, and the passage from origin weight to\nfull component weight are all correctly normalized. This is a contraction\nfor the image-origin contribution, but it leaves the primitive source\nP_S uncontrolled.\n\n## 9. SG1-SG12 audit and final verdict\n\nSG1 and SG2 use the exact XOR/birth identities and the even-birth BR2\ntransfer with the correct strict inequalities. The finite edge cases\ne(1)=e(2)=0 and e(4m+2)=0 are correctly handled.\n\nSG3 is correct: S is injective with image 2 modulo 3, the valuation of\nn_j+1 drops by one along each odd step, and every actual S-component is\nfinite with weight at most 3/a. SG4 is the essential correction to the\nprevious local audit. Its strip formula n=X_(h,1), the merger at\n3^h v, the h=1 case, and the excluded corner (q,n)=(1,2) all check.\nThe positivity condition 3^(h-1)v>=3 is valid; for h=1 it follows from\nv>=3 after the e(4m+2) exclusion.\n\nSG5-SG8 are then valid: charged q's have depth at least two, charged\ndepths on either branch are separated by at least two, and the A_h/R\nrecurrence gives 7/(9b) for the complete components charged to one birth.\nSG9 correctly uses origin cutoff b\u003cX/6 and does not truncate components at\nX. SG10 follows by finite subtraction:\nE_beta(X)\u003c=3E_PS(X)+(7/9)E_beta(X/6), and\nE_beta(X/6)\u003c=E_beta(X) imply\n\n    E_beta(X) \u003c= (27/2) E_PS(X),\n    E_PS(X) \u003c= E_beta(X).\n\nThus E_beta is finite iff the primitive-source energy E_PS is finite.\nThis is an equivalence of finite-cutoff comparisons, not a proof that\neither energy is finite. SG11's old-graph caveat and SG12's scope do not\noverclaim a global descent or Collatz conclusion.\n\nThe earlier provisional 4/3 estimate in this report is superseded by\nSG4's proved depth-two lemma and the sharper 7/9 image-origin estimate.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_TERMINAL_COLOR_SPACING.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — terminal color spacing for S/R edges\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no program, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean or rendering was used. The current PROJECT_STATE,\nCOLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md, FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md,\nand SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md were read for this\nbounded audit. The goal is a terminal charging statement without double\ncounting; no terminal sparsity theorem is claimed.\n\n## 1. Exact terminal types and the common spacing rule\n\nLet x be a terminal birth and y=F(x). Then e(y)=0 and the exact S/R color\nidentities give d(y)=1.\n\nFor an S-terminal, x is odd, y=S(x), and if c=beta(x),\n\n    beta(y)=c, beta(y+1)=1-c, beta(2y+1)=1-c.\n\nThus d(2y)=1 and d(2y+1)=0: the forced next active boundary is 2y. Since\ny is an S-image, y=2 mod 3, so 2y=1 mod 3.\n\nFor an R-terminal, x=4m, y=R(x)=3m, and\n\n    beta(y)=1-c, beta(y+1)=c, beta(2y+1)=1-c.\n\nThus d(2y)=0 and d(2y+1)=1: the forced next active boundary is 2y+1.\nSince y is an R-image, y=0 mod 3, so 2y+1=1 mod 3.\n\nEvery terminal target y is itself an arithmetic image, hence is 0 or 2\nmodulo 3. Its forced active child is 1 modulo 3 and cannot itself be\nanother terminal target. Along either active ancestry path, terminal targets\ntherefore occur at depths separated by at least two. Starting from a birth\nb, the all-type target bound is\n\n    sum_(all terminal targets on both paths) 1/y \u003c= 4/(3b).  (1)\n\nThe two paths and all targets are disjoint, so (1) has no double counting.\n\nThe exact reciprocal costs are\n\n    1/x = 3/(2y) + 3/[2y(2y-1)]       for S,\n    1/x = 3/(4y)                       for R.            (2)\n\nThe S correction summed over distinct y>=2 is at most\n(sum_{y>=2}3/[2y(y-1)]=3/2). Combining (1)–(2) recovers the complete\nendpoint estimate\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c= 2 E_beta((3X+1)/4) + 3/2.        (3)\n\nThis is the joint all-terminal coefficient. It is already free of terminal\ndouble counting and does not assume a terminal counting sparsity bound.\n\n## 2. S-terminal strip strengthening for h>=2\n\nLet an S-terminal birth be x=2^h v-1 with v odd and\n(h=v_2(x+1)\\ge2). In strip notation,\n\n    y=S(x)=X_(h,1),\n    floor(y/2)=X_(h-1,1).                              (4)\n\nThe birth x gives (C_h\\ne C_{h+1}). The strip merger identity gives\n(C_{h-1}=C_h). Since\n\n    beta(y)=C_h,       beta(y+1)=B_1,\n    beta(floor(y/2))=C_(h-1)=C_h,\n    beta(floor(y/2)+1)=B_1,\n\nwe have\n\n    d(y)=d(floor(y/2)).                                 (5)\n\nTerminality gives d(y)=1, hence both y and floor(y/2) are active\nboundaries. The nearest birth ancestor is consequently at depth at least\ntwo, and\n\n    b \u003c= floor(y/4).                                    (6)\n\nFor these high-h S-targets, the two-path spacing gives\n\n    sum_(h>=2 S-targets charged to b) 1/y \u003c= 2/(3b).    (7)\n\nSince x=(2y-1)/3, (2), (6), and (7) yield the separate refined estimate\n\n    E_(S,h>=2)(X)\n       \u003c= E_beta((3X+1)/8) + 3/2.                      (8)\n\nThe cutoff is (b\\le y/4=(3x+1)/8). Equation (8) is a bound for this\nsubclass only; it must not be added to the full estimate (3) as though the\nsame ancestor budget were available twice.\n\n## 3. The h=1 S-terminal and R-terminal remainder\n\nFor h=1, SC3 forces v=3 mod 4. Hence\n\n    x=2v-1=5 mod 8,\n    y=S(x)=3v-1=0 mod 4.                               (9)\n\nTerminality still gives d(y)=1 and forced active child 2y, but the strip\nidentity used in (5) does not identify d(floor(y/2)); no depth-two nearest\nbirth conclusion has been established for this h=1 class. Its first target\nmay therefore occur at depth one.\n\nR-terminals likewise have forced active child 2y+1 but no additional\nuniform depth-two conclusion. Their target y is 0 mod 3, whereas all S\ntargets are 2 mod 3, so the two terminal types are disjoint. The h=1 S\nclass and R class may both occupy the depth-one allowance on different\nactive paths; without a further color identity, their joint remainder\nmust retain the all-type budget in (1).\n\nFor reference, separate coarse bounds are\n\n    E_(S,h=1)(X) \u003c= 2 E_beta((3X+1)/4) + 3/2,\n    E_R(X)       \u003c= E_beta(3X/8),                       (10)\n\nbut summing (8) and (10) is weaker than the single no-double-count bound\n(3), with the refined coefficient 1 in (8) only for the h>=2 S subclass.\nThe preliminary joint coefficient at this stage was 2; it is superseded\nby the improvements in Sections 5--6 below.\n\n## 4. Scope and remaining gap\n\nThe strip color identity gives a real terminal-spacing improvement for\nS-terminals with (v_2(x+1)\\ge2), including its tighter cutoff. It does\nnot provide a uniform improvement for h=1 S-terminals or R-terminals, and\ntherefore does not prove a power-saving bound for all terminals. The BM/EC\nconditional endpoint profiles remain conditional: no actual terminal\nsparsity, finite total energy, pointwise constancy, cycle exclusion, or\nfull Collatz conclusion follows here.\n\n## 5. Joint weighted improvement from the universal 4 mod 8 exclusion\n\nThere is a universal boundary identity\n\n    d(8t+4)=0.                                       (11)\n\nIndeed, with q=2t+1 odd,\n\n    beta(4q)=beta(q)=beta(3q+1)=beta(4q+1).\n\nHence a terminal target y, which has d(y)=1, cannot satisfy\n(v_2(y)=2). In particular, an even S-target has (v_2(y)\\ge3). For\nh=1 this removes the n=13 mod 16 subcase, leaving n=5 mod 16; for h>=2\nthe S-target is odd and already has the strip depth-two property.\n\nThere is a second exact exclusion. Two S-terminal targets y and 4y cannot\nboth occur. If y=S(n) for a birth n, the source for 4y as an S-target\nwould be n'=4n+1. Birth colors give\n\n    beta(4n+1)=beta(3n+1)=beta(n),\n    beta(4n+2)=beta(2n+1)=1-beta(n),\n\nso d(4n+1)=1 and n' is not a birth. Thus a target at gap two after an\nS-target cannot be S; the only possible gap-two target is R at 4y+1.\nAfter an R-target y, the left grandchild 4y+2 has v2=1 and residue 2 mod 3,\nso it cannot be an S-target, while the right grandchild 4y+3 is R-residue.\nTherefore after every endpoint target, a next target at gap two is R, and\nthe next S-target (if any) is at gap at least three.\n\nUse main reciprocal costs\n\n    a_S=3/2,       a_R=3/4.\n\nFor a finite target string beginning at an S-target, normalize by the\nreciprocal of that target. The transition rule gives the backward-inductive\nbounds\n\n    V_S >= 3/2 + max(V_R/4,V_S/8),\n    V_R >= 3/4 + max(V_R/4,V_S/8).\n\nThese are supersolution inequalities; for the selected values below both\nare equalities.\n\nThe pair\n\n    V_S=7/4,       V_R=1                              (12)\n\nis stable under these inequalities: the common future maximum is\nmax(1/4,7/32)=1/4. Applying this to finite strings and passing to increasing\nlengths proves the same upper bounds for arbitrary target strings, without\nassuming their total energy is finite.\n\nAt a birth ancestor b, the left active path starts at an even boundary and\nthe right path at an odd boundary. A depth-one S-target is therefore\npossible only on the left, and it must be the even S case. Hence the left\npath contributes at most\n\n    max(V_S/2,V_R/2,max(V_S,V_R)/4)=7/8,\n\nwhile the right path contributes at most (V_R/2=1/2): its depth-one target\ncannot be S, and a first target at depth at least two costs at most V_S/4.\nThus the joint main cost of all terminal targets charged to b is at most\n\n    7/8+1/2=11/8,\n\nso the charge is 11/(8b).                           (13)\n\nThe S reciprocal correction remains globally at most 3/2. With the usual\ncutoffs (b\\le(3x+1)/4) for S-terminals and (b\\le3x/8) for R-terminals,\nall terminal births are therefore counted without overlap by the sharpened\nbound\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c= (11/8) E_beta((3X+1)/4) + 3/2.   (14)\n\nThis is an endpoint estimate, not a contraction for total energy: the\ncoefficient 11/8 is still larger than one, and no terminal counting\nsparsity follows. It does, however, improve the previous joint coefficient\n2 while retaining the exact S/R masks and the h=1 exception. The actual\nfinite-component mass and the PD-free pointwise step remain open.\n\n## 6. Ancestor-residue weighted endpoint coefficients\n\nThe common target DP can be sharpened at the first depth from an ancestor b.\nWrite (V_S=7/4), (V_R=1) for the normalized future bounds in (12).\nAll coefficients below refer to the main reciprocal cost; the single global\nS-correction (3/2) from (2) is added only once at the end.\n\nIf (b=0\\pmod3), the left depth-one boundary (2b) is R-residue, while\nthe right depth-one boundary (2b+1) is residue 1 and cannot be a target.\nAt depth two on the right, (4b+2) is residue 2 with (v_2=1), hence\ncannot be S, and (4b+3) is R-residue. Thus the left path costs at most\n(V_R/2=1/2), the right path at most (V_R/4=1/4), and\n\n    terminal main cost charged to b \u003c= 3/(4b).          (15)\n\nIf (b=2\\pmod3), there is no depth-one target: (2b) is residue 1 and\n(2b+1) is odd S-forbidden at depth one. The left S-candidate (4b) at\ndepth two is also impossible. Indeed, write (b=3s+2), let c=beta(b),\nand set (n=8s+5), the S-source of (4b). Then\n\n    beta(n)=beta(b)=c,\n    beta(n+1)=beta(4s+3)=beta(6s+5)=beta(2b+1)=1-c,\n\nso d(n)=1 and n is not a birth. The other depth-two left candidate\n(4b+1) is R-residue. Hence the left path costs at most (V_R/4=1/4).\nOn the right, the first possible target is the S-target (4b+3) at depth\ntwo, costing at most (V_S/4=7/16). Therefore\n\n    terminal main cost charged to b \u003c= 11/(16b).         (16)\n\nIf (b=1\\pmod3) is odd, the left depth-one boundary (2b) has (v_2=1)\nand cannot be an S-target; it is not R-residue either. The right depth-one\nboundary (2b+1) is R-residue and costs at most (V_R/2=1/2). The first\npossible left S-target is at depth two, with cost at most (V_S/4=7/16).\nThus\n\n    terminal main cost charged to b \u003c= 15/(16b).         (17)\n\nIf (b=1\\pmod3) is even, an even birth is divisible by four. The generic\nleft/right bounds from (13) apply: the left path may start with an even\nS-target at depth one and costs at most (V_S/2=7/8), while the right path\ncosts at most (V_R/2=1/2). Hence\n\n    terminal main cost charged to b \u003c= 11/(8b).          (18)\n\nThe four ancestor classes are disjoint, and every endpoint target has one\nnearest birth ancestor and one path, so (15)–(18) do not double count. For\nterminal births x\u003c=X, all charged ancestors satisfy\n(b\\le(3X+1)/4). Therefore the residue-weighted estimate is\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c=\n      (3/4)  E_beta(B; b=0 mod 3)\n    + (11/16) E_beta(B; b=2 mod 3)\n    + (15/16) E_beta(B; b=1 mod 3, b odd)\n    + (11/8)  E_beta(B; b=1 mod 3, b even)\n    + 3/2,\n\n    B=floor((3X+1)/4).                               (19)\n\nThis refines the uniform (11/8) bound when the ancestor residue classes\nare separately controlled. It is still only an endpoint-energy estimate;\nwithout such classwise control it gives no terminal counting power saving\nand no contraction for total birth energy.\n\n## 7. Final audit of WS1--WS9\n\nThe full root file\n`WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md` was read. WS1--WS4 are\nconsistent with the exact masks: the identity d(8t+4)=0, the odd S-target\ndepth-two strip argument, the S/S gap-two exclusion, and the S/R costs all\nhave the stated indices. The finite-string DP gives V_S=7/4 and V_R=1;\nits initial left/right depths yield 11/8, and the four ancestor classes give\n3/4, 11/16, 15/16, and 11/8 exactly as in (19). The old display (13) is\nnow explicitly normalized: 7/8+1/2=11/8, hence 11/(8b).\n\nWS5--WS7 are consistent at the stated surface level. The U-vector\n(232,172,184)/189 satisfies the two displayed backward inequalities, the\nWS6 table follows from the residue of the first odd boundary and the\ndepth factors, and 241/378 is the largest of the three combined-origin\ncoefficients. These are origin charges only; WS9 does not bound the full\nretained component mass and does not supply endpoint/origin sparsity.\nNo cycle exclusion, nonconstant invariant, or PD-free pointwise step is\nclaimed.\n","FOLLOWUP_0431_TWO_PRIMITIVE_BIRTHS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0431 — two primitive ternary births on one column\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no programs, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean or numerical experiments.\n\n## The two masks and their actual paths\n\nLet \\(m\\) be odd, \\(3\\nmid m\\), and suppose \\(2\\le a\u003cb\\) with primitive\nbirths at \\(z_a=3^a m\\) and \\(z_b=3^b m\\). Their common primitive color\nis \\(B\\), while both birth middle points\n\\[\n\\mu_j=2\\cdot3^j m+1\n\\]\nhave the opposite color \\(A=1-B\\):\n\\[\n\\beta(3^j m)=\\beta(3^j m+1)=B,\\qquad\n\\beta(\\mu_j)=A,\\qquad j\\in\\{a,b\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe genuine middle path is\n\\[\n\\mu_j\\longmapsto 3^{j+1}m+2=3z_j+2,\n\\]\nso (1) is exactly the birth mask at \\(z_j\\). The birth mask gives\n\\(\\beta(z_j+1)=\\beta(z_j)\\), while the primitive hypothesis gives\n\\(\\beta(z_j/3)=\\beta(z_j)\\). The actual transfer is\n\\[\nT(z_j+1)=T(z_j/3)=(z_j+1)/2,\n\\]\nnot an equality \\((z_j+1)/2=T(z_j)\\). No fictitious path between\n\\(\\mu_a\\) and \\(\\mu_b\\) is available.\n\nThe primitive chain between the two births only says\n\\[\n\\beta(3^a m)=\\cdots=\\beta(3^b m)=B. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus the two middle points have the same color \\(A\\), and a hypothetical\nfinite path merging \\(\\mu_a\\) with \\(\\mu_b\\) would be color-consistent,\nnot contradictory.\n\n## What a genuine two-mask contradiction would require\n\nA contradiction would follow if one could exhibit, for some arithmetic\nsubclass and gap \\(d=b-a\\), a real finite \\(T\\)-path from either\n\\(\\mu_a\\) or \\(\\mu_b\\) to a member \\(3^\\ell m\\) of the primitive column:\nthat path would force \\(A=B\\). The displayed maps\n\\[\n\\mu_j=2\\cdot3^j m+1\\longmapsto3^{j+1}m+2\n\\]\ndo not have that form, and the next odd step depends on the residue of\n\\(3^{j+1}m+2\\) modulo \\(4\\). No gap-only collision follows from (1)--(2).\nSimilarly, a path between the two middle points merely equates \\(A\\) with\n\\(A\\).\n\nFor \\(b=a+1\\), the two masks give the local pattern\n\\[\n\\beta(3^a m)=\\beta(3^a m+1)=B,\\quad\n\\beta(2\\cdot3^a m+1)=A,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\beta(3^{a+1}m)=\\beta(3^{a+1}m+1)=B,\\quad\n\\beta(2\\cdot3^{a+1}m+1)=A.\n\\]\nThis is two repeated birth patterns, not a new smaller birth or a\ncontradiction. In particular \\(h(3^j m)=0\\) for \\(j=0,\\ldots,b-1\\)\nunder the two primitive masks (with \\(2\\le a\u003cb\\)); the endpoint\n\\(3^b m\\) is itself a birth and is not included in this zero range.\nThus TL's last-change charge is unavailable on the preceding column\nsegment.\n\n## Residue exclusions remain one-mask inputs\n\nThe inherited \\(e(n)=0\\) exclusion for \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod8\\) still rules out\nthe two-mask configuration whenever either \\(3^a m\\) or \\(3^b m\\) is\n\\(1\\bmod8\\). The BR actual-smaller-birth route for \\(n\\equiv5\\bmod16\\)\nstill applies to either birth separately. These are valid consequences,\nbut they use one birth mask at a time and do not impose a new restriction\non the gap \\(b-a\\).\n\nTherefore the two-mask hypothesis alone yields no additional\nparameterized exclusion or strict smaller-birth transfer from the finite\npaths currently available. The exact missing input is a gap-dependent\npath collision from a middle point to the primitive column, or another\ncolor identity involving both masks. No such identity is claimed here;\nnonconstant-invariant existence and all remaining primitive mass stay open.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_BACKWARD_COUNT_TRANSPORT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — backward count transport from origins\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Ручная проверка без\nматематических программ, CAS, переборов и Lean. Проверены PROJECT_STATE.md,\nSR и BM. Цель — проверить условный перенос оценки числа origins на полное\nчисло births через конечную backward-глубину.\n\n## 1. Точная finite-depth декомпозиция\n\nПусть \\(R_\\beta(X)\\) — число births \\(n\\le X\\), а \\(O_\\beta(Y)\\) — число\norigins retained-графа \\(F_\\beta\\), то есть births без входящего retained\nребра. Arithmetic inverse branches имеют вид\n\n\\[\n B(y)=4y/3\\quad(y\\equiv0\\pmod3),\\qquad\n B(y)=(2y-1)/3\\quad(y\\equiv2\\pmod3).\n\\]\n\nКаждый backward шаг увеличивает положительное значение не более чем в\n\\(4/3\\) раза. Для целого \\(k\\ge1\\) разделим births \\(n\\le X\\) на два\nкласса. Если у \\(n\\) есть не менее \\(k\\) retained predecessors, то он\nлежит в arithmetic backward prefix длины \\(k\\). SR даёт для этого класса\n\n\\[\n \\#\\{n\\le X:\\text{есть }k\\text{ backward steps}\n \\}\\le X(2/3)^k+2^k.\n\\]\n\nЕсли backward-depth равен \\(j\u003ck\\), то после \\(j\\) обратных шагов мы\nприходим к единственному origin \\(o\\), причём\n\\[\n o\\le (4/3)^j n\\le (4/3)^kX.\n\\]\nУ одного origin есть не более одного descendant на каждой фиксированной\nглубине. Поэтому короткие цепи следует суммировать по глубине, а не заменять\nвсю сумму её максимальным членом:\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= X(2/3)^k + 2^k\n                 + sum_{j=0}^{k-1} O_beta((4/3)^j X).       (1)\n\nЦиклические и бесконечные backward-компоненты не теряются: у их вершин\nесть k backward steps для любого фиксированного k, поэтому они попадают\nв первый arithmetic prefix. Для конечной цепи origin-counting учитывает\nвсе глубины 0,...,j\u003ck без повторного счёта.\n\nЭто не утверждает существование хорошей оценки для \\(O_\\beta\\). Оно лишь\nточно сводит полный count к origin-count при наличии такой оценки.\n\n## 2. Polynomial origin count\n\nПредположим условно, что для некоторого 0 \u003c= theta \u003c 1\nO_beta(Y)=O(Y^theta). В (1) положим k=floor(a ln X), a>0. Если theta>0,\nгеометрическая сумма по коротким глубинам имеет порядок\nX^theta (4/3)^(k theta), поэтому показатели трёх членов равны\n\n    f1(a)=1-a ln(3/2),\n    f2(a)=a ln 2,\n    f3(a)=theta+a theta ln(4/3).\n\nБаланс f1=f2 даёт a1=1/ln 3 и показатель\n gamma=ln 2/ln 3. Баланс f1=f3 даёт\n\n    a2=(1-theta)/(ln(3/2)+theta ln(4/3)),\n    rho2=theta ln 2/(ln(3/2)+theta ln(4/3)).\n\nВыбирая соответствующий баланс, получаем без дополнительного\nлогарифмического множителя\n\n    R_beta(X)=O(X^rho),\n    rho=max{ ln 2/ln 3,\n             theta ln 2/(ln(3/2)+theta ln(4/3)) } \u003c 1.       (2)\n\nЕсли rho2 \u003c= gamma, берём a1; третий показатель не превосходит gamma.\nЕсли rho2 > gamma, то theta>1/2, берём a2; второй показатель не превосходит\nтретий. При theta=0 глубинная сумма равна O(k), а выбор a1 поглощает этот\nфактор в O(X^gamma). Таким образом, удаление k действительно сохраняет\nтот же rho и убирает прежний лишний ln X.\n\nЭто conditional reduction, а не безусловный верх для births: оно требует\nотдельной оценки origins.\n\n## 3. Logarithmic origin count and energy\n\nТеперь предположим условно\nO_beta(Y)=O(Y/(ln Y)^q), q>0. Для больших X выбираем\n\n    k=floor((q/ln 2) ln ln X).\n\nПервые два члена в (1) дают\n\n    X(2/3)^k = O(X/(ln X)^p),\n    2^k = O((ln X)^q),\n    p=q ln(3/2)/ln 2.\n\nДля каждой короткой глубины j имеем Y_j=(4/3)^j X >= X, поэтому\n(ln Y_j)^q >= (ln X)^q. Геометрическая сумма даёт\n\n    sum_{j\u003ck} O_beta(Y_j)\n      = O(X(4/3)^k/(ln X)^q)\n      = O(X/(ln X)^p).\n\nСледовательно, без прежнего лишнего ln ln X,\n\n    R_beta(X)=O(X/(ln X)^p).                              (3)\n\nПо partial summation это влечёт finite birth energy\nsum e(n)/n \u003c infinity при p>1, то есть при\n\n    q > ln 2 / ln(3/2).                                    (4)\n\nРавенство p=1 этим расчётом не закрывается. Малые X, где ln ln X не\nположительна, поглощаются константой; выбор k нужен только для больших X.\n\n## 4. Scope and remaining gap\n\nThe finite-depth transport (1) is rigorous once the SR arithmetic prefix\nbound and the unique-origin property are used. It handles cycles and\ninfinite backward paths by the first term, and it uses no probabilistic\nindependence. Equations (2)--(4) are genuine conditional consequences:\nsublinear origin counts would force sublinear birth counts, and sufficiently\nstrong logarithmic origin sparsity would force finite birth energy.\n\nThey do not provide the required origin estimate. In particular, the\nexisting coefficient bounds for the origin classes (P_1,D_S,D_R) do not\nimply either \\(O_\\beta(Y)=O(Y^\\theta)\\) or the logarithmic estimate in\nSection 3. Therefore this transport does not close the full-component mass\nproblem and does not yield a Collatz proof. It is a reduction from an\norigin bound to a birth bound, not a general upper bound for actual births.\n\n\n## 5. General monotone origin profile\n\nThe same transport admits a profile form that does not require slow variation.\nLet H(Y) be an origin-count upper bound satisfying\n\n    H(Y) \u003c= C Y/L(Y),    L(Y) >= 2,\n\nwhere L is nondecreasing. Put r=2/3, g=4/3=2r, and\neta=ln(3/2)/ln 2 = 1 - ln(g)/ln 2. For sufficiently large X choose\n\n    k = floor(log_2 L(X)).\n\nThe first term in (1) is O(X/L(X)^eta), while 2^k \u003c= L(X). Since\nL(g^j X) >= L(X), the short-depth sum satisfies\n\n    sum_{j\u003ck} H(g^j X)\n      \u003c= C X/L(X) * sum_{j\u003ck} g^j\n      = O(X g^k/L(X))\n      = O(X/L(X)^eta).\n\nConsequently\n\n    R_beta(X) \u003c= C' X/L(X)^eta + L(X).                 (5)\n\nThe floor in k changes only absolute constants: g^k/L(X) is bounded by a\nconstant times L(X)^(ln(g)/ln(2)-1). No slow variation, differentiability,\nor asymptotic ratio for L was used.\n\nBy partial summation, (5) is sufficient for finite birth energy whenever\nboth tail integrals\n\n    integral dt/[t L(t)^eta]  and  integral L(t) dt/t^2\n\nare finite. This includes the logarithmic substitutions above and also\ngives a possibly weaker bound for polynomial profiles than the separate\noptimized power calculation. It remains conditional on the origin profile\nH; current origin-class bounds supply no such profile.\n\n## 6. Final audit of EC2--EC7\n\nThe complete EC file was read. EC2 is the valid forward analogue of the\nbackward transport: births with at least k retained forward steps are in the\narithmetic prefix, while a vertex whose first terminal occurs at depth j\u003ck\nhas a unique predecessor for that terminal and cutoff at most\n(3/2)^j (X+1)-1. This counts actual terminals and preserves cycles,\nforward-infinite components, and backward-infinite chains; none is silently\nremoved.\n\nEC3 is the corresponding actual-origin transport already checked above.\nIts short-depth sum must be kept depth by depth, which removes the spurious\nfactor k. EC4's two exponent balances are algebraically consistent for the\nforward row (r=3/4, g=3/2) and backward row (r=2/3, g=4/3). The theta=0\ncase is correctly absorbed by the arithmetic-prefix exponent.\n\nEC5 is valid for a nondecreasing profile L with no slow-variation assumption:\nall relevant cutoffs are at least X, the geometric sum is taken before the\nprofile is replaced by L(X), and the residual 2^k term is retained as L(X).\nEC6 and EC9 give the stated logarithmic and critical-logarithmic thresholds;\npartial summation requires exactly the two tail integrability conditions\nrecorded there.\n\nEC7 correctly identifies the remaining input: endpoint or origin sparsity\nis not supplied by BM's self-referential endpoint energy inequality or by\nSR's 17/18 origin coefficient. The EC10 contrapositive is valid with the\nexplicit qualification that beta is nonconstant: under PD170, an exponent\nbelow one would force finite birth energy and hence constancy. For a constant\nbeta both endpoint counts vanish, so EC10 must not be read as an unconditional\nstatement for that case.\n\nThis audit covered EC2--EC7 and the logical qualification of EC10. It adds a\nconditional transport only; it does not establish the required actual\nendpoint/origin sparsity or solve the full Collatz problem.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_BOUNDARY_BIRTH_DIRICHLET.md":"# AC — Dirichlet transport on active boundaries and the birth graph\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual bounded audit only;\nno programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents. This report uses the\nexact invariant, active boundary d, birth e, and the S/R graph from SR and\nWS. It does not construct a nonconstant invariant or claim a Collatz result.\n\n## AC1. Exact binary identities and the A/B grammar\n\nWrite d(n)=|beta(n+1)-beta(n)| and e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1). For every q,\n\n    d(2q) XOR d(2q+1)=d(q).\n\nFor odd q, invariance gives the additional exact identity\n\n    d(2q)=d(3q+1).                                      (AC1)\n\nIndeed beta(2q)=beta(q), beta(2q+1)=beta(3q+2), while\nd(3q+1)=d(2 S(q)) with S(q)=(3q+1)/2 and beta(S(q))=beta(q).\n\nIf q is an active odd boundary, exactly one child is active. There are two\npossibilities:\n\n    A(q)=2q+1, when d(2q)=0 and d(2q+1)=1;\n    B(q)=4q+1, when d(2q)=1 and d(2q+1)=0.\n\nIn the second case (AC1) and the identity d(4q+1)=d(2q), valid for odd q,\nshow that 4q+1 is the next active odd boundary. These are actual color\ntransitions, not free binary choices. Their residue maps are\n\n    A: i -> 2i+1,       B: i -> i+1       (mod 3).\n\n## AC2. Exact Dirichlet corrections along one active path\n\nFor q odd and an A or B transition, at s=1 one has\n\n    1/A(q) = (1/2)(1/q) - 1/[2q(2q+1)],\n    1/B(q) = (1/4)(1/q) - 1/[4q(4q+1)].              (AC2)\n\nFor s>1 the corresponding exact positive correction is\n\n    (a q)^(-s) - (a q+1)^(-s),  a=2 or 4,\n\nso that (a q+1)^(-s)=a^(-s)q^(-s) minus this correction. If\nq_{j+1}=a_jq_j+1 along a path and Lambda_j=product_{i\u003cj}a_i, then\n\n    q_L^(-s)=Lambda_L^(-s)q_0^(-s)\n      - sum_{i\u003cL} [(a_iq_i)^(-s)-(a_iq_i+1)^(-s)]\n        / product_{i\u003cj\u003cL}a_j^s.\n\nAll corrections have the same sign. Since q_{j+1}>2q_j,\n\n    sum_{j>=0} q_j^(-s) \u003c= q_0^(-s)/(1-2^(-s)),  s>1,              (AC3)\n\nand at s=1 the right side is 2/q_0. This bound does not require finite\nbirth energy: it is a pointwise path estimate followed by monotone finite\nprefix limits. The critical branch ratios are 1/2 for A and 1/4 for B;\nthey do not by themselves force a fixed positive proportion of B steps.\n\n## AC3. Births supply active paths, but this is not a birth identity\n\nIf e(b)=1, then d(2b)=d(2b+1)=1. Hence every birth supplies a right odd\nactive path starting at q_R=2b+1. Its left path starts at 2b; if it has\nan odd active member, the first such member has the form 2^ell b+1 with\nell>=2, as in PC/WS. Active boundaries have a unique nearest birth, so\nthese paths are disjoint when charged to their births.\n\nConsequently (AC3) gives, for s>1, the rough full odd-active-boundary\nestimate obtained by summing the two paths:\n\n    D_odd,s \u003c= (1-2^(-s))^(-1)\n       [sum_b e(b)(2b+1)^(-s) + sum_b e(b)(4b+1)^(-s)],\n\nwhere omitting a nonexistent left path only improves the inequality. In\nthe harmonic limit its leading coefficient is at most 1+1/2=3/2 relative\nto E_beta. Conversely, the right path alone gives\n\n    D_odd,s >= sum_b e(b)(2b+1)^(-s).\n\nThus along a divergent E_s subsequence the direct right paths force a\nliminf D_odd,s/E_s >= 1/2, while (AC3) supplies only an upper bound of\n3/2. Neither bound determines the active-boundary mass or its residues.\n\n## AC4. Exact coupling to S and R origins\n\nLet q be odd and active and put y=S(q)=(3q+1)/2. If y is a birth, then\nd(2y)=1. By (AC1), d(2q)=1, so q follows the B branch. If q were also a\nbirth, it would be a retained S predecessor; if q is not a birth, then\nd(2q+1)=0 (otherwise e(q)=1), so q is the active inverse charged by D_S.\nThus a D_S target can only be charged at an active q on a B transition;\nthe B transition alone does not imply that y is a birth. The exact weight\ntransport is\n\n    1/S(q)=2/(3q)-2/[3q(3q+1)].                       (AC4)\n\nThis is the positive correction behind the usual 2/(3q) bound.\n\nFor a D_R target, the SR inverse description writes the target as\ny=2^h q with q odd active and h>=2. Distinct charges on one path have\nnonadjacent h, hence\n\n    1/y=2^(-h)/q,\n    sum_charges 1/y \u003c= (1/4+1/16+...)/q=1/(3q).       (AC5)\n\nThe S and R charges may occur at the same q. Therefore one cannot subtract\none of the two contributions merely from disjointness of their target\nimages. Equations (AC4)--(AC5) are exact domain/image restrictions, but\nthey remain upper transports from active boundaries to selected origins.\n\n## AC5. Critical normalized ratios and the remaining gap\n\nAt s downarrow 1 the S ratio in (AC4) tends to 2/3, while the maximal\nnonadjacent R ratio in (AC5) is 1/3. Their sum can equal one when both\ntypes charge the same active q. The A/B path ratios remain 1/2 or 1/4.\nThese facts are compatible with the normalized PC12 bookkeeping:\n\n    E_0/E -> 2/3,   E_1/E -> 0,   E_2/E -> 1/3,\n\nunder its stated endpoint/origin-negligibility hypothesis. The direct right\nboundary sends birth residues 0 and 2 to active residues 1 and 2,\nrespectively, so PC12 determines part of the starting active mass, but the\nsubsequent A/B branches and all left paths are not fixed by those three\nbirth ratios. In particular, no lower bound for D_S or D_R relative to E\nfollows from (AC1)--(AC5). Setting those selected failure masses to zero is\nonly a scalar compatibility assignment, not a construction of beta; the\nactual masks may impose further correlations.\n\nThe conclusion in this preliminary AC5 paragraph is superseded by AC7\nbelow: the A/B relations alone do not exclude PC12, but their joint use with\nthe four-state binary residue laws does.\n\n## AC6. Four-state channel audit and the corrected global identities\n\nThere is a useful exact four-state table. Define\n\n    L(n)=d(n)d(2n),   Rb(n)=d(n)d(2n+1).\n\nUsing the XOR identity, the only possible states are\n\n    state 0: (d(n),d(2n),d(2n+1))=(0,0,0),\n    state E: (0,1,1),\n    state L: (1,1,0),\n    state R: (1,0,1).\n\nThus d(2n)=e(n)+L(n) and d(2n+1)=e(n)+Rb(n). For odd n, put\ny=S(n)=(3n+1)/2. The exact identity d(2y)=d(2n) gives\n\n    e(y)+L(y)=e(n)+L(n).                              (AC6)\n\nIt is the total left channel that is preserved. Individual L is not\npreserved: if n is an odd birth and S(n) is an active nonbirth output (the\nfailed S alternative), then L(n)=0 but L(S(n))=1. This output is not a\nD_S target, since D_S consists of birth outputs with nonbirth predecessors.\nLikewise, for n=4m and y=R(n)=3m, direct invariance gives\nd(8m+1)=d(6m+1)=d(2y+1): explicitly,\nbeta(8m+1)=beta(12m+2)=beta(6m+1) and\nbeta(8m+2)=beta(4m+1)=beta(6m+2). Hence\n\n    e(y)+Rb(y)=e(n)+Rb(n).                             (AC7)\n\nAgain only the total right channel is preserved; an R-failure can change an\nE state into an active R state (also distinct from a D_R birth target). The\nchannel flags are therefore preserved as\nE/L versus 0/R under S, and E/R versus 0/L under R.\n\nFor s>1 sum (AC6) over odd n. Write L_2,s for the L-sum on targets\ncongruent to 2 modulo 3, L_odd,s for the L-sum on odd sources, and Rb_0,s\nand Rb_4,s analogously. Since S maps odd n bijectively to the\n2-modulo-3 integers, and\n\n    S(n)^(-s)=(2/3)^s n^(-s)-c_L,s(n),\n    c_L,s(n)=(2/3)^s n^(-s)[1-(1+1/(3n))^(-s)] >=0,\n\none obtains the exact channel identity\n\n    E_2,s+L_2,s=(2/3)^s(A_s+L_odd,s)-C_all,s,         (AC8)\n\nwhere C_all,s is the sum of c_L,s(n) weighted by e(n)+L(n) over odd n.\nThe correction C_ret,s in the PC residue balance is only over retained odd\nbirth sources, namely e(n)=1 and e(S(n))=1; it is not the correction over\nall odd e-sources. Put DeltaC_s=C_all,s-C_ret,s. Thus DeltaC_s consists\nof the L-weighted correction over all odd L-sources plus the e-weighted\ncorrection over terminal-source births Z_S. For 1\u003c=s\u003c=2,\n\n    0\u003c=DeltaC_s\u003c=(4/9) sum_(n odd) n^(-2),\n\nso this correction is uniformly bounded and has a finite s downarrow 1\nlimit. The R identity has no affine correction, because R(n)=3n/4:\n\n    E_0,s+Rb_0,s=(4/3)^s(B_s+Rb_4,s).               (AC9)\n\nHere all even births are multiples of four, so the source sum is exactly\nB_s; the image of R is exactly the 0 modulo 3 row.\n\nSubtracting the PC residue balances yields the signed source/sink forms\n\n    L_2,s=(2/3)^s(L_odd,s+Z_S,s)-D_S,s-DeltaC_s,     (AC10)\n    Rb_0,s=(4/3)^s(Rb_4,s+Z_R,s)-D_R,s.             (AC11)\n\nThese are exact for every s>1. Nonnegativity gives upper constraints on\nD_S and D_R, but no lower bound from these two equations alone. In\nparticular, after division by a divergent E_s and passage to a subsequence,\nif endpoint/origin terms are negligible and the channel ratios have limits,\nthe critical relations are\n\n    l_2=(2/3)l_odd,       rb_0=(4/3)rb_4.\n\nThe scalar channel equations by themselves would be compatible with PC12;\nthe next section checks the missing binary residue equations and shows that\nthe combined system is inconsistent.\n\n## AC7. Joint binary-residue closure: the PC12 contradiction\n\nThis section audits the stronger coupling rather than the scalar equations\nalone. Define normalized masses by residue modulo 3, and put\n\n    D_i=L_i+R_i,   D=L+R.\n\nFor s>1 the even-child and odd-child sums are, respectively,\n\n    X_(2i),s=2^(-s)(E_i,s+L_i,s),\n    Y_(2i+1),s=2^(-s)(E_i,s+R_i,s)-Delta_i,s,\n\nwhere Delta_i,s is the positive correction from replacing (2n+1)^(-s)\nby (2n)^(-s). Since d,e,L,Rb are bounded indicators,\nsum_i Delta_i,s is uniformly bounded for 1\u003c=s\u003c=2. Therefore, if E_s\ntends to infinity, all Delta_i,s/E_s vanish. After taking a further\nsubsequence, the exact sum of the two child equations gives\n\n    (1-2^(-s))D_s=2^(1-s)E_s-sum_i Delta_i,s,\n\nso D_s/E_s stays bounded and tends to 2. We may therefore take a further\nsubsequence on which the six nonnegative channel ratios have limits. The limiting\nbinary laws are\n\n    2D_0=E_0+L_0+E_1+R_1,\n    2D_1=E_2+L_2+E_0+R_0,\n    2D_2=E_1+L_1+E_2+R_2.                            (AC12)\n\nSumming (AC12) gives D=2 because E_0+E_1+E_2=1. This is the correct\nnormalization; the factor two comes from the two active children of a birth.\n\nUnder PC12, E_0=2/3, E_1=0, E_2=1/3. The first and third equations in\n(AC12) become\n\n    L_0+2R_0-R_1=2/3,\n    2L_2+R_2-L_1=1/3.                              (AC13)\n\nWriting U=L_odd and V=R_4, AC10--AC11 and negligible origins/terminals give\n\n    L_2=(2/3)U,       R_0=(4/3)V.                    (AC14)\n\nAll even L-states are supported on multiples of four: if n=2q with q odd,\nthen d(4q)=0 because beta(4q+1)=beta(4q)=beta(q). Let W=L_even, supported\non multiples of four. Reindexing d(2m)=e(m)+L(m) over even m gives the\nexact limiting parity identity\n\n    W+V=(1/2)(B+W),  hence W+2V=B=1/2.               (AC15)\n\nFrom (AC13),\n\n    R-L=-3L_2+3R_0-E_0+E_2\n        =-2U+4V-1/3.                                (AC16)\n\nOn the other hand, D=2, L=U+W, and (AC15) give\n\n    R-L=2-2(U+W)=1-2U+4V.                            (AC17)\n\nEquating (AC16) and (AC17) would require -1/3=1, an impossibility.\nThus the full PC12 regime (divergent E_s with negligible origins and\nterminals) is excluded once the channel coupling and binary residue laws\nare combined. This is a conditional exclusion of that normalized regime,\nnot yet a finite-energy theorem or a full Collatz conclusion: the argument\ndoes not prove that origins/terminals are negligible, nor that E_s diverges.\n\n## AC8. Finite-cutoff check of the stronger birth-row transport\n\nThere is a sharper finite-cutoff formulation. For an indicator f write\nH_f(X)=sum_(n\u003c=X) f(n)/n, and use A=H_odd-birth, B=H_even-birth,\nE_i=H_(births congruent to i mod 3), and P=E_1. Fixed affine changes of\ncutoff can be replaced by X with an O(1) error: the omitted interval has\nbounded logarithmic length, and every weight replacement has a summable\nO(n^(-2)) error. The odd-child denominator correction is likewise O(1),\nuniformly in X.\n\nThe binary child laws then give\n\n    B-A=E_0-E_2+O(1),\n    A=E_2+P/2+O(1),\n    B=E_0+P/2+O(1).                                 (AC18)\n\nThe exact S/R birth-image rows at the same cutoff are\n\n    E_2=(2/3)(A-Z_S)+D_S+O(1),\n    E_0=(4/3)(B-Z_R)+D_R+O(1).                       (AC19)\n\nThe first O(1) includes the positive affine correction for S; the R map\nhas exact slope 3/4 and no such correction. Substituting (AC18) into\n(AC19) and solving gives\n\n    A=(3/2)P-2Z_S+3D_S+O(1),\n    B=4Z_R-3D_R-(3/2)P+O(1),                         (AC20)\n\nand hence\n\n    E=A+B=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1).                 (AC21)\n\nThe signs and coefficients are forced by the two affine slopes; they are\nnot an independent multiplication of densities. Since all terms are\nnonnegative, (AC21) proves the conditional equivalence\n\n    total harmonic birth energy is finite\n    iff both Z_R and D_S have finite harmonic energy.                (AC22)\n\nThe forward implication is immediate because Z_R and D_S are birth\nsubseries. For the reverse implication, drop the negative terms in (AC21).\nAlso B>=0 in (AC20) gives the useful source estimate\n\n    P \u003c= (8/3)Z_R+O(1).                              (AC23)\n\nIn particular, if E(X) diverges while all origins and terminal-source\nclasses are o(E(X)), then dividing (AC21) by E(X) gives 1=0. This directly\nexcludes the PC12 normalized regime and confirms the AC7 contradiction by a\nfinite-cutoff argument. It still does not prove that Z_R and D_S are finite,\nso it is not a finite-energy or full-Collatz conclusion.\n\n## AC9. Final audit of the complete root AC1--AC18 file\n\nThe full root file was read. Its four-state definitions are correct, including\nthe stronger support fact that an even L-state is divisible by eight: for\nn=2q with q odd, d(4q)=0, and for n=4q with q odd, d(4q)=0. The conserved\nquantities are correctly the total channels e+L and e+Rb; the source/target\ndistinction between Z_S,Z_R and D_S,D_R is maintained.\n\nThe finite-cutoff O(1) convention is valid. Affine reindexing changes each\nreciprocal by a summable O(n^(-2)) term, while replacing a fixed affine\ncutoff by X changes only a fixed multiplicative annulus of uniformly bounded\nharmonic mass. The odd-child correction is treated in this same way. No\nfinite-energy or density-zero assumption is used in AC4--AC6.\n\nAC12 follows from the three residue equations after the bounded odd-child\nerrors are discarded only at the finite-cutoff O(1) level. AC13 has the\ncorrect signs and affine factors. Solving AC12 and AC13 gives AC14 exactly:\n\n    A=(3/2)P-2Z_S+3D_S+O(1),\n    B=4Z_R-3D_R-(3/2)P+O(1),\n\nand hence AC15--AC17. The equivalence in AC16 is sound: the forward\ndirection uses that Z_R and D_S are birth subseries; the reverse direction\ndrops the negative terms in the finite identity and then takes monotone\nlimits. AC17 is only a conditional lower requirement on the combined\npositive charge when E diverges.\n\nThe AC18 Dirichlet transfer is also correct. If Q(X) is the bounded\ncumulative combination from AC14, writing each coefficient as q(n)/n and\nusing epsilon=s-1 gives\n\n    sum q(n)n^(-s)=epsilon integral Q(t)t^(-epsilon-1) dt.\n\nThe integral has absolute value at most the uniform bound on Q, for every\ns>1. This uses absolute convergence for s>1, not finiteness at s=1, and\nthe bound persists as s downarrow 1. Therefore the root file correctly\nexcludes the endpoint/origin-negligible divergent regime, while leaving open\nthe required finiteness of Z_R and D_S and any full Collatz conclusion.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_COMPONENT_ESCAPE_CHARGES.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — component escape charges beyond terminal spacing\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Ручной аналитический\nаудит без программ, CAS, переборов, Lean и численных экспериментов.\nПрочитаны PROJECT_STATE.md, FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md\n(BM) и BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md (SR). Цель — проверить,\nможет ли точная цветовая маска перенести endpoint spacing на полную массу\nконечной retained F-компоненты.\n\n## 1. Точная маска внутреннего retained ребра\n\nПусть \\(e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1)\\), где\n\\(d(n)=|\\beta(n+1)-\\beta(n)|\\). Из XOR-правила следует, что при рождении\n\\(e(n)=1\\) имеем \\(d(n)=0\\), а \\(d(2n)=d(2n+1)=1\\). Для odd birth и\neven birth \\(n=4m\\) SR даёт соответственно\n\n\\[\n e(S(n))=1-d(S(n)),\\qquad e(R(n))=1-d(R(n)).\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, retained ребро \\(n\\to F(n)\\), у которого оба конца — births,\nв точности требует \\(d(F(n))=0\\). При этом инвариантность сохраняет\nвнешний цвет: если \\(c=\\beta(n)=\\beta(n+1)\\), то\n\\(\\beta(F(n))=\\beta(F(n)+1)=c\\). Поэтому вся retained цепь одного\nкомпонента имеет один и тот же внешний цвет, а каждый её внутренний vertex\n\\(v\\) удовлетворяет\n\n\\[\n e(v)=1,\\quad d(v)=0,\\quad d(2v)=d(2v+1)=1,\n \\quad \\beta(v)=\\beta(v+1)=c.\n\\]\n\nЭто усиливает точную локальную картину, но не ограничивает длину цепи:\nусловие на \\(2v,2v+1\\) относится к active boundaries, а не к следующему\nарифметическому vertex \\(F(v)\\). В частности, из этих равенств нельзя\nвывести, что следующая retained ветвь должна иметь меньший индекс.\n\n## 2. Почему endpoint spacing не переносится на внутренние vertices\n\nДля terminal boundary \\(y\\) BM использует \\(d(y)=1\\). У \\(S\\)-terminal\nактивен child \\(2y\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), у \\(R\\)-terminal активен child\n\\(2y+1\\equiv1\\pmod3\\). Такой child не может быть новым endpoint target,\nпоскольку arithmetic images \\(S\\) и \\(R\\) имеют residues \\(2\\) и \\(0\\)\nmodulo \\(3\\). Это даёт несоседние глубины и сумму \\(2/(3b)\\) на branch.\n\nУ внутреннего birth \\(v\\), напротив, \\(d(v)=0\\), а оба children\n\\(2v,2v+1\\) являются active boundaries. Nearest-birth charging каждого\nиз них останавливается уже в \\(v\\). Терминальное правило с forced residue-1 использует именно условие \\(d(y)=1\\).\nДля \\(v\\) имеем \\(d(v)=0\\), поэтому этот вывод к нему неприменим.\nНекоторые из его children могут арифметически лежать в image \\(F\\), но это\nсамо по себе не даёт ни residue-1 spacing, ни ограничения на следующий\nretained vertex компоненты. Поэтому endpoint estimate\n\n\\[\n E_{\\rm terminal}(X)\\le 2E_\\beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2\n\\]\n\nне даёт оценки для \\(\\sum_{v\\in C}1/v\\): terminal boundary может быть\ncharged к внутреннему birth той же компоненты, и этот birth уже несёт свою\nсобственную массу \\(1/v\\). Замена такого internal anchor на исходный\nminimum или origin требует отдельной оценки кратности и не следует из\nnearest-birth map.\n\n## 3. Точный slope barrier для простых harmonic potentials\n\nДля одного retained шага точны следующие преобразования:\n\n\\[\n {1\\over S(n)}={2\\over3n+1}\u003c {2\\over3n},\n \\qquad\n {1\\over R(n)}={4\\over3n}.\n\\]\n\nThus an \\(S\\)-step contracts \\(1/n\\) asymptotically, while an \\(R\\)-step\nexpands it by \\(4/3\\). More generally, for \\(V_q(n)=n^{-q}\\), the\nleading edge ratios are \\((2/3)^q\\) on \\(S\\) and \\((4/3)^q\\) on \\(R\\).\nNo fixed real \\(q\\) makes both ratios strictly less than one: \\(q>0\\)\nexpands on \\(R\\), \\(q\u003c0\\) expands on \\(S\\), and \\(q=0\\) is neutral. This\nis a genuine obstruction to a one-parameter power potential; it does not\nexclude a potential using colors, residues, or a variable coefficient.\n\nFor a word with \\(s\\) S-letters and \\(r\\) R-letters, its affine form is\n\\(v_j=\\lambda_jv_0+B_j\\), with\n\\(\\lambda_j=(3/2)^{s_j}(3/4)^{r_j}\\). An R-run is finite because each\nR-step lowers the 2-adic valuation by two, but an S-step can create a new\n2-adic factor and restart an R-run. The exact retained mask supplies no\nproved global inequality relating the accumulated counts \\(s_j,r_j\\).\nThus this local observation does not yield an aggregate contraction.\n\n## 4. The residual-weight obstruction\n\nFor a finite retained chain \\(C=(v_0,\\ldots,v_L)\\), the exact decomposition\n\\[\n W(C)={1\\over v_L}+\\sum_{j\u003cL}{1\\over v_j}\n\\]\nshows what endpoint charging misses. The first term is controlled only after\nidentifying the terminal boundary and its nearest birth. Every internal\n\\(v_j\\) is itself a birth with \\(d(v_j)=0\\), so the active-boundary tree\ncharges its children to \\(v_j\\), not to an earlier component origin. There is\nno disjointness statement saying that the collection of internal births has\nbounded harmonic mass relative to the origins.\n\nThe arithmetic BM envelope makes the logical gap explicit: words selected by\nslope guards can have \\(k+1\\) vertices in a common interval comparable to\none starting scale, and then \\(W(C)\\) is of order \\(k/v_0\\). Those words\nare not counterexamples for an exact \\(\\beta\\): BM does not realize all\nbirth masks, and they need not satisfy the actual terminal-spacing masks.\nThey show only that guard arithmetic, without a new correlation for actual\ncolors, cannot supply the missing full-component estimate.\nAn actual-color proof would need a new correlation statement excluding this\nkind of long retained prefix or charging its internal births separately.\n\nThe sufficient terminal test \\(F(x)\u003c2m\\) remains valid: it places the\nnearest charged birth below the component minimum. It says nothing about\ninternal vertices before the terminal, and failure of that test is not a\ncounterexample to descent.\n\n## 5. Bounded verdict\n\nThe actual masks give three rigorous facts useful for any future charging\nargument: retained vertices preserve one outer color; each retained output\nhas \\(d=0\\); and each birth creates two active boundary children. They do\nnot currently give a residue spacing theorem for internal vertices, a bound\non the number of internal births per component, or an origin-based harmonic\ncontraction. The BM terminal estimate therefore remains endpoint-only.\n\nThe precise remaining gap is an aggregate inequality of the form\n\\[\n \\sum_{v\\in C}{1\\over v}\n \\le K\\,\\mathcal E(\\text{origins or escaped boundaries})+O(1)\n\\]\nfor actual retained components, with a finite universal \\(K\\) or a strict\ncontraction. No such inequality was obtained in this pass. Mixed cycles and\ninfinite retained components are also outside the finite-component argument.\nNo claim about the full Collatz conjecture or a nonconstant exact invariant\nis made.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_COMPONENT_WEIGHT_TELESCOPING.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — telescoping component weights beyond origins\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Ручной проход без программ,\nCAS, переборов и Lean. Прочитаны PROJECT_STATE.md, WS,\nBIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md и связанные SG/EC bounds.\nЦель — проверить, можно ли перенести origin/endpoint weights на все\nвнутренние vertices retained F-компонент.\n\n## 1. Exact edge telescoping\n\nFor a retained edge u -> v there are two exact identities. On the S branch,\n\n    v=(3u+1)/2,\n    1/u = (3/2)(1/v) + 3/[2v(2v-1)].                 (1)\n\nOn the R branch,\n\n    v=3u/4,\n    1/u = (3/4)(1/v).                                (2)\n\nThus a finite word v_0 -> ... -> v_L with edge multipliers\n\na_j=3/2 for S and a_j=3/4 for R satisfies the exact weighted expansion (all terms here are nonnegative)\n\n    1/v_0 = P_L/v_L + sum_{j=0}^{L-1} P_j epsilon_j,\n\nwhere P_j=product_{i\u003cj} a_i, epsilon_j is the S correction in (1) and\nzero on an R edge. This is a genuine identity for every actual retained\nword. It controls a weighted endpoint expression, not the unweighted sum\nsum_j 1/v_j.\n\nThe direct difference form makes the obstruction clearer:\n\n    1/u - 1/S(u) > 0,\n    1/u - 1/R(u) = -1/(3u).                          (3)\n\nA mixed finite chain can cancel positive S increments against negative R\nincrements. Endpoint telescoping alone therefore gives no upper bound for\nsum_j 1/v_j unless the number and placement of R steps are controlled.\n\n## 2. Why actual masks do not yet control the slope product\n\nEvery retained output is a birth with d(v)=0 and has the same outer color as\nits source. These are exact color constraints, but they do not give a proved\ninequality between the cumulative S count and R count along a retained F\ncomponent. The A/B recurrence from WS controls odd active ancestry boundaries\nused to charge origins; it is not a recurrence for the successive arithmetic\nvertices v_j of an F-component.\n\nFor the word prefix, the affine slope is\n\n    P_j=(3/2)^(number of S steps)*(3/4)^(number of R steps).\n\nAn R-run is finite because it lowers the 2-adic valuation by two at every\nstep, but an S-step can create new factors of two and restart an R-run. The\nactual masks presently prove neither P_j \u003c= C lambda^j with lambda\u003c1 nor a\nuniform bound for the P_j-weighted correction sum in (1). The per-edge\nepsilon_j is nonnegative; an unweighted global summability statement would\nrequire a separate source-count estimate and is not supplied here. Replacing\nactual masks by all arithmetic guards would be invalid for the claimed color\ntheorem.\n\nA simple class-weight attempt also fails. For W_q(n)=n^(-q), the leading\nratios W_q(S(n))/W_q(n) and W_q(R(n))/W_q(n) are (2/3)^q and (4/3)^q.\nNo real q makes both strictly contractive. A coefficient depending only on\nn modulo 3 cannot fix this on the R branch: a source and output both in\nresidue 0 can have the same coefficient while the reciprocal weight grows\nby 4/3. More elaborate residue or valuation potentials remain possible, but\nnone is supplied by WS/SG/EC.\n\n## 3. Endpoint and origin charges do not account for internal mass\n\nWS gives a strict joint origin coefficient 241/378 after the universal A/B\nactive-boundary recurrence. This is a bound on D_S and D_R origin targets.\nThe primitive P_1 class remains an F-origin by residue 1 modulo 3. Neither\nstatement counts an internal vertex v_j of a retained component as an origin\ncharge from an earlier birth.\n\nIf v_j is internal, then e(v_j)=1 and d(v_j)=0. Its two active children are\ncharged back to v_j by the nearest-birth map. The terminal spacing argument\nstarts from d(y)=1 and therefore does not apply at v_j. Consequently a\nterminal estimate or the 241/378 origin estimate cannot simply be summed over\nall component vertices. Doing so would either omit internal births or charge\nthe same birth tree more than once.\n\nFor P_1 there is an additional exact obstruction: residue 1 is absent from\nboth arithmetic images S and R, so P_1 has no incoming F edge. Any full\ncomponent telescoping inequality must therefore control its forward retained\nmass from the source itself; no predecessor charge can remove this term.\n\n## 4. Explicit sufficient condition and the missing input\n\nA sufficient full-component route would be a positive weight W comparable to\n1/n, with constants 0\u003cc\u003c=C, and either a uniform edge contraction\n\n    W(F(n)) \u003c= lambda W(n),  lambda\u003c1,\n\nor a signed coboundary identity whose residual is summable over all retained\nvertices. The first condition would imply a geometric bound on every finite\ncomponent and would also exclude retained cycles. The second would need a\nseparate bound on the total residual, not merely endpoint cancellation.\n\nNeither condition has been derived. Equation (1) is the exact candidate\ncoboundary, but its products P_j can grow on S-rich prefixes and the actual\nmask gives no global compensation. The arithmetic BM words show why guard\nconditions alone cannot establish such compensation; they are not actual\nbeta counterexamples and need not satisfy the retained color masks.\n\n## 5. Bounded verdict\n\nThe exact telescoping identity is (1), and the exact obstruction is the\nopposite S/R slope behavior in (2)--(3). WS's improved coefficient remains\nvalid for the actual D_S/D_R origin targets, but no transfer to the full\ninternal-component mass follows. A complete transfer still requires one of:\n\n* a color-sensitive relation controlling cumulative S/R slopes;\n* a positive variable potential with summable residual on every actual\n  retained component; or\n* a direct bound on internal birth multiplicities relative to P_1 and other\n  origins.\n\nNo such condition is presently proved. Mixed cycles and infinite retained\ncomponents remain outside any finite-component telescoping argument. No\nglobal Collatz conclusion is claimed.\n\n## 6. Exact global Dirichlet identity for s>1\n\nFor s>1 define\n\n    A_s = sum_{n odd birth} n^(-s),\n    B_s = sum_{n even birth} n^(-s),\n    Z_S,s = sum_{n odd terminal birth} n^(-s),\n    Z_R,s = sum_{n even terminal birth} n^(-s),\n    O_s = sum_{n origin birth} n^(-s).\n\nAll these sums converge absolutely for s>1. Since F is injective, every\nnon-origin birth is the output of exactly one retained edge. For a retained\nodd source n, write v=S(n). Then\n\n    v^(-s)=(2/3)^s n^(-s)-c_s(n),\n    c_s(n)=(2/3)^s n^(-s)[1-(1+1/(3n))^(-s)] > 0.\n\nFor a retained even source n, v=R(n) and v^(-s)=(4/3)^s n^(-s)\nexactly. Summing outputs over all retained edges gives the exact identity\n\n    A_s+B_s-O_s\n      =(2/3)^s(A_s-Z_S,s)+(4/3)^s(B_s-Z_R,s)-C_s,       (10)\n\nwhere C_s is the sum of c_s(n) over retained odd sources. Equivalently,\n\n    O_s=(1-(2/3)^s)A_s+(1-(4/3)^s)B_s\n        +(2/3)^s Z_S,s+(4/3)^s Z_R,s+C_s.               (11)\n\nNo finite-component assumption is used: cycles and infinite components are\nincluded because the Dirichlet sums converge absolutely for s>1.\n\n## 7. Uniformity of the affine correction and the s downarrow 1 boundary\n\nFor 1\u003c=s\u003c=2, the inequality 1-(1+x)^(-s)\u003c=s x gives\n\n    0 \u003c= c_s(n) \u003c= (4/9)n^(-s-1) \u003c= (4/9)n^(-2).\n\nHence C_s is uniformly bounded near one and converges to C_1 by dominated\nconvergence. If E_1=A_1+B_1 is finite, monotone convergence lets (10)--(11)\npass directly to s=1. This is a valid global harmonic identity in the finite\nenergy case, but it does not itself prove finite energy.\n\nIf E_s diverges as s downarrow 1, divide (10) by E_s. Along any subsequence\nwhere the corresponding ratios have limits, put\n\n    a_s=A_s/E_s, b_s=B_s/E_s,\n    zS_s=Z_S,s/E_s, zR_s=Z_R,s/E_s, o_s=O_s/E_s,\n    a_s+b_s=1.\n\nSince C_s/E_s tends to zero, every such subsequential limit satisfies\n\n    1-o = (2/3)a+(4/3)b-(2/3)zS-(4/3)zR.              (12)\n\nThis is the exact endpoint-limsup constraint. For example, if one could\nseparately prove o=zS=zR=0 along the same subsequence, then b=a=1/2 would\nfollow. Neither that negligibility nor the needed endpoint ratios follows\nfrom WS: the 241/378 bound is relative to the unknown E_beta, and P_1\nremains in the origin term. Thus (12) is a constraint, not a contradiction.\n\n## 8. Strict logical boundary of combining WS with (10)\n\nWS controls selected origin and terminal subsets; (10) contains the total\nodd and even birth masses with opposite effective coefficients after\nrearrangement. In particular, the R contribution has coefficient\n1-(4/3)^s\u003c0 in (11), so a positive endpoint bound cannot be substituted for\nit to obtain a monotone upper bound on E_s. The affine correction is positive\nand uniformly harmless, but it does not remove this signed A_s/B_s term.\n\nTherefore the exact Dirichlet identity is a global bookkeeping relation,\nnot a transfer of endpoint weights to internal component mass. A full energy\nargument would still need an independent odd/even balance, a color-sensitive\npotential, or a direct bound on internal retained vertices and the primitive\nP_1 source. No such input is established by WS, and no global Collatz\nconclusion follows.\n\n## 9. Final audit of PC7--PC14\n\nThe complete PC file was read. PC7 is an exact residue decomposition, not a\nfinite-component assertion: residue 0 consists of retained R outputs plus\nD_R origins, residue 2 of retained S outputs plus D_S origins, and residue 1\nis P_1. The affine correction C_s has the same positive formula as in\nSection 6, is uniformly bounded for 1\u003c=s\u003c=2, and converges by dominated\nconvergence. PC8 and PC9 follow by adding these three disjoint residue rows.\n\nPC11 is the correct normalized limit of PC9. Under the additional\nsubsequence assumption that origins and terminals are negligible relative to\nE_s, it gives a=b=1/2 and the residue proportions in PC12. The scalar mass\nassignment displayed there satisfies the selected equations and PC6, so it\nis a valid feasibility witness for the projected constraints only; it is not\nan invariant or an actual component construction.\n\nThe Dirichlet prefix estimate PC13 is also sound: the constant term counts\n2^k word classes, while the singular term uses the summed class densities\n(3/4)^k. PC14 correctly retains each terminal depth separately and uses the\ncutoff z\u003c=2(3/2)^j n. Its reverse-prefix analogue has the corresponding\n(2/3)^k density and (4/3)^j cutoff.\n\nNo hidden assumption E_1\u003cinfinity is used in PC14. If E_1 is finite, the\nscaled quantity (s-1)E_s tends to zero trivially. If E_s diverges, the proof\nworks along a sequence with Z_s/E_s -> 0 (or O_s/E_s -> 0): for fixed k,\nK_k(s) is bounded, so after moving the term\nK_k(s)(Z_s/E_s)(s-1)E_s to the left, the coefficient tends to one. This\njustifies limsup (s-1)E_s \u003c= (3/4)^k, and then k is sent to infinity. The\nresult is only a sub-1/(s-1) singularity statement, not finite energy.\n\nThus PC7--PC14 are algebraically and logically consistent. They leave the\nsame precise gap: actual mask correlations must exclude the projected\ncritical balance or otherwise control the primitive and internal masses.\nNo contradiction or full Collatz conclusion follows from these sections.\n\n### Audit of the added normalized-PC6 paragraph (within §9)\n\nThe replacement of Y=(3X+2)/4 by X is valid for X>=2, since Y\u003c=X and\neach cumulative birth function is monotone. There are no births below 2,\nso integrating the resulting inequality against s X^(-s-1) dX over\n[2,infinity) gives\n\n    B_s \u003c= (3/2)E_0,s+(5/8)E_1,s+(3/8)E_2,s+(1/4)2^(-s).\n\nThe displayed +1/4 in PC4 is therefore a valid looser bound. For every\ns>1, all these Dirichlet sums are automatically finite because they are\nsubseries of the full sum over n>=2 of n^(-s); no hidden E_infinity or\nE_1\u003cinfinity assumption is used. If E_s tends to infinity as s decreases\nto 1, the bounded correction divided by E_s vanishes. The paragraph is thus correct, but it\nremains only a scalar normalized inequality and does not close the odd/even\nbalance or control internal mass.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_CRITICAL_AFFINE_CORRECTION.md":"# Critical affine correction for the \\(q=1\\) weight\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual audit only; no\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, or Lean. This report checks whether the exact\nmultiplicative correction along odd states improves the critical \\(W_1\\)\nestimate in [the positive-integer resolvent](POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md).\n\n## 1. One genuine path: exact product and affine form\n\nLet \\(x_0=m,x_1,\\ldots,x_K=n\\) be a positive shortcut trajectory, and let\n\\(\\mathcal O\\) be the set of indices \\(j\u003cK\\) at which \\(x_j\\) is odd. Put\n\\(P=|\\mathcal O|\\) and \\(A=2^K/3^P\\), the inverse slope used in PR. At an\neven state and at an odd state respectively,\n\\[\n \\frac{x_{j+1}}{x_j}=\\frac12,\\qquad\n \\frac{x_{j+1}}{x_j}\n =\\frac{3x_j+1}{2x_j}\n =\\frac32\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_j}\\right).\n\\]\nTherefore, with\n\\[\n S(m\\to n):=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal O}\n       \\log\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_j}\\right),\n\\]\nthe exact product identity is\n\\[\n \\frac nm=A^{-1}e^{S(m\\to n)},\\qquad\n A^{-1}=\\frac nm\\,e^{-S(m\\to n)}.                 \\tag{CA1}\n\\]\nFor a path with at least one odd step \\(P\\ge1\\), \\(S>0\\). An all-even\nnonempty path has \\(P=0\\) and \\(S=B=0\\). If its inverse affine form is\n\\(m=An-B\\), \\(B=C/3^P\\), then (CA1) is equivalently\n\\[\n e^S=1+\\frac Bm,\\qquad\n S=\\log\\left(1+\\frac Bm\\right),\\qquad\n A^{-1}=\\frac n{m+B}.                             \\tag{CA2}\n\\]\nThus the correction is exact, and is precisely the affine intercept already\npresent in PR2; it is not a separate probabilistic gain.\n\nFor comparison with a raw harmonic sum, set\n\\[\n U=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal O}\\frac1{3x_j},\\qquad\n V=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal O}\\frac1{9x_j^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le1/(3x_j)\\le1/3\\),\n\\[\n U-\\frac12V\\le S\\le U.                             \\tag{CA3}\n\\]\nThe logarithmic accumulation and the raw reciprocal accumulation therefore\ndiffer only by the explicit quadratic error \\(V/2\\), but (CA2) remains the\nstronger exact statement.\n\nFor every path with \\(P\\ge1\\), the universal intercept bound \\(B\\ge1/3\\)\ngives the slightly sharpened individual estimate\n\\[\n A^{-1}\\le\\frac n{m+1/3}\u003c\\frac nm                         \\tag{CA4}\n\\]\nThis is a genuine shift of the harmonic denominator, but it is only a\nconstant shift.\n\n## 2. Consequence for the critical resolvent\n\nFor a nonperiodic structural target \\(n\\), separate the empty path and let\n\\(M_*(n)\\) be the positive sources whose first-hit path is nonempty. Applying\n(CA4) termwise gives\n\\[\n W_1(n)\\le\n 1+n\\sum_{m\\in M_*(n)}\\frac1{m+1/3}.                \\tag{CA5}\n\\]\nFor a periodic target, the same estimate applies to each first-hit path and\nthe cycle-repetition factor from PR3 may be put in front:\n\\[\n W_1(n)\\le c_1(n)\\left(\n 1+n\\sum_{m\\in M_*(n)}\\frac1{m+1/3}\\right),\n \\qquad c_1(n)\\le3n+1.                              \\tag{CA6}\n\\]\nThe shifted integer series still has harmonic divergence:\n\\(\\sum_{m\\ge1}(m+1/3)^{-1}=\\infty\\). Consequently, (CA5)--(CA6) do not\nprove \\(W_1(n)\u003c\\infty\\). They only replace the earlier \\(n/m\\) majorant by\nthe exact constant-intercept refinement \\(n/(m+1/3)\\).\n\nNo lower bound on \\(S\\) growing with \\(m\\) follows from positivity. The\nfollowing explicit family shows why the constant shift cannot be enlarged\nfrom the one-path affine data alone. For \\(s\\ge2\\), let\n\\[\n m_s=\\frac{4^s-1}{3}.\n\\]\nThen \\(m_s\\) is odd and\n\\[\n m_s\\longmapsto 2^{2s-1}\\longmapsto\\cdots\\longmapsto1\n\\]\nhas one odd step and \\(2s-1\\) even steps. Its data are\n\\[\n K=2s,\\quad P=1,\\quad A=\\frac{4^s}{3},\\quad B=\\frac13,\\quad\n A^{-1}=\\frac3{4^s}\n =\\frac1{m_s+1/3}.\n\\]\nThis is an actual shortcut path, not a claim that \\((m_s,m_s+1)\\) is an\nordered structural pair. It demonstrates that arbitrarily large source\nvalues can have the minimal intercept correction \\(B=1/3\\). Any stronger\ncritical estimate must use additional source-cylinder arithmetic or\nmultiplicity information.\n\n## 3. Synchronous ordered pair\n\nSuppose \\((m,m+1)\\) maps synchronously to \\((n,n+1)\\) with common \\(K,P\\),\nbut allow the two paths to have different odd-state lists. Write\n\\(S_L,S_R\\) for their logarithmic accumulations and \\(B_L,B_R\\) for their\naffine intercepts. Applying (CA1) to both paths gives\n\\[\n \\frac nm=A^{-1}e^{S_L},\\qquad\n \\frac{n+1}{m+1}=A^{-1}e^{S_R}.                    \\tag{CA7}\n\\]\nSubtracting the logarithms yields the exact difference law\n\\[\n S_R-S_L\n =\\log\\frac{m(n+1)}{n(m+1)}\n =\\log\\left(1+\\frac1n\\right)\n  -\\log\\left(1+\\frac1m\\right).                     \\tag{CA8}\n\\]\nThe same identity in affine coordinates is\n\\[\n B_R-B_L=A-1,                                      \\tag{CA9}\n\\]\nbecause \\(m=An-B_L\\) and \\(m+1=A(n+1)-B_R\\). Equations (CA8) and (CA9)\nare equivalent after using \\(S_L=\\log(1+B_L/m)\\) and\n\\(S_R=\\log(1+B_R/(m+1))\\).\n\nFor a nonempty common ordered block, both sides have \\(P\\ge1\\), hence\n\\(B_L,B_R\\ge1/3\\), and the common critical weight obeys\n\\[\n A^{-1}\\le\n \\min\\left\\{\\frac n{m+1/3},\\frac{n+1}{m+4/3}\\right\\}. \\tag{CA10}\n\\]\nThis is the full direct upper consequence of the pair identity from the\navailable positivity/intercept information. The difference law does not\nsupply a uniform positive lower bound for either \\(S_L\\) or \\(S_R\\):\nthe two odd-state lists can carry the required difference. In particular,\nfor fixed \\(n\\), summing (CA10) over distinct left sources still has no\ndeduced convergent majorant; the first term remains a shifted harmonic\nweight, and which term is smaller depends on the source and target sizes.\n\n## 4. Verdict and exact missing input\n\nThe logarithmic correction is mathematically exact:\n\\[\n A^{-1}=(n/m)\\exp\\!\\left[-\\sum_{\\text{odd }x}\n       \\log\\left(1+\\frac1{3x}\\right)\\right].\n\\]\nFor a synchronized pair its two accumulations satisfy (CA8), while the\nintercepts satisfy (CA9). These formulas sharpen PR2 by a constant\n\\(1/3\\)-shift and give the pairwise minimum (CA10), but they do not close the\ncritical \\(W_1\\) problem. A useful new upper bound would require a\nCollatz-specific estimate forcing either the source set \\(M_*(n)\\) to have a\nsummable reciprocal tail or the intercepts \\(B\\) (equivalently the\naccumulations \\(S\\)) to grow with the source. Neither follows from the\npresent positivity, common-\\(K,P\\), or deterministic-cylinder hypotheses.\n\n\n## 5. Audit of the regular-variation superharmonic obstruction\n\nAssume, in addition, a positive function \\(V\\) satisfies\n\\[\n \\mathcal L_qV(n)\\le V(n)\\qquad(n\\ge1),              \\tag{CA11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal L_q\\) is the positive sum over the minimal ordered inverse\nblocks, with a block of inverse slope \\(a\\) contributing\n\\(a^{-q}V(F(n))\\) whenever its positive integral source exists. Suppose\n\\[\n V(n)\\sim c_{[n]_d}\\,n^s\\ell(n),\\qquad\n 0\u003cc_{\\min}\\le c_r\\le c_{\\max}\u003c\\infty,              \\tag{CA12}\n\\]\non each residue class modulo a fixed \\(d\\), and \\(\\ell\\) has the usual\nfixed-affine-scale ratio \\(\\ell(an+b)/\\ell(n)\\to1\\). This section audits the\nclaim only in this regular-variation class.\n\n### 5.1 Iterating the admissible contraction\n\nLet \\(H=S^2\\) be the accepted ordered contraction, with inverse slope\n\\[\n \\alpha=\\frac{2^{22}}{3^{14}}\u003c1.\n\\]\nIf \\(H\\) is written as \\(r\\) minimal blocks, then the \\(H^t\\) path is one\nsummand of \\(\\mathcal L_q^{rt}\\), with weight \\(\\alpha^{-qt}\\). The exact\nguard from PR6 is\n\\[\n 3^{14t}\\mid 139n+4310.                             \\tag{CA13}\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(t\\), (CA13) defines one unbounded positive arithmetic\nprogression of \\(n\\). On that progression all earlier \\(H\\)-iterates are\nintegral; for sufficiently large \\(n\\) they are positive. Therefore\niterating (CA11) and retaining this one nonnegative summand gives\n\\[\n \\alpha^{-qt}V(H^t(n))\\le V(n).                     \\tag{CA14}\n\\]\nSince \\(H^t(n)/n\\to\\alpha^t\\), (CA12), with \\(c_{\\min}/c_{\\max}\\) absorbing\nthe possible residue of \\(H^t(n)\\), yields for every fixed \\(t\\)\n\\[\n 1\\ \\ge\\\n \\liminf_{\\substack{n\\to\\infty\\\\(CA13)}}\n \\frac{\\alpha^{-qt}V(H^t(n))}{V(n)}\n \\ \\ge\\\n \\frac{c_{\\min}}{c_{\\max}}\\,\n \\alpha^{\\,t(s-q)}.                                \\tag{CA15}\n\\]\nIf a fixed \\(t\\)-progression splits into residue classes modulo \\(d\\), one\nmay either pass to one subprogression or use the displayed\n\\(c_{\\min}/c_{\\max}\\) bound; no residue assumption on \\(H^t(n)\\) is needed.\nBecause \\(\\alpha\u003c1\\), (CA15) is impossible for arbitrarily large \\(t\\) when\n\\(s\u003cq\\). Hence\n\\[\n s\\ge q.                                            \\tag{CA16}\n\\]\nThis step uses the exact repeated guard and positivity, not a formal\n3-adic value. It also uses iteration of the superharmonic inequality; a\nsingle \\(H\\)-term would not by itself force (CA16).\n\n### 5.2 The \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) branches finish the contradiction\n\nThe previously verified minimal \\(P\\le2\\) ordered blocks are\n\\[\n L(n)=\\frac{4n-1}{3},\\qquad\n M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\n\\]\nwith inverse slopes \\(a_L=4/3\\) and \\(a_M=32/9\\), respectively. Take\n\\[\n n=1+9dj,\\qquad j\\longrightarrow\\infty.             \\tag{CA17}\n\\]\nBoth sources are positive integers and\n\\[\n L(n)=1+12dj\\equiv1\\pmod d,\\qquad\n M(n)=3+32dj\\equiv3\\pmod d.                          \\tag{CA18}\n\\]\nThus the two nonnegative terms in \\(\\mathcal L_qV(n)/V(n)\\) have the limits\n\\[\n a_L^{-q}\\frac{V(L(n))}{V(n)}\n \\longrightarrow \\left(\\frac43\\right)^{s-q},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n a_M^{-q}\\frac{V(M(n))}{V(n)}\n \\longrightarrow\n \\left(\\frac{32}{9}\\right)^{s-q}\n \\frac{c_{3\\bmod d}}{c_{1\\bmod d}}.                 \\tag{CA19}\n\\]\nSince all other terms are nonnegative, (CA11) implies\n\\[\n 1\\ge\\liminf_{j\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\mathcal L_qV(1+9dj)}{V(1+9dj)}\n \\ge\n \\left(\\frac43\\right)^{s-q}\n +\\left(\\frac{32}{9}\\right)^{s-q}\n  \\frac{c_{3\\bmod d}}{c_{1\\bmod d}}.                \\tag{CA20}\n\\]\nBut (CA16) makes the first term at least \\(1\\), while the second term is\nstrictly positive. Hence the right side of (CA20) is \\(>1\\), a contradiction.\n\nThe residue cases requested explicitly cause no exception:\nfor \\(d=1\\), \\(c_{3\\bmod d}=c_{1\\bmod d}\\); for \\(d=2\\), \\(3\\equiv1\\pmod2\\);\nfor \\(d=3\\), the second coefficient is \\(c_0/c_1>0\\). For every \\(d>3\\)\nboth coefficients remain positive, which is all the argument needs.\n\n### 5.3 Scope\n\nThe audited conclusion is a valid no-go statement for a positive\nsuperharmonic \\(V\\) with one fixed polynomial/regularly varying exponent and\nstrictly positive periodic residue coefficients, provided \\(\\mathcal L_q\\)\ncontains the minimal \\(L,M\\) blocks and the \\(r\\)-block decomposition of\n\\(H\\). It does not exclude irregular \\(V\\), residue-dependent exponents,\nvanishing residue coefficients, or a superharmonic inequality that omits\none of these genuine branches. It is therefore an obstruction to this\nstandard Lyapunov ansatz, not a critical \\(W_1\\) proof or a Collatz result.\n\n\n## 6. Audit of the critical-weight arithmetic candidate\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). I reread the candidate\ncompletely. Its CX1 product identity and the density implication labelled\n(CX5) are correct, with the following exact scope.\n\n### 6.1 CX1 and the structural target restriction\n\nFor a genuine path, the stepwise product is exactly\n\\[\n \\frac nm=\\frac{3^P}{2^K}\n \\prod_{\\substack{j\u003cK\\\\x_j\\ {\\rm odd}}}\n \\left(1+\\frac1{3x_j}\\right)\n =A^{-1}e^S.\n\\]\nThe affine identity \\(m=An-B\\) then gives\n\\(e^S=1+B/m\\) and \\(A^{-1}=n/(m+B)\\). The \\(P=0\\) all-even case has\n\\(S=B=0\\), while a structural ordered block cannot have \\(P=0\\): one of\ntwo consecutive sources is odd, and the common odd count is therefore\npositive on both sides. Thus the candidate's \\(B\\ge1/3\\) statement is\ncorrect exactly for nonempty structural blocks (and for arbitrary paths with\n\\(P\\ge1\\)).\n\nThe target congruence argument is also valid. After an odd shortcut step,\n\\[\n T(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2}\\equiv2\\pmod3,\n\\]\nso the state is a unit modulo \\(3\\). An even step preserves being a unit\nbecause \\(2\\) is invertible modulo \\(3\\), and a later odd step again outputs\n\\(2\\pmod3\\). A structural block has an odd step on each side, because its\nsource pair is consecutive and its two paths have the same odd count.\nConsequently both final coordinates \\(n,n+1\\) are nonzero modulo \\(3\\).\nThe only consecutive unit pair modulo \\(3\\) is \\((1,2)\\), hence every\nnonempty structural target satisfies\n\\[\n n\\equiv1\\pmod3.                                   \\tag{CX-AUD1}\n\\]\nTherefore, for \\(n\\not\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), no first inverse structural block is\navailable; every longer schedule also begins with such a block. The empty\nschedule remains, so \\(W_q(n)=1\\) for all \\(q>0\\). This is an exact target\nrestriction, not a density assertion about arbitrary Collatz trajectories.\n\n### 6.2 CX5: Abel implication and notation\n\nFor the structural predecessor set \\(M_R(n)\\), the first-hit affine identity\ngives \\(A_m^{-1}=n/(m+B_m)\\), with \\(B_n=0\\) for the empty path. Including a\npossible positive cycle multiplies all first-hit weights by the already\nverified factor \\(c_1(n)\\). Hence\n\\[\n W_1(n;X)=c_1(n)\n \\sum_{\\substack{m\\in M_R(n)\\\\m\\le X}}\\frac n{m+B_m}\n \\le c_1(n)n\\,H(X),\n \\qquad\n H(X)=\\sum_{\\substack{m\\in M_R(n)\\\\m\\le X}}\\frac1m.  \\tag{CX-AUD2}\n\\]\nWith \\(N(X)=\\#(M_R(n)\\cap[1,X])\\), Abel summation gives\n\\[\n H(X)=\\frac{N(X)}X+\\int_1^X\\frac{N(t)}{t^2}\\,dt\n\\]\n(up to an immaterial bounded endpoint convention). If\n\\(D=\\limsup N(X)/X\\), then \\(H(X)/\\log X\\) has limsup at most \\(D\\), and\ntherefore\n\\[\n \\eta:=\\limsup_{X\\to\\infty}\\frac{W_1(n;X)}{\\log X}\n \\le c_1(n)nD.\n\\]\nThus the extra premise \\(\\eta>0\\) indeed implies\n\\[\n \\overline d(M_R(n))=D\\ge\\frac{\\eta}{c_1(n)n}>0.    \\tag{CX-AUD3}\n\\]\nThis is only a conditional lower-growth-to-upper-density implication; no\npositive \\(\\eta\\) is proved.\n\nThere are two distinct uses of the label CX5 in the candidate: equation\n(CX5) in its section CX2 is the density implication just audited, whereas\nthe later section headed CX5 is the regular-variation no-go theorem and its\ncorresponding displayed inequality is (CX10). These references must not be\nidentified.\n\n### 6.3 Limits\n\nThe audit does not turn \\(W_1\\) divergence into a lower growth rate, does not\ncover pairs with unequal odd counts, and does not extend the exact\n\\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) restriction from structural targets to arbitrary\nCollatz targets. The candidate's stated limits are therefore appropriate.\n\n\n\n## 7. Audit of one \\(3\\)-adic valuation factor\n\nConsider the enlarged ansatz, for \\(n>1\\),\n\\[\n V(n)=(c_{[n]_d}+o(1))\\,n^s\\ell(n)\\,\n       3^{\\gamma v_3(n-1)},                         \\tag{CX-AUD4}\n\\]\nwith \\(c_r>0\\), the same fixed-affine-scale condition on \\(\\ell\\), and\n\\(V(1)\\) arbitrary but finite. The preceding superharmonic assumption is\n\\(\\mathcal L_qV\\le V\\).\n\n### 7.1 Positive \\(\\gamma\\): an \\(M\\)-branch with prescribed valuations\n\nWrite the genuine branch\n\\[\n M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\\qquad A_M=\\frac{32}{9}.\n\\]\nFix \\(t\\ge1\\) and choose\n\\[\n m=1+3^t u,\\qquad 3\\nmid u,\n\\]\nsubject to \\(9m+5\\equiv0\\pmod {32}\\). Such \\(u\\) exist in an unbounded\narithmetic progression: \\(3^t\\) is invertible modulo \\(32\\), and after the\nunique condition modulo \\(32\\), one chooses a CRT subprogression avoiding\n\\(u\\equiv0\\pmod3\\). Put\n\\[\n n=\\frac{9m+5}{32}.\n\\]\nThen \\(n\\) is a positive integer, \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\), and \\(M(n)=m\\). Also\n\\[\n n-1=\\frac{9(m-3)}{32},\\qquad\n v_3(m-1)=t,\\qquad v_3(m-3)=0,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n v_3(m-1)=t,\\qquad v_3(n-1)=2.                     \\tag{CX-AUD5}\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(t\\), let \\(m\\to\\infty\\) in this progression. The \\(M\\)-term\nin the superharmonic inequality has lower asymptotic ratio\n\\[\n A_M^{-q}\\frac{V(M(n))}{V(n)}\n \\ \\ge_{\\liminf}\\\n \\frac{c_{\\min}}{c_{\\max}}\n \\left(\\frac{32}{9}\\right)^{s-q}\n 3^{\\gamma(t-2)}.                                  \\tag{CX-AUD6}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\gamma>0\\), the right side is unbounded as \\(t\\to\\infty\\), whereas each\nfinite-\\(t\\) inequality has liminf at most \\(1\\). Hence no such\nsuperharmonic \\(V\\) exists for \\(\\gamma>0\\). The construction uses only\nlinear congruences and does not evaluate any cases.\n\n### 7.2 Negative \\(\\gamma\\): the \\(H^k\\) guard and the \\(L\\)-branch\n\nLet \\(H=S^2\\), \\(\\alpha=2^{22}/3^{14}\u003c1\\), and fix \\(k\\ge1\\). Choose an\nexact annulus \\(v_3(n-z_*)=R>14k+2\\), where\n\\(z_*=-4310/139\\equiv7\\pmod9\\). It gives the guard\n\\(3^{14k}\\mid139n+4310\\), and the same affine relation implies\n\\(H^k(n)\\equiv7\\pmod9\\). This is an unbounded positive progression for each\nfixed \\(k\\), with\n\\[\n v_3(n-1)=v_3(H^k(n)-1)=1.                          \\tag{CX-AUD7}\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large members all \\(H\\)-iterates are positive, and the\nguard makes the entire \\(k\\)-fold \\(H\\) word integral. The valuation factors\nin (CX-AUD4) therefore cancel between \\(n\\) and \\(H^k(n)\\). The iterated\nsuperharmonic inequality gives the same lower bound as in (CA15), up to\n\\(c_{\\min}/c_{\\max}\\):\n\\[\n 1\\ge \\frac{c_{\\min}}{c_{\\max}}\\alpha^{k(s-q)}\n \\quad\\text{for every }k.\n\\]\nThus \\(s\\ge q\\) is necessary also when \\(\\gamma\u003c0\\).\n\nNow take \\(n=1+9dj\\) and \\(j\\to\\infty\\). The \\(L\\)-source satisfies\n\\[\n L(n)=\\frac{4n-1}{3},\\qquad\n v_3(L(n)-1)=v_3(n-1)-1,\n\\]\nwhile \\(n\\equiv L(n)\\equiv1\\pmod d\\). Its term in\n\\(\\mathcal L_qV(n)/V(n)\\) consequently has limit\n\\[\n \\left(\\frac43\\right)^{s-q}3^{-\\gamma}.             \\tag{CX-AUD8}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\gamma\u003c0\\), (CA16) gives \\(s\\ge q\\), so (CX-AUD8) is \\(>1\\), a\ncontradiction.\n\n### 7.3 Zero case and scope\n\nFor \\(\\gamma=0\\), the regular-variation no-go in §5 already applies. Thus\nthe single factor \\(3^{\\gamma v_3(n-1)}\\) is incompatible with\n\\(\\mathcal L_qV\\le V\\) for every real \\(\\gamma\\), within the fixed-exponent,\npositive-periodic-coefficient, common-scale ansatz (CX-AUD4). The arbitrary\nfinite value \\(V(1)\\) is irrelevant: all contradiction sequences use\n\\(n>1\\) and sources tending to infinity. This does not exclude valuation\nfactors at other centers, residue-dependent exponents, or irregular\nsuperharmonic functions.\n\n\n\n## 8. Audit of the finite \\(3\\)-adic-centre extension\n\nConsider a finite set \\(E\\subset\\mathbb Z_3\\) and\n\\[\n V(n)=(c_{[n]_d}+o(1))\\,n^s\\ell(n)\n       \\prod_{a\\in E}3^{\\gamma_a v_3(n-a)}\n\\]\nfor large positive integers, with finite exceptional values allowed at\nordinary integers and all \\(c_{[n]_d}>0\\). Put \\(\\gamma_b=0\\) when\n\\(b\\notin E\\). The finite-centre extension in §9 of the coverage-interface\ncandidate is correct after making its annulus and order-of-limits argument\nexplicit.\n\n### 8.1 Exact annuli and positive branch sources\n\nFor every \\(b\\in\\mathbb Z_3\\) and \\(R\\ge0\\), the set\n\\[\n \\{n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}:v_3(n-b)=R\\}\n\\]\nis nonempty and unbounded: choose an integer residue modulo \\(3^{R+1}\\)\ncongruent to \\(b+3^Ru\\) with \\(3\\nmid u\\), then add arbitrary positive\nmultiples of \\(3^{R+1}\\). Thus an exact valuation annulus can be combined\nwith \\(n\\to\\infty\\).\n\nFor\n\\[\n G_L(a)=\\frac{3a+1}{4},\\qquad\n G_M(a)=\\frac{9a+5}{32},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n L(n)-a=\\frac43\\bigl(n-G_L(a)\\bigr),\\qquad\n M(n)-a=\\frac{32}{9}\\bigl(n-G_M(a)\\bigr).            \\tag{CX-AUD9}\n\\]\nBoth \\(G_L(a)\\) and \\(G_M(a)\\) lie in \\(\\mathbb Z_3\\); moreover\n\\(G_L(a)\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) and \\(G_M(a)\\equiv1\\pmod9\\). Hence sufficiently\nhigh annuli around these centres consist of admissible target integers for\nthe genuine \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) branches, and their positive sources tend to\ninfinity.\n\n### 8.2 Positive exponents are impossible\n\nSuppose \\(\\gamma_a>0\\). Fix \\(F=L\\) or \\(M\\), let \\(b=G_F(a)\\), and take\n\\(n\\to\\infty\\) in an exact annulus \\(v_3(n-b)=R\\), with \\(R>P_F\\) and\nbeyond all relevant pairwise separations of the finitely many centres. The\nvaluation at the output centre \\(a\\) changes by \\(R-P_F\\), where \\(P_L=1\\)\nand \\(P_M=2\\); the input valuation at \\(b\\) is \\(R\\) if \\(b\\in E\\), and is\nfixed if \\(b\\notin E\\). All other centre valuations are fixed in this\nannulus. After taking \\(n\\to\\infty\\) for this fixed \\(R\\), the \\(F\\)-term in\n\\(\\mathcal L_qV/V\\) contains\n\\[\n 3^{(\\gamma_a-\\gamma_b)R+O(1)},                    \\tag{CX-AUD10}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\gamma_b=0\\) outside \\(E\\). The \\(O(1)\\) is independent of \\(R\\)\nonce the separation threshold is passed; periodic coefficients and the\naffine slow-scale ratio are bounded by fixed positive constants.\n\nSuperharmonicity therefore requires\n\\[\n \\gamma_{G_L(a)}\\ge\\gamma_a,\\qquad\n \\gamma_{G_M(a)}\\ge\\gamma_a                           \\tag{CX-AUD11}\n\\]\nfor every positive exponent \\(\\gamma_a\\). Thus the positive-exponent set\n\\(E_+=\\{a:\\gamma_a>0\\}\\) is closed under both \\(G_L\\) and \\(G_M\\).\nNow\n\\[\n G_L^j(a)=1+\\left(\\frac34\\right)^j(a-1).\n\\]\nIf \\(a\\ne1\\), these are infinitely many distinct \\(3\\)-adic points, since\n\\(3/4\\) is not a root of unity. A finite \\(E_+\\) closed under \\(G_L\\) must\ntherefore be contained in \\(\\{1\\}\\). But\n\\[\n G_M(1)=\\frac7{16}\\ne1,\n\\]\ncontradicting closure under \\(G_M\\). Hence\n\\[\n \\gamma_a\\le0\\quad\\text{for every finite centre }a.  \\tag{CX-AUD12}\n\\]\n\n### 8.3 The contraction forces \\(s\\ge q\\)\n\nLet \\(z_*=-4310/139\\in\\mathbb Z_3\\) be the fixed point of \\(H\\). For fixed\n\\(k\\ge1\\), choose\n\\[\n R>14k+2+\\max\\bigl(0,v_3(z_*-a):a\\in E,\\ a\\ne z_*\\bigr)\n\\]\nand take \\(n\\) in the exact annulus \\(v_3(n-z_*)=R\\). This automatically satisfies the\n\\(H^k\\) guard, since\n\\[\n v_3(139n+4310)=v_3(n-z_*)=R\\ge14k.\n\\]\nSince \\(z_*\\equiv7\\pmod9\\) and\n\\[\n H^k(n)-z_*=\\alpha^k(n-z_*),\n\\]\nthe same annulus already gives \\(H^k(n)\\equiv7\\pmod9\\), while positivity\nholds for sufficiently large members. The exact affine relation gives\n\\[\n v_3(H^k(n)-z_*)=R-14k.\n\\]\nFor a centre \\(z_*\\), its valuation factor in the \\(H^k\\) ratio is\n\\(3^{-14k\\gamma_{z_*}}\\ge1\\) by (CX-AUD12); for all other centres it is\nunchanged once \\(R\\) exceeds their separations. Thus, after first taking\n\\(n\\to\\infty\\) for each fixed \\(k\\), the \\(H^k\\) term gives\n\\[\n 1\\ge\\frac{c_{\\min}}{c_{\\max}}\\alpha^{k(s-q)}\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\). Letting \\(k\\to\\infty\\) forces \\(s\\ge q\\).\n\n### 8.4 The final \\(L+M\\) contradiction\n\nChoose a fixed \\(R\\) so large that \\(R\\ge v_3(9d)\\),\n\\(R-1>v_3(1-a)\\) for every \\(a\\in E\\) with \\(a\\ne1\\), and\n\\(R-2>v_3(3-a)\\) for every \\(a\\in E\\) with \\(a\\ne3\\). Let \\(n\\to\\infty\\)\nthrough\n\\[\n n=1+9dj,\\qquad v_3(n-1)=R.\n\\]\nThis is an unbounded positive progression after choosing \\(R\\ge\n2+v_3(d)\\). The \\(L\\)-branch remains in residue \\(1\\pmod d\\), and\n\\[\n v_3(L(n)-1)=v_3(n-1)-1.\n\\]\nAll other centre valuations are fixed by the displayed separation bounds.\nTherefore its normalized contribution is\n\\[\n \\left(\\frac43\\right)^{s-q}3^{-\\gamma_1}\\ge1,        \\tag{CX-AUD13}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\gamma_1=0\\) if \\(1\\notin E\\). The \\(M\\)-branch is simultaneously\nadmissible and contributes a strictly positive finite amount \\(C_R\\) for\nthis fixed annulus. If \\(3\\in E\\), its output valuation is \\(R-2\\); this can\nmake \\(C_R\\) small when \\(\\gamma_3\u003c0\\), but \\(C_R>0\\). Since the operator\nterms are nonnegative, (CX-AUD13) plus \\(C_R\\) makes\n\\(\\liminf\\mathcal L_qV(n)/V(n)>1\\), contradicting superharmonicity.\n\nHence the finite-centre extension is valid for the stated common exponent,\npositive periodic factors, and affine slow scale. It does not cover\ninfinitely many centres, unbounded residue precision, centre-dependent\npowers, or fully irregular potentials.\n\n\n## 9. Audit of the residue-dependent exponent extension\n\nThe CX8 argument in the candidate was checked for \\(d=3^h\\). Let\n\\[\n V(n)=(c_{[n]_d}+o(1))\\,n^{s_{[n]_d}}\\ell(n),\n \\qquad c_r>0.\n\\]\nFor a branch with source residue \\(r\\) and terminal residue \\(G_F(r)\\),\nthe single-branch inequality gives\n\\[\n s_r\\le s_{G_F(r)}.                                \\tag{CX-AUD14}\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n G_L^j(r)=1+\\left(\\frac34\\right)^j(r-1),\n\\]\none has \\(G_L^h(r)=1\\pmod{3^h}\\), hence\n\\(S:=\\max_r s_r=s_1\\). For \\(14k\\ge h\\), the \\(H^k\\) guard fixes the\nterminal residue to \\(z_*=-4310/139\\pmod{3^h}\\), while changing the terminal\nby \\(3^{14k}\\) changes the source by the unit \\(2^{22k}\\pmod{3^h}\\).\nThus every source residue occurs, giving \\(s_z=S\\). The iterated \\(H^k\\)\nterm forces \\(S\\ge q\\).\n\nThe terminal progression \\(n=1+3^{h+1}t\\) has\n\\(L(n)=1+4\\cdot3^h t\\), so both residues are \\(1\\pmod{3^h}\\). Its single\nterm forces \\(s_1\\le q\\). Therefore\n\\[\n s_r\\le q\\quad\\text{for all }r,\\qquad s_1=s_z=q.     \\tag{CX-AUD15}\n\\]\nThe coefficient inequalities are also correctly oriented. For any \\(r\\)\nwith \\(s_r=q\\), an \\(L^h\\) path can be obtained by choosing the higher\nterminal digits so that its source residue is \\(r\\); it gives\n\\(c_r\\le c_1\\). The \\(H^k\\) path with source residue \\(1\\) gives\n\\(c_1\\le c_z\\), while \\(r=z\\) in the first inequality gives\n\\(c_z\\le c_1\\). Hence \\(c_z=c_1\\), and\n\\(s_r=q\\) propagates \\(c_r\\le c_{G_F(r)}\\le c_1\\).\n\nThe three displayed tables are arithmetically correct:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\nd&n&L(n)&M(n)\\\\ \\hline\n3&19+27t&25+36t&67+96t\\\\\n9&46+81t&61+108t&163+288t\\\\\n27&73+243t&97+324t&259+864t\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor \\(d=3\\), all four residues are \\(1\\); for \\(d=9\\), the terminal and\n\\(M\\)-source are \\(1\\), while the \\(L\\)-source is \\(7=z\\); for \\(d=27\\),\nthe terminal is \\(19\\) and both sources are \\(16=z\\). In the last case\n\\[\n z=16,\\qquad G_L(16)=19,\\qquad G_L(19)=1\\pmod{27}.\n\\]\nThus \\(s_{16}=q\\) first gives \\(s_{19}=q\\) by (CX-AUD14), and the coefficient\ninequalities give\n\\[\n c_{16}\\le c_{19}\\le c_1,\\qquad c_{16}=c_z=c_1,\n\\]\nso \\(c_{16}=c_{19}=c_1\\). Consequently each of the two \\(L,M\\) terms in\neach table tends to \\(1\\), and their sum contradicts\n\\(\\mathcal L_qV\\le V\\). For \\(d=1\\) the earlier common-exponent theorem\nalready applies.\n\nFor \\(h\\ge4\\), the necessary exponent and coefficient inequalities remain\nvalid, but the two required source residue paths need not share a terminal\nwith compatible higher \\(3\\)-adic digits. The candidate correctly leaves\nthat as a gap; no contradiction for all \\(h\\ge4\\) follows from these tables.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_DEFECT_PARITY_CLOSURE.md":"# Defect parity closure on the 3-mod-4 and 1-mod-4 classes\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded manual analysis of the shifted-pair\ncandidate; no programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents.\n\n## 1. The exact 3-mod-4 relation\n\nFor (n=4k+3), the two shortcut paths give\n\n    n -> 6k+5 -> 9k+8,\n    3n ->18k+14 ->9k+7.\n\nConsequently\n\n    g(n)=beta(9k+7)-beta(9k+8),\n    h(n)=d(9k+7).                                  (SP1)\n\nEquivalently, with (q=(9n+1)/4=9k+7), this is the exact finite-cutoff\ntransport\n\n    sum_(n\u003c=X,n=3 mod4) h(n)/n\n      =sum_(q\u003c= (9X+1)/4, q=7 mod9) 9 d(q)/(4q-1).  (SP2)\n\nThe target is larger than the source by asymptotic factor (9/4), so SP2 is\nnot an energy contraction. It is nevertheless a genuine color identity and\nhas no symbolic-mask assumption.\n\n## 2. The 1-mod-4 shifted cocycle\n\nFor (n=4k+1), put (j=3k+1=(3n+1)/4). Direct T paths give\n\n    beta(n)=beta(j),\n    beta(3n)=beta(9j-1),\n\nand hence\n\n    g(n)=beta(9j-1)-beta(j),       j=1 mod3.         (SP3)\n\nThe SC2 merge has to be applied with care. If (j=1) modulo (4), then\n(3j=3) modulo (4), so SC2 with (q=3j) gives\n\n    beta(9j-1)=beta(3j-1).\n\nBoth endpoints on the right are even, and their one-step predecessors are\nadjacent:\n\n    beta(3j-1)=beta((3j-1)/2),\n    beta(j)=beta((3j+1)/2).\n\nThus this is in fact a direct boundary identity\n\n    h(n)=d((3j-1)/2),   j=1 mod4.                    (SP4)\n\nFor (j=12s+1), equivalently (n=16s+1), this becomes\n\n    h(16s+1)=d(18s+1).                                (SP5)\n\nThe explicit paths are\n\n    16s+1 ->24s+2 ->12s+1 ->18s+2,\n    3(16s+1) ->72s+5 ->108s+8 ->54s+4 ->27s+2,\n    18s+1 ->27s+2.\n\nThe source weight is therefore an expanding, rather than contracting,\nmultiple of the target boundary weight:\n\n    1/(16s+1)=(9/8)(1/(18s+1))+O(s^(-2)).              (SP6)\n\nConversely, if (j=3) modulo (4), SC2 with (q=j) gives\nbeta(3j-1)=beta(j-1), but there is then no SC2 merge of beta(9j-1) with\nbeta(3j-1), since (3j=1) modulo (4). The two residue classes therefore\nhave different behavior: the first gives the boundary transport (SP4),\nwhile the second leaves a shifted endpoint difference.\n\n## 3. Why the split does not close the remaining energy\n\nThe 3-mod-4 class is exactly a progression-restricted boundary transport\n(SP2). The 1-mod-4 class contains the direct boundary transport (SP4),\nincluding the n=16s+1 subfamily (SP5), but its coefficient is 9/8 and thus\nsupplies no contraction. For j even, and for odd j=3 modulo 4, no complete\nSC2 closure is forced by the displayed paths. The target maps have positive\naffine growth, and their inverse multiplicities are not bounded by a common\ncontraction factor.\n\nTherefore the shared beta colors yield genuine partial boundary transports\non both parity classes, but not a positive estimate of the full\n(G=sum h(n)/n): SP2 expands by 9/4, while SP6 expands by 9/8. Any further\ncontraction must control the remaining shifted-cocycle classes jointly; the\npresent identities do not supply that control.\n\n## 4. Scope\n\nSP1--SP10 are exact integer-orbit identities with the stated congruence guards.\nThey do not assert finite boundary mass, finite (G), or a global energy\ncontraction. In particular, the n=16s+1 relation is an exact d-boundary\ntransport with expanding coefficient 9/8, not an energy improvement for all\nn=1 modulo 4.\n\n## 5. An exact descending family for the signed defect\n\nThere is nevertheless a separate, genuinely descending family. Let (kge0),\nlet (m>0) be odd, and assume\n\n    3^(k+1)m = 7 modulo 8.\n\nSet\n\n    n=1+2^(2k+4)m,       p=1+4*3^(k+1)m.\n\nWriting (a=4^k m) and (x=9a+1), the direct paths give\n\n    beta(n)=beta(x),\n    beta(3n)=beta(3x-1),\n    q0=2x-1=1+18*4^k m.\n\nSince (T(2x-1)=3x-1) and beta(2x)=beta(x), this proves the signed identity\n\n    g(n)=-delta(q0),   delta(q)=beta(q+1)-beta(q).  (SP7)\n\nFor every (i\u003ck), the current value\n\n    q_i=1+18*3^i*4^(k-i)m\n\nis 1 modulo 4. The two paths from (q_i) and (q_i+1) to the adjacent\nendpoints for (F(q)=(3q+1)/4) show\n\n    delta(q_i)=delta(F(q_i)).                         (SP8)\n\nAfter (k) such steps,\n\n    q=1+18*3^k m = 43 modulo 48,\n    r=(q+1)/4 = 3 modulo 4,\n    p=(8r-1)/3=1+4*3^(k+1)m.\n\nThe congruences ensure every displayed quotient is integral. The required\ncolor equalities are explicit: beta(q)=beta(9r-1) by two ordinary steps;\nSC2 applied to (9r), which is 3 modulo 4, gives\nbeta(9r-1)=beta(27r-1); and three ordinary steps give\nbeta(27r-1)=beta(8r-1)=beta(3p). Also beta(q+1)=beta(4r)=beta(r)=beta(p).\nThus delta(q)=-g(p), and hence\n\n    g(n)=g(p),\n    p-1=(3/4)^(k+1)(n-1)\u003cn-1.                       (SP9)\n\nFor (k=0), the guard is (m=5) modulo 8 and the first instance is\n(n=128t+81, p=96t+61). This is a strict parameterized descent for the\nsigned defect on the stated arithmetic family. It is not a coverage result:\nno claim is made that every positive start, or every active (h=1) start,\nenters this family.\n\nAs a conditional minimality consequence, if a least positive integer with\nh=1 existed, it could not lie in this family: (SP9) would give a smaller\npositive p with the same nonzero binary defect, hence h(p)=1. This only\nexcludes the displayed guarded family from such a least start; it does not\nestablish existence of a least start or rule out other residue classes.\n\n## 6. Removing the auxiliary odd guard\n\nThe preceding family admits a stronger two-branch form. Let q be any\npositive integer with q=7 modulo 12, put r=(q+1)/4=2^a w with w odd, and\ndefine\n\n    p_minus=(2r-1)/3=(q-1)/6,\n    p_plus =(8r-1)/3=(2q+1)/3.\n\nHere r=2 modulo 3, so both displayed numbers are positive integers. The\nstrip paths give\n\n    beta(q)=C_(a+2)(w),       beta(q+1)=beta(r)=beta(w),\n    beta(p_minus)=beta(p_plus)=beta(w),\n    beta(3p_minus)=C_(a+1)(w),\n    beta(3p_plus)=C_(a+3)(w).\n\nExactly one of 3^(a+1)w and 3^(a+2)w is 3 modulo 4. If the first is, SC2\ngives C_(a+1)=C_(a+2) and hence\n\n    -delta(q)=g(p_minus).\n\nIf the second is, SC2 gives C_(a+2)=C_(a+3) and instead\n\n    -delta(q)=g(p_plus).                              (SP10)\n\nThus exactly one SC2 condition certifies the selected branch. The two\nnumerical equalities need not be distinct: for a constant beta both sides\nvanish. The uniqueness is a uniqueness of the certificate, not of the value.\n\nNow take any k>=0 and odd m>0, without the former condition on\n3^(k+1)m, and set n=1+2^(2k+4)m. The identity (SP7) still gives\ng(n)=-delta(q0), and k applications of F are legal because all\nintermediate q_i are 1 modulo 4. The terminal value is\n\n    q=1+18*3^k m = 7 modulo 12.\n\nApplying (SP10) therefore gives g(n)=g(p_minus) or g(n)=g(p_plus). In\neither case\n\n    p_plus-1=12*3^k m \u003c16*4^k m=n-1,\n\nso this is a strict descent for every n with even v2(n-1)>=4. Consequently\na hypothetical least positive h=1 start cannot lie in this entire\neven-valuation family. The statement remains a restricted descent theorem\nand gives no coverage of odd valuations or of v2(n-1)\u003c4.\n\n## 7. Inverse-weight audit for the even-valuation family\n\nFor the application above, the selected targets have the exact forms\n\n    p_minus=3^(k+1)m,          p_plus=1+12*3^k m.\n\nHence the minus targets are odd and divisible by (3), while the plus\ntargets are (13) modulo (24); the two target classes are disjoint. For a\nfixed minus target (p), all possible inverse indices satisfy\n(0le kle v_3(p)-1), and\n\n    n=1+(16/3)(4/3)^k p.\n\nTherefore the total source weight over its inverse fiber is at most\n\n    sum_(k>=0) 1/((16/3)(4/3)^k p) = 3/(4p).\n\nFor a fixed plus target (p), the inverse indices satisfy\n(1le jle v_3(p-1)), with\n\n    n=1+(4/3)^j(p-1).\n\nSince (p=13) modulo (24), (pge13), and its fiber weight is at most\n\n    sum_(j>=1) 1/((4/3)^j(p-1)) = 3/(p-1) \u003c= 13/(4p).\n\nThese bounds verify the claimed (3/(4p)) and (13/(4p)) coefficients and\nthe absence of double counting between the two branch target classes. The\nsource split itself is by the unique SC2 certificate, not by comparing the\npossibly equal numerical values of (g(p_-)) and (g(p_+)). A\nglobal estimate such as (G\\le17G_{core}+4/7) additionally needs the\nprecise definitions and disjoint source/target partition of the named\nexceptional sets (Zplus, Zold, and B3); those sets are not defined in this\nreport. The inverse-fiber calculation alone therefore certifies the weights,\nnot that final global constant.\n\n## 8. Final audit of SHIFTED_BOUNDARY_DESCENT_AND_CORE\n\nThe complete nine-section root file was checked. SP5's two arithmetic\nbranches are genuine: p_minus=3^(k+1)m and p_plus=1+12*3^k*m are\ndisjoint target classes, and the selector is determined by the terminal\nresidue, not by assumed color independence. The zero-family descriptions\nhave the stated valuation parity and mod-8 separation; N and Zplus are\ndisjoint, while both are disjoint from Z and B_(3:5), whose guards are\n7 modulo 8.\n\nThe inverse sums in SP10 and SP12 give respectively 3/(4p) and\n3/(p-1)\u003c=13/(4p). In LB10 the targets are 1 modulo 18, hence are not\ndivisible by 3; they are therefore disjoint from the odd-multiple-of-3\ntargets in SP10. Since all associated maps descend, a common cutoff X is\nvalid. The coefficient 171/256\u003c3/4 then yields SP14, and the exact source\npartition gives\n\n    G \u003c= G_Cstar + (13/4)G_Cstar + (3/4)G + 1/7,\n\nso SP15 follows as G_Cstar\u003c=G\u003c=17G_Cstar+4/7. This proves only\nequivalence of finite mass with the smaller core; it does not prove either\nmass finite. No correction to the root file is required.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_FINITE_COMPONENT_ENDPOINTS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — endpoint charges for finite retained \\(S/R\\)-components\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная аналитика без программ, CAS,\nпереборов и Lean. Проверены PROJECT_STATE.md и\nBIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md. Цель — точные endpoint\nfacts для finite retained components, без предположения contraction.\n\n## 1. Combined retained graph\n\nПусть \\(F=S\\sqcup R\\), где\n\\[\n S(q)={3q+1\\over2}\\quad(q\\ {\\rm odd}),\\qquad\n R(n)={3n\\over4}\\quad(4\\mid n).\n\\]\nРёбра retained только когда источник и output являются actual births.\n\\(S\\)-образ лежит в \\(2\\pmod3\\), \\(R\\)-образ — в \\(0\\pmod3\\), поэтому\nвходящий тип arithmetic edge единственен. Mixed cycles всё ещё не\nисключены.\n\nОба retained edge сохраняют birth color. Для \\(S\\) это\n\\(\\beta(S(q))=\\beta(q)\\). Для \\(R\\), если \\(n=4m\\) — birth цвета \\(c\\),\nто \\(\\beta(3m+1)=c\\) и \\(\\beta(6m+1)=1-c\\); при \\(d(3m)=0\\) получаем\n\\(\\beta(3m)=c\\), то есть цвет также сохраняется.\n\n## 2. Exact terminal identities\n\nПусть \\(x\\) — terminal vertex finite \\(F\\)-component.\n\nЕсли \\(x\\) нечётен, terminality означает отказ \\(S\\)-маски:\n\\[\n y=S(x)={3x+1\\over2},\\qquad d(y)=1.\n\\]\nBirth identity при outer color \\(c=\\beta(x)\\) даёт\n\\[\n d(2y)=1,\\qquad d(2y+1)=0.                              \\tag{1}\n\\]\nПоэтому \\(y\\) — active boundary, его nearest birth \\(b\\) удовлетворяет\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor {y\\over2}\\right\\rfloor\n   =\\left\\lfloor {3x+1\\over4}\\right\\rfloor.             \\tag{2}\n\\]\nТочный reciprocal endpoint weight выражается через boundary:\n\\[\n {1\\over x}={3\\over 2y-1}.                              \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(x=4m\\) чётен, terminality означает отказ \\(R\\)-маски:\n\\[\n y=R(x)=3m={3x\\over4},\\qquad d(y)=1.\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(\\beta(y+1)=c\\), \\(\\beta(2y+1)=1-c\\), а failure даёт\n\\(\\beta(y)=1-c\\). Поэтому\n\\[\n d(2y)=0,\\qquad d(2y+1)=1.                              \\tag{4}\n\\]\nАктивная boundary \\(y\\) имеет nearest birth\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor {y\\over2}\\right\\rfloor\n   =\\left\\lfloor {3x\\over8}\\right\\rfloor,               \\tag{5}\n\\]\nи здесь точный weight identity есть\n\\[\n {1\\over x}={3\\over4y}.                                 \\tag{6}\n\\]\nИтак, \\(S\\)-terminal принудительно выбирает левый child \\(2y\\), а\n\\(R\\)-terminal — правый child \\(2y+1\\). Это точные color-mask facts.\n\n## 3. What endpoint charging does and does not give\n\nAncestry даёт на каждой глубине не более двух active boundaries, каждый\nне меньше \\(2^\\ell b\\). Поэтому, если суммировать все terminal\n\\(S\\)-endpoints, charged к одному birth \\(b\\), грубо\n\\[\n \\sum {1\\over x}\n =\\sum {3\\over2y-1}\n \\le\\sum {3\\over y}\n \\le {6\\over b};\n\\]\nдля \\(R\\)-endpoints соответственно\n\\[\n \\sum {1\\over x}\n =\\sum {3\\over4y}\n \\le {3\\over2b}.                                       \\tag{7}\n\\]\nЭти bounds относятся к endpoint births charged к \\(b\\), а не к\nпроизвольным origins; они не дают сами по себе сжатия при суммировании\nпо всем \\(b\\). Forced child bits (1) и (4) могут улучшать spacing в\nподклассах, но без общей классификации чередований \\(S/R\\) нельзя\nзаменять (7) единым меньшим коэффициентом.\n\nFor a finite retained chain \\(x_0\\to\\cdots\\to x_L\\), the terminal\nboundary is thus a valid endpoint charge, but its nearest birth need not\nbe the origin \\(x_0\\). In particular, \\(x_L>x_0\\) can occur after S-growth,\nand a later R-edge can decrease the index without identifying the terminal\nancestor with \\(x_0\\).\n\n## 4. Color and the Collatz motion of \\(\\mu=2n+1\\)\n\nFor an \\(S\\)-edge \\(q\\to S(q)=m\\),\n\\[\n T(2q+1)=3q+2=2m+1.\n\\]\nFor an \\(R\\)-edge \\(n=4m\\to R(n)=r=3m\\),\n\\[\n T^2(2n+1)=T^2(8m+1)=6m+1=2r+1.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mu(n)=2n+1\\) follows the ordinary Collatz map along every retained\n\\(F\\)-edge. If the birth color is \\(c\\), then\n\\(\\beta(\\mu(n))=1-c\\); retained edges preserve this opposite color as\nwell. This is an exact orbit identity, not a probability or averaging\nstatement.\n\nThe \\(\\mu\\)-identity does not by itself supply a telescoping harmonic\nweight. It transfers the endpoint problem to an ordinary Collatz path,\nwhose return times and endpoint sizes are not controlled here.\n\n## 5. No common telescoping from the elementary potential \\(1/n\\)\n\nFor the elementary potential \\(\\phi(n)=1/n\\), the two edge increments have\nopposite signs:\n\\[\n \\phi(n)-\\phi(S(n))\n ={n+1\\over n(3n+1)}>0,\n \\qquad\n \\phi(n)-\\phi(R(n))\n =-{1\\over3n}\u003c0.                                       \\tag{8}\n\\]\nThus a signed sum of endpoint differences can cancel along a mixed chain,\nwhile the desired energy \\(\\sum 1/n\\) is positive. A common telescoping\nidentity would require additional control of the order and multiplicity of\n\\(S\\)- and \\(R\\)-edges; color preservation and the \\(\\mu\\)-orbit identity\ndo not provide it. Pure \\(S\\)-chains have the known geometric weight\nbound, and pure \\(R\\)-chains have terminal-based weight \\(\\le4/a\\), but\nthese bounds do not combine into a bound by the directed origin.\n\n## 6. Bounded verdict\n\nThe exact endpoint facts are:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{terminal type}&y=F(x)&\\text{forced active child}&\n\\text{nearest-birth bound}\\\\ \\hline\nS\\text{-terminal}&(3x+1)/2&2y&\nb\\le\\lfloor y/2\\rfloor\\\\\nR\\text{-terminal}&3x/4&2y+1&\nb\\le\\lfloor y/2\\rfloor\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nRetained chains preserve one birth color, and \\(\\mu=2n+1\\) follows the\nordinary Collatz orbit with the opposite color. These facts do not exclude\nmixed cycles and do not yield a joint telescoping bound for the full\nfinite-component energy. Any further contraction must control terminal\nancestor multiplicities together with the origin-to-terminal index change.\n\n## 7. Spacing of endpoint charges and the resulting endpoint-only estimate\n\nThere is a useful extra separation, but it applies only to endpoint charges.\nEvery \\(S\\)-target \\(y\\) has \\(y\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), and its forced active child\nis \\(2y\\equiv1\\pmod3\\). Every \\(R\\)-target has \\(y\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), and its\nforced active child is \\(2y+1\\equiv1\\pmod3\\). Hence two endpoint targets on\none ancestry branch cannot occur at consecutive depths: the forced next\nactive boundary has residue \\(1\\), whereas an endpoint target is an \\(S\\)-\nor \\(R\\)-image and has residue \\(2\\) or \\(0\\). If a charge is at depth\n\\(\\ell\\) above its nearest birth \\(b\\), then \\(y\\ge 2^\\ell b\\). Thus, on\neach branch, the allowed depths give\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\ell\\ge1,\\ \\ell\\ {\\rm nonconsecutive}}{1\\over 2^\\ell b}\n \\le {2\\over3b}.\n\\]\n\nThere are at most two directed branches from a birth, so the total of\n\\(1/y\\) over endpoint charges attached to \\(b\\), with depth at least one,\nis at most \\(4/(3b)\\). The exact identities behind (3) and (6) are\n\n\\[\n {1\\over x_S}={3\\over2y}+{3\\over2y(2y-1)},\\qquad\n {1\\over x_R}={3\\over4y}\\le {3\\over2y}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the main terms on all branches attached to \\(b\\) contribute at\nmost \\(2/b\\). The correction terms occur only for distinct \\(S\\)-targets\n\\(y\\ge2\\), and\n\n\\[\n {3\\over2y(2y-1)}\\le {3\\over2y(y-1)},\\qquad\n \\sum_{y\\ge2}{3\\over2y(y-1)}={3\\over2}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(E_\\beta(X)=\\sum_{b\\le X,\\ b\\ {\\rm birth}}1/b\\), this gives the\nendpoint-only bound\n\n\\[\n E_{\\rm terminal}(X)\n \\le 2E_\\beta\\!\\left({3X+1\\over4}\\right)+{3\\over2}.\n \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHere the \\(S\\)-cutoff \\(b\\le\\lfloor(3X+1)/4\\rfloor\\) is the larger of the\ntwo terminal cutoffs; the \\(R\\)-cutoff is \\(b\\le\\lfloor3X/8\\rfloor\\).\nInjectivity of each branch and disjointness of the two image residues ensure\nthat the correction sum does not count the same target twice. Formula (9)\ndoes not bound the energy of whole finite components: internal vertices,\nrepeated returns, and the choice of a nearest birth remain outside this\nendpoint estimate.\n\n## 8. A bounded \\(S\\) followed by \\(R^r\\) analysis\n\nLet \\(n>1\\) be an odd birth and write\n\n\\[\n a=\\nu_2(3n+1),\\qquad 3n+1=2^a u,\\qquad u\\ {\\rm odd}.\n\\]\n\nAfter the first shortcut \\(S(n)\\), there are \\(a-1\\) factors of \\(2\\) in\nthe output. If \\(a=2r+1\\) is odd and the intervening \\(R\\)-edges are all\nretained, the resulting output is\n\n\\[\n y=3^r u.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(r=0\\) this is the usual one-odd-step \\(S\\)-growth case, and for\n\\(r=1\\) it is the known \\(G_3\\)-growth case. If \\(r\\ge2\\), then\n\n\\[\n n={2^{2r+1}u-1\\over3},\\qquad\n y\u003cn,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(2^{2r+1}-3^{r+1}>1/u\\) (the left side is \\(5\\) at \\(r=2\\) and\nincreases thereafter). Thus a completely retained odd-\\(a\\) block with\n\\(a\\ge5\\) gives a genuine descent.\n\nIf \\(a=2r\\) is even, full retention through the available \\(R\\)-edges ends\nat \\(2\\cdot3^{r-1}u\\equiv2\\pmod4\\), which is not a birth. Therefore some\nmask fails before that endpoint. A failure at the initial \\(S\\)-output\ncharges to a birth at most\n\n\\[\n \\left\\lfloor {S(n)\\over2}\\right\\rfloor\n =\\left\\lfloor {3n+1\\over4}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn\n\\]\n\nfor \\(n>1\\). A failure at an \\(R\\)-output from a current \\(x\\) charges to at\nmost \\(\\lfloor3x/8\\rfloor\\); throughout this block \\(x\\le S(n)\\), hence the\ncharge is at most \\((9n+3)/16\u003cn\\). The entire failed path has height at most\n\\(S(n)\\). This recovers the already identified \\(S\\)-growth and \\(G_3\\)\nmodes after the \\(S\\)-step; it is a bounded block statement, not a proof of\nglobal termination or of a contraction for mixed components.\n\n## 9. Final audit of the balanced-barrier file (BM6--BM8)\n\nThe BM endpoint calculation is consistent with the preceding identities.\nFor an \\(S\\)-terminal, \\(d(y)=1\\) gives \\(d(2y)=1\\) and\n\\(d(2y+1)=0\\); for an \\(R\\)-terminal it gives \\(d(2y)=0\\) and\n\\(d(2y+1)=1\\). Since the arithmetic images have residues \\(2\\) and \\(0\\)\nmodulo \\(3\\), respectively, the forced child in either case has residue\n\\(1\\), so it cannot itself be a new endpoint target. The nonconsecutive-depth\nsum is \\(2/(3b)\\) per branch, hence \\(4/(3b)\\) over the two branches.\nMultiplication by the common main cost \\(3/(2y)\\) gives \\(2/b\\), and the\nexact \\(S\\)-correction is bounded by the telescoping majorant \\(3/2\\).\nThus BM's endpoint bound \\(2E_\\beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2\\) is correct;\nit is not a bound for internal component vertices.\n\nThe sufficient BM8 descent test is also exact: \\(F(x)\u003c2m\\) implies the\nnearest charged birth is \\(\u003cm\\), with the displayed inequalities\n\\(3x+1\u003c4m\\) for \\(S\\) and \\(3x\u003c8m\\) for \\(R\\). It is only sufficient.\nBM7's \\(R\\)-run compression is the same bounded \\(S\\)-then-\\(R^r\\) split\nchecked above: even \\(a\\) must fail at \\(2\\bmod4\\), odd \\(a\\ge5\\) descends,\nand only \\(a=1,3\\) remain as \\(S,G_3\\) growth modes. This does not identify\nthe whole mixed graph with the earlier \\(S/G_3\\) graph.\n\nFinally, BM8 preserves the logical scope of the energy bridge. Finite\n\\(\\sum e(n)/n\\) implies finite \\(\\sum d(n)/n\\), and the SB174 argument gives\nconstancy outside a density-zero set. Pointwise constancy uses the named\nPD170 predecessor-density premise; it is an accepted conditional bridge,\nnot a PD-free consequence established here. The source file explicitly\nretains that premise and does not claim to have re-audited its entire\nanalytic proof. The full energy upper bound remains necessary for this\nroute. A PD-free version additionally needs its own pointwise bridge;\nBM6--BM8 closes neither obligation. These two wording clarifications\nwere made by root after reading the final audit.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_FINITE_CUTOFF_DEFECT_BALANCE.md":"# Finite-cutoff defect balance for the odd-source map\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual bounded audit of the odd-source\nmap; no programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents.\n\n## 1. Uniform reindexing lemma\n\nLet\n\n    U(n)=oddpart((3n+1)/2)       (n odd),\n\nand let (f) be bounded and dyadic-invariant, (f(2m)=f(m)). Put\n\n    F_i(X)=sum_(m\u003c=X, m odd, m congruent to i mod 3) f(m)/m.\n\nFor (n) odd, write ((3n+1)/2=2^{k-1}m), with (m) odd and (k>=1).\nThen\n\n    n=(2^k m-1)/3,\n\nand the inverse exists exactly when (2^k m congruent to 1 mod 3). For\nthe finite cutoff (n\u003c=X), the additional condition is (2^k m\u003c=3X+1).\nConsequently\n\n    sum_(n odd, n\u003c=X) f(U(n))/n\n      =sum_(m odd) f(m)\n        sum_(k>=1, 2^k m=1 mod3, 2^k m\u003c=3X+1)\n             3/(2^k m-1).                           (FC1)\n\nThe variable inverse tail is uniformly harmless. Terms with (m>X) imply\n((3n+1)/2> X), hence (n>(2X-1)/3), and their total (1/n)-weight is\nbounded by a fixed constant. For (m\u003c=X), replacing\n\n    3/(2^k m-1) by 3/(2^k m)\n\nhas total error (O(sum_m m^{-2})=O(1)). The omitted geometric terms with\n(2^k m>3X+1) also have total (O(1)): for each (k), their contribution is\nbounded by\n\n    (3/2^k) sum_((3X+1)/2^k\u003cm\u003c=X) 1/m,\n\nand summing the resulting (O((k+1)2^{-k})) bound over (k) is finite.\nThis proves uniformity in (X), including the denominator shift by (-1).\n\nThe infinite geometric coefficients are\n\n    sum_(j>=1) 3/(4^j m)=1/m,       m congruent to 1 mod 3,\n    sum_(j>=0) 3/(2*4^j m)=2/m,     m congruent to 2 mod 3,\n\nand there is no inverse when (3|m). Hence the exact finite-cutoff formula is\n\n    sum_(n odd, n\u003c=X) [f(U(n))-f(n)]/n\n       =F_2(X)-F_0(X)+O_f(1).                         (FC2)\n\nThe constant depends only on the bound for (f), not on (X). This is a\nfinite-cutoff statement; it does not rely on an unjustified uniform limit\n(s down to 1) of a Dirichlet series.\n\n## 2. Application to the absolute defect\n\nLet (g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n)), (h(n)=|g(n)|), and for odd (n) put\n\n    v(n)=(9n+1)/2,       d(v)=|beta(v+1)-beta(v)|.\n\nThe exact commutator gives (g(U(n))-g(n)=beta(v+1)-beta(v)). Also\nbeta(U(n))=beta(n), because U differs from S(n) only by powers of two.\nSince each of g(U(n)) and g(n) lies in ({-1,0,1}) and their difference is a\nsingle beta difference in ({-1,0,1}), the forbidden sign change of size two\ncannot occur. Thus a nonzero right side changes the absolute value by one,\nwith the direction determined by (h(n)). Define\n\n    c_+(n)=(1-h(n))d(v(n)),\n    c_-(n)=h(n)d(v(n)).                              (FC3)\n\nThen pointwise on odd (n),\n\n    h(U(n))-h(n)=c_+(n)-c_-(n),\n    c_+(n)+c_-(n)=d(v(n)).                            (FC4)\n\nApplying (FC2) to (f=h) gives the positive creation/death balance\n\n    sum_(n odd, n\u003c=X) [c_+(n)-c_-(n)]/n\n       =H_2(X)-H_0(X)+O(1),                           (FC5)\n\nwhere (H_i) is the harmonic mass of (h) on odd numbers congruent to\n(i\\pmod3). Thus the exact result accounts for the common colors: creation\nis possible only where (h(n)=0), and death only where (h(n)=1).\n\n## 3. Precise strength and remaining gap\n\nEquation (FC5) is only an equality between the unknown creation-minus-death\nsum and (H_2-H_0), up to O(1). Neither side is known to be bounded. In\nparticular, it does not imply bounded drift of H_2-H_0 or force H_0 and H_2\nto diverge together when G diverges; even a one-sided projected balance is\nnot excluded by this identity. The middle class H_1 is not touched.\n\nThe second identity in (FC4) only identifies the total sampled boundary mass:\n\n    sum (c_++c_-)/n = sum d((9n+1)/2)/n.\n\nWithout an independent sparse or summable bound for this sampled (d)-mass,\nthe positive balance cannot be converted into a bound for (G). Therefore\n(FC2)--(FC5) are a genuine finite-cutoff color-sensitive refinement, but they\ndo not yield summability, contraction, or constancy of beta.\n\n## 4. Conditional positive correction at the critical weight\n\nThe denominator expansion keeps a useful sign. For odd m not divisible by 3,\nlet a_m=1 for m congruent to 1 mod 3 and a_m=2 for m congruent to 2 mod 3.\nThen\n\n    epsilon(m)=sum_(k admissible) 3/(2^k m-1)-a_m/m >=0,\n    epsilon(m)=O(m^(-2)),                            (FC6)\n\nand sum over odd m of epsilon(m) is finite. If G is assumed finite, all terms\nmay be summed absolutely and (FC5) sharpens to the conditional identity\n\n    sum c_+(n)/n-sum c_-(n)/n\n       =sum_(m congruent 2 mod 3) h(m)/m\n        -sum_(m congruent 0 mod 3) h(m)/m\n        +sum_(3 does not divide m) epsilon(m)h(m).    (FC7)\n\nThe correction is nonnegative and absolutely convergent, but this identity is\nconditional on the very summability of G that the argument seeks. It supplies\nno independent upper bound or contraction and therefore does not close the\nremaining defect problem.\n\n## 5. Final audit of DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION\n\nI read the complete root file `DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md`.\nThe correction estimate is valid: for every admissible (A=2^k m\\ge4),\n\n    0 \u003c= 3/(A-1)-3/A \u003c= 4/A^2.\n\nSumming over all k gives epsilon(m)\u003c=4/(3m^2), and the coarse total bound\nsum epsilon(m)\u003c=8/3 is correct. The finite remainder decomposition is exact.\nThe extra-target part has weight at most 2 for a nonnegative f, and the\ninverse-tail part has weight at most 2, so the signed bound is |R_f|\u003c=4M;\nfor 0\u003c=f\u003c=M their difference indeed has |R_f|\u003c=2M. These estimates are\nuniform in X, including the small cutoff case.\n\nThe root DF4 identity therefore correctly strengthens FC5 by retaining the\npositive epsilon correction. Its infinite version is conditional on G\u003cinfinity;\nno unjustified s-down-to-1 passage is made.\n\nThe finite-set test DF6 is also sound. For any finite odd set F, the function\nf_F based on oddpart is dyadically invariant, vanishes on any prescribed\nfinite initial range after enlarging F, yet has infinite odd harmonic mass.\nIts variation is bounded by the finite source mass on F plus the finite inverse\nkernel mass sum over m in F of W(m). This shows that even total U-variation\ncannot control the relaxed class. The example was not constructed from an actual common beta, so it is not\na verified counterexample to the Collatz-invariant problem; no impossibility\nof such a representation is being proved here.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_FIRST_PRIMITIVE_COLUMN.md":"# First primitive birth on a ternary column\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analytical audit only; no\nprograms, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or GPU.\n\n## 1. Column and first primitive exponent\n\nFix an odd (m) with (3\\nmid m). A primitive birth on this column at\nexponent (a\\ge2) is the event\n\n    e(3^a m)=1,\n    h(m)=h(3m)=...=h(3^(a-1)m)=0.\n\nLet (mathcal A_m) be the set of such exponents and, if it is nonempty,\nlet (A=minmathcal A_m). This definition imposes no claim that a first\nprimitive exponent exists, or that later exponents belong to the set.\nIn particular, a birth at exponent (A) does not by itself determine\n(h(3^A m)), so it does not automatically exclude later primitive births.\n\nFor (a\\inmathcal A_m), put (u_a=3^{a-1}m) and (p_a=4u_a+1).\nThe exact PB plus identity gives the finite fiber\n\n    F_{a,m}=\\{n_{a,\\ell}=1+4u_a(4/3)^\\ell:\n                     0\\le\\ell\\le a-1\\}.\n\nEvery member has the relevant PB defect value, while at a finite cutoff\nonly members with (n_{a,\\ell}\\le X) are retained. No finite-energy\nassumption is used here.\n\n## 2. Injectivity and overlaps\n\nFor (n=n_{a,\\ell}),\n\n    n-1=2^{2\\ell+2}3^{a-1-\\ell}m.                (FP1)\n\nThus (v_2(n-1)=2\\ell+2) recovers (ell). After that,\n\n    u_a=(n-1)3^\\ell/4^{\\ell+1}\n\nrecovers (u_a), and its (3)-adic valuation recovers (a-1) and the\n3-free column representative (m). Consequently fibers belonging to\ndifferent pairs ((a,m)) are disjoint. This is an actual source\ninjectivity statement; a reindexed target charge can still overlap a\nseparate accounting sum.\n\nThe complete arithmetic majorant of one fiber is\n\n    W_a(m)\u003c\\overline W_a(m):\n    = [1-(3/4)^a]/[3^{a-1}m].                    (FP2)\n\nThe strict inequality is from the positive `+1` in each denominator. A\nfinite-cutoff fiber (W_{a,X}(m)) is at most this majorant.\n\n## 3. Geometric tail after the first primitive birth\n\nIf (A=minmathcal A_m), dropping the actual primitive masks for the\nlater exponents gives\n\n    sum_(a>A) W_{a,X}(m)\n      \u003c= sum_(a>A) overline W_a(m)\n      = [3^(1-A)/2 - 4^(-A)]/m.                  (FP3)\n\nIndeed the two geometric sums are\n`sum_(a>A)3^(1-a)=3^(1-A)/2` and\n`sum_(a>A)3^(1-a)(3/4)^a=4^(-A)`.\n\nRelative to the first fiber's majorant, the tail coefficient is\n\n    R_A = [1/2-(1/3)(3/4)^A]/[1-(3/4)^A].       (FP4)\n\nFor (A\\ge2), (R_A\\le5/7\u003c1), with equality of the majorants at\n(A=2). This is a genuine geometric contraction of the *majorant tail*\nrelative to the first-fiber majorant. By itself this majorant comparison\ndoes not compare two truncated actual fibers; the same-cutoff argument in\n§5 supplies that stronger comparison.\n\nRelative instead to the harmonic weight of the first birth itself,\n(1/(3^A m)), the later-fiber bound is\n\n    3/2-(3/4)^A.                                (FP5)\n\nThis is below one for (A=2), but exceeds one for every sufficiently\nlarge (A) (already (A=3)). Thus this displayed unmasked majorant does\nnot supply a uniform coefficient below one relative to the single birth\nweight. It does not rule out a sharper bound using the actual masks.\nIncluding the first fiber gives the larger majorant ratio\n\n    9/2-4(3/4)^A\n\nrelative to (1/(3^A m)).\n\n## 4. Honest finite-cutoff relation and remaining gap\n\nLet (M_{\\rm prim}(X)) be the total PB-fiber mass from primitive births\nwith (p_a\\le X). By source injectivity and positivity,\n\n    M_{\\rm prim}(X)\n      \u003c= sum_m sum_(a in A_m, p_a\u003c=X) W_{a,X}(m). \n\nFor each column with a first exponent (A), split the right side into\nthe first fiber and the later tail, then apply (FP2)--(FP3). This is a\nvalid finite-cutoff relation and remains true if the inner sums are\nrestricted to actual later primitive exponents. It does not bound the\nnumber or harmonic mass of columns having a first primitive birth, and it\ndoes not control the first fiber itself; §5 reduces the tail to that\nuncontrolled first-fiber mass at the same cutoff.\n\nThe displayed unmasked majorant relative to the single harmonic birth\nweight is not uniformly subunit. That limitation of this estimate does\nnot prove that a stronger actual-mask charge is impossible. The later fibers do admit\nthe same-cutoff reduction to the first actual fiber in §5. The primitive\ncore and total termination remain open; no arbitrary color realization or\nfinite-energy hypothesis was used.\n\n## 5. Independent same-cutoff refinement\n\nThe tail can be compared with the *truncated* first fiber at the same\ncutoff, so the preceding full-fiber comparison is not the strongest finite\nstatement. Write\n\n    x_l=4^(l+1)3^(A-1-l)m,\n    n_{A,l}=1+x_l,   0\u003c=l\u003c=A-1.\n\nSince (A\\ge2) and (m\\ge1), every (x_l\\ge x_0=4\\cdot3^{A-1}m\\ge12).\nThe first majorant is\n\n    W_A^0=sum_l 1/x_l=(3-3(3/4)^A)/(3^A m),\n\nand the full later tail is at most\n\n    (3/2-(3/4)^A)/(3^A m).\n\nTheir ratio is at most (5/7). Also\n\n    W_A=sum_l 1/(1+x_l) >=(12/13)W_A^0,\n\nso when the first fiber is complete the later tail is at most\n(65/84\\,W_A).\n\nIf the cutoff (X) is below its last first-fiber member, any later\nsource below (X) must have (l\\le A-2): for (l\\ge A-1), its first\nlater value already exceeds (1+x_{A-1}). For a fixed remaining (l),\nthe later exponents give (x_l3^r), (r\\ge1), and hence\n\n    sum_(r>=1)1/(1+x_l3^r) \u003c=1/(2x_l)\n                              \u003c=(13/24)/(1+x_l).\n\nSumming over the first-fiber members retained at the same cutoff gives a\nstronger (13/24) bound in this partial case. Therefore in both cutoff\ncases the later primitive fibers are bounded by (65/84) times the\ntruncated first fiber. Across columns where some later exponents are\nabsent, the bound only improves. If (F_{\\rm first}(X)) denotes the\nmass of all first primitive fibers retained at (X), then\n\n    M_{\\rm prim}(X) \u003c= F_{\\rm first}(X)+65/84 F_{\\rm first}(X)\n                    =149/84 F_{\\rm first}(X).  (FP6)\n\nThis is a genuine same-cutoff reduction, but (F_{\\rm first}(X)) itself\nis uncontrolled; (FP6) does not solve the primitive core or imply finite\ntotal energy.\n\n## 6. Final audit of CF1--CF11\n\nThe complete `FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION.md` was checked. CF1--CF5\nagree with the column decomposition above, and CF6's exponent-one boundary\nis arithmetically correct: the reciprocal multipliers in its tail sum to\none, their squared sum is (1/5), and therefore\n\n    S(x) >= 1/x-1/(5x^2) > 1/(x+1),  x=4m>=4.\n\nThis is only an unmasked arithmetic statement; it does not realize actual\nprimitive births at every exponent. CF7 is also valid for (A\\ge3). With\n(y=3^{A-1}m), firstness gives (e(y)=0), while the birth at (3y) gives\n(e(3y)=1). Thus (\\beta(2y+1)=\\beta(y)=B). If the smaller point\n(2y+1) has (h=1), it supplies a genuine lower defect. If it has (h=0),\nthe four-color residual mask in CF11 follows from the exact even and odd\nT-path identities. No contradiction is claimed, and (A=2) is correctly\nexcluded from this argument because exponent one is outside the primitive\ndefinition.\n\nFinally, CF8--CF9 correctly preserve the same finite cutoff and yield\n\n    F(X) \u003c= J_prim(X) \u003c= (149/84)F(X).\n\nThe new conclusion is a reduction to first primitive fibers, not a bound on\ntheir mass. The earlier majorant-only wording in §§3--4 has been narrowed\naccordingly; the actual same-cutoff contraction is the one proved in §5.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_FIXED_SEED_POSITIVITY.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — fixed-seed positivity and consecutive contractions\n\n18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной анализ положительных inverse\nschedules for full ordered structural blocks. Программ, переборов, CAS и\nLean не использовалось. The statement is seed-dependent and does not claim\na uniform bound over all positive terminal seeds.\n\n## 1. Affine schedules and the endpoint inequality\n\nWrite one full inverse block as\n\\[\n F_i(x)=\\alpha_i x-\\beta_i\n       =\\frac{2^{K_i}x-C_i}{3^{P_i}},\n \\qquad \\alpha_i>0,\\quad\\beta_i=\\frac{C_i}{3^{P_i}}>0.\n\\]\nFor a nonempty genuine word \\(C_i>0\\). A finite schedule\n\\(w=i_1\\cdots i_t\\) has\n\\[\n F_w(x)=\\alpha_w x-\\beta_w,\\qquad\n \\alpha_w=\\prod_{r=1}^t\\alpha_{i_r},\\quad \\beta_w>0,       \\tag{FS1}\n\\]\nbecause composition sends \\((\\alpha,\\beta),(\\alpha',\\beta')\\) to\n\\((\\alpha\\alpha',\\beta+\\alpha\\beta')\\).\n\nFix a positive terminal seed \\(n\\), and let \\(w\\) be nonempty. If the\nschedule is locally integral and produces a positive integer endpoint\n\\(x=F_w(n)\\), then\n\\[\n \\alpha_w n=x+\\beta_w>x,\n \\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\n \\alpha_w>\\frac{x}{n}.                                \\tag{FS2}\n\\]\nFor every \\(q>0\\),\n\\[\n \\alpha_w^{-q}\u003c\\left(\\frac{n}{x}\\right)^q.              \\tag{FS3}\n\\]\nThus all schedules from this fixed \\(n\\) whose endpoint is at least \\(Y\\)\nhave reciprocal-\\(q\\)-weight \\(\u003c(n/Y)^q\\). This is a per-schedule bound;\nit does not bound the number of schedules or exclude collisions.\n\nLocal integrality means that every intermediate \\(x_r\\) satisfies the\n3-adic guard of the next block. For a genuine block, a positive integral\ninput automatically gives a positive source and its prescribed parity word\nby the first-bit induction. Therefore positivity is checked at every\nprefix, not inferred from a final formal rational value.\n\n## 2. What a consecutive contracting run can do\n\nCall a block contracting when \\(\\alpha_i\u003c1\\), equivalently\n\\(2^{K_i}\u003c3^{P_i}\\). For \\(x>0\\),\n\\[\n F_i(x)=\\alpha_i x-\\beta_i\u003cx.\n\\]\nIf its output is a positive integer, it is at most \\(x-1\\). Consequently a\nschedule consisting only of contracting blocks, starting at fixed \\(n\\), has\nat most \\(n-1\\) nonempty steps. More generally, a consecutive contracting\nrun beginning at a current positive integer \\(m\\) has length at most \\(m-1\\).\nThis elementary bound does not control a schedule that alternates expansions\nand contractions.\n\nFor one fixed contracting block\n\\[\n F(x)=\\frac{A x-B}{D},\\qquad A=2^K,\\quad D=3^P,\\quad\n 0\u003cA\u003cD,\\quad B>0,\n\\]\nput\n\\[\n E(x)=(D-A)x+B.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n E(F(x))=\\frac{A}{D}E(x),\\qquad\n F^t(n)=\\frac{A^tE(n)-BD^t}{(D-A)D^t}.                 \\tag{FS4}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\gcd(A,D)=\\gcd(D-A,D)=1\\), the numerator after division by\n\\(D-A\\) is integral and\n\\[\n F^t(n)\\in\\mathbb Z\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n D^t\\mid E(n).                                        \\tag{FS5}\n\\]\nThe same condition makes every earlier iterate integral. For positive\n\\(n\\), \\(E(n)>0\\), so a repeated contracting block has the exact finite\ninteger budget\n\\[\n t\\le\n \\left\\lfloor\\frac{v_3(E(n))}{P}\\right\\rfloor.          \\tag{FS6}\n\\]\nPositivity can only shorten this budget; for a genuine block with the guard\nit is already automatic at each integral prefix.\n\n## 3. The ordered contraction \\(H=S^2\\)\n\nThe reversing block in the audited IR4 calculation is\n\\[\n S(x)=\\frac{2048x-4310}{2187}.\n\\]\nIts ordered square is\n\\[\n H(x)=S^2(x)\n =\\frac{A x-B}{D}\n =\\frac{4\\,194\\,304x-18\\,252\\,850}{4\\,782\\,969},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n A=2^{22},\\qquad D=3^{14},\\qquad B=18\\,252\\,850,\\qquad A\u003cD.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n D-A=588\\,665=139\\cdot4235,\\qquad\n B=4310\\cdot4235.\n\\]\nThe fixed point is\n\\[\n z_*=-\\frac{4310}{139},\n\\]\nand, with \\(E_H(x)=139x+4310\\),\n\\[\n E_H(H(x))=\\frac{A}{D}E_H(x).                         \\tag{FS7}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n H^t(n)=\n \\frac{A^t(139n+4310)-4310D^t}{139D^t}.               \\tag{FS8}\n\\]\nBecause \\(139\\mid(D-A)\\), one has \\(A^t\\equiv D^t\\pmod {139}\\); because\n\\(\\gcd(A,D)=1\\), (FS8) yields the exact repeated guard\n\\[\n H^t(n)\\in\\mathbb Z\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n 3^{14t}=D^t\\mid 139n+4310\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n v_3(139n+4310)\\ge14t.                                \\tag{FS9}\n\\]\nAll earlier H-iterates are then integral as well. For positive \\(n\\), the\nvalid iterates are positive: \\(E_H(n)>0\\), and the divisibility\n\\(D^t\\mid E_H(n)\\) gives \\(E_H(n)\\ge D^t\\), hence\n\\(E_H(H^j(n))\\ge A^j>4310\\) for the relevant \\(j\\ge1\\).\n\nThe one-step guard is the unique class\n\\[\n n\\equiv2\\,959\\,216\\pmod {4\\,782\\,969}.                \\tag{FS10}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n 139\\cdot2\\,959\\,216+4310\n =411\\,335\\,334\n =86\\cdot4\\,782\\,969.\n\\]\nThe valuation of this representative is exactly \\(14\\), since \\(3\\nmid86\\);\nit therefore admits one H repetition and not two. For any fixed positive\n\\(n\\), the maximum number of consecutive H blocks is exactly\n\\[\n t_{\\max}(n)=\n \\left\\lfloor\\frac{v_3(139n+4310)}{14}\\right\\rfloor.   \\tag{FS11}\n\\]\nThe real contraction is also strict:\n\\[\n H(x)-x=-\\frac{(D-A)x+B}{D}\u003c0\\qquad(x>0).              \\tag{FS12}\n\\]\n\n## 4. Fixed-seed consequences and limits\n\nFor a fixed \\(n\\), (FS2)--(FS3) imply that a schedule reaching a large\npositive endpoint has small reciprocal weight. A purely contracting schedule\nhas at most \\(n-1\\) steps, and a consecutive H-run has the sharper\nvaluation bound (FS11). These are exact restrictions on fixed-seed\nconsecutive compressions.\n\nThey do not control arbitrary schedules containing expanding blocks. Such a\nschedule can first move to a larger integer and later use a contracting\nblock; (FS2) controls only the product slope relative to the final endpoint,\nwhile local guards control which blocks can actually follow. In particular,\nthe existence for every \\(t\\) of a different positive seed in the H^t guard\ndoes not imply that any one positive seed has unbounded H depth.\n\nThe fixed point \\(z_*=-4310/139\\) is 3-adically admissible and is not a\npositive integer. The fixed-seed valuation formula shows exactly where the\npositive-integer question differs: every fixed positive integer has finite\n\\(v_3(139n+4310)\\), hence finite H depth. No conclusion about the full\npaired-predecessor set or Collatz follows.\n\n\n## 5. Final independent audit of `POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md`\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). I reread PR1--PR6\nline by line and find no arithmetic correction required in the claims that\nare used here. The following checks record the precise scope.\n\n### 5.1 PR1--PR2: the individual inverse-weight estimate\n\nFor a nonempty genuine inverse word with \\(P\\) odd steps, its intercept has\nthe form\n\\[\n C=\\sum_{j=1}^{P}3^{P-j}2^{i_j},\\qquad 0\\le i_1\u003c\\cdots\u003ci_P,\n\\]\nso \\(C\\ge 3^{P-1}\\) and \\(B=C/3^P\\ge1/3\\). A composition of blocks is\nagain one genuine word, hence the same estimate applies to its total\nintercept. If its positive endpoint is \\(m\\) and its positive terminal is\n\\(n\\), then\n\\[\n m=A n-B,\\qquad A=2^K/3^P,\n\\]\nand therefore \\(A=(m+B)/n>m/n\\), giving\n\\[\n A^{-q}\u003c(n/m)^q \\quad(q>0).\n\\]\nThe empty word is the only equality case. This verifies PR1--PR2; it does\nnot bound alternating expanding and contracting schedules, as the candidate\ncorrectly states.\n\nThe positivity assertion used there is also correctly scoped. Divisibility\nby the terminal guard gives each prescribed parity bit by the usual first-bit\ninduction. A genuine forward word cannot start from a nonpositive integer\nand end at a positive integer, since both branches of \\(T\\) preserve\nnonpositivity. Thus, at a positive terminal, an integral guarded inverse word\nis positive; this is not an extra independent positivity hypothesis.\n\n### 5.2 PR3--PR4: cycles and the \\(q>1\\) resolvent\n\nThe induced ordered map \\(R\\) is partial but deterministic because the source\ncylinders are disjoint. If \\(m\\) reaches \\(n\\) at two depths \\(d\u003ce\\), then\n\\(R^{e-d}(n)=n\\); hence, for a nonperiodic \\(n\\), each endpoint has one\ndepth. For a periodic \\(n\\), all depths after the first hit differ by the\nleast period \\(r\\), and the inverse slopes are \\(A_mA_c^t\\). This justifies\nthe geometric factor in PR3 (the affine intercept is irrelevant to the\nweight).\n\nFor a nonempty positive cycle word in the ordered alphabet, \\(P_c\\ge1\\):\nthe consecutive source pair contains an odd member, so an all-even block\ncannot preserve the ordered pair. Hence \\(B_c=C_c/3^{P_c}\\ge1/3\\), and its\nfixed-cycle identity is \\(n=A_c n-B_c\\). Consequently\n\\[\n A_c=1+B_c/n\\ge1+1/(3n)>1,\n \\qquad\n \\frac1{1-A_c^{-q}}\\le3n+1\\quad(q\\ge1).\n\\]\nThis confirms both the coarse \\(B_c\\ge1/3\\) bound and the factor used in\nPR3--PR4. Since \\(M(n)\\subset\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\), the remaining estimate by\n\\(\\sum_{m\\ge1}m^{-q}=\\zeta(q)\\) is valid only for \\(q>1\\). No \\(q=1\\)\nconclusion is silently obtained.\n\n### 5.3 PR6: exact budget for \\(H=S^2\\)\n\nWith \\(A=2^{22}\\), \\(D=3^{14}\\), and \\(B=18\\,252\\,850\\), the identities\n\\[\n D-A=139\\cdot4235,\\qquad B=4310\\cdot4235\n\\]\ngive \\(a=4310/139\\), \\(H(x)+a=(A/D)(x+a)\\), and\n\\[\n H^t(n)=\n \\frac{A^t(139n+4310)-4310D^t}{139D^t}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\gcd(139,D)=\\gcd(A,D)=1\\), and \\(A\\equiv D\\pmod {139}\\),\nthe displayed value is integral exactly when\n\\[\n D^t=3^{14t}\\mid 139n+4310,\n \\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad\n t\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{v_3(139n+4310)}{14}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe implication in both directions is worth recording: the congruence\n\\(A\\equiv D\\pmod {139}\\) first removes the harmless denominator \\(139\\),\nand invertibility of \\(A\\) modulo \\(D^t\\) then reduces the condition to\n\\(D^t\\mid139n+4310\\). The same divisibility holds at every earlier power,\nso this is the exact integral repetition budget for fixed \\(n\\).\n\nFor \\(n>0\\), if the \\(t\\)-fold guard holds then \\(139n+4310\\ge D^t\\), and\n\\[\n 139H^j(n)+4310=(A/D)^j(139n+4310)\\ge A^j>4310\n \\quad(1\\le j\\le t).\n\\]\nThus every integral intermediate value is positive; equivalently, positivity\nis supplied by the genuine guarded parity word, not by an order on\n\\(\\mathbb Z_2\\). The real contraction gives the additional necessary bound\n\\[\n H^t(n)\\ge1\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n t\\le\\frac{\\log((n+a)/(1+a))}{\\log(1/\\alpha)},\n \\qquad \\alpha=A/D\u003c1,\n\\]\nand the divisibility gives \\(139n+4310\\ge3^{14t}\\). These are compatible\nnecessary bounds; they do not assert that every seed in the congruence class\nhas an arbitrary additional orbit property.\n\n**Verdict.** PR1--PR2, the cycle estimate \\(B_c\\ge1/3\\), and PR6 are\ncorrect within their stated deterministic structural scope. The report makes\nno \\(q=1\\), density, full-predecessor, or full-Collatz inference, and no\nchange to the existing fixed-seed conclusions is needed.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_GENERAL_BLOCK_LENGTH.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — a general fixed-\\(P\\) length bound for ordered structural lifts\n\n18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной анализ общего ordered structural\nкласса с одинаковыми длиной \\(K\\), odd count \\(P\\) и terminal gap\n\\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{+1,-1\\}\\). Таблицы \\(P=4\\), программ, переборов, CAS и\nLean не использовались.\n\n## 1. Setup and prefix quantity\n\nLet the left and right genuine parity words have common length \\(K\\) and\ncommon odd count \\(P\\), with prefix odd counts \\(p_L(k),p_R(k)\\) and prefix\nintercepts \\(C_L(k),C_R(k)\\) after the first \\(k\\) bits. If the terminal\nright-minus-left gap is \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{+1,-1\\}\\), then\n\\[\n 3^P+C_R-C_L=\\varepsilon 2^K.                  \\tag{G1}\n\\]\nThe standard prefix/suffix decomposition gives\n\\[\n D_k=\\frac{3^P+3^{P-p_R(k)}C_R(k)-3^{P-p_L(k)}C_L(k)}{2^k}. \\tag{G2}\n\\]\nThe numerator is divisible by \\(2^k\\): decompose each full intercept into\nits prefix part multiplied by the remaining \\(3\\)-power and its suffix part\nmultiplied by \\(2^k\\), then use (G1). Thus \\(D_k\\in\\mathbb Z\\). At the\nendpoints,\n\\[\n D_0=3^P,\\qquad D_K=\\varepsilon.                  \\tag{G3}\n\\]\nThis is the signed-coefficient form of\n\\(3^P+C_R-C_L-\\varepsilon2^K=0\\), with all prefix cancellations exposed.\n\n## 2. A proper low-position cancellation cannot make \\(D_k=0\\)\n\nSuppose first that \\(p_L(k)\\ne p_R(k)\\). The terms in the numerator of\n(G2) have 3-adic orders \\(P\\), \\(P-p_R(k)\\), and \\(P-p_L(k)\\). We use\n\\(v_3(0)=+\\infty\\), so a zero prefix intercept causes no exception. The term\nwith the larger prefix odd count has the uniquely smallest order. Its prefix\nintercept is a 3-unit whenever that prefix contains an odd step: modulo 3,\nits last odd contribution is a nonzero power of 2. Hence the numerator has a\nunique lowest 3-adic order and cannot vanish.\n\nIf \\(p_L(k)=p_R(k)=p\\), then the numerator is\n\\[\n 3^{P-p}\\bigl(3^p+C_R(k)-C_L(k)\\bigr).           \\tag{G4}\n\\]\nIt can vanish only if\n\\[\n 3^p+C_R(k)-C_L(k)=0.                              \\tag{G5}\n\\]\nThis is an actual trajectory statement, not an extra assumption: choose a\nsufficiently large positive terminal integer in the unique \\(3^P\\)-guard\nfor the left word. Relation (G1) makes the right inverse integral as well;\nfirst-bit induction then makes both inverse sources positive genuine word\ntrajectories. For these sources, (G5) says that the two prefix trajectories\nfrom \\(x,x+1\\) have coalesced:\n\\[\n T^k_R(x+1)-T^k_L(x)\n =\\frac{3^p+C_R(k)-C_L(k)}{2^k}=0.\n\\]\nFrom a common integer state the shortcut map is deterministic. Therefore\nthe remaining suffixes must follow the same parity bits and have the same\nfinal state, contradicting the prescribed distinct final pair, whether its\norder is \\((n,n+1)\\) or the reversed \\((n+1,n)\\). Consequently\n\\[\n D_k\\ne0\\qquad(0\\le k\\le K).                       \\tag{G6}\n\\]\nThis is the precise obstruction to a proper low-position subsum vanishing.\nIt uses genuine ordered trajectories; a purely formal signed identity with\nunrelated suffixes would not justify the coalescence step.\n\n## 3. Recurrence and uniform size bound\n\nLet \\(e_L(k),e_R(k)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) indicate whether the next bit at position\n\\(k\\) is odd. The prefix intercept recurrence gives\n\\[\n D_{k+1}=\n \\frac{D_k+e_R(k)3^{P-p_R(k)-1}\n            -e_L(k)3^{P-p_L(k)-1}}{2}.          \\tag{G7}\n\\]\nWhen a next bit is even, its increment is defined to be zero; this avoids\nwriting a term with a negative exponent when the current odd count is \\(P\\).\nEach nonzero increment term is at most \\(3^{P-1}\\), so if\n\\(|D_k|\\le3^P\\), then\n\\[\n |D_{k+1}|\\le\\frac{3^P+3^{P-1}}2\\le3^P.             \\tag{G8}\n\\]\nTogether with \\(D_0=3^P\\), induction gives\n\\[\n 0\u003c|D_k|\\le3^P\\quad\\text{for all }k.               \\tag{G9}\n\\]\nThe strict lower bound is (G6), not an assumed positivity of \\(D_k\\); the\nsequence may change sign.\n\nIf both next bits are even, (G7) is \\(D_{k+1}=D_k/2\\). A consecutive run of\nsuch common E-bits of length \\(q\\) therefore requires \\(2^q\\mid D_a\\) at\nits start. By (G9),\n\\[\n q\\le L_P:=\\left\\lfloor\\log_2(3^P)\\right\\rfloor\n =\\left\\lfloor P\\log_2 3\\right\\rfloor.             \\tag{G10}\n\\]\nThus no unbounded zero-run is possible at fixed \\(P\\).\n\n## 4. Counting runs and the resulting K bound\n\nAt the first position the two sources \\(x,x+1\\) have opposite parity, so the\nfirst pair of word bits is not EE (and cannot be OO). Let \\(q\\) be the number\nof positions at which at least one of the two words has an O. Since each word\nhas exactly \\(P\\) O-bits,\n\\[\n 1\\le q\\le2P.                                      \\tag{G11}\n\\]\nThe first such position is position zero. After each of the \\(q\\) event\npositions there is one common-E run, including the final run after the last\nevent. By (G10), each has length at most \\(L_P\\). Therefore\n\\[\n K\\le q(1+L_P)\n \\le2P\\left(1+\\left\\lfloor P\\log_2 3\\right\\rfloor\\right). \\tag{G12}\n\\]\nThis is an effective finite length bound for every genuine ordered structural\npair with fixed odd count \\(P\\). It is deliberately not claimed sharp.\n\nFor \\(P=1,2,3\\), the bound is only a coarse check and is much larger than\nthe exact classifications already audited. No \\(P=4\\) table is needed for\n(G12).\n\n## 5. Scope and signed corollary\n\nThe argument rules out an unbounded \\(K\\) caused solely by inserting common\nzero/EE runs: such a run would force \\(D_k\\) to be a nonzero integer with\nmore powers of 2 than its uniform size allows. It also shows why a proposed\nproper low-position signed subsum cannot simply be set to zero: unequal\nprefix odd counts are separated 3-adically, while equal counts would be an\nactual trajectory coalescence and hence would destroy the final ordered gap.\n\nThe result is restricted to genuine same-time ordered pairs with equal \\(K\\),\nequal \\(P\\), and terminal sign \\(\\varepsilon=\\pm1\\). It does not classify the\nfinitely many possibilities for a given larger \\(P\\), and does not cover\nasynchronous paths, unequal odd counts, or the full paired predecessor set.\nNo density or full-Collatz conclusion follows.\n\nFor the usual terminal order, \\(\\varepsilon=+1\\) and \\(D_K=1\\). For the\nreversed terminal order, \\(\\varepsilon=-1\\) and \\(D_K=-1\\); the proof of\n(G6)--(G12) is unchanged. Thus the same signed length bound applies to both\nterminal orientations.\n\n\n\n\n## 6. Final on-disk audit of GB1--GB3 and IR4\n\n18 сентября 2026. Сохранённый GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md\nпроверен вручную по GB1--GB3 и IR4. Signed relation\n\\(3^P+C_R-C_L=\\varepsilon2^K\\), common-source admissibility, convention\n\\(v_3(0)=+\\infty\\), nonvanishing carry, recurrence and bound\n\\(K\\le2P(1+\\lfloor P\\log_2 3\\rfloor)\\) all pass, including\n\\(\\varepsilon=-1\\).\n\nArithmetic IR4 also passes: the two length-11 words have\n\\(C_q=6358\\), \\(C_{q'}=2123\\), difference \\(4235=2048+2187\\), and the\nreversed guard is \\(n=205\\pmod{2187}\\) with sources\n\\(2048m+190,2048m+191\\). The composed constants are\n\\(18,252,850\\) and \\(17,664,185\\), whose difference is\n\\(-588,665=2^{22}-3^{14}\\). The guard\n\\(n=2,959,216\\pmod{4,782,969}\\) gives the checked chain\n\\(2,959,216\\to2,771,134\\to2,595,006\\); the composite slope is below one.\nNo arithmetic correction is required. The scope remains restricted to\nsynchronous structural words and does not become a theorem about all paired\npredecessors or positive integer exceptional sets.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_H_G3_SWITCH_BLOCK.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — H→G3 и смешанные ростовые блоки\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной аналитический проход после SC.\nПроверены PROJECT_STATE.md и COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md.\nМатематические программы, CAS, переборы и Lean не использовались.\n\n## 1. Нечётный H-конец\n\nПусть исходный H-вход имеет вид\n\\[\n n_0=2^a u-1,\\qquad a=2r+1,\\qquad u\\ {\\rm odd},\n\\]\nи является настоящим birth. Из SC3 для birth следует\n\\(3^a u\\equiv1\\pmod4\\). Так как \\(a\\) нечётно, это равносильно\n\\(u\\equiv3\\pmod4\\).\n\nПосле \\(r\\) H-шагов получается\n\\[\n g_0=n_r=2\\cdot9^r u-1=2v-1,\\qquad v=9^r u.\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(v\\equiv u\\pmod8\\), поскольку \\(9^r\\equiv1\\pmod8\\), и потому\n\\(g_0\\equiv5\\pmod8\\). Все предыдущие H-переходы являются точными\n\\(T^2\\)-путями, так что\n\\[\n \\beta(g_0)=\\beta(n_0),\\qquad \\beta(g_0+1)=\\beta(v).\n\\]\nЕсли весь H-префикс состоит из births, то эти два цвета совпадают, а\n\\(\\beta(2g_0+1)\\) противоположен им.\n\nДля retained G3 требуется не только \\(g_0\\equiv5\\pmod8\\), но именно\n\\(g_0\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\). Это эквивалентно\n\\[\n v\\equiv7\\pmod8,\n\\]\nто есть \\(u\\equiv7\\pmod8\\). Если \\(u\\equiv3\\pmod8\\), то \\(g_0\\equiv5\n\\pmod{16}\\); SC/BR в этом классе дают меньший birth относительно \\(g_0\\),\nа G3-retention отсутствует. Эта меньшая точка не обязана быть ниже\nисходного \\(n_0\\).\n\n## 2. Точный H→G3 переход\n\nПри \\(v\\equiv7\\pmod8\\) арифметический G3-выход равен\n\\[\n g_1=G_3(g_0)=\\frac{9g_0+3}{8}\n     =\\frac{9v-3}{4}.                                  \\tag{1}\n\\]\nИз output-birth guard SC4 следует\n\\[\n e(g_1)=1,\\qquad d(g_1)=d(g_1+1)=0.\n\\]\nВместе с middle identity SC9 это даёт точные цвета\n\\[\n \\beta(g_1)=\\beta(g_0),\\qquad\n \\beta(g_1+1)=\\beta(g_0),\\qquad\n \\beta(2g_1+1)=\\beta(2g_0+1).\n                                                               \\tag{2}\n\\]\nПоследнее равенство — это общий middle color: \\(2g_1+1=T^3(2g_0+1)\\).\nПервое требует actual output-birth guard; из SC9 одного оно не следует.\n\nСледующий H-переход возможен ровно по арифметическому условию\n\\(g_1\\equiv3\\pmod4\\). Из (1) оно равносильно\n\\[\n v\\equiv7\\pmod{16}.\n\\]\nТогда\n\\[\n g_2=H(g_1)=\\frac{9g_1+5}{4}\n          =\\frac{81v-7}{16}.                            \\tag{3}\n\\]\nЕсли \\(g_2\\) также retained birth, то \\(d(g_2)=0\\), \\(e(g_2)=1\\), и\n\\[\n \\beta(g_2)=\\beta(g_1)=\\beta(g_0),\\qquad\n \\beta(2g_2+1)=\\beta(2g_1+1)=\\beta(2g_0+1).\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(g_0\\to g_1\\) — структурный G3-переход, а для middle-числа это\nслово \\(T^3\\); последующий \\(g_1\\to g_2\\) — H-переход со словом \\(T^2\\).\nПоэтому это точная цветовая propagation по двум различным genuine словам,\nа не по двум \\(T^2\\)-переходам.\n\nЕсли \\(v\\equiv15\\pmod{16}\\), то \\(g_1\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), и H непосредственно\nне продолжает G3. Возможный следующий retained G3 требует\n\\(g_1\\equiv13\\pmod{16}\\), что эквивалентно\n\\[\n v\\equiv63\\pmod{64}.                                    \\tag{4}\n\\]\nВо всех остальных residue cases SC оставляет меньший-birth альтернативу\nили иной локальный режим; сама арифметическая формула (1) не превращает\nеё в actual birth.\n\n## 3. Зеркальная G3-серия и точная длина её арифметического run\n\nДля анализа повторного G3 положим\n\\[\n g_t=2v_t-1,\\qquad\n v_{t+1}=\\frac{9v_t+1}{8}.\n\\]\nТогда \\(g_{t+1}=G_3(g_t)\\), когда \\(v_t\\equiv7\\pmod8\\). Введя\n\\(w_t=v_t+1\\), получаем точное линейное соотношение\n\\[\n w_{t+1}=\\frac{9}{8}w_t,\\qquad\n w_t=\\frac{9^t}{8^t}w_0.                                \\tag{5}\n\\]\nСледовательно, \\(L\\) последовательных арифметических G3-переходов\nвозможны тогда и только тогда, когда\n\\[\n v_2(w_0)\\ge3L.\n\\]\nЕсли \\(v_2(w_0)=3L+\\rho\\), \\(0\\le\\rho\u003c3\\), то после этих \\(L\\) переходов\n\\(v_2(w_L)=\\rho\\), и следующий G3-вход уже невозможен. При \\(\\rho=0\\)\nарифметический endpoint \\(g_L\\) является H-входом (его \\(v_L\\) чётно).\nПри \\(\\rho=1\\) имеем \\(g_L\\equiv1\\pmod8\\), так что этот endpoint вообще\nне может быть birth по унаследованному исключению \\(e(8m+1)=0\\). При\n\\(\\rho=2\\) имеем \\(g_L\\equiv5\\pmod{16}\\); если endpoint является birth,\nSC/BR дают меньший перенос. Это точное арифметическое утверждение о\nrun-length, а не утверждение, что каждый \\(g_t\\) является birth.\n\nFor the H-origin above, \\(v=9^r u\\), so the maximum possible consecutive\nG3 length is\n\\[\n \\left\\lfloor\\frac{v_2(9^r u+1)}{3}\\right\\rfloor\n =\\left\\lfloor\\frac{v_2(u\\,9^r+1)}{3}\\right\\rfloor.       \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe first G3 already requires the valuation at least \\(3\\). If the\nvaluation is exactly \\(3L\\), the natural next mode is H; if it is\n\\(3L+1\\) or \\(3L+2\\), no next G3 is arithmetically available, though\nthe actual birth routing still requires the corresponding SC alternative.\n\n## 4. Comparison with the original birth\n\nThe H endpoint and its first G3 output are larger than the original\n\\(n_0\\) for \\(r\\ge1\\):\n\\[\n g_0+1=2\\cdot9^r u,\\qquad n_0+1=2^{2r+1}u,\n\\]\nso \\(g_0>n_0\\), and\n\\[\n g_1=\\frac{9^{r+1}u-3}{4}>n_0.\n\\]\nThe second inequality follows already from\n\\(9^{r+1}/4>2^{2r+1}\\) for \\(r\\ge1\\).\n\nIf \\(u\\equiv3\\pmod8\\), the first SC alternative after the H ladder is a\nsmaller birth below \\(g_0\\), but no bound here places it below \\(n_0\\).\nIf \\(u\\equiv7\\pmod8\\) and G3 is retained, (1) is again larger than \\(n_0\\).\nA subsequent H or repeated G3 is still increasing. The first forced\nsmaller transfer occurs only when one of these retained masks fails; the\nSC/BR bound then compares it with the current endpoint, not uniformly with\n\\(n_0\\). Thus the H→G3 switch supplies a precise residue/valuation\nclassification but no parameter descent to the original seed.\n\nThe same obstruction appears in the reverse ordering G3→H: condition\n\\(v\\equiv7\\pmod{16}\\) permits the H formula (3), while its actual birth\nand mask are independent requirements. The exact middle colors in (2)\nare preserved, but they do not force a smaller index.\n\n## 5. Conclusion and scope\n\nThe new exact facts are:\n\n1. an odd H-length \\(a=2r+1\\) ends at \\(g_0=2\\cdot9^ru-1\\);\n2. G3 retention requires \\(u\\equiv7\\pmod8\\);\n3. G3→H requires \\(9^ru\\equiv7\\pmod{16}\\);\n4. consecutive G3 arithmetic length is\n   \\(\\lfloor v_2(9^ru+1)/3\\rfloor\\);\n5. all color propagation statements require the stated actual-birth guards.\n\nThese facts do not exclude an actual mixed H/G3 chain, do not prove a\nsmaller birth below the initial \\(n_0\\), and do not imply a Collatz result.\n\n\n## 6. Аудит BC-оценки для длинных H/G3-цепей\n\nПредложенная оценка проверяется на уровне арифметических growth-слов.\nЗа один H-блок знаменатель равен \\(4=2^2\\), за один G3-блок —\n\\(8=2^3\\). Для фиксированного слова \\(W\\) из \\(r\\) букв H/G3 с\n\\(h\\) буквами H и \\(g=r-h\\) буквами G3 его полная рациональная формула\nимеет двоичный знаменатель\n\\[\n 2^{B(W)},\\qquad B(W)=2h+3g.\n\\]\nУсловия, что каждый промежуточный вход имеет требуемый odd residue, а\nпоследний endpoint остаётся нечётным, задают не более одного класса\nпо модулю \\(2^{B(W)+1}\\). Это можно доказать последовательным подъёмом\nостатка: каждая следующая affine-формула фиксирует ровно нужный новый\nдвоичный разряд; разные слова также не требуют складывания классов, так\nкак при первом различии букв их входные residue-cylinders разделены.\n\nСледовательно, среди \\(1\\le n\\le X\\) один фиксированный word \\(W\\) даёт не\nболее\n\\[\n \\frac{X}{2^{B(W)+1}}+1\n\\]\nисточников. Суммируя по всем \\(2^r\\) словам длины \\(r\\), получаем\n\\[\n N_r(X)\\le\n \\frac X2\\sum_{h=0}^r\\binom rh4^{-h}8^{-(r-h)}+2^r\n =\\frac X2\\left(\\frac38\\right)^r+2^r.                 \\tag{BC1}\n\\]\nЭто верхняя оценка и для arithmetic chains, и для actual birth chains\nс теми же \\(r\\) retained growth blocks: цветовые маски только уменьшают\nмножество источников.\n\nВыбирая \\(r\\) так, что\n\\[\n 2^r\\le\\sqrt X\u003c2^{r+1},\n\\]\nимеем \\((3/8)^r\\le2^{-r}\\) и потому\n\\[\n N_r(X)\\le 2\\sqrt X.                                  \\tag{BC2}\n\\]\nВ частности, для \\(\\Gamma\\), состоящего из starts с бесконечной\nарифметической H/G3-цепью, \\(\\#(\\Gamma\\cap[1,X])\\le2\\sqrt X\\).\n\nЧастичная summation даёт\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\in\\Gamma}\\frac1n\n \\le \\lim_{X\\to\\infty}\\left(\n \\frac{N_\\Gamma(X)}X+\\int_1^X\\frac{N_\\Gamma(t)}{t^2}\\,dt\\right)\n \\le 4.                                               \\tag{BC3}\n\\]\nЗдесь нет finite exception: BC7 работает уже при \\(r=0\\), а\n\\(\\int_1^\\infty2t^{-3/2}\\,dt=4\\). Эта масса относится только к\n\\(\\Gamma\\) (и, следовательно, к actual births с бесконечной growth-цепью).\nДля фиксированного конечного префикса \\(S_r\\) оценка BC7 имеет линейный\n\\(X\\)-член, так что его гармонический ряд вообще может расходиться.\n\nЭта проверка не утверждает, что каждый birth имеет конечный или\nбесконечный H/G3-маршрут: это требует SC/BR масок и выбора ветви.\nОна также не даёт спуска для отдельного старта. Она показывает лишь\nсуммируемость бесконечной arithmetic core; конечные growth-блоки по\nотдельности этой границей не контролируются. Контроль их совокупной\nмассы и масок остаётся отдельным вопросом.\n\n\n## 7. Полный аудит MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE\n\nBC1 проверена: для H и G3 соответственно\n\\[\n n'=(9n+5)/4,\\qquad n'=(9n+3)/8,\n\\]\nпоэтому \\(b=2,3\\), \\(\\gamma=5,3\\), а среднее\n\\(\\mu=2n+1\\) в обоих случаях имеет числитель \\(9\\mu+5\\). Формулы\nнакопления для \\(n_r,\\mu_r,S_r\\) корректны. Представление\n\\(n_r=\\lambda_w(n_0+c_w)-c_w\\), \\(1\\le c_w\\le3\\), является выпуклой\nкомбинацией коэффициентов и не является рангом спуска.\n\nBC2 также корректен в указанной области: для конкретного активного\nвыхода \\(K\\), у которого ближайший birth-предок получается после \\(\\ell\\)\nделений, \\(a=\\lfloor K/2^\\ell\\rfloor\\), поэтому \\(a\u003cn_0\\iff\nK\u003c2^\\ell n_0\\). Необходимо сохранять различие между \\(K=n_r+\\varepsilon\\)\nи другими boundary-выходами; BC4 применим только к первому типу. Условие\n\\(\\ell>\\log_2\\lambda_w\\) — необходимое сравнение, не оценка фактической\nглубины ancestry.\n\nBC3–BC7 проверены как чистая арифметическая верхняя оценка. H и G3 имеют\nнепересекающиеся domains и положительный рост. Для слова \\(W\\) из \\(r\\)\ngrowth-букв двоичные shortcut-слова имеют длину \\(B=2h+3g\\), а suffix\n\\(11\\) фиксирует нечётный endpoint. Поэтому один word задаёт не более\nодного класса для \\(n_0\\) по модулю \\(2^{B+1}\\), с числом представителей\nне более \\(X/2^{B+1}+1\\). Суммирование по словам даёт точно\n\\[\n \\frac X2(1/4+1/8)^r+2^r\n =\\frac X2(3/8)^r+2^r.\n\\]\nНезависимость цветов и masks здесь не используется.\n\nBC8–BC9 дают \\(N_\\Gamma(X)\u003c2\\sqrt X\\) при выборе\n\\(2^r\\le\\sqrt X\u003c2^{r+1}\\), а Tonelli даёт ровно\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\in\\Gamma}\\frac1n\n =\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{N_\\Gamma(t)}{t^2}\\,dt\\le4.\n\\]\nГраница \\(4\\) относится только к \\(\\Gamma\\), то есть к бесконечным\nарифметическим H/G3-цепям и их actual-birth подмножеству. Она не относится\nк фиксированному \\(S_r\\) или к конечным, но длинным growth-префиксам:\nBC7 для фиксированного \\(r\\) содержит линейный член по \\(X\\), и такой\nгармонический ряд может расходиться.\n\nBC10 разделяет \\(I_\\beta\\), births с бесконечным retained growth-routing,\nи \\(R_\\beta\\), все остальные births, включая нулевую длину блока. При\nпринятом SC-routing \\(I_\\beta\\subseteq\\Gamma\\), поэтому\n\\[\n 0\\le E_\\beta(X)-E_R(X)\\le4.\n\\]\nЭто корректная энергетическая локализация; pointwise-спуск отдельного\nстарта из неё не следует. Финитные блоки не получили самостоятельной\nсуммируемости, а masks не были заменены независимыми цветами. Открытым\nостаётся именно контроль \\(E_R\\) с реальными multiplicities и masks.\n\n\n\n## 8. Аудит BC11–BC13\n\nBC11 проверена напрямую:\n\\[\n H(4t+3)=9t+8\\equiv8\\pmod9,\\qquad\n G_3(16t+13)=18t+15\\equiv6\\pmod9.\n\\]\nОбе ветви индивидуально инъективны, а их образы непересекаются. Поэтому\nпосле ограничения на actual births и реальные masks граф retained-growth\nимеет входную и выходную степень не более единицы. Все его рёбра строго\nувеличивают индекс, так что компонента является конечной цепью или\nодносторонней бесконечной цепью с единственным первым vertex; это\nграфовая классификация, не утверждение об ordinary T-орбите.\n\nBC12 также корректна: каждый рост умножает индекс как минимум на \\(9/8\\),\nпоэтому для finite chain с origin \\(a\\)\n\\[\n \\sum_j\\frac1{n_j}\\le\\frac1a\\sum_{j\\ge0}(8/9)^j=\\frac9a.\n\\]\nBC13 следует после разбиения births на finite-chain origins \\(A_\\beta\\),\nостальные vertices finite chains и infinite-chain vertices. Для каждого\n\\(X\\) truncation внутри одной finite component не ухудшает оценку, поэтому\n\\[\n E_A(X)\\le E_R(X)\\le9E_A(X),\\qquad\n E_A(X)\\le E_\\beta(X)\\le9E_A(X)+4.\n\\]\nПоследняя константа \\(4\\) приходит только от BC9 и потому относится к\ninfinite-growth component vertices.\n\nЭто корректно убирает повторное суммирование по потенциальным словам в\nэтом bookkeeping, но не даёт оценки \\(E_A\\): origins могут иметь\nрасходящуюся harmonic mass. Также terminal smaller-birth transfers не\nобразуют здесь доказанную убывающую связь между origins; такой переход\nможет попасть в ту же или другую component. Поэтому BC11–BC13 не дают\npointwise descent или конечности общей birth energy.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_DIRECT_INDIRECT_MASK.md":"# Joint direct/indirect masks at one ternary target\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analytical audit only; no\nprograms, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or GPU.\n\n## 1. The two mechanisms\n\nUse the exact binary invariant beta, with\n`g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n)`, `h(n)=|g(n)|`, and the PB notation for an odd\ntarget `t`:\n\n    r=(3t+1)/2=2^a w,   w odd,   C_i=beta(3^i w-1).\n\nThen beta(t)=beta(w)=:A and beta(3t)=C_(a+1). A direct PB-plus\nretention at t means h(t)=h(4t+1)=1 and the plus arithmetic selector.\nSince h(t)=1, C_(a+1)=1-A. The plus selector gives\nC_(a+2)=C_(a+3), while h(4t+1)=1 gives C_(a+3) != A. Therefore the\nexact joint direct mask is\n\n    C_(a+1)=C_(a+2)=C_(a+3)=1-A.                (JD1)\n\nThis uses only PB's genuine paths and the selector; it does not assign\nindependent colors.\n\nAn indirect last-change birth from the same target has\n\n    z=3^k t,  k>=2,\n    h(t)=1,  h(3t)=...=h(3^(k-1)t)=0.\n\nWriting A=beta(t), this says\n\n    beta(3^j t)=1-A  (1\u003c=j\u003c=k),\n    beta(2*3^k t+1)=A.                          (JD2)\n\nThe last equality is the birth mask. Its PB target is\np_k=4*3^(k-1)t+1, and the exact signed transfer gives\n\n    g(p_k)=2A-1=-g(t).                           (JD3)\n\nFor the direct target p_0=4t+1, (JD1) instead gives\ng(p_0)=1-2A=g(t). Thus simultaneous direct and indirect charges at t\ncarry opposite signed values at their distinct PB targets. This is a\nprecise signed relation, not a contradiction: no finite T-path identity\nin the checked inputs identifies p_0 with p_k or forces their colors to\nagree.\n\n## 2. Arithmetic selector overlap and its limitation\n\nAn indirect birth at z=3^k t requires the plus selector at\nu=3^(k-1)t, because e(3u)=1 always forces plus. This selector is not\nthe selector at t. For k=2, put r=2^a w as above. Since\n\n    (3*(3t)+1)/2=3r-1,\n\nif a>=2 then 3r-1 is odd and is 3 modulo4, so the selector at 3t is\nplus. Hence there is no arithmetic incompatibility between a plus or\nminus selector at t and the required plus selector at 3t in this range.\n\nThere is one exact small valuation obstruction. If a=1, then r=2w. A\nplus selector at t requires w=1 modulo4, and a minus selector requires\nw=3 modulo4. In either case 3r-1=6w-1=1 modulo4, so its valuation is\nzero and its selector is minus, not plus. Consequently a k=2 indirect\nbirth cannot coexist with either direct selector at a target whose r has\nv_2(r)=1. For a=0, and for k>2, the residue of the later selector is\nnot fixed by the selector at t; no general exclusion follows from these\nguards.\n\nFor a minus target branch, the same conclusion applies: the target's\nminus selector does not itself preclude an indirect birth, since the\nbirth tests the selector at 3^(k-1)t. The exact color restrictions remain\n(JD2); no additional equality follows from the minus route's finite\ntransfer. Actual colors could still rule out particular instances, but\nthat requires a genuine path merge or another invariant.\n\n## 3. Consequence for TL9 accounting\n\nThe actual PB fibers for p_0 and p_k are distinct. More precisely, in a\nfiber member `n_l(u)`, the valuation `v_2(n_l(u)-1)=2l+2` recovers `l`,\nand then\n`u=(n_l(u)-1)3^l/4^(l+1)` recovers the target; the unique last-change\nlabel recovers the associated `t,k`. The same target t can nevertheless\noccur in both the direct coefficient accounting and the last-change\nreindexing; this is target-charge overlap, not a collision of actual\nsource fibers. Equations (JD1)--(JD3) show that the overlap has an\nopposite signed orientation, but TL9 sums nonnegative weights, so this\nsign does not lower its coefficients without a proved signed telescoping\nidentity.\n\nThe available finite-path identities establish (JD1)--(JD3) and the\na=1,k=2 selector obstruction. They do not prove incompatibility for all\nk, nor do they prove that any jointly masked target is realized by an\nactual invariant beyond the assumed branch. No arbitrary free color\nassignment, finite-energy hypothesis, or global termination claim is\nused.\n\n## 4. A valuation-parity refinement of the weight bound\n\nThere is a valid arithmetic refinement on the residue classes where the\nselector at the indirect target is forced by the mod-8 class. If\n`u=3 mod 8`, then `(3u+1)/2=1 mod 4`, so its PB selector is minus. Hence\nan indirect birth `z=3^k t` requires `k` odd when `t=1 mod 8`, and\nrequires `k` even when `t=3 mod 8`; these are necessary conditions, not\nsufficient ones. Summing the TL6 fiber bound only over those allowed\nparities gives\n\n    C_odd(v)  = 1/8 - (1/20)(3/4)^v,   k=3,5,...,\n    C_even(v) = 3/8 - (1/5)(3/4)^v,    k=2,4,... .\n\nIndeed the first geometric sums are `1/8` and `3/8`, while\n`sum 3^(1-k)(3/4)^k` over odd `k>=3` and even `k>=2` is respectively\n`1/20` and `1/5`. Thus, where the corresponding direct branch is present,\nthe safe combined coefficients are\n\n    t=9 mod 16 (direct minus):  7/8-(1/20)(3/4)^v,\n    t=1 mod 16 (direct plus):   9/8-(4/5)(3/4)^v,\n    t=3 mod 8  (direct minus):  9/8-(1/5)(3/4)^v.\n\nFor `v_3(t)=0` the direct PB target term may be absent from the TL9\npartition, so only the relevant indirect coefficient should be retained;\nadding the direct term is merely a looser upper bound. Other residue\nclasses have no selector conclusion here. These refinements do not remove\nthe primitive term or establish a global contraction.\n\n## 5. Exact mod-16 refinement on four cycling classes\n\nOn the unit classes `1,3,9,11 mod16`, direct inspection gives\n\n    u mod16   1          3          9           11\n    r         2          5          14          17\n    selector  plus       minus      minus       minus.\n\nThus the PB plus selector holds exactly when `u=1 mod16` on this cycle.\nMultiplication by `3` cycles these four classes as\n`1 -> 3 -> 9 -> 11 -> 1`. Therefore the least admissible indirect\nexponent (`K>=2`) is\n\n    t mod16   1   3   9   11\n    K          5   4   3    2,\n\nand all admissible exponents are (k=K+4q), (q\\ge0). This is a\nnecessary arithmetic condition; the selector remains insufficient for an\nactual birth.\n\nWith (v=v_3(t)), summing the TL6 bound over this progression gives\n\n    C_K(v)=3^(5-K)/80 - [3*4^(4-K)/255](3/4)^v.\n\nThus\n\n    C_5=1/80 - 1/340 alpha^v,\n    C_4=3/80 - 1/85  alpha^v,\n    C_3=9/80 - 4/85  alpha^v,\n    C_2=27/80 - 16/85 alpha^v,\n\nwhere `alpha=3/4`. Adding the direct plus coefficient\n`1-alpha^(v+1)` on class 1, or the direct minus coefficient `3/4` on\nthe other three classes, yields respectively\n\n    t=1 mod16: 81/80 - 64/85 alpha^v,\n    t=3 mod16: 63/80 - 1/85  alpha^v,\n    t=9 mod16: 69/80 - 4/85  alpha^v,\n    t=11 mod16:87/80 - 16/85 alpha^v.\n\nFor `v_3(t)=0` the direct term is not part of the relevant TL9 source\npartition, so these combined expressions are then only looser optional\nbounds; the indirect terms `C_K` remain valid. At cutoff `X`, the\n*indirect* target support is restricted by\n\n    t \u003c= (X-1)/(4*3^(K-1)).\n\nThe direct term is still extended to its common target range `t\u003c=X`; this\ncutoff must not be imposed on the combined coefficient as a whole. The\nrefinement is therefore a genuine finite-cutoff arithmetic saving, not a\nglobal contraction or an existence statement for jointly masked births.\n\n## 6. Final audit of RF1--RF6\n\nThe complete `RESIDUE_FILTERED_LAST_CHANGE_WEIGHTS.md` was checked. The\nselector table on `1,3,9,11 mod16` is correct, and multiplication by three\ngives the least admissible exponents `K=5,4,3,2`. The filtered geometric\nsum is\n\n    C_K(v)=3^(5-K)/80-[3*4^(4-K)/255](3/4)^v,\n\nwhich yields the four displayed values in RF3. The combined RF4\ncoefficients follow by adding the direct coefficient\n`1-(3/4)^(v+1)` on the plus row and `3/4` on the minus rows; no sign or\nfactor error was found.\n\nThe source reconstruction is also valid: `v_2(n_l-1)=2l+2` recovers `l`,\nthen `u=(n_l-1)3^l/4^(l+1)` recovers the target, while the last-change\nlabel recovers `t,k`. RF3's cutoff applies only to the indirect fibers.\nThe direct PB contribution remains extended to `t\u003c=X`, as stated above.\nRF5 is a conservative finite-cutoff inequality; it does not establish a\nglobal contraction because the primitive term and other residue classes\nremain uncontrolled.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — joint spacing of D_S and D_R origins\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Ручной аудит без программ,\nCAS, переборов и Lean. Прочитаны SR7--SR11, SG4--SG9 и EC. Цель —\nпроверить, можно ли улучшить origin coefficient 4/9 + 1/2 = 17/18,\nобъединив реальные active ancestry paths D_S и D_R.\n\n## 1. Что именно уже доказано раздельно\n\nДля D_S charged odd boundary q имеет d(q)=1, forced child 2q is even,\nи следующий charged odd boundary на той же active path не может находиться\nна соседней глубине. SG8 усиливает origin estimate до full D_S-component\nmass 7/(9b) per charged birth b, но в origin-only форме это даёт\n\n    E_D_S(X) \u003c= (4/9) E_beta(X/6).\n\nДля D_R target имеет вид n=2^h k, h>=2, где k=3u odd и d(k)=1.\nДопустимые exponents h are nonadjacent, so the target weights over one k\nsum to at most 1/(3k). Charging k to its nearest birth b gives odd k mass\nat most 1/b on the right active path and 1/(2b) on the left. Hence\n\n    E_D_R(X) \u003c= (1/2) E_beta(X/8).\n\nThe two origin classes are disjoint as targets because D_S lies in image\nresidue 2 modulo 3 and D_R in image residue 0 modulo 3. This proves the\nexisting 17/18 sum, but does not yet say that their charged boundaries are\ndisjoint.\n\n## 2. The possible common charge is not excluded by the masks\n\nLet q be an odd active boundary with d(q)=1. The D_S mask at q has the\nform, for c=beta(q),\n\n    beta(q)=c, beta(q+1)=1-c,\n    d(2q)=1, d(2q+1)=0,\n\nwith S(q) a birth of outer color c. The first two equalities are simply the\nactive boundary, and the last two are the forced-child identity used by SG5.\n\nThe same q can be the D_R charge k=3u. In SR7, for D_R one has\nbeta(k)=c and beta(k+1)=beta(u)=1-c, while the target 2^h k is a birth for\nat least one h>=2. This is compatible with the D_S pattern: both classes\nuse the same local pair (beta(q), beta(q+1)) and require d(q)=1. The D_S\ncondition fixes d(2q)=1 and d(2q+1)=0; the D_R target condition at h>=2\nconcerns masks at 2^h q and 2^h q+1. No SR or SG identity equates those\nhigher masks with the D_S condition.\n\nThus the two classes can, at the level of the proved exact equations, charge\nthe same active boundary. Disjointness of the origin targets does not imply\ndisjointness of their nearest-birth charges. Any proposed improvement based\non simply deleting one of the two weights would require a new theorem that\nexcludes this common-charge configuration.\n\n## 3. Why one-sided spacing is insufficient\n\nThe D_S forced child does give one directional exclusion: after a D_S charge\nat depth l, the next active boundary is the even child 2q, so an odd D_R\ncharge cannot occur at depth l+1. This does not give a symmetric exclusion.\nA D_R charge supplies only d(k)=1; without an additional value of\nbeta(2k+1), it does not determine whether its active child is even or odd.\nConsequently a D_S charge may occur immediately after a D_R charge, and a\ncommon charge at the same depth is not ruled out either.\n\nEven if one imposed disjointness by an extra hypothesis, the resulting\noptimization would need the exact cross-depth pattern and the different\nweights 2/(3q+1) for D_S versus 1/(3k) after the D_R exponent sum. The\ninitial audit stopped at the safe sum 4/9+1/2=17/18 because this A/B\nrecurrence had not yet been used. That preliminary conclusion is superseded\nby the explicit A/B supersolution in Section 6.\n\n## 4. The primitive P_1 class has no F-predecessor\n\nP_1 consists of births n congruent to 1 modulo 3. The arithmetic images of\nS and R are respectively 2 and 0 modulo 3. Hence every P_1 birth is an\norigin of the retained F graph, independently of the colors. It cannot be\nreduced to D_S or D_R by adding an incoming F edge.\n\nSG4 supplies a smaller-birth argument inside the separate I_S residue-2\nclass, but it does not apply to P_1. A reduction of P_1 would require a\nnon-F predecessor relation or a new color identity connecting its birth to\nan earlier birth. No such identity is present in SR7--SR11 or SG4--SG9.\nTherefore the primitive P_1 contribution remains an actual open source in\nSR11, rather than a bookkeeping artifact of the separate charging bounds.\n\n## 5. Revised bounded verdict before the A/B refinement\n\nПервоначальный joint audit правильно установил, что target-disjointness не\nозначает disjointness charged boundaries и что один только D_S forced child\nне даёт симметричного запрета для D_R. Это было барьером для наивного\nсуммирования, но не запретом на более точный one-sided recurrence.\n\nP_1 остаётся F-origin классом по residue 1 modulo 3 и не сокращается через\nSG4. Улучшение для D_S union D_R возможно только после учёта общей A/B\nancestry, а не удалением одного из двух origin weights.\n\n## 6. Audit of the universal A/B continuation and improved coefficient\n\nThe missing local identity is universal:\n\n    d(8t+4)=0.\n\nIndeed beta(8t+4)=beta(2t+1), while\nbeta(8t+5)=beta(12t+8)=beta(3t+2)=beta(2t+1). If q is odd and\n d(q)=1, exactly one child of q is active. If d(2q+1)=1, the next odd\nactive boundary is\n\n    A(q)=2q+1.\n\nIf d(2q)=1, then d(4q)=0 by the identity above, so the next active odd\nboundary is\n\n    B(q)=4q+1.\n\nThus every odd active charge has an odd continuation A at depth one or B at\ndepth two. Their residues modulo 3 are A(q) = 2i+1 and B(q) = i+1 when\nq is i modulo 3.\n\nAt a D_S charge q the forced pattern is d(2q)=1,d(2q+1)=0, so B is forced.\nThe D_S origin weight is at most 2/(3q). If i=0, a D_R charge may coexist;\nits nonadjacent exponent sum contributes at most 1/(3q). Therefore the local\nD_S-plus-D_R cost is bounded by 2/(3q)+delta_(i=0)/(3q), followed by\nV_(i+1)/(4q). Without D_S, a D_R cost is possible only for i=0, followed\nby either A or B.\n\nThe following normalized values dominate both cases:\n\n    V_0=232/189,  V_1=172/189,  V_2=184/189.\n\nThe required inequalities are\n\n    V_i >= 2/3 + delta_(i=0)/3 + V_(i+1)/4,\n    V_i >= delta_(i=0)/3\n          + max(V_(2i+1)/2, V_(i+1)/4),\n\nwith indices modulo 3. Direct arithmetic verifies them:\n\n    V_0 = 1 + V_1/4,\n    V_1 = 2/3 + V_2/4,\n    V_2 = 2/3 + V_0/4,\n\nand the second row has right sides 149/189, 116/189, 92/189 for i=0,1,2,\nrespectively, all below V_0,V_1,V_2.\n\nFor the left path from a birth b, an odd boundary at depth two is exactly\nq=4b+1, so its total joint origin cost is at most V_(b+1 mod 3)/(4b).\nThe path may instead continue through even zero bits. If its first odd boundary\nhas depth ell>=3, then q>=8b and its cost is at most max(V_i)/(8b). Since\nmax(V_i)/8=29/189 is at most min(V_i)/4=43/189, this is still bounded by\nV_(b+1 mod 3)/(4b). If no odd boundary occurs, the charge is zero. On the\nright path the first odd boundary is 2b+1. It cannot be D_S at depth one.\nIf b is 1 modulo 3 it may carry the D_R cost; otherwise only the A/B future\nis available. The three resulting bounds are\n\n    b=0 mod 3: 101/(189 b),\n    b=1 mod 3: 241/(378 b),\n    b=2 mod 3: 104/(189 b).\n\nHence the uniform joint origin charge is\n\n    \u003c= 241/(378 b) \u003c 17/(18 b).\n\nFor origins counted up to X, D_S charges have nearest birth b\u003cX/6 by SG4,\nand D_R charges have b\u003cX/8 by SR9. Thus b\u003cX/6 safely covers both types; the\nrecurrence may overcount targets beyond X, which is harmless for an upper\nbound. The estimate concerns origin weights, not full retained-component\nmass, and does not remove the primitive P_1 source.\n\nThis corrects the initial barrier: a strict improvement is obtained from the\nuniversal A/B continuation, while the earlier observation about possible\ncommon D_S/D_R charges remains valid and is handled additively by the\nsupersolution. No global density, termination, or full Collatz conclusion\nfollows.\n\n## 7. Final audit of WS5--WS6\n\nThe complete WS file was read. WS5's joint-charge model is correct: D_S and\nD_R targets remain disjoint, while a common odd charge q is allowed and its\nweights are added. The universal identity d(8t+4)=0 gives the exact A/B\ncontinuation, so the two supersolution inequalities in WS7 cover every finite\nodd-boundary prefix. The listed vector U=(232,172,184)/189 satisfies them;\nthe second-row right sides are 149/189, 116/189, and 92/189.\n\nWS6 correctly handles the delayed first odd boundary on the left. At depth\ntwo it is 4b+1. If the first odd boundary is at depth at least three, the\nbound max(U)/8 \u003c= min(U)/4 supplies the same U_(b+1)/(4b) estimate; a path\nwith no odd boundary contributes zero. Thus no immediate-depth assumption for\neven b is hidden.\n\nThe right initial boundary is 2b+1, and SG4's nearest-birth depth\ncondition excludes a D_S charge there. A D_R charge can occur only when b is\n1 modulo 3; this residue condition is necessary, not an existence claim.\nApplying the second supersolution inequality gives the table\n101/189, 241/378, 104/189 for b modulo 3. The middle value is the maximum,\nso the uniform coefficient 241/378 is correct.\n\nThe cutoffs are also valid: a D_S origin charged through q has b\u003cX/6 by\nSG4, while a D_R origin has b\u003cX/8 by SR9. Replacing both by X/6 is a safe\nupper-bound enlargement. The resulting WS8 and WS9 statements count actual\norigin targets and preserve the primitive P_1 source; they do not bound full\ncomponent mass or supply EC sparsity by themselves.\n\nTherefore the earlier preliminary statement that the safe coefficient\n17/18 was the current endpoint was superseded by the A/B refinement. No\nerror was found in WS5--WS6 or in the stated scope of WS7.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_LONG_BLOCK_DYNAMICS.md":"# Long-block dynamics: exact descent range and the growth obstruction\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded manual analysis of the next-block\ncandidate; no programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents.\n\n## 1. Guard and exact block identity\n\nLet n=2^L m-1, L>=3, m odd. After L-3 forced odd steps,\n\n    x=S^(L-3)(n)=8*3^(L-3)m-1.\n\nThe condition 3^(L-3)m congruent to 1 modulo 16 is exactly x congruent to 7\nmodulo 128: writing A=3^(L-3)m, one has x=8A-1, and 8A-1 congruent to 7\nmodulo 128 iff A is congruent to 1 modulo 16. The selected swap block is\ntherefore integral,\n\n    P(n)=(9x+1)/64=(3^(L-1)m-1)/8.                 (LB1)\n\nThe signed preservation rule on the preceding forced prefix and the selected\nswap give the exact relation\n\n    g(n)=g(x)=-g(P(n)),\n    h(n)=h(P(n)).                                   (LB2)\n\nThe guard also proves integrality of P; positivity is clear from\n3^(L-1)m>=9. No unguarded affine branch is being used.\n\n## 2. Exact criterion for a smaller target\n\nSubtracting the two positive integers gives\n\n    P(n)\u003cn\n    iff (2^(L+3)-3^(L-1))m>7.                       (LB3)\n\nFor L=3,...,7, the coefficient is respectively\n\n    55, 101, 175, 269, 295,\n\nso (LB3) holds for every positive admissible m. For L=8, the coefficient is\n2048-2187=-139, and it remains negative for all larger L because\n\n    2^(L+4)-3^L=2(2^(L+3)-3^(L-1))-3^(L-1).\n\nConsequently\n\n    P(n)\u003cn exactly for 3\u003c=L\u003c=7,\n    P(n)>n for every guarded L>=8.                 (LB4)\n\nEquality cannot occur: in the first range the coefficient is at least 55,\nand in the second it is negative. The boundary case L=3,m=1 is n=7,P=1,\na genuine strict descent; the relation does not assert that every target is\na new block source.\n\n## 3. Potential and cyclic limitations\n\nFor a fixed L the real multiplier is\n\n    P(n)/n = (3^(L-1)m-1)/(8(2^Lm-1)),\n\nwhich tends to 3^(L-1)/2^(L+3) as m grows. It is below one for L\u003c=7 and\nabove one for L>=8. Thus when P\u003cn the reciprocal weight 1/P is larger than\n1/n, while when P>n it is smaller; no global strict size descent or harmonic\npotential monotonicity is\navailable from this rule alone. The sign flip in (LB2) disappears after two\nblocks, so it supplies no contradiction to a hypothetical cycle. A cycle\nwould require the next target to satisfy another block guard; (LB4) neither\nexcludes nor constructs such a return.\n\nThe useful bounded statement is therefore conditional on staying in the\nsmall-depth guarded family: each such block strictly lowers the integer and\npreserves h. The large-depth guarded family is a genuine growth branch, and\nany harmonic or potential argument must account for its inverse multiplicity\nand possible later return. No universal termination or summability follows.\n\nAn odd cycle of selected blocks would force (g) to equal its negative and\ntherefore force (g=0) on that cycle. An even cycle has no such sign\ncontradiction; the sign swap alone does not exclude it.\n\n## 4. All-growing chains are not settled by the guard\n\nIf a target P is itself the source of another selected block with depth\n(L'\\ge8), then (v_2(P+1)=L'\\ge8). Since\n\n    P+1=(3^(L-1)m+7)/8,\n\nthe original guard makes this an integer, but the next depth imposes the\nadditional congruence (v_2(3^(L-1)m+7)\\ge11). Such a condition is a deeper\n2-adic congruence on m; it is not implied by the first guard. Every selected\ntarget does satisfy (P\\equiv1\\pmod{18}), because the D-source is\n(x=128s+7) and (P=18s+1), but this residue does not prevent\n(v_2(P+1)\\ge8).\n\nThus the present arithmetic gives finite-depth congruence restrictions and\nstrict growth at every L>=8, but it does not prove that a positive integer\ncannot satisfy all successive guards. Conversely, no infinite all-growing\nchain has been constructed. The correct status is an open nested-congruence\nquestion, not a termination claim.\n\n## 5. A necessary condition for a positive selected-block cycle\n\nWrite\n\n    alpha_L=3^(L-1)/2^(L+3).\n\nThe affine formula has the exact form\n\n    P(n)=alpha_L n+(alpha_L-1/8),\n\nand alpha_L>1/8 for every L>=3. Hence every positive selected block obeys\n(P(n)>alpha_L n). If a positive cycle has depths (L_1,\\ldots,L_k), then\n\n    product_(i=1..k) alpha_(L_i)\u003c1,\n\nor equivalently\n\n    3^(sum L_i-k)\u003c2^(sum L_i+3k),\n    (1/k)sum L_i \u003c log(24)/log(3/2).              (LB5)\n\nThus an all-growing cycle with every (L_i\\ge8) is impossible, since its\nmean depth is at least 8 while the right side is less than 8. Mixed cycles\nwith some small depths are not excluded by (LB5). If a cycle carries\n(h=1), its signed relation (LB2) additionally requires even cycle length;\nodd length would force (g=-g) and hence (g=0). These are necessary\nconditions only and do not settle mixed even cycles.\n\n## 6. Scope\n\nThe report verifies the guard, integrality, sign relation, exact comparison,\nand the small/large L split. It does not claim that P(n) remains in the same\nfamily, that a repeated chain exists, or that the remaining core is finite.\n\n## 7. Final audit of LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER\n\nThe full root report was reread. LB1--LB3 match the exact guarded identity and\nthe cycle inequality already checked here. In particular, the finite inverse\nfamily in root LB4 has (L=3,\\ldots,v_3(8u+1)+1), with no lost multiplicity,\nand the bound\n\n    u \u003c= (X+1)^(log_2 3)/24 - 1/8\n\nfollows by maximizing (3^{L-1}/2^L) under (2^L\u003c=X+1).\n\nThe geometric weight in root LB6 is also exact: the reciprocal of\n(n_L+1) is (3^{L-1}/(2^L(8u+1))), so summing (L=3\\ldots v+1)\ngives the stated factor (9/[4(8u+1)]). The first three descending depths\nhave coefficients (9/64+27/128+81/256=171/256); solving the resulting\ninequality gives (256/85) and (256/595) exactly. The denominator error\nbound (1/7) is valid because every source is at least 7.\n\nFinally, the LB6 function (f(n)=1) iff oddpart(n) is 3 modulo 24 correctly\nvanishes on the listed source and target classes and has divergent odd\nharmonic mass, while it is not an actual beta defect (already beta(3)=beta(9)\nfor every exact invariant). Its infinite U-variation is consistent with the\nconditional finite-variation lemma. The root report therefore has the stated\nscope: a sharper core reduction and a relaxed-model barrier, not a proof that\nthe actual core energy is finite.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_MASKED_H_BLOCK.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — точные цветовые ограничения для H-блоков\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной аналитический аудит; программ,\nCAS, переборов и Lean не использовалось. Проверены PROJECT_STATE.md и\nCOMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md (BR1–BR7). Ниже отделены точные тождества\nот открытого вопроса о спуске относительно исходного индекса.\n\n## 1. Общая координатная лемма\n\nПусть \\(u\\) — положительное нечётное число, \\(h\\geq0\\), \\(0\\leq s\\leq h\\), и\n\n\\[\n X_{h,s}=3^s2^{h-s}u-1>0.\n\\]\n\nОбозначим\n\n\\[\n C_h=\\beta(3^hu-1),\n \\qquad B_s=\\beta(3^su).\n\\]\n\nКаждое утверждение ниже следует из конечной цепочки точных \\(T\\)-шагов.\nИз \\(X_{h,s}+1=3^s2^{h-s}u\\), после \\(h-s\\) чётных шагов, получаем\n\n\\[\n \\beta(X_{h,s}+1)=B_s.\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(s\u003ch\\), то \\(X_{h,s}\\) нечётно и один нечётный шаг переводит его в\n\n\\[\n \\frac{3X_{h,s}+1}{2}=X_{h,s+1}.\n\\]\n\nПосле \\(h-s\\) таких шагов получаем \\(3^hu-1\\), поэтому\n\n\\[\n \\beta(X_{h,s})=C_h.\n\\]\n\nЭто же равенство тривиально при \\(s=h\\). Наконец,\n\n\\[\n 3X_{h,s}+2=X_{h+1,s+1},\n \\qquad\n \\beta(3X_{h,s}+2)=C_{h+1}.\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, для \\(d(x)=|\\beta(x+1)-\\beta(x)|\\) и birth-индикатора\n\\(e(x)\\) имеем точные формулы\n\n\\[\n d(X_{h,s})=|C_h-B_s|,\n \\qquad\n e(X_{h,s})=1\\iff C_h=B_s\\ne C_{h+1}.                 \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nВ частности, если \\(C_h\\ne C_{h+1}\\), то бинарность цветов даёт\n\n\\[\n e(X_{h,s})+d(X_{h,s})=1.                              \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nБез условия \\(C_h\\ne C_{h+1}\\) равенство (2) неверно: при \\(C_h=C_{h+1}\\)\nbirth отсутствует независимо от \\(B_s\\). Точка \\(h=s=0,u=1\\) даёт \\(X=0\\)\nи исключается из положительной области; других совпадений орбит здесь не\nпредполагается — использованы только явно выписанные конечные \\(T\\)-пути.\n\n## 2. Специализация к H-блоку\n\nПусть \\(a\\geq2\\), \\(u\\) нечётно и\n\n\\[\n n_j=9^j2^{a-2j}u-1=X_{a,2j},\n \\qquad 0\\leq j\\leq\\lfloor a/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nТогда \\(n_0\\equiv3\\pmod4\\). Для \\(j\u003c\\lfloor a/2\\rfloor\\) показатель\n\\(a-2j\\geq2\\), и два нечётных шага дают\n\n\\[\n T^2(n_j)=\\frac{9n_j+5}{4}=n_{j+1}.                 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nКонечная точка \\(j=\\lfloor a/2\\rfloor\\) может уже не быть H-входом; это\nне меняет формулу (1). Положим \\(C=C_a\\), \\(C^+=C_{a+1}\\). Тогда\n\n\\[\n \\beta(n_j)=C,\n \\qquad \\beta(n_j+1)=B_{2j}=\\beta(9^ju),\n \\qquad \\beta(3n_j+2)=C^+.                         \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nОтсюда\n\n\\[\n e(n_j)=1\\iff B_{2j}=C\\ne C^+,\n \\qquad d(n_j)=|C-B_{2j}|.                           \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nЭто даёт требуемые общие цветовые ограничения: если \\(n_j\\) — настоящий\nbirth, то все три значения в (4) имеют вид\n\n\\[\n \\beta(n_j)=\\beta(9^ju)=C,\n \\qquad \\beta(3n_j+2)=1-C.                           \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(C\\ne C^+\\) и H-маска на следующем узле равна \\(d(n_{j+1})=0\\), то\n\n\\[\n B_{2j+2}=C,\n \\quad e(n_{j+1})=1,\n \\quad \\beta(9^{j+1}u)=C.                             \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nТаким образом, последовательность нулевых масок действительно передаёт\nbirth и постоянный цвет вдоль всех соответствующих \\(9^\\ell u\\). Это не\nтребует отдельного совпадения орбит: \\(n_j\\to n_{j+1}\\) и\n\\(n_j+1\\to9^ju\\) — конкретные конечные пути.\n\n## 3. Что означает отказ H-маски\n\nПусть \\(n_j\\) — birth и \\(d(n_{j+1})=1\\). Тогда по (5) и бинарности\n\n\\[\n B_{2j+2}=C^+,\n \\qquad e(n_{j+1})=0.                               \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nЭто не разрешает считать \\(n_{j+1}\\) birth. Применение BR5 к исходному\nbirth \\(n_j\\), с \\(k=n_{j+1}=(9n_j+5)/4\\) и \\(p=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\), даёт\nдве содержательные альтернативы, но их статус зависит от endpoint:\n\n* если \\(d(p)=0\\), то \\(e(p)=1\\);\n* если \\(d(p)=1\\), то существует birth \\(q\\leq\\lfloor k/4\\rfloor\\)\n  (для малого края используется та же локальная BR-ветвь).\n\nКогда показатель \\(a-2j-2\\geq1\\), \\(k\\) нечётно. Для настоящего birth SC3\nдаёт \\(C_{a-1}=C_a\\), поэтому в этом нечётном endpoint\n\n\\[\n d(k)=d(p).\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, комбинация \\(d(k)=1,d(p)=0\\), которая могла бы выглядеть\nкак отдельная G1-ветвь, невозможна. Если \\(d(k)=1\\), обе границы активны и\nBR5 даёт меньший birth относительно текущего \\(n_j\\). Координатная форма\nпограничного \\(p\\) при этом такова:\n\n\\[\n p=\\frac{k-1}{2}=X_{a-1,\\,2j+2}.\n\\]\n\nПо (1) условие \\(e(p)=1\\) тогда эквивалентно\n\n\\[\n C_{a-1}=B_{2j+2}\\ne C_a.\n\\]\n\nС учётом (8) формальная проверка \\(e(p)=1\\) потребовала бы\n\\(C_{a-1}=C^+\\ne C\\), но этот G1-сценарий уже исключён равенством\n\\(d(k)=d(p)\\). Для конечного чётного endpoint формула\n\\(p=X_{a-1,2j+2}\\) не применяется. При \\(d(p)=0\\) имеется чётный\nbirth \\(p\\), к которому применим BR2; при \\(d(p)=1\\) используется\nболее глубокий birth-предок. К самому \\(k\\) со сбойной маской BR2\nне применяется, поскольку \\(e(k)=0\\).\n\n## 4. Сравнение с исходным \\(n_0\\)\n\nТочный рост H-координат равен\n\n\\[\n n_j+1=\\left(\\frac94\\right)^j(n_0+1),\n \\qquad\n k=n_{j+1}=\\left(\\frac94\\right)^{j+1}(n_0+1)-1.       \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nПри нечётном endpoint G1 уже исключён, поэтому \\(p=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\)\nне является самостоятельной retained-growth ветвью. При конечном чётном\nendpoint BR2 композирует G5 с меньшим birth относительно текущего входа.\nЭтот меньший birth не обязан быть ниже первого \\(n_0\\).\n\nДля оставшейся активной ветви BR5, \\(d(p)=1\\), имеется оценка\n\\(q\\leq\\lfloor k/4\\rfloor\\), действительно ниже текущего \\(n_j\\) при\n\\(n_j>5\\), поскольку\n\n\\[\n \\frac{k}{4}=\\frac{9n_j+5}{16}\u003cn_j.\n\\]\n\nНо сравнение с \\(n_0\\) меняется по \\(j\\). Для первой H-маски\n\n\\[\n \\frac{k}{4}=\\frac{9n_0+5}{16}\u003cn_0\\quad(n_0>1),              \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nтак что отказ первой маски при \\(j=0\\) и условие \\(d(p)=1\\) дают\nнастоящий birth ниже исходного \\(n_0\\). Для любого \\(j\\geq1\\), напротив,\n\n\\[\n \\frac{k}{4}-n_0\n \\geq \\frac{17n_0+65}{64}>1,\n\\]\n\nпоэтому из этой оценки не выведено, что\n\\(q\\leq\\lfloor k/4\\rfloor\\) ниже \\(n_0\\); она лишь ниже текущего \\(n_j\\).\n\nИтак, SC3 устраняет гипотетический G1-рост на нечётных endpoints, а\nчётный endpoint закрывается G5+BR2 ниже текущего входа. Только для первой\nотказавшей маски и активной \\(d(p)\\)-ветви приведённая оценка гарантирует\nспуск ниже \\(n_0\\). Для поздней маски общий спуск относительно \\(n_0\\) и\nконтроль переключений режимов остаются открытыми.\n\n\n\n## 5. Независимый аудит COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES\n\nПроверка SC1–SC7 проходит. SC1 совпадает с координатной леммой выше.\nУсловие слияния SC2 действительно имеет два пути к \\((3q-1)/4\\) при\n\\(q\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), поэтому \\(C_h=C_{h+1}\\). Отсюда SC3 и SC4 корректны:\nдля birth \\(n=2^au-1\\) необходимо \\(3^au\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), а при \\(a\\ge2\\)\nимеем \\(C_{a-1}=C_a\\).\n\nЭто действительно исключает BR-ветвь G1. При \\(a\\ge3\\),\n\\(k=H(n)=X_{a,2}\\) и \\(p=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor=X_{a-1,2}\\); SC4 даёт\n\\(\\beta(k)=\\beta(p)\\), а \\(k+1\\) и \\(p+1\\) имеют один и тот же удалённый\nчётный хвост \\(9u\\), так что \\(d(k)=d(p)\\). Поэтому комбинация\n\\(d(k)=1,d(p)=0\\), которая порождала G1, невозможна. Если обе границы\nактивны, BR7 даёт предка не выше \\(\\lfloor(9n+5)/16\\rfloor\u003cn\\).\n\nКрай \\(a=2\\) также разобран верно. SC3 даёт \\(u\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), то есть\n\\(n\\equiv3\\pmod{16}\\), \\(k=9u-1\\) кратно4 и \\(p=k/2\\) чётно. В случае\n\\(d(k)=1,d(p)=0\\) BR2 даёт\n\\[\n a'\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{3p+2}{4}\\right\\rfloor\n =\\left\\lfloor\\frac{27n+31}{32}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn\n\\]\nпри \\(n>31/5\\); единственный положительный край \\(n=3\\) имеет \\(e(3)=0\\).\nТак что при настоящем birth в классе \\(3\\bmod4\\) сохраняются только H\nили меньший birth. В прежнем отчёте раздел 3 следует читать с этой\nпоправкой: отказ H-маски не оставляет гипотетического G1-роста.\n\nSC8 и SC9 проверены как точные ordinary-orbit identities. Для\n\\(\\mu=2n+1\\):\n\\[\n 2H(n)+1=T^2(\\mu)=\\frac{9\\mu+5}{4}\n \\quad(n\\equiv3\\bmod4),\n\\]\nа при \\(n\\equiv5\\bmod8\\),\n\\[\n 2G_3(n)+1=T^3(\\mu)=\\frac{9\\mu+5}{8}.\n\\]\nВторая формула использует \\(\\mu\\equiv11\\bmod16\\), поэтому третий вход\nдействительно чётен; слово имеет вид \\(110\\). Эти формулы относятся к\nорбите \\(\\mu\\), а не превращают G3 в обычный шаг на самом \\(n\\).\nИз birth следует противоположность цветов \\(\\beta(n)\\) и \\(\\beta(\\mu)\\);\nдля H их перенос вдоль H-цепочки оправдан прямым равенством\n\\(T^2(n)=H(n)\\). Для G3 одна только SC9 не даёт равенства\n\\(\\beta(G_3(n))=\\beta(n)\\); root-уточнение корректно добавляет это\nравенство при actual output-birth guard:\n\\[\n\\beta(G_3(n))=1-\\beta(2G_3(n)+1)=1-\\beta(\\mu)=\\beta(n).\n\\]\nЭто следует из маски BR4 вместе с SC9, а не из SC9 отдельно.\n\nSC10 совпадает с (5)–(7) выше. Условие H-префикса\n\\(B_0=B_2=\\cdots=B_{2r}=c\\) точно эквивалентно birth на всех его узлах,\nесли исходный \\(e(n_0)=1\\). SC11 применим только при нечётном \\(n_j\\),\nто есть \\(a-2j\\ge1\\): тогда \\(\\lfloor n_j/2\\rfloor\\) имеет\n\\(d=1\\), и его ближайший birth-предок не превосходит \\(\\lfloor n_j/4\\rfloor\\).\nДля \\(j=1\\) это ниже \\(n_0\\), а для \\(j\\ge2\\) из этой оценки такой вывод\nне следует. При конечном чётном endpoint нужен отдельный случай SC3:\nBR2 применим к чётному birth-родителю при нулевой родительской границе,\nа иначе берётся более глубокий предок; формула SC11 туда не переносится.\n\nНаконец, арифметика reset-семьи SC6 верна:\n\\(2^L\\equiv-1\\pmod9\\), \\(m=16(2^L+1)/9-3\\), и\n\\(G_3(m)=2^{L+1}-1\\). Root явно фиксирует там core \\(u=1\\), то есть\n\\(C_h=\\beta(3^h-1)\\); вспомогательное \\(u_*=(2^L+1)/9\\) является другим\nпараметром. Поэтому обозначение согласовано после этой ясности. Само\nпредположение минимального birth даёт \\(C_A=\\beta(2^A-1)=\\beta(1)\\) и\n\\(C_{A+1}\\ne\\beta(1)\\), но не закрывает дальние H-пробы.\n\nИтог: SC1–SC11 сокращают локальный набор существенных ростовых режимов\nдо G3 и H и дают точные средние-орбитные формулы. Они не дают общего\nспуска относительно исходного birth и не доказывают равенство цветов\nмежду выходами G3 и входами без дополнительных масок.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_MINIMAL_BAD_INTEGER.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — minimal bad integer and structural inverse limits\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Manual analytic report only: no\nprograms, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or numerical experiments. The\nhypothetical minimal bad integer is assumed only for deriving necessary\nconditions. The full Collatz conjecture is not claimed.\n\n## 1. Setup and exact scope\n\nUse\n\\[\nT(n)=\\begin{cases}n/2,&n\\ {\\rm even},\\\\(3n+1)/2,&n\\ {\\rm odd}.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nCall a positive integer good if its forward orbit reaches \\(1\\), and bad\notherwise. Assume, conditionally, that \\(\\mu>1\\) is the least bad positive\ninteger. Then \\(\\mu\\) is odd, \\(\\mu+1\\) is good, and every positive\nforward iterate of \\(\\mu\\) is bad and at least \\(\\mu\\).\n\nFor the structural return map \\(R\\) on ordered pairs, a genuine inverse\npredecessor \\(m\\) of \\(\\mu\\) means that a common-\\((K,P)\\), ordered\nadjacent block sends \\((m,m+1)\\) to \\((\\mu,\\mu+1)\\). Its left trajectory\nis bad at every intermediate positive time: if an intermediate value were\ngood, its later orbit could not reach the bad value \\(\\mu\\). Minimality\ntherefore gives\n\\[\nm\\ge\\mu,\\qquad T^j(m)\\ge\\mu\\quad(0\\le j\\le K).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe same conclusion applies to every structural predecessor, not merely\nto the first one.\n\nThis report concerns the structural inverse tree at the fixed target\n\\(\\mu\\). It does not turn the 3-adic mass \\(\\kappa_0>1\\) into an integer\ncount and does not address ordinary unequal-count inverse paths.\n\n## 2. Target congruence and the residue split\n\nEvery nonempty common-odd-count block has \\(P\\ge1\\) on each arm. On an odd\ninput, the next value \\((3x+1)/2\\) is \\(2\\pmod3\\), hence is a 3-adic\nunit; subsequent even steps preserve being a unit. Therefore both terminal\nvalues of a nonempty ordered block are units modulo \\(3\\). If they are\nadjacent, the left terminal value must be\n\\[\nn\\equiv1\\pmod3.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIndeed, among \\(n,n+1\\), the only pair with both members nonzero modulo\n\\(3\\) is \\(1,2\\).\n\nConsequently all nonempty first-level structural inverse guards are\ncontained in \\(1+3\\mathbb Z_3\\), and for every positive integer target\n\\(n\\not\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) the structural predecessor set is exactly\n\\(\\{n\\}\\). In the notation of PR,\n\\[\nM_R(n)=\\{n\\},\\qquad W_q(n)=1\\quad(q>0),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nbecause only the empty schedule can terminate at such a target. This\ntarget restriction is already recorded in\n[CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER.md](CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER.md);\nit is used here to specialize the minimal-bad question.\n\nThere is also an elementary residue restriction on \\(\\mu\\). If\n\\(\\mu\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), then\n\\[\nT^2(\\mu)=\\frac{3\\mu+1}{4}\u003c\\mu,\n\\]\nand it is bad whenever \\(\\mu\\) is bad, contradicting minimality. Thus\n\\[\n\\mu\\equiv3\\pmod4,\\qquad\n\\mu\\equiv3,7,\\text{ or }11\\pmod{12}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nFor the \\(3\\) and \\(11\\) classes, \\(\\mu\\not\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), so (3) gives\na singleton structural inverse closure and \\(W_q(\\mu)=1\\). This does not\nexclude such a \\(\\mu\\): ordinary inverse paths with unequal odd counts\nare outside the structural tree. Only the class \\(\\mu\\equiv7\\pmod{12}\\)\ncan support a nonempty structural predecessor of \\(\\mu\\).\n\n## 3. Minimality forbids inverse slopes \\(\\alpha\\le1\\)\n\nLet a nonempty genuine structural predecessor have\n\\[\nF(\\mu)=\\alpha\\mu-B=m,\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac{2^K}{3^P},\\qquad B=\\frac C{3^P}\\ge\\frac13.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nBy (1), \\(m\\ge\\mu\\), so\n\\[\n\\alpha\\mu\\ge\\mu+B,\\qquad\n\\alpha\\ge1+\\frac{B}{\\mu}\\ge1+\\frac1{3\\mu}>1.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nIn particular, a minimal bad target cannot have a nonempty structural\ninverse branch of slope \\(\\alpha\\le1\\). Equality \\(\\alpha=1\\) is already\nimpossible because the positive intercept lowers the source below the\ntarget. This is a concrete restriction on admissible \\((K,P)\\):\n\\[\n2^K>3^P.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe height corridor (1) gives a second exact inequality. For the forward\npath \\(x_j=T^j(m)\\) of the block,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mu}{m}\n =2^{-K}\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le j\u003cK\\\\x_j\\ {\\rm odd}}}\n       \\left(3+\\frac1{x_j}\\right)\n \\le 2^{-K}\\left(3+\\frac1\\mu\\right)^P.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nm\\ge \\mu\\,\\frac{2^K}{(3+1/\\mu)^P}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nThis uses the full minimality corridor, rather than only positivity. It\nstill supplies no bound on the number of distinct \\((K,P)\\) branches:\nlarge \\(K,P\\) can satisfy (6)--(8), and congruence guards may select\ndifferent source classes.\n\nFor \\(\\mu\\equiv3,11\\pmod{12}\\), (3) is stronger than (6): there are no\nnonempty structural branches at all. For \\(\\mu\\equiv7\\pmod{12}\\), (6)--(8)\nare necessary conditions but not a finiteness theorem for the critical\nharmonic sum.\n\n## 4. Mixed bad/good pairs have only finitely many forward returns\n\nThe pair \\((\\mu,\\mu+1)\\) is mixed: its left member is bad and its right\nmember is good. Let\n\\[\nh=\\min\\{j\\ge0:T^j(\\mu+1)\\in\\{1,2\\}\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nThis is finite because \\(\\mu+1\\) is good.\n\nSuppose a positive genuine structural block of length \\(K\\) sends a mixed\npair \\((x,x+1)\\), with bad left member and good right member, to another\nordered adjacent pair. Then\n\\[\nK\u003ch_x,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nwhere \\(h_x\\) is the first time the right member reaches \\(\\{1,2\\}\\).\nIf \\(K\\ge h_x\\), the right terminal is \\(1\\) or \\(2\\), since the\n\\(\\{1,2\\}\\)-orbit alternates. Ordered adjacency would make the left\nterminal \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). The first is not a positive state, and the\nsecond is good, contradicting that a forward image of a bad state remains\nbad. This proves (10).\n\nAfter a return with \\(K\u003ch_x\\), no earlier right hit has occurred and\ndeterminism gives the exact residual time\n\\[\nh_{\\rm new}=h_x-K.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nA positive structural return has \\(K\\ge2\\): at one step adjacent inputs\nhave opposite parity, so their odd-counts cannot be equal. Therefore a\nchain of successive mixed forward returns has at most\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{h-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nblocks. In particular the pair \\((\\mu,\\mu+1)\\) cannot lie on a positive\ncycle of \\(R\\): a return would strictly decrease \\(h\\) while returning\nto the same pair.\n\nThis gives the useful PR consequence\n\\[\nc_q(\\mu)=1\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nin the cycle-multiplicity notation of PR. More generally it proves\nfinite forward-return depth for every mixed bad/good pair whose good\nright side has finite \\(h\\).\n\nThe bound (12) is a forward-return bound. It is not an upper bound on the\nnumber of distinct inverse predecessors of \\(\\mu\\): an incoming block of\nlength \\(K\\) starts from a right height whose first hit time is\n\\(K+h\\), so inverse branches can move to larger \\(h\\) rather than consume\nthe fixed \\(h\\) in (9).\n\n## 5. What this does and does not say about critical \\(W_1\\)\n\nFor the target \\(\\mu\\), (13) removes the cycle factor from PR3. If\n\\(\\mu\\equiv3\\) or \\(11\\pmod{12}\\), the stronger statement (3) gives\n\\(W_q(\\mu)=1\\) for every \\(q>0\\). If \\(\\mu\\equiv7\\pmod{12}\\), every\nnonempty predecessor is a distinct bad integer \\(m>\\mu\\) and its weight\nsatisfies\n\\[\nA^{-q}\u003c\\left(\\frac{\\mu}{m}\\right)^q\n\\]\nby PR2 and (6). This is useful pointwise information, but at \\(q=1\\)\nthe possible sum over distinct \\(m\\) is still only harmonically\ncontrolled. Minimality gives no independent upper bound on\n\\[\n\\sum_{m\\in M_R(\\mu)}\\frac1m.\n\\]\nHence neither \\(\\kappa_0>1\\) nor (12) proves critical \\(W_1(\\mu)\u003c\\infty\\).\n\nThe exact obstruction is therefore clear. The 3-adic mass counts\nguarded branches by \\(3^{-P}\\), while minimality controls their positive\ninteger sources only through lower height and the slope condition\n\\(\\alpha>1\\). Without a new arithmetic estimate on the number or spacing\nof the sources \\(m\\), this does not yield a positive-integer growth\ncontradiction or universal descent.\n\nThe residue split, the \\(\\alpha>1\\) restriction, and the mixed-pair return\nbound are necessary consequences of a hypothetical minimal bad seed.\nThey are not an exclusion of that seed. The frequency input and\nminimal-neighbor scope overlap\n[R183 H](../../../work/round183-h-minimal-bad-counter.md) and its audit\n[R183 G to H](../../../work/round183-audit-minimal-bad-counter.md);\nthe inverse-tree and cycle notation overlap\n[PR](POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md), while the target-unit fact is\nalready in [CX](CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER.md).\nNo distinct priority claim is made.\n\n## 6. Scope correction from the swapped adjacent pair\n\nMinimality also gives that \\(\\mu-1\\) is good. It is therefore necessary\nto inspect the ordered pair \\((\\mu-1,\\mu)\\), not only\n\\((\\mu,\\mu+1)\\). The target left residues are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n\\mu\\bmod12 & \\mu\\bmod3 & (\\mu-1)\\bmod3\\\\ \\hline\n3&0&2\\\\\n7&1&0\\\\\n11&2&1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThus for \\(\\mu\\equiv3\\pmod{12}\\), both orientations have singleton\nstructural inverse closure. For \\(\\mu\\equiv7\\pmod{12}\\), the original\npair \\((\\mu,\\mu+1)\\) can have incoming structural blocks, while the\nswapped pair cannot. For \\(\\mu\\equiv11\\pmod{12}\\), the original pair has\nno such incoming block, but the swapped pair can. Therefore the residue\nargument is a scope restriction on a chosen orientation, not a prohibition\nof the whole adjacent-pair method.\n\nThe forward-return clock also has the reversed mixed orientation. If\n\\((x,x+1)\\) has good left member and bad right member, let \\(h_x\\) be the\nfirst time the left member reaches \\(\\{1,2\\}\\). A structural return of\nlength \\(K\\ge h_x\\) would end with left value \\(1\\) or \\(2\\), and right\nvalue \\(2\\) or \\(3\\). Both possibilities are good on the right:\n\\(2\\) is in the \\(1\\leftrightarrow2\\) cycle, while\n\\(3\\to5\\to8\\to4\\to2\\to1\\). This contradicts a bad right member. Hence\nagain \\(K\u003ch_x\\), and the residual clock decreases by \\(K\\). The same\n\\(K\\ge2\\) argument gives at most \\(\\lfloor(h_x-1)/2\\rfloor\\) successive\nreturns and excludes a positive structural cycle in the reversed mixed\npair as well.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_MINIMUM_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0459 — minimum odd defect cutoff\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный ручной аудит утверждения о минимальном положительном нечётном \\(h\\)-дефекте. Использованы точные пути PB1 и merging identity SC2–SC3. Программ, CAS, переборов и Lean не использовал.\n\n## 1. Точная event-to-defect лемма\n\nПусть \\(u\\) — любое положительное нечётное число, без условия \\(3\\nmid u\\). Положим\n\\[\n p=4u+1,\\qquad z=3u,\\qquad r=\\frac{3u+1}{2}=2^a w,\n\\]\nгде \\(a\\ge0\\), а \\(w\\) положительно и нечётно. Обозначим\n\\[\n C_j=\\beta(3^j w-1)\\quad(j\\ge1),\\qquad B=\\beta(w).\n\\]\nВсе эти аргументы положительны, включая \\(u=1\\), где \\(a=1,w=1\\).\n\nТочные \\(T\\)-пути дают\n\\[\n \\beta(p)=\\beta(u)=B,\\quad\n \\beta(3p)=C_{a+3},\\quad\n \\beta(z)=C_{a+1},\\quad\n \\beta(z+1)=B,\\quad\n \\beta(2z+1)=C_{a+2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nНапример, \\(p\\) и \\(u\\) после удаления powers of two имеют нечётный конец \\(w\\); равенства для \\(3p,z,2z+1\\) следуют соответственно из\n\\[\n 3p=8r-1,\\qquad z=2r-1,\\qquad 2z+1=4r-1.\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(e(z)=1\\), то по определению рождения\n\\[\n C_{a+1}=B\\ne C_{a+2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nЕсли бы \\(3^{a+1}w\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), SC2 дала бы \\(C_{a+1}=C_{a+2}\\), противореча (2). Поэтому\n\\[\n 3^{a+1}w\\equiv1\\pmod4,\\qquad 3^{a+2}w\\equiv3\\pmod4.\n\\]\nПоследнее условие — surviving plus selector. Применение той же SC2 к индексу \\(a+2\\) даёт\n\\[\n C_{a+2}=C_{a+3}.\n\\]\nВместе с (1)–(2) это означает\n\\[\n g(p)=\\beta(3p)-\\beta(p)=C_{a+3}-B=C_{a+2}-B=\\pm1,\n \\qquad h(p)=1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nИтак, доказано без условия \\(3\\nmid u\\):\n\\[\n \\boxed{e(3u)=1\\Longrightarrow h(4u+1)=1.} \\tag{4}\n\\]\nСобытие само уже достаточно для (4). Дополнительное предположение \\(h(u)=0\\) не требуется; более того, из (1)–(2) оно автоматически следует, поскольку \\(\\beta(3u)=\\beta(u)=B\\). Если \\(g(p)=\\pm1\\), это просто знак в (3), а не дополнительная маска.\n\n## 2. Минимальный нечётный дефект\n\nПредположим, что существует минимальный положительный нечётный \\(q\\) с \\(h(q)=1\\). Если \\(u\\) нечётно и\n\\[\n 4u+1\u003cq,\n\\]\nто (4) запрещает \\(e(3u)=1\\), поскольку тогда положительный нечётный \\(h\\)-дефект \\(4u+1\\) был бы меньше \\(q\\). Следовательно,\n\\[\n 4u+1\u003cq,\\quad u\\ {\\rm odd}\n \\Longrightarrow e(3u)=0. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nЭто именно минимальность по \\(h\\), а не утверждение о минимальном birth-индексе.\n\n## 3. Следствие для first fiber, включая \\(A=2\\)\n\nРассмотрим first primitive fiber CF с\n\\[\n q=4y+1,\\qquad y=3^{A-1}m,\\qquad A\\ge2,\n\\]\nгде \\(m\\) положительно, нечётно и \\(3\\nmid m\\), и предположим, что этот \\(q\\) является указанным минимальным нечётным \\(h\\)-дефектом. Положим\n\\[\n u=\\frac y3=3^{A-2}m.\n\\]\nТогда \\(u\\) нечётно и \\(3u=y\\), а\n\\[\n 4u+1=4y/3+1\u003c4y+1=q.\n\\]\nПо (5) получаем\n\\[\n e(y)=e(3u)=0. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nПри \\(A\\ge3\\) это также следует из CF-firstness: exponent \\(A-1\\ge2\\) уже лежал бы перед первым primitive birth. Новое содержание минимального-\\(h\\) аргумента — случай \\(A=2\\): тогда \\(y=3m\\) имеет exponent \\(1\\), поэтому CF-firstness само по себе \\(e(y)=0\\) не давало; (5) всё равно даёт его, так как меньший дефект \\(4m+1\u003cq=12m+1\\) был бы вынужден событием \\(e(3m)=1\\).\n\n## 4. Финальный аудит MC1–MC7\n\nВесь MINIMUM_DEFECT_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md перечитан. MC1 использует только \\(C_j\\) при \\(j\\ge1\\), поэтому при \\(w=1\\) не вводится ненужное значение \\(C_0=\\beta(0)\\). Точные пути для \\(p,u,3p,3u,3u+1,6u+1\\) дают именно MC1; merging при остатке \\(3\\pmod4\\) даёт требуемое равенство соседних \\(C_j\\). MC2 корректно различает минимальность по \\(h\\) и минимальность рождения. В MC3 предпосылка явно ограничена: \\(q\\) одновременно является first-fiber source и минимальным нечётным \\(h\\)-дефектом. Тогда \\(u=y/3\\) нечётно, \\(4u+1\u003cq\\), и случай \\(A=2\\) действительно закрывается. Значение \\(W=\\beta(2q+1)\\) не определяется тождествами MC4–MC7; существование инварианта с произвольным выбором W не утверждается.\n\n## 5. Область и предел\n\nЛемма (4) использует только точные \\(T\\)-пути, SC2 и binary birth definition; она действует для всех положительных нечётных \\(u\\), включая \\(3\\mid u\\). Она не утверждает существование минимального \\(q\\), first primitive birth или nonconstant invariant. Если минимальный odd \\(h\\)-дефект существует, то (5) и (6) безусловны внутри этой условной системы.\n\nЭто локальное отсечение не даёт само по себе descent для всех рождений: оно запрещает только birth \\(e(3u)=1\\), когда соответствующий \\(4u+1\\) уже меньше выбранного минимального \\(h\\)-дефекта. Остальные цвета, primitive fibers и глобальная finite-energy оценка остаются вне этого аудита.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_MULTIPLICATIVE_COLOR_COCYCLE.md":"# Multiplicative color cocycle: cofinite obstruction and its limit\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded manual analysis; no programs, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean, or new agents. Current verified result: an exact zero set\nof density 1/16, the affine descent G\u003c=7G_core/6, and the conditional\nfinite-variation density-zero lemma. Earlier cofinite/cocycle paragraphs are\nhistory and scope; the remaining core-energy finiteness is open. This report\nseparates a cofinite statement from the much stronger finite-harmonic-energy\nclaim.\n\n## 1. Exact 3-cocycle identities\n\nFor g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), direct telescoping gives\n\n    g(3n)+g(n)=beta(9n)-beta(n).                     (MC1)\n\nIf h(n)=|g(n)|=1 at both n and 3n, then g(3n)=-g(n) and the right side of\n(MC1) is zero. Iterating along a 3-chain on which h remains one gives\n\n    beta(3^(2j)n)=beta(n),\n    beta(3^(2j+1)n)=1-beta(n),\n\nwith no sign or absolute-value cancellation being assumed.\n\n## 2. What cofinite h=1 forces using T-invariance\n\nAssume only for this section that h(n)=1 for every sufficiently large odd n.\nFor odd n, the exact commutator is\n\n    g(U(n))-g(n)=beta(v_n+1)-beta(v_n),\n    v_n=(9n+1)/2.\n\nLet F be the finite exceptional odd set where h may vanish. The statement\nthat U(n) is large is not automatic: infinitely many n can have U(n) in a\nfixed finite set. However, the inverse kernel has finite harmonic mass on\neach fixed m, so the exceptional source set {n: U(n) belongs to F} has finite\nharmonic mass. Outside this source set and for n>N, both n and U(n) are in\nthe cofinite h=1 set. Since both g-values are compared with the same base\ncolor beta(n), their signs cannot be opposite: a difference of opposite signs\nwould have magnitude two, while the right side is a single binary color\ndifference. Hence\n\n    g(U(n))=g(n),\n    beta(v_n+1)=beta(v_n).                           (MC2)\n\nWriting n=2r+1 gives the concrete eventual boundary identity\n\n    beta(9r+5)=beta(9r+6)                             (MC3)\n\nfor all r outside a set of finite harmonic mass (after the affine change\nn=2r+1). Thus cofinite h=1 forces a zero boundary on the progression\n9r+5,9r+6 modulo a finite-harmonic exceptional set, in addition to the\nalternating 3-generation law from MC1.\n\nThe same conclusion follows from the two T images: v_n=T(3n) and\nv_n+1=3S(n), while beta(S(n))=beta(n) and beta(3n)=1-beta(n) under the\ncofinite assumption. No density or independence assumption enters.\n\n## 3. Why this is not a contradiction or a finite-G estimate\n\nMC1--MC3 do not by themselves conflict with exact T-invariance. They impose\ncolors on the progressions 3^j n and equality of adjacent colors on\n9r+5,9r+6 outside a finite-harmonic exceptional set, but they do not identify\nthose two progressions in a finite T-orbit or produce an opposite color\nassignment to the same integer. No global constancy follows from these\nrelations alone.\n\nNor does excluding a cofinite h=1 regime imply finite defect energy. A set of\nodd indices where h=0 can have infinite harmonic mass, even if it is not\ncofinite; likewise an eventual AP of zero boundaries has no summability\nconsequence for the complementary absolute defect. The cocycle is signed,\nand taking absolute values only bounds it by already known boundary energy.\n\nThe exact useful outcome is limited: any proof that rules out cofinite h=1\nmay record this finite-harmonic version of MC3 as an additional necessary\npattern, but the remaining problem\nis still to control the harmonic mass of h on all odd integers. This report\nfinds no positive mass estimate or contradiction beyond the cofinite-pattern\nobstruction.\n\n## 4. An actual positive-density zero cylinder\n\nThere is, however, an exact common-orbit identity that rules out the cofinite\nassumption for every genuine invariant. For every integer (t\\ge0), put\n\n    n=32t+23.\n\nThe two ordinary T trajectories are\n\n    n -> 48t+35 -> 72t+53 -> 108t+80 -> 54t+40 -> 27t+20,\n\nand\n\n    3n -> 144t+104 -> 72t+52 -> 36t+26 -> 18t+13 -> 27t+20.\n\nThe parity words are respectively OOOEE and OEEEO, so every displayed step\nis valid for all (t\\ge0). Exact T-invariance therefore gives\n\n    beta(32t+23)=beta(3(32t+23)),\n    h(32t+23)=0.                                  (MC4)\n\nAmong odd integers, this is a density-(1/16) cylinder; among all positive\nintegers its natural density is (1/32). Since the odd harmonic sum is\n\n    sum_(n\u003c=X, n odd) 1/n = (1/2)log X+O(1),\n\nwhile the cylinder contributes ((1/32)\\log X+O(1)), every exact invariant\nsatisfies the unconditional upper bound\n\n    G(X) \u003c= (15/32)log X+O(1).                       (MC5)\n\nThis immediately excludes cofinite (h=1), but it does not imply finite\nharmonic defect energy.\n\nThe common identity is stable under a simultaneous even pullback, since\n(T(2n)=n) and (T(6n)=3n), but that only produces the corresponding even\npair and no new odd cylinder. Pulling back both endpoints through an odd\ninverse requires separate congruence guards for (n) and (3n); the present\nword does not supply those guards uniformly in (t). Thus MC4--MC5 are a\ngenuine positive-density zero result, while a transfer to a larger odd cover\nrequires a new common-word family and is not obtained here.\n\n## 5. Propagation through all 2-adic depths\n\nThe zero cylinder propagates uniformly. First, if x is 15 modulo 16, write\nv=(9x+1)/2=4r, where r is odd. Then\n\n    beta(4r)=beta(r),\n    beta(4r+1)=beta(3r+1)=beta((3r+1)/2)=beta(r),\n\nso d(v)=0. The commutator therefore gives the exact preservation\n\n    g(x)=g(Sx),       Sx=(3x+1)/2.                  (MC6)\n\nFor L>=3, let m be odd and set x_0=2^L m-1. The forced odd steps are\n\n    x_j=2^(L-j)3^j m-1,\n    x_(j+1)=S(x_j),\n    0\u003c=j\u003c=L-4,\n\nand (MC6) preserves g through all L-3 steps, ending at\n\n    x_(L-3)=8*3^(L-3)m-1.                          (MC7)\n\nChoose m=4t+a_L, where a_L=3 for odd L and a_L=1 for even L. Then\n3^(L-3)m is congruent to 3 modulo 4, so x_(L-3) is congruent to 23 modulo\n32. The previous coalescence identity MC4 gives h(x_(L-3))=0, hence\n\n    h(2^L(4t+a_L)-1)=0.                             (MC8)\n\nFor L=3, this is exactly the original 32t+23 cylinder; for larger L, the\nclasses are disjoint because v2(x_0+1)=L. The density of the L-th class is\n2^(-L-2), so the union has density\n\n    sum_(L>=3) 2^(-L-2)=1/16                         (MC9)\n\namong all integers, or 1/8 relative to odd integers. Summing the harmonic\nweights of these arithmetic progressions gives\n\n    sum_(x\u003c=X, x in union Z_L) 1/x=(1/16)log X+O(1).\n\nThe O(1) is uniform after summing over L: each progression has step 2^(L+2),\nits endpoint error is O((L+1)2^(-L)), including the logarithm of the\nprogression modulus, and the unrepresented tail L>log_2 X\ncontributes O(1). Therefore every exact invariant satisfies\n\n    G(X)\u003c= (7/16)log X+O(1).                         (MC10)\n\nThis is a stronger unconditional logarithmic upper bound, still far from\nfinite G. The propagation uses exact common colors and is not the relaxed\nfinite-set construction; no further density transfer is obtained here.\n\n## 7. Final audit of COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY\n\nI read the complete root file `COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md`.\nThe two five-step affine paths in CZ2 have parity words 11100 and 10001 and\nthe same endpoint, so the zero class 23 modulo 32 is exact. CZ3 correctly\nuses (d(4r)=0) for odd r, giving signed g-preservation on 15 modulo 16.\nThe CZ4 induction, the parity choice of a_L, and disjointness by\nv2(n+1)=L are all correct.\n\nThe density normalization is also correct: Z_L has modulus 2^(L+2), so the\nunion has density sum_(L>=3)2^(-L-2)=1/16 among all integers and 1/8 among\nodds. The count is X/16+O(log X). Partial summation makes the harmonic error\nO(1), yielding the 7/16 logarithmic upper bound with the correct order of\nlimits and no exchange of infinitely many asymptotics.\n\nUnder the separate finite-variation hypothesis, CZ6 correctly uses\npreimages (not projections) of the zero-density Delta support. For every\nfixed j, finitely many affine T^j inverse branches preserve density zero;\nthe finite union is taken before the r limit. The exact parity-word count\ngives (31/32)^r, and the order X to infinity first, then r to infinity, is\nnecessary and correctly stated. The resulting density-zero and o(log X)\nclaims do not imply finite G, and the hypothesis itself remains unproved.\n\n## 6. Conditional variation lemma and its exact scope\n\nThere is a useful conditional consequence, but its hypothesis is not yet\nproved for the actual defect. Let\n\n    V_h=sum_(n odd) |h(U(n))-h(n)|/n.\n\nIf (V_h\u003c\\infty), define Δ(n)=|h(T(n))-h(n)|. Dyadic invariance gives\nΔ(n)=0 for even n, and for odd n it is exactly the summand in (V_h).\nThus the support of Δ has natural density zero. For every fixed (j),\nthe (j)-step inverse image of this support also has density zero: (T^j)\nhas only finitely many affine parity branches, each injective with output\nbounded by a constant times its input, so a zero-density target has a\nzero-density source preimage. Finite unions over (j\u003c5r) remain\nzero-density.\n\nThe zero cylinder (23\\pmod{32}) has parity word (11100) for the first five\nT steps. Every length-(5r) parity word is one residue class modulo\n(2^{5r}). If h(n)=1 and no Delta-change occurs during the first 5r steps,\nthe orbit must avoid the zero cylinder at the block starts 0,5,...,5(r-1);\notherwise the zero value would be reached without a change. Hence each of\nthe r disjoint five-bit blocks avoids 11100.\nThe exceptional residue classes therefore have density exactly at most\n\n    (31/32)^r.\n\nAdding the zero-density preimages of the Δ-support and then letting\n(r\\to\\infty) proves the conditional statement\n\n    V_h\u003cinfinity  =>  upper natural density {n:h(n)=1}=0.  (MC11)\n\nThis is only a natural-density conclusion. Density zero does not imply finite\n(G=sum h(n)/n), and (V_h\u003c\\infty) itself remains unproved for the actual\n(h=|\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n)|). The lemma therefore supplies a precise conditional\nbridge, not a completion of the defect-energy argument.\n\n## 8. An exact affine descent on a further zero-disjoint class\n\nFor n=128s+7 and u=18s+1=(9n+1)/64, the two actual paths are\n\n    n ->192s+11->288s+17->432s+26->216s+13\n      ->324s+20->162s+10->81s+5,\n\nand\n\n    3n->576s+32->288s+16->144s+8->72s+4\n      ->36s+2->18s+1=u.\n\nThe endpoint 81s+5 equals T(3u). Hence exact invariance gives\n\n    g(n)=beta(u)-beta(3u)=-g(u),\n    h(n)=h(u).                                      (MC12)\n\nSince n=(64u-1)/9 >= 7u for u>=1, the harmonic contribution from\nD={128s+7:s>=0} satisfies\n\n    G_D(X) \u003c= (1/7)G_(u congruent to 1 mod 18)(X/7) \u003c= G(X)/7. (MC13)\n\nThe class D has v2(n+1)=3, while the earlier Z_L classes use their distinct\nprescribed residue at that valuation or have v2(n+1) different from 3; in\nparticular D is disjoint from the exhibited zero family Z. Writing\nG=G_core+G_D therefore yields\n\n    G(X) \u003c= (7/6)G_core(X).                          (MC14)\n\nThis exact source-target descent proves only the equivalence of finite G with\nfiniteness of the remaining core energy. It supplies no proof that the core\nenergy is finite.\n\n## 9. Final read confirmation of CZ1--CZ8\n\nThe complete `COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md` was reread.\nIts current CZ7 descent matches MC12--MC14, including the sign swap, the\nsource/target guard (n=128s+7 to u=18s+1), disjointness from Z, and the exact\nfactor 7/6. CZ1--CZ6 retain the correct zero density 1/16, logarithmic bound,\nand conditional finite-variation density result. No finite-G conclusion is\nasserted; the uncontrolled core is the current remaining scope.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_ORIENTED_CHAIN_RETURN.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — oriented self-return в retained-growth цепях\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной аналитический аудит BR/SC/BC;\nпрограмм, CAS, переборов и Lean не использовалось.\n\n## 1. Ориентированная граница от birth\n\nПоложим\n\\[\n \\delta(k)=\\beta(k+1)-\\beta(k)\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n\\]\nТочная dyadic identity имеет вид\n\\[\n \\delta(2k)+\\delta(2k+1)=\\delta(k).                 \\tag{1}\n\\]\nЕсли \\(\\delta(k)=\\pm1\\), то ровно один из двух детей имеет ту же\nненулевую ориентированную границу, а второй имеет \\(\\delta=0\\). Поэтому\nпосле первого уровня от активного boundary знак \\(\\delta\\) сохраняется\nна единственном активном ребёнке.\n\nЕсли \\(b\\) — birth и\n\\[\n \\beta(b)=\\beta(b+1)=c,\\qquad \\beta(2b+1)=1-c,\n\\]\nто\n\\[\n \\delta(2b)=1-2c,\\qquad\n \\delta(2b+1)=-(1-2c).                                \\tag{2}\n\\]\nТаким образом, от одного birth выходят две активные ветви с\nпротивоположными знаками; далее каждая ветвь имеет единственное\nориентированное продолжение до тех пор, пока boundary остаётся активной.\n\n## 2. Точный criterion для nearest ancestor\n\nПусть \\(K\\) — failed H/G3 boundary, а \\(b_j\\) — один из более ранних\nbirths в той же retained-growth цепи. Тогда \\(b_j\\) является ближайшим\nbirth ancestor для \\(K\\) тогда и только тогда, когда существует \\(\\ell\\ge1\\)\nи \\(0\\le t\u003c2^\\ell\\), такие что\n\\[\n K=2^\\ell b_j+t,                                      \\tag{3}\n\\]\nи для\n\\[\n K_m=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{K}{2^{\\ell-m}}\\right\\rfloor\n     =2^m b_j+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{t}{2^{\\ell-m}}\\right\\rfloor,\n \\qquad 1\\le m\\le\\ell,\n\\]\nвыполнено \\(\\delta(K_m)\\ne0\\), тогда как \\(\\delta(b_j)=0\\).\nЭто просто точная запись binary prefix: \\(t\\) задаёт все dyadic child\nchoices, а nonzero conditions говорят, что prefix не встретил нулевой\nboundary раньше \\(b_j\\).\n\nЕсли все retained births цепи имеют один и тот же цвет \\(c\\), то знак\nпервого child в (3) определяется старшим битом \\(t\\):\n\\[\n t\u003c2^{\\ell-1}\\Rightarrow \\delta(K)=1-2c,\\qquad\n t\\ge2^{\\ell-1}\\Rightarrow \\delta(K)=-(1-2c).          \\tag{4}\n\\]\nПоследующие биты должны выбирать единственного активного ребёнка.\nУравнение (3) вместе с (4) — необходимый и достаточный structural\ncriterion; одной arithmetic близости недостаточно.\n\n## 3. Непосредственный failed H/G3 output не возвращается в предыдущий birth\n\nПусть \\(b_i\\) — current retained birth.\n\nДля H failure \\(K=H(b_i)=(9b_i+5)/4\\). При \\(b_i\\ge3\\)\n\\[\n 2b_i\u003cK\u003c4b_i.\n\\]\nДля \\(\\ell\\ge2\\) условие (3) невозможно, так как \\(K\u003c4b_i\\).\nДля \\(\\ell=1\\) cylinder от \\(b_i\\) имеет только \\(2b_i\\) и\n\\(2b_i+1\\); но\n\\[\n K-2b_i=\\frac{b_i+5}{4}\\ge2,\n\\]\nтак что \\(K\\) не лежит в нём. Следовательно failed H boundary не имеет\nсамого \\(b_i\\) ближайшим ancestor.\n\nДля retained G3 failure \\(p=G_3(b_i)=(9b_i+3)/8\\) активная граница\nравна \\(K=p\\), как уточняется в разделе4. Для G3 input \\(b_i\\ge13\\)\n\\[\n K\\le\\frac{9b_i+11}{8}\u003c2b_i.\n\\]\nЛюбой dyadic descendant на положительной глубине от \\(b_i\\) не меньше\n\\(2b_i\\), поэтому непосредственный self-return также невозможен. Это\nне использует независимость цветов.\n\n## 4. Более ранний origin: точное арифметическое условие\n\nПусть \\(b_j\\) и \\(b_i\\) разделены retained word, а затем следует failed\nH или G3 output. Композиция имеет вид\n\\[\n K=\\frac{9^m b_j+A_W}{2^B}+\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n \\varepsilon=\n \\begin{cases}\n 0,&\\text{H failure},\\\\\n 0,&\\text{retained G3 failure at }p,\n \\end{cases}                                         \\tag{5}\n\\]\nгде \\(B\\) — суммарное число shortcut bits, \\(A_W\\) — целый affine\nintercept. Self-return к \\(b_j\\) требует\n\\[\n 2^{B+\\ell}b_j\n \\le 9^m b_j+A_W+2^B\\varepsilon\n \u003c2^{B+\\ell}(b_j+1),                                  \\tag{6}\n\\]\nдля некоторого \\(\\ell\\ge1\\), плюс активный binary prefix (3).\n\nДля H-failure знак также фиксирован:\nпри общем chain-color \\(c\\), \\(\\beta(K)=c\\) и\n\\(\\beta(K+1)=1-c\\), поэтому\n\\[\n \\delta(K)=1-2c.\n\\]\nЗначит в (4) разрешён только первый, left, branch. Для G3-domain\n\\(q=16t+13\\), \\(p=G_3(q)=18t+15\\), имеются безусловные конечные пути\n\\[\n \\beta(q)=\\beta(p+1),\\qquad \\beta(q+1)=\\beta(p+2).\n\\]\nЕсли \\(q\\) — birth цвета \\(c\\), то \\(d(p+1)=d(q)=0\\). При failed\noutput guard \\(p\\) не является retained birth; BR4 тогда вынуждает\n\\(d(p)=1\\), а не \\(d(p+1)=1\\). Поэтому\n\\[\n \\beta(p)=1-c,\\qquad \\delta(p)=c-(1-c)=-(1-2c).\n\\]\nG3-failure, как и H-failure, фиксирует одну сторону binary prefix:\nдля общего chain-color разрешён только right branch в (4). Это относится\nтолько к retained G3-domain \\(13\\bmod16\\), не ко всему классу \\(5\\bmod8\\).\n\n## 5. Что действительно исключено и что остаётся\n\nРезультат исключает self-return к непосредственно предыдущему retained\nbirth на первом failed H или G3 guard. Для более раннего birth он сводит\nвопрос к (6) и точному активному binary prefix; \\(\\delta\\)-сохранение\nсамо по себе не запрещает такое попадание. Даже если affine ratio близок\nк \\(2^\\ell\\), нужны одновременно интервальное условие (6), знак (4),\nвсе nonzero prefixes и настоящие SC masks.\n\nПоэтому BC11–BC13 остаются корректны: actual-growth граф инъективен как\nграф retained edges, но failed boundary может, в принципе, иметь\ndyadic birth ancestor из более раннего места цепи. Установленный запрет\nне превращается в глобальный descending rank. Не доказано существование\nтакого self-return для какого-либо actual invariant; доказан только его\nточный необходимый критерий и невозможность непосредственного возврата.\n\n\n## 6. Аудит INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION\n\nOR1–OR4 проверены. Образы H и G3 действительно\n\\[\n I_H=\\{9t+8\\},\\qquad I_G=\\{18t+15\\}\n\\]\nи не пересекаются. Для H-образа \\(n=9u-1\\) inverse \\(q=4u-1\\);\nесли \\(n\\) birth цвета \\(c\\), то \\(d(q)=|\\beta(u)-c|\\). Координаты\n\\(q=X_{a+2,0}\\), \\(p=\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor=X_{a+1,0}\\) при\n\\(u=2^av\\) дают \\(d(p)=d(q)\\) через \\(C_{a+1}=C_{a+2}\\). При failed\ninverse guard оба boundary активны, и depth \\(\\ge2\\) даёт\n\\(b\\le\\lfloor q/4\\rfloor=u-1\u003cn\\). Край \\(u=1\\) корректно означает, что\nтакой failed case невозможен.\n\nДля G3-образа \\(n=18t+15\\), inverse \\(q=16t+13\\), все OR5-пути\nпроверяются напрямую. Они дают OR6:\n\\[\n e(q)=1-d(n+1),\\qquad d(q)=d(\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor)=d(n+1).\n\\]\nПоэтому OR7 и origin formula OR8 корректны: в образе H origin condition\n\\(e(n)|\\beta(u)-\\beta(n)|\\), в образе G3 — \\(e(n)d(n+1)\\). Это требует\nнастоящего birth цвета; произвольные цветовые assignments сюда не\nподставляются.\n\nOR9 проверена по depth charge. Для двух active levels nearest ancestor\nhas depth at least2, откуда на один origin приходится\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\ell\\ge2}2\\cdot2^{-\\ell}/b=1/b.\n\\]\nВес inverse H не превосходит \\(4/(9q)\\), G3 — \\(8/(9q)\\); при \\(n\\le X\\)\nв обоих случаях достаточно \\(q\u003c8X/9\\), значит \\(b\u003c2X/9\\). Отсюда\n\\[\n E_D(X)\\le\\frac89 E_\\beta(2X/9).\n\\]\nЭто оценка только image-origins. При переходе к полной энергии BC\nдаёт\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le9E_{O_\\beta}(X)\n \\le9E_{P_\\beta}(X)+8E_\\beta(2X/9);\n\\]\nкоэффициент действительно \\(8\\), а не \\(8/9\\): \\(E_D\\) содержит только\norigins внутри arithmetic image. Для контроля последующих vertices\nполная энергия оценивается через массу origins с множителем \\(9\\).\n\nOR5 арифметически корректен: \\(144t+99\\equiv0\\pmod9\\) и \\(3\\pmod{16}\\),\nа H-выход \\(324t+224\\equiv0\\pmod4\\), поэтому арифметический F останавливается\nпосле одной дуги. Это лишь arithmetic progression; существование actual\nbirths на ней не утверждается, так что divergence её superset не даёт\ndivergence actual origin mass.\n\nНаконец, исправление к прежнему разделу 4 существенно: для retained G3\ndomain \\(q=16t+13\\), \\(p=G_3(q)\\), пути OR5 дают\n\\[\n \\beta(q)=\\beta(p+1),\\qquad \\beta(q+1)=\\beta(p+2).\n\\]\nПри actual birth \\(q\\) имеем \\(d(p+1)=0\\). Если output guard fails, активен\nтолько \\(d(p)=1\\), поэтому \\(\\delta(p)=-(1-2c)\\); G3 self-return может\nидти только по right branch. Это точное ограничение относится к\n\\(13\\bmod16\\), а не ко всему \\(5\\bmod8\\). Общий self-return к более\nраннему origin всё ещё требует affine interval и полного active prefix.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_PRIMITIVE_EVEN_EXIT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — primitive origin \\(144t+99\\): even exit\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная проверка OR/SC/BR; программ,\nCAS, переборов и Lean не использовалось.\n\n## 1. Семейство и первый H-выход\n\nПоложим\n\\[\n n=144t+99,\\qquad m=H(n)=324t+224=2s,\n \\qquad s=162t+112.                                  \\tag{1}\n\\]\nИмеем \\(n\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), \\(n\\equiv0\\pmod9\\), \\(m\\equiv0\\pmod4\\).\nПоэтому \\(n\\) находится вне образов H/G3 из OR, но является допустимым\nH-входом. Арифметическая H-дуга от \\(n\\) имеет единственный выход \\(m\\),\nпосле которого partial growth map останавливается.\n\nПредположим, что \\(n\\) — настоящий birth цвета \\(c\\). Если \\(d(m)=1\\),\nэто failed H guard. BR5 применим с \\(k=m\\) и\n\\(p=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor=s\\).\n\n## 2. Failed H guard даёт меньший birth ниже исходного \\(n\\)\n\nРассмотрим две BR-ветви.\n\nЕсли \\(d(s)=1\\), то boundaries \\(m=2s\\) и \\(s\\) активны. Поэтому их\nnearest birth ancestor удовлетворяет\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac m4\\right\\rfloor\n   =\\left\\lfloor81t+56\\right\\rfloor\u003cn.                  \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(d(s)=0\\), BR5 даёт \\(e(s)=1\\), то есть \\(s\\) — even birth.\nПишем \\(s=2r\\), где \\(r=81t+56\\). BR2 для этого even birth даёт активную\nграницу\n\\[\n K_0=3r+1=243t+169.\n\\]\nЕё nearest birth ancestor находится не выше\n\\[\n \\left\\lfloor\\frac{K_0}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n \\le\\frac{243t+169}{2}\u003c144t+99=n.                       \\tag{3}\n\\]\nСледовательно, failed H guard на этой progression всегда даёт настоящий\nbirth строго ниже исходного \\(n\\). Здесь не требуется предположение, что\n\\(n\\) минимален.\n\n## 3. Retained H output and the only higher-return mask\n\nЕсли \\(d(m)=0\\), то BR5 с \\(d(k)=0\\) даёт \\(e(m)=1\\): выход \\(m\\) —\nнастоящий even birth и H-edge retained. BR2 с \\(m=2s\\) даёт активную\nграницу\n\\[\n K=3s+1=486t+337.                                      \\tag{4}\n\\]\nЕё first parent равен\n\\[\n a_1=\\left\\lfloor\\frac K2\\right\\rfloor=243t+168.\n\\]\nСравнение с origin:\n\\[\n \\frac K4=121.5t+84.25\u003c144t+99=n\u003c a_1\n\\]\nдля всех \\(t\\ge0\\). Поэтому если \\(d(a_1)=1\\), nearest birth ancestor\nимеет depth at least two и удовлетворяет\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac K4\\right\\rfloor\u003cn.               \\tag{5}\n\\]\nЕдинственная remaining possibility for a transfer not below \\(n\\) is\n\\[\n d(a_1)=0.                                               \\tag{6}\n\\]\nТогда (1) и \\(\\delta\\)-identity show that \\(a_1\\) itself is a birth:\n\\(K=2a_1+1\\) is active, so its other child is active as well. Moreover\n\\(a_1>n\\), so this is a genuine higher terminal birth, not a descent.\n\n## 4. Orientation and minimality\n\nThe color equations for the even birth \\(m=2s\\) are\n\\[\n \\beta(s)=\\beta(m)=\\beta(m+1)=c,\\qquad\n \\beta(3s+1)=1-c,\\qquad \\beta(3s+2)=c.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n \\delta(K)=\\beta(K+1)-\\beta(K)=2c-1.                    \\tag{7}\n\\]\nIf (6) holds, \\(d(a_1)=0\\); since \\(K\\) is the right child of \\(a_1\\),\nthe identity\n\\(\\delta(2a_1)+\\delta(2a_1+1)=0\\) makes the left child active too, and\n\\(a_1\\) is a birth of color \\(c\\). Its right-child orientation is exactly\n\\(2c-1\\), so orientation does not exclude (6); it confirms the precise\nright branch.\n\nMinimality of \\(n\\) does not exclude (6), because \\(a_1>n\\). If \\(t\\) is\nodd, then \\(a_1=243t+168\\equiv15\\pmod{18}\\) lies in the G3 image and its\ninverse is\n\\[\n q=16\\frac{a_1-15}{18}+13=216t+149=T(n)>n.\n\\]\nOR gives either \\(q\\) as a birth or a birth at most\n\\(\\lfloor q/4\\rfloor\u003cn\\). Under the additional assumption that \\(n\\) is\nminimal, the latter alternative is excluded, so the former larger birth\n\\(q\\) is forced. If \\(t\\) is even, \\(a_1\\) is even and BR2 only supplies\nthe next active boundary \\(3(a_1/2)+1\\); it gives no comparison below\n\\(n\\). Thus orientation does not remove the exceptional mask.\n\n## 5. Verdict\n\nFor the primitive arithmetic progression \\(144t+99\\):\n\n- a failed H output always yields a birth below the initial \\(n\\);\n- a retained even H output yields a birth below \\(n\\) unless the exact mask\n  \\(d(243t+168)=0\\) holds;\n- that exceptional mask is orientation-compatible and creates a higher\n  terminal birth \\(243t+168\\);\n- minimality of \\(n\\) does not exclude the exception; for odd \\(t\\) it\n  transfers the issue to the larger inverse birth \\(q=216t+149\\), while\n  for even \\(t\\) BR2 gives only a further boundary.\n\nThus the progression supplies a sharp local mask, not a global obstruction\nor a complete terminal-return theorem.\n\n\n## 6. Независимая сверка single-odd inverse graph\n\nДополнительно вручную сверены FOLLOWUP_0431_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH.md и\nFOLLOWUP_0483_SINGLE_ODD_INVERSE.md. Пусть \\(q\\) — нечётный birth цвета\n\\(c\\), а \\(n=S(q)=(3q+1)/2\\). Тогда\n\n\\[\n \\beta(n)=\\beta(q)=c,\n \\qquad \\beta(2n+1)=\\beta(3q+2)=1-c,\n\\]\n\nи потому \\(d(2n)=1,\\ d(2n+1)=1-d(n)\\), то есть\n\n\\[\n e(S(q))=1-d(S(q)).\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, при отказе выходной birth-маски \\(d(S(q))=1\\), а BR даёт\nbirth-предка не выше\n\\(\\lfloor S(q)/2\\rfloor=\\lfloor(3q+1)/4\\rfloor\u003cq\\)\nдля \\(q\\ge3\\); случай \\(q=1\\) исключается \\(e(2)=0\\).\n\nПроверим inverse-утверждение во всех слоях. Пишем\n\\(q=2^h v-1\\), где \\(h=\\nu _2(q+1)\\ge1\\), \\(v\\) нечётно. В strip-обозначениях\n\\(n=X_{h,1}\\), и рождение \\(n\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n C_h=B_1\\ne C_{h+1},\n \\qquad 3^h v\\equiv1\\pmod4.\n\\]\n\nДля \\(h\\ge2\\) это ровно условие SC3, после которого слияние соседних\nпутей даёт \\(C_{h-1}=C_h\\). Поэтому если \\(q\\) не является birth, то\n\\(B_0=C_{h+1}\\), тогда\n\n\\[\n d(q)=1,\n \\qquad d(\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor)=1.\n\\]\n\nСлой \\(h=1\\) действительно требует отдельной проверки. Здесь\n\\(n=3v-1\\) чётно; \\(e(4m+2)=0\\) заставляет \\(n\\equiv0\\pmod4\\), то есть\n\\(v\\equiv3\\pmod4\\). При \\(v>1\\) два настоящих пути\n\n\\[\n v-1\\longrightarrow (v-1)/2\\longrightarrow(3v-1)/4,\n \\qquad\n 3v-1\\longrightarrow(3v-1)/2\\longrightarrow(3v-1)/4\n\\]\n\nсливаются, так что \\(C_0=C_1\\). Значение \\(v=1\\) есть \\(q=1,n=2\\) и\nне является birth. Значит и при \\(h=1\\) отказ inverse-маски даёт те же\n\\(d(q)=d(\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor)=1\\), а ближайший birth находится не выше\n\\(\\lfloor q/4\\rfloor\\). Последний вывод использует также то, что у birth\n\\(b\\) всегда \\(d(b)=0\\): из \\(e(b)=1\\) и\n\\(d(2b)\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}d(2b+1)=d(b)\\).\n\nДля энергетического charge это означает следующее. Если нечётный \\(q\\)\nявляется inverse-источником image-origin и не является birth, то, при\n\\(a=\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor\\), из\n\\(d(a)=d(2a+1)=1\\) следует \\(d(2a)=0\\). С другой стороны, из birth\n\\(n=S(q)\\) и \\(d(q)=1\\) получаем\n\n\\[\n d(2q)=1,\\qquad d(2q+1)=0.\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, такой charged нечётный источник имеет активного потомка\n\\(2q\\), который чётен; два charged нечётных источника не могут находиться\nна соседних dyadic-глубинах. Его глубина от ближайшего birth не меньше двух,\nа на каждой из не более двух активных ancestry-ветвей\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{j\\ge1}\\frac1{2^{2j}b}=\\frac1{3b}.\n\\]\n\nПоэтому charge одного birth не превосходит \\(2/(3b)\\). Для\n\\(n=S(q)\\le X\\) имеем \\(1/n=2/(3q+1)\\le2/(3q)\\) и \\(b\u003cX/6\\), откуда\n\n\\[\n E_{D_S}(X)\\le \\frac49 E_\\beta(X/6).\n\\]\n\nКаждая S-цепочка конечна, поскольку\n\\(\\nu _2(n_j+1)\\) уменьшается на единицу; её полный вес не превосходит\n\\(3/a\\) при начале \\(a\\). С \\(P_S\\) — birth-началами вне образа S —\nполучается точная проверенная рекурсия\n\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le 3E_{P_S}(X)+\\frac43E_\\beta(X/6).\n\\]\n\nКоэффициент \\(4/3>1\\), а \\(E_{P_S}\\) не контролируется; это не contraction\nи не глобальный descent. Аудит подтверждает inverse lemma, включая\n\\(h=1\\), но не превращает её в доказательство Коллатца.\n\n\n## 7. Проверка усиления до коэффициента \\(7/9\\)\n\nПроверено также более точное суммирование, сохраняющее длину S-цепочки у\nкаждого charged-источника. Если \\(q\\) нечётен и\n\\(h=\\nu _2(q+1)\\), то полная арифметическая S-цепочка, начинающаяся с\n\\(S(q)\\), имеет вершины\n\\[\n n_j+1=(3/2)^{j+1}(q+1),\\qquad 0\\le j\u003ch,\n\\]\nпосле чего вершина становится чётной. Так как\n\\(n_j>(3/2)^{j+1}q\\), её вес удовлетворяет\n\\[\n W(Sq)\\le {a_h\\over q},\\qquad\n a_h=2\\bigl(1-(2/3)^h\\bigr)\u003c2.                          \\tag{8}\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(W\\) можно брать как полный вес цепочки; усечение по \\(X\\) только\nуменьшает его.\n\nПусть \\(q'\\) — следующий charged нечётный источник на той же активной\nгранице после charged \\(q\\), на расстоянии \\(r\\). Из\n\\(d(2q)=1,\\ d(2q+1)=0\\) следует, что первый следующий бит равен нулю,\nпоэтому \\(r\\ge2\\), \\(q'\\ge2^r q\\), и\n\\[\n \\nu _2(q'+1)\\le r-1.                                   \\tag{9}\n\\]\nПоложим \\(R=2/9\\). Для всех \\(r\\ge2\\)\n\\[\n {a_{r-1}+R\\over2^r}\\le R;                              \\tag{10}\n\\]\nпри \\(r=2\\) это равенство, при \\(r=3\\) левая часть равна \\(1/6\\), а\nпри \\(r\\ge4\\) она не превосходит \\(20/(9\\cdot16)\u003cR\\).\nОбратная индукция по конечному числу будущих charged-источников (с\nпоследнего к первому), а затем монотонный предел, даёт: масса всех\ncharged-источников после данного \\(q\\), включая следующий источник и его\nS-цепочку, не превосходит \\(R/q\\). Важно, что это утверждение относится\nк хвосту после \\(q\\); собственный член \\(W(Sq)\\) добавляется отдельно.\n\nРассмотрим две активные boundary-ветви birth \\(b\\). На левой ветви первый\nбит равен нулю. Первый charged нечётный источник имеет глубину\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\), а из первого нулевого бита следует\n\\(\\nu _2(q+1)\\le\\ell-1\\). Поэтому (10) непосредственно даёт для всей\nлевой charged-массы\n\\[\n {a_{\\ell-1}+R\\over2^\\ell b}\\le {R\\over b}={2\\over9b}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nНа правой ветви первый бит равен единице; но первый charged источник также\nимеет \\(\\ell\\ge2\\), поскольку при \\(\\ell=1\\) его родитель есть birth \\(b\\),\nа \\(d(b)=0\\). Здесь достаточно грубой оценки \\(a_h\u003c2\\), и потому\n\\[\n {2+R\\over2^\\ell b}\\le {2+R\\over4b}={5\\over9b}.         \\tag{12}\n\\]\nЕсли на ветви нет charged-источника, соответствующий вклад равен нулю.\nИтак, полная масса S-цепочек, чьи image-origins charge одному birth \\(b\\),\nне превосходит\n\\[\n {2\\over9b}+{5\\over9b}={7\\over9b}.                     \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nСуммируя по charged origins с \\(S(q)\\le X\\), как и в §6, имеем\n\\(q\u003c2X/3\\) и \\(b\u003cX/6\\). Если \\(E_{D_S}^{\\rm chain}(X)\\) обозначает\nполный (или усечённый) вес retained S-цепочек, начинающихся в D-образе и\nимеющих origin не выше \\(X\\), то\n\\[\n E_{D_S}^{\\rm chain}(X)\\le {7\\over9}E_\\beta(X/6).       \\tag{14}\n\\]\nДля всех births это даёт улучшенную, но всё ещё неполную рекурсию\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le 3E_{P_S}(X)+{7\\over9}E_\\beta(X/6).       \\tag{15}\n\\]\nКоэффициент обратной связи теперь меньше единицы, однако\n\\(E_{P_S}\\) — масса primitive beginnings вне образа S — по-прежнему не\nоценена. Поэтому (15) не является полным доказательством конечности\nэнергии и не даёт глобального спуска; это только усиление charge-части\nпредыдущего результата.\n\n## 8. Финальная сверка root SG1--SG12\n\nПолностью прочитан файл\nSINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md, включая SG4 и SG7--SG9.\nСущественной математической ошибки не найдено.\n\nВ SG4 переход от рождения \\(n=S(q)\\) к\n\\(C_h\\ne C_{h+1}\\), затем к \\(3^hv\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), корректен: при\nостатке \\(3\\pmod4\\) два явно указанных положительных пути сливаются и\nпротиворечат \\(C_h\\ne C_{h+1}\\). Для \\(h=1\\) отдельное условие\n\\(e(4m+2)=0\\) даёт \\(v\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), а слияние путей даёт\n\\(C_0=C_1\\); угол \\(v=1\\) действительно исключён \\(e(2)=0\\).\nПосле этого \\(d(q)=d(\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor)=1\\) при отказе inverse-маски.\nГлубина ближайшего birth не меньше двух, поскольку у любого birth\n\\(d(b)=0\\), что следует из \\(e(b)=1\\) и XOR-тождества для \\(d(2b)\\),\n\\(d(2b+1)\\).\n\nSG7 корректно использует forced child \\(2q\\): следующий charged источник\nна расстоянии \\(r\\ge2\\) имеет \\(q'\\ge2^rq\\) и\n\\(\\nu_2(q'+1)\\le r-1\\). Неотрицательная конечная обратная индукция и\nмонотонный предел дают хвост \\(R/q\\) при \\(R=2/9\\). SG8 правильно\nразделяет левую ветвь (первый бит нулевой, вклад \\(2/(9b)\\)) и правую\n(первая charge-глубина не меньше двух, вклад \\(5/(9b)\\)).\nSG9 корректно учитывает полные компоненты D-начал, даже если они выходят\nза cutoff \\(X\\), и даёт\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le3E_{P_S}(X)+\\frac79E_\\beta(X/6),\n \\qquad\n E_\\beta(X)\\le\\frac{27}{2}E_{P_S}(X).\n\\]\nЭто лишь сокращение image-origin части; масса primitive source \\(P_S\\)\nостаётся открытой. Область результата не расширяется до глобального\nспуска или полного Коллатца.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_PRIMITIVE_ONE_CLASS.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — odd primitive births in the class \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\)\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная аналитика без программ, CAS,\nпереборов и Lean. Проверены PROJECT_STATE.md и\nSINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md. Цель — изолировать\nостаток \\(P_S\\) для нечётных primitive births \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\).\n\n## 1. Точная координата и остатки S-цепочки\n\nПусть \\(n\\) нечётно, \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), и \\(n\\) — actual birth вне\nарифметического образа \\(I_S=\\{m:m\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}\\). Положим\n\\[\n h=\\nu _2(n+1)\\ge1,\\qquad n+1=2^h v,\\qquad v\\ {\\rm odd}.\n\\]\nТогда \\(n=2^h v-1\\) и\n\\[\n 2^h v\\equiv2\\pmod3;\n\\]\nто есть \\(v\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) при нечётном \\(h\\) и\n\\(v\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) при чётном \\(h\\). Это единственное следствие\nостатка \\(1\\pmod3\\); оно не ограничивает \\(h\\) сверху.\n\nАрифметические successive S-выходы имеют вид\n\\[\n n_j=3^j2^{h-j}v-1,\\qquad 0\\le j\\le h.\n\\]\nДля \\(j\\ge1\\), \\(n_j\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), так что первый выход уже лежит в\n\\(I_S\\), а \\(n_0=n\\) — primitive source. Формула действительна до\nпервого отказа birth-маски; при полном прохождении \\(n_h=3^h v-1\\)\nчётен и S-цепочка останавливается.\n\nДля нечётного текущего birth \\(n_j\\) отказ следующей маски даёт active\nboundary с birth-предком не выше\n\\[\n \\left\\lfloor {n_{j+1}\\over2}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn_j.\n\\]\nПри \\(j=0\\) это действительно descent ниже исходного \\(n\\). При\n\\(j\\ge1\\) полученная оценка относится к текущему \\(n_j\\) и не сравнивает\nпредка с primitive start \\(n\\).\n\n## 2. Терминальный even transfer не даёт спуска к \\(n\\)\n\nЕсли все необходимые S-маски до terminal even endpoint проходят, то\n\\(N=n_h=3^h v-1\\). BR для even birth даёт лишь birth ancestor не выше\n\\[\n B_h=\\left\\lfloor {3N+2\\over4}\\right\\rfloor\n     =\\left\\lfloor {3^{h+1}v-1\\over4}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nСравнение с исходным \\(n=2^h v-1\\) имеет точную разность числителей\n\\[\n (3^{h+1}v-1)-4(2^h v-1)\n =v\\bigl(3^{h+1}-2^{h+2}\\bigr)+3.\n\\]\nДля \\(h\\ge1\\) она положительна и не меньше \\(4\\). Поэтому\n\\(B_h\\ge n+1\\): terminal BR-оценка находится выше primitive start и\nсама по себе не даёт descent. Это не утверждает, что фактический birth\nancestor больше \\(n\\); BR допускает и меньший ancestor. При гипотетической\nминимальности \\(n\\) лишь исключается меньший вариант, а большой birth\nостаётся допустимым.\n\nНа первом шаге минимальность даёт только условное утверждение: если\n\\(d(S(n))=1\\), то BR ancestor не выше\n\\(\\lfloor S(n)/2\\rfloor\u003cn\\), что противоречило бы минимальности.\nСледовательно, минимальный такой \\(n\\) обязан иметь \\(d(S(n))=0\\).\nПосле этого отказы на более поздних \\(n_j\\) могут дать ancestors между\n\\(n\\) и \\(n_j\\), поэтому этот факт не замыкает спуск к исходному start.\n\n## 3. Точная цветовая маска и отсутствие остаточного критерия\n\nДля нечётного birth \\(n\\), с \\(c=\\beta(n)=\\beta(n+1)\\), invariance даёт\n\\[\n \\beta(2n)=c,\\qquad\n \\beta(2n+1)=\\beta(3n+2)=1-c.\n\\]\nСледовательно,\n\\[\n e(n)=1\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\beta(n)=\\beta(n+1)\\ne\\beta(3n+2).\n\\]\nУсловие \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) означает только, что \\(2n+1\\) делится на\n\\(3\\); оно не определяет ни один из трёх цветов и не заменяет actual\nbirth mask. Арифметическое множество нечётных \\(1\\pmod3\\) допустимо как\nверхнее супермножество, но имеет положительную плотность и расходящийся\nгармонический ряд, поэтому такая верхняя оценка недостаточна для\nконечности actual birth energy.\n\n## 4. Почему разбиение \\(P_S\\) на parity/residue не даёт оценки\n\nФормально\n\\[\n P_S=\n (P_S\\cap\\{n\\ {\\rm odd},\\,n\\equiv0\\})\n \\sqcup\n (P_S\\cap\\{n\\ {\\rm odd},\\,n\\equiv1\\})\n \\sqcup\n (P_S\\cap\\{n\\ {\\rm even},\\,n\\equiv0\\})\n \\sqcup\n (P_S\\cap\\{n\\ {\\rm even},\\,n\\equiv1\\})\n\\]\nгде сравнения остатков понимаются по модулю \\(3\\). Это разбиение точное,\nно для нечётных классов нет residue-only birth criterion. Супермножество\nможно применять как верхнюю оценку, однако оно расходится и потому\nтребует дополнительного сжатия. Для чётного birth \\(n=2s\\) BR и ancestry\nдают ограниченное weighted routing, записанное ниже; сам факт меньшего\nancestor без контроля кратности был бы недостаточен.\n\n## 5. Bounded verdict\n\nДля нечётного primitive \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) установлены точные факты:\n\n1. первый S-output отказавшейся маски дал бы birth ниже \\(n\\), поэтому\n   он исключается только при дополнительной минимальности \\(n\\);\n2. поздний S-отказ даёт лишь descent относительно текущего выхода;\n3. terminal even BR-bound удовлетворяет \\(B_h\\ge n+1\\), так что\n   uniform comparison с исходным \\(n\\) отсутствует;\n4. Разбиение само по себе не оценивает odd primitive часть \\(Q\\); even\n   singleton часть \\(V\\) допускает routing (17), но этого недостаточно для\n   контроля всего \\(P_S\\).\n\nСледовательно, этот primitive one-class audit локализует открытый барьер,\nно не даёт ни finite energy, ни универсального descent, ни результата о\nполном Коллатце.\n\n## 6. Исправление scope и точный split primitive source\n\nПредыдущее утверждение о супермножестве требует уточнения: любое\nарифметическое множество допустимо как верхняя оценка actual births.\nПроблема класса odd \\(1\\pmod3\\) в том, что эта верхняя оценка имеет\nположительную плотность и расходящуюся harmonic mass; она не даёт\nконечности без дополнительного сжатия. Actual color mask по-прежнему\nнельзя заменить равенством с этим супермножеством.\n\nПоложим \\(Q\\) равным odd births в \\(P_S\\), а \\(V\\) — even births в\n\\(P_S\\). Тогда \\(P_S=Q\\sqcup V\\). Все even births являются\n\\(0\\pmod4\\), поскольку \\(e(4m+2)=0\\). Именно even births из \\(V\\)\nимеют singleton-компоненты в S-графе: они не лежат в S-образе\n\\(2\\pmod3\\), а outgoing S-edge допускается только из нечётного источника.\nПоэтому разложение компонент даёт\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le\n 3E_Q(X)+E_V(X)+{7\\over9}E_\\beta(X/6).                 \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nДля \\(n=4m\\) even birth BR заряжает \\(n\\) к активной границе\n\\(k=6m+1\\), а её ближайший birth \\(b\\) удовлетворяет\n\\(b\\le3m=3n/4\\). Различные \\(n\\) дают различные \\(k\\), и\n\\[\n {1\\over n}={3\\over2k}+{3\\over2k(k-1)}.\n\\]\nНа каждой ancestry-глубине к одному \\(b\\) относятся не более двух\nграниц, поэтому первая сумма даёт не более \\(3E_\\beta(3X/4)\\).\nСумма ошибок по \\(k=6m+1\\) не превосходит \\(1/4\\), как в BR3. Так что\n\\[\n E_V(X)\\le3E_\\beta(3X/4)+{1\\over4}.                     \\tag{17}\n\\]\nПодстановка допустима: положительные копии \\(E_\\beta\\) справа являются\nверхними routing-оценками и не требуют дизъюнктности с \\(Q\\)-частью.\nСовместно (16)--(17):\n\\[\n E_\\beta(X)\\le\n 3E_Q(X)+3E_\\beta(3X/4)+{1\\over4}\n +{7\\over9}E_\\beta(X/6).                                \\tag{18}\n\\]\nЭто корректная верхняя рекурсия, но она не оценивает \\(E_Q\\), в частности\nне закрывает odd primitive class \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\). Кроме того, из неё\nнельзя заключить конечность \\(E_\\beta\\) по конечности \\(E_Q\\): при грубой\nзамене обеих положительных копий на \\(E_\\beta(X)\\) общий коэффициент\nравен \\(3+7/9=34/9>1\\). Никакой оценки\n\\(E_\\beta(3X/4)\\le(3/4)E_\\beta(X)\\) здесь нет.\n\nНаконец, при полном прохождении S-цепочки primitive start\n\\(n=2^hv-1\\) имеет terminal \\(N=3^hv-1\\), а следующий предыдущий узел\n\\(p=n_{h-1}=3^{h-1}2v-1\\) уже является actual birth, поскольку retained\nцепочка включает этот edge. Для terminal even \\(N\\) BR-граница имеет вид\n\\[\n K={3N\\over2}+1={3^{h+1}v-1\\over2}.\n\\]\nНаличие уже существующего birth \\(p\\) и эта граница не дают нового\nгарантированного birth ниже исходного primitive \\(n\\); это согласуется с\n\\(B_h\\ge n+1\\). Следовательно, terminal transfer не закрывает odd\n\\(1\\pmod3\\) source.\n\n## 7. Проверка дополнительного \\(R\\)-маршрута и смешанных циклов\n\nДля even birth \\(n=4m\\) положим\n\\[\n R(n)=3m={3n\\over4}.\n\\]\nИз \\(e(4m)=1\\) и invariance следуют\n\\[\n \\beta(3m+1)=\\beta(4m)=:c,\\qquad\n \\beta(6m+1)=1-c.\n\\]\nПоэтому\n\\[\n e(R(n))=1-d(R(n)).                                    \\tag{19}\n\\]\nПри \\(d(R(n))=0\\) \\(R(n)\\) — retained birth того же цвета и\n\\(R(n)\u003cn\\). При отказе \\(d(R(n))=1\\) активна граница \\(3m\\), а её\nближайший birth удовлетворяет\n\\[\n b\\le\\left\\lfloor {3m\\over2}\\right\\rfloor\n   =\\left\\lfloor {3n\\over8}\\right\\rfloor\u003cn.             \\tag{20}\n\\]\nЭто настоящая color-mask dichotomy, а не арифметическое утверждение.\nЛинейная карта \\(R\\) injective, и её образ лежит в \\(0\\pmod3\\), тогда как\nобраз \\(S\\) лежит в \\(2\\pmod3\\); следовательно, эти два входящих типа\nимеют непересекающиеся арифметические образы.\n\nЧистые \\(R\\)-цепочки конечны: на каждом retained edge источник делится\nна \\(4\\), а выход умножается на \\(3\\), поэтому\n\\(\\nu _2\\) источника уменьшается на \\(2\\). Если \\(a\\) — последний,\nнаименьший terminal в такой цепочке, её полный вес не превосходит\n\\[\n \\sum_{j\\ge0}{(3/4)^j\\over a}={4\\over a}.               \\tag{21}\n\\]\nЭто не даёт оценки по origin: terminal \\(a\\) может быть значительно\nменьше начала.\n\nRetained \\(S\\)- и \\(R\\)-рёбра сохраняют birth color, но это не исключает\nсмешанный цикл. \\(S\\)-только цепь строго растёт, \\(R\\)-только цепь\nконечна, однако чередование возрастающих \\(S\\)- и убывающих \\(R\\)-рёбер\nне покрывается этими двумя монотонными аргументами. Непересечение\nобразов даёт единственность типа входящего arithmetic edge, но не\nзапрещает возврат к origin. Поэтому нужен отдельный mixed-cycle\nаргумент; его здесь нет.\n\nДля ясности итоговый split имеет вид\n\\[\n P_S=Q_{\\rm odd}\\sqcup V_{\\rm even},\\qquad\n E_V(X)\\le3E_\\beta(3X/4)+{1\\over4},\n\\]\nи рекурсия (18) остаётся допустимой routing-оценкой. Она не является\nзамыканием на \\(Q_{\\rm odd}\\): even feedback сохраняется, а смешанные\nциклы и actual \\(Q_{\\rm odd}\\)-масса остаются открытыми.\n\n## 8. Аудит обратного coding lemma для combined \\(S/R\\)-графа\n\nДля retained edges объединённого графа обратный arithmetic map имеет вид\n\\[\n B(y)=\n \\begin{cases}\n  4y/3,&y\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\\n  (2y-1)/3,&y\\equiv2\\pmod3,\\\\\n  \\text{не определено},&y\\equiv1\\pmod3.\n \\end{cases}                                             \\tag{22}\n\\]\nПервая ветвь обращает \\(R(n)=3n/4\\), вторая — \\(S(q)=(3q+1)/2\\).\nОбе ветви сохраняют положительность на своих положительных классах;\nнулевой цикл к области положительных целей не относится.\n\nДля фиксированного слова длины \\(k\\) из двух ветвей каждая делимость\nпоследовательно задаёт ровно один класс начального \\(y\\) по модулю\n\\(3^k\\). Это следует индуктивно: после \\(j\\) шагов имеем affine\nчислитель с коэффициентом, являющимся произведением только \\(4\\) и\n\\(2\\), то есть единицей modulo \\(3\\); следующий branch guard задаёт\nединственный lift modulo \\(3^{j+1}\\). Каждый из \\(2^k\\) слов имеет\nположительные арифметические представители при достаточно большом\nначальном \\(y\\). Это arithmetic coding, без утверждения о retained\ncolor masks на каждом слове.\n\nСледовательно, число \\(k\\)-шаговых backward survivors в \\([1,X]\\)\nудовлетворяет\n\\[\n N_k(X)\\le 2^k\\left({X\\over3^k}+1\\right)\n =X(2/3)^k+2^k.                                          \\tag{23}\n\\]\nПоложим \\(\\gamma=\\log_3 2\u003c1\\) и\n\\(k=\\lfloor\\log_3X\\rfloor\\). Если \\(X=3^k t\\), \\(1\\le t\u003c3\\), то правая\nчасть (23) равна \\((t+1)2^k\\), а \\(X^\\gamma=t^\\gamma2^k\\). На этом\nинтервале \\((t+1)/t^\\gamma\\le2\\), и, в частности, безопасно\n\\[\n N_\\Delta(X)\\le {5\\over2}X^\\gamma,                      \\tag{24}\n\\]\nгде \\(\\Delta\\) — цели, допускающие backward paths всех конечных длин.\nДаже более грубая константа была бы достаточна.\n\nЧастичное summation из (24) даёт\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\in\\Delta}{1\\over n}\n \\le {5\\over2}\\int_1^\\infty t^{\\gamma-2}\\,dt\n = {5\\over2(1-\\gamma)}.                                 \\tag{25}\n\\]\nЭто суммируемая backward core. Она не доказывает termination: возможный\nfinite mixed \\(S/R\\)-cycle или backward-infinite chain может целиком лежать в \\(\\Delta\\).\nНа forward edges стрелки действительно обращаются \\(B\\): \\(B(S(q))=q\\)\nи \\(B(R(n))=n\\), когда соответствующие retained edges существуют.\nCoding counts arithmetic branch words and does not replace the color\nmasks or prove that every coded word is an actual retained path.\n\nThus (22)--(25) supply a valid harmonic bound for the infinite-backward\narithmetic core, while the actual primitive-source estimate and\nmixed-cycle exclusion remain open.\n\n## 9. Inverse-origin lemma для \\(R\\) и combined-origin split\n\nПусть \\(n=3m\\) — birth цвета \\(c\\), а \\(q=4m\\) — его единственный\nарифметический \\(R\\)-предшественник. Из рождения \\(n\\) и invariance\nполучаем\n\\[\n \\beta(3m+1)=c,\\qquad \\beta(6m+1)=1-c.\n\\]\nСледовательно,\n\\[\n e(q)=1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\beta(m)=c.         \\tag{27}\n\\]\nЕсли \\(m\\) нечётно, то \\(\\beta(m)=\\beta(3m+1)=c\\) автоматически, поэтому\n\\(q\\) — birth и \\(n\\) не является \\(R\\)-origin. При inverse failure \\(m\\)\nчётно; поскольку \\(n=3m\\) — even birth, \\(e(4r+2)=0\\) даёт \\(4\\mid m\\).\n\nПишем \\(m=2^h u\\), где \\(h\\ge2\\), \\(u\\) нечётно, и \\(k=3u\\). Тогда\n\\(n=2^h k\\), \\(k\\) нечётно, а failure означает\n\\(\\beta(u)\\ne c=\\beta(k)\\). Так как\n\\(\\beta(k+1)=\\beta(3u+1)=\\beta(u)\\), имеем\n\\[\n d(k)=1.\n\\]\nЗначение \\(d(k+1)\\) из этих данных не определяется и далее не используется.\nИспользуя boundary \\(k\\), ближайший birth \\(b\\)\nудовлетворяет\n\\[\n b\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\n   \u003c {n\\over2^{h+1}}\\le {n\\over8}.                      \\tag{28}\n\\]\nЭто исправляет возможную off-by-one ошибку при использовании только\nграницы \\(k+1\\).\n\nДля фиксированного нечётного \\(k\\) возможные origin-цели \\(2^h k\\),\n\\(h\\ge2\\), не могут иметь соседние \\(h\\): из \\(e(2^h k)=1\\) следует\n\\(e(2^{h+1}k)=0\\). Поэтому\n\\[\n \\sum_{\\substack{h\\ge2\\\\2^h k\\ {\\rm origin}}}{1\\over2^h k}\n \\le {1\\over3k}.                                        \\tag{29}\n\\]\nНа двух активных ancestry-ветвях одного birth \\(b\\) нечётные charged\n\\(k\\) дают не более \\(1/b\\) на правой ветви (глубина \\(\\ge1\\)) и\n\\(1/(2b)\\) на левой (первый левый child чётен, так что нечётный boundary\nначинается с глубины \\(\\ge2\\)). Умножая на (29), получаем\n\\[\n E_{D_R}(X)\\le {1\\over2}E_\\beta(X/8),                   \\tag{30}\n\\]\nгде \\(D_R\\) — \\(R\\)-origins, а cutoff (28) обеспечивает \\(b\u003cX/8\\).\nЭто bound только на origin energy.\n\nДля combined graph \\(F=S\\sqcup R\\) входящие арифметические образы имеют\nостатки \\(2\\pmod3\\) и \\(0\\pmod3\\). Поэтому origins раскладываются на\nbirths \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), S-inverse failures в остатке \\(2\\), и\nR-inverse failures в остатке \\(0\\). С предыдущей S-charge оценкой\nполучается\n\\[\n E_{O_F}(X)\\le E_{P_1}(X)\n +{4\\over9}E_\\beta(X/6)\n +{1\\over2}E_\\beta(X/8),                                \\tag{31}\n\\]\nгде \\(P_1=\\{n:e(n)=1,\\ n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\}\\). Формула (31) не является\nоценкой полной component energy: retained components могут иметь\nдальнейшие S/R-вершины, а mixed cycles не исключены. Она также не\nзамыкает \\(P_1\\); это точный bounded-origin split с сохранением actual\ncolor masks.\n\n## 10. Арифметический \\(F\\)-барьер для uniform component weights\n\nБез утверждения о реализации birth-масок существует следующая точная\nсемья arithmetic combined components. Пусть \\(L\\ge1\\), \\(v>0\\) нечётно,\n\\[\n v\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\qquad 3^L v\\equiv1\\pmod8,\n\\]\nи положим \\(a=4^L v\\). Эквивалентно,\n\\(v\\equiv1\\pmod{24}\\) при чётном \\(L\\) и\n\\(v\\equiv19\\pmod{24}\\) при нечётном \\(L\\). Тогда \\(a\\equiv1\\pmod3\\),\nпоэтому \\(a\\) не имеет арифметического входящего \\(S\\)- или \\(R\\)-ребра.\n\nАрифметические \\(R\\)-шаги дают\n\\[\n a\\to {3a\\over4}\\to\\cdots\\to R^L(a)=y=3^L v.\n\\]\nЧисло \\(y\\) нечётно и \\(y\\equiv1\\pmod8\\), поэтому следующий\nарифметический шаг \\(S\\) даёт\n\\[\n y\\to z=S(y)={3y+1\\over2}\\equiv2\\pmod4.\n\\]\nЧисло \\(z\\) уже не является источником ни \\(R\\), ни \\(S\\), так что это\nконечная arithmetic \\(F\\)-компонента. Её вес \\(W\\) удовлетворяет уже по\nодной вершине \\(y\\)\n\\[\n a\\,W\\ge {a\\over y}=\\left({4\\over3}\\right)^L.            \\tag{32}\n\\]\nСледовательно, на арифметическом супермножестве не существует uniform\nоценки \\(W\\le C/a\\) с фиксированным \\(C\\). Это не противоречит actual\nмаскам: \\(y\\equiv1\\pmod8\\) имеет \\(e(y)=0\\), так что \\(y\\) не может быть\nactual birth и последний \\(S\\)-шаг (а фактически уже соответствующее\nretention) отсекается. Семья лишь показывает, почему unmasked arithmetic\ncomponents нельзя автоматически использовать для bounded actual\ncomponent weights.\n\n## 11. Полная сверка root SR1--SR12\n\nПолностью прочитан BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md. Существенных\nарифметических ошибок в SR1--SR12 не найдено.\n\nSR1--SR3 корректно устанавливают XOR-идентичности, две активные\nancestry-ветви, injective partial graph \\(F=S\\sqcup R\\), непересекающиеся\nобразы \\(2\\pmod3\\) и \\(0\\pmod3\\), а также обратную карту \\(B\\).\nSR4 верно считает слово длины \\(k\\): слово с \\(s\\) буквами \\(S\\) и\n\\(r\\) буквами \\(R\\) имеет modulus \\(2^{s+2r}\\), а суммарная масса\nостаточных классов даёт\n\\(X(1/2+1/4)^k+2^k=X(3/4)^k+2^k\\). Граница\n\\(N_{\\Gamma_+}(X)\\le(7/3)X^\\alpha\\) с\n\\(\\alpha=\\log_{8/3}2\\) согласуется с floor-cutoff.\n\nSR5 использует тот же корректный lift-count для \\(B\\): каждое слово\nдлины \\(k\\) даёт один класс modulo \\(3^k\\), а\n\\(N_{\\Gamma_-}(X)\\le(5/2)X^\\gamma\\) — безопасная граница. SR6 правильно\nвключает finite cycles и все непериодические non-finite components в\n\\(\\Gamma_+\\cup\\Gamma_-\\); из этого следует только\n\\[\n E_{\\rm fin}(X)\\le E_\\beta(X)\\le E_{\\rm fin}(X)+C_++C_-.\n\\]\nКонечность полной энергии и отсутствие циклов не следуют.\n\nSR7--SR8 корректно используют только \\(d(k)=1\\) для inverse-\\(R\\)\nfailure; SR7--SR8 дают cutoff \\(b\u003cX/8\\) и\n\\(E_{D_R}(X)\\le\\frac12E_\\beta(X/8)\\). В SR9 coefficient \\(17/18\\)\nотносится исключительно к origin energy \\(E_{O_F}\\), не к полной массе\nкомпонентов. SR10 правильно показывает arithmetic obstruction\n\\(aW\\ge(4/3)^L\\), одновременно учитывая \\(e(y)=0\\) для actual masks.\n\nИсправлены ссылки в текущем отчёте: после (29) используются (29) и (28),\nа singleton-утверждение ограничено \\(V\\subset P_S\\). Общий bounded\ncomponent-energy estimate, mixed-cycle exclusion и контроль primitive\n\\(P_1\\) остаются открытыми.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_SIGNED_BALANCE_AND_TELESCOPING.md":"# Signed balance: path estimates and a limited potential obstruction\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual bounded analysis\nfollowing the AC channel identities. No programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean,\nor new agents. The goal is to test whether a state-only potential removes\nthe endpoint terms; no nonconstant invariant or Collatz conclusion is claimed.\n\n## 1. The four-state transition graph\n\nThe nonzero states are E=(0,1,1), L=(1,1,0), and R=(1,0,1), with\nthe zero state invisible to the birth/channel masses. The conserved channel\nflags are e+L under S and e+Rb under R. Thus the only changes relevant to\nthe endpoint terms are\n\n    S: E -> L (Z_S),   L -> E (D_S),\n    R: E -> Rb (Z_R),  Rb -> E (D_R).\n\nThe same-state transitions contribute no state-potential difference. This\nis an exact statement about the actual masks; it does not assert that all\nfour displayed transitions occur.\n\nFor a state-only potential with values phi_E, phi_L, phi_Rb, the unscaled\ntransition-count contribution (before harmonic transport), when source and\ntarget transition counts are matched edgewise, is therefore\n\n    (phi_L-phi_E)(C_ZS-C_DS)\n      +(phi_Rb-phi_E)(C_ZR-C_DR).                    (SB1)\n\nHere C denotes unweighted matched edge counts, not the harmonic masses\ndenoted by Z and D elsewhere in the report.\n\nThis gives a necessary condition for coefficientwise elimination on the\nrelaxed transition table: eliminating every endpoint coefficient requires\n\n    phi_E=phi_L=phi_Rb.                              (SB2)\n\nThis ansatz does not analyze the zero-state value or additional arithmetic\nconstraints. No impossibility result for those broader potentials is claimed.\n\n## 2. Dirichlet slopes do not create a hidden cancellation\n\nAt the critical harmonic exponent, S transports source weight with factor\n2/3 and R with factor 4/3, up to the uniformly summable affine correction\nfrom 3n+1. Equal state values merely make the unscaled flip differences\nzero; this fact alone supplies no new harmonic identity. The separately\nproved AC channel and residue calculation gives\n\n    E=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1),                       (SB3)\n\nwhich is AC14 itself. No general classification of harmonic potentials is\nproved by the unscaled count argument.\n\nAllowing unequal state values does not yield a coefficientwise endpoint-free\nidentity on this relaxed table: it only replaces (SB1) by a different signed\ncombination of the same four unknown classes. No positivity relation between\nZ_S and D_S, or between Z_R and D_R, is available from the transition table\nalone.\n\nThere is also a direct contraction obstruction on the R pair. If a positive\npotential were required to contract every R transition by a common factor\nrho, the two possible flips would require, ignoring the bounded affine\nerror,\n\n    (4/3) phi_Rb/phi_E \u003c= rho,\n    (4/3) phi_E/phi_Rb \u003c= rho.\n\nTheir product is (4/3)^2, so rho>=4/3. In particular no rho\u003c1 state-only\nsuperharmonic potential can meet these two explicit edgewise inequalities.\nThis assumes both relaxed orientations must be covered; restrictions from\nactual color or arithmetic data require a separate analysis.\n\n## 3. Relation to the finite-cutoff identities\n\nAt finite cutoff, affine reindexing contributes only O(1), uniformly in the\ncutoff. A linear combination of the two conserved channel equations and the\nbinary residue equations gives AC12--AC14. Within this coefficientwise\nstate-only ansatz, telescoping has the same transition table and leaves the\nendpoint differences (SB1); an assertion about every possible state-\ndependent or color-sensitive potential would require a separate proof. No\nThe older statement that no improvement of AC14 follows is superseded by\n§5 below: the residue-sensitive S-potential gives a one-class bound, although\nit still does not bound that remaining class.\n\nThe finite identity still has a useful consequence: if Z_R and D_S have\nfinite harmonic mass then E is finite, and if E diverges then\n4Z_R+3D_S must have comparable mass. This is a conditional positive-charge\nrequirement, not a contraction. It excludes the endpoint/origin-negligible\nPC12 regime, but it does not establish finiteness of either positive class.\n\n## 4a. A residue potential for the signed odd-path reward\n\nThere is a useful refinement after the negative even-(L_2) term has been\nsubtracted.  Write (i=q mod 3) for an odd active boundary and let (A)\ndenote the next boundary (2q+1), while (B) denotes (4q+1).  The residue\nmaps are\n\n    A:i -> 2i+1,       B:i -> i+1       (mod 3).\n\nFor the signed reward produced by the corresponding channel bookkeeping take\n\n    r_A=(3,0,0),       r_B=(7/2,2,-1),\n\nin residue order (0,1,2).  The negative entry for (B) at residue (2) is\npart of the signed reward; it is not discarded.  The nonnegative potential\n\n    P=(P_0,P_1,P_2)=(4,2,0)\n\nsatisfies all six Bellman equalities\n\n    P_i=r_A(i)+(1/2)P_{2i+1},\n    P_i=r_B(i)+(1/4)P_{i+1}.                         (SB4)\n\nIndeed the (A)-rows are (4=3+2/2), (2=0+4/2), and (0=0+0/2); the\n(B)-rows are (4=7/2+2/4), (2=2+0/4), and (0=-1+4/4).\n\nFor a finite path (q_{j+1}=a_jq_j+1), (a_jin{2,4}), (SB4) gives\n\n    r_j/q_j=P_{i_j}/q_j-P_{i_{j+1}}/(a_jq_j)\n            \u003c=P_{i_j}/q_j-P_{i_{j+1}}/q_{j+1},\n\nbecause (q_{j+1}>a_jq_j) and (Pgeq0).  Thus the signed sum is at most\n(P_{i_0}/q_0); the terminal potential is simply discarded with the correct\nsign.  This remains valid when a (B)-step has reward (-1), and it also\napplies to finite prefixes of a path that later terminates or switches.  It\ndoes not assert that the signed reward equals a nonnegative birth mass.\n\nFor completeness, the first odd boundary on the right of a birth (b) is\n(q=2b+1).  The potential bounds, up to harmless finite-cutoff (O(1)), are\n\n    b mod 3       0       1       2\n    right         1/b     2/b     0.\n\nOn the left the first odd boundary is (q=2^\\ell b+1), with\n\\(\\ell\\ge2\\).  All later left boundaries are covered by the same potential\nbound.  The corresponding bounds are\n\n    b mod 3       0       1       2\n    left          1/(2b)  1/(2b)  1/b.\n\nFor residue (1), the even (ell) case has potential (0), and odd\n(ell\\ge3) has residue (0) with (q\\ge8b); for residue (2), odd\n(ell) has potential (0), while even (ell) has (q\\ge4b).  Therefore\nthe combined signed path reward is bounded by\n\n    (3/2) E_0 + (5/2) E_1 + E_2 + O(1),              (SB5)\n\nwhere (E_i) is the harmonic birth mass in residue (i\\pmod3).  Under the\nformal PC12 residue proportions this is at most (4E/3+O(1)).  This is a\nvalid signed upper bound including every possible initial left depth; it is\nnot a contraction, and no finite-energy conclusion follows from (SB5).\n\n## 5. A sharper residue potential for the S-signed balance\n\nThe preceding potential was for a larger signed reward. For the particular\ncombination\n\n    H := 3D_S-2Z_S,\n\nthe edge reward after the negative even-L2 contribution is subtracted is\nsmaller. Using the established actual-mask path decomposition of this H (so\nthe following is not a free-color relaxation), on an odd boundary of residue\ni=q mod 3, the A-edge has reward\nr_A(i)=0 for all i, while the B-edge has r_B=(2,2,-1).\n\nThe nonnegative vector Q=(5/2,2,0) satisfies the six Bellman inequalities\n\n    Q_i >= r_A(i)+(1/2)Q_(2i+1),\n    Q_i >= r_B(i)+(1/4)Q_(i+1).                       (SB6)\n\nThe rows are 5/2>=1 and 5/2=2+2/4 at i=0; 2>=5/4 and\n2=2+0/4 at i=1; and 0>=0 and 0>=-1+(5/2)/4=-3/8 at i=2.\n\nFor any finite A/B path, the same q_(j+1)=a_j q_j+1>a_j q_j argument as in\n§4a gives\n\n    sum_j r_j/q_j \u003c= Q_(i_0)/q_0.                    (SB7)\n\nThis remains valid for a signed prefix: the negative B-reward is retained,\nand the nonnegative terminal potential is discarded only with the favorable\nsign. At a finite cutoff, truncate each path at its last represented odd\nboundary; omitting a subsequent negative term can only increase the upper\nbound. Thus no infinite-path or finite-energy assumption is hidden here.\n\nFor a right path from a birth b, q_0=2b+1, so the coefficients of 1/b for\nb mod 3=0,1,2 are respectively 1, 5/4, 0. For a left path the first odd\nboundary is q_0=2^ell b+1, ell>=2. The pre-first-odd terms with\n2^j b congruent to 2 mod 3 have reward -3 and therefore only improve the\nupper bound. If b congruent to 0, the first-potential bound is\nQ_1/(4b)=1/(2b). If b congruent to 1, an even ell starts in residue 2 with\nzero potential; an odd ell>=3 starts in residue 0 with q_0>=8b, while the\npreceding term -3/(2b) dominates Q_0/(8b). Hence the bound is 0. If b\ncongruent to 2, the worst case is even ell=2, giving Q_0/(4b)=5/(8b);\nlater negative pre-terms only lower it. Consequently\n\n    H(X) \u003c= (3/2)E_0(X/2)+(5/4)E_1(X/2)+(5/8)E_2(X/2)+O(1).  (SB9)\n\nThe root path inequality for K has no error; the O(1) is needed only for\nthe channel transport H=K+O(1). Since all E_i are nonnegative, replacing X/2 by X is legitimate.\nNow AC12--AC14 give, at the same cutoff,\n\n    E_0=4Z_R-3D_R-2P+O(1),\n    E_2=P-2Z_S+3D_S+O(1),\n\nwhere P=E_1. Substituting these in (SB9), and using H=3D_S-2Z_S, gives\n\n    3D_S-2Z_S \u003c= 16Z_R-12D_R-3P+O(1).               (SB10)\n\nFinally AC14 yields the sharper one-sided energy bound\n\n    E \u003c= 20Z_R-15D_R-3P+O(1) \u003c= 20Z_R+O(1).          (SB11)\n\nThis is a genuine finite-cutoff consequence of the signed S-channel\npotential, rather than a contraction of the full state system. In particular,\nfinite harmonic mass of Z_R is sufficient for finite birth energy; AC16 then\nalso gives finite D_S. No claim is made here that Z_R itself is finite, or\nthat this closes the Collatz argument.\n\n## 6. Verdict\n\nNo endpoint-free signed contraction is obtained from this coefficientwise\ncount ansatz. Equal state weights make the unscaled flip differences zero;\nunequal weights preserve those differences, and the R flip pair rules out a uniform\nfactor below one for a positive potential covering both orientations. Section\n5 nevertheless proves the sharper criterion that total birth energy is finite\niff the single remaining terminal class Z_R has finite harmonic mass. This is\na sharp limitation of the tested ansatz, not a prohibition on all\ncolor-sensitive potentials; it still does not prove finiteness of Z_R.\n\n## 7. Final audit of root SB1--SB18\n\nI read the complete root file\n`SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md`. The finite signed\nprefix estimate is sound: for q'=a q+1, a in {2,4}, nonnegative terminal\npotential gives the stated inequality even when the edge reward is negative.\nTruncating at the last represented odd boundary therefore does not hide an\ninfinite-energy assumption.\n\nThis report's section1 formula SB1 is a count-level, unscaled transition\ncontribution; the root file's SB1 instead lists the inherited harmonic\nidentities. These label systems must not be confused. The H=K+O(1) transport has the\ncorrect scope. The depth split in SB6--SB8 includes all left depths and the\nnegative even-prefix terms.\n\nThe algebra in SB9--SB10 checks directly:\n\n    E_0=M-2P+O(1),   E_2=P+H+O(1),   E=M+H+O(1),\n\nwith M=4Z_R-3D_R. Substitution into the SB8 upper bound yields\n\n    H \u003c= 4M-3P+O(1),\n    E \u003c= 20Z_R-15D_R-3P+O(1),\n\nso the one-class criterion in root SB11 and section 5 here is valid. It is a\none-sided reduction, not a proof that Z_R has finite mass.\n\nThe R-column masks SB12--SB14 are consistent with e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1):\nwhen e(y)=1, both child differences imply beta(y+1)=beta(q); when\nRb(y)=1, the corresponding target is the terminal case exactly when\nd(q)=1. The a_h telescoping and a_0=1, a_2=0 scope are correct. The\ntop-column cutoff error is uniformly bounded because the top indices are\ndistinct in (X/2,X].\n\nThe weights in SB15--SB17 also check: a target 4(2^h q)/3 has weight\n3/(4*2^h q), giving the factors 3/8, 7/8, then 1/8, 7/24, 35/6, and 8\nexactly. SB18 is an exact signed identity: with v=(9n+1)/2,\nv=T(3n), v+1=3T(n), and invariance gives\ng(Sn)-g(n)=beta(v+1)-beta(v). It supplies no summability by itself.\n\nThe remaining limitation is therefore precisely finiteness of the single\nmultiplicative-defect class Z_R (equivalently G under SB15--SB17), not a\nmissing second endpoint estimate and not a blanket no-go for color-sensitive\npotentials.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_STRUCTURAL_LIFT_ARITHMETIC.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — genuine structural ordered lifts\n\n18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручной аудит `FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md`\nи вопроса, являются ли все структурные подъёмы степенями\n\\(L(n)=(4n-1)/3\\). Математических программ, переборов, CAS и Lean не\nиспользовалось. Ниже рассматриваются только настоящие shortcut parity words;\nформальная аффинная целость сама по себе не подменяет admissibility и\nположительность.\n\n## 1. Общая форма структурного правила\n\nПусть \\(w\\) — shortcut-слово длины \\(K\\), с \\(P\\) нечётными входами.\nЕго точная аффинная форма имеет вид\n\\[\n T^K(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C_w}{2^K},\n\\]\nгде \\(C_w\\) — целое, определённое словом. Для положительного источника\nостаток \\(x\\pmod {2^K}\\) фиксирован самим словом; обратная формула\n\\[\n x=\\frac{2^K n-C_w}{3^P}\n\\]\nвместе с целостью и положительностью восстанавливает этот parity cylinder.\nДополнительное условие first-hit остаётся отдельным условием.\n\nПусть два слова \\(w_L,w_R\\) структурно переводят \\((n,n+1)\\) в\nсоседние источники \\((x,x+1)\\) на целой арифметической прогрессии целей.\nСравнение коэффициентов при \\(n\\) даёт\n\\[\n K_L=K_R=:K,\\qquad P_L=P_R=:P,\n\\]\nа сравнение свободных членов даёт точное условие\n\\[\n C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P.                                      \\tag{SL1}\n\\]\nОбратно, (SL1) вместе с admissibility левого слова даёт правое равенство:\n\\[\n \\frac{2^K(n+1)-C_R}{3^P}\n =\\frac{2^Kn-C_L}{3^P}+1.\n\\]\nЦели имеют прогрессию\n\\[\n n\\equiv 2^{-K}C_L\\pmod {3^P},                       \\tag{SL2}\n\\]\nи positivity требует положительности двух числителей. Поэтому равенство\nсвободных членов — точный критерий структурного gap 1 на этой прогрессии,\nно не утверждение first-hit.\n\nПри \\(P=0\\) оба слова состоят только из удвоений, их константы нулевые,\nа разность источников равна \\(2^K\\). Следовательно, gap 1 остаётся только\nпри пустом слове \\(K=0\\), то есть в тождественном случае. Каждый\nнетождественный структурный ordered lift имеет \\(P\\ge1\\).\n\n## 2. Ручной genuine контрпример гипотезе «все степени L»\n\nРассмотрим источники \\(32m+3\\) и \\(32m+4\\), \\(m\\ge0\\). Их первые пять\nshortcut-битов и состояния таковы:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccc|c}\n x&1&2&3&4&5&T^5(x)\\\\ \\hline\n 32m+3&O&O&E&E&E&9m+1\\\\\n 32m+4&E&E&O&E&O&9m+2.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nДействительно,\n\\[\n 32m+3\\to48m+5\\to72m+8\\to36m+4\\to18m+2\\to9m+1,\n\\]\n\\[\n 32m+4\\to16m+2\\to8m+1\\to12m+2\\to6m+1\\to9m+2.\n\\]\nОба слова имеют \\(K=5,P=2\\), а их forward constants равны соответственно\n\\(C_L=5\\) и \\(C_R=28\\). Поэтому\n\\[\n C_R-C_L=23=32-9,\n\\qquad\n M(n):=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\\quad n=9m+1,\n\\]\nа правая inverse map имеет отдельную константу:\n\\[\n G(n+1):=\\frac{32(n+1)-28}{9}=M(n)+1.\n\\]\nВсе состояния положительны при \\(m\\ge0\\), так что это genuine\nstructural ordered lift, а не только формальная аффинная запись. Он не\nявляется степенью \\(L\\): \\(L^j\\) has \\((K,P)=(2j,j)\\), тогда как здесь\n\\((K,P)=(5,2)\\). При \\(m=0\\) это подъём \\((1,2)\\leftarrow(3,4)\\);\nнаоборот, правило \\(M\\) из цели \\((3,4)\\) неприменимо, поскольку\n\\(3\\not\\equiv1\\pmod9\\). Это также показывает, что нельзя считать\nструктурные правила единственной цепочкой степеней одного lift.\n\n## 3. Финальные биты и точный критерий по модулю 3\n\nПусть слово содержит хотя бы один \\(O\\), и пусть \\(e\\) — число конечных\n\\(E\\)-битов после последнего \\(O\\). Если последний \\(O\\) стоит на позиции\n\\(q=K-1-e\\), то в константе \\(C_w\\) все слагаемые от более ранних\nнечётных шагов кратны 3, а слагаемое последнего \\(O\\) даёт\n\\[\n C_w\\equiv2^q\\pmod3.\n\\]\nИз (SL2) следует\n\\[\n n\\equiv2^{-K}C_w=2^{-(e+1)}=2^{e+1}\\pmod3.       \\tag{SL3}\n\\]\nТаким образом, для левого слова соседней пары:\n\\[\n e\\text{ нечётно}\\Rightarrow n\\equiv1\\pmod3,\n \\qquad\n e\\text{ чётно}\\Rightarrow n\\equiv2\\pmod3.\n\\]\n\nОднако цель слова с хотя бы одним нечётным входом никогда не делится на 3:\nнечётный shortcut-шаг даёт \\((3x+1)/2\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), а последующие\nделения на 2 сохраняют ненулевость modulo 3. Поэтому в нетождественной\nordered паре обе цели \\(n,n+1\\) не делятся на 3. Необходимый и достаточный\nдля этой очевидной фильтрации вариант —\n\\[\n n\\equiv1\\pmod3,\n\\]\nа затем (SL3) уточняет финальные биты: у левого слова \\(e_L\\) нечётно\n(значит, оно оканчивается как \\(O E^{\\,e_L}\\) с нечётным положительным\n\\(e_L\\)); у правого \\(e_R\\) чётно (нулевое значение означает финальный\nбит \\(O\\), положительное — чётное число конечных \\(E\\)). В примере выше\n\\(e_L=3,e_R=0\\). Это ограничение необходимо для любого genuine правила,\nно не классифицирует внутренние биты.\n\n## 4. Ветвление из одного target\n\nИз (SL1) следует точная арифметическая проверка каждой ветви, но не\nединственность ветви. Уже цель \\((1,2)\\) имеет две различные genuine\nструктурные реализации:\n\n* слово-пара \\(OE,EO\\), то есть lift \\(L(1)=1,L(1)+1=2\\);\n* слово-пара \\(OOEEE,EEOEO\\), то есть lift \\(M(1)=3,M(1)+1=4\\).\n\nЭто ручной контрпример любой гипотезе «из одного target бывает не более\nодного ordered lift» и одновременно контрпример гипотезе «все lifts —\nстепени \\(L\\)». При фиксированных \\(K,P\\) число возможных левых слов\nне превосходит \\(\\binom KP\\), а для каждого слова \\(C_R\\) уже однозначно\nзадан (SL1). Более сильная равномерная bound на число admissible ветвей\nиз одного target здесь не выведена; positivity и first-hit могут это число\nуменьшить.\n\n## 5. Почему FL-предел не покрывает variable switching\n\nFL2–FL3 корректно применяются, когда одно и то же правило повторяется или\nправила следуют по фиксированному конечному периоду. Для произвольной\nпоследовательности из конечного набора \\(F_i(n)=(2^{K_i}n-C_i)/3^{P_i}\\)\nкаждый конечный word-композит снова имеет аффинную форму, но его\n\\((K,C,P)\\) зависят от всего слова. Нельзя подставить такой меняющийся\nкомпозит в FL2 как один фиксированный self-map: FL2 оценивает повторение\nодного и того же композита, а не непериодическую цепочку различных\nкомпозитов.\n\nСледовательно, конечный набор переменных правил **не исключён** FL\nарифметикой: switching может обходить именно периодический предел. Это\nне утверждение, что такой бесконечный положительный admissible путь\nсуществует. Точный оставшийся объект — ориентированный граф на целях,\nгде ребро разрешено только при (SL2), positivity, parity cylinder и\nfirst-hit. Если все цели на допустимом пути ограничены, получается конечный\nориентированный граф. Он допускает и непериодический обход уже посещённых\nсостояний, так что периодичность actual switching отсюда не следует.\nОднако множество различных целей тогда конечно; каждый цикл имеет\nфиксированное периодическое слово, и FL3 показывает, что такой цикл не\nсоздаёт новых источников. Поэтому бесконечная схема с бесконечным числом\nновых источников обязана иметь неограниченные цели. Это лишь точный scope\nFL, не global finiteness theorem.\n\n## 6. Verdict\n\nГипотеза «все genuine structural ordered gap-1 rules — степени\n\\(L\\)» неверна: explicit family \\(M(n)=(32n-5)/9\\) имеет words\n\\(OOEEE/EEOEO\\), \\((K,P)=(5,2)\\), и работает на всей прогрессии\n\\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\). Общая классификация задаётся равенством (SL1),\nцелостью (SL2), положительностью и отдельными first-hit условиями.\nЛюбое нетождественное правило вынуждено иметь \\(P\\ge1\\) и target\n\\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\); финальные E-хвосты имеют противоположную parity,\nкак в §3.\n\nFL2–FL3 сами по себе остаются ограничением fixed rules и fixed periodic\nschedules и не контролируют произвольное switching общего конечного\nнабора. Ниже, в §§7–9, дополнительный остаточный анализ доказывает\nлогарифмический счёт источников для полностью классифицированного\nнабора с P≤2. Возможность бесконечной разреженной цепочки и общий\nслучай P≥3 этим не решены. Root уточнил эту границу после дополнений.\n\n## 7. Отдельная проверка алфавита \\(\\{L,M\\}\\)\n\nДля двух конкретных genuine правил\n\\[\n L(n)=\\frac{4n-1}{3},\\qquad M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9}=2L^2(n)+1\n\\]\nобласти определения левых целей равны соответственно\n\\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) и \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\). Оба правила строго увеличивают\nчисло при \\(n>1\\); при этом\n\\[\n L(n)-1=\\frac43(n-1),\\qquad\n M(n)-1=\\frac{32(n-1)+18}{9}>\\frac43(n-1).       \\tag{SL4}\n\\]\n\nПолная ручная проверка остатков даёт:\n\n* если \\(n\\equiv4\\pmod9\\), единственный возможный шаг \\(L\\) переводит в\n  число \\(\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), то есть дальше шагов нет;\n* если \\(n\\equiv7\\pmod9\\), единственный \\(L\\) переводит в число\n  \\(\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), и дальше шагов нет;\n* если \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod{27}\\), то \\(M(n)\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), а\n  \\(L(n)\\equiv1\\pmod9\\): только ветвь \\(L\\) может иметь продолжение;\n* если \\(n\\equiv10\\pmod{27}\\), то \\(M(n)\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), а два\n  последовательных шага \\(L\\) заканчиваются числом \\(\\equiv2\\pmod3\\):\n  ни одна ветвь не имеет бесконечного продолжения;\n* если \\(n\\equiv19\\pmod{27}\\), то два шага \\(L\\) заканчиваются числом\n  \\(\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), тогда как \\(M(n)\\equiv1\\pmod3\\): только ветвь\n  \\(M\\) может иметь продолжение.\n\nСледовательно, из каждой вершины есть не более одной ветви, которая может\nиметь глубину больше двух; это не утверждает, что actual switching обязан\nбыть периодическим. Все остальные дочерние поддеревья имеют глубину не\nболее двух. Поэтому дерево достижимых целей из фиксированного \\(n_0>1\\)\nимеет одну spine-ветвь и лишь bounded боковые поддеревья. По (SL4) число\nшагов spine до уровня \\(X\\) удовлетворяет\n\\[\n H_{n_0}(X)\\le\n \\left\\lfloor\\log_{4/3}\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}\\right\\rfloor+1.\n\\]\nС двумя возможными рёбрами bounded боковые поддеревья дают абсолютную\nконстанту узлов на каждой spine-вершине (например, грубая оценка\n\\(7(H_{n_0}(X)+1)\\)). Таким образом,\n\\[\n \\#\\{\\text{различных достижимых целей }\\le X\\}=O_{n_0}(\\log X).\n\\]\nДля seed \\(n_0=1\\) имеем \\(L(1)=1\\), \\(M(1)=3\\), а у 3 нет следующего\nшага; цикл \\(L\\) и переход \\(M\\to3\\) дают только конечное множество\n\\(\\{1,3\\}\\). Этот вывод относится именно к алфавиту \\(\\{L,M\\}\\), не к\nпроизвольному конечному набору structural lifts.\n\n## 8. Ручная классификация всех ordered lifts с \\(P\\le2\\)\n\nДля слова с одним \\(O\\), стоящим на позиции \\(i\\), имеем\n\\(C=2^i\\), \\(0\\le i\u003cK\\). При \\(P=1\\) условие (SL1) требует\n\\[\n 2^j-2^i=2^K-3.\n\\]\nПри \\(K=1\\) единственное значение \\(C=1\\), а требуемая правая константа\nравна нулю, что не является словом с одним \\(O\\). Для \\(K\\ge2\\) правая\nчасть нечётна и положительна, поэтому одна из \\(i,j\\) равна нулю и\nвозможен только \\(i=0\\), \\(j\\ge1\\). Тогда\n\\(2^j=2^K-2\\), что возможно лишь при \\(K=2,j=1\\). Это ровно \\(L\n\\) (слова \\(OE,EO\\)); других ordered lifts с \\(P=1\\) нет.\n\nПри \\(P=2\\), если нечётные позиции \\(i\u003cj\\), то\n\\[\n C=3\\,2^i+2^j.                                  \\tag{SL5}\n\\]\nПри \\(K=2\\) единственное значение \\(C=5\\), а при \\(K=3\n\\) значения \\(5,7,10\\); проверка разности \\(2^K-9\\) не даёт пары.\nПусть теперь \\(K\\ge4\\), так что требуемая разность положительна.\n\nЕсли \\(C_L\\) нечётно, то \\(i_L=0\\), и \\(C_L=3+2^j\\). При \\(j=1\n\\) имеем \\(C_R=2^K-4\\), его точный порядок 2 заставляет \\(i_R=2\\),\nпосле чего \\(12+2^{j_R}=2^K-4\\). Единственное решение —\n\\(K=5,j_R=4\\), то есть \\(M\\). При \\(j>1\\), число\n\\(C_R=2^K-6+2^j\\) имеет точный порядок 1, поэтому \\(i_R=1\\), и\n\\[\n 2^{j_R}-2^j=2^K-12.\n\\]\nПравая часть имеет точный порядок 2. Следовательно, \\(j=2\\), затем\n\\(2^{j_R}=2^K-8\\), что возможно только при \\(K=4,j_R=3\\). Это ровно\n\\(L^2\\), с constants \\(C_L=7,C_R=14\\).\n\nЕсли \\(C_R\\) нечётно, а \\(C_L\\) чётно, то\n\\(C_R\\le3+2^{K-1}\\), тогда как \\(C_L\\ge10\\). Поэтому\n\\[\n C_R-C_L\\le2^{K-1}-7\u003c2^K-9,\n\\]\nчто невозможно. Итак, с учётом identity при \\(P=0\\), все genuine\nordered lifts с \\(P\\le2\\) суть ровно\n\\[\n \\mathrm{id},\\qquad L\\ (K=2,P=1),\\qquad\n L^2\\ (K=4,P=2),\\qquad M\\ (K=5,P=2).\n\\]\nСледовательно, для любого конечного switching, использующего только слова\nс не более чем двумя нечётными шагами, §7 даёт \\(O_{n_0}(\\log X)\\)\nразличных достижимых целей до \\(X\\). Это ограничение не распространяется\nна structural rules с \\(P\\ge3\\).\n\n## 9. Финальный on-disk audit `SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS`\n\nПолный on-disk текст `SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md`, SL1--SL10,\nпроверен вручную. Индукция по первому биту действительно показывает, что\nцелость inverse formula восстанавливает именно prescribed parity word:\nдля первого `E` источник чётен, а для первого `O` нечётен и после\nподстановки \\(z=(3x+1)/2\\) получается tail equation. Положительная цель и\nцелый источник исключают нулевой и отрицательный источник, поскольку\n\\(T(0)=0\\), а отрицательные целые остаются отрицательными; все\nпромежуточные состояния поэтому положительны. Через (SL1) это же верно\nдля правого плеча. First-hit по-прежнему является дополнительным фильтром.\n\nКлассификация SL3--SL6 действительно покрывает все structural ordered\nlifts с \\(P\\le2\\): identity, \\(L\\), \\(L^2\\), \\(M\\); \\(L^2\\) из\nалфавита switching избыточен как два шага \\(L\\). Остаточный анализ\n\\(\\{L,M\\}\\) корректно даёт единственную потенциально продолжающуюся\nветвь и боковые поддеревья глубины не более двух. Для фиксированного seed\n1 имеем ровно \\(\\{1,3\\}\\). При \\(n_0>1\\) оценка (SL9) и подсчёт боковых\nветвей дают точный грубый bound\n\\[\n \\#(A(n_0)\\cap[1,X])\\le3B_X+1\n \\le4+3\\left\\lfloor\\log_{4/3}\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nЭто относится ко всем switching из классифицированного алфавита, включая\nне-first-hit источники. Полное множество paired predecessors, а также\nслова с \\(P\\ge3\\), этим выводом не покрываются.\n","FOLLOWUP_0459_THREE_ODD_LIFT_CLASSIFICATION.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0459 — classification of structural ordered lifts with (P=3)\n\n18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ручная проверка всех ordered structural\ninverse pairs с тремя нечётными шагами на каждом плече. Программ,\nпереборов, CAS и Lean не использовалось. В отличие от общего paired\npredecessor problem, здесь сохраняется один и тот же ordered gap (1),\nобщая длина (K) и общий odd count (P=3).\n\n## 1. Exact equation\n\nFor a word with odd positions \\(0\\le i\u003cj\u003c\\ell\u003cK\\), its intercept is\n\\[\n C(i,j,\\ell)=9\\,2^i+3\\,2^j+2^\\ell.\n\\]\n\nFor a left/right ordered pair, equality of slopes gives the same (K) and\n(P=3), and the gap-one condition is exactly\n\\[\n C_R-C_L=2^K-27.                                  \\tag{P3.1}\n\\]\n\nBy the first-bit induction already checked in `SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md`,\nthe congruence \\(n\\equiv2^{-K}C_L\\pmod {27}\\), together with \\(n\\ge1\\),\ngives integral positive genuine sources and the prescribed parity words.\nFirst-hit is an additional optional restriction and is not used in the\nclassification.\n\nFor \\(K=3\\) the only constant is 19, while \\(2^K-27=-19\\), so the\nright constant would be 0. For \\(K=4\\) the constants are\n19,23,29,38, and the required difference is -11; no two of these\nhave that difference. Hence only \\(K\\ge5\\) remains, when the required\ndifference is positive and odd. Therefore the two constants have opposite\nparity.\n\n## 2. Left constant odd, right constant even\n\nThe left first position is (i_L=0). Write its later positions as\n(j\u003c\\ell), so\n\\[\n C_L=9+3\\,2^j+2^\\ell.\n\\]\n\n### 2.1. (j=1,\\ell=2)\n\nHere (C_L=19) and (C_R=2^K-8). Its exact 2-adic order is (3),\nso the right first position is (3). If the remaining right positions\nare (r\u003cs),\n\\[\n 3\\,2^r+2^s=2^K-80.                              \\tag{P3.2}\n\\]\nThe right side has exact order (4), hence (r=4). Thus\n(2^s=2^K-128). For (K\\le7) this is nonpositive; for (K>8) its\n2-adic order is (7) but its odd factor is greater than (1). The only\nsolution is\n\\[\n (K,C_L,C_R)=(8,19,248),\n \\qquad (i,j,\\ell)_L=(0,1,2),\\quad (i,j,\\ell)_R=(3,4,7).\n\\]\n\n### 2.2. (j=1,\\ell\\ge3)\n\nNow (C_R=2^K-12+2^\\ell) has exact order (2), hence its first\nposition is (2). With right positions (2\u003cr\u003cs), (P3.1) becomes\n\\[\n 3\\,2^r+2^s-2^\\ell=2^K-48.                      \\tag{P3.3}\n\\]\nThe right side has exact order (4).\n\n* If (r\u003c\\ell), the left side has order (r), so (r=4). Since\n  (4\u003c\\ell\u003cK), the remaining equality is\n  (2^s-2^\\ell=2^K-96). The negative/sign and valuation cases leave\n  \\(\\ell=5,s=6,K=7\\). This gives\n  \\((C_L,C_R)=(47,148)\\), with positions\n  \\((0,1,5)\\) and \\((2,4,6)\\).\n* If (r=\\ell), then the left side is (2^{\\ell+1}+2^s), so\n  \\(\\ell=3\\). The remaining equation is (2^s=2^K-64), whose only\n  admissible solution is (K=7,s=6). This gives\n  \\((C_L,C_R)=(23,124)\\), with positions\n  \\((0,1,3)\\) and \\((2,3,6)\\).\n* If (r>\\ell), first valuation gives \\(\\ell=4\\), then the remaining\n  left side has order (r), giving \\(r=5\\). The equation becomes\n  \\(2^s=2^K-128\\), hence (K=8,s=7\\). This gives\n  \\((C_L,C_R)=(31,260)\\), with positions\n  \\((0,1,4)\\) and \\((2,5,7)\\).\n\nThe sign subcases suppressed in the first bullet cannot add solutions:\nwhen (s\u003c\\ell), positivity of the right side forces (K=6), but there\nis no pair of indices (4\u003cs\u003c\\ell\u003c6); (s=\\ell) is impossible. The same\npower-of-two check handles the other two bullets.\n\n### 2.3. (j\\ge2)\n\nHere (C_R=2^K-18+3\\,2^j+2^\\ell) has exact order (1), so the right\nfirst position is (1). Write the other right positions (r\u003cs), with\n(r\\ge2). Equation (P3.1) is\n\\[\n 3(2^r-2^j)+2^s-2^\\ell=2^K-36.                  \\tag{P3.4}\n\\]\nThe right side has exact order (2).\n\n* If (r=j), the remaining powers have exponents at least (3), so\n  their difference cannot have order (2).\n* If (r\u003cj), valuation forces (r=2). Then\n  (2^s-2^\\ell=2^K-48+3\\,2^j). For (j=3) the positive right side has\n  order (3), so valuation forces (s=3\u003c\\ell), making the left side\n  negative although the right side is positive; for (j=4) it is (2^K), impossible\n  as a difference of distinct powers with \\(\\ell\u003cK\\); for (j\\ge5\\) it\n  has order (4). Since \\(\\ell>j\\), valuation forces (s=4\u003c\\ell), so\n  (2^s-2^\\ell\u003c0), contradicting the positive right side.\n* If (r>j), valuation forces (j=2), and (P3.4) becomes\n  \\[\n   3\\,2^r+2^s-2^\\ell=2^K-24.                    \\tag{P3.5}\n  \\]\n  If \\(\\ell\u003cr\\), then \\(\\ell=3,r=4\\), and (K=7,s=6), giving\n  \\((C_L,C_R)=(29,130)\\), positions \\((0,2,3)\\) and \\((1,4,6)\\).\n  If \\(\\ell>r\\), then \\(r=3,\\ell=4\\), and (K=6,s=5), giving\n  \\((C_L,C_R)=(37,74)\\), positions \\((0,2,4)\\) and \\((1,3,5)\\).\n  If \\(\\ell=r\\), the left side is (2^{r+1}+2^s). If (s=r+1),\n  its order is (r+2), forcing (r=1); if (s>r+1), its order is\n  (r+1), forcing (r=2). Both contradict (r>j=2).\n\nThis exhausts the left-odd orientation.\n\n## 3. Right constant odd, left constant even\n\nNow i_R=0, while the left first position is i>=1. Let the right later\npositions be r\u003cs, and the left later positions be j\u003cell. Subtraction gives\n\\[\n 3\\,2^r+2^s-9\\,2^i-3\\,2^j-2^\\ell=2^K-36.       \\tag{P3.6}\n\\]\nThe right side has exact 2-adic order 2 for every K>=5.\n\nIf r\u003ci, the term 3·2^r is the unique lowest term, so r=2. Then i>=3,\nand (P3.6) rearranges to\n\\[\n 2^K-2^s=48-9\\,2^i-3\\,2^j-2^\\ell\u003c0,\n\\]\nwhereas the left side is positive because s\u003cK. Contradiction.\n\nIf r>i, the term -9·2^i is uniquely lowest, so i=2. Cancelling the\ncommon -36 gives\n\\[\n 3(2^r-2^j)+2^s-2^\\ell=2^K.\n\\]\nIf r!=j, its lowest term has exponent below K and cannot equal 2^K. If\nr=j, it reduces to 2^s-2^ell=2^K, impossible for distinct s,ell\u003cK.\n\nIt remains r=i. Combining the first two terms gives\n\\[\n -6\\,2^i+2^s-3\\,2^j-2^\\ell=2^K-36.             \\tag{P3.7}\n\\]\nIf s=i+1, the left side is negative with absolute value at least 28,\nwhile the right side is at least -4. If s>i+1 and j=i+1, the left side\nis divisible by 2^(i+2), contradicting the exact order 2 on the right.\nFinally, if j>i+1, the unique lowest term forces i=1, and then\n\\[\n 2^K-2^s=24-3\\,2^j-2^\\ell\u003c0\n\\]\nbecause j>=3 and ell>=4, again contradicting s\u003cK. Thus this orientation\nhas no solutions, and the six solutions in §2 are all P=3 structural\nordered lifts.\n\n## 4. Genuine words, guards, and the complete list\n\nThe six left constants, lengths, target guards, and source progressions are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}\nK&C_L&C_R& n\\pmod {27}&x&\\text{left/right words}\\\\ \\hline\n6&37&74&1&64m+1,64m+2& OEOEOE/EOEOEO\\\\\n7&29&130&19&128m+89,128m+90& OEOOEEE/EOEEOEO\\\\\n7&47&148&1&128m+3,128m+4& OOEEEOE/EEOEOEO\\\\\n7&23&124&16&128m+75,128m+76& OOEOEEE/EEOOEEO\\\\\n8&19&248&16&256m+151,256m+152& OOOEEEEE/EEEOOEEO\\\\\n8&31&260&19&256m+179,256m+180& OOEEOEEE/EEOEEOEO\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHere (m\\ge0), and (x) denotes the left source. The guard is obtained\nfrom (2^K n\\equiv C_L\\pmod {27}). Every displayed source is positive and\nthe first-bit induction supplies the exact listed word; hence all six are\ngenuine structural pairs. The first three include (L^3) and the two\nmixed (L,M) compositions (up to composition-order naming); the last\nthree include the additional-to-this-local-analysis (K=7,C_L=23), (K=8,C_L=19), and (K=8,C_L=31)\nrules. Their existence is a classification statement for ordered\nstructural pairs with exactly (P=3), not for all paired predecessors.\n\n## 5. Scope\n\nNo conclusion about positive density, arbitrary asynchronous depths,\norder reversals, or structural words with (P\\ge4) follows. The result is\nthe finite exact list above within the fixed ordered gap-one, equal-(K),\nequal-(P=3) class.\n\n## 6. Final audit of on-disk SL3.1--SL3.6\n\n18 сентября 2026. Полный сохранённый `THREE_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md`\nпроверен по SL3.1--SL3.6. Формула intercept, отсутствие K-cutoff,\nоба parity orientations и все valuation cases согласуются с данным\nразбиением; guards modulo 27 и шесть строк таблицы совпадают с ручными\nисточниками этого отчёта. Порядок композиций также корректен:\n(128n-29)/27 = L after M, (128n-47)/27 = M after L, а\n(64n-37)/27 = L^3. Поэтому после удаления identity, L^2, L^3,\nL after M и M after L полный класс P\u003c=3 действительно сводится к пяти\nразличным generators L,M,J,N,R. Это только reduction структурных ordered\nlifts; полного класса paired predecessors он не покрывает. Ошибок в\nSL3.1--SL3.6 не найдено.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_ACTIVE_COUPLING_SOURCE_AUDIT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — active-boundary coupling and source scope\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-19, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05).  Bounded source/logic audit; no programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, state, or map edits.\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete PC candidate\n[`PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md`](PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md), the complete WS file\n[`WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md`](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md), and the local CE/SR/BR definitions.  For primary external comparison I used the already inspected HTML of:\n\n- Olivier Rozier, *Parity sequences of the 3x+1 map on the 2-adic integers and Euclidean embedding*, arXiv:1805.00133v6: \u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1805.00133>.\n- Olivier Rozier and Claude Terracol, *Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences*, arXiv:2502.00948v5: \u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948>.\n\nI checked their finite parity-vector, inverse-cylinder, conjugacy, affine-remainder, and fixed-length remainder statements.  I also checked accessible metadata for Lothar Berg and Günter Meinardus, *The 3n+1 Collatz Problem and Functional Equations*, at \u003chttps://www.math.uni-rostock.de/math/pub/romako/heft48/berg2.html>, and the arXiv metadata for Maxwell Siegel's *Functional Equations Associated to Collatz-Type Maps on Integer Rings of Algebraic Number Fields*, \u003chttps://arxiv.org/abs/2111.07882>.  The latter record explicitly says that the submission was withdrawn because of an error in Section 3.  The Meinardus PDF and landing page remain blocked by a repository proof-of-work page, so I did not import its Dirichlet formulas:\n\n\u003chttps://madoc.bib.uni-mannheim.de/1608/1/261_2001.pdf>\n\nThis is a targeted source check, not an exhaustive literature survey.\n\n## Exact active-boundary identity\n\nFor an odd positive integer $q$, exact $T$-invariance gives\n\n\\[\n \\beta(2q)=\\beta(q),\\qquad\n \\beta(3q+1)=\\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{3q+1}{2}\\right)=\\beta(Tq)=\\beta(q),\n\\]\n\nand, because $2q+1$ is odd,\n\n\\[\n \\beta(2q+1)=\\beta(3q+2).\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n d(2q)=|\\beta(2q+1)-\\beta(2q)|\n      =|\\beta(3q+2)-\\beta(3q+1)|=d(3q+1).\n\\]\n\nThis is an exact identity for every odd $q$; it uses only the two one-step orbit relations above.  It is consistent with CE/BR/WS and does not require a density, endpoint, or component hypothesis.\n\nThe logical consequence is narrower than a birth transfer.  If $d(2q)=1$, then $d(3q+1)=1$, so activity transfers from the even boundary $2q$ to the affine index $3q+1$.  If $q$ is a birth, both $d(2q)$ and $d(2q+1)$ equal one, but the identity still certifies only an active boundary at $3q+1$.  It does not say that $3q+1$ is a birth: its nearest birth may be a different ancestor under repeated halving.  Nor is $q\\mapsto3q+1$ one of the retained odd-boundary ancestry maps $A(q)=2q+1$ and $B(q)=4q+1$ from WS.  Conversely, when $d(2q)=0$ the identity gives $d(3q+1)=0$; the other active child $2q+1$ is then selected by the binary boundary law, and the WS $B$ transition arises only after the additional $d(8t+4)=0$ exclusion.\n\nThus this identity is a useful coupling between the binary boundary mask and an affine Collatz-related index, but it does not by itself provide a positive or negative transfer for $E_\\beta$, the primitive $P_1$, or the full retained component.\n\n## Comparison with accessible primary formulations\n\nRozier's finite parity vectors $V_j(n)$ and inverse congruences say that a finite orbit-parity word is one residue class modulo $2^j$.  His 2-adic coding $Q$ is an isometry conjugating $T$ to a shift, with functional equations for $Q$ and $Q^{-1}$.  Rozier–Terracol similarly write\n\n\\[\n T^j(n)=\\frac{3^q}{2^j}n+E_j(n),\n\\]\n\nwhere the remainder is determined by the parity vector, and prove fixed-length remainder bounds and averages.  These are operator-like parity/cylinder descriptions, but their state variable is an orbit parity word.  The project state variable is instead a pre-existing invariant color $\\beta$ and its neighboring mask $d$; the identity above compares two such colors after using orbit invariance.  Neither inspected paper gives an operator theorem for arbitrary exact invariant sequences $\\beta$ or a Dirichlet bound for $\\sum e(n)/n$.\n\nThe Berg–Meinardus metadata describes analytic functional-equation reformulations of the Collatz problem.  The accessible metadata does not state a result about arbitrary orbit-invariant binary colorings, and the linked full text was not read.  The Meinardus repository copy was inaccessible.  Siegel's accessible arXiv record describes a generalization of this functional-equation approach but marks the paper withdrawn for a Section 3 error.  These records establish that analytic operator/Dirichlet reformulations exist for map-induced generating functions, not that they control the local neighbor-color mask $d/e$ used here.  No claim about their full contents or priority is made.\n\n## PC/WS consequence and verdict\n\nThe PC candidate's scalar balances PC7–PC14 concern birth and retained-image Dirichlet sums after absolute convergence regularization.  The active-boundary identity can be inserted as an exact local relation, but it does not change the PC conclusion: PC6 is an even-subfamily estimate with largest coefficient $3/2$. PC11–PC12 alone allow a scalar endpoint-negligible assignment, but the combined parity-resolved channel identities below rule out that regime. PC13–PC14 still give only a conditional weakened singularity when terminal/origin ratios vanish.  The missing step remains a mask-sensitive estimate on $P_1$ and internal vertices, or a direct smaller-birth descent.\n\n**Bounded verdict:** the identity $d(2q)=d(3q+1)$ is correct and potentially useful for future coupling, but it is an active-boundary identity rather than an actual birth/component transfer.  The accessible primary parity and functional-equation sources provide cylinder/operator context, not a theorem controlling arbitrary exact invariant masks or the full critical energy.  No external theorem closes AC, PC, WS, or the full Collatz problem.\n\n## Audit of the proposed PC12 channel contradiction\n\nThe latest proposed contradiction was checked separately against the four-state definitions.  Write $L(n)=d(n)d(2n)$ and $R_b(n)=d(n)d(2n+1)$, and use logarithmic normalized limits whenever the birth energy diverges.\n\nTwo ingredients in the proposal are valid.  First, $d(n)=L(n)+R_b(n)$ pointwise, so the total boundary mass $D=L+R_b$ satisfies $D=2$ after normalization by $E$ by the CE doubling identity.  Second, if $W=L_{\\rm even}$ and $V=R_{b,\\,4\\mid n}$, then\n\\[\n B=\\sum_{n\\ \\mathrm{even}}\\frac{e(n)}n\n   =2\\sum_{4\\mid m}\\frac{d(m)}m-W\n   =W+2V+O(1),\n\\]\nbecause $e(n)=d(2n)-L(n)$ and $d(8t+4)=0$ makes the active $4\\mid m$ terms split into the even $L$ and $R_b$ channels.  Thus $D/E\\to2$ and $W+2V\\to B/E$ are sound (with the finite-cutoff $O(1)$ correction understood).\n\nThe conserved-channel limits are also the previously audited AC8–AC11 relations: under endpoint/origin negligibility, $L_2=(2/3)U$ and $R_{b,0}=(4/3)V$.  The initially suspected gap in the residue laws is closed by retaining parity and then summing.  Let $X_i=D_{\\rm even,i}$ and $Y_i=D_{\\rm odd,i}$, with residues modulo $3$.  The exact child identity gives\n\\[\n X_0=\\frac{E_0+L_0}{2},\\quad Y_0=\\frac{E_1+R_{b,1}}2,\n \\qquad\n X_2=\\frac{E_1+L_1}{2},\\quad Y_2=\\frac{E_2+R_{b,2}}2.\n\\]\nSince $D_i=X_i+Y_i=L_i+R_{b,i}$, this yields\n\\[\n L_0+2R_{b,0}-R_{b,1}=E_0+E_1,\n \\qquad\n 2L_2+R_{b,2}-L_1=E_1+E_2.\n\\]\nThus the PC12 case $E_1/E\\to0$ gives the displayed residue laws, while for general limits the $E_1$ terms are retained.\n\nThe $E_1$ terms cancel in the difference, so the exact normalized relation is\n\\[\n R_b-L=3(R_{b,0}-L_2)-E_0+E_2.\n\\]\nUnder PC12, $E_0=2/3$, $E_2=1/3$, and the conserved channels give\n\\[\n R_b-L=-2U+4V-\\frac13.\n\\]\nOn the other hand, $D=2$, $L=U+W$, and $W+2V=B=1/2$ give\n\\[\n R_b-L=D-2L=1-2U+4V.\n\\]\nThe two expressions differ by $4/3$, independent of $U,V$.  Therefore the endpoint/origin-negligible PC12 regime is inconsistent with the combined exact channel, residue, and CE identities.  This is a genuine obstruction to that normalized regime, not a full finite-energy proof: PC13–PC14 only gave endpoint/origin-negligibility as a conditional regime, and the remaining possibility is that terminal/origin ratios retain nonzero critical mass or that the critical energy does not have the assumed limiting ratios.\n\n## Full AC1–AC18 audit and finite-cutoff caveat\n\nI then read the complete root candidate\n[`ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md`](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md), including AC1–AC18.  The scope is a manual identity audit only; I did not run code, CAS, Lean, enumeration, or import the conditional PD170 input.\n\nAC1–AC3 are pointwise or finite-cutoff statements.  The four-state table, the identities\n$d=L+R_b$, $d(2n)=e+L$, and $d(2n+1)=e+R_b$, the exclusion $e(4t+2)=0$, and the two conserved channels are consistent.  The affine reindexing convention in AC3 is also sufficient: for each fixed affine map the reciprocal error is $O(n^{-2})$, while a fixed multiplicative cutoff annulus has bounded harmonic mass.  This gives an $O(1)$ independent of $X$ and of the coloring.  It does not require bounded total birth energy.\n\nAC4–AC8 correctly retain parity before summing over residues.  In particular, AC6 gives the four parity-resolved equations, and summing the two relevant pairs gives the AC7 identities with the $E_1$ terms.  Their subtraction yields AC8; no independence of parity and residue is silently used.  AC9–AC12 then consistently combine the two channel transports with AC4–AC5.  In the notation of AC11–AC12, the global endpoint identity is\n\n\\[\n B-A=E_0-E_2+O(1),\\qquad\n A=E_2+P/2+O(1),\\qquad\n B=E_0+P/2+O(1).\n\\]\n\nAC13 uses the correct source/target orientation: $Z_S,Z_R$ are terminal source defects, while $D_S,D_R$ are missing-predecessor target defects.  Solving those equations gives AC14 and the one-sided estimate AC15.  Consequently AC16 is a valid reduction: finite total birth energy is equivalent to finiteness of both $Z_R$ and $D_S$, because the forward implication is by subset inclusion and the reverse uses the nonnegative AC15 bound and monotone convergence.  This is a reduction, not a proof that either charge is finite.  AC17 likewise supplies only a necessary lower share for divergent energy.\n\nThe apparent partial-summation typo in AC7 is not a typo after the root file's\nnormalization is read literally.  There $Q(X)$ is the *harmonically weighted*\ncumulative quantity\n\\[\n Q(X)=E(X)-4Z_R(X)-3D_S(X)+2Z_S(X)+3D_R(X)\n      =\\sum_{n\\le X}\\frac{q_n}{n},\n\\]\nnot the unweighted cumulative sum $\\sum_{n\\le X}q_n$.  Therefore, with\n$s=1+\\varepsilon$, partial summation applied to the coefficients $q_n/n$\nindeed gives\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\ge1}q_n n^{-s}\n   =\\varepsilon\\int_1^\\infty Q(t)t^{-\\varepsilon-1}\\,dt.\n\\]\nThe alternative formula $s\\int Q(t)t^{-s-1}dt$ would apply only if $Q$ were\ndefined as the unweighted cumulative sum.  With the actual weighted $Q$,\n$|Q(t)|\\le C$ gives the same uniform $O(1)$ bound for every $s>1$.\nConsequently AC18 remains valid and excludes only a divergent-energy regime\nin which both endpoint/origin terms are negligible; it neither gives\nconvergence at $s=1$ nor proves finite energy.\n\nThe final logical boundary is therefore precise.  AC1–AC18 establish a uniform finite-cutoff transport identity and exclude the selected endpoint/origin-negligible PC12 limit after the parity-resolved correction.  They leave open regimes with nonzero critical terminal/origin mass, and the actual finiteness of $Z_R$ and $D_S$ (or another joint charge estimate).  The candidate contains no PD170 step and no theorem imported from the unread/withdrawn Meinardus/Siegel materials.  A later finite-energy result would still need the separately recorded pointwise bridge before it could imply full Collatz.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_BALANCED_CHAIN_BARRIER.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — balanced arithmetic finite-chain barrier\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded manual audit only;\nno programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents were used. This report\nis separate from the finite-energy obstruction: it audits an unmasked\narithmetic envelope and does not assert the existence of an actual birth\ncomponent for an exact invariant.\n\n## Construction audited\n\nUse the partial arithmetic branches\n\n    S(x)=(3x+1)/2       when x is odd,\n    R(x)=3x/4           when 4 divides x.\n\nChoose a word greedily from the cumulative slope. Start with lambda_0=1;\nat stage j choose S when lambda_j is at most 1 and R when lambda_j is\nlarger than 1. The new cumulative slope is respectively\n\n    lambda_(j+1)=(3/2)lambda_j or (3/4)lambda_j.\n\nIf lambda_j lies in [3/4,3/2], the next value remains in that interval:\nS is used only below 1 and R only above 1. Thus all lambda_j lie in\n[3/4,3/2].\n\nWriting the affine iterate as\n\n    n_j=lambda_j n+B_j,\n\nwith lambda_i denoting the cumulative slope after stage i, the exact\nintercept is\n\n    B_j=lambda_j sum_{i\u003cj, S at i} 1/(2 lambda_(i+1)).\n\nEach summand after multiplication by lambda_j is at most 1, because\nlambda_j is at most 3/2 and lambda_(i+1) is at least 3/4. Hence\n0\u003c=B_j\u003c=j. This verifies the candidate's intercept bound; it would be\nfalse for an arbitrary all-S word, but is valid for the balanced greedy\nword.\n\n## Guard realization by one binary class and CRT\n\nFor a word with s S-steps and r R-steps, the successive guards select one\nresidue class modulo 2^A, A=s+2r. This follows inductively: an S guard\nselects one of two lifts (oddness), and an R guard selects one of four lifts\n(divisibility by four). After the k-step word, the extra requirement\nn_k congruent to 2 modulo 4 selects one of four further lifts, hence one\nclass modulo 2^(A+2). The coefficient of the starting variable in the\ninteger affine output is odd after the denominator is cleared, so the final\nfour lifts really are distinct modulo 4 and exactly one satisfies the\nterminal guard.\n\nAdding n congruent to 1 modulo 3 is compatible by CRT, since the moduli\n3 and 2^(A+2) are coprime. There is therefore a positive solution n at\nleast k. Choosing the least such solution gives\n\n    n \u003c= k + 3*2^(A+2) \u003c= k + 12*4^k \u003c= 13*4^k\n\nfor k at least 1, since A=s+2r is at most 2k. The lower cutoff n>=k\nensures B_j\u003c=j\u003c=n.\n\nThe initial residue n congruent to 1 modulo 3 has no arithmetic F-predecessor:\nS-images are 2 modulo 3 and R-images are 0 modulo 3. The terminal residue\nn_k congruent to 2 modulo 4 is outside the domain of F. Thus the realized\nword is the entire finite arithmetic F-component of n, not merely a prefix.\nThe vertices are positive because\n\n    n_j >= (3/4)n > 0.\n\nThey are distinct. If n_i=n_j with i\u003cj, injectivity would give\nn=F^(j-i)(n), producing an incoming arithmetic edge to the origin n,\ncontrary to n congruent to 1 modulo 3.\n\n## Harmonic-mass consequence\n\nFor n>=k and 0\u003c=j\u003c=k, the bounds above give\n\n    3n/4 \u003c= n_j \u003c= (3/2)n+j \u003c= (5/2)n.\n\nLet m=min_j n_j be the numerical minimum of this finite arithmetic\ncomponent. Then m>=3n/4, while every one of the k+1 vertices is at most\n(5/2)n. Consequently\n\n    m sum_{j=0}^k 1/n_j\n      >= (3n/4)(k+1)/((5/2)n)\n      = 3(k+1)/10.\n\nSince n>=k, and in fact the CRT construction gives n\u003c=13*4^k, these\ncomponents have m tending to infinity while m times their harmonic mass\nalso tends to infinity. Therefore the arithmetic envelope admits no\nuniform estimate W\u003c=C/m based on the internal minimum m.\n\nThe same lower bound rules out a bound of the form\nW\u003c=C(1/n+1/n_k), with C independent of the word: both n and n_k are at\nleast 3n/4 and m\u003c=n, so m(1/n+1/n_k) is bounded by an absolute constant,\nwhereas mW grows linearly in k.\n\n## Exact scope and limitation\n\nThis is an unmasked arithmetic construction. The terminal vertex has\nF undefined, but it was not shown to be a birth; in fact the construction\nis deliberately independent of beta and its masks. Hence it is not an\nactual counterexample to any uniform estimate for retained birth\ncomponents of an exact invariant. It shows only that arithmetic prefix\ncounts, origin/terminal placement, or the existence of an internal\nminimum cannot by themselves provide the desired uniform component-energy\nbound. Any actual-component theorem must use the birth masks and their\ncorrelations in addition to this arithmetic geometry.\n\nThe result is stronger in scope than the earlier origin-based arithmetic\nbarrier, but it does not alter the finite-energy obstruction report and\nsupplies no constancy, cycle exclusion, or full Collatz conclusion.\n\n## Audit of BM3-BM5 and BM8\n\nI read the complete `FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md`.\nThe BM3 affine recursion is correct for the greedy word: the cumulative\nslope stays in [3/4,3/2], and\n\n    B_j = lambda_j sum_{i\u003c=j, S at i} 1/(2 lambda_i)\n\nhas 0\u003c=B_j\u003c=j because every lambda_j/(2 lambda_i) is at most 1. This\nbound uses the balanced word and would not hold for an arbitrary all-S word.\n\nBM4's lifting is also valid. A prescribed S/R word fixes one class modulo\n2^A, A=s+2r. The final condition n_k congruent to 2 modulo 4 fixes one\nof four lifts because the cleared affine coefficient is 3^k, which is odd.\nCRT with n congruent to 1 modulo 3 gives an origin without an arithmetic\npredecessor. The least solution above k obeys the displayed\nn\u003ck+3*2^(A+2)\u003c=13*4^k bound. Positivity, terminality and distinctness then\nmake the word a complete finite arithmetic component; no actual birth mask\nis being asserted.\n\nBM5 follows from 3n/4\u003c=n_j\u003c=5n/2: with k+1 vertices,\n\n    m W(C_k) >= 3(k+1)/10,\n    W(C_k)/(1/n+1/n_k) >= 6(k+1)/35.\n\nThe logarithmic lower loss in BM6 follows from n\u003c=13*4^k and m>=3n/4.\nThus the arithmetic envelope cannot have a uniform minimum- or two-endpoint\nweight bound. This does not contradict a bound for retained birth\ncomponents, because the terminal n_k is 2 modulo 4 and is not a birth.\n\nBM8 is consistent with the finite-energy obstruction audited earlier:\nfinite sum e(n)/n gives finite sum d(n)/n, then the SB174 profile argument\ngives constancy outside a natural-density-zero set. The accepted QS170\nbridge under named PD170 does close the remaining pointwise step: an\nexceptional n can be divided to an odd u, and a=3u+1 has the same color;\npositive lower density of predecessors of a contradicts the density-zero\nexceptional set. This is conditional on the accepted PD170/QS170 input,\nwhich was not re-audited here. Without PD170, pointwise-coalescence or\nuniversal descent is still missing. The balanced arithmetic construction\ndoes not provide that bridge and does not itself realize an exact invariant.\nRoot's reported Elia-Tucker reading and failed Bernstein--Lagarias fetch are\nnot counted as new source reading here.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES — SL3 source audit\n\nДата: 18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный ручной\nисточниково-критический аудит, без программ, CAS, переборов и Lean.\nПрочитаны `PROJECT_STATE.md` и\n`outputs/collatz-four-methods/team1000/SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md`.\nИз первичных текстов повторно сверены Definitions 1--2 и §§2--5\nElia--Tucker, *Consecutive Integers and the Collatz Conjecture*,\n[arXiv:1511.09141](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141). Дополнительно\nпросмотрены доступные страницы оригинальной работы LaTourette,\n*Explorations of the Collatz Conjecture* (Moravian College Senior Honors\nThesis, 2007), в частности [страница 29 с Definitions 4--5, Theorem 5 и\nTable 4.1](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college/29).\n\n## 1. Что уже есть в первичных источниках\n\nElia--Tucker Definition 2 называет block prefix парой слов одинаковой\nдлины, для которой формально\n\\[\n       T_b(x)+1=T_{b'}(x+1)\n\\]\nдля всех положительных \\(x\\). Это тот же affine\nadjacency-preserving интерфейс, который используется в SMALL_ODD. Их\nDefinition 1 (corresponding stems) добавляет другое условие о proper\nprefixes и coalescence. Эти понятия нельзя смешивать: §5 Elia--Tucker\nобсуждает новые coalescing stems, но это не классификация block prefixes.\n\nПоправка от 18 сентября 2026: прежняя формулировка ошибочно\nотождествляла Definition 5 с block-тождественностью. LaTourette использует\nDefinition 2 для ordered “block” с identity\n\\[\n       T_b(x)+1=T_{b'}(x+1),\n\\]\nа Definitions 4--5 для “string” и “string prefix” с обратной ориентацией\n\\[\n       T_q(x)=T_{q'}(x+1)+1.\n\\]\nDefinition 4 добавляет separation условие к string; Definition 5 не\nявляется той же ordered identity. Table 4.1 озаглавлена\n“Corresponding Blocks”. В таблице явно присутствуют, среди прочих,\nстроки\n\\[\n       10/01,\\qquad 11000/00101,\n\\]\nа также более длинные пары. Следовательно, ручной pair\n\\[\n       OOEEE/EEOEO=11000/00101\n\\]\nуже находится в доступном первичном источнике LaTourette. Его нельзя\nпредставлять как новый для литературы пример; ранее записанный пример\nостаётся только независимой проверкой таблицы.\n\nLaTourette также говорит, что таблица 4.2 перечисляет strings до длины\n11, с отмеченными новыми строками, и передаёт conjecture Garner о\nбесконечности списка blocks and strings. На доступной странице нет\nобщей классификационной теоремы всех string prefixes. Theorem 5\nиспользует заданный string prefix для построения некоторых пар\nпоследовательных целых из уже имеющейся пары равного total stopping time;\nэто не fixed-\\(P\\) classification и не density theorem.\n\nТаким образом, источник уже содержит именно тот non-elementary finite\nblock, который был проверен вручную, но не содержит общего вывода,\nисключающего или классифицирующего все более длинные variable lifts.\n\n## 2. Проверка формулы для таблицы и source interface\n\nЕсли \\(w\\) имеет длину \\(K\\), odd positions\n\\(0\\le i_1\u003c\\cdots\u003ci_P\u003cK\\), его формальный intercept равен\n\\[\n C(I)=\\sum_{r=1}^{P}3^{P-r}2^{i_r},\n \\qquad T_w(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C(I)}{2^K}.\n\\]\nДля ordered gap-one pair с теми же \\(K,P\\) необходимо и достаточно на\nуровне affine identities, что\n\\[\n       C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nДля \\(11000\\) (odd positions 0,1) имеем \\(C_L=3+2=5\\), а для\n\\(00101\\) (odd positions 2,4) имеем \\(C_R=3\\cdot4+16=28\\). Поэтому\n\\(C_R-C_L=23=32-9\\), ровно как в Table 4.1. The inverse left rule is\n\\(M(n)=(32n-5)/9\\), with target guard \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\), and the\nsource cylinder is \\(x\\equiv3\\pmod{32}\\). This agrees with the primary\ntable and with the independent exact trajectory check in SMALL_ODD.\n\nFor a genuine word, the inverse formula\n\\[\n       x=\\frac{2^K n-C(I)}{3^P}\n\\]\nhas two separate guards: integrality of \\(x\\) is a congruence on the\nterminal \\(n\\) modulo \\(3^P\\), while the parity cylinder is the source\ncongruence \\(x\\equiv-C(I)3^{-P}\\pmod{2^K}\\). A formal intercept without a\ngenuine word does not by itself establish actual trajectory bits.\n\n## 3. What a general fixed-(P) bound does give\n\nThe intercept formula gives a clean non-enumerative necessary condition,\nbut not a finite bound on \\(K\\) for every fixed \\(P\\). For\n\\(0\\le i_1\u003c\\cdots\u003ci_P\u003cK\\),\n\\[\n C_{min}=3^P-2^P,\n \\qquad\n C_{max}=2^{K-P}(3^P-2^P),\n\\]\nattained by positions \\(0,1,\\ldots,P-1\\) and\n\\(K-P,\\ldots,K-1\\), respectively. Hence every pair satisfying (2.1)\nmust obey\n\\[\n 2^K-3^P\n \\le (3^P-2^P)(2^{K-P}-1)                 \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nwhen the left-to-right difference is positive. This is useful as a\nnecessary check, but for fixed \\(P\\ge2\\) its asymptotic right side is\nlarge enough that (3.1) alone does not bound \\(K\\).\n\nThere is a parity refinement. Since \\(2^K-3^P\\) is odd, exactly one of\n\\(C_L,C_R\\) is odd; an intercept is odd exactly when its first odd\nposition is \\(i_1=0\\). If the left intercept is even, its minimum is\n\\(2(3^P-2^P)\\), while the largest odd right intercept is\n\\[\n 3^{P-1}+2^{K-P+1}(3^{P-1}-2^{P-1}).             \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nFor \\(P=1,2\\), comparing (3.2) with \\(2^K-3^P\\) supplies the strong\none-sided obstruction used in the SMALL_ODD proof. For \\(P\\ge3\\), the\nleading coefficient in (3.2) is\n\\((3/2)^{P-1}-1\\ge5/4\\), so this extremal parity bound does not even\nhave the correct asymptotic size to exclude the orientation. The opposite\norientation is bounded only by the global range (3.1). Additional\ncongruence or carry information is therefore required for a general\nfixed-\\(P\\) classification.\n\nThis distinction matters: the complete list identity, \\(L\\), \\(L^2\\),\n\\(M\\) for \\(P\\le2\\) is an algebraic argument with the explicit\nintercept forms \\(2^i\\) and \\(3\\,2^i+2^j\\), not a finite numerical\ncutoff and not a theorem that extends automatically to \\(P\\ge3\\).\n\n## 4. Source limits and verdict\n\nThe LaTourette source was accessed through a page-rendered copy. The\nlanding page says the ePaper download is unavailable; pages around the\ndefinition/table were readable, while several later page URLs were cache\nmisses. I therefore import only the exact visible Definitions 4--5,\nTable 4.1 entry, Theorem 5 statement and the stated finite-table scope.\nNo claim is made about unread portions of the thesis, Garner's complete\narticle, or global literature priority.\n\nBounded verdict: fixed-P gap-one structural pairs are a known block/string\ntopic, and the specific (OOEEE/EEOEO) pair is explicitly listed by\nLaTourette. The independent intercept calculation validates it and proves\nthe SMALL_ODD classification for (P\\le2). LaTourette's later section gives\nan exact displacement criterion and visible P=3 examples, but no general\nP=3 classification in the read scope. The general extremal formula gives\nonly bounds (3.1)--(3.2), not a finite length bound for arbitrary fixed\nP. No conclusion about all variable lifts, positive density, or full\nCollatz follows.\n\n## 5. Additional landing-page audit: visible P=3 rows and §4.2.4\n\nThe direct `/29` page endpoint was intermittently an internal error, but\nthe landing page's complete OCR was readable at the URL below. Its visible\nTable 4.1 contains the consecutive rows\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n1101000&0011001,\\\\\n11100000&00011001,\\\\\n11001000&00100101.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEach displayed pair has exactly three 1s on each side. Thus the three\nP=3 pairs called J, N and R in the current project are already visibly\nlisted in LaTourette's Corresponding Blocks table. This is a source\ncorrection only; no priority claim is made, and the OCR does not by itself\nidentify the project's labels.\n\nThe same landing OCR exposes §4.2.3--4.2.4 (around lines 2036--2341).\nTheorem 7 states an iff test for the ordered block orientation in terms of\nthe displacement obtained by moving the t-th 1; Lemma 6 gives the individual\nmove contribution\n\\[\n       \\Delta_t=\\frac{3^{n-t}}{2^{k-a_t+1}}(2^{r_t}-1),\n\\]\nand the proof imposes the exact affine gap condition\n\\[\n       2^k\\Delta=2^k-3^n.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\Delta=T_{b'}(x)-T_b(x)\\). For the reversed string orientation,\nwith \\(\\Delta=T_{q'}(x)-T_q(x)\\), the corresponding signed condition is\n\\[\n       2^k\\Delta=-2^k-3^n.\n\\]\nThe OCR is damaged, so one must not import one formula for both\norientations; each criterion is orientation-specific. This is a general\nsymbolic criterion equivalent to the relevant intercept equation, not a\nfinite classification by odd count.\n\nTheorem 8 handles the extreme case of one zero (number of ones \\(n=k-1\\))\nand concludes that the only length is two, the pair \\(10/01\\). Theorem 9\nhandles one one (\\(n=1\\)) and reaches the same length-two conclusion.\nAfter those two proofs the text says that analogous proofs could be\ncontinued for two and three ones, but that the number of cases grows\nrapidly and it becomes unreasonable to continue much beyond three ones\n(OCR lines 2305--2314). It then gives Conjecture 2 for the observed\none-density ratio, explicitly as a conjecture based on Tables 4.1--4.2,\nnot a theorem.\n\nTherefore this source check finds prior visible P=3 examples and a general\niff displacement test, but not a completed P=3 classification or an\nunbounded fixed-P length theorem in the inspected material. Any current\nP=3 algebra must be presented as an internal audit/extension of the exact\ncriterion, without novelty or literature-absence claims.\n\nURLs:\n\n- [Elia--Tucker primary HTML](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141)\n- [LaTourette page 29, Definitions 4--5 and Table 4.1](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college/29)\n- [LaTourette landing page](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college)\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_CHAIN_FLUX_IDENTITY.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — chain-flux identity for retained growth\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded analytic audit; no mathematical\nprogram, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, or new agent. I read `PROJECT_STATE.md`,\nthe full BC candidate `MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md`, and\n`INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md`. No external renewal or Lyapunov source\nis needed: the identity below is an elementary consequence of injectivity and\nstrict growth.\n\n## 1. Retained graph and exact telescoping\n\nLet B_beta be the birth set and let E be the retained growth edges\n(n\\to F(n)): the source and output are actual births and the SC output mask\nretains the transition. BC11 gives injectivity of F after combining H and G3,\nand every retained edge satisfies F(n)>n. Therefore every component is a\nfinite chain or a one-sided infinite chain with one origin.\n\nPut w(n)=1/n. For a source n with a retained edge define\n\n    flux(n)=w(n)−w(F(n));\n\nfor a terminal vertex (no retained outgoing edge), define\n\n    flux(n)=w(n).\n\nFor a finite component\n(a=n_0\\to n_1\\to\\cdots\\to n_L), direct telescoping gives\n\n    Σ_(j=0..L) flux(n_j) = 1/a.\n\nFor an infinite component, the partial sum through n_K is\n(1/a−1/n_{K+1}); strict growth forces n_K→∞, so the limit is again 1/a.\nTonelli then gives the exact extended-value identity\n\n    E_O := Σ_(a∈O_beta)1/a\n         = Σ_(n∈B_beta) flux(n),                         (CF1)\n\nwhere O_beta contains origins of both finite and infinite components. In\nexpanded form,\n\n    E_O = Σ_(n with retained edge)(1/n−1/F(n))\n          + Σ_(terminal vertices)1/n.                   (CF2)\n\nThe terminal sum in CF2 is over all terminal retained-graph vertices; for\nfinite components it is the finite-chain endpoint sum, while infinite\ncomponents contribute no terminal term.\n\nThere is also an exact finite-cutoff boundary identity. For X≥1,\n\n    Σ_(n∈B_beta,n≤X) flux(n)\n      = E_O(X) − Σ_(n→m∈E, n≤X\u003cm) 1/m.                 (CF3)\n\nThe subtracted term is the first vertex beyond X on each chain crossing the\ncutoff. Edges cannot enter [1,X] from a source above X because all retained\nedges increase. Equivalently, E_O(X) equals the left-hand flux sum plus this\nboundary term. The term vanishes for a component once its whole chain is\nincluded; a global X→∞ conclusion requires the corresponding convergence,\nwhich is why CF1 is stated as an extended-value componentwise identity.\n\nFinally, because every non-origin birth is the unique output of one retained\nedge,\n\n    E_B = E_O + Σ_(n→m∈E)1/m,                           (CF4)\n\nagain as an extended identity. This is a partition identity, not a new bound\non the birth energy.\n\n## 2. Actual H/G3 fluxes\n\nThe edge flux is positive and has the exact branch formulas\n\n    1/n−1/H(n)  = 5(n+1) / [n(9n+5)],\n    1/n−1/G₃(n) = (n+3)  / [n(9n+3)].                  (CF5)\n\nThe terminal contribution is 1/n. In particular\n\n    1/n−1/H(n)  ≥ 5/(9n),\n    1/n−1/G₃(n) ≥ 1/(9n),\n\nbut these are lower estimates for the positive flux, not an upper estimate\nfor the unknown origin mass. They show that CF2 can be viewed as edge flux\nplus terminal remainder, while the remainder remains tied to which masks\nactually stop a chain.\n\nFor a finite chain, CF2 is exactly\n\n    1/a = Σ_(j\u003cL)(1/n_j−1/n_(j+1)) + 1/n_L.\n\nIt does not give the total chain birth weight\n(sum_{j=0}^L1/n_j). That total is the quantity bounded in BC12 by 9/a.\nThe telescoping cancellation removes downstream copies of the origin weight;\nit does not cancel the positive weights of all vertices in E_R.\n\n## 3. Does flux improve the factor 9?\n\nNo uniform improvement follows from the retained affine slopes alone. Every\nedge has multiplier at least 9/8, so BC12 gives\n\n    Σ_(j=0..L)1/n_j ≤ (1/a)Σ_(j≥0)(8/9)^j = 9/a.\n\nFor a formal all-G3 arithmetic prefix, the exact center identity is\n(n_j+3=(9/8)^j(a+3)). Finite parity coding supplies a nonempty residue\nclass for every fixed finite G3 prefix with an odd endpoint. Taking large\nrepresentatives makes the additive +3 negligible, and taking the prefix\nlength long makes the geometric partial sum approach the factor 9. These\nare arithmetic candidate chains only; no actual beta masks or births are\nclaimed. Thus injectivity plus affine growth cannot lower the worst-case\nconstant below 9 by itself.\n\nThe flux identity does provide an exact reformulation of the origin problem:\ncontrol of the edge-flux sum and of the terminal remainder would control\nO_beta. But the terminal remainder is positive and its aggregate mass is\nprecisely part of the unresolved stopping/mask information. Restricting CF2\nto finite components gives\n\n    E_A = Σ_(finite-chain edges) flux(n)\n          + Σ_(finite-chain terminals)1/n,\n\nwhich still does not bound E_A. The relation to BC is therefore bookkeeping,\nnot a positive-residual contraction.\n\n## 4. Scope relative to OR and BC\n\nOR9--OR10 charge origins lying in the arithmetic image to smaller birth\nancestors and leave the primitive-origin term E_P. CF1 is compatible with\nthat reduction: it decomposes every origin into local positive edge flux and\nterminal mass, but it supplies no estimate for E_P or for finite terminal\nmass. The all-length infinite arithmetic core Γ has bounded harmonic mass,\nwhile finite prefixes and their terminal masks remain outside that bound.\n\nThe identity is valid for retained actual masks because it uses only the\nactual partial graph and its injectivity; it does not replace those masks by\narithmetic admissibility. No classical external source was imported, and no\nfull stopping-time or Collatz conclusion follows.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe exact conservation law is CF1/CF2, with CF3 giving the finite-cutoff\nboundary term. It identifies the origin mass as edge flux plus terminal\nremainder, but does not improve the 9/a bound for total finite-chain birth\nweights. The unresolved positive remainder is exactly the terminal/mask\ninformation needed for a genuine harmonic estimate.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_CRITICAL_WEIGHT_SOURCES.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483_CRITICAL_WEIGHT_SOURCES — q=1 and affine correction\n\nДата: 18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный ручной\nисточниковый проход, без программ, переборов, CAS и Lean. Прочитаны\n`PROJECT_STATE.md` и\n`outputs/collatz-four-methods/team1000/POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md`.\nЦель прохода — отделить pointwise critical Green sum от 3-adic/Haar\naverages, predecessor-density lower bounds и Abel-regularized generating\nfunctions.\n\n## 1. Exact correction along one positive path\n\nFor a shortcut path of length (K), let (P) positions be odd and let\n(x_j>0) be the state immediately before an odd step. The identity at an\nodd step is\n\n\\[\n \\frac{3x_j+1}{2}=\\frac{3x_j}{2}\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_j}\\right).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the (K) step ratios gives the exact path identity\n\n\\[\n \\frac{n}{m}=\\frac{3^P}{2^K}\n \\prod_{j\\,\\mathrm{odd}}\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_j}\\right).\n \\tag{CW1}\n\\]\n\nWriting (A=2^K/3^P), \n\\(\\Pi=\\prod_j(1+1/(3x_j))\\), and the inverse affine relation\n(m=A n-B), this is equivalently\n\n\\[\n \\frac{m}{n}=\\frac{A}{\\Pi},\n \\qquad A^{-1}=\\frac{n}{m}\\,\\Pi^{-1}.\n \\tag{CW2}\n\\]\n\nFor (P=0), the product is empty, so (Pi=1) and (B=0), including a\nnonempty all-even path. For (P\\ge1), (Pi>1) and (B\\ge1/3); every\nnonempty structural block in the root's alphabet has (P\\ge1). Thus the\npositive affine correction is an exact multiplicative factor, not merely\nan inequality. PR1 in the root file is the estimate obtained by discarding\n(Pi^{-1}\u003c1). The exact factor could in principle improve the\ncritical sum, but a useful lower growth estimate for (Pi), uniform over\nall admissible first-hit paths to one fixed target, is not supplied by the\ngeneric affine identity.\n\n## 2. Wirsching: predecessor density and the opposite direction of control\n\nThe primary author-hosted version of Wirsching, *On positive predecessor\ndensity in (3n+1) dynamics* (2003 preliminary version), was read in full:\n[posden.pdf](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf).\nIt defines (E_{\\ell,k}(a)) as paths ending at (a), with ℓ odd and (k)\neven steps, and (e_\\ell(k,a)=|E_\\ell(k,a)|). The union over\n((\\ell,k)) surjects onto the predecessor set (P(a)), and it is injective\nfor non-cyclic (a) (PDF pp. 2--3, extracted lines 174--205). The source\nalso gives\n\n\\[\n a\\ge \\frac{3^\\ell}{2^{k+\\ell}} b\n \\qquad(b\\in E_{\\ell,k}(a)),\n \\tag{CW3}\n\\]\n\nwhich is an exact source-size cutoff for path indices (pp. 3--4, lines\n257--276).\n\nThe paper's estimating series (s_y(a)) is a sum of these path counts,\nand the paper states Haar integrability on \\(\\mathbb Z_3^\\times\\) together\nwith a positive averaged lower asymptotic (pp. 4--5, lines 277--299).\nIts Theorem 1 still assumes a uniform ratio condition on the path counts\nalong a central-window class (A_\\delta) (lines 301--313); the abstract\nexplicitly records three unbridged conjectural gaps.\n\nThis literature has the opposite counting orientation from the needed\npointwise q=1 upper bound: it seeks many predecessor sources below (X).\nIf a fixed predecessor set has positive lower density, then the raw\nharmonic sum \\(\\sum_{m\\le X,\\,m\\in P(a)}1/m\\) grows logarithmically. That\ndoes not decide the corrected sum\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{m\\in P(a)}\\frac{1}{m}\\,\\Pi(m\\to a)^{-1},\n \\tag{CW4}\n\\]\n\nbecause the factors \\(\\Pi^{-1}\\) may suppress it. Wirsching's density\nprogram contains no theorem giving this suppression, and its positive\ndensity goal is therefore neither a proof of q=1 convergence nor a direct\ncounterexample to it.\n\nThe earlier primary paper Wirsching, *An improved estimate concerning\n3n+1 predecessor sets*, Acta Arith. 63 (1993), 205--210, is available via\n[journal metadata](https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/acta-arithmetica/all/63/3/107778/an-improved-estimate-concerning-3n-1-predecessor-sets)\nand a [scanned PDF](https://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/aa/aa63/aa6332.pdf).\nIts stated result is a lower power estimate for predecessor counts, not a\nweighted pointwise upper estimate. Wirsching's 1996 primary paper likewise\nstudies combinatorial representations and averaging heuristics for\npredecessor sets, as stated in the publisher abstract:\n[ScienceDirect record](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0012365X9400243C).\nFull 1996 text was not imported in this pass.\n\n## 3. Berg--Krüppel and Berg--Meinardus: analytic analogues, not the q=1 tree sum\n\nBerg--Krüppel, *On the Solution of an Integral-Functional Equation with a\nParameter*, Z. Anal. Anwend. 17 (1998), 159--181, was read from the full\npublisher PDF:\n[EMS PDF](https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/34973?nt=1).\nFor parameter (a>1), Theorem 1.1 gives a unique normalized compactly\nsupported solution of an integral-functional equation (PDF pp. 159--161).\nThe Laplace transform has an absolutely/uniformly convergent infinite\nproduct (their equations (1.1)--(1.2), pp. 160--161). The paper notes that\nthe case (a=3) is the density of a transition probability studied by\nWirsching (p. 160). Its Section 9 gives asymptotics of this continuous\ndensity near zero.\n\nThis product is a product in the Laplace transform of a continuous\ninvariant-density equation. It is not the discrete Collatz path product\n(\\Pi=\\prod_{odd}(1+1/(3x_j))), and it gives no pointwise theorem about\n(\\sum A_w^{-1}) for a fixed positive integer. It should not be imported\nas a q=1 Abelian regularization of the inverse tree.\n\nBerg--Meinardus, *The 3n+1 Collatz Problem and Functional Equations*,\nRostock. Math. Kolloq. 48 (1995), 11--18, is identified by the official\nauthor metadata as a report of two analytic versions of the Collatz\nconjecture:\n[official record](https://www.math.uni-rostock.de/math/pub/romako/heft48/berg2.html).\nThe accessible metadata gives only that scope. The related 1994/1995\nfunctional-equation papers concern generating functions and holomorphic\nsolutions; they do not state a fixed-target positive-integer Green-sum\ntheorem in the material read here. In particular, setting a generating\nvariable to (1) outside its convergence domain is not a proof of q=1\nconvergence for the discrete inverse tree.\n\n## 4. Bounded source verdict\n\nThe inspected primary literature provides three nearby but distinct\nobjects:\n\n1. exact finite path encodings and source-size inequalities;\n2. Haar/Markov averages and conjectural positive predecessor density;\n3. continuous integral-functional equations and their Laplace products.\n\nNone of these supplies a pointwise theorem for the corrected critical sum\n\\(\\sum A_w^{-1}\\) at one fixed positive terminal integer. The elementary\nidentity (CW1)--(CW2) shows precisely what remains: either prove enough\ngrowth of \\(\\Pi\\) along the fixed-target predecessor set, or prove a\nseparate weighted sparsity estimate. Positive predecessor density, if\navailable, points toward a divergent *raw* harmonic envelope, while the\ncorrected sum remains undecided. This is a bounded literature conclusion,\nnot a claim of worldwide absence or novelty.\n\n## 5. Scope audit of the root arithmetic no-go\n\nThe root's CX5 obstruction is an internal arithmetic argument using the\nactual guarded blocks (H), (L), and (M), not a source theorem. Its\nconclusion is self-contained once those block identities and guards are\naccepted; no predecessor-density theorem is needed. The affine scaling\ncondition on ell is stated explicitly and the order of limits is fixed\n(first n for each fixed iterate t, then t); no unstated\nregular-variation result is being imported. World priority is unknown.\n\nThe PL176 obligation concerns paired targets with (3\\nmid ac) and\ndifferent hitting times. This source pass does not re-import its older\nsingle-target positive-density input as a proved external theorem. The\nsource comparison above remains limited to the read primary texts.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_DEFECT_BOUNDARY_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — the n≡3 (mod 4) defect boundary and its source scope\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\n\n## Reading scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete scratch candidate\n`work/lb-next-shifted-pair-candidate.md`, and the relevant portions of\n`COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md`, including SC1–SC4 and the cited\ntwo-path merger in `work/round143-l-general-merging.md`. I also read the\nolder archive's `work/round175-h-boundary-transport.md` at BT8–BT9 and the\naccepted signed-channel report at SB7 and SB18. The two current peer drafts\n`FOLLOWUP_0431_DEFECT_BOUNDARY_TRANSFER.md` and\n`FOLLOWUP_0459_DEFECT_PARITY_CLOSURE.md` were checked only as current\ncomparisons; they are not treated here as previously accepted archival\nevidence. For primary comparison I used the already recorded finite\nparity/affine scope:\nRozier–Terracol, *Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences*,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948>, and the previously audited Rozier parity\ncoding source \u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1805.00133>. The source reading was\nlimited to finite parity vectors, affine iterate formulas, and residue\ncylinders. No source theorem is claimed for the special beta-color identity\nbelow; it is a direct calculation from shortcut invariance. No program, CAS,\nenumeration, Lean run, or numerical experiment was used.\n\n## Exact all-n≡3 (mod 4) identity\n\nWrite n=4k+3, k≥0. The two genuine shortcut paths are\n\n    n -> 6k+5 -> 9k+8,\n    3n -> 18k+14 -> 9k+7.\n\nThe first path uses two odd inputs, (n) and (6k+5). The second uses the\nodd input (3n) followed by the even input (18k+14). Thus shortcut\ninvariance gives\n\n    beta(n)=beta(9k+8),\n    beta(3n)=beta(9k+7).\n\nConsequently, with d(r)=|beta(r+1)-beta(r)| and\nh(n)=|beta(3n)-beta(n)|,\n\n    h(4k+3)=d(9k+7)=d((9n+1)/4).                 (SP1)\n\nThis is valid for every positive n≡3 (mod 4), including n=3. It is an exact\npullback of the neighbor boundary under the affine map k -> 9k+7, not an\nassertion that the boundary vanishes. The target progression is 7 (mod 9),\nand the source progression has density 1/4. The current peer drafts 0431 and\n0459 write the same formula, but root has not accepted those drafts and they\nare not independent provenance. The older BT8–BT9 archive gives only the\none-sided neighboring transport (b(4k+3)=b(6k+5)) and its d-consequence;\nSB7 and SB18 give related terminal and signed-cocycle identities. Thus the\ncomplete SP1 path calculation is best recorded as a direct manual check with\nlimited prior-art scope, without a novelty claim. It does not by itself prove\nfinite harmonic defect energy: it only transfers the question to d-mass on\none target progression.\n\nFor reference, the corresponding harmonic sum is exactly\n\n    sum_{n≤X, n≡3 (mod 4)} h(n)/n\n      = sum_{0≤k≤(X-3)/4} d(9k+7)/(4k+3).        (SP2)\n\nThe current peer draft 0431 writes the equivalent exact weight `9/(4q-1)`\nand a uniformly bounded summable denominator correction; that draft is not\nbeing used as an accepted input here. No estimate converting this restricted\ntarget progression into the full G(X)=sum_{r≤X, r odd} h(r)/r is present in\nthe accepted inputs.\n\n## Relation to the old 27 (mod 32) scratch identity\n\nThe shifted-pair scratch uses n=8r+3, j=9r+4, and the exact paths\n\n    n -> 12r+5 -> 18r+8 -> j,\n    3n -> 36r+14 -> 18r+7 -> 3j-1.\n\nHence h(n)=|beta(3j-1)-beta(j)|. If j≡3 (mod 4), the genuine two-path\nmerger from SC2 gives\n\n    beta(3j-1)=beta(j-1),\n\nbecause both j-1 and 3j-1 reach (3j-1)/4 in two shortcut steps. The\ncondition j=9r+4≡3 (mod 4) is r≡3 (mod 4), so n=32s+27 and j=36s+31.\nTherefore the scratch formula is\n\n    h(32s+27)=d(36s+30).                            (SP3)\n\nThe general identity SP1, applied to the same n=32s+27=4(8s+6)+3,\nsimultaneously gives\n\n    h(32s+27)=d(72s+61).                            (SP4)\n\nThus the two descriptions imply the genuine boundary relation\n\n    d(36s+30)=d(72s+61), s≥0.                      (SP5)\n\nSP5 is an equality of two boundary masks on this particular progression; it\nis not a generic identity d(2r)=d(r), nor does it imply that either side\nvanishes. The old scratch result is therefore a specialization with a second\nmerger representation, rather than an independent contradiction or a\nreplacement for the direct SP1 calculation.\n\n## Correction to the current peer comparison: a 1-mod-4 path\n\nAn earlier version of the 0459 peer draft rejected the auxiliary identity\n(h(16s+1)=d(18s+1)); that rejection was incorrect and is now corrected. Direct shortcut paths\ngive\n\n    16s+1 -> 24s+2 -> 12s+1 -> 18s+2,\n    3(16s+1)=48s+3 -> 72s+5 -> 108s+8 -> 54s+4 -> 27s+2,\n    18s+1 -> 27s+2.\n\nAll displayed transitions are genuine positive shortcut transitions. Hence\n\n    beta(16s+1)=beta(18s+2),\n    beta(3(16s+1))=beta(18s+1),\n\nand therefore\n\n    h(16s+1)=d(18s+1).                            (SP6)\n\nThis is a correction of the peer draft's scope, not a claim that the current\npeer draft is accepted or that SP6 is a new literature result. SP6 concerns a\ndifferent source class from SP1 and still supplies only a positive-growth\nboundary transport; by itself it gives no finite-energy or pointwise\nconclusion.\n\n## What the primary sources do and do not establish\n\nRozier–Terracol’s checked finite parity-vector/affine formula says that a\nfinite binary parity word determines a residue cylinder and an affine iterate;\nthe analogous coding statements are also present in Rozier’s 2-adic parity\nwork. These sources justify treating the displayed finite paths as genuine\nguarded affine branches and provide the standard parity-cylinder framework.\nThey do not state SP1, SP3, or SP5, and they do not impose any relation on an\narbitrary exact positive-integer invariant beta. The color equalities in SP1\nand SP3 instead come from direct shortcut invariance and the internal SC2\nmerger. Accordingly, I make no literature-priority or novelty claim for\nthese special identities.\n\nThe older archive already records the finite merger mechanism (R143 §2),\nthe zero family Z, the selected D-descent, and the one-sided BT8–BT9/SB\ntransports. The primary sources support only the finite parity/affine coding\nframework; they do not supply the special beta-color equalities.\nThose equalities are manual consequences of shortcut invariance and the\ninternal SC2 merger. The result does not prove G\u003c∞, pointwise constancy of\nbeta, positive density of a target set, or the full Collatz conjecture. The\nremaining obligation is a genuine estimate for the transferred d-mass (or\nanother correlation/coverage input); rephrasing the defect through SP1 does\nnot supply that estimate.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe all-n≡3 (mod 4) formula (SP1) is correct by direct path checking. The\ncurrent peer drafts 0431/0459 contain the same calculation, but are not\ntreated as accepted archival provenance. The present file checks its paths\nand its relation to the old 27 (mod 32) scratch restriction. On 27 (mod 32),\nthe old and new finite-path descriptions are both valid and yield SP5. The\nprimary sources support only the finite parity/affine coding framework,\nwhile the special boundary identities are manually derived. This is a real\nscope clarification for the remaining core, but it is a transport identity\nrather than a closure of the harmonic-energy or pointwise barrier.\n\n\n## Audit of the current A_k and Z_k^+ candidates\n\nThe following checks concern a current root candidate only; no current peer\ndraft is treated as accepted. Put a=4^k m, with m odd, and\n\n    n=1+16a=1+2^(2k+4)m,\n    q0=1+18a.\n\nThe exact paths\n\n    n -> 2+24a -> 1+12a -> 2+18a=q0+1,\n    3n -> 5+72a -> 8+108a -> 4+54a -> 2+27a,\n    q0 -> 2+27a\n\nshow beta(n)=beta(q0+1) and beta(3n)=beta(q0). With\ndelta(q)=beta(q+1)-beta(q), this gives\n\n    g(n)=-delta(q0).                              (SP7)\n\nFor q congruent to 1 modulo 4, the paths q -> 6r+2 -> 3r+1 and\nq+1=4r+2 -> 2r+1 -> 3r+2, with q=4r+1, prove\n\n    delta(q)=delta((3q+1)/4).                       (SP8)\n\nFor k>=1, the first k iterates in SP8 are legal because the intermediate\narguments are 1+18*4^(k-j)*3^j*m congruent to 1 modulo 4 for 0\u003c=j\u003ck.\nThus\n\n    F^k(q0)=1+18*3^k*m.\n\nThe guard 3^(k+1)*m congruent to 7 modulo 8 implies 3^k*m congruent to 5\nmodulo 8, so F^k(q0) is congruent to 43 modulo 48. Write this final\nargument as q=4r-1. Then r=(q+1)/4 is congruent to 11 modulo 12, and\np=(8r-1)/3 is a positive odd integer. The finite paths\n\n    q -> 6r-1 -> 9r-1,\n    3p -> 12r-1 -> 18r-1 -> 27r-1,\n    p -> 4r -> 2r -> r\n\ntogether with beta(9r-1)=beta(27r-1), from the SC2 common-endpoint\nidentity for 9r congruent to 3 modulo 4, show beta(q)=beta(3p) and\nbeta(q+1)=beta(r)=beta(p). The SC2 identity is not a direct T arrow. Hence\ndelta(q)=-g(p), and SP7-SP8 yield\n\n    g(1+2^(2k+4)m)=g(1+4*3^(k+1)m),\n    p-1=(3/4)^(k+1)*(n-1)\u003cn-1.                   (SP9)\n\nFor k=0, the F^k step is empty and the same guard directly gives q0\ncongruent to 43 modulo 48; the rest of the argument still applies. Therefore\nSP9 is a valid strict descent of the argument while preserving the signed\ng-value on the displayed classes, and therefore also preserving h=|g|. It\ndoes not force that value to vanish and does not cover all odd inputs.\n\nThe classes A_k are disjoint because v2(n-1)=2k+4. For fixed k, the guard\nchooses one odd residue m modulo 8, hence one residue class for n modulo\n2^(2k+7), of density 2^(-(2k+7)). Their disjoint union has natural density\n\n    sum_(k>=0) 2^(-(2k+7)) = 1/96,\n\nwith the infinite-union conclusion justified by finite partial unions and a\ngeometric tail bound.\n\nThere is also a valid zero-family calculation. For k>=1, put\n\n    n=1+2^(2k+3)m=1+8*4^k*m,\n    q0=1+9*4^k*m,\n\nwith m congruent to 3^(1-k) modulo 8. The paths\n\n    n -> 2+12a -> 1+6a -> 2+9a=q0+1,\n    3n -> 5+36a -> 8+54a -> 4+27a -> 2+(27a)/2,\n    q0 -> 2+(27a)/2,\n\nwhere a=4^k*m, give g(n)=-delta(q0). The same F-preservation applied k\ntimes gives F^k(q0)=1+9*3^k*m congruent to 4 modulo 8. The old BT11\nidentity d(8t+4)=0 therefore yields h(n)=0. These classes are disjoint by\nv2(n-1)=2k+3, and have total density\n\n    sum_(k>=1) 2^(-(2k+6)) = 1/192.\n\nThe A_k and Z_k^+ calculations are bounded exact residue-family results.\nThey do not establish that the remaining defect energy is finite, that all\nodd inputs are covered, or that the current root candidate is accepted.\n\n## Universal terminal lemma for q congruent to 7 modulo 12\n\nThe current root extension is also valid. Let q be any positive q congruent to\n7 modulo 12 and write r=(q+1)/4=2^a w with w positive odd. Then\n\n    beta(q)=C_(a+2)(w),    beta(q+1)=beta(w),\n    p_minus=(q-1)/6,       p_plus=(2q+1)/3.\n\nBoth p values are positive odd. The paths p_minus -> r and p_plus -> 4r\n-> r give beta(p_minus)=beta(p_plus)=beta(w), while\n3p_minus=2^(a+1)w-1 and 3p_plus=2^(a+3)w-1 give\n\n    beta(3p_minus)=C_(a+1)(w),\n    beta(3p_plus)=C_(a+3)(w).\n\nExactly one of the two congruence conditions, \"3^(a+1)w is 3 modulo 4\" and\n\"3^(a+2)w is 3 modulo 4\", holds. The SC2 merger at that index identifies the\ncorresponding adjacent C-values, so at least one of the two displayed\nalternative identities holds. If beta is constant, delta(q)=0 and both\nidentities can hold numerically; the conclusion is about the SC condition,\nnot uniqueness of an equality.\n\nThus\n\n    -delta(q)=g(p_minus) or -delta(q)=g(p_plus).       (SP10)\n\nThe edge q=7 has r=2 and gives p_minus=1 or p_plus=5 according to this same\ncase split, so positivity is retained at the smallest edge.\n\nFor every odd m and k>=0, the prefix argument\nq=1+18*3^k*m is 7 modulo 12. Applying SP10 to the preceding SP7-SP8\ntransport gives an argument p with\n\n    g(1+2^(2k+4)m)=g(p),\n    p \u003c= (3n+1)/4 \u003c n,\n    n=1+2^(2k+4)m.\n\nThus the earlier guarded A_k family extends to all odd m: it is a strict\nargument descent preserving g and h, on the disjoint family of density\n1/24. It still covers only the even values v2(n-1)>=4 and gives no finite\nG conclusion by itself.\n\nOn the subfamily 3^k*m congruent to 1 modulo 8, r is odd and r is 1 modulo\n4, so the p_minus branch is forced. Then\n\n    p=p_minus=3^(k+1)m,    p is 3 modulo 24.\n\nFor fixed target p in this branch, the source relation is\nn-1=(16/3)*(4/3)^k*p; hence the inverse harmonic scale between successive\nlevels is the geometric factor 3/4 (the exact weights 1/n have the harmless\n+1 denominator correction). This is the appropriate meaning of the\ninverse-coefficient claim; it is not an exact equality for 1/n. The current\n0431 draft discusses the corresponding full-weight correction, but remains a\npeer draft rather than accepted provenance.\n\nSP10 and the density computation are bounded exact-family facts. They do not\ncover the complementary odd inputs, and they do not imply finite defect\nenergy or pointwise constancy.\n\n## Scope audit of the current N partition and energy closure\n\nLet N be the full family n=1+2^(2k+4)m with k>=0 and m odd. Its selector is\nthe SC branch in SP10. For the minus branch, p=(q-1)/6=3^(k+1)m is odd and\ndivisible by 3. Summing its inverse branches at a fixed target gives the\nupper weight 3/(4p). For the plus branch,\np=(2q+1)/3=1+12*3^k*m is 13 modulo 24 and hence 5 modulo 8. Its inverse\nsources have the form\n\n    n=1+(4/3)^(k+1)*(p-1),\n\nso their total source weight is at most 3/(p-1). Since p>=13,\n3/(p-1) \u003c= 13/(4p). The selector can only remove branches, so these are\nvalid upper bounds even when all arithmetic inverse branches are summed.\n\nThe four source pieces are disjoint: N has v2(n-1) even and at least 4;\nZplus has v2(n-1) odd and at least 5; and Zold and B_(3:5) lie in 7 modulo\n8. The plus targets are 5 modulo 8, so they lie outside N, Zplus, Zold,\nand B_(3:5), hence in the corresponding core. The minus targets are odd\nmultiples of 3, whereas the LB B_(3:5) targets are 1 modulo 18 and are\ntherefore not divisible by 3. This target disjointness is the needed one for\ncombining the minus and B_(3:5) bounds; it is not a claim that all source\nfamilies are target-disjoint.\n\nWith Cstar equal to the positive odd integers after removing\nN, Zplus, Zold, and B_(3:5), the exact finite-cutoff decomposition and the\nseparate bounds give\n\n    G_Nminus + G_B_(3:5) \u003c= (3/4) G + 1/7,\n    G_Nplus \u003c= (13/4) G_Cstar,\n    G \u003c= G_Cstar + G_Nminus + G_Nplus + G_B_(3:5).\n\nConsequently\n\n    G \u003c= (17/4) G_Cstar + (3/4)G + 1/7,\n    G \u003c= 17 G_Cstar + 4/7.                          (SP11)\n\nThis arithmetic partition and inequality are valid as a scope result,\nassuming the previously stated LB B_(3:5) finite-cutoff estimate. They do not\nprove G finite: all remaining difficulty has been transferred to G_Cstar.\nThe current 0431 formulas are peer material for comparison, not accepted\nprovenance for this audit.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_DEFECT_RIGIDITY_BRIDGE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — defect rigidity bridge after finite variation\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\n\n## Scope and historical status\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`,\n`ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md`, the complete\n`SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md` (SB1–SB18), and\n`FOLLOWUP_0483_FINITE_VARIATION_POINTWISE_AUDIT.md`. I also used the primary\nsource scope already recorded in\n`FOLLOWUP_0483_MULTIPLICATIVE_RIGIDITY_SCOPE.md`: Boshernitzan's primary\ntext, Theorems 1.1–1.2, in the author-accessible copy\n\u003chttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/243065977_Elementary_proof_of_Furstenberg%27s_Diophantine_result>\nand the journal record\n\u003chttps://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9939-1994-1195714-X>, together with Rudolph's\nprimary paper\n\u003chttps://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/64243AD8323B37089540F911F8CC77EB/S0143385700005629a.pdf/2_and_3_invariant_measures_and_entropy.pdf>.\nThe earlier source report records the exact theorem scopes and their\nlimitations; this pass does not claim a new literature result.\n\nThe local `round170-qualitative-density-synthesis.md` records an earlier\ninternal acceptance of the H170/K170/N170/G170 interfaces, the R169 seed\ninput, and the named Tao mixing input. The present audit neither rechecks\nnor revokes that acceptance. It only preserves the fact that a complete\nanalytic/Lean dependency closure was not re-certified in this pass.\n\n## What the SB defect reduction says exactly\n\nFor an exact shortcut invariant beta, write\n\n    g(u) = beta(3u) - beta(u).\n\nThe SB report proves, with exact cutoff constants, that total harmonic birth\nenergy is finite if and only if\n\n    G(Y) = sum_{u \u003c= Y, u odd} |g(u)|/u\n\nis finite. This uses beta(2u)=beta(u), the R-column masks, and the identity\n`Q(Y)=G(Y/3)/3`; it is not merely a density estimate. In particular, finite\nG is a concrete extra hypothesis on the multiplicative 3-defect.\n\nDyadic invariance gives the exact relation\n\n    g(2u) = g(u).\n\nThus the nonzero set of g is a union of dyadic rays from odd representatives,\nand the contribution of one such ray is\n\n    sum_{k >= 0} |g(u)|/(2^k u) = 2|g(u)|/u.\n\nThis is the first obstruction to a pointwise conclusion from finite G: a\nnonempty dyadic ray has finite harmonic mass. The observation is only a\nlogical obstruction to the proposed implication; it does not construct a\nsecond exact Collatz invariant.\n\nFor odd u, SB18 gives the further exact identity. With\n\n    S(u) = (3u+1)/2,       v = (9u+1)/2,\n\none has\n\n    g(S(u)) - g(u) = beta(v+1) - beta(v).\n\nThe right side is an ordinary neighboring boundary term at the specified\naffine index. Summing absolute values therefore returns a subcollection of\nthe already assumed boundary variation. It does not force g(u)=0, and it\ndoes not provide a positive lower bound for the boundary created by a single\nnonzero g(u). In particular, the identity cannot be turned into pointwise\nrigidity by taking absolute values: the available estimate is summability of\nthe right side, not a reverse inequality for |g(u)|.\n\nThe same issue appears along S-iterates. A finite weighted sum of changes of\na binary quantity controls changes, but without a recurrence or a terminal\nvalue it does not determine the eventual value on every affine path. The\ndyadic identity and SB18 supply transport relations; the current archive\ndoes not supply the missing recurrence/return argument that would force the\ntransported value to be zero.\n\n## Why the primary multiplicative results do not close this gap\n\nBoshernitzan's checked theorem concerns the Furstenberg Diophantine\nstatement for closed sets invariant under multiplication by 2 and 3; its\ndense-orbit conclusion is a topological/closed-set result. Rudolph's checked\nmeasure theorem concerns invariant Borel probability measures and entropy,\nwith the zero-entropy regime left outside the needed rigidity conclusion.\nNeither theorem applies directly to an arbitrary binary integer coloring with\na sparse exceptional set and finite harmonic defect G. The exact identities\nabove also do not make the integer coloring a continuous function on the\n2-adic or logarithmic completion. Rozier/Tao parity or random-offset\ntransport, as recorded in the SB source report, supplies no reverse estimate\nfrom the transported defect to |g|.\n\nConsequently, finite G currently proves the already recorded almost-everywhere\nprofile constancy, but no pointwise constancy without an additional input.\nThis is an applicability boundary, not a claim that a Collatz-specific\nrigidity theorem cannot exist.\n\n## How weak a predecessor input would suffice?\n\nThe exact pointwise bridge is simple. Let N be the exceptional set after the\nfinite-variation profile argument. If N is nonempty and beta is exactly\ninvariant, ordinary-C invariance follows from\n\n    beta(C(n)) = beta(n):\n    C(n)=T(n) for even n, and beta(3n+1)=beta((3n+1)/2)=beta(n)\n    for odd n.\n\nChoose any n in N and move along its positive ordinary orbit to an eligible\ntarget a with 3 not dividing a. Then P_C(a), the ordinary predecessor set of\na, is contained in N. Therefore the following is sufficient and strictly\nmore targeted than an all-target theorem:\n\n    every nonempty exceptional invariant set N arising this way contains\n    an eligible a for which P_C(a) has positive lower natural density.\n\nMazur's Theorem 1.1 supplies the stronger fixed-target statement for every\npositive a with 3 not dividing a, subject to the dependency boundary recorded\nin the finite-variation audit.\n\nMere harmonic divergence is not a substitute. The condition\n\n    sum_{x in P_C(a)} 1/x = infinity\n\ncan hold for a natural-density-zero set, so it does not contradict N having\nnatural density zero. For example, a union of the intervals\n`[2^j, 2^j(1+1/j)]` has natural density zero while its harmonic sum diverges.\nThe same warning applies to an unquantified logarithmic\nor harmonic-density assertion: divergence alone does not provide a positive\nlogarithmic density. A positive logarithmic density would in fact contradict\nthe already established natural-density-zero property of N (by partial\nsummation, logarithmic upper density cannot exceed natural upper density),\nbut no such positive logarithmic lower bound follows from harmonic\ndivergence. Thus the usable weaker input must include an explicit quantitative\ncomparison, rather than just divergence of a predecessor harmonic series.\n\nA genuinely weaker but still sufficient replacement would be a quantitative\nboundary-transfer lemma of the following form: for every nonempty exact\ninvariant exceptional set N, or for every eligible target a selected from N,\nthe inclusion P_C(a) subset N forces a lower bound on\n\n    sum_{n in N} |1_N(n+1)-1_N(n)|/n\n\nthat contradicts the finite boundary variation of beta. No lemma of this\nform is proved in the current archive or supplied by the checked primary\nsources. It would have to use the exact beta/color structure; generic\nharmonic divergence alone cannot provide it.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nFinite SB defect G is a substantial multiplicative strengthening of\nnatural-density sparsity, and it is equivalent here to finite birth-energy.\nIts exact dyadic scaling and SB18 transport identity do not, by themselves,\neliminate isolated or sparse exceptional points. The missing pointwise bridge\ncan be supplied by the accepted conditional PD170/Mazur fixed-target lower\ndensity input, or by a new quantitative boundary-transfer lemma. No such\nPD-free lemma was established in this bounded pass. The historical internal\nH170/K170/N170/G170 acceptance remains recorded and is not altered here; no\nunconditional Collatz conclusion or novelty claim follows.\n\n## Independent audit of DF5–DF6\n\nI read the complete root candidate\n`DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md`, including DF1–DF6. The relaxed\ncounterexample in DF5 is valid in its stated class. For a finite set F of odd\nrepresentatives, define f on all positive integers by\n\n    f(n) = 0 if oddpart(n) is in F, and 1 otherwise.\n\nThen f is binary and exactly dyadically invariant. If F contains every odd\ninteger up to B, every n \u003c= B has oddpart in F, so this also realizes any\nfinite initial block of prescribed zero observations. Removing finitely many\nodd terms leaves the odd harmonic series divergent.\n\nFor odd n, a change f(U(n)) != f(n) requires n in F or U(n) in F. The first\nclass contributes sum_(n in F) 1/n. For a fixed odd target m, the inverse\ncolumns of U are n=(2^k m-1)/3 with the admissible powers k, and their\nreciprocal sum is the finite W(m) used in DF3. Hence\n\n    sum_(n odd) |f(U(n))-f(n)|/n\n    \u003c= sum_(n in F) 1/n + sum_(m in F) W(m) \u003c infinity.\n\nThis verifies DF6's claimed obstruction: even finite total U-change cost,\ndyadic invariance, binary range, and arbitrarily many finite zero observations\ndo not force finite harmonic mass for a general f. No representation of the construction as the defect of an exact beta\ninvariant has been established; compatibility with the full common-color\nconstraints is not asserted. It is therefore a limitation of the relaxed\ncoercivity argument, not a Collatz counterexample and not evidence that the\nactual defect h has finite or infinite energy.\n\nThe source/full-goal boundary in DF6 is also correctly stated. The SB\nequivalence reduces the actual problem to the genuine h or g and does not\nprove finiteness. A proof for the actual beta must add a common-color/channel\nproperty that excludes f_F, or a separate pointwise/predecessor estimate.\nSummable U-variation alone cannot supply that missing interface. The\nhistorical R170/H170/K170/N170/G170 internal acceptance remains preserved;\nthis audit neither re-certifies nor withdraws its analytic or Lean closure.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_EVEN_PRIMITIVE_ENERGY.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — even primitive births and compatibility with SG charges\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded manual audit only;\nno mathematical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, new source search, or\nagent was used. I read `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete\n`SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md` (SG1–SG12),\n`COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md` (BR1–BR7), and the full archival\n`FOLLOWUP_0431_ZERO_CLASS_BIRTH_TRANSFER.md`. The latter already contains\nthe local (n\\equiv0\\pmod4) transfer and its coefficient-three estimate;\nthis report audits its interaction with the SG primitive/image split rather\nthan claiming a new lemma.\n\n## 1. Exact transfer for (n=4m)\n\nFor an even birth (n=2s), exact invariance gives\n\n    β(s)=β(3s+2)≠β(3s+1).\n\nWith (n=4m), (s=2m), so the boundary\n\n    K=6m+1\n\nis active: (d(K)=1). The case (m=0) gives (n=0), outside the positive-\ninteger domain, so (m\\ge1) and (K\\ge7). (The separate exclusion (e(2)=0)\nconcerns the class (n\\equiv2\\pmod4).) Let (a) be the nearest birth ancestor of (K),\nobtained by repeatedly applying (\\lfloor\\cdot/2\\rfloor) through active\nboundaries. BR1 gives (a\u003c n), and in fact (a\\le3m). This is an exact\ncolor implication; it does not use a formal arithmetic candidate in place\nof the birth mask.\n\nThe weight identity is\n\n    n=4m=2(K−1)/3,\n    1/n=3/(2K)+3/(2K(K−1)).                         (EV1)\n\nDistinct (m) give distinct (K). At dyadic depth \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) from a\nfixed (a), there are at most two active boundaries and (K\\ge2^\\ell a).\nConsequently the main term in (EV1) is at most\n\n    Σ_(K→a) 3/(2K) ≤ (3/2)Σ_(ell≥1)2/ (2^ell a)=3/a.\n\nThe remainder telescopes over distinct (K\\ge7):\n\n    Σ_(K≥7)3/[2K(K−1)] ≤ 1/4.\n\nThus, for the complete (4m) class,\n\n    U_0(X):=Σ_(4m≤X)e(4m)/(4m) ≤ 3E_β(X)+1/4.       (EV2)\n\nThe exact identity (1/n=3/[2(K−1)]) is equivalent to (EV1), but using\nit directly would lose the telescoping remainder and give a weaker bound.\n\n## 2. Residues and the primitive/image split\n\nThe SG image is (I_S=\\{n:n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}). For (n=4m), membership\nin (I_S) is (m\\equiv2\\pmod3), since (4\\equiv1\\pmod3). Hence an\neven primitive SG origin in this class must have (m\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3),\nbut the transfer (EV1) itself is valid for every actual birth (e(4m)=1).\nThe active boundary always satisfies (K=6m+1\\equiv1\\pmod3); this residue\ndoes not force either of its two ancestry paths to have a fixed first bit.\n\nThere is no additional sign choice hidden in (EV1): all terms are positive,\nand the (1/4) bound is a global telescoping upper bound. The known\nexclusions (e(4r+2)=0) and (e(8r+1)=0) do not eliminate the (4m)\nbirth class. In particular, “outside (2\\pmod3)” is a statement about\nthe SG origin classification, not a new color restriction on (K).\n\n## 3. The correct joint bookkeeping with SG9\n\nThe transfer charges an even primitive birth (n=4m\\) to the nearest birth\nancestor (a) of the active boundary (K). That ancestor can be an SG\nprimitive origin, an SG image origin, or an interior birth in an S-component;\nthe origin sets alone do not partition all births. Positive upper bounds may\nstill be combined, but the partition must be stated correctly. Let\n\n    Q_odd = {odd births outside I_S},\n    V_even = {even births outside I_S}.\n\nEvery vertex in Q_odd is an S-component origin with chain mass at most three\ntimes its origin weight; every vertex in V_even is a singleton as an S-graph\nvertex because an even birth cannot be an S-source. The remaining S-component\norigins are D_S⊂I_S, whose full component masses are controlled by SG8.\nTherefore the valid disjoint upper bound is\n\n    E_β(X) ≤ 3E_(Q_odd)(X)+E_(V_even)(X)+(7/9)E_β(X/6). (EV3)\n\nThe even primitive transfer applies to V_even, but it also has to be\ncombined with the correct ancestor categories above. The SG9 bound\n\n    E_β(X)≤3E_(P_S)(X)+(7/9)E_β(X/6)\n\nalready counts the full masses of D_S-components via the (7/9) term. The\nearlier coarse form with P_S is safe but obscures this partition; one must\nnot add EV2 to it as though the same births were disjoint. Using the sharper\nancestor cutoff a≤3m=3n/4, the even primitive part obeys\n\n    E_(V_even)(X) ≤ 3E_β(3X/4)+1/4.              (EV4)\n\nConsequently EV3 gives the valid but noncontractive combined estimate\n\n    E_β(X) ≤ 3E_(Q_odd)(X)+3E_β(3X/4)\n             +(7/9)E_β(X/6)+1/4.                (EV5)\n\nThis does not close the primitive source. If (a∈D_S), the fact that (K)\nlies on an active dyadic ancestry path does not by itself put (K) inside an\nS-growth component, so SG5–SG8 supply no automatic extra bound. If (a) is an\ninterior S-component birth, the same issue is handled only by the full\ncomponent accounting in SG8, not by the origin labels. The congruences\n(n=4m\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3) and (K\\equiv1\\pmod3) do not resolve these cases.\n\nBefore using the additional colors in the next section, the one forced local\nfact is (d(K)=1). Whether (d(\\lfloor K/2\\rfloor))\nvanishes (giving an immediate birth at that parent) or remains active (giving\ncontinued ancestry) is exactly the BR split; neither branch is synchronized\nwith the SG forced child (2q) by residue information alone. The exact\nthree-mask synchronization is recorded next.\n\n## 4. Exact three-mask coupling at the boundary K\n\nThere is nevertheless a valid local coupling with the S-child masks. Since\ne(n)=1 implies e(2n)=e(2n+1)=0 by the XOR law, the birth n=4m gives the\ncolors, for some c,\n\n    β(K)=1−c,   β(K+1)=c,   K=6m+1.               (EV6)\n\nSet r=S(K)=(3K+1)/2. Writing x=β(2K+1), exact invariance gives\nβ(r)=1−c and β(2r+1)=β(2K+1)=x. Also β(2r)=β(r). Hence\n\n    e(r)=d(2K)·(1−d(r)),\n    d(2K)=1 iff x=c.                              (EV7)\n\nThus the exact alternatives are:\n\n1. e(S(K))=1 forces d(2K)=1, so the active child of K is the left child\n   2K. Since K is then not a birth, the S-image birth S(K) is an S-origin\n   and belongs to the SG D-type; its component can be charged by SG8.\n2. e(S(K))=0 with d(2K)=0 forces the right active child 2K+1.\n3. e(S(K))=0 with d(2K)=1 leaves the left active child 2K, but the\n   neighboring boundary at S(K) is active; this is a genuine third\n   possibility.\n\nTherefore the tempting equivalence e(S(K))=1 iff β(2K+1)=c is too strong:\nthe right side only gives d(2K)=1; one also needs d(S(K))=0. The corrected\nimplication is\n\n    e(S(K))=1 ⇒ left active child and S-origin.\n\nFailure of e(S(K)) does not force the right child. This coupling does not\nyet give a contraction, but it prevents an invalid addition of two\nindependent worst cases and identifies the exact mask that a stronger joint\ntransfer must control.\n\n## 5. What finite energy would and would not imply\n\nSG establishes only\n\n    E_β(X)≤3E_(P_S)(X)+(7/9)E_β(X/6),\n\nand therefore (E_β(X)\\le(27/2)E_{P_S}(X)) at finite cutoff. It does not\nprove finite total energy. Even if a separate argument supplied\n\\(\\sum e(n)/n\u003c\\infty\\), that alone is not the same as a proof that every\nactive boundary disappears: a summable birth set can still be infinite and\nits two active ancestry paths can be sparse. To deduce constancy of \\(\\beta\\),\none needs an additional theorem linking finite birth energy to absence of\nnonconstant exact T-invariants (or a direct universal smaller-birth/descent\nargument). The archived density routes are conditional interfaces and are\nnot re-established by EV1–EV2.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe exact even-primitive lemma is valid: (e(4m)=1) forces the active\nboundary (K=6m+1), a smaller birth ancestor (a\\le3m\u003c4m), and the\ntelescoping harmonic estimate (EV2). The correct bookkeeping is EV3–EV5;\nthe ancestor need not be an origin, and positive bounds may be combined only\nafter the Q_odd/V_even/D_S partition. EV6–EV7 give a genuine three-mask\ncoupling. In particular, an S(K) birth forces the left active child and an\nS-origin, but its failure does not force the right child. No contraction,\nconstancy, or full Collatz conclusion follows from this audit.\n\nThe later F-coding and R-inverse audit sharpen this only at origin level:\nthe R-failure contribution is at most (1/2 E_β(X/8)), and together with\nthe S-origin term the coefficient is (17/18). This does not bound finite\nacyclic component masses or the (n\\equiv1\\pmod3) origin source.\n\n## 6. Bounded-exponent/inverse-map source comparison\n\nThe proposed union of the one-odd-step map and the (4\\mid n) transfer has\nthe elementary arithmetic rules\n\n    S(q)=(3q+1)/2,   q odd,\n    R(n)=3n/4,       4\\mid n.\n\nTheir direct inverse equations are\n\n    S^{-1}(y)=(2y−1)/3,   y≡2 (mod 3),\n    R^{-1}(y)=4y/3,        y≡0 (mod 3).\n\nThe images are respectively (2\\pmod3) and (0\\pmod3), hence disjoint;\nthis is an arithmetic injectivity fact. It does not say that every inverse\nchoice is an actual birth transfer, because the required (e)-masks remain\npart of the hypotheses.\n\nFor comparison, the fully read local source audit\n`FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_RUN_SOURCES.md` records the standard odd-only\naccelerated map\n\n    S_odd(x)=(3x+1)/2^{ν₂(3x+1)}\n\nand the exact finite prefix (x_j+1=3^j2^{r-j}u). The primary Terras and\nEverett records cited there concern almost-all finite stopping/first\ndecrease, not a theorem that a fixed start follows every inverse branch or\nthat all inverse branches terminate. Their full proof texts were not\navailable in that pass; no stronger attribution is made here.\n\nIf one instead restricts to an odd target μ and the two possible odd outputs\n((3a+1)/2^j), (j=1,2), solving the affine equation gives\n\n    a=(2μ−1)/3  when μ≡2 (mod 3),\n    a=(4μ−1)/3  when μ≡1 (mod 3),\n\nwith no such odd inverse when (3\\mid μ). This is just the finite\nparity/integrality calculation; it is not an externally imported stopping\ntheorem. The (3\\mid μ) stop means only that these two odd inverse guards\nfail, not that another route, cycle, or long growth chain is impossible.\n\nThus the bounded-exponent inverse grammar is compatible with the archive's\nTerras/Everett scope and with the exact VR1–VR2 odd-run formulas, but no\nprimary result read here closes its boundary cycles or proves termination of\nall inverse words. The local mask formula (EV7), rather than a bare inverse\ncongruence, is required for an actual joint (R/S) transfer.\n\n## 7. Coding bounds for the partial union F\n\nFor the arithmetic envelope of the actual (R/S) graph, fix a forward word\nof length (k), containing (s) S-letters and (r) R-letters. The S guard\nuses one binary digit and the R guard (4\\mid n) uses two. Solving the\nsuccessive affine congruences backwards shows that this word selects at most\none residue class modulo (2^{s+2r}). Birth masks can only delete members.\nSumming the class counts over the (2^k) words gives\n\n    N_k^+(X) ≤ X(1/2+1/4)^k+2^k\n             = X(3/4)^k+2^k.                       (EV8)\n\nEvery vertex admitting arbitrarily long actual forward continuation survives\nevery such finite horizon; this includes vertices on directed cycles. Choose\n\n    k=floor(log_(8/3) X),   α=log(2)/log(8/3).\n\nUsing (2^k\\le X^α) and (X(3/4)^k\\le(4/3)X^α) gives the safe bound\n\n    N_+ (X)≤(7/3)X^α,\n    Σ_{n∈Γ_+}1/n≤7/[3(1−α)].                    (EV9)\n\nThe same argument backwards uses the two inverse guards (y\\equiv0,2\n\\pmod3). A fixed inverse word of length (k) selects at most one residue\nclass modulo (3^k), hence\n\n    N_k^−(X)≤X(2/3)^k+2^k.                         (EV10)\n\nWith (k=floor(log_3X)) and (γ=log(2)/log(3)), the elementary bound on\n(X/3^k\\in[1,3)) gives the deliberately loose estimate\n\n    N_−(X)≤(5/2)X^γ,\n    Σ_{n∈Γ_−}1/n≤5/[2(1−γ)].                      (EV11)\n\nThese are direct residue-class counts; they use no independence and no\nexternal parity-distribution theorem.\n\nThe arithmetic envelope is injective: the S and R images are respectively\n(2\\pmod3) and (0\\pmod3), and each branch is injective on its domain.\nTherefore every actual component of the partial graph is a finite chain, a\ndirected cycle, a one-sided infinite chain, or a two-sided infinite chain.\nEvery vertex admitting arbitrarily long forward (respectively backward)\ncontinuation lies in (\\Gamma_+) (respectively (\\Gamma_−)); directed cycles\nare included in both coding cores. Thus all births in one-sided, two-sided,\nor cyclic actual components have total harmonic mass bounded by the sum of the\ntwo finite constants in EV9 and EV11. Finite acyclic components can switch between\nthe increasing S branch and decreasing R branch and may have no uniform\norigin-weight bound from this coding alone. Consequently EV8–EV11 do not\nclose the finite-component primitive source or prove finite total (E_β).\n\n## 8. Independent audit of the R-inverse origin lemma\n\nFor a birth target (n=3m) in the R-image, put (q=4m), so (R(q)=n),\nand write (c=β(3m)=β(3m+1)). Exact paths give\n\n    β(q)=β(m),\n    β(q+1)=β(4m+1)=β(3m+1)=c,\n    β(3q+2)=β(6m+1)=1−c.\n\nTherefore\n\n    e(q)=1  iff  β(m)=c.                         (EV12)\n\nIf (m) is odd, (β(m)=β(3m+1)=c) by the ordinary odd shortcut step,\nso R-inverse failure can occur only for even (m). If (n=3m) itself is\nan even birth, the inherited (e(4r+2)=0) forces (4\\mid n), hence\n(4\\mid m). Write (m=2^h u), (h\\ge2), (u) odd, and (k=3u). Then\n\n    n=2^h k,\n    β(k)=c\\neβ(u)=β(k+1),\n\nso (d(k)=1) at the odd boundary (k). Its nearest birth ancestor obeys\n\n    b\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\u003cn/2^{h+1}\\le n/8.       (EV13)\n\nThe same odd (k) cannot arise at adjacent exponents (h): the\ncorresponding targets (2^h k) and (2^{h+1}k) are (n) and (2n), and\nthe XOR law gives (e(2n)=0) whenever (e(n)=1). Thus, for fixed (k),\n\n    Σ_{h\\ge2\\,\\mathrm{admissible}}1/(2^h k)≤1/(3k). (EV14)\n\nFor a fixed birth ancestor (b), the right active path starts at depth one\nand contributes at most (\\frac13\\sum_{\\ell\\ge1}(2^\\ell b)^{-1}=1/(3b))\nto (EV14); the left path starts at depth two and contributes at most\n(1/(6b)). Therefore all R-inverse-failure origins satisfy\n\n    E_{D_R}(X)≤(1/2)E_β(X/8).                     (EV15)\n\nTogether with the S-origin bound (E_{D_S}(X)≤(4/9)E_β(X/6)), and with\n(P_1) the births outside both arithmetic images ((n\\equiv1\\pmod3)),\nthe F-origin mass obeys\n\n    E_O(X)≤E_{P_1}(X)+(4/9)E_β(X/6)+(1/2)E_β(X/8). (EV16)\n\nThe coefficient (4/9+1/2=17/18) concerns origins only. It is not a bound\nfor the full mass of finite acyclic F-components; those components may\nalternate between S and R before terminating. Cycles are included in the\ncoding core because every vertex on a directed cycle has arbitrarily long\nforward and backward continuations. The inverse lemma is therefore a valid\norigin-level refinement, while a full finite-energy conclusion still needs\ncontrol of finite acyclic component masses and of (E_{P_1}).\n\n### Independent check of the R-inverse estimate\n\nThe three color identities used above are exact: the birth at 3m gives\n\n    beta(3m)=beta(3m+1)=c,   beta(9m+2)=1-c,\n\nwhile q=4m gives beta(q)=beta(m),\nbeta(q+1)=beta(3m+1), and\nbeta(3q+2)=beta(6m+1)=beta(9m+2). Hence (EV12) has no unstated\nparity assumption. If m is odd, the ordinary odd step indeed gives\nbeta(m)=beta(3m+1), so only even m can be an R-inverse failure.\nFor an even birth 3m, the previously established exclusion of births\ncongruent to 2 modulo 4 forces 4 divides m, yielding h=v2(m) at least 2.\n\nFor a fixed odd active boundary k, the allowed exponents h at least 2\ncannot be consecutive because the corresponding targets are n and 2n,\nand e(n)=1 forces e(2n)=0. Their total weight is therefore\n\n    sum_h (2^h k)^(-1) \u003c= (1/4+1/16+...)/k = 1/(3k).\n\nOn the two active paths from a birth b, odd k's occur at depths\nell at least 1 and ell at least 2, respectively. Thus the two path sums are\nat most (1/3) sum over ell at least 1 of (2^ell b)^(-1)=1/(3b) and\n(1/3) sum over ell at least 2 of (2^ell b)^(-1)=1/(6b), proving the 1/(2b)\ncharge in (EV15). Since b is at most n/8, truncation at n at most X gives\nthe stated E_beta(X/8) bound. This verifies the R-origin estimate, while\nleaving the finite-component and P_1 terms genuinely uncontrolled.\n\n## 9. Independent audit of SR1-SR12, with focus on SR4-SR9\n\nI read the complete `BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md`, not\nonly the displayed middle sections. The SR4-SR5 residue counts are valid:\na word with s S-steps and r R-steps fixes one class modulo 2^(s+2r),\nand a backward B-word fixes one class modulo 3^k. Summing the at most\nX/2^A+1 and X/3^k+1 representatives over 2^k words gives\n\n    N_k^+(X) \u003c= X(3/4)^k + 2^k,\n    N_k^-(X) \u003c= X(2/3)^k + 2^k.\n\nWith the stated floors, the first bound is at most (7/3) X^alpha,\nalpha=log(2)/log(8/3), and the second is at most (5/2) X^gamma,\ngamma=log(2)/log(3). The latter constant is deliberately loose: writing\nX=3^(k+theta), the two terms divided by X^gamma are\n(3/2)^theta and 2^(-theta), whose sum is at most 2, so 5/2 is safe.\nTonelli's identity sum_{n in A} 1/n = integral N_A(t)t^(-2) dt\nthen gives the stated finite harmonic constants; no prior convergence is\nbeing assumed.\n\nThe SR6 component classification is also correct for the retained graph:\ninjectivity and out-degree at most one leave finite chains, directed cycles,\none-sided infinite chains, and two-sided infinite chains. A cycle cannot have\nan external incoming tail because its vertices already have their unique\npredecessors. Every vertex outside a finite acyclic component has an\ninfinite forward or backward retained path and therefore belongs to the\ncorresponding arithmetic core. This proves only\nE_fin(X) \u003c= E_beta(X) \u003c= E_fin(X)+C_plus+C_minus. It does not bound the\nfinite-prefix residue classes, whose individual harmonic sums diverge.\nCycles are included in both cores because all their forward and backward\niterates remain defined.\n\nThe SR7-SR8 inverse-R calculation agrees with the earlier EV12-EV15 audit.\nFor a residue-zero birth n=3m, q=4m has e(q)=1 exactly when beta(m)=c.\nOdd m cannot be an inverse failure; an even birth n forces 4|m, giving\nm=2^h u with h>=2 and k=3u. The nearest birth ancestor satisfies b\u003cn/8.\nAdjacent exponents h are impossible because e(n)=1 implies e(2n)=0;\ntherefore each fixed k contributes at most 1/(3k). The odd k's on the two\nactive paths from b start at depths one and two, so their contributions are\nat most 1/(3b) and 1/(6b), respectively. Thus the 1/2 coefficient and\nE_D_R(X) \u003c= (1/2) E_beta(X/8) are justified.\n\nSR9's origin decomposition is exact because the S and R images are the\nclasses 2 and 0 modulo 3, while class 1 has no arithmetic predecessor.\nConsequently the 17/18 coefficient applies only to origins, after adding\nD_S and D_R estimates; it is not a contraction for total birth energy.\nFinite acyclic mixed components and P_1 remain uncontrolled. SR10's\nfamily is only an arithmetic-envelope obstruction and explicitly has\ne(y)=0 at the final R-output (the F-terminal is z=S(y)), so it is not\nan actual-birth counterexample.\nThis full audit introduces no new external theorem and does not establish\nconstancy, absence of cycles, or full Collatz convergence.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_FINITE_ENERGY_OBSTRUCTION.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — finite birth energy does not yet imply pointwise constancy\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded analytic audit;\nno programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or new agents were used.\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete SR1--SR12 file\n`BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md`, the complete CE interface\n`ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md`, its acceptance\n`REVIEW_0485_CRITICAL_BIRTH_ENERGY.md`, and the complete local proof\n`work/round174-n-summable-boundary-rigidity.md` (SB174.1--9, SB1--14).\nI also read the relevant exact-transfer text in\n`work/round175-h-boundary-transport.md` (BT175.1--12, BT1--20), the\nindependent finite-birth rigidity audit `work/round177-audit-finite-birth-rigidity.md`,\nand the accepted C180/R181 boundary-dependency audit\n`FOLLOWUP_0483_UNIFORM_BOUNDARY_DEPENDENCY_AUDIT.md`.\nFor source comparison I use the already recorded limited reading of Terras\nand Everett in `FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_RUN_SOURCES.md`: official primary\nrecords and indexed statements were checked, while the full PDFs were not\navailable in that pass. No broader source search is claimed.\n\n## 1. Exact implication of finite birth energy\n\nFor an exact binary invariant beta, write\n\n    d(n)=|beta(n+1)-beta(n)|,\n    e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1),\n    E_1(N)=sum_{n\u003c=N} e(n)/n,\n    D_1(N)=sum_{n\u003c=N} d(n)/n.\n\nThe exact XOR law is\n\n    d(2n)+d(2n+1)=d(n)+2e(n).\n\nThe CE finite-cutoff identity gives\n\n    0 \u003c= E_1(N)-D_1(N)/2 \u003c= 3/2.\n\nTherefore\n\n    sum_n e(n)/n \u003c infinity  =>  sum_n d(n)/n \u003c infinity.\n\nThis is also the BT175.14--15 nonnegative-series equivalence. No density,\nPD, or probabilistic input is involved in this step.\n\nThe SB174 profile theorem then applies. Its conclusion is exact and limited:\nthere is a color c such that\n\n    beta(n)=c outside a set of natural density zero.\n\nThe proof constructs the dyadic real L1 limit of\n`F_k(x)=beta(floor(2^k x))`, shows binary logarithmic-scale invariance\nunder multiplication by 3, and uses irrational rotation by log_2(3) to\nobtain a constant limit. The final return to integers is only a natural-\ndensity statement. It does not identify the values at the remaining\nexceptional integers.\n\n## 2. Why the pointwise step is genuinely absent\n\nFinite harmonic boundary energy permits an infinite sparse boundary. Even\nat the exact binary recurrence level, an active boundary d(k)=1 has one\nactive binary child, while a birth e(b)=1 starts two active paths. At depth\nell those paths contain at most two indices, each at least 2^ell b, so their\nharmonic contribution is bounded by a geometric series. Thus the local\nbirth graph does not turn finiteness of sum d(n)/n into emptiness of d.\nThe SR sparse-core bounds strengthen this for retained cycles and infinite\ncomponents, but leave finite acyclic component mass open; they do not supply\nthe missing pointwise conclusion.\n\nThe accepted C180/R181 input is stronger than natural density zero for the\nboundary: it gives zero upper Banach density for every fixed exact invariant.\nThat still does not imply that the boundary is empty. An infinite set can\nhave zero upper Banach density and finite harmonic weight; the residue-class\nargument in R181 gives no pointwise coverage of a prescribed exceptional\ninteger. C180 also has a fixed-shift Haar-almost-sure quantifier and does not\nsay that every integer pair coalesces or that the common endpoint reaches 1.\n\nThe accepted R177 result closes only the narrower case of finitely many\nbirths: a finite birth set forces beta to be constant. Finite harmonic\nenergy does not imply finitely many births, so R177 cannot be substituted\nfor the missing implication.\n\n## 3. The accepted conditional pointwise bridge and the PD-free gap\n\nQS170, with the named PD170 input, already supplies one accepted conditional\nbridge. Under PD170, every fixed positive ordinary-C target a not divisible\nby 3 has a predecessor set of positive lower natural density. If beta(n)\ndiffers from the SB174 constant c, divide n by powers of two to an odd u.\nThen a=3u+1 is positive, 3 does not divide a, and exact T-invariance gives\nbeta(a)=beta(u)=beta(n). Every ordinary predecessor of a has this same\ncolor. PD170 therefore gives a positive lower-density subset of the\nexceptional color, contradicting the density-zero conclusion. Hence\n\n    finite birth energy + PD170 => beta is pointwise constant.\n\nThis is an accepted conditional archive bridge; this pass did not re-audit\nthe full PD170 proof or QS170's full dependency chain. Without PD170, the\nremaining possible bridges are:\n\n1. A pointwise coalescence theorem saying that every prescribed integer is\n   connected, by a finite pairwise color-preserving path, to a set already\n   known to have the constant color. Fixed-shift Haar coalescence is not\n   enough; the quantifier must cover the prescribed integer.\n2. A direct theorem that every nonconstant exact binary T-invariant has\n   infinitely many births with a lower bound incompatible with finite\n   harmonic energy. The current birth graph proves neither such a lower\n   bound nor a universal descent.\n\nThe currently accepted local identities, including CE10 and the SR S/R\norigin estimates, retain unresolved masks and finite-component endpoints.\nThey do not establish either PD170 or a PD-free replacement.\n\n## 4. Primary stopping-time comparison\n\nThe limited primary-source check recorded in\n`FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_RUN_SOURCES.md` gives Terras's finite-stopping-time\nframework and Everett's almost-everywhere finite-decrease statement. Their\nscope is almost-all starting integers; it does not provide total convergence\nfor every fixed start, nor a pointwise predecessor-density theorem for the\nexceptional color. The exact odd-run formula and its valuation boundary are\nlocal identities, not imported universal stopping results.\n\nTherefore finite-stopping results do not remove the sparse exceptional set\nleft by SB174. A positive-density predecessor theorem or a genuinely\npointwise coalescence/descent theorem would still be required.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe checked theorem is\n\n    finite sum e(n)/n\n      => finite sum d(n)/n\n      => beta is constant outside a set of natural density zero.\n\nWith the accepted PD170/QS170 conditional input, the implication to beta\nbeing constant on every positive integer is proved by the bridge in section\n3. Without PD170, C180/R181, Terras/Everett almost-all stopping, and the SR\nsparse-core estimates do not supply pointwise constancy. This is a precise\nPD-free remaining obligation, not a proof that some nonconstant exact\ninvariant with finite energy exists.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_FIXED_SEED_DENSITY_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — fixed-seed density scope\n\nDate: 18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded source and analytic\naudit; no programs, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or builds. I read\nPROJECT_STATE.md, INTEGER_FIRST_HIT_DEPTH.md,\nPOSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md, and\nwork/round176-g-paired-orbit-literature.md. I also used the already-read\nprimary-source scope recorded in the local tree-source report, especially\nApplegate--Lagarias, Wirsching, and Elia--Tucker. This report does not\nrepeat the Tao route and makes no priority claim.\n\n## 1. The exact missing transition\n\nSuppose a minimal bad positive ordinary-Collatz start mu exists. After\nremoving initial powers of two, it is odd, and\n\n    a = C(mu) = 3 mu + 1\n\nis also bad. If b is the exact binary reachability color, then b(a)=0 and\nb(1)=1. The full paired set is\n\n    Q_C(a,1) = {m > 0 : C^r(m)=a and C^s(m+1)=1\n                         for some r,s >= 0}.\n\nThe accepted fixed-shift boundary input gives\n\n    Q_C(3 mu + 1,1) subset {m : b(m+1) != b(m)},\n\nand the latter boundary has upper Banach density zero. Thus the missing\nstep is a lower bound of the form\n\n    d_B^*(Q_C(3 mu + 1,1)) > 0,                         (FD1)\n\nfor the particular target pair generated by every possible minimal bad mu,\nor another pointwise rigidity statement of equal strength.\n\nOne witness m in Q is far from (FD1). Even infinitely many witnesses, a\npower-law count X^theta with theta \u003c 1, or a count of order log X is\ninsufficient. Upper Banach density requires intervals of unbounded length\non which a fixed positive fraction of sources occur:\n\n    limsup_(N -> infinity) sup_(u >= 0)\n       |Q_C(a,1) intersect {u+1,...,u+N}| / N > 0.\n\nMinimality supplies a lower cutoff on possible sources, but no repeated\nintervals, no positive fraction, and no correlation between m and m+1.\nPositive upper natural density would imply (FD1), while (FD1) is weaker\nbecause its witnessing intervals may be translated far from the origin.\n\n## 2. What fixed-target inverse results actually count\n\nThe primary Applegate--Lagarias source read for the tree comparison is\n*Density Bounds for the 3x+1 Problem I. Tree-Search Method*:\n\n[Applegate--Lagarias author PDF](https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/applegateI.pdf)\n\nIts fixed-target results count vertices/predecessors in one inverse tree:\nthe stated n_k(a) counts depth-k preimages, and the pi_a(x) estimate\ncounts integers up to x that ever reach one fixed target a. The reported\nbounds are aggregate one-target estimates. They do not count the\ncorrelated incidence\n\n    Pred_C(a) intersect (Pred_C(1) - 1).\n\nDepth/path multiplicity also matters. A deterministic forward orbit can\ngive the same endpoint at different depths when a target is periodic.\nConversely, a path count at a prescribed depth is not automatically a\ncount of distinct sources over all depths. The local resolvent audit\nhandles repetitions by a geometric cycle factor, but at q=1 it leaves the\nharmonic critical estimate unresolved. Neither endpoint counts nor path\ncounts from one tree impose the second condition on m+1.\n\nWirsching's primary paper, *On positive predecessor density in (3n+1)\ndynamics*, was read through its parity-path encoding, endpoint\ninjectivity qualification, and conditional density theorem:\n\n[Wirsching primary PDF](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf)\n\nIt gives a 3-adic/averaged predecessor framework and records the\nadditional uniform ratio input needed for positive density. It does not\ngive a two-target neighboring-source lower bound. The primary\nElia--Tucker paper gives finite coalescence and parity-word constructions,\nbut not (FD1):\n\n[Elia--Tucker, arXiv:1511.09141](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141)\n\nThe project’s accepted single-target positive-density interface, when its\nnamed internal dependencies are admitted, is a different route. It does\nnot remove the need for (FD1) in the direct full-Q route.\n\n## 3. Why marginal density, ternary mass, and binary mass do not supply FD1\n\nPositive densities of Pred_C(a) and Pred_C(1) separately do not imply a\npositive density, or positive upper Banach density, of their shifted\nintersection. The adjacency constraint is a two-point correlation. A\nfinite parity-word pair gives compatible residue classes for m and m+1;\nit does not create an infinite family of compatible words with a uniform\nlower count.\n\nThe accepted finite-subfamily inequality kappa_0 > 1 is an integral\nstatement for the unweighted 3-adic first-return operator:\n\n    integral L_0 C dmu_3 = kappa_0 integral C dmu_3.\n\nIt rules out a nonzero finite-integral nonnegative profile with\nL_0 C \u003c= C, subject to the profile hypotheses. It does not count\npositive integer endpoints, distinguish distinct sources from repeated\npaths, or enforce the second endpoint m+1 -> 1. In particular, it does\nnot imply (FD1).\n\nThe binary source-cylinder/Kraft weighting sum of 2^{-K} is a different\nmeasure from the ternary branch mass sum of 3^{-P}. A bound such as\nbinary mass at most 15/16 cannot be converted into a positive natural or\nBanach density of full Q. Conversely, kappa_0 > 1 is not a replacement\nfor a lower count: it is a profile/transfer obstruction.\n\n## 4. The minimality observation and the proposed inverse-slope claim\n\nFor a nonempty structural inverse word with at least one odd step to a\nfixed positive target n, write its source as\n\n    m = A_w n - B_w,       B_w > 0,\n\nwhere A_w = 2^K/3^P. Then\n\n    A_w = (m + B_w)/n > m/n.\n\nIf minimality gives m >= n for every relevant structural predecessor of the bad\nseed n=mu, then A_w > 1. This is exactly the elementary positivity\ninequality already recorded in the fixed-seed resolvent audit. It is a\nlower endpoint cutoff plus an individual slope bound, not a density\nestimate. It gives no upper bound on the number of admissible endpoints,\nno harmonic summability at q=1, and no information about whether m+1\nreaches the second target. The all-even and empty words have B_w=0; this\nscope distinction is essential because ordinary full-Q paths need not\ncontain a structural odd step.\n\nFor the target a=3mu+1 itself, minimality gives only m >= mu, so the\nsame inequality gives\n\n    A_w > mu/a,\n\nwhich is below 1. One must not silently use the target-mu slope\ninequality for a word ending at a. The valid forward relation is\nmu -> a, hence Pred(mu) is contained in Pred(a); there is no reverse\nstep a -> mu without an additional cycle assumption. A source path that\nreaches mu can be extended forward to a, but this does not turn an\narbitrary predecessor of a into a predecessor of mu and still does not\nimpose the neighboring condition on m+1.\n\nThe pointwise q>1 resolvent bound is correspondingly not a q=1 density\ntheorem. At q=1 the generic endpoint comparison is harmonic; its\ndivergence is a weakness of the comparison bound, not a proof that the\nactual resolvent diverges. Conversely, q>1 summability does not produce a\nlower count.\n\n## 5. Structural closure has an additional residue obstruction\n\nThe proposed structural-return route is even narrower than full Q. In\nthe accepted ordered-block guard calculation, every nonempty common-P\nblock has terminal consecutive outputs both nonzero modulo 3; hence its\nterminal seed must satisfy n congruent to 1 modulo 3. Therefore a target\nseed n not congruent to 1 modulo 3 has no nonempty structural inverse\nblock, so its structural predecessor closure is the singleton M_R(n)={n},\ncontaining only the empty schedule, and the structural resolvent is\nW_q(n)=1.\n\nMinimality gives mu congruent to 3 modulo 4. The possible residue types\nmodulo 12 are 3, 7, and 11; only the type 7 is 1 modulo 3. Thus a plan\nthat tries to obtain positive Banach density from the structural closure\nof every possible minimal bad mu is blocked for the two residue types\nmu congruent to 3 or 11 modulo 12. The full ordinary inverse tree still\nhas even predecessors (and can have asynchronous paths), so this\nstructural singleton does not say that full Q is empty. It only shows\nthat a new full-Q ingredient is required.\n\nThe related ordinary-tree fact that a target divisible by 3 has only\npower-of-two immediate predecessor chains is consistent with this\nresidue issue; it is not a joint neighboring-source theorem.\n\n## 6. Bounded verdict\n\nThe precise missing interface is a correlated, distinct-source lower\nbound for the full pair Q_C(3mu+1,1), preferably positive upper Banach\ndensity (FD1). Fixed-target endpoint counts, path counts, q>1 resolvent\nfiniteness, ternary mass over one, and binary Kraft bounds each address a\ndifferent object and do not imply it.\n\nThe proposed slogan “every positive predecessor m >= mu has inverse\nslope greater than one” is a direct consequence of minimality and the\npositive affine intercept for the inverse tree to mu. It is useful\nbookkeeping but does not advance the missing pair-density transition\nwithout a new correlation or coverage theorem. No inspected primary\nsource supplies that theorem. This is a bounded source-scope conclusion,\nnot a claim of complete literature absence and not a proof or\ndisproof of Collatz.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_GENERAL_BLOCK_SOURCES.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483_GENERAL_BLOCK_SOURCES — fixed odd count and block length\n\nДата: 18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный первичный\nисточниковый проход, без программ, CAS, переборов и Lean. Прочитаны\n`PROJECT_STATE.md` и\n`outputs/collatz-four-methods/team1000/FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md`.\nИз Elia--Tucker проверен полный доступный HTML-текст, включая Definitions\n1--2, Proposition 1, Theorem 4.1 и §5:\n[arXiv:1511.09141](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141). Из LaTourette\nпрочитан доступный landing-page OCR §4.2.1--4.2.4 и Table 4.1--4.2:\n[*Explorations of the Collatz Conjecture*, 2007](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college).\nGarner проверен только по издательской странице и abstract; full text\nвозвращал 403:\n[Garner, *On heights in the Collatz 3n+1 problem*](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X85800200),\nDOI [10.1016/S0012-365X(85)80020-0](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-365X(85)80020-0).\n\n## 1. Точный статус первичных результатов\n\nПоправка от 18 сентября 2026: прежняя формулировка смешивала две\nориентации. LaTourette Definition 2 задаёт ordered block identity\n\\[\n       T_b(x)+1=T_{b'}(x+1),\n\\]\nтогда как Definitions 4--5 задают string/string prefix в обратной\nориентации\n\\[\n       T_q(x)=T_{q'}(x+1)+1.\n\\]\nTheorem 7 (OCR landing, примерно строки 2041--2112) даёт общий iff-тест\nдля ordered block: displacement, полученный перестановкой позиции каждой\nединицы, должен удовлетворять affine gap equation\n\\[\n       2^k\\Delta=2^k-3^n,\n\\]\nгде \\(\\Delta=T_{b'}(x)-T_b(x)\\). Для reversed string orientation, если\n\\(\\Delta=T_{q'}(x)-T_q(x)\\), знак меняется:\n\\[\n       2^k\\Delta=-2^k-3^n.\n\\]\nOCR повреждён, поэтому нельзя импортировать одну формулу для обеих\nориентаций; iff-формулировки должны оставаться раздельными.\nДля движения t-й единицы на \\(r_t\\) позиций из стартовой позиции\n\\(a_t\\) Lemma 6 даёт вклад\n\\[\n       \\Delta_t=\n       \\frac{3^{n-t}}{2^{k-a_t+1}}(2^{r_t}-1),\n\\]\nсо знаком, задаваемым направлением движения. Это символический\nкритерий admissibility, а не finite-state classification по числу единиц.\n\nВ §4.2.4 LaTourette доказывает только крайние случаи:\n\n- Theorem 8: при одном нуле, то есть \\(n=k-1\\), единственный block или\n  string prefix имеет длину 2 и слова \\(10/01\\);\n- Theorem 9: при одной единице \\(n=1\\) также возможна только длина 2,\n  слова \\(10/01\\).\n\nПосле этого текст говорит, что аналогичные доказательства можно писать\nдля двух и трёх единиц, но число случаев быстро растёт и метод становится\nнеразумным уже существенно дальше трёх единиц (OCR примерно\n2305--2314). В Tables 4.1--4.2 приведены конечные данные; затем\nConjecture 2 утверждает наблюдаемое соотношение\n\\[\n       \\frac13\\le \\frac{n}{k}\\le\\frac7{11},\n\\]\nи среднее \\(n/k\\approx5/11\\). Это явно conjecture, основанная на\nтабличных данных, а не доказательство универсальной границы \\(k\\le3n\\).\n\nTable 4.1 уже содержит видимые пары с \\(n=3\\), включая\n\\(1101000/0011001\\), \\(11100000/00011001\\) и\n\\(11001000/00100101\\). Поэтому эти P=3 examples являются prior art;\nих наличие не следует трактовать как новые пары текущего разбора.\n\nElia--Tucker используют Definitions 1--2 для различения coalescing\nstems и block prefixes. Их Theorem 4.1 даёт бесконечное семейство\nконтрпримеров к conjectural классификации Garner stems через Terras\nparity-prefix bijection. Он не утверждает fixed-odd-count bound для\nblock prefixes, не доказывает \\(k\\le3n\\) и не является S-unit или\nfinite-state classification.\n\nGarner's издательский abstract сообщает, что пары consecutive integers\nодинаковой height встречаются бесконечно часто и в бесконечно многих\npatterns, и что используется algorithm восстановления trajectory из\nparity vector. Full article недоступен в этом проходе, поэтому нельзя\nчестно вывести из него точную теорему о bounded length при фиксированном\nчисле единиц, irreducibility или counterexample к \\(k\\le3n\\).\n\n## 2. Независимый общий intercept bound\n\nДля shortcut word длины \\(K\\), с odd positions\n\\(0\\le i_1\u003c\\cdots\u003ci_P\u003cK\\),\n\\[\n       C(I)=\\sum_{r=1}^{P}3^{P-r}2^{i_r},\n       \\qquad T_I(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C(I)}{2^K}.\n\\]\nДля ordered gap-one pair с теми же \\(K,P\\) affine equality требует\n\\[\n       C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nБез перебора доступны только экстремальные bounds:\n\\[\n C_{\\min}=3^P-2^P,\n \\qquad\n C_{\\max}=2^{K-P}(3^P-2^P).\n\\]\nСледовательно, при положительной правой стороне\n\\[\n 2^K-3^P\n \\le (3^P-2^P)(2^{K-P}-1).                    \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nДля \\(P=1\\) этот bound уже асимптотически исключает большие \\(K\\); для\n\\(P\\ge2\\) правая сторона недостаточно мала и не даёт общего конечного\nограничения по \\(K\\).\n\nParity refinement также не решает общий случай. Intercept нечётен тогда\nи только тогда, когда первая odd position равна нулю. Если левый\nintercept чётен, минимальный такой intercept равен\n\\(2(3^P-2^P)\\), а максимальный нечётный правый равен\n\\[\n 3^{P-1}+2^{K-P+1}(3^{P-1}-2^{P-1}).\n\\]\nЕго главный коэффициент при \\(2^K\\) равен\n\\((3/2)^{P-1}-1\\): для \\(P=1,2\\) это даёт сильную one-sided\nobstruction, использованную в принятом P\\(\\le2\\) proof, но для \\(P\\ge3\\)\nкоэффициент уже не меньше \\(5/4\\). Значит, extremal magnitude/carry\nbound сам по себе не доказывает конечность \\(K\\) при фиксированном\nпроизвольном \\(P\\). Требуется дополнительная congruence или exact carry\nanalysis.\n\nЭтот вывод не является counterexample к conjectural \\(k\\le3P\\): он лишь\nпоказывает, что одна общая оценка диапазона intercepts такую границу не\nвыводит. В прочитанном первичном scope доказанного counterexample к\n\\(k\\le3P\\) не найдено; это не утверждение мирового отсутствия.\n\n## 3. Irreducibility и infinite families\n\nLaTourette distinguishes strings (prefixes with proper-prefix\nseparation) from the broader string-prefix/block condition, но Theorem 7\nпроверяет сам affine condition и не даёт общей factorisation theorem.\nElia--Tucker's new stems are coalescence objects, not an irreducible\nclassification of all block prefixes. Garner's abstract says “infinitely\nmany different patterns” for equal-height consecutive pairs, but that is\nnot a theorem about infinitely many irreducible fixed-\\(P\\) block prefixes;\nfull article access would be needed to inspect the exact definitions and\nscope.\n\nNo simple carry finite-state closure or S-unit argument was found in the\nactually read primary text. The available exact criterion is the finite\nword intercept/displacement equation above. It can validate a proposed\nword pair and handle extreme one-bit cases, but it does not supply the\nmissing uniform length bound for arbitrary fixed \\(P\\).\n\n## 4. Bounded verdict and access limits\n\nThe primary material supports the following limited conclusions:\n\n1. Block/string prefixes and their exact affine criterion are established\n   prior structures; the current P=3 table entries are already visible in\n   LaTourette.\n2. LaTourette proves only the one-one and one-zero length-two extremes,\n   then states a data-based ratio conjecture. The text does not provide a\n   completed P=3 or general fixed-P length classification in the inspected\n   scope.\n3. Garner's abstract supports infinite equal-height patterns, but the\n   exact full theorem text was inaccessible. No bounded-length or\n   irreducibility assertion is imported from it.\n4. The intercept extremal calculation gives (2.2) and the parity\n   refinement, but no universal \\(K\\le3P\\) theorem and no counterexample\n   to that conjectural bound.\n\nLaTourette's landing OCR is readable at the URL below; direct page\nendpoints were intermittently cache-missed and the ePaper download was\nunavailable. OCR formulas are used only where their assumptions and\nmeaning are clear from the surrounding proof. No claim of complete\nliterature absence or world priority is made.\n\n## 5. The \\(7/11\\) row: string reversal, not an ordered block\n\nThe landing OCR makes the distinction visible. Around lines 1363--1417 it\nsays that Table 4.1 is the “complete list of unique blocks (no prefixes)\nup to length 11” and displays the final block rows. No displayed block has\nseven ones in length 11; the largest relevant rows have six ones. Thus the\nsource does not show an ordered block with \\(P=7,K=11\\) in Table 4.1.\n\nTable 4.2 is different. Around lines 1461--1516 it displays the final\ncorresponding-string row\n\\[\n       q=01111100011,\n       \\qquad q'=11111101000,\n\\]\nmarked with an asterisk. Both words have length 11 and exactly seven\nones. Direct intercept calculation gives\n\\[\n       C_q=6358,\\qquad C_{q'}=2123,\n       \\qquad C_q-C_{q'}=4235=2^{11}+3^7.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n       T_q(x)=T_{q'}(x+1)+1,\n\\]\nwhich is the string-prefix orientation. It reverses the ordered pair:\nthe outputs from \\((x,x+1)\\) are \\((m+1,m)\\), not \\((m,m+1)\\). Its\ninverse slope magnitude is\n\\[\n       \\alpha=2^{11}/3^7=2048/2187\u003c1,\n\\]\nbut this contraction belongs to the string reversal, not to an ordered\nblock in Table 4.1.\n\nThe reversal can be squared into an ordered pair. Writing\n\\(A=T_q\\), \\(B=T_{q'}\\), the identity is \\(A(x)=B(x+1)+1\\). If\n\\(m=B(x+1)\\), applying \\(q'\\) to the left output \\(m+1\\) and \\(q\\) to\nthe right output \\(m\\) gives\n\\[\n       (B(m+1),A(m))=(z,z+1).\n\\]\nHence the composite ordered words are \\(qq'\\) and \\(q'q\\), of length\n22 and odd count 14, with inverse slope\n\\[\n       2^{22}/3^{14}=\\alpha^2\u003c1.\n\\]\nUsing the composition rule for intercepts, their forward constants are\n\\[\n C_{qq'}=3^7C_q+2^{11}C_{q'}=18{,}252{,}850,\n \\qquad\n C_{q'q}=3^7C_{q'}+2^{11}C_q=17{,}664{,}185,\n\\]\nand their difference is\n\\(C_{q'q}-C_{qq'}=-588{,}665=2^{22}-3^{14}\\), confirming the\nordered affine gap exactly. The single-reversal sample below should not\nbe confused with a sample on the composite source cylinder.\nFor the one-reversal parameterization, the exact manual inverse relation\nis\n\\[\n       S(m)=\\frac{2048m-4310}{2187},\n       \\qquad m\\equiv205\\pmod{2187};\n\\]\nindeed \\(S(205)=190\\), and the pair \\(190,191\\) maps to \\(206,205\\)\nunder \\(q,q'\\), respectively. This confirms the reversed orientation and\nshould not be relabeled as the ordinary ordered block inverse.\n\nThe source conclusion is therefore precise: there is no visible \\(P=7\\),\n\\(K=11\\) ordered block in the complete Table 4.1 list, but there is an\nexplicit \\(P=7,K=11\\) corresponding string in Table 4.2, and two such\nreversals yield an ordered \\(P=14,K=22\\) composition with inverse\ncontraction. This is prior source material, not a novelty claim.\n\nURLs:\n\n- [LaTourette landing OCR](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college)\n- [LaTourette page 29/Table 4.1](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college/29)\n- [Elia--Tucker primary HTML](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141)\n- [Garner publisher abstract](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X85800200)\n- [Garner DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-365X(85)80020-0)\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_INVARIANT_COLOR_SOURCE_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — exact invariant/color masks: primary-source scope\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-19, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05).  **Role:** bounded primary-source and logic audit.  No programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or state/map edits.\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete WS1–WS9 file\n[`WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md`](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md), and the local definitions/interfaces in\n[`ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md`](ACTUAL_BIRTH_CRITICAL_ENERGY_INTERFACE.md),\n[`BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md`](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md), and\n[`COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md`](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md).\n\nFor external comparison I read the arXiv HTML of Olivier Rozier, *Parity sequences of the 3x+1 map on the 2-adic integers and Euclidean embedding*, arXiv:1805.00133v6 (18 February 2025; journal reference *Integers* 19 (2019), A8), at:\n\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1805.00133>\n\nThe inspected parts were the abstract/introduction, Definitions 1–5, Lemma 1, Theorem 1, Corollaries 1–2, the 2-adic parity-sequence and conjugacy discussion, Theorem 2 and its functional-equation proof, and the surrounding ergodicity/cycle remarks.  I also reread the relevant Sections 1–2 of Olivier Rozier and Claude Terracol, *Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences*, arXiv:2502.00948v5 (17 May 2026):\n\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948>\n\nThat second source was inspected for its parity-vector definition, the affine iterate formula, stopping-time/remainder distinctions, the parity-vector partial order, and Theorem 2.4/Lemma 2.5.  No computational results or heuristic conclusions from either paper are imported.  This is a targeted source check, not an exhaustive priority survey.\n\n## The local object and the exact logical target\n\nThe project fixes an arbitrary exact binary invariant\n\n\\[\n  \\beta:\\mathbb N_{>0}\\to\\{0,1\\},\\qquad \\beta(Tn)=\\beta(n),\n\\]\n\nfor the shortcut map $T(n)=(3n+1)/2$ on odd $n$ and $T(n)=n/2$ on even $n$.  It then defines\n\n\\[\n d(n)=|\\beta(n+1)-\\beta(n)|,\n \\qquad e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1),\n \\qquad E_\\beta(X)=\\sum_{n\\le X}e(n)/n.\n\\]\n\nThus $d$ is a boundary mask of an orbit-invariant coloring, while $e$ records a birth where the two children of a zero-boundary interval become active.  The exact local identities used in CE/BR/WS, for example\n\n\\[\n d(2n)+d(2n+1)=d(n)+2e(n),\n \\qquad e(n)=1\\Longleftrightarrow\n \\beta(n)=\\beta(n+1)\\ne\\beta(3n+2),\n\\]\n\nare consequences of the invariant and elementary binary interval splitting.  WS estimates selected terminal and origin subfamilies, but retains the primitive class $P_1$ and the full internal energy.\n\n## What the primary parity literature actually supplies\n\nRozier’s Definition 1 is the finite parity vector\n\n\\[\n V_j(n)=(n,T(n),\\ldots,T^{j-1}(n))\\pmod 2.\n\\]\n\nThe paper states and proves the standard inverse-transform congruence: a prescribed length-$j$ binary parity word is one residue class modulo $2^j$, and every finite word occurs on integers.  Its Lemma 1 gives the explicit residue in terms of partial odd-counts; Theorem 1 gives the complementary inverse expression.  The 2-adic extension makes the infinite parity map $V_\\infty$ a bijection with binary sequences.  Definition 5 packages this as the 2-adic parity coding $Q$, an isometry, and the conjugacy\n\n\\[\n T=Q^{-1}\\circ\\mathcal S\\circ Q\n\\]\n\nwith the binary shift $\\mathcal S$.  Theorem 2 records functional equations such as $Q(2x)=2Q(x)$ and a guarded equation for $Q(2x+1)$.  These are statements about parity coding and 2-adic dynamics.  They do not define, or constrain, an arbitrary positive-integer coloring $\\beta$ that is constant along $T$-orbits.\n\nRozier and Terracol use the same parity-vector cylinders and write every finite iterate as\n\n\\[\n T^j(n)=\\frac{3^q}{2^j}n+E_j(n),\n\\]\n\nwhere $q$ is the number of odd entries and the remainder depends on the parity vector.  Their Theorem 2.4 controls extremal remainders for a fixed length and odd count; Lemma 2.5 gives the arithmetic mean of the remainder over one complete length-$j$ period.  These results provide exact cylinder/residue bookkeeping.  They do not produce a bound on the boundary mask $d(n)$, the birth mask $e(n)$, the primitive $P_1$ class, or $E_\\beta$.\n\nThe distinction is substantive.  A parity vector records the orbit of one starting integer for a finite time.  The local mask compares two neighboring starting integers after an invariant coloring has already been chosen.  A parity-cylinder count therefore bounds how many sources realize a prescribed orbit word, but does not say whether the two source colors agree, nor whether a word contributes to (P_1).  The 2-adic bijection likewise permits arbitrary parity sequences in the 2-adic extension; it does not supply a positive-integer realization of a nonconstant exact \\(\\beta\\) with the required mask correlations.\n\n## Consequence for P1 and the full-energy route\n\nThe WS identities are compatible with all of the cited parity coding.  In particular, the derived equality $d(8t+4)=0$, the A/B active-boundary transitions, and the terminal/origin charge inequalities are local consequences of exact invariance; they should not be attributed to Rozier's $Q$-functional equations.  Conversely, no inspected primary theorem turns parity-cylinder regularity into a sparsity theorem for the invariant-color boundary.\n\nThe unresolved class $P_1=\\{n:e(n)=1,\\ n\\equiv1\\pmod 3\\}$ is precisely the obstruction to promoting\n\n\\[\n E_{\\rm origins}(X)\\le E_{P_1}(X)+(241/378)E_\\beta(X/6)\n\\]\n\nto a contraction of the full $E_\\beta$.  A coefficient below one for terminal points or for $D_S\\cup D_R$ is only a subfamily estimate.  It does not bound the mass of all internal vertices, and it does not bound $P_1$.  The inspected parity sources give no theorem that would remove this term.\n\nThe CE implication remains the correct scope: an independently proved finite \\(E_\\beta\\) would force \\(\\beta\\) to be constant outside a set of natural density zero by the local dyadic/rigidity argument.  Pointwise constancy still needs a separate bridge (for example the project’s named conditional positive-density input).  Nothing in the inspected parity/conjugacy literature supplies that bridge or a direct finite-energy bound.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe primary sources provide a precise and useful language for finite parity words, affine remainders, congruence cylinders, and 2-adic conjugacy.  They are relevant background for checking the arithmetic A/B tree calculations, but they do not yield a theorem controlling the exact binary invariant \\(\\beta\\), its boundary/birth masks \\(d,e\\), the primitive (P_1) births, or the full critical energy.  The remaining required input is therefore genuinely a mask-correlation or descent statement: a uniform sparsity/energy bound for (P_1) and the internal retained graph, or a separate pointwise descent proving that a nonconstant exact invariant cannot exist.  This report makes no claim that such a result is absent from all literature and makes no novelty or priority claim.\n\n**Bounded conclusion:** the inspected primary parity results do not close WS/CE; they also do not contradict the local WS identities.  The exact mismatch is “parity-word/cylinder information” versus “neighbor-color mask information.”\n\n## Additional Dirichlet/functional-equation source boundary\n\nAs a scope check I also attempted the repository copy of Günter Meinardus,\n*Some Analytic Aspects Concerning the Collatz Problem*:\n\n\u003chttps://madoc.bib.uni-mannheim.de/1608/1/261_2001.pdf>\n\nand its repository landing page \u003chttps://madoc.bib.uni-mannheim.de/1608/>.  Both were blocked by the repository's JavaScript proof-of-work page in this pass, so I did **not** read the paper's theorem statements or proofs and import none of its claimed Dirichlet identities.  The accessible metadata page for the related Berg–Meinardus paper,\n*The 3n+1 Collatz Problem and Functional Equations*, identifies it as Rostocker Mathematische Kolloquium 48 (1995), pp. 11–18, and describes functional-equation formulations:\n\n\u003chttps://www.math.uni-rostock.de/math/pub/romako/heft48/berg2.html>\n\nThat abstract-level metadata is not evidence for a bound on $E_\\beta$.  In particular, an analytic/Dirichlet series built from parity or orbit coefficients would be a different object from the nonnegative birth series $\\sum e(n)/n$ and from the neighbor-color mask $d(n)$.  Any transform relating those series would require its exact hypotheses and proof; because the primary PDF was inaccessible here, no such relation is used.  This access limitation narrows the source assessment and is not a claim about the contents or priority of those papers.\n\n## Full audit of PC4–PC6 and PC11–PC14\n\nI also read the complete root candidate\n[`PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md`](PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md), and checked its PC4–PC6 and PC11–PC14 against the CE/SR/WS definitions already read above.  The checks below are an audit of the displayed algebra, not an independent proof of the inherited CE/SR graph lemmas.\n\n1. **PC4 and PC5.**  With $W=(1,2,1/4)$, the three recurrence right sides are respectively $1$, $3/2+1/2=2$, and $1/4$; hence all three PC4 inequalities are equalities.  For an even birth $n=4m$, $q=6m+1\\ge7$, and\n   \\[\n     \\frac1n=\\frac{3}{2q}+\\frac{3}{2q(q-1)}.\n   \\]\n   Distinctness of the $q$'s and the telescoping integer upper sum give the displayed correction at most $1/4$.  The right initial boundary $q=2b+1$ gives coefficients $1,1/2,1/8$ for $b\\equiv0,1,2\\pmod3$.  For a first left odd boundary $2^\\ell b+1$ with $\\ell\\ge2$, its even/odd $ell$ residues give left coefficients $1/2,1/8,1/4$ in the same three birth classes.  These are consistent with the stated potential recurrence and the cutoff $b\\le q/2=(3n+2)/4$.\n\n2. **PC6 scope.**  The resulting inequality\n   \\[\n   E_{\\rm even}(X)\\le \\tfrac32E_0(Y)+\\tfrac58E_1(Y)+\\tfrac38E_2(Y)+\\tfrac14,\n   \\qquad Y=(3X+2)/4,\n   \\]\n   is a valid even-birth/internal-vertex estimate conditional on the local SR/WS ancestry facts.  Its largest coefficient is $3/2$; it is therefore not a contraction and says nothing by itself about the complementary odd primitive class or total $E_\\beta$.  The correction is a distinctness/telescoping estimate, not a density estimate.\n\n3. **PC11–PC12.**  Dividing the absolutely convergent PC7–PC9 identities by $E_s$ and taking a subsequence as $s\\downarrow1$ gives\n   \\[\n     a+b=1,\\qquad 3o=a-b+2z_S+4z_R.\n   \\]\n   If terminal and origin ratios vanish along that subsequence, PC7 gives residue limits $E_0/E_s\\to2/3$, $E_1/E_s\\to0$, and $E_2/E_s\\to1/3$, with odd/even mass $a=b=1/2$.  The displayed scalar allocation in PC4 satisfies these equations, but it is only a feasible assignment of limiting ratios; it is not a coloring, a birth set, or a Collatz counterexample.\n\n4. **PC13–PC14 and the weakened singularity.**  For each fixed $k$, the forward prefix classes have total density $(3/4)^k$ and at most $2^k$ residue representatives, giving the stated $H_k(s)$ bound.  The first-terminal transport factor $[2(3/2)^j]^s$ yields the displayed finite $K_k(s)$.  More explicitly, if $\\delta_s=K_k(s)Z_s/E_s\\to0$, PC14 rearranges to\n   \\[\n   (s-1)E_s\\le\n   \\frac{2^k(s-1)+(3/4)^k}{1-\\delta_s}.\n   \\]\n   Thus along that same sequence $\\limsup (s-1)E_s\\le(3/4)^k$, and then $k\\to\\infty$ gives $(s-1)E_s\\to0$.  The reverse-prefix argument under $O_s/E_s\\to0$ has the analogous role.  This is only a weakened singularity conclusion under a relative endpoint/origin hypothesis; it does not imply finite $E_1$, and it does not establish the hypothesis.  It also does not rule out the PC12 endpoint-negligible regime.\n\n**Audit verdict.**  PC4–PC6 and PC11–PC14 are algebraically consistent within their stated inherited assumptions.  Their strongest verified content is an even-subfamily estimate and a conditional statement that endpoint/origin-negligibility weakens the possible Dirichlet singularity.  The unresolved $P_1$ and odd/internal mask correlations remain; no full-energy bound or pointwise constancy follows.  The additional source paragraph above has been corrected to attribute the Mannheim paper to Günter Meinardus; Lothar Berg is the separate author in the Rostock metadata.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_LONG_BLOCK_SOURCE_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — long-block affine source scope and fixed-target multiplicity\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\n\n## Scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete\n`COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md`, and the unaccepted scratch\ncandidate `work/cz-next-block-candidate.md`. For primary comparison I used\nthe already checked finite parity/affine portions of Rozier–Terracol,\n*Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences*, especially the affine iterate\nformula and parity-cylinder statement,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948>, together with the bounded\nApplegate–Lagarias weak-semigroup scope,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/math/0411140>.\nNo source theorem is imported for the special long-block family, and no\nprogram, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, or numerical experiment was used.\n\n## The genuine affine identity and its guard\n\nLet L >= 3 and write\n\n    n = 2^L m - 1,\n\nwith m positive odd, so v_2(n+1)=L. Directly along the odd branch,\n\n    S^j(n) = 2^(L-j) 3^j m - 1,       0 \u003c= j \u003c= L-3,\n\nand hence\n\n    x := S^(L-3)(n) = 8*3^(L-3)m - 1.\n\nThe selected swap class is x=7 modulo 128. Its exact integer guard is\n\n    3^(L-3)m = 1 mod 16.                         (LB1)\n\nFor A=3^(L-3)m satisfying (LB1), the proposed endpoint is\n\n    P(n) = (9x+1)/64 = (9A-1)/8\n         = (3^(L-1)m-1)/8.                       (LB2)\n\nThe divisibility in (LB2) follows from A=1 modulo 16; positivity is clear.\nThis is a genuine affine identity on the guarded residue classes. The\nleading multiplier with respect to n is\n\n    (9/64)(3/2)^(L-3).\n\nIt exceeds 1 already at L=8, so the selected block is not a universal\nsmaller-index descent when L grows. This is the main arithmetic obstruction\nto iterating the block as if it were a contraction.\n\n## Fixed target and inverse multiplicity\n\nFix a positive target P(n)=u. From (LB2),\n\n    3^(L-3)m = A = (8u+1)/9.                      (LB3)\n\nTherefore the guard first forces\n\n    u = 1 mod 18.                                 (LB4)\n\nIndeed A=1 modulo 16 is equivalent to 8u+1=9 modulo 144, which is\nequivalent to u=1 modulo 18. Conversely, for u=1 modulo 18, A in (LB3) is\na positive odd integer and satisfies the guard.\n\nPut v=v_3(8u+1). Then v>=2, and the permissible L are exactly\n\n    3 \u003c= L \u003c= v+1,\n\nwith\n\n    m_L = (8u+1)/3^(L-1),\n    n_L = 2^L(8u+1)/3^(L-1) - 1.                  (LB5)\n\nThus the number of guarded long-block sources mapping to one fixed eligible\ntarget u is\n\n    v_3(8u+1) - 1.                                (LB6)\n\nThe sources are distinct because their values have different\nv_2(n_L+1)=L. This multiplicity is finite for each u but has no uniform\nbound as u varies: the congruence u=1 modulo 18 can be combined with\narbitrarily high divisibility of 8u+1 by powers of 3. Hence a fixed-target\ncount cannot silently be replaced by one source per target or by a uniform\nfinite multiplicity.\n\nThe guarded source union itself covers only one odd residue of m modulo 16\nat each fixed L, hence one residue class modulo 2^(L+4) for n. To justify\nthe infinite-union density with an actual cutoff, put\nK=floor(log_2(X+1)). Only L\u003c=K can contribute. Summing the class counts gives\n\n    A(X) = X sum_(L=3 to K) 2^(-(L+4)) + O(K),\n\nwhile the omitted geometric tail times X is O(1). Therefore\n\n    A(X)=X/64+O(log(X+1)),\n    density(B)=sum_(L>=3)2^(-(L+4))=1/64.          (LB7)\n\nAll starts with L\u003c3 are outside this construction, and at every L the other\nodd m residues are also outside it. Thus this family cannot satisfy an\n“all starts are covered” requirement. Reapplying the block to uncovered\nclasses would require new guards and new affine relations; no such closure\nis supplied by (LB1)–(LB2).\n\n## Diagnostic relaxed coloring\n\nThere is a useful obstruction to overreading these zero and preservation\nidentities. Define a diagnostic function on positive integers by\n\n    f(n)=1 iff oddpart(n)=3 mod 24, and f(n)=0 otherwise.\n\nIt is exactly dyadically invariant. Every member of the accepted zero family\nZ is 7 modulo 8 as an odd integer, so f vanishes on Z. Every source of B\nhas L>=3 and is also 7 modulo 8, so f vanishes on B, including\nD={n=7 mod128}; its long-block targets\nu=1 modulo 18 are 1 modulo 3 and hence also have f(u)=0. Thus f(n)=f(P(n))\non every guarded source in B. For x=16k+15, both x and S(x)=24k+23 avoid the\nclass 3 modulo 24, so f(x)=f(S(x))=0 as well.\n\nOn odd integers f is the single residue class 3 modulo 24, and therefore\n\n    sum_(n\u003c=X, n odd) f(n)/n = (1/24)log X + O(1).\n\nThis f is not asserted to be an exact beta-defect, and it need not satisfy\nthe remaining common-beta channel identities or finite-variation conditions.\nIt is only a diagnostic relaxed coloring: even after imposing all displayed\nZ, D/P, and 15-mod-16 preservation zero information, these identities alone\ndo not force harmonic sparsity. It is not a Collatz counterexample.\n\n## Relation to the primary affine literature\n\nRozier–Terracol's checked finite-word formula gives exactly the kind of\naffine expression used in (LB1)–(LB2), and the parity-cylinder bijection\nvalidates the residue-class interpretation. It does not state this particular\nlong-block family, prove that its guards cover all starts, or provide a\nuniform source-to-target multiplicity bound. Applegate–Lagarias prove a\nweak-semigroup reachability theorem, but their semigroup products need not\nbe one genuine T-orbit and therefore cannot fill this coverage gap for an\nexact beta invariant.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe long-block candidate is a valid guarded affine source family with the\nfixed-target formulas (LB3)–(LB6). Its two precise limitations are: the\ninverse multiplicity is target-dependent and unbounded over targets, and the\nguarded source union leaves most starts uncovered. For L>=8 its real slope\nalso expands, so local endpoint reduction does not imply a global descent.\nThese facts preserve the current core obstruction: no finite G estimate,\nuniversal coverage theorem, or full Collatz conclusion follows from this\nblock. No novelty or worldwide-literature claim is made.\n\n## Final audit of LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER\n\nI read the complete root file\n`LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER.md`, including all seven LB sections\nand formulas LB1–LB12. The guarded\nsigned identity, the branch-growth threshold L=8, and the exact inverse\nmultiplicity agree with the preceding audit. The finite-cutoff regrouping in\nLB5 is valid: each guarded source has one target, the target cutoff cannot be\nsilently replaced by `u\u003c=X` on growing branches, and the first three\ndescending depths give the displayed coefficient\n\n    9/64 + 27/128 + 81/256 = 171/256.\n\nThus LB5 yields only the equivalence of finite G with the smaller remaining\ncore C_5, not finite G itself.\n\nLB8 is an equality of extended nonnegative sums. The replacement of each\nsource weight `1/n` by `1/(n+1)` has nonnegative error, and the source-to-target\nmap is single-valued, so the total denominator error is bounded by\n\n    sum_(n in B) 1/[n(n+1)] \u003c= 1/7.\n\nNo convergence of the actual h-weighted series is assumed in this Tonelli\nstep. However, the unbounded factor in LB9 belongs to the arithmetic inverse\nweight W(u); it does not assert h(u)=1 on the high-valuation targets and is\nnot an obstruction theorem for actual beta defects without additional color\ninformation.\n\nThe diagnostic function in LB12 is correctly limited to unsigned identities.\nIt has the claimed divergent odd harmonic mass and matches the displayed\nzero/preservation/long-block masks, but it is excluded as an exact beta\ndefect already by f(3)=1 versus beta(3)=beta(9) for every exact invariant.\nIts U-variation is also divergent on n=24t+3. Therefore it neither\ncontradicts the conditional finite-V lemma nor supplies a Collatz\ncounterexample. The arithmetic W barrier, the relaxed f model, and the\nactual h problem must remain separate.\n\nThe source boundary is unchanged: the checked Rozier–Terracol affine/parity\nformula supports the coding framework, not LB's special family or core\nestimate. No fresh Bernstein–Lagarias evidence is imported. Finite G, finite\ncore mass, universal guarded coverage, and the full Collatz conclusion remain\nopen.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_MINIMUM_BIRTH_EQUIVALENCE.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — minimum birth versus the first nonconstant color\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded manual/source audit. I read\n`PROJECT_STATE.md`, `COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md`,\n`FOLLOWUP_0431_FIRST_BIRTH_DESCENT.md`, and\n`MINIMAL_BAD_PAIR_RETURN_BOUNDARY.md`. No program, CAS, enumeration,\nLean build, or numerical experiment was used. The local ancestry fact used\nbelow is the accepted BR1 fact: every active boundary has a nearest birth\nancestor.\n\n## Exact equivalence\n\nLet T be the shortcut map, let β be an exact binary invariant β(Tn)=β(n),\nand write c=β(1), d(j)=β(j) XOR β(j+1), and e(j)=d(2j)d(2j+1).\nAssume β is nonconstant and put\n\n    μ = min{j >= 1 : β(j) != c}.\n\nExact doubling gives β(2r)=β(r), so μ is odd. It cannot be 1 mod 4:\nfor odd μ in that class, T^2(μ)=(3μ+1)/4\u003cμ, while invariance would give\nthe same non-c color at this smaller integer. Hence μ=3 mod 4. Set\n\na=(μ-1)/2.\n\nThen a is odd. By minimality, β(1),...,β(μ-1) all equal c. Also\nβ(μ+1)=β((μ+1)/2)=c, because (μ+1)/2\u003cμ. Therefore\n\nd(2a)=d(μ-1)=1,    d(2a+1)=d(μ)=1,\n\nso e(a)=1. For k\u003ca, both 2k and 2k+1 are below μ-1, hence e(k)=0.\nThus the first birth is exactly\n\n    a=(μ-1)/2,     μ=2a+1,     a odd.\n\nIn particular, the stronger prefix statement is valid:\n\n    β(1)=β(2)=...=β(2a)=c,    β(2a+1) != c.\n\nThe parent boundary itself is not active: d(a)=0. A birth means that both\nchild boundaries d(2a) and d(2a+1) are active.\n\nConversely, if a is the least index with e(a)=1, the accepted BR1\nnearest-birth-ancestor statement rules out an active d(j) for j\u003c2a: such a\nboundary would have a birth ancestor \u003ca. Hence β(1),...,β(2a) have color c,\nwhile e(a)=1 forces the first different color at 2a+1. The equivalence is\nexact, not merely a one-way lower-bound transfer. The equality is already\nidentified in the archive as BP176.4 / FOLLOWUP_0431; it is not a new descent\ntheorem.\n\n## Is this an ordinary least counterexample?\n\nOnly after fixing the meaning of β. If\n\n    β(n) = 1_{the positive T-orbit of n reaches 1},\n\nthen β is an exact invariant, β(1)=1, and a failure of Collatz gives a least\nbad integer μ. The preceding argument says that the first birth is the\nreparametrization a=(μ-1)/2, and the prefix through 2a is the all-good prefix\nbelow the least bad integer. Conversely, if this particular good-set\ninvariant is nonconstant, its first non-c color is a least bad integer.\nThus a proof that this specific invariant has no birth would prove Collatz,\nbut it would be a pointwise least-counterexample proof in new notation.\n\nFor an arbitrary exact binary invariant, μ is only the first color change. It\nneed not be a bad integer, and the color-c prefix does not force the invariant\nto be constant beyond 2a+1. The local birth machinery studies all such exact\ninvariants; it cannot silently replace one by the good-set indicator. An\nadjacent-pair structural tree is also not the full unequal-clock predecessor\nset of an ordinary target.\n\n## What minimality legitimately gives to H/G3 mixed blocks\n\nAt the global least birth a, the following are valid:\n\n* no birth index b\u003ca exists, and no active boundary d(j) with j\u003c2a exists;\n* any H, G3, or mixed H/G3 calculation whose actual color masks imply a\n  birth b\u003ca, or an active boundary below 2a whose accepted ancestry has birth\n  ancestor \u003ca, is impossible;\n* if a branch only produces a larger arithmetic endpoint p>=a, or merely\n  establishes integrality of p, minimality gives no contradiction. The\n  endpoint must still satisfy the beta-color and boundary masks.\n\nThis is the honest use of minimum birth in BR3-BR7. A growth output such as\nG3(n)=(9n+3)/8, or H(n)=(9n+5)/4, is not itself a smaller birth. The\nremaining obligation is a uniform argument that every possible sequence of\nmasked mode switches eventually produces a birth below the starting minimum\n(or another pointwise contradiction). Fixed-mode valuation bounds do not\nestablish this; the reset/mixed-mode examples in the birth-routing archive\nshow why mode switching must remain explicit.\n\n## Primary stopping-time source check\n\nI checked the following official or author-hosted records, with the stated\naccess limits.\n\n1. **Riho Terras, “A stopping time problem on the positive integers,” Acta\n   Arith. 30 (1976), 241-252, DOI 10.4064/aa-30-3-241-252.** The official\n   IMPAN record gives title, author, pages, DOI, and a free-download link:\n   https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/acta-arithmetica/all/30/3/101028/a-stopping-time-problem-on-the-positive-integers\n   The linked PDF returned HTTP 403 in this pass. I therefore do not import\n   an exact theorem number from the PDF or claim a full proof audit.\n\n2. **C. J. Everett, “Iteration of the number-theoretic function\n   f(2n)=n, f(2n+1)=3n+2,” Advances in Mathematics 25 (1977), 42-45,\n   DOI 10.1016/0001-8708(77)90087-1.** The official ScienceDirect record\n   and abstract were readable:\n   https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0001870877900871\n   Its abstract states that almost every m has an iterate f^k(m)\u003cm, in\n   natural-density sense. This is descent below the start for almost all\n   integers, not a pointwise least-counterexample theorem and not a theorem\n   about first births.\n\n3. **J. C. Lagarias, “The 3x+1 Problem and its Generalizations”\n   (author-hosted HTML, 1996).** I read the graph/conjecture definitions and\n   stopping-time section:\n   https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node2.html\n   https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node4.html\n   The text records Terras's Theorem A (finite stopping time has limiting\n   density tending to one) and Theorem C (fixed coefficient stopping-time\n   classes are eventually congruence classes, up to finitely many\n   exceptions). These organize parity-vector/stopping-time densities; they\n   do not assert a least-counterexample descent, a pointwise lower birth, or\n   a full two-coordinate predecessor theorem. This is an exposition, not a\n   substitute for reading Terras's inaccessible PDF.\n\nThe source check supports the standard distinction between “almost every\ninteger has a descent” and “every integer descends.” It supplies no published\nimplication from stopping-time density one to absence of a first birth.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe new birth target is not an independent counterexample target. For the\nspecific good-set invariant it is exactly the ordinary least bad integer,\nwith parent reindexing a=(μ-1)/2; for a generic exact invariant it is only\nthe first nonconstant color. Minimality proves the long constant prefix and\nforbids any certified smaller birth, but it does not control larger\nendpoints, unequal-time predecessor paths, or arbitrary H/G3 mode switches.\nNo new stopping-time theorem or completed Collatz proof is claimed.\n\n## Addendum: provenance check for the proposed C_h row implication\n\nI also checked the newly suggested row identity against the local archive,\nwithout treating an unlocated formula as new. For odd u and a positive h with\n3^h u=3 mod 4, the elementary orbit calculation is sound:\n\n    x=3^h u-1  ->  (3^h u-1)/2  ->  (3^(h+1)u-1)/4,\n\nwhere x is even and the displayed next state is odd. Exact doubling then gives\nβ((3^(h+1)u-1)/4)=β(3^(h+1)u-1), so the two color values\nβ(3^h u-1) and β(3^(h+1)u-1) agree. This is a useful two-step equality,\nwith the stated congruence guard.\n\nThe narrow archive search located the related R143 general-merging family\n(work/round143-l-general-merging.md and its audit), including the explicit\npair b=(a-1)/2 and exact common suffixes. It does not by itself state the\nfull X-row notation. However, the new SC1 calculation supplies precisely\nthe missing bridge: for every 0\u003c=s\u003c=h,\n\n    beta(X_{h,s})=C_h,   beta(3X_{h,s}+2)=C_{h+1},\n    beta(X_{h,s}+1)=B_s.\n\nThus SC2 is an accepted finite-word identity, and when SC2 gives\nC_h=C_{h+1}, it really does imply e(X_{h,s})=0 for the entire row. My\nprevious caveat that C_h=C_{h+1} alone left the e-conclusion unproved was\ntoo strong and is corrected here. This verifies the local strip implication,\nwhile the R143 source comparison still does not establish a global descent.\n\n## Bounded SC1-SC3 audit\n\nI read COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md in full and compared SC2's two\nmeeting paths with R143 §2. The paths are the same genuine shortcut paths:\nfor q=3^h u=3 mod 4, q-1 reaches (3q-1)/4 in two steps, while 3q-1\nreaches it in two even steps. Therefore C_h=C_{h+1}; SC1 then correctly\npropagates this equality through all row values X_{h,s}.\n\nThe arithmetic of SC3 is consistent in its stated scope. For a>=3,\nk=H(n)=X_{a,2} and p=floor(k/2)=X_{a-1,2}; SC4 gives beta(k)=beta(p)\nand doubling gives beta(k+1)=beta(p+1), so d(k)=d(p). This removes the\nBR5 branch d(k)=1,d(p)=0 that would have been labeled G1. If both are\nactive, BR5 gives a smaller birth. For a=2, SC3 forces u=1 mod 4 and\np is even; the remaining d(k)=1,d(p)=0 branch goes through the accepted\neven-birth transfer and is smaller for n>31/5, with n=3 treated separately.\nThus G5 is not a surviving unrestricted growth branch in this actual-birth\ncase.\n\nThis is a valid local reduction to the two guarded alternatives G3 and H.\nIt does not prove universal descent: SC5/SC11 only give a smaller birth in\nthe listed masked cases, while the present bounds do not prove descent\nbelow the initial birth after arbitrary guarded mode switches. No independent\nre-audit of every BR estimate was undertaken, and no source-priority or full-Collatz claim is made.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_MULTIPLICATIVE_COLOR_SOURCE_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — primary-source scope for eventual x3 color reversal\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\n\n## Target and reading scope\n\nFor an exact binary shortcut invariant beta, let\n\n    h(n) = |beta(3n) - beta(n)|.\n\nFor odd n, h(n)=1 is the eventual x3 color-reversal statement\n\n    beta(3n) = 1 - beta(n).\n\nExact shortcut invariance also gives\n\n    beta(3n+1) = beta(n),\n\nso such an n has the forced neighboring pattern\n`(beta(3n), beta(3n+1)) = (1-beta(n), beta(n))`.\nThe question is whether primary affine-code, orbit-equivalence, or weak\nsemigroup results prohibit this pattern for all sufficiently large odd n.\n\nI read the relevant primary source portions already recorded in the local\nsource audits: Rozier, *Parity sequences of the 3x+1 map on the 2-adic\nintegers and Euclidean embedding*, Definitions 1–5, Lemma 1, Theorems 1–2\nand the surrounding conjugacy discussion,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1805.00133>; Rozier–Terracol, *Paradoxical behavior\nin Collatz sequences*, the parity-vector, affine-iterate, and remainder\nsections,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948>; Bernstein–Lagarias, *The 3x+1\nConjugacy Map*, the source copy and the audited local excerpts,\n\u003chttps://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/bernstein.pdf>; and\nMonks–Yazinski, *The Autoconjugacy of the 3x+1 Function*, the definitions,\n2-adic coding, rational 2-adic orbit statements, and the checked proofs in\nthe local report,\n\u003chttps://monks.scranton.edu/files/pubs/AutoConjV13.pdf>.\n\nFor the weak-semigroup comparison I read the arXiv HTML introduction and\nSection 2, including Theorem 1.1 and Lemmas 2.1–2.2, of Applegate–Lagarias,\n*The 3x+1 Semigroup*,\n\u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/math/0411140>.\nThis is a bounded primary-source check, not an exhaustive literature or\npriority survey. No programs, numerical experiments, CAS, Lean, or\nenumeration were used.\n\n## Exact affine facts supplied by the primary sources\n\nThe parity sources give the following finite statement. Every binary parity\nword of length j occurs on one residue class modulo 2^j, and on that class\nthe j-step map has an affine form\n\n    T^j(x) = 3^s x / 2^j + E,\n\nwhere s is the number of odd inputs and E is determined by the word. The\n2-adic parity map is a conjugacy with the binary shift. Bernstein–Lagarias\nand Rozier formulate this as a bijective 2-adic coding; Monks–Yazinski also\nstudy the complementary parity autoconjugacy and rational 2-adic orbit\ncases.\n\nThese are exact orbit-code and affine-cylinder statements. They do not\nidentify beta with a continuous function of the parity code, and they do not\nrelate the colors of two different starting integers 3n and n. In\nparticular, a finite parity cylinder can prove an identity between two\naffine orbit endpoints only after the relevant endpoint and common-color\nconditions have been separately established. It cannot turn\n\n    beta(3n) = 1 - beta(n)\n\ninto an impossible parity word.\n\nThe autoconjugacy has the same limitation. Complementing a parity sequence\ndefines a 2-adic dynamical conjugacy; it is not an exact positive-integer\ncoloring invariant and does not say that a common beta must agree with, or\noppose, that complement. Rational 2-adic periodicity statements likewise\nconcern the coded point and its T-orbit, not the neighbor-color mask h on\npositive integers.\n\n## What the weak semigroup theorem does and does not transfer\n\nApplegate–Lagarias define the 3x+1 semigroup generated by 2 and the\nbackward multipliers `(2k+1)/(3k+2)`. Their Theorem 1.1 identifies this\nsemigroup with all positive rationals a/b in lowest terms with\n`b not congruent to 0 mod 3`; in particular it contains every positive\ninteger. The paper explicitly calls this the weak 3x+1 conjecture and\nexplains that semigroup products may represent inverse moves that do not\nform one genuine forward T-orbit.\n\nThat distinction is decisive here. An exact beta invariant satisfies\n\n    beta(T(x)) = beta(x)\n\nalong one genuine orbit. It does not follow that beta is preserved under a\nproduct of weak-semigroup multipliers, because those multipliers may switch\nbetween unrelated affine branches and need not encode a single trajectory\nfrom x. Therefore the weak-semigroup theorem cannot be used to transport\nbeta(n) to beta(3n), nor can it rule out eventual x3 color reversal.\n\nThe same source's Section 2 explicitly retains a resistant residue class\n`-1 mod 2^j` for finite multiplier lists and explains why the weak-semigroup\nargument needs an infinite family there. This reinforces the scope limit:\nfinite affine branch families do not automatically give a global\npointwise orbit relation. It is not a counterexample to the weak theorem or\nto Collatz; it is only a warning against replacing genuine orbit\nequivalence by semigroup reachability.\n\n## A direct exact consequence and the remaining gap\n\nIf h(n)=1 for all sufficiently large odd n, then for every fixed odd n the\nrelation can be iterated along powers of 3 after entering that range. For\nsome J=J(n),\n\n    beta(3^k n) = beta(3^J n) XOR (k-J),   k >= J,\n\nand this is consistent as a binary assignment on\nthe selected multiplicative ray. The shortcut relation supplies the\nneighbor value at `3^k n + 1`, but no primary affine-code theorem forces two\nsuch rays to meet at a common positive integer with incompatible colors.\n\nFinite parity words do produce many residue classes and affine endpoint\nidentities, but a contradiction would require an additional global\ncollision/coalescence statement that preserves the same beta color on the\ntwo colliding endpoints. That is exactly the missing common-color input;\nthe parity literature alone does not supply it.\n\nThe previously audited finite-variation route is different. If\n\n    sum_n |beta(3n)-beta(n)|/n \u003c infinity,\n\nthen the dyadic/multiplicative profile argument gives almost-everywhere\nconstancy, and the named fixed-target predecessor input can remove the\nremaining exceptional points. Eventual h=1 is expected to have divergent\nG, so that finite-variation route cannot simply be invoked to prohibit it.\nThe primary code and semigroup results do not supply a replacement estimate\nfor this divergent-defect regime.\n\n## Pre-witness source-only verdict (before the internal coalescence witness)\n\nThe checked primary results provide exact parity cylinders, affine iterate\nformulas, 2-adic conjugacies, and a weak-semigroup reachability theorem. At\nthe time of this source-only audit, none of the inspected source theorems\nitself ruled out an exact positive-integer shortcut invariant with eventual\n`beta(3n)=1-beta(n)` on odd n. The weak-semigroup theorem is strictly weaker\nthan genuine orbit equivalence and cannot be used for this color transfer.\nThe missing proof ingredient identified at that time was a collision or\ncoalescence identity with common-beta control, or a quantitative\npredecessor/boundary input. The later internal coalescence witness below\nsupplies such a local identity; this dated paragraph is retained for audit\nhistory and is not the current final conclusion. No worldwide literature\nabsence or novelty claim is made.\n\n## Independent audit of the exact coalescence witness\n\nI also checked the new internal identity supplied after the source pass. For\n\n    n = 32t + 23,\n\nthe first five shortcut inputs and outputs are\n\n    n       -> 48t+35 -> 72t+53 -> 108t+80 -> 54t+40 -> 27t+20,\n    3n      -> 144t+104 -> 72t+52 -> 36t+26 -> 18t+13 -> 27t+20.\n\nThe first path has input parities `1,1,1,0,0`, hence word 11100 and\nthree odd inputs. The second has `1,0,0,0,1`, hence word 10001 and two odd\ninputs. Every displayed division is integral for every nonnegative integer t,\nand both paths end at exactly the same integer `27t+20` after five steps.\n\nEquivalently, the parity-cylinder affine formulas have the same numerator\nconstant 19:\n\n    T^5(x) = (27x + 19)/32  for x = 32t+23,\n    T^5(y) = ( 9y + 19)/32  for y = 3(32t+23).\n\nSubstituting y=3n makes the two endpoints identical. This is exactly the\nkind of finite affine collision covered by the primary parity-word formula:\nthe source words determine their residue classes and affine remainders, while\nthe common endpoint supplies the beta equality. It is an internal checked\ninstance, not a theorem attributed to Rozier or to any other source.\n\nSince beta is T-invariant, the witness gives\n\n    beta(3n) = beta(n),\n    h(32t+23) = 0  for every t >= 0.\n\nThe progression has infinitely many members (indeed its harmonic sum\ndiverges), so it rules out the relaxed possibility that h(n)=1 outside a\nfinite set. It also directly excludes the finite-mask example f_F from DF5\nif that example were additionally required to obey the genuine common-beta\ncoalescence identities. The example remains valid as a counterexample to the\nrelaxed U-variation coercivity claim because f_F was never asserted to be an\nexact beta.\n\nThis witness does not prove that G is finite: h may still be nonzero on other\nresidue classes with divergent harmonic mass. Nor does one arithmetic\nprogression provide a global collision theorem or a full pointwise rigidity\nresult. The source comparison therefore changes the bounded conclusion only\nby excluding cofinite eventual x3 reversal; the harmonic-energy and full\nCollatz gaps remain.\n\n## Additional audit: a positive-density zero strip for h\n\nI independently checked the further pullback extension. First, exact\ninvariance gives, for every positive odd q,\n\n    beta(4q) = beta(q) = beta(3q+1) = beta(4q+1),\n\nso d(4q)=0. If n is 15 modulo 16, then 3n is odd and\n\n    T(3n) = (9n+1)/2 = 4(18k+17), with odd quotient 18k+17,\n    3S(n) = (9n+3)/2 = T(3n)+1,\n\nwhere S(n)=(3n+1)/2. Since beta(S(n))=beta(n) and d(T(3n))=0,\n\n    g(S(n)) = g(n),       g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n).\n\nThis is an exact signed preservation law on the residue class 15 modulo 16,\nnot an averaged assertion.\n\nFor L >= 3, put a_L=3 when L is odd and a_L=1 when L is even, and define\n\n    Z_L = { 2^L(4t+a_L)-1 : t >= 0 }.\n\nFor n in Z_L, the odd-only iterates satisfy, for 0 \u003c= j \u003c= L-3,\n\n    S^j(n) = 2^(L-j) 3^j (4t+a_L)-1.\n\nAll earlier states with j \u003c L-3 are 15 modulo 16. At the last state,\n\n    S^(L-3)(n) = 8*3^(L-3)(4t+a_L)-1 = 23 mod 32;\n\nthe last congruence uses L odd with a_L=3, or L even with a_L=1. The exact\ncoalescence witness audited above gives g=0 on the 23-mod-32 class. Repeated\nsigned preservation therefore gives\n\n    h(n)=0 for every n in every Z_L.\n\nThe Z_L are disjoint because v_2(n+1)=L on Z_L. Each has natural density\n2^(-(L+2)), so their union has natural density\n\n    sum_(L >= 3) 2^(-(L+2)) = 1/16.\n\nThe same arithmetic-progression harmonic estimate gives, with ordinary\ninteger cutoffs,\n\n    sum_{n \u003c= X, n in union Z_L} 1/n = (1/16) log X + O(1).\n\nThe tail over L greater than log_2 X contributes only O(1), and the\nper-progression constants are summable; this is a cutoff statement for this\nexplicit union, not a claim about every density-zero or sparse set.\n\nThis extension strengthens the preceding coalescence conclusion: it rules\nout h=1 outside a finite set, and in fact supplies a fixed positive-density\nand divergent-harmonic-mass zero set for h. It still does not prove finite G,\nbecause h may be nonzero on the complementary classes with divergent total\nharmonic mass. The relaxed DF5 example was never an exact beta and remains a\nvalid obstruction to arguments that use only dyadic invariance and finite\nU-change cost; the new strips show precisely why the actual common-beta\ncoalescence condition is an additional essential input.\n\n## Conditional parity-block consequence for transport variation of h\n\nI also checked the proposed conditional extension against the primary parity\ncylinder scope. Assume, in addition to exact beta invariance and\n`h(2n)=h(n)`, that the odd-source transport variation is finite:\n\n    V_h = sum_(n odd) |h(U(n))-h(n)|/n \u003c infinity.\n\nFor odd n, h(U(n))=h(T(n)) because U(n) is obtained from T(n) by removing\npowers of 2, which do not change h. For even n, h(T(n))=h(n) by dyadic\ninvariance. Therefore the full support of\n\n    Delta(n)=|h(T(n))-h(n)|\n\nhas finite harmonic sum, and hence has natural density zero. Pulling this\nsupport back under any fixed T^j preserves density zero: split the domain\ninto the finitely many parity cylinders modulo 2^j, use the affine formula\non each cylinder, and apply the density-zero estimate to the corresponding\naffine image. This is exactly the finite-cylinder information supplied by\nthe checked Rozier parity coding; no random or Haar statement is needed.\n\nThe audited coalescence gives h=0 on the cylinder 23 modulo 32, whose first\nfive parity inputs are 11100. Partition the first 5r steps into r consecutive\nblocks. By the primary parity-word bijection, each concatenation of r\nfive-bit words is one residue class modulo 2^(5r), and exactly 31^r of the\n32^r classes avoid 11100 in every block. If an orbit has a 11100 block and\nthere has been no Delta event before that block, h is zero there and hence\nalso at the initial point by transport. Thus, for each fixed r,\n\n    {n \u003c= X : h(n)=1}\n    is contained in the r-block avoidance classes\n    union the first-5r-step Delta pullbacks.\n\nAfter X tends to infinity, the second term has density zero and the first\nhas upper density at most (31/32)^r. Sending r to infinity proves the\nconditional conclusion\n\n    finite V_h + exact coalescence cylinder 23 mod 32\n    => h=1 has natural density zero.\n\nThe limits must be taken in this order: first X tends to infinity for fixed\nr, then r tends to infinity. This is a density conclusion only. It does not\nprove finite multiplicative defect G, pointwise h=0, or positive-density\npredecessors for an h-exceptional point. In particular, it cannot be\ncombined with the finite-G pointwise bridge unless finite G is separately\nproved. The primary source supports the residue/block count, while the\ncoalescence and the finite V_h hypothesis are project-specific inputs.\n\n## Final audit of CZ1–CZ7\n\nI read the complete root file\n`COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md`, including CZ1–CZ7. The\nfive-step paths in CZ2, their parity words 11100 and 10001, the common\nnumerator constant 19, and the exact beta equality were checked above and\nare correct. CZ3's preservation law follows from d(4q)=0 and the identity\n`3S(x)=T(3x)+1` for x=15 modulo 16. CZ4's induction keeps all earlier\nstates in that residue class and sends the final state to 23 modulo 32;\nthe odd/even choice of a_L is correct. The Z_L are disjoint by\nv_2(n+1)=L.\n\nCZ5's cutoff accounting is sound: each class has modulus 2^(L+2), the\ngeometric density sum is 1/16, the O(1) count error over\nL \u003c= log_2(X+1) is O(log X), and partial summation gives the stated\nharmonic asymptotic. Thus the explicit zero family excludes cofinite h=1\nfor every actual exact beta. This is a new internal manual consequence;\nthe primary Rozier–Terracol parity formula supplies the coding language but\ndoes not receive attribution for the special family.\n\nThe earlier DF5 statement must be read historically. Before CZ2/CZ4, f_F\nwas only a relaxed dyadic/U-variation example and its realization as an\nactual h was undecided. CZ now excludes that realization because every\nactual h has the explicit Z zero family. This does not invalidate DF5's\nrelaxed coercivity obstruction, and it does not retroactively change the\nearlier source-only conclusion.\n\nCZ6 is also correct within its hypothesis. Finite V_h makes the full\nT-change support Delta density zero because even inputs have zero change by\ndyadic invariance. Each fixed T^j pullback remains density zero by the\nfinite affine parity-branch decomposition. For fixed r, the r disjoint\nfive-bit blocks have 31^r avoiding choices out of 32^r, so the upper-density\nbound is (31/32)^r plus a finite union of density-zero pullbacks. The order\nof limits is essential: X tends to infinity first for fixed r, then r tends\nto infinity. No uniform-in-r pullback estimate is being used.\n\nThe current boundary is unchanged: CZ7 does not prove V_h or finite G,\ndoes not make the support of h T-invariant, and therefore does not permit\napplying the fixed-target predecessor theorem directly to h. It gives\nnatural density zero for h=1 only under the separately stated V_h\nhypothesis, not pointwise h=0. The historical R170/H170/K170/N170/G170\nacceptance remains preserved and is neither re-certified nor withdrawn by\nthis audit.\n\n## Root CZ7/CZ8 confirmation\n\nI reread the complete current CZ file, including its added CZ7 sign-swap\ndescent and CZ8 source boundary. The CZ7 formulas are exact: the class\n`n=128s+7` maps bijectively to `u=18s+1`, satisfies `n=(64u-1)/9 >= 7u`,\nand gives `g(n)=-g(u)` and `h(n)=h(u)`. Its class is disjoint from Z, so\n\n    G_C(X) \u003c= G(X) \u003c= (7/6)G_C(X)\n\nis valid by monotonicity and the exact decomposition of the odd domain. This\nis only a selected-class reduction; it proves no finiteness of G or of the\nremaining core C. The stated 55/128 density is the geometric density of C\namong all positive integers (55/64 relative to odd integers), not a solved\nfraction of the Collatz problem.\n\nCZ8's Rozier–Terracol attribution is limited to the standard finite affine\nparity coding, while the coalescence family and the sign-swap descent are\nmanual internal identities. Applegate–Lagarias Theorem 1.1 remains a weak\nsemigroup statement and is not used as genuine orbit equivalence. The\ncurrent top-level conclusion therefore supersedes the dated pre-witness\nsource-only paragraph: exact zero strips and selected descent are proved,\nbut the core harmonic mass, finite G, and full pointwise conclusion remain\nopen. No additional expansion, source claim, or PD application is made.\n\n## Audit of the selected 7-step descent class\n\nI also checked the new internal descent identity. Let\n\n    n = 128s+7,       u = 18s+1 = (9n+1)/64.\n\nThe first orbit has the successive values\n\n    n, 192s+11, 288s+17, 432s+26, 216s+13,\n    324s+20, 162s+10, 81s+5,\n\nso `T^7(n)=81s+5`. On the other side,\n\n    3n -> 576s+32 -> 288s+16 -> 144s+8 -> 72s+4\n       -> 36s+2 -> 18s+1=u,\n\nand `T(3u)=81s+5`. Exact T-invariance therefore gives\n\n    beta(n)=beta(3u),   beta(3n)=beta(u),\n    g(n)=-g(u),         h(n)=h(u).\n\nThe parameter map is injective, u is positive odd, and\n\n    n-7u = 2s >= 0.\n\nConsequently the harmonic mass on this class satisfies the finite-cutoff\nbound\n\n    sum_(n\u003c=X, n=128s+7) h(n)/n \u003c= (1/7) G(X/7),\n\nwhere G is the odd multiplicative-defect sum. This is an exact selected-class\npullback estimate; it is not a bound for all odd n.\n\nThe class is disjoint from the previously forced zero family Z. Indeed every\nsuch n has v_2(n+1)=3 and odd part `16s+1`, which is 1 modulo 4, whereas the\nonly Z_L with valuation 3 is Z_3, whose odd part is 3 modulo 4. Therefore a\ncore obtained by removing Z and this D_7 class is a genuine disjoint split.\nIf G_core denotes the remaining odd mass, the displayed estimate yields the\nrecursive inequality\n\n    G(X) \u003c= G_core(X) + G(X/7)/7.\n\nIteration gives `G(X) \u003c= (7/6) G_core(X)` by monotonicity (or the\ncorresponding global inequality when the sums converge). The coefficient is\nvalid for this one residue class only. No estimate for the complementary\ncore, and hence no finiteness of G, follows. This audit introduces no new\nliterature input and does not change the preceding source or full-goal\nboundaries.\n\n## Final terminal status\n\nThe current report's conclusion is the post-witness one: exact zero strips\nand the selected 7-step descent are accepted within their displayed scopes;\nfinite G, the remaining core estimate, and full pointwise rigidity remain\nopen. The dated pre-witness source-only language is retained only as history\nand is explicitly superseded above.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_ORIGIN_ARITHMETIC_BARRIER.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — arithmetic barrier for finite-growth origins\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded analytic/archive audit; no\nmathematical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, or new agent. I read\n`PROJECT_STATE.md`, the full `MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md`,\nand the BC audit in `FOLLOWUP_0459_H_G3_SWITCH_BLOCK.md`. No new external\nprimary source was needed: the issue is determined by the displayed affine\nbranches and by the already recorded finite-prefix coding.\n\n## 1. Arithmetic predecessor images\n\nFor the arithmetic superset used by BC, set\n\n    D_H={n>0:n≡3 (mod 4)},       H(n)=(9n+5)/4,\n    D_G={n>0:n≡13 (mod 16)},     G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8.\n\nWriting n=4t+3 and n=16t+13 gives\n\n    H(D_H)={9t+8:t≥0},\n    G₃(D_G)={18t+15:t≥0}.\n\nThese are the mod-9-separated images already checked in BC11: the first\nlies in 8 mod 9 and the second in 6 mod 9. Hence the set with no arithmetic\nH/G₃ predecessor is\n\n    U_arith = N_{>0} \\ ( {9t+8:t≥0} ∪ {18t+15:t≥0} ).\n\nThis condition is much too weak to imply harmonic summability. For example,\nall numbers 9k+1 belong to U_arith, and\n\n    Σ_{k≥0} 1/(9k+1) = ∞.\n\nIn fact the image has natural density 1/9+1/18=1/6, while its complement\nhas density 5/6; the density statement is only context, not an assumption\nabout births.\n\nThere is also a direction-of-inference issue. Let O_β be the origins of all\nretained actual-birth growth components, let A_β⊂O_β be the finite-component\norigins, and let B_β be the actual birth set. If n∈B_β∩U_arith, then n has\nno arithmetic predecessor and therefore is an origin. Thus\n\n    B_β∩U_arith ⊆ O_β.\n\nThe reverse inclusion is not established by arithmetic: one must also\ncontrol whether the arithmetic predecessor is retained by the actual masks.\nThus this reasoning supplies neither O_β⊆U_arith nor A_β⊆U_arith.\nNo actual counterexample to either inclusion is constructed. U_arith is a\nreservoir of candidate origins after the birth mask is imposed. If one\nartificially assumes all origins have no arithmetic predecessor, one obtains\nonly O_β⊆U_arith, whose\nharmonic majorant still diverges. Neither interpretation yields summability.\nNo actual birth in the progression 9k+1 is asserted, since existence of a\nnonconstant exact β is not assumed here.\n\n## 2. Fixed finite prefixes are a separate obstruction\n\nBC9 controls Γ, the intersection of all prefix requirements, and does not\ncontrol any fixed finite-prefix set. This can be seen without the aggregate\nword count. For every r≥0 take the word H^r and append the terminal 11\nsuffix used in BC6. Its middle parity word is\n\n    (11)^r 11,\n\nso μ=2n+1 has its lowest 2r+2 bits equal to one. Equivalently the starts\nare the single progression\n\n    C_r = {n>0:n≡−1 (mod 2^(2r+1))}.\n\nEvery n∈C_r follows r arithmetic H steps and has an odd endpoint, by the\nfinite parity-vector realization already proved in BC6. For each fixed r,\n\n    Σ_{n∈C_r} 1/n = ∞,\n\nbecause it is a nonempty arithmetic progression. This is compatible with\nBC7: its bound has a linear X-term for fixed r. The H-only examples C_r are\nnested as r grows. The general prefix sets P_r are nested as well: F is\ndeterministic, and an (r+1)-step chain has an odd r-th endpoint because that\nendpoint is the input of the next F-step. Thus P_(r+1)⊆P_r. In either case\nΓ requires compatible prefixes at every length, and can have finite harmonic\nmass even though every individual C_r diverges.\n\nThus an argument of the form “there is no long arithmetic predecessor chain”\nwould have to control the union over all finite lengths with a length-dependent\ncutoff or a genuine stopping/mask estimate. It cannot replace the Γ\nintersection by one fixed prefix or by the finite set of forbidden image\nresidues.\n\n## 3. Consequence for origin-energy bookkeeping\n\nBC11–BC13 correctly reduce the actual finite-growth contribution to\n\n    E_A(X) ≤ E_R(X) ≤ 9 E_A(X),\n\nbut injectivity and mod-9 image separation give no estimate on E_A. The\narithmetic predecessor test only identifies where an actual origin is forced\nonce a birth is known. To prove finite origin energy one would need an\nadditional statement controlling births in U_arith and also births whose\narithmetic predecessors are discarded by masks. A statement about finite\nprefixes must likewise retain the actual beta masks; finite parity coding\nalone supplies arithmetic residue classes, not actual births.\n\nA valid route would be either a uniform harmonic bound for the masked origin\nset itself, or a decreasing relation between origins after the terminal\nsmaller-birth transfer. Neither is supplied by absence of an arithmetic\nH/G₃ predecessor. The summability of Γ therefore does not extend to finite\nprefix classes or to A_β by the current arithmetic information.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe combined H/G₃ predecessor image is explicit and leaves a divergent\narithmetic complement, with the progression 9k+1 as a concrete witness.\nThe fixed-prefix progression C_r gives an independent divergent family for\nevery finite r. These are arithmetic candidate sets, not claims of actual\nbirths. Hence the present no-predecessor condition does not by itself imply\nsummability of origins, and BC9's finite mass remains specific to the\nall-length infinite core Γ. The finite origin-energy question stays open.\n\n## 4. Audit of OR9--OR10 and the progression 144t+99\n\nI read `INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md` in full. The distinction there\nbetween O_beta, the origins of all retained-growth components, and\nA_beta⊂O_beta, the origins of finite components, is essential. OR9 and OR10\nconcern O_beta first; they do not silently turn an\nall-component estimate into a finite-component estimate.\n\nThe OR9 charge is arithmetically consistent, assuming the already stated\nOR2--OR7 inverse boundary identities. If n∈O_beta∩(I_H∪I_G), its unique\narithmetic predecessor q cannot be a birth, or it would give a retained\nincoming edge. OR2/OR6 then place the nearest birth ancestor b at depth at\nleast two, so q≥4b. At each ancestry depth there are at\nmost two boundary positions, giving\n\n    Σ_(q charged to b) 1/q ≤ 2/b Σ_(ell≥2)2^(−ell) = 1/b.\n\nFor the two possible outputs,\n\n    1/H(q) = 4/(9q+5) ≤ 4/(9q),\n    1/G₃(q) = 8/(9q+3) ≤ 8/(9q).\n\nUsing the common looser bound q\u003c8X/9 whenever the corresponding output is at\nmost X, every charged b is below 2X/9. Therefore\n\n    E_D(X) ≤ (8/9) E_β(2X/9),\n\nwith D_beta=O_beta∩(I_H∪I_G). The constants are valid; the\nH-specific bound could be sharpened but is not needed.\n\nSince births outside the arithmetic image are origins, write\nP_beta=B_beta\\(I_H∪I_G). Then O_beta=P_beta⊔D_beta. The BC chain estimate\napplies to all components, finite or infinite, so E_beta(X)≤9E_O(X). Combining\nthis with OR9 gives exactly\n\n    E_β(X) ≤ 9E_P(X) + 8E_β(2X/9).\n\nThus the coefficient is 8 after multiplying OR9 by the chain factor 9. It\nis not the contraction coefficient 8/9, and the inequality leaves the\nprimitive-origin term E_P unresolved. Since A_beta⊂O_beta,\nthis is also not a finite-origin estimate by itself; separating finite from\ninfinite components requires an additional argument. The finite harmonic\nbound for the arithmetic all-length core Γ does not supply that missing\nprimitive term.\n\nThe explicit OR5 progression is also correct. For n=144t+99, one has\nn≡0 mod 9 and n≡3 mod 16, so it\nis outside both arithmetic image classes and lies in the H input class. Its\nunique H edge is\n\n    H(n)=324t+224,\n\nwhich is 0 mod 4, hence outside both domains of F. Its arithmetic\ngrowth component therefore has one edge and then stops, and\n\n    Σ_(t≥0) 1/(144t+99) = ∞\n\nby comparison with Σ_(t≥0)1/[144(t+1)]. If such an n is an actual\nbirth, it is an origin of the retained graph regardless of whether the H\noutput mask retains the edge: the edge either is retained and terminates at\nthe even output, or is absent and the component is a singleton. No member\nof this progression is asserted to be an actual birth.\n\nThis progression is therefore an arithmetic barrier to proving summability\nfrom image exclusion alone. It does not contradict BC9, which concerns the\nintersection of all infinite-prefix conditions, and it does not decide the\nactual masked birth or finite-origin mass.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_PD170_DEPENDENCY_AUDIT.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — PD170 dependency audit\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). This is a bounded source and\ndependency audit. No programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean build, or state/map\nchanges were used.\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `work/round170-qualitative-density-synthesis.md` (QS170.1–7)\nand the relevant full source report\n`work/round169-g-positive-density-sources.md` (GP169.1–10). I also checked the\ncurrent finite-energy reports, only to preserve their already accepted\nconditional status. Externally, I read the complete eight-page Mazur\nall-target manuscript, §§1–6 and Appendix A, together with the public theorem\ncard and source/provenance page:\n\n- [Mazur, *Positive Lower Density of Collatz Predecessors*, v1.0, 6 September 2026](https://www.proofatlas.ai/papers/positive-lower-density-collatz-predecessors/paper-v1.pdf)\n- [public theorem interface and formalization provenance](https://www.proofatlas.ai/proofs/artifact.collatz.predecessors_positive_lower_density.v001.html)\n- [source supplement and pinned build information](https://www.proofatlas.ai/sources/positive-lower-density-collatz-predecessors/)\n\nThe earlier target-1 Mazur manuscript, the limited Wirsching source scope,\nand the Krasikov–Lagarias source scope are those recorded in GP169; they were\nnot silently treated as stronger all-target inputs here.\n\n## Exact external statement and match to QS170\n\nMazur's Theorem 1.1 says, for the ordinary map `C(n)=n/2` on even `n` and\n`C(n)=3n+1` on odd `n`, that for every fixed positive integer target `a`\nwith `3 ∤ a`, there are constants `c_a>0` and finite `X_a` such that, for\nevery integer `X≥X_a`, the number of positive starts `n\u003cX` reaching `a`\nunder some finite number of ordinary `C`-steps is at least `c_a X`. The paper\nallows zero steps, even targets, and periodic targets. It does not assert a\ndensity limit, a target-independent constant, an effective uniform recipe,\nor that the target reaches 1.\n\nThis is exactly the interface used as QS170.1/QS1, up to harmless endpoint\nconventions (`[1,X)` versus `[1,Y]` and a change of the threshold). The\nquantifiers are eventual-for-every-cutoff, not merely a limsup statement.\nThe restriction `3 ∤ a` is explicit and is not removable by this\ntheorem: the paper notes that a target divisible by 3 has only the dyadic\ninverse branch and therefore cannot have the asserted positive density.\n\n## What the visible proof actually establishes\n\nThe proof architecture in the manuscript is coherent conditional on its\ncentral analytic input:\n\n1. Lemmas 2.1–2.3 construct, for a prescribed ternary residue, a large odd\n   seed `R` which reaches the fixed target and has no positive Syracuse\n   return.\n2. Proposition 3.1 supplies, at every sufficiently large scale, a finite\n   family of exact valuation words and a positive weighted lower bound. Its\n   statement explicitly warns that the auxiliary weight sum is not itself a\n   source count and that sources need not initially be distinct.\n3. Lemma 4.1 uses the no-return property to make the relevant word-to-source\n   map injective; Lemma 4.2 controls the residue bookkeeping. Section 4 then\n   removes the weights and obtains a positive constant at every sufficiently\n   large cutoff.\n\nThe displayed constant calculation is consistent: after fixing the finite\nparameter `m`, the manuscript obtains a positive terminal mass, loses a\n   fixed factor in removing weights, and converts sources in `[X,32X)` to a\n   lower bound below `Y` by taking `X=Y/32`. Thus the visible steps do not\nshow a quantifier reversal or a hidden dependence of the seed on the final\ncutoff.\n\n## The actual dependency boundary\n\nProposition 3.1 is explicitly labelled an input from the preceding Mazur\npaper. It packages the difficult fine-scale mixing, prescribed-residue\nselection, exact-path coverage, and all-large-scale transfer. Appendix A\npoints back to the preceding paper's propositions and lemmas for these\nestimates. The eight-page manuscript does not reprove that proposition as a\nself-contained argument, and it says that this combined proposition is not\nseparately formalized.\n\nAccordingly, this audit finds no apparent circularity in the visible route:\nTheorem 1.1 invokes Proposition 3.1; it does not derive that proposition from\nTheorem 1.1, from convergence to 1, or from an almost-everywhere conclusion.\nThe manuscript also treats Tao's almost-bounded result as context rather than\nas a substitute for fixed-target convergence. The unresolved issue here is\ndependency verification: this pass did not reconstruct every inherited\nmixing, histogram, overlap, and exact-path estimate in the preceding paper.\n\nThe public formalization page reports a Lean closure of 388 files, a pinned\nbaseline/commit and toolchain, and the three public theorem interfaces. Those\nare author/ProofAtlas provenance claims, not an independent cold Lean replay,\nexternal peer review, or an audit of the transitive proof closure. The source\npage itself records those limits. Therefore the source is a primary\nauthor-hosted manuscript/formalization release, but its formal build status\nmust not be described as independently verified in this report.\n\n## Earlier results and what they do not provide\n\n- The earlier [Mazur target-1 manuscript](https://www.proofatlas.ai/papers/positive-density-log-time-collatz/Mazur_Explicit_Positive_Density_Collatz_Convergence_in_Logarithmic_Time_v2.pdf)\n  concerns the fixed target 1 (with an explicit logarithmic-time bound). It\n  does not imply the all-target theorem by itself.\n- The checked [Krasikov–Lagarias paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0205002)\n  gives a fixed-target predecessor lower\n  bound of sublinear type (the recorded exponent is `0.84` in its stated\n  accelerated setting), not a positive lower natural density for every target.\n- The limited Terras/Everett material concerns almost-all stopping or descent\n  statements. It does not supply the fixed-target, every-large-cutoff\n  positive-density conclusion used by QS170.\n- [Wirsching's source](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf)\n  is a close historical predecessor, but the read source explicitly leaves\n  distributional gaps/conjectural assumptions; it is not imported here as an\n  unconditional all-target proof. Its journal metadata are [DCDS 9 (2003),\n  771–787](https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2003.9.771).\n\nThus the all-target positive lower natural-density input checked in this scope\nis Mazur v1.0, not an inference assembled from these older statements.\n\n## Dependent project verdict\n\nQS170/PD170 is a precisely stated named input with matching ordinary-map\nquantifiers, and the visible Mazur transfer from Proposition 3.1 to Theorem\n1.1 is not circular on its face. The project may continue to use it as the\nalready accepted **conditional/internal input** recorded by QS170. This report\ndoes not revoke that status.\n\nThe report also does not independently certify the full analytic proof or its\n388-file formal dependency closure. The substantive open audit boundary is\nProposition 3.1 and the inherited mixing/coverage/overlap estimates on which\nit rests. Separately, PD170 still does not prove density one of starts reaching\n1; the QS170 pointwise contradiction remains conditional on that additional\ndensity-one premise (or on another route such as the finite-energy bridge).\n\n## Conditional terminal-count route to finite birth energy\n\nFor the separate root-count route, assume the stated terminal estimate\n\n`Z(Y)=O(Y/(ln Y)^q)` for some fixed `q>0`, and assume the retained-graph\ncounting inequality\n\n`R(X) ≤ X(3/4)^k + 2^k + k Z((3/2)^k (X+1)-1)`.\n\nTake `k=floor((q/ln 2) ln ln X)` for sufficiently large `X`. Then\n`2^k=O((ln X)^q)`, while\n\n`(3/2)^k = O((ln X)^(q ln(3/2)/ln 2))`\n\nand the terminal argument `Y=(3/2)^k(X+1)-1` satisfies\n`ln Y=ln X+O(ln ln X)`. Consequently\n\n`Z(Y)=O(X/(ln X)^p)`, up to a harmless fixed constant, where\n\n`p=q ln(4/3)/ln 2`.\n\nIndeed, the power lost in the terminal denominator is\n`q - q ln(3/2)/ln 2 = q ln(4/3)/ln 2`. Since `k=O(ln ln X)`, the terminal\nterm is `O(X ln ln X/(ln X)^p)`. The first term has the smaller bound\n`O(X/(ln X)^p)`, and the polylogarithmic term `2^k` is negligible. Thus the\nconditional conclusion is\n\n`R(X)=O(X ln ln X/(ln X)^p)`.\n\nPartial summation gives\n\n`Σ_{n≤X} e(n)/n = R(X)/X + O(1) + ∫_2^X R(t)/t^2 dt`.\n\nThe comparison integral `∫ ln(ln t)/(t (ln t)^p) dt` converges at infinity\nwhen `p>1`, i.e.\n`q>ln 2/ln(4/3)`. This is a valid conditional implication, with endpoint\nrounding and the floor in `k` changing only constants. It does not establish\nthe assumed terminal estimate, and it does not control arbitrary mixed-chain\ntimes beyond the counting inequality supplied as an additional premise.\n\n## Sharpened terminal sum and the critical logarithmic case\n\nThe stronger counting inequality supplied for the same route is\n\n`R(X) ≤ X(3/4)^k + 2^k + Σ_{j=0}^{k-1} Z((3/2)^j(X+1)-1)`.\n\nIts improvement is structural: at a fixed terminal depth `j`, at most one\nretained predecessor is charged, so the previous factor `k Z(...)` is not\nneeded. Put `Y_j=(3/2)^j(X+1)-1`. For `X` sufficiently large, every\n`Y_j≥X`, and therefore the assumed bound\n`Z(Y)=O(Y/(ln Y)^q)` gives\n\n`Σ_{j\u003ck} Z(Y_j) = O(X (3/2)^k/(ln X)^q)`,\n\nbecause the geometric sum of the `Y_j` is `O(X(3/2)^k)`. With\n`k=floor((q/ln 2) ln ln X)`, this is\n`O(X/(ln X)^p)`, where `p=q ln(4/3)/ln 2`; the first term has the same\norder and `2^k=O((ln X)^q)` is negligible. Thus the sharpened conditional\nconclusion removes the earlier extraneous `ln ln X` factor:\n\n`R(X)=O(X/(ln X)^p)`.\n\nThere is also a critical refinement. Let\n`q0=ln 2/ln(4/3)` and assume, for a fixed `r>0`,\n\n`Z(Y)=O(Y/[(ln Y)^q0 (ln ln Y)^r])`.\n\nChoose\n`k=floor((q0 ln ln X + r ln ln ln X)/ln 2)`.\nFor all `Y_j` in the sum, `ln Y_j=ln X+O(ln ln X)` uniformly in the relevant\nrange and `ln ln Y_j` is bounded below by a constant multiple of\n`ln ln X`. Hence the same geometric summation gives\n\n`Σ_{j\u003ck} Z(Y_j) = O(X/[ln X (ln ln X)^(r/q0)])`.\n\nThe first term `X(3/4)^k` has this same order, while `2^k` is polylogarithmic.\nTherefore\n\n`R(X)=O(X/[ln X (ln ln X)^(r/q0)])`.\n\nPartial summation then reduces the birth-energy tail to a constant multiple\nof `∫ dv/v^(r/q0)`, with `v=ln ln t`. Consequently this critical route\nguarantees finite `Σ e(n)/n` when `r>q0`. Both sharpened conclusions are\nconditional on the corresponding `Z` estimate and the strengthened counting\ninequality; neither estimate is established by PD170 or by this audit.\n\n## Audit of EC1–EC8 and the conditional EC10 consequence\n\nI read the complete\n[ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md](ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md),\nincluding EC1–EC8. The generic profile calculation in EC5 is correct for the\nstated monotone hypothesis `H(Y)≤C Y/L(Y)`: with `k=floor(log_2 L(X))`, the\ngeometric endpoint sum contributes `O(X g^k/L(X))`, and the identity\n`g=2r` gives `g^k/L(X)≤L(X)^(-eta)`; the floor prevents claiming equality.\nThe residue-class term `2^k≤L(X)` must\nbe retained; EC5 does retain it. The integral condition EC6 is the direct\nnonnegative counting criterion obtained by partial summation.\n\nThe exponents in EC4 are also consistent. For terminals,\n`r=3/4`, `g=3/2`, and `eta=ln(4/3)/ln 2`; for origins,\n`r=2/3`, `g=4/3`, and `eta=ln(3/2)/ln 2`. Balancing the decreasing prefix\nterm against the endpoint-growth term gives the displayed `rho_Z` and\n`rho_O`, both strictly below 1 when `theta\u003c1`. EC6–EC9 then give the stated\nlogarithmic and critical-logarithmic thresholds. These are sufficient\nconditional implications, not endpoint sparsity theorems.\n\nEC10 is logically valid under the already named PD170/QS170 bridge. If a\nnonconstant exact invariant had finite birth energy, PD would force pointwise\nconstancy by the exceptional-target argument; hence its birth energy is\ninfinite. If either endpoint count had limsup logarithmic exponent below 1,\nan exponent `theta\u003c1` between that limsup and 1 would give an eventual\n`O(X^theta)` bound, and EC3/EC4 would force finite energy, a contradiction.\nSince endpoint counts are at most `X`, the resulting conditional limsup\nexponent is exactly 1 for both endpoint sets. This does not construct a\nnonconstant invariant, prove endpoint density, or remove PD.\n\nThe source scope in EC8 is accurately limited: the full eight-page Mazur\nall-target manuscript was read, while the Krasikov–Lagarias check used its\nabstract/bibliographic record in that audit, not a fresh full-paper\nverification. No transitive analytic closure or Lean rebuild is thereby\ncertified. The EC endpoint transport is an internal conditional counting\nargument and does not alter the PD170 dependency verdict above.\n\nUnder the separately named PD170/QS170 input, finite birth energy already has\nthe recorded conditional pointwise bridge: an exceptional integer produces an\nexceptional target `a=3u+1` with `3∤a`, and all ordinary predecessors of `a`\nare exceptional, contradicting their positive lower density. This remains a\nconditional PD consequence, not an unconditional replacement for the\nterminal-count estimate above.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_POINTWISE_TREE_SOURCES.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483_POINTWISE_TREE_SOURCES — weighted predecessor trees\n\nДата: 18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный ручной\nисточниково-аналитический проход, без программ, переборов, CAS и Lean.\nПрочитаны `PROJECT_STATE.md` и\n`outputs/collatz-four-methods/team1000/GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md`,\nвключая его distinction между Haar-усреднением структурного transfer\noperator и pointwise утверждением для фиксированного положительного seed.\n\n## 1. Что действительно доказано в первичных работах\n\n### Applegate--Lagarias: counts of a fixed-target inverse tree\n\nВ первичной работе *Density Bounds for the (3x+1) Problem I. Tree-Search\nMethod*, Math. Comp. 64 (1995), 411--426, abstract формулирует для\n(a\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3): если (n_k(a)) считает целые (n) с\n(T^k(n)=a), то при больших (k)\n\n\\[\n  1.302^k\\le n_k(a)\\le1.359^k,\n\\]\n\nа для (pi_a(x)), числа (n) с (|n|\\le x), которые когда-либо\nдостигают (a), доказано (pi_a(x)\\ge x^{0.65}). Источник прямо\nпомечает доказательства как computer-intensive. Это aggregate counting\nпо всем вершинам дерева фиксированной глубины/высоты; в inspected\nstatement нет Dirichlet-веса (A^{-q}), deterministic first-return map\nили pointwise upper bound для одного seed.\n\nВ работе *Density Bounds for the (3x+1) Problem II. Krasikov\nInequalities* (Applegate--Lagarias, 1995) полный доступный PDF даёт\nTheorem 1.1: для каждого (a\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3) и достаточно больших (x)\n\\[\n  \\pi_a(x)\\ge c_a x^{0.81}.\n\\]\nТам же введено ограниченное счётное множество\n\\(\\pi_a^*(x)\\), где все промежуточные итераты остаются в диапазоне\n\\(|T^i(n)|\\le x\\), но цель остаётся нижней aggregate-оценкой. Введение\nявно говорит, что метод не даёт верхних оценок для соответствующих\nquantities. Это не нужный pointwise weighted estimate.\n\nURLs:\n\n- [Applegate--Lagarias I, primary PDF/landing](https://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/applegateI.pdf)\n- [Applegate--Lagarias II, full primary PDF](https://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/applegateII.pdf)\n- [Lagarias publication list](https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/PUBLICATIONS/papers.html)\n\n### Crandall: aggregate lower count versus weighted upper bound\n\nCrandall, *On the “3x+1” Problem*, Math. Comp. 32 (1978), 1281--1292,\nDOI 10.1090/S0025-5718-1978-0480321-3, studies the Syracuse map on odd\nintegers and states in the abstract a positive power-law lower bound for\nthe number of (m\u003cx) which reach (1). The displayed AMS extraction is\ndamaged in the exponent, so no exact exponent is imported here. It\nalso connects failures/cycles with (2^u-3^v=p). The AMS PDF returned\n403 in this pass, so no more precise theorem is imported from Crandall.\nThe target 1 is fixed, but the theorem counts many successful starts\nbelow x. It does not provide a weighted upper bound on the inverse\nbranches of one prescribed terminal integer.\n\nURL: [Crandall primary article](https://www.ams.org/mcom/1978-32-144/S0025-5718-1978-0480321-3/S0025-5718-1978-0480321-3.pdf).\n\n### Wirsching: exact path encoding, 3-adic averaging, and its gap\n\nThe author-hosted preliminary paper *On positive predecessor density in\n(3n+1) dynamics* was read by the source role through its 18-page PDF:\n[posden.pdf](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf).\nSection1 encodes predecessors by parity paths; endpoint labelling is\ninjective exactly for noncyclic targets. It records the size inequality\na>=3^ell*b/2^(k+ell), with ell odd and k even steps. These are close\nprecedents for our generic inputs. Its estimating series is Haar-integrable,\nbut Theorem1 needs an additional uniform lower ratio assumption for\npositive predecessor density. The introduction explicitly leaves three\nconjectural gaps. This does not establish our critical pointwise sum.\n\nThe earlier primary paper *An improved estimate concerning (3n+1)\npredecessor sets*, Acta Arith. 63 (1993), 205--210, is available through\nthe journal metadata and its scanned PDF landing. Its abstract/intro\nstates a lower estimate of the form \\(\\vartheta_a(x)\\ge x^{0.48}\\) for\n(a\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3). Again this counts predecessors, rather than\nbounding a weighted branch sum from one fixed target.\n\nURLs:\n\n- [Wirsching 2003 primary PDF](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf)\n- [Wirsching 1993 Acta Arithmetica metadata](https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/acta-arithmetica/all/63/3/107778/an-improved-estimate-concerning-3n-1-predecessor-sets)\n- [Wirsching 1993 scanned PDF search result](https://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/aa/aa63/aa6332.pdf)\n\n## 2. Comparison with the candidate (q>1) estimate\n\nThe candidate's proposed lemma is an elementary deterministic-tree\nstatement, not something stated in the sources above. For the specified\nminimal ordered structural-return map R, PR1--PR3 in\n`POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md` establish the needed hypotheses.\nLet n be a fixed positive terminal integer. Every inverse schedule has a\npositive integer source m>=1, and its affine coefficient A satisfies the\nexplicitly checked inequality\n\n\\[\n       A^{-q}\\le (n/m)^q. \n\\]\n\nIf n is nonperiodic, PR3 gives one schedule per source m. If n is\nperiodic, raw endpoint multiplicity is infinite, but repetitions of the\npositive cycle have a geometric inverse-slope factor. The relevant\nquantity is therefore the weighted cycle factor\n\n\\[\n c_q(n)=\\frac1{1-A_c^{-q}},\n\\]\n\nnot a finite count of schedules. PR3 proves c_q(n)\u003c=3n+1 for q>=1.\nConsequently, for q>1,\n\n\\[\n W_q(n):=\\sum_{\\text{admissible schedules}}A^{-q}\n \\le c_q(n)n^q\\sum_{m\\ge1}m^{-q}\n \\le (3n+1)n^q\\zeta(q).\n\\]\n\nFor the ordinary shortcut inverse tree the same audit applies: an even\ninverse edge has beta=0, so PR1 becomes a weak inequality, while every\npositive cycle contains an odd step and has B_c>=1/3. Thus the bound is\nunconditional for the indicated R, and also for that ordinary shortcut\ninverse tree. It is still an elementary summability argument, not a\ntheorem about all Collatz trajectories. The inspected primary papers use\nrelated affine path inequalities and exact predecessor encodings, but do\nnot state this (3n+1)n^q zeta(q) lemma in the above form.\n\nAt (q=1), the same comparison for sources m\u003c=X only gives the harmonic\nupper envelope\n\n\\[\n n\\sum_{m\\le X}m^{-1}\\le n(1+\\log X),\n\\]\n\nwhose bound grows with the cutoff. This divergence is a failure of the\ncomparison bound; it does not prove that the actual W_1(n) diverges.\nSince W_1 is a nonnegative schedule sum, cancellation cannot improve the\nbound, but additional sparsity or stronger weights could. Thus the\ncandidate's q>1 result does not settle the critical exponent q=1. To\nclose that case one needs genuine\nadditional sparsity of admissible sources, a stronger coefficient lower\nbound, or a nontrivial collision/multiplicity estimate. A finite-return\nfactor or a Haar-a.e. transfer bound does not supply this missing\nharmonic summability for a prescribed positive integer.\n\n## 3. Bounded audit of POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT\n\nThe root candidate's PR1--PR4 are internally consistent in the stated\ndomain. Disjoint source cylinders make the induced ordered return relation\ndeterministic, so a fixed positive source m contributes at most one\nnonperiodic first-hit schedule. For an inverse word,\n\n\\[\n       m=A_w n-B_w,\\qquad B_w\\ge1/3,\n\\]\n\nand therefore (A_w=(m+B_w)/n>m/n), giving the required weight bound.\nFor a periodic terminal point, repeated visits are not bounded in number;\nPR3 correctly sums them by the geometric factor\n\\(c_q(n)=(1-A_c^{-q})^{-1}\\). A positive cycle has\n\\(A_c=1+B_c/n\\ge1+1/(3n)\\), hence \\(c_q(n)\\le3n+1\\) for \\(q\\ge1\\).\n\nThe same sign argument is valid for the ordinary shortcut inverse tree:\neven edges have \\(B=0\\) and give a weak inequality, while a positive cycle\ncontains an odd edge and has positive total intercept at least \\(1/3\\).\nThus PR4 is an unconditional \\(q>1\\) statement for the specified\ndeterministic return map (and for the ordinary shortcut inverse tree under\nthe same schedule convention), including arbitrary alternating expanding\nand contracting branches. It does not assume that all branches expand.\n\nPR5 identifies the real remaining issue. At \\(q=1\\), comparison with all\ninteger sources gives only a harmonic cutoff bound; its divergence does not\nshow that the actual nonnegative resolvent \\(W_1(n)\\) diverges. Pointwise\nfiniteness at \\(q=1\\) requires extra source sparsity or a sharper weight\nestimate. PR6 shows that the known ordered contraction\ncan repeat only finitely many times for each fixed positive integer, with a\nvaluation/size budget; this does not control arbitrary mixed schedules.\n\n## 4. Verdict and scope limits\n\nThe primary literature already contains: (i) exact finite-path and\npredecessor-set encodings; (ii) strong aggregate lower bounds for fixed\ntargets; and (iii) 3-adic averaged/Markov-operator methods. In the\nactually read material I found no theorem stated as a Dirichlet/Poincare\nseries with a critical exponent that yields the candidate's fixed-seed\npointwise upper bound, and no source resolving the (q=1) harmonic\nborderline. This is a bounded literature-scope statement, not a claim of\nworldwide absence.\n\nThe candidate is therefore a proved q>1 fixed-seed lemma for the specified\ndeterministic return map, while the true unresolved interface remains\nq=1. It does not convert Wirsching's\nHaar-integrable estimating series or Applegate--Lagarias aggregate counts\ninto a theorem for one prescribed positive seed.\n\nRoot editorial clarification, 18 September 2026: the contraction budget is\nPR6. The later PR7 (slope escape) and PR8 (abstract countermodel) have\nseparate full audits in FOLLOWUP_0485_POINTWISE_RETURN_TREE.md sections7--8.\nThe primary-source synopsis was shortened without changing its scope.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_SIGNED_ENERGY_SOURCE_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — signed harmonic energy and primary-source scope\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md` and the complete AC candidate\n[`ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md`](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md),\nincluding AC1–AC18. For external primary comparison I read the relevant\nHTML portions of:\n\n- Terence Tao, *Almost all orbits of the Collatz map attain almost bounded\n  values*, arXiv:1909.03562v7, §§1.1–1.4, Definition 1.2,\n  Proposition 1.9, Remarks 1.10/1.13/1.16, Propositions 1.14 and 1.17:\n  \u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1909.03562>.\n- Olivier Rozier, *Parity sequences of the 3x+1 map on the 2-adic integers\n  and Euclidean embedding*, arXiv:1805.00133v6, the parity-coding,\n  2-adic-conjugacy and functional-equation portions of the HTML:\n  \u003chttps://arxiv.org/html/1805.00133>.\n\nThis is a targeted source check. I did not use the withdrawn Siegel paper\nor the inaccessible Berg–Meinardus full text, and I did not run programs,\nCAS, Lean, or enumeration.\n\n## What the primary sources actually control\n\nTao's Definition 1.2 samples integers with logarithmic weight\n\\(\\sum_{N\\le x}1/N\\). Theorem 1.3 (equivalently Theorem 1.6 for the\nSyracuse map) gives an almost-all logarithmic-density statement for the\nminimal orbit value. The mechanism is probabilistic: Proposition 1.9\napproximates finite Syracuse valuation vectors by iid geometric variables\nunder a residue-uniformity hypothesis, and Proposition 1.14 gives\nfine-scale 3-adic oscillation/mixing for the random Syracuse offset:\nfor \\(1\\le m\\le n\\), its oscillation at scale \\(3^{-m}\\) is\n\\(O_A(m^{-A})\\). Proposition 1.17 is the equivalent superpolynomial\nFourier-decay formulation. These are estimates for random finite\n3-adic offset laws, not estimates for a deterministic exact invariant\n\\(\\beta\\), its boundary \\(d(n)\\), or the signed AC coefficient\n\\[\nq(n)=e(n)-4\\,1_{Z_R}(n)-3\\,1_{D_S}(n)\n       +2\\,1_{Z_S}(n)+3\\,1_{D_R}(n).\n\\]\n\nTao explicitly records the relevant averaging obstruction in the\nintroduction: logarithmic/uniform measure is not invariant under the\nCollatz map, so an almost-all statement can send mass into a sparse\nexceptional set and cannot simply be iterated. This is a direct primary\nsource warning against transferring an averaged transport estimate to\npointwise control of an arbitrary exceptional set. Tao's Remark 1.16\nalso says that upgrading the logarithmic-density result to natural density\nwould require fine-scale mixing of the *entire* random affine map, not only\nits offset; that stronger statement is not proved there.\n\nRozier's primary result gives a one-to-one 2-adic parity coding/conjugacy\nand functional equations for the parity coding map. It supplies exact\nfinite parity-cylinder and affine identities. It does not turn a\ndeterministic integer coloring invariant under \\(T\\) into a regular\nfunction of the parity code, nor does it give a harmonic-variation or\nDirichlet bound for \\(d,e\\). The paper itself notes that some displayed\nfunctional equations apply only on specified congruence classes, so local\ncoding identities do not automatically become a global density statement.\n\n## Precise implication for AC\n\nAC already proves a bounded *signed* harmonic cumulative remainder:\n\\[\n Q(X)=\\sum_{n\\le X}\\frac{q(n)}n=O(1).\n\\]\nIts Dirichlet transfer is therefore legitimate: with \\(s=1+\\varepsilon\\),\n\\[\n \\sum_{n\\ge1}q(n)n^{-s}\n =\\varepsilon\\int_1^\\infty Q(t)t^{-\\varepsilon-1}\\,dt=O(1).\n\\]\nThis is cancellation information. It is not a bound for\n\\[\n \\sum_n\\frac{|q(n)|}{n},\n\\]\nnor for the positive birth energy \\(E=\\sum e(n)/n\\). The elementary\nlogical obstruction is that a bounded signed cumulative sum can coexist\nwith divergent variation (for example alternating harmonic signs); this is\nonly a non-implication example, not a proposed Collatz coloring. In AC\nlanguage, one still needs a shared-color/path estimate pairing the\npositive charges \\(4Z_R+3D_S\\) with the negative charges\n\\(2Z_S+3D_R\\), or an independent finite bound on \\(Z_R,D_S\\).\n\nTao's fine-scale mixing cannot be inserted into that missing step without\nnew hypotheses identifying the deterministic \\(\\beta\\)-mask or its charge\nsets with admissible test sets for the random Syracuse law, together with\nuniform control of the exceptional sets after affine transport. Neither\nTao nor Rozier supplies that interface. In particular, a logarithmic\ndensity-one statement for orbit descent does not imply that the harmonic\nvariation of every exact invariant boundary is finite.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe primary literature gives useful 2-adic/3-adic transport and mixing\nfor parity-coded random orbit variables, and Tao directly documents why\naveraging contractions lose control of transported exceptional sets. It\ndoes not provide a bounded-variation theorem applicable to the AC signed\nenergy. The AC endpoint identity remains a valid global finite-cutoff\nidentity and excludes its selected endpoint/origin-negligible regime, but\nfull finite energy remains open. No PD input, withdrawn source, or\nunproved regularity of \\(\\beta\\) has been imported.\n\n## Audit of the stronger actual-mask S/R estimate\n\nI also read the complete internal signed-balance derivation in\n[`FOLLOWUP_0459_SIGNED_BALANCE_AND_TELESCOPING.md`](FOLLOWUP_0459_SIGNED_BALANCE_AND_TELESCOPING.md)\nand the exact endpoint-mask derivation in\n[`FOLLOWUP_0431_SIGNED_ENDPOINT_MASKS.md`](FOLLOWUP_0431_SIGNED_ENDPOINT_MASKS.md).\nThis is an internal manual argument, not an external literature theorem.\n\nFor the S-channel reward \\(H=3D_S-2Z_S\\), the Bellman potential\n\\(Q=(5/2,2,0)\\) passes all six residue checks for the actual odd-boundary\nmaps \\(A(q)=2q+1\\) and \\(B(q)=4q+1\\):\n\n\\[\n Q_i\\ge Q_{2i+1}/2,\n \\qquad Q_i\\ge (2,2,-1)_i+Q_{i+1}/4.\n\\]\n\nKeeping the negative residue-2 reward on B-prefixes gives the initial right\ncoefficients \\((1,5/4,0)\\) and left coefficients \\((1/2,0,5/8)\\) by birth\nresidue \\(0,1,2\\). Thus the claimed bound\n\n\\[\n H(X)\\le \\tfrac32E_0(X)+\\tfrac54P(X)+\\tfrac58E_2(X)+O(1)\n\\]\n\nis consistent with the finite path decomposition, provided that the\nalready stated unique-birth-ancestor/path partition is used. The negative\nprefix terms must remain in this estimate; discarding them would invalidate\nthe residue-1 and residue-2 left bounds.\n\nThe subsequent coefficient algebra is also correct. AC14 gives\n\\[\n E_0=4Z_R-3D_R-2P+O(1),\n \\qquad E_2=P+H+O(1),\n \\qquad E=4Z_R-3D_R+H+O(1).\n\\]\nSubstitution into the preceding inequality yields\n\\[\n H\\le16Z_R-12D_R-3P+O(1),\n \\qquad\n E\\le20Z_R-15D_R-3P+O(1)\\le20Z_R+O(1).\n\\]\nThe \\(-3P\\) coefficient follows from the \\(-2P\\) in \\(E_0\\); omitting that\nterm would produce the wrong coefficient. Since \\(Z_R\\) is a birth subset,\nthis proves, within the AC finite-cutoff scope, that finite \\(Z_R\\) is\nequivalent to finite total birth energy (the converse is immediate).\n\nThe R-column comparison is exact on the filter\n\\[\n \\mathcal Q=\\{q:q\\text{ odd},\\ 3\\mid q, d(q)=1\\}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(a_h=d(2^hq)\\), the universal identity \\(a_2=0\\) gives\n\\(e(2^hq)=(1-a_h)a_{h+1}\\) and\n\\(R_b(2^hq)=a_h(1-a_{h+1})\\). A first fall \\(1\\to0\\) occurs at\n\\(h=0\\) or \\(1\\), unless it occurs at \\(h\\ge3\\); subsequent falls are\nseparated by at least two levels. Hence the fall weight\n\\(\\sum_{h\\,\\mathrm{fall}}2^{-h}\\) lies between \\(1/2\\) and\n\\(1+1/8+1/32+\\cdots=7/6\\). Accounting for the source weight\n\\(1/(2^{h+2}q/3)=3/(4\\,2^hq)\\) and exact cutoffs gives\n\\[\n \\frac38 Q(3X/8)\\le Z_R(X)\\le\\frac78 Q(3X/4),\n \\qquad\n Q(Y)=\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal Q,\\ q\\le Y}\\frac1q.\n\\]\nThe lower cutoff (3X/8) ensures that either possible first fall is\nrepresented; the upper cutoff follows from (q\\le3X/4) for every R-source.\nConsequently \\(Z_R\\) has finite harmonic mass iff \\(Q\\) does, and the\nstronger actual-mask bridge reduces finite energy to \\(Q\\)-finiteness. This\nstill does not prove \\(Q\\)-finiteness.\n\nFinally, for odd \\(u\\), exact invariance gives\n\\(\\beta(3u+1)=\\beta(u)\\). With \\(q=3u\\), therefore\n\\[\n Q(Y)=\\frac13\\sum_{u\\le Y/3,\\ u\\text{ odd}}\n \\frac{|\\beta(3u)-\\beta(u)|}{u}.\n\\]\nThis identifies the remaining obstruction with the corresponding odd\n\\(\\times3\\) harmonic variation. It is an exact reformulation, not a\nfiniteness result and not a primary-source claim.\n\n## Final audit of root SB1–SB18\n\nI read the complete root file\n[`SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md`](SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md),\nincluding SB1–SB18. The following is a manual audit only; no program, CAS,\nLean build, or enumeration was used.\n\nThe inherited AC identities in SB1 and the subtraction\n\\(H=3D_S-2Z_S=2L_{\\rm odd}-3L_{2\\bmod3}+O(1)\\) are consistent. The\npotential \\(V=(5/2,2,0)\\) satisfies the six residue inequalities for\n\\(A(q)=2q+1\\) and \\(B(q)=4q+1\\), including the negative B reward at residue\n2. For a finite prefix, \\(r/q\\le V_i/q-V_j/(aq)\\le V_i/q-V_j/q'\\) is valid\nbecause \\(q'=aq+1\\) and \\(V_j\\ge0\\). Thus terminal potentials may be\nremoved with the favorable sign, but only for finite prefixes.\n\nThe all-depth path accounting is also correctly scoped to the stated\nunique nearest-birth partition. The right coefficients are \\((1,5/4,0)\\),\nand retaining the negative even-prefix terms gives left coefficients\n\\((1/2,0,5/8)\\). With \\(M=4Z_R-3D_R\\), the substitutions\n\\[\n E=M+H+O(1),\\qquad E_0=M-2P+O(1),\\qquad E_2=P+H+O(1)\n\\]\nturn SB8 into\n\\[\n H\\le \\tfrac32M-\\tfrac98P+\\tfrac58H+O(1),\n\\]\nso \\(H\\le4M-3P+O(1)\\) and\n\\[\n E\\le20Z_R-15D_R-3P+O(1)\\le20Z_R+O(1).\n\\]\nThe \\(-3P\\) coefficient is therefore algebraically correct. Since \\(Z_R\\)\nis a birth subset, finite \\(Z_R\\) is equivalent to finite total harmonic\nbirth energy, but neither SB nor AC proves that \\(Z_R\\) is finite.\n\nThe R-column claims are exact on\n\\(\\mathcal Q=\\{q:q\\text{ odd},3\\mid q,d(q)=1\\}\\). For \\(a_h=d(2^hq)\\),\n\\(a_0=1\\) and \\(a_2=d(4q)=0\\), while\n\\[\n e(2^hq)=(1-a_h)a_{h+1},\\qquad\n R_b(2^hq)=a_h(1-a_{h+1}).\n\\]\nThe first fall is necessarily at \\(h=0\\) or \\(1\\); later falls are\nseparated by at least two levels and occur only at \\(h\\ge3\\). Hence the\nfall weight lies in \\([1/2,7/6]\\). Exact source weights and cutoffs then\nindeed yield\n\\[\n \\frac38Q(3X/8)\\le Z_R(X)\\le\\frac78Q(3X/4),\n \\qquad Q(Y)=\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal Q,\\,q\\le Y}\\frac1q.\n\\]\nThe lower cutoff includes either possible first fall, and the upper cutoff\nuses \\(q\\le3X/4\\). Thus \\(Z_R\\), \\(Q\\), and the odd multiplication-by-three\nvariation \\(G(Y)=\\sum_{u\\le Y,\\,u\\text{ odd}}|\\beta(3u)-\\beta(u)|/u\\) are finite\nsimultaneously, with \\(Q(Y)=G(Y/3)/3\\). This is an exact reduction, not a\nfiniteness proof.\n\nOne presentation point in SB6 is harmless but should be read precisely: the\nomitted term after truncating at \\(J=\\lfloor\\log_2(X/q)\\rfloor\\) is attached\nto the next index \\(2^{J+1}q\\in(X,2X]\\), while the included top index\n\\(2^Jq\\) lies in \\((X/2,X]\\). Distinct next indices have bounded harmonic\nmass on \\((X,2X]\\), so the claimed uniform truncation (O(1)) is valid; the\nphrase “top indices ... hence omitted terms” should not be interpreted as\nidentifying those two index ranges.\n\nFinally, SB18 is an exact identity for the defect\n\\(g(n)=\\beta(3n)-\\beta(n)\\): dyadic invariance gives \\(g(2n)=g(n)\\), and\nfor odd \\(n\\), setting \\(v=(9n+1)/2\\) gives\n\\(g(S(n))-g(n)=\\beta(v+1)-\\beta(v)\\). Taking absolute values would simply\nrecreate a boundary-variation problem; no summable estimate follows. The\nSB endpoint remains the same as AC: no PD input or primary mixing theorem is\nused, and a future finite-energy result would still need the separately\nrecorded pointwise bridge for a full Collatz conclusion.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_SINGLE_ODD_INVERSE.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — single odd-step inverse and origin charge\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded analytic/archive audit; no\nmathematical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, or new agent. I read\n`PROJECT_STATE.md`, the full `COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md`,\n`COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md`, `VARIABLE_RUN_AND_POINTWISE_DESCENT_AUDIT.md`,\nand the relevant odd-only sections of `work/round185-g-odd-tree-prior-art.md`\nand `work/round185-h-odd-backward-chain.md`. No new external source was\nneeded. The odd-only map and its inverse layers are already present in the\nR185/OT archival material; no novelty claim is made.\n\n## 1. The one-odd-step identity\n\nFor an odd actual birth q, let\n\n    n=S(q)=(3q+1)/2,\n\nand write c=β(q)=β(q+1). Since 2n+1=3q+2, exact invariance gives\nβ(n)=c and β(2n+1)=1−c. Also\nβ(2n)=β(n)=c. The binary boundary law then gives\n\n    d(2n+1)=1−d(n),\n    d(2n)=1,\n    e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1)=1−d(n).                (SO1)\n\nThe second equality follows because (d(2n)\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}d(2n+1)=d(n))\nand (d(2n+1)=1-d(n)). Thus:\n\n* if e(S(q))=1, the output is an actual birth and q→S(q) is the retained\n  single-odd growth edge;\n* if e(S(q))=0, then d(S(q))=1, so S(q) is an active boundary. Its nearest\n  birth ancestor is at most floor(S(q)/2)\u003cq. This is a strict smaller-birth\n  alternative; if the relevant endpoint is an even birth, BR2 supplies the\n  same type of strict descent.\n\nThis is an exact local dichotomy for an actual birth q, not an unconditional\nstatement about arbitrary odd q or about all positive orbits.\n\n## 2. Finiteness and injectivity of the S-growth graph\n\nOn positive odd inputs S(q)=(3q+1)/2 is injective, has image\n\n    I_S={3k+2:k≥0} = {n>0:n≡2 (mod 3)},\n\nand satisfies S(q)>q. If q+1=2^h v with v odd, then\n\n    S(q)+1=3·2^(h−1)v,\n    ν₂(S(q)+1)=h−1.                              (SO2)\n\nWhenever the next S-edge is available, its source S(q) must be odd, hence\nh≥2; each edge decreases the valuation of the next odd source by exactly\none. The next output is therefore even after finitely many edges. Every\nS-growth component is finite, including singleton components. The affine\nmultiplier is at least 3/2, so a chain with origin a satisfies\n\n    Σ_(j=0..L) 1/n_j ≤ (1/a)Σ_(j≥0)(2/3)^j = 3/a.   (SO3)\n\nThis is the one-branch analogue of the BC 9/a estimate. It uses retained\nactual-birth edges; arithmetic admissibility alone does not assert masks.\n\nThe h=1 edge deserves explicit handling. Then S(q) is even. In the inverse\nbirth calculation below, q=1 gives S(1)=2 and e(2)=0, so it cannot be an\nactual output-birth case. For q>1 with h=1, the same finite-chain statement\nholds: the output is even and no next S-source exists.\n\n## 3. Inverse image of a birth and the h=1 boundary\n\nEvery odd q has a unique representation q=2^h v−1 with h=ν₂(q+1)≥1 and\nv odd. Its S-image is\n\n    n=S(q)=3·2^(h−1)v−1 = X_(h,1)\n\nin the SC notation. If h≥2 and e(n)=1, SC2 gives\nC_h=B_1≠C_{h+1}, in particular C_h≠C_{h+1}. Now SC3 applies to the odd\nbirth n and gives 3^h v≡1 mod 4; hence 3^(h−1)v≡3 mod 4 and the\nelementary merging identity gives\n\n    C_(h−1)=C_h.\n\nFor h=1, n=3v−1 is even. The inherited exclusion e(4m+2)=0 forces\nn≡0 mod 4 whenever e(n)=1, hence v≡3 mod 4. For v>1 the genuine paths\n\n    v−1 → (v−1)/2 → (3v−1)/4,\n    3v−1 → (3v−1)/2 → (3v−1)/4\n\nmerge and give C_0=C_1. The only edge q=1 has n=2 and e(2)=0. Thus the\nmerge also holds in the h=1 inverse-birth case; the even endpoint must be\nhandled through this separate mod-4 argument, rather than by applying SC3\nto n.\n\nIf n=S(q) is an actual birth but q is not, SC1 gives\nβ(q)=C_h=β(floor(q/2)) and\nβ(q+1)=β(floor(q/2)+1)=B_0. Since (C_h\\ne C_{h+1}), q would be a birth\nexactly when (C_h=B_0). Therefore q not a birth implies\n\n    d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=1.                         (SO4)\n\nThe nearest birth ancestor is then at depth at least two and obeys\n\n    b≤floor(q/4)\u003cn/6\u003cn,\n\nbecause n=(3q+1)/2. Thus an image birth whose inverse source fails the\nbirth mask still has a strict smaller birth, uniformly in h, with q=1\nalready excluded by e(2)=0.\n\n## 4. Image-origin charge and its exact scope\n\nLet D_S be origins of the retained S-growth graph that lie in I_S. Such an\norigin n=S(q) must have an inverse q that is not an actual birth; otherwise\nthe edge q→n is retained. The h=1 case is included here: its merger was\nproved above, and the same SO4 conclusion applies. Charge q to its nearest\nbirth ancestor b. If q is not a birth, SO4 gives d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=1;\nthe XOR law then gives d(2 floor(q/2))=0, so floor(q/2) is not a birth.\nThus every charged q is at dyadic depth at least two from b, and\nb≤floor(q/4).\n\nThere is a useful spacing refinement. For a charged odd q, write\nn=S(q) and c=β(n). The birth at n gives β(q)=c and\nβ(2q+1)=β(3q+2)=1−c, while d(q)=1 gives β(q+1)=1−c. Hence\n\n    d(2q)=1,   d(2q+1)=0.                         (SO5)\n\nThe active child of q is therefore uniquely 2q, which is even. Along\neach of the at most two active paths descending from b, two charged odd\nsources cannot occur at adjacent dyadic depths. Since the first possible\ndepth is two, the harmonic contribution of one path is at most\n\n    (1/b)Σ_(j≥1)2^(−2j)=1/(3b).\n\nSumming the two paths gives\n\n    Σ_(q charged to b)1/q ≤ 2/(3b).               (SO6)\n\nThis uses only the accepted two-path ancestry bound and the displayed\nlocal color identities; it does not assert that both paths are realized.\nSince 1/S(q)=2/(3q+1)≤2/(3q), and n≤X implies q\u003c2X/3 and\nb≤q/4\u003cX/6, the full image-origin charge satisfies\n\n    E_(D_S)(X) ≤ (4/9) E_β(X/6).                  (SO7)\n\nIf the S-growth graph is defined on all actual birth vertices (with isolated\neven births allowed), every birth is in a finite S-component and SO3 applies.\nWriting P_S for births outside I_S, the chain decomposition and SO7 yield\n\n    E_β(X) ≤ 3E_(P_S)(X)+(4/3)E_β(X/6).           (SO8)\n\nThe coefficient 4/3 is still larger than one, so this refinement does not\ngive a contraction. The primitive term E_(P_S) remains uncontrolled. If the\nproposed S graph is restricted only to odd-source vertices, SO8 must be read\nfor that restricted vertex set; applying it to all E_beta requires adding\neven births as terminal vertices.\n\n## 5. Archival precedence and verdict\n\nThis is the intermediate uniform-chain estimate; §6 below refines its\n4/3 coefficient to7/9 by retaining valuation-dependent component weights.\n\nThe exact odd-only acceleration, its valuation\n(S(n)=(3n+1)/2^{\\nu_2(3n+1)}), and the inverse family\n(m=(2^a n−1)/3) are already recorded in the fully read R185/OT material,\nespecially OT185.2–OT185.4 and the `VARIABLE_RUN_AND_POINTWISE_DESCENT_AUDIT`.\nThose records also distinguish finite odd-run descent from a global pointwise\nproof. The present S(q) identity is the one-shortcut-step specialization of\nthat standard odd-run framework, with the additional β birth/mask dichotomy\nSO1. The h=1 inverse case is included after the separate even/mod-4 merger\ncheck above; it is not an omitted exception in the charge estimate.\n\nThe bounded result is thus: single-odd retained growth is injective and\nnecessarily finite; the h=1 inverse-birth merger is valid through the\nseparate even/mod-4 argument; and all image-origin failures admit the\nrefined coefficient 4/9 before the chain factor, hence 4/3 in the\nall-vertex recursion. This is not a contraction, does not control the\nprimitive term, does not assert that any displayed arithmetic progression\ncontains actual births, and does not prove full Collatz or a universal\nsmaller-birth theorem.\n\n## 6. Independent audit of the valuation-sensitive refinement SG1–SG12\n\nI read the complete root candidate\n`SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md`, including SG1–SG12.\nThe correction in its SG4 inequality is essential: from a charged inverse\nsource one has\n\n    b≤floor(q/4)\u003cn/6\u003cn,\n    n/6=q/4+1/12,\n\nnot `floor(q/4)\u003cq/6`. With that correction, the h=1 merger and the depth-two\nstart are valid: if p=floor(q/2), then q=2p+1 and d(q)=d(p)=1, so the XOR\nlaw gives d(2p)=0 and e(p)=0. The h=1 argument uses e(4m+2)=0 to force v≡3 (mod 4),\nthen the two displayed paths genuinely meet; q=1 is separately excluded by\ne(2)=0.\n\nThe SG5 forced-child step is exact: for a charged q, β(q)=β(S(q)) and\nβ(q+1)=β(2q+1)=1−β(q), so d(2q)=1 and d(2q+1)=0. Hence the next active\nchild is 2q, and it is even. The accepted two-path ancestry bound therefore\ngives the non-adjacent charged-depth estimate used in SG5. The valuation\nbound in SG6 follows from\n\n    n_j+1=(3/2)^j(q+1),\n\nso 1/n_j≤(2/3)^j/q; the endpoint and h=1 cases are covered by the stated\nfinite prefix. For a later charged source r depths away, the forced first\nzero bit gives q'≥2^r q and h'≤r−1. The potential inequality\n\n    (A_(r−1)+2/9)/2^r≤2/9,  r≥2,\n\nis equality at r=2 and strict for r≥3, so finite backward induction and\nmonotone limits justify SG7 without assuming finite total energy. The left\npath costs at most 2/(9b); the right path costs at most\n(2+2/9)/(4b)=5/(9b), giving 7/(9b) in SG8. The cutoff b\u003cn/6 then yields\nSG9 and the coefficient 7/9; rearranging finite nonnegative sums gives the\ncorrect finite-cutoff comparison Eβ(X)≤(27/2)E_(P_S)(X), without proving\nfiniteness of either full series as X tends to infinity.\n\nThus I find no remaining mathematical gap in SG4 or SG6–SG9 within their\nstated ancestry and exact-invariance inputs. The result contracts only the\nimage-origin contribution. The primitive source P_S, realization of a\nnonconstant β, and full Collatz descent remain outside this bounded audit.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_TERMINAL_COUNT_LOGIC.md":"# FOLLOWUP 0483 — terminal-count logic and the missing color correlation\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Bounded analytical and source\naudit; no programs, CAS, enumeration, Lean, or state/map edits.\n\n## Read scope\n\nI read the current `PROJECT_STATE.md`, the complete endpoint transport file\n[EC1–EC8](ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md), the finite-chain\nbarrier [BM](FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md), and the full\nlocal color-strip file [SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md). For the\nprimary comparison I read the relevant definitions and results in Olivier\nRozier and Claude Terracol, [*Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences*,\narXiv:2502.00948](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00948): the abstract and\nintroduction, Definitions 1.1–1.2 and 2.1, Lemma 2.3, Theorem 2.4, Lemma\n2.5, and the surrounding discussion of parity vectors and residue classes.\nThis was a targeted source check, not a survey or a priority claim.\n\n## What EC proves unconditionally once the endpoint input is supplied\n\nFor an exact binary invariant beta, the retained map `F_beta` is injective,\nand every birth either has at least `k` retained forward steps or first reaches\na true terminal at some depth `j\u003ck`. Injectivity gives at most one predecessor\nof a fixed terminal at a fixed depth. This proves the EC forward inequality\n\n`R_beta(X) ≤ X(3/4)^k + 2^k\n              + Σ_{j\u003ck} Z_beta((3/2)^j(X+1)-1)`.\n\nThe reverse inequality using origins is analogous. These are valid transport\ninequalities for all vertices: those admitting `k` retained steps are in the\nprefix class, while vertices that first reach a terminal at depth `j\u003ck` are\ncounted by the endpoint sum. Cycles and infinite-forward paths are in the\nprefix class; a path with a terminal at finite forward depth belongs to the\nshort-depth part even if its backward history is infinite. The\nlogarithmic/profile calculations EC4–EC9 correctly turn an\nindependently supplied subpower or monotone-profile bound on `Z_beta` or\n`O_beta` into a finite birth-energy bound.\n\nThe logical direction stops there. The endpoint sums contain the actual masks\n`e(n)d(F(n))` and the exact origin partition. The arithmetic prefix estimates\ncount possible guarded F-words before those color masks are imposed. They\ndo not upper-bound the number of true terminal masks by a subpower. Replacing a\ntrue terminal set by all arithmetic guards only enlarges it, and the enlarged\nset has no required sparsity in EC. Thus the EC transport does not derive its\nown endpoint hypothesis.\n\n## Why the BM and SC information does not close the gap\n\nBM's terminal estimate is an energy comparison of the form\n`E_terminal(X)≤2E_beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2`. It is a bound by the same unknown\nbirth energy at a rescaled cutoff, with coefficient greater than one and an\nadditive term. It is therefore not a contraction and cannot prove finite\nenergy or sparse terminals. The finite-component minimum and two-endpoint\nbarriers likewise concern unmasked arithmetic components; they do not control\nthe color-realized subset.\n\nSC1–SC5 give genuine local color identities, merging rules, and a reduction\nof possible increasing transitions. Those identities can force a particular\nbirth or boundary to fail in a specified strip, but the current file contains\nno uniform count of the strips that survive all masks. SC11 itself records\nthat a failed mixed H-mask need not yet give a smaller birth than the initial\none. Consequently the remaining input is a joint correlation statement:\none needs a uniform upper bound for the actual terminal mask (or for the\nactual origin partition), not just a count of parity cylinders or guarded\narithmetic paths.\n\nUnder the named PD170/QS170 input, the conditional logic is different but\nalready recorded: finite birth energy implies density-one constancy, and PD\nturns any exceptional integer into an exceptional target with positive-density\npredecessors, forcing pointwise constancy. This gives the conditional EC10\ncontrapositive that a hypothetical nonconstant invariant would have endpoint\ncount exponent one. It does not furnish an endpoint upper bound or construct\nsuch an invariant.\n\n## Primary parity-vector comparison\n\nRozier–Terracol work with the accelerated map `T(n)=(3n+1)/2` on odd inputs\nand `n/2` on even inputs. They write a length-`j` iterate as\n`T^j(n)=(3^q/2^j)n+E_j(n)`, where `q` is the number of odd terms and the\nremainder depends only on the parity vector. Their Definitions 2.1 and the\ndiscussion immediately after Lemma 2.3 state that two integers have the same\nlength-`j` parity vector exactly when they lie in the same residue class\nmodulo `2^j`; every parity vector occurs once among a complete set of such\nclasses. Theorem 2.4 identifies extremal remainders by explicit residue\nclasses, and Lemma 2.5 computes the mean remainder over one full period.\n\nThis is precisely the type of finite parity-cylinder fact used in EC's\n`X(3/4)^k+2^k` and related prefix estimates. It provides no information on\nthe independent-looking binary invariant beta or on the mask `d(F(n))`.\nTheir paradoxical-sequence conditions concern the affine coefficient and\nremainder of an orbit segment, not the number of true terminal births of an\narbitrary exact invariant. The source therefore supports the arithmetic\ncoding interface, while leaving the EC endpoint-sparsity obligation\nuntouched.\n\n## Verdict and exact remaining obligation\n\nThere is no valid implication in the read material from a harmonic endpoint\ncoefficient below one, a finite-component prefix estimate, or parity-cylinder\ncounting alone to finite total birth energy. `E_terminal` is a subset of\n`E_beta`; a bound on that subset only helps after a genuine sparse or\ncorrelated mask estimate has been proved. The strongest clean sufficient\ninputs remain those already isolated in EC3/EC4/EC8: a power saving or a\nspecified monotone logarithmic profile for the true terminal or origin set.\n\nThe missing statement can be formulated as a uniform estimate for\n`#{n≤X:e(n)d(F(n))=1}` or for the exact origin partition, stable in all\ntranslated cutoffs and compatible with the color identities. No such estimate\nis established by BM, SC, EC, or the targeted primary parity-vector source.\nThis report makes no claim that the missing estimate is impossible or that\nthe full Collatz problem is otherwise resolved.\n\n## Audit of the proposed terminal coefficient `11/8`\n\nThe new spacing calculation has several valid local pieces. The identity\n`d(8t+4)=0` follows by writing `q=2t+1` and using\n`beta(4q)=beta(q)=beta(3q+1)=beta(4q+1)`. Thus a terminal target cannot have\n`v_2(y)=2`. An even S-target with `v_2(y)=1` is also impossible for an\nactual terminal birth: if `y=8r+2` or `8r+6` is an S-image, integrality of\n`x=(2y-1)/3` gives respectively `x=16s+1` or `x=16s+9`, hence `x≡1 mod 8`,\nwhere the inherited identity `e(8s+1)=0` rules out a birth source. Therefore\nan even S-target has `v_2(y)≥3`.\n\nThe gap rules are then valid at the stated level: after an endpoint target,\nthe forced active child is `1 mod 3`; at the next possible target depth the\nS candidate after an S-target is `4y`, but its proposed birth source\n`4n+1` has `d(4n+1)=1`, while the S candidate `4y+2` after an R-target has\n`v_2=1`. Hence a gap-two continuation can only be R, and a subsequent\nS-target has gap at least three. The finite backward induction with main\ncosts `3/2` and `3/4` therefore gives the stable future bounds\n`V_S=7/4` and `V_R=1`.\n\nThe initially suspected gap in the depth-one classification is closed by the\nstrip lemma in §2 of the terminal-spacing report. If an odd S-target `y` is\npresent, its S-source `n` satisfies `n≡3 mod 4`, so write\n`n=2^h v-1` with `h≥2` and `v` odd. Since `n` is a birth, SC2–SC3 give\n`C_(h-1)=C_h`. The strip identities give `y=X_(h,1)`,\n`floor(y/2)=X_(h-1,1)`, and equal color pairs at these two locations, hence\n`d(y)=d(floor(y/2))`. If this odd S-target were the right depth-one boundary\n`y=2b+1` of a birth `b`, then `floor(y/2)=b` and `d(b)=0`, whereas\nterminality requires `d(y)=1`, a contradiction. Thus a depth-one S-target can\noccur only on the left, and the left target is even; the `v_2(y)≥3` exclusion\nabove applies.\n\nWith this strip input, the displayed left/right potentials yield the claimed\n`7/8+1/2=11/8` main coefficient uniformly. Even a proved terminal estimate\n`E_terminal(X)≤(11/8)E_beta(cX)+O(1)` would not be a full-energy contraction:\nthe coefficient is greater than one and the right side still uses the total\nunknown birth energy. It supplies no terminal-count sparsity and does not\nclose EC3/EC4/EC8. The remaining input is a direct uniform sparsity estimate\nfor the true terminal/origin masks (or another independent finite-energy\nroute); the `11/8` endpoint coefficient does not provide it.\n\n## Audit of the combined D_S/D_R origin refinement\n\nI also read §6 of\n[FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md](FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md)\nand checked its continuation rules against SG4–SG9. The universal identity\n`d(8t+4)=0` implies that an odd active boundary `q` has its next odd active\nboundary either `A(q)=2q+1` (if `d(2q+1)=1`) or `B(q)=4q+1` (if\n`d(2q)=1`); `4q` is inactive in the latter case. Modulo 3 these have\nresidues `2i+1` and `i+1` when `q≡i`. A D_S charge forces B and costs at\nmost `2/(3q)`; a D_R charge is possible only at `i=0` and costs at most\n`1/(3q)` after its nonadjacent valuation exponents are summed.\n\nThe normalized supersolution is arithmetically correct:\n\n`(V_0,V_1,V_2)=(232,172,184)/189`,\n\nwith `V_0=1+V_1/4`, `V_1=2/3+V_2/4`, and\n`V_2=2/3+V_0/4`. The alternative D_R bounds are respectively\n`149/189`, `116/189`, and `92/189`, all below the corresponding V-value.\nThe apparent depth-two issue on the left is handled correctly: if the first\nodd boundary has depth at least three, `max(V_i)/8≤min(V_i)/4`, so the\nleft-path bound `V_(b+1 mod 3)/(4b)` remains valid. SG4 excludes a D_S\ncharge at the right depth-one odd boundary, because every D_S charge has its\nnearest birth ancestor at depth at least two.\n\nCombining the left and right paths gives the classwise main coefficients\n\n`101/189` for `b≡0`, `241/378` for `b≡1`, and `104/189` for `b≡2 (mod 3)`.\n\nThese fractions follow by multiplying the depth-one/right-path factors by\nthe displayed V-values; the largest is `241/378\u003c17/18`. For an origin target\n`n≤X`, SG4 gives `b\u003cX/6` for D_S and SR9 gives `b\u003cX/8` for D_R, so `b\u003cX/6`\nis a safe common cutoff. This refinement is therefore a valid improvement\nfor the union `D_S∪D_R`, after charging overlap additively through the\nsupersolution. The primitive origin class `P_1` is untouched, so the result\nis origin-only and does not produce a bound for all births or a full-energy\ncontraction. In particular, a coefficient below one for this origin subset\nwould still not supply the terminal/origin sparsity required by EC.\n\n## Full audit of WS1–WS9\n\nI read the complete\n[WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md)\nand cross-checked WS7–WS9 against the preceding combined-origin audit. WS1's\n`A/B` ancestry rule follows from `d(8t+4)=0`; the residues and depth changes\nare correct. WS2's even S-target restriction and odd-target depth lemma are\nalso correct. In particular, the earlier objection in this report to the\nright depth-one S-target was too broad: SG4 supplies the missing hypothesis.\nFor an odd S-target whose birth source is `n=2^h v-1`, `h≥2`, SC2–SC3 give\n`C_(h-1)=C_h`, and the strip identities give\n`d(y)=d(floor(y/2))`. A right depth-one target would have\n`floor(y/2)=b` for a birth `b`, forcing `d(y)=d(b)=0`, contrary to\nterminality. The corrected WS statement does not assert `e(n)=0` for every\nresidue-class integer.\n\nThe WS3 finite-prefix induction is valid: after an S-target, gap two can\nonly be the R candidate; after an R-target, the valuation-one S candidate is\nforbidden. The values `V_S=7/4` and `V_R=1` therefore give the endpoint\ncoefficient `11/8`, with the global S reciprocal correction added only once.\nWS4's residue/parity refinement is consistent with these path bounds.\n\nThe combined-origin formulas WS7–WS9 are also correct. The recurrences\n\n`U_i ≥ 2/3 + 1_(i=0)/3 + U_(i+1)/4`,\n`U_i ≥ 1_(i=0)/3 + max(U_(2i+1)/2,U_(i+1)/4)`\n\nare the right alternatives for simultaneous D_S/D_R charges and subsequent\nA/B continuation. The proposed `(232,172,184)/189` supersolution satisfies\nthem. The table coefficients `101/189`, `241/378`, `104/189` and common\ncutoff `X/6` follow with the left-depth correction for first odd depth\n`≥3`; the maximum `241/378` is below the former `17/18` coefficient.\n\nThe logical boundary in WS7 and WS9 is correctly preserved. These are\norigin-only and endpoint-only energy estimates. `P_1` remains in WS9, and\nneither estimate bounds all birth energy, proves endpoint counting sparsity,\nor yields a contraction for `E_beta`. No transition from a coefficient below\none on a subset to finite total energy is valid without an additional bound\non the complementary births.\n\nThe external-source scope in WS8 is compatible with the earlier targeted\nRozier–Terracol parity-vector reading recorded above; this full-file audit did\nnot claim a new independent re-read of a different source version. No new\nsource result is needed for the WS algebra. The only correction to my earlier\naudit is the now-closed depth-one objection; no WS7–WS9 arithmetic or\nlogical error remains within its stated origin-only scope.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_TWO_SYMBOL_CODE_SCOPE.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483 — scope of the two-symbol 11/110 growth code\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Bounded archival and primary-source audit;\nno mathematical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean run, or new agent. I read\nPROJECT_STATE.md and COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md in full. I read the\nrelevant exact sections of R150/N119/K150, R154/EI154, R155/SI155, and\nR159/AP159. The report does not re-audit all SC/BR estimates.\n\n## The two middle-orbit transitions\n\nFor a birth n and middle integer μ=2n+1, the SC identities are genuine\nordinary shortcut-orbit identities, subject to their residue and birth masks.\n\n* If n=3 mod 4, then μ=7 mod 8 and the first two shortcut inputs of μ are\n  odd. Direct substitution gives\n\n      T^2(μ)=(9μ+5)/4=2 H(n)+1,\n      H(n)=(9n+5)/4.\n\n  The word is 11. Positivity is automatic for positive n; the residue class\n  supplies the actual parity and divisibility guards.\n\n* For the retained G3 case n=13 mod 16 (the raw arithmetic branch is n=5\n  mod 8), μ=11 mod 16. The first two inputs are odd and the third is even,\n  giving\n\n      T^3(μ)=(9μ+5)/8=2 G3(n)+1,\n      G3(n)=(9n+3)/8.\n\n  The word is 110. The output must still satisfy the SC birth/color masks;\n  the affine value alone is not an actual birth.\n\nThus a chain of retained transitions would give a specific ordinary T orbit\nof μ whose consecutive macro-words are 11 or 110. It is an orbit of the\nmiddle integers, not an assertion that H or G3 is an ordinary orbit step on\nn itself.\n\n## Local archival precedence\n\nThe relevant ingredients already occur in separate accepted archival layers.\n\n1. **Repeated 110.** R150/K150 and its cited N119/H120 material give the\n   genuine word 110 and\n\n      F(n)=(9n+5)/8,       F(n)+5=(9/8)(n+5).\n\n   The full initial repetition requires the exact 2-adic guard on n+5; for a\n   positive start it is finite because a fixed positive integer has finite\n   (v_2(n+5)). These are the same parity-word mechanisms behind the raw\n   G5 branch, but G5 is not by itself the retained SC growth transition.\n\n2. **Terminal 11 and variable external code.** R154/EI154.3–6 and\n   R155/SI155.2 classify a different first-return interface using continuing\n   groups 00 and 10 and terminal groups 0, 01, and 11. The terminal 11\n   endpoint is exactly ((9n+5)/4). The classification is deterministic\n   under its J/V control-state hypotheses, but it is not a theorem that all\n   SC birth strips have those control states.\n\n3. **Independent-time variable words.** R159/AP159.2–4 gives a genuine\n   inverse family with word (1^C(10)^B100), reaching (T(mu)) and, with\n   one extra step, (T^2(mu)) at independent times. It checks integrality,\n   positivity, and every intermediate parity. This is useful precedent for\n   variable parity strings and a warning that common endpoint identities do\n   not imply equal clocks. It does not classify arbitrary forward mixtures\n   of 11 and 110 from one positive middle start.\n\nTherefore SC's two-symbol reduction has clear local archival components, but\nno priority or global classification claim is justified.\n\n## What “ordinary orbit” requires\n\nFor a fixed finite word, an affine formula is not enough. One must verify:\n\n* the input residue makes each displayed division integral;\n* each intermediate state has the parity prescribed by the word;\n* all states remain in the positive domain when positivity is claimed; and\n* for SC, the output birth and beta-color/boundary masks hold.\n\nFor the two SC formulas above, the stated congruence classes verify the first\ntwo items, and positivity follows from positive n. The fourth item remains a\nseparate hypothesis. For arbitrary formal compositions, one cannot infer an\nordinary orbit from slope or integrality alone.\n\nThe variable R154/R155 interface also shows why a terminal formula is not an\nunconditional stopping-time statement: its guards are checked at the actual\ncontrol state, and the order of 00/10 groups affects the affine intercept.\nSimilarly, R159 explicitly uses different times on the two arms.\n\n## Growth-only code versus the full stopping-time problem\n\nA homogeneous run has an elementary valuation bound. For the H middle map\n(F_2(mu)=(9mu+5)/4),\n\n    F_2(μ)+1=(9/4)(μ+1),\n\nso every further H step consumes two powers of 2 from the finite integer\n(mu+1). For the G3 middle map\n(F_3(mu)=(9mu+5)/8),\n\n    F_3(μ)+5=(9/8)(μ+5),\n\nand a homogeneous G3 run consumes three powers of 2 from (mu+5). Hence\nneither homogeneous code can be infinite from a positive integer.\n\nA mixed 11/110 code has two different centers, (-1) and (-5). A switch\ncan reset which 2-adic valuation is relevant. The present local bounds do not\nprove descent below the initial birth after arbitrary guarded switches, and I\ndid not find an accepted theorem in the read archive that rules out every\nsuch positive mixed code. Even if such a theorem were proved, it would cover\nonly the growth-only subclass with the SC birth masks. Other Collatz parity\nwords, smaller-birth transfers, and possible cycles remain outside that\nsubclass. It therefore would not by itself be equivalent to the full\nstopping-time conjecture.\n\nConversely, Everett/Terras density-one descent results allow a fixed exceptional\nstart and do not rule out a single positive mixed code. “Almost every” cannot\nbe substituted for the pointwise quantifier needed here.\n\n## Primary finite-code and 2-adic sources\n\n**Bernstein–Lagarias, The 3x+1 Conjugacy Map (author PDF):**\nhttps://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/bernstein.pdf\n\nI read the abstract, Introduction (1.1)–(1.6), and the discussion of the\nsolenoidal map. The paper states that the 2-adic shortcut map is conjugate to\nthe binary 2-adic shift, that the conjugacy is solenoidal (compatible with\nreduction modulo every (2^k)), and gives the explicit parity-coordinate\nformula\n\n    Φ^{-1}(x) = sum_{i>=0} (T^i(x) mod 2) 2^i.\n\nThis establishes the 2-adic coding and finite-modulus compatibility. It does\nnot assert that an arbitrary infinite parity code is realized by a positive\ninteger, nor that density-one descent removes a prescribed exceptional\ninteger. The paper itself distinguishes the 2-adic conjugacy from the\npositive-integer Collatz conjecture and states the periodicity conjecture\nseparately.\n\n**Marcus Elia and Amanda Tucker, _Consecutive Integers and the Collatz\nConjecture_:**\nhttps://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141\n\nI read the Introduction, Definitions 1–2, Proposition 1, Theorem 2.1, and\nTheorem 4.1. The text defines ordinary C parity vectors and shortcut-T parity\nvectors, states the explicit 4 mod 8 / 5 mod 8 three-step coincidence, and\nrecords Terras's finite fact that length-k parity vectors are in bijection\nwith integer residue classes modulo (2^k). Its Definition 2 block-prefix\nidentity is an all-positive-input identity, while Definition 1 stems impose\nadditional proper-prefix restrictions. Theorem 4.1 gives infinitely many\ncounterexamples to Garner's proposed stem classification. These sources\nsupport finite-word realization and the distinction between stems, blocks,\nand actual coalescing trajectories; they do not classify the SC 11/110 birth\ncode or prove its global termination.\n\n**Terras, A stopping time problem on the positive integers:** official\nmetadata at\nhttps://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/acta-arithmetica/all/30/3/101028/a-stopping-time-problem-on-the-positive-integers\n\nThe IMPAN page confirms the 1976 paper, author, pages, DOI, and download\nlink. The linked PDF was not available in this pass (HTTP 403), so exact\nTerras theorem numbering is imported only through the explicitly read\nElia–Tucker statement, not through an independent full Terras proof audit.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nThe 11 and 110 middle transitions are genuine finite ordinary-orbit words\nunder their explicit residue guards, and their local mechanisms predate SC in\nseparate R150/R154/R155/R159 components. A finite parity vector determines a\nresidue class modulo a power of two, and the 2-adic conjugacy records this\ncoding, but neither fact supplies a positive integer with an arbitrary\ninfinite code or excludes one exceptional start. The unresolved issue is the\npointwise control of arbitrary guarded switches between the two growth modes;\nthat is a narrower stopping-time subclass, not a completed Collatz argument.\n\n## Independent audit of the BC arithmetic core\n\nI read MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md in full. The finite-word\ncount is valid in the stated arithmetic superset. If a word has r labels,\nwith H contributing two T bits and G3 contributing three, append the final\n11 suffix. The middle start μ=2n+1 then has a parity word W followed by 11\nof length B_r+2. The first bit μ=1 is fixed, so this specifies one n-class\nmodulo 2^(B_r+1).\n\nThe guard check is exact: an H position has bits 111 at its beginning and\ntherefore n=3 mod4; a G3 position has bits 11011 and therefore n=13 mod16.\nThe appended 11 ensures the final n_r is odd. Conversely, an iterated partial\nF-chain with odd final n_r supplies these same words. No beta-mask assumption\nis used in the superset Γ.\n\nThe elementary parity-class bijection can be proved by induction. Two lifts\nof a residue modulo 2^k share the first k shortcut parities; their kth images\ndiffer by 3^o, where o is the number of odd inputs in that prefix, hence by\nan odd number. Exactly one lift realizes each desired next parity. Thus the\nword count does not assume probabilistic independence.\n\nFor a word with h H labels and g G3 labels, the class modulus is\n2^(B_r+1)=2*4^h*8^g. Summing over all 2^r label words gives\n\n    (1/2)(1/4+1/8)^r + 2^r/X\n  = (1/2)(3/8)^r + 2^r/X\n\nas the count-per-X upper coefficient. Choosing\n2^r \u003c= sqrt(X) \u003c 2^(r+1), using (3/8)^r \u003c= 2^(-r), yields\nN_Γ(X) \u003c 2 sqrt(X). Tonelli then gives\n\n    sum_{n in Γ} 1/n = integral_1^infinity N_Γ(t)t^(-2) dt \u003c= 4.\n\nThis is a valid harmonic bound for the arithmetic set Γ of starts admitting\nan infinite F-chain. It does not prove Γ empty, and Γ is an upper superset of\nactual infinite-growth births because actual beta birth/output masks are not\nimposed.\n\nThe energy localization is also logically sound: once SC supplies a smaller\nbirth whenever a guarded growth step is unavailable, births split into\nfinite-growth-block births R_beta and never-stopping growth births I_beta,\nwith I_beta contained in Γ. Hence the latter contribute at most 4 to the\nbirth harmonic energy. If an independent argument forces divergence of total\nbirth energy, divergence must therefore occur in R_beta. This does not give\nthat finite-block divergence, does not supply a pointwise smaller transfer\nbelow the initial birth, and does not exclude a Collatz counterexample. It is\nstrictly a summability reduction for the accepted SC routing.\n\n## Bounded audit of BC11--BC13: combined injectivity and chain energy\n\nI read BC6 (BC11--BC13) of `MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md`\nand compared it with the relevant local routing material in\n`COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md`, `COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md`,\n`work/round150-g-global-criterion.md`, and `work/round150-k-fixed-110.md`.\nThe narrow archive search did not locate an earlier statement of the exact\ncombined H/G₃ image separation below; this is not a claim about the wider\nliterature.\n\nThe displayed arithmetic is correct. With H(n)=(9n+5)/4 on n=4t+3,\n\n    H(4t+3)=9t+8 ≡ 8 (mod 9),\n\nand with G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8 on n=16t+13,\n\n    G₃(16t+13)=18t+15 ≡ 6 (mod 9).\n\nEach branch is strictly increasing and injective in its own parameter, and\n8 mod 9 and 6 mod 9 are disjoint. Therefore their union is injective on the\nunion of the two displayed domains. Passing to the retained actual-birth\nedges can only delete edges, so it preserves global injectivity. Also\nH(n)-n=(5n+5)/4>0 and G₃(n)-n=(n+3)/8>0 on the positive domains.\n\nConsequently the retained growth relation is a partial directed graph with\nin-degree and out-degree at most one and strictly increasing edges. A\nbackward path is a strictly decreasing sequence of positive integers, hence\ncannot be infinite. Each nonempty component therefore has one source; it is\na finite chain if its forward path terminates and a one-sided infinite chain\notherwise. This is a statement about the retained partial growth graph, not\nabout the full Collatz graph or about the arithmetic superset Γ.\n\nThe harmonic estimate is also correct conditional on the SC routing used in\nBC5. If a finite component is\n`a=n_0\u003cn_1\u003c...\u003cn_L`, each edge has multiplier at least 9/8, so\n`n_j >= (9/8)^j a` and\n\n    Σ_{j=0}^L 1/n_j \u003c= (1/a)Σ_{j>=0}(8/9)^j = 9/a.\n\nFor a cutoff X, a component with origin a>X has no vertex ≤X because the\nchain is increasing. Thus summing disjoint finite components gives\n`E_A(X) \u003c= E_R(X) \u003c= 9 E_A(X)`, provided `R_beta` is the union of the\nfinite-chain vertices. That identification is exactly what BC5's accepted\nrouting supplies: a retained edge continues the unique growth procedure,\nwhile failure of the retained mask invokes the smaller-birth transfer; so a\nfinite retained component is precisely a stopped (R_beta) growth block,\nincluding its origin. Without that routing dichotomy, BC11 alone would not\njustify the first inequality.\n\nSince `A_beta` is a subset of births and BC10 gives the never-stopping\narithmetic superset contribution at most 4, the stated bounds\n\n    E_A(X) \u003c= E_beta(X) \u003c= 9 E_A(X)+4\n\nfollow under the same accepted SC/BC definitions. They establish equivalence\nof finiteness or divergence of the total birth harmonic sum with that of the\nfinite-component origins, up to the additive bounded Γ contribution. They do\nnot bound `E_A`, prove that every component is finite, or provide descent for\nthe terminal smaller-birth transfer. In particular, a finite chain may end at\na vertex still larger than its origin, and the origin graph has not been\nshown to have a decreasing rank.\n\nThe archival comparison is limited but relevant. `COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md`\nrecords injectivity within each individual large branch and explicitly leaves\npossible overlap between different branch images unresolved. R150/K150 records\nthe single repeated-110 affine branch, not the combined H/G₃ map. Thus BC11\nis a valid elementary strengthening for this two-branch retained graph, while\nits energy consequence remains conditional on the already stated SC routing\nand is not a full stopping-time result.\n\n## Bounded verdict\n\nBC11--BC13 pass the arithmetic audit with the above scope qualification. The\nmod-9 separation proves global injectivity of the two retained arithmetic\nbranches, and the 9-geometric chain bound is valid. The only non-arithmetic\ninput is the identification of stopped actual growth procedures with the\nfinite components; that is available from BC5's accepted local routing, not\nfrom injectivity alone. No exact combined-injectivity theorem was found in the\nnarrow archival passages read, and no priority claim is made. The result\nreduces a harmonic-energy question to finite-chain origins but leaves that\norigin mass and the full Collatz problem open.\n","FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_LIFT_SOURCES.md":"# FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_LIFT_SOURCES — variable structural lifts\n\nДата: 18 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Ограниченный ручной\nисточниково-аналитический аудит. Прочитаны `PROJECT_STATE.md`,\n`FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md` и `FOLLOWUP_0483_INCIDENCE_SOURCES.md`.\nВнешним первичным источником проверен полный доступный HTML-текст:\n[Elia--Tucker, *Consecutive Integers and the Collatz Conjecture*,\narXiv:1511.09141](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141), включая введение,\n§§2--5, Definitions 1--2, Proposition 1 и Theorem 4.1. PDF издательской\nверсии также указан здесь: [Integers 15 (2015), A54](https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/p54/p54.pdf).\nGarner's исходная статья и полный текст Terras в этом проходе не\nперечитывались; их утверждения используются только в том виде, в каком\nони явно пересказаны Elia--Tucker. Программ, CAS, переборов, Lean и\nматематических вычислительных экспериментов не было.\n\n## 1. Что именно даёт первичный текст\n\nElia--Tucker определяют shortcut map\n\\[\n T(x)=x/2\\quad(x\\text{ even}),\\qquad\n T(x)=(3x+1)/2\\quad(x\\text{ odd}).\n\\]\nВ Definition 1 (строки 110--113 HTML) пара parity sequences называется\ncorresponding stems, если их формальные траекторные значения на \\(x\\) и\n\\(x+1\\) совпадают в конце, а на каждом proper equal-length prefix не\nсовпадают и не отличаются на один. В Definition 2 (строки 114--116)\nblock prefix — это пара слов \\(b,b'\\) одной длины, для которой\n\\[\n       T_b(x)+1=T_{b'}(x+1)\n\\]\nдля всех положительных \\(x\\). Здесь \\(T_b\\) — формальная композиция\nветвей, заданных словом; реальные источники должны дополнительно лежать\nв соответствующих parity cylinders. Таким образом, Definition 2 уже\nохватывает любые равные по длине и slope adjacency-preserving words, а\nне только повторение двухшаговых слов.\n\nTheorem 2.1 (HTML, строки 88--101) доказывает для обычной карты \\(C\\),\nчто \\(8m+4\\) и \\(8m+5\\) совпадают после трёх шагов, с endpoint\n\\(6m+4\\). Это нельзя без оговорки переименовывать в shortcut endpoint.\nВ §3 Proposition 1 переводит предполагаемые Garner stems между \\(C\\) и\n\\(T\\); в §4 Theorem 4.1 утверждает бесконечное число контрпримеров к\nconjectural классификации Garner. Доказательство использует найденную\nпару 3067, 3068 и Terras-бijection для повторения фиксированного\nparity-prefix по классам \\(2^{19}m+3067\\). Это не классификация всех\nblock prefixes и не theorem о плотности фиксированных целей.\n\nСущественно, сам §5 подчёркивает, что у первых контрпримеров появляются\nновые stems и что авторы не нашли простого расширения Garner-класса\n(HTML, строки 168--172). Эти stems являются объектами coalescence из\nDefinition 1 и не должны отождествляться с block prefixes из Definition\n2. Поэтому этот пассаж сам по себе не доказывает существование новых\nDefinition-2 adjacency-preserving words и не даёт их классификации.\n\n## 2. Явный неэлементарный block prefix\n\nНиже приведён ручной exact check, а не приписываемый Elia--Tucker\nновый результат. Для \\(m\\ge0\\) shortcut trajectories имеют вид\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n32m+3&\\xrightarrow{O}48m+5\\xrightarrow{O}72m+8\n \\xrightarrow{E}36m+4\\xrightarrow{E}18m+2\n \\xrightarrow{E}9m+1,\\\\\n32m+4&\\xrightarrow{E}16m+2\\xrightarrow{E}8m+1\n \\xrightarrow{O}12m+2\\xrightarrow{E}6m+1\n \\xrightarrow{O}9m+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nСледовательно, слова\n\\[\n       w=OOEEE,\\qquad w'=EEOEO\n\\]\nимеют одинаковые length \\(K=5\\) и odd count \\(P=2\\), и\n\\[\n T_w(32m+3)=9m+1,\\qquad T_{w'}(32m+4)=9m+2.\n\\]\nФормально\n\\[\n       T_w(x)=\\frac{9x+5}{32},\\qquad\n       T_{w'}(x)=\\frac{9x+28}{32},\n\\]\nпоэтому \\(T_w(x)+1=T_{w'}(x+1)\\) тождественно как affine branch\nidentity. Этот pair является Definition-2 block prefix и не является\nповторением elementary двухшагового pair \\((OE,EO)\\): длина 5 не кратна\n2, а сами слова имеют другой pattern. Мировая новизна этого ручного\nпримера не заявляется.\n\nДля сравнения, elementary pair действительно даёт\n\\[\n T^2(4m+1)=3m+1,\\qquad T^2(4m+2)=3m+2,\n\\]\nс формальными словами \\((OE,EO)\\). Новый pair показывает только наличие\nещё одного finite structural lift; он не контролирует switching между\nразными словами или unbounded periods.\n\n## 3. Точный source/parity-cylinder интерфейс\n\nДля любого genuine parity word \\(w\\) длины \\(K\\), с \\(P\\) odd\npositions, запишем его affine branch как\n\\[\n       T_w(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C_w}{2^K}.\n\\]\nЕсли target равен \\(n\\), обратная формула имеет вид\n\\[\n       x=\\frac{2^K n-C_w}{3^P}.\n\\]\nОтсюда есть две разные congruence-информации:\n\n1. Целость inverse требует\n   \\(2^K n\\equiv C_w\\pmod{3^P}\\), то есть\n   \\(n\\equiv 2^{-K}C_w\\pmod{3^P}\\).\n2. Из forward equation по модулю \\(2^K\\) следует\n   \\[\n       x\\equiv -C_w(3^P)^{-1}\\pmod{2^K}.\n   \\]\n   Для genuine word это именно его unique parity cylinder; поэтому\n   обратный integer source, попавший в эту residue class, реализует все\n   prescribed word bits. Это утверждение опирается на genuine word и\n   parity coding, а не на произвольный формальный integer \\(C_w\\).\n\nДля 5-step pair \\(C_w=5\\), \\(C_{w'}=28\\). Так как\n\\(9^{-1}=25\\pmod{32}\\), source residues равны\n\\[\n -5\\cdot25\\equiv3\\pmod{32},\\qquad\n -28\\cdot25\\equiv4\\pmod{32}.\n\\]\nTarget condition for the left branch is \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\); right\nbranch then targets \\(n+1\\). The ordered inverse pair is therefore\n\\[\n       M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\\qquad M(n)+1,\n       \\qquad n\\equiv1\\pmod9.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(M(n)>0\\), and the residue calculation gives exact\nsource words, so positivity and parity are not being inferred from a\nformal branch alone.\n\n## 4. What the example does and does not establish\n\nThe map \\(M\\) is one fixed affine inverse rule, with\n\\[\n       D=2^5-3^2=23,\\qquad E=Dn-C_w=23n-5.\n\\]\nThe fixed-rule calculation already recorded in `FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md`\ngives\n\\[\n       M^j(n)\\in\\mathbb Z\n       \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 9^j\\mid(23n-5).\n\\]\nThus a nonzero \\(E\\) permits only finitely many successive repetitions,\nat most \\(\\lfloor v_3(E)/2\\rfloor\\); the exceptional equation \\(E=0\\)\nhas no integer solution. For example, \\(M(1)=3\\), but \\(M(3)=91/9\\),\nso the first seed does not generate an unbounded integer lift. This is\nexactly the fixed-periodic obstruction already proved in FL, not a result\nabout arbitrary variable switching.\n\nThe primary source therefore supports the following bounded verdict:\n\n* Definitions 1--2 provide a broad formal language for structural\n  adjacency-preserving word pairs.\n* Elia--Tucker give explicit non-Garner patterns and infinitely many\n  repeated parity-prefix instances, but no classification of all\n  Definition-2 words and no fixed-target positive-density theorem.\n\nSeparately, the manual 5-step \\((OOEEE,EEOEO)\\) calculation above is a\nvalid additional finite block prefix, with exact 2-adic source cylinders\nand a 3-adic target guard. Its repeated inverse has finite 3-adic budget.\nThe example alone does not close the variable-lift problem. It disproves\nthe assertion that all such lifts reduce to repeated \\((OE,EO)\\), but by\nitself proves no useful variable-switching or density theorem. Root\nseparated this manual conclusion from the primary-source list.\n\n## 5. Independent audit: all gap-1 structural rules with at most two odd steps\n\nThis is a manual check of the bounded candidate supplied by root, not a\nliterature or priority claim. Let a structural ordered lift use words of\nlength \\(K\\), with \\(P\\) odd positions, and left forward intercept\n\\(C_L\\). The right word must have\n\\[\n       C_R=C_L+2^K-3^P. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nFor \\(P=0\\), both intercepts are zero. Equation (5.1) then forces\n\\(K=0\\), giving only the identity.\n\nFor \\(P=1\\), if the unique odd position is \\(i\\), with\n\\(0\\le i\u003cK\\), then \\(C=2^i\\). For \\(K=1\\) the required difference is\n\\(-1\\) and there is no second one-odd intercept. For \\(K\\ge3\\), the\ndifference of two powers of two is odd only if one is 1, but\n\\(2^K-3+1=2^K-2\\) is not a power of two. At \\(K=2\\), the unique pair is\n\\(C_L=1,C_R=2\\), namely the elementary \\(L\\) rule with words \\((OE,EO)\\).\n\nFor \\(P=2\\), write the two odd positions as \\(i\u003cj\u003cK\\). Then\n\\[\n       C=3\\,2^i+2^j. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nAt \\(K=2\\), the only intercept is 5 and the required right intercept is\n0, so there is no pair. At \\(K=3\\), the intercepts are \\(5,7,10\\), and\nthe required difference is \\(-1\\); again no pair exists.\n\nAssume now \\(K\\ge4\\), so the required difference in (5.1) is\n\\(2^K-9>0\\). If \\(C_L\\) is even, then \\(i\\ge1\\), while \\(C_R\\) is\nodd and hence has \\(i_R=0\\). The largest possible odd right intercept is\n\\(3+2^{K-1}\\), and the smallest possible even left intercept is 10.\nConsequently\n\\[\n C_R-C_L\\le 2^{K-1}-7\u003c2^K-9,\n\\]\nwhich is impossible. Thus \\(C_L\\) is odd and \\(i=0\\), so\n\\[\n       C_L=3+2^j,\\qquad C_R=2^K-6+2^j. \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nIf \\(j=1\\), then \\(C_R=2^K-4\\) has 2-adic valuation 2. A representation\nof this number in the form (5.2) must therefore have left odd position\n2, giving\n\\[\n       12+2^\\ell=2^K-4.\n\\]\nThe only solution with \\(2\u003c\\ell\u003cK\\) is \\(K=5,\\ell=4\\). This is the\nmanual \\(M\\) rule, with \\(C_L=5,C_R=28\\).\n\nIf \\(j>1\\), then (5.3) has 2-adic valuation 1. Hence the right\nrepresentation has left odd position 1, so\n\\[\n       6+2^\\ell=2^K-6+2^j,\\qquad\n       2^\\ell-2^j=2^K-12. \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell>j\\), the right side has valuation 2, forcing \\(j=2\\); then\n\\(2^\\ell=2^K-8\\), whose only solution with \\(\\ell>2\\) is\n\\(K=4,\\ell=3\\). This is \\(L^2\\), with \\(C_L=7,C_R=14\\). The subcase\n\\(\\ell\u003cj\\) is immediately impossible in (5.4), since its left side is\nnegative while \\(2^K-12>0\\) for \\(K\\ge4\\).\n\nTherefore the bounded classification is exactly\n\\[\n       \\text{identity},\\quad L\\ (K,P)=(2,1),\\quad\n       L^2\\ (K,P)=(4,2),\\quad M\\ (K,P)=(5,2).\n\\]\nThis checks only fixed equal-slope structural rules with \\(P\\le2\\). It\ndoes not classify \\(P\\ge3\\), unequal lengths, asynchronous words, or\narbitrary switching among block prefixes.\n\n## URLs and access limits\n\n- Primary full HTML: https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141\n- arXiv record: https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.09141\n- Publisher PDF: https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/p54/p54.pdf\n- Elia--Tucker reference to Garner is [1] in the paper; Garner's full\n  article was not reread here.\n- Elia--Tucker reference to Terras is [4]; only the explicit bijection\n  statement as reproduced in their Proposition 1/Theorem 4.1 was used.\n\nNo conclusion about full Collatz, arbitrary switching, positive-density\npaired targets, or world-literature priority follows from this bounded\nsource check.\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_CRITICAL_COVERAGE_INTERFACE.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — the critical W1 sum and coverage of neighboring pairs\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual analysis only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents. This note isolates the\nlogical interface between the critical inverse weight and the actual\nfixed-target pair set.\n\n## 1. Exact structural subset represented by W1\n\nFor a terminal left value n, let M_R(n) be the positive endpoints m for\nwhich the deterministic first structural ordered-return map satisfies\nR^d(m)=n for some d>=0. Let A_m be the inverse slope of the least such\npath. If n is periodic, let\n\n    c_1(n)=1/(1-A_c^(-1));\n\notherwise put c_1(n)=1. The deterministic multiplicity calculation gives\n\n    W_1(n)=c_1(n) sum_(m in M_R(n)) A_m^(-1).          (CI1)\n\nFor each nonempty endpoint path, with inverse affine form m=A_m n-B_m and\nB_m>0,\n\n    A_m^(-1)=n/(m+B_m)\u003cn/m.                           (CI2)\n\nThe empty endpoint m=n has A_m=1 and B_m=0, so it contributes equality\ninstead of the strict inequality in (CI2).\n\nThe set M_R(n) is a structural subset of the neighboring fixed-target\npredecessor set. In the notation\n\n    P(a)={x>0: some shortcut iterate of x reaches a},\n    Q(a,c)=P(a) intersect {x: x+1 belongs to P(c)},\n\none has, for the ordered target pair (a,c)=(n,n+1),\n\n    M_R(n) is contained in Q(n,n+1).\n\nThe inclusion is strict already at n=1: the source pair (6,7) reaches\n(1,2) first at time 10, with odd counts 4 and 5. Thereafter the pair\nalternates between (1,2) and (2,1); equal counts occur only with the latter\nsigned orientation, so there is no later ordered (+1) structural return.\nThus 6 belongs to Q(1,2) but not to M_R(1). More generally Q allows unequal\nodd counts, unequal times, non-structural adjacency, and separate first\nhits. Therefore W1 is not a weight for all of Q. No implication between\nW1 and density of the full Q has been established for the actual Collatz\nmap; the abstract countermodels below only show why the generic affine\nproperties are insufficient.\n\n## 2. What finite or infinite W1 implies about source counts\n\nWrite N_R(X)=#(M_R(n) intersect [1,X]). The exact truncated identity is\n\n    W_1(n;X)=c_1(n) sum_(m in M_R(n), m\u003c=X) n/(m+B_m).    (CI3)\n\nOnly the upper comparison\n\n    W_1(n;X) \u003c= c_1(n)n sum_(m\u003c=X) 1/m\n\nholds without an intercept estimate. There is no lower comparison with\n1/m, because B_m can be much larger than m in the generic affine data.\nConsequently:\n\nThe following implications are not established from these inequalities:\nfinite W1 implying N_R(X)=o(X), infinite W1 implying positive upper density\nof M_R(n), or positive density of full Q implying positive density of M_R.\nThe last two are logical gaps for the actual Collatz map, not claims of\ncounterexamples within its genuine parity-word system.\n\nAn exact conditional bridge is available if one proves an arithmetic\nintercept bound\n\n    B_m \u003c= Lambda (m+1) for every m in M_R(n).             (CI4)\n\nThen CI3 gives a lower harmonic comparison\n\n    A_m^(-1) >= n/((1+Lambda)m+Lambda),\n\nso finite W1 forces sum_{m in M_R(n)}1/m\u003cinfinity. In particular M_R(n)\nhas upper natural density zero, since every set of positive upper density\nhas divergent harmonic sum. Conversely, under CI4, positive upper density\nof M_R(n) forces W1(n)=infinity. This is a necessary obstruction, not a\npositive-density theorem.\n\nThere is one useful sufficient lower-growth interface that is compatible with\nthe harmonic upper bound. Let D_R^*=limsup_X N_R(X)/X. Abel summation gives\n\n    limsup_X (sum_(m in M_R(n), m\u003c=X) 1/m)/log X \u003c= D_R^*.\n\nSince CI3 gives W_1(n;X)\u003c=c_1(n)n times this harmonic sum, a proved estimate\n\n    limsup_X W_1(n;X)/log X >= eta>0\n\nwould force D_R^*>=eta/(c_1(n)n), and hence positive upper density of M_R\nand of Q(n,n+1). This requires a lower critical growth statement, not\nfiniteness of W1. Mere convergence or divergence of W1 is too weak.\n\n## 3. Two abstract interface countermodels\n\nThe following examples are deliberately outside Collatz parity and residue\ncylinders. They show that no implication can be obtained from determinism,\npositive affine intercepts, and integer endpoints alone.\n\nTake n=1, let R(m)=1 on a chosen set M of integers m>=2, and leave R\nundefined elsewhere and at 1. Give the edge m -> 1 the inverse affine\nbranch F_m(x)=A_m x-B_m on the one-point domain {1}, with\nB_m=A_m-m>0. There are no cycles and only one edge to each endpoint.\n\nFor M={2,3,4,...}, choose A_m=(m+1)^2. Then B_m>0,\nM has density one, but\n\n    W_1(1)=1+sum_(m>=2) (m+1)^(-2)\u003cinfinity.\n\nFor the zero-density set m_j=ceil(j log(j+1)) with j>=2, choose A_{m_j}=m_j+1\nand B_{m_j}=1. The harmonic series over m_j diverges by comparison with\nsum 1/(j log(j+1)), while M has density zero. Hence W1 is infinite\nwithout positive density.\n\nThese are logical countermodels only; they do not assert that their\nbranches are genuine shortcut words. They identify exactly what a\nCollatz-specific arithmetic estimate must add before W1 can interface with\nQ.\n\n## 4. Supersolution formulation at the critical exponent\n\nLet the positive structural inverse branches at n be F_i(n)=A_i n-B_i,\nwith their terminal guards understood. Define the positive transfer operator\n\n    (T_1 V)(n)=sum_i A_i^(-1) V(F_i(n)),\n\nwhere only positive genuine branches are included. A finite supersolution\n\n    V(n) >= 1 + (T_1 V)(n),       V(n)>=1,                (CI5)\n\nwould imply by iteration W1(n)\u003c=V(n). This is the exact useful\nsupersolution criterion and does not assume termination.\n\nThe initial candidate size functions V(n)=C(1+log(n+1)) and V(n)=C n^theta\nare ruled out by the subsequent theorem in section6, even after positive\nperiodic residue multipliers are included. The\ndyadic Kraft inequality controls source-cylinder masses 2^(-K_i), whereas\nCI5 contains 3^(P_i)/2^(K_i) and the values at the affine sources. Haar\naveraging therefore does not establish CI5. No valid supersolution has\nbeen obtained in this pass.\n\n## 5. Consequence for the full Collatz objective\n\nEven a future proof of W1(n)\u003cinfinity for every fixed n would only give a\nweighted statement for the structural subset M_R(n), and perhaps zero\ndensity if an additional intercept comparison such as CI4 were proved. It\nwould not prove positive density of Q(a,c), because Q contains\nnon-structural and unequal-odd-count pairs. It would also not imply\nuniversal forward descent or exclude positive cycles.\n\nConversely, a positive-density theorem for Q(a,c) would need a direct\njoint lower count for the two histories. Individual predecessor density,\nthe structural W1 upper estimate, and the product of marginal laws do not\nsupply that joint lower bound.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe exact factorization (CI1) is useful for pointwise weighted control, but\nno implication from critical W1 finiteness or divergence to positive density\nof the full neighboring-pair set is established. A useful bridge requires\nnew arithmetic control of intercepts and endpoint distribution, plus a\nseparate coverage theorem from structural equal-odd-count returns to actual\nQ(a,c). Neither bridge is established here.\n\n## 6. No-go for regular supersolutions\n\nThere is a sharp obstruction to proving the critical estimate with a regular\npower-type supersolution. For q>0 let L_q be the ordered irreducible-block\noperator\n\n    (L_q V)(n)=sum_i A_i^(-q) V(F_i(n)),\n\non positive guarded branches. Assume V is finite, positive, and\nL_q V\u003c=V at every positive integer. Suppose that for some period Q, exponent\ns, positive constants c_0,...,c_(Q-1), and positive slowly varying l,\n\n    V(n) = c_(n mod Q) n^s l(n) (1+o(1)),\n\nwith l(an+b)/l(n)->1 for every fixed a>0,b and with this convergence uniform\nacross the finitely many residue classes. This includes logarithmic powers.\n\nFactor the ordered contraction H=S^2 into r irreducible blocks and write its\nslope as alpha=4194304/4782969\u003c1. For each fixed t, H^t has a nonempty guard\nmodulo 3^(14t), and sufficiently large positive representatives have all\nprefixes positive. Thus along this one admissible path,\n\n    (L_q^(rt)V)(n) >= alpha^(-qt) V(H^t(n)).\n\nSince H^t(n)=alpha^t n-beta_t for a fixed beta_t, take n to infinity in its\nfixed-t guard. With c_min=min c_j and c_max=max c_j, regular variation gives\n\n    1 >= (c_min/c_max+o(1)) alpha^((s-q)t).\n\nLetting t grow shows necessarily s>=q. This step uses only a finite path for\neach fixed t, so it does not assert one positive seed supports all H^t.\n\nNow use the two genuine irreducible rules\n\n    L(n)=(4n-1)/3,       n=1 mod 3,\n    M(n)=(32n-5)/9,      n=1 mod 9.\n\n(The second rule is sometimes given another letter in the P\u003c=3 notation.)\nBoth are irreducible in the full structural alphabet: for M the proper\nprefix odd-count pairs are (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), (2,1), and equality first\noccurs at its terminal gap-1 event.\nFor n_j=1+9Q j, both guards hold; moreover L(n_j)=1+12Q j has residue\n1 mod Q, while M(n_j)=3+32Q j has residue 3 mod Q. Dividing the two branch\ncontributions by V(n_j) and using the assumed asymptotic gives\n\n    liminf_j (L_q V)(n_j)/V(n_j)\n      >= (4/3)^(s-q)\n         + (c_(3 mod Q)/c_(1 mod Q)) (32/9)^(s-q).\n\nWhen s>=q the right side is strictly greater than one, contradicting\nL_q V\u003c=V. Therefore no finite positive supersolution for L_q can have this\nperiodic-multiplier power-times-slowly-varying asymptotic, for any q>0.\n\nThis includes q>1, where the already-proved W_q is finite and superharmonic:\nW_q=1+L_q W_q. Hence W_q must have more arithmetic irregularity than the\nexcluded asymptotic class. The no-go result does not rule out supersolutions\nwith residue precision increasing without bound, nonperiodic multipliers, or\nfully irregular behavior, and it does not decide the actual critical W_1.\n\n## 7. Final audit of CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER\n\nCX2 is correct. With H(X)=sum_{m in M_R(n),m\u003c=X}1/m and\nD=limsup N_R(X)/X, Abel summation gives\nH(X)=N_R(X)/X+integral_1^X N_R(t)/t^2 dt and limsup H(X)/log X\u003c=D.\nThe pointwise upper bound W_1(n;X)\u003c=c_1(n)n H(X) therefore has the direction\neta:=limsup W_1(n;X)/log X \u003c= c_1(n)nD. Thus eta>0, if proved, gives the\npositive upper-density lower bound D>=eta/(c_1(n)n). A linear lower bound in X\nwould contradict the already established O(log X) envelope and is not used.\n\nCX3 is also correct. The explicit source 6 reaches (1,2) at time 10 with\nodd counts 4 and 5. Thereafter the pair alternates; the equal-count returns\nhave the opposite signed orientation, so no later ordered (+1) structural\nreturn occurs. Hence 6 belongs to Q(1,2) but not to M_R(1). The restriction\nthat a target divisible by 3 has only doubling predecessors is a separate\nvalid unit-modulo-3 observation, not a pair-density theorem.\n\nCX4 states the right supersolution hierarchy: V>=1+L_qV is sufficient, while\nL_qV\u003c=V is the weaker condition used for the obstruction. Nonnegative\niteration needs no unjustified interchange. CX5 is manually consistent: each\nfixed t uses one nonempty guard progression for H^t and large positive\nrepresentatives; first taking n to infinity and only then t gives s>=q.\nFor n=1+9d j, the irreducible branches L and M have residues 1 and 3\nmodulo d, and their two positive contributions force liminf L_qV/V>1 when\ns>=q. The conclusion covers all q>0 in the stated periodic-multiplier,\npower-times-affine-slow-scale class only. It does not cover irregular\npotentials, unbounded residue precision, or the actual q=1 finiteness\nquestion.\n\nThe source conclusion that CX5 gives one useful weighted version refers to\nthe equation labelled (CX5), namely the eta/logarithmic-growth premise, not\nto a section number. No mathematical claim depends on that editorial label.\n\n## 8. One 3-adic valuation factor does not repair the no-go\n\nA bounded refinement of the excluded potential class also fails. Suppose for\nn>1 that\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod Q)+o(1)) n^s ell(n) 3^(gamma v_3(n-1)),\n\nwith the same positive periodic coefficients and affine slow-variation\ncondition as in §6, and gamma real.\n\nFirst let gamma>0. Use the genuine irreducible branch\nM(n)=(32n-5)/9. Fix t>=1 and write m=1+3^t u with 3 not dividing u. Choose\nu in an arithmetic progression modulo 32 so that\nn=(9m+5)/32 is an integer; infinitely many such u remain after excluding\nmultiples of 3, and n tends to infinity. The M guard is then n=1 mod 9.\nMoreover\n\n    v_3(m-1)=t,\n    n-1=9(m-3)/32,\n    v_3(n-1)=2.\n\nFor fixed t, the M contribution divided by V(n) has lower limit at least\n\n    (c_min/c_max) (32/9)^(s-q) 3^(gamma(t-2)).\n\nLetting t grow contradicts L_qV\u003c=V for every s, because gamma>0.\n\nNow let gamma\u003c0. The H^k guard can be strengthened by two further powers of\n3 so that both the terminal n and H^k(n) are congruent to the fixed point\nz_*=-4310/139=7 mod 9. Hence both valuations v_3(n-1) and\nv_3(H^k(n)-1) equal 1. The H^k lower-path argument from §6 then still forces\ns>=q; the valuation factors cancel. Finally take n=1+9Qj in the L branch.\nBoth n and L(n)=1+12Qj have residue 1 modulo Q, while\n\n    v_3(L(n)-1)=v_3(n-1)-1.\n\nThe L contribution divided by V(n) therefore has limiting lower bound\n\n    (4/3)^(s-q) 3^(-gamma) > 1,\n\nbecause s>=q and gamma\u003c0, again contradicting L_qV\u003c=V. The case gamma=0\nis §6. Thus one fixed valuation weight centered at n-1 cannot repair the\nno-go for any real gamma. This does not exclude several valuation centers,\nunbounded residue precision, or fully irregular potentials, and it does not\nsettle the genuine critical W_1.\n\n## 9. Audit: finitely many 3-adic valuation centres do not repair the no-go\n\nThe same argument extends to a finite set E of distinct centres in Z_3. Assume\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod Q)+o(1)) n^s ell(n)\n         product_(a in E) 3^(gamma_a v_3(n-a))\n\nfor large positive integers, with all c positive, the affine slow-variation\ncondition, and finite values overriding the asymptotic at any exceptional\nfinite integer centres. Let gamma_b=0 when b is not in E.\n\nFirst suppose gamma_a>0 for some a. For either inverse branch L or M, let\nG_L(a)=(3a+1)/4 or G_M(a)=(9a+5)/32, respectively. These are in Z_3 and\nsatisfy the corresponding terminal guards. Choose positive integers n tending\nto infinity in a fixed sufficiently high 3-adic neighbourhood of\nb=G_F(a), with v_3(n-b)=R. Then v_3(F(n)-a)=R-P, where P=1 for L and\nP=2 for M. Taking n to infinity first, all other centre valuations are\nfixed after choosing R beyond their pairwise separations. The F contribution\nrelative to V(n) therefore contains the factor\n\n    3^((gamma_a-gamma_b)R+O(1)).\n\nThe superharmonic inequality forces gamma_b>=gamma_a; otherwise R tending\nto infinity makes the ratio diverge. Hence the finite set of positive\ncentres E_+ is closed under both G_L and G_M. Since G_L is injective and\nG_L^j(a)=1+(3/4)^j(a-1), every finite G_L-orbit consists only of a=1.\nThus E_+ is contained in {1}. But G_M(1)=7/16 is different from 1, and\nclosure under G_M is impossible. Consequently every finite centre has\n\n    gamma_a\u003c=0.                                      (CV1)\n\nNow use the ordered contraction H=S^2. Choose n in a sufficiently high\n3-adic neighbourhood of z_*=-4310/139, with v_3(n-z_*)=R>14k and R beyond\nall centre separations, and on the H^k guard. Then\nv_3(H^k(n)-z_*)=R-14k, while all other centre valuations are unchanged.\nBecause every gamma_a is nonpositive, the valuation factor in the H^k\ncontribution is at least 1 (it equals 3^(-14k gamma_z) if z_* is a centre,\nand 1 if it is absent). The same order-of-limits argument as in §6 forces\ns>=q.\n\nFinally choose n=1+9Qj tending to infinity with v_3(n-1)=R fixed and R\nlarger than all separations from the centre 1. The L branch has the same\nresidue modulo Q and satisfies v_3(L(n)-1)=R-1; all other centre valuations\nare unchanged. Its ratio is therefore\n\n    (4/3)^(s-q) 3^(-gamma_1) >= 1.\n\nThe M branch is simultaneously admissible and contributes a strictly\npositive finite amount: M(n)=3+32Qj, with its valuations at every finite\ncentre fixed for this chosen R (the centre 3, if present, has valuation\nR-2 at the output). Since the two branch terms are nonnegative, their sum\nis strictly greater than 1, contradicting superharmonicity. Thus no finite\nfamily of valuation factors, with common real power, positive periodic\nmultipliers, and the stated affine slow scale, repairs the obstruction.\nThis still leaves potentials with different powers, infinitely many centres,\nunbounded residue precision, or fully irregular arithmetic dependence.\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_FINITE_LIFT_OUTPUT_BOUNDS.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — finite-alphabet output bounds for variable structural lifts\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual analysis only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents. The object here is the set\nof distinct source/output coordinates generated from one fixed terminal seed\nby arbitrary switching among a finite alphabet of structural ordered inverse\nlifts. This is not a count of terminal input seeds.\n\n## 1. Setup and the exact weighted output tree\n\nLet the finite alphabet be \\(\\mathcal A\\), with inverse maps\n\\[\n F_i(x)=\\alpha_i x-\\beta_i,\\qquad\n \\alpha_i=\\frac{2^{K_i}}{3^{P_i}},\\quad\n \\beta_i=\\frac{C_i}{3^{P_i}}.\n\\tag{FO1}\n\\]\nA word \\(w=i_1\\cdots i_s\\) is admissible from \\(n_0\\) when every successive\n\\(F_{i_r}\\) is defined at the current positive integer and all intermediate\nsources remain positive. Let \\(x_w=F_{i_s}\\circ\\cdots\\circ F_{i_1}(n_0)\\),\nand put\n\\[\n \\alpha_w=\\prod_{r=1}^s\\alpha_{i_r}.\n\\]\nFirst-hit requirements may be imposed by deleting words from this tree; they\nnever create new output coordinates.\n\nAssume there is a real \\(B\u003cn_0\\) such that, for every admissible \\(i\\) and\nevery \\(x>B\\),\n\\[\n F_i(x)-B\\ge\\alpha_i(x-B),\\qquad \\alpha_i>1.\n\\tag{FO2}\n\\]\nFor the displayed affine maps, a sufficient choice is\n\\[\n B\\ge\\max_{i\\in\\mathcal A}\\frac{\\beta_i}{\\alpha_i-1}.\n\\tag{FO3}\n\\]\nThe condition is a source-growth hypothesis. It is separate from the\n\\(3\\)-adic congruences that decide which branches are admissible.\n\nBy induction on word length, (FO2) gives\n\\[\n x_w-B\\ge(n_0-B)\\alpha_w.\n\\tag{FO4}\n\\]\nFor \\(X>B\\), every admissible word of length \\(s\\) with \\(x_w\\le X\\) obeys\n\\[\n 1\\le\\frac{X-B}{n_0-B}\\,\\alpha_w^{-1}.\n\\tag{FO5}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal W_s(n_0)\\) be the admissible words of length \\(s\\), and define\n\\[\n \\sigma_s(n_0)=\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal W_s(n_0)}\\alpha_w^{-1}.\n\\tag{FO6}\n\\]\nSince distinct words can collide at one source, word counting is an upper\nbound. Equations (FO5)--(FO6) therefore imply\n\\[\n \\#\\{x_w\\le X:|w|=s\\}\n \\le\\frac{X-B}{n_0-B}\\,\\sigma_s(n_0).\n\\tag{FO7}\n\\]\n\n### Weighted-tree criterion\n\nIf\n\\[\n \\sum_{s\\ge0}\\sigma_s(n_0)\u003c\\infty,\n\\tag{FO8}\n\\]\nthen the set \\(A(n_0)=\\{x_w:w\\text{ admissible}\\}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n \\#(A(n_0)\\cap[1,X])=o(X).\n\\tag{FO9}\n\\]\nIndeed, for any fixed \\(S\\), the words of lengths \\(\u003cS\\) contribute only\nfinitely many coordinates, while (FO7) bounds the remaining contribution\nafter division by \\(X\\) by\n\\[\n \\frac{1}{n_0-B}\\sum_{s\\ge S}\\sigma_s(n_0).\n\\]\nLet \\(X\\to\\infty\\) and then \\(S\\to\\infty\\). First-hit filtering preserves\n(FO9). This is a precise sufficient criterion under the stated growth\nhypothesis: \\(\\sigma_s(n_0)\\) is the exact weighted branching quantity\nwhose tail must be controlled. It makes the missing input explicit rather\nthan replacing output counting by a terminal-seed density estimate.\n\n## 2. A checkable subcritical slope criterion\n\nA uniform sufficient condition follows by forgetting congruence pruning:\n\\[\n \\rho:=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal A}\\alpha_i^{-1}\u003c1.\n\\tag{FO10}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n \\sigma_s(n_0)\\le\n \\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal A^s}\\alpha_w^{-1}\n =\\left(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal A}\\alpha_i^{-1}\\right)^s\n =\\rho^s,\n\\tag{FO11}\n\\]\nso (FO8) holds. This proves \\(o(X)\\) output from every fixed \\(n_0>B\\)\nfor which the finite alphabet satisfies (FO2), without any CRT product\nclaim and without counting input seeds.\n\nThe criterion is only sufficient. It requires every rule to expand relative\nto one common cone \\(x>B\\). If some \\(\\alpha_i\\le1\\), or if no such\ninvariant cone lies above the chosen seed, (FO4) is unavailable and no\noutput-density conclusion follows from this argument.\n\n## 3. Application to the accepted \\(L,M\\) alphabet\n\nFor\n\\[\n L(n)=\\frac{4n-1}{3},\\qquad M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\n\\]\nthe slopes and reciprocal sum are\n\\[\n \\alpha_L=\\frac43,\\qquad \\alpha_M=\\frac{32}{9},\\qquad\n \\alpha_L^{-1}+\\alpha_M^{-1}\n =\\frac34+\\frac9{32}=\\frac{33}{32}>1.\n\\tag{FO12}\n\\]\nThus the subcritical slope criterion does not prove the accepted\n\\(O(\\log X)\\) bound for \\(L,M\\). The latter came from the stronger\nmodulo-\\(27\\) fact that there is at most one continuing chain and only\nbounded side branches. This illustrates why reciprocal slope mass alone is\nnot a necessary criterion: it can fail even when congruence pruning proves\n\\(o(X)\\).\n\nFor \\(L,M\\), \\(B=1\\) satisfies (FO2) for all \\(n>1\\):\n\\[\n L(n)-1=\\frac43(n-1),\\qquad\n M(n)-1=\\frac{32}{9}(n-1)+2.\n\\]\nThe exceptional seed \\(n_0=1\\) is handled separately, with\n\\(A(1)=\\{1,3\\}\\).\n\n## 4. The first \\(P=3\\) branch defeats the old modulo-\\(27\\) pruning\n\nThe root \\(P=3\\) candidate\n\\[\n N(n)=\\frac{256n-19}{27},\\qquad n\\equiv16\\pmod{27},\n\\tag{FO13}\n\\]\nhas \\(K=8,P=3\\), left word \\(OOOEEEEE\\), and right word \\(EEEOOEEO\\).\nTheir constants are\n\\[\n C_L=9+6+4=19,\\qquad C_R=9\\cdot8+3\\cdot16+128=248,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n C_R-C_L=229=256-27,\n\\]\nso the ordered gap-one identity is exact. The right source is\n\\((256(n+1)-248)/27=N(n)+1\\). The domain \\(n\\equiv16\\pmod{27}\\) is\n\\(n\\equiv7\\pmod9\\), where \\(M\\) is unavailable.\n\nConsequently the \\(L,M\\) modulo-\\(27\\) dead branches can revive. For example,\nfrom \\(73\\),\n\\[\n L(73)=97,\\qquad M(73)=259,\n\\]\nand both \\(97,259\\equiv16\\pmod{27}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n N(97)=919,\\qquad N(259)=2455,\n\\]\nand both outputs are \\(1\\pmod3\\), so an \\(L\\)-step remains available from\neach. This proves that the old bounded-side-branch argument cannot simply\nbe extended by adding \\(N\\). It does not prove two infinite branches or\npositive-density output.\n\nThe reciprocal slope sum for the three-rule alphabet is\n\\[\n \\frac34+\\frac9{32}+\\frac{27}{256}\n =\\frac{291}{256}>1.\n\\tag{FO14}\n\\]\nThus the easy subcritical criterion also gives no result for\n\\(\\{L,M,N\\}\\). The weighted criterion (FO8) reduces the remaining question to\nthe weighted congruence tree \\(\\sigma_s(n_0)\\): one needs a proof that its\ntail is summable, or some different direct output bound. No such bound is\nclaimed at this preliminary stage; Section 6 supplies such a bound for the\nspecified five-rule alphabet.\n\n## 5. Scope and precise gap\n\nThe weighted-tree theorem applies to any finite structural alphabet whose\nmaps satisfy the common expanding-cone hypothesis (FO2); it is therefore a\ngenuine criterion beyond \\(P\\le2\\), but it is conditional. The\nsubcritical test \\(\\rho\u003c1\\) is a convenient uniform way to verify it and\nfails already for \\(L,M\\), as well as for the displayed \\(P=3\\) candidate.\n\nThe actual \\(3\\)-adic admissibility tree can be much thinner than the full\nword tree, but no independence or CRT product estimate is available for\nthat thinning. A finite quotient argument also cannot be assumed uniformly:\nto determine an output modulo \\(3^q\\) after a rule with denominator\n\\(3^{P_i}\\), the input generally must be known modulo \\(3^{q+P_i}\\), so\nfixed finite residue data do not automatically close under arbitrary\nswitching.\n\nBefore the refined audit in Section 6, the unresolved task for a \\(P\\ge3\\) alphabet\nwas to control \\(\\sigma_s(n_0)\\), or another source-output branching\ninvariant, rather than to count terminal seeds. Section 6 supplies that\ncontrol for the specified five-rule alphabet. Arbitrary \\(P\\), additional\nstructural rules, and the full predecessor tree remain outside scope; no\npositive-density conclusion or Collatz completion follows.\n\n## 6. Independent square-root audit for the supplied \\(P=3\\) candidate alphabet\n\nThis section audits the proposed \\(P=3\\) rules and the weighted-branch\nargument. It treats the displayed alphabet as an assumed finite alphabet;\nit does not assert that the list is complete among all \\(P=3\\) structural\npairs.\n\n### 6.1 The three non-\\(L,M\\) rules\n\nThe proposed rules are\n\\[\n J(n)=\\frac{128n-23}{27},\\qquad\n N(n)=\\frac{256n-19}{27},\\qquad\n R(n)=\\frac{256n-31}{27}.\n\\tag{FO15}\n\\]\nTheir guards are respectively\n\\[\n J:\\ n\\equiv16\\pmod{27},\\qquad\n N:\\ n\\equiv16\\pmod{27},\\qquad\n R:\\ n\\equiv19\\pmod{27}.\n\\tag{FO16}\n\\]\nThe affine gap constants are \\(C_R-C_L=128-27=101\\) for \\(J\\), and\n\\(256-27=229\\) for both \\(N,R\\).\n\nThe constants are realized by complete words. For \\(J\\), the left odd\npositions \\(0,1,3\\) give \\(9+6+8=23\\), while right odd positions \\(2,3,6\\)\ngive \\(36+24+64=124\\). For \\(N\\), the words\n\\(OOOEEEEE\\) and \\(EEEOOEEO\\) give \\(19\\) and \\(248\\). For \\(R\\), left odd\npositions \\(0,1,4\\) give \\(9+6+16=31\\), and right positions \\(2,5,7\\) give\n\\(36+96+128=260\\). Thus each displayed map has a genuine ordered\ngap-one word pair, with \\((K,P)=(7,3),(8,3),(8,3)\\), respectively.\n\nTheir source slopes are\n\\[\n \\alpha_J=\\frac{128}{27},\\qquad\n \\alpha_N=\\alpha_R=\\frac{256}{27}.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(L,M\\), use the square-root weights\n\\[\n l=\\sqrt{\\frac34},\\quad m=\\sqrt{\\frac9{32}},\\quad\n j=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{128}},\\quad r=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{256}},\n\\tag{FO17}\n\\]\nwhere \\(r\\) applies to both \\(N\\) and \\(R\\). All five maps expand relative\nto \\(B=1\\) on \\(n>1\\); for example\n\\[\n N(n)-1=\\frac{256}{27}(n-1)+\\frac{70}{9},\\qquad\n R(n)-1=\\frac{256}{27}(n-1)+\\frac{22}{3},\n\\]\nand the analogous identities for \\(L,M,J\\) have nonnegative additive\nterms. Hence every admissible word from \\(n_0>1\\) satisfies\n\\[\n x_w-1\\ge(n_0-1)\\alpha_w.\n\\tag{FO18}\n\\]\n\n### 6.2 Residue transitions\n\nPartition the live residues \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) modulo \\(27\\) as\n\\[\n A=\\{1\\},\\quad B=\\{10\\},\\quad C=\\{19\\},\\quad D=\\{16\\},\\quad\n E=\\{4,7,13,22,25\\},\n\\]\nwith all residues \\(0\\) or \\(2\\pmod3\\) denoted \\(Z\\). The guards are\n\\(L\\) on all live states, \\(M\\) on \\(A,B,C\\), \\(J,N\\) on \\(D\\), and \\(R\\)\non \\(C\\). Direct substitution gives:\n\n- \\(Z\\) has no outgoing rule, and \\(E\\) has only \\(L\\to Z\\);\n- \\(A\\) has \\(L\\to A\\cup B\\cup C\\) and \\(M\\to Z\\);\n- \\(B\\) has \\(L\\to E\\) and \\(M\\to Z\\);\n- for \\(C=n=27q+19\\), writing \\(q\\bmod3=t\\):\n  \\(t=0\\) gives \\(L,M\\to E\\), \\(R\\to Z\\);\n  \\(t=1\\) gives \\(L\\to E\\), \\(M\\to A\\cup B\\cup C\\), \\(R\\to Z\\);\n  \\(t=2\\) gives \\(L,M\\to D\\cup E\\) and \\(R\\) to a live state;\n- for \\(D=n=27q+16\\), writing \\(q\\bmod3=t\\):\n  \\(t=0\\) gives \\(L,J\\to Z\\), \\(N\\) to a live state;\n  \\(t=1\\) gives \\(L,J,N\\to Z\\);\n  \\(t=2\\) gives \\(L,N\\to Z\\), \\(J\\) to a live state.\n\nFor example,\n\\[\n J(27q+16)=128q+75,\\qquad N(27q+16)=256q+151,\n\\]\nso at most one of \\(J,N\\) can remain live. Likewise,\n\\(R(27q+19)=256q+179\\), which is live only in the \\(t=2\\) case.\nThis verifies the stated residue pruning without assuming a finite quotient\nfor arbitrary future depths.\n\n### 6.3 Finite-depth weighted induction\n\nFor a state \\(x\\), let \\(T_s(x)\\) be the total weight of all admissible\nwords of length at most \\(s\\), with the empty word contributing \\(1\\), and\neach rule contributing its weight from (FO17). Let \\(H_s\\) be the supremum\nover all positive integer states whose residue is live, not merely over the\nnine residue labels. The transitions above imply, with earlier depths on\nthe right,\n\\[\n E_s\\le1+l,\n\\]\n\\[\n A_s\\le1+m+lH_{s-1},\\qquad\n B_s\\le1+m+l(1+l),\n\\]\n\\[\n D_s\\le1+l+r+jH_{s-1}.\n\\tag{FO19}\n\\]\nThe \\(D\\)-bound is safe in the \\(t=0\\) case because \\(j\\ge r\\) and\n\\(H_{s-1}\\ge1\\); in the \\(t=2\\) case the \\(J\\)-branch is simply bounded by\n\\(H_{s-1}\\), regardless of which live residue it reaches.\n\nFor \\(C\\), the three \\(q\\bmod3\\) cases give\n\\[\n C_{0,s}\\le1+r+(l+m)(1+l),\n\\]\n\\[\n C_{1,s}\\le1+r+l(1+l)+mH_{s-1},\n\\]\nand, using the common \\(D/E\\) bound for the \\(L,M\\) branches in the \\(t=2\\)\ncase,\n\\[\n C_{2,s}\\le\n 1+(l+m)(1+l+r)+\\big((l+m)j+r\\big)H_{s-1}.\n\\tag{FO20}\n\\]\nThe \\(R\\)-branch in \\(C_2\\) is bounded directly by \\(H_{s-1}\\); this is why\nits coefficient is \\(r\\), rather than another factor \\(j\\).\n\nNow\n\\[\n l\u003c\\frac78,\\qquad m\u003c\\frac34,\\qquad j\u003c\\frac12,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n c:=(l+m)j+r\n   =\\frac{18\\sqrt2+21\\sqrt3}{64}\n   \u003c\\frac{255}{256}.\n\\tag{FO21}\n\\]\nThe last strict inequality follows from\n\\(\\sqrt2\u003c3/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3\u003c7/4\\). All additive constants in\n(FO19)--(FO20) are \\(\u003c7\\): for instance\n\\(1+(l+m)(1+l+r)\u003c7\\), and the other cases are smaller.\n\nThe quantities \\(H_s\\) are nondecreasing in \\(s\\). In the \\(C_2\\) estimate,\nthe refined \\(D\\)-bound at depth \\(s-1\\) may use \\(H_{s-2}\\le H_{s-1}\\);\nfor \\(s=1\\), the depth-zero value is \\(H_0=1\\). Starting from \\(H_0=1\\),\ninduction on \\(s\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n H_s\\le2048\n\\tag{FO22}\n\\]\nfor every finite \\(s\\). Indeed, each nonconstant coefficient is at most\n\\(7/8,3/4,1/2\\), or \\(255/256\\), and\n\\(255\\cdot2048/256+7=2047\u003c2048\\). Letting \\(s\\to\\infty\\), the total\nsquare-root weight of all admissible words from any fixed \\(n_0>1\\) is at\nmost \\(2048\\).\n\nCombining this with (FO18), if \\(x_w\\le X\\) then\n\\[\n 1\\le\n \\sqrt{\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}}\\,\\alpha_w^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nConsequently the number of distinct generated source coordinates obeys\n\\[\n \\#(A(n_0)\\cap[1,X])\n \\le 2048\\sqrt{\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}}\n =O_{n_0}(\\sqrt X).\n\\tag{FO23}\n\\]\nFirst-hit filtering only decreases this count. The seed \\(n_0=1\\) remains\nthe separate case \\(A(1)=\\{1,3\\}\\).\n\n### 6.4 Scope of the audited conclusion\n\nThe square-root estimate is valid for the supplied finite alphabet\n\\(\\{L,M,J,N,R\\}\\), assuming those are all rules allowed in the switching\ntree. It extends the alphabet covered beyond \\(L,M\\), while being weaker\nnumerically than the \\(O(\\log X)\\) estimate already proved on the\n\\(L,M\\)-only subset. It does not require a reciprocal-slope sum below \\(1\\).\nIt is not a complete \\(P=3\\) classification: an omitted structural \\(P=3\\)\nrule, a \\(P\\ge4\\) rule, order reversal, or a non-adjacency-preserving block\nlies outside this audit. No positive-density theorem for the full\npredecessor set and no Collatz completion follows.\n\n## 7. Audit verdict for the saved THREE_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md\n\nI checked SL3.7--SL3.9 as written. The residue table is correctly coarse:\nthe \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) branches use only a \\(t\\bmod3\\) split, and every\ndestination declared \\(S\\) is an arbitrary live state. In particular, no\nfalse restriction to \\(D\\) or \\(E\\) is used. The rules in \\(D\\) have at most\none live branch, while \\(C_2\\) sends \\(L,M\\) to \\(D/E\\) and \\(R\\) directly\nto an arbitrary live state.\n\nThe SL3.8 induction is valid as a strong induction. Its \\(H_h\\) is\nunderstood as the supremum over all positive integer live states, not a\nsingle representative of each residue. The displayed \\(D\\)-bound does not\nclose a cycle: the raw case with a live \\(N\\)-branch is\n\\(1+a+c+dH\\), which is bounded by \\(1+a+d+cH\\) because \\(c>d\\) and\n\\(H\\ge1\\). In the \\(C_2\\) estimate, a \\(D\\)-subtree at depth \\(h-1\\)\nuses \\(H_{h-2}\\le H_{h-1}\\); the depth-zero case is \\(H_0=1\\). Thus the\ncoefficient\n\\[\n (a+b)c+d\u003c\\frac{255}{256}\n\\]\nand the other coefficients \\(a\u003c7/8\\), \\(b\u003c3/4\\), \\(c\u003c1/2\\) support the\nuniform \\(H_h\\le2048\\) induction. No cyclic-state assumption is hidden.\n\nSL3.9 correctly converts this word-weight bound into a count of distinct\nsources: each source is represented by at least one admissible word, so\ncounting words is an upper bound even when collisions occur. The growth\ninequality \\(F_i(n)-1\\ge\\alpha_i(n-1)\\) for \\(n>1\\) gives the square-root\nfactor, and first-hit restrictions only remove sources. The exceptional\nseed is correctly \\(A(1)=\\{1,3\\}\\), since only \\(L,M\\) are initially legal,\n\\(L(1)=1\\), \\(M(1)=3\\), and \\(3\\) is dead.\n\nVerdict: SL3.7--SL3.9 are correct in the stated complete-\\(P\\le3\\)\nstructural class. The conclusion is conditional on the preceding\nclassification and does not extend to arbitrary \\(P\\), order reversal,\nasynchronous blocks, or the full paired predecessor set.\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_FIRST_RETURN_BLOCK_TREE.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — first-return block trees, Kraft bounds, and the fixed-seed gap\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual analysis only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents. The question is whether\nminimal ordered-adjacency return blocks give a pointwise output bound for one\nfixed terminal seed. The answer separates a valid prefix/Kraft lemma from a\nmeasure statement that does not control exceptional integer seeds.\n\n## 1. Structural first returns and unique factorization\n\nAn actual simultaneous ordered-adjacency event need not be structural. The\nleft and right prefix words may have different odd counts. For example,\n\\[\n (6,7)\\to(3,11)\\to(5,17)\\to(8,26)\\to(4,13)\\to(2,20)\n \\to(1,10)\\to(2,5)\\to(1,8)\\to(2,4)\\to(1,2)\n\\]\nhas its first positive-time ordered return at \\(K=10\\), but the two odd\ncounts are \\(4\\) and \\(5\\).\n\nCall a return structural only when the two prefix words have equal odd count\n\\(P\\) and the endpoint gap is \\(+1\\) (or, for a signed version, \\(-1\\)).\nEqual counts give the same affine slope \\(3^P/2^K\\); therefore a gap\n\\(\\pm1\\) at one source implies the corresponding affine identity on the\nwhole admissible progression. For the rest of this report, the alphabet\nand transfer use the ordered orientation only. Its inverse left coordinate\nhas the form\n\\[\n F_i(n)=\\frac{2^{K_i}n-C_i}{3^{P_i}}.\n\\tag{FR1}\n\\]\n\nTake the next structural return after each structural event, when it exists.\nThe parity path is deterministic, so the first such structural event and its\nword are unique; recursively, the resulting factorization is unique. This\nfactorization applies only to structural returns. It does not factor every\nactual adjacency event, order reversal, asynchronous hit, or path with only\nfinitely many structural returns.\n\nFor a structural block, its complete left parity word fixes one source\nresidue \\(\\rho_i\\pmod{2^{K_i}}\\), hence one dyadic cylinder\n\\[\n U_i=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Z_2:x\\equiv\\rho_i\\pmod{2^{K_i}}\\},\n \\qquad \\mu_2(U_i)=2^{-K_i}.\n\\tag{FR2}\n\\]\n\n## 2. Prefix-free cylinders and the Kraft bound\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal B\\) be the global countable family of nonempty minimal\nstructural-return left words. A member has a seed-dependent \\(3\\)-adic\nintegrality guard, but the word alphabet itself is not seed-dependent. No\nword in \\(\\mathcal B\\) can be a proper prefix of another: if \\(u\\) were a\nprefix of \\(v\\), the structural return at the end of \\(u\\) would contradict\nminimality of \\(v\\). Distinct words of equal length have distinct parity\nresidues. Therefore the cylinders \\(U_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, and the\nbinary Kraft inequality gives\n\\[\n \\kappa:=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal B}2^{-K_i}\\le1.\n\\tag{FR3}\n\\]\nThe empty identity block must be omitted; otherwise it is a prefix of every\nnonempty word.\n\nThis is a global countable prefix-free family, while the usable guards\ndepend on the current terminal seed. The unique factorization only applies\nalong paths that actually return structurally; it does not turn all\nadjacency events into members of \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n## 3. The associated 3-adic transfer identity\n\nFor each block define its terminal \\(3\\)-adic domain\n\\[\n D_i=\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z_3:3^{P_i}\\mid2^{K_i}n-C_i\\}.\n\\]\nThis is one residue class modulo \\(3^{P_i}\\), of Haar measure \\(3^{-P_i}\\).\nThe affine inverse \\(F_i:D_i\\to\\mathbb Z_3\\) is a bijection. For nonnegative\nmeasurable \\(f\\), define\n\\[\n (\\mathcal Lf)(n)=\n \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal B}\n \\frac{3^{P_i}}{2^{K_i}}\\,\n 1_{D_i}(n)\\,f(F_i(n)).\n\\tag{FR4}\n\\]\nA change of variables on \\(D_i\\) gives\n\\[\n \\int_{D_i} f(F_i(n))\\,d\\mu_3(n)\n =3^{-P_i}\\int_{\\mathbb Z_3}f(x)\\,d\\mu_3(x),\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb Z_3}\\mathcal Lf\\,d\\mu_3\n =\\left(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal B}2^{-K_i}\\right)\n  \\int_{\\mathbb Z_3}f\\,d\\mu_3\n =\\kappa\\int_{\\mathbb Z_3}f\\,d\\mu_3.\n\\tag{FR5}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\kappa\u003c1\\), positivity and iteration yield\n\\[\n \\int\\sum_{s\\ge0}\\mathcal L^s1\\,d\\mu_3\n =\\sum_{s\\ge0}\\kappa^s=\\frac1{1-\\kappa}.\n\\tag{FR6}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\sum_s\\mathcal L^s1(n)\u003c\\infty\\) for \\(\\mu_3\\)-almost every \\(n\\).\nAt a point \\(n\\), \\(\\mathcal L^s1(n)\\) is the sum over admissible\n\\(s\\)-block paths of the reciprocal slope products\n\\[\n \\prod_{r=1}^s\\frac{3^{P_{i_r}}}{2^{K_{i_r}}}\n =\\prod_{r=1}^s\\alpha_{i_r}^{-1},\n \\qquad \\alpha_i=\\frac{2^{K_i}}{3^{P_i}}.\n\\tag{FR7}\n\\]\n\nThis is a genuine integrated/cylinder result. It is not a pointwise\nfixed-integer conclusion. The positive integers form a countable\n\\(\\mu_3\\)-null set, so a statement holding for \\(\\mu_3\\)-almost every seed\ndoes not control any prescribed positive integer. If the block maps also\nsatisfy a common expanding-cone inequality and one separately knows the\npointwise finiteness of the reciprocal-slope tree at a particular \\(n_0\\),\nthe usual weighted output argument gives \\(o(X)\\) for that \\(n_0\\). The\nKraft/transfer identity alone does not provide that pointwise hypothesis.\n\n## 4. Explicit logical counterexample to “prefix-free implies sparse fixed-seed outputs”\n\nTake the binary prefix code \\(\\{0,1\\}\\). Its cylinders are disjoint,\nprefix-free, and have Kraft sum \\(1\\). Let the two abstract inverse branches\nbe\n\\[\n G_0(n)=2n,\\qquad G_1(n)=2n+1.\n\\]\nStarting from \\(n_0=1\\), the outputs after exactly \\(s\\) blocks are\n\\[\n \\{2^s+j:0\\le j\u003c2^s\\}=[2^s,2^{s+1}-1].\n\\]\nTheir union over \\(s\\) contains every positive integer from \\(1\\) onward, so\nthe fixed-seed output set has density \\(1\\), despite unique binary\nfactorization and the Kraft bound.\n\nThis is an abstract coding model, not a claim about a Collatz structural\npair. It proves the logical point: prefix-free cylinders by themselves\ncontrol Haar/cylinder mass, not the number of integer outputs from one\nseed. A strict \\(\\kappa\u003c1\\) gives the almost-everywhere statement (FR6),\nbut the null exceptional set can contain all positive integers; a separate\npointwise arithmetic argument is required.\n\n## 5. Why arbitrary-\\(P\\) source growth is an additional gap\n\nThe output conversion needs a pointwise lower growth estimate such as\n\\[\n F_i(x)-B\\ge\\alpha_i(x-B),\\qquad \\alpha_i>1.\n\\tag{FR8}\n\\]\nNo such expanding-cone property follows from prefix-freeness. For larger\nodd counts, an inverse slope \\(\\alpha_i=2^{K_i}/3^{P_i}\\) may be at most\none, and even when all slopes exceed one the factor \\(3^{P_i}\\) is absent\nfrom the Kraft mass \\(2^{-K_i}\\). Thus (FR3) does not bound the\nreciprocal-slope weights needed for a fixed-seed source count.\n\nA checked order-reversing example makes the first limitation concrete. The\nlength-11 words\n\\[\n q=01111100011,\\qquad q'=11111101000\n\\]\nboth have \\(P=7\\), with forward constants\n\\[\n C_q=6358,\\qquad C_{q'}=2123,\\qquad\n C_q-C_{q'}=4235=2048+2187.\n\\]\nThey reverse ordered adjacency: for a source pair \\((x,x+1)\\), the \\(q\\)\nword on \\(x\\) and \\(q'\\) on \\(x+1\\) produce \\((n+1,n)\\). Solving for the\nsource from the lower target \\(n\\) gives\n\\[\n S(n)=\\frac{2048n-4310}{2187},\\qquad n\\equiv205\\pmod{2187},\n\\tag{FR9}\n\\]\nwith sources \\(2048m+190,2048m+191\\) and reversed targets\n\\(2187m+206,2187m+205\\). The orientation matters: this is not an ordered\nadjacency-preserving block.\n\nComposing the reversal twice gives an ordered block with \\(K=22,P=14\\):\n\\[\n S^2(n)=\n \\frac{4194304\\,n-18252850}{4782969},\\qquad\n \\alpha(S^2)=\\frac{4194304}{4782969}\u003c1,\n\\tag{FR10}\n\\]\nwith guard \\(n\\equiv2959216\\pmod{4782969}\\). If this ordered block is\nfactored into primitive ordered returns, the product of their slopes is\nstill \\(4194304/4782969\u003c1\\), so at least one primitive factor has slope\nbelow one. The hand chain\n\\[\n 2959216\\longmapsto2771134\\longmapsto2595006\n\\]\nchecks the numerator and denominator arithmetic. The one-step map \\(S\\) is\noutside the ordered tree, but its square lies inside it; consequently an\norientation restriction alone does not restore a universal expanding-cone\nargument.\n\n## 6. Verdict and required missing input\n\nMinimal simultaneous ordered-return blocks have a useful unique\nfactorization and a rigorous prefix-free/Kraft bound. The 3-adic transfer\noperator upgrades Kraft to an almost-everywhere reciprocal-slope summability\nstatement when \\(\\kappa\u003c1\\). Neither statement alone yields a density or\n\\(o(X)\\) bound for outputs from every fixed positive integer.\n\nTo obtain a fixed-seed theorem beyond the currently audited \\(P\\le3\\) class,\none needs at least one additional pointwise ingredient: a seed-dependent\nweighted branching bound, a direct arithmetic proof that\ninteger seeds cannot lie in the exceptional 3-adic set, or another growth\nand collision estimate. No arbitrary-\\(P\\) completion or positive-density\nconclusion follows from Kraft alone.\n\n## 7. A strict Kraft improvement from a dead coalescing cylinder\n\nThere is a concrete source cylinder that cannot contain any nontrivial\nsimultaneous structural first return, in either orientation. Write\n\\[\n x=16m+2,\\qquad x+1=16m+3.\n\\]\nThe two four-step parity words are \\(EOEO\\) and \\(OOEE\\), and their forward\ntrajectories are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rcl}\n16m+2&\\to&8m+1\\to12m+2\\to6m+1\\to9m+2,\\\\\n16m+3&\\to&24m+5\\to36m+8\\to18m+4\\to9m+2.\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{FR11}\n\\]\nAt the first three positive-time prefixes, the left/right odd-count pairs\nare respectively \\((0,1),(1,2),(1,2)\\). Hence none is a structural\n\\(\\pm1\\) return, since structurality requires equal odd counts. At time four\nthe counts are equal but the pair coalesces with gap \\(0\\), and all later\nstates are equal. Therefore the entire dyadic cylinder\n\\[\n U_{\\mathrm{dead}}=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Z_2:x\\equiv2\\pmod{16}\\}\n\\]\nis disjoint from every nonempty minimal structural-return cylinder, in either\norientation, including its \\(2\\)-adic points. Since\n\\(\\mu_2(U_{\\mathrm{dead}})=1/16\\), the prefix-free family satisfies the\nstrict bound\n\\[\n \\kappa=\\sum_i2^{-K_i}\\le\\frac{15}{16}.\n\\tag{FR12}\n\\]\nUnder the global transfer operator (FR4), this improves (FR6) to\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb Z_3}\\sum_{s\\ge0}\\mathcal L^s1\\,d\\mu_3\n \\le\\frac1{1-15/16}=16.\n\\tag{FR13}\n\\]\nThis strengthens the almost-everywhere reciprocal-slope statement, but does\nnot decide whether any positive integer is exceptional. It remains a\nmeasure/cylinder result rather than a fixed-seed output theorem.\n\n## 8. Explicit exceptional \\(3\\)-adic point for the transfer bound\n\nThe almost-everywhere qualification in (FR6) is essential even for the\nordered square of the reversal block. The fixed point of\n\\[\n S(n)=\\frac{2048n-4310}{2187}\n\\]\nis\n\\[\n z_*=-\\frac{4310}{139}\\in\\mathbb Z_3,\n\\tag{FR14}\n\\]\nbecause \\(139\\) is a \\(3\\)-adic unit and \\(S(z_*)=z_*\\). The ordered map\n\\(S^2\\) has reciprocal slope\n\\[\n \\alpha(S^2)^{-1}\n =\\left(\\frac{2187}{2048}\\right)^2>1.\n\\]\nFactor \\(S^2\\), if desired, into its finite sequence of minimal structural\nordered-return blocks. Full \\(3\\)-adic integrality of the composed affine\nmap implies integrality of every prefix, by the same nested-divisibility\nidentity used for affine compositions. Hence this repeated block path is\nadmissible at \\(z_*\\), and for some finite block length \\(r\\),\n\\[\n \\mathcal L^{rs}1(z_*)\\ge\n \\left(\\frac{2187}{2048}\\right)^{2s}\n \\qquad(s\\ge1).\n\\tag{FR15}\n\\]\nThe point \\(z_*\\) is not a positive integer, but it is an explicit\nexceptional point where the transfer resolvent diverges despite\n\\(\\kappa\u003c1\\). Thus the Kraft/transfer argument is not pointwise uniform\neven on the ordered structural tree; an integer-seed theorem needs an\nadditional arithmetic exclusion of such exceptional \\(3\\)-adic behavior.\n\n## 9. Final audit of GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md (IR1--IR5)\n\nIR1 is correct in the saved scope: it defines structural events by equal\nprefix odd counts plus a signed gap, and therefore does not promote the\n\\((6,7)\\) actual adjacency at time \\(10\\) with counts \\(4,5\\) to a\ncommon-\\(P\\) block. The earliest structural cut is unique, giving one\nglobal countable alphabet with seed-dependent guards.\n\nIR2 correctly gives pairwise disjoint source cylinders and\n\\(\\kappa\\le15/16\\). The dead-cylinder conclusion is genuinely \\(2\\)-adic:\non \\(x\\equiv2\\pmod{16}\\), the first three prefix odd-count pairs are\n\\((0,1),(1,2),(1,2)\\), while the fourth prefix coalesces. Thus no\nstructural signed return occurs at any \\(2\\)-adic point of the cylinder.\nThe saved mention of positive representatives is unnecessary but does not\nchange the valid cylinder argument.\n\nIR3's Haar identity is valid for the countable global alphabet. Each guard\nhas measure \\(3^{-P_i}\\), \\(F_i\\) is a bijection from that guard to\n\\(\\mathbb Z_3\\), and the weighted change of variables contributes\n\\(2^{-K_i}\\). Countable nonnegative summation therefore gives\n\\(\\int\\mathcal Lf\\,d\\mu_3=\\kappa\\int f\\,d\\mu_3\\) and the resolvent bound\n\\(\\le16\\), with no finite-alphabet assumption. This remains an\nalmost-everywhere statement, not a positive-integer statement.\n\nIR4's arithmetic checks pass:\n\\(C_q=6358\\), \\(C_{q'}=2123\\), the reversal inverse is\n\\(S(n)=(2048n-4310)/2187\\) on \\(n\\equiv205\\pmod{2187}\\), and two reversals\ngive the ordered \\(K=22,P=14\\) map with constants\n\\(18{,}252{,}850\\) and \\(17{,}664{,}185\\). Its slope is\n\\(4194304/4782969\u003c1\\), so any irreducible ordered factorization contains a\nfactor of slope below one.\n\nIR5 is also valid. The fixed point\n\\(z_*=-4310/139\\in\\mathbb Z_3\\) is returned by the ordered \\(S^2\\) block.\nFactoring that block into finitely many irreducible structural blocks is\nlegitimate; nested \\(3\\)-adic divisibility makes every prefix admissible.\nRepeating the factorization gives reciprocal weight at least\n\\((2187/2048)^{2t}\\), so the pointwise resolvent diverges at \\(z_*\\) while\nits Haar integral remains finite. The point is not a positive integer, so\nthis is an explicit nonuniformity obstruction, not a Collatz counterexample.\n\n## 10. Uniform constants over all positive integer seeds fail\n\nLet \\(H=S^2\\) be the ordered contraction with slope\n\\[\n \\alpha=\\frac{4194304}{4782969}\u003c1,\n\\]\nand let its irreducible structural factorization have \\(r\\ge1\\) blocks.\nFor each \\(t\\ge1\\), the composed map \\(H^t\\) has denominator\n\\(4782969^t=3^{14t}\\) and numerator coefficient \\(4194304^t\\), a\n\\(3\\)-adic unit. Consequently its integrality condition is one residue class\nmodulo \\(3^{14t}\\). Choose a sufficiently large positive integer \\(n_t\\) in\nthat class. Because only finitely many prefixes occur for this fixed \\(t\\),\ntaking \\(n_t\\) large also makes every intermediate inverse value positive.\nNested divisibility and the genuine-word lemma then make the full\n\\(H^t\\) factorization admissible.\n\nFor any \\(\\gamma>0\\), define the reciprocal-weight tree\n\\[\n W_\\gamma(n)=\\sum_{\\text{admissible irreducible words }w\\text{ from }n}\n \\alpha_w^{-\\gamma}.\n\\]\nThe one admissible \\(H^t\\) path from \\(n_t\\) contributes\n\\[\n W_\\gamma(n_t)\\ge(\\alpha^t)^{-\\gamma}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n \\sup_{n\\in\\mathbb N_{>0}}W_\\gamma(n)\n \\ge(\\alpha^t)^{-\\gamma}\\quad\\text{for every }t,\n\\]\nand the supremum is infinite. This is an actual failure of any uniform\nweighted constant over positive integer seeds, including a uniform\n\\(H=2048\\)-type bound. The integers \\(n_t\\) vary with \\(t\\); the argument\ndoes not produce one positive integer with an infinite \\(H\\)-trajectory,\nnor does it contradict the possibility that each fixed integer has finite\n\\(W_\\gamma(n)\\).\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_MINIMAL_BAD_RESOLVENT.md":"# Minimal-bad-seed resolvent: exact residue obstruction and the critical barrier\n\n18 September 2026, Asia Tashkent. Manual analysis only; no programs,\nenumeration, CAS, Lean, or numerical experiments. This note audits the\npositive-integer return resolvent for the deterministic ordered structural\nmap. It does not prove the existence of a bad seed, finiteness of \\(W_1\\),\npositive pair density, or Collatz.\n\n## 1. Incoming structural blocks force the target residue\n\nA genuine nonempty ordered block with odd count \\(P\\geq1\\) has terminal\naffine value\n\\[\n T_w(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C_w}{2^K}.\n\\]\nThe last odd contribution in \\(C_w\\) is a power of \\(2\\), while every earlier\ncontribution has a factor \\(3\\). Thus \\(C_w\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), and the\nterminal value is a 3-adic unit. Both terminal endpoints of an ordered\nadjacent block are therefore units modulo \\(3\\). If its target pair is\n\\((n,n+1)\\), this forces\n\\[\n n\\not\\equiv0,\\qquad n+1\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3,\n \\qquad\\text{hence }n\\equiv1\\pmod3.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, for every positive integer \\(n\\not\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), there is no\nnonempty incoming structural block. The deterministic inverse closure is\nexactly\n\\[\n \\mathcal M(n)=\\{n\\},\\qquad W_q(n)=1\\quad(q>0).\n\\]\nFor a minimal bad \\(\\mu\\equiv3\\pmod4\\), the possible classes modulo \\(12\\)\nare \\(3,7,11\\). The \\(3\\) and \\(11\\) classes have this singleton-closure\nproperty; the \\(7\\) class is the only possible minimal-bad class admitting\nnonempty structural predecessors. This is an exact arithmetic obstruction,\nnot a proof that a bad seed exists or that bad seeds have positive density.\n\n## 2. Deterministic multiplicity for a minimal bad seed\n\nLet \\(R\\) be the partial deterministic map induced by first ordered\nstructural returns. For an inverse schedule \\(w\\) ending at a target \\(n\\),\nwrite\n\\[\n A_w=\\frac{2^{K_w}}{3^{P_w}},\\qquad\n B_w=\\frac{C_w}{3^{P_w}}>0.\n\\]\nIts source \\(m\\) satisfies\n\\[\n m=A_w n-B_w,\\qquad\n A_w=\\frac{m+B_w}{n}>\\frac mn.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n A_w^{-q}\u003c\\left(\\frac nm\\right)^q\n\\]\nfor every nonempty schedule. The empty schedule has \\(m=\\mu\\), \\(A=1\\),\nand \\(B=0\\); this case must be kept separate from the strict inequality.\n\nNow suppose \\(\\mu\\) is minimal bad while \\(\\mu+1\\) is good, with finite\nfirst shortcut hitting time \\(h(\\mu+1)\\) to \\(\\{1,2\\}\\). Along any actual\nstructural block from \\((m,m+1)\\) to \\((R(m),R(m)+1)\\), the block length \\(K\\)\nis strictly before that first hit:\n\\[\n K\u003ch(m+1),\\qquad h(R(m)+1)=h(m+1)-K.\n\\]\nFor an incoming path of total length \\(K_{\\rm tot}\\) from \\(m\\) to \\(\\mu\\),\niteration gives the exact reverse relation\n\\[\n h(m+1)=K_{\\rm tot}+h(\\mu+1).\n\\]\nThus the good-side height grows along inverse paths; it bounds the number of\nforward returns from a fixed source, but does not bound the number or size of\ndistinct inverse endpoints.\nEvery nonempty ordered structural block has \\(K\\ge2\\). Hence \\(h\\) decreases\nby at least \\(2\\) along every outgoing \\(R\\)-edge before the good endpoint is\nreached. In particular, \\(\\mu\\) cannot be \\(R\\)-periodic.\n\nSince \\(R\\) is deterministic, a nonperiodic target has at most one hitting\ntime from each source endpoint \\(m\\). If two paths from the same \\(m\\) hit\n\\(\\mu\\) at different times, the segment between the hits would make \\(\\mu\\)\nperiodic. Thus there is no cycle multiplicity:\n\\[\n W_q(\\mu)=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal M(\\mu)} A_m^{-q},\n\\]\nwhere each \\(m\\) contributes its unique first-hit path and the empty path\n\\(m=\\mu\\). Minimality of \\(\\mu\\) implies every nonempty predecessor satisfies\n\\(m>\\mu\\): a smaller \\(m\\) reaching the bad seed would itself be bad, while\n\\(m=\\mu\\) would be a forbidden cycle. Therefore, for \\(q>1\\), taking finite\npartial sums first and then using monotone convergence,\n\\[\n W_q(\\mu)\n \\le 1+\\mu^q\\sum_{\\substack{m\\in\\mathcal M(\\mu)\\\\m>\\mu}}m^{-q}\n \\le 1+\\mu^q\\sum_{m>\\mu}m^{-q}\n \\le 1+\\frac{\\mu}{q-1}.\n\\]\nThis reproduces the elementary \\(q>1\\) finiteness bound, with the cycle\nfactor equal to \\(1\\) for this minimal-bad configuration.\n\nThe same argument does not prove the critical case:\n\\[\n W_1(\\mu;X)\n \\le 1+\\mu\\sum_{\\substack{m\\in\\mathcal M(\\mu)\\\\\\mu\u003cm\\le X}}\\frac1m\n \\le 1+\\mu\\log\\frac X\\mu,\\qquad X\\ge\\mu.\n\\]\nHere \\(W_1(\\mu;X)\\) is the endpoint-truncated sum; the empty term is the\nonly contribution when \\(X=\\mu\\). The untruncated \\(W_1(\\mu)\\) is obtained\nby monotone limit, and the displayed bound grows harmonically. The right side\ncan remain harmonic. The finite good-side height bounds the\nnumber of successive structural returns along one path, but gives no\narithmetic sparsity estimate for the set of distinct endpoints \\(m\\).\nThus no \\(W_1(\\mu)\u003c\\infty\\) conclusion follows.\n\n## 3. The three weights must not be conflated\n\nFor a block of binary length \\(K\\) and odd count \\(P\\), the relevant quantities\nare different:\n\\[\n \\text{binary source-cylinder mass}=2^{-K},\n \\qquad\n \\text{3-adic inverse-guard mass}=3^{-P},\n \\qquad\n \\text{resolvent weight }A^{-1}=\\frac{3^P}{2^K}.\n\\]\nThe first is a disjointness measure for source parity cylinders; the second\nis the ternary mass used in the Hutchinson transfer; the third is the\nindividual inverse-slope weight in \\(W_1\\). Neither Kraft control of\n\\(\\sum2^{-K}\\) nor a bound on \\(\\sum3^{-P}\\) directly controls the critical\nsum \\(\\sum3^P2^{-K}\\). In particular, source-cylinder disjointness cannot be\nused as a substitute for endpoint sparsity in \\(\\mathcal M(\\mu)\\).\n\n## 4. Sharp scope\n\nThe residue calculation proves \\(W_q(n)=1\\) for every target\n\\(n\\equiv0,2\\pmod3\\), independently of whether that target is bad. For a\nminimal bad \\(\\mu\\equiv7\\pmod{12}\\), the good right coordinate eliminates\nperiodic multiplicity and yields the sharp comparison above, but leaves the\ncritical endpoint harmonic sum unresolved.\n\nA seed switch cannot be justified from this one-sided closure: passing to an\nasynchronous pair or a different target requires the full two-target\ncorrelation problem. Even if \\(W_1(\\mu)\\) were finite, it would control only\nthe deterministic structural-return predecessor family, not all unequal-odd-\ncount trajectories or the positive-density lower bound for \\(Q(a,c)\\).\n\n\n## 8. Final audit of the minimal-bad pair boundary\n\nMB1--MB2 are correct. A nonempty structural target is \\(1\\bmod3\\), so the\norientation table is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n\\mu\\bmod12&(\\mu,\\mu+1)\\text{ bad/good}&(\\mu-1,\\mu)\\text{ good/bad}\\\\\\hline\n3&\\text{singleton}&\\text{singleton}\\\\\n7&\\text{possible incoming}&\\text{singleton}\\\\\n11&\\text{singleton}&\\text{possible incoming}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n“Possible” only removes the immediate congruence obstruction; it does not\nassert any predecessor.\n\nThe good-coordinate clock is valid in both orientations. If the right side is\ngood, its hit time satisfies\n\\[\nK\u003ch(m+1),\\qquad h(R(m)+1)=h(m+1)-K.\n\\]\nIf the left side is good, the same argument uses \\(h(m)\\); after hitting\n\\(1\\) or \\(2\\), its adjacent coordinate is \\(2\\) or \\(3\\), and these remain\ngood. For an incoming path to \\(\\mu\\), the exact reverse relation is\n\\[\nh(m+1)=K_{\\rm total}+h(\\mu+1),\n\\]\nso the clock controls forward depth from a fixed source but gives no inverse\nendpoint sparsity.\n\nMB4--MB5 are also correct after separating the empty schedule. Minimality\nforces every nonempty predecessor \\(m>\\mu\\), the cycle factor is \\(1\\), and\n\\[\nW_q(\\mu)\\le1+\\frac{\\mu}{q-1}\\quad(q>1),\\qquad\nW_1(\\mu;X)\\le1+\\mu\\log(X/\\mu).\n\\]\nMB7 is a valid individual source-size lower bound, not a multiplicity bound.\nMB5’s direction is correct: \\(\\operatorname{Pred}_C(\\mu)\\subseteq\n\\operatorname{Pred}_C(3\\mu+1)\\); no reverse forward step is supplied.\nAll-even and empty-word cases must remain separate, as stated.\n\nThe report correctly preserves the unresolved interface: neither orientation\nyields a critical \\(W_1\\) estimate, and the structural closure does not replace\nthe asynchronous full \\(Q_C(a,c)\\) problem.\n\n\n\n## 9. The odd target \\(b=(3\\mu+1)/2\\)\n\nFor a least bad \\(\\mu\\equiv3\\pmod4\\),\n\\[\n v_2(3\\mu+1)=1,\\qquad\n a=3\\mu+1,\\qquad b=\\frac{3\\mu+1}{2}.\n\\]\nThus \\(b\\) is odd, \\(b\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), and\n\\(b-\\mu=(\\mu+1)/2>0\\). Since the ordinary map sends\n\\(\\mu\\mapsto a\\mapsto b\\), \\(b\\) is bad whenever \\(\\mu\\) is bad. For the\nfull ordinary pair sets,\n\\[\n Q_C(a,1)\\subseteq Q_C(b,1),\n\\]\nbecause any source reaching \\(a\\) reaches \\(b\\) one ordinary step later; this\nis inclusion, not equality. The seed \\(\\mu\\) itself witnesses both sets.\n\nIf the accepted R181 boundary statement is run with an arbitrary fixed odd\nbad target, positive upper Banach density of \\(Q_C(b,1)\\) is therefore also a\nlegitimate sufficient input. This uses the target \\(b\\) directly and does not\ninfer density for the smaller set \\(Q_C(a,1)\\). In inverse normalization for\nwords ending at \\(b\\), minimality gives only \\(m\\ge\\mu\\), not \\(m\\ge b\\):\nthe source \\(\\mu\u003cb\\) is already a witness. Hence no \\(A>1\\) or slope\ncomparison relative to \\(b\\) may be imported into this target switch.\n\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_POINTWISE_RETURN_TREE.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — pointwise reciprocal weight for one positive seed\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual analysis only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents.\n\n## 1. Deterministic first-return map\n\nFor a positive integer x, follow the genuine shortcut trajectories from\nx and x+1. If their first structural ordered return exists (equal prefix\nodd count and terminal gap +1), define R(x) as the left terminal value.\nIf the return or the required positive-genuine condition is absent, R(x) is\nundefined. The parity trajectory is deterministic, so the first return is\nunique and R is a partial function.\n\nThe irreducible ordered blocks are exactly the possible first-return pieces\nof this map. Hence a positive admissible inverse schedule from terminal n\nto endpoint m gives R^s(m)=n, where s is its number of blocks. First-return\nfactorization gives at most one schedule for a fixed m and s. Conversely,\neach positive orbit segment R^s(m)=n gives its unique factorized schedule.\nThis uniqueness is the reason the argument cannot be applied to an arbitrary\ncollection of formal affine words.\n\n## 2. Endpoint size controls inverse weight\n\nThe composite inverse of a nonempty schedule has the form\n\n    F(n) = A n - B,       A>0, B>0,\n\nwhere A is the product of the inverse slopes 2^K/3^P. If F(n)=m>0, then\nA n>m, and for q>0\n\n    A^(-q) \u003c (n/m)^q.                         (PR1)\n\nThe empty schedule has m=n and A=1. Positivity of B follows by composing\nthe positive intercepts of genuine inverse words, so (PR1) does not assume\nthat every inverse branch expands.\n\n## 3. Periodic endpoint multiplicity\n\nSuppose two schedules from n end at the same m. Their forward images satisfy\nR^s(m)=n=R^t(m). If s=t, deterministic first-return factorization makes\nthe schedules identical. If s\u003ct, then R^(t-s)(n)=n, so n is periodic.\nThus a nonperiodic seed has at most one schedule to each endpoint.\n\nIf n is periodic with period r, let d_m be the least nonnegative time with\nR^(d_m)(m)=n. All possible times are d_m+t r. If A_m is the inverse slope\nfor the least path and A_c is the inverse slope of one full cycle at n,\nthe weights to m are\n\n    (A_m A_c^t)^(-q),       t >= 0.            (PR2)\n\nThe cycle is nonempty and has inverse form F_c(y)=A_c y-B_c, B_c>0.\nSince F_c(n)=n,\n\n    A_c = 1 + B_c/n > 1.                     (PR3)\n\nFor a nonempty genuine word with total odd count P>=1, its intercept C\nsatisfies C>=3^(P-1), hence B_c=C/3^P>=1/3. Therefore\n\n    A_c^(-q) \u003c= (3n/(3n+1))^q.                (PR4)\n\nFor q>=1,\n\n    sum_(t>=0) A_c^(-q t)\n      \u003c= 1/(1-(3n/(3n+1))^q)\n      \u003c= 3n+1.                               (PR5)\n\nThe last inequality uses (1-x)^q \u003c= 1-x for 0\u003cx\u003c1 and q>=1.\n\n## 4. Pointwise theorem for q>1\n\nLet W_q(n) be the sum of A^(-q) over all finite positive genuine inverse\nschedules in the irreducible ordered structural alphabet, including the\nempty schedule. Group schedules by their positive endpoint m. By (PR1),\neach endpoint contributes at most (n/m)^q in the nonperiodic case. In the\nperiodic case (PR5) multiplies this by at most 3n+1. Since endpoints are\npositive integers,\n\n    W_q(n) \u003c= (3n+1) n^q sum_(m>=1) m^(-q)\n            = (3n+1) n^q zeta(q),            q>1.    (PR6)\n\nThis is pointwise for every fixed positive seed. It uses only determinism,\nfirst-return factorization, positivity, and zeta convergence. It assumes\nneither Collatz termination nor absence of positive cycles. The factor\n3n+1 is unnecessary for a nonperiodic seed, but gives one bound covering\nboth cases.\n\nThis is a weighted-path result, not a source-count result. It does not say\nthat every actual adjacent-pair witness factors into this structural\nalphabet; unequal odd counts remain outside the map.\n\n## 5. The critical endpoint q=1\n\nWith an endpoint cutoff m\u003c=M, the same argument gives\n\n    W_(1,\u003c=M)(n)\n       \u003c= (3n+1)n sum_(m\u003c=M) 1/m\n       \u003c= (3n+1)n (1+log M).                 (PR7)\n\nThus q=1 has only a logarithmic harmonic envelope here. The full W_1(n)\nneed not be decided by this estimate: an infinite endpoint set may be\nsparse enough for its reciprocal sum to converge, while the unrestricted\ninteger envelope diverges. For 0\u003cq\u003c1, the same majorant has no automatic\nsummability. The strict threshold q>1 is exactly where this argument closes\nwithout an additional endpoint-sparsity theorem.\n\nThe dyadic Kraft bound does not by itself improve (PR7). It controls the\nsource-cylinder masses of the global block alphabet, whereas the terms in\n(PR7) are indexed by integer endpoints and weighted by their affine inverse\nslopes. A bridge from cylinder mass to the reciprocal endpoint sum would be\nan additional theorem, not a consequence of prefix-freeness.\n\n## 6. Scope and verdict\n\nFor every fixed positive integer seed and q>1, the total reciprocal weight\nof finite positive genuine inverse schedules in the deterministic ordered\nstructural-return tree is finite, with the explicit bound (PR6). Periodic\nseeds cause only the geometric multiplicity (PR2), since positivity forces\nthe cycle inverse slope above one. Nonperiodic seeds have no repeated\nendpoint.\n\nThe result does not settle q=1, does not count actual unequal-odd-count\nadjacency paths, and does not give a seed-independent constant. In\nparticular, the earlier H=S^2 contraction produces positive seeds varying\nwith t for which uniform bounds over all seeds fail; that does not conflict\nwith the present seed-dependent pointwise theorem.\n\n## 7. Consequence for an infinite positive inverse branch\n\nFix n>0 and q>1. Since W_q(n)\u003cinfinity, for every B>0 there are only\nfinitely many admissible finite schedules w with A_w\u003c=B: each such schedule\ncontributes at least B^(-q). Hence, for every B, all schedules of sufficiently\nlarge block-depth satisfy A_w>B. Thus along any infinite positive inverse\nbranch, A_w tends to infinity as the block-depth tends to infinity. This is\nqualitative only; it gives no rate and allows finite oscillations from mixed\ncontracting and expanding blocks. Empty levels cause no issue, since the\nassertion is then vacuous at those depths.\n\nThe same conclusion has a deterministic endpoint proof. An infinite inverse\npath either stays on the periodic cycle through n, where each full cycle\nmultiplies the inverse slope by A_c>1, or eventually leaves that cycle. After\nleaving, its positive endpoints are distinct: a repeat would force the\nforward deterministic path back through the cycle. Distinct positive integers\ntend to infinity, and m=A_w n-B_w with B_w>0 gives A_w>m/n. This concerns\ninverse paths only and is not a claim about forward-orbit slope growth.\n\n## 8. Why the generic argument cannot close q=1\n\nConsider the abstract positive partial map R(m)=1 for every m>=2, with R(1)\nundefined. Its inverse branches from terminal value 1 are\n\n    F_m(x)=(m+1)x-1,       domain {1},       m>=2.\n\nThey have positive intercept B=1 and distinct positive endpoints. There are\nno cycles, and after one inverse edge no second edge is available. Thus the\nonly inverse schedules from 1 are the empty schedule and the single-edge\nschedules, with\n\n    W_q(1)=1+sum_(m>=2)(m+1)^(-q).\n\nThis is finite for q>1 and infinite for q=1. The example has the deterministic\npartial-map and positive-affine properties used in PR1--PR7, but it has no\nCollatz dyadic or 3-adic cylinder constraints and is not a Collatz model. It\nshows that this proof of q=1 would require additional input, such as\narithmetic endpoint sparsity or a sharper weight estimate;\ndeterminism, positivity, distinct endpoints, and absence of cycles alone do\nnot provide it.\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_RESIDUE_EXPONENTS.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — residue-dependent exponents and the potential barrier\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual analysis only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents. The modulus is d=3^h with\nh>=1. This note tests potentials with different real powers on residue\nclasses, without valuation factors.\n\nThe final scope is in sections5--6: for every h the maximal exponent is\nexactly q; the ansatz is impossible for h=1,2,3. For h>=4 only necessary\nprofile restrictions have been proved. Section4 records the earlier\nintermediate restriction and rejected all-h shortcut, not the final limit.\n\n## 1. Ansatz and inverse branches\n\nLet L_q be the nonnegative operator over positive irreducible ordered\nstructural inverse blocks, with a branch of inverse slope A contributing\nA^(-q)V(F(n)). Assume a finite positive superharmonic function\n\n    L_q V \u003c= V\n\nand the residue-class asymptotic\n\n    V(n)=(c_(n mod d)+o(1)) n^(s_(n mod d)) ell(n),\n\nwhere every c_r>0, the exponents s_r are finite real numbers, and ell is\npositive with ell(an+b)/ell(n)->1 for every fixed a>0,b along positive\ninteger arguments. The o(1) is uniform over the finitely many classes.\n\nThe relevant genuine inverse branches are\n\n    F_L(n)=(4n-1)/3,       A_L=4/3,\n    F_M(n)=(32n-5)/9,      A_M=32/9,\n\nwith their exact guards. Their forward residue maps are\n\n    Phi_L(r)=(3r+1)/4,\n    Phi_M(r)=(9r+5)/32\n\nin Z/(3^h), where the denominators are units.\n\n## 2. L-branch ordering of exponents\n\nFor every source residue r, choose large positive terminal integers in the\nguard progression with source F_L(n) congruent to r modulo d. Comparing the\nsingle L contribution in L_qV\u003c=V shows\n\n    s_r \u003c= s_(Phi_L(r)).                         (RE1)\n\nSince\n\n    Phi_L^j(r)=1+(3/4)^j(r-1),\n\nwe have Phi_L^h(r)=1 modulo 3^h. Thus\n\n    s_r \u003c= s_1 for every residue r.                 (RE2)\n\nLet S=max_r s_r=s_1.\n\n## 3. The H path forces S>=q even with residue exponents\n\nUse H=S^2 from the structural-block report, with inverse slope\nalpha=4194304/4782969\u003c1 and fixed point z_*=-4310/139 in Z_3. Choose k\nlarge enough that 14k>=h. The H^k guard fixes the terminal residue modulo d\nto z_* modulo d. Varying the terminal integer by multiples of 3^(14k)\nchanges the source H^k(n) by a unit multiple of the variation modulo d.\nTherefore, while n remains in the same H^k guard and tends to infinity,\nthe source residue can be prescribed arbitrarily.\n\nThe rt-block path contribution gives\n\n    V(n) >= alpha^(-qk) V(H^k(n)).\n\nIf a prescribed source residue r had s_r>s_(z_* mod d), the right side\nwould exceed V(n) by a positive power of n along that progression. Hence\ns_r\u003c=s_(z_* mod d) for every r. Together with (RE2), this gives\n\n    s_(z_* mod d)=S.\n\nNow prescribe source residue 1 in the same H^k guards. For each fixed k,\nlet n tend to infinity and use the affine slow variation. The resulting\ninequality has the form\n\n    1 >= C alpha^((S-q)k)\n\nwith C>0 independent of k up to the fixed residue multipliers. Letting k\ngrow forces\n\n    S>=q.                                         (RE3)\n\nNo common exponent has been assumed; the source-residue variation is what\nmakes the H path give (RE3).\n\n## 4. What is forced, and the guard-overlap limit\n\nPut b=Phi_M(1)=7/16 modulo d. The M branch gives s_1\u003c=s_b, while applying\nPhi_L repeatedly to b and using (RE1) gives s_b\u003c=s_1. Thus both residues\n1 and b have exponent S. The H and L arguments give the exact intermediate\nconclusion\n\n    max_r s_r = s_1 = s_(z_* mod d) >= q.             (RE4)\n\nFor h=1, there is an immediate strict contradiction: a terminal progression\nn=19 modulo 27 has residue 1 modulo 3, and both L and M have source residue\n1 modulo 3. At S=q the M contribution is asymptotically 1 (same source and\nterminal coefficient), while the L contribution is strictly positive; for\nS>q either contribution already exceeds 1.\n\nFor h=2, take n=1+9j with j=5 modulo 9, so n=46 modulo 81. The L source is\n61 modulo 9, namely 7 modulo 9, while the M source is 163 modulo 9, namely\n1 modulo 9. The residue 7 is z_* modulo 9 and both source exponents are S.\nAgain the M term is asymptotically 1 at S=q and the L term is positive, so\nthe superharmonic inequality fails.\n\nFor h>=3, the preceding data do not justify a general contradiction. The\nhigh 3-adic guards for two residue paths with terminal class 1 can be\ndisjoint. In particular, the false shortcut would be to use n=1 modulo\n3^(h+2) and claim that both an all-L path and an L^h-then-M path have source\nresidue 1: the latter source is 3+32*4^h u modulo 3^h, while the all-L source\nneed not be 1 for the same u. A locally constant leading profile can therefore\nplace lower exponents on the other M preimage classes without violating the\nsingle-branch comparisons established above. No all-h no-go is claimed.\n\n## Intermediate verdict before sections5--6\n\nFor every h>=1, the residue-dependent ansatz forces the exact restriction\n(RE4); it is ruled out by this argument for h=1 and h=2. For h>=3 the\nargument leaves a genuine guard-overlap problem: whether two max-exponent\npaths can be made simultaneously available at one terminal progression.\nThe h=0 common-exponent case is the earlier potential obstruction. None of\nthese results covers valuation factors, different slow scales by residue,\nunbounded residue precision, infinitely many residue centres, fully\nirregular potentials, or the full Collatz conjecture.\n\n## 5. Strengthening: the maximal exponent is exactly q\n\nThe inequality S>=q in (RE3) can be sharpened to equality. Take\nn=1+3^(h+1)t and let t tend to infinity. Then n and\nF_L(n)=1+4*3^h t are both 1 modulo 3^h. The single L term in\nL_qV\u003c=V therefore gives\n\n    1 >= (4/3)^(s_1-q).\n\nSince S=s_1 and (RE3) gives S>=q, this proves\n\n    s_r\u003c=s_1=q for every r,\n    s_(z_* mod 3^h)=s_1=q.                         (RE5)\n\nThere is also a coefficient restriction on the max-exponent profile. For\nany residue r with s_r=q, the L^h path from terminal residue 1 to source r\nis available on a suitable high guard. Its asymptotic contribution gives\nc_r\u003c=c_1. Conversely, the H^k path from terminal z_* to source residue 1\nhas equal exponent q and gives c_1\u003c=c_(z_* mod 3^h). Since z_* is a\nmax-exponent residue, c_(z_* mod 3^h)\u003c=c_1, hence\n\n    c_(z_* mod 3^h)=c_1.                            (RE6)\n\nMore generally, on max-exponent residues the forward maps\nPhi_L and Phi_M make coefficients nondecreasing: if s_r=s_(Phi_F(r))=q,\nthen the single F branch gives c_r\u003c=c_(Phi_F(r)). The L dynamics sends every\nresidue to 1 modulo 3^h, so all max-class coefficients are bounded above by\nc_1; the H comparison identifies the z_* coefficient with c_1. These are\nlocal profile restrictions only. At this intermediate step, h>=3 retains a\nhigh-precision guard-overlap problem, so no broader no-go is claimed.\n\n## 6. Exact additional contradiction for h=3\n\nFor d=27, the fixed point satisfies z_* congruent to 16 modulo 27. The L\nresidue map gives\n\n    Phi_L(16)=19,       Phi_L(19)=1       modulo 27.\n\nBy (RE5)--(RE6) and the coefficient monotonicity, residues 16 and 19 both\nhave exponent q and coefficient c_1: residue 16 is z_*, its coefficient is\nc_1, while 16 -> 19 -> 1 forces the same coefficient at 19.\n\nTake the common terminal progression\n\n    n=73+243u.\n\nThen n is 19 modulo 27, and the two exact inverse branches give\n\n    F_L(n)=97+324u =16 modulo 27,\n    F_M(n)=259+864u=16 modulo 27.\n\nBoth guards hold, and all values are positive for large u. Thus the L and M\nterms in L_qV(n)/V(n) both have limiting value 1: their source and terminal\nexponents are q, their coefficient ratio is c_1/c_1, and the affine slowly\nvarying factor cancels. Their sum tends to 2, contradicting\nL_qV\u003c=V. Therefore the residue-power ansatz is impossible for h=3 as well.\nNo claim is made here for h>=4; the high-precision guard-overlap problem\nremains open in this bounded analysis.\n","FOLLOWUP_0485_VARIABLE_PAIR_LIFTS.md":"# Follow-up 0485 — variable ordered pair lifts and prefix integrality\n\n18 September 2026 (Asia/Tashkent). Manual algebra only: no mathematical\nprograms, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or new agents. The issue is a finite\nsequence of different structural ordered gap-one inverse word pairs, not a\nperiodic schedule.\n\n## 1. One structural ordered pair\n\nLet pair \\(i\\) have a left inverse word\n\\[\n F_i(x)=\\frac{2^{K_i}x-C_i}{3^{P_i}},\n \\qquad K_i\\ge0,\\ P_i\\ge0.\n\\tag{VL1}\n\\]\nThe right inverse word has the same \\(K_i,P_i\\) and an intercept \\(C_i^R\\).\nIt preserves ordered gap one exactly when\n\\[\n C_i^R=C_i+2^{K_i}-3^{P_i},\n\\tag{VL2}\n\\]\nbecause then\n\\[\n \\frac{2^{K_i}(x+1)-C_i^R}{3^{P_i}}\n =F_i(x)+1.\n\\]\nThe word pair is called structural only when its two complete parity words\nreally exist, together with their admissible parity cylinders. Equation\n(VL2) is merely the affine gap condition; it does not by itself certify a\nword, positivity, or a first-hit label.\n\nGiven an ordered list \\(i_1,\\ldots,i_s\\), apply \\(F_{i_1}\\) first, then\n\\(F_{i_2}\\), and so on. Put\n\\[\n K_r=\\sum_{q=1}^rK_{i_q},\\qquad\n P_r=\\sum_{q=1}^rP_{i_q}.\n\\]\nThe \\(r\\)-prefix composition is\n\\[\n F^{(r)}(x)=\\frac{2^{K_r}x-C_r}{3^{P_r}},\n\\tag{VL3}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n C_r=\\sum_{q=1}^r\n 2^{K_r-K_q}3^{P_{q-1}}C_{i_q},\n \\qquad K_0=P_0=0.\n\\tag{VL4}\n\\]\nIn particular, the full composition has \\(K_*=K_s,P_*=P_s,C_*=C_s\\).\nIts right endpoint is \\(F^{(s)}(x)+1\\), by repeated use of (VL2).\n\n## 2. Exact criterion for all prefix integers\n\nFor a terminal integer \\(n\\), define \\(x_r=F^{(r)}(n)\\). The exact\naffine integrality conditions are\n\\[\n 3^{P_r}\\mid 2^{K_r}n-C_r,\\qquad 1\\le r\\le s.\n\\tag{VL5}\n\\]\nA useful strengthening is that for any composition of these rational affine\nmaps with integer intercepts (word genuineness is not needed), the conditions are nested: final\ndivisibility alone is equivalent to all-prefix divisibility. Indeed, for\nevery \\(r\u003cs\\), write the tail composition as\n\\[\n F_{i_s}\\circ\\cdots\\circ F_{i_{r+1}}(x)\n =\\frac{2^{K_s-K_r}x-D_r}{3^{P_s-P_r}}\n\\]\nwith an integer \\(D_r\\). Then\n\\[\n 2^{K_s}n-C_s\n =2^{K_s-K_r}(2^{K_r}n-C_r)-3^{P_r}D_r.\n\\tag{VL6}\n\\]\nIf the left side is divisible by \\(3^{P_s}\\), it is divisible by\n\\(3^{P_r}\\); since \\(2^{K_s-K_r}\\) is a \\(3\\)-adic unit, (VL6) forces\n\\(3^{P_r}\\mid2^{K_r}n-C_r\\). The converse is immediate by composing\ninteger affine maps. Thus\n\\[\n 3^{P_s}\\mid2^{K_s}n-C_s\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\text{all conditions in (VL5)}.\n\\tag{VL7}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, each prefix prescribes a compatible \\(3\\)-adic residue\n\\[\n n\\equiv 2^{-K_r}C_r\\pmod{3^{P_r}},\n\\]\nand the final residue automatically projects to every earlier one. This\nequivalence is specific to the composed affine maps; a lone final\ncongruence from an unrelated affine formula need not certify its alleged\nprefixes.\n\n## 3. Parity cylinders, positivity, and first-hit killing\n\nThese are logically distinct from the \\(3\\)-adic divisibility test.\n\nFor the left word \\(i_r\\), let \\(\\rho_{i_r}\\pmod{2^{K_{i_r}}}\\) be its exact\nsource parity cylinder. At stage \\(r\\), the source \\(x_r\\) must satisfy\n\\[\n x_r\\equiv\\rho_{i_r}\\pmod{2^{K_{i_r}}}.\n\\tag{VL8}\n\\]\nThe concatenated left words give one final cylinder for the final source\n\\(x_s\\), not for the terminal seed \\(n\\):\n\\[\n x_s\\equiv\\rho_{\\mathbf i}\\pmod{2^{K_s}}.\n\\tag{VL9}\n\\]\nThere is an analogous right cylinder, and (VL2) requires the two stage\ncylinders to be compatible as an ordered pair. For genuine complete\nshortcut words, a positive integer inverse satisfying (VL5) automatically\npasses the reverse parity tests: the final \\(3\\)-divisibility can be peeled\nback one odd inverse step at a time, and the concatenated word then supplies\n(VL9). For arbitrary affine constants, however, (VL8)--(VL9) are additional\nguards and cannot be inferred from a formal rational identity.\n\nOne must also retain\n\\[\n x_r>0,\\qquad x_r+1>0\n\\tag{VL10}\n\\]\nat every stage, together with the first-hit killing condition: the stage\nsource and its internal word path must not have already hit the target\nlabelled for that stage. For genuine complete words and a positive terminal\ninteger, reverse steps preserve positivity once the required divisibility\nholds; the killing condition remains a separate label restriction. Thus\n(VL5), parity cylinders, positivity, and first-hit status should be recorded\nseparately even where some are redundant under certified word hypotheses.\n\n## 4. Separate terminal-seed and source-output bounds\n\nFor a fixed sequence, (VL7) gives one terminal-seed class modulo\n\\(3^{P_s}\\). Therefore, before positivity and first-hit killing,\n\\[\n \\#\\{1\\le n\\le X:\\text{this sequence is integrally valid}\\}\n \\le \\frac{X}{3^{P_s}}+1.\n\\tag{VL11}\n\\]\nThe dyadic cylinder applies separately to the final source. If instead the\nsource bound is \\(1\\le x_s\\le X\\), then (VL9) gives\n\\[\n \\#\\{1\\le x_s\\le X:\\text{this sequence is dyadically valid}\\}\n \\le \\frac{X}{2^{K_s}}+1.\n\\tag{VL12}\n\\]\nThe affine relation between the two variables is\n\\[\n n=\\frac{3^{P_s}x_s+C_s}{2^{K_s}},\n\\tag{VL13}\n\\]\nso a source interval \\(x_s\\le X\\) becomes the terminal interval\n\\[\n n\\le\\frac{3^{P_s}X+C_s}{2^{K_s}},\n\\tag{VL14}\n\\]\nwith positivity and killing still imposed separately. There is generally no\nCRT product modulus \\(2^{K_s}3^{P_s}\\) for the terminal seed \\(n\\).\n\nThe elementary lift \\(L(n)=(4n-1)/3\\) makes this distinction explicit:\nintegral terminal seeds are all \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), while\n\\(x=L(n)\\equiv1\\pmod4\\). Explicitly, \\(n=1+3t\\) gives \\(x=1+4t\\), so\nthe two congruences belong to different variables; the terminal seed is not\nfixed modulo \\(4\\).\nFor a certified nontrivial word pair, \\(P_s\\ge1\\) unless every word is\nall-even; the all-even rule preserves gap one only in the empty case.\nThus repeated structural lifts make a single sequence increasingly sparse\nin the terminal seed variable through \\(3^{P_s}\\), and separately sparse in\nthe final source variable through \\(2^{K_s}\\).\n\nThis is the useful obstruction to a false transfer from one fixed lift:\n(VL11) controls terminal seeds for one prescribed variable sequence, while\n(VL12) controls its final sources. Neither bounds the union over variable\nsequences. If \\(\\mathscr S_s\\) is a family of \\(s\\)-step sequences, the\nimmediate terminal-seed union estimate is\n\\[\n \\#\\bigcup_{\\sigma\\in\\mathscr S_s}E_\\sigma(X)\n \\le \\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\mathscr S_s}\n       \\left(\\frac{X}{3^{P_\\sigma}}+1\\right),\n\\tag{VL15}\n\\]\nand this is useful only with an independent bound on the number and overlap\nof admissible sequences. Different sequences can have different \\(3\\)-adic classes,\nor can coincide on the same class. No \\(2^s\\) global bound follows merely\nfrom composing \\(s\\) affine lifts.\n\nThere is, however, a finite-alphabet terminal-seed consequence. Suppose the\nallowed nonidentity rules form a finite alphabet \\(\\mathscr A\\), each with\n\\(P_i\\ge1\\), and\n\\[\n \\rho=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathscr A}3^{-P_i}\u003c1.\n\\tag{VL16}\n\\]\nThe union of all length-\\(s\\) schedules that pass affine integrality has\nupper natural density at most\n\\[\n \\limsup_{X\\to\\infty}\\frac1X\n \\#\\bigcup_{\\sigma\\in\\mathscr A^s}E_\\sigma(X)\n \\le \\rho^s.\n\\tag{VL17}\n\\]\nIndeed, sum (VL11) over schedules and then let \\(X\\to\\infty\\); the\n\\(|\\mathscr A|^s\\) additive terms disappear for fixed \\(s\\). Consequently,\nthe terminal seeds supporting every length \\(s\\) have upper natural density\nat most \\(\\rho^s\\) for every fixed \\(s\\). Since the prefix tree has finite\nbranching, König's lemma identifies the intersection over all \\(s\\) with\nseeds supporting an infinite schedule; that set therefore has density zero.\nThis is a terminal-seed statement with\nthe order of limits \\(X\\to\\infty\\) first and \\(s\\to\\infty\\) second; it is\nnot a finite-\\(X\\) cutoff and does not bound source outputs generated from\none fixed seed.\n\n## 5. The compatible \\(3\\)-adic seed of a variable infinite schedule\n\nThe prefix congruences have an exact limit description. Put\n\\[\n A_r=K_r,\\qquad B_r=P_r.\n\\]\nFrom (VL4),\n\\[\n 2^{-A_s}C_s\n =\\sum_{q=1}^s C_{i_q}\\,\n     3^{B_{q-1}}2^{-A_q}\n \\pmod{3^{B_s}}.\n\\tag{VL18}\n\\]\nIf \\(B_s\\to\\infty\\), the right side is a compatible sequence of\n\\(3\\)-adic residues and defines\n\\[\n r_\\infty=\\sum_{q\\ge1}C_{i_q}\\,\n     3^{B_{q-1}}2^{-A_q}\\in\\mathbb Z_3.\n\\tag{VL19}\n\\]\nA fixed positive integer terminal seed \\(n\\) can support the entire\ninfinite schedule only if its \\(3\\)-adic embedding equals \\(r_\\infty\\).\nThis condition may hold for specially designed schedules, but it neither\nexcludes positive integers nor gives a density estimate. Under the genuine\npositive-word hypotheses of §3, parity and positivity are already forced by\nthe admissibility condition; first-hit/killing remains the independent\nfilter. If the affine constants are treated formally without those word\nhypotheses, the parity, positivity, and killing guards must all be retained.\n\n## 6. What this says about variable lifts\n\nFor a fixed finite sequence, (VL7), (VL9), and the separate bounds\n(VL11)--(VL14) give a complete, checkable criterion. The distinction matters:\nformal affine integrality is a \\(3\\)-adic condition; exact parity cylinders\nare \\(2\\)-adic word conditions; positivity and first-hit status are\ndynamical conditions.\n\nThe fixed-periodic repetition budget in FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md\ntherefore does not automatically extend to arbitrary switching. It does,\nhowever, identify the missing input for any such extension: a bound on the\nnumber, overlap, or total \\(3\\)-adic residue mass of admissible variable sequences. Without\nthat input, one fixed start may lie on many compatible sequence branches,\nwhile each branch separately remains sparse.\n\nFor genuine positive words, the final \\(3\\)-divisibility test in (VL7)\ntherefore already supplies the exact parity and positivity requirements;\nonly first-hit/killing remains to be imposed. No positive-density theorem,\ncounterexample to Collatz, or novelty claim is made.\n\n\n## 7. Independent audit of SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md\n\nThis audit concerns only the finite structural alphabet with at most two odd\nsteps per word. It is a stronger fixed-seed output statement than the\nterminal-seed density estimate in §4, and it has a different quantifier. It\ndoes not classify words with \\(P\\ge3\\).\n\n### 7.1 Exact admissibility and the seed/source distinction\n\nFor a complete word \\(w\\) of length \\(K\\), odd count \\(P\\), and constant\n\\(C_w\\), the forward identity is\n\\[\n T_w(x)=\\frac{3^P x+C_w}{2^K}.\n\\]\nIf \\(x=F(n)=(2^K n-C_w)/3^P\\) is an integer, the first-bit induction in\nSMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md is correct. For an initial even bit,\n\\(C_w=2C_{\\rm tail}\\), so the numerator forces \\(x\\) even and division by\n\\(2\\) gives the tail equation. For an initial odd bit,\n\\(C_w=3^{P-1}+2C_{\\rm tail}\\), so \\(x\\) is odd and\n\\(z=(3x+1)/2\\) satisfies the tail equation. Thus, for a genuine word,\n\\[\n n\\ge1,\\qquad 3^P\\mid 2^K n-C_w\n\\tag{SA1}\n\\]\nare sufficient for the exact parity word and positivity as well as\nnecessary. Positivity follows because \\(T\\) maps every nonpositive integer\nto a nonpositive integer, so a positive terminal \\(n\\) cannot have a\nnonpositive source.\n\nThe dyadic condition is on the source\n\\[\n x=F(n)\\equiv-C_w3^{-P}\\pmod{2^K},\n\\tag{SA2}\n\\]\nnot on the terminal seed \\(n\\). The first-hit condition is a separate\nrestriction: it removes candidate sources whose internal path has an earlier\nhit, and cannot add any source to the set counted by the structural maps.\n\n### 7.2 Complete \\(P\\le2\\) classification\n\nThe classification in the root report checks out. For \\(P=0\\), the only\ngap-one rule is the empty identity. For \\(P=1\\), the constants are\n\\(2^i\\). The required difference is \\(2^K-3\\), forcing the smaller constant\nto be \\(1\\), then\n\\[\n 2^j=2^K-2.\n\\]\nIts 2-adic valuation forces \\(j=1\\) and \\(K=2\\). Hence the sole\nnonidentity pair is \\(OE,EO\\), with\n\\[\n L(n)=\\frac{4n-1}{3},\\qquad n\\equiv1\\pmod3.\n\\tag{SA3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(P=2\\), constants have the form \\(3\\cdot2^i+2^j\\), \\(i\u003cj\\).\nThe cases \\(K=2,3\\) have no difference \\(2^K-9\\). For \\(K\\ge4\\), parity\nand the size bound for an odd right constant force the left constant to\nbe \\(3+2^j\\): otherwise \\(C_R-C_L\\le2^{K-1}-7\u003c2^K-9\\).\nIf \\(j=1\\), the valuation of the\nright constant forces \\(K=5\\), yielding\n\\[\n M(n)=\\frac{32n-5}{9},\\qquad n\\equiv1\\pmod9,\n\\tag{SA4}\n\\]\nwith words \\(OOEEE,EEOEO\\). If \\(j\\ge2\\), valuation comparison forces\n\\(j=2,\\ell=3,K=4\\), yielding \\(L^2(n)=(16n-7)/9\\), with words\n\\(OEOE,EOEO\\). Therefore the complete list in this scope is\nidentity, \\(L\\), \\(L^2\\), and \\(M\\); no conclusion about arbitrary \\(P\\)\nfollows.\n\nThe extra rule \\(M\\) is verified by\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n 32m+3&\\to48m+5\\to72m+8\\to36m+4\\to18m+2\\to9m+1,\\\\\n 32m+4&\\to16m+2\\to8m+1\\to12m+2\\to6m+1\\to9m+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe right inverse word has a different constant:\n\\(G(n+1)=(32(n+1)-28)/9=M(n)+1\\). Applying \\(M\\) itself to\n\\(n+1\\) would not describe this right inverse word. Root clarified this\nnotation and the size argument above after reading the final audit.\n\n### 7.3 Modulo-27 pruning and finite branching\n\nThe domain conditions are \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) for \\(L\\) and\n\\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\) for \\(M,L^2\\). For \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod9\\), the three\nclasses modulo \\(27\\) give the following robust continuation rules:\n\n- \\(n\\equiv1\\pmod{27}\\): \\(M(n)\\equiv0\\pmod3\\), while \\(L\\) remains in\n  the \\(1\\pmod9\\) domain;\n- \\(n\\equiv10\\pmod{27}\\): \\(L(n)\\equiv4\\pmod9\\), so its next \\(L\\)-step\n  is \\(2\\pmod3\\), and \\(M(n)\\equiv2\\pmod3\\); all branches terminate\n  within the stated two-step allowance;\n- \\(n\\equiv19\\pmod{27}\\): \\(L(n)\\equiv7\\pmod9\\), so the next \\(L\\)-step\n  is \\(0\\pmod3\\), whereas \\(M(n)\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), leaving \\(M\\) as the\n  only branch that can continue.\n\nFor \\(n\\equiv4,7\\pmod9\\), only \\(L\\) is available and its image is,\nrespectively, \\(2,0\\pmod3\\). The identity and \\(L^2\\) add no new branch,\nand \\(M(n)=2L^2(n)+1\\) wherever the latter is defined. Hence the\nmodulo-27 argument gives at most one continuing chain; the remaining\nbranches have uniformly bounded length, with the class-10 terminal state\naccounting for at most three extra descendants. This proves finite\nbranching, but does not prove that the continuing chain is infinite.\n\n### 7.4 Fixed-seed logarithmic output bound\n\nLet \\(A(n_0)\\) be the distinct positive source coordinates reachable from\none fixed seed using this classified alphabet. At \\(n_0=1\\),\n\\(L(1)=1\\), \\(M(1)=3\\), and \\(3\\) is terminal, so\n\\[\n A(1)=\\{1,3\\}.\n\\tag{SA5}\n\\]\nFor \\(n_0>1\\), along the selected continuing chain,\n\\[\n L(n)-1=\\frac43(n-1),\\qquad\n M(n)-1=\\frac{32}{9}(n-1)+2\\ge\\frac43(n-1).\n\\tag{SA6}\n\\]\nThus the number \\(B_X\\) of chain nodes at most \\(X\\) satisfies\n\\[\n B_X\\le1+\\left\\lfloor\n \\log_{4/3}\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\tag{SA7}\n\\]\nEvery chain node has at most two off-chain descendants, except a possible\nterminal class-10 node with at most three. Therefore\n\\[\n \\#(A(n_0)\\cap[1,X])\n \\le4+3\\left\\lfloor\n \\log_{4/3}\\frac{X-1}{n_0-1}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\tag{SA8}\n\\]\nThis is a logarithmic bound on distinct source/output coordinates generated\nfrom one fixed seed, not a bound on terminal seeds in an interval and not a\nstatement about all structural words. Imposing first-hit labels only takes a\nsubset and preserves (SA8). The finite alphabet and the restriction\n\\(P\\le2\\) are essential; this audit supplies no arbitrary-\\(P\\) conclusion.\n","FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md":"# Целая ветвь при полной точности совпадения\n\n16 сентября2026. Кандидат координатора проверен **0221 и 0222**.\nКоординатор полностью прочитал\nоба отчёта и проверил вычисления вручную. Математических исполнений —0.\n\nПусть ξ=a/b∈Z₂, b>0 нечётно, U — настоящий префикс длины i с S\nединицами, W — непустое слово длины q с R единицами. Обозначим\n\n\\[\n c=C_W,\\quad k=2^q-3^R,\\quad A=2^ic-kC_U,\n \\quad r=\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)=\\frac{A}{3^S k}.\n\\]\n\nПредположим **полное совпадение** кода ξ и UW∞ на первых\nN=i+q+L символах, L≥0, и ξ≠r. Последнее следует, например, из\nнепериодичности исходного кода. При k\u003c0 достаточно ξ≥0 в обычном\nпорядке рациональных чисел: A>0 и r\u003c0.\n\nТогда для любого знака k\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{3^S\\mid A\\ \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2^{S+L}\\le(|a|+b)\\max\\{1,(3/2)^R-1\\}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nПри k\u003c0 максимум с единицей можно снять:\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{3^S\\mid A\\ \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2^{S+L}\\le(|a|+b)((3/2)^R-1).}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЭто отличается от [прежнего ограничения](INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md):\nтам W могло быть произвольным, здесь требуется настоящее совпадение\nдо N. Именно оно позволяет использовать делимость на 2^N вместо 2^i.\n\n## Доказательство\n\nk нечётно и ненулево. На ветви 3^S|A положим z=A/3^S∈Z.\nИзометрия кодирования и нечётность bk дают\n\n\\[\n 2^N\\mid ka-bz\\ne0.\n\\]\n\nТочная граница конечного слова\nC_U≤2^(i−S)(3^S−2^S) включает S=0. Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n 1&\\le |ka-bz|2^{-N}\\\\\n &\\le 2^{-L}\\left[\n |a|\\frac{|k|}{2^q}2^{-i}\n +b\\left\\{\\frac c{2^q}3^{-S}\n +\\frac{|k|}{2^q}(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\\right\\}\\right].\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nПри B_R=max(1,(3/2)^R−1) имеем c/2^q≤B_R и |k|/2^q≤B_R:\nпервая оценка следует из c≤2^(q−R)(3^R−2^R), вторая — из q≥R.\nПоскольку i≥S, правая часть не больше\n(|a|+b)B_R2^(−S−L), что доказывает(1).\nПри k\u003c0 обе дроби ограничены самим (3/2)^R−1, что даёт(2).\n\n## Предельное следствие и граница высот\n\nДля одной фиксированной ξ на семье совпадений, если\n\n\\[\n S+L\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n \\limsup\\frac{R}{S+L}\u003c\\frac{\\log2}{\\log(3/2)},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nто ветвь 3^S|A невозможна на хвосте: B_R≤(3/2)^R, и(1)\nпротиворечит строгой линейной разнице логарифмов. Для A≠0 получаем\nv₃(A)\u003cS. Равенство в пороге отдельно не разрешено.\n\nПри k\u003c0 и S≥1 [известная оценка Бейкера](BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md)\nпосле этого даёт log(h/A#)=O(log(q+1)), а при q\u003cN — o(N).\nСкорость орбиты и фиксированный зазор степеней не нужны.\nПри k>0 остаётся обычная отмена в A; это сравнение высот без\nдополнительной нижней оценки |A| не переносится.\n\nЕсли k\u003c0,R≤S, дополнительно верна q-зависимая граница\n2^(N−i+S)≤(|a|+b)3^R. Она вместе с q≥R даёт\nSlog(4/3)+Llog2≤log(|a|+b), исключая такую целую ветвь при S→∞.\nЭта дополнительная граница и(2) оцениваются по конкретным параметрам;\nуниверсальное превосходство одной над другой не утверждается.\n\n## Края\n\nЕсли U содержит первую единицу в позиции t₀\u003ci, то v₂(C_U)=t₀,\nследовательно v₂(A)=t₀ и A≠0. Поэтому A=0 ровно при полностью\nнулевых U,W. Тогда r=0, а ξ≠r требует a≠0; полное совпадение\nдаёт N≤v₂(a). На N→∞ этот случай исключён отдельно, без определения\nv₃(0) или A# для нуля. При k\u003c0 нуль A невозможен.\n\nS=0 и R=0 допустимы в(1); при k\u003c0 обязательно R≥1.\nУсловие ξ≠r нельзя удалять для отрицательных периодических стартов:\nU=11,W=00001 дают k=29,A=−81,S=2,z=−9,r=−9/29, и при ξ=r\nнужное кратное равно нулю. Рациональность сама по себе не означает\nни равенства с этим конкретным r, ни периодичности произвольного кода.\n\nНи существование семейств с(3), ни общая положительная маржа не доказаны.\nЭто необходимое ограничение совместимых приближений, а не решение Коллатца.\nМировой приоритет конкретной записи не установлен.\n\n[Приёмка0221/0222](REVIEW_0221_0222.md) · [0221](task-0221.md) · [0222](task-0222.md).\n","GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md":"# GB/IR — fixed-odd-count finiteness, first structural returns, and their limits\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual candidate and\naudit record. No mathematical programs, numerical enumeration, CAS or Lean.\nThe objects are synchronous affine word pairs, not all pairs of Collatz\npredecessors. The full conjecture is not proved. World priority is unestablished.\n\n## GB1. Words, signed adjacency, and compatible sources\n\nUse T(x)=x/2 for even x, and T(x)=(3x+1)/2 for odd x. A word of\nlength K with P odd positions i_1\u003c...\u003ci_P has intercept\n\n    C=sum_(r=1)^P 3^(P-r) 2^(i_r),\n    T_w(x)=(3^P x+C)/2^K.\n\nConsider two words of the same K and P such that their terminal\nright-minus-left difference on (x,x+1) is identically epsilon, where\nepsilon is +1 or -1. This is equivalent to\n\n    3^P+C_R-C_L=epsilon*2^K.             (GB1)\n\nEach word fixes one source class modulo 2^K. The left class makes its\nterminal value integral; (GB1) makes the right value integral too.\nThe first-bit integrality induction supplies both prescribed actual words.\nChoose a sufficiently large positive source in that class to obtain a\ncommon positive integer pair with both genuine trajectories. Thus the\ncommon-source requirement is justified, not silently imposed on arbitrary\nunrelated formal words. The previous\n[integral-inverse lemma](SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md) is a named dependency.\n\nEvery nonempty such pair has P>=1. A zero-odd pair would begin EE,\nwhich adjacent integer sources cannot do. P=0 only allows the empty\nordered identity, excluded below.\n\n## GB2. An integer carry that never vanishes\n\nLet p_L(k),p_R(k) be prefix odd counts through positions0,...,k-1,\nand C_L(k),C_R(k) the corresponding prefix intercepts. Define\n\n    D_k=[3^P+3^(P-p_R(k)) C_R(k)\n               -3^(P-p_L(k)) C_L(k)]/2^k.\n\nSplitting each full intercept into prefix and suffix gives\n\n    C_side=3^(P-p_side(k)) C_side(k)+2^k C_suffix.\n\nConsequently GB1 implies D_k is an integer, with D_0=3^P and\nD_K=epsilon. If p_L(k)!=p_R(k), the numerator has a unique\nterm of smallest 3-adic order P-max(p_L,p_R). Indeed a nonempty\nodd-prefix intercept is a 3-adic unit: its last odd contribution is\na power of2 and all earlier contributions have a factor3. For p=0\nthe intercept is0, assigned valuation +infinity. Thus D_k cannot vanish\nwhen the prefix counts differ.\n\nIf p_L(k)=p_R(k)=p, then D_k=0 would imply\n\n    3^p+C_R(k)-C_L(k)=0.\n\nThe actual integer prefix trajectories would then coalesce. Determinism\nforces all their later states to coincide, contrary to terminal difference\nepsilon. Therefore D_k!=0 for every k, in either signed orientation.\n\n## GB3. Effective length bound for every fixed P\n\nAt a step k set h_side(k)=0 for an E bit, and\nh_side(k)=3^(P-p_side(k)-1) for an O bit. In the latter case\np_side(k)\u003c=P-1; no negative power is used for a zero increment.\nDirect prefix algebra gives\n\n    D_(k+1)=[D_k+h_R(k)-h_L(k)]/2.\n\nEach h is in [0,3^(P-1)], so |h_R-h_L|\u003c=3^(P-1).\nStarting from D_0=3^P, induction yields\n\n    0\u003c|D_k|\u003c=3^P.\n\nA run of g consecutive EE positions halves this nonzero integer g times.\nTherefore 2^g divides the carry at the start of the run, and\n\n    g\u003c=floor(P log_2 3).\n\nThere are at most 2P positions containing at least one O. Position0\nis such an event, since the two sources have opposite parity. Decompose\nthe whole word into event positions followed by their EE runs, including\nthe possibly empty final run. This proves\n\n    K\u003c=2P[1+floor(P log_2 3)].             (GB2)\n\nThus for each fixed P there are only finitely many signed structural\npairs, with an explicit length bound. This is a quadratic upper bound,\nnot the stronger conjectural K\u003c=3P. It neither classifies all pairs at\ngeneral P nor controls their aggregate number as P tends to infinity.\nIt does not cover unequal stopping depths or unequal total odd counts.\n\n## IR1. Which return events admit a structural factorization\n\nAn event used here must have BOTH equal prefix odd counts and ordered\nadjacency of the two prefix endpoints. At equal counts their difference\nis independent of the source variable, so an actual gap1 proves the\naffine structural identity on the whole cylinder. Define an irreducible\nordered structural block by the absence of a proper positive-time event\nof this specified kind. The empty identity is excluded.\n\nEvery finite ordered structural block factors uniquely at its earliest\nsuch event and then recursively. A cut subtracts the same odd count\nfrom both sides, and the remaining words form another ordered structural\nblock. The first available cut is uniquely determined by the word pair.\nThis yields one global countable alphabet B of irreducible ordered blocks;\nthe allowed inverse branches at a terminal seed vary according to their\nguards, but the alphabet itself is fixed.\n\nAn actual adjacency event alone is insufficient. For example the first\npositive-time ordered adjacency of (6,7) is\n\n    (6,7) -> (3,11) -> (5,17) -> (8,26) -> (4,13)\n          -> (2,20) -> (1,10) -> (2,5) -> (1,8) -> (2,4) -> (1,2).\n\nThe two ten-step words have respectively4 and5 odd steps. This event\ndoes not give a common-P affine structural block. Hence the factorization\nabove does not assert coverage of every actual adjacent-pair path.\n\n## IR2. Prefix-free source cylinders and a strict mass loss\n\nFor each i in B let K_i,P_i,C_i be its left data and let U_i be\nits source cylinder in Z_2. Then mu_2(U_i)=2^(-K_i). These cylinders\nare pairwise disjoint. If two intersect, the shorter left parity word\nis a prefix of the longer one. The right prefix is also determined by\nthe same adjacent source cylinder. The shorter structural return would\nthen violate irreducibility of the longer block.\n\nThere is an explicit omitted cylinder. For x=16m+2, the trajectories are\n\n    16m+2 -> 8m+1 -> 12m+2 -> 6m+1 -> 9m+2,\n    16m+3 -> 24m+5 -> 36m+8 -> 18m+4 -> 9m+2.\n\nThe first three prefix-count pairs are (0,1),(1,2),(1,2), so none\nis a structural signed-adjacency event. At time4 the trajectories\ncoalesce, precluding every subsequent nonzero gap. Thus no U_i meets\nthe cylinder x=2 modulo16. One can justify the cylinder claim using\npositive representatives of any proposed nonempty dyadic intersection;\nno assertion about individual exceptional 2-adic adjacency times is needed.\n\nCountable additivity gives the strict Kraft bound\n\n    kappa=sum_(i in B) 2^(-K_i) \u003c=15/16.  (IR1)\n\nThe same omitted cylinder works if first structural returns of either\nsign are used, but the transfer below is defined on the ordered alphabet.\n\n## IR3. Exact integrated transfer for the full ordered alphabet\n\nFor i in B, let\n\n    F_i(n)=(2^K_i n-C_i)/3^P_i,\n    V_i={n in Z_3: 2^K_i n=C_i modulo 3^P_i},\n    alpha_i=2^K_i/3^P_i.\n\nThe set V_i is one cylinder of measure 3^(-P_i), and F_i maps it\nbijectively onto Z_3. For nonnegative measurable f define\n\n    (L f)(n)=sum_i alpha_i^(-1) 1_(V_i)(n) f(F_i(n)).\n\nHaar change of variables, followed by monotone summation, gives\n\n    integral_(Z_3) Lf dmu_3 = kappa integral_(Z_3) f dmu_3.\n\nTherefore the nonnegative resolvent satisfies\n\n    integral_(Z_3) sum_(s>=0) L^s 1 dmu_3\n       =1/(1-kappa)\u003c=16.                 (IR2)\n\nIts value at n is the total reciprocal-slope weight of all finite\nadmissible inverse schedules from n. It is finite for Haar-almost every\n3-adic n. This is an arbitrary-P statement, without a finite alphabet\nassumption. It does not give finiteness at any prescribed positive integer:\nall positive integers together are a countable Haar-null subset of Z_3.\nIt also does not directly count source coordinates of size at most X.\n\n## IR4. An ordered inverse contraction from a known reversing string\n\nThe pair q=01111100011, q'=11111101000 is listed in LaTourette's\n2007 Table4.2. Root read the [primary thesis copy](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college).\nIt is a reversing string, distinct from the ordered blocks of Table4.1.\nThe following computations are manual verifications and consequences,\nnot claims of new example priority.\n\nBoth words have K=11,P=7. Their odd-position sums are\n\n    C_q=1458+972+648+432+288+1536+1024=6358,\n    C_q'=729+486+324+216+144+96+128=2123.\n\nTheir difference6358-2123=4235=2048+2187 proves reversed adjacency.\nFrom the lower terminal n, the left source is\n\n    S(n)=(2048n-4310)/2187,\n    n=205 modulo2187.\n\nIts exact progressions are sources2048m+190,2048m+191 and reversed\ntargets2187m+206,2187m+205. Composing two such reversals gives ordered\nforward words qq' and q'q, with K=22,P=14. The forward constants are\n\n    C_L=2187*6358+2048*2123=18,252,850,\n    C_R=2187*2123+2048*6358=17,664,185,\n    C_R-C_L=-588,665=2^22-3^14.\n\nThe ordered inverse is\n\n    S^2(n)=(4,194,304 n-18,252,850)/4,782,969,\n    n=2,959,216 modulo4,782,969.\n\nThe hand-checked chain is 2,959,216 -> 2,771,134 -> 2,595,006.\nBoth stages are in their required domains. The composite slope\n4,194,304/4,782,969 is strictly below1, so it reduces every positive\nadmissible terminal coordinate. The ordered class therefore contains\ninverse contractions. Even if this block is decomposed into irreducibles,\ntheir slope product is below1, so at least one irreducible slope is below1.\n\nConsequently the expanding-slope hypothesis of the SL3 source-count\nargument fails for the unrestricted ordered alphabet. Excluding reversals\nas generators does not repair it: the composite itself is ordered.\n\n## IR5. An actual 3-adic exception to the averaged estimate\n\nThe rational number z_*=-4310/139 is in Z_3 because139 is a 3-adic\nunit. It is fixed by S and S^2. Factor the latter ordered block into\nr>=1 irreducible ordered structural blocks. Full 3-adic divisibility\nimplies every intermediate prefix domain condition, so this r-block\ninverse schedule is admissible at z_* and returns to z_*.\n\nRepeating it t times contributes at least\n\n    (2187/2048)^(2t)\n\nto L^(rt)1(z_*). Hence the resolvent in IR2 is infinite at z_*.\nThis is compatible with its finite Haar integral. It supplies a specific\ncounterexample to a uniform pointwise conclusion on Z_3.\n\nThe exceptional value is a negative rational, not a positive integer.\nIt establishes no counterexample to Collatz and does not show that any\npositive integer belongs to the exceptional set. Positivity and ordinary\nsize are precisely additional constraints still needing an argument.\n\nThere is nevertheless a uniformity obstruction on positive integers.\nFor every t>=1, the finite ordered word H^t=(S^2)^t has one nonempty\nterminal congruence class modulo3^(14t). Choose a sufficiently large\npositive integer n_t in that class. Prefix integrality and genuine-word\nadmissibility give a positive inverse path realizing all t copies of H.\nFor any q>0, define W_q(n) as the sum of alpha_w^(-q) over all\nadmissible schedules in the irreducible ordered alphabet. This path gives\n\n    W_q(n_t)>=(2187/2048)^(2qt).\n\nIt follows that sup_(n positive integer) W_q(n)=infinity for every q>0.\nThus no uniform constant analogous to SL3's 2048 can hold over the full\nalphabet, even if attention is restricted to positive integer seeds.\nThe n_t vary with t. This does not prove W_q(n)=infinity for any one\npositive integer or preclude bounds depending on that fixed integer.\n\n## IR6. Relation to the full objective\n\nGB2 proves fixed-P finiteness without a finite numerical cutoff. IR2\ncontrols the full structural alphabet in an averaged 3-adic sense. IR4–IR5\nidentify two exact barriers to extending SL3 uniformly: inverse contraction\nand an exceptional 3-adic fixed point. These results do not establish\npositive upper density of Q(a,c), convergence of each positive integer,\nor factorization of arbitrary two-path witnesses.\n\nA possible next route is a pointwise positive-integer bound that uses the\nordinary decrease of contracting branches together with the growth of\nother branches. It must address arbitrary alternating choices and actual\nfirst-hit pairs. Haar averaging alone cannot supply the missing step.\nAll prior claims retain their earlier restricted scopes; the full proof\ngraph is not reaudited by this pass.\n\nIndependent audits:\n\n- [0459: signed length bound and contraction arithmetic](FOLLOWUP_0459_GENERAL_BLOCK_LENGTH.md).\n- [0485: factorization, transfer, exceptions and uniformity](FOLLOWUP_0485_FIRST_RETURN_BLOCK_TREE.md).\n- [0483: primary sources and orientation correction](FOLLOWUP_0483_GENERAL_BLOCK_SOURCES.md).\n","GROWING_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md":"# Растущие периоды: достаточные условия сравнения высот\n\n16 сентября 2026. Ручная приёмка 0210/0211. Математических исполнений — 0.\n\nСохраняем обозначения [фиксированного периода](FIXED_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md),\nно теперь W_i, q_i, R_i, c_i и k_i могут меняться. Старт ξ=a/b∈Z₂\nфиксирован, b>0 нечётно, его код не периодичен в конечном счёте.\nU_i — настоящий префикс длины i, S_i — число единиц,\nm_i=T^i(ξ), A_i=2^i c_i−k_i C_(U_i).\n\nДостаточные простые условия\n\n\\[\n q_i=o(S_i),\\qquad q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\n\\]\n\nдают на хвосте A_i≠0, v₃(A_i)\u003cS_i и\n\n\\[\n 1\\le h_i/A_i^\\#\\le6\\,3^{q_i}.\n\\]\n\nДля совместимых повторов с N_i=i+q_i+L_i, L_i>0, получаем\nlog(h_i/A_i#)=o(N_i). Поэтому две положительные относительные маржи\nэквивалентны на этих семьях. Существование таких семей не утверждается.\n\n## Точные достаточные условия\n\nПервому аргументу достаточно\n\n\\[\n F_i:=(c_i+|k_i|)2^{-S_i}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nПоскольку i≥S_i, это также даёт |k_i|2⁻ⁱ→0. На ветви 3^S_i | A_i,\nвключая A_i=0, положим z_i=A_i/3^S_i∈Z. Аффинный префикс даёт\n2^i | k_i a−bz_i и\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|k_i a-bz_i|}{2^i}\n \\le |a||k_i|2^{-i}+b(c_i+|k_i|)2^{-S_i}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nЛевая часть — неотрицательное целое. Равенство k_i a=bz_i означало бы\nξ=z_i/k_i=Φ₂(U_iW_i∞), противоречие непериодичности. При A_i=0\nоно означало бы ξ=0. Таким образом, A_i≠0 и v₃(A_i)\u003cS_i на хвосте;\nвалюация нулю не приписывается. Из F_i→0 также следует S_i→∞.\n\nДля обычного размера числителя положим t_i=C_(U_i)/3^S_i≥1/3 и\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i=\\frac{c_i(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k_i||m_i|}.\n\\]\n\nНепериодичность исключает m_i=0. Из точной формулы\n\n\\[\n A_i/3^{S_i}=-k_it_i+c_i(\\xi+t_i)/m_i\n\\]\n\nследует |A_i|/3^S_i≥|k_i|t_i(1−ρ_i). При ρ_i→0 на хвосте ρ_i≤1/2,\nпоэтому |A_i|/3^S_i≥|k_i|/6. Тогда\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{6\\,2^{q_i}}{|k_i|},\n                 \\frac{6\\,3^{R_i}}{|k_i|}\\right).\n\\]\n\nЭлементарная оценка даёт o(N_i), если\n\n\\[\n (q_i\\log2-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i),\\qquad\n (R_i\\log3-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i).\n\\]\n\nБолее точная форма сохраняет t_i в знаменателях и множитель\n1/(1−ρ_i). При ρ_i→0 логарифм этого множителя равен o(1).\nПоследующее [применение известной теоремы Бейкера, 0217](BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md)\nдаёт обе положительные части O(log(q_i+1)) автоматически. Поэтому при\nq_i\u003cN_i условия F_i→0 и ρ_i→0 уже достаточны для\nlog(h_i/A_i#)=O(log(q_i+1))=o(N_i); отдельное условие на зазор\nстепеней теперь не требуется.\n\n## Проверка простого следствия и границы\n\nОценки c_i,|k_i|≤3^q_i и |k_i|≥1 показывают, что q_i=o(S_i)\nдаёт F_i→0, а q_i=o(log|m_i|) даёт ρ_i→0. Так как S_i≤i\u003cN_i,\nпервое условие также даёт q_i=o(N_i), и log(h_i/A_i#)≤log 6+q_i log3=o(N_i).\nВ логарифме |m_i| подразумевается хвост |m_i|>1; существование такого\nхвоста следует из ухода непериодической рациональной орбиты по модулю\nна сетке фиксированного знаменателя.\n\nПри c_i=0 дефект ρ_i нулевой. Знаки ξ,k_i,A_i не меняют доказательства,\nи делимость 3|k_i не мешает сравнению по модулю 2^i. Случай S_i=0\nне входит в хвост S_i→∞.\n\nОдно q_i=o(N_i) не обеспечивает F_i→0 и ρ_i→0 **посредством приведённых\nоценок**. Формальные независимые параметры не являются контрпримером\nв классе совместимых рациональных орбит. Возможность иного аргумента\nэтой проверкой не исключена. Всеобщая маржа остаётся открытой.\n\n[Приёмка](REVIEW_0210_0211.md) · [0210](task-0210.md) · [0211](task-0211.md).\n","INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md":"# Необходимая длина периода при делимости числителя\n\n16 сентября 2026. Лемма координатора проверена агентом 0213; уточнение\nпо числу единиц проверено 0215/0216. Координатор прочитал отчёты и\nповторно проверил доказательства вручную. Математических исполнений — 0.\n\nПусть ξ=a/b∈Z₂, b>0 нечётно, его код не периодичен в конечном счёте.\nПусть U — любой настоящий префикс длины i с S единицами, W — любое\nнепустое бинарное слово длины q с R единицами. Положим\n\n\\[\n c=C_W,\\qquad k=2^q-3^R,\\qquad A=2^i c-kC_U.\n\\]\n\nТогда\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{3^S\\mid A\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2^S\\le(|a|+b)3^q.}\n\\]\n\nНе требуется, чтобы W действительно шло после U в исходном коде.\nНужен только настоящий начальный префикс U. Условие включает A=0;\nоно не определяет в этом случае A# или конечную валюацию.\n\n## Доказательство\n\nПусть z=A/3^S∈Z. Аффинный префикс и нечётность b дают\n\n\\[\n 2^i\\mid 3^S a+bC_U,\\qquad 2^i\\mid ka-bz.\n\\]\n\nЕсли ka=bz, то ξ=z/k=Φ₂(UW∞), противоречие непериодичности кода.\nПоэтому ka−bz — ненулевое кратное 2^i. Формула периодического\nпродолжения применима к любому W, поскольку 3^S k нечётно.\n\nДля позиций единиц t_r≤i−S+r−1 получаем точную оценку\n\n\\[\n 0\\le C_U\\le2^{i-S}(3^S-2^S).\n\\]\n\nОна включает S=0. Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n 1&\\le\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\\\\\n &\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+bc3^{-S}\n       +b|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\\\\\n &\\le (|a|+b)3^q2^{-S}.\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nПоследний шаг использует i≥S, c,|k|≤3^q. Два члена с 3⁻ˢ\nкомпенсируются. Делимость 3|k и знаки a,k,A не влияют на аргумент.\n\n## Граница применения\n\nДля ненулевого A это даёт необходимое условие\n\n\\[\n v_3(A)\\ge S\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n q\\ge\\frac{\\log2}{\\log3}S-\n       \\frac{\\log(|a|+b)}{\\log3}.\n\\]\n\nТаким образом, если (|a|+b)3^q\u003c2^S, целая ветвь исключена сразу,\nбез предельного аргумента и без оценки скорости орбиты.\nСокращённая запись a/b даёт наиболее полезную константу данного вида.\n\nНепериодичность используется для исключения конкретного равенства\nξ=Φ₂(UW∞). Она не выводится из рациональности. Лемма задаёт\nнеобходимое ограничение одной ветви и не обеспечивает положительную\nмаржу, наличие повторов или решение Коллатца.\n\n## Более сильная граница при расширяющем периоде\n\nЕсли k\u003c0, показатель q можно заменить числом единиц R:\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{3^S\\mid A\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2^S\\le(|a|+b)3^R.}\n\\]\n\nПри ξ≥0 непериодичность для этой импликации не нужна: A>0 и\nz/k\u003c0, поэтому ka−bz≠0 по знаку. Для отрицательного ξ сохраняется\nусловие непериодичности либо отдельная проверка ξ≠z/k.\n\nВот короткое доказательство координатора того же уточнения 0216.\nЕсли R>S, то 3^R>2^S и вывод уже верен. При R≤S точная оценка слова W\nдаёт\n\n\\[\n c\\le2^{q-R}(3^R-2^R)\n =2^q((3/2)^R-1)\\le2^q((3/2)^S-1).\n\\]\n\nПоложим K=|k|=3^R−2^q. Тогда\n\n\\[\n c3^{-S}+K(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n \\le(2^q+K)(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n \\le3^R2^{-S}.\n\\]\n\nПодстановка в прежнюю оценку ненулевого кратного 2^i вместе с\nK≤3^R и i≥S даёт нужную константу |a|+b. Край S=0 уже покрывается\nслучаем R>S, поскольку k\u003c0 требует R≥1.\n\nСледовательно, при S→∞ и limsup R/S\u003clog2/log3 ветвь v₃(A)≥S\nисключена на хвосте, даже если условие на q/S не выполнено.\nНаличие подходящих повторов этим по-прежнему не доказано.\n\n[Приёмка уточнения 0215/0216](REVIEW_0215_0216.md) ·\n[Приёмка 0213](REVIEW_0213.md) · [аудит](task-0213.md) ·\n[исходная записка координатора](../../../work/collatz-period-length-candidate-0213.md).\n","INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md":"# OR — exact inverse masks and the remaining primitive-origin problem\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\nbounded audits accepted in [the review](REVIEW_0485_INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGINS.md).\nMathematical programs, enumeration, CAS and Lean:0.\nUse [BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md), [SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md)\nand [BC](MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md), with all actual masks.\nNo nonconstant invariant is assumed to exist.\n\nLet β be an exact binary invariant for shortcut Collatz T, put\nd(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)|, e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1), and call e(n)=1 a birth.\nLet F be the partial arithmetic map H=(9n+5)/4 on3mod4 and\nG₃=(9n+3)/8 on13mod16. Its image is the disjoint union\n\n    I_H={9t+8:t≥0}, I_G={18t+15:t≥0}.                    (OR1)\n\nLet O_β be the origins of all actual retained-growth components, including\ninfinite components. Let A_β⊂O_β be the finite-component origins from BC.\nThese sets must not be identified using only a finite local test.\n\n## OR1. Inverting H, with the missing color retained\n\nSuppose n=9u−1∈I_H is a birth, u≥1, and write c=β(n)=β(n+1).\nIts only arithmetic predecessor is q=4u−1, with H(q)=n. Genuine paths\ngive β(q)=c and β(2q+1)=β(2n+1)=1−c. Also β(q+1)=β(u).\nThus\n\n    e(q)=1 iff β(u)=c; otherwise d(q)=1.                 (OR2)\n\nThe parent p=floor(q/2)=2u−1 has exactly the same boundary as q.\nTo see this without assuming q is a birth, write u=2^a v with v odd,\na≥0. In the notation of SC, n=X_(a+2,2), q=X_(a+2,0),\np=X_(a+1,0). The actual birth at n implies C_(a+2)≠C_(a+3), hence\n3^(a+2)v≡1mod4. The preceding merging identity gives\nC_(a+1)=C_(a+2)=c. The two upper endpoints p+1 and q+1 both have\ncolor β(u). Consequently\n\n    d(p)=d(q)=|β(u)−c|.                                 (OR3)\n\nIf e(q)=1, it supplies the retained H edge q→n and a birth smaller\nthan n. If not, both q and p are active boundaries; their nearest birth\nancestor b has\n\n    b≤floor(q/4)=u−1\u003cn.                                 (OR4)\n\nBR ensures a positive ancestor whenever these two boundaries are active;\nthe bound0 when u=1 shows that this failure case cannot occur at that edge.\nThere is no need to introduce an exceptional contribution to a harmonic sum.\n\n## OR2. Inverting G₃ and its output guard\n\nSuppose n=18t+15∈I_G is a birth, t≥0, and c=β(n)=β(n+1).\nIts only arithmetic predecessor is q=16t+13. All displayed arrows below\nare genuine shortcut steps:\n\n    q→24t+20→12t+10→6t+5→9t+8,\n    n+1→9t+8,\n    q+1→8t+7→12t+11→18t+17=n+2,\n    floor(q/2)=8t+6→4t+3→6t+5→9t+8.                    (OR5)\n\nAlso 2q+1 follows110 to2n+1. Thus β(q)=β(floor(q/2))=c,\nβ(q+1)=β(floor(q/2)+1)=β(n+2), and the middle color is1−c.\nIt follows exactly that\n\n    e(q)=1−d(n+1),\n    d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=d(n+1).                          (OR6)\n\nWhen d(n+1)=0, q is a birth and every retained G₃ output guard at n\nholds. When d(n+1)=1, q and its parent are active boundaries and the\nnearest birth ancestor b obeys\n\n    b≤floor(q/4)=4t+3\u003cn.                                (OR7)\n\nThese identities rely on e(n)=1. An arbitrary assignment of colors does\nnot license either inverse transition.\n\n## OR3. Exact origin indicator and a minimum-birth restriction\n\nLet o(n)=1 if n∈O_β, and0 otherwise. Combined injectivity from BC and\nOR2–OR6 give a finite local formula for all-component origins:\n\n    n∉I_H∪I_G:  o(n)=e(n),\n    n=9u−1:     o(n)=e(n)|β(u)−β(n)|,\n    n=18t+15:   o(n)=e(n)d(n+1).                         (OR8)\n\nIn particular every birth in I_H∪I_G has some smaller birth, whether\nor not its arithmetic predecessor is itself a birth. Therefore, if the\nbirth set is nonempty, its least member lies outside I_H∪I_G. Combining\nwith SC, that least member is odd, belongs to3mod4 or13mod16, and\nhas residue neither6 nor8 modulo9. These are necessary conditions only.\n\nThe formula o(n) does not say whether the subsequent component is finite.\nThat is the additional nonlocal restriction defining A_β.\n\n## OR4. A subunit charge for origins inside the arithmetic image\n\nLet D_β=O_β∩(I_H∪I_G), and write\n\n    E_D(X)=Σ_(n∈D_β,n≤X)1/n, E_β(X)=Σ_(n≤X)e(n)/n.\n\nEach n∈D_β has an arithmetic predecessor q that is not a birth, with\nd(q)=d(floor(q/2))=1. Charge it to the nearest birth ancestor b of q.\nAll charged q are distinct, because F is injective. Their depths from b\nare at least2; at each depth there are at most two such boundaries.\nHence\n\n    Σ_(q charged to b)1/q ≤ (2/b)Σ_(ell≥2)2^(−ell)=1/b.\n\nFor H and G₃ respectively, the original weights satisfy the exact bounds\n\n    1/n=4/(9q+5)≤4/(9q),\n    1/n=8/(9q+3)≤8/(9q).\n\nFor n≤X, q\u003c8X/9 and b≤q/4\u003c2X/9. Therefore\n\n    E_D(X) ≤ (8/9) E_β(2X/9),   X≥1.                    (OR9)\n\nNo summable error is needed. The factor below1 controls only these origins;\nit is not a contraction for the full energy. If P_β is the birth set\noutside I_H∪I_G, then O_β=P_β disjoint-union D_β. Every increasing\ncomponent, finite or infinite, has total weight at most9 times its origin\nweight by the BC geometric estimate. Thus\n\n    E_β(X)≤9 E_(O_β)(X)\n           ≤9 E_(P_β)(X)+8 E_β(2X/9).                  (OR10)\n\nThe coefficient in the resulting full-energy inequality is8, not8/9.\nUsing OR9 as a full contraction would drop the mass of later chain vertices.\nNeither OR9 nor OR10 bounds the unknown primitive-origin term E_(P_β).\n\n## OR5. An explicit barrier to deleting the actual masks\n\nThe arithmetic primitive-origin set contains the complete progression\n\n    n=144t+99, t≥0.\n\nIt lies in0mod9 and3mod16, hence outside both image classes and within\nthe H input class. Its unique arithmetic step is\n\n    H(n)=324t+224≡0mod4,\n\nafter which F is undefined. Thus each such start is an origin of a finite\narithmetic growth chain, with exactly one edge. Its harmonic sum diverges:\n144t+99≤144(t+1) supplies a lower bound1/[144(t+1)].\n\nIf a member of this progression happens to be an actual birth, it belongs\nto A_β: its actual chain either has the retained H edge and stops at an\neven birth, or is a singleton because that edge's mask fails. No actual\nbirth in the progression is asserted to exist. The example proves only\nthat the arithmetic superset of finite origins cannot supply the needed\nharmonic upper bound without genuine color information. It does not rule\nout proving such an upper bound with that information.\n\n## OR6. The retained G₃ domain has a fixed failure orientation\n\nThe paths in OR5 do not themselves require that the image n is a birth.\nFor every q=16t+13 and n=G₃(q)=18t+15 they give\n\n    β(q)=β(n+1), β(q+1)=β(n+2), d(q)=d(n+1).             (OR11)\n\nIf q is an actual birth of outer color c, this makes d(n+1)=0\nautomatic, while the middle color at n is1−c. There are exactly two\npossibilities:\n\n    β(n)=c:   e(n)=1, the retained G₃ edge is available;\n    β(n)=1−c: d(n)=1, with δ(n)=−(1−2c),                (OR12)\n\nwhere δ(k)=β(k+1)−β(k). In the failure case the charged boundary is\nn itself; the alternative n+1 cannot be active on this retained input\ndomain. The broader BR table on5mod8 is not changed by this assertion.\n\nFor comparison, at a failed H output K from an actual birth of color c,\nβ(K)=c, so δ(K)=1−2c. The exact signed dyadic law\nδ(2k)+δ(2k+1)=δ(k) shows that the unique active child of an active\nboundary preserves its sign. At a birth b of color c, the two first\nchild signs are1−2c and−(1−2c), respectively.\n\nThus, if a failed-output boundary K has a prior birth b in the same\nretained-growth component as its nearest birth ancestor at depth ell,\nwrite K=2^ell b+t,0≤t\u003c2^ell. Necessarily\n\n    H failure: 0≤t\u003c2^(ell−1),\n    G₃ failure:2^(ell−1)≤t\u003c2^ell.                       (OR13)\n\nEvery boundary along that binary prefix must also be active. The interval\ncondition and sign alone are not sufficient. This sharpens the oriented\ntest for a possible self-return; it does not exclude every return to an\nearlier member of the component. No actual self-return is constructed.\n\n## Remaining pointwise and energy obligations\n\nFor a minimal birth, inverse-image classes have now been excluded by\nexplicit smaller-birth alternatives. The primitive arithmetic classes\nremain numerous. Terminal forward transfers still need comparison with\nthe original component origin. Oriented dyadic boundary paths restrict\npossible self-returns, but no global rank excluding all of them is proved.\nThe full Collatz problem, all positive cycles and a possible isolated\nbad start remain unresolved. No worldwide novelty claim is made.\n","LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER.md":"# LB — guarded long blocks, exact inverse weights and the remaining core\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual analysis.\nNo mathematical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean or GPU. Full goal incomplete.\nThe preceding CZ turn made progress; this pass audits its explicit next candidate.\n\n## LB1. Definitions and the genuine signed identity\n\nLet T(n)=n/2 for even n and (3n+1)/2 for odd n. Let beta be any\nbinary exact T-invariant, g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), and h(n)=|g(n)|.\nWrite G(X)=sum_(odd n\u003c=X) h(n)/n. Keep CZ's forced-zero set Z.\n\nFor each L>=3, define the guarded source class\n\n    B_L={n=2^L m-1 : m positive odd, 3^(L-3)m=1 mod16},\n    B=union_(L>=3) B_L.\n\nThe classes are disjoint because v_2(n+1)=L. On a source in B_L,\n\n    S^j(n)=2^(L-j)3^j m-1, 0\u003c=j\u003c=L-3,\n    x=S^(L-3)(n)=8*3^(L-3)m-1=7 mod128.\n\nFor j\u003cL-3 the source is15 mod16, so CZ's signed preservation applies.\nIts exact swap rule at x then gives\n\n    P(n)=(9x+1)/64=(3^(L-1)m-1)/8,\n    g(n)=-g(P(n)), h(n)=h(P(n)).                    (LB1)\n\nAll parameters are integers on the guard. The target is positive and\nP(n)=1 mod18: put 3^(L-3)m=16t+1 to obtain P(n)=18t+1.\nThe case L=3 has no prefix and is exactly CZ's selected class D.\nB and Z are disjoint: their prefix endpoints have respectively\n3^(L-3)m=1 mod16 and3 mod4. No arbitrary affine branch is imported.\n\n## LB2. Exactly which blocks descend; limited cycle information\n\nFor a guarded source, direct subtraction gives\n\n    P(n)\u003cn iff (2^(L+3)-3^(L-1))m>7.              (LB2)\n\nFor L=3,...,7 the coefficient is55,101,175,269,295, respectively.\nAt L=8 it is -139, and negativity persists since the next coefficient\nis twice the preceding one minus3^(L-1). Therefore every guarded\nblock descends for3\u003c=L\u003c=7 and grows forL>=8; equality never occurs.\nIn particular 1/P(n)>1/n on descending blocks, not the reverse.\n\nWriting alpha_L=3^(L-1)/2^(L+3)>1/8 gives\n\n    P(n)=alpha_L n+alpha_L-1/8 > alpha_L n.\n\nIf a positive guarded P-cycle of length k existed, multiplication\nof these inequalities would force\n\n    product_i alpha_(L_i)\u003c1,\n    3^(sum L_i-k)\u003c2^(sum L_i+3k),\n    (sum L_i)/k \u003c log24/log(3/2) \u003c8.               (LB3)\n\nAt a point of such a cycle with h=1, the sign flip additionally forces\nk to be even. Odd cycles force g=0 on the cycle. These are necessary\nconditions, not a proof excluding even mixed cycles. No cycle entirely\nof growing blocks exists, but an infinite increasing chain has not been\nexcluded. A target need not be another guarded source.\n\n## LB3. All inverse branches and the finite cutoff\n\nFix an eligible target u=1 mod18 and put v=v_3(8u+1)>=2. The inverse\nsources are exactly\n\n    L=3,...,v+1,\n    m_L=(8u+1)/3^(L-1),\n    n_L(u)=2^L(8u+1)/3^(L-1)-1.                  (LB4)\n\nThe quotient m_L is positive odd. Conversely (8u+1)/9=1 mod16,\nso every listed source satisfies the original guard. Different L give\ndifferent sources. Thus the multiplicity is v-1, finite for each u\nbut unbounded over eligible targets.\n\nDefine W_X(u)=sum_(3\u003c=L\u003c=v+1,n_L(u)\u003c=X)1/n_L(u). Regrouping a finite\nset of sources, with no dropped multiplicity, gives the exact identity\n\n    G_B(X)=sum_(u=1 mod18) h(u) W_X(u).            (LB5)\n\nOnly finitely many terms contribute at a fixed X. On a growing branch\nu may exceed X. Indeed L\u003c=log_2(X+1) implies the safe upper bound\n\n    u \u003c= (X+1)^(log_2 3)/24 -1/8.\n\nThere is no general permission to replace the target cutoff by u\u003c=X.\nThe complete inverse weight W(u) is obtained by omitting n_L\u003c=X.\n\n## LB4. A summable denominator error and an unbounded transfer factor\n\nPut W_0(u)=sum_L 1/(n_L(u)+1). A finite geometric sum gives\n\n    W_0(u)=9/[4(8u+1)] * ((3/2)^(v-1)-1).        (LB6)\n\nThe discarded denominator minus one has an explicit global cost:\n\n    E(u)=W(u)-W_0(u)=sum_L 1/[n_L(u)(n_L(u)+1)] >=0,\n    sum_(u=1 mod18) E(u)\n      =sum_(n in B) 1/[n(n+1)] \u003c=1/7.            (LB7)\n\nThe equality uses the unique target P(n) of each source; the final\nbound is the telescoping sum over all integers n>=7. Consequently,\nas an equality of extended nonnegative sums, without assuming finiteness,\n\n    sum_(n in B) h(n)/n\n      =(9/4) sum_(u=1 mod18)\n           h(u)*((3/2)^(v_3(8u+1)-1)-1)/(8u+1) + E_h,\n    0\u003c=E_h\u003c=1/7.                                  (LB8)\n\nThis identifies a precise additional weighted target mass. It does\nnot estimate that mass as finite for an actual h.\n\nThere is no constant C with W(u)\u003c=C/u for every eligible u. Set\nv=4k+2 and u=(3^v-1)/8, k>=0. Then u=1 mod18 and v_3(8u+1)=v.\nThe branch L=v+1 has n_L=2^(v+1)-1, whence\n\n    u W(u) >= (3^v-1)/[8(2^(v+1)-1)] -> infinity.  (LB9)\n\nThis refutes a universal bound for the arithmetic transfer operator.\nIt does NOT assert h(u)=1 on those targets, or give a counterexample\nto an estimate restricted to actual beta defects using extra structure.\n\n## LB5. A larger core reduction from the first three depths\n\nLet B_(3:5)=B_3 union B_4 union B_5. Every one of these blocks has\nP(n)\u003cn. For its finite-cutoff sum, replace 1/n_L by1/(n_L+1) and\npay at most1/7 by LB7. Since 8u+1>=8u,\n\n    G_(B_(3:5))(X)\n      \u003c= (9/64+27/128+81/256) G(X)+1/7\n      = (171/256) G(X)+1/7.                       (LB10)\n\nHere the target is odd and below X, and restricting it to u=1 mod18\nor to the relevant v-values can only reduce the bound. Define\n\n    C_5={positive odd integers} minus (Z union B_(3:5)).\n\nSince h vanishes on Z, subtraction of the common finite-cutoff sums\ngives\n\n    G_(C_5)(X) \u003c= G(X)\n      \u003c= (256/85) G_(C_5)(X)+256/595.              (LB11)\n\nThus finite G is equivalent to finite G_(C_5). The new core is strictly\nsmaller than CZ's core (which removed only B_3). Neither mass is proved\nfinite. This is a bounded refinement of the criterion, not a proof\nof the needed estimate. Including every descending depth naively would\nlose this contraction; descending individual integers and controlling\nall their harmonic inverse weights are different requirements.\n\n## LB6. Geometric rarity is not sufficient; an explicit limited model\n\nB_L is one class modulo2^(L+4). Its least member is at least2^L-1,\nso at cutoff X only L\u003c=floor(log_2(X+1)) contribute. Per-class count\nerrors sum to O(log(X+1)); the omitted geometric tail times X is O(1).\nPartial summation therefore justifies\n\n    #B up to X = X/64+O(log(X+1)),\n    sum_(n\u003c=X,n in B)1/n=(log X)/64+O(1).\n\nFor B_(>=8) the identical proof gives coefficient1/2048 in place of1/64.\nBoth statements concern the unweighted source sets, not h or its actual\ntrajectory frequencies. In particular the growing tail still has divergent\nharmonic mass. Its small density does not imply a summable error.\n\nMore directly, the unsigned zero/preservation/block identities alone\ndo not force finite harmonic mass. Define a binary function on all n by\n\n    f(n)=1 iff oddpart(n)=3 mod24, otherwise0.     (LB12)\n\nIt is dyadically invariant. Every odd source in Z or B is7 mod8, so\nf=0 there. Every target P(B) is1 mod18 and not divisible by3, so f=0\nthere too. Thus f(n)=f(P(n)) on every guarded block. For x=15 mod16,\nboth x and S(x) are7 mod8, so the earlier preservation identity also\nholds for f. Nevertheless its odd harmonic mass is(log X)/24+O(1).\n\nThis is only a model of the listed UNSIGNED identities. It does not\nsatisfy all constraints of an exact beta defect. In fact f(3)=1 whereas\nevery exact beta has beta(3)=beta(9): the paths3->5 and\n9->14->7->11->17->26->13->20->10->5 meet. Also its total U-variation\nis infinite: for n=24t+3, U(n)=36t+5 is not divisible by3, so f drops\nfrom1 to0 on a set of divergent harmonic mass. It does not contradict\nCZ's conditional finite-variation lemma and is not a Collatz counterexample.\n\n## LB7. Sources, remaining obligations and next analytical step\n\nRoot freshly read the standard affine iterate formula and finite parity\nclass statement in Rozier–Terracol, Introduction equation(2) and the\ndiscussion after Lemma2.3:\n[primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948).\nThey justify the standard finite coding framework, not this special\nguarded identity or a universal termination theorem. The attempted fresh\nBernstein–Lagarias PDF read failed; it is not treated as fresh evidence here.\nNo novelty claim is made for any of these elementary orbit identities.\n\nThe new task is sharper than merely extending the same depth L: LB8\nshows the precise high-valuation target weight that must be controlled,\nand LB12 shows that the current unsigned module leaves independent\nclasses untouched. One must use additional actual beta relations there,\nor prove a core estimate summable over scales. Density and a finite\nor infinite list of local identities by themselves do not supply it.\n\nSB's finite-E/finite-ZR/finite-G equivalence is unchanged. LB uses no\nPD170 input. Historical R170 acceptance is preserved, while its full\nanalytic/Lean dependency closure is not freshly recertified. Full Collatz,\nfinite G and finite G_(C_5) remain open. All work is local; public\nsnapshot425 and the completed distribution remain unchanged.\n\n[CZ predecessor](COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md) ·\n[Inverse audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_BLOCK_INVERSE_WEIGHTS.md) ·\n[Dynamics audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_LONG_BLOCK_DYNAMICS.md) ·\n[Scope audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_LONG_BLOCK_SOURCE_SCOPE.md).\n","MINIMAL_BAD_PAIR_RETURN_BOUNDARY.md":"# MB — minimal bad pairs: exact return clocks and a coverage barrier\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual derivation and\ninternal audit only. No mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS or Lean.\nThese are conditional consequences of a hypothetical least counterexample;\nthey do not prove that such a counterexample exists or exclude it.\nThe unit-target fact already appears in CX1. No world-priority claim is made.\n\n## MB1. Objects and the fixed seed\n\nUse the shortcut map T(x)=x/2 for even x and (3x+1)/2 for odd x.\nA positive integer is good if its T-orbit reaches 1, bad otherwise.\nSuppose μ is the least bad positive integer. Then μ>1 is odd, every\npositive integer below μ is good, and μ+1 is good because\nT(μ+1)=(μ+1)/2\u003cμ. Also μ−1 is good.\n\nIf μ=1 mod4, the actual two-step value (3μ+1)/4 is below μ and is bad,\na contradiction. Thus μ=3 mod4. This elementary necessary condition\nleaves the residue types 3,7,11 mod12; it does not assert that any type\ncontains a counterexample or that stronger known sieves have been applied.\n\nLet R be the partial deterministic map of first ordered structural\nreturns from [GB/IR](GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md). A block has\nthe same length K and odd count P on both arms and sends (m,m+1) to\n(n,n+1). Every nonempty block has P≥1 and K≥2. Its inverse is\nF(n)=A n−B with A=2^K/3^P and B=C/3^P≥1/3.\n\nLet M_R(n) be the sources of finite R-schedules ending at n, including\nthe empty schedule. These sources form only a subset of the full\npaired predecessor set; synchronization and equal odd counts are genuine\nrestrictions, not normal forms for arbitrary pairs.\n\n## MB2. The congruence obstruction, with both orientations retained\n\nFor a word with P≥1,\n\n    T_w(x)=(3^P x+C_w)/2^K,\n\nwhere C_w is a unit modulo3: the last odd contribution is a power of2,\nand each preceding contribution has a factor3. Thus both terminal\ncoordinates of a structural block are 3-adic units. Consecutive units\nmust be 1,2 modulo3. Every inverse guard is consequently contained in\n1+3Z_3. In particular, exactly as already proved in CX1,\n\n    n≠1 mod3  ⇒  M_R(n)={n},  W_q(n)=1 for every q>0.       (MB1)\n\nApplying this existing fact to the two known mixed pairs gives:\n\n| μ mod12 | Seed μ for (bad,good) | Seed μ−1 for (good,bad) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 3 | 0 mod3: singleton closure | 2 mod3: singleton closure |\n| 7 | 1 mod3: incoming blocks possible | 0 mod3: singleton closure |\n| 11 | 2 mod3: singleton closure | 1 mod3: incoming blocks possible |\n\n“Possible” means only that this particular obstruction is absent. It is\nnot an assertion of a nonempty branch, large tree, or positive density.\nSwapping to (μ−1,μ) avoids the immediate residue obstruction in type11;\nboth immediate mixed-pair structural trees are singletons in type3.\nNo residue class of counterexamples has thereby been excluded.\n\nThis invalidates the proposed unconditional route that would force\npositive density from M_R(μ) for every hypothetical μ without another\ninput. A proof excluding the obstructed μ types, or a genuinely broader\nfamily, would be needed. The full Q_C(3μ+1,1) is not this structural tree.\n\n## MB3. An exact clock on mixed pairs\n\nFor a mixed pair (x,x+1) with bad left side and good right side, put\n\n    h(x+1)=min{j≥0:T^j(x+1)∈{1,2}}.\n\nThis finite hitting time is positive. If a structural block has length\nK≥h(x+1), its right terminal is1 or2, hence its left terminal is0 or1.\nNeither can be a positive bad state. Therefore every outgoing block has\n\n    K\u003ch(x+1),  h(R(x)+1)=h(x+1)−K.                       (MB2)\n\nSince K≥2, at most floor((h(x+1)−1)/2) successive R-returns are possible\nfrom this fixed source. In particular R has no periodic mixed pair.\nThis is a terminating rank on a class already assumed to have one good\ncoordinate, not a rank proving that arbitrary Collatz orbits are good.\n\nThe same argument works for a good-left/bad-right pair using h(x): after\nthe good coordinate reaches1 or2 its adjacent right coordinate would be\n2 or3, both good. The latter elementary fact is witnessed by\n3→5→8→4→2→1. Thus the orientation change in MB2 is legitimate.\n\nFor an incoming schedule from (m,m+1) to (μ,μ+1), the clock instead obeys\n\n    h(m+1)=K_total+h(μ+1).                               (MB3)\n\nIndeed an earlier visit of the right orbit to {1,2} would preclude its\nlater visit to μ+1≥4. Backward paths increase this clock. Finite forward\ndepth gives no uniform upper bound on inverse depth or endpoint count.\n\n## MB4. What minimality adds to the resolvent\n\nEvery left intermediate state of a path ending at μ is bad and at least μ.\nOtherwise it would be good and could not later reach μ. For a nonempty\nstructural incoming schedule, m=Aμ−B≥μ implies\n\n    A≥1+1/(3μ)>1, hence 2^K>3^P.                        (MB4)\n\nMB3 excludes a nonempty schedule with m=μ. Consequently every nonempty\nsource satisfies m>μ. Determinism then gives at most one R-hitting time\nper endpoint: two hits would make μ periodic. The cycle factor c_q(μ)\nfrom [PR](POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md) is exactly1.\n\nSeparate the empty term, whose B is0. For q>1 the nonnegative sums give\n\n    W_q(μ) ≤ 1+μ^q Σ_(m>μ) m^(−q) ≤ 1+μ/(q−1).          (MB5)\n\nThe last comparison is with the integral from μ to infinity. At q=1,\nfor X≥μ, it only supplies\n\n    W_1(μ;X) ≤ 1+μ Σ_(μ\u003cm≤X)1/m ≤ 1+μ log(X/μ).         (MB6)\n\nThese are upper bounds, not lower growth or convergence of the critical\nsum. In the singleton cases W_q=1; replacing the first weak inequality\nby a strict one would be incorrect. The untruncated critical sum may\nbe infinite, so no inequality involving infinity is made strict.\n\nThe complete bad corridor also gives an elementary individual bound:\n\n    μ/m = 2^(−K) product_(odd x_j)(3+1/x_j)\n         ≤ 2^(−K)(3+1/μ)^P,\n    m ≥ μ 2^K/(3+1/μ)^P.                                (MB7)\n\nIt bounds a source from below and does not bound how many sources there are.\n\n## MB5. The full-pair obligation and the direction of normalization\n\nFor the ordinary map C(x)=x/2 when even and 3x+1 when odd, let\n\n    Q_C(a,1)={m>0: C^r(m)=a, C^s(m+1)=1 for some r,s≥0}.\n\nTake a=3μ+1=C(μ), so μ is a witness in Q_C(a,1). Every structural\nsource in M_R(μ) is also a witness, but the converse is not proved and\nis generally not a valid structural reduction. The hit times and odd\ncounts defining full Q are independent.\n\nThe valid one-target inclusion is Pred_C(μ)⊂Pred_C(a). There is no\nforward step a→μ supplied by the setup. For a word ending at a,\nminimality only gives m≥μ, not m≥a; its inverse slope need not exceed1.\nFor P≥1, m=Aa−B gives A>μ/a. All-even words have B=0 and must be\nhandled separately; the same distinction applies to empty words.\n\nUnder the accepted fixed-shift boundary input C180/R181, a sufficient\nnew argument would be positive upper Banach density of Q_C(a,1) for\nevery possible minimal bad μ. That is a statement about a fixed positive\nfraction in intervals of unbounded length, not merely infinitely many\nsources. No such lower bound is obtained here.\n\nThere is a sufficient odd-target version, which makes the existing odd-target\ninteger operator applicable without claiming equality of predecessor sets.\nSince μ=3 mod4,\n\n    v_2(3μ+1)=1,  b=(3μ+1)/2 is odd,  b=2 mod3,  b>μ.\n\nThe actual ordinary trajectory is μ→a→b. Thus b is bad, μ witnesses both\npaired sets, and\n\n    Q_C(a,1) ⊆ Q_C(b,1) ⊆ {m: m bad, m+1 good}.           (MB8)\n\nHere the subset symbol denotes inclusion, with no assertion of strictness\nor equality. Under R181, positive upper Banach density of Q_C(b,1) would\nalso give a contradiction. This is a sufficient replacement target, not\nan inverse implication about hitting a. Every source reaching b is at\nleast μ, but need not be at least b, so MB4's slope conclusion at target μ\ncannot be reused at target b.\n\nMoreover b=2 mod3 makes M_R(b)={b}, whereas μ\u003cb is a witness of Q_C(b,1).\nThus even at this convenient normalized target, the adjacent structural\ntree cannot represent the full pair operator. The latter has target\ncoordinates (b,1), not (b,b+1).\n\nThe three weights remain different: binary source mass2^(−K), ternary\nguard mass3^(−P), and critical resolvent weight3^P/2^K. RG's κ0>1\nneither removes the singleton obstruction nor supplies pair density.\nEven full W_1 finiteness would not supply this lower-density theorem.\n\n## MB6. Audit, precedent and the next decision\n\nRoot read all three reports fully, then reread the corrections:\n\n- [0459: minimality, congruence and return clocks](FOLLOWUP_0459_MINIMAL_BAD_INTEGER.md).\n- [0485: multiplicity and sharpened integer bounds](FOLLOWUP_0485_MINIMAL_BAD_RESOLVENT.md).\n- [0483: the missing full-Q interface and source scope](FOLLOWUP_0483_FIXED_SEED_DENSITY_SCOPE.md).\n\nThe guard fact and critical harmonic barrier already occur in\n[CX1–CX3](CRITICAL_WEIGHT_ARITHMETIC_AND_POTENTIAL_BARRIER.md).\nThe current contribution is their explicit application to both minimal\nneighbor pairs, the exact good-side clock, and the corrected route choice.\nThis is an elementary refinement, not a claimed new mathematical field.\n\nRoot reopened the introduction and relevant initial tree definitions of\n[Applegate–Lagarias, Density Bounds I](https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/applegateI.pdf).\nThey already distinguish fixed-target source counts from depth counts,\nand explain the special predecessor chains at targets divisible by3.\nThis is a precedent for the scope distinctions, not a paired-density theorem.\nThe source agent used its recorded prior readings; it did not perform a\nnew literature search in this pass. No exhaustive novelty check is claimed.\n\nNext, work on the existing full integer pair operator with odd targets\n(b,1) from MB8 and its two source orientations with unequal depths/odd\ncounts. Preserve first-hit labels,\nthe source interval and divisibility; do not cap intermediate trajectory\nheight by the source cutoff. A useful next result must control a family\nof deep joint layers or provide a pointwise contradiction. Repeating\nthe synchronized tree or the excluded regular profile ansatz cannot\nsubstitute for that missing argument. Full Collatz remains unresolved.\n","MINIMUM_DEFECT_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md":"# MC — a minimum-defect cutoff for births and the first-fiber boundary\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual proof.\nNo programs, enumeration, CAS or Lean. No actual nonconstant invariant or\nprimitive birth is assumed to exist. Full Collatz remains unproved.\n\n## MC1. Definitions and an event implication for every odd source\n\nLet beta be an exact binary invariant of shortcut T, and put\n\n    g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h(n)=|g(n)|,\n    d(n)=|beta(n+1)-beta(n)|,\n    e(n)=1 iff beta(n)=beta(n+1) != beta(2n+1).\n\nIn particular beta(2n)=beta(n) and h(2n)=h(n). For any positive odd u,\nset r=(3u+1)/2=2^a w, with w odd, a>=0, and p=4u+1. Use only indices\nj>=1 in C_j=beta(3^j w-1); all those arguments are positive.\n\nRepeated genuine odd steps give\nT^j(2^j w-1)=3^j w-1. Also p reaches r in three shortcut steps and u\nreaches r in one. With B=beta(w), therefore\n\n    beta(p)=beta(u)=B,\n    beta(3p)=C_(a+3), beta(3u)=C_(a+1),\n    beta(3u+1)=B, beta(6u+1)=C_(a+2).              (MC1)\n\nIf L=3^j w is3 mod4, the two paths\n\n    L-1 -> (L-1)/2 -> (3L-1)/4,\n    3L-1 -> (3L-1)/2 -> (3L-1)/4\n\nare genuine shortcut paths. Hence C_j=C_(j+1) in this case.\nThe event e(3u)=1 forces C_(a+1)=B != C_(a+2), so the first of these\ntwo indices cannot have3^(a+1)w=3 mod4. It has residue1; the next has\nresidue3. Thus C_(a+2)=C_(a+3). Consequently\n\n    e(3u)=1 => h(u)=0 and g(4u+1)=1-2B !=0.        (MC2)\n\nThis holds for ALL positive odd u, including3-free u. It is not the\nunguarded equality e(3u)=(1-h(u))h(4u+1); that equality requires the\nplus selector. MC2 uses the selector only after the actual event forces it.\n\n## MC2. What a least odd defect forbids\n\nSuppose {n positive odd: h(n)=1} is nonempty and q is its least member.\nEvery positive r\u003cq has h(r)=0, by removing factors of2 if necessary.\nSince4u+1 is odd, MC2 gives the exact strict cutoff\n\n    u positive odd, 4u+1\u003cq => e(3u)=0.            (MC3)\n\nEquivalently, every odd birth z divisible by3 satisfies\nz>=3(q-1)/4. Equality is not excluded. This is a bound relative to\nthe minimum h-defect, not to the least changed beta-color or least birth.\n\n## MC3. The A=2 boundary closes under this additional minimality\n\nAssume q is specifically a first primitive-fiber source in CF:\n\n    q=4y+1, y=3^(A-1)m, A>=2, m positive odd, 3 not dividing m,\n    e(3y)=1, beta(m)=...=beta(3^A m)=B.\n\nOnly this subclass of possible least defects is considered. With u=y/3,\nMC3 applies because4u+1\u003cq. Hence e(y)=0, including when A=2.\nMoreover h(y/3)=0 and the common endpoint\nT(y/3)=T(y+1)=(y+1)/2 give beta(y+1)=B. The zero birth at y now forces\nbeta(2y+1)=B. Put t=(3y+1)/2 and v=2y+1. These are below q, so\n\n    beta(t)=beta(v)=B, h(t)=h(v)=0.               (MC4)\n\nThe genuine path T(v)=3y+2, doubling, the birth at3y and h(v)=0 yield\n\n    (beta(6y),...,beta(6y+4))=(B,1-B,B,B,B).       (MC5)\n\nAlso beta(q+1)=beta(v)=B, so d(q)=0. The first and middle colors in\nthe birth tests at2y and t are equal: beta(2y)=beta(4y+1)=B and\nbeta(t)=beta(2t+1)=beta(3y+2)=B. Therefore\n\n    e(y)=e(2y)=e(t)=0, d(q)=0.                   (MC6)\n\nAll conclusions hold for A>=2 under minimum-h minimality. CF without\nthis additional assumption still has its unresolved A=2 boundary.\n\n## MC4. The remaining bit is not eliminated\n\nWrite W=beta(2q+1). The two even steps from2q and2q+2 give endpoint\ncolors beta(q)=beta(q+1)=B. Thus\n\n    e(q)=d(2q)=d(2q+1)=|W-B|.                    (MC7)\n\nThese identities do not determine W; neither value is asserted to be\nrealizable by an actual invariant. Both proposed smaller defects t and v\nare forced to have h=0. No smaller nonzero defect, contradiction, new\nexcluded residue class, finite energy or global descent follows here.\n\n## MC5. Checks, corrections and archive scope\n\nThe event/cutoff argument was independently checked in\n[0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_MINIMUM_BIRTH_CUTOFF.md); the additional first-fiber\nmask was checked and corrected in\n[0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_MINIMUM_FIRST_FIBER_MASK.md). Both roles then completed\nwhole-file MC1–MC7 audits of this assembled note; root read their final\nsections and corrected passages. PB's separate scope audit is still pending.\n\nThe discussion caught an off-by-one arrow: T(2q+1)=3q+2, not3q+1.\nRoot initially copied that wrong arrow in feedback and incorrectly\nsuggested beta(4q+1)=W. In fact beta(4q+1)=beta(3q+1)=beta(q)=B,\nby its two-step path and T(q). MC7 uses only the two even steps and\ndoes not use either false statement. The earlier false CF residual\ndecomposition remains withdrawn as documented in CF7.\n\nThe bounded archive comparison with\n[minimum birth equivalence](FOLLOWUP_0483_MINIMUM_BIRTH_EQUIVALENCE.md)\ndistinguishes its first changed-color index mu and first birth (mu-1)/2\nfrom the minimum multiplicative-defect index q here. The finite merge in\nMC1 is already SC2; the event implication is already PB2. The present\nadditional consequence is MC3 and its A=2 use under minimum-h minimality.\nNo literature-wide novelty claim or completed four-role registration is made.\n","MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md":"# BC — exact block compensation and a summable infinite-growth core\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\nbounded audits accepted in [the review](REVIEW_0485_SPARSE_GROWTH_CORE.md).\nNo mathematical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean.\nWe use the [SC reduction](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md), including\nall its actual-birth guards. This note neither asserts existence of a\nnonconstant invariant nor excludes every possible positive start.\nHere T(n)=n/2 for even n and (3n+1)/2 for odd n. An exact binary\ninvariant satisfies β(T(n))=β(n). Write d(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)| and\ne(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1); an index with e(n)=1 is called a birth.\n\n## BC1. Exact affine accumulation\n\nFor a word of r growing transitions let b_i=2 for H and b_i=3 for G₃,\nB_0=0,B_i=b_1+...+b_i. Put γ_2=5,γ_3=3. When the word is admissible,\n\n    n_i=(9n_{i−1}+γ_{b_i})/2^b_i,\n    μ_i=2n_i+1=(9μ_{i−1}+5)/2^b_i.\n\nConsequently\n\n    n_r=[9^r n_0+Σ_(i=1..r)9^(r−i)γ_{b_i}2^B_(i−1)]/2^B_r,\n    μ_r=[9^r μ_0+5S_r]/2^B_r,\n    S_r=Σ_(i=1..r)9^(r−i)2^B_(i−1).                      (BC1)\n\nThe multiplier λ_w=9^r/2^B_r depends on the numbers of H/G₃ steps;\nthe additive term also depends on their order. Each map has the form\nF_i(n)=λ_i n+(λ_i−1)c_i, with c_i=1 for H and c_i=3 for G₃.\nExpanding the composition gives, for r≥1,\n\n    n_r=λ_w(n_0+c_w)−c_w,       1≤c_w≤3.                 (BC2)\n\nIndeed the coefficient weights of c_i are\n(λ_i−1)∏_(j>i)λ_j>0 and sum to λ_w−1. Dividing by λ_w−1 makes\nc_w a convex combination. In particular λ_w≥(9/8)^r>1. This is an\nexact arithmetic bound, not a decreasing rank for the actual birth process.\n\n## BC2. What an exit must compensate\n\nLet K be an actual active boundary. Its nearest birth ancestor is\na=floor(K/2^ell), where ell is the first depth with zero boundary:\nall shallower boundaries are active and d(a)=0. Such a positive ancestor\nexists by BR ancestry and d(1)=0. Then\n\n    a\u003cn_0  iff  K\u003c2^ell n_0.                            (BC3)\n\nFor an exit boundary K=n_r+ε, ε∈{0,1}, BC1 makes this equivalent to\n\n    9^r(2n_0+1)+5S_r\n      \u003c 2^B_r [2^(ell+1)n_0+1−2ε].                      (BC4)\n\nOther actual exit boundaries must be inserted into BC3 themselves;\nthey must not be silently identified with n_r. In particular two known\nactive ancestor levels imply ell≥2, not ell=2. At an even failed output\nwith e=0, BR2 cannot be applied to that output; SC3 first checks its\nparent, applying BR2 only if that parent is an even birth.\n\nFor K=n_r+ε the growth bound gives K/n_0>λ_w. Hence ell must exceed\nlog₂(λ_w) if BC3 is to hold. A fixed lower bound such as ell≥2 does\nnot establish this for arbitrary long words. This does not prove a\ncounterexample to compensation: the actual ell may be much larger.\n\n## BC3. An arithmetic core, with no color assumptions\n\nDefine the partial map on positive integers\n\n    F(n)=H(n)=(9n+5)/4,   if n≡3 mod4;\n    F(n)=G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8, if n≡13 mod16;\n    F is undefined otherwise.\n\nThe two domains are disjoint; outputs are positive integers and strictly\nlarger than their inputs. Let Γ be the set of positive n for which F can\nbe iterated forever. This is an arithmetic superset of actual births that\ncontinue forever through retained SC growth steps. It includes no imposed\ne/d masks. Neither Γ=∅ nor Γ≠∅ is asserted here.\n\nFor r≥0 let P_r contain starts with r F-steps and odd final n_r.\nAn infinite F-chain has odd inputs at every stage, so\n\n    Γ⊂P_r for every r.                                  (BC5)\n\nP_r is a set of starts, distinct from the affine sum S_r in BC1.\nThe audit reports called this finite-prefix set S_r; only its notation\nhas changed in this final note.\n\nFor a word w of r labels H/G₃ write W for the shortcut parity word\nobtained by replacing H by11 and G₃ by110. Its length is B_r.\nThe starts of that word ending at odd n_r are exactly the middle starts\nμ_0=2n_0+1 whose first B_r+2 parity bits are\n\n    W followed by11.                                    (BC6)\n\nTo verify the guards, an H position has next bits111: its11 block and\nthe first bit of the next block, or of the final suffix. These bits give\nμ≡7 mod8, hence n≡3 mod4. A G₃ position has bits11011, giving\nμ≡27 mod32, hence n≡13 mod16. The next11 exists even at the last\nblock because it was appended. The final11 means μ_r≡3 mod4, or n_r\nodd. Conversely the F-domains and odd final n_r supply precisely these\nwords, by the genuine middle-orbit identities in SC8/SC9.\n\nEvery finite shortcut parity word of length k corresponds to one residue\nclass modulo2^k. One elementary proof lifts a class from k to k+1 bits:\nthe two lifts differ by2^k, their first k bits agree, and their k-th\nimages differ by3^o, where o is the number of odd inputs among those k\nbits. This difference is odd, so exactly one lift has the desired next\nbit. Start with the two parity classes modulo2. Positive representatives\nexist in every such class; no mathematical search is involved.\n\nThus each word in BC6 gives one n-class modulo2^(B_r+1). Its number\nof positive representatives at most X is at most X/2^(B_r+1)+1.\nSumming over the 2^r words gives, for every real X≥1,\n\n    #P_r∩[1,X] ≤ (X/2)(1/4+1/8)^r+2^r\n               = (X/2)(3/8)^r+2^r.                    (BC7)\n\nDisjointness of the classes is not needed for this upper bound. The\nargument is a finite-class count, not independence of β colors.\n\n## BC4. A uniform square-root count and harmonic bound\n\nChoose the integer r≥0 with 2^r≤√X\u003c2^(r+1). By BC5/BC7 and\n(3/8)^r≤2^(−r),\n\n    N_Γ(X):=#Γ∩[1,X]\n       ≤X/2^(r+1)+2^r \u003c2√X.                            (BC8)\n\nThe weak bound N_Γ(X)≤2√X will suffice. Tonelli's theorem for\nnonnegative functions gives\n\n    Σ_(n∈Γ)1/n = ∫_1^∞ N_Γ(t)/t² dt\n                ≤∫_1^∞ 2t^(−3/2)dt =4.               (BC9)\n\nThus the complete arithmetic infinite-growth core has a finite harmonic\nsum, uniformly bounded by4. This does not rule out even one member of Γ.\nThe result is stronger than a density-zero statement but remains a size\nbound on this particular restricted orbit class, not on all bad starts.\nFor fixed finite r the sets P_r consist of positive arithmetic residue\nclasses and have divergent harmonic sums. BC9 applies only to Γ, the\nintersection over all prefix lengths; it is not a bound for P_r.\n\n## BC5. Localize the open birth-energy problem\n\nFix any exact binary invariant β and start the following procedure at an\nactual birth n. Use the SC growing transition when its input domain and\nactual output masks hold. If they do not hold, SC supplies a birth smaller\nthan the current input, possibly via the two-stage even transfer. Stop\nthe growing block at this first smaller transfer. This procedure has a\nunique growing choice at each stage; no distribution of masks is assumed.\n\nLet I_β be the births for which this procedure never stops, and let R_β\nbe the other births, including those with zero initial growing steps.\nEvery member of I_β lies in Γ. Hence, with\n\n    E_β(X)=Σ_(n≤X)e(n)/n,\n    E_R(X)=Σ_(n≤X,n∈R_β)1/n,\n\nwe have the unconditional identity and bound\n\n    E_β(X)=E_R(X)+Σ_(n≤X,n∈I_β)1/n,\n    0≤E_β(X)−E_R(X)≤4.                                 (BC10)\n\nTherefore Σe(n)/n diverges if and only if the same weighted sum over\nfinite-growth-block births R_β diverges. This implication alone needs no\nPD theorem or positive-density hypothesis. If a separate argument forces\nthe total birth energy to diverge, that argument now forces E_R to diverge.\nThe separate assumptions of the earlier CE/SB/H results are retained;\nBC does not establish their density hypotheses or reverify PD170.\n\nConsequently the energy route does not require a pointwise exclusion of\nevery infinite growing code. A finite total upper bound for E_R would also\nbound E_β. Such an upper bound has not been proved. Likewise a finite\ngrowing block ends below its current endpoint, and may still end above\nits original input: the missing requirement in BC3 has not disappeared.\n\n## BC6. Injective growth and finite-chain origins\n\nThe arithmetic growth map F is injective even when its two branches are\ncombined. Indeed\n\n    H(4t+3)=9t+8,       t≥0,\n    G₃(16t+13)=18t+15, t≥0.                             (BC11)\n\nThe images lie respectively in8 mod9 and6 mod9, so do not overlap;\neach individual branch is injective. Restricting to actual births and\ntheir retained masks preserves this property. The resulting growth\ngraph has in-degree and out-degree at most one and strictly increasing\nedges. A backwards path strictly decreases positive integers, so every\ncomponent has a unique first vertex. Components are finite chains or\none-sided infinite chains. They need not be ordinary orbits of n.\n\nLet A_β be the first vertices of the finite components, and put\nE_A(X)=Σ_(a∈A_β,a≤X)1/a. A finite chain a=n_0\u003cn_1\u003c...\u003cn_L obeys\nn_j≥(9/8)^j a, hence\n\n    1/a ≤Σ_(j=0..L)1/n_j≤9/a.                           (BC12)\n\nThe same upper bound holds for the portion of this chain below X.\nDisjointness of components therefore gives, for every X≥1,\n\n    E_A(X)≤E_R(X)≤9E_A(X),\n    E_A(X)≤E_β(X)≤9E_A(X)+4.                            (BC13)\n\nThus the finite total birth-energy question is equivalent to finite\nharmonic mass of the origins of finite actual growth chains. This\nremoves an exponential sum over all potential growth words from this\nparticular bookkeeping step: an endpoint has at most one actual\ngrowing predecessor. It does not bound E_A. The terminal smaller-birth\ntransfer may return into the same chain or another chain; no decreasing\norder on their origins has been proved.\n\n## What remains open\n\nThe outstanding tasks are either a finite harmonic bound on the finite\nchain origins A_β (equivalently on E_R), with actual masks retained,\nor a pointwise compensation theorem BC3 below the initial birth. Iterating the smaller\ntransfer can return to an earlier index without providing a decreasing\nglobal rank; termination is not supplied by the local block decomposition.\n\nThe finite-parity coding and affine-word mechanisms are classical. The\nbounded combination here is checked directly, without claiming external\nnovelty. No full Collatz proof, positive-cycle exclusion, all-positive\nstopping theorem, or actual realization of a nonconstant coloring is claimed.\n\nFor the classical coding context see [Bernstein–Lagarias, The 3x+1\nConjugacy Map](https://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/bernstein.pdf),\nIntroduction (1.1)–(1.6), and [Elia–Tucker, Consecutive Integers and the\nCollatz Conjecture](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141), Definitions 1–2\nand Proposition 1. These sources do not establish the termination of\nthe present guarded two-mode routing. The finite-code counting used here\nhas the independent elementary proof above.\n\nThe subsequent [OR reduction](INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md)\nclassifies all-component origins by their inverse masks and bounds the\nimage-origin part. It keeps the finite-origin mass and full descent open.\n","PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md":"# PC — all-even birth weights and the critical parity/residue balance\n\n**Later AC clarification (19 September 2026):** the scalar assignment below is feasible only for the stated PC projection. The added joint channel constraints in [AC](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md) exclude the endpoint/origin-negligible divergent regime for actual masks. Full energy finiteness remains open; the old projected feasibility claim is not withdrawn.\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual root derivation\nwith bounded independent checks. No mathematical programs, CAS,\nenumeration or Lean. Previous WS was progress. Full Collatz is incomplete.\n\n## PC1. Objects and the primitive-class limitation\n\nUse the exact binary invariant beta, active boundary d, birth e, energy\nE_beta and retained injective S/R graph from\n[SR](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md). The exact odd active\nancestry A(q)=2q+1 or B(q)=4q+1 is from\n[WS](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md).\nIt must not be confused with iteration of the retained S/R graph.\n\nWrite E_i(X) for birth energy restricted to n=i mod3, and E_even(X)\nfor all even births, including internal vertices. P_1 is exactly the\nresidue-one primitive-origin class. Its even members have a smaller birth\nby SR2; its odd members still have the exact S alternative: a larger\nretained S-birth, or an active S-target with a smaller birth ancestor.\nThose are inherited statements, not a new universal descent.\n\nThe suggested lift n -> 4n+1 never lifts a birth to a birth. If n is odd\nand e(n)=1, beta(4n+1)=beta(n) differs from beta(4n+2)=beta(2n+1), so\nd(4n+1)=1. If n is even, 4n+1=1 mod8, an excluded birth class. Hence\n\n    e(n)=1 implies e(4n+1)=0.                         (PC1)\n\nThis combines already available identities. It does not control P_1.\n\n## PC2. A weight estimate for every even birth\n\nEvery even birth n is 4m. Put q=6m+1. The exact birth colors give\nbeta(q) different from beta(q+1), hence q is an odd active boundary\nin residue 1 modulo3. Distinct n give distinct q, and\n\n    1/n = 3/(2q)+3/[2q(q-1)].                         (PC2)\n\nSince q>=7, the total correction over all such q is at most\n(3/2) sum_(q>=7)1/[q(q-1)]=1/4. This uses distinctness, not a bound\non the number of births.\n\nOverestimate the main charges by allowing every odd active q=1 mod3\nto receive weight 3/(2q). For i=q mod3 the potentials\n\n    (W_0,W_1,W_2)=(1,2,1/4)                           (PC3)\n\nsatisfy\n\n    W_i >= (3/2)1_(i=1)\n               + max(W_(2i+1)/2,W_(i+1)/4).           (PC4)\n\nAll three are equalities: the maxima are 1, 1/2 and 1/4. These\npotentials ALREADY include the factor 3/2 in the reward. A finite-prefix\nbackward induction bounds the entire main charge from q onward by W_i/q.\nThe actual next q is larger than 2q or 4q, which only improves the bound.\nIncreasing finite prefixes gives the infinite version without presuming\nfinite total birth energy.\n\nFor a birth b, the right initial odd boundary is 2b+1. Its coefficients\nin units of 1/b, for b=0,1,2 mod3, are respectively 1, 1/2, 1/8.\nThe first odd boundary on the left, when it exists, has the form\n2^ell b+1 with ell>=2. Before this first odd boundary all child bits\nare zero. When ell is even its residue is b+1, and when ell is odd\nits residue is 2b+1. The maximum left coefficients are therefore\n\n    b=0: W_1/4=1/2;\n    b=1: max(W_2/4,W_0/8)=1/8;\n    b=2: max(W_0/4,W_2/8)=1/4.                        (PC5)\n\nLater depths of the same parity are smaller by powers of four; a path\nthat never becomes odd has no charges of this kind. Since the nearest\nbirth b of q satisfies b\u003c=q/2=(3n+2)/4, this proves, for Y=(3X+2)/4,\n\n    E_even(X) \u003c= (3/2)E_0(Y)+(5/8)E_1(Y)\n                         +(3/8)E_2(Y)+1/4.           (PC6)\n\nThis estimates an entire parity class of vertices, not just endpoints.\nIts largest coefficient remains greater than one. No contraction of\nthe total energy or bound on the complementary odd class follows.\n\n## PC3. Exact global balances in the convergent Dirichlet region\n\nFor real s>1, let A_s and B_s be the sums of n^(-s) over odd and even\nbirths. Set E_s=A_s+B_s. Let Z_S,s and Z_R,s be the corresponding\nterminal-source sums. Let P_s, D_S,s and D_R,s be the three disjoint\norigin sums, so O_s=P_s+D_S,s+D_R,s. Let E_i,s denote all birth sums\nin residue i modulo3. All these series converge absolutely for s>1.\n\nPut a_s=(2/3)^s and b_s=(4/3)^s. For an odd retained source n,\n\n    S(n)^(-s)=a_s n^(-s)-c_s(n),\n    c_s(n)=a_s n^(-s)[1-(1+1/(3n))^(-s)].\n\nLet C_s be the sum of c_s(n) over retained odd sources. Injectivity,\nand the disjoint S and R images, give the exact residue balances\n\n    E_0,s = b_s(B_s-Z_R,s)+D_R,s,\n    E_2,s = a_s(A_s-Z_S,s)-C_s+D_S,s,\n    E_1,s = P_s.                                     (PC7)\n\nAdding them yields\n\n    E_s-O_s = a_s(A_s-Z_S,s)+b_s(B_s-Z_R,s)-C_s,       (PC8)\n    O_s = (1-a_s)A_s+(1-b_s)B_s\n                      +a_s Z_S,s+b_s Z_R,s+C_s.      (PC9)\n\nNo finite-component hypothesis is needed. Cycles and infinite components\nare included because every non-origin birth has precisely one retained\npredecessor and the series are absolutely convergent.\n\nFor 1\u003c=s\u003c=2, 1-(1+x)^(-s)\u003c=s x gives\n\n    0\u003c=c_s(n)\u003c=(4/9)n^(-s-1)\u003c=(4/9)n^(-2).            (PC10)\n\nThus C_s is uniformly bounded and tends to C_1 by dominated convergence.\nIf E_1 is finite, PC7–PC9 pass to s=1. If it is infinite, writing these\nidentities directly as differences of divergent harmonic sums is invalid.\nThe s>1 formulation and normalized limits below avoid that error.\n\n## PC4. A possible limiting balance is not yet excluded\n\nSuppose, conditionally, E_s tends to infinity as s decreases to1. This\nis equivalent to divergence of the harmonic birth energy by monotone\nconvergence. Ratios of any subfamily to E_s lie in [0,1], so any sequence\ns decreasing to1 has a subsequence where the finitely many ratios in\nPC7–PC9 all converge. Write a,b,z_S,z_R,o for the normalized limits of\nA_s,B_s,Z_S,s,Z_R,s,O_s. Then\n\n    a+b=1,\n    3o=a-b+2z_S+4z_R.                                (PC11)\n\nIn particular, if the origins and terminals are negligible relative to\nE_s along that subsequence, then\n\n    a=b=1/2,\n    E_0,s/E_s -> 2/3, E_1,s/E_s -> 0,\n    E_2,s/E_s -> 1/3.                                (PC12)\n\nThe origin partition is nonnegative, so each origin subclass is also\nnegligible under this assumption. Equation PC12 follows from PC7 and\nthe vanishing normalized affine correction.\n\nThese numbers do not contradict the presently proved scalar inequalities.\nFor the normalized form of PC6 it is enough to replace Y by X, since\nY\u003c=X for X>=2 and there are no births below that threshold. Integrating\nthe resulting cumulative inequality against s X^(-s-1) dX gives\nB_s\u003c=(3/2)E_0,s+(5/8)E_1,s+(3/8)E_2,s+1/4. Its bounded correction\nvanishes after division by divergent E_s.\nFor example, allocate normalized even mass 1/2 to residue0, odd mass\n1/6 to residue0, and odd mass 1/3 to residue2. Set all endpoint/origin\nmasses to zero. The PC7–PC11 limiting balances hold; PC6's right-side\ncoefficient becomes 9/8 while its left side is 1/2. WS endpoint bounds\nare satisfied with zero left sides. This is only a feasible assignment\nto these selected scalar constraints. It is NOT a beta coloring, a\nconstruction of actual birth components or a Collatz counterexample.\n\nAccordingly, these projected balances alone cannot force a contradiction.\nA new inequality using masks, longer words or correlations could still\nexclude this assignment for actual invariants. No impossibility theorem\nfor that stronger approach is claimed.\n\n## PC5. Negligible endpoints force a weaker Dirichlet singularity\n\nThe arithmetic forward-prefix domains from SR4 are disjoint residue\nclasses, one for each word of length k, with total density (3/4)^k.\nFor a class r modulo m, 1\u003c=r\u003c=m and s>1, integral comparison gives\n\n    sum_(j>=0)(r+jm)^(-s) \u003c= 1+1/[m(s-1)].\n\nLet H_k(s) sum n^(-s) over integers admitting k forward F-steps.\nConsequently\n\n    H_k(s)\u003c=2^k+(3/4)^k/(s-1).                        (PC13)\n\nEvery birth either admits k retained forward steps or first reaches a\nterminal z at a depth j\u003ck. At a fixed j, injectivity gives at most one\nsource for each terminal. Since F(x)+1\u003c=(3/2)(x+1),\n\n    z \u003c= (3/2)^j(n+1)-1 \u003c= 2(3/2)^j n.\n\nWith Z_s=Z_S,s+Z_R,s, this proves\n\n    E_s \u003c= 2^k+(3/4)^k/(s-1)+K_k(s)Z_s,\n    K_k(s)=sum_(0\u003c=j\u003ck)[2(3/2)^j]^s.                 (PC14)\n\nFor each fixed k, K_k(s) is bounded for 1\u003cs\u003c=2. Therefore, along ANY\nsequence decreasing to1 for which Z_s/E_s tends to zero, multiplying\nPC14 by (s-1), absorbing that ratio and taking the upper limit gives\n\n    limsup (s-1)E_s \u003c= (3/4)^k.\n\nAs k was arbitrary, (s-1)E_s tends to zero along that sequence.\nThe same conclusion follows from O_s/E_s tending to zero: the reverse\nprefix domains have density (2/3)^k and each depth-j origin o obeys\no\u003c=(4/3)^j n, giving the analogous bound with coefficient\nsum_(j\u003ck)(4/3)^(js) on O_s.\n\nThis is a direct analytic consequence of the inherited EC/SR transport,\nnot a new independent density input. A singularity smaller than\n1/(s-1) can still diverge. Thus PC14 does not prove finite energy and\ndoes not exclude the conditional limiting balance PC12.\n\n## PC6. What must be added to finish this route\n\nPC6 now bounds the full even subfamily. PC7–PC9 include all internal\nvertices and all component types, but are signed balance identities;\nin PC9 the coefficient of B_s is negative. Neither these identities\nnor the WS origin bound controls the remaining odd primitive masks.\n\nThe concrete missing input is a mask-sensitive inequality ruling out\nunbounded critical mass, including the endpoint-negligible regime of\nPC12–PC14, or a direct universal smaller-birth descent. A statement\nthat endpoints are merely a small fraction of total energy cannot\nreplace this input. The named conditional PD pointwise bridge is\nunchanged; no PD premise was used in PC1–PC14.\n\nRoot read the abstract/metadata of Berg–Meinardus,\n[The 3n+1 Collatz Problem and Functional Equations](https://www.math.uni-rostock.de/math/pub/romako/heft48/berg2.html),\nRostock Math. Kolloq.48 (1995),11–18. It records earlier analytic\nreformulations; no theorem from it is imported. The linked PostScript\nwas not readable with the web tool. The Mannheim repository copy of\nMeinardus's Dirichlet-series paper was blocked by its access challenge;\nits proof was not read. The separate source report specifies its\nprimary parity-coding readings and their limited relevance here.\nNo priority or novelty claim is made.\n\nIndependent scopes and corrections are recorded in\n[the PC review](REVIEW_0485_PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE.md).\n","PLUS_BIRTH_ROUTING_AND_CRITICAL_WEIGHTS.md":"# PB — selected plus defects, smaller births and critical inverse weights\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nPrevious SP turn made progress and updated the authoritative record. This\npass audits its next candidate. No mathematical program, enumeration, CAS,\nLean or GPU. Full Collatz remains unresolved; no external blocker.\n\n## PB1. Exact paths, with an arithmetic selector\n\nKeep T, the exact binary T-invariant beta, g, h, d, e and G from\n[SP](SHIFTED_BOUNDARY_DESCENT_AND_CORE.md) and\n[BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md). In particular\n\n    h(n)=|beta(3n)-beta(n)|,\n    e(z)=1 iff beta(z)=beta(z+1) differs from beta(2z+1).\n\nFor positive odd u set\n\n    p=4u+1, z=3u, r=(3u+1)/2=2^a w,\n    a>=0, w positive odd, C_j=beta(3^j w-1), j>=1.\n\nThe plus selector sigma(u)=+ means 3^(a+2)w=3 modulo4. Its complement\nsigma(u)=- means 3^(a+1)w=3 modulo4. Exactly one arithmetic condition\nholds. Plus implies C_(a+2)=C_(a+3) by SC's common-endpoint merge; that\ncolor equality alone does NOT imply the arithmetic selector.\n\nThe genuine paths p->4r->2r->r and u->r, and the identities\n3p=8r-1, z=2r-1, z+1=2r, 2z+1=4r-1, give\n\n    beta(p)=beta(u)=beta(w),\n    beta(3p)=C_(a+3), beta(z)=C_(a+1),\n    beta(z+1)=beta(w), beta(2z+1)=C_(a+2).          (PB1)\n\nFor the odd-word values use the exact SC identity\nbeta(2^j w-1)=beta(3^j w-1). All indices are positive. This is not\nan assertion that the displayed affine identities are single T steps.\n\n## PB2. A binary event identity, not a closed two-type descent\n\nOn sigma(u)=+, put A=C_(a+1), B=beta(w), C=C_(a+2)=C_(a+3) and define\nell(u)=1 iff A differs from B=C. Direct binary case distinction gives\n\n    h(p)=h(u)+e(3u)-ell(u),\n    h(u)e(3u)=0,\n    e(3u)=(1-h(u))h(p), ell(u)=h(u)(1-h(p)).       (PB2)\n\nConsequently h(p)\u003c=h(u)+e(3u). A nonzero left side either has a smaller\ndefect at u, or a smaller birth at 3u; both indices are strictly below p.\nThe second alternative changes the quantity being followed. It supplies\nno rule reducing that birth to another defect or birth below itself.\nThe reverse identity for e in PB2 also shows that the birth charge is\nprecisely the unaccounted creation event of this same local comparison.\n\nFor EVERY positive odd u, even before imposing plus, e(3u)=1 implies plus.\nIndeed PB1 makes e(3u)=1 imply C_(a+1)!=C_(a+2). If\n3^(a+1)w=3 mod4, SC would make these equal. Thus it is1 mod4,\nand 3^(a+2)w=3 mod4. Also beta(3u+1)=beta(u), so h(u)e(3u)=0\nwithout any additional guard. This does not extend PB2 to minus inputs.\nThe edge u=1 (p=5,z=3) is positive and compatible with the finite cycle.\n\nThe single-source weight bound is\n\n    sum_(u odd,sigma=+,4u+1\u003c=X) h(4u+1)/(4u+1)\n      \u003c=(1/4) sum_(same u) h(u)/u\n         +(3/4) sum_(z=3u,same u) e(z)/z.         (PB3)\n\nThe birth term is not known finite. Dropping ell is an upper bound, not\na proof that it has any quantitatively useful positive mass.\n\n## PB3. Exact complementarity and complete inverse fibers\n\nNow restrict to the SP targets. Define\n\n    A_+={u positive odd:3|u, sigma(u)=+},\n    A_-={u positive odd:3|u, sigma(u)=-},\n    P_+={4u+1:u in A_+}.\n\nP_+ is EXACTLY the set of targets of SP's N_+ sources. A source\nn=1+2^(2k+4)m with odd m has plus target p=1+4*3^(k+1)m;\nput u=3^(k+1)m. Conversely any u in A_+ has at least the k=0\nsource with m=u/3. The terminal boundary is q=6u+1 in both descriptions.\n\nThat is also the terminal q of an N_- fiber targeting u. It selects\nminus precisely when sigma(u)=-. Therefore no N_- fiber targets A_+.\nThis is target exclusivity, stronger than disjointness of source classes.\n\nFor u in A_+, let v=v3(u)>=1. Its p itself, together with all N_+ sources\nmapping to p, consists exactly of\n\n    n_j(u)=1+4u(4/3)^j, 0\u003c=j\u003c=v.                  (PB4)\n\nFor j>=1, v2(n_j-1)=2j+2 and m=u/3^j recover k=j-1 and the unique\nSP source. For j=0, n_0=p has valuation2 and is outside N. Thus the\nfibers are mutually disjoint, and n_j increases strictly with j.\nAll have h(n_j)=h(p); for j>=1 this is SP's signed equality.\n\nAt finite cutoff retain the EXACT weight\n\n    W_X(u)=sum_(0\u003c=j\u003c=v,n_j(u)\u003c=X) 1/n_j(u).\n\nIt is nonzero only if p\u003c=X; replacing it by the complete fiber weight\nis an upper bound, not an equality at arbitrary cutoff.\n\n## PB4. Critical coefficient and an explicit arithmetic limitation\n\nWrite W(u) for the complete fiber weight and rho_v=(3/4)^(v+1). Set\n\n    W_0(u)=(1-rho_v)/u.\n\nGeometric summation and the positive +1 in every denominator give\n\n    0\u003cW_0(u)-W(u)\u003c=1/(7u^2),\n    W_X(u)\u003c=W(u)\u003c(1-rho_v)/u\u003c1/u.                 (PB5)\n\nIndeed, for b_j=4u(4/3)^j, each denominator error is\n1/b_j-1/(b_j+1)=1/[b_j(b_j+1)]\u003c=1/b_j^2, and their sum is at most\n(1/(16u^2))*sum_(j>=0)(9/16)^j=1/(7u^2). The leading finite geometric\nsum is W_0. No passage to an unknown infinite defect mass is used.\n\nThe strict inequality in each fiber does NOT give a uniform coefficient\nc\u003c1. Take the purely arithmetic family u_k=7*9^k, k>=1. It belongs to\nA_+: u_k=7 mod8 makes r odd and r=3 mod4. Its valuation is v=2k.\nBy PB5,\n\n    1-(3/4)^(2k+1)-1/(7u_k) \u003c=u_k W(u_k)\n                              \u003c1-(3/4)^(2k+1),\n    u_k W(u_k) ->1.                               (PB6)\n\nChoosing X>=n_(2k)(u_k) includes each whole finite fiber. Thus no uniform\nW_X(u)\u003c=c/u with c\u003c1 holds on all arithmetic plus targets and cutoffs.\nThis is a limitation of the arithmetic weights only. No h(u_k)=1,\ne(3u_k)=1, nonconstant invariant, or Collatz counterexample is asserted.\n\n## PB5. A finite-cutoff interface isolating births divisible by nine\n\nUse G_S(X)=sum_(n odd,n\u003c=X,n in S)h(n)/n. Put\n\n    M(X)=G_N(X)+G_(P_+)(X),\n    D(X)=sum_(u in A_+,4u+1\u003c=X) W_X(u) ell(u),\n    G_0(X)=sum_(u odd,3|u,u\u003c=X) h(u)/u,\n    Omega(X)=(1/4)G_(A_-)(X)\n                +sum_(u in A_+,u\u003c=X) rho_(v3(u)) h(u)/u,\n    E_9(Y)=sum_(z odd,9|z,z\u003c=Y) e(z)/z,\n\nwith sums empty below their first positive argument. Exact reindexing and\nPB2 first give\n\n    G_(N_+)(X)+G_(P_+)(X)+D(X)\n       =sum_(u in A_+,4u+1\u003c=X) W_X(u)[h(u)+e(3u)]. (PB7)\n\nSP gives G_(N_-)(X)\u003c=(3/4)G_(A_-)(X). For the h part of PB7 use PB5\nand extend its positive upper bound to u\u003c=X. A_+ and A_- partition all\nodd multiples of3. For the birth part, PB2 implies that every nonzero\ne(3u) with u odd and divisible by3 is already on A_+; no birth is lost\nby removing that selector. Since p\u003c=X means 3u\u003c=3(X-1)/4, we obtain\n\n    M(X)+D(X)+Omega(X)\n       \u003c= G_0(X)+3E_9(3(X-1)/4), X>=1.           (PB8)\n\nThere is no cutoff error or convergence assumption in this inequality.\nThe full G_0 term has coefficient1. Omega is nonnegative, but its plus\ncoefficient rho_v has no uniform positive lower bound. Neither D nor E_9\nhas the extra bound needed to close the inequality. In particular, using\nSB's equivalence of finite E and finite G to assume E_9 finite here would\nleave an unproved input. SP's established Cstar bound remains unchanged.\n\n## PB6. The residual arithmetic class still contains an old obstruction\n\nThe E_9 term has not escaped the earlier primitive-origin problem. The\nfamily from [OR5](INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md),\n\n    z_t=144t+99, u_t=z_t/3=48t+33, p_t=4u_t+1, t>=0,\n\nhas z_t odd and divisible by9. Here u_t=1 mod16 makes a=1,w=1 mod4,\nso sigma(u_t)=+. These z_t lie in3 mod16 and outside the two arithmetic\ngrowth-image classes8 and6 mod9. Their only arithmetic growing step is\n\n    H(z_t)=324t+224=0 mod4,\n\nwhere the growing map F is undefined. Thus the arithmetic candidate class\nincludes the same finite primitive origins already recorded in OR5.\nIts unrestricted harmonic sum diverges, since z_t\u003c=144(t+1). This does\nnot say any of those indices is an actual birth. It excludes only a bound\nobtained by deleting the actual color masks and counting that arithmetic\nsuperset. Actual E_9 summability remains an open quantitative question.\n\n## PB7. Sources, audit boundary and next step\n\nThe color-strip and merge inputs are in\n[SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md); the birth definition and\nrouting limitations are in BR; the source fibers are audited from SP.\nThe binary event structure is adjacent to the prior birth-mask and\n[DF](DEFECT_FLUX_AND_VARIATION_OBSTRUCTION.md) toggle laws, not an entirely\nnew type of invariant. The exact selected identity was not found in the\nbounded nearby archive search; that is not a literature novelty claim.\n\nRoot and the scope role freshly read Rozier–Terracol's\n[primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948), equation(2) and the\nparagraph after Lemma2.3. It supplies the classical finite affine/parity\nframework only; PB1–PB8 and no birth-energy theorem are attributed to it.\n\nNext test the actual E_9 masks jointly with D and the valuation-dependent\ngap Omega, or find a new smaller-birth route for this exact class. A uniform\ncoefficient inferred solely from W\u003c1/u is now ruled out by PB6. Merely\nrenaming E_9 as h(p) where h(u)=0 returns the same event and is not closure.\nNo finite G, finite birth energy, universal descent, or full proof is obtained.\nPD170 is not used. Historical R170 acceptance and its unrecertified full\nanalytic/Lean closure remain unchanged. All new work is local.\n","POLYNOMIAL_BOUNDARY_BIRTH_LOWER_BOUND.md":"# PBK — polynomial lower bound for exact boundary births\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual analytic derivation.\nNo numerical experiments, enumeration, CAS, Lean, or mathematical programs.\nThis is a necessary condition for a hypothetical nonconstant invariant,\nnot a proof of the Collatz conjecture or of existence of such an invariant.\n\n## 1. Statement and named inputs\n\nLet T(n)=n/2 for even n and T(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n. Suppose\nβ:N₊→{0,1} satisfies β(T(n))=β(n) for every n, and both color sets have\nlower natural density at least δ, where 0\u003cδ≤1/2. Put\n\n    d(n) = |β(n+1)−β(n)|,\n    e(n) = d(2n)d(2n+1),\n    B(X) = Σ_{1≤n\u003cX} d(n),\n    R(X) = Σ_{1≤n\u003cX} e(n).\n\nA birth e(n)=1 means that the two child edges are boundaries although\nthe parent edge is not: d(2n)=d(2n+1)=1 and d(n)=0.\n\nAssume θ=log₂3 satisfies, for some c>0 and κ≥1,\n\n    ||qθ|| ≥ c q^(−κ)       for every integer q≥1.                 (PBK1)\n\nThen there are constants D>0 and X₀ such that\n\n    R(X) ≥ D X^(1/(κ+2))       for every real X≥X₀.                (PBK2)\n\nD may be chosen in terms of δ,c,κ alone. The threshold also depends on\nthe threshold in the two lower-density assumptions for this β.\nFor θ=log₂3 the classical input below permits κ=8, giving exponent 1/10.\n\nThe project input PD170 implies the two positive color densities for any\nnonconstant exact β: each nonempty color contains a target not divisible\nby 3 and all of its predecessors. To see the first fact, remove factors\nof 2 from an element of that color; if its odd part is divisible by 3,\napply the odd branch once. The resulting target is a 3-unit in the same\ncolor. Thus PBK2 applies to a nonconstant exact invariant under PD170.\nThe theorem as stated only assumes the two densities, independently of\nhow they are obtained. C180 and its coalescence estimates are not inputs.\n\n## 2. The classical Diophantine input\n\n[Lagarias–Soundararajan, arXiv:math/0509175v2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0509175v2),\nDefinition 4.2 and Lemma 5.3, gives Diophantine exponent 7.616 for\n(log_B(3/2),log_B(1/2)). At B=2 its second component is an integer and\nthe first differs from θ by an integer, so PBK1 holds with κ=7.616.\nWeakening the exponent to 8 is sufficient here.\n\nTheir proof attributes the logarithmic estimate to Rhin's Padé method,\nequation (5.11). Equivalently, set u₀=0,u₁=−p,u₂=q there, with p nearest\nto qθ and |p|≤2q. Then |q ln3−p ln2|=(ln2)||qθ|| and the same conclusion\nfollows after adjusting the positive constant.\n\nThis is classical input, not a new estimate of logarithms. Definition 4.2\ncontains ||qθᵢ||, with no extra factor q outside the norm. The norms are\nbadly represented in the extracted PDF text; see the source audit for the\ncorrected reading. The original Rhin proof has not been newly re-proved.\n\n## 3. A polynomial gap for step functions on a circle\n\nLet h:R/Z→{0,1} be a step function with at most M jumps, where M≥1, and let\n\n    ρ = ∫₀¹h(t)dt,    a≤ρ≤1−a,    0\u003ca≤1/2,\n    A = a(1−a),       dθ(h) = ||h(·+θ)−h||₁.\n\nFor every integer Q≥1, the pigeonhole principle gives 1≤q≤Q with\n0\u003cτ=||qθ||≤1/Q. The points jτ, 0≤j≤floor(1/τ), form a 1/Q-net on\nthe circle. They are signed multiples lθ with\n\n    |l| ≤ q/τ ≤ c^(−1) q^(κ+1) ≤ c^(−1)Q^(κ+1).                 (PBK3)\n\nNo denominator or orbit has to be found computationally. For step functions,\n\n    ||h(·+s)−h(·+t)||₁ ≤ M||s−t||.\n\nApproximate an arbitrary shift s by one of the multiples in PBK3 and\ntelescope along its |l| rotations. This gives\n\n    ||h(·+s)−h||₁ ≤ c^(−1)Q^(κ+1)dθ(h)+M/Q.                    (PBK4)\n\nThe integral of the left side over all s is exactly 2ρ(1−ρ). With\nQ=ceil(M/A), the last term is at most A, while 2ρ(1−ρ)≥2A. Hence\n\n    dθ(h) ≥ c A Q^(−κ−1) ≥ L M^(−κ−1),\n    L = c A(1+A^(−1))^(−κ−1) > 0.                              (PBK5)\n\nThis bound is uniform in the positions of the jumps. It is not a spectral\ngap for all measurable functions: it decreases with M. A binary periodic\nfunction with both masses positive actually has at least two jumps;\nallowing the formal upper bound M≥1 creates no exceptional case.\n\n## 4. Positive mass in every late dyadic window\n\nLet N=2^k and F_k(x)=β(floor(Nx)) on I=[1,2). Define\n\n    H₀ = ceil(log₂(4/δ)),    v = δ/(2H₀),    a = v/4.\n\nFor either color, write w(k) for its fraction in [2^k,2^(k+1)).\nExact doubling β(2n)=β(n) embeds the color occurrences in each of the\nlast H₀ windows injectively into the current window. Thus for k≥H₀,\n\n    #{n\u003c2^(k+1): β(n)=color}/2^(k+1)\n       ≤ 2^(−H₀)+(H₀/2)w(k).\n\nThe left side is at least δ/2 for all sufficiently large k, simultaneously\nfor both colors. Since 2^(−H₀)≤δ/4, both window masses are at least v.\nFor h_k(t)=F_k(2^t), the change of variables dx=(ln2)2^t dt≤2dt\nshows that both circle masses are at least v/2, and therefore at least a.\nCall a valid common threshold K_β(δ). This is the QP176.2 argument with\nthe lower-density threshold retained explicitly.\n\n## 5. The pointwise QP176 estimate allows growing M\n\nThe exact local odd-branch estimate already proved in\n[QP176, QP4–QP10](../../../work/round176-k-quantitative-profile-obstruction.md)\nstates that for every k≥4 and every binary H on at most M intervals of I,\n\n    ε=||F_k−H||₁,\n    h(t)=H(2^t),\n    dθ(h) ≤ (64ε+69M2^(−k))/ln2.                                (PBK6)\n\nFor clarity, its inputs are finite arithmetic and edge counting: exact\ndoubling controls the previous two scales by\n||F_(k−r)−H||₁≤2^rε+(M−1)2^r/N, r=1,2. Extending H by doubling to\n[1/4,2) gives error L₀≤3ε+2(M−1)/N. The sum of adjacent edge errors on\n[N/4,2N−2], divided by N, is at most 2L₀+(3M−1)/N. The exact odd\nbranch bounds ||F_k−F_k(·/3)||₁ by nine times that sum plus 24/N;\nreplacing F_k by H adds ε+3L₀. These are the constants in PBK6.\nThe four possible outer edges are included in 24/N.\n\nNow choose H=F_k itself, and let its actual number of constant intervals be\n\n    M_k = 1+Σ_{N≤n≤2N−2}d(n).                                   (PBK7)\n\nThen ε=0. The circle version h_k has at most M_k jumps, counting the\npossible seam at 0. PBK6 is a pointwise bound in k and M; no limit with\na fixed M is used. Consequently it is legal to take M=M_k, even when\nM_k grows without bound. Combining PBK5 and PBK6 for k≥max(4,K_β(δ)),\n\n    L M_k^(−κ−1) ≤ 69M_k/(N ln2),\n    M_k ≥ D₀ N^γ,\n    γ = 1/(κ+2),    D₀ = (L ln2/69)^γ > 0.                      (PBK8)\n\n## 6. Exact conversion from window jumps to births\n\nThe binary relation d(2n) XOR d(2n+1)=d(n) gives an integer identity\n\n    d(2n)+d(2n+1)=d(n)+2e(n).\n\nAlso d(1)=0 because β(1)=β(2). Summing for 1≤n\u003cN yields, for each\ninteger N≥1, the existing FB3 identity\n\n    B(2N)=B(N)+2R(N).                                            (PBK9)\n\nPBK7 omits only the possible edge 2N−1 from the window boundary count.\nThus\n\n    M_k ≤ 1+B(2N)−B(N)=1+2R(N).\n\nTogether with PBK8 this implies\n\n    R(2^k) ≥ (D₀ 2^(kγ)−1)/2 ≥ (D₀/4)2^(kγ)                   (PBK10)\n\nonce k is large enough that D₀2^(kγ)≥2. For any sufficiently large real\nX choose N=2^floor(log₂X), so N≤X\u003c2N. Monotonicity then gives\n\n    R(X) ≥ R(N) ≥ D X^γ,    D=D₀/(4·2^γ).                       (PBK11)\n\nThis proves PBK2 for every large cutoff, not just a subsequence.\nOne also obtains B(X)≥2R(floor(X/2)) for large X from PBK9 and\nmonotonicity, hence a polynomial boundary lower bound with another constant.\nBirths are not themselves boundary edges at their parent indices;\nthe last conclusion uses PBK9, not an unjustified assertion B(X)≥R(X).\n\n## 7. Scope and remaining gap\n\nFor κ=8, PBK11 requires R(X)≥DX^(1/10) under the stated hypotheses.\nThe exponent is conservative, not optimized. The finite-grid idea already\nexists in QP176; PBK replaces its elementary exponential loss by a known\nDiophantine estimate and applies the resulting bound to actual birth counts.\nThis is a refinement within this project; worldwide novelty is not asserted.\n\nEarlier [FB177](../../../work/round177-n-finite-birth-rigidity.md) proves\nR(X)→∞ for nonconstant exact invariants without PD. Earlier\n[SB174](../../../work/round174-n-summable-boundary-rigidity.md) excludes\nsummable boundary tails under PD; that qualitative/global restriction is\ndifferent from PBK's polynomial lower bound at every sufficiently large cutoff.\n\nThe C180/R182 upper estimate B(X)≲X/(log X)^r for r\u003c1/2 remains compatible\nwith PBK. Neither positive boundary density nor constant β follows. The\nleast bad positive integer, nontrivial cycles, and divergent integer orbits\nare not excluded by this result. No percentage or completion time follows.\n\nNext substantive obligation: obtain an upper bound for these same births\nthat contradicts PBK11, or a pointwise descent preserving a fixed integer\nstart. Repeating the XOR identity or assuming pair mixing supplies neither.\n","POSITIVE_INTEGER_RETURN_RESOLVENT.md":"# PR: positive-integer inverse weights and the critical exponent\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nNo mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS, or Lean. Independent internal\naudits are linked below. The full Collatz conjecture remains open in this work.\nThis is an elementary deterministic-tree argument; novelty is not claimed.\n\n## PR1. Domain and deterministic induced map\n\nKeep the shortcut map T(x)=x/2 for even x and (3x+1)/2 for odd x.\nUse the global alphabet of minimal ordered structural blocks established\nin [GB/IR](GENERAL_STRUCTURAL_BLOCK_CONTROL.md). A return requires both\nordered adjacency and equal odd counts. Its source cylinders are disjoint.\nConsequently every positive integer m has at most one outgoing block:\nthe partial deterministic map R sends the pair (m,m+1) to (R(m),R(m)+1).\nR need not be defined everywhere. Its definition does not assume Collatz.\n\nAn inverse block has F_i(n)=alpha_i*n-beta_i, where\nalpha_i=2^K_i/3^P_i, beta_i=C_i/3^P_i>0. The first odd contribution\nto C_i is at least 3^(P_i-1), hence beta_i>=1/3.\nEvery nonempty composite has the same properties, with total K,P,C.\n\nAt a positive integer n, each 3-adic terminal guard is ordinary integer\ndivisibility. Integrality forces the prescribed parity word, by the\nprevious first-bit induction. Its source must be positive: T sends all\nnonpositive integers to nonpositive integers. Thus the guarded inverse\nschedules at a positive integer are precisely positive genuine schedules;\nno additional unproved positivity condition is being substituted.\n\nLet W_q(n) be the sum of A_w^(-q) over all finite inverse block schedules\nstarting at n, including the empty schedule with weight1. Distinct words\nare counted; repeated endpoints have not yet been identified. Here q>0\nand A_w=2^K_w/3^P_w. At q=1 this is the GB/IR resolvent at n.\n\n## PR2. Positivity bounds every individual weight\n\nFor a nonempty schedule ending at the positive integer m,\n\n    m=A_w*n-B_w,   B_w=C_w/3^P_w>=1/3.\n\nIt follows that\n\n    A_w=(m+B_w)/n>m/n,\n    A_w^(-q)\u003c(n/m)^q.                         (PR1)\n\nThe empty word satisfies the weak inequality with equality. Contracting\nindividual blocks are allowed without restriction. Any consecutive run\nof them strictly decreases positive integer coordinates, so has length\nat most its starting coordinate minus1. This does not bound alternating\ncontractions and expansions, which can replenish the coordinate.\n\n## PR3. Exactly how endpoint multiplicities arise\n\nWrite M(n)={m>=1: R^d(m)=n for some integer d>=0}. For each m in M(n),\nlet d_m be the least such d, and A_m the inverse slope of that first-hit\npath. Determinism gives one path of any specified depth from m.\n\nIf n is not periodic under R, its hitting time from a given m is unique:\ntwo hits with times d\u003ce would imply R^(e-d)(n)=n. Therefore one inverse\nword corresponds to each m, including m=n and the empty word.\n\nIf n is periodic, let r>=1 be its least R-period and A_c the inverse\nslope of this one cycle. Every hitting time from m is exactly d_m+t*r,\nt>=0. To justify this, start at the first hit and follow the unique cycle;\nits returns occur precisely at multiples of r. The corresponding inverse\nslope is A_m*A_c^t. A positive cycle satisfies\n\n    n=A_c*n-B_c,   B_c>=1/3,\n    A_c=1+B_c/n>=1+1/(3n)>1.                 (PR2)\n\nThus repetitions of a positive cycle have summable inverse weights.\nThis does not rule out unknown positive Collatz cycles; it handles them\nconditionally whenever they occur, without needing their classification.\n\nDefine c_q(n)=1 for a nonperiodic n, and\nc_q(n)=1/(1-A_c^(-q)) for a periodic n. Nonnegative summation gives\n\n    W_q(n)=c_q(n) * sum_(m in M(n)) A_m^(-q). (PR3)\n\nFor q>=1, PR2 yields c_q(n)\u003c=3n+1 in both cases.\n\n## PR4. Pointwise finiteness for every q>1\n\nCombining PR1 and PR3 with the convergent integer series gives\n\n    W_q(n)\u003c=c_q(n)*n^q*sum_(m in M(n)) m^(-q)\n           \u003c=(3n+1)*n^q*zeta(q)\u003cinfinity,     (PR4)\n\nfor every positive integer n and every q>1. The alphabet can be infinite,\nits slopes may be below1, and arbitrary alternation is included. No uniform\nconstant over n is asserted, in agreement with the GB/IR obstruction.\n\nThis argument also works for the ordinary shortcut inverse tree: an even\ninverse edge has beta=0, so PR1 becomes weak, while every positive cycle\ncontains an odd step and still has B_c>=1/3. This generality shows that\nPR4 is an elementary tree bound, not a special mechanism resolving Collatz.\nThe only structural input is a deterministic induced return map.\n\n## PR5. The unresolved critical case is explicit\n\nFor X>=1 let W_1(n;X) count all schedules whose final source m\u003c=X,\nincluding arbitrarily many cycle repetitions. Equation PR3 gives\n\n    W_1(n;X)\u003c=c_1(n)*n*sum_(m\u003c=X) 1/m\n             \u003c=(3n+1)*n*(1+log X).          (PR5)\n\nThe bound increases with X. It does not prove W_1(n)\u003cinfinity, nor may\none set q=1 in PR4: zeta(q) diverges as q decreases to1. The Haar bound\nfor q=1 from GB/IR is an average on Z_3 and does not remove this issue.\n\nA sufficient, independently meaningful extra estimate would be\nsum_(m in M(n))1/m\u003cinfinity. For example, if for one fixed seed\nN_n(X)=#(M(n) intersect [1,X]) is at most\nC_n*X/(log X)^(1+epsilon) for some epsilon>0 and all sufficiently\nlarge X, partial summation would supply it. Such an estimate has NOT\nbeen proved here. It is sufficient, not necessary, since PR1 discards\nthe nonnegative intercept and may overestimate actual weights.\n\nEven pointwise finiteness at q=1 would only control this structural\npredecessor family. It would not give coverage of arbitrary pairs with\nunequal odd counts, a positive lower density for Q(a,c), or universal\nforward descent. Those separate gaps must remain visible.\n\n## PR6. Fixed positive seeds cannot follow the known contraction forever\n\nThe ordered contraction from GB/IR is\n\n    H(n)=(4194304*n-18252850)/4782969,\n    alpha=4194304/4782969\u003c1,  a=4310/139>0.\n\nIt satisfies H^t(n)+a=alpha^t*(n+a). The earlier\n[fixed repetition lemma](FIXED_PAIR_LIFT_REPETITION.md) gives, because\n2^22-3^14=-4235*139 and 18252850=4235*4310 with 3 not dividing4235,\n\n    H^t(n) is an integer iff 3^(14t) divides 139n+4310. (PR6)\n\nFor a fixed positive integer n, 139n+4310 is nonzero, so the maximal\nintegral repetition count is floor(v_3(139n+4310)/14). Positivity also\ngives the necessary bound\n\n    t\u003c=log((n+a)/(1+a))/log(1/alpha).         (PR7)\n\nIn fact integrality at a positive terminal seed already implies positivity\nof the entire genuine word as in PR1, so the valuation budget is exact\nfor admissible H repetitions; PR7 is a consistent extra real constraint,\nnot an independent reason to assume that a permitted integer path exists.\n\nFor any positive seed allowing t repetitions, ordinary integer size gives\n\n    139n+4310>=3^(14t),\n    n>=(3^(14t)-4310)/139.                  (PR8)\n\nThus the previously constructed seeds for increasingly long contraction\nruns must escape exponentially with t. This quantitative version does\nnot control arbitrary changing schedules. Fixed-period integrality was\nalready proved in FL; this section is an application, not a new principle.\n\n## PR7. Qualitative escape of the total inverse slope\n\nFix n>0 and any one q>1. For every real B>0, PR4 implies that there\nare only finitely many finite schedules w with A_w\u003c=B: each contributes\nat least B^(-q) to a finite nonnegative sum. Hence for every B there\nexists a block-depth d_0(n,B) beyond which every existing admissible\nschedule has A_w>B. If no schedules exist at those depths, this statement\nis vacuous. In particular, every infinite positive inverse branch has\n\n    A_w=2^K_w/3^P_w -> infinity.             (PR9)\n\nThis allows arbitrary alternation of contractions and expansions. Another\nproof uses determinism: an inverse branch either stays on the seed's\ncycle, whose inverse slope per circuit exceeds1, or leaves that cycle\npermanently (or starts outside any cycle). Thereafter its endpoints are\ndistinct positive integers, so tend to infinity and PR1 applies.\n\nThe existence of d_0 is qualitative. A finite summable collection of\nlow-slope words gives no computable bound here on their largest depth.\nThis is about backward branches at a fixed target; it is not a theorem\nthat forward orbits from arbitrary positive starts descend or converge.\n\n## PR8. Why the generic argument cannot alone prove q=1\n\nConsider an abstract partial deterministic map, not Collatz:\nR(m)=1 for all integers m>=2, and R(1) is undefined. For each m>=2\nits inverse branch on the single-point domain {1} is represented by\n\n    F_m(x)=(m+1)*x-1,   F_m(1)=m.\n\nAll intercepts are positive and at least1/3; endpoints are positive,\nunique, and the seed is not periodic. The only schedules from1 are\nempty or one edge. Nevertheless\n\n    W_q(1)=1+sum_(m>=2)(m+1)^(-q)\n\nis finite for q>1 and infinite for q=1. Thus the generic inputs used in\nPR1--PR4 cannot by themselves prove the critical bound. This example\ndoes not satisfy or claim Collatz parity-word or residue-cylinder rules;\nit is a counterexample only to an abstract strengthening. Collatz-specific\narithmetic remains a possible additional input, and is still missing here.\n\n## Primary-source comparison and audits\n\nRoot read the introduction and section1, including Theorem1 and its proof,\nof [Wirsching's primary preliminary paper](https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/baake/algdyn/posden.pdf).\nIt already distinguishes paths from endpoints, injectivity for noncyclic\ntargets, and the affine source-size inequality. Its density program has\nexplicit conjectural gaps. These are close precedents for our elementary\ninputs, not a theorem supplying the critical weighted estimate.\n\n- [0459: positivity and the exact repeated-contraction budget](FOLLOWUP_0459_FIXED_SEED_POSITIVITY.md).\n- [0485: deterministic tree, cycles, pointwise bound and limits](FOLLOWUP_0485_POINTWISE_RETURN_TREE.md).\n- [0483: primary sources and bounded comparison](FOLLOWUP_0483_POINTWISE_TREE_SOURCES.md).\n\n## Scope and next step\n\nPR4 supplies fixed-integer control in the easier range q>1 for the entire\nstructural alphabet, rather than a finite list of blocks. The remaining\ncritical question is q=1, where the raw integer comparison is harmonic.\nThe wider Collatz goal still additionally requires control of all actual\ntrajectories. No percentage of completion or reliable time to proof follows.\n\nThe next missing argument must use more than generic tree positivity: for\nexample a verified arithmetic estimate on the critical first-hit sum.\nIt must also be related explicitly to actual forward coverage before it\ncould support the full Collatz goal. Internal audits are not external review.\n","RESIDUE_FILTERED_LAST_CHANGE_WEIGHTS.md":"# RF — exact residue filters for nonprimitive last-change weights\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation;\ntwo independent manual arithmetic checks have been read. This is a local\nworking result: PB's final scope audit is still running, so neither this\nnote nor TL is registered as having a completed four-role acceptance.\nNo numerical programs, enumeration, CAS, Lean or GPU were used.\n\n## RF1. Objects and the retained arithmetic information\n\nUse the shortcut Collatz map T and an arbitrary exact binary T-invariant\nbeta. Put g(t)=beta(3t)-beta(t), h(t)=|g(t)|. An actual birth e(z)=1\nmeans beta(z)=beta(z+1) differs from beta(2z+1). The existence of any\nnonconstant invariant or birth is not assumed.\n\nFor a nonprimitive birth divisible by nine, [TL](TERNARY_LAST_CHANGE_AND_SIGNED_BUNDLE_TRANSFER.md)\nselects its unique last preceding ternary change t:\n\n    z=3^k t, k>=2, h(t)=1,\n    h(3t)=...=h(3^(k-1)t)=0.\n\nIts PB input and first fiber member are u=3^(k-1)t and p=4u+1.\nWrite v=v3(t), alpha=3/4. The full fiber upper weight from\n[PB](PLUS_BIRTH_ROUTING_AND_CRITICAL_WEIGHTS.md) is\n\n    W_X(u)\u003c=W(u)\u003c[1-alpha^(v+k)]/[3^(k-1)t].       (RF1)\n\nCrucially, the actual birth forces PB's arithmetic selector sigma(u)=+.\nThis information was discarded in TL6 when all k>=2 were summed. It\ncan be retained without assigning free colors or assuming finite energy.\n\n## RF2. The selector on a complete multiplication-by-three residue orbit\n\nFor odd u, put r=(3u+1)/2=2^a w with w odd. The plus condition is\n3^(a+2)w=3 modulo4. For u in Q={1,3,9,11} modulo16:\n\n| u modulo16 | Exact information about r | Selector |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | r=2 modulo8, a=1, w=1 modulo4 | plus |\n| 3 | r=5 modulo8, a=0, w=1 modulo4 | minus |\n| 9 | r=6 modulo8, a=1, w=3 modulo4 | minus |\n| 11 | r=1 modulo8, a=0, w=1 modulo4 | minus |\n\nThe entries for r are congruences, not exact values.\nEach conclusion is unchanged when u is increased by16.\nThus sigma(u)=+ iff u=1 modulo16 on this orbit. Multiplication by3\ncycles 1 ->3 ->9 ->11 ->1. Consequently:\n\n| t modulo16 | Necessary k modulo4 | Least k>=2, called K(t) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 5 |\n| 3 | 0 | 4 |\n| 9 | 3 | 3 |\n| 11 | 2 | 2 |\n\nWithin Q this is an exact condition for the arithmetic selector at u;\nit is only a necessary condition for an actual birth. It does not\ndescribe the other orbit {5,7,13,15} modulo16.\n\n## RF3. Full inverse weight with the residue filter\n\nFor a target in Q only k=K+4j, j>=0, can contribute. Summing RF1 gives\n\n    C_K(v)=sum_(j>=0)3^(1-K-4j)[1-alpha^(v+K+4j)]\n          =3^(5-K)/80-[3*4^(4-K)/255]alpha^v.       (RF2)\n\nThe first geometric series has ratio1/81. The second equals\n3*alpha^v*sum_(j>=0)4^(-K-4j), with ratio1/256. In particular:\n\n| K | C_K(v) |\n|---|---|\n| 5 | 1/80 - alpha^v/340 |\n| 4 | 3/80 - alpha^v/85 |\n| 3 | 9/80 - 4alpha^v/85 |\n| 2 | 27/80 - 16alpha^v/85 |\n\nLet Jnp,Q(X) retain the TL nonprimitive fiber mass whose last-change\ntarget belongs to Q. Its actual source fibers are disjoint: a member\nn_l(u) has v2(n_l-1)=2l+2, recovering l, and then\nu=(n_l-1)3^l/4^(l+1). The actual birth's last change recovers t,k.\nThe necessary source support p=1+4*3^(k-1)t\u003c=X implies\n\n    t\u003c=(X-1)/(4*3^(K(t)-1)).                       (RF3)\n\nDropping the sharper j-dependent cutoff and all other actual masks\nonly enlarges the nonnegative sum. Therefore, at every finite X,\n\n    Jnp,Q(X)\u003c=sum_(t odd, t mod16 in Q,\n                    t\u003c=(X-1)/(4*3^(K(t)-1)))\n                    C_(K(t))(v3(t))*h(t)/t.       (RF4)\n\nRF3 applies to this INDIRECT contribution only. It must not be imposed\non the direct PB contribution below. No limit of an unknown infinite\ndefect sum or summability hypothesis is used.\n\n## RF4. A stronger combined bound and its exact limits\n\nKeep M(X)=G_N(X)+G_Pplus(X) and Jprim(X) from TL. For v>=1 the\ndirect branch at t is selector-exclusive: plus has coefficient\n1-alpha^(v+1), minus has coefficient3/4. Adding RF2 gives:\n\n| t modulo16 | Combined coefficient b(t), v=v3(t)>=1 |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | 81/80 - (64/85)alpha^v |\n| 3 | 63/80 - alpha^v/85 |\n| 9 | 69/80 - (4/85)alpha^v |\n| 11 | 87/80 - (16/85)alpha^v |\n\nFor v=0 there is no direct Aplus/Aminus target in M's partition;\nset b(t)=C_(K(t))(0). Outside Q retain TL9's coefficient a(t).\nExtending all positive upper bounds to the common target cutoff t\u003c=X\ngives the valid conservative inequality\n\n    M(X)\u003c=Jprim(X)+sum_(t odd,t\u003c=X) b(t)h(t)/t.    (RF5)\n\nIn particular the combined coefficient on class3 is below63/80 and\non class9 below69/80, including v=0. These are controlled parts of\none transfer estimate, not percentages solved. No invariance or closure\nof those residue classes under all subsequent transfers is established.\n\nThe first and fourth rows tend to81/80 and87/80 as v grows. This\nshows that this particular arithmetic upper bound is not uniformly\nsubunit; it does NOT prove that the actual transfer has coefficient\nabove one, because its color masks and losses were dropped. The other\nfour residue classes still use TL9, and Jprim remains uncontrolled.\nThus RF5 gives no global contraction, finite G, or full Collatz proof.\n\n## RF5. Joint signs do not remove positive target charges\n\nFor a direct plus retention with A=beta(t), h(t)=1 gives g(t)=1-2A,\nand PB gives g(4t+1)=g(t). A nonprimitive indirect source at the same\nlast-change target gives\n\n    g(4*3^(k-1)t+1)=2A-1=-g(t).                    (RF6)\n\nTheir source fibers are distinct. The opposite signs do not cancel\nthe positive mass h(t)/t used in RF5. The finite path identities\nchecked so far neither prove universal incompatibility of these masks\nnor prove their simultaneous realization by an actual invariant.\nThe useful new restriction here is arithmetic (RF2), not a claimed\nexistence result for an arbitrary coloring.\n\n## RF6. Audit, source boundary, and next obligation\n\nRoot has read both complete checks:\n[0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_MOD16_CHARGE_FILTER.md) and\n[0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_DIRECT_INDIRECT_MASK.md).\nThey independently checked the selector orbit, both geometric sums,\nthe four combined coefficients and the finite source support. Root\nalso caught and required correction of the reversed numerical signs\nin the initial JD report. PB's separate full scope audit remains live.\n\nThe primitive-mask test in\n[0431's separate report](FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_TERNARY_DESCENT.md)\nonly reuses the old exclusions e(z)=0 on1 modulo8 and the old smaller\nbirth route on5 modulo16. It supplies no new use of all the ternary\ncolor equalities and is not counted as new primitive descent.\n\nFor primary background, root reread\n[Rozier–Terracol, arXiv:2502.00948v5](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948),\nequation(2), on19 September2026. It gives the classical finite affine\nrepresentation for a fixed parity word. It is not evidence for RF's\ninvariant-energy inequalities; no external novelty claim is made.\n\nThe next substantive obligation is to exploit the actual primitive\nmask or a compatible target weight across ALL branches. Refining more\nresidue classes without such a mechanism cannot by itself justify\nclosing RF5. Public materials and the accepted SP map are unchanged.\n","REVIEW_0207_0209.md":"# Приёмка 0207/0209 — любой фиксированный период\n\n16 сентября 2026. Оба отчёта полностью прочитаны координатором и\nпроверены вручную. Математических исполнений — 0.\n\nПринято [сравнение для одного рационального старта](FIXED_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md):\nдля любого фиксированного W на хвосте непериодического рационального\nкода v₃(A)\u003cS и h/A# ограничено явной константой по W.\nАгент 0207 проверил два предложенных координатором аргумента,\n0209 независимо проверил делимость, рост состояний на сетке знаменателя,\nнижнюю оценку размера A и конечный набор W.\n\nИсправления исходных записок:\n\n- Убран ошибочный минус в r=A/(3^S k). Делимость целых относится\n  к z=A/3^S, а не к предположению целочисленности самого r.\n- Число ξ не отождествляется со словом: используется Φ₂(UW∞)=ξ.\n  Точка c/k фиксирована для T^q с паритетом W.\n- В аудите 0209 исправлен край S=0: при C_W≠0 его валюация равна\n  нулю, а не может быть положительной; при C_W=0 получаем A=0.\n\nПроверены оба знака k, 3|k, нулевые A и m_i, рост S_i и необходимость\ni→∞ у совпадений длины N→∞. Пороги зависят от ξ и W.\nИз сравнения марж не следует сама маржа, и всеобщий вывод не заявляется.\n","REVIEW_0210_0211.md":"# Приёмка 0210/0211 — растущий период при явных условиях\n\n16 сентября 2026. Координатор полностью прочитал оба отчёта и вручную\nпроверил формулы. Математических исполнений — 0.\n\nПринят [условный перенос сравнения](GROWING_PERIOD_RATIONAL_HEIGHT.md):\nдля одной совместимой семьи фиксированного рационального непериодического\nстарта условия q=o(S), q=o(log|m_i|) дают log(h/A#)=o(N).\nПриняты также точные условия через F_i, ρ_i и положительные части\nлогарифмов. Наличие этих условий у всех орбит не утверждается.\n\nПроверены и уточнены:\n\n- A=0 включено в целую ветвь посредством 3^S | A, без определения v₃(0).\n- Нормированный модуль |ka−bz|/2^i является неотрицательным целым.\n- Условие (c+|k|)2⁻ˢ→0 уже влечёт |k|2⁻ⁱ→0, поскольку i≥S.\n- Для точного варианта сохранены отдельные условия на логарифмический\n  разрыв; q=o(N) используется только как достаточный способ огрубления.\n- Формальные параметры не доказывают строгую неимпликацию в классе\n  настоящих рациональных орбит. Исправленная граница говорит лишь,\n  что приведённые оценки не выводят нужные условия из одного q=o(N).\n\nКрая S=0,c=0,3|k, знаки и N−q→∞ при i→∞ проверены.\nЗелёный статус относится к импликации с явными предпосылками;\nэти предпосылки и полный Коллатц не объявлены доказанными.\n","REVIEW_0213.md":"# Приёмка 0213 — явная цена делимости числителя\n\n16 сентября 2026. Координатор полностью прочитал независимый отчёт\n0213 и повторно проверил предложенное им доказательство вручную.\nМатематических исполнений — 0.\n\nПринята [лемма 2^S≤(|a|+b)3^q](INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md)\nна ветви 3^S | A для фиксированного рационального старта с\nнепериодическим в конечном счёте кодом. Агент независимо проверил\nконстанту |a|+b, точную верхнюю оценку C_U, ненулевое кратное 2^i,\nзнаки, S=0,A=0,3|k и произвольность слова W после настоящего префикса U.\n\nВ отчёте уточнён индекс длины при добавлении единицы в индукционном\nдоказательстве: (i,S) меняется на (i+1,S+1).\nКлючевой аргумент использует конкретное равенство Φ₂(UW∞)=ξ,\nа не недоказанное общее следствие рациональности.\n\nЛемма является необходимой границей арифметической ветви. Мировой\nприоритет и новый всеобщий охват кодов не установлены.\n","REVIEW_0215_0216.md":"# Приёмка 0215/0216 — число единиц вместо длины периода\n\n16 сентября 2026. Оба полных отчёта прочитаны координатором;\nдоказательства проверены вручную, математических исполнений — 0.\n\n0215 доказал необходимую границу 2^S≤(|a|+2b)3^R для ветви\n3^S|A при k\u003c0. 0216 усилил константу до |a|+b, оценив совместно\nдва положительных члена c3⁻ˢ и |k|(2⁻ˢ−3⁻ˢ). Его выпуклая\nоценка с x=(2/3)^S,d=(2/3)^R,m=2^(q−R) проверена: при R≤θS,\nθ=log2/log3, имеем x≤d и 1+x(m−1)−md≤1. В противоположном\nслучае 3^R>2^S и вывод тривиален.\n\nВ [канонической лемме](INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md) координатор\nдобавил более короткое доказательство той же сильной константы:\nразделить R>S и R≤S, затем использовать\nc≤2^q((3/2)^S−1). Это непосредственно складывается с\n|k|(2⁻ˢ−3⁻ˢ), поскольку 2^q+|k|=3^R.\n\nПроверены S=0, знаки старта и ненулевость ka−bz. При ξ≥0 знак\nотрицательного периодического продолжения исключает равенство без\nнепериодичности. Для отрицательных периодических стартов это снимать\nнельзя: ξ=−1,U=1^i,W=1^q даёт равенство и разрушает аргумент\nненулевого кратного.\n\nПринята только указанная необходимая граница и её условное следствие.\nСтрогий новый охват одного бесконечного кода и полный Коллатц не заявлены.\n\n[0215](task-0215.md) · [0216](task-0216.md).\n","REVIEW_0217.md":"# Приёмка 0217 — известный вход Бейкера\n\n16 сентября 2026. Координатор полностью прочитал task-0217.md и\nсам проверил Corollary 1.6 на стр. 2 и его доказательство на стр. 3\nавторского конспекта Evertse April 2011. Программы не запускались.\n\nПриняты [равномерная оценка зазора и её следствие для высот](BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md).\nВыбор отношения меньшей степени к большей важен: он непосредственно\nдаёт |2^q−3^R|/max(2^q,3^R). Максимум модулей показателей равен q.\nНенулевость проверена через простые 2,3; Corollary 1.8 не используется.\n\nПри k\u003c0,S≥1 положительность A и C_U/3^S≥1/3 дают нижнюю обычную\nоценку. Подстановка в точную формулу h/A# оставляет только\n3^max(v₃(A)−S,0) с полиномиальным по q множителем. После отдельного\nисключения v≥S логарифмический разрыв равен o(N) при q\u003cN.\n\nПроверены S=0,R=0,q=1,R=q, оба знака k и точный нуль A. Дополнительно\nкоординатор подставил уже принятую оценку |A|/3^S≥|k|/6 из 0210/0211:\nпосле условий F,G прежний отдельный контроль степенного зазора\nбольше не нужен. Это прямое следствие той же общей формулы.\n\nНовизна известного диофантова входа не заявляется. Ни целая ветвь,\nни универсальное существование хороших повторов этой оценкой не закрыты.\n\n[Отчёт 0217](task-0217.md).\n","REVIEW_0221_0222.md":"# Приёмка 0221/0222 — делимость на полную точность\n\n16 сентября2026. Координатор полностью прочитал оба отчёта и\nвручную проверил собственную лемму, её усиление и независимые аудиты.\nМатематических исполнений —0.\n\nПринята [лемма полной точности](FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md):\n3^S|A при ξ≠Φ₂(UW∞) и настоящем совпадении N=i+q+L требует\n2^(S+L)≤(|a|+b)max(1,(3/2)^R−1). Для k\u003c0 максимум с единицей\nснимается. Основа — ненулевое целое, делящееся на 2^N, и точная\nвыпуклая оценка двух слагаемых после нормировки на 2^q.\n\nПроверены оба знака k и старта, S=0,R=0, нулевой A, нечётность\nзнаменателей и изометрия кодирования. Прежняя граница через q или R\nдопускала произвольный W; новая требует совпадения всех N символов.\nПредельное условие R/(S+L)\u003clog2/log(3/2) исключает целую ветвь,\nно не создаёт такие повторы. Бейкер переносится на сравнение высот\nавтоматически только в ветви k\u003c0 с S≥1.\n\nДва уточнения к0221: q-зависимая граница при R≤S — дополнительная,\nне универсально более сильная, чем граница через (3/2)^R−1.\nПоследняя выведена непосредственно из формулы(5), сохраняя\n|k|/2^q≤(3/2)^R−1; её точная константа не выводится автоматически\nиз более грубой формулы(16), уже потерявшей вычитание единицы\nв члене с |a|. Основные доказательства(8),(10) корректны.\n\nКоординатор исправил пример0222: U=1,W=0 не удовлетворял 3^S|A.\nАгент заменил его на U=11,W=00001, ξ=−9/29, где все предпосылки\nцелой ветви выполнены, кроме намеренно нарушенного ξ≠r.\n\nПриоритет, строгий новый охват одного бесконечного кода и полный\nКоллатц не заявлены. Следующая проверка0223 сравнивает количественную\nверсию со старыми оценками высоты и известным ограничением Dubickas.\n\n[0221](task-0221.md) · [0222](task-0222.md).\n","REVIEW_0227_0229.md":"# Приёмка0227/0229 — локальный спуск и высоты\n\n16 сентября2026. Координатор полностью прочитал оба отчёта и проверил\nвручную аффинное тождество, оценку и края. Математических исполнений —0.\n\nПринята [дихотомия настоящего блока](BLOCK_DESCENT_HEIGHT_DICHOTOMY.md):\nA=k3^Sξ+2^j(m_j−m_i); для положительного рационального ξ на блоках\nm_j≥m_i отношение h/A# ограничено полиномом по q, умноженным на\n3^max(v₃(A)−S,0). Коэффициент max(3,1/ξ) проверен для обоих знаков,\nвключая отдельный S=0 при k\u003c0, где h/A#≤3.\n\nИсправлена противоречивая начальная фраза0227: k\u003c0 не исключается\nусловием m_j≥m_i, а автоматически даёт строгий рост. Уточнено также,\nчто v\u003cS — достаточное условие; для o(N) достаточно\nmax(v−S,0)=o(N), а v≤S уже даёт полиномиальный контроль.\n\nПеренос полной точности требует всех её предпосылок: лишних L\nсовпадающих символов, ξ≠r и условий роста. Проверены A=0,R=0,\nравенство концов и различие m_j\u003cm_i от m_j\u003cξ. При равенстве концов\nξ=r, поэтому соответствующее исключение высокой ветви неприменимо.\nЛинейный разрыв требует локального спуска лишь на достаточно больших\nчленах выбранной подпоследовательности.\n\nЭто непосредственная алгебра и применение известного Бейкера,\nне общий закон спуска и не доказательство Коллатца. Приоритет не заявлен.\n\n[0227](task-0227.md) · [0229](task-0229.md).\n","REVIEW_0473.md":"# Acceptance0473 — actual coalescence and finite plateau deadlines\n\n18 September2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\nThe corrected follow-up reached terminal status. Root fully read the entire\n[final independent audit](task-0473.md) and checked J1–J4 and J6 by hand.\n\nAccepted: the exact loss k-r of finite coefficient-survival levels after\ncoalescence with a smaller admissible source; the consequent deadline\nmu_(s+k-r)>mu_s when such a source exists for mu_s; the explicit family\nn=128t+79, m=108t+67, common endpoint162t+101; and the conditional bound\ntau(n)\u003c=tau(m)+5. Every assertion includes all integer t>=0 and actual parities.\nThis is a limited family and a sufficient transfer lemma, not universal coverage.\n\nThe helper initially called stabilization of mu_L necessary to exclude the\nsmallest tau=infinity seed. Root corrected this: J1 already gives a smaller\ntau=infinity seed directly. Stabilization is true as an alternative lemma,\nbut no extra hypothesis is required. The corrected final text was read in full.\nThe q=0 case in C_s!=1 and nesting E_(L+1) subset E_L are explicit.\n\n[Source comparison and independent algebra](../../../work/collatz-source-scope-0473.md)\nidentify smaller merging as an existing framework. The finite run family uses\nknown affine identities. J4 is an equivalent formulation of full Collatz, not\na new proof. None of the later sieve or almost-all results is imported.\n\nOne sequential light helper; four conceptual roles shared with root. No math\nprogram, numerical experiment, CAS, enumeration, local heavy computation or\nchild agents. No full proof, counterexample, universal witness theorem or ETA.\n","REVIEW_0474.md":"# Acceptance0474 — variable runs and bounded witness complexity\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00).\nRoot fully read the final [independent audit](task-0474.md), including its K7\nand K8 follow-ups, after terminal confirmation. All arithmetic checks were\nmanual. The accepted scope is K1–K5, K7, and the application of the explicitly\nnamed external input E1 in K8. K6 records limits, not another theorem.\n\nFor a genuine word 1^p0^d with coefficient greater than one, the least\npure-odd source of its endpoint has an exact size comparison. The possible\nequality e=K/D is automatically admissible when D divides K; removing it\nrequires a cycle result. It would produce a nontrivial positive 1-cycle.\nThe established exclusion of such cycles, E1, therefore gives strict size\nthroughout the band d \u003c alpha p \u003c d+alpha b. This imports an established\ntheorem and does not prove it again or exclude unrestricted positive cycles.\n\nFor any actual path from a fixed positive m with at most h odd runs, accepted\nbounds control its height, coefficient, odd-input count and full length.\nThe full-length bound does not require a separate budget for even steps.\nFor a smaller witness m\u003cn these give necessary restrictions at a proposed\ncommon endpoint. They do not bound the independent forward time k from n,\nprove that a witness exists, or forbid every strategy using early witnesses.\n\nThe height proof and geometric sum already occur in FB162.2–3 / AFB162.2–3;\nthe affine inverse comparison has a predecessor in GV166. They are reused\nopenly. The present coefficient and full-time consequences are explicit\napplications to473, with no claim of worldwide novelty. See the exact\n[archive/source scope](../../../work/collatz-source-scope-0474.md).\n\nRoot requested a correction to the audit's indexing: before odd run j\n(numbered from one), the exponent is rho^(j-1); after it, rho^j. The final\ntext contains this correction. The actual formula in K5 was already correct.\nThe edges h=0, m=1, truncated first/last runs and exact parity valuations\nwere checked. Endpoint ratios are distinguished from affine coefficients.\n\nOne sequential light helper and follow-ups, no children; four conceptual\nroles shared with root. Zero mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS,\nnumerical experiments or proof-assistant runs. Full Collatz, universal\ncoverage and an ETA remain unresolved. A green map node means this limited\nclaim passed internal audit, not external peer review or percent completion.\n","REVIEW_0485_ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BRIDGE.md":"# AC review — global energy, selected endpoints and the excluded PC12 regime\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). AC is bounded progress;\nthe full Collatz goal remains active and incomplete, with no external\nblocker. No completion percentage or time estimate is justified.\n\n## Accepted mathematical scope\n\nThe complete [root AC1–AC18](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md)\nderives, for any exact binary T-invariant and common finite cutoff X,\n\n    A=E_2+P/2+O(1), B=E_0+P/2+O(1),\n    E=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1).\n\nThe errors are bounded independently of X and the coloring. The proof\nuses the exact four-state table, the conserved channels e+L on S and\ne+Rb on R, and parity-resolved binary child identities. A fixed affine\ntransport contributes only a summable reciprocal correction and a bounded\ncutoff annulus. The residue E_1 terms are retained and cancel explicitly\nin a difference. All harmonic subtractions are of finite sums.\n\nThe two positive classes are even terminal birth SOURCES Z_R and\nmissing-predecessor birth TARGETS D_S. They control total energy:\nE(X)\u003c=4Z_R(X)+3D_S(X)+C. Consequently total harmonic birth energy is\nfinite iff both selected class energies are finite. Also\nP(X)\u003c=(8/3)Z_R(X)-2D_R(X)+C. No selected-class finiteness is proved.\n\nThe bounded finite-cutoff signed remainder transfers by partial summation\nto a uniformly bounded Dirichlet remainder for s>1. Thus divergent E_s\nrequires a nonnegligible positive charge 4Z_R,s+3D_S,s. The actual-mask\nendpoint/origin-negligible regime PC12 is excluded. The older scalar\nassignment remains feasible only for the older projected constraints;\nit was never an actual coloring. Its correctly scoped feasibility result\nis not withdrawn. Other divergent regimes remain open.\n\nThis is a global transport identity. It is not a per-component mass/origin\nbound, nor a direct universal descent, all-cycle exclusion or full proof.\n\n## Four roles and corrections during independent checking\n\nRoot derived and checked the complete finite-sum argument and read all\nthree reports, including their corrected final full-file audits:\n\n- [0431: four states and exact color coupling](FOLLOWUP_0431_ACTIVE_BOUNDARY_COUPLING.md).\n- [0459: weighted transport and Dirichlet transfer](FOLLOWUP_0459_BOUNDARY_BIRTH_DIRICHLET.md).\n- [0483: source scope and limiting logic](FOLLOWUP_0483_ACTIVE_COUPLING_SOURCE_AUDIT.md).\n\nAll three reused helpers completed and read the complete root AC file.\nThese are internal manual checks, not external peer review or formal proof\nverification. Their preliminary conclusions were not accepted without\nchecking. Material corrections are preserved in the reports:\n\n1. Individual L and Rb are not conserved. The conserved quantities are\n   e+L and e+Rb, respectively.\n2. The initially alleged missing parity/residue identity follows by\n   summing the explicit even/odd child rows and using D_i=L_i+Rb_i.\n   The two E_1 terms cancel in the difference; they cannot simply be\n   omitted in the separate rows.\n3. A claimed extra term 3(Rb_0-L_2) in the endpoint identity came from\n   confusing channel masses with birth-only image masses. The correct\n   endpoint image rows contain E_2 and E_0 alone. Direct substitution\n   into A=E_2+P/2 and B=E_0+P/2 removes that spurious term.\n4. In the s>1 path expansion, products carry power s. The original\n   first-power notation was corrected.\n5. The PC affine correction sums over retained odd births, not all odd\n   births. Subtracting it from the total channel correction leaves the\n   corrections of odd L sources AND Z_S sources.\n6. Q(X) is already weighted by 1/n. The correct partial-summation factor\n   is epsilon=s-1; the proposed replacement by s would apply to an\n   unweighted counting cumulative function. The root file now spells out\n   both coefficient and cumulative definitions. No change to the original\n   mathematical identity was required.\n\nThere are three new follow-up scopes, zero new distinct agents. Counts\nremain 485 distinct / 484 main reports reviewed / 0095 rejected / 515\nreserved. Historical PBK0485 and FP/UD limitations remain recorded.\nNo numerical script, CAS, enumeration or Lean was used. File verification\nand rendering are sequential with one configured worker and no GPU;\nthis is not an OS-wide CPU cap.\n\n## Sources, missing input and next step\n\nThe source role records its targeted reading of Rozier and Rozier–Terracol;\nroot freshly checked their arXiv metadata and versions:\n[Rozier v6](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.00133v6) and\n[Rozier–Terracol v5](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00948v5).\nRoot also checked that [Siegel's record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.07882)\nis marked withdrawn for an error in Section3. No formula or theorem from\nthat withdrawn work or unread Berg–Meinardus full text is used in AC.\nThis is targeted source screening, not an exhaustive priority review.\n\nThe remaining analytic input is finiteness of the Z_R and D_S harmonic\nweights, or a joint signed estimate in AC14 that forces bounded E(X).\nThe next bounded investigation should charge 4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R along\nactual A/B boundary paths, retaining shared colors, repeated incidences\nand every possible initial path depth. Independent endpoint coefficient\nimprovements are insufficient unless they yield a total contraction.\n\nThe named conditional PD170 input is not used in AC. Even a later finite\nenergy proof would still need the separately recorded pointwise bridge\nfor full Collatz. Its full analytic dependencies and Lean build have not\nbeen independently recertified. No finite energy, nonconstant beta,\ncounterexample, full theorem, novelty or proximity-to-solution claim is made.\n\n## Local map and service verification\n\nThe three new green nodes record channel conservation, the coupled\nparity/residue balance and the selected endpoint-energy bridge. The old\nID CriticalBulkExclusionOpen is retained for continuity but now has a\ngreen, narrowly revised label recording the PC12 exclusion. The new\nSelectedEndpointEnergyOpen and all full-energy/full-Collatz obligations\nremain unfilled. The public snapshot425 and completed distribution/cleanup\nare unchanged.\n\nПолная карта: 601 узел, 1098 связей; обзор: 207 узлов, 431 связь.\nИсходник сохранён, live Mermaid-блоков нет. Offline-проверки синтаксиса,\nэлементов управления и границ подписей прошли без переполнений. Проверены\nтри follow-up и 38 выбранных цветов. Root просмотрел обзор и пять\nувеличенных подписей: четыре зелёные и одна незакрашенная; текст помещается.\nОбзор требует увеличения. Предупреждение Graphviz о метрике кириллицы\nсохраняется; свежая проверка в браузере не выполнялась. Метаданные:\n1386 документов, 5620 локальных ссылок, нет отсутствующих ссылок или\nконфликтов классов. Это служебные проверки, не доказательство математики.\n","REVIEW_0485_BIDIRECTIONAL_CORES.md":"# SR acceptance — two arithmetic cores and masked origin weights\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root acceptance after manual\nderivation, full report reads and three independent bounded audits.\nThis is internal review, not a formal proof check or external peer review.\nNo new distinct agent was launched; the previous 485/484/1/515 counters\nand the historical missing PBK audit remain unchanged.\n\n## Accepted scope\n\n[SR1–SR12](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md) defines F=S∪R\non odd integers and multiples of four, with R(n)=3n/4. Actual birth\nvertices retain an edge only when the output is also a birth. The even\nrule is exact; the disjoint S/R images make the partial graph injective.\n\nArithmetic forward words occupy one residue modulo 2^(s+2r), backward\nwords one residue modulo 3^k. The two infinite-iteration sets have counting\nbounds (7/3)X^α and (5/2)X^γ, with α=ln2/ln(8/3)\u003c1, γ=ln2/ln3\u003c1.\nTheir harmonic sums are at most C+=7/[3(1−α)] and C−=5/[2(1−γ)].\nEvery retained cycle and infinite component lies in their union. Therefore\n\n    E_fin(X)≤Eβ(X)≤E_fin(X)+C++C−,\n\nwhere E_fin counts births in finite acyclic retained components. This\nisolates, but does not bound, their aggregate mass. Cycles are included\nin the core estimate, not ruled out.\n\nFor an inverse-R origin, n=2^h k, h≥2, k odd active. Nonadjacent h\ngive total weight at most 1/(3k); the two nearest-birth ancestry paths\ngive 3/(2b). Hence E_D_R(X)≤(1/2)Eβ(X/8). Together with SG's\ninverse-S bound, the exact origin partition gives\n\n    E_O_F(X)≤E_P1(X)+(4/9)Eβ(X/6)+(1/2)Eβ(X/8).\n\nThe coefficient 17/18 applies ONLY to origins. There is no bound on\nE_P1, no total-energy contraction, and no permission to multiply by\na constant component factor from a different graph. Origins can exceed\na cutoff while their later R-vertices lie below it.\n\nAn explicit finite arithmetic family a=4^L v, R^L(a)=3^L v=y,\nv≡1 mod3 and y≡1 mod8, has aW≥(4/3)^L. Thus a uniform arithmetic\nW≤C/a is impossible. This is not an actual-birth counterexample:\ne(y)=0, so the full displayed component cannot be retained.\n\n## Reports read and audit roles\n\n- Root read the complete [0431 report](FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_ZERO_CLASS.md),\n  including §§1–11. It checks primitive zero-class masks, R, forward coding,\n  inverse-R charging, the arithmetic family, and the full root SR text.\n- Root read the complete [0459 report](FOLLOWUP_0459_PRIMITIVE_ONE_CLASS.md),\n  including §§1–11. It checks the one-class primitive obstruction, backward\n  coding, cutoffs, inverse-R charge and the full root SR text.\n- Root read the complete [0483 report](FOLLOWUP_0483_EVEN_PRIMITIVE_ENERGY.md),\n  including §§1–9 and the appended inverse-R audit. It checks the even\n  primitive split, exact three-mask coupling, and the full root SR text,\n  with an independent focus on SR4–SR9.\n\nAll three helpers completed. Root also checked all displayed equations\ndirectly. No mathematical scripts, numerical samples, CAS, enumeration or\nLean were used. Metadata and map render checks are separate service QA.\nOnly one local worker/thread is configured for those jobs; that is not\nan OS-wide CPU limit. No new publication or remote mathematical validation\nwas performed by this acceptance.\n\n## Corrections preserved\n\n1. A terminal inverse on a fully retained S-run is already the preceding\n   birth, not an independent unknown mask or new descent.\n2. An arithmetic superset is a valid positive upper bound; its divergent\n   harmonic mass makes the coarse bound insufficient, not invalid.\n3. Overlapping positive ancestor bounds can be added as overestimates;\n   a correct vertex/component partition is still needed. Ancestors may\n   be interior S-vertices, not only origins.\n4. The even primitive bound and SG combine with feedback 3+7/9=34/9,\n   not 9/4. There is no linear scaling rule Eβ(cX)≤cEβ(X).\n5. Root's proposed equivalence e(S(K))=1 iff d(2K)=1 was withdrawn.\n   The exact identity is e(S(K))=d(2K)(1−d(S(K))); failure does not\n   force the right active child.\n6. The first inverse-R draft asserted d(k+1)=1 without justification.\n   It was removed. Only d(k)=1 is proved and used for b\u003cn/8.\n7. Even vertices outside the S-image are singletons; even vertices\n   inside it may be endpoints with incoming S-edges. The helper's\n   overbroad singleton sentence was corrected.\n8. Root made editorial fixes to section cross-references, the phrase\n   “infinite cycle” (replaced by an infinite chain), and identification\n   of y as the last R-output; the F-terminal in the example is S(y).\n\nThese corrections do not supply the missing finite-component estimate.\nThe earlier SG coefficient 7/9 still controls full S-only image-component\nmass in its own graph and remains valid in that scope.\n\n## Sources and unresolved work\n\nRoot's primary-source scope is recorded in SR12: the introduction and\nbeginning of Section 2 of Applegate–Lagarias, manuscript dated 15 December\n1993, were read as PDF text. Its inverse-tree lower-bound problem differs\nfrom the upper counts here. No numerical exponent or certificate was\nimported. Earlier finite-word source checks retain their original scope.\nThis is not a comprehensive novelty search; priority is not claimed.\n\nNext: derive a mask-sensitive estimate of aggregate finite acyclic\ncomponent mass, including endpoint/internal-minimum multiplicities and\nthe residue-one source P1. Finite energy itself would still need a\njustified implication to constancy or a replacement by universal descent.\nFull Collatz, all-cycle exclusion and general descent remain open; no\npercentage or time-to-solution is established. The goal remains active.\n","REVIEW_0485_ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT.md":"# EC review — exact depth counts and an explicit remaining sparsity problem\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Previous BM was progress;\nthe latest bookkeeping turn completed its late audit and map verification.\nThis EC turn is progress: a global count transport, sufficient profiles,\nindependent audit and source-dependency clarification. Full Collatz remains\nincomplete; no external blocker is claimed.\n\n## Accepted scope\n\n[EC1–EC8](ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md) proves exact\nforward/backward counting inequalities for every exact binary invariant.\nAt each fixed depth a terminal/origin has at most one relevant vertex.\nSumming the source counts at the depth-dependent cutoffs removes an\nunnecessary factor k; internal vertices and all component types are covered.\n\nConditional power savings for actual terminal or origin counts transfer\nto a full birth-count power saving with the displayed rho_Z/rho_O. General\nnondecreasing source profiles H(Y)\u003c=CY/L(Y) give\n\n    R_beta(X)\u003c=C'X/L(X)^eta+L(X),\n    eta=ln(4/3)/ln2 for terminals,\n    eta=ln(3/2)/ln2 for origins.\n\nBoth terms remain in the general energy integral. Logarithmic and critical\nlogarithmic criteria follow without a spurious ln ln X loss. No source\nsparsity hypothesis is proved. Under named PD and nonconstant beta,\nthe conditional contrapositive forces upper counting exponent1 for both\nendpoint sets. It does not assert their positive density or existence.\n\nThe initial minimum/length refinement is also correct in its bounded scope:\nthe finite-chain suffix integral I_L(m) is exact and global summation\nretains distinct minima. Its required weighted sum is not bounded.\nThe internal-mask audit rejects transferring terminal spacing to internal\nvertices; it supplies no new escape estimate. Those limits motivated the\ncount route and are not counted as an unconditional energy upper bound.\n\n## Four mathematical roles and reading\n\nRoot read all four saved reports and their final additions:\n\n- [0431: component packing and forward count transport](FOLLOWUP_0431_COMPONENT_PACKING.md).\n- [0459: limits of internal escape charges](FOLLOWUP_0459_COMPONENT_ESCAPE_CHARGES.md).\n- [0459: backward transport and profile audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_BACKWARD_COUNT_TRANSPORT.md).\n- [0483: PD dependencies and critical-profile audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_PD170_DEPENDENCY_AUDIT.md).\n\nAll three reused helpers read the complete root EC file. 0431 independently\nchecked the counts, cutoffs, power optimization and general profile;\n0459 checked the backward route, profiles and conditional contrapositive;\n0483 checked the critical profile, PD dependence and source scope. All\nthree completed. These are internal manual checks, not formal verification\nor external peer review. Root re-derived the formulas directly.\n\nNo new distinct agents:485 distinct,484 main reports reviewed,0095 rejected,\n515 reserved. Four new follow-up scopes do not increase those counts.\nHistorical missing PBK0485 and FP/UD scope restrictions remain unchanged.\nNo mathematical scripts, numerical experiments, CAS, enumeration or Lean.\nService file checks and map rendering run sequentially with one configured\nworker; this is not an OS-wide CPU limit.\n\n## Corrections made during audit\n\n- The forward partition is vertexwise. A backward-infinite component with\n  a terminal can have vertices in the short forward-depth class.\n- Some children of an internal birth can be arithmetic images of F. The\n  valid obstruction is that terminal spacing needs d(y)=1, whereas an\n  internal birth has d=0. The stronger initial claim was removed.\n- The BM arithmetic family does not realize actual masks or terminal\n  spacing; its obstruction is explicitly restricted to unmasked bounds.\n- Keeping the exact-depth geometric sum removes the coarse factors ln X\n  and ln ln X. Earlier coarse bounds in reports remain valid but weaker.\n- With k=floor(log_2 L), g^k/L\u003c=L^(-eta), not equality. Root corrected\n  the final source-role audit's notation; the root proof already used \u003c=.\n- The generic profile theorem does not claim the sharp power exponent\n  supplied by the separate optimized calculation.\n\n## Primary-source boundary\n\nRoot freshly read the full eight-page Mazur all-target manuscript and\nthe Krasikov–Lagarias abstract/metadata. The source role also checked the\nauthor's provenance pages and the archived QS170/GP169 reports. The\nmatching PD quantifiers and visible transfer are confirmed within that\nscope. Central analytic dependencies and a complete Lean rebuild are not\nindependently certified. See the detailed source report; PD remains a\nnamed conditional input, not a silently imported unconditional result.\nNo exhaustive priority check or novelty claim is made for EC.\n\n## Next step and map scope\n\nThe precise open input is a uniform count bound for actual terminal masks\ne(n)d(F(n)) or the actual origin partition, with a power saving or a\nlogarithmic profile meeting EC8. It must retain correlations imposed by\nbeta. Existing endpoint energy and origin17/18 inequalities do not imply\nthat input. Alternatively, universal smaller-birth descent remains open.\n\nThe local map marks only the count transport and its conditional criterion\ngreen. The source sparsity and full Collatz nodes remain unfilled. Public\nsnapshot425, all completed publication records and cleanup are unchanged.\nNo completion percentage or time estimate is supported.\n\n## Service verification — 2026-09-19T11:10:59+05:00\n\nMap590 nodes/1073 edges, overview196/406. Offline syntax, controls and label bounds pass. Four follow-ups and27 selected colors pass. Root viewed overview and three enlarged new labels; two green and one unfilled, labels fit. Overview needs zoom; known Graphviz metric warning remains. No fresh browser check and no mathematical execution. [Metadata](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_0485.json) and [color receipt](EC_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json).\n","REVIEW_0485_FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS.md":"# BM review — individual weights, an arithmetic barrier and explicit dependencies\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). The previous SR turn was\nprogress. This turn gives a parametric obstruction to a proposed arithmetic\nshortcut and a bounded endpoint estimate. Full Collatz remains incomplete;\nthere is no external blocker. All mathematics was manual, with zero CAS,\nenumerations, numerical experiments or Lean runs.\n\n## Accepted scope\n\n[BM1–BM9](FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_AND_BALANCED_BARRIER.md) gives:\n\n- An individual upper bound for the entire harmonic weight of a finite\n  retained component with numerical minimum m:\n  W≤7m^(α−1)/[3(1−α)]+(ln m+1)/[m ln(8/3)]. Its sum over all\n  component minima is not bounded here.\n- A family of complete finite arithmetic components of length k+1 with\n  every vertex in [3n/4,5n/2], no incoming edge at n and a terminal\n  at 2 modulo 4. It gives mW≥3(k+1)/10, including a logarithmic\n  lower loss along unbounded minima. No uniform arithmetic factor from\n  the minimum or two endpoints is possible. This is not an actual-birth\n  counterexample and does not disprove a mask-sensitive bound.\n- Exact terminal S/R masks force the next active boundary to be\n  1 modulo 3. Endpoint charges on a path cannot be adjacent in depth,\n  giving E_terminal(X)≤2Eβ((3X+1)/4)+3/2. Internal vertices are\n  not counted by this bound, and coefficient 2 is not a contraction.\n- The sufficient endpoint descent test F(terminal)\u003c2 min C, with no\n  claim that every finite component passes it. Compressing S followed\n  by R-steps recovers earlier S/G₃ alternatives and connects to SC;\n  it does not identify the whole mixed graph with the old growth graph.\n\nRoot re-read CE and SB174 in full. Finite birth energy implies finite\nboundary energy and then constancy outside a set of natural density zero.\nWithout PD, a pointwise bridge remains necessary for that proof route.\nThe archive already supplies the conditional implication under named PD170;\nroot re-read QS170 and freshly checked Mazur's theorem statement and\nSections 3–5 without re-auditing the full analytic dependencies or formal\nbuild. PD170 is not withdrawn. Its historical scope is not upgraded by BM.\n\n## Four roles and full reads\n\nRoot fully read these four reports, including all saved additions:\n\n- [0431: component minimum and balanced family](FOLLOWUP_0431_FINITE_COMPONENT_MINIMUM.md).\n- [0459: endpoint masks, spacing and R-run compression](FOLLOWUP_0459_FINITE_COMPONENT_ENDPOINTS.md).\n- [0483: exact finite-energy conclusion](FOLLOWUP_0483_FINITE_ENERGY_OBSTRUCTION.md).\n- [0483: independent balanced-family audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_BALANCED_CHAIN_BARRIER.md).\n\n0431 and0483 read the complete root BM text and completed their stated\naudits, including the final PD-scope correction. 0431 checked BM1–BM7;\n0483 independently checked BM3–BM5 and the conditional BM8 bridge.\n0459's endpoint report through §9 was saved and fully read; it contains\nthe terminal masks, spacing estimate, compressed R-run and a final audit\nof BM6–BM8. The final arrived after root's interruption request and the\nprovisional state save; the live agent status now confirms completion.\nThis late result supersedes the provisional missing-audit status. No full\nBM1–BM9 read by0459 is claimed. Root and0431 also independently checked\nthe endpoint estimates. Root clarified two phrases in §9: the additive\nS correction is bounded by a telescoping majorant, and a PD-free bridge\ndoes not replace the still-needed energy upper bound.\nAll four mathematical roles were used in these explicitly limited scopes.\n\nThese are internal manual checks, not formal verification or external\npeer review. No new distinct agents were launched: 485 distinct,\n484 main reports reviewed, 0095 rejected, 515 reserved. Follow-ups are\nseparate. The historical missing PBK audit0485 and FP/UD qualifications\nare preserved. Service rendering/checks use one configured local worker\nat a time; this does not enforce an OS-wide CPU quota.\n\n## Corrections and limitations\n\nThe individual minimum bound is not described as stronger than the SR\norigin estimate; they bound different quantities. Opposite increments\nof 1/n do not rule out every other positive potential. A pointwise\ncoalescence or positive-density premise is not silently imported from\nan almost-everywhere statement. The arithmetic family ends at a known\nnonbirth, so it is explicitly not a realization of all birth masks.\nThe initial description of a missing pointwise bridge was too broad:\nit is missing without PD170, while the archived conditional bridge remains.\nThe old SR10 origin barrier is referenced by its section, not confused\nwith the equation label SR12. Root coordinates the final project-state\nupdate; an early helper progress note was retained in the state backup.\n\nRoot freshly read Elia–Tucker's map definitions, definitions 1–2 and\nProposition 1 as stated in BM9. Bernstein–Lagarias could not be loaded\nin this pass. No fresh reading is falsely attributed to that PDF or\nto a helper, and no exhaustive novelty conclusion is made.\n\n## Next obligation\n\nAn actual-mask estimate must control complete finite components, including\ntheir internal visits and multiplicities, rather than only their endpoints.\nThe arithmetic barrier narrows that search without ruling out such an\nestimate. Using the energy route must retain its named PD dependency or\nprove a PD-free pointwise implication. A direct universal smaller-birth\nargument would be an alternative. Cyclic/infinite F-components are\nsummable by SR, not absent.\nThere is no proof of Collatz, no justified completion percentage and no ETA.\nThe map and all new results are local; public snapshot425 is unchanged.\n\n## Saved service verification — 2026-09-19T11:01:01+05:00\n\nMap587nodes/1065edges, overview193/398. Offline syntax, controls and\nlabel bounds pass; four follow-up records and28 selected colors pass.\nRoot viewed the overview and four enlarged labels; no clipped node text\nwas visible there. Full-map typography needs zoom. Existing Graphviz\nmetric warning remains. No fresh browser check of the local map and no\nmathematical execution. [Metadata](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_FINITE_COMPONENT_WEIGHTS_0485.json)\nand [color evidence](BM_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json).\n","REVIEW_0485_INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGINS.md":"# OR review — inverse growth masks and primitive origins\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual internal review;\nno numerical programs, enumeration, CAS, Lean or new distinct agents.\nThe preceding BC turn was progress: it accepted the sparse-core estimate,\nchanged the authoritative state and identified the origin-energy question.\nThis pass verifies a narrower part of that remaining question; the full\nCollatz goal remains active and incomplete, without an external blocker.\n\n## Proof and independent reading\n\nAccepted scope: [OR1–OR13](INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGIN_REDUCTION.md), using\nthe inherited [SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md),\n[BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md) and\n[BC](MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md).\n\nRoot read all three reports, their addenda and subsequent corrections:\n\n- [0431, inverse masks](FOLLOWUP_0431_GROWTH_ORIGIN_MASKS.md): H/G₃\n  reverse dichotomies, all-origin indicator, full OR1–OR13 audit and\n  the explicit finite arithmetic progression.\n- [0459, oriented returns](FOLLOWUP_0459_ORIENTED_CHAIN_RETURN.md):\n  signed ancestry, exact return criterion, OR1–OR10 audit and the\n  G₃ sign refinement in OR11–OR13. Its no-immediate-return observation\n  is local; it does not exclude returns to earlier chain members.\n- [0483, arithmetic barrier](FOLLOWUP_0483_ORIGIN_ARITHMETIC_BARRIER.md):\n  arithmetic images and divergent candidate classes, all-origin versus\n  finite-origin distinction, OR9–OR10 conditional on OR2–OR7. This agent\n  did not independently reprove every earlier BR or SC lemma.\n\nRoot independently checked the inverse paths, H strip argument, signed\ndoubling identity, positivity edges, charge multiplicity and cutoffs.\nAll three agents completed. The unchanged counts are485 distinct,\n484 main reports accepted,0095 rejected,515 reserved. Follow-ups do not\nincrease distinct launches. Historical unreceived PBK audit0485 and\nFP/UD scope limits remain preserved in the preceding records.\n\n## Accepted mathematical content\n\nFor an image birth n=9u−1, the H inverse q=4u−1 is either itself\na birth or has d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=1. In the latter case its nearest\nbirth ancestor is at mostu−1. For an image birth n=18t+15, the G₃\ninverse q=16t+13 satisfies\n\n    e(q)=1−d(n+1), d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=d(n+1).\n\nThe failed inverse again gives a birth at mostfloor(q/4). Thus every\nbirth in the two arithmetic image classes has a smaller birth. A least\nbirth, if one exists, must lie outside these classes. Combined with SC,\nits possible residues are3mod4 or13mod16, with neither6 nor8mod9.\nThis does not exclude every possible least birth.\n\nLet O be all actual growth origins, A the origins of finite components,\nD the origins inside the arithmetic image, and P the births outside\nthat image. Then O=P disjoint-union D and the exact local indicator\nis OR8. In particular O and A are not interchangeable.\n\nDistinct inverse boundaries, depth at least2 and at most two descendants\nper depth give the error-free estimate\n\n    E_D(X)≤(8/9)E_β(2X/9).\n\nCombining it with the chain factor9 gives\n\n    E_β(X)≤9E_P(X)+8E_β(2X/9).\n\nThe coefficient8/9 is not a contraction of the full energy; the full\ninequality has coefficient8 and leaves E_P uncontrolled.\n\nThe arithmetic progression144t+99 lies outside both inverse-image\nclasses, has one H edge to324t+224 (an even output), and has divergent\nharmonic mass. If any member is an actual birth it is in A, regardless\nof that edge's retention. The statement asserts no such actual birth.\nIt only rules out deriving summability from the arithmetic candidate\nset alone.\n\nFinally, the genuine identities β(q)=β(G₃(q)+1) and\nβ(q+1)=β(G₃(q)+2) hold on13mod16. At an actual input birth they\nmake d(G₃(q)+1)=0 automatic. Failure is only at G₃(q), with sign\nopposite to the failed H sign. Within a same-color component, a putative\nH return uses the left half of its dyadic ancestor interval; a G₃ return\nuses the right half. Every intermediate boundary still must be active.\nThese are necessary filters, not a proof that all self-returns fail.\n\n## Corrections before acceptance\n\n- Early0431 text treated the neighboring G₃ guard as independent after\n  the inverse birth was known. The exact paths make it redundant; the\n  early text and residual inverse-failure discussion were corrected.\n- Early0459 text left the G₃ failure sign undetermined. OR11 fixes it.\n  Root aligned the earlier failure formula with K=p and corrected the\n  explanation of the chain factor: E_D contains origins only, not later\n  vertices. The all-energy estimate accounts for those vertices.\n- Early0483 notation conflated O with A and stated that P_r need not\n  be nested. It now uses O for all origins and P_(r+1)⊂P_r, because\n  F is deterministic and each next input is odd.\n- Root replaced an unsupported assertion of non-inclusion in0483 with\n  the precise statement that the proposed inclusion was not proved;\n  no actual invariant counterexample was constructed.\n\n## Sources, dependencies and remaining task\n\nNo new external theorem is used in OR: the displayed genuine paths,\nfinite strip identities and elementary geometric/harmonic sums establish\nits claims. The primary-source finite-coding and consecutive-integer\nscope verified in the [BC review](REVIEW_0485_SPARSE_GROWTH_CORE.md)\nremains the bibliographic baseline; those papers are not cited as proving\nOR or the full stopping theorem. No new external-source audit or worldwide\npriority search was performed in this pass. No novelty claim is made.\n\nOR uses no PD or positive-density premise. The separate CE/SB/H route\nfrom two positive color densities to divergent birth energy retains its\nown assumptions; physical/mixing/histogram inputs and all PD170 have not\nbeen re-audited here. Full Collatz and positive-cycle exclusion remain open.\n\nThe next target is control of actual primitive origins P together with\nthe accumulated chain mass and transfer multiplicities, or a strict\ndecrease between origins after terminal transfers. A finite bound on a\nraw arithmetic superset has not been obtained and the exhibited\nprogression prevents that particular superset bound.\n\n## Artifact status\n\nGreen InverseGrowthOriginMasks records only OR's audited local scope.\nPrimitiveOriginTransferOpen, FiniteGrowthOriginEnergyOpen, global block\ndescent and the full Collatz node remain uncolored.\n\nThe local map has576 nodes/1039 edges; its overview has182/372. Full\nsource preservation, offline JavaScript syntax, controls and SVG label\nbounds passed. The PNG overview was viewed; labels require HTML/SVG zoom.\nThe existing Graphviz Cyrillic metric warning remains. No fresh browser\nview of the local map was made.\n[Metadata](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_INVERSE_GROWTH_ORIGINS_0485.json)\nfound no broken local links, conflicting colors or missing read markers.\n[Three follow-ups and17 colors](OR_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json)\npassed separately. These checks concern artifacts and bookkeeping, not\nformal mathematical verification.\n\nResults are local. Public snapshot425, all five publications, completed\nCAPTCHA and selected local media cleanup are unchanged. No external\nsubmission, video render or deletion occurred in this research pass.\n","REVIEW_0485_PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE.md":"# PC review — all-even weights and the remaining critical mass\n\n**Later AC clarification (19 September 2026):** the scalar assignment below is feasible only for the stated PC projection. The added joint channel constraints in [AC](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md) exclude the endpoint/origin-negligible divergent regime for actual masks. Full energy finiteness remains open; the old projected feasibility claim is not withdrawn.\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Previous WS was progress.\nThis PC pass is progress: a full-parity-class bound, exact global balances\nand a verified limitation of the current projected inequalities. Full\nCollatz remains incomplete, with no external blocker.\n\n## Accepted scope\n\n[PC1–PC14](PARITY_RESIDUE_BALANCE_AND_PRIMITIVE_MASS.md) proves\n\n    E_even(X) \u003c= (3/2)E_0(Y)+(5/8)E_1(Y)+(3/8)E_2(Y)+1/4,\n    Y=(3X+2)/4.\n\nEvery even birth, including internal vertices, is included. The proof\ncharges its active boundary q=6m+1, uses the odd A/B grammar with\nW=(1,2,1/4), and includes every possible initial left depth. The largest\ncoefficient exceeds one; the odd complement is not bounded by this result.\n\nAbsolute convergence at s>1 permits exact residue and global Dirichlet\nidentities for all retained components. The affine S correction is uniformly\nbounded near s=1. Harmonic sums are not subtracted if they diverge.\nUnder the additional hypothesis of divergent total energy and negligible\nrelative origin/terminal weights, normalized odd and even weights must\nboth approach 1/2, and residue weights approach (2/3,0,1/3).\n\nAn explicit nonnegative assignment satisfies these selected scalar\nconstraints and the current WS and PC6 inequalities. It is not an actual\ninvariant, birth realization or Collatz counterexample. It identifies\nwhat these projections alone fail to exclude.\n\nFixed-k prefix transport further shows that negligible relative terminal\nor origin Dirichlet weight implies (s-1)E_s tends to zero along the same\nsequence. This follows from the already proved EC/SR arithmetic transport;\nit is not an independent new sparsity theorem. It permits divergent E_s.\n\nThe lift exclusion e(n)=1 implies e(4n+1)=0 and the primitive P_1\nalternatives combine inherited local identities. They do not solve the\nodd primitive class or provide a universal smaller-birth descent.\n\n## Four roles and actual review\n\nRoot read all three reports and their final complete-file audits:\n\n- [0431: primitive masks and all-even charging](FOLLOWUP_0431_PRIMITIVE_ORIGIN_MASKS.md).\n- [0459: telescoping and global Dirichlet balances](FOLLOWUP_0459_COMPONENT_WEIGHT_TELESCOPING.md).\n- [0483: source scope and critical-limit logic](FOLLOWUP_0483_INVARIANT_COLOR_SOURCE_SCOPE.md).\n\nAll three reused helpers completed and read the complete root PC file.\n0431 checked the potential, initial depths, correction and cutoff;\n0459 checked the global balances, correction, projected witness and prefix\nestimates; 0483 checked the limiting logic, source distinction and scope.\nRoot independently derived and checked the formulas. These are internal\nmanual audits, not external peer review or formal verification.\n\nThere are three new follow-up scopes and no new distinct agents. Counts\nremain 485 distinct / 484 main reports reviewed / 0095 rejected / 515\nreserved. Historical PBK0485 and FP/UD limitations are unchanged. No\nmathematical script, enumeration, CAS or Lean was used. Service file checks\nand rendering are sequential with one configured worker, not an OS CPU cap.\n\n## Corrections and scope controls\n\n- 0431 initially multiplied the potential by its reward 3/2 twice. Root\n  identified the duplicate factor; the corrected report gives PC6 exactly.\n- The component expansion has nonnegative affine corrections. Only the\n  direct edge difference and the rearranged global balance have mixed signs.\n- Products with factors 3/2 on S and 3/4 on R grow on S-rich words, not\n  R-rich words. The original report's reversed description was corrected.\n- Unweighted corrections over distinct sources are uniformly summable;\n  this does not bound the path-product-weighted correction uniformly.\n- The critical scalar assignment is explicitly separated from an actual\n  beta realization. Neither it nor the prefix limit is a counterexample.\n- Endpoint negligibility is an additional hypothesis, not a consequence\n  of WS or of nonconstant invariance.\n\n## Primary sources and what is still missing\n\nThe source role read the stated parts of Rozier and Rozier–Terracol on\nparity vectors and conjugacy. These do not supply the missing neighbor-color\nmask estimate. Root read the Berg–Meinardus Rostock abstract/metadata;\nits PostScript was unsupported by the web reader. The Mannheim paper was\ninaccessible behind its access challenge. No theorem or formula from the\nunread paper is imported. The author attribution is Günter Meinardus.\nNo exhaustive priority check or novelty claim is made.\n\nThe named conditional PD bridge remains unchanged and is not an input\nto PC1–PC14. Its full analytic dependencies and Lean build were not\nindependently recertified. Full energy finiteness, pointwise constancy,\nall-cycle exclusion and full Collatz remain open.\n\nThe next concrete direction is to combine the active-boundary A/B rules\nwith the S/R birth transfers in a single estimate retaining their color\nmasks and repeated incidences. It must control odd primitive births and\ninternal mass, including the possible regime PC12, rather than merely\nimprove an endpoint coefficient.\n\n## Local map and service verification\n\nThree green nodes record the all-even estimate, exact Dirichlet balances\nand conditional prefix consequence. One unfilled node identifies the\nremaining endpoint-negligible mass regime. The full-energy, endpoint-sparsity\nand full-Collatz nodes remain unfilled. Public snapshot425, publication\nrecords and completed cleanup are unchanged.\n\nПолная карта: 597 узлов, 1090 связей; обзор: 203 узла, 423 связи.\nИсходник сохранён, live Mermaid-блоков нет. Offline-проверки синтаксиса,\nэлементов управления и границ подписей прошли без переполнений. Проверены\nтри follow-up и 34 выбранных цвета. Root просмотрел обзор и четыре\nувеличенные новые подписи: три зелёные и одна незакрашенная; текст помещается.\nОбзор требует увеличения. Предупреждение Graphviz о метрике кириллицы\nсохраняется; свежая проверка локальной карты в браузере не выполнялась.\nМетаданные: 1381 документ, 5602 локальные ссылки; отсутствующих ссылок\nи конфликтов классов нет. Это служебные проверки, не доказательство математики.\n","REVIEW_0485_SIGNED_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md":"# SB review — signed path control and one remaining multiplicative defect\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Previous AC was progress.\nSB is further bounded analytical progress; full Collatz remains incomplete\nand the goal active. There is no external blocker.\n\n## Accepted scope\n\nThe complete [SB1–SB18](SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md)\nkeeps the negative terms in H=3D_S-2Z_S=2L_odd-3L_2+O(1). Its\nnonnegative odd-path potential (5/2,2,0) bounds the signed rewards on\nall finite A/B prefixes. The initial right coefficients are (1,5/4,0),\nand the left coefficients are (1/2,0,5/8), by birth residue modulo3.\nNegative even-L2 terms before the first odd boundary are retained.\nEvery possible initial depth, truncation and never-odd case is included.\n\nThus the path sum K has an exact upper bound with birth cutoff X/2;\nonly transport from K to H introduces O(1). The previous AC identities\nthen yield uniformly in X and beta\n\n    E(X)\u003c=20Z_R(X)-15D_R(X)-3P(X)+C\u003c=20Z_R(X)+C.\n\nSince Z_R is a birth subset, finite total birth energy is equivalent to\nfinite Z_R harmonic mass. D_S finiteness now follows automatically if\nZ_R is finite. The constant20 is not a contraction of total energy.\n\nExact R-column masks on y=2^h q, q odd and0 mod3, share the filter\nd(q)=1. Their bits a_h=d(2^h q) satisfy a_0=1,a_2=0. Terminal targets\nare falls1->0, missing-predecessor birth targets are rises0->1. The\nfirst fall necessarily occurs at h=0 or1; later falls are nonadjacent,\nat h>=3. This gives fall weights between1/2 and7/6 and exact cutoffs\n\n    G(X/8)/8 \u003c= Z_R(X) \u003c= (7/24)G(X/4),\n    G(Y)=sum_(u\u003c=Y, u odd)|beta(3u)-beta(u)|/u.\n\nConsequently E(X)\u003c=(35/6)G(X/4)+C and G(X)\u003c=8E(8X). Finiteness\nof E, Z_R and G is equivalent. Finiteness of G itself is NOT proved.\nThe signed R-column telescope has uniformly bounded source/target annulus\nand column-top errors. No divergent harmonic series are subtracted.\n\nThe exact next interface is g(2n)=g(n) and\ng(S(n))-g(n)=beta((9n+3)/2)-beta((9n+1)/2) for odd n. It records a\ncommutator with multiplication by3; no summability follows merely from\nthis identity.\n\n## Four roles, reading and corrections\n\nRoot independently derived the estimates and read all three complete\nreports and their final audits of the complete root SB file:\n\n- [0431: exact signed endpoint masks](FOLLOWUP_0431_SIGNED_ENDPOINT_MASKS.md).\n- [0459: path potentials and telescoping](FOLLOWUP_0459_SIGNED_BALANCE_AND_TELESCOPING.md).\n- [0483: sources, cutoffs and logical scope](FOLLOWUP_0483_SIGNED_ENERGY_SOURCE_SCOPE.md).\n\nAll three reused helpers are completed and read SB1–SB18. These are\ninternal manual checks, not external peer review or formal verification.\nCorrections and explicit scope controls include:\n\n- The h=1 R-column source is8u when q=3u. No additional d(8q)=0\n  condition is required; the base filter and exact colors suffice.\n- The column bracket is nonnegative and at least1/(4q). A preliminary\n  claim that it could have either sign was removed. The total finite\n  source/target expression still carries its uniformly bounded error.\n- Column truncation contributes an error in addition to the affine\n  source/target cutoff. Its coefficient denominators are top indices\n  in (X/2,X]; the next-bit indicators belong to (X,2X].\n- Initial even negative terms and the possibility of never reaching an\n  odd boundary are included, so residue1's zero left coefficient is\n  justified. A finite signed-prefix potential needs a nonnegative\n  terminal potential; that hypothesis is verified.\n- The auxiliary state-potential obstruction is restricted to the two\n  explicit relaxed R flip inequalities. Multiplying them gives\n  rho>=4/3. This does not prohibit broader arithmetic/color potentials.\n  Unweighted transition counts were separated from harmonic masses;\n  broad claims classifying every state potential were not accepted.\n- The root and auxiliary report reused equation labels SB1 differently;\n  that presentation ambiguity was corrected in the report. No root\n  harmonic formula was replaced by an unweighted count identity.\n\nThere are three follow-ups and no new distinct agents. Counts remain\n485 distinct / 484 main reports reviewed / 0095 rejected / 515 reserved.\nHistorical PBK0485 and FP/UD limits remain recorded. No mathematical\nprogram, CAS, enumeration or Lean was used. File checks and rendering are\nsequential with one configured worker; no GPU or OS-wide CPU claim.\n\n## Primary sources and remaining obligation\n\nRoot freshly read the introduction's transported-exceptional-set argument,\nProposition1.14 and Remark1.16 in\n[Tao's primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1909.03562v7). Its mixing\nestimates concern random Syracuse offsets. They do not directly bound\nthis deterministic G; applying them would require a new justified\ninterface controlling the color tests and transported exceptional sets.\nThe source role also records its targeted Rozier reading. No such\nexternal theorem is imported into SB, and no exhaustive novelty claim\nis made. The contribution comparison already in the archive is not redone.\n\nThe next concrete task is to combine SB18 with g(2n)=g(n), retaining\nshared colors and signs, and test for an independent summable bound on\n|g|. A bound on a signed cumulative sum alone is insufficient.\n\nThe named conditional PD170 input is unused here and remains a separate\ndependency. Even a future finite-energy proof still needs the recorded\npointwise step for full Collatz; its analytic dependencies and Lean build\nhave not been independently recertified. No full proof, counterexample,\nall-cycle exclusion, completion percentage or deadline is supplied.\n\n## Local map verification\n\nFour new green nodes record R-column compensation, the signed S potential,\nthe single-terminal criterion and the multiplicative-defect criterion.\nThe new G-finiteness obligation and the existing Z_R/full-energy/full-Collatz\nnodes remain unfilled. Public snapshot425, publications, CAPTCHA and cleanup\nare unchanged. No upload or video rendering occurred.\n\nПолная карта: 606 узлов, 1110 связей; обзор: 212 узлов, 443 связи.\nИсходник сохранён, live Mermaid-блоков нет. Offline-проверки синтаксиса,\nэлементов управления и границ подписей прошли без переполнений. Проверены\nтри follow-up и 43 выбранных цвета. Root просмотрел обзор и пять\nувеличенных новых подписей: четыре зелёные, одна незакрашенная; текст\nпомещается. Обзор требует увеличения. Предупреждение Graphviz о метрике\nкириллицы сохраняется; свежая браузерная проверка не выполнялась.\nМетаданные: 1391 документ, 5637 локальных ссылок; отсутствующих ссылок и\nконфликтов классов нет. Служебная QA не является проверкой доказательства.\n","REVIEW_0485_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH.md":"# SG review — finite chains and the 7/9 image-component estimate\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root review; the name 0485\ncontinues the review series and does not mean another distinct agent launch.\nAll three helpers completed; root fully read the final five reports.\n\n## Result and exact acceptance boundary\n\n[SG1–SG12](SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md) concerns an\narbitrary exact binary shortcut-Collatz invariant β. It constructs no\nnonconstant invariant. The retained S(q)=(3q+1)/2 graph contains all birth\nvertices, including even endpoints, and retains an edge only when both\nends are births. This graph has finite components and is distinct from\nthe old H/G₃ graph. Its weight bound is W(a)≤3/a.\n\nThe inverse-image lemma proves d(q)=d(floor(q/2))=1 for every failed\ninverse at an image origin. Its positive-path merger includes h=1.\nThis supplies depth≥2 and b\u003cS(q)/6; the forced even child alone would\nnot suffice. It also forces the next charged odd inverse to skip at\nleast one depth. Keeping its valuation h, rather than multiplying every\norigin by3, gives W(Sq)≤2(1−(2/3)^h)/q.\n\nA nonnegative finite-tail induction with R=2/9 and then monotonicity\ngive full charged-component weights ≤2/(9b) on the left path and\n≤5/(9b) on the right. Distinct origins have distinct inverses, and the\ncomponents are disjoint. With P_S the actual births outside2mod3,\n\n    Eβ(X)≤3E_Ps(X)+(7/9)Eβ(X/6),\n    E_Ps(X)≤Eβ(X)≤(27/2)E_Ps(X).\n\nThe final comparison subtracts finite quantities only. No circular\nassumption of finite total energy enters the argument. The uncontrolled\nprimitive term remains essential; neither its finiteness nor full\nCollatz follows. Older density-based contradictions require their own\nassumptions and are not certified by this review.\n\n## Five report scopes\n\nRoot read all five reports, including their corrected final additions.\nThree existing helpers were reused; these are not five new agent launches.\n\n| Report | Accepted scope |\n|---|---|\n| [0431: old chain weights](FOLLOWUP_0431_EXACT_CHAIN_WEIGHT.md) | Exact centered telescoping, word-sensitive upper bound, worst ordering, arithmetic primitive examples approaching9. Does not prove actual masks realize those examples. |\n| [0483: old chain flux](FOLLOWUP_0483_CHAIN_FLUX_IDENTITY.md) | Positive component flux identity, minus outgoing term at finite cutoff, no upper bound from an unknown terminal mass. |\n| [0459: primitive even exit](FOLLOWUP_0459_PRIMITIVE_EVEN_EXIT.md) | Corrected exceptional higher-return mask; independent SG inverse, h=1, charge,7/9 and full root-text audit. |\n| [0431: single odd graph](FOLLOWUP_0431_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH.md) | Finite graph, arithmetic sharpness3, independent SG1–SG12 audit after separately checking SG4's depth premise. |\n| [0483: single odd inverse](FOLLOWUP_0483_SINGLE_ODD_INVERSE.md) | Full root-text read; independently checked inverse h=1/depth≥2, valuation weights, tail induction,7/9, cutoffs and finite subtraction. Prior §§1–3 check the finite graph. |\n\nRoot also freshly read BR and SC, checked the merging arithmetic,\ncomponent partition, tail induction and cutoff inequalities directly.\nThis is internal manual review, not formal verification or an independent\nexternal expert endorsement. Main accepted-task counts are not raised by\nthese follow-ups:485 distinct/484 main accepted/0095 rejected/515 reserved.\nHistorical missing PBK0485 and FP/UD limitations remain in the state history.\nThe longer pending0483 follow-up was interrupted and resumed with the same\nagent and a bounded final audit; the completed report above is the evidence.\n\n## Corrections and rejected inferences\n\n1. The initial 0459 primitive-family report incorrectly rejected a birth\n   a₁>n because n was smallest. Root rejected that inference: minimality\n   excludes smaller births only. The corrected family144t+99 leaves\n   d(243t+168)=0 open; it can create the higher birth243t+168. For odd t,\n   minimality forces a still larger inverse birth216t+149, not a contradiction.\n2. An initial 0483 concern left h=1 uncovered. The explicit positive\n   merger v−1 and3v−1, with v≡3mod4, closes that case; v=1 would n=2,\n   which is excluded. No positive exceptional mass is dropped.\n3. The displayed inequality q/4\u003cq/6 in 0483's intermediate draft was\n   wrong. The correct cutoff is floor(q/4)\u003cn/6=q/4+1/12. The intended\n   X/6 estimate and root proof use this corrected formula.\n4. 0431 correctly observed that the forced-child identities alone do not\n   establish depth≥2. The full SG4 inverse lemma supplies the missing\n   premise and was then independently checked. Its earlier limited-local\n   warning is marked historical; it does not refute SG4.\n5. The intermediate4/9 origin estimate times a uniform3 gives4/3,\n   not a contraction. The accepted7/9 estimate instead charges FULL\n   components with valuation-dependent costs; it must not be described\n   as a multiplication of the former two constants.\n6. Finiteness of S-growth components is not terminal descent below the\n   initial origin and does not empty oldΓ. Finite E_Ps is not proved;\n   even proving it would leave any needed finite-energy-to-constancy\n   argument to be justified separately.\n\n## Primary-source check and novelty scope\n\nRead Sections1–2 of Rozier–Terracol,\n[Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences, v5,17May2026](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948).\nThe finite affine formula and the additive remainder are pertinent to\nkeeping exact odd words. Their computational and conditional conclusions\nwere not imported. The full arXiv2605.13886 paper was not read after an\nHTML failure; a search abstract is not treated as proof input.\nArchive R185/OT and the variable-run audit already contain odd acceleration\nand inverse layers. No worldwide novelty or priority assertion follows.\nThere is no new use of Tao, probabilistic independence or PD density claims.\n\n## Map, QA and next obligation\n\nOnly SingleOddFiniteChains and SingleOddImageContraction are green for the\nbounded SG statements. SingleOddPrimitiveEnergyOpen and all existing\nglobal obligations remain unfilled. The next obligation is actual P_S\nmass with its exact colors and multiplicities, initially separating\nresidues0 and1mod3; an alternative is true terminal descent below an\noriginal origin. The present source term can contain the remaining\ndifficulty. No percentage or completion time can be inferred.\n\nПолная карта579 узлов/1046 связей; обзор185/379. Исходник сохранён.\nOffline синтаксис JavaScript, ID элементов управления и границы подписей\nпроверены без ошибок. Обзор PNG просмотрен: мелкие подписи требуют\nмасштаба HTML/SVG. Три новые SG-подписи отдельно просмотрены в увеличении,\nобрезания текста нет. Предупреждение Graphviz о метриках кириллицы\nсохраняется; свежего браузерного просмотра локальной карты не было.\n[Metadata](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_0485.json)\nне выявил битых ссылок, конфликтов цветов или пропущенных отметок чтения.\n[Пять follow-up и20 выбранных цветов](SG_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json)\nпроверены отдельно. Это служебное QA, не формальная проверка математики.\n\nAll work is local. Public snapshot425, the movie and five-platform\ndistribution are unchanged. The repeated CAPTCHA approval was already\nfulfilled: the loaded Hugging Face discussion2 was read again, with\nboth authors, all links and the scope warning visible. No duplicate post,\nupload, render of the movie or deletion occurred.\n","REVIEW_0485_SMALL_ODD_LIFTS.md":"# Root review — VL/SL2: variable lifts and the complete two-odd-step class\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Four mathematical roles:\nroot and reused0485/0459/0483. Zero numerical programs, enumeration,\nCAS or Lean; no new distinct agents. The preceding AR/FL pass was\nprogress. This pass is progress because it excludes an entire variable\namplification strategy, rather than just one prescribed periodic rule.\n\nFull Collatz remains open. This is a density-zero theorem for a\nspecified generated family, not an exclusion of a new class of bad orbits.\n\n## Main accepted theorem\n\n[Root proof SL1–SL10](SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md).\n\nConsider genuine shortcut parity-word pairs that structurally preserve\nordered adjacency and have at most TWO odd steps on each side. The two\nwords must have the same length K and odd count P. With forward\nconstants C_L,C_R, the structural condition is\n\n    C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P.\n\nThere are exactly four rules, without any a priori bound on K:\n\n| Rule | Left inverse expression | K,P | Terminal-seed condition |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| identity | n | 0,0 | all n |\n| L | (4n-1)/3 | 2,1 | n=1 modulo3 |\n| L^2 | (16n-7)/9 | 4,2 | n=1 modulo9 |\n| M | (32n-5)/9 | 5,2 | n=1 modulo9 |\n\nThe classification uses the exact intercepts 2^i when P=1 and\n3*2^i+2^j when P=2, then valuation and sign comparisons. Only K=2,3\nin the two-odd case are checked by their three symbolic intercepts.\nNo computational search is used.\n\nThe M pair is OOEEE/EEOEO and is genuine on sources32m+3,32m+4,\nwith targets9m+1,9m+2. It disproves the tentative assertion that all\nstructural rules are powers of L. Root found and verified the example;\n0459 and0483 independently checked it and the classification after\nreceiving the candidate.\n\nFor ANY sequence of these four rules, including nonperiodic choices,\nlet A(n_0) count distinct positive source coordinates generated from one\nfixed terminal seed n_0. L^2 is redundant as two L steps. The mod27\ntransition table for L and M leaves at most one continuing chain;\nevery discarded branch has at most two further steps. If n_0=1,\nA(1)={1,3}. For X>=n_0>1,\n\n    #(A(n_0) intersect [1,X])\n      \u003c= 4+3 floor(log_(4/3)((X-1)/(n_0-1)))\n      = O_(n_0)(log X).\n\nThe count includes non-first-hit sources, so every killed subset obeys\nthe same upper bound. It counts distinct sources, not word schedules.\nA loop can create arbitrarily many schedules without adding sources.\n\nIf n_0 is one witness in Q_T(a,c), each generated pair remains a\nwitness by following its return to n_0,n_0+1 and then the original\ntarget paths. Thus these rules cannot amplify one witness into a\npositive-density family. The whole Q_T(a,c) need not equal this family.\nFor fixed odd targets the C/T predecessor interface is retained from\nID/I; even-target substitution is not asserted.\n\nThe theorem permits an infinite sparse chain. It does not prove its\nexistence or impossibility, and does not cover P>=3, order reversals,\ngeneral weighted relations, unequal first-hit depths, or all ways to\nreach the two original targets.\n\n## Variable-word interface and a separate seed-density bound\n\n[0485's corrected report](FOLLOWUP_0485_VARIABLE_PAIR_LIFTS.md)\ngives the exact composition. With cumulative K_r,P_r,\n\n    C_r=sum_(q=1)^r 2^(K_r-K_q) 3^(P_(q-1)) C_(i_q),\n    F^(r)(n)=(2^K_r n-C_r)/3^P_r.\n\nFinal divisibility implies every earlier prefix divisibility. This\nalgebraic fact already holds for integer intercepts; genuine words are\nneeded to identify the integer maps with actual Collatz paths.\n\nFor a genuine word and n>0, an integral inverse automatically has the\nprescribed parity word and is positive. Root proved this by induction\non the first bit and by invariance of nonpositive integers under T;\n0459 independently checked the on-disk proof. First-hit labels remain\na separate restriction.\n\nThere are two variables and two different counting statements:\n\n- terminal seeds lie in one class modulo3^P_r and number at most\n  X/3^P_r+1 up to X;\n- sources lie in one class modulo2^K_r and number at most\n  X/2^K_r+1 up to X.\n\nOne must transform the interval through the affine relation when changing\nvariables. There is no additional terminal-seed modulus2^K_r.\n\nFor a finite alphabet of nonidentity genuine rules with\nrho=sum_i 3^(-P_i)\u003c1, the upper natural density of terminal seeds\nsupporting s steps is at most rho^s. Fix s before letting X grow;\nthe finitely many additive counting errors then disappear. The set\nsupporting an infinite schedule has density0. Finite branching supplies\nthe path compactness when a seed supports every finite depth.\nThis counts seeds across all integers; it does not bound the sources\ngenerated by one fixed seed. The latter is the separate SL2 theorem.\n\nA prescribed infinite schedule with cumulative P_r tending to infinity\ndetermines the compatible 3-adic seed\n\n    sum_(q>=1) C_(i_q) 3^(P_(q-1)) 2^(-K_q).\n\nThe uniqueness of that 3-adic value is not an argument excluding positive\ninteger seeds. The constant-L schedule at seed1 is a direct counterexample\nto such an exclusion.\n\n## Rejected draft arguments and corrections\n\nRoot read all three reports and requested the following substantive\ncorrections before acceptance.\n\n1. The initial 0485 draft placed the dyadic SOURCE cylinder on the\n   TERMINAL seed and derived X/(2^K 3^P)+1 by CRT. This bound was rejected.\n   The exact counterexample is L: all n=1+3t are legal terminal seeds,\n   with sources1+4t. The revision separates both variables and bounds.\n2. Parity and positivity were initially called independent filters even\n   under genuine-word hypotheses. Final integral inverse admissibility\n   makes them automatic; only first-hit killing remains independent.\n   Formal arbitrary constants do not inherit this shortcut.\n3. 0459 initially wrote M(n+1)=M(n)+1. The correct right map is\n   G(n+1)=(32(n+1)-28)/9=M(n)+1. Its affine constant differs from M's.\n4. A bounded graph does not force a given switching sequence to be\n   periodic. It only bounds its set of distinct states. A finite graph\n   may be traversed nonperiodically. The overstatement was removed.\n5. 0483 initially conflated coalescing stems with adjacency-preserving\n   blocks in Elia–Tucker. The two definitions are distinct; the manual\n   M example, not the cited counterexamples to Garner, establishes our\n   particular additional block. Root also separated manual results\n   from the source list and fixed malformed LaTeX.\n6. The one continuing chain conclusion is proved by the particular\n   mod27 table for L,M. It is not a generic property of finite alphabets,\n   finite branching, or general Collatz predecessor trees.\n\n## Independent audits and source scope\n\n[0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_STRUCTURAL_LIFT_ARITHMETIC.md) checked the\nclassification, generated-source bound, and the entire saved SL1–SL10\nproof, including the exact3B_X+1 count and seed1.\n[0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_VARIABLE_LIFT_SOURCES.md) independently audited\nthe classification and distinguished the primary definitions from the\nmanual arithmetic. Root read the revised reports and their additions.\n\n0485 independently checked the saved SL2 proof and the fixed-seed output\nbound in its final section7. Root read that addition and clarified two\nsentences: parity plus a size bound forces the left intercept to be odd,\nand the right word uses G(n+1), never M(n+1). These corrections do not\nchange the accepted proof or its numerical constants.\n\nRoot read the available [Elia–Tucker HTML](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141),\nespecially Definitions1–2, Proposition1, Theorem4.1 and its conclusion.\nThe paper supplies prior language for these blocks and separate results\nabout coalescing stems. Its computational examples were not re-enumerated.\nOriginal Garner and Terras texts were not newly audited. This is a bounded\nsource check; worldwide novelty of SL2 has not been established.\n\n## Remaining route\n\nA successful amplification must use something beyond the classified\nP\u003c=2 ordered structural rules: longer irreducible blocks, reversed\norder, or weighted relations that do not preserve adjacency at every\nselected boundary. Next, inspect P=3 symbolically and distinguish\ncompositions of known rules from genuinely new branches; alternatively\nuse the exact AR recurrence to retain intermediate weighted offsets.\n\nNo lower bound for general Q(a,c), actual RAP, universal descent, all\npositive cycles, or full Collatz is supplied here. The number of green\nmap nodes is not a percentage of solution, and no completion time follows.\n\n\n## Source update — 18 September 2026, SL3 follow-up\n\nM is explicitly present in LaTourette's 2007 Table 4.1. Its earlier\ndescription as new meant only new to our local analysis. See the dated\n[source audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md) and\n[SL3 acceptance](REVIEW_0485_THREE_ODD_LIFTS.md); no mathematical claim\nin the P\u003c=2 proof is changed by this attribution correction.\n","REVIEW_0485_SPARSE_GROWTH_CORE.md":"# BC review — sparse infinite growth, finite-chain origins still open\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Manual root review with\nthree reused agents. No new distinct agent, numerical experiment, CAS,\nenumeration or Lean. This is an internal bounded audit, not external\npeer review or a formal proof of Collatz.\n\nReviewed proof: [BC1–BC13](MIXED_GROWTH_COMPENSATION_AND_SPARSE_CORE.md).\nInherited input: [SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md), with its\nactual birth/color guards and [prior review](REVIEW_0485_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md).\n\n## Material read and accepted scope\n\n- [0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_MIXED_BLOCK_COMPENSATION.md): exact mixed affine\n  composition, ancestry compensation, BC1–BC13 audit. Root read the full\n  report and subsequent corrections.\n- [0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_H_G3_SWITCH_BLOCK.md): H/G₃ switch residues,\n  finite homogeneous run lengths, BC1–BC13 audit. Root read the full\n  report including the corrected finite-prefix scope and final addendum.\n- [0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_TWO_SYMBOL_CODE_SCOPE.md): bounded archive and\n  primary-source scope, BC3–BC13 audit. Root read the full report and\n  bibliographic corrections. This agent did not re-audit all inherited\n  BR estimates or the earlier physical/mixing claims.\n\nRoot independently checked the BC affine identities, exact residue guards,\nfinite-class counting, harmonic integral and chain decomposition. All three\nhelpers are completed. No count of distinct launches changes:\n485 distinct,484 main reports accepted,0095 rejected,515 reserved. Follow-ups\nare tracked separately. Historical unreceived PBK audit0485 and FP/UD\nscope limitations remain in the earlier acceptance records.\n\n## Accepted statements\n\n1. For a mixed growth word, n_r=λ(n_0+c)−c with1≤c≤3 and\n   λ≥(9/8)^r. The multiplier depends on the numbers of H/G₃ steps;\n   their order affects the additive term. For an actual active exit K\n   whose first zero-boundary ancestor is at depth ell, descent below\n   n_0 is exactly K\u003c2^ell n_0. No universal depth bound is supplied.\n2. Let Γ be the positive integers admitting an infinite arithmetic chain\n   under H=(9n+5)/4 on3mod4 and G₃=(9n+3)/8 on13mod16, without\n   color masks. A length-r word with odd final index corresponds exactly\n   to one middle parity class for W followed by11. Thus its finite-prefix\n   count is at most (X/2)(3/8)^r+2^r. Choosing2^r≤√X\u003c2^(r+1)\n   gives N_Γ(X)\u003c2√X and Σ_(n∈Γ)1/n≤4.\n3. The bound4 is for Γ only. It neither excludes one positive member nor\n   bounds the harmonic mass of any fixed finite-prefix set. It does not\n   bound all possible bad Collatz starts.\n4. H(4t+3)=9t+8 and G₃(16t+13)=18t+15 have disjoint image\n   classes8mod9 and6mod9. The combined map is injective. After applying\n   actual birth masks, components remain increasing finite chains or\n   one-sided infinite chains, each with a unique origin.\n5. Let A_β be origins of finite actual growth components; E_A(X) is their\n   sum of1/a up to X. With E_β(X)=Σ_(n≤X)e(n)/n,\n\n       E_A(X) ≤ E_β(X) ≤ 9 E_A(X)+4.\n\n   The constant9 follows from n_j≥(9/8)^j a on one chain;4 bounds\n   all infinite growth vertices via Γ. This is a reduction to origin\n   mass, not a finite upper bound on that mass.\n\nThe formulas hold for every exact binary invariant, including a constant\none; they do not assert existence of a nonconstant coloring. The energy\ndecomposition uses the accepted SC routing. BC does not use PD170 or\npositive-density hypotheses. Any separate inference that total energy\nmust diverge retains its own CE/SB/H hypotheses.\n\n## Corrections made before acceptance\n\n- Two active ancestry levels give ell≥2, not ell=2.\n- An earlier 0431 paragraph wrongly let the multiplier depend on word\n  order; only the affine offset has that dependence.\n- Root found an incorrect intermediate bound≤√X/2 in 0431. It was\n  replaced by X/2^(r+1)\u003c√X. The accepted bound\u003c2√X and root BC8\n  were already stated correctly. The unsupported comment about possibly\n  identical residue classes of different mode words was removed.\n- 0459 distinguishes the G₃ middle path T³ from the subsequent H path\n  T², and distinguishes terminal valuation residues1 and2. Its earlier\n  suggestion that long finite prefixes have total harmonic mass≤4,\n  or that a finite exception is required, was removed.\n- Bernstein–Lagarias uses Φ^{-1} in (1.5), not Φ_1. Elia–Tucker's\n  exact title was corrected after root opened the primary sources.\n- In the final BC note the finite-prefix set is P_r, to distinguish it\n  from the affine sum S_r. This is a notation change only.\n\n## Primary-source scope and precedence\n\nRoot and0483 read the Introduction and coding formulas of\n[Bernstein–Lagarias, The 3x+1 Conjugacy Map](https://dept.math.lsa.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/bernstein.pdf).\nThe 2-adic conjugacy and finite-modulus compatibility supply classical\ncontext; they do not turn an arbitrary infinite code into a positive integer.\n\nRoot opened the primary text of [Marcus Elia and Amanda Tucker,\nConsecutive Integers and the Collatz Conjecture](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141)\nand checked its definitions, Proposition1, finite-parity attribution to\nTerras, and the scope of the Garner counterexamples. The ordinary C and\nshortcut T conventions must remain separate. Terras's original full proof\nwas not re-audited in this pass; its direct PDF was unavailable to0483.\nBC supplies its own finite-class argument and does not depend on a global\nstopping theorem from these sources.\n\n0483's archive comparison covered named parts of R150/R154/R155/R159\nand the current BR/SC reports. BR had separate branch injectivity, with\ncross-branch overlap left open. No earlier exact combined mod9 separation\nwas found in that limited reading. This is not a worldwide novelty claim.\n\n## Map, next obligation and verification\n\nGreen SparseGrowthCoreEnergy means only the accepted BC scope. New\nFiniteGrowthOriginEnergyOpen remains uncolored. The full Collatz node,\npositive-cycle exclusion, critical upper bound and global block descent\nremain open. The next concrete target is a structural estimate on origins\nof finite actual growth chains, or an origin-to-origin transfer with a\nstrictly decreasing quantity. A terminal transfer smaller than the current\nendpoint need not be smaller than the origin, and may return to its chain.\n\nLocal map rendered sequentially with574 nodes/1034 edges; overview180/367.\nFull source preserved. Offline JavaScript syntax, controls and SVG text\nbounds passed; the PNG overview was viewed. Labels require zoom at this\nscale. The existing Graphviz Cyrillic metric warning remains; no new browser\nview of the local map was made. [Metadata check](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_SPARSE_GROWTH_CORE_0485.json)\nfound no broken links, conflicting colors or missing read markers.\n[Three follow-ups and15 selected colors](BC_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json)\npassed separately. These are artifact checks, not mathematical proof checks.\n\nAll results in this review are local. The published snapshot425 and video\nare unchanged; publication, CAPTCHA and selected media cleanup were already\ncompleted. No new public submission or deletion was made here. The full\nresearch goal remains active; no defensible percentage or completion time\nfollows from the count of accepted local lemmas.\n","REVIEW_0485_THREE_ODD_LIFTS.md":"# Root acceptance — SL3/FO: three odd steps and arbitrary switching\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root plus reused helpers\n0459,0485,0483 supplied four roles. All three follow-ups and their corrections\nwere read. No new distinct agent, mathematical program, numerical enumeration,\nCAS or Lean. This extends a restricted counting theorem; full Collatz remains\nopen and no new class of bad forward orbits has been excluded.\n\n## Accepted result and its significance\n\n[Complete root proof SL3.1–SL3.10](THREE_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md).\n\nFor ordered adjacency-preserving shortcut word pairs with equal length K\nand exactly P=3 odd steps, there are exactly six rules:\n\n    (K,C_L,C_R) = (6,37,74), (7,29,130), (7,47,148),\n                  (7,23,124), (8,19,248), (8,31,260).\n\nNo upper bound on K is assumed. The proof splits both orientations of the\nodd intercept and exhausts the valuations and sign cases. The first three\nrules are L^3,L after M,M after L. Combining the accepted P\u003c=2 list with\nthis P=3 classification reduces arbitrary compositions to five generators:\n\n    L=(4n-1)/3,      n=1 mod3;\n    M=(32n-5)/9,     n=1 mod9;\n    J=(128n-23)/27,  n=16 mod27;\n    N=(256n-19)/27,  n=16 mod27;\n    R=(256n-31)/27,  n=19 mod27.\n\nThe finite-domain reduction preserves all intermediate integrality and\npositivity. It does not claim that every pair of Collatz predecessors\nfactors into these rules.\n\nFor all finite compositions of ALL ordered structural rules with P\u003c=3,\nlet A(n_0) be the distinct generated positive source coordinates from\none fixed terminal seed. Then A(1)={1,3}; for X>=n_0>1,\n\n    #(A(n_0) intersect [1,X]) \u003c=2048 sqrt((X-1)/(n_0-1)).\n\nThus this family has density zero, even with arbitrary nonperiodic\nswitching. First-hit filtering only reduces the count. The result rules\nout positive-density amplification from one witness by this entire class\nof small ordered lifts. It neither bounds the full Q(a,c) nor rules out\nan infinite sparse chain. The preceding O(log X) theorem remains the\nsharper estimate on the smaller L,M alphabet.\n\nThe proof uses a new-to-this-pass counting argument: sum inverse-square-root\nslopes over all admissible words. Residue information bounds this total by\n2048 uniformly over positive integer seeds. The decisive coefficient is\n\n    (sqrt(3/4)+sqrt(9/32))*sqrt(27/128)+sqrt(27/256)\n       = (18sqrt(2)+21sqrt(3))/64 \u003c255/256.\n\nGrowth of n-1 converts this summability into a source count. The residue\ntable is a set of uniform coarse inequalities, not an assumed closed\nfinite-state model for future 3-adic digits.\n\n## Audits and corrections before acceptance\n\n- [0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_THREE_ODD_LIFT_CLASSIFICATION.md) independently\n  classified P=3. Root required explicit sign contradictions in two\n  subcases and both valuation subcases when powers coincide. These\n  omissions were expanded and malformed LaTeX corrected. Its final\n  section6 audits the saved root SL3.1–SL3.6, including domains and\n  composition order.\n- [0485](FOLLOWUP_0485_FINITE_LIFT_OUTPUT_BOUNDS.md) proved a general\n  finite-alphabet weighted-tree criterion and independently audited\n  the square-root argument. Its final section7 checks the saved root\n  SL3.7–SL3.9. Destinations labelled live can be any live state; they\n  must not be incorrectly narrowed to D or E. Strong induction uses\n  earlier-depth D bounds, so there is no circular supremum argument.\n- Its phrase claiming a stronger estimate than O(log X) was corrected:\n  O(sqrt X) is a weaker bound for a larger family. A heading saying\n  four extra rules was corrected to three. Preliminary open statements\n  in sections4–5 are now explicitly superseded for the five generators.\n- [0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md) checked primary-source\n  scope. Root independently inspected the accessible thesis landing-page\n  OCR after the separate page29 endpoint failed. All five generator\n  pairs are prior examples. Their rederivation is not a novelty claim;\n  the literature priority of our counting theorem has not been established.\n\nThe old bounded-side-branch proof cannot be extended unchanged:\n73 has children97 and259 under L,M; both admit N, producing919 and2455,\nboth of which admit L. This exact example is accepted only as a failure\nof that old pruning argument, not as evidence of infinite branching.\n\n## Sources and preserved dependencies\n\nLaTourette, *Explorations of the Collatz Conjecture* (2007), Table4.1 and\nSection4.2.4 were inspected in the [primary thesis copy](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college).\nThe source report records the precise overlap and OCR limitations. No\nunread theorem or global absence claim is imported.\n\nThe genuine-word integral-inverse lemma and complete P\u003c=2 classification\nremain named dependencies from [VL/SL2](REVIEW_0485_SMALL_ODD_LIFTS.md).\nThe general criterion in 0485's FO1–FO11 also remains valid: for expanding\nmaps with a common shifted growth cone, summability of reciprocal-slope\nweights implies o(X) distinct outputs. The unrestricted slope sum test\nfails here; actual admissibility pruning supplies the square-root bound.\n\nAR/FL, integer-target ID/I, and the earlier QS170/PD,C180,R1–R6,SF/AE\nclaims retain their stated scopes; this pass does not reaudit the entire\nhistorical proof graph. C/T reachability substitution remains restricted\nto the previously verified fixed-odd-target interface.\n\n## Open obligations and next step\n\nP>=4 rules, order reversal, asynchronous paths, general affine gap\nrelations and factorization of all paired predecessors remain uncovered.\nPositive upper density of the full Q(a,c) from one witness, RAP of the\nactual invariant indicator, universal descent and all positive cycles\nremain open. No percent-complete or completion time can be deduced.\n\nThe next useful target is a symbolic control of irreducible blocks across\nodd counts, or a rigorous obstruction to extending the weighted bound.\nKeep the exact affine relation between the two paths. Merely adding\nanother finite P table would not by itself resolve the density gap.\n\nLocal map: add only the checked SL3 counting node with an explicit\nlimitation edge to the still-uncolored paired-density obligation. Public\nsnapshot425 stays unchanged. Publication, CAPTCHA and selected media\ncleanup were already completed; the loaded HF discussion2 was read again\nwithout resubmission. No upload, movie rendering or deletion this pass.\n","REVIEW_0485_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md":"# Приёмка SC — два существенных режима роста и точные маски\n\n19 сентября 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Предыдущий BR/OC-проход —\nпрогресс: принят полный локальный разбор и условный all-scale переход,\nсохранены карта и state. Этот проход выводит дополнительное цветовое\nограничение и сокращает действительный остаток. Полная цель Коллатца\nактивна и не достигнута; внешнего блокера нет.\n\n## Принятая область\n\n[SC1–SC6](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md) проверены вручную.\nПри X_{h,s}=3^s2^(h−s)u−1>0, u нечётном,0≤s≤h, конечные настоящие\nслова дают β(X)=C_h,β(X+1)=B_s,β(3X+2)=C_{h+1}, где\nC_h=β(3^h u−1),B_s=β(3^s u). Поэтому e(X)=1 ровно при\nC_h=B_s≠C_{h+1}. Угол u=1,h=s=0 исключён как X=0.\n\nЕсли3^h u≡3mod4, два явных пути сливают3^h u−1 и3^(h+1)u−1.\nТогда C_h=C_{h+1} и все e на строке нулевые. Следовательно, для\nbirth n=2^a u−1 с a≥2 необходимо3^a u≡1mod4, и C_{a−1}=C_a.\n\nЭто уточняет BR5. При a≥3 у k=H(n),p=floor(k/2) имеем d(k)=d(p):\nG₁-ветвь d(k)=1,d(p)=0 невозможна. При a=2 активность даёт\nn≡3mod16 и чётный G₅-выход p; его композиция с even-transfer даёт\na'≤floor((27n+31)/32)\u003cn. Край n=3 исключён прямыми конечными путями.\nДля любого birth3mod4 остаётся меньший birth либо H-growth.\nСовместно с BR остаются только два существенных растущих перехода:\n\n- G₃(n)=(9n+3)/8 на13mod16 с e(output)=1,d(output)=d(output+1)=0;\n- H(n)=(9n+5)/4 на3mod4 с e(output)=1,d(output)=0.\n\nЭто возможности с масками, а не утверждение, что такие births существуют.\nG₅ не объявлен арифметически невозможным: его ростовой промежуточный\nшаг закрывается ниже своего входа. G₁ исключён именно при actual birth.\n\nДля μ=2n+1 растущие переходы равны T³(μ) и T²(μ), слова110 и11.\nПротивоположные outer/middle цвета сохраняются; для G₃ обязательно\nиспользован output-birth guard. G₃ не является общим T-шагом на n.\n\nH-ladder имеет e(n_j)=1 iff β(9^j u)=C_a. При первом сбое с нечётным\nendpoint обе соседние по ancestry границы активны, и имеется birth\n≤floor(n_j/4). Для первого перехода это ниже начального n₀; для\nболее позднего j сама оценка может быть больше n₀. При чётном endpoint\nсо сбойной маской e(endpoint)=0: BR2 применяется только к родителю,\nесли тот доказан чётным birth, иначе используется более глубокий предок.\n\nReset-семья остаётся условным тестом. Минимальность даёт один цвет на\n1,...,2m и контролирует соответствующие9^j лишь до этого порога.\nГлобальный контроль степеней3, смешанных блоков или общий верх энергии\nне получен. Не доказана и невозможность получить такой контроль другим\nаргументом. Невыведенное следствие не объявляется независимым или ложным.\n\n## Четыре роли и исправления\n\nRoot,0431,0459,0483; три исполнителя завершены. Три окончательных отчёта\nполностью прочитаны вместе с исправлениями. Root перечитал весь SC.\n0431 и0459 проверили весь SC;0483 независимо проверил минимальность,\nSC1–SC3, область и предшествование. Он не переаудировал все старые BR-оценки.\n\n| Отчёт | Принятая область |\n|---|---|\n| [0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_RESET_COLOR_TRACE.md) | Две настоящие серии reset-семьи, точные H-маски, минимальный префикс и полный SC-аудит. |\n| [0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_MASKED_H_BLOCK.md) | Общая strip-формула, H-блок, исходный против текущего индекса, полный SC-аудит. |\n| [0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_MINIMUM_BIRTH_EQUIVALENCE.md) | Минимальный birth как первый сдвиг цвета, первичные источники, SC1–SC3 и архивное слияние. |\n\nИсправлена оговорка0483 о недостаточности C-равенства: SC1 действительно\nсвязывает оба конца birth и завершает row-zero. Убраны устаревшие G₁\nварианты из двух отчётов. Уточнены core1 и вспомогательное u_* в reset,\noutput-birth для сохранения цвета G₃ и запрет BR2 на небirth чётном конце.\nRoot внёс в0459 последние пояснения применения к родителю и исправил\nопечатки LaTeX; это не изменение его принятого математического вывода.\n\nСчётчики485 различных /484 основных приняты /0095 отклонён /515 резерв\nне изменены; повторные поручения не новые агенты. Исторический неполученный\nаудит0485 и ограничения FP/UD не заменены текущими проверками.\n\n## Предшествование и первичные источники\n\nМинимальный birth a уже в BP176 и прежнем0431 равен(μ−1)/2, где μ —\nпервый другой цвет. Для индикатора достижения1 это ровно наименьшее\nплохое число, а не новое более слабое универсальное обязательство.\nПарное слияние SC2 уже присутствует в явных путях\n[R143, §2](../../../work/round143-l-general-merging.md). Контроль L-цен\nиз того отчёта здесь не импортируется. Весь архив и мировая литература\nне проверялись заново; приоритет простых формул не заявляется.\n\n0483 сверил официальную запись Terras/IMPAN, запись и abstract Everett,\nа также авторский обзор Lagarias. Root повторно открыл IMPAN и\n[раздел о stopping time](https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node4.html):\nпочти-всюду спуск там не является утверждением для каждого старта.\nЧасть формул в HTML не извлекается; полного переаудита доказательств нет.\nУ root ScienceDirect ответил403, а PDF Terras был недоступен0483;\nполные статьи здесь не объявляются прочитанными. Эти источники служат\nпроверке области и истории, новые SC-формулы доказаны непосредственно.\nPD170/OC и прежние глубокие входы не использованы в SC.\n\n## Карта, ограничения, следующий шаг\n\nНовый зелёный ColorStripTwoGrowthModes означает только принятую локальную\nобласть SC. MaskedBirthBlockDescentOpen и Full остаются неокрашенными.\nПолная карта572 узла/1029 связей, обзор178/362; исходник сохранён.\nOffline JavaScript, controls и границы подписей прошли. PNG просмотрен:\nмелкие подписи требуют масштаба HTML/SVG. Предупреждение Graphviz о\nкириллических метриках остаётся; свежего браузерного просмотра не было.\n[Metadata](METADATA_CHECK_2026-09-19_TWO_GROWTH_MODES_0485.json) не выявил\nбитых ссылок, конфликтов цветов и отсутствующих отметок чтения.\n[Отдельный контроль](SC_FOLLOWUP_AND_COLOR_CHECK_2026-09-19.json) подтвердил\nтри follow-up записи и13 выбранных цветов. Служебный recorder сначала\nне разобрал неэкранированные скобки формул в f-string до любых изменений;\nпосле исправления выполнен один успешный приёмочный запуск. Это служебное\nQA, а не математический эксперимент или формальное доказательство.\n\nСледующий шаг: совместный блок из G₃,H до первого меньшего переноса,\nс оценкой относительно начального birth. Можно использовать точную\nобычную орбиту middle μ и реальные B/C-маски; нельзя заменять их\nсвободными символами или одной целочисленностью. Прежние E-критерии\nсохраняются как другой достаточный путь. Срок и процент решения неизвестны.\n\nМатематических запусков/CAS/переборов/Lean0. Служебная обработка и рендер\nпоследовательны, настроены на один поток без GPU, без общей квоты CPU macOS.\nPublic snapshot425 неизменён; этот SC локальный. Новой публикации,\nбраузерной проверки публикаций, видео или удаления не было. В прошлом\nфинальном ответе ссылка с подписью «обновлённая локальная карта» ошибочно\nвела на публичный snapshot; это исправлено в сообщении и главном state.\n","REVIEW_0485_WEIGHTED_ENDPOINT_SPACING.md":"# WS review — exact active-path rules and weighted endpoint bounds\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Previous EC was progress.\nThis WS pass is progress: two improved, bounded energy comparisons and\ntheir manual audits. Full Collatz remains incomplete; no external blocker.\n\n## Accepted mathematical scope\n\n[WS1–WS9](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md) uses exact\nbinary T-invariance. The identity d(8t+4)=0 forces an odd active boundary\nto continue to its next odd boundary through A(q)=2q+1 or B(q)=4q+1.\nThese are ancestry transitions, not ordinary Collatz iterates.\n\nThe terminal masks, the odd S-target depth lemma and the exclusion of\nsuccessive S-targets two depths apart give the finite-prefix potentials\nV_S=7/4, V_R=1. Thus\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c= (11/8) E_beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2.\n\nThe ancestor classes 0 mod3, 2 mod3, odd 1 mod3 and even 1 mod3 have\nrespective coefficients 3/4, 11/16, 15/16 and 11/8. The additive 3/2\nis included once. This improves the earlier coefficient 2 for terminals.\n\nCombining D_S and D_R on the same odd ancestry, including simultaneous\ncharges rather than deleting overlaps, gives the supersolution\nU=(232,172,184)/189. The two initial paths yield coefficients 101/189,\n241/378 and 104/189 for the three ancestor residues. Consequently\n\n    E_D_S(X)+E_D_R(X) \u003c= (241/378) E_beta(X/6),\n    E_origins(X) \u003c= E_P_1(X)+(241/378) E_beta(X/6).\n\nThis improves the earlier image-origin coefficient 17/18. The first odd\nboundary on the left can occur later than depth two; the proof explicitly\ncovers this with max(U)/8 \u003c= min(U)/4. All infinite path bounds follow\nfrom increasing finite prefixes, without assuming finite total energy.\n\n## Four roles and actual reading\n\nRoot read all three reports, including final full-file audits and corrections:\n\n- [0431](FOLLOWUP_0431_TERMINAL_COLOR_SPACING.md): detailed terminal\n  masks, strip depth, gap rules, potentials and ancestor-class coefficients;\n  surface consistency check of the joint-origin part.\n- [0459](FOLLOWUP_0459_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md): detailed joint-origin\n  charges, U-vector, delayed left depth, residue table and cutoffs.\n- [0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_TERMINAL_COUNT_LOGIC.md): exact rules, recurrences,\n  logical scope, endpoint-count gap and targeted primary-source comparison.\n\nAll three reused helpers read the complete root WS file and completed their\nbounded tasks. Root independently checked the local identities, exclusions,\nrecurrences and initial factors. These are internal manual audits, not\nexternal peer review or formal verification. Three new follow-up scopes\ndo not increase the distinct-agent counts: 485 distinct, 484 main reports\nreviewed, 0095 rejected and 515 reserved. Historical PBK0485 and FP/UD\nlimitations remain unchanged. No mathematical scripts, CAS, enumeration\nor Lean; file checks and rendering use one configured worker sequentially.\n\n## Corrections preserved in the record\n\n- 0431's initial coefficient 2 is superseded by Sections 5–6. Its\n  supersolution signs were corrected to >=, with equality at the selected\n  constants; normalized 11/8 is separated from the actual cost 11/(8b).\n- 0483 initially questioned the right depth-one S-target exclusion.\n  The strip equality d(y)=d(floor(y/2)) closes that objection.\n- 0459's initial obstruction concerned naive removal of overlapping\n  charges. The A/B recurrence gives an improvement while retaining both.\n- A first odd left boundary need not occur at depth two. Later depths\n  are included explicitly; no immediate-depth assumption remains.\n- For image origins, the right depth-one D_S exclusion uses SG4.\n  The residue condition on D_R is necessary, not a sufficiency claim.\n\n## Source and remaining mathematical obligation\n\nThe targeted Rozier–Terracol reading in the root proof and source report\ncovers the indicated definitions and parity-vector results in Sections 1–2.\nNo numerical source result, full priority survey or novelty claim is imported.\nThe current formulas use the exact archive identities. The named PD input\nis unchanged and is not used in these WS inequalities; its full analytic\ndependency chain and Lean build have not been independently recertified.\n\nThe primitive class P_1 is still unbounded. Neither endpoint sum is the\nfull birth energy, so even a coefficient below one for an endpoint subset\ndoes not give a contraction for all births. The independent endpoint/origin\ncounting sparsity required by EC remains open. No full finite-component\nmass bound, universal descent or exclusion of all cycles follows.\n\nThe next concrete question is whether the A/B rules yield a new bound on\nactual P_1 masks or a uniform count of actual terminal masks. Local charge\nimprovements alone must not be promoted to such a bound.\n\n## Map and publication scope\n\nThree new green nodes record only the exact odd-boundary rule, the terminal\ncomparison and the joint image-origin comparison. EndpointSparsityOpen,\nActualBirthUpperBoundOpen, BidirectionalFiniteChainMassOpen and Full remain\nunfilled. The map and WS reports are local. Public snapshot425, completed\nfive-platform publication and media cleanup are unchanged. No justified\npercentage solved or completion time is available.\n\nПолная карта: 593 узла, 1081 связь; обзор: 199 узлов, 414 связей.\nИсходник сохранён, live Mermaid-блоков нет. Offline-проверки синтаксиса,\nэлементов управления и границ подписей прошли: переполнений нет.\nПроверены три follow-up и 30 выбранных цветов. Root просмотрел обзор\nи три новые увеличенные подписи: все зелёные, текст помещается. Обзор\nтребует увеличения. Предупреждение Graphviz о метрике кириллицы сохраняется.\nСвежая проверка локальной карты в браузере не выполнялась. Метаданные:\n1376 документов, 5585 локальных ссылок, отсутствующих ссылок и конфликтов\nклассов нет. Это служебные проверки, не доказательство математики.\n","SHIFTED_BOUNDARY_DESCENT_AND_CORE.md":"# SP — signed boundary descent and a smaller actual-defect core\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual proof.\nNo mathematical program, enumeration, CAS, Lean or GPU. Full goal incomplete.\nThe preceding LB turn made progress. SP audits its shifted-pair candidate,\nthen uses actual common beta values to obtain an additional core reduction.\n\n## SP1. Definitions and what was already known\n\nUse T(n)=n/2 for even n and (3n+1)/2 for odd n. Let beta be any\nbinary exact T-invariant. On positive integers define\n\n    delta(q)=beta(q+1)-beta(q), d(q)=|delta(q)|,\n    g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h(n)=|g(n)|,\n    G(X)=sum_(odd n\u003c=X)h(n)/n.\n\nThe archive SB7 already states h(n)=d(3n) for odd n; this is NOT a\nnew result. The endpoint identities in BT8 imply the following signed\nboundary transfer; BT9 records its unsigned consequence:\n\n    delta(4t+1)=delta(3t+1), t>=0.                 (SP1)\n\nThus F(q)=(3q+1)/4 preserves delta on q=1 mod4. The unsigned BT11\nalso gives d(q)=0 for q=4 mod8. The archive SC2 proves\n\n    beta(z-1)=beta(3z-1) when z=3 mod4, z>0,       (SP2)\n\nby two genuine paths meeting at (3z-1)/4. All new uses below retain\nthis congruence and positivity. Merely renaming h as a boundary does\nnot improve the old energy estimate.\n\n## SP2. Two useful signed boundary representations\n\nFor n=4t+3 the paths\n\n    n ->6t+5 ->9t+8,\n    3n=12t+9 ->18t+14 ->9t+7\n\ngive g(n)=-delta((9n+1)/4). The exact harmonic transport is\n\n    sum_(n\u003c=X,n=3 mod4) h(n)/n\n      =sum_(q\u003c=floor((9X+1)/4),q=7 mod9) 9d(q)/(4q-1). (SP3)\n\nIts 9/4 leading coefficient is not a contraction. It accounts for\nthe old shifted-pair scratch but does not close the mass problem.\n\nMore useful for descent is n=16s+1. Its genuine paths are\n\n    n ->24s+2 ->12s+1 ->18s+2,\n    3n=48s+3 ->72s+5 ->108s+8 ->54s+4 ->27s+2,\n    18s+1 ->27s+2.\n\nConsequently\n\n    g(n)=-delta(q_0), q_0=(9n-1)/8=18s+1.        (SP4)\n\nThis uses beta(12s+1)=beta(18s+2) by ONE ordinary odd step. A draft\nrejection based on needing two incompatible SC2 merges was incorrect.\nThe two target progressions in SP3 and SP4 are disjoint, so a bare\nsigned sum of those two representations supplies no cancellation.\n\n## SP3. A terminal boundary lemma with two arithmetic choices\n\nTake any q=7 mod12. Put r=(q+1)/4, so r is a positive integer with\nr=2 mod3, r>=2. Write r=2^a w, a>=0 and w positive odd. Define\n\n    C_j=beta(3^j w-1), j>=1,\n    p_-=(2r-1)/3=(q-1)/6,\n    p_+=(8r-1)/3=(2q+1)/3.\n\nBoth targets are positive odd integers, including q=7 where they are1,5.\nThe finite odd-word identity beta(2^j w-1)=C_j and ordinary even\ndivisions give\n\n    beta(q)=C_(a+2), beta(q+1)=beta(w),\n    beta(p_-)=beta(p_+)=beta(w),\n    beta(3p_-)=C_(a+1), beta(3p_+)=C_(a+3).        (SP5)\n\nFor the middle equality, p_- goes to r in one shortcut step; p_+\ngoes to4r and then r by two even divisions. No negative or zero\ninteger occurs in any C index, since a+1>=1.\n\nExactly one of the following ARITHMETIC conditions holds:\n\n    if 3^(a+1)w=3 mod4, choose p=p_-;\n    otherwise 3^(a+2)w=3 mod4, choose p=p_+.\n\nIn the first case SP2 gives C_(a+1)=C_(a+2); in the second it gives\nC_(a+2)=C_(a+3). Therefore the selected target always satisfies\n\n    -delta(q)=g(p).                               (SP6)\n\nThe selector is arithmetic and independent of beta. It does not claim\nuniqueness of the equality itself: for constant beta both targets work.\n\n## SP4. Strict descent on every even valuation at least four\n\nLet\n\n    N={n=1+2^(2k+4)m : k>=0, m positive odd}.\n\nThis is exactly the positive n>1 with v_2(n-1) even and at least4.\nFor n in N, SP4 starts at q_0=1+18*4^k m. For0\u003c=j\u003c=k,\n\n    q_j=F^j(q_0)=1+18*3^j*4^(k-j)m.\n\nEvery j\u003ck has q_j=1 mod4, so SP1 preserves signed delta. The final\nq=1+18*3^k m is7 mod12. SP6 now gives, with its arithmetic selector,\n\n    g(n)=g(p), h(n)=h(p),\n    p in {3^(k+1)m, 1+4*3^(k+1)m},\n    p \u003c= (2q_0+1)/3=(3n+1)/4 \u003c n.                (SP7)\n\nThis holds for ALL odd m: the previous narrower condition on m modulo8\nis unnecessary for existence of a descending target. No growth block\noccurs in this family, no matter how large k is. A least odd index\nwith h=1, if it exists, cannot belong to N. This does not say every\nodd index belongs to N or eventually enters it.\n\n## SP5. An additional forced-zero family\n\nFor k>=1 let\n\n    Z_k^+={n=1+2^(2k+3)m : m positive odd,\n                               3^k m=3 mod8},\n    Z^+=union_(k>=1) Z_k^+.\n\nSuch n are1 mod16. Its SP4 boundary is q_0=1+9*4^k m. The same\nk applications of F give q=1+9*3^k m=4 mod8. BT11 therefore gives\n\n    h(n)=g(n)=0 for all n in Z^+.                 (SP8)\n\nThese sources have odd v_2(n-1)=2k+3, so Z^+ and N are disjoint.\nBoth are1 mod8, unlike CZ's Z and LB's B_L, which are7 mod8.\n\nFor reference, N has one residue class at each valuation2k+4 and\ndensity sum_(k>=0)2^(-(2k+5))=1/24. Each Z_k^+ is one class modulo\n2^(2k+6), giving density1/192 for Z^+. For both infinite unions,\nonly O(log X) classes contribute up to X; the count errors sum to\nO(log(X+1)) and the omitted geometric tail times X is O(1).\nPartial summation justifies the corresponding harmonic coefficients\nwith O(1) error. These are sizes of exhibited sets, not percentages\nof a proof completed.\n\n## SP6. All minus-branch inverse weights contract\n\nPartition N into N_- and N_+ according to the arithmetic selector\nin SP3. On a minus branch p=3^(k+1)m is a positive odd multiple of3.\nFor a fixed such p, q=1+6p and r=(q+1)/4=(3p+1)/2 are independent\nof k. Thus its selector is also independent of k. If it selects minus,\nall inverse sources are exactly\n\n    0\u003c=k\u003c=v_3(p)-1,\n    n_k(p)=1+(16/3)(4/3)^k p.                    (SP9)\n\nDividing p by3^(k+1) recovers a positive odd m and the required source\nvaluation. Different k give different sources. If the selector is plus,\nthis p contributes no N_- sources. The finite cutoff keeps n_k(p)\u003c=X;\nevery retained target satisfies p\u003cn_k(p)\u003c=X.\n\nThe positive +1 in the source denominator helps the upper bound:\n\n    sum_(minus inverse sources)1/n_k(p)\n       \u003c= (3/(16p)) sum_(k>=0)(3/4)^k =3/(4p).\n\nTherefore, without a convergence assumption or cutoff error,\n\n    G_(N_-)(X) \u003c= (3/4) G_(odd multiples of3)(X).  (SP10)\n\nIn particular the earlier guarded subclass 3^k m=1 mod8 selects minus,\nhas p=3 mod24 and is covered by SP10. No independence assumption about\ndifferent beta colors or source frequencies is used.\n\n## SP7. All plus-branch targets lie in the remaining core\n\nOn a plus branch, p=1+4*3^(k+1)m=13 mod24. In particular p>=13,\np=5 mod8 and v_2(p-1)=2. Put j=k+1. For a fixed eligible p the\ninverse sources are exactly\n\n    1\u003c=j\u003c=v_3(p-1),\n    n_j(p)=1+(4/3)^j(p-1),                        (SP11)\n\nprovided its fixed terminal q=(3p-1)/2 selects plus; otherwise there\nare no N_+ inverse sources at that p. Divisibility, oddness of m and\nthe source valuation follow from p=13 mod24. The selector again\ndepends on p alone, not on j. Every retained p is below its source.\n\nThe total inverse weight satisfies\n\n    sum_j 1/n_j(p)\n      \u003c=(1/(p-1)) sum_(j>=1)(3/4)^j\n      =3/(p-1) \u003c=13/(4p).                         (SP12)\n\nThe larger coefficient here will NOT be absorbed into G. Its targets\nare5 mod8, outside every family removed in the next section.\n\n## SP8. Combining disjoint targets yields an actual core reduction\n\nKeep CZ's zero set Z and LB's B_(3:5)=B_3 union B_4 union B_5.\nDefine\n\n    C_*={positive odd integers} minus\n                      (Z union Z^+ union N union B_(3:5)).\n\nThese four removed families are pairwise disjoint: N,Z^+ have opposite\nvaluation parity and are1 mod8; Z,B have distinct guards and are7 mod8.\nEvery N_+ target is5 mod8 and therefore belongs to C_*. SP12 gives\n\n    G_(N_+)(X) \u003c=(13/4) G_(C_*)(X).                (SP13)\n\nRetain the target restriction in the previously proved LB10:\n\n    G_(B_(3:5))(X) \u003c=(171/256)G_(1 mod18)(X)+1/7.\n\nIts target class is disjoint from the odd multiples of3 in SP10.\nSince171/256\u003c3/4, the two estimates combine as\n\n    G_(N_-)(X)+G_(B_(3:5))(X) \u003c=(3/4)G(X)+1/7.    (SP14)\n\nThis uses disjoint TARGET sets, not just disjoint sources. All cutoffs\nare the same X because every source-to-target map used here descends.\nSince h vanishes on Z and Z^+, the exact source partition and SP13–14\ngive\n\n    G(X) \u003c= G_(C_*)(X)+(13/4)G_(C_*)(X)+(3/4)G(X)+1/7,\n    G_(C_*)(X) \u003c=G(X) \u003c=17G_(C_*)(X)+4/7.           (SP15)\n\nThus finite G is equivalent to finite mass on the strictly smaller C_*.\nNo bound proving either mass finite has been obtained. This removes the\nentire N family using its two different target roles; an unqualified\nsum of all inverse coefficients would have missed this distinction.\n\n## SP9. Audit, sources and remaining work\n\nThe older inputs are explicitly cited as internal archive results:\n[BT8–BT11](../../../work/round175-h-boundary-transport.md),\n[SC1–SC2](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md),\n[SB7–SB18](SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md),\n[CZ](COALESCENCE_ZERO_FAMILY_AND_DEFECT_DENSITY.md), and\n[LB](LONG_BLOCK_TRANSFER_AND_WEIGHT_BARRIER.md).\nCurrent peer files are audits from THIS pass, not previously accepted\narchive evidence. Their agreement is not substituted for these paths.\n\nThe finite affine/parity framework is also the scope already checked\nin Rozier–Terracol, Introduction equation(2) and the discussion following\nLemma2.3 of the [primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948).\nThe special beta identities and SP15 are derived above, not attributed\nto that source. No literature priority or novelty is claimed.\n\nNext: characterize the actual defects on C_* and use additional exact\nrelations there, keeping source and target partitions explicit. In\nparticular N_+ targets are5 mod8 and now constitute a concrete surviving\nclass. Another enlargement with overlapping target weights must not be\ndeclared a contraction without checking the common cutoff and coefficients.\n\nSB's finite-E/finite-ZR/finite-G interface is unchanged. SP uses no\nPD170 input. Historical R170 acceptance remains, with full analytic/Lean\nclosure not freshly recertified. C_* summability and full Collatz remain\nopen. All results local; public snapshot425 and publication unchanged.\n\n[0431 weights audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_DEFECT_BOUNDARY_TRANSFER.md) ·\n[0459 dynamics audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_DEFECT_PARITY_CLOSURE.md) ·\n[0483 scope audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_DEFECT_BOUNDARY_SCOPE.md).\n\n## SP10. Independent final audit by role 0483 of SP1-SP15\n\nI read the complete nine-section file. The path checks, guards, and endpoint\nidentities are consistent. In particular, the q=7 modulo 12 lemma has\nr=(q+1)/4=2 modulo 3, both p choices are positive odd, and the q=7 edge\nhas p_minus=1 and p_plus=5. Exactly one SC congruence selector is active;\nthis is not a uniqueness assertion about the numerical beta equality, since\nconstant beta makes both equalities true.\n\nFor N, q_j=1+18*3^j*4^(k-j)*m is 1 modulo 4 for j\u003ck, while the final q\nis 7 modulo 12. The bound p \u003c= (3n+1)/4 \u003c n is valid for both selector\nbranches. The minus and plus inverse formulas, their source valuations, and\nweights 3/(4p) and 3/(p-1) are correctly normalized. The plus target lower\nbound p>=13 justifies 3/(p-1)\u003c=13/(4p).\n\nThe source partition is sound: N and Zplus have opposite exact v2(n-1)\nparity; Z and B_(3:5) are disjoint by their checked guards and both lie in\n7 modulo 8; N and Zplus lie in 1 modulo 8. Plus targets lie in 5 modulo 8,\nso they are in C_*. Minus targets are odd multiples of 3, disjoint from the\nB_(3:5) target class 1 modulo 18. Thus the common-cutoff combination in SP14\nand the rearrangement to SP15 are valid. The signed BT transfer used in SP1\nis obtained from the two BT8 endpoint identities (BT9 records the unsigned\nconsequence); this does not change any formula.\n\nThe displayed SP15 bound is therefore a valid finite-cutoff core reduction,\nconditional only on the previously stated LB B_(3:5) estimate. It proves no\nfinite G, no pointwise constancy, and no full Collatz result. Rozier–Terracol\nsupports only the finite affine/parity framework; the special beta identities\nremain manual. The historical peer-file correction notes in the report are\nretained accurately, and no current peer agreement is used as acceptance.\n","SIGNED_CHANNEL_POTENTIAL_AND_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md":"# SB — a signed path estimate and the multiplication-by-three defect\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation,\nwith four mathematical roles and bounded independent audits. No numerical\nprograms, CAS, enumeration or Lean. Full Collatz remains incomplete.\n\n## SB1. Definitions and inherited exact identities\n\nUse T, beta, d, e, L, Rb and the source/target classes from\n[AC](ACTIVE_CHANNEL_BALANCE_AND_ENDPOINT_ENERGY_BRIDGE.md). Beta is any\nexact binary T-invariant; no nonconstant beta is constructed. All harmonic\nmass symbols refer to the same finite cutoff X unless otherwise indicated.\nA/B are odd/even birth masses; E_i are birth masses in residues modulo3;\nP=E_1; E=A+B. Inherited uniform finite-cutoff identities are\n\n    A=E_2+P/2+O(1), B=E_0+P/2+O(1),\n    E=4Z_R+3D_S-2Z_S-3D_R+O(1),\n    B=4Z_R-3D_R-(3/2)P+O(1).                         (SB1)\n\nEvery O(1) here is bounded independently of X and beta. It comes from\nfixed affine cutoff annuli or summable reciprocal corrections, as proved\nin AC. We never subtract divergent harmonic series.\n\nSet H=3D_S-2Z_S. Subtract the S birth-image row from the conserved\ne+L channel row in AC:\n\n    H(X)=K(X)+O(1),\n    K(X)=2L_odd(X)-3L_(2 mod3)(X).                   (SB2)\n\nKeeping the negative term is essential. Bounding D_S separately loses it.\n\n## SB2. A nonnegative potential for a signed reward\n\nThe exact odd active-boundary grammar from\n[WS](WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md) is\n\n    A(q)=2q+1 when Rb(q)=1,\n    B(q)=4q+1 when L(q)=1.\n\nIn the B case the intermediate even boundary 2q is Rb, since d(4q)=0,\nand contributes zero to K. Hence after the first odd boundary the entire\nK reward is captured on odd boundaries. For residue i=q mod3 its reward\nis zero on A and r_i/q on B, where\n\n    (r_0,r_1,r_2)=(2,2,-1).\n\nThe nonnegative vector\n\n    (V_0,V_1,V_2)=(5/2,2,0)                          (SB3)\n\nsatisfies, with indices modulo3,\n\n    V_i >= V_(2i+1)/2,\n    V_i >= r_i+V_(i+1)/4.                           (SB4)\n\nThe A right sides are (1,5/4,0); the B right sides are (5/2,2,-3/8).\nThese six elementary inequalities were checked manually.\n\nFor an actual path step q'=a q+1, a=2 or4, a reward r/q satisfies\n\n    r/q \u003c= V_i/q-V_j/(a q) \u003c= V_i/q-V_j/q',          (SB5)\n\nbecause V_j>=0. Telescope over any FINITE prefix. Its signed reward\nis at most V_i/q after discarding a nonnegative terminal potential.\nThis argument remains valid for the negative reward -1 and for a cutoff\nthat stops immediately after it. No infinite-energy assumption or\nrealization of an arbitrary symbolic word is used.\n\n## SB3. All initial depths, including negative even terms\n\nEvery active boundary has a unique nearest birth ancestor: repeatedly\nhalve its index until the first inactive parent. Such a parent exists\nbecause d(1)=0; by the XOR law its two children are active and it is a\nbirth. Each birth b starts two disjoint active ancestry paths. All terms\nof K(X) therefore partition over births b\u003c=X/2.\n\nThe right path begins at 2b+1. SB5 gives the respective coefficients\nof 1/b, for b in residues0,1,2,\n\n    right: (1,5/4,0).                               (SB6)\n\nThe left path begins at even 2b. Its first odd boundary, if present, is\n2^ell b+1 for some ell>=2. The preceding even L-states are exactly\n2^j b for 1\u003c=j\u003c=ell-2; the final even state at j=ell-1 is Rb.\nEach preceding L-state in residue2 contributes -3/(2^j b) to K.\nTogether with the odd continuation potential, this gives\n\n    left: (1/2,0,5/8).                              (SB7)\n\nHere is the complete depth check. If b=0 mod3, there are no negative\nresidue2 prefix terms, and the first odd potential is at most V_1/(4b).\nIf b=1 mod3 and ell is even, the first odd residue is2, with potential\nzero. If ell is odd, then ell>=3; the first negative prefix term is\n-3/(2b), while the positive odd potential is at most V_0/(8b)=5/(16b).\nThus the whole left path is at most zero in this class. If b=2 mod3,\neven ell gives potential at most V_0/(4b)=5/(8b), while odd ell gives\nzero; all prefix terms are nonpositive. A path that never becomes odd,\nor whose cutoff occurs before its first odd boundary, has only\nnonpositive even contributions. These cases are included.\n\nThus, with no error in the path estimate itself,\n\n    K(X) \u003c= (3/2)E_0(X/2)+(5/4)P(X/2)+(5/8)E_2(X/2). (SB8)\n\nAll three coefficients on the right are nonnegative, so replacing X/2\nby X preserves the inequality. Combining with SB2 adds only uniform O(1).\n\n## SB4. One terminal class now controls all birth energy\n\nPut M=4Z_R-3D_R, a signed quantity at finite cutoff. SB1 implies\n\n    E=M+H+O(1), E_0=M-2P+O(1), E_2=P+H+O(1).        (SB9)\n\nSubstitute these expressions into SB8 at cutoff X and SB2:\n\n    H \u003c= (3/2)(M-2P)+(5/4)P+(5/8)(P+H)+O(1)\n      = (3/2)M-(9/8)P+(5/8)H+O(1).\n\nMoving the last term to the left gives H\u003c=4M-3P+O(1), and hence\n\n    E(X) \u003c= 20Z_R(X)-15D_R(X)-3P(X)+C\n          \u003c=20Z_R(X)+C.                            (SB10)\n\nC is absolute and uniform. Since Z_R is a birth subset, monotonicity of\npositive finite sums proves the equivalence\n\n    total harmonic birth energy is finite\n    iff the harmonic mass of Z_R is finite.         (SB11)\n\nThis strengthens the two-class AC criterion: D_S need not be bounded\nindependently. It follows automatically if Z_R is finite. SB10 does not\nprove that Z_R is finite, nor is its coefficient20 a contraction of E.\n\n## SB5. Exact masks in an R-column\n\nLet q=3u be positive odd, y=2^h q, and n=4y/3=2^(h+2)u. Then\n\n    beta(y)=beta(q), beta(n)=beta(u),\n    beta(q+1)=beta(u), beta(n+1)=beta(y+1).\n\nThe last equality follows from 4m+1 -> 6m+2 -> 3m+1 when y=3m.\nIf Rb(y)=1, beta(y+1) differs from beta(q), so d(n)=0 iff d(q)=1.\nThe conserved R channel then makes n a terminal birth exactly in that case.\nIf e(y)=1, beta(y+1)=beta(q), so d(n)=1 iff d(q)=1; then n is an\nRb nonbirth predecessor, making y a D_R target. Thus, exactly,\n\n    y is an R-terminal TARGET iff Rb(y)=1 and d(q)=1;\n    y belongs to D_R iff e(y)=1 and d(q)=1.          (SB12)\n\nWrite a_h=d(2^h q). The XOR identity gives\n\n    e(2^h q)=(1-a_h)a_(h+1),\n    Rb(2^h q)=a_h(1-a_(h+1)),\n    Rb(2^h q)-e(2^h q)=a_h-a_(h+1).                (SB13)\n\nIn the relevant columns a_0=1 and a_2=0. There is no extra predecessor\ncondition at h=1: it is already encoded in d(q)=1 and the channel colors.\n\n## SB6. Signed R compensation with a uniform truncation error\n\nReplace the R-source cutoff by the target cutoff X; the difference is\nconfined to the fixed annulus (3X/4,X] and is uniformly bounded. For\neach active odd q\u003c=X in residue0 put J=floor(log_2(X/q)). Finite\ntelescoping gives\n\n    sum_(h=0 to J)(a_h-a_(h+1))/2^h\n       =2-sum_(h=0 to J)a_h/2^h-a_(J+1)/2^J.\n\nThe top indices 2^J q are distinct and lie in (X/2,X], so their total\nreciprocal weight, and hence the omitted last terms, is uniformly bounded.\nThe omitted indicator a_(J+1) belongs to the next index in (X,2X]; its\ncoefficient is 1/(2^J q), the included top index's reciprocal. The uniform\nbound uses these coefficients, not their indicators' indices.\nConsequently\n\n    4Z_R(X)-3D_R(X)\n      =3 sum_(q\u003c=X, q odd, 3|q, d(q)=1)\n         [2/q-sum_(h>=0, 2^h q\u003c=X)d(2^h q)/(2^h q)]+O(1).       (SB14)\n\nThis explicitly accounts for both the R cutoff change AND the column\ntruncation boundary. It is genuine signed cancellation on actual masks.\nThe bracket is nonnegative: a_0=1, a_2=0 and 0\u003c=a_h\u003c=1 give an\ninfinite column sum at most 1+1/2+1/4=7/4. In particular it is at\nleast 1/(4q), even for a finite column. This fact does not bound the\nnumber or harmonic mass of the bases q.\n\n## SB7. Positive R-terminal weights and the multiplicative defect\n\nDefine\n\n    Q(Y)=sum_(q\u003c=Y, q odd, 3|q, d(q)=1)1/q.\n\nR-terminal targets are precisely the falls a_h=1,a_(h+1)=0 in SB12.\nSince a_0=1 and a_2=0, the first fall is at h=0 or h=1. Later falls\nare nonadjacent and occur at h>=3. Their geometric weight is at most\n2^(-3)+2^(-5)+...=1/6. Thus each full column's fall weight F_q satisfies\n\n    1/2 \u003c= F_q=sum_(h>=0)a_h(1-a_(h+1))/2^h \u003c=7/6.\n\nThere is no assumption that extremal binary columns can arise from beta.\nFor a target y, its source has weight 1/n=3/(4y). A column can contribute\nto Z_R(X) only if q\u003c=3X/4; its guaranteed first fall is included whenever\nq\u003c=3X/8. Distinct columns have distinct targets and sources. Therefore\nthe following inequalities have NO O(1) error:\n\n    (3/8)Q(3X/8) \u003c= Z_R(X) \u003c= (7/8)Q(3X/4).        (SB15)\n\nFor positive odd u, beta(3u+1)=beta(u). Define the multiplication-by-three\ndefect g(u)=beta(3u)-beta(u), and its nonnegative harmonic sum\n\n    G(Y)=sum_(u\u003c=Y, u odd)|g(u)|/u.\n\nThen d(3u)=|g(u)|, so Q(Y)=G(Y/3)/3. SB15 becomes\n\n    G(X/8)/8 \u003c= Z_R(X) \u003c= (7/24)G(X/4).             (SB16)\n\nCombining with SB10 gives\n\n    E(X) \u003c= (35/6)G(X/4)+C,\n    G(X) \u003c=8Z_R(8X)\u003c=8E(8X).                       (SB17)\n\nHence total birth energy, Z_R energy, Q and G are finite simultaneously.\nThis is a precise reduction of the remaining analytic obstruction to a\nsingle nonnegative multiplicative defect. It is not a proof that G is\nfinite and not a new section of mathematics or a novelty claim.\n\n## SB8. A concrete next transport question\n\nExtend g(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n) to all positive n. Then g(2n)=g(n).\nFor odd n, with v=(9n+1)/2, S(3n)=v and 3S(n)=v+1. Invariance gives\n\n    g(S(n))-g(n)=beta(v+1)-beta(v).                  (SB18)\n\nThus its signed change is an actual neighboring-color difference at a\nspecified affine index. For even n, g(T(n))=g(n). The next task is to\ntest whether SB18, the dyadic invariance and the common colors yield\na summable estimate for |g|; taking absolute values and summing can\notherwise simply return the original boundary-energy problem. No such\nsummable estimate has yet been established.\n\nA positive state potential required to contract EACH of the two relaxed\nR flips E->Rb and Rb->E by a common factor rho would require rho>=4/3,\nby multiplying the two edge inequalities. This only excludes that explicit\nedgewise ansatz. It does not exclude potentials retaining arithmetic or\ncolor constraints, and it was not used to prove SB10.\n\n## SB9. Source scope and full-goal boundary\n\nThe source audit is in\n[0483](FOLLOWUP_0483_SIGNED_ENERGY_SOURCE_SCOPE.md). Root also read the\nexceptional-set discussion and Proposition1.14/Remark1.16 in\n[Tao's primary paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1909.03562v7). Its random\noffset mixing does not supply a bound for this deterministic defect G;\nan additional interface and control of transported exceptional sets would\nbe required. No averaged statement is substituted for pointwise control.\n\nThe named conditional PD170 bridge is not an input to SB1–SB18. Even\nafter a future finite-energy theorem, the separately recorded pointwise\nstep would remain to be verified for a full Collatz conclusion. Its full\nanalytic dependencies and Lean build have not been independently recertified.\nNo complete proof, counterexample, cycle exclusion, percentage or time\nestimate follows. Public snapshot425 and completed publication/cleanup\nare unchanged. Exact review scope is recorded in\n[the SB review](REVIEW_0485_SIGNED_MULTIPLICATIVE_DEFECT.md).\n","SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH_AND_SPACED_INVERSE_CHARGES.md":"# SG — finite single-odd chains and a contracted image contribution\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nNo mathematical program, CAS, enumeration or Lean. This is a bounded result\nfor an exact binary invariant, not a proof of Collatz or a novelty claim.\nIndependent audit scopes and corrections are recorded in\n[the review](REVIEW_0485_SINGLE_ODD_GROWTH.md).\n\n## SG1. Definitions and the two active paths\n\nLet T(n)=n/2 for even n and T(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n. Let\nβ:N>0→{0,1} satisfy β(T(n))=β(n) exactly. No nonconstant β is constructed.\nPut d(n)=|β(n+1)−β(n)| and e(n)=d(2n)d(2n+1). A birth means e(n)=1;\nEβ(X)=Σ_(n≤X)e(n)/n. All sums at finite X are finite.\n\nSince β(2n)=β(n) and β(2n+1)=β(3n+2),\n\n    d(2n) XOR d(2n+1)=d(n),\n    e(n)=1 iff β(n)=β(n+1)≠β(3n+2).\n\nAlso d(1)=0. Every active boundary k (d(k)=1) has a unique nearest\nbirth ancestor b: repeatedly take floor(k/2), and stop at the first\nzero boundary. Its active child forces the other child to be active too.\nEach active boundary subsequently has exactly one active child. Hence\nthere are two active paths from b, and at most two descendants at each\ndepth ℓ, all at least 2^ℓ b. No independence of colors is used.\n\nThe finite path 3→5→8→4→2→1 proves e(1)=e(2)=0.\nFor n=4m+2, β(n)=β(2m+1)=β(3m+2)=β(3n+2), so e(n)=0.\nThese reproduce the needed portions of [BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md).\n\n## SG2. A complete local alternative with one growing step\n\nFor odd birth q of outer color c, define S(q)=(3q+1)/2. Then\nβ(S(q))=c and β(2S(q)+1)=β(3q+2)=1−c, whence\n\n    e(S(q))=1−d(S(q)).\n\nEither S(q) is a birth of the same outer color, or S(q) is an active\nboundary with a birth ancestor b≤floor((3q+1)/4)\u003cq. The excluded q=1\ndoes not cause an equality case.\n\nFor an even birth n=2s, invariance gives\nβ(s)=β(3s+2)≠β(3s+1). Thus 3s+1 is active and has a birth ancestor\nb≤floor((3n+2)/4)\u003cn for n≥4; n=2 is already excluded.\nThese descents are relative to the CURRENT index, not necessarily to\nthe original start of a previous growing chain.\n\n## SG3. The graph is injective and every component is finite\n\nUse ALL births as vertices, including even isolated/terminal vertices.\nRetain q→S(q) exactly when q is an odd birth and S(q) is a birth.\nS is injective, strictly increasing, and its arithmetic image is\nI_S={3k+2:k≥0}. Backward paths decrease, so each component has an origin.\n\nIf a+1=2^h v with v odd, the successive arithmetic odd steps are\n\n    n_j=3^j 2^(h−j)v−1,  0≤j≤h.\n\nAt each edge v₂(n_j+1) decreases by one. The final n_h is even and\ncannot be a source. Actual masks can stop sooner. Every component is\ntherefore finite, including an even-origin singleton (h=0). Since\nn_j>(3/2)^j a for j≥1, its total weight W(a) obeys\n\n    1/a ≤ W(a) ≤ 3/a.\n\nThis is a different graph from the earlier H/G₃ graph. Finiteness here\ndoes not assert that the H/G₃ infinite core Γ is empty.\n\n## SG4. The inverse mask, including h=1\n\nSuppose n=S(q) is a birth, q=2^h v−1 odd, h≥1, v odd.\nFor the finite strip X_(h,s)=3^s2^(h−s)v−1, define\nC_j=β(3^j v−1), B_s=β(3^s v) whenever the argument is positive.\nExact odd words give β(X)=C_h, β(X+1)=B_s, β(3X+2)=C_(h+1).\nThus n=X_(h,1) being a birth implies C_h≠C_(h+1).\n\nFor any positive r≡3 mod4, the genuine paths\n\n    r−1 → (r−1)/2 → (3r−1)/4,\n    3r−1 → (3r−1)/2 → (3r−1)/4\n\nmeet. Taking r=3^h v shows that 3^h v must instead be 1 mod4.\nHence 3^(h−1)v≡3 mod4, is at least 3, and the same merger gives\nC_(h−1)=C_h. In particular all arguments just used are positive.\n\nFor clarity, at h=1 the output n=3v−1 is even. The exclusion e(4m+2)=0\nforces v≡3 mod4, and the displayed paths with r=v give C_0=C_1.\nThe corner v=1,q=1,n=2 is not a birth. There is no missing exception.\n\nPut p=floor(q/2)=2^(h−1)v−1. The strip formulas give\nβ(q)=β(p)=C_h, β(q+1)=β(p+1)=B_0. Since C_h≠C_(h+1), either q\nis a birth or\n\n    d(q)=d(p)=1.\n\nIn the latter case the nearest birth b is at depth ℓ≥2, and\n\n    b≤floor(q/4)\u003cn/6,   because n/6=q/4+1/12.\n\nEvery birth in I_S therefore has a smaller birth. This excludes I_S\nfor a hypothetical smallest birth; it does not exclude all births in I_S.\n\n## SG5. Origins and the forced even child\n\nLet O_S be all origins of this finite-chain graph, P_S the births outside\nI_S, and D_S=O_S∩I_S. Then O_S=P_S disjoint union D_S. These sets are\nspecific to S and must not be identified with the old H/G₃ origin sets.\nFor n=S(q) in D_S, q is not a birth. Charge its component to the unique\nnearest birth ancestor b of its active inverse q. Distinct origins have\ndistinct q; components are disjoint.\n\nWrite c=β(q)=β(n). The inverse failure gives β(q+1)=1−c, while the\nbirth at n gives β(2q+1)=β(3q+2)=1−c. Consequently\n\n    d(2q)=1, d(2q+1)=0.\n\nThe active child of every charged odd q is 2q, an even integer. Thus\ncharged depths on one active path cannot be adjacent. The first depth\nis at least two. Grouping depths (2,3),(4,5),... yields\nΣ_charged 1/q≤2/(3b) over the two paths. As 1/S(q)≤2/(3q),\n\n    E_(D_S)(X)≤(4/9)Eβ(X/6).\n\nMultiplication by the uniform chain factor 3 would give coefficient 4/3.\nThe next calculation retains the actual valuation instead and improves it.\n\n## SG6. Valuation-sensitive weight of an image-origin component\n\nFor a charged q set h=v₂(q+1)≥1 and\n\n    A_h=Σ_(j=1..h)(2/3)^j=2(1−(2/3)^h).\n\nThe entire component from n=S(q) is a prefix of these h arithmetic\noutputs. Therefore, including its even endpoint if present,\n\n    W(S(q)) ≤ A_h/q.                                  (SG6)\n\nThere is no extra uniform factor 3 on this bound. If h=1, the component\nis an even singleton and A_1=2/3. If q' is the next charged boundary on\nthe same active path, r≥2 depths after q, its first binary child bit is\n0. Thus q'=2^r q+t with 0≤t\u003c2^(r−1). Since q' is odd, its number of\ntrailing binary ones is h'=v₂(q'+1)≤r−1.\n\n## SG7. A potential for all later charges on one path\n\nLet R=2/9. For every integer r≥2,\n\n    (A_(r−1)+R)/2^r ≤ R.                              (SG7)\n\nFor r=2 this is equality. At r=3 the left side is 1/6. For r≥4 it\nis at most (2+2/9)/16=5/36\u003c2/9.\n\nTake any finite sequence of charges after a fixed charged q. Working\nbackward from the last one, (SG6), q'≥2^r q and (SG7) show that the\nsum of component weights at all later charges is at most R/q.\nThe infinite statement follows by increasing finite prefixes of a\nnonnegative series. This does not assume the energy is finite.\n\nThe same induction applies from any starting boundary b whose first\nchild bit is 0, provided the first charge is at depth at least two.\nIts total charged-component mass is at most R/b.\n\n## SG8. Full component mass charged to a birth: 7/9\n\nFrom a birth b there are two active paths. On the left path the first\nbit is 0, and all charged q have depth≥2, so SG7 bounds its full\ncharged-component mass by 2/(9b).\n\nOn the right path, if there is a first charge q, it has depth≥2 and\nq≥4b. Its own component costs at most A_h/q≤2/q and all subsequent\ncharged components cost at most R/q. The right path contributes at\nmost (2+R)/(4b)=5/(9b). The empty path contributes zero. Hence\n\n    Σ_(D_S components charged to b) W(origin) ≤ 7/(9b). (SG8)\n\nThis controls the full weights of those components, not just origins.\nThe two paths are disjoint; each component has one origin and one charge.\n\n## SG9. A contraction with an unresolved primitive source\n\nFor each birth≤X its origin is also≤X. P_S-origin components contribute\nat most 3E_(P_S)(X). For a D_S origin≤X, SG4 gives its charged ancestor\nb\u003cX/6. Applying SG8, even though a full component may extend above X,\ngives the valid upper bound\n\n    Eβ(X) ≤ 3E_(P_S)(X) + (7/9)Eβ(X/6).               (SG9)\n\nThis contracts the image-origin contribution. It leaves an additive\nsource 3E_(P_S)(X), with no finite uniform upper bound proved here.\nSince Eβ(X/6)≤Eβ(X), all finite quantities can be rearranged to give\n\n    E_(P_S)(X) ≤ Eβ(X) ≤ (27/2)E_(P_S)(X).             (SG10)\n\nConsequently full birth energy is finite if and only if the energy on\nbirths outside residue 2 mod3 is finite. This equivalence supplies no\nproof that either is finite. Moreover, turning finite energy into\nconstancy would require a separate justified obstruction to a\nnonconstant invariant of finite energy; older conditional density\narguments are not re-certified by SG. Universal smaller-birth descent\nwould suffice directly, but has not been proved for P_S.\n\n## SG10. Why finite chains and factor 3 alone do not close the problem\n\nAn even endpoint of an odd run from a=2^h v−1 is 3^h v−1. BR's even\ntransfer gives at best floor((3^(h+1)v−1)/4), which need not be below a.\nStopping a growth chain and descending below its final index therefore\ndoes not imply descent below its original index. Higher returns remain.\n\nEven the uniform factor 3 cannot be reduced on the unmasked arithmetic\nsuperset: a=4^L−1 is 0 mod3 and has a full odd run of length 2L. For\neach fixed j, a/n_j tends to (2/3)^j as L increases. The upper bound 3\nand arbitrary finite geometric partial sums give\naΣ_(j=0..2L)1/n_j→3. These are arithmetic candidates, not actual births.\nSG8 succeeds by coupling valuation, masks and spacing, not by asserting\na uniform factor less than 3 for every component.\n\n## SG11. Auxiliary checks and corrections\n\nThe older graph still has its exact positive flux identity and arithmetic\nsharpness of factor 9; see [0431's weight audit](FOLLOWUP_0431_EXACT_CHAIN_WEIGHT.md)\nand [0483's flux audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_CHAIN_FLUX_IDENTITY.md). In a finite\ncutoff the outgoing boundary term has a MINUS sign in the flux sum.\nNeither identity bounds its unknown terminal mass.\n\nFor the old primitive family 144t+99, a failed H mask yields a smaller\nbirth. A retained even exit can instead create birth 243t+168>144t+99.\nAn initial helper argument wrongly excluded that higher birth by\nminimality; it was rejected and corrected. Minimality forbids smaller\nbirths, not larger ones. The exceptional mask remains open. See\n[0459's corrected audit](FOLLOWUP_0459_PRIMITIVE_EVEN_EXIT.md).\n\n## SG12. Prior work and exact scope\n\nFinite odd words, inverse layers and the need to retain the affine\nintercept already appear in the archive, including\n[the variable-run audit](VARIABLE_RUN_AND_POINTWISE_DESCENT_AUDIT.md).\nThe current primary-source check read Sections 1–2 of Rozier–Terracol,\n[Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences, v5, 17 May 2026](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948):\nthey give the affine parity-word formula and remainder bounds. Their\ncomputational or conditional conclusions are not imported here. A search\nalso located arXiv:2605.13886, but its full text was not read and it is\nnot a theorem input. No broad priority review or novelty conclusion follows.\n\nSG1–SG10 were derived directly from exact invariance and finite/infinite\nnonnegative sums. No Taо almost-everywhere result, random parity model,\nPD density claim, external numerical verification, or assumed realization\nof a nonconstant invariant is used. The full Collatz conjecture, all-cycle\nexclusion, primitive energy bound and general descent remain open.\n","SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md":"# SL2 — variable structural lifts with at most two odd steps\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent. Root derivation, manual algebra only.\nNo enumeration program, numerical experiment, CAS, or Lean.\nThis report concerns ordered, structurally adjacent inverse word pairs.\nIt does not cover all Collatz predecessors or arbitrary bad trajectories.\nIndependent acceptance is recorded in the associated root review.\n\n## 1. Words, direction, and exact admissibility\n\nUse T(x)=x/2 for even x and T(x)=(3x+1)/2 for odd x.\nA binary shortcut word w of length K with P odd positions has formal map\n\n    T_w(x)=(3^P x+C_w)/2^K.\n\nA structural ordered inverse pair has a left word w and a right word w'.\nThey send terminal targets (n,n+1) back to (F(n),F(n)+1) identically\non an arithmetic progression, with the order preserved. Equality of\naffine slopes forces the two words to have the same K,P. Their constants\nsatisfy\n\n    C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P,     F(n)=(2^K n-C_L)/3^P.          (SL1)\n\nThis is an identity in n, not an accidental equality at one integer.\n\nFor a genuine binary word and an integer terminal target n, integrality\nof F(n) already enforces the exact parity word. One way to check this\nwithout importing a coding theorem is induction on the first bit.\n\nFor a first E, C_w=2 C_tail; the integral numerator equation forces x\neven, and dividing by2 gives the integral tail equation for x/2.\nFor a first O, with the tail having P-1 odd bits,\n\n    C_w=3^(P-1)+2 C_tail.\n\nThe numerator equation forces x odd, and, with z=(3x+1)/2, becomes\n3^(P-1)z+C_tail=2^(K-1)n. Apply induction to the tail. Thus the\nprescribed formal word is the actual integer trajectory.\n\nMoreover, T preserves nonpositive integers: T(0)=0 and T(x)\u003c=-1 for\neach negative integer x. Consequently, a positive terminal n and an\nintegral F(n) force F(n)>0; all intermediate states are positive.\nThe right source F(n)+1 satisfies its own genuine word by the same\nargument and (SL1). Hence exact positivity and parity are automatic here:\n\n    n>=1,  n = 2^(-K) C_L (mod 3^P)                    (SL2)\n\nare sufficient as well as necessary for the genuine pair. First-hit\nrestrictions remain separate and can only remove sources.\n\nThe dyadic cylinder is a condition on the SOURCE F(n), namely\nF(n)=-C_L 3^(-P) (mod 2^K). It is not an additional dyadic\ncondition on the terminal seed n.\n\n## 2. Complete classification for P\u003c=2\n\nThe empty word is the identity. If P=0, both constants vanish and the\ninverse gap is 2^K; structural gap1 forces K=0.\n\nIf P=1, the possible word constants are 2^i, 0\u003c=i\u003cK.\nK=1 fails (SL1). For K>=2 the positive odd difference\n2^j-2^i=2^K-3 forces i=0\u003cj. Then\n\n    2^j=2^K-2.\n\nThe right side has valuation1, so j=1 and K=2. The unique pair is\n(OE,EO), giving\n\n    L(n)=(4n-1)/3,       n=1 (mod3).                   (SL3)\n\nIf P=2, write the two odd positions as 0\u003c=i\u003cj\u003cK. The constant is\n\n    C=3*2^i+2^j.                                      (SL4)\n\nFor K=2 only C=5 exists, and the required difference -5 is impossible.\nFor K=3 the three constants are5,7,10; no difference equals -1.\nThese are three symbolic choices, checked directly, not a computational\nenumeration.\n\nLet K>=4. The required difference 2^K-9 is positive and odd, so exactly\none of C_L,C_R is odd. If C_R is odd and C_L is even, then\n\n    C_R\u003c=3+2^(K-1), C_L>=10,\n    C_R-C_L\u003c=2^(K-1)-7 \u003c 2^K-9,\n\na contradiction. Therefore C_L=3+2^j, j>=1, and C_R is even.\n\nIf j=1, then C_R=2^K-4 has valuation2. Its first odd position is2, so\nC_R=12+2^ell with ell>2. Therefore\n\n    2^ell=2^K-16.\n\nK=4 is impossible; for K>=5 the valuation is4, hence ell=4 and K=5.\nThis gives the pair (OOEEE,EEOEO), and\n\n    M(n)=(32n-5)/9,      n=1 (mod9).                  (SL5)\n\nIf j>=2, then C_R=2^K-6+2^j has valuation1. Its first odd position\nis1, so C_R=6+2^ell. We obtain\n\n    2^ell-2^j=2^K-12>0.\n\nThus ell>j, and comparison of valuations forces j=2. The remaining\nequation 2^ell=2^K-8 forces ell=3,K=4. This is (OEOE,EOEO),\ngiving L composed with itself:\n\n    L^2(n)=(16n-7)/9,    n=1 (mod9).                  (SL6)\n\nThis exhausts P\u003c=2, without imposing a length bound in advance.\nThe complete list is identity, L, L^2, M.\n\n## 3. Direct verification of the extra rule M\n\nFor m>=0, the exact positive trajectories are\n\n    32m+3 -> 48m+5 -> 72m+8 -> 36m+4 -> 18m+2 -> 9m+1,\n    32m+4 -> 16m+2 -> 8m+1  -> 12m+2 -> 6m+1  -> 9m+2.\n\nThe constants are5 and28, whose difference23 is32-9. The right inverse\nmap is G(n+1)=(32(n+1)-28)/9=M(n)+1; it is NOT M(n+1).\nThis rule is not a power of L: its (K,P)=(5,2).\n\nThe broad structural definition is prior work, for example Definition2\nof [Elia–Tucker](https://arxiv.org/html/1511.09141). The explicit example,\nclassification and bounds here were derived and checked manually; world\nnovelty has not been established. Their coalescing stems must not be\nconfused with ordered adjacency-preserving block prefixes.\n\n## 4. Arbitrary switching reduces to one continuing chain with short branches\n\nAllow any finite sequence of the classified rules, retaining every\npositive integral source, whether or not its word is a first hit.\nIdentity adds nothing and L^2 is exactly two admissible L steps.\nThus the set of distinct reachable first coordinates is precisely the\nclosure generated by L and M.\n\nNo map is available at n=0 or2 (mod3). At n=4 or7 (mod9), only L is\navailable and its image is respectively2 or0 (mod3), so it is terminal.\nFor n=1 (mod9), split into the three classes modulo27:\n\n| n modulo27 | L(n) | M(n) | Only branch that can keep going beyond two steps |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | no further universal restriction needed | 0 modulo3, terminal | L |\n| 10 | 4 modulo9, then only L, ending2 modulo3 | 2 modulo3, terminal | none |\n| 19 | 7 modulo9, then only L, ending0 modulo3 | 1 modulo3 | M |\n\nThese residues follow by writing n=27q+1,27q+10,27q+19 and substituting.\nEquivalently M(n)=2L^2(n)+1 wherever both are defined.\n\nChoose the indicated continuing branch when there is one. Every other\nbranch ends after at most two steps. At a terminal chain state of class10,\nthere are at most three extra descendants L(n),L^2(n),M(n). This constructs\none possible continuing chain, with uniformly bounded finite branches.\nIt neither asserts that the chain is infinite nor excludes that possibility.\nIf the chain is infinite, its label sequence is unique for this alphabet;\na finite directed graph in general need not force periodic labels.\n\n## 5. Logarithmic bound on distinct generated sources\n\nLet A(n_0) be all distinct positive integer first coordinates reachable\nfrom a fixed seed n_0 by any finite sequence of the classified rules.\nFor n_0=1, L(1)=1 and M(1)=3, which is terminal, so\n\n    A(1)={1,3}.                                        (SL7)\n\nFor n>1, both maps strictly increase n, and\n\n    L(n)-1=(4/3)(n-1),\n    M(n)-1=(32/9)(n-1)+2 >= (4/3)(n-1).                (SL8)\n\nHence, for X>=n_0>1, the number B_X of nodes on the chosen continuing\nchain not exceeding X is at most\n\n    B_X \u003c= 1+floor(log_(4/3)((X-1)/(n_0-1))).           (SL9)\n\nNo descendant of a chain node above X can fall back into [1,X].\nEach chain node has at most two descendants off the chain, except for\nthe possible final class10 node, which has at most three. Counting the\nchain nodes as well gives\n\n    #(A(n_0) intersect [1,X])\n       \u003c= 3 B_X+1\n       \u003c= 4+3 floor(log_(4/3)((X-1)/(n_0-1))).          (SL10)\n\nThis is an upper bound; different words may produce the same source.\nIt counts distinct coordinates, not words, so arbitrarily many copies\nof a fixed loop do not affect it. In particular A(n_0) has natural\ndensity0 for every fixed positive seed, even with arbitrary switching.\nAny first-hit subset satisfies the same bound.\n\n## 6. What is ruled out, and what remains\n\nA positive-density family of predecessors of one ordered adjacent\ntarget pair cannot be produced solely by arbitrary compositions of\nstructural ordered lifts with at most two odd steps on each side.\nThis extends FL's fixed-periodic obstruction to ALL variable sequences\nin this completely classified class.\n\nMore generally, let Q_T(a,c) consist of n for which T^i(n)=a and\nT^j(n+1)=c for some independent nonnegative times i,j. If n_0 is one\nwitness in Q_T(a,c), every pair generated by these lifts is another\nwitness: it returns to (n_0,n_0+1) before following the existing paths.\nSL10 shows that this particular amplification of one witness has density0.\nThe targets a,c need not themselves be adjacent. This statement uses\nshortcut predecessors; for fixed odd targets they coincide with ordinary-C\npredecessors, as recorded in ID/I. No unrestricted C/T replacement for\neven targets is made.\n\nIt does not say that the full paired predecessor set has density0.\nThat set can contain words with three or more odd steps, paths that\ndo not return to ordered adjacency at the chosen block boundaries,\norder reversals, and unequal first-hit depths. No factorization of all\npaths into the classified rules has been established.\n\nThe next substantive question is whether structural lifts with P>=3\nsupply a new continuing branch, and how one controls the general weighted\nrelations P u-Q v=H from AR when adjacency is not preserved between blocks.\nNo claim about full Collatz or any new excluded class of bad orbits\nis made here.\n\n\n## Source update — 18 September 2026, SL3 follow-up\n\nM is explicitly present in LaTourette's 2007 Table 4.1. Its earlier\ndescription as new meant only new to our local analysis. See the dated\n[source audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md) and\n[SL3 acceptance](REVIEW_0485_THREE_ODD_LIFTS.md); no mathematical claim\nin the P\u003c=2 proof is changed by this attribution correction.\n","TERNARY_LAST_CHANGE_AND_SIGNED_BUNDLE_TRANSFER.md":"# TL — last ternary changes and signed transfer of nonprimitive births\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual proof draft.\nNo numerical program, CAS, enumeration, Lean or GPU. Full goal incomplete.\nPB's final scope audit is still pending; its displayed identities were\nchecked by root and two mathematical roles. This note states its precise\nPB dependencies and does not certify that pending audit as complete.\n\n## TL1. Definitions and the forced last zero\n\nLet beta be an exact binary invariant of shortcut Collatz T. Keep\ng(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h=|g|, and\ne(z)=1 iff beta(z)=beta(z+1) differs from beta(2z+1).\nWrite G(X)=sum_(t positive odd,t\u003c=X) h(t)/t and\nE9(X)=sum_(z positive odd,9|z,z\u003c=X) e(z)/z.\n\nEvery positive odd z divisible by9 has a UNIQUE representation\n\n    z=3^a m, a=v3(z)>=2, m positive odd, 3 not dividing m.\n\nPut y=z/3. Since y is odd, T(y)=(z+1)/2=T(z+1), so\nbeta(y)=beta(z+1). At an actual birth e(z)=1 this gives\n\n    beta(3^a m)=beta(3^(a-1)m) differs from beta(2z+1),\n    h(3^(a-1)m)=0.                               (TL1)\n\nThe equality uses a common endpoint, not a fictitious step from z+1 to\n3z+2. No nonconstant beta or actual birth is assumed to exist.\n\n## TL2. Unique last-change charge\n\nCall such an actual birth ternary-primitive if\n\n    h(3^j m)=0 for every 0\u003c=j\u003c=a-1.               (TL2)\n\nLet E9prim(X) be its harmonic mass. This definition is different from\nOR's primitive origins of the H/G3 growth graph: the two primitive\nnotions must not be identified.\n\nFor a nonprimitive birth choose the GREATEST j with h(3^j m)=1.\nBy TL1, j\u003c=a-2. Put t=3^j m and k=a-j>=2. Thus z=3^k t, h(t)=1.\nFor fixed (t,k) there is at most one source z. Each actual source is\ncharged once because its last change is unique. Since z\u003c=X implies\nt\u003c=X/9, exact positive reindexing followed by a geometric bound gives\n\n    E9(X)-E9prim(X)\n      \u003c=sum_(t odd,t\u003c=X/9) h(t)/t *sum_(k>=2)3^(-k)\n      =(1/6)G(X/9).                             (TL3)\n\nThis holds at each finite cutoff. Extending the inner k sum is an upper\nbound; an infinite unknown defect sum was not assumed convergent.\nThe earlier split e(9s) with h(s)=1 is the k=2 subcase, not necessarily\nj=0 when s itself is divisible by3.\n\nFor a primitive birth the remaining actual mask is exactly\n\n    beta(m)=beta(3m)=...=beta(3^a m)\n                     differs from beta(2*3^a m+1).       (TL4)\n\nThese equalities do not say that the ternary chain is one ordinary T orbit.\nNo displayed path returns the opposite endpoint to this chain in general.\nThe special edge m=1,a=2 is excluded by the finite paths for 3,9 and19;\nit supplies no all-a statement.\n\n## TL3. A signed descent for the nonprimitive birth branch\n\nUse [PB1–PB4](PLUS_BIRTH_ROUTING_AND_CRITICAL_WEIGHTS.md). For a birth z\ndivisible by9 put u=z/3 and p=4u+1. Its actual birth automatically selects\nPB's arithmetic plus branch, and h(u)=0, h(p)=1. The complete PB source\nfiber is n_l(u)=1+4u(4/3)^l, 0\u003c=l\u003c=v3(u); n_0=p.\n\nFor a nonprimitive z with last-change target t as above, set A=beta(t)\nand B=beta(3t). Then A differs from B, and lastness means\nbeta(3t)=...=beta(z)=B. The birth's middle color is A by binarity.\nPB's genuine paths therefore give beta(p)=B and beta(3p)=A. Hence\n\n    g(p)=-g(t), g(n_l(u))=-g(t),\n    t\u003c=z/9\u003cp\u003c=n_l(u).                           (TL5)\n\nThis is a strict signed-defect descent on this ACTUAL nonprimitive birth\nbranch. It is not asserted for a primitive birth or for all p=5 mod8.\nIf instead a selected plus p has h(p)=h(u)=1, PB gives g(p)=g(u),\na different direct branch. Their unsigned target masses may overlap.\n\n## TL4. All inverse weights charged to a last change\n\nLet Jnp(X) denote the mass of PB plus fibers, including their p, whose\nassociated birth z=3u is nonprimitive. It includes ONLY the birth branch\nh(u)=0,h(p)=1. A retained source has p=1+4*3^(k-1)t\u003c=X, so\nt\u003c=(X-1)/12. Put v=v3(t). For its possible k>=2, v3(u)=v+k-1.\nPB's full-fiber upper weight is\n\n    W_X(u)\u003c=W(u)\u003c[1-(3/4)^(v+k)]/[3^(k-1)t].\n\nThe actual fibers are disjoint: p identifies u, the source valuation\nidentifies l, and the last-change rule identifies t and k. Dropping the\nremaining birth/lastness masks only enlarges the nonnegative sum. Thus\n\n    Jnp(X)\u003c=sum_(t odd,t\u003c=(X-1)/12) C_v h(t)/t,\n    C_v=sum_(k>=2)3^(1-k)[1-(3/4)^(v+k)]\n       =1/2-(1/4)(3/4)^v.                       (TL6)\n\nFor the second geometric sum use\nsum_(k>=2)3^(1-k)(3/4)^k=3*sum_(k>=2)4^(-k)=1/4.\nThere is no denominator or cutoff error in this upper bound. In particular\nC_0=1/4 and C_v\u003c1/2 for every v. This is stronger than replacing all\nsource weights by 3/z before charging. It remains a bound for one part\nof the full defect mass.\n\n## TL5. What happens when direct and indirect targets are combined\n\nLet Jprim(X) be the complementary primitive-birth fiber mass. It obeys\n\n    Jprim(X)\u003c=3E9prim(3(X-1)/4).                  (TL7)\n\nWith PB's Aplus/Aminus and M(X)=G_N(X)+G_Pplus(X), all nonzero sources\npartition into N-minus, direct plus (h(p)=h(u)=1), nonprimitive birth\nplus, and primitive birth plus. At a target t, the first two alternatives\nare mutually exclusive by the arithmetic selector. Indirect last-change\ncharges are NOT known disjoint from them.\n\nExtending positive target bounds to t\u003c=X gives the valid but conservative\ncombined estimate\n\n    M(X)\u003c=Jprim(X)+sum_(t odd,t\u003c=X) a(t)h(t)/t,   (TL8)\n\nwhere, with v=v3(t),\n\n    v=0:                a(t)=1/4;\n    v>=1, sigma(t)=+:   a(t)=3/2-(3/4)^v;\n    v>=1, sigma(t)=-:   a(t)=5/4-(1/4)(3/4)^v.   (TL9)\n\nIndeed the direct plus bound is 1-(3/4)^(v+1), the minus bound is3/4,\nand the indirect coefficient is C_v. Their stated sums give TL9.\nThe direct plus loss ell and actual indirect masks have been dropped,\nso TL8 is not claimed optimal. Some coefficients exceed one, and Jprim\nis unknown. The subunit coefficient in TL6 alone therefore does not\nprovide a contraction for M, still less for all G.\n\n## TL6. Scope, existing results and next requirement\n\nTL3 controls only births with an earlier change on their ternary column.\nTL4 identifies the untouched mask; TL5–TL6 give a genuine signed descent\nand smaller inverse coefficient for the controlled part. None bounds\nE9prim, Jprim, or G. Composing only TL3 with PB8 yields\n3E9(Y)\u003c=3E9prim(Y)+(1/2)G(Y/9); PB8 still has its full G0 term.\nThe existing SP Cstar bound is neither replaced nor strengthened here.\n\nThe old OR5 arithmetic family 144t+99 may not be called ternary-primitive\nwithout checking the added color equalities. Its divergent arithmetic\nsuperset is not evidence of actual births or primitive masks.\n\nThe next question is quantitative control of the actual mask TL4, or a\nproved incompatibility between direct retention and indirect last-change\ncharges at the same target in TL8. Signed equality by itself does not\ncancel the nonnegative mass G. No artificial free choice of beta colors,\nfinite-E hypothesis, density input, or PD170 is used. Historical R170\nacceptance and its unrecertified full analytic/Lean closure are unchanged.\n\nThe local tools come from [SC](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md),\n[BR](COMPLETE_LOCAL_BIRTH_ROUTING.md), and PB. The finite affine/parity\nbackground was freshly checked in the preceding PB pass in\n[Rozier–Terracol](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948), equation(2) and the\nparagraph after Lemma2.3. That source does not prove TL's energy bound.\nA bounded archive phrase search found no earlier identical last-change\nformula; no external novelty claim is made. Full Collatz remains open.\n","TERNARY_WEIGHTED_FIBER_CONTRACTION.md":"# WC — weighted fibers and a remaining actual-defect core\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual derivation.\nNo mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS, Lean or GPU. Full Collatz\nremains unresolved. The new claims below require their own scope audit;\ncompletion of the earlier PB audit does not automatically accept them.\n\n## WC1. Definitions and the exact source partition\n\nLet beta be any exact binary invariant of shortcut Collatz T. Set\ng(n)=beta(3n)-beta(n), h=|g|, and let e be the actual birth indicator\nfrom [PB](PLUS_BIRTH_ROUTING_AND_CRITICAL_WEIGHTS.md). For positive odd n put\n\n    omega(n)=1 if 3 divides n, and omega(n)=1/4 otherwise,\n    G_omega(X)=sum_(n odd,n\u003c=X) omega(n)h(n)/n.\n\nIn particular G(X)/4\u003c=G_omega(X)\u003c=G(X) at every finite X. No convergence\nis assumed. Keep the arithmetic sets N, Aplus, Aminus and Pplus of PB/SP.\nN and Pplus are disjoint: their v2(n-1) are respectively even>=4 and2.\nUse the TOTAL mass on their union:\n\n    M_omega(X)=G_(N,omega)(X)+G_(Pplus,omega)(X),\n    R_omega(X)=G_omega(X)-M_omega(X).\n\nThus R is the actual h-mass on the complementary odd source set. In the\nnonzero part of M, [TL5](TERNARY_LAST_CHANGE_AND_SIGNED_BUNDLE_TRANSFER.md)\ngives four disjoint types: N-minus; direct plus with h(u)=h(4u+1)=1;\nnonprimitive birth plus; and primitive birth plus. Write Jprim_omega for\nthe LAST type, including all its retained source lifts. In particular\nJprim_omega is INCLUDED in M_omega; it is not an additional source mass.\n\nAll these statements refer to actual beta masks. A source need not exist\nin any of the nonzero types when beta is constant.\n\n## WC2. The weight cancels the valuation dependence of full fibers\n\nFor an odd target u with v=v3(u)>=1, PB gives the full plus fiber\n\n    n_l(u)=1+4u(4/3)^l, 0\u003c=l\u003c=v.\n\nWhen l\u003cv, n_l=1 mod3, so its omega is1/4. At l=v it is at most1.\nPut alpha=3/4 and replace each denominator 1+x by x. This enlarges the\npositive sum, and the resulting numerator is\n\n    (1/4)sum_(l=0..v-1)alpha^l+alpha^v=1.\n\nConsequently its full weighted mass is bounded by\n\n    Wplus_omega(u)\u003c=1/(4u).                         (WC1)\n\nFor a minus target the exact SP range is l=1,...,v, without l=0.\nIts corresponding numerator is3/4, including v=1 when the first sum\nis empty. Hence\n\n    Wminus_omega(u)\u003c=3/(16u).                       (WC2)\n\nAt v=0 there are no direct targets from these SP/PB source sets.\nAt any finite cutoff, only retained members are counted; expanding to\nthe complete fiber is an upper bound, never an equality assertion.\n\n## WC3. Nonprimitive sources and combined target coefficients\n\nFor a nonprimitive actual birth z, TL chooses its unique last-change\ntarget t with h(t)=1 and writes z=3^k t, k>=2. The plus input is\nu=3^(k-1)t. Every source n in this fiber satisfies\n\n    t\u003c=z/9\u003c4u+1\u003c=n.\n\nBy WC1 its full source weight is at most1/(4*3^(k-1)t). Summing over\nall k>=2, after dropping only nonnegative actual-birth restrictions,\ngives the charge1/(8t). A retained source also implies\nt\u003c=(X-1)/12. Thus\n\n    Jnp_omega(X)\n      \u003c=sum_(t odd,t\u003c=(X-1)/12) h(t)/(8t).          (WC3)\n\nThis geometric extension uses no unknown infinite h-sum. The inner\narithmetic geometric series converges for each retained target.\n\nFor each target t divisible by3, the direct plus and minus alternatives\nare mutually exclusive by the arithmetic selector. Their respective\nbounds are1/(4t) and3/(16t). Indirect last-change charges at that same\nt may coexist with either, so their1/(8t) bound must be ADDED:\n\n    v3(t)=0:                 1/(8t)=(1/2)omega(t)/t;\n    v3(t)>=1, sigma(t)=+:    3/(8t)=(3/8)omega(t)/t;\n    v3(t)>=1, sigma(t)=-:    5/(16t)=(5/16)omega(t)/t.\n\nEvery charge is multiplied by h(t). Each coefficient is at most1/2.\nRetained targets lie below retained sources, so extending their cutoffs\nto X gives the finite-cutoff inequality\n\n    M_omega(X)-Jprim_omega(X) \u003c= (1/2)G_omega(X).  (WC4)\n\nThere is no double counting of sources. For n in a fiber,\nl=(v2(n-1)-2)/2 and u=(n-1)3^l/4^(l+1) uniquely recover its input.\nThe selector then fixes plus or minus. Within a plus fiber, the direct\ncase has h(u)=1 whereas the actual birth case has h(u)=0; these cannot\noverlap even if inputs from different charge descriptions coincide.\nPrimitive versus nonprimitive is the exact TL classification, and the\nlast-change target of a nonprimitive birth is unique. Uniqueness of the\ninput alone must not be confused with disjointness of target charges.\n\n## WC4. What contracts, and what does not\n\nSince G_omega=M_omega+R_omega, WC4 rearranges at each finite X to\n\n    Jprim_omega(X)+R_omega(X) \u003c= G_omega(X)\n       \u003c=2[Jprim_omega(X)+R_omega(X)].             (WC5)\n\nThe first inequality uses the exact disjoint source partition. The\nsecond uses only finite sums. Thus the masses are comparable at every\nfinite cutoff; neither is proved uniformly bounded in X. WC4 resolves the\nremaining target-coefficient problem of TL/RF INSIDE M for this weight.\nIt supplies no direct bound on primitive sources or on the complement R.\n\n## WC5. Weighted reduction from later to first primitive fibers\n\nFor each odd3-free m let A_m and A=min A_m be the ACTUAL primitive\nexponents a>=2 in [CF](FIRST_PRIMITIVE_FIBER_REDUCTION.md). Empty columns\ncontribute nothing. Their source labels are\n\n    n_(a,l)=1+4^(l+1)3^(a-1-l)m, 0\u003c=l\u003c=a-1.\n\nLet W_(a,X),omega be the retained weighted sum in this fiber. All h=1\nthere by the actual primitive mask. Its full harmonic majorant, obtained\nby replacing n by n-1, is denoted W^0_(a,omega).\n\nFor l\u003ca-1 the source is1 mod3 and its weight is1/4. For l=a-1,\nn=1+4^a m is2 mod3 if m=1 mod3 and0 mod3 if m=2 mod3. In particular\nthe terminal weight is independent of a within a fixed column. Therefore\n\n    if m=1 mod3:\n      W^0_(a,omega)=(1-alpha^a)/(4*3^(a-1)m);\n    if m=2 mod3:\n      W^0_(a,omega)=1/(4*3^(a-1)m).               (WC6)\n\nIn the first case all weights are1/4 and the CF geometric calculation\napplies after that common factor: the tail after A divided by the first\nmajorant is at most5/7. In the second case it is exactly1/2, since the\nratio between successive majorants is1/3. As A>=2, every denominator\nx_l=n_(A,l)-1 is at least12; hence\n\n    W_(A,omega)>=(12/13)W^0_(A,omega).\n\nFor a complete first fiber, the full later arithmetic tail is consequently\nat most65/84 of its actual weighted mass when m=1 mod3 and13/24 when\nm=2 mod3. Dropping actual masks only enlarged the tail.\n\nThe cutoff proof must also be retained. Let N_A=1+4^A m be the last\nfirst-fiber member. If X>=N_A the preceding complete bound applies.\nIf X\u003cN_A, every retained later source has l\u003c=A-2: the minimum later\nsource with l>=A-1 is already greater than N_A. Such a source has the\nform1+x_l*3^r, r=a-A>=1, and its first target1+x_l is also retained.\nBoth weights are1/4. The sum over all r>=1 is at most\n\n    (1/4)/(2x_l) \u003c= (13/24)*(1/4)/(1+x_l).\n\nThus both cutoff regimes imply, without any convergence assumption,\n\n    sum_(a in A_m,a>A) W_(a,X),omega\n       \u003c=(65/84)W_(A,X),omega.                    (WC7)\n\nThis is not an automatic consequence of the unweighted inequality:\nthe terminal weights and the truncated matching were checked explicitly.\n\n## WC6. The remaining actual core and the exact open obligation\n\nLet F_omega(X) be the weighted mass of first primitive fibers, summed\nover columns. CF's source reconstruction from v2(n-1), v3(n-1) and m\nshows all distinct column/fiber labels have disjoint sources. Thus WC7 gives\n\n    F_omega(X)\u003c=Jprim_omega(X)\u003c=(149/84)F_omega(X). (WC8)\n\nCombining WC5 with WC8, and defining C_omega=R_omega+F_omega, yields\n\n    C_omega(X)\u003c=G_omega(X)\n       \u003c=2R_omega(X)+(149/42)F_omega(X)\n       \u003c=(149/42)C_omega(X).                      (WC9)\n\nConsequently finite G is equivalent to finite C_omega, since omega lies\nbetween1/4 and1. This is an actual-mask reduction, not a proof of its\nfinite-mass premise. It is not asserted that this core is contained in\nSP's Cstar or that its comparison constant can be transferred unchanged\nto unweighted G. For example the immediate unweighted bound would be\nG\u003c=4G_omega\u003c= (298/21)C_omega, a different comparison.\n\nThe arithmetic complement of N union Pplus still contains EVERY odd\nn=3 mod4, since both removed source sets are1 mod4. Even the subfamily\nn=8k+3 has divergent unrestricted harmonic mass, by comparison with\n1/[8(k+1)]. Thus erasing h from R cannot prove the desired finiteness.\nThis asserts no actual defect on any of those indices. First primitive\nfibers remain another unresolved actual-mask contribution.\n\n## WC7. Source scope and audit discipline\n\nThese estimates are derived above from the exact local PB/SP/TL paths\nand finite geometric sums; no random independence, density assumption,\nterminal theorem or PD170 is used. Root freshly opened\n[Rozier–Terracol, equation(2)](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948): it supplies\nthe standard finite affine/parity framework, not the present weighted\nenergy theorem. No external novelty claim or external peer review is made.\n\nThe previous unweighted arithmetic obstruction uW(u)->1 is not contradicted:\nit concerns different weights. Choosing omega changes the norm, while the\nremaining core in WC9 still carries the unresolved mass. Improving the\nconstant alone would not eliminate this missing input.\n\nRelated audit corrections are tracked separately. In the minimum-fiber\ntwo-bit report, p=(9q+3)/8 was erroneously called smaller than q; in fact\np-q=(q+3)/8>0. Minimum-h says nothing about h(p). The independent small\nbirth ancestors from BR keep their own bounds. Neither that mistaken\ndescent nor any arbitrary beta-color realization is used in WC1–WC9.\n\nNext mathematical requirement: an actual-mask estimate or strict descent\ncovering R and the first primitive fibers. The full goal is active and\nincomplete; a percentage or completion date is not justified.\n","THREE_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md":"# SL3 — complete three-odd-step classification and a square-root output bound\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root manual proof, independently\naudited in separate arithmetic and weighted-count reports. No mathematical\nprogram, enumeration, CAS or Lean was used. This is an internal research result\nfor an explicitly restricted family; full Collatz and literature priority are\nnot established.\n\n## SL3.1. Exact scope and statement\n\nUse the shortcut map T(x)=x/2 for even x and T(x)=(3x+1)/2 for odd x.\nA structural ordered lift consists of two genuine parity words of common\nlength K and common odd count P that send (x,x+1) to (n,n+1) as an affine\nidentity on the corresponding source progression. It is not an arbitrary\npair of first-hit paths, an order reversal, or an asynchronous pair.\n\nFor odd positions 0\u003c=i_1\u003c...\u003ci_P\u003cK, the forward intercept and map are\n\n    C(I)=sum_(r=1)^P 3^(P-r) 2^(i_r),\n    T_w(x)=(3^P x+C(I))/2^K.\n\nThus the structural condition and inverse left coordinate are exactly\n\n    C_R-C_L=2^K-3^P,\n    F(n)=(2^K n-C_L)/3^P.\n\nThe right inverse has its own constant:\nG(n+1)=(2^K(n+1)-C_R)/3^P=F(n)+1. In general F(n+1) is not F(n)+1.\nFor a genuine word and positive integer terminal n, integrality of its\ninverse implies both positivity and the prescribed parity word. The\nfirst-bit induction establishing this is in\n[SL1–SL10](SMALL_ODD_STRUCTURAL_LIFTS.md). First-hit restrictions are additional.\n\n**Classification.** There are exactly six P=3 ordered structural rules,\nwithout any assumed upper cutoff on K:\n\n| Rule | K | C_L | C_R | n modulo 27 | Left/right forward words |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| L^3 | 6 | 37 | 74 | 1 | OEOEOE / EOEOEO |\n| L after M | 7 | 29 | 130 | 19 | OEOOEEE / EOEEOEO |\n| M after L | 7 | 47 | 148 | 1 | OOEEEOE / EEOEOEO |\n| J | 7 | 23 | 124 | 16 | OOEOEEE / EEOOEEO |\n| N | 8 | 19 | 248 | 16 | OOOEEEEE / EEEOOEEO |\n| R | 8 | 31 | 260 | 19 | OOEEOEEE / EEOEEOEO |\n\nHere O/E refer to the parity immediately before each forward shortcut step.\nCombining this with the accepted P\u003c=2 classification, all allowed finite\ncompositions have the same output set as the five generators\n\n    L(n)=(4n-1)/3,       n=1 modulo 3,\n    M(n)=(32n-5)/9,      n=1 modulo 9,\n    J(n)=(128n-23)/27,   n=16 modulo 27,\n    N(n)=(256n-19)/27,   n=16 modulo 27,\n    R(n)=(256n-31)/27,   n=19 modulo 27.\n\n**Counting theorem.** Let A(n_0) be the set of all distinct positive left\nsource coordinates generated from the one fixed terminal seed n_0 by\narbitrary finite compositions of ALL structural ordered rules with P\u003c=3.\nThen A(1)={1,3}. For X>=n_0>1,\n\n    #(A(n_0) intersect [1,X]) \u003c= 2048 sqrt((X-1)/(n_0-1)).\n\nIn particular this family has natural density zero. There is no assumption\nthat switching is periodic. The earlier O(log X) bound remains sharper\non the smaller L,M family; the present square-root estimate covers more rules.\n\n## SL3.2. Exhaustive classification: initial reductions\n\nFor P=3 write C=9*2^i+3*2^j+2^ell with 0\u003c=i\u003cj\u003cell\u003cK.\nWhen K=3, C=19 is the only choice, so difference 2^3-27=-19 cannot\noccur. When K=4 the four symbolic intercepts are 19,23,29,38; none\ndiffer by -11. These are direct symbolic cases, not a computational search.\n\nFor every K>=5 the required difference 2^K-27 is positive and odd.\nExactly one intercept is odd, and an intercept is odd exactly when its\nfirst odd position is zero. We treat both orientations below. Throughout,\nv_2 denotes the exact exponent of 2 dividing a nonzero integer; it applies\nto negative integers as well. Every odd position is strictly below K.\n\n## SL3.3. Left intercept odd: first two positions 0,1\n\nWrite C_L=9+3*2^j+2^ell, with 1\u003c=j\u003cell\u003cK. Then\n\n    C_R=2^K-18+3*2^j+2^ell.\n\nIf j=1 and ell=2, C_R=2^K-8 has valuation 3. The right positions\nare (3,r,s), with 3\u003cr\u003cs\u003cK, and\n\n    3*2^r+2^s=2^K-80.\n\nThe right side has valuation 4, giving r=4 and 2^s=2^K-128.\nFor K\u003c=7 the latter right side is nonpositive. For K>7 its valuation\nis 7, so s=7 and K=8. This gives (K,C_L,C_R)=(8,19,248).\n\nNow suppose j=1 and ell>=3. The right first position is 2, since\nC_R=2^K-12+2^ell has valuation 2. Write its remaining positions\nas 2\u003cr\u003cs\u003cK. The equation becomes\n\n    3*2^r+2^s-2^ell=2^K-48,                 (A)\n\nwhose right side has valuation 4.\n\n- If r\u003cell, its uniquely lowest term gives r=4. Thus s,ell>=5,\n  K>=6, and 2^s-2^ell=2^K-96. At K=6 the only possible s,ell\n  would both be 5, which cannot give -32. At K>=7 the right side\n  is positive with valuation 5, so ell=5\u003cs. Then 2^s=2^K-64\n  forces s=6,K=7. This gives C_L=47,C_R=148.\n- If r>ell, valuation first gives ell=4. Equation (A) becomes\n  3*2^r+2^s=2^K-32. Its valuation gives r=5, followed by\n  2^s=2^K-128 and s=7,K=8. This gives C_L=31,C_R=260.\n- If r=ell, the left side of (A) is 2^(ell+1)+2^s. If s=ell+1,\n  its valuation is ell+2>=5, a contradiction. Otherwise its\n  valuation is ell+1, so ell=3. Now 2^s=2^K-64 gives s=6,K=7.\n  This gives C_L=23,C_R=124.\n\n## SL3.4. Left intercept odd: second position j>=2\n\nHere C_R has valuation 1. Its positions are (1,r,s), with 1\u003cr\u003cs\u003cK.\nThe equation is\n\n    3(2^r-2^j)+2^s-2^ell=2^K-36,          (B)\n\nwhose right side has valuation 2.\n\nIf r=j, the left side is divisible by 8, impossible.\nIf r\u003cj, the unique lowest term gives r=2 and\n\n    2^s-2^ell=2^K-48+3*2^j.\n\nFor j=3 the right side is positive of valuation 3, forcing s=3\nbecause ell>3. The left side would then be negative. For j=4 the\nright side is 2^K, impossible for a difference of powers with both\nexponents below K. For j>=5 it is positive of valuation 4, forcing\ns=4\u003cell and again a negative left side. Thus r\u003cj is impossible.\n\nIf r>j, the unique lowest term of (B) gives j=2 and\n\n    3*2^r+2^s-2^ell=2^K-24,               (C)\n\nwhose right side has valuation 3.\n\n- If ell\u003cr, first ell=3; then 3*2^r+2^s=2^K-16 forces r=4.\n  The remaining 2^s=2^K-64 gives s=6,K=7: C_L=29,C_R=130.\n- If ell>r, first r=3; then 2^s-2^ell=2^K-48. At K=5 both\n  s and ell would have to equal 4, so cannot give -16. At K>=6\n  positivity and valuation 4 give ell=4 and 2^s=2^K-32.\n  Thus s=5,K=6: C_L=37,C_R=74.\n- If ell=r>=3, the left side of (C) is 2^(r+1)+2^s. If s=r+1\n  its valuation is r+2>=5; otherwise it is r+1>=4. Neither is 3.\n\nThese are all six left-odd solutions.\n\n## SL3.5. Right intercept odd: no solutions\n\nWrite right positions (0,r,s), 1\u003c=r\u003cs\u003cK, and left positions\n(i,j,ell), 1\u003c=i\u003cj\u003cell\u003cK. The equation is\n\n    3*2^r+2^s-9*2^i-3*2^j-2^ell=2^K-36. (D)\n\nIf r\u003ci, its unique lowest term gives r=2, so i>=3. Rearranging,\n\n    2^K-2^s=48-9*2^i-3*2^j-2^ell\u003c0,\n\ncontradicts s\u003cK. If r>i, valuation gives i=2, and cancellation of\n-36 yields 3(2^r-2^j)+2^s-2^ell=2^K. For r!=j the unique lowest\nexponent is min(r,j)\u003cK, impossible. For r=j a difference of two\npowers below 2^K cannot equal 2^K.\n\nIt remains r=i. The left side of (D) becomes\n\n    2^s-3*2^(i+1)-3*2^j-2^ell.\n\nIf s=i+1 this is at most -28, whereas 2^K-36>=-4. If s>i+1\nand j=i+1, it is divisible by 2^(i+2), with i+2>=3, contradicting\nvaluation 2. Finally, if s>i+1 and j>i+1, its unique lowest term\nforces i=1. Then\n\n    2^K-2^s=24-3*2^j-2^ell\u003c0\n\nbecause j>=3,ell>=4. Again s\u003cK gives a contradiction.\nThe six rules of SL3.1 are therefore exhaustive, with no length cutoff.\n\n## SL3.6. Domains, redundancy and an actual branching example\n\nSolving 2^K n=C_L modulo 27 gives the six guards in SL3.1.\nThe first three inverse formulas are respectively\n(64n-37)/27, (128n-29)/27, (128n-47)/27, equal to L^3,\nL composed with M, and M composed with L. Their composite domains\nagree with the displayed guards: final integrality forces every prefix\nintegral, as proved in the previous variable-lift report. Positivity follows\nfrom the genuine-word property. Thus replacing these rules by their\nL,M factorizations preserves admissibility and the generated output set.\nIdentity and L^2 are redundant for the same reason.\n\nJ,N,R cannot be compositions of positive-P rules from P\u003c=2: at total\nP=3 the only such possibilities have (K,C_L)=(6,37),(7,29),(7,47).\nThis means irreducible relative to that smaller alphabet, not new to literature.\n\nThe new alphabet really requires a different branching argument:\n\n    L(73)=97,       M(73)=259,\n    N(97)=919,      N(259)=2455.\n\nBoth 97 and 259 equal 16 modulo 27, and both final outputs equal\n1 modulo 3, so L can continue on both branches. This invalidates\ntransplanting the old side-depth-two proof unchanged. It asserts\nneither two infinite branches nor positive-density outputs.\n\n## SL3.7. Residue information sufficient for a weighted bound\n\nCall n live if n=1 modulo 3; every generator requires a live input.\nPartition positive inputs by residues modulo 27:\n\n    A={1}, B={10}, C={19}, D={16},\n    E={4,7,13,22,25}, Z={n: n=0 or 2 modulo 3}.\n\nLet S=A union B union C union D union E. Direct substitution gives:\n\n| Input class | Allowed rules and output classes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Z | none |\n| E | L to Z |\n| A | L to A/B/C; M to Z |\n| B | L to E; M to Z |\n| C, n=27t+19, t=0 modulo 3 | L,M to E; R to Z |\n| C, t=1 modulo 3 | L to E; M to A/B/C; R to Z |\n| C, t=2 modulo 3 | L,M to D/E; R to S |\n| D, n=27t+16, t=0 modulo 3 | L,J to Z; N to S |\n| D, t=1 modulo 3 | L,J,N to Z |\n| D, t=2 modulo 3 | L,N to Z; J to S |\n\nFor the two nontrivial classes the exact formulas are\n\n    L(27t+19)=36t+25,  M(27t+19)=96t+67, R(27t+19)=256t+179,\n    L(27t+16)=36t+21,  J(27t+16)=128t+75, N(27t+16)=256t+151.\n\nIn particular at most one of J,N stays live. The S destinations must\nremain arbitrary live states: restricting them to D/E would be incorrect.\nThe t cases use n modulo 81, but no finite closed automaton for all\nfuture iterates is assumed. These are uniform one-step bounds valid for\nevery value of the unobserved higher digits.\n\n## SL3.8. Bounded total inverse-square-root weight\n\nThe five slopes alpha_i=2^K_i/3^P_i exceed one. Write\n\n    a=sqrt(3/4), b=sqrt(9/32),\n    c=sqrt(27/128), d=sqrt(27/256),\n\nfor the weights of L,M,J,and each of N,R. Let T_h(n) be the sum\nover all admissible words of length at most h from n of alpha_w^(-1/2),\nwhere alpha_w is the product of the slopes and the empty word weighs one.\nWords are counted separately even when they reach the same integer.\nFor h>=1 the exact recurrence is\n\n    T_h(n)=1+sum_(i admissible at n) alpha_i^(-1/2) T_(h-1)(F_i(n)).\n\nWe prove T_h(n)\u003c=H=2048 for every positive integer n and h>=0 by\nstrong induction. The base h=0 is immediate. All lower depths are\nbounded by H. From the table, the following are bounds at depth h:\n\n    Z: 1,\n    E: 1+a,\n    A: 1+b+aH,\n    B: 1+b+a(1+a),\n    D: 1+a+d+cH.\n\nFor D with a live N branch the raw bound is 1+a+c+dH. It is\nno larger than the displayed bound since c>d and H>=1. When both\nJ,N die the displayed bound also holds. The same refined D bound\nis valid at any smaller depth, including depth zero. In class C:\n\n    t=0: 1+d+(a+b)(1+a),\n    t=1: 1+d+a(1+a)+bH,\n    t=2: 1+(a+b)(1+a+d)+[(a+b)c+d]H.\n\nFor the last bound, L and M enter D or E, where the refined bound\n1+a+d+cH applies. This refinement at depth h-1 only uses earlier\ndepths; the depth-zero case is bounded by it as well. The R child\ncan be any live state and is bounded by H directly.\n\nEvery additive constant above is less than 7, and\n\n    a\u003c7/8, b\u003c3/4, c\u003c1/2, d\u003c1,\n    (a+b)c+d=(18 sqrt(2)+21 sqrt(3))/64 \u003c255/256\u003c1.\n\nThe last inequality follows from sqrt(2)\u003c3/2 and sqrt(3)\u003c7/4.\nThus all bounds are less than or equal to\n\n    7+(255/256)*2048=2047\u003c2048.\n\nInduction proves the uniform bound. Monotone convergence of the\nnonnegative partial sums yields\n\n    sum_(all admissible words from n) alpha_w^(-1/2) \u003c=2048.\n\nThe self-loop L at n=1 causes no problem for this weighted series.\nIdentity and redundant composite generators were removed before counting\nwords; otherwise an identity would give artificial infinite multiplicity.\n\n## SL3.9. Convert weights into a count of integer sources\n\nFor every generator and n>1,\n\n    F_i(n)-1 >= alpha_i(n-1).\n\nThe additive differences for L,M,J,N,R are respectively\n0,2,26/9,70/9,22/3. Hence every word from n_0>1 obeys\n\n    x_w-1 >= (n_0-1) alpha_w.\n\nWhenever x_w\u003c=X, it follows that\n\n    1 \u003c= sqrt((X-1)/(n_0-1)) alpha_w^(-1/2).\n\nSum over all admissible words whose outputs are at most X, and then\nover all words. Collisions only increase this upper bound relative to the\nnumber of distinct outputs. SL3.8 proves the stated square-root theorem.\nFor n_0=1 only L and M are initially legal: L(1)=1, M(1)=3,\nand 3 has no outgoing rule. Thus A(1)={1,3}.\n\nIf a seed pair already witnesses reachability of two fixed targets, every\ngenerated pair retains that reachability by first returning to the seed.\nIts generated family has density zero, including after first-hit filtering.\nThis blocks a positive-density amplification argument using only these\nrules. It does not show that the entire paired predecessor set has density\nzero or that every relevant pair factors into these blocks.\n\n## SL3.10. Sources, audits and remaining gap\n\nAll five generator word pairs appear in LaTourette's 2007 Table 4.1.\nSection 4.2.4 also proposes fixed-odd-count case analysis through three\nones. These examples and that strategy are prior work. Root checked the\n[primary thesis copy](https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/31124373/explorations-of-the-collatz-conjecture-moravian-college),\nTable 4.1 and Section 4.2.4; some OCR formulas are damaged. No world\npriority is claimed for either our explicit classification or output bound.\n\n- [0459: independent complete classification](FOLLOWUP_0459_THREE_ODD_LIFT_CLASSIFICATION.md).\n- [0485: independent weighted-tree and square-root audit](FOLLOWUP_0485_FINITE_LIFT_OUTPUT_BOUNDS.md).\n- [0483: primary-source audit](FOLLOWUP_0483_BLOCK_PREFIX_SOURCES.md).\n\nUnbounded P, order reversals, unequal depths, general affine offsets,\nfactorization of all paired predecessors, and positive upper density of\nthe full Q(a,c) from nonemptiness remain open. No bad orbit class is\nexcluded by this counting theorem. An infinite sparse generated chain is\nneither asserted nor ruled out. The full conjecture has no established\ncompletion date or defensible percentage solved.\n","VARIABLE_RUN_AND_POINTWISE_DESCENT_AUDIT.md":"# Variable runs and pointwise boundary descent — reproduction audit\n\n18 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root's manual synthesis.\nNo mathematical programs, enumeration, CAS, or Lean. This part of the\ncurrent pass adds no new descent theorem and no new green research node.\n\n## Exact run and the equality case\n\nFor an odd n=2^r u−1 with u odd, r=ν₂(n+1)≥1, put\ns=ν₂(3^r u−1). With S the odd-only map and T the shortcut map,\n\n    y=S^r(n)=T^(r+s)(n)=(3^r u−1)/2^s.\n\nThere are r−1 initial S steps with valuation one; the last odd-only\nstep has valuation s+1. The exact descent test is\n\n    y\u003cn  iff  (2^(r+s)−3^r)u > 2^s−1.\n\nIf 2^(r+s)>3^r then\n\n    y−n=−(2^(r+s)−3^r)u/2^s+1−2^(−s)\u003c1.\n\nSince y−n is an integer, y≤n. Equality is precisely the periodic return\n(2^(r+s)−3^r)u=2^s−1. If 2^(r+s)\u003c3^r then y>n. No cycle exclusion is\nsilently imported: the primary Steiner paper was not fully read in this\npass, and is not used here. For a minimal bad μ, later blocks must stay\nabove the original μ; they need not stay above each block's own start.\n\n## Corrections made before accepting these reports\n\n- The ratio (3^r u−1)/(2^r u−1) is strictly greater than (3/2)^r,\n  not less. It decreases in u and is at most (3^r−1)/(2^r−1).\n  The first inequality derived from minimality is non-strict.\n- The h=1 compatibility test requires the factor 2 on its left:\n  2(2^r u−1)≤3^r u−1. For r≥2 this follows from 3^r>2^(r+1).\n- The inequality y−n\u003c1 with integer endpoints reduces the contracting\n  case to nonincrease and possible equality. Treating the affine\n  correction as an arbitrary uncontrolled obstruction here was too weak.\n- The exclusion μ≡3 mod16 is old finite stopping-time sieving, not a\n  new result of this pass.\n\nRoot checked overlap with the complete relevant texts of\n[R143](../../../work/round143-l-general-merging.md),\n[R167](../../../work/round167-n-tail-compensation.md), and\n[R153](../../../work/round153-n-repeated-return.md), together with\n[the block-height note](BLOCK_DESCENT_HEIGHT_DICHOTOMY.md),\n[MB](MINIMAL_BAD_PAIR_RETURN_BOUNDARY.md), and the source-scope note for\n[0474](../../../work/collatz-source-scope-0474.md).\nThe beginning through K5 of\n[the variable-run note](VARIABLE_RUN_COALESCENCE_AND_WITNESS_BUDGETS.md)\nwas also checked; this was not a fresh full read of its later sections.\nThe archive already has stronger parameterized finite-run restrictions.\nRepeated growing blocks with changing starts do not give one infinite\nbad orbit from a fixed positive integer.\n\n## What the least-boundary substitution actually says\n\nFor a hypothetical least bad μ, let β mark bad numbers, d its boundary,\nr=(μ−1)/2, and b=(3μ+1)/2. Then d(μ−1)=d(μ)=1. The exact identity\n\n    d(2r) XOR d(2r+1)=d(r)\n\nreads 1 XOR 1=0. The other identity gives\n\n    1 XOR d(μ+1)=d(b) XOR d(b+1) XOR d(b+2).\n\nThe right side is odd only if d(μ+1)=0; it is even if d(μ+1)=1.\nMinimality does not fix β(μ+2). These substitutions do not furnish a\nsmaller nonzero boundary. This is not a theorem that the full exact XOR\nsystem cannot imply descent: that system is equivalent to exact\ninvariance, and its full rigidity is part of the unsolved problem.\n\nThis reproduction/negative audit motivated the separate quantitative\n[PBK birth estimate](POLYNOMIAL_BOUNDARY_BIRTH_LOWER_BOUND.md). The PBK\nresult is the mathematical progress recorded for this pass. Neither\npart excludes a new class of fixed bad starts or proves full Collatz.\n","VARIABLE_RUN_COALESCENCE_AND_WITNESS_BUDGETS.md":"# 0474 — parametric coalescence and a bound on inverse-run complexity\n\n18 September2026, Asia/Tashkent. Internally checked hand derivation, with explicit external input in K8. No mathematical\nprogram, enumeration, CAS, factorization or numerical experiment. No novelty claim.\n\nT(n)=n/2 for even n and T(n)=(3n+1)/2 for odd n. C_s(n)=3^q_s/2^s,\nwhere q_s counts odd inputs in the first s steps. rho=log_2 3, alpha=rho-1.\nE_L={n>0: C_s(n)>1 for every 1\u003c=s\u003c=L}, mu_L=min E_L; these are infinite\nperiodic sets, not finite lists. tau(n) is the first s>=1 with C_s(n)\u003c1,\nor infinity if absent. h always denotes a nonnegative integer.\n[473 supplies the exact coalescence transfer](COALESCENCE_AND_FINITE_PLATEAU_DEADLINES.md).\n\n## K1. One full increasing run, then its full halving run\n\nLet n=2^p e-1 with p>=1, e a positive odd integer. Let\nd=v_2(3^p e-1)>=1 and y=(3^p e-1)/2^d. Thus y is positive odd and\nthe actual word n->y is1^p0^d, of length k=p+d. Assume\n\n    C=3^p/2^(p+d)>1, equivalently d\u003calpha p.\n\nEvery coefficient prefix of this word is>1. All positive sources with\nthese fixed p,d are exactly the class\n\n    3^p e == 1+2^d (mod2^(d+1)), e positive.\n\nThe congruence forces e odd. No condition3 does not divide e is needed.\n\nPut b=v_3(2^d-1). Then b\u003cp: otherwise3^p\u003c=2^d-1, contradicting C>1.\nThe two terms in3^p e+2^d-1 have different3-valuations, so\n\n    v_3(y+1)=b.\n\nFor odd d, b=0. For even d, b=1+v_3(d). The latter is elementary LTE,\nor expansion of4^(d/2)-1 followed by successive cubing.\n\n## K2. Exact sign and the equality case cannot be discarded\n\nThe least source on the pure-odd inverse branch of y is\n\n    u=(2/3)^b(y+1)-1.\n\nFor b=0 it is y with zero inverse time. For b>=1 it is a positive odd\ninteger with actual word1^b to y, and all its coefficient prefixes>1.\nAll shorter pure-odd inverse sources are at least u.\n\nPut\n\n    R=C/(3/2)^b =3^(p-b)/2^(p+d-b),\n    c=(2/3)^b(1-2^(-d)),\n    D=2^(p+d-b)-3^(p-b), K=(2^d-1)/3^b.\n\nHere b\u003cd (for even d, b\u003c=d/2; for odd d, b=0), 0\u003cc\u003c1, R!=1, and\n\n    u+1=R(n+1)+c,\n    n-u=(D e-K)/2^(d-b).\n\nIf D\u003c0 then u>n and no pure-odd source to this endpoint is smaller.\nIf D>0 then R\u003c1 implies n-u>-1, hence u\u003c=n by integrality. Strict\ndecrease is EXACTLY D e>K. Equality can occur only at e=K/D.\n\nFor fixed p,d in the assumed C>1 regime, with D>0, equality occurs in\nthe true source class iff D divides K. If it divides, e=K/D is positive\nodd, and\n\n    3^p e-1=2^d(3^b 2^(p-b)e-1)\n\nforces the exact halving valuation d since p-b>=1. Therefore the candidate\nequality is automatically admissible. This is a criterion, NOT a claim that\nsuch p,d exist. No cycle-exclusion theorem is silently used.\n\nIf equality held, T^b(n)=T^(p+d)(n), and the segment starting at\nz=T^b(n) would be a positive cycle with word1^(p-b)0^d and z>1.\nIndeed D>0 with C>1 forces b>=1; then z=3^b2^(p-b)e-1>=5.\nThus deleting equality without proof would hide a cycle obligation.\n\nCoefficient dominance for the alternative source is equivalent to D>0.\nIn terms of run lengths the successful-slope band is\n\n    d \u003c alpha p \u003c d+alpha b.\n\nBoth endpoint equalities are impossible by powers of2 and3. For a fixed\neven d this band contains exactly b integers p: floor(d/alpha)+1\nthrough floor(d/alpha)+b. Odd d has b=0 and no band. This is a count of\nparameter values, not a density or an orbit-visitation assertion.\n\n## K3. Transfer to finite minima, only with strict size\n\nWhen D e>K, b>=1, u\u003cn and C_b(u)=(3/2)^b>C, so J1/J2 of473 apply.\nIf n=mu_s then\n\n    mu_(s+p+d-b)>mu_s.\n\nIf tau(u) is finite, tau(n)\u003c=tau(u)+p+d-b.\nFor each fixed allowed pair p,d, every sufficiently large e in its\nactual class meets the strict inequality; in fact all class members do,\nexcept possibly the equality candidate in K2.\nThe known family473 is p=4,d=2,b=1,D=5,K=1, e=8t+5.\nNo p,d are chosen freely for a fixed n: p,d are determined by its actual\ntrajectory. Universal occurrence of the useful band is not proved.\n\n## K4. Any positive path with at most h odd runs has a height bound\n\nArchive provenance: the underlying proof and geometric sum already occur in\nFB162.2–3 and AFB162.2–3. This is a reapplication with the number of runs as\nthe direct budget, not a newly discovered height mechanism.\n\nAn odd run means a maximal consecutive string of odd INPUTS inside the\nfinite prescribed word; first/last runs may be truncated portions of the\nactual longer orbit. Let a positive integer source m follow an arbitrary\nfinite actual path containing at most h such runs. Even steps may occur\nbefore, after, or between them. Then for EVERY point x on this path,\n\n    x+1 \u003c= (m+1)^(rho^h).\n\nProof: if p consecutive odd inputs start at x, then2^p divides\nx+1, p\u003c=log_2(x+1), and the endpoint satisfies\n\n    T^p(x)+1=(3/2)^p(x+1)\u003c=(x+1)^rho.\n\nThe same bound holds at intermediate points of that increasing run.\nEach even step reduces x+1. Applying this successively for at most h\nruns proves the bound, including h=0.\n\n## K5. A coefficient bound and a necessary complexity for a smaller witness\n\nIn the setting K4, the j-th odd run has length\n\n    p_j \u003c= rho^(j-1) log_2(m+1).\n\nThus q=sum p_j \u003c= [(rho^h-1)/(rho-1)] log_2(m+1). Dropping only the\nhalving factors from C_end yields\n\n    C_end \u003c= (3/2)^q \u003c= (m+1)^(rho^h-1).\n\nNow fix n>1 and any smaller integer m\u003cn coalescing at y=T^k(n) after an\nactual path from m with at most h odd runs. Necessarily\n\n    y+1 \u003c= n^(rho^h).\n\nIf in addition this path supplies the coefficient dominance needed by J1,\nthen necessarily\n\n    C_k(n) \u003c= n^(rho^h-1).\n\nFor C_k(n)>1 this implies the integer lower bound\n\n    h >= ceil(log_rho(1+log(C_k(n))/log n)).\n\nThe endpoint bound also implies\n\n    h >= ceil(log_rho(log(y+1)/log n))\n\nwhen y+1>n. These are necessary restrictions on a specific witness, not\nexistence theorems and not lower bounds on the time to prove Collatz.\n\nFor a fixed n and fixed h, if its orbit tends to infinity then sufficiently\nlate endpoints cannot have a smaller witness with at most h odd runs.\nAn early useful witness is NOT excluded. If C_k(n)->infinity, the same\nrestriction holds eventually for dominant coefficient witnesses. These\nare conditional limits; no divergent positive orbit is asserted to exist.\n\n## K6. Strategy limits\n\nK1–K3 generalize a family and retain the equality/cycle condition in the algebra; K8 removes equality using the named established theorem E1.\nK4–K5 apply to arbitrary actual words with bounded number of odd runs and\nshow why arbitrarily lengthening a fixed number of runs cannot indefinitely\ncompensate unbounded future growth at a fixed original n. A proof may still\nuse an early witness, an unbounded number of inverse runs, or a different\nargument. The full goal, universal coverage and all cycles remain open.\nKnown run/inverse identities and coalescence are not claimed as new.\n\n## K7. A finite bound on the FULL length, even without an even-step budget\n\nFor any actual positive path from m with q odd and z even inputs, put N=m+1.\nAt an odd input the quantity x+1 is multiplied by3/2 exactly. At a positive\neven input x>=2, its ratio after the step is\n\n    (x+2)/(2x+2) \u003c=2/3.\n\nThe final state plus1 is at least2. Therefore\n\n    2 \u003c= N (3/2)^(q-z),\n    z \u003c= q+log_(3/2)(N/2).\n\nCombining with K5's bound q\u003c=((rho^h-1)/alpha)log_2 N gives, for the\ntotal length r=q+z of ANY actual path with at most h odd runs,\n\n    r \u003c= ((2rho^h-1)log_2 N-1)/alpha.\n\nFor smaller m\u003cn, N\u003c=n, hence\n\n    r \u003c= floor(((2rho^h-1)log_2 n-1)/alpha).\n\nThe edge h=0 is included; m=1,h=0 only admits the empty path. This does not\nbound the independent forward time k from n, nor give a full Collatz algorithm\nwith a known terminating choice of h. It only makes the alternative-word\nsearch finite for each fixed n,h. No such enumeration is run here.\n\n## K8. Separate corollary using a KNOWN external theorem on one-peaked cycles\n\nExternal input E1: the shortcut plus map has no nontrivial positive1-cycle,\nwhere a1-cycle has one increasing odd run and one decreasing even run.\nThis is a known theorem of Steiner, also covered by Simons–deWeger2005,\nTheorem3(b), whose published statement includes1\u003c=m\u003c=68. Root read §1.2,\n§1.4 and Theorem3(b) in the primary journal PDF:\nhttps://www.impan.pl/shop/en/publication/transaction/download/product/82263\nThe full historical proof and its computational inputs were not rerun.\n\nK2's equality would give exactly such a cycle through z>=5. With E1,\nequality is excluded, so D>0 gives u\u003cn for EVERY genuine start in the\nfixed p,d class; K3 then applies without a remaining exceptional e.\nThis corollary uses an established result, not a new proof of E1 and not\nan exclusion of cycles with unrestricted numbers of local maxima.\n\n\n[Full corrected audit](task-0474.md) · [Acceptance and limits](REVIEW_0474.md) · [Archive and primary-source scope](../../../work/collatz-source-scope-0474.md).\n","WEIGHTED_TERMINAL_AND_JOINT_ORIGIN_SPACING.md":"# WS — exact active-path rules improve endpoint charges\n\n19 September 2026, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Root analytic derivation,\nwith bounded independent audits. No numerical program, CAS, enumeration\nor Lean. Previous EC was progress. Full Collatz remains incomplete.\n\n## WS1. Setup and a universal missing boundary class\n\nUse the exact binary invariant beta, d(n), e(n), two active ancestry\npaths per birth and retained F graph from\n[SR](BIDIRECTIONAL_BIRTH_GRAPH_AND_SPARSE_CORES.md).\nThe endpoint-count problem from\n[EC](ENDPOINT_COUNT_TRANSPORT_AND_CRITICAL_PROFILES.md) remains open.\nAll sums below concern actual births; no nonconstant beta is constructed.\n\nFor every positive odd q, exact invariance gives\n\n    beta(4q)=beta(q)=beta(3q+1)=beta(4q+1).\n\nConsequently\n\n    d(8t+4)=0, t>=0.                                      (WS1)\n\nAn odd active boundary q has exactly one active child. If it is odd,\nthe next odd active boundary is A(q)=2q+1. Otherwise 2q is active,\n4q is inactive by WS1, and 4q+1 is active. Thus the next odd boundary\nis B(q)=4q+1. These are exact ancestry transitions, not ordinary orbit\nsteps or free color choices. Their depth increments are respectively 1\nand 2; modulo 3 they send i to 2i+1 and i+1.\n\nThe left path from a birth b starts at even 2b. Its first odd boundary\nmay occur at any depth >=2, or never; when b is even one must not assume\nit occurs immediately at depth2. The right path begins at odd 2b+1.\n\n## WS2. Types of terminal targets and the odd-target depth condition\n\nFor a terminal birth n put y=F(n). The exact masks from BM give:\n\n| Type | Residue of y | Forced active child | Exact cost 1/n |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S | 2 mod3 | 2y | 3/(2y)+3/[2y(2y-1)] |\n| R | 0 mod3 | 2y+1 | 3/(4y) |\n\nEvery y is active. The forced child is 1 mod3, so it cannot be another\nterminal target. Distinct terminal births have distinct targets.\n\nAn even S-target must be divisible by8. Indeed its odd birth source\nis 1 mod4, but births 1 mod8 are excluded, so the source is5 mod8 and\nthe target is divisible by4. WS1 rules out exact valuation2.\n\nAn odd S-target cannot occur at depth1 from its nearest birth. To prove\nthis, write its birth source n=2^h v-1 with v odd and h>=2. In the\n[SC strip](COLOR_STRIPS_AND_TWO_GROWTH_MODES.md), y=X_(h,1) and\nfloor(y/2)=X_(h-1,1). The birth and merging identities give\nC_(h-1)=C_h and the same neighbor color B_1 in both rows. Hence\n\n    d(y)=d(floor(y/2))=1.                              (WS2)\n\nThe nearest birth therefore lies at depth>=2, with b\u003c=floor(y/4).\nIn particular the right depth-one boundary 2b+1 of a birth b cannot\nbe an S-target. This uses the birth condition at the source n; it does\nnot assert e(n)=0 for all n in a residue class.\n\n## WS3. A two-type bound for all later terminal charges\n\nTwo S-targets y and4y cannot both occur. If y=S(n), the proposed S-source\nof4y is n'=4n+1, but at a birth n\n\n    beta(4n+1)=beta(n),\n    beta(4n+2)=beta(2n+1)=1-beta(n).\n\nThus d(n')=1 and n' is not a birth. After an S-target, the only possible\ntarget at depth gap2 is consequently the R-residue candidate4y+1.\nAfter an R-target, the candidates are4y+2 and4y+3. The first is an even\nS-residue number with valuation1, forbidden by WS2; the second is R-residue.\n\nEvery subsequent target is at least two depths later, and a subsequent\nS-target is at least three depths later. Use only the main costs\na_S=3/2 and a_R=3/4. The constants\n\n    V_S=7/4, V_R=1\n\nbound all main costs from a starting target y by V_type/y. For finite\ntarget strings this follows by backward induction from\n\n    a_type + max(V_R/4,V_S/8) \u003c= V_type,\n    max(1/4,7/32)=1/4.                               (WS3)\n\nLonger gaps only decrease these upper bounds. Increasing finite prefixes\ngive the infinite-string bound without assuming energy convergence.\n\nAt a birth b the left path has cost at most7/(8b): its first S-target\ncan occur at depth1, or any first target can be later. The right path\nhas cost at most1/(2b): depth-one S is forbidden, a depth-one R costs\nat most1/(2b), and a later first S costs at most7/(16b).\n\nThe S correction sums over distinct y>=2 to at most\nsum_(y>=2)3/[2y(y-1)]=3/2. With the inherited ancestor cutoffs this proves\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c= (11/8) E_beta((3X+1)/4)+3/2.       (WS4)\n\nThis improves the old coefficient2, for endpoints only.\n\n## WS4. Refinement by the ancestor's residue and parity\n\nLet E_C(Y)=sum_(b\u003c=Y, e(b)=1, b in C)1/b and Y=(3X+1)/4. The same\nargument gives the sharper estimate\n\n    E_terminal(X) \u003c= (3/4)E_(0 mod3)(Y)\n      +(11/16)E_(2 mod3)(Y)\n      +(15/16)E_(1 mod3, odd)(Y)\n      +(11/8)E_(1 mod3, even)(Y)+3/2.                 (WS5)\n\nHere are the initial-path bounds; every later possibility is no larger.\n\n* b=0 mod3: the left depth-one target can only be R. On the right,\n  depth1 is excluded; at depth2 only R is possible, since4b+2 is an\n  even S-residue of valuation1. Costs are1/(2b) and1/(4b).\n* b=2 mod3: both depth-one targets are excluded. The left S-candidate4b\n  at depth2 is also impossible: write b=3s+2 and let n=8s+5 be its\n  S-source. Then beta(n)=beta(b), while\n  beta(n+1)=beta(4s+3)=beta(6s+5)=beta(2b+1) differs from beta(b).\n  Thus the left costs at most1/(4b), and the right at most7/(16b).\n* b=1 mod3 odd: left depth1 is an even S-residue of valuation1, hence\n  excluded. Costs are at most7/(16b) left and1/(2b) right.\n* b=1 mod3 even: retain the general7/(8b) and1/(2b).\n\nFor example, when the first possible target on a path is R at depth2,\nits bound1/(4b) also dominates a first S at depth>=3, whose bound is\nat most7/(32b). The global correction3/2 is added once, not per class.\n\n## WS5. Joint origin charges on the exact odd ancestry\n\nKeep the SR/SG image-origin classes D_S and D_R. Their target origins\nare disjoint, but they may charge the same odd active boundary q.\n\nA D_S origin at S(q) contributes at most2/(3q), forces the B transition,\nand has nearest-birth depth at least2. A D_R origin has form2^h q,\nh>=2, q odd and0 mod3. Nonadjacent exponents h give total weight at\nmost1/(3q) for a fixed q. The D_R and D_S charges are both included\nwhen present at the same q; neither is deleted.\n\nLet i=q mod3. The normalized future weights\n\n    (U_0,U_1,U_2)=(232,172,184)/189                    (WS6)\n\nbound the combined origin charges from q onward by U_i/q. Induct\nbackwards on a finite odd-boundary prefix, treating two possibilities:\n\n    U_i >= 2/3 + 1_(i=0)/3 + U_(i+1)/4,\n    U_i >= 1_(i=0)/3 + max(U_(2i+1)/2,U_(i+1)/4).      (WS7)\n\nIndices are modulo3. The first line applies when D_S is present and B\nis forced; the second allows either exact continuation when D_S is absent.\nThe first three inequalities are equalities. The second-line right sides\nare149/189,116/189,92/189, respectively, each below U_i. Actual A(q)\nand B(q) are larger than2q and4q, which only improves the inequalities.\nIncreasing finite prefixes again requires no prior energy convergence.\n\n## WS6. Initial paths and the improved image-origin coefficient\n\nOn the left path from birth b, if the first odd boundary is at depth2\nit is4b+1, with cost at most U_(b+1)/(4b). If its first odd boundary\noccurs at depth>=3, its cost is at most max(U_i)/(8b), which is at most\nmin(U_i)/(4b). If it never becomes odd, it has no D_S/D_R charges.\nThus U_(b+1)/(4b) is valid in all cases.\n\nThe right path starts at q=2b+1, where D_S is forbidden at depth1.\nIt may carry a D_R charge only when b=1 mod3, followed by A or B.\nApplying the second line of WS7 and q>=2b gives the table:\n\n| b mod3 | Left coefficient, denominator b | Right coefficient | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 43/189 | 58/189 | 101/189 |\n| 1 | 46/189 | 149/378 | 241/378 |\n| 2 | 58/189 | 46/189 | 104/189 |\n\nFor origins at most X, their charged birth b is \u003cX/6 for D_S and \u003cX/8\nfor D_R. Summing the disjoint origin targets yields\n\n    E_D_S(X)+E_D_R(X)\n      \u003c= (101/189)E_(0 mod3)(X/6)\n        +(241/378)E_(1 mod3)(X/6)\n        +(104/189)E_(2 mod3)(X/6)\n      \u003c= (241/378)E_beta(X/6).                         (WS8)\n\nSince all origins split as P_1 disjoint union D_S disjoint union D_R,\n\n    E_origins(X) \u003c= E_P_1(X)+(241/378)E_beta(X/6).       (WS9)\n\nThis strengthens the former image-origin coefficient17/18. It counts\norigins, not their whole retained components. The primitive class P_1\nand the internal component mass are not bounded by WS9.\n\n## WS7. Why these improvements do not supply EC's input\n\nNeither E_terminal nor E_origins is the full energy. Even a coefficient\nbelow1 on an endpoint subset is not an inequality of the form\nE_beta(X)\u003c=kappa E_beta(cX)+C for kappa\u003c1. WS9 also retains P_1.\nNo rearrangement can identify its left side with E_beta. The scalar\ninequality A(X)\u003c=kappa B(cX)+C is compatible with divergent B when A is\nonly a subquantity; this observation constructs no actual invariant.\n\nEC remains applicable if a separate counting bound for actual endpoints\nor origins is proved. WS1–WS9 improve exact local correlations and\nconstants but do not prove such a uniform sparsity bound. There is also\nno universal descent below the original birth or exclusion of all cycles.\nThe named conditional PD bridge is unchanged; no PD input was used in\nWS1–WS9 themselves.\n\n## WS8. Sources and review\n\nRoot and the source role freshly read the definitions and parity-vector\nresults in Sections1–2 of Rozier–Terracol,\n[Paradoxical behavior in Collatz sequences](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00948),\nv5 dated17May2026. Finite residue coding and affine remainders concern a\ndifferent object from the true color masks needed here. Their computational\nresults and conditional conclusions are not imported. WS is derived from\nexact local invariance and the named archive lemmas. No exhaustive priority\nsearch, external novelty claim or full formal verification was made.\n\nThe precise independent scopes and corrections are in\n[the WS review](REVIEW_0485_WEIGHTED_ENDPOINT_SPACING.md).\n","task-0207.md":"# Task 0207 — фиксированный период \\(W\\): сравнение марж\n\n16 сентября 2026. Ручная проверка обобщения леммы 0204/0206 с\n\\(V=1^d\\) на один фиксированный блок \\(W\\) длины \\(q\\). Программ, CAS,\nперебора и тестов не использовалось. Документ устанавливает только\nсравнение высотной маржи с числительной маржой; гипотеза Коллатца и\nсуществование самой маржи здесь не рассматриваются.\n\n## Постановка и точная формула\n\nПусть \\(\\xi=a/b\\in\\mathbb Z _2\\), \\(b>0\\) нечётно, и код \\(\\xi\\) не\nявляется периодическим в конечном счёте. Для префикса \\(U\\) длины \\(i\\)\nпишем \\(S=\\#U\\), \\(C_U\\) — его аффинную константу, а\n\\(m_i=T^i(\\xi)\\). Фиксируем слово \\(W\\) длины \\(q\\ge1\\), положим\n\n\\[\n R=\\#W,\\qquad c=C_W,\\qquad k=2^q-3^R\\ne0.\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(2^q\\) чётно, \\(k\\) нечётно, в том числе когда \\(3\\mid k\\).\nПериодическое завершение \\(UW^\\infty\\) имеет старт\n\n\\[\n r=\\frac{A}{3^S k},\\qquad A=2^i c-kC_U,\n\\]\n\nпоскольку \\(2^q3^S-3^{S+R}=3^S k\\). Для \\(A\\ne0\\) обозначим\n\n\\[\n v=v_3(A),\\qquad A^\\#=\\frac{|A|}{3^v},\\qquad\n h=\\frac{H(A,\\,2^q3^S,\\,3^{S+R})}{3^{\\min(v,S)}}.\n\\]\n\nИз определения высоты непосредственно следует\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n =3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n  \\max\\!\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\n                  \\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right).          \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nПоэтому достаточно исключить \\(v\\ge S\\) на хвосте и получить нижнюю\nоценку для \\(|A|/3^S\\).\nВ рассматриваемой последовательности \\(S\\to\\infty\\), так что начальные\nслучаи \\(S=0\\) автоматически остаются вне этого хвоста.\n\n## Проверка первого аргумента: ветвь \\(v\\ge S\\)\n\nПоложим \\(z=A/3^S\\). При \\(v\\ge S\\) это целое. Аффинная формула для\nпрефикса\n\n\\[\n 2^iT^i(\\xi)=3^S\\xi+C_U\n\\]\n\nпосле умножения на \\(b\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n a\\equiv-b3^{-S}C_U\\pmod {2^i}.                    \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь \\(bT^i(\\xi)\\) целое: нечётный знаменатель сохраняется при каждом\nшаге \\(T\\). С другой стороны,\n\n\\[\n 3^S z=A=2^ic-kC_U\\equiv-kC_U\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nУмножая (2) на \\(k\\) и сравнивая с последним сравнением, а затем\nсокращая нечётный множитель \\(3^S\\) по модулю \\(2^i\\), получаем\n\n\\[\n             2^i\\mid ka-bz.                         \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nЭто рассуждение требует только \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(k\\ne0\\), и его\nнечётности; обратимость \\(k\\) по модулю \\(3\\) нигде не нужна.\n\nИз стандартной границы для слова \\(U\\),\n\\(C_U\\le2^{i-S}3^S\\), следует\n\n\\[\n |z|\\le \\frac{2^ic}{3^S}+\\frac{|k|C_U}{3^S}\n      \\le(c+|k|)2^{i-S}.                             \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(i,S\\to\\infty\\), то при фиксированных \\(a,b,c,k\\)\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n \\le |ka|2^{-i}+b(c+|k|)2^{-S}\\longrightarrow0.     \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nСовместно с (3) это для всех достаточно больших \\(i\\) даёт\n\\(ka-bz=0\\). Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n \\xi=\\frac ab=\\frac zk=r.                          \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nНо \\(r\\) по построению имеет код \\(UW^\\infty\\): сравнение\n\\(3^Sk r=A\\) по модулю \\(2^i\\) задаёт префикс \\(U\\), а после него\nфиксированная точка \\(c/k\\) карты \\(T^q\\), с паритетами W, задаёт\n\\(W^\\infty\\). Равенство (6) поэтому\nделает код \\(\\xi\\) в конце концов периодическим, что противоречит\nусловию. Итак, для фиксированного непериодического рационального старта\n\n\\[\n                 v_3(A)\u003cS                         \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nна всех достаточно длинных совместимых префиксах.\n\nВажно, что этот вывод используется только в ветви \\(v\\ge S\\). При\n\\(v\u003cS\\) число \\(z=A/3^S\\) не является целым, поэтому использованный\nцелочисленный шаг (3) к нему неприменим без знаменателя.\nПериодический старт имеет знак \\(r=+A/(3^Sk)\\), а не минус.\n\n## Проверка второго аргумента: рост \\(m_i\\) и нижняя граница\n\nУ всех \\(m_i=T^i(a/b)\\) знаменатель после записи со знаменателем \\(b\\)\nостаётся целым делителем \\(b\\). Поэтому в любой ограниченной по модулю\nобласти имеется лишь конечное число возможных \\(m_i\\). Если бы\n\\(|m_i|\\) не стремилось к бесконечности, повторилось бы некоторое\nсостояние \\(m_i=m_j\\), \\(i\u003cj\\). Детерминированность \\(T\\) тогда сделала\nбы хвост орбиты и её код периодическими. Значит, для непериодического\nрационального кода\n\n\\[\n                         |m_i|\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(S\\ge1\\) положим \\(t_i=C_U/3^S\\). Для слова с единицами\n\\(t_i\\ge1/3\\). Непериодичность также исключает \\(m_i=0\\), поскольку\nпосле нуля код равен \\(0^\\infty\\). Из аффинной формулы\n\n\\[\n 2^im_i=3^S(\\xi+t_i)\n\\]\n\nполучаем точное тождество\n\n\\[\n \\frac A{3^S}=-kt_i+c\\,\\frac{\\xi+t_i}{m_i}.        \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nДля всех достаточно больших \\(i\\) выполнено\n\n\\[\n |m_i|\\ge \\frac{2c(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k|}.                 \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(c>0\\), то, используя \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\), имеем\n\n\\[\n |\\xi|+t_i\\le(1+3|\\xi|)t_i,\n\\]\n\nи потому\n\n\\[\n c\\frac{|\\xi+t_i|}{|m_i|}\n \\le \\frac{|k|}{2}\\frac{|\\xi|+t_i}{1+3|\\xi|}\n \\le \\frac{|k|t_i}{2}.\n\\]\n\nИз (9) следует\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A|}{3^S}\n \\ge |k|t_i-c\\frac{|\\xi+t_i|}{|m_i|}\n \\ge \\frac{|k|t_i}{2}\n \\ge \\frac{|k|}{6}.                                \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(c=0\\), второй член в (9) исчезает и сразу\n\\(|A|/3^S=|k|t_i\\ge|k|/3\\), так что (11) тем более верна. В частности,\nна этом хвосте \\(A\\ne0\\), и \\(A^\\#\\) определено.\n\nПодставляя (7) и (11) в (1), получаем требуемое сравнение:\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n \\le \\max\\!\\left(1,\\frac{6\\,2^q}{|k|},\n                         \\frac{6\\,3^R}{|k|}\\right)\n }\n\\qquad\\text{на достаточно длинном хвосте}.         \\tag{12}\n\nЭто именно сравнение для одного фиксированного \\(W\\). Для конечного\nнабора слов \\(W\\) конечность набора позволяет тем же выбором\nподпоследовательности зафиксировать \\(W\\), если бы \\(i\\) не рос, а общий\nхвост выбирается как максимум соответствующих порогов и правых частей\n(12).\n\n## Края \\(A=0\\) и \\(R=0\\)\n\nЕсли \\(A=0\\), периодическое завершение имеет старт \\(r=0\\). При\nсовпадениях с непериодическим кодом на длинах \\(N\\to\\infty\\) это возможно\nлишь конечное число раз: иначе код \\(\\xi\\) совпал бы с кодом \\(0\\), то\nесть был бы \\(0^\\infty\\). Кроме того, нижняя оценка (11) сама исключает\n\\(A=0\\) после входа в хвост (включая \\(c=0\\)). Поэтому \\(A=0\\) просто\nисключается из отношения \\(h/A^\\#\\), а не создаёт дополнительную ветвь.\n\nЕсли \\(R=0\\), то \\(W=0^q\\), \\(c=0\\), \\(k=2^q-1\\ne0\\); формула (9)\nстановится \\(\\,A/3^S=-kt_i\\), и оценка даже усиливается до\n\\(|A|/3^S\\ge|k|/3\\). При чётном \\(q\\) здесь действительно \\(3\\mid k\\),\nно доказательство не делит на \\(k\\) по модулю \\(3\\), так что этот край\nполностью покрыт.\n\n## Почему \\(i\\to\\infty\\) у совместимых приближений\n\nПусть \\(U_jW^\\infty\\) совпадает с кодом \\(\\xi\\) с начала на длине\n\\(N_j\\to\\infty\\), а \\(W\\) фиксировано; положим \\(i_j=|U_j|\\). Если бы\n\\(i_j\\) не стремилось к бесконечности, существовала бы подпоследовательность\n\\(i_j\\le M\\). Конечность алфавита и числа слов длины не более \\(M\\) дают\nещё одну подпоследовательность с одним и тем же \\(U\\). Все её кандидаты\nодинаковы и равны \\(UW^\\infty\\), а длина совпадения с \\(\\xi\\) стремится к\nбесконечности. Значит, \\(\\xi=\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)\\), то есть её код в конце концов\nпериодичен — противоречие. Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n                         i_j\\to\\infty.              \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nЧисло единиц \\(S_{i_j}\\) также стремится к бесконечности: если бы оно\nоставалось ограниченным, монотонность \\(S_i\\) сделала бы хвост кода\nнулевым и периодическим. Поэтому оба предела, использованные в (5),\nдействительно имеют место. Одновременно (8) применяется к\n\\(m_{i_j}\\), и все условия хвостовой оценки (12) выполняются.\n\nТаким образом, при фиксированном \\(W\\) (и, после взятия конечного\nмаксимума, при конечном наборе \\(W\\)) получено только нужное сравнение\n\\(h/A^\\#\\); дополнительных выводов о марже или о гипотезе Коллатца этот\nаргумент не даёт.\n","task-0209.md":"# Task 0209 — независимый аудит леммы для фиксированного периода\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена вручную по task-0207.md; программ,\nCAS, тестов, перебора и дочерних агентов не использовалось. Проверяется\nтолько сравнение двух условий для фиксированного слова \\(W\\), а не\nсуществование самой маржи и не гипотеза Коллатца.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nОба аргумента корректны на достаточно длинном хвосте совместимых\nпрефиксов при фиксированных \\(a/b\\) и \\(W\\). В первом аргументе есть\nодна знаковая опечатка: при \\(v\u003cS\\) периодический старт равен\n\n\\[\n r=\\frac{A}{3^Sk},\n\\]\n\nа не \\(-A/(3^Sk)\\). Кроме того, следует говорить о равенстве чисел\n\\(\\xi=r\\) и о равенстве их кодов, например\n\\(\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)=r=\\xi\\), а не отождествлять число с бинарным\nсловом. Эти поправки не меняют вывода.\n\n## 1. Ветвь \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\)\n\nПусть \\(z=A/3^S\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Для\n\\(\\xi=a/b\\), где \\(b\\) нечётно, аффинная формула даёт\n\n\\[\n 2^i b m_i=3^S a+bC_U,\n \\qquad\n 3^S a+bC_U\\equiv0\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nИз \\(3^S z=A=2^ic-kC_U\\) следует\n\\(3^S z\\equiv-kC_U\\pmod {2^i}\\). После умножения первого сравнения\nна \\(k\\), второго — на \\(b\\), получаем\n\n\\[\n 3^S(ka-bz)\\equiv0\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(3^S\\) обратимо по модулю \\(2^i\\),\n\n\\[\n 2^i\\mid ka-bz. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь не используется обратимость \\(k\\) по модулю \\(3\\); достаточно того,\nчто \\(k=2^q-3^R\\) нечётно и ненулево.\n\nПри стандартных оценках \\(c=C_W\\ge0\\) и\n\\(C_U\\le2^{i-S}3^S\\) имеем\n\n\\[\n |z|\\le\\frac{2^ic+|k|C_U}{3^S}\n      \\le(c+|k|)2^{i-S}.\n\\]\n\nПоэтому при фиксированных \\(a,b,c,k\\) и \\(i,S\\to\\infty\\)\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n \\le |ka|2^{-i}+b(c+|k|)2^{-S}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nВместе с (1) это даёт \\(ka-bz=0\\) на достаточно длинном хвосте и,\nследовательно,\n\n\\[\n \\xi=\\frac ab=\\frac zk=r,\n \\qquad r=\\frac{A}{3^Sk}.\n\\]\n\nКорректная формулировка периодического шага такова: нечётность \\(k\\)\nпревращает \\(3^Sk r=A\\) в сравнение, задающее префикс \\(U\\), а\nфиксированная точка \\(c/k\\) отображения \\(T^q\\) имеет периодический код\n\\(W^\\infty\\). Поэтому код кандидата \\(r\\) есть конкретное слово\n\\(UW^\\infty\\), то есть\n\n\\[\n \\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)=r=\\xi.\n\\]\n\nКод \\(\\xi\\) тогда в конце концов периодичен, что противоречит исходному\nусловию. Значит, на хвосте\n\n\\[\n v_3(A)\u003cS. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nВ исходном тексте строка о ветви \\(v\u003cS\\) содержит только опечатку знака:\nнужно заменить \\(-A/(3^Sk)\\) на \\(+A/(3^Sk)\\). Смысл замечания верен:\nпри \\(v\u003cS\\) знаменатель \\(3^{S-v}\\) сохраняется, и целочисленный шаг\n(1) нельзя переносить на \\(r\\) как на целый старт.\n\n## 2. Рост \\(m_i\\) и нижняя оценка\n\nПосле каждого шага \\(T\\) рациональное \\(m_i=T^i(a/b)\\) можно записать\nсо знаменателем, делящим фиксированное нечётное \\(b\\). В ограниченном\nинтервале существует лишь конечное число таких рациональных состояний.\nЕсли бы \\(|m_i|\\not\\to\\infty\\), некоторое состояние повторилось бы:\n\\(m_i=m_j\\), \\(i\u003cj\\). Детерминированность \\(T\\) сделала бы последующий\nпаритетный код периодическим. Поэтому при непериодическом коде\n\n\\[\n |m_i|\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nНепериодичность также исключает \\(m_i=0\\): после нуля код равен\n\\(0^\\infty\\). Для \\(S\\ge1\\) положим \\(t_i=C_U/3^S\\). Наличие единицы\nв \\(U\\) даёт \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\), а аффинная формула даёт\n\n\\[\n 2^i m_i=3^S(\\xi+t_i),\n \\qquad\n \\frac A{3^S}=-kt_i+c\\,\\frac{\\xi+t_i}{m_i}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(c>0\\), после достижения порога\n\n\\[\n |m_i|\\ge \\frac{2c(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k|},\n\\]\n\nиз \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\) следует\n\n\\[\n c\\frac{|\\xi+t_i|}{|m_i|}\n \\le\\frac{|k|}{2}\\frac{|\\xi|+t_i}{1+3|\\xi|}\n \\le\\frac{|k|t_i}{2}.\n\\]\n\nПрименение обратного неравенства треугольника к (4) даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A|}{3^S}\\ge\\frac{|k|t_i}{2}\\ge\\frac{|k|}{6}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(c=0\\), второй член в (4) исчезает и получается более сильное\n\\(|A|/3^S=|k|t_i\\ge|k|/3\\). Знак \\(k\\) здесь не важен: используется\nтолько \\(|k|\\). В частности, (5) одновременно исключает \\(A=0\\) после\nвхода в хвост.\n\nПорог зависит от \\(\\xi\\) и \\(W\\): через \\(c,k\\) в (5), а в первом\nаргументе ещё через \\(a,b,c,k\\) и требуемые значения \\(i,S\\). Это не\nуниверсальный порог, но для фиксированных данных он конечен.\n\nПодстановка (2) и (5) в точную формулу\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}=3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\n              \\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right)\n\\]\n\nдаёт на хвосте\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n \\le \\max\\left(1,\\frac{6\\,2^q}{|k|},\n                    \\frac{6\\,3^R}{|k|}\\right). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nЭто именно сравнение \\(h/A^\\#\\); из него сама маржа не следует.\n\n## 3. Края \\(A=0\\), \\(R=0\\), \\(3\\mid k\\), \\(S=0\\), \\(m_i=0\\)\n\nПри \\(A=0\\) величина \\(A^\\#\\) не определена, поэтому отношение нужно\nрассматривать только после исключения этого края. На достаточно длинном\nхвосте его исключает (5). Независимо от (5), если кандидаты с \\(A=0\\)\nсовпадают с непериодическим кодом на длинах \\(N\\to\\infty\\), то их код\nравен коду старта \\(r=0\\), то есть \\(0^\\infty\\), что невозможно.\n\nЕсли \\(R=0\\), то \\(W=0^q\\), \\(c=0\\), \\(k=2^q-1\\ne0\\). Возможность\n\\(3\\mid k\\) (при чётном \\(q\\)) безвредна: все сравнения используют\nмодуль \\(2^i\\), где \\(k\\) обратимо, а в оценке появляется только\n\\(|k|\\). При \\(S\\ge1\\) формула (4) сразу даёт (5) с усилением.\n\nОсобый набор \\(R=0,A=0,S=0\\) означает \\(U=0^i\\), \\(C_U=0\\), \\(c=0\\)\nи \\(r=0\\). Если дополнительно \\(m_i=0\\), то\n\\(m_i=\\xi/2^i=0\\), следовательно \\(\\xi=0\\), и его код \\(0^\\infty\\)\nпериодичен. В допустимой непериодической задаче этот набор не возникает;\nк тому же \\(h/A^\\#\\) здесь просто не определено. При \\(S=0\\) и\n\\(m_i\\ne0\\) всё равно \\(U=0^i\\), \\(C_U=0\\), но\n\\(A=2^ic\\). При c≠0 последняя единица W даёт v₃(c)=0, поэтому\nv₃(A)=0; прежнее замечание о возможной положительной валюации\nисправлено координатором. При c=0 имеем A=0 и валюация не определяется. Поэтому\n\\(S=0\\) нельзя включать в доказательство (5); это начальный край,\nкоторый автоматически исчезает на рассматриваемом хвосте, поскольку\nдля непериодического кода \\(S_i\\to\\infty\\).\n\n## 4. Фиксированный конечный набор слов \\(W\\)\n\nДля одного \\(W\\) сначала выбирается хвост, на котором одновременно\nвыполнены (2), порог для (3), \\(S\\ge1\\), и (5). Если\n\\(\\mathcal W\\) — конечный набор слов, берётся максимум конечного числа\nпорогов по \\(W\\in\\mathcal W\\). Нижняя оценка может быть унифицирована\nчерез \\(\\min_{W\\in\\mathcal W}|k_W|>0\\), а правую часть (6) — через\nмаксимум конечного набора констант. Делимость в первом аргументе не\nтребует никакой дополнительной проверки при \\(3\\mid k_W\\).\n\n## 5. Почему \\(i\\to\\infty\\) при \\(N\\to\\infty\\)\n\nПусть периодические кандидаты \\(U_jW^\\infty\\) совпадают с кодом\n\\(\\xi\\) с начала на длине \\(N_j\\to\\infty\\), а \\(i_j=|U_j|\\). Если\n\\(i_j\\) не стремится к бесконечности, существует ограниченная\nподпоследовательность. Среди конечного числа слов \\(U_j\\) фиксированной\nограниченной длины выбирается одно и то же \\(U\\). Для фиксированного\n\\(W\\) все кандидаты на этой подпоследовательности одинаковы, и\nсовпадения произвольно большой длины дают\n\n\\[\n \\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)=\\xi,\n\\]\n\nто есть eventual periodicity — противоречие. Для конечного набора \\(W\\)\nдополнительно выбирается подпоследовательность с одним и тем же \\(W\\);\nконечность набора делает этот шаг законным. Следовательно,\n\\(i_j\\to\\infty\\). Непериодичность также даёт \\(S_{i_j}\\to\\infty\\):\nограниченное число единиц означало бы в конце концов нулевой хвост.\n\nИменно поэтому пределы, использованные в (5) и в первом аргументе,\nдействительно применимы к совместимым приближениям. Для разрозненных\nсовпадений на сдвинутых интервалах такой вывод об \\(i_j\\) сам по себе\nне следует.\n\nТаким образом, после исправления знака и уточнения равенства кода\nдоказательство даёт требуемое ограниченное сравнение двух условий для\nфиксированного \\(W\\), а после конечного максимума — для конечного набора\n\\(W\\). Оно не доказывает существование самой маржи и не содержит вывода\nо гипотезе Коллатца.\n","task-0210.md":"# Task 0210 — растущий период \\(W_i\\): условия переноса леммы\n\n16 сентября 2026. Ниже проверяется только арифметическое сравнение\nвысотной и числительной маржи для последовательности совместимых\nприближений. Не утверждается, что у всякой гипотетической плохой орбиты\nимеются такие приближения, нужные повторы или нужная скорость роста\n\\(|m_i|\\).\n\n## 1. Обозначения и точная формула\n\nПусть \\(\\xi=a/b\\in\\mathbb Z _2\\), \\(b>0\\) нечётно, а код \\(\\xi\\) не\nявляется периодическим в конце. Для совместимого приближения с префиксом\n\\(U_i\\) длины \\(i\\) положим\n\n\\[\n S_i=\\#U_i,\\qquad C_i=C_{U_i},\\qquad m_i=T^i(\\xi).\n\\]\n\nПериодический блок \\(W_i\\) имеет длину \\(q_i\\ge1\\), число единиц\n\\(R_i\\), константу \\(c_i=C_{W_i}\\), и\n\n\\[\n k_i=2^{q_i}-3^{R_i}\\ne0,\\qquad\n A_i=2^i c_i-k_i C_i.\n\\]\n\nЗдесь \\(k_i\\) может быть отрицательным. Всегда используются \\(|k_i|\\) и\n\\(|A_i|\\); знак нигде не заменяется предположением о положительности.\nКак обычно, \\(v_i=v_3(A_i)=v_3(|A_i|)\\) при \\(A_i\\ne0\\),\n\\(A_i^\\#=|A_i|/3^{v_i}\\). Для\n\n\\[\n B_i=2^{q_i}3^{S_i},\\qquad C'_i=3^{S_i+R_i}\n\\]\n\nимеем\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n =3^{\\max(v_i-S_i,0)}\n  \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^{q_i}3^{S_i}}{|A_i|},\n                 \\frac{3^{S_i+R_i}}{|A_i|}\\right). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nФормула верна и при отрицательном \\(A_i\\), поскольку высота берёт\nабсолютные значения.\n\nДалее рассматривается последовательность, на которой периодическое\nслово \\(U_iW_i^\\infty\\) совпадает с кодом \\(\\xi\\) на длине \\(N_i\\), где\n\\(N_i\\ge i+q_i\\), и предполагаются\n\n\\[\n q_i=o(S_i),\\qquad q_i=o(\\log |m_i|). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЛогарифм можно брать в любом фиксированном основании. Первое условие\nсразу даёт \\(S_i\\to\\infty\\), \\(i\\to\\infty\\), и \\(q_i=o(i)\\), так как\n\\(S_i\\le i\\). Второе условие, поскольку \\(q_i\\ge1\\), даёт\n\\(|m_i|\\to\\infty\\).\n\n## 2. Ветвь \\(v_i\\ge S_i\\): делимость и исключение периодического старта\n\nПредположим временно \\(A_i\\ne0\\) и \\(v_i\\ge S_i\\), и положим\n\n\\[\n z_i=A_i/3^{S_i}\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\n\nАффинная формула для \\(U_i\\) имеет вид\n\n\\[\n 2^i m_i=3^{S_i}\\xi+C_i.\n\\]\n\nПосле умножения на \\(b\\), с учётом того, что \\(b m_i\\) целое,\n\n\\[\n a\\equiv-b3^{-S_i}C_i\\pmod {2^i}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nС другой стороны,\n\n\\[\n 3^{S_i}z_i=A_i=2^ic_i-k_iC_i\\equiv-k_iC_i\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nУмножение (3) на \\(k_i\\), сравнение с последней конгруэнцией и\nсокращение обратимого по модулю \\(2^i\\) множителя \\(3^{S_i}\\) дают\n\n\\[\n                 2^i\\mid k_i a-bz_i. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nВ частности, деление по модулю \\(3\\) или по модулю \\(2^i\\) на \\(k_i\\)\nздесь не производится. Поэтому возможная делимость \\(3\\mid k_i\\) (край\n\\(R_i=0\\), \\(q_i\\) чётно) не является проблемой.\n\nИспользуем стандартную границу \\(0\\le C_i\\le2^{i-S_i}3^{S_i}\\) и не\nпредполагаем знак \\(A_i\\). Тогда\n\n\\[\n |z_i|\\le |c_i|\\,2^{i-S_i}+|k_i|\\,2^{i-S_i},\n\\]\n\nи из (4)\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|k_i a-bz_i|}{2^i}\n \\le |a|\\,|k_i|\\,2^{-i}\n       +b\\,(|c_i|+|k_i|)\\,2^{-S_i}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nПри выполнении\n\n\\[\n |k_i|\\,2^{-i}\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad\n (|c_i|+|k_i|)\\,2^{-S_i}\\longrightarrow0 \\tag{F}\n\\]\n\nправая часть (5) стремится к нулю. Левая часть — нормированный модуль\n|k_i a−bz_i|/2^i, то есть неотрицательное целое в силу делимости.\nПоэтому для всех достаточно больших\n\\(i\\) получаем \\(k_i a-bz_i=0\\). Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n \\xi=\\frac ab=\\frac{z_i}{k_i}\n      =\\frac{A_i}{3^{S_i}k_i}=r_i. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nПериодический старт \\(r_i\\) по построению имеет код \\(U_iW_i^\\infty\\).\nРавенство (6) делает код \\(\\xi\\) в конце концов периодическим, что\nпротиворечит предположению. Поэтому на любом хвосте, где выполнено\n(F),\n\n\\[\n                         v_3(A_i)\u003cS_i. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nГрубые оценки из условия задачи действительно обеспечивают (F) при\nпервом условии (2):\n\n\\[\n |k_i|,\\,|c_i|\\le3^{q_i},\\qquad\n \\frac{|k_i a-bz_i|}{2^i}\n \\le |a|3^{q_i}2^{-i}+2b3^{q_i}2^{-S_i}\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nДействительно, \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\) и \\(S_i\\le i\\) дают\n\\(q_i=o(i)\\), а\n\\(q_i\\log3-S_i\\log2\\to-\\infty\\). Это проверяет именно нужный\nпереход, включая отрицательные \\(k_i,A_i\\).\n\n## 3. Ветвь \\(v_i\u003cS_i\\): точная нижняя оценка\n\nПосле входа в хвост \\(S_i\\ge1\\). Положим\n\n\\[\n t_i=\\frac{C_i}{3^{S_i}}.\n\\]\n\nДля непустого префикса с единицей стандартно \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\). Кроме того,\n\\(m_i\\ne0\\): попадание в нуль сделало бы весь последующий код равным\n\\(0^\\infty\\). Из\n\n\\[\n 2^i m_i=3^{S_i}(\\xi+t_i)\n\\]\n\nполучаем точное тождество, без предположения о знаках \\(k_i,m_i\\):\n\n\\[\n \\frac{A_i}{3^{S_i}}\n =-k_i t_i+c_i\\,\\frac{\\xi+t_i}{m_i}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\),\n\n\\[\n |\\xi+t_i|\\le|\\xi|+t_i\\le(1+3|\\xi|)t_i. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nВведём точный относительный дефект\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i=\\frac{|c_i|(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k_i|\\,|m_i|}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nИз (9)--(10) следует\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A_i|}{3^{S_i}}\n \\ge |k_i|t_i(1-\\rho_i). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nПоэтому достаточное условие второй ветви имеет вид\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{G}\n\\]\n\nНа хвосте \\(\\rho_i\\le1/2\\), и тогда\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A_i|}{3^{S_i}}\\ge\\frac{|k_i|t_i}{2}\\ge\\frac{|k_i|}{6}>0. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nЭто одновременно исключает \\(A_i=0\\) и, вместе с (7), разрешает\nподставить \\(v_i\u003cS_i\\) в (1).\n\nУсловие (2) действительно даёт (G), но только через второе его\nусловие:\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i\\le(1+3|\\xi|)\\frac{3^{q_i}}{|m_i|}\\longrightarrow0, \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nпоскольку \\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\) означает\n\\(q_i\\log3-\\log|m_i|\\to-\\infty\\). Если \\(c_i=0\\), то (9) сразу даёт\n\\(|A_i|/3^{S_i}=|k_i|t_i\\), и (G) выполняется с нулевым дефектом.\n\n## 4. Высотная маржа и более точные условия\n\nИз (1), (7) и (12) получаем до огрубления точную полезную форму:\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n \\le \\frac1{1-\\rho_i}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^{q_i}}{|k_i|t_i},\n                  \\frac{3^{R_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(\\rho_i\\le1/2\\), \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\):\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n \\le\\max\\left(1,\\frac{6\\,2^{q_i}}{|k_i|},\n                  \\frac{6\\,3^{R_i}}{|k_i|}\\right). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(R_i\\le q_i\\), \\(|k_i|\\ge1\\), это даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\\le6\\,3^{q_i},\\qquad\n \\log\\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\\le\\log6+q_i\\log3. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nА поскольку \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\), \\(S_i\\le i\\), и \\(N_i\\ge i+q_i\\), имеем\n\\(q_i=o(N_i)\\), так что правая часть (17) есть \\(o(N_i)\\).\n\nБолее точный набор достаточных условий, не использующий грубую замену\n\\(c_i,|k_i|\\) на \\(3^{q_i}\\), таков:\n\n1. \\(S_i\\ge1\\) на хвосте и выполнено (F), чтобы исключить ветвь\n   \\(v_i\\ge S_i\\);\n2. выполнено (G), чтобы получить (12) и исключить \\(A_i=0\\);\n3. для высотной маржи\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\log\\frac{2^{q_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right)_+=o(N_i),\\qquad\n   \\left(\\log\\frac{3^{R_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right)_+=o(N_i),\\qquad\n   -\\log(1-\\rho_i)=o(N_i). \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\nЗдесь \\(x_+=\\max(x,0)\\). Условия (18) следуют, например, из более\nгрубых, но всё ещё более точных по \\(k_i\\) условий\n\n\\[\n (q_i\\log2-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i),\\qquad\n (R_i\\log3-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i), \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nпоскольку \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\). В формулах (F), (G), (18) видны две реальные\nцены: \\(c_i\\) должен быть мал относительно \\(|k_i||m_i|\\), а \\(|k_i|\\)\nдолжен учитываться в знаменателях высотной оценки. Это существенно\nсильнее, чем одна запись \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\).\n\n## 5. Края \\(A_i=0\\), \\(S_i=0\\) и знаки\n\nЕсли \\(A_i=0\\), формула старта даёт \\(r_i=0\\), поскольку\n\\(3^{S_i}k_i\\ne0\\). Орбита нуля имеет код \\(0^\\infty\\). Поэтому\nсовпадение с непериодическим кодом \\(\\xi\\) на длинах \\(N_i\\to\\infty\\)\nневозможно для бесконечного числа таких индексов: иначе код \\(\\xi\\) был бы\nнулевым. На хвосте это исключается также непосредственно (13), так что\nотношение \\(h_i/A_i^\\#\\) не оставляет нерассмотренной нулевой ветви.\n\nСлучай \\(S_i=0\\) означает \\(U_i=0^i\\), \\(C_i=0\\), и потому\n\\(A_i=2^ic_i\\). Оценка \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\) к нему не относится. Однако при\n\\(q_i=o(S_i)\\), \\(q_i\\ge1\\), он может происходить лишь конечное число\nраз, поскольку \\(S_i\\to\\infty\\). Если же нулевые префиксы имели бы\nнеограниченную длину сами по себе, код \\(\\xi\\) был бы \\(0^\\infty\\), что\nтакже исключено. Таким образом, \\(S_i=0\\) не влияет на заявленный\nасимптотический хвост.\n\nНикакой смены знака в (9), (12) не скрыто: используется\n\\(|-k_it_i|=|k_i|t_i\\), а добавочный член оценивается треугольным\nнеравенством. В первой ветви используется только целочисленность\n\\(z_i\\) и делимость (4); на \\(k_i\\) нигде не делят по модулю \\(3\\).\n\n## 6. Что имеющиеся оценки не выводят из одного \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\)\n\nУточнение после 0211: формальные параметры ниже не доказывают строгую\nнеимпликацию в классе настоящих совместимых рациональных орбит.\nВсе утверждения «нельзя вывести» в этом разделе относятся к\nиспользованным оценкам; другой арифметический аргумент не исключён.\n\nУсловие \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) само по себе не даёт \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\). На уровне\nпараметров можно иметь \\(q_i=j\\), \\(N_i\\asymp j^2\\), но\n\\(S_i\\asymp\\sqrt j\\). Тогда \\(q_i/N_i\\to0\\), а верхняя оценка\n\\(3^{q_i}2^{-S_i}\\) не стремится к нулю. Значит, из одного \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\)\nнельзя вывести (F) или исключить ветвь \\(v_i\\ge S_i\\).\n\nАналогично \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) ничего не связывает с \\(|m_i|\\). Даже при\n\\(N_i\\) существенно большем \\(q_i\\) оно не запрещает медленный рост\n\\(|m_i|\\) по сравнению с возможным отношением\n\\(|c_i|/|k_i|\\); поэтому из него не следует (G), то есть\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|c_i|(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k_i||m_i|}\\to0.\n\\]\n\nФормальные последовательности параметров, показывающие такую\nнезависимость, не являются утверждениями о существовании орбиты или\nсовместимых слов. Факт \\(|m_i|\\to\\infty\\) для данного фиксированного\nнепериодического рационального \\(\\xi\\) получается отдельно: при\nограниченных \\(|m_i|\\) возможны лишь конечное число рациональных\nсостояний с фиксированным нечётным знаменателем, а повтор состояния\nделает код хвоста периодическим. Но этот факт не сообщает требуемую\nскорость относительно произвольно растущего \\(q_i\\), и тем более не\nутверждает, что все гипотетические плохие орбиты предоставляют\nнеобходимые повторы и оценки.\n\nИтак, корневая гипотеза (2) строго достаточна для переноса сравнения\n\\(h_i/A_i^\\#\\le6\\,3^{q_i}\\) и для вывода\n\\(\\log(h_i/A_i^\\#)=o(N_i)\\) на уже выбранной совместимой\nпоследовательности. Одно лишь \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) даёт только последнюю\nформальную оценку после установления (7), (13); оно не устанавливает\nсами эти два предварительных шага.\n","task-0211.md":"# Task 0211 — независимый аудит условий для растущего \\(W_i\\)\n\n16 сентября 2026. Task-0210 прочитан полностью. Ниже приведена ручная\nпроверка; программ, CAS, тестов, перебора и дочерних агентов не\nиспользовалось. Проверяется только арифметическое сравнение для уже\nвыбранной последовательности совместимых слов. Наличие такой\nпоследовательности у произвольной плохой орбиты здесь не доказывается.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nОсновной перенос корректен. Достаточные условия для исключения ветви\n\\(v_3(A_i)\\ge S_i\\) (с включением края \\(A_i=0\\)) таковы:\n\n\\[\n |k_i|2^{-i}\\longrightarrow0,\n \\qquad (c_i+|k_i|)2^{-S_i}\\longrightarrow0, \\tag{F}\n\\]\n\nгде, как обычно для константы бинарного слова, \\(c_i\\ge0\\). Если\nконвенция допускает знаковое \\(c_i\\), всюду следует заменить \\(c_i\\) на\n\\(|c_i|\\). Для второй ветви достаточно\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i:=\n \\frac{c_i(1+3|\\xi|)}{|k_i|\\,|m_i|}\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{G}\n\\]\n\nПосле этого из \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\) и\n\\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\) действительно следуют (F), (G), а также\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\\le 6\\,3^{q_i},\n \\qquad\n \\log\\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}=o(N_i),\n\\]\n\nпоскольку одновременно получается \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\). Более точные условия\nна \\(k_i,t_i,\\rho_i\\) сами по себе не обязаны давать \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\);\nтогда \\(o(N_i)\\) нужно требовать непосредственно через эти точные\nусловия (или добавить \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) для грубой оценки \\(6\\,3^{q_i}\\)).\n\n## 1. Пределы индексов и базовые края\n\nИз \\(q_i\\ge1\\) и \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\) следует \\(S_i\\to\\infty\\). Так как\n\\(S_i\\le i\\), имеем \\(i\\to\\infty\\) и\n\n\\[\n \\frac{q_i}{i}\\le \\frac{q_i}{S_i}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nПри \\(N_i\\ge i+q_i\\) это даёт \\(N_i\\to\\infty\\) и\n\\(q_i=o(N_i)\\). Это последнее следствие использует именно исходное\nусловие \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\); из одного \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) обратного вывода нет.\n\nУсловие \\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\) понимается на хвосте, где \\(m_i\\ne0\\) и\n\\(|m_i|>1\\), и даёт \\(|m_i|\\to\\infty\\). Независимо от этого, для\nфиксированного рационального \\(\\xi=a/b\\) с нечётным знаменателем\nнепериодичность кода также даёт \\(|m_i|\\to\\infty\\): состояния с\nфиксированным знаменателем, лежащие в ограниченном интервале, образуют\nконечное множество; повтор состояния при детерминированном \\(T\\)\nсделал бы последующий код периодическим. Это не даёт, однако,\nкакой-либо скорости роста относительно произвольного \\(q_i\\).\n\nПосле достаточно большого индекса \\(S_i\\ge1\\). Поэтому край \\(S_i=0\\)\nне входит в асимптотический аргумент. Если нулевые префиксы имели бы\nнеограниченную длину, сам код был бы \\(0^\\infty\\), что исключено.\n\nКрай \\(c_i=0\\) безвреден: тогда \\(\\rho_i=0\\), и формула ниже сразу\nдаёт точную нижнюю оценку без добавочного члена. Возможность\n\\(3\\mid k_i\\) также безвредна, в том числе при \\(R_i=0\\) и чётном\n\\(q_i\\): в делимости используется только модуль \\(2^i\\), а на \\(k_i\\)\nнигде не делят по модулю \\(3\\). Знак \\(k_i\\) не меняется и не\nпредполагается положительным; в оценках используется \\(|k_i|\\).\n\n## 2. Ветвь \\(A_i=0\\) и \\(v_3(A_i)\\ge S_i\\)\n\nЭти два края можно и следует рассматривать одной ветвью, не приписывая\nнулю конечную валюацию. Пусть\n\n\\[\n E_i:=\\{A_i=0\\}\\,\\cup\\,\\{A_i\\ne0,\\ v_3(A_i)\\ge S_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nНа \\(E_i\\) положим \\(z_i=A_i/3^{S_i}\\). Это целое: при \\(A_i=0\\)\nпросто \\(z_i=0\\), а в ненулевой части это следует из условия на\nвалюацию. При этом нигде не используется \\(v_3(0)\\).\n\nАффинная формула\n\n\\[\n 2^i m_i=3^{S_i}\\xi+C_i\n\\]\n\nпосле умножения на \\(b\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n a\\equiv-b3^{-S_i}C_i\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nИз \\(3^{S_i}z_i=A_i=2^ic_i-k_iC_i\\) следует\n\n\\[\n 3^{S_i}z_i\\equiv-k_iC_i\\pmod {2^i}.\n\\]\n\nСравнение этих двух соотношений после умножения на \\(k_i\\) и на \\(b\\),\nсоответственно, и сокращение обратимого множителя \\(3^{S_i}\\) дают\n\n\\[\n 2^i\\mid k_i a-bz_i. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nСтандартная граница \\(0\\le C_i\\le2^{i-S_i}3^{S_i}\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n |z_i|\n \\le c_i2^{i-S_i}+|k_i|2^{i-S_i}\n =(c_i+|k_i|)2^{i-S_i}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nПоэтому\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|k_i a-bz_i|}{2^i}\n \\le |a|\\,|k_i|2^{-i}\n       +b(c_i+|k_i|)2^{-S_i}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nПри (F) правая часть стремится к нулю. Левая часть в силу (1) есть\nнеотрицательное целое (это именно нормированный модуль целого,\nкратного \\(2^i\\)). Значит, для всех достаточно больших индексов\n\n\\[\n k_i a-bz_i=0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(A_i\\ne0\\), это даёт\n\n\\[\n \\xi=\\frac ab=\\frac{z_i}{k_i}\n      =\\frac{A_i}{3^{S_i}k_i}=:r_i.\n\\]\n\nПо построению \\(r_i\\) имеет код \\(U_iW_i^\\infty\\), поэтому код \\(\\xi\\)\nв конце концов периодичен — противоречие.\n\nЕсли \\(A_i=0\\), то \\(z_i=0\\), и (4) даёт \\(k_i a=0\\). Так как\n\\(k_i\\ne0\\), это означает \\(a=0\\), то есть \\(\\xi=0\\), чей код\n\\(0^\\infty\\) периодичен. Это та же контрадикция, причём без всякого\nобозначения \\(v_3(0)\\). Следовательно, на хвосте\n\n\\[\n A_i\\ne0,\\qquad v_3(A_i)\u003cS_i. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nЗамечание: при \\(c_i\\ge0\\), \\(S_i\\le i\\), второе условие (F) уже\nвлечёт первое, поскольку\n\n\\[\n |k_i|2^{-i}\\le |k_i|2^{-S_i}\n \\le(c_i+|k_i|)2^{-S_i}.\n\\]\n\nОставлять оба предела полезно, поскольку именно оба слагаемых видны в\nоценке (3), но это не два независимых требования в стандартной\nнеотрицательной конвенции для \\(c_i\\).\n\n## 3. Ветвь \\(v_3(A_i)\u003cS_i\\): точная нижняя оценка\n\nНа хвосте (5) положим\n\n\\[\n t_i=\\frac{C_i}{3^{S_i}}.\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(S_i\\ge1\\), в префиксе есть единица, и стандартная оценка\nдаёт \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\). Непериодичность исключает \\(m_i=0\\), поскольку\nпосле нулевого состояния код равен \\(0^\\infty\\). Из аффинной формулы\n\n\\[\n 2^im_i=3^{S_i}(\\xi+t_i)\n\\]\n\nполучается точное тождество\n\n\\[\n \\frac{A_i}{3^{S_i}}\n =-k_it_i+c_i\\frac{\\xi+t_i}{m_i}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nПоскольку \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\),\n\n\\[\n |\\xi+t_i|\\le|\\xi|+t_i\n \\le(1+3|\\xi|)t_i. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nС определением \\(\\rho_i\\) из вердикта треугольное неравенство даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A_i|}{3^{S_i}}\n \\ge |k_i|t_i(1-\\rho_i). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nПри (G) на хвосте \\(\\rho_i\\le1/2\\), и тогда\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|A_i|}{3^{S_i}}\n \\ge\\frac{|k_i|t_i}{2}\n \\ge\\frac{|k_i|}{6}>0. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nЭто одновременно исключает \\(A_i=0\\) в нижней ветви и обеспечивает\nположительный знаменатель для последующей оценки.\n\nПодстановка (5), (8) в точную формулу для \\(h_i/A_i^\\#\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n \\le\\frac1{1-\\rho_i}\n \\max\\left(1,\n     \\frac{2^{q_i}}{|k_i|t_i},\n     \\frac{3^{R_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(\\rho_i\\le1/2\\), \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\),\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n \\le\\max\\left(1,\n     \\frac{6\\,2^{q_i}}{|k_i|},\n     \\frac{6\\,3^{R_i}}{|k_i|}\\right)\n \\le 6\\,3^{q_i}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nВсе эти шаги используют абсолютные значения; знак \\(k_i\\), знак\n\\(m_i\\) и возможный знак \\(A_i\\) не меняют доказательство.\n\n## 4. Проверка исходных условий \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\) и\n\\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\)\n\nДля бинарного слова длины \\(q_i\\) имеются грубые оценки\n\n\\[\n c_i\\le3^{q_i},\\qquad |k_i|\\le3^{q_i},\\qquad |k_i|\\ge1.\n\\]\n\nИз \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\) и \\(S_i\\le i\\) следует\n\n\\[\n 3^{q_i}2^{-i}\\to0,\n \\qquad 3^{q_i}2^{-S_i}\\to0.\n\\]\n\nОни дают оба условия (F) (причём первое, как отмечено выше, уже\nследует из второго при \\(c_i\\ge0\\)). Далее\n\n\\[\n \\rho_i\n \\le(1+3|\\xi|)\\frac{3^{q_i}}{|m_i|}\\to0,\n\\]\n\nпоскольку \\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\). Значит, (G) также выполнено. Важен\nименно второй исходный предел: рост \\(S_i\\) не контролирует дефект,\nкоторый содержит \\(m_i\\).\n\n## 5. Логарифмическая высотная маржа\n\nГрубая оценка (11) даёт\n\n\\[\n 0\\le\\log\\frac{h_i}{A_i^\\#}\n \\le\\log6+q_i\\log3.\n\\]\n\nДля вывода \\(o(N_i)\\) из этой грубой формы нужно отдельно иметь\n\n\\[\n q_i=o(N_i). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nВ исходном простом следствии (12) автоматически следует из\n\\(q_i=o(S_i)\\), \\(S_i\\le i\\), \\(N_i\\ge i+q_i\\). Поэтому там вывод\n\\(\\log(h_i/A_i^\\#)=o(N_i)\\) верен.\n\nДля более точного варианта достаточно, после (G), потребовать\n\n\\[\n \\left(\\log\\frac{2^{q_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right)_+=o(N_i),\n \\quad\n \\left(\\log\\frac{3^{R_i}}{|k_i|t_i}\\right)_+=o(N_i),\n \\quad\n -\\log(1-\\rho_i)=o(N_i). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nТогда логарифм правой части (10) есть \\(o(N_i)\\). Более грубые\nусловия\n\n\\[\n (q_i\\log2-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i),\\qquad\n (R_i\\log3-\\log|k_i|)_+=o(N_i) \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nвместе с (G) влекут (13), потому что \\(t_i\\ge1/3\\), а\n\\(-\\log(1-\\rho_i)=o(1)\\). Но (13) или (14) не обязаны сами по себе\nвлечь \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\): большие \\(q_i\\) могут компенсироваться большими\n\\(|k_i|\\) в этих отношениях. Поэтому нельзя автоматически применять\nгрубую правую часть \\(\\log6+q_i\\log3\\) в точном режиме без (12).\n\n## 6. Что действительно следует из одного \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\)\n\nОдно условие \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) не даёт \\(q_i=o(S_i)\\), а потому из него\nнельзя вывести (F) посредством имеющейся оценки. Оно также не связывает\n\\(q_i\\) с \\(|m_i|\\) и потому не даёт (G) посредством имеющейся оценки.\nНапример, на уровне независимых числовых параметров можно взять\n\\(q_j=j\\), \\(N_j\\asymp j^2\\), \\(S_j\\asymp\\sqrt j\\), что сохраняет\n\\(q_j=o(N_j)\\), но не даёт затухания\n\\(3^{q_j}2^{-S_j}\\). Аналогично можно формально выбирать рост\n\\(|m_j|\\), слишком медленный по сравнению с \\(3^{q_j}\\), чтобы\nотношение в (G) не стремилось к нулю.\n\nЭто лишь показывает, что из одного \\(q=o(N)\\) нужные шаги не следуют\nиз данных оценок. Такие формальные наборы не являются совместимыми\nсловами или настоящими рациональными орбитами и не доказывают\nсуществование непериодического рационального контрпримера. Вопрос о\nсуществовании такого реального контрпримера здесь не решается и не\nдолжен формулироваться как установленный факт.\n\nИтак, после исключения общей ветви \\(A_i=0\\) или \\(v_i\\ge S_i\\) и\nполучения (G), сравнение высот корректно. Простая гипотеза\n\\(q_i=o(S_i)\\), \\(q_i=o(\\log|m_i|)\\) обеспечивает все нужные пределы,\nвключая (12); точный вариант требует явно сохранить (13) (либо\nотдельно добавить \\(q_i=o(N_i)\\) при использовании грубой оценки).\n","task-0213.md":"# Независимый ручной аудит количественной леммы 0213\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена только ручной алгеброй по формулировке\n`work/collatz-period-length-candidate-0213.md`; программ, CAS, тестов,\nперебора и дочерних агентов не использовалось.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nЛемма верна в заявленной условной области. Для фиксированного\n\\(\\xi=a/b\\in\\mathbb Z_2\\), где \\(b>0\\) нечётно и код \\(\\xi\\) не является\neventually periodic, для любого настоящего префикса \\(U\\) длины \\(i\\) с\n\\(S\\) единицами и любого непустого бинарного слова \\(W\\) длины \\(q\\ge1\\),\nесли\n\\[\nA=2^iC_W-(2^q-3^R)C_U,\n\\qquad 3^S\\mid A,\n\\]\nто действительно\n\\[\n2^S\\le (|a|+b)3^q.\n\\]\nКонстанта \\(|a|+b\\) достаточна; она может зависеть от выбранного\nпредставления \\(a/b\\), но при любом таком представлении с указанным\nположительным нечётным \\(b\\) доказательство работает. Сокращённое\nпредставление даёт естественную более резкую нормировку.\n\n## Аффинная формула и ненулевость\n\nИспользуется стандартная поправка\n\\[\nC_V=\\sum_{j=0}^{|V|-1}v_j2^j3^{\\#\\{\\text{единиц правее }j\\}},\n\\]\nдля которой\n\\[\nT_V(x)=\\frac{3^{\\#1(V)}x+C_V}{2^{|V|}},\n\\qquad\nC_{UV}=3^{\\#1(V)}C_U+2^{|U|}C_V.\n\\]\nПоложим \\(c=C_W\\), \\(R=\\#1(W)\\), \\(k=2^q-3^R\\). Поскольку \\(q\\ge1\\),\n\\(k\\) нечётно и ненулево: равенство двух степеней \\(2\\) и \\(3\\) возможно\nтолько при \\(q=R=0\\), что здесь исключено.\n\nПовторение \\(W\\) имеет в \\(\\mathbb Z_2\\) фиксированную точку\n\\[\ny=\\Phi_2(W^\\infty)=\\frac c k,\n\\]\nпоскольку \\(k y=c\\). Обратная ветвь с префиксом \\(U\\) даёт конкретное\nпродолжение\n\\[\n\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)\n =\\frac{2^iy-C_U}{3^S}\n =\\frac{2^ic-kC_U}{3^Sk}\n =\\frac A{3^Sk}.\n\\]\nЕсли \\(3^S\\mid A\\), то при \\(z=A/3^S\\in\\mathbb Z\\) это равно \\(z/k\\).\nЗдесь не требуется, чтобы \\(W\\) было фактическим словом после \\(U\\): это\nлишь произвольное периодическое продолжение, а формула определена в\n\\(\\mathbb Z_2\\), так как \\(3^Sk\\) нечётно.\n\nИз того, что \\(U\\) является фактическим префиксом кода \\(\\xi=a/b\\),\nследует\n\\[\n2^i\\mid 3^Sa+bC_U.\n\\]\nУмножая на \\(k\\) и используя \\(A=3^Sz\\), получаем\n\\[\n3^S(ka-bz)=k(3^Sa+bC_U)-b2^ic,\n\\]\nа значит, поскольку \\(3^S\\) нечётно,\n\\[\n2^i\\mid ka-bz.\n\\]\n\nКлючевое исключение нуля корректно именно при условии непериодичности.\nЕсли бы \\(ka-bz=0\\), то \\(\\xi=z/k=\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)\\). Единственность\n2-адического паритетного кода (два различных кода уже на первом отличии\nдают разные классы по соответствующей степени двойки) тогда означала бы,\nчто код \\(\\xi\\) равен \\(UW^\\infty\\), то есть имеет конечный предпериод и\nпериод \\(W\\). Это противоречит данной непериодичности. Поэтому\n\\(ka-bz\\) — ненулевое кратное \\(2^i\\), и\n\\[\n1\\le\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}.\n\\]\n\nЭто также покрывает случай \\(A=0\\): тогда \\(z=0\\), и ненулевость равна\nненулевости \\(ka\\). Поскольку \\(k\\ne0\\), нуль мог бы возникнуть только\nпри \\(a=0\\), но \\(\\xi=0\\) имеет периодический (нулевой) код и исключён\nгипотезой. Поэтому условие \\(A=0\\) не требует исключения из самой леммы;\nоно лишь не является случаем, где надо говорить о каком-либо\n«ненулевом A-сертификате».\n\n## Точная оценка поправки префикса\n\nДля слова длины \\(i\\) с \\(S\\) единицами верно\n\\[\n0\\le C_U\\le 2^{i-S}(3^S-2^S). \\tag{1}\n\\]\nЭто следует прямой индукцией по добавлению последнего символа. При\nдобавлении нуля \\(C_{V0}=C_V\\), а правая часть (1) увеличивается вдвое;\nпри добавлении единицы \\(C_{V1}=3C_V+2^{|V|}\\), и подстановка\nиндукционной границы даёт ровно правую часть с параметрами \\((i+1,S+1)\\),\nесли i — длина слова до добавления символа.\nЭкстремальное слово имеет все нули перед всеми единицами (с точностью до\nвырожденных случаев), что также подтверждает точность коэффициента.\n\nПри \\(S=0\\) слово \\(U=0^i\\), \\(C_U=0\\), а правая часть (1) равна нулю;\nтак что край включён буквально, без деления на ноль или скрытого\nстрогого неравенства.\n\n## Количественная цепочка и знаки\n\nИз определения \\(z\\), (1) и треугольного неравенства следует\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n&\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+b\\frac{|z|}{2^i}\\\\\n&\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+bc3^{-S}\n   +b|k|\\frac{C_U}{3^S2^i}\\\\\n&\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+bc3^{-S}\n   +b|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nПоследний член неотрицателен для всех \\(S\\ge0\\), включая нулевой случай,\nгде он равен нулю. Никакого предположения о знаке \\(a\\), \\(k\\), \\(A\\) или\n\\(z\\) здесь нет: все они заключены в абсолютные значения. В частности,\nпри \\(R\\ge1\\) число \\(k\\) может быть отрицательным; это только означает,\nчто периодическая 2-адическая фиксированная точка \\(c/k\\) может быть\nотрицательной, и на оценку не влияет.\n\nТак как \\(S\\le i\\), \\(c=C_W\\ge0\\), а для любого слова длины \\(q\\)\n\\[\nc\\le 3^q,\n\\qquad |k|=|2^q-3^R|\\le3^q,\n\\]\nполучаем точную компенсацию двух членов с \\(3^{-S}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n&\\le |a|3^q2^{-S}+b3^q3^{-S}\n +b3^q(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\\\\\n&=(|a|+b)3^q2^{-S}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nВместе с нижней границей это даёт требуемое\n\\[\n2^S\\le(|a|+b)3^q.\n\\]\nОтсюда также корректно следует\n\\[\nq\\ge\\frac{\\log 2}{\\log 3}S-\n\\frac{\\log(|a|+b)}{\\log3}.\n\\]\n\nКрай \\(3\\mid k\\) не создаёт исключения. При \\(R\\ge1\\) фактически\n\\(k\\equiv2^q\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3\\); при \\(R=0\\) делимость возможна\nдля некоторых чётных \\(q\\), но ни один шаг доказательства не сокращает\nна \\(k\\) по модулю \\(3\\). Нужно лишь \\(k\\ne0\\), что уже обеспечено\n\\(q\\ge1\\). Край \\(S=0\\) разобран выше; край \\(A=0\\) также безопасен.\n\n## Область утверждения\n\nНепериодичность кода \\(\\xi\\) — самостоятельная гипотеза, необходимая\nтолько для исключения равенства \\(ka=bz\\). Это не вывод из рациональности\n\\(\\xi=a/b\\). Общее утверждение «рациональный 2-адический источник имеет\nпериодический или eventually periodic код» здесь не используется и остаётся\nотдельным нерешённым вопросом в общем коллатцевском контексте. Если код\nпериодичен, равенство \\(ka-bz=0\\) уже нельзя исключить: для совпадающего\nпериодического продолжения оно возникает всякий раз, когда одновременно\nвыполнено рассматриваемое условие \\(3^S\\mid A\\) (так что \\(z\\in\\mathbb Z\\));\nэквивалентно, в этой ситуации периодический источник имеет вид \\(z/k\\).\nПоэтому без непериодичности нижняя граница на ненулевое кратное \\(2^i\\)\nне имеет общего основания, хотя для конкретной периодической дроби\nделимость \\(3^S\\mid A\\) может, конечно, не выполниться.\n\nИтак, расширение квантора на произвольное слово \\(W\\), не обязанное идти\nпосле \\(U\\) в исходном коде, корректно: используется только фактическая\nпрефиксная делимость по \\(U\\), а \\(W\\) входит в независимо построенное\nпродолжение \\(UW^\\infty\\). Результат является необходимой границей одной\nарифметической ветви; он не утверждает существование повторов, положительную\nмаржу или решение гипотезы Коллатца и не объявляется новым мировым\nрезультатом.\n","task-0215.md":"# Task 0215 — новая граница через число единиц \\(R\\) при \\(k\u003c0\\)\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена ручной алгеброй по\ntask-0213.md и кандидату work/collatz-period-length-candidate-0213.md;\nпрограмм, CAS, тестов, перебора и дочерних агентов не использовалось.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nРасширение корректно. Пусть \\(\\xi=a/b\\in\\mathbf Z _2\\), где \\(b>0\\)\nнечётно, \\(U\\) — настоящий префикс кода длины \\(i\\) с \\(S\\) единицами,\nа \\(W\\) — непустое бинарное слово длины \\(q\\) с \\(R\\) единицами. Положим\n\n\\[\n c=C_W,\\qquad k=2^q-3^R,\\qquad A=2^ic-kC_U.\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(3^S\\mid A\\), и код \\(\\xi\\) не является\neventually periodic, то\n\n\\[\n                    2^S\\le (|a|+2b)3^R. \\tag{V}\n\\]\n\nТу же границу можно получить без непериодичности, если \\(\\xi\\ge0\\) в\nобычном рациональном порядке: в ветви \\(k\u003c0\\) равенство, которое могло\nбы разрушить нижнюю оценку, имело бы вид \\(\\xi=z/k\u003c0\\). Для отрицательной\n\\(\\xi\\) непериодичность остаётся необходимой в общем доказательстве.\n\n## 1. Знаки и точная модульная нижняя граница\n\nТак как \\(q\\ge1\\) и \\(k\u003c0\\), имеем \\(3^R>2^q\\), следовательно,\n\\(R\\ge1\\). Поэтому \\(c=C_W>0\\). Кроме того, \\(C_U\\ge0\\), и\n\n\\[\n A=2^ic+|k|C_U>0. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nВ частности, при \\(3^S\\mid A\\) число\n\\(z=A/3^S\\) является положительным целым. Это сразу исключает край\n\\(A=0\\), в том числе при \\(S=0\\).\n\nФактический префикс даёт в \\(\\mathbf Z/2^i\\mathbf Z\\)\n\n\\[\n 3^Sa+bC_U\\equiv0\\pmod {2^i}, \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nа из \\(3^Sz=A=2^ic-kC_U\\) следует\n\n\\[\n 3^Sz+kC_U\\equiv0\\pmod {2^i}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nУмножение (2) на \\(k\\), (3) на \\(b\\), вычитание и сокращение\nобратимого по модулю \\(2^i\\) множителя \\(3^S\\) дают\n\n\\[\n                    2^i\\mid ka-bz. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(ka-bz=0\\), то \\(\\xi=z/k\\). Периодическое продолжение с кодом\n\\(UW^\\infty\\) имеет именно значение \\(z/k\\): фиксированная точка блока\n\\(W\\) равна \\(c/k\\), а после префикса \\(U\\) получается\n\\[\n \\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)=\\frac{A}{3^Sk}=\\frac zk.\n\\]\nПоэтому при непериодическом коде \\(\\xi\\) равенство невозможно. Если\n\\(\\xi\\ge0\\), оно также невозможно по знакам: \\(z>0\\), \\(k\u003c0\\), так что\n\\(z/k\u003c0\\). В этих случаях \\(ka-bz\\) — ненулевое кратное \\(2^i\\), и\n\n\\[\n                    1\\le\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nИменно здесь используется непериодичность; остальные оценки знака\n\\(\\xi\\) не требуют. Для отрицательной периодической \\(\\xi\\) равенство\nможет возникнуть, так что без одного из этих двух исключений (5) общего\nоснования не имеет.\n\nЭто не только формальная возможность. Для \\(\\xi=-1\\), \\(U=1^i\\) и\n\\(W=1^q\\) имеем \\(S=i\\), \\(R=q\\), \\(k=2^q-3^q\u003c0\\),\n\\(C_U=3^i-2^i\\), \\(c=3^q-2^q\\), и\n\\[\n A=(3^q-2^q)3^i,\\qquad z=3^q-2^q=|k|.\n\\]\nПоэтому \\(3^S\\mid A\\), но \\(ka-bz=0\\). Значит, для отрицательного\nпериодического источника замена непериодичности одним лишь условием\n\\(k\u003c0\\) действительно была бы ошибкой.\n\n## 2. Оценка \\(C_U\\), \\(C_W\\) и выражение для \\(E\\)\n\nДля слова длины \\(n\\) с \\(s\\) единицами стандартная индукция по\nпоследнему символу даёт\n\n\\[\n 0\\le C_V\\le2^{n-s}(3^s-2^s). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nИменно, при дописывании справа нуля и единицы соответственно\n\\(C_{V0}=C_V\\) и \\(C_{V1}=3C_V+2^{|V|}\\); подстановка этих двух\nрекуррентностей в (6) даёт индукционный переход.\n\nПри \\(s=0\\) это точное равенство \\(C_V=0\\), поэтому край не требует\nделения на \\(s\\). Применение (6) к \\(U\\) и раскрытие\n\\(z=(2^ic-kC_U)/3^S\\) дают\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n &\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+b\\frac{|z|}{2^i}\\\\\n &\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+bc3^{-S}\n       +b|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S}). \\tag{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nПоследний член неотрицателен при всех \\(S\\ge0\\). Для \\(W\\) с длиной\n\\(q\\) и \\(R\\) единиц та же точная граница имеет вид\n\n\\[\n             c\\le2^{q-R}(3^R-2^R). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nОна применима и здесь, поскольку \\(R\\ge1\\). Также \\(k\u003c0\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n                  |k|=3^R-2^q\u003c3^R. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n## 3. Случай \\(R\\le\\theta S\\)\n\nПоложим \\(\\theta=\\log2/\\log3\\). Из \\(k\u003c0\\) следует\n\n\\[\n q\u003cR/\\theta. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(R\\le\\theta S\\), то\n\n\\[\n q-R\u003c(1/\\theta-1)R\\le(1-\\theta)S. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nВ этом случае \\(S>0\\): при \\(S=0\\) условие \\(R\\le\\theta S\\) означало бы\n\\(R=0\\), несовместимое с \\(k\u003c0\\). Из (11) и\n\n\\[\n \\log3-(2-\\theta)\\log2\n   =\\frac{(\\log3-\\log2)^2}{\\log3}>0 \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nполучаем\n\n\\[\n (q-R+S)\\log2-S\\log3\n \u003c\\big((2-\\theta)\\log2-\\log3\\big)S\\le0. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nС использованием (8) это даёт\n\n\\[\n c3^{-S}\\le 3^R2^{-S}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nДействительно, после замены \\(3^R-2^R\\) на \\(3^R\\) неравенство (14)\nэквивалентно \\(2^{q-R+S}\\le3^S\\), а это следует из (13); фактически\nна этом непустом случае знак получается строгим.\n\nТак как \\(i\\ge S\\), (7), (9), (14) и\n\\(2^{-S}-3^{-S}\\ge0\\) дают\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n &\\le |a|3^R2^{-S}+b3^R2^{-S}\n       +b3^R(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\\\\\n &\\le (|a|+2b)3^R2^{-S}. \\tag{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nВместе с (5) это даёт (V).\n\n## 4. Случай \\(R>\\theta S\\)\n\nЗдесь непосредственно\n\n\\[\n R\\log3>\\theta S\\log3=S\\log2,\\qquad\\text{то есть}\\qquad 3^R>2^S.\n\\]\n\nПоскольку \\(b\\ge1\\), \\(|a|+2b\\ge2\\), отсюда сразу следует (V), без\nиспользования делимости или непериодичности. Таким образом, оба случая\n\\(R\\le\\theta S\\) и \\(R>\\theta S\\) покрывают все \\(S\\ge0\\), включая\nвырожденный край \\(S=0\\) (который попадает только во второй случай).\n\n## 5. Асимптотическое следствие и \\(\\delta\\)-разрыв\n\nИз (V), при фиксированных \\(a,b\\), следует\n\n\\[\n \\frac RS\\ge\\theta-\\frac{\\log(|a|+2b)}{S\\log3}\\qquad(S>0). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, если вдоль рассматриваемой последовательности\n\\(S\\to\\infty\\) и \\(\\limsup R/S\u003c\\theta\\), ветвь\n\\(k\u003c0,\\ 3^S\\mid A\\) невозможна на хвосте. В частности, это может\nисключить высокую \\(3\\)-адическую валюацию даже там, где старая оценка\nчерез \\(q/S\\) не исключает её (например, \\(q/S\\ge\\theta\\)). Для\nне-eventually-periodic бинарного кода \\(S\\to\\infty\\) автоматически:\nиначе код имел бы хвост \\(0^\\infty\\).\n\nДля связи с высотной оценкой предположим дополнительно, что на хвосте\n\n\\[\n                  3^R\\ge(1+\\delta)2^q,\\qquad\\delta>0. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nТогда\n\n\\[\n |k|=3^R-2^q\\ge\\delta2^q. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(S\\ge1\\), то \\(C_U/3^S\\ge1/3\\): вклад первой единицы в \\(C_U\\)\nравен как минимум \\(3^{S-1}\\) (с дополнительным множителем \\(2^j\\ge1\\)).\nИз (1) получается\n\n\\[\n \\frac A{3^S}\\ge\\frac{|k|}{3}. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nПосле исключения ветви \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\), формула для высоты даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{3\\,2^q}{|k|},\\frac{3\\,3^R}{|k|}\\right).\n\\]\n\nИз (17)--(18) следует\n\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\\le \\frac{3(1+\\delta)}{\\delta}, \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nна этом хвосте. Это ограничение — только арифметическое следствие для\nуже выбранной совместимой ветви; оно не объявляет новые общие орбиты,\nповторы или существование нужной последовательности приближений.\n","task-0216.md":"# task-0216: ручная проверка усиления через число единиц периода\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена вручную по\nINTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md: без программ, CAS, тестов, перебора и\nдочерних агентов. Здесь проверяется только необходимая оценка для уже\nвыбранных \\(U,W\\); существование соответствующей реальной орбиты и новое\nпокрытие кодов не утверждаются.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nПусть \\(\\xi=a/b\\in\\mathbb Z _2\\), \\(b>0\\) нечётно, код \\(\\xi\\) не является\nв конечном счёте периодическим, \\(U\\) — настоящий префикс длины \\(i\\) с\n\\(S\\) единицами, а \\(W\\) — непустое слово длины \\(q\\) с \\(R\\) единицами.\nПоложим\n\n\\[\nc=C_W,\\qquad k=2^q-3^R\u003c0,\\qquad A=2^ic-kC_U.\n\\]\n\nТогда из \\(3^S\\mid A\\) следует, в частности,\n\n\\[\n 2^S\\le (|a|+2b)3^R.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nБолее того, в той же области треугольную оценку можно аккуратно собрать и\nполучить более сильную константу\n\n\\[\n 2^S\\le (|a|+b)3^R. \n \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nПоследнее усиление не требуется для следствия, но показывает, что множитель\n\\(2b\\) не является необходимым завышением: два последних слагаемых в\nоценке \\(E\\) совместно дают один множитель \\(b\\).\n\n## Ненулевость и знаки\n\nТак как \\(k\u003c0\\), имеем \\(R\\ge1\\), а потому \\(c=C_W>0\\). Следовательно,\n\n\\[\nA=2^ic+|k|C_U>0. \n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nЭто верно также при \\(S=0\\), когда \\(C_U=0\\). Таким образом, случай\n\\(A=0\\) на рассматриваемой ветви невозможен и должен быть явно отделён от\nобщей леммы, в которой знак \\(k\\) не фиксирован.\n\nЕсли \\(3^S\\mid A\\), положим \\(z=A/3^S>0\\). Сравнение префиксной\nконгруэнции с \\(A=3^Sz\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n 2^i\\mid ka-bz. \n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nЕсли бы \\(ka-bz=0\\), то\n\n\\[\n \\xi=z/k=A/(3^Sk)\u003c0\n\\]\n\nсовпадала бы с 2-адическим значением периодического продолжения\n\\(UW^\\infty\\). Для \\(\\xi>0\\) такое равенство уже исключается знаком\n\\(z/k\u003c0\\), то есть здесь непериодичность не нужна. Для \\(\\xi\u003c0\\) знак не\nисключает равенство, и нужно исходное предположение о непериодичности кода:\nравенство означало бы в конечном счёте периодический код. При \\(\\xi=0\\)\nравенство также невозможно, поскольку \\(z>0\\) и \\(k\u003c0\\). Поэтому во всех\nтрёх случаях\n\n\\[\n 1\\le E:=\\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}. \n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n## Оценка поправки \\(C_W\\)\n\nЕсли позиции единиц \\(W\\) равны \\(t_0\u003c\\cdots\u003ct_{R-1}\\), то стандартная\nформула для аффинной поправки даёт\n\n\\[\nC_W=\\sum_{j=0}^{R-1}2^{t_j}3^{R-j-1}.\n\\]\n\nПри фиксированных \\(q,R\\) сумма максимальна при\n\\(t_j=q-R+j\\). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n c\\le 2^{q-R}\\sum_{j=0}^{R-1}2^j3^{R-j-1}\n   =2^{q-R}(3^R-2^R). \n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nЭто и есть нужная оценка с учётом числа единиц, вместо грубой \\(c\u003c3^q\\).\n\nПоложим \\(\\theta=\\log 2/\\log 3\\). Из \\(k\u003c0\\) следует\n\n\\[\n q\u003cR/\\theta. \n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(R\\le\\theta S\\), то \\(q\u003cR/\\theta\\le S\\), и, более того,\n\\(S>R\\), поскольку \\(0\u003c\\theta\u003c1\\) и \\(R\\ge1\\). Из (6) следует\n\n\\[\n c3^{-S}\\le 3^R2^{-S}, \n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nпоскольку достаточно проверить\n\n\\[\n (q-R+S)\\log2-S\\log3\\le0.\n\\]\n\nДействительно, (7) и \\(R\\le\\theta S\\) дают\n\n\\[\n q-R+S\u003c(2-\\theta)S,\n\\]\n\nа\n\n\\[\n ((2-\\theta)\\log2-\\log3)S\n =-\\frac{(\\log3-\\log2)^2}{\\log3}S\u003c0. \n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nТем самым заявленная в вопросе цепь корректна. Если же \\(R>\\theta S\\),\nто \\(3^R>2^S\\), и (1), а также (2), следуют тривиально, поскольку\n\\(|a|+b\\ge1\\). При \\(S=0\\) автоматически \\(R>\\theta S\\), так что этот\nкрай тоже закрыт отдельно; в частности, деление на \\(S\\) нигде не нужно.\n\n## Нижняя граница и оценка \\(E\\)\n\nТочная оценка префикса из исходной леммы:\n\n\\[\n 0\\le C_U\\le2^{i-S}(3^S-2^S). \n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nИз (5), треугольного неравенства и (10) получается\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n E\\le |a||k|2^{-i}+bc3^{-S}\n       +b|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S}). \n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nВ случае \\(R\\le\\theta S\\), первый член удовлетворяет\n\n\\[\n |a||k|2^{-i}\\le |a|3^R2^{-S}, \n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nтак как \\(i\\ge S\\) и \\(|k|=3^R-2^q\u003c3^R\\). Раздельное применение (8) и\n\\(|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\u003c3^R2^{-S}\\) даёт\n\n\\[\n E\u003c(|a|+2b)3^R2^{-S},\n\\]\n\nчто вместе с (5) противоречит \\(2^S>(|a|+2b)3^R\\), и доказывает (1).\n\nМожно не терять один из двух одинаковых множителей \\(b\\). Обозначим\n\n\\[\nm=2^{q-R}\\ge1,\\qquad d=(2/3)^R,\\qquad x=(2/3)^S.\n\\]\n\nТогда (6) и \\(k\u003c0\\) дают\n\n\\[\nc\\le m3^R(1-d),\\qquad |k|=3^R(1-md),\n\\]\n\nи \\(x\u003cd\\), так как \\(S>R\\). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nc3^{-S}+|k|(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n &=2^{-S}\\{xc+(1-x)|k|\\}\\\\\n &\\le 2^{-S}3^R\\{1+x(m-1)-md\\}\\\\\n &\\le 3^R2^{-S},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nпоследнее неравенство следует из \\(x(m-1)\\le md\\) (при \\(m=1\\) оно\nочевидно, при \\(m>1\\) достаточно \\(x\u003cd\\)). С (12) это даёт\n\n\\[\n E\\le(|a|+b)3^R2^{-S}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nВместе с \\(E\\ge1\\) получаем усиление (2).\n\n## Следствие для высокой 3-адической валюации\n\nПусть имеется последовательность таких ветвей с \\(S\\to\\infty\\) и\n\n\\[\n\\limsup R/S\u003c\\theta.\n\\]\n\nТогда существует \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), для которого на хвосте\n\\(R\\le(\\theta-\\varepsilon)S\\). При фиксированном \\(\\xi\\) число\n\\(C_0=|a|+b\\) постоянно, а потому\n\n\\[\n C_0 3^R/2^S\\le C_0\\,3^{-\\varepsilon S}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nНеобходимое условие (2) нарушается на хвосте. Следовательно, ветвь\n\n\\[\n v_3(A)\\ge S\n\\]\n\nисключается; это и есть требуемое снятие высокой валюации. Условие\n\\(S\\to\\infty\\) здесь существенно для такого предельного вывода.\n\nПосле исключения этой ветви, при \\(S\\ge1\\), первая единица \\(U\\) даёт\n\\(C_U/3^S\\ge1/3\\), так что из (3)\n\n\\[\n A/3^S\\ge |k|/3.\n\\]\n\nС принятой точной формулой для отношения \\(h/A^\\#\\) это даёт\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n \\le3\\frac{\\max(2^q,3^R)}{|k|}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nЕсли одновременно\n\n\\[\n 3^R\\ge(1+\\delta)2^q,\\qquad \\delta>0,\n\\]\n\nто \\(|k|=3^R-2^q\\ge\\delta2^q\\), и\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n \\le3\\frac{3^R}{3^R-2^q}\n \\le\\frac{3(1+\\delta)}{\\delta}. \n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nЭто ограничение не требует \\(q/S\u003c\\theta\\) (или даже его предела):\nотношение \\(q/S\\) может быть не меньше \\(\\theta\\), пока условие на\n\\(R/S\\) исключает \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\). Все эти выводы остаются необходимыми\nусловиями для выбранных параметров; они не объявляют существование такой\nреальной орбиты и не дают строгого нового покрытия кодов.\n\n","task-0217.md":"# Task 0217 — проверка применения Corollary 1.6 к зазору \\(2^q-3^R\\)\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена аналитически по указанному\nавторскому конспекту J.-H. Evertse, без программ, CAS, тестов,\nперебора и дочерних агентов. Проверяется только локальная\nарифметическая оценка зазора и её подстановка в уже полученное точное\nсравнение высот.\n\n## Источник и точная применяемая теорема\n\nИсточник: [J.-H. Evertse, *Linear Forms in Logarithms*, April 2011](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~evertsejh/dio2011-linforms.pdf).\nCorollary 1.6 напечатано на стр. 2, его доказательство — на стр. 3.\nДля алгебраических \\(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_m\\ne0,1\\) и целых\n\\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\), при условии\n\\[\n\\alpha_1^{b_1}\\cdots\\alpha_m^{b_m}\\ne1,\n\\]\nCorollary 1.6 утверждает\n\\[\n\\left|\\alpha_1^{b_1}\\cdots\\alpha_m^{b_m}-1\\right|\n   >(eB)^{-C_0},\\qquad B=\\max_j|b_j|,\n\\]\nгде \\(C_0\\) эффективно вычислима и зависит только от \\(m\\) и баз.\nЗдесь достаточно \\(m=2\\), \\((\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2)=(2,3)\\). Условие\n\\(2^a3^b=1\\Rightarrow a=b=0\\) следует из единственности разложения\nрационального числа на простые множители, поэтому 2 и 3\nмультипликативно независимы. Corollary 1.8 не используется.\n\n## Униформенная оценка зазора\n\nПусть \\(q\\ge1\\), \\(0\\le R\\le q\\),\n\\[\nX=2^q,\\qquad Y=3^R,\\qquad k=X-Y,\\qquad M=\\max(X,Y).\n\\]\nРавенство \\(X=Y\\) невозможно: при \\(R=0\\) оно означало бы\n\\(2^q=1\\), а при \\(R\\ge1\\) противоречило бы независимости простых 2 и\n3. Следовательно, \\(k\\ne0\\). Обозначим \\(L=\\min(X,Y)\\), \\(H=M\\),\n\\(\\rho=L/H\\). Тогда\n\\[\n\\frac{|k|}{M}=1-\\rho=|\\rho-1|.\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(X>Y\\), то\n\\[\n\\rho=2^{-q}3^R,\\qquad (b_1,b_2)=(-q,R),\\qquad B=q,\n\\]\nпоскольку \\(0\\le R\\le q\\). Если \\(Y>X\\), то\n\\[\n\\rho=2^q3^{-R},\\qquad (b_1,b_2)=(q,-R),\\qquad B=q.\n\\]\nВ обоих случаях вектор показателей ненулевой, так что\n\\(\\rho\\ne1\\), и Corollary 1.6 даёт\n\\[\n\\frac{|k|}{M}>(eq)^{-C_0},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\frac{M}{|k|}\u003c(eq)^{C_0}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nВ частности, требуемая запись с \\(\\le\\) получается после безвредного\nзамыкания строгого неравенства. Константа зависит только от баз 2 и 3,\nа не от \\(q,R\\), слов \\(U,W\\) или источника.\n\nКрая входят непосредственно в эту проверку. При \\(R=0\\) имеем\n\\(k=2^q-1>0\\), \\(\\rho=2^{-q}\\), а нулевой показатель при базе 3\nдопустим в Corollary 1.6. При \\(q=1\\) возможны только \\(R=0,1\\): это\nсоответственно \\(k=1\\) и \\(k=-1\\), и в обоих случаях \\(B=q=1\\). При\n\\(R=q\\ge1\\) имеем \\(k\u003c0\\), но показатели отношения равны \\((q,-q)\\),\nпоэтому это также обычный случай Corollary 1.6; равенства самих\nстепеней здесь нет.\n\n## Подстановка при \\(k\u003c0\\) и \\(S\\ge1\\)\n\nВ этой ветви \\(3^R>2^q\\), поэтому \\(R\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nc=C_W>0,\\qquad A=2^ic+|k|C_U>0.\n\\]\nЕсли \\(U\\) содержит \\(S\\ge1\\) единиц и \\(t_0\\) — позиция первой\nединицы, её вклад в \\(C_U\\) равен \\(2^{t_0}3^{S-1}\\). Поэтому\n\\[\nt:=\\frac{C_U}{3^S}\\ge\\frac{2^{t_0}}3\\ge\\frac13,\\qquad\n\\frac{A}{3^S}=\\frac{2^ic}{3^S}+|k|t\\ge\\frac{|k|}{3}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nЭто единственное свойство \\(U,W\\), нужное здесь, и оно единообразно по\nвсем словам при данных \\(i,S,q,R\\). В частности, на этой ветви\n\\(A\\ne0\\), так что \\(A^\\#=|A|/3^{v_3(A)}\\) определено.\n\nПоложим \\(v=v_3(A)\\). Из точной формулы для примитивной высоты,\nиспользуемой в task-0214,\n\\[\n\\frac h{A^\\#}\n=3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{A},\\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\right). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nПервый множитель в максимуме даёт нижнюю оценку. Из (2) следует\n\\[\n\\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{A},\\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\right)\n\\le\n\\max\\left(1,\\frac{3\\,2^q}{|k|},\\frac{3\\,3^R}{|k|}\\right)\n\\le 3\\,\\frac{M}{|k|}.\n\\]\nВместе с (1) получаем точное требуемое сравнение\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\\le\\frac h{A^\\#}\\le\n3(eq)^{C_0}\\,3^{\\max(v-S,0)}.} \\tag{4}\n\\]\nОно униформно по всем допустимым \\(U,W\\); константа в нём зависит\nтолько от баз 2,3.\n\nЕсли отдельно исключена ветвь \\(v\\ge S\\), то есть установлено \\(v\u003cS\\),\nто (4) упрощается до\n\\[\n1\\le\\frac h{A^\\#}\\le3(eq)^{C_0},\\qquad\n\\log\\frac h{A^\\#}=O(\\log(q+1)), \\tag{5}\n\\]\nгде логарифм естественный и константа абсолютна относительно \\(U,W\\).\nПоэтому при \\(q\u003cN\\) и \\(N\\to\\infty\\) имеем\n\\(\\log(h/A^\\#)=O(\\log(q+1))=o(N)\\). В этом режиме не требуется ни\nфиксированный \\(\\delta\\)-разрыв \\(3^R\\ge(1+\\delta)2^q\\), ни более сильное\nусловие \\(q=o(N)\\). Если \\(v\\ge S\\), формула (4) всё же остаётся\n\\[\n\\log\\frac h{A^\\#}=(v-S)\\log3+O(\\log(q+1));\n\\]\nсам Baker-оценочный вход не исключает эту валюационную ветвь.\n\n## Проверка знаков и вырожденных краёв\n\n* При \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(S\\ge1\\), положительность обоих слагаемых в \\(A\\)\n  исключает точное \\(A=0\\). При \\(S=0\\) имеем \\(C_U=0\\) и\n  \\(A=2^iC_W>0\\), причём последний ненулевой вклад в \\(C_W\\) даёт\n  \\(3\\nmid C_W\\), так что \\(v_3(A)=0\\). Оценка\n  \\(C_U/3^S\\ge1/3\\) здесь уже неприменима; это отдельный начальный\n  край, не входящий в (4).\n* При \\(k>0\\) формула имеет вид \\(A=2^iC_W-kC_U\\), и близкая отмена\n  возможна. Поэтому (2)--(4) на ветвь \\(k>0\\) переносить нельзя.\n  Если \\(S\\ge1\\), то \\(k\\) нечётно, а для первой единицы \\(U\\)\n  \\(v_2(C_U)=t_0\u003ci\\); следовательно,\n  \\(v_2(kC_U)=t_0\u003cv_2(2^iC_W)\\), так что \\(A\\ne0\\), хотя отдельная\n  оценка его размера всё ещё нужна. Если \\(S=0\\), \\(C_U=0\\), и\n  \\(A=0\\) ровно в случае \\(C_W=0\\), то есть \\(R=0\\) (слово \\(W\\)\n  состоит из нулей); тогда \\(A^\\#\\) не определено.\n* При \\(R=0\\) всегда \\(k>0\\), поэтому этот край не относится к\n  положительной ветви (4). При \\(q=1\\) случаи \\(R=0,1\\) уже покрыты\n  выше. Случай \\(k=0\\) при \\(q\\ge1\\) отсутствует.\n\n## Объём вывода\n\nВход Corollary 1.6 — известная теорема Бейкера в форме Evertse; её\nприменение к фиксированным базам 2 и 3 и к отношению меньшей степени к\nбольшей даёт (1) без вычисления \\(C_0\\). Полученный результат — только\nлокальная арифметическая лемма для уже выбранных совместимых\nпериодических приближений и, после отдельного условия \\(v\u003cS\\), оценка\nих высотного разрыва. Он не доказывает существование такой\nпоследовательности для произвольной непериодической орбиты, не является\nполным доказательством гипотезы Коллатца и не заявляет приоритет или\nновизну результата.\n","task-0221.md":"# Task 0221 — сильная делимость из всей длины совпадения\n\n16 сентября 2026. Проверка выполнена аналитически, без программ, CAS,\nтестов, перебора и дочерних агентов. Отчёт касается только уже данного\nсовпадения кодов и необходимого арифметического условия. Он не доказывает\nсуществование таких повторов, наличие общей маржи или гипотезу Коллатца.\n\n## Установка и ненулевое кратное \\(2^N\\)\n\nПусть\n\n\\[\n \\xi=\\frac ab\\in\\mathbf Z_2,\n \\qquad b>0\\text{ нечётно},\n\\]\n\nи \\(U\\) — настоящий префикс длины \\(i\\), содержащий \\(S\\) единиц. Пусть\n\\(W\\) — слово длины \\(q\\ge1\\), содержащее \\(R\\) единиц, и\n\n\\[\n k=2^q-3^R\u003c0,\n \\qquad K:=|k|=3^R-2^q>0,\n \\qquad c:=C_W.\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(R\\le q\\) и \\(k\u003c0\\), имеем \\(R\\ge1\\). Поэтому \\(c>0\\), а\n\n\\[\n A=2^i c+K C_U>0.\n\\]\n\nПредположим, что \\(3^S\\mid A\\), и положим \\(z=A/3^S>0\\). Периодическое\nпродолжение \\(r=\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)\\) имеет значение\n\n\\[\n r=\\frac{z}{k}.\n\\]\n\nПо условию \\(\\xi\\ne r\\). Если коды \\(\\xi\\) и \\(UW^\\infty\\)\nсовпадают на первых \\(N\\) позициях, изометрия \\(\\Phi_2\\) и нечётность\n\\(b,k\\) дают\n\n\\[\n 2^N\\mid D,\n \\qquad D:=ka-bz\\ne0.\n\\]\n\nИтак,\n\n\\[\n 1\\le \\frac{|D|}{2^N}.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nЭто усиление прежней делимости на \\(2^i\\): здесь используется вся длина\nсовпадения \\(N\\), а не только настоящий префикс.\n\nДля \\(\\xi\\ge0\\) условие \\(\\xi\\ne r\\) следует уже из знаков, поскольку\n\\(r=z/k\u003c0\\). Для отрицательной \\(\\xi\\) оно требует отдельного\nпредположения; его, например, даёт непериодичность кода \\(\\xi\\). Знак\n\\(a\\) нигде далее не фиксируется: он входит только через \\(|a|\\).\n\n## Точная вещественная верхняя оценка\n\nДля слова длины \\(n\\) с \\(s\\) единицами используется стандартная граница\n\n\\[\n 0\\le C_V\\le 2^{n-s}(3^s-2^s).\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(s=0\\) это означает \\(C_V=0\\). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n C_U\\le2^{i-S}(3^S-2^S),\n \\qquad\n c\\le2^{q-R}(3^R-2^R).\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nИз \\(z=(2^ic+K C_U)/3^S\\), \\(z>0\\), и треугольного неравенства следует\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\frac{|D|}{2^N}\n &\\le |a|K2^{-N}\n  +b2^{i-N}\\left(c3^{-S}+K(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nПоложим\n\n\\[\n L:=N-i-q\\ge0.\n\\]\n\nТогда (4) переписывается без потери масштаба как\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|D|}{2^N}\n\\le 2^{-L}\\left[\n |a|\\frac K{2^q}2^{-i}\n +b\\left\\{\n \\frac c{2^q}3^{-S}\n +\\frac K{2^q}(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n \\right\\}\\right].\n \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nПоложим \\(u=3/2\\). Из (3), \\(q\\ge R\\), и \\(K=3^R-2^q\\) получаем\n\n\\[\n \\frac c{2^q}\\le u^R-1,\n \\qquad\n \\frac K{2^q}=\\frac{3^R}{2^q}-1\\le u^R-1.\n \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nКроме того, \\(i\\ge S\\), так что \\(2^{-i}\\le2^{-S}\\). Подстановка (6) в\n(5) даёт\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\frac{|D|}{2^N}\n &\\le 2^{-L}(u^R-1)\\left[\n |a|2^{-S}+b\\left(3^{-S}+2^{-S}-3^{-S}\\right)\n \\right] \\\\\n &=2^{-(L+S)}(|a|+b)(u^R-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nСовместно с (1) это даёт новую лемму, действующую при всех \\(R>S\\),\n\\(R=S\\), \\(R\u003cS\\) и \\(S=0\\):\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{\n 2^{S+L}\\le (|a|+b)\\left(\\left(\\frac32\\right)^R-1\\right),\n \\qquad L=N-i-q\\ge0.\n }\n \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nНикакой подстановки \\(N=i\\) здесь нет: показатель \\(L\\) является точным\nостатком полной длины совпадения после первых \\(i+q\\) позиций.\n\n## Запрошенная проверка ветви \\(R\\le S\\)\n\nВ ветви \\(R\\le S\\) сохраняется более сильная оценка, в которой виден\nсам \\(N-i\\). Поскольку \\(R\\le q\\) для любого слова, здесь\n\\(R\\le\\min(S,q)\\). Из (3) и \\(R\\le S\\) имеем\n\n\\[\n c\\le 2^q\\left(\\left(\\frac32\\right)^S-1\\right),\n\\]\n\nи потому\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n c3^{-S}+K(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n &\\le 2^q(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n   +K(2^{-S}-3^{-S}) \\\\\n &=3^R(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n \\le 3^R2^{-S}.\n\\end{aligned}\n \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nИз (4), \\(K\u003c3^R\\), \\(i\\ge S\\), и (9) следует\n\n\\[\n 1\\le \\frac{|D|}{2^N}\n \\le (|a|+b)3^R2^{i-N-S},\n\\]\n\nто есть\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{2^{N-i+S}\\le (|a|+b)3^R.}\n \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nПоследняя минимизация проверяется непосредственно на области\n\n\\[\n 0\\le R\\le\\min(S,q),\n \\qquad q\\ge1.\n\\]\n\nПри \\(R\\ge1\\) имеем \\(q\\ge R\\), и\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n &(S+q)\\log2-R\\log3 \\\\\n &\\quad\\ge S\\log2+R(\\log2-\\log3) \\\\\n &\\quad\\ge S\\log2+S(\\log2-\\log3)\n =S\\log(4/3).\n\\end{aligned}\n \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nВторая строка использует \\(R\\le S\\) и отрицательность\n\\(\\log2-\\log3\\). Равенство в этой нижней границе достигается формально\nпри \\(q=R=S\\); такая точка действительно совместима с \\(k\u003c0\\) при\n\\(S=R\\ge1\\). Поэтому из (10) следует\n\n\\[\n S\\log(4/3)\\le \\log(|a|+b),\n \\qquad\n \\boxed{S\\le\\frac{\\log(|a|+b)}{\\log(4/3)}}.\n \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(S=0\\), условие \\(R\\le S\\) означало бы \\(R=0\\), что невозможно\nпри \\(k\u003c0\\) и \\(q\\ge1\\). Следовательно, край \\(S=0\\) не пропущен:\nон целиком лежит в противоположной ветви \\(R>S\\) и покрыт (8). Все знаки\nтакже учтены: \\(K,z>0\\), \\(A>0\\), а знак \\(a\\) поглощён в \\(|a|\\).\n\nТаким образом, для любой семьи настоящих совпадений с \\(S\\to\\infty\\)\nветвь \\(R\\le S\\) и \\(3^S\\mid A\\) невозможна. Это условное следствие\nсуществования уже выбранных совпадений; оно не утверждает, что такие\nсовпадения существуют или что их параметры обязаны иметь данный режим.\n\n## Что даёт (8) при \\(R>S\\)\n\nОценка (8) является аккуратной границей для противоположной ветви и не\nтребует ложного переноса (9). В частности,\n\n\\[\n S+L\\le\n \\frac{\\log(|a|+b)+\\log((3/2)^R-1)}{\\log2}\n \u003c\\frac{\\log(|a|+b)}{\\log2}+R\\log_2(3/2).\n \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nСледовательно, при фиксированном \\(\\xi\\) ветвь \\(R>S\\) также исключается\nна любой семье с \\(S+L\\to\\infty\\), для которой\n\n\\[\n \\limsup\\frac{R}{S+L}\u003c\\frac{\\log2}{\\log(3/2)}.\n \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nЭто условие допускает \\(R>S\\), например \\(R/(S+L)\\) немного больше\nединицы; оно существенно шире прежнего порога\n\\(\\log2/\\log3\\) для оценки только через \\(3^R\\). Если (14) не выполнено,\n(8) остаётся необходимым ограничением, но сама по себе не исключает\nветвь.\n\nДля контроля того, где именно возникло дополнительное преимущество,\nможно сохранить \\(q\\)-зависимость. При \\(R>S\\), с\n\n\\[\n m=2^{q-R},\\qquad d=(2/3)^R,\\qquad x=(2/3)^S,\n\\]\n\nимеем \\(m\\ge1\\), \\(x>d\\), и из (3)\n\n\\[\n c3^{-S}+K(2^{-S}-3^{-S})\n \\le 3^R2^{-S}\\,B,\n \\qquad\n B=1-2^q3^{-R}+x(m-1).\n \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nЗдесь \\(B>0\\), поскольку \\(B=(1-x)+m(x-d)\\). Поэтому (4) даёт более\nточную, но менее компактную границу\n\n\\[\n \\boxed{2^{N-i+S}\\le 3^R\\bigl(|a|+bB\\bigr).}\n \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(R>S\\) уже нельзя утверждать \\(B\\le1\\): это было следствием\n\\(x\\le d\\), то есть именно \\(S\\ge R\\). Формула (15) показывает, почему\nпростая замена старого доказательства в противоположной ветви была бы\nошибочной. Граница (8) получается из (16) после нормировки на\n\\(2^q\\), и безопасна для всех \\(R>S\\), включая \\(S=0\\).\n\n## Следствие для высот Бейкера\n\nПусть \\(v=v_3(A)\\), \\(A^\\#=|A|/3^v\\), и \\(h\\) — примитивная высота из\n[BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md](BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md). Для \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(S\\ge1\\),\nтам установлено\n\n\\[\n \\frac h{A^\\#}\n \\le 3(eq)^C\\,3^{\\max(v-S,0)}.\n \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nДля настоящих совпадений в ветви \\(R\\le S\\), если \\(S\\to\\infty\\),\n(12) исключает \\(v\\ge S\\) на хвосте. Тогда\n\n\\[\n \\log(h/A^\\#)=O(\\log(q+1)).\n \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nВ ветви \\(R>S\\) тот же вывод следует при условии (14), поскольку (8)\nтогда также исключает \\(v\\ge S\\). Без (14) нельзя честно заключать\nиз одной лишь делимости \\(3^S\\mid A\\), что \\(v\u003cS\\): остаётся только\nнеобходимая граница (8). Край \\(S=0\\) не является входом в (17) с\nусловием \\(v\u003cS\\) и рассматривается отдельно; он не участвует в\nасимптотическом утверждении \\(S\\to\\infty\\).\n\nНаконец, ни (8), ни (10), ни (16) не дают существования настоящих\nсовпадений, положительной маржи для всех стартов или решения Коллатца.\nОни являются условными арифметическими ограничениями для уже\nпредположенного совпадения \\(UW^\\infty\\) с кодом фиксированного\nрационального \\(\\xi\\).\n","task-0222.md":"# task-0222 — независимый аудит смешанной оценки координатора\n\n16 сентября 2026. Ручная проверка `INTEGER_BRANCH_PERIOD_LENGTH.md` и\n`BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md`; математических программ, CAS, тестов, перебора и\nчисленных исполнений не использовалось.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nКандидатная лемма верна при явно сохранённых предпосылках: \\(U\\) — настоящий\nпрефикс кода \\(\\xi\\), первые \\(N=i+q+L\\) битов действительно совпадают с\n\\(UW^\\infty\\), \\(k\\ne0\\), и \\(\\xi\\ne r=\\Phi_2(UW^\\infty)\\). При этих условиях\n\n\\[\n 3^S\\mid A,\\quad z=A/3^S\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2^N\\mid ka-bz\\ne0.\n\\]\n\nТочная оценка действительно имеет вид\n\n\\[\n 2^{S+L}\\le (|a|+b)M_R,\n \\qquad M_R=\\max\\{1,(3/2)^R-1\\},\n\\]\n\nдля любого знака \\(k\\). Если \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(M_R\\) можно заменить на\n\\((3/2)^R-1\\). Поэтому при \\(S+L\\to\\infty\\) и\n\n\\[\n\\limsup\\frac{R}{S+L}\u003c\\frac{\\log2}{\\log(3/2)}\n\\]\n\nненулевая ветвь \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\) действительно невозможна. Существенные\nоговорки: случай \\(A=0\\) нужно разбирать отдельно; одна лишь\nрациональность \\(\\xi\\) не даёт \\(\\xi\\ne r\\); а оценка Бейкера для \\(k>0\\)\nне даёт контроля высоты без отдельного нижнего ограничения на \\(|A|/3^S\\).\n\n## 1. Кодовая точность и знак формулы\n\nПри принятой нормировке аффинной карты префикса\n\n\\[\n 3^S\\Phi_2(x)+C_U=2^i\\Phi_2(x_{i:}),\n\\]\n\nпоэтому для \\(\\xi=a/b\\), \\(b\\) нечётного,\n\n\\[\n 2^i\\mid 3^Sa+bC_U. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nДля периодического хвоста \\(W^\\infty\\) его значение равно \\(c/k\\), где\n\\(c=C_W\\), \\(k=2^q-3^R\\). После префикса \\(U\\)\n\n\\[\n r=\\frac{2^i(c/k)-C_U}{3^S}\n   =\\frac{A}{3^S k},\n \\qquad A=2^ic-kC_U. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nЭто проверяет нужные знаки: здесь не следует подменять \\(A\\) на\n\\(2^ic+kC_U\\) при \\(k>0\\).\n\nЕсли два бинарных кода совпадают на первых \\(N\\) позициях, то\n\n\\[\n |\\Phi_2(x)-\\Phi_2(y)|_2\\le 2^{-N}.\n\\]\n\nБолее точно, это эквивалентность: совпадение первых \\(N\\) битов равносильно\nделимости разности на \\(2^N\\), а при первом различии на позиции \\(N\\)\nвалюация ровно \\(N\\). Действительно, первый различающий член ряда имеет\nвид \\(\\pm2^N\\) умножить на 2-адическую единицу, а все следующие члены\nделятся на \\(2^{N+1}\\). Таким образом, условие совпадения должно быть\nполным совпадением с \\(UW^\\infty\\) до длины \\(i+q+L\\). Произвольное слово\n\\(W\\), не являющееся фактическим продолжением, такой оценки не даёт.\n\nИз (2), \\(3^S\\mid A\\), и нечётности \\(b,k,3^S\\) следует\n\n\\[\n \\xi-r=\\frac{ka-bz}{bk},\n \\qquad z=A/3^S. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nПоэтому полное совпадение даёт \\(2^N\\mid ka-bz\\). Ненулевость в точности\nэквивалентна \\(\\xi\\ne r\\).\n\n## 2. Точная смешанная оценка\n\nПусть \\(t_r\\) — позиции единиц \\(U\\), начиная с \\(r=1\\). Так как \\(U\\)\nимеет длину \\(i\\) и \\(S\\) единиц,\n\n\\[\n t_r\\le i-S+r-1.\n\\]\n\nИз определения аффинного числителя получается точная крайняя оценка\n\n\\[\n 0\\le C_U\n \\le \\sum_{r=1}^S2^{i-S+r-1}3^{S-r}\n =2^{i-S}(3^S-2^S). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nОна также верна при \\(S=0\\), когда \\(C_U=0\\). Из \\(2^N\\mid ka-bz\\ne0\\),\n\\(N=i+q+L\\), следует\n\n\\[\n 2^{q+L}\n \\le \\frac{|ka-bz|}{2^i}\n \\le |a||k|2^{-i}\n   +b\\bigl[c3^{-S}+|k|2^{-S}(1-(2/3)^S)\\bigr]. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nПосле умножения на \\(2^S\\), первый член справа не превосходит \\(|a||k|\\),\nпоскольку \\(S\\le i\\), а выражение в квадратных скобках превращается в\nвыпуклую комбинацию \\(c\\) и \\(|k|\\):\n\n\\[\n c(2/3)^S+|k|(1-(2/3)^S). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nЕсли \\(W\\) имеет \\(R\\) единиц, то аналогично (позиции единиц ставятся как\nможно правее)\n\n\\[\n 0\\le c\\le2^{q-R}(3^R-2^R),\n \\qquad \\frac c{2^q}\\le(3/2)^R-1. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nТак как \\(0\\le R\\le q\\),\n\n\\[\n \\frac{|k|}{2^q}=\\left|1-\\frac{3^R}{2^q}\\right|\n \\le\\max\\{1,(3/2)^R-1\\}=M_R. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nИз (5)--(8) после сокращения \\(2^q\\) получается\n\n\\[\n 2^{S+L}\\le(|a|+b)M_R. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(k\u003c0\\) обязательно \\(R\\ge1\\), и \\(3^R>2^q\\). Тогда\n\n\\[\n |k|/2^q=3^R/2^q-1\\le(3/2)^R-1,\n\\]\n\nтак что оба \\(c/2^q\\) и \\(|k|/2^q\\) ограничены самим\n\\((3/2)^R-1\\), без максимума с единицей. Край \\(R=1,q=1\\) здесь допустим:\n\\(k=-1\\), \\(c=1\\), и множитель равен \\(1/2\\).\n\n## 3. Предельное следствие и нулевой \\(A\\)\n\nПоложим \\(T=S+L\\). Всегда \\(M_R\\le\\exp(R\\log(3/2))\\). Если\n\\(\\limsup R/T\u003c\\log2/\\log(3/2)\\), существует \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), для\nкоторого на хвосте \\(M_R\\le2^{(1-\\varepsilon)T}\\). Правая часть (9) тогда\nменьше \\(2^T\\) при достаточно больших \\(T\\), что противоречит (9).\nСледовательно, это исключает \\(3^S\\mid A\\), то есть \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\), для\nвсех достаточно больших членов с \\(A\\ne0\\). На границе равенства\nнеположительный запас не появляется автоматически.\n\nНулевой случай полностью классифицируется. Если \\(U\\) содержит первую\nединицу в позиции \\(t_0\u003ci\\), то\n\n\\[\n v_2(C_U)=t_0,\n\\]\n\nпоскольку первый вклад после деления на \\(2^{t_0}\\) нечётен, а все\nпоследующие члены чётны. При нечётном \\(k\\) и \\(t_0\u003ci\\) это даёт\n\\(v_2(A)=t_0\\), так что \\(A\\ne0\\). Если \\(U=0^i\\), то \\(A=2^ic\\), и\n\\(A=0\\) тогда и только тогда, когда \\(W=0^q\\). Следовательно,\n\n\\[\n A=0 \\Longleftrightarrow U=0^i,\\ W=0^q,\\ S=R=0. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nПри \\(k\u003c0\\) этот случай невозможен, поскольку тогда \\(R\\ge1\\) и \\(c>0\\).\nПри \\(A=0\\) выражение \\(A^\\#\\) и обычная ветвь \\(v_3(A)\\) не определены.\nЕсли всё же требуется исключить бесконечную последовательность таких\nсовпадений при фиксированной ненулевой \\(\\xi=a/b\\), то \\(r=0,z=0\\) и\nделимость даёт \\(2^N\\mid ka\\); так как \\(k\\) нечётно, \\(N\\le v_2(a)\\).\nПри \\(a=0\\) имеем \\(\\xi=r=0\\), что нарушает отдельное условие\n\\(\\xi\\ne r\\). Значит \\(A=0\\) не мешает предельному выводу, но его надо\nобрабатывать отдельно, а не подставлять в \\(A^\\#\\).\n\n## 4. Почему нужно явно исключать \\(\\xi=r\\)\n\nРациональность сама по себе не исключает равенство с периодическим\nрациональным приближателем. Отрицательные примеры видны уже на коротких\nсловах:\n\n* \\(U=\\varnothing\\), \\(W=1\\): \\(q=R=c=1\\), \\(k=-1\\), \\(A=1\\), и\n  \\(r=-1=\\Phi_2(1^\\infty)\\);\n* \\(U=11\\), \\(W=00001\\): \\(i=S=2,q=5\\), \\(C_U=5,c=16,k=29\\),\n  \\(A=64-145=-81\\), \\(z=-9\\), и \\(r=-9/29=\\Phi_2(11(00001)^\\infty)\\).\n  Здесь уже \\(3^S\\mid A\\) и \\(ka-bz=0\\) для \\(\\xi=-9/29\\).\n\nВ обоих случаях \\(ka-bz=0\\) при соответствующем рациональном \\(\\xi\\),\nнесмотря на любое конечное совпадение. Поэтому допустимые основания для\n\\(\\xi\\ne r\\) — непериодичность исходного кода вместе с инъективностью\n2-адического кодирования, либо отдельная проверка. Для \\(k\u003c0\\), если\n\\(\\xi=a/b\\ge0\\) в обычном порядке, то \\(A>0\\), \\(k\u003c0\\), значит \\(r\u003c0\\), и\n\\(\\xi\\ne r\\) следует только из знака. Для отрицательной рациональной\n\\(\\xi\\) этот знак аргумента не работает; нужны непериодичность или явная\nпроверка. Знак в \\(\\mathbb Q_2\\) сам по себе не определён, так что фразу\n«\\(\\xi\\ge0\\)» можно использовать только для рационального представителя\nв обычном порядке.\n\n## 5. Что даёт и чего не даёт Бейкер\n\nДля \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(S\\ge1\\), имеем \\(A=2^ic+|k|C_U>0\\), а первая единица\nв \\(U\\) даёт \\(C_U\\ge3^{S-1}\\). Поэтому\n\n\\[\n A/3^S\\ge|k|/3. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nВместе с оценкой Бейкера \\(M/|k|\\le(eq)^C\\), где\n\\(M=\\max(2^q,3^R)=3^R\\) при \\(k\u003c0\\), и с \\(v_3(A)\u003cS\\), это действительно\nдаёт \\(h/A^\\#\\le3(eq)^C\\), то есть\n\\(\\log(h/A^\\#)=O(\\log(q+1))\\). Случай \\(S=0\\) (где \\(C_U=0\\)) этой\nнижней оценкой не покрывается.\n\nДля \\(k>0\\) с \\(c,C_U>0\\) слагаемые \\(2^ic\\) и \\(kC_U\\) имеют\nпротивоположные знаки. Обычная архимедова отмена может сделать \\(|A|\\)\nмалым, и оценка Бейкера для \\(2^q\\) и \\(3^R\\) эту отмену не контролирует.\nПоэтому после исключения \\(v_3(A)\\ge S\\) нельзя автоматически заключать\n\\(\\log(h/A^\\#)=O(\\log(q+1))\\) в ветви \\(k>0\\). Такое заключение требует\nотдельного условия вида \\(|A|/3^S\\ge|k|/K_0\\) (или иной оценки снизу).\n\nИтог: новая смешанная граница корректна и сильнее старой границы с\n\\(2^i\\), но только для настоящих совпадений кода и при явном ненулевом\nразличии с периодическим рациональным значением. Она исключает целую\nветвь при указанном отношении \\(R/(S+L)\\), однако сама по себе не создаёт\nповторов и не даёт контроля высоты в положительной \\(k\\)-ветви.\n","task-0227.md":"# Task 0227 — обычная отмена и настоящий спуск блока\n\n16 сентября 2026. Полная самостоятельная ручная аналитическая проверка\nкандидата 0227 по `collatz-descent-gap-candidate-0227.md`,\n`FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md`, `BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md` и\n`FULL_PRECISION_VALUATION_BOUND.md`. Программ, CAS, тестов, перебора и\nдочерних агентов не использовалось.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nКандидат верен в следующей локальной форме. Пусть \n\n\\[\n \\xi>0,\\qquad \\xi=a/b,\\quad b>0\\text{ нечётно},\n\\]\n\nи пусть \\(U\\) и \\(W\\) — настоящие последовательные блоки кода орбиты\n\\(m_t=T^t\\xi\\): длина \\(U\\) равна \\(i\\), длина непустого \\(W\\) равна\n\\(q\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n j=i+q,\\qquad S=\\#1(U),\\qquad R=\\#1(W),\\qquad\n m_i=T^i\\xi,\\qquad m_j=T^j\\xi.\n\\]\n\nПишем (C_U,C_W) для аффинных констант слов и\n\\[\n c=C_W,qquad k=2^q-3^R,qquad A=2^ic-kC_U.\n\\]\nТогда (k\\ne0), а точное тождество имеет вид\n\\[\n \\boxed{A=k3^S\\xi+2^j(m_j-m_i).}\n \\tag{D}\n\\]\n\nИсправление координатора после полного чтения: при k\u003c0 всегда\nm_j>m_i, поэтому эта ветвь входит в область без уменьшения.\nПри k>0 и m_j≥m_i имеем\n\\[\n A\\ge k3^S\\xi>0.\n\\]\nЕсли (k\u003c0), то (R\\ge1) и всегда (m_j>m_i). В этой ветви при\n\\(S\\ge1\\) работает прежняя оценка с коэффициентом (3), а при (S=0)\nдействительно получается отдельная граница (h/A^\\#\\le3). Поэтому для\nвсех настоящих блоков без обычного уменьшения состояния, то есть при\n\\(m_j\\ge m_i), верна единая условная оценка\n\\[\n \\boxed{\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\n \\le \\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C\n       3^{\\max(v_3(A)-S,0)}\n },\n \\tag{E}\n\\]\nпри (A\\ne0). Здесь (h) — примитивная высота тройки из\n`BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md`, а (A^\\#=|A|/3^{v_3(A)}).\n\nПосле отдельного доказанного исключения ветви (v_3(A)\\ge S) это даёт\n\\[\n \\log(h/A^\\#)=O_\\xi(\\log(q+1))=o(N)\n \\qquad(q\u003cN).\n \\tag{F}\n\\]\nСледовательно, линейно большой оставшийся разрыв в этой величине может\nприходиться только на настоящие строгие локальные спуски\n\\(m_j\u003cm_i\\), при условии что высоковалюационная ветвь действительно\nисключена. Это не утверждает существование такого спуска для каждого\nстарта и не превращает бесконечную последовательность локальных спусков\nв глобальную убывающую цепь.\n\n## 1. Точные аффинные формулы\n\nДля слова (V) длины (n) с (s) единицами истинная последовательность\nшагов имеет форму\n\\[\n T^n(x)=\\frac{3^s x+C_V}{2^n}.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nЕсли биты слова записаны в хронологическом порядке, то\n\\[\n C_V=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{n-1}\n \\varepsilon_\\ell 2^\\ell 3^{\\#\\{t>\\ell:\\varepsilon_t=1\\}},\n \\qquad \\varepsilon_\\ell\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nНастоящесть блоков нужна именно здесь: (U,W) должны быть фактическими\nпаритетами положительной орбиты, а не произвольными формальными словами.\n\nПрименяя (1) к (U), а затем к (W), получаем\n\\[\n m_i=\\frac{3^S\\xi+C_U}{2^i},\n \\qquad\n m_j=\\frac{3^R m_i+c}{2^q}.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nВычитание концов даёт\n\\[\n m_j-m_i=\\frac{c-km_i}{2^q},\n \\qquad k=2^q-3^R.\n \\tag{4}\n\\]\nУмножая (4) на \\(2^j=2^{i+q}\\) и подставляя\n\\(2^im_i=3^S\\xi+C_U\\), находим\n\\[\n 2^j(m_j-m_i)=2^ic-k(3^S\\xi+C_U)=A-k3^S\\xi,\n\\]\nчто и доказывает (D), включая равенство (m_j=m_i).\n\nЧисло (k) ненулевое: степени простых (2) и (3) не могут быть\nравны; при (R=0) это просто (k=2^q-1>0). Также (k) нечётно,\n\\(0\\le S\\le i\\), \\(0\\le R\\le q\\), а (q\\ge1).\n\nИз (2) следуют две точные элементарные границы:\n\\[\n C_V\\le 2^{n-s}(3^s-2^s),\n \\tag{5}\n\\]\nи, если (s\\ge1),\n\\[\n C_V\\ge 3^{s-1}.\n \\tag{6}\n\\]\nДействительно, в (2) вклад первой единицы уже равен не менее\n\\(3^{s-1}). Последняя единица показывает также\n\\[\n 3\\nmid C_V\\quad(s\\ge1),\n \\tag{7}\n\\]\nпоскольку все более ранние слагаемые делятся на (3), а вклад последней\nединицы нет.\n\n## 2. Проверка знаков и настоящего спуска\n\nИз (4), а также (m_i>0), следует:\n\n* Если (k\u003c0), то\n  \\[\n   m_j-m_i=\\frac{|k|m_i+c}{2^q}>0.\n   \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n  Здесь автоматически (R\\ge1), поэтому (c>0). Значит ветвь\n  (k\u003c0) никогда не является спуском (m_j\u003cm_i), а (A) имеет форму\n  \\[\n   A=2^ic+|k|C_U>0.\n   \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n* Если (k>0) и (m_j\\ge m_i), то из (D)\n  \\[\n   A\\ge k3^S\\xi>0.\n   \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n  При равенстве концов это равенство заменяется точной формулой\n  (A=k3^S\\xi), так что нулевого края здесь нет.\n\n* Если (k>0) и (m_j\u003cm_i), знак (A) уже не определяется одной\n  формулой (D): положительный член (k3^S\\xi) конкурирует с отрицательным\n  членом (2^j(m_i-m_j)). Именно эту ветвь нельзя включать в оценку\n  «без уменьшения».\n\nЭто сравнение обычных положительных рациональных чисел. Оно не означает\nсравнение (m_j) с первоначальным стартом \\(\\xi\\): (m_j\u003cm_i) может\nоставлять (m_j>\\xi), а блок с (m_j\\ge m_i) может происходить после\nпредыдущего падения и иметь (m_j\u003c\\xi).\n\n## 3. Высотная оценка при (k>0)\n\nДля (A\\ne0) используем точную формулу из\n`BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md`:\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\n =3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n  \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\n              \\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right),\n \\qquad v=v_3(A).\n \\tag{11}\n\\]\nДля (k>0) на ветви (m_j\\ge m_i) применяем (10):\n\\[\n \\frac{2^q3^S}{A}\\le\\frac{2^q}{k\\xi},\n \\qquad\n \\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\le\\frac{3^R}{k\\xi}.\n \\tag{12}\n\\]\nПусть (M=\\max(2^q,3^R)). Из принятой оценки Бейкера\n\\[\n \\frac{M}{|k|}\\le(eq)^C\n \\tag{13}\n\\]\nследует\n\\[\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{A},\n              \\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\right)\n \\le \\max\\{1,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C.\n \\tag{14}\n\\]\nПодстановка в (11) даёт требуемую часть (E). При (S=0) этот вывод\nостаётся корректным, если блок действительно без уменьшения; никакая\nоценка (C_U/3^S\\ge1/3) при этом не используется.\n\n## 4. Ветвь (k\u003c0), включая (S=0)\n\nПусть сначала (S\\ge1). Из (6), (9) имеем\n\\[\n \\frac{A}{3^S}\\ge |k|\\frac{C_U}{3^S}\n \\ge \\frac{|k|}{3}.\n \\tag{15}\n\\]\nПоэтому\n\\[\n \\frac{2^q3^S}{A}\\le\\frac{3\\,2^q}{|k|},\n \\qquad\n \\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\le\\frac{3\\,3^R}{|k|}.\n \\tag{16}\n\\]\nЗдесь (M=3^R), поскольку (k\u003c0), и (13) даёт\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\\le\n 3(eq)^C3^{\\max(v-S,0)}.\n \\tag{17}\n\\]\nЭто ровно старое \\(k\u003c0\\) ограничение с коэффициентом \\(3\\).\n\nОстаётся (S=0). Тогда (C_U=0),\n\\[\n A=2^ic,qquad c=C_W>0,qquad v_3(A)=0\n \\tag{18}\n\\]\nпо (7). Первая единица (W) даёт (c\\ge3^{R-1}), а (k\u003c0) даёт\n\\(2^q\u003c3^R). Поэтому\n\\[\n \\frac{3^R}{A}\\le\\frac{3^R}{c}\\le3,\n \\qquad\n \\frac{2^q}{A}\u003c\\frac{3^R}{A}\\le3.\n \\tag{19}\n\\]\nФормула (11) при (S=v=0) теперь даёт\n\\[\n \\boxed{h/A^\\#\\le3.}\n \\tag{20}\n\\]\nЭто объясняет, почему в единой оценке (E) можно оставить коэффициент\n\\(\\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}), хотя в этой подветви Бейкер вообще не нужен.\n\n## 5. Нулевые и пограничные случаи\n\nЕсли (U) содержит первую единицу в позиции (t_0\u003ci), то из (2)\n\\[\n v_2(C_U)=t_0.\n \\tag{21}\n\\]\nПоскольку (k) нечётно, (v_2(kC_U)=t_0\u003ci\\), тогда как\n\\(v_2(2^ic)\\ge i). Следовательно, (A=0) невозможно. Поэтому\n\\[\n A=0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n U\\text{ и }W\\text{ состоят только из нулей}.\n \\tag{22}\n\\]\nВ этом случае (S=R=0), (c=C_U=0), периодическое значение равно\nнулю, а (A^\\#) и (v_3(A)) не определяются. Для положительного\n\\(\\xi=a/b\\) совпадение с нулевым кодом возможно только на конечной длине\n\\(N\\le v_2(a)); его нельзя подставлять в (11) или (E).\n\nЕсли (S=0) и (W) не нулевое, то (18) верно и (A>0) при любом\nзнаке (k). Если (R=0), то (W) нулевое и\n \\[\n c=0,\\qquad k=2^q-1>0.\n \\tag{23}\n\\]\nПри (S>0) имеем (A=-kC_U\u003c0), а сам настоящий блок является строгим\nспуском (m_j=m_i/2^q\u003cm_i). При (S=0) это уже нулевой случай (22).\nТаким образом, (R=0) не даёт скрытой ветви без уменьшения.\n\nРавенство (m_j=m_i) допускается только в (k>0) ветви и даёт\nненулевое (A=k3^S\\xi). Равенство (j=i) не рассматривается, так как\nздесь (q\\ge1); все формулы требуют именно непустого блока (W).\n\n## 6. Высокая валюация и (o(N))-следствие\n\nОценка (E) сама содержит фактор\n\\(3^{\\max(v_3(A)-S,0)}\\). Достаточный, но не необходимый, способ\nисключить его влияние — отдельный доказанный факт\n\\[\n v_3(A)\u003cS\n \\tag{24}\n\\]\nдля рассматриваемых блоков. Для o(N) в действительности достаточно\nmax(v₃(A)−S,0)=o(N), как уточнено координатором и0229.\nОдно только полное совпадение кода и\n\\(UW^\\infty\\) не запрещает (24) без дополнительных условий.\n\nДействительно, если (3^S\\mid A), то полная точность даёт из\n`FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md`\n\\[\n 2^{S+L}\\le (|a|+b)B_R,\n \\qquad B_R=\\max\\{1,(3/2)^R-1\\},\n \\tag{25}\n\\]\nа при (k\u003c0) максимум можно заменить на ((3/2)^R-1). Чтобы (25)\nисключала эту ветвь на хвосте, нужны условия роста, например\n\\(S+L\\to\\infty) вместе с\n\\[\n \\limsup\\frac{R}{S+L}\u003c\\frac{\\log2}{\\log(3/2)}.\n \\tag{26}\n\\]\nБез такого отдельного входа высокая валюация остаётся возможным\nостатком и сама по себе не может быть приписана строгому спуску.\n\nПри принятом исключении (24) фактор степени 3 в (E) равен единице.\nДля фиксированного положительного \\(\\xi\\)\n\\[\n 1\\le h/A^\\#\n \\le \\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C,\n \\tag{27}\n\\]\nоткуда при (q\u003cN), (N\\to\\infty),\n\\[\n 0\\le \\log(h/A^\\#)=O_\\xi(\\log(q+1))=o(N).\n \\tag{28}\n\\]\nЗдесь существенно, что (q\u003cN) явно предполагается; граница не\nутверждает линейную оценку при оставшейся высокой валюации.\n\nИтак, после отдельного исключения (24), если в полном семействе блоков\nобнаружен разрыв порядка (N) в логарифме (h/A^\\#), блок не может быть\nветвью (m_j\\ge m_i). Он должен лежать в ветви (m_j\u003cm_i). Это только\nклассификация источника возможного разрыва, а не доказательство, что\nтакой разрыв существует или что каждый настоящий спуск опускается ниже\nисходного \\(\\xi\\).\n\n## 7. Что именно не следует\n\nТождество (D) и оценка (E) не дают универсального перехода к спуску.\nОни сравнивают концы одного уже данного настоящего блока. Даже если на\nодной орбите имеется бесконечно много блоков с (m_j\u003cm_i), это не создаёт\nмонотонной цепи: между блоками могут быть повышения, а (m_j\u003cm_i) не\nозначает (m_j\u003c\\xi). Нельзя телескопировать локальные сравнения с разными\nлевыми концами в глобальную индукцию.\n\nРавным образом, локальная оценка не доказывает существование нужных\nблоков для всех стартов, не исключает бесконечную положительную орбиту и\nне является доказательством гипотезы Коллатца. Проверенный результат —\nточная дихотомия для уже предъявленного настоящего блока: на ветви без\nуменьшения действует (E), а после независимого исключения высокой\nвалюации линейно большой высотный разрыв может остаться только на\nнастоящей строгой локальной ветви (m_j\u003cm_i).\n","task-0229.md":"# Task 0229 — враждебный аудит локальной дихотомии 0227\n\n16 сентября 2026. Полная ручная проверка task-0227.md,\nBAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md, FULL_PRECISION_INTEGER_BRANCH.md и\nwork/collatz-descent-gap-candidate-0227.md. Математических программ,\nCAS, тестов, перебора и дочерних агентов не использовалось.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nКандидат 0227 корректен как локальное утверждение о уже данном настоящем\nблоке. Для \n\\[\n \\xi>0,\\quad \\xi=a/b,\\ b>0\\text{ нечётно},\\quad j=i+q,\n\\]\nгде \\(U,W\\) — фактические последовательные слова орбиты длины \\(i,q\\),\n\\(q\\ge1\\), с \\(S,R\\) единицами, положим\n\\[\n c=C_W,\\quad k=2^q-3^R,\\quad A=2^ic-kC_U,\n \\quad v=v_3(A),\\quad A^\\#=|A|/3^v.\n\\]\nПри \\(A\\ne0\\) точное тождество имеет вид\n\\[\n \\boxed{A=k3^S\\xi+2^j(m_j-m_i).}\n \\tag{D}\n\\]\nНа ветви \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\) знак \\(k\u003c0\\) вполне возможен и автоматически\nдаёт \\(m_j>m_i\\); для \\(k>0\\) на этой ветви\n\\(A\\ge k3^S\\xi>0\\). Поэтому, условно на принятой в\nBAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md оценке \\(M/|k|\\le(eq)^C\\),\n\\[\n \\boxed{\\frac{h}{A^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C3^{\\max(v-S,0)}}\n \\tag{E}\n\\]\nдля всех таких настоящих блоков. Равенство \\(m_j=m_i\\) входит в эту\nветвь и не создаёт нулевого края.\n\nТочный асимптотический вывод имеет чуть более общий вид, чем формулировка\nкандидата: если для рассматриваемой семьи блоков независимо установлено\n\\[\n u_N:=\\max(v_3(A)-S,0)=o(N),\n\\]\nи \\(q\u003cN\\), то из (E) следует\n\\[\n \\log(h/A^\\#)=O_\\xi(\\log(q+1))+u_N\\log3=o(N)\n\\]\nна ветви \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\). Следовательно, любое линейно большое значение\nэтого логарифма может остаться только при настоящем строгом локальном\nспуске \\(m_j\u003cm_i\\). Это не означает \\(m_j\u003c\\xi\\), не сцепляет разные\nлокальные спуски и не даёт полного решения задачи Коллатца.\n\nЭто результат локальной алгебры, стандартной высотной оценки Бейкера и\nуже имеющегося полного совпадения/делимости; самостоятельной новой\nтеоремы или доказательства гипотезы здесь нет.\n\nОтдельная редакционная ошибка в начале task-0227: фразу «при\n\\(m_j\\ge m_i\\) обязательно \\(k>0\\), а при \\(k\u003c0\\) это невозможно» нельзя\nоставлять. При \\(k\u003c0\\) как раз всегда \\(m_j>m_i\\), так что отрицательная\n\\(k\\)-ветвь входит в область \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\). Правильная дихотомия такова:\n\\(k\u003c0\\) автоматически даёт строгий рост; при \\(k>0\\) без уменьшения\n\\(m_j\\ge m_i\\) и только там используется нижняя оценка \\(A\\ge k3^S\\xi\\).\n\n## 1. Проверка тождества и настоящести\n\nДля фактического слова \\(V\\) длины \\(n\\), содержащего \\(s\\) единиц,\nпоследовательное применение шагов даёт\n\\[\n T^n(x)=\\frac{3^s x+C_V}{2^n}.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nПри хронологической записи битов\n\\[\n C_V=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{n-1}\n \\varepsilon_\\ell2^\\ell3^{\\#\\{t>\\ell:\\varepsilon_t=1\\}}.\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nПоэтому\n\\[\n m_i=\\frac{3^S\\xi+C_U}{2^i},\\qquad\n m_j=\\frac{3^Rm_i+c}{2^q},\n\\]\nи\n\\[\n m_j-m_i=\\frac{c-km_i}{2^q}.\n\\]\nПосле умножения на \\(2^j=2^{i+q}\\) и подстановки \\(2^im_i=3^S\\xi+C_U\\)\nполучается ровно (D). Знак при \\(C_U\\) здесь минус; замена на плюс была\nбы ошибкой.\n\nУсловие «настоящий блок» необходимо именно для (1)--(2): \\(U,W\\) должны\nбыть фактическими паритетами положительной орбиты, а не двумя формальными\nсловами. Из \\(q\\ge1\\), \\(0\\le R\\le q\\) следует, что \\(k\\) нечётен и\nненулевой; \\(k=0\\) невозможен по простому разложению.\n\n## 2. Знаки, равенство концов и локальный смысл спуска\n\nЕсли \\(k\u003c0\\), то автоматически \\(R\\ge1\\), \\(c>0\\), и\n\\[\n m_j-m_i=\\frac{c+|k|m_i}{2^q}>0,\n \\qquad A=2^ic+|k|C_U>0.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nЗначит отрицательная \\(k\\)-ветвь никогда не является ветвью\n\\(m_j\u003cm_i\\). Если \\(k>0\\) и \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\), (D) даёт\n\\[\n A\\ge k3^S\\xi>0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nПри \\(m_j=m_i\\) это усиливается до точного равенства\n\\(A=k3^S\\xi\\), поэтому \\(A\\ne0\\), а высота определена.\n\nТолько для \\(k>0\\) и \\(m_j\u003cm_i\\) знак \\(A\\) не фиксирован: два слагаемых\nв (D) конкурируют. Это и есть ветвь, которую оценка без уменьшения не\nконтролирует.\n\nВо всех этих утверждениях сравниваются два конца одного блока. Локальное\nнеравенство \\(m_j\u003cm_i\\) не означает \\(m_j\u003c\\xi\\): левый конец уже мог\nнаходиться выше или ниже первоначального старта. Поэтому даже бесконечно\nмного локальных понижений не дают сцепленной глобальной убывающей цепи.\n\n## 3. Проверка формулы высоты и оценки (E)\n\nДля вектора \\((A,2^q3^S,3^{S+R})\\) его общий делитель — только степень\n3, поскольку второй и третий координаты имеют общий делитель \\(3^S\\), а\nих взаимный простой множитель не содержит 2. Примитивная высота поэтому\nдаёт точную формулу из BAKER_PERIOD_HEIGHT.md:\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}=3^{\\max(v-S,0)}\n \\max\\left(1,\\frac{2^q3^S}{|A|},\n              \\frac{3^{S+R}}{|A|}\\right). \n \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n### \\(k>0\\), \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\)\n\nИз (4)\n\\[\n \\frac{2^q3^S}{A}\\le\\frac{2^q}{k\\xi},\\qquad\n \\frac{3^{S+R}}{A}\\le\\frac{3^R}{k\\xi}.\n\\]\nЕсли \\(M=\\max(2^q,3^R)\\), принятие\n\\(M/k\\le(eq)^C\\) даёт\n\\[\n \\frac{h}{A^\\#}\\le\n \\max\\{1,1/\\xi\\}(eq)^C3^{\\max(v-S,0)}.\n \\tag{6}\n\\]\nПереход к коэффициенту \\(\\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}\\) в (E) ослабляет (6) и\nобеспечивает единый коэффициент для обеих знаковых ветвей.\n\n### \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(S\\ge1\\)\n\nПервая единица \\(U\\) в (2) даёт \\(C_U\\ge3^{S-1}\\). Вместе с (3),\n\\[\n \\frac{A}{3^S}\\ge\\frac{|k|}{3}.\n\\]\nЗдесь \\(M=3^R\\), так что обе дроби в (5) ограничены после умножения на\nкоэффициент 3 величиной \\(3(eq)^C\\). Это даёт (E).\n\n### \\(k\u003c0\\), \\(S=0\\)\n\nТеперь \\(C_U=0\\), \\(A=2^ic>0\\), а последняя единица \\(W\\) показывает\n\\(3\\nmid c\\), то есть \\(v=v_3(A)=0\\). Первая единица даёт\n\\(c\\ge3^{R-1}\\), тогда как \\(2^q\u003c3^R\\), и потому\n\\[\n \\frac{3^R}{A}\\le\\frac{3^R}{c}\\le3,\\qquad\n \\frac{2^q}{A}\u003c3.\n\\]\nФормула (5) даёт \\(h/A^\\#\\le3\\), так что этот край действительно\nпокрывается (E), хотя аргумент \\(C_U/3^S\\ge1/3\\) здесь неприменим.\n\n## 4. Нулевые и другие края\n\nЕсли первая единица \\(U\\) стоит в позиции \\(t_0\u003ci\\), то\n\\(v_2(C_U)=t_0\\), а \\(k\\) нечётно. Член \\(2^ic\\) делится на \\(2^i\\),\nпоэтому его нельзя сократить с \\(kC_U\\), и \\(A\\ne0\\). Если же \\(U=0^i\\),\nто \\(C_U=0\\), \\(A=2^ic\\). Следовательно,\n\\[\n A=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad U=0^i,\\ W=0^q,\\ S=R=0. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nВ этом случае \\(v_3(A)\\) и \\(A^\\#\\) не определены; формулы (5), (E) к\nнему не применяются. Периодическое значение равно 0. Для фиксированного\nположительного \\(\\xi=a/b\\) полное совпадение с нулевым кодом возможно\nтолько пока \\(N\\le v_2(a)\\), так что оно не образует бесконечный хвост.\n\nЕсли \\(R=0\\), то \\(c=0\\), \\(k=2^q-1>0\\). При \\(S>0\\),\n\\(A=-kC_U\u003c0\\), а нулевой \\(W\\)-блок даёт\n\\(m_j=m_i/2^q\u003cm_i\\). При \\(S=0\\) получается именно исключённый\nнулевой случай (7). Это подтверждает, что \\(R=0\\) не скрывает ветвь\nбез уменьшения.\n\n## 5. Перенос полной точности 0223/0221\n\nОтчёты 0221/0223 используют дополнительную предпосылку, которой нет у\nпроизвольного настоящего блока: код \\(\\xi\\) и периодическое продолжение\n\\(UW^\\infty\\) совпадают на всей длине\n\\[\n N=i+q+L,\\qquad L\\ge0,\n \\tag{8}\n\\]\nи их значения различны, \\(\\xi\\ne r=A/(3^Sk)\\). Настоящесть даёт только\nпервые \\(i+q\\) битов; следующие \\(L\\) битов должны действительно повторять\n\\(W\\). Поэтому полную точность нельзя молча переносить на каждый блок\nорбиты.\n\nПри \\(A\\ne0\\), \\(m=\\min(v,S)\\), 0223 корректно выводит из ненулевого\nцелого, кратного \\(2^N\\),\n\\[\n 3^m2^L\\le (|a|+b)(3/2)^S B_R,\n \\quad B_R=\\max\\{1,(3/2)^R-1\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nВ эквивалентной форме\n\\[\n 2^{S+L}\\le (|a|+b)3^{S-m}B_R. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nПри \\(k\u003c0\\) единицу в \\(B_R\\) можно снять, как установлено в 0221/0223.\nДля исключения \\(v\\ge S\\) на хвосте нужны также условия роста, например\n\\[\n S+L\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n \\limsup\\frac{R}{S+L}\u003c\\frac{\\log2}{\\log(3/2)}, \\tag{11}\n\\]\nпри фиксированном \\(\\xi\\) и \\(\\xi\\ne r\\). Одной лишь рациональности,\nнастоящести \\(U,W\\) или полного совпадения конечной длины недостаточно.\n\nДля 0221 (сильная версия) дополнительно требуется \\(k\u003c0\\); там же\nветвь \\(R\\le S\\) исключается при \\(S\\to\\infty\\), а ветвь \\(R>S\\)\nтребует порог (11) или эквивалентное отношение. Поэтому ссылку в\nкандидате на «исключение высокой валюации полным совпадением» следует\nпонимать условно: полное \\(N\\)-совпадение само по себе не доказывает\n\\(v\u003cS\\) и не всегда даёт даже \\(u_N=o(N)\\). Нужны (8),\n\\(\\xi\\ne r\\), знаковая ветвь и соответствующая асимптотическая\nгипотеза.\n\nНулевой \\(A\\) в 0223 также исключён из определения \\(v\\) и должен быть\nразобран отдельно по (7). Для \\(\\xi=r\\) ненулевое кратное \\(2^N\\) исчезает,\nтак что условие различия существенно; один пример отрицательной\nрациональной периодической точки уже опровергает удаление этого условия.\n\n## 6. Точное следствие для высотного разрыва\n\nПусть имеется семейство настоящих блоков с \\(N\\to\\infty\\), \\(A\\ne0\\),\nфиксированным \\(\\xi>0\\), \\(q\u003cN\\), и независимо доказано\n\\[\n \\max(v-S,0)=o(N). \\tag{12}\n\\]\nТогда для любой ветви \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\) из (E)\n\\[\n 0\\le\\log(h/A^\\#)\n \\le \\log\\max\\{3,1/\\xi\\}+C\\log(eq)+o(N).\n\\]\nПоскольку \\(q\u003cN\\) даёт \\(\\log(q+1)=o(N)\\), правая часть есть \\(o(N)\\).\nСледовательно, если вдоль некоторой подпоследовательности\n\\(\\log(h/A^\\#)\\ge\\delta N\\) при фиксированном \\(\\delta>0\\), такая\nподпоследовательность не может иметь \\(m_j\\ge m_i\\); она обязана иметь\n\\[\n \\boxed{m_j\u003cm_i,\\qquad j=i+q,\\quad q\u003cN.} \\tag{13}\n\\]\nЭто необходимая классификация источника возможного линейного разрыва,\nа не утверждение существования разрыва или такого блока. Условие\n\\(v\u003cS\\) является достаточным способом получить (12), но не необходимо:\nдостаточно любого контроля хвоста \\(v-S=o(N)\\) сверху.\n\n## 7. Границы интерпретации и новизна\n\nТождество (D) — непосредственное вычитание двух точных аффинных формул.\nОценка (E) — применение принятой оценки Бейкера к уже заданной\nпримитивной высоте, с отдельным разбором знаков и краёв. Делимость и\nоценка (9)--(10) — та же элементарная целая/2-адическая преграда,\nпроверенная в 0221/0223, с обязательным полным \\(N\\)-совпадением.\nСледовательно, результат следует подавать как алгебраическую запись и\nуточнение области действия существующего препятствия, без заявления\nновизны.\n\nНи (D), ни (E), ни (9) не доказывают существование нужных повторов,\nисчезновение всех бесконечных положительных орбит, спуск ниже\nпервоначального \\(\\xi\\), сцепление локальных сравнений или гипотезу\nКоллатца.\n","task-0473.md":"# Независимый ручной аудит candidate-0473 (J1–J4)\n\nДата аудита: 2026-09-18, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Проверка выполнена\nтолько ручной математикой: без программ, перебора, CAS, факторизации,\nчисленного эксперимента и дочерних агентов.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nJ1, J2, J3 и J4 корректны в заявленной области после двух явных\nуточнений формулировки: в J1 надо использовать вложение\n`E_{L+1} subset E_L` при переходе от полученного более длинного горизонта\nк `E_L`, а исключение «самого маленького tau=infinity seed» в J3 следует\nнепосредственно из переноса `tau=infinity` на меньший `m` по J1. Лемма о\nстабилизации целочисленных минимумов `mu_L` ниже приведена как отдельная\nальтернативная связь с конечными минимумами, но для этого исключения не\nнужна. С этими уточнениями контрпримера не найдено. Результат не является доказательством\nсуществования нужного свидетеля для каждого старта и не доказывает\nглобальную сходимость Коллатца.\n\n## Предварительные факты и обозначения\n\n`C_s(x)` есть произведение множителей `3/2` на нечётных входах и `1/2`\nна чётных входах первых `s` шагов. Поэтому для общей точки слияния\n`T^k(n)=T^r(m)=y` произведение множителей на хвосте после этой точки\nодинаково для обеих орбит:\n\n`C_{r+u}(m)/C_r(m) = C_{k+u}(n)/C_k(n)`.\n\nКроме того, `E_{L+1} subset E_L`; следовательно, существующие минимумы\n`mu_L` неубывают по `L`. Равенство `C_s(x)=1` для `s>0` невозможно:\nоно потребовало бы `3^q=2^s` при `q>=0`; при `q=0` имеем `1\u003c2^s`, а\nпри `q>0` взаимная простота оснований исключает равенство. Это последнее\nзамечание полезно только для дополнительного следствия о конечном времени\nпервого провала.\n\n## J1: точный перенос через действительную точку слияния\n\nГипотезы J1 дают `C_i(m)>1` для `1\u003c=i\u003c=r`. Для `L>=k` и\n`1\u003c=u\u003c=L-k` получаем\n\n`C_{r+u}(m) = C_r(m) C_{k+u}(n)/C_k(n)`.\n\nТак как `n in E_L`, `C_{k+u}(n)>1`; так как\n`C_r(m)>=C_k(n)>0`, отношение `C_r(m)/C_k(n)` не меньше единицы.\nЗначит `C_{r+u}(m)>1`. Вместе с первыми `r` префиксами это даёт\n`m in E_{r+L-k}`.\n\nЭто использует настоящий общий положительный целочисленный endpoint и\nодинаковый последующий хвост; формальная обратная ветвь здесь не подменяет\nорбиту. Коэффициентные условия достаточны для переноса, но не необходимы\nдля самого факта слияния.\n\nЕсли `tau(n)=infinity`, для любого требуемого горизонта `N` можно взять\nдостаточно большое `L>=k`, чтобы `r+L-k>=N`; первые `r` уровней уже\nданы гипотезой J1. Поэтому `tau(m)=infinity`.\n\nПри `r>=k` полученное `m in E_{r+L-k}` и вложение множеств дают\n`m in E_L`. Поэтому при `n=mu_L` это действительно противоречит\n`m\u003cn`. Важно, что при `r\u003ck` из J1 следует только\n\n`m in E_{L-(k-r)}`,\n\nто есть теряются ровно `k-r` уровней; вывод `m in E_L` в этом случае\nбыл бы ошибочным. Это главный конечный-горизонтный барьер, и в тексте\nкандидата он сформулирован правильно.\n\n## J2: срок окончания конкретного плато\n\nПусть `n=mu_s`, `s>=1`. Поскольку J1 уже даёт `m in E_r` и `m\u003cn`,\nслучай `s\u003c=r` невозможен: тогда `m in E_s`, вопреки минимальности\n`n=mu_s`.\n\nЕсли бы `r>=k`, то `s>r>=k`, и J1 при `L=s` дал бы\n`m in E_{r+s-k}`. Длина этого горизонта не меньше `s`, так что снова\n`m in E_s`, противоречие. Следовательно, любой свидетель J1, который\nможет сопровождать сохраняющийся минимум, обязан иметь `r\u003ck`.\n\nПоложим `d=k-r>0`. Из `s>r` следует `s+d>k`, поэтому J1 применим к\n`L=s+d`. Если бы `n in E_{s+d}`, перенос дал бы\n\n`m in E_{r+s+d-k}=E_s`,\n\nчто невозможно. Значит `n not in E_{s+d}`. Поскольку\n`E_{s+d} subset E_s` и `mu_s=n`, имеем одновременно\n`mu_{s+d}>=n` и `mu_{s+d}!=n`; следовательно,\n\n`mu_{s+d}>mu_s`.\n\nРезультат является сроком для данного конкретного свидетеля. Он не\nутверждает существование свидетеля с ограниченным `d` для всех\nгипотетически сохраняющихся стартов, поэтому универсальный вывод о\nконечности всех плато из J2 сам по себе не следует.\n\n## J3: семейство `128t+79`\n\nДля каждого целого `t>=0`:\n\n`n=128t+79` нечётно, `m=108t+67` нечётно,\n`y=162t+101` нечётно, и все три положительны. Имеем\n\n`n+1=16(8t+5)`.\n\nДля `0\u003c=j\u003c=4`\n\n`T^j(n)=3^j 2^{4-j}(8t+5)-1`.\n\nПри `j=0,1,2,3` это нечётное число, так как перед вычитанием единицы\nесть множитель `2`; поэтому первые четыре входа действительно нечётны.\nПри `j=4`:\n\n`T^4(n)=81(8t+5)-1=648t+404=4(162t+101)`.\n\nСледующие два входа чётны, и потому\n`T^5(n)=324t+202`, `T^6(n)=162t+101=y`, причём `y` нечётно.\nТаким образом, слово по входам имеет ровно паритеты `111100`.\n\nДля меньшего старта:\n\n`T(m)=(3(108t+67)+1)/2=162t+101=y`.\n\nАффинная связь и строгий порядок проверяются тождествами\n\n`m=(27n+11)/32`, `n-m=20t+12>0`.\n\nНа шестишаговом пути из `n` первые четыре шага нечётны, последние два\nчётны, поэтому\n\n`C_1(n)=3/2`, `C_2(n)=9/4`, `C_3(n)=27/8`, `C_4(n)=81/16`,\n`C_5(n)=81/32`, `C_6(n)=81/64`.\n\nВсе они строго больше единицы. Для одного шага из `m`,\n`C_1(m)=3/2=96/64>81/64=C_6(n)`. Следовательно, J1 и J2 применимы\nс `k=6`, `r=1`, `d=5`, и действительно\n\n`mu_s=128t+79  =>  mu_{s+5}>mu_s`.\n\nЭто верно равномерно для всех `t>=0`; проверка не использует делимость\nили исключение отдельных параметров. При `t=3u+1` получаются\n`n=384u+207` и `m=324u+175`; их делимость на три не меняет будущего\nслияния и не создаёт обратной ветви. Пример `207 -> ... -> 263` и\n`175 -> 263` согласуется с формулами, но сам по себе не является\nдоказательством расходимости или сходимости.\n\nФраза о том, что ни один член семейства не может быть наименьшим\n`tau=infinity` seed, следует прямо из J1 и не требует обращения к\nконечному минимуму `mu_s`. Если `n_*` — наименьший положительный старт с\n`tau(n_*)=infinity` и одновременно член семейства, J3 даёт меньший\n`m\u003cn_*` и условия J1; перенос в J1 даёт `tau(m)=infinity`, что сразу\nпротиворечит минимальности `n_*`. Если таких seed вообще нет,\nутверждение о невозможности для членов семейства, конечно, вакуумно\nверно.\n\nОтдельно, как полезная связь с конечными минимумами: если `n_*` — любой\nнаименьший старт с `tau(n_*)=infinity`, то `mu_L\u003c=n_*` для всех `L`. Если\nбы `mu_L\u003cn_*` для каждого `L`, неубывающая целочисленная\nпоследовательность `mu_L`, ограниченная сверху `n_*`, стабилизировалась бы\nна некотором `a\u003cn_*`; стабильное значение лежало бы во всех `E_L` и имело\nбы `tau(a)=infinity`, противореча минимальности `n_*`. Значит,\n`n_*=mu_s` для некоторого конечного `s`. Это альтернативная связь с\nминимумами, но не необходимый шаг для исключения семейства.\n\n## J4: что нужно для полной сходимости\n\nУсловие «для каждого `n>1` существуют `m\u003cn` и конечные\n`k,r>=0` такие, что `T^k(n)=T^r(m)`» действительно достаточно.\nСильная индукция по `n`: для меньшего `m` уже известна сходимость к `1`;\nточка `T^r(m)` поэтому также достигает `1`, а поскольку она равна\n`T^k(n)`, к `1` достигает и `n`. База `n=1` непосредственна. Для\nчётного `n` условие уже выполняется с `m=n/2`, `k=1`, `r=0`.\n\nОбратное направление также точно: если все старты сходятся, для каждого\n`n>1` можно взять `m=1`, `r=0` и конечное `k`, для которого `T^k(n)=1`.\nТаким образом, это эквивалентный критерий, а не доказанный новый\nуниверсальный механизм.\n\nДля наименьшего контрпримера к сходимости достаточно любого меньшего\nсливающегося `m`: по индукции общий endpoint сойдётся, и значит сойдётся\n`n`. Для наименьшего старта с `tau=infinity` этого недостаточно: обычное\nслияние не переносит условие `C_s>1`. Нужны именно условия J1, гарантирующие\n`tau(m)=infinity`; только тогда меньший `m` противоречит минимальности в\nобъекте `tau=infinity`. Эти два минимума логически различны. Отдельный\nаргумент с входом G100 из предыдущего материала не заменяется J4 и сам по\nсебе не исключает нетривиальные циклы.\n\n## Дополнительное проверенное следствие конечного провала\n\nЕсли в J1 `tau(m)=a\u003cinfinity` обозначает первый индекс с\n`C_a(m)\u003c1`, то `a>r`, поскольку `m in E_r`. Если `tau(n)>=k`,\nсамо по себе это ещё не даёт права применить J1 к более длинному\nгоризонту. Правильная контрапозиция такова: положим\n`L=a+k-r`; это число строго больше `k`. Если бы `tau(n)>L`, то\n`n in E_L`, и J1 дал бы `m in E_a`, противоречие. Поэтому всегда\n\n`tau(n)\u003c=a+k-r`.\n\nДля J3 это даёт лишь `tau(128t+79)\u003c=tau(108t+67)+5` при конечном\n`tau(m)`; это не является универсальным сроком и не заменяет J2.\n\n## Итоговый terminal verdict\n\nPASS — J1–J4 математически выдерживают независимую ручную проверку.\nСохранить оговорку: при `r\u003ck` перенос имеет только горизонт\n`L-(k-r)`; исключение наименьшего `tau=infinity` seed для J3 следует\nнепосредственно из J1. Лемма о стабилизации `mu_L` остаётся верной как\nальтернативная связь, но не является необходимой гипотезой. Контрпримеров арифметике, паритетам, квантору\n`t>=0`, равенству случаев или различению двух типов минимальности не\nобнаружено. Доказательства остаются локальными и не дают универсального\nслияния для всех стартов или полной теоремы Коллатца.\n","task-0474.md":"# Независимый ручной аудит Candidate0474 (K1–K5)\n\nДата аудита: 2026-09-18, Asia/Tashkent (UTC+05:00). Проверка выполнена\nтолько ручной аналитикой: без математических программ, CAS, численных\nопытов, перебора, факторизации и дочерних агентов.\n\n## Вердикт\n\nK1–K5 корректны в заявленной условной области. Существенная граница K2\nсохранена: равенство не следует отбрасывать молча. При `D>0` оно возможно\nарифметически ровно тогда, когда `D | K`; тогда кандидат `e=K/D`\nавтоматически нечётен и имеет именно требуемую 2-адическую длину `d`.\nСамо равенство порождает настоящий положительный цикл с одним блоком\nнечётных входов, поэтому его устранение требует отдельного внешнего\nзапрета циклов. Если явно импортировать известный запрет нетривиальных\nодноблочных циклов (Steiner, в изложении Simons–de Weger 2005, §1.4), он\nснимает этот единственный случай; в настоящем аудите такой внешний proof\nне воспроизводится и молча не используется.\n\nK4 — правильная повторная индукция уже принятого архивного аргумента\nFB162.2–3 из `work/round162-n-finite-excess-bounds.md` и\n`work/round162-audit-finite-excess-bounds.md`, а не новый самостоятельный\nрезультат. K5 является его явным следствием для коэффициента и\nнеобходимой сложности свидетеля. Универсального существования свидетелей,\nохвата всех стартов или доказательства Коллатца эти пункты не дают.\n\n## K1: точная серия `1^p0^d`\n\nПри `n=2^p e-1`, `p>=1`, `e` нечётном, итерация нечётной ветви даёт\n\n`T^p(n)=3^p e-1`.\n\nЕсли `d=v_2(3^p e-1)>=1`, последующие `d` входов чётны и конец\n`y=(3^p e-1)/2^d` нечётен; значит слово по входам действительно имеет\nдлину `p+d` и паритеты `1^p0^d`. Обратное условие на фиксированные `p,d`\nесть\n\n`3^p e == 1+2^d (mod 2^(d+1))`.\n\nОно одновременно даёт точность `v_2(...)=d` и нечётность `e`, поскольку\nправая часть нечётна. Поэтому это ровно класс всех положительных источников\nс указанными `p,d`, без дополнительного условия `3∤e`.\n\nКоэффициенты первых `p` префиксов равны `(3/2)^j`; после этого они\nубывают при делениях и минимальны на конце как `C=3^p/2^(p+d)`. Поэтому\n`C>1` действительно означает, что все префиксы больше единицы, и\nэквивалентно `d\u003calpha p`, где `alpha=log_2(3)-1`.\n\nПоложим `b=v_3(2^d-1)`. Из `C>1` следует `3^p>2^(p+d)>2^d-1`, так\nчто `b\u003cp`. В числителе\n\n`y+1=(3^p e+2^d-1)/2^d`\n\nдва слагаемых имеют 3-адические порядки не менее `p` и ровно `b`\nсоответственно; они различны, поэтому `v_3(y+1)=b`. Формулы\n`b=0` при нечётном `d` и `b=1+v_3(d)` при чётном `d` следуют из LTE,\nприменённой к `4^(d/2)-1` (либо из последовательного разложения по\nстепеням тройки). Также `b\u003cd`, что нужно ниже.\n\n## K2: минимальный нечётный источник и равенство\n\nГлубочайший допустимый чисто нечётный прообраз `y` имеет вид\n\n`u=(2/3)^b(y+1)-1`.\n\nОн целый и нечётный: `3^b | y+1`, а `y+1` чётно, так что `u+1` имеет\nдостаточную степень двойки. Для `b>=1` он положителен; при `b=0` это\nпросто `u=y`. Источники на более коротких чисто нечётных ветвях, если они\nесть, не меньше `u`, поскольку каждый дополнительный обратный шаг\nумножает `y+1` на `2/3`.\n\nС обозначениями кандидата прямое раскрытие даёт\n\n`R=C/(3/2)^b=3^(p-b)/2^(p+d-b)`,\n\n`u+1=R(n+1)+c`, где\n`c=(2/3)^b(1-2^(-d))`, и\n\n`n-u=(D e-K)/2^(d-b)`,\n\nгде `D=2^(p+d-b)-3^(p-b)` и `K=(2^d-1)/3^b`. Здесь `0\u003cc\u003c1` и `R!=1`;\nравенство `R=1` было бы равенством ненулевых степеней двойки и тройки.\n\nЕсли `D\u003c0`, то `n-u\u003c0` и чисто нечётный источник не меньше `n`.\nЕсли `D>0`, то `R\u003c1`, следовательно `n-u>-1`; так как это целое,\n`u\u003c=n`. Строгое уменьшение имеет место ровно при `D e>K`.\n\nРавенство возможно ровно при `e=K/D`. В режиме `D>0` число `D` нечётно,\nкак разность чётного и нечётного, а `K` нечётно. Поэтому `D|K`\nавтоматически делает `e` положительным нечётным целым. Более того,\nиз `De=K` после умножения на `3^b` следует точное тождество\n\n`3^p e-1 = 2^d(3^b 2^(p-b)e-1)`.\n\nСкобка справа нечётна, поскольку `p-b>=1`; значит `v_2(3^p e-1)=d`\nровно. Таким образом, условие `D|K` не просто формально решает линейное\nравенство: оно автоматически возвращает кандидата в исходный допустимый\nкласс `1^p0^d`. Обратно, любой допустимый случай равенства даёт `De=K`,\nто есть `D|K`. Это точный критерий, а не утверждение о существовании\nтакой пары `p,d`.\n\nПри равенстве `n=u`, и две настоящие траектории из одного `n` дают общий\nконец: чисто нечётная ветвь длины `b` и исходная ветвь длины `p+d`.\nСледовательно, для `z=T^b(n)` остаток исходного слова `1^(p-b)0^d`\nвозвращает `z` в `z`. Это положительный цикл; при `D>0` и `C>1`\nобязательно `b>=1`, а `z=3^b2^(p-b)e-1>=5`. Поэтому равенство является\nотдельным циклическим обязательством, а не допустимой заменой строгого\nуменьшения.\n\nСравнение коэффициентов альтернативного источника с исходным точно\nэквивалентно `D>0`. В логарифмической форме это\n\n`d \u003c alpha p \u003c d+alpha b`.\n\nОба края невозможны: первый дал бы `3^p=2^(p+d)`, второй —\n`3^(p-b)=2^(p-b+d)`. При фиксированном чётном `d` интервалы для целого\n`p` имеют вид\n\n`floor(d/alpha)+1, ..., floor(d/alpha)+b`,\n\nи содержат ровно `b` значений. Здесь `b\u003cd` и `alpha\u003c1`, поэтому условие\n`p>b` уже обеспечено нижней границей. При нечётном `d` имеем `b=0` и\nполоса пуста. Это только счёт параметров, не плотность и не утверждение\nо посещении этих параметров орбитами.\n\n## K3: перенос на минимумы\n\nПри `D>0` и строгом `De>K` имеем `b>=1`, `u\u003cn`, настоящий общий endpoint\nи `C_b(u)=(3/2)^b>C`. Все префиксы пути из `u` до endpoint больше единицы,\nпоэтому условия J1 выполнены с `r=b`, `k=p+d`. Для `n=mu_s` при `s>=1`\nJ2 даёт\n\n`mu_(s+p+d-b)>mu_s`.\n\nЭто применимо только при строгом размере; равенство не даёт меньшего\nисточника и потому не даёт J2. Если `tau(u)` конечна, тот же J1/J6 даёт\n`tau(n)\u003c=tau(u)+p+d-b`; это условное неравенство не превращается в\nравномерный срок без контроля `tau(u)`.\n\nДля фиксированных `p,d` с `D>0` все положительные члены класса, кроме\nвозможного единственного `e=K/D`, удовлетворяют строгому `De>K`.\nОднако у фиксированного `n` значения `p,d` определяются его реальной\nтраекторией; универсальное попадание в полосу не доказано.\n\n## K4: высота при ограниченном числе нечётных блоков\n\nЗдесь требуется явно понимать `h` как целое `h>=0`, а «путь» — как\nконечный настоящий положительный путь. Для серии из `p` последовательных\nнечётных входов, в том числе для усечённой первой или последней серии,\n\n`T^p(x)+1=(3/2)^p(x+1)`, `2^p | x+1`,\n\nоткуда `p\u003c=log_2(x+1)` и\n`T^p(x)+1\u003c=(x+1)^rho`, `rho=log_2 3`. То же неравенство верно для\nкаждой промежуточной вершины серии. Чётный шаг уменьшает положительное\nсостояние и не увеличивает `x+1`.\n\nИндукция по числу уже пройденных серий даёт для **одномерной** нумерации:\nперед `j`-й серией степень `rho^(j-1)`, а после неё степень `rho^j`.\nПоэтому при не\nболее чем `h` сериях каждая вершина удовлетворяет\n\n`x+1\u003c=(m+1)^(rho^h)`.\n\nКрай `h=0` корректен: путь не содержит нечётных входов, все чётные шаги\nне увеличивают `x+1`, а при нулевой длине остаётся исходная вершина.\nИменно эта аргументация, включая усечённые блоки и край `h=0`, уже\nсодержится в FB162.2–3; K4 переносит её в другую запись с `rho`.\n\n## K5: коэффициент и необходимые потолки\n\nЕсли `p_j` — длина `j`-го нечётного блока, K4 до его начала даёт\n\n`p_j\u003c=rho^(j-1) log_2(m+1)`.\n\nДля `q=sum p_j` имеем\n\n`q\u003c=((rho^h-1)/(rho-1)) log_2(m+1)`.\n\nЕсли `C_end` обозначает линейный коэффициент аффинного пути\n`3^q/2^s`, то отбрасывание факторов `1/2` чётных шагов даёт\n\n`C_end\u003c=(3/2)^q\u003c=(m+1)^(rho^h-1)`.\n\nЗдесь `C_end` — именно коэффициент аффинного отображения; через чётные\nшаги его нельзя ошибочно отождествлять с отношением `(T^s(m)+1)/(m+1)`.\nДля K5 требуется только коэффициентная оценка, а высота endpoint берётся\nотдельно из K4.\n\nЕсли `m\u003cn` и этот путь из `m` приходит в `y=T^k(n)` с не более чем `h`\nнечётными блоками, то `m+1\u003c=n`, поэтому\n\n`y+1\u003c=(m+1)^(rho^h)\u003c=n^(rho^h)`.\n\nЕсли одновременно выполнено доминирование J1,\n`C_r(m)>=C_k(n)`, то K5 даёт необходимое условие\n\n`C_k(n)\u003c=(m+1)^(rho^h-1)\u003c=n^(rho^h-1)`.\n\nПри `C_k(n)>1` и целом `h>=0` отсюда точно следует\n\n`h>=ceil(log_rho(1+log(C_k(n))/log n))`.\n\nЕсли `y+1>n`, из endpoint-оценки отдельно следует\n\n`h>=ceil(log_rho(log(y+1)/log n))`.\n\nОбе формулы используют положительные логарифмы в заявленных областях;\nэто необходимые ограничения конкретного свидетеля, а не существование\nсвидетеля и не нижняя граница времени доказательства Коллатца.\n\nДля фиксированных `n,h` потолок `n^(rho^h)` конечен. Поэтому если орбита\nфиксированного `n` действительно стремится к бесконечности, достаточно\nпоздние endpoint'ы не могут иметь меньшего свидетеля с не более чем `h`\nблоками. Это не исключает ранний свидетель. Аналогично, если\n`C_k(n)->infinity`, потолок коэффициента исключает поздние доминирующие\nсвидетельства при фиксированном `h`; это условный барьер, а не утверждение\nо существовании расходящейся орбиты.\n\n## Итоговый terminal verdict (K1–K5)\n\nPASS — K1–K5 выдерживают независимую ручную проверку при явных условиях\n`p>=1`, `d>=1`, `C>1`, `h in Z_{>=0}` и при различении коэффициента\nаффинного пути от отношения значений. Контрпримеров формулам, паритетам,\nточному равенству, автоматической допустимости `e=K/D`, подсчёту `p`, краю\n`h=0` или целым потолкам не найдено. Equality-case нельзя удалять без\nотдельного запрета соответствующего положительного цикла; внешний запрет\nможно подключить только явно. K4 повторяет известный архивный аргумент,\nK5 остаётся условной необходимой оценкой. Полная универсальная\nсходимость, охват всех стартов и исключение всех положительных циклов этим\nаудитом не установлены.\n\n## Дополнительный аудит K7\n\nK7 также корректен при тех же условиях: путь конечный и положительный,\n`h` — целое неотрицательное число, а `q,z` — соответственно числа\nнечётных и чётных входов. Положим `N=m+1`. На нечётном входе множитель для\n`x+1` равен ровно `3/2`. На чётном положительном входе\n\n`(T(x)+1)/(x+1)=(x+2)/(2x+2)\u003c=2/3`,\n\nпоскольку `x>=2`. Если конечная вершина положительна, её значение плюс\nединица не меньше двух. Поэтому после всего пути\n\n`2 \u003c= N(3/2)^q(2/3)^z=N(3/2)^(q-z)`,\n\nи\n\n`z\u003c=q+log_(3/2)(N/2)`.\n\nЭто не требует предварительного бюджета на чётные шаги. Вместе с K5,\n`q\u003c=((rho^h-1)/alpha)log_2 N`, и с тождеством\n`log_(3/2)(N/2)=(log_2 N-1)/alpha` получаем\n\n`q+z \u003c= ((2rho^h-1)log_2 N-1)/alpha`.\n\nДля `m\u003cn` имеем `N\u003c=n`; так как длина целая, отсюда следует заявленный\nпотолок\n\n`r\u003c=floor(((2rho^h-1)log_2 n-1)/alpha)`.\n\nКрай `h=0` согласован: тогда `q=0`, а разрешённый путь состоит только из\nчётных шагов. Для `m=1` и `h=0` непустой путь невозможен (первый вход был\nбы нечётным), и формула даёт `r\u003c=0`. Вывод ограничивает только время\nальтернативного пути из меньшего `m`; независимая длина `k` прямого пути из\n`n` к endpoint остаётся неограниченной этим аргументом. Следовательно,\nK7 делает поиск слов фиксированных `n,h` конечным лишь в своём\nальтернативном плече; он не является алгоритмом полной сходимости и не\nтребует выполнения такого поиска.\n\nИтог по K7: PASS. Потолок полной длины следует из точного отношения на\nнечётном входе, верхнего отношения `2/3` на чётном положительном входе и\nнижней границы `T^r(m)+1>=2`; край `h=0`, включая `m=1`, и независимое\nвремя `k` из `n` обработаны корректно.\n\n## Логическое следствие K8 при внешнем E1\n\nВнешний вход E1 принимается именно в установленном виде: у shortcut-plus\nmap нет нетривиального положительного 1-цикла, то есть цикла с одним\nвозрастающим нечётным блоком и одним убывающим чётным блоком. Это известный\nрезультат Steiner, покрытый также Simons–de Weger (2005), Theorem 3(b),\nс терминологией §1.2; здесь не перепроверяются его историческое\nдоказательство и вычислительные входы.\n\nРавенство K2 даёт цикл со словом `1^(p-b)0^d`. При `D>0` и `C>1`\nимеем `b>=1`, `p-b>=1`, `d>=1` и `z>=5`, поэтому это ровно нетривиальный\nположительный 1-цикл в смысле E1. Следовательно, E1 исключает единственный\nкандидат равенства. Поскольку при `D>0` уже доказано `n-u>=0`, остаются\nтолько равенство или `n-u>0`; после исключения равенства получаем\n\n`u\u003cn`\n\nдля каждого настоящего старта фиксированного класса `p,d`. Значит, в этой\nобласти K3/J1–J2 применяются без оставшегося исключительного `e=K/D`.\nЭто лишь применение установленной внешней теоремы, а не новая теорема об\n1-циклах и не запрет циклов с произвольным числом нечётных блоков.\n"};
